SGO_COMMENTARY.push({"id":"jerome-more-gen-mal","author":"St Jerome","work":"Commentary on Matthew; Commentary on Isaiah; Commentary on Ezekiel; Homilies on Psalms; and other works","translator":"New English translations from the Historical Christian Faith Writings Database, made from Migne's Greek and Latin text or the 19th-century Russian and dedicated to the public domain, many of them machine-assisted; plus public-domain ANF/NPNF texts","lang":"en","license":"Public Domain","source":"https://github.com/HistoricalChristianFaith/Commentaries-Database (Jerome/)","versification":"kjv","tradition":"west","notes":{"GEN.1.1":["I have received the desired letters of my Desiderius, who in a foretelling of things to happen has obtained with Daniel a certain name [see Vulgate Daniel 9.23: quia vir desideriorum es tu, \"for you are a man of desires\"], beseeching that I might hand over to our hearers a translation of the Pentateuch in the Latin tongue from the Hebrew words. Certainly a dangerous work, open to the barkings of detractors, who accuse me of insult to the Seventy to prepare a new interpretation from the old ones, thus approving ability (or \"genius\") like wine. As has very often been testified by me, I, for my part, am able to offer a portion in the Tabernacle of God, without the riches (or \"abilities\") of one being damaged by the poverties of others.\nBut that I may have dared, the effort of Origen provoked me, who mixed the translation of Theodotion to the ancient edition, with asterisk and obelus, that is, star and spit, a work distinguishing everything, while he either makes to shine those things which were previously lacking, or he slays and pierces through everything superfluous. And especially by the authority of the Evangelists and the Apostles, in which we read many things from the Old Testament which are not found in our books, as it is (with): \"Out of Egypt I have called My Son,\" and \"For He shall be called a Nazarene,\" and \"They will look on Him Whom they have pierced,\" and \"Rivers of living waters shall flow from his belly,\" and \"Things which no eye has seen, nor ear heard, nor has arisen in the heart of man, which God has prepared for those loving Him,\" and many others which are desiring a proper context (or \"book\" [Jerome uses a Greek word here: συνταγμα]).\nTherefore let us ask them where these are written, and when they are unable to say, we may produce them from the Hebrew books. The first witness is in Hosea, the second in Isaiah, the third in Zechariah, the fourth in Proverbs, the fifth is also in Isaiah, of which many are ignorant, the follies of apocrypha being followed, preferring Iberian dirges to authentic books.\nThe cause of the error is not for me to explain. The Jews say it was done wisely in deliberation, so Ptolemy, the worshipper of one god, might not yet discover a double divinity with the Hebrews; he made them (do so) chiefly for this reason, because he was seen to fall into the dogma of Plato. Accordingly, wherever anything sacred in Scripture is witnessed of the Father and Son and Holy Spirit, they are either translated otherwise, or they have passed over all in silence, so they might both satisfy the king, and might not divulge the secret of the Faith.\nAnd I don't know who was the first author to construct with his lying the seventy cells in Alexandria, into which were divided those who wrote, with Aristeas the champion [another Greek word: υπερασπιστης] of the same Ptolemy, and many after the time of Josephus having reported no such thing, but rather (for them) to have gathered in groups, writing in one basilica, (and) not to have prophesied.\nFor it is one thing to be a seer, another to be an interpreter. In that one the Spirit predicts things to come; in this one by his learning and abundance of words he translates those things he has understood. Unless Tullius (Cicero) is understood to have translated, by inspiration of the spirit of rhetoric, the Economics of Xenophon, the Protagoras of Plato, and the For Ctesiphon by Demosthenes. Or the Holy Spirit wove together the witnesses of these books one way through the Seventy interpreters and another way through the Apostles, so that what they passed over in silence, what was written by these was invented [? - obscure].\nTherefore, what? We condemn the ancients? By no means! But after those earlier in the House of God, we work at what we can. They are interpreted before the coming of Christ and what they didn't know, they tranlated in ambiguous (or \"uncertain\") sentences. We write after His Passion and Resurrection, not so much prophecy as history. For in the one are told what things were heard, in the other what were seen. What we understand better, we also translate better.\nHear, therefore, O rival; listen, O detractor! I do not condemn, I do not censure the Seventy, but I confidently prefer the Apostles to all of them. Christ speaks to me through their mouth, who I read were placed before the prophets among the Spiritual gifts, among which interpreters hold almost the last place. Why are you tortured by spite? Why do you incite ignorant souls against me? If anywhere in the translation I have been seen by you to err, ask the Hebrews. Consult the teachers of the many different cities. What theirs have of Christ, yours do not have. It is another matter if they have afterward removed the testimonies used by the Apostles against them, and the Latin copies are more correct than the Greek, (and) the Greek than the Hebrew! Truth is against these enviers.\nNow I pray you, dearest Desiderius, so that in such a great work which you have made me undertake and take up a beginning from Genesis, you might help in (your) prayers, how I might, by the same Spirit by Whom the books were written, be able to translate them into Latin words.\n— Prologue to Genesis","Chapter 1, Verse 1. In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth. Many people believe, as it is also written in the Altercation of Jason and Papiscus, and Tertullian in his book against Praxeas argues: and also Hilary in the exposition of a certain Psalm affirms, that it is found in Hebrew: 'In the son, God created the heavens and the earth', which is false, as the truth of the matter itself proves. For even the Seventy Interpreters, and Symmachus, and Theodotion translated it as 'In the beginning'. And it is written in Hebrew, Bresith (); which Aquila interprets, in the chapter: and not Baben (), which is called ((Al. interpreted)), in the son. Therefore, it can be understood more according to the meaning than according to the literal translation about Christ: who is approved both in the very beginning of Genesis, which is the head of all books, and also in the beginning of the Gospel of John, as the creator of heaven and earth. Hence, in the Psalms (Psalm 39:9), he says about himself: In the chapter ((Al. chapter)) of the book it is written about me, that is, in the beginning of Genesis. And in the Gospel: All things were made through him, and without him nothing was made (John 1:3). But it should also be known that among the Hebrews this book is called B'reishit: having the custom of giving names to their volumes from their beginnings.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.1.2":["(Verse 2.) And the spirit of God was moving over the waters. Because in our books it is written 'was moving', in Hebrew it has 'Merefeth', which we can call 'hovering' or 'brooding', in the likeness of a bird, warming the eggs with the heat of life. From which we understand that it is not said to be the spirit of the world, as some suppose, but the Holy Spirit, who is also said to be the giver of life to all things from the beginning. And if a giver of life, then also a creator. But if You are the creator and God. For, He says, send forth Your Spirit, and they shall be created (Psalm 103, 30).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.1.10":["(10) And he called the gatherings of waters seas. It should be noted that every gathering of waters, whether they be salty or fresh, according to the language of the Hebrews, are called seas. Therefore, Porphyry falsely accuses the Evangelists of ignorance in performing a miracle, because the Lord walked on the sea, that he called the lake of Genezareth a sea, when every lake and gathering of waters are called seas.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.1.27":["Have you not read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female? This is written at the beginning of Genesis. But by saying male and female, he shows that second marriages are to be avoided. For he does not say: male and females, which is what was sought by the repudiation of the former wives, but male and female, so that the partnership of a single marriage might be bound together.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GEN.2.2":["(Chapter 2, Verse 2) And on the sixth day God finished his work that he had done, and he rested on the seventh day from all his work that he had done. Therefore, we will refute the Jews, who boast of the rest of the Sabbath, because even then, at the beginning, the Sabbath was dissolved, since God works on the Sabbath, completing his work in it, and blessing the day itself: because in it he has completed everything.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.8":["(Verse 8.) And the Lord God planted a garden in Eden, in the east. Instead of paradise, in Hebrew it has the word 'hortum' which means garden. And Eden is translated as 'deliciae'. Symmachus translated it as a flourishing paradise. Also, what follows, 'in the east', in Hebrew it is written as 'Mecedem', which Aquila translated as 'ἀπὸ ἀρχῆς', and we can say 'from the beginning'. Symmachus, however, uses 'ἐκ πρώτης', and Theodotion uses 'ἐν πρώτοις', which also signifies not the east, but the beginning. From this it is most clearly shown that before God made heaven and earth, He had already established paradise, as it is read in Hebrew: But the Lord God had planted a paradise in Eden from the beginning.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.9":["But we shall combine spiritual things with spiritual things and read of the tree of life that was planted in Paradise, the tree of life and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. This tree of life was planted in the Garden of Eden and in Eden there rose a river that separated into four branches... Likewise we read in Solomon—if one accepts that book as Solomon's, for he speaks there of wisdom (Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God)—then, as I was saying, where Solomon says: \"She is a tree of life to those who grasp her,\" he is speaking of wisdom. Now, if wisdom is the tree of life, Wisdom itself, indeed, is Christ. You understand now that the man who is blessed and holy is compared to this tree, that is, he is compared to Wisdom. Consequently, you see, too, that the just man, that blessed man who has not followed in the counsel of the wicked—who has not done that but has done this—is like the tree that is planted near running water. He is, in other words, like Christ, inasmuch as He \"raised us up together, and seated us together in heaven.\" You see, then, that we shall reign together with Christ in heaven; you see, too, that because this tree has been planted in the Garden of Eden, we have all been planted there together with Him.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"GEN.2.11":["(Verse 11.) The name of one is Phison. They believe this to be the river Ganges in India.\n\n(Verse 12.) Where (or There) is the carbuncle and the green stone. Some have translated carbuncle as βδέλλιον and the green stone as ὄνυχα.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.15":["(Verse 15.) And the Lord God took the man, and put him into the garden of delight. For delight, in Hebrew, it is called Eden. Therefore, the Seventy have interpreted it as delight. But Symmachus, who had just before translated it as flourishing paradise, here puts it as 'in the garden of the shore', which also signifies pleasantness and luxury.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.17":["(Verse 17) But on whatever day you eat from it, you shall surely die. Symmachus interpreted it better, saying, you shall be mortal.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.21":["(Ver. 21.) And the Lord God sent a trance upon Adam. For trance, that is, the departure of the mind, is called Thardema in Hebrew, which Aquila interprets as descent, and Symmachus as heavy and deep sleep. Finally, it follows: And he slept. The same word is used in Jonah (1:5) for a deep sleep.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.23":["(Verse 23.) Now this is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man. It does not seem to sound in Greek and Latin why she is called Woman, because she was taken out of Man; but the etymology is preserved in the Hebrew language. For Man is called 'Is', and Woman is called 'Issa'. Therefore, she was rightly called Woman from Man. Hence, Symmachus also desired to preserve the etymology even in Greek, saying: She shall be called ἀνδρὶς, because she was taken from ἀνδρός, which we can say in Latin: She shall be called virago, because she was taken out of Man. Furthermore, Theodotius suspected another etymology, saying: This will be called 'assumptio' because it is taken from a man. Indeed, 'Issa' can be understood according to the variation of accent and assumption.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.2.24":["For this reason a man leaves father and mother and will cleave to his wife. Likewise he says: will cleave to his wife, not to wives.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And the two shall be in one flesh. The reward of marriage is that from two there comes to be one flesh. Chastity joined to the spirit becomes one spirit.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GEN.3.1":["(Chapter 3, Verse 1) Now the serpent was more cunning than any other wild animal that was on the earth. In Hebrew, the word for cunning is Arom (אָרוּם), which Aquila and Theodotion translate as πανοῦργον. This means wicked and deceitful. Therefore, from this word, it is demonstrated more of a craftiness and cunningness than wisdom.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.8":["We read in Genesis that when Adam transgressed, when he paid heed to the serpent rather than to God, when he hid himself from the face of God, then God came into the garden and was walking about in the cool of day. Now listen to what the Scripture says. God sought out Adam, not at midday but in the evening. Adam had already lost the sunlight, for his high noon was over.\n— Homily 1 (on Psalm 1)","(Verse 8) And they heard the voice of the Lord God walking in paradise at evening. In many Latin manuscripts, instead of 'at evening,' it has 'in the afternoon' because we cannot translate the Greek word 'δειλινὸν' word for word. In Hebrew it is written 'Larue Aiom,' which Aquila interpreted as 'in the wind of the day,' that is, in the wind of the day. But Symmachus interpreted it as 'through the spirit of the day.' Moreover, Theodotion clearly showed in the spirit the cooling of the day, so that after the heat of noon had passed, he displayed the refreshing breath of the breeze.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.14":["(Verse 14.) You shall tread upon your chest and your stomach. The Septuagint translators added the stomach, but in Hebrew it only has the chest, to reveal the cunning and cunning thoughts, that all his steps are wickedness and deceit. But also what follows: You shall eat dust. In Hebrew it is written 'Aphar,' which we can say is ashes and dust.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.15":["(Verse 15) He himself will keep your head, and you will keep his heel. It is better in Hebrew: He will crush your head, and you will crush his heel; for our steps are hindered by the serpent, and the Lord will quickly crush Satan under our feet.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.16":["(Verse 16) I will multiply your sorrows and your groanings. For sorrow and groaning, it has pains and conception in Hebrew.\n\nAnd for your conversion. Regarding the conversion of Aquila, the society: Symmachus transferred the desire or impulse.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","And also subject to their husbands: lest, puffed up by riches and rank, they not remember the sentence of God by which they are subjected to their husbands. For he says to the woman: \"Your turning shall be to your husband, and he shall rule over you\" (Gen. 3:16). In this the prudence of holy Scripture is to be considered: that the Lord did not say this to the man, saying, \"You shall rule over your wife\", but to the woman herself, so as to leave her the reward of compliance, since it is in her own power, if she wishes to obey the precepts of God, to serve the man and to be subject to her husband, so that it might in some way be a free servitude, and full of love; she is therefore serving her husband while she fears to offend him. For man was not created for the woman, but woman for the man. And since the man is the head of the woman, and Christ the head of the man, any wife who is not subject to her husband, that is, to her head, is guilty of the same crime as the man is if he is not subject to Christ, his head. And the word of the Lord is blasphemed either when the first sentence of God is despised and counted for nothing, or when the Gospel of Christ is defamed, while she who is a Christian, and subject by the law of God, desires, against the law and the faith of nature, to command her husband: when even Gentile women serve their husbands by the common law of nature.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"GEN.3.17":["(Verse 17.) Cursed is the ground because of your actions. The works here do not refer to cultivating the land, as many think, but rather sins are signified. As it is written in Hebrew: And Aquila does not disagree, saying: Cursed is the soil because of you. And Theodotion: Cursed is the earth because of your transgression.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.18-19":["But he who sows among thorns, this is he who hears the word, and the care of this world and the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, and it is made fruitless. It seems to me that what is said to Adam according to the letter, Among thorns and thistles you shall eat your bread, also signifies mystically that whoever gives himself to the pleasures of the world and the cares of this world eats the heavenly bread and the true food among thorns. And he aptly added: the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word. For riches are alluring, doing one thing and promising another. Their possession is slippery, since they are carried about hither and thither, and with unsteady step they either desert those who have them or stuff those who do not have them. Hence the Lord also asserts that the rich enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, since riches choke the word of God and soften the rigor of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GEN.3.20":["(Verse 20) And Adam called his wife's name Eve, because she is the mother of all living. Therefore, Eve, which means life, is called so, because she is the mother of all living. Eve indeed translates to life.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.3.22-24":["But if we read according to the Seventy, who rendered the sense from the Hebrew rather than the words, the days of the tree of life, we understand the tree of life which is set in paradise. Lest Adam should stretch out his hand to it and live, he was cast out of paradise (Gen 3). To guard it were set the Cherubim, that is, a multitude of knowledge, and a fiery sword, to keep the way of the tree of life, lest Adam, placed in sin and not yet acknowledging his offenses, should eat of it and die the death of impenitence, despair and pride.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"GEN.3.24":["(Verse 24.) And Adam was cast out, and he made him dwell opposite the paradise of pleasure. And he established Cherubim and a flaming sword, which turned to guard the way to the tree of life. There is much more meaning in the Hebrew than is understood here. For it says: And Adam was cast out, no doubt by the Lord: And he made him dwell before the paradise of pleasure, Cherubim, and a flaming sword, which turned, and guarded the way to the tree of life. Not that Adam himself, whom God had cast out, was made to dwell opposite the paradise of pleasure; but that after his expulsion, before the doors of paradise, Cherubim and a flaming sword were placed to guard the entrance to paradise, so that no one could enter.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.1":["(Chapter 4, Verse 1) And she conceived and gave birth to Cain, and said, I have gained a man from God. Cain, meaning acquisition or possession, is interpreted as κτῆσις in Greek, and expressing its etymology, it is said, Canithi, meaning possession of a man from God.\n\n(Ver. 4.) And God looked upon Abel and upon his offerings: but upon Cain and his offerings he did not look. And Cain was very angry, and his countenance fell. So Cain knew that God had accepted his brother's offerings and rejected his own, unless that interpretation which Theodotion has given is true. And the Lord was angry with Abel and his offering: but with Cain and his offering he was not angry. But fire used to come from heaven to devour the sacrifice: and we read of it in the dedication of the temple under Solomon, and when Elijah built an altar on Mount Carmel (2 Chronicles 7:1; 1 Kings 18:38).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.6":["(Verse 6.) And the Lord said to Cain: Why is your face downcast? If you do what is right, will you not be accepted? But if you do not do what is right, sin is crouching at your door; it desires to have you, but you must master it. We are compelled to linger longer on each individual. For even now the meaning is very different in the Hebrew than in the translators of the Septuagint. For the Lord said to Cain: Why are you angry? Why is your face downcast? If you do what is right, will you not be accepted? But if you do not do what is right, sin is crouching at your door; it desires to have you, but you must master it. And to you is his alliance: but you must rule over him more. But, he says: Why are you angry, and why are you tormented by envy towards your brother, with the bitterness of jealousy, you turn your face to the ground? If you do well, will not your sin be forgiven you? Or, as Theodotion says, will it be acceptable: that is, I will accept your offering, as I have accepted your brother's? But if you do evil, then sin will immediately sit before your doorstep, and you will be accompanied by such a guardian. But because you have free will, I advise you to not be conquered by sin, but to conquer sin. The mistake that the Septuagint interpreters made is this: that the word for sin, which is 'Attath' in Hebrew, is masculine gender, but in Greek it is feminine gender. And those who translated it, translated it with the masculine gender (as it was in Hebrew).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.8":["(Verse 8) And Cain said to his brother Abel. It is understood what the Lord said. Therefore, it is superfluous, what is found in the Samaritan and our volume, Let us go to the field.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.15":["(Verse 15.) Everyone who kills Cain will suffer sevenfold vengeance. Aquila interpreted it sevenfold. Symmachus, the seventh. Theodotion, by the week. On this chapter, our letter to Bishop Damasus exists.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.16":["(Verse 16.) And he dwelt in the land of Naid. Which the Septuagint translated as Naid, but in Hebrew it is called Nod, meaning 'shaking', that is, unstable and fluctuating, with an uncertain dwelling place. Therefore, it is not the land of Naid, as our common people think, but the divine sentence is fulfilled, that he wandered and fled here and there.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.23":["And so that we may know that hearing is more sacred than that which sounds in the ears, let us learn it from Isaiah, who says: Hear, O heaven, and perceive with your ears, O earth. Those who are old men and heavenly hear spiritually; those who dwell on the earth and are called earthly perceive with their ears. And this must be noted in all the Scriptures where these two words are placed together. We read this also in the case of Lamech, the sinner, who spoke to his wives Adah and Zillah: Hear my words, wives of Lamech, perceive my words with your ears, for I have killed a man to my wounding, and a young man to my bruising; he knew that what he was saying was obscure, and therefore he called his wives not only to the simple sound of the words, but also to the hidden understanding of what was said.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"GEN.4.25":["(Verse 25) And he called his name Seth. For God has provided me another offspring in place of Abel, whom Cain killed. Seth, properly θέσις, that is, position, is called because God had set him in place of Abel. Finally, Aquila says: And he called his name Seth, saying: For God has placed another offspring for me.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.4.26":["(Verse 26) And he called his name Enos: this one hoped to invoke the name of the Lord God. Just as Adam () is interpreted as 'man': so also Enos () according to the variety of the Hebrew language is called 'man' or 'vir'. And it is beautiful because he had this word, it is written about him, 'Then was the beginning of invoking the name of the Lord God': although many Hebrews interpret it differently, that at that time idols were first made in the name of the Lord and in his likeness.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","Whoever therefore believe shall be blessed with faithful Abraham, who, because of his outstanding faith in God, is related to have been the first to believe in him; just as Enosh, because of his preeminent hope in God, one that stood out among the rest, is written to have hoped to call upon the Lord God (Gen. 4:26). Not that Abel also, of whom the Lord says: \"The voice of your brother's blood cries to me\" (ibid. 4:10), and the others after him, did not hope to call upon God; but that each one is named from that quality which he possesses most of all.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.5.2":["(Chapter 5 - Verse 2) He created them male and female, and blessed them. And he named them Adam, that is, man. The name man applies equally to both men and women.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.5.3":["(Verse 3.) Adam lived for two hundred and thirty years, and he begot in his own image and likeness, and he called his name Seth. It should be known that until the Flood, where it is said that somebody is said to have begotten two hundred and something years in our codices, in the Hebrew it has one hundred years and the rest that follow.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.5.4":["(Verse 4) Now the days of Adam after he fathered Seth were seven hundred years. And he fathered other sons and daughters. And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years, and he died.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.5.24":["We first read the word \"transfer\" in Genesis (Gen. 5), where \"God transferred Enoch, and he was not found\". And afterwards in the books of Kingdoms (1 Kings 21), when his wife Jezebel transferred Ahab from the worship of God to the veneration of idols, so that he did according to all that the Amorite did, whom the Lord destroyed from before the face of the children of Israel. But although each is a transfer, the one is God's, the other the devil's. He who is transferred by God is not found by his enemies, nor can the one who lies in wait creep up on him. This indeed is what I judge \"and he was not found\" to signify. But he who is transferred by the devil is transferred into that which seems to be, but is not.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.5.25":["(Verse 25.) And Methuselah lived for 167 years and begot Lamech. And Methuselah lived, after he begot Lamech, for 802 years, and he begot sons and daughters. And all the days of Methuselah, that he lived, were 969 years, and he died. The famous question, and the subject of dispute among all the churches, is that, according to careful calculation, Methuselah is said to have lived fourteen years after the flood. For when Methuselah was sixty-seven years old, he begot Lamech. And again, Lamech, when he was eighty-eight years old, begot Noah. And they lived together until the day of Noah's birth, which was three hundred and fifty-five years after Methuselah's birth. But in the six hundredth year of Noah's life, the flood occurred. And by this calculation, it is proven that the flood happened in the nine hundred and fifty-fifth year of Methuselah's life. However, since he is said to have lived for more than 969 years, there is no doubt that he lived for 14 years after the flood. And how is it true that only eight souls were saved in the ark? Therefore, it remains that just as there is an error in number in many things, there is also an error in this. For in both the Hebrew and Samaritan books, I found it written: And Methuselah lived 187 years and fathered Lamech. And Methuselah lived, after he begot Lamech, seven hundred eighty-two years and begot sons and daughters. And all the days of Methuselah were nine hundred sixty-nine years, and he died. And Lamech lived one hundred eighty-two years and begot Noah. From the day of the birth of Methuselah to the day of the birth of Noah, there are three hundred sixty-nine years. Add to these six hundred years of Noah's life: because in the six hundredth year of his life the deluge occurred: and so it happens that in the nine hundred and sixty-ninth year of his life Methuselah died, in that year when the deluge began.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.5.29":["(Verse 29.) And he called his name Noah, saying: This one shall bring us rest from our work and from the toil of our hands, because of the ground which the Lord hath cursed.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.1-4":["The book of Genesis also relates (Chapter 6) that after men began to become many, a number that is always associated with vice, and daughters were born to them, they were taken, not by Angels, but by the sons of God, from whom the giants were born: or, as it is written in the Hebrew, ἐπιπίπτοντες, that is, those who fall upon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"GEN.6.2":["(Chapter VI - Verse 2) But the sons of God saw the daughters of men, that they were fair. The Hebrew word Elohim (), is of common gender: and both God and gods are called by this name. Hence, Aquila, in the plural number, dared to call them Sons of gods, understanding them to mean the Holy Ones or Angels. For God stands in the assembly of gods: but in the midst of them He judgeth gods. (Alternate reading: judgeth). (Psalm LXXXI, 1). Therefore, Symmachus, following a similar interpretation, says: The sons of the powerful saw the daughters of men, and so on.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.3":["(Version 3.) And the Lord God said: My spirit will not dwell in these men forever, because they are flesh. In Hebrew, it is written, My spirit will not judge these men forever, because they are flesh: that is, because the condition of man is fragile, I will not subject them to eternal torment; but here I will restore to them what they deserve. Therefore, it signifies not severity, as it is read in our codices, but rather the mercy of God, while the sinner is visited here for his crime. And an angry God speaks to some: I will not visit their daughters, when they have fornicated, and their wives, when they have committed adultery (Hosea IV, 14). And in another place: I will visit their iniquities with a rod, and their sins with scourges: nevertheless, I will not take away my mercy from them (Ps. LXXXVIII, 33). Furthermore, so that it does not appear cruel on His part for not giving a place of repentance to sinners, He added: But their days shall be one hundred and twenty years. This is, they will have 120 years to do penance. Therefore, human life, as many err, is not condensed into 120 years: but to that generation, 120 years were given for penance. For we find that after the flood, Abraham lived for 175 years, and others for over 200 and 300 years. But because they refused to do penance, God did not want to wait for the appointed time: but with 20 years cut off, He brought about the flood in the 100th year designated for doing penance.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.4":["(Version 4.) Now there were giants upon the earth in those days. For after the sons of God went in to the daughters of men, and they brought forth children, these are the mighty men of old, men of renown. For falling ones, or giants, Symmachus interpreted them as violent ones. And it is fitting for the name of falling ones to apply to angels and the children of the saints.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.9":["(Verse 9) Noah was a righteous and blameless man in his generation, pleasing to God. Specifically, it says 'in his generation' to show that his righteousness was not according to the perfected righteousness, but according to the righteousness of his generation. And this is what is said in Hebrew: Noah was a righteous man, blameless in his generations, he walked with God; that is, he followed in his footsteps.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.14":["(Verse 14) Make for yourself an ark of square wooden planks. For the square planks, we read it is coated with pitch in Hebrew.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.16":["(Verse 16.) Assembling, you shall make an ark, and you shall finish it with a cubit from above. As for the reason why it says, assembling, you shall make an ark, in Hebrew it has, 'you shall make the middle of the ark,' which Symmachus interpreted more clearly, saying 'transparent,' that is, making the ark clear, intending for a window to be understood.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.6.18":["And when the flood was impending, Noah is commanded: Enter into the ark, you and your sons, and your wife, and the wives of your sons. And afterwards, when the flood was over, it is said to him: Go out, you and your wife, and your sons, and their wives, so that those who had been separated in the ark while the peril hung over them, once restored to the world, might devote themselves to begetting and to children.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"GEN.8.2":["(Chapter 8, Verse 2) And the water subsided, and the fountains of the deep were revealed, and the floodgates of heaven were opened. The interpreters have translated the phrase 'fountains of the deep were revealed' as 'the closed and sealed fountains were opened'. And regarding the following phrase: 'the water subsided from the earth', it is written that 'the waters returned from the earth, going and coming' (Ecclesiastes 1:7). Note, according to Ecclesiastes, that all waters and torrents return to the hidden veins of the abyss.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.8.4":["Ararat is a level region in Armenia, through which the Araxes flows, of incredible fertility, at the roots of mount Taurus, which extends as far as there. So the Ark too, in which Noah was delivered with his children when the Flood ceased, was carried not to the mountains of Armenia in general, which is called Ararat, but to the highest mountains of Taurus, which overhang the plains of Ararat.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"GEN.8.6":["(Verse 6) After forty days, Noah opened the door of the ark which he had made, and released a raven, but it did not return to him until the waters had dried up from the earth. As for the dove, it is said differently: He released a raven, and it went out, going and coming back until the waters had dried up from the earth.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.8.16":["And when the flood was impending, Noah is commanded: Enter into the ark, you and your sons, and your wife, and the wives of your sons. And afterwards, when the flood was over, it is said to him: Go out, you and your wife, and your sons, and their wives, so that those who had been separated in the ark while the peril hung over them, once restored to the world, might devote themselves to begetting and to children.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"GEN.9.5-6":["Therefore what is said to Noah: And the blood of your souls I will require at the hand of all the beasts, and at the hand of man, and at the hand of his brother I will require the soul of man; whoever sheds the blood of man, for his blood shall his blood be poured out, is both ridiculous to believe of the resurrection and cannot hold in this life. For how many have shed blood, and their own blood has not been poured out? And others have killed a man by poison, or by hanging, and yet, though the man is dead, has blood been poured out? How then is the Lord going to pour out their blood in retaliation, when he who killed did not pour out blood? The blood of a man, therefore, must be understood as τὸ ζωτικὸν αἶτος, that is, the vital principle, by which he is quickened and sustained and lives; and whoever has poured this out, whether by a stumbling block or by perversity of doctrine, will have it poured out from him on the day of judgment, that is, he will be forced to lose what he seemed to himself to have as his vital principle.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"GEN.9.18":["(Chapter 9, Verse 18) The sons of Noah who came out of the ark were Shem, Ham, and Japheth. The Septuagint translators often struggled to render the letter Heth (), which has a double aspiration sound, into Greek. They added the Greek letter chi (χ) to instruct us to aspirate in such words. Therefore, in this passage, they translated Cham as Ham, because Ham is called \"Ham\" in the Hebrew language, and in Egyptian language, Egypt is called \"Ham\" until this day.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.9.25":["Now in proof of this point, that the first impulse of thoughts, or rather the slight prompting of the mind, is by no means punished by God, but only if you complete in deed what you have conceived in the mind, that passage of Genesis must be brought forward: Ham sinned in mocking his father's nakedness, and the sentence was received not by him who laughed but by his son Canaan: Cursed, he says, be Canaan; a servant he shall be to his brothers. For what righteousness is there in this, that the father should have sinned and the sentence be pronounced against the son? Likewise what the Apostle sets down in the opposite direction, that the woman is saved if her son remains in faith and holiness and chastity, would seem not to have the righteousness of a sentence, namely that if the sons and grandsons are good, the parents are saved; for how many parents are holy and have wicked sons, and, on the contrary, how many are sinners and beget holy sons? Therefore all that we said above is to be taken according to this sense: that the sins of parents and begetters are punished in the branches, not in the root.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"GEN.9.27":["(Verse 27) May God enlarge Japheth, and may he dwell in the tents of Shem. From Shem come the Hebrews, and from Japheth, the people of the nations are born. Therefore, because the multitude of believers has been enlarged from the breadth, which is called Japheth, it has received the name \"breadth\". But what he says, \"And may he dwell in the tents of Shem,\" is prophesying about us, who are engaged in the teaching and knowledge of the Scriptures, with Israel being cast out.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.9.29":["(Verse 29.) And all the days of Noah were nine hundred and fifty years. Behold, after the flood Noah lived three hundred and fifty years. From which it is clear that one hundred and twenty years were given to that generation, as we have said before, for repentance, and not for the establishment of mortal life.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.2":["(Chapter 10, Verse 2.) The sons of Japheth were Gomer, Magog, Madai, Javan, Tubal, Meshech, and Tiras. Japheth, son of Noah, had seven sons who possessed the land in Asia from Mount Amanus and Mount Taurus to the mountains of Syria Coele and Cilicia, all the way to the River Tanais. In Europe, they journeyed as far as Gades, leaving names to places and tribes: many of these were later changed, but others remain as they were. Gomer became the Galatians, Magog became the Scythians, Madai became the Medes, Javan became the Ionians, also known as the Greeks, hence the Ionian Sea. Thubal the Iberians, who are also called Hispani, from whom the Celtiberi, although some suspect they are Italians. Mosoch, the Cappadocians, from whom the city is still called Mazaca among them. (Furthermore, the Septuagint interpreters consider the Cappadocians to be the Caphthorim.) Thiras, the Thracians, whose name has not been sufficiently changed. I know that someone named Gog and Magog, both from present location and from Ezekiel, recently related a story of the Goths who were recently reveling in our land, which is demonstrated to be true by the end of the battle itself. And certainly all the educated Goths, more accustomed to call the Getae than Gog and Magog. Therefore, these seven nations, which I mentioned came from the line of Japheth, inhabit the northern part.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.3":["(Verse 3) The sons of Gomer are Ashkenaz, Riphath, and Togarmah. The Greeks call Ashkenaz 'Reginos', Riphath is the ancestor of the Paphlagonians, and Togarmah is the ancestor of the Phrygians.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.4":["(Ver. 4.) The sons of Javan were Elisha and Tarshish, Kittim and Dodanim. From them were divided the islands of the nations in their lands, each according to its language, its kindred, and its people. From the Ionians, that is, the Greeks, were born the Aeolians, who are called Aeolians: hence the fifth language of Greece is called Aeolian, which they call 'the fifth dialect.' Josephus considers Tarshish to be Cilicia, stating that the letter θ was mistakenly corrupted by later generations into τ: hence their metropolis is called the city of Tarsus, glorious to the Apostle Paul. Cethim, they are the Cetians, from whom even today the city of Cyprus is also called Citium. Dodanim, the Rhodians: for so the Septuagint translators have rendered it. Let us read the books of Varro on Antiquities, and those of Sisinnius Capito, and the Greek Phlegon, and other most learned men: and we will see almost all the islands, and the shores of the whole world, and the lands near the sea, occupied by Greek settlers: who, as we have said, possess all the coastal areas from the mountains of Amanus and Taurus to the British Ocean.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.6":["(Verse 6) The sons of Ham: Cush, Egypt, Put, and Canaan. Cush, even to this day, is called Ethiopia by the Hebrews; Egypt is called Mizraim, and Put is called Libya. From Put, the river of Mauritania is called as such, and the entire region surrounding it is known as the Putian region. Many Greek and Latin writers attest to this. However, it is not the place or time to discuss why the ancient name of Libya has remained only in a certain part of the region, and the rest of the land is called Africa. Furthermore, Canaan obtained the land, which the Jews subsequently possessed, after driving out the Canaanites.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.7":["(Verse 7) The sons of Chus are Saba, Aevila, Sabatha, Regma, and Sabathaca. Saba is the progenitor of the Sabaei, of whom Virgil speaks (II Georgics).\n\nThe golden wand is from the Sabaeans. And elsewhere (Book I of the Aeneid).\n\n. . . . . and a hundred altars warm with Sabean incense. The Evili, Getulians, are connected in the more remote part of the desert of Africa. Sabatha, from which the Sabatheni, who are now called Astabari, are derived. However, Regma and Sabathaca gradually lost their ancient names, and what they now have in place of the old names is unknown.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","(Verse 7.) The sons of Regma are Saba and Dadan. Saba is spelled with a Sin (), but above it with a Samech (), from which we said they are called Sabaeans. Now Saba is interpreted as Arabia, for in the seventy-first psalm, where we have, 'The kings of the Arabians and Saba will offer gifts,' it is written in Hebrew, 'The kings of Saba and Saba,' the first name with Sin, the second with Samech. Dadan is a nation of Ethiopia in the western region.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.8":["(Verse 8) And Cush begot Nimrod. He began to be powerful in the land. And after a little while:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","(Verse 8) And Cush begot Nimrod. He began to be powerful in the land... Nimrod, the son of Chus, first seized an unprecedented tyranny among the people.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.10":["(Verse 10.) And it was, he said, the head of his kingdom, Babel and Arach, and Achad, and Chalanne in the land of Sennaar. Nimrod, the son of Chus, first seized an unprecedented tyranny among the people, and he reigned in Babylon, which was named after him because the tower of the builders' confusion occurred there. For Babel means confusion. However, he also reigned in Arach, which is Edessa, and in Achad, which is now called Nisibis: and in Chalanne, which later, under a different name, was called Seleucia by King Seleucus, or certainly what is now called Ktesiphon.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.11":["(Verse 11) From that land came Assur, and he built Nineveh and the city of Rehoboth. From this land of the Assyrians, the empire sprouted, founded by Ninus, son of Belus, and it is a great city which the Hebrews call Nineveh. The entire prophecy of Jonah pertains to its destruction or repentance. And when it says 'Nineveh and the city of Rehoboth,' we should not think that there are two cities, but because Rehoboth means streets, it should be read as 'And he built Nineveh, and the streets of the city.'\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.13":["(Verse 13) And Mesraim fathered Ludim, and Anamim, and Laabim, and Nephtuim, and Phetrosim, and Chasloim, from whom came the Philistines and the Caphtorim. Except for the Laabim, who were later called Libyans, and the Chasloim, who were later called Philistines, whom we incorrectly call Palestinians, the other six nations are unknown to us, for they were destroyed in the Ethiopian war and their names have been forgotten. But they possessed the land from Gaza to the furthest boundaries of Egypt.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.15":["(Verse 15.) And Canaan begot his firstborn Sidon, and the Hittites, and the Jebusites, and the Amorites, and the Girgashites, and the Hivites, and the Arkites, and the Sinites, and the Arvadites, and the Zemarites, and the Hamathites. From Canaan, Sidon was born, from whom the city of Sidon in Phoenicia is named. Then the Arkite, who founded Ark, a town situated opposite Tripolis at the roots of Mount Lebanon. Not far from there was another city named Sini, which, after various events of war, was destroyed and only retained its original name. There are Aradians, who possessed the island of Aradu, separated by a narrow strait from the Phoenician coast. The Samaritans, to whom Emesa, a noble city of Coele Syria, belongs. Amath, up until our time, is called by both the Syrians and the Hebrews, as it was called by the ancients. The Macedonians, who ruled in the East after Alexander, called it Epiphania. Some believe that Antioch was called by that name. Others, though not truly, yet consoling their opinion with a seemingly plausible word, think that the first dwelling of Emath, as they journeyed from Antioch, was called Edessa: and that it is the same as Emach, which was called by the ancients.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.19":["(Verse 19.) And the boundary of the Canaanites was from Sidon, as you come to Gerar, as far as Gomorrah, and Admah, and Zeboiim, to Lasha. These are the cities of Sidon and Gerar, and Sodom and Gomorrah, and Admah and Zeboiim, which are known to all, but it is important to note that Lasha itself is now called Callirhoe, where the hot springs flow into the Dead Sea.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.22":["(Verse 22.) The sons of Shem, Elam, and Asshur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram. These hold the part of Asia from the Euphrates River to the Indian Ocean. Now Elam, from whom the Elamite princes of Persia descend. Concerning Asshur, it has already been said that he founded the city of Nineveh. Arphaxad, from whom the Chaldeans descend. Lud, from whom the Lydians descend. Aram, from whom the Syrians descend, with Damascus as their metropolis.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.23":["(Verse 23.) The sons of Aram were Us, Ul, Gether, and Mes. Us founded Trachonitis and Damascus, and he held the principality between Palestine and Coele-Syria. From him, the Seventy Interpreters translated the land of Us, the region of Ausitis, as if it were Usitis, in the Book of Job where it is written in Hebrew. Ul is the ancestor of the Armenians; Gether is the ancestor of the Acarnanians, or the Carians. And Mes, whom the Seventy Interpreters called Mosoch, are now called Maeones.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.24-25":["(Verse 24.) Arphaxad begot Shelah, and Shelah begot Eber. From Eber were born two sons: one was named Peleg, because in his days the earth was divided, and his brother's name was Joktan. Eber, from whom the Hebrews are descended, gave his son Peleg the name which means 'division', because in his days the languages were divided in Babylon.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.24":["(Verse 24.) Arphaxad begot Shelah, and Shelah begot Eber. From Eber were born two sons: one was named Peleg, because in his days the earth was divided, and his brother's name was Joktan. Eber, from whom the Hebrews are descended, gave his son Peleg the name which means 'division', because in his days the languages were divided in Babylon.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.10.26":["(Verse 26.) Jectan begat Helmodad, and Saleph, and Asermoth, and Jare, and Aduram, and Uzal, and Decla, Ebal, Abimael, Seba, Ophir, Evila, and Jobab. I could not find the names of the twelve other nations; but up to the present time, because they are far from us, or because they are called by different names, or because they have been changed, they are unknown. However, they possessed the whole region of India, which is called Hieria, from the river Cophene.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.11.28":["(Chapter 11, Verse 28) And Haran died before his father in the land of his birth, in the region of the Chaldeans. As we read, in the region of the Chaldeans, in Hebrew it is written, in Ur of the Chaldeans, that is, in the fire of the Chaldeans. However, the Hebrews relate a legend from this occasion: that Abraham was thrown into the fire because he refused to worship the fire that the Chaldeans worship, and with God's help, he was freed and escaped from the fire of idolatry. This is written in the following passages, that Terah went out with his family from the region of the Chaldeans, for which it is written in Hebrew, from the burning of the Chaldeans. And this is what is now said: Aran died before the sight of his father Thare in the land of his nativity, in the fire of the Chaldeans: namely, because he did not wish to adore the fire, he was consumed by the fire. But the Lord spoke afterwards to Abraham: I am the one who brought you out of the fire of the Chaldeans.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.11.29":["(Verse 29) And Abram and Nachor took wives for themselves. The name of Abram's wife was Sarai, and the name of Nachor's wife was Milcah, the daughter of Haran. The father of Milcah was also the father of Iscah. Haran fathered two daughters, Milcah and Iscah, who were both called by the same name. Of these, Milcah became the wife of Nachor, and Sarai became the wife of Abram. At that time, marriage between cousins and between siblings was not yet prohibited by law, as it later became among all people, even among brothers and sisters.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.11.31":["(Verse 29) And Abram and Nachor took wives for themselves. The name of Abram's wife was Sarai, and the name of Nachor's wife was Milcah, the daughter of Haran. The father of Milcah was also the father of Iscah. Haran fathered two daughters, Milcah and Iscah, who were both called by the same name. Of these, Milcah became the wife of Nachor, and Sarai became the wife of Abram. At that time, marriage between cousins and between siblings was not yet prohibited by law, as it later became among all people, even among brothers and sisters.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.12.2":["But what other sons of the Church can we understand as named, except the apostles? Look, I pray you, at Peter and Paul and Matthew and John, and consider that what was promised to Abraham: I will magnify your name, has been accomplished in deed in them. Daily it is named in the Church, daily their name is magnified, not because it profits them to be named by us in the Church, but so that we, magnifying their name and reading over what they wrote, may obtain salvation.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"GEN.12.4":["(Chapter 12, Verse 4) Now Abram was seventy-five years old when he departed from Haran. There arises an unsolvable question: for if Terah, the father of Abram, when he was still in the land of the Chaldeans, at seventy years old begot Abram, and afterwards died in Haran at the age of two hundred and five: how is it now mentioned that Abram, after the death of Terah, when departing from Haran, was seventy-five years old? Since from the birth of Abram until the death of his father, it is said that one hundred and thirty-five years passed. Therefore, that tradition of the Hebrews, which we mentioned above, is true: that Thara went out with his sons from the fire of the Chaldeans, and that Abram, surrounded by the Babylonian fire, was liberated by the help of God because he refused to worship it; and from that time, the days of his life, and the reckoning of his age, are considered from when he confessed the Lord, rejecting the idols of the Chaldeans. However, it is possible that because the Scripture left it uncertain, a few years ago Thara set out from Chaldea and came to Haran, where he died. Certainly, after the persecution, he came to Haran and stayed there for a longer time. Therefore, if anyone is opposed to this explanation, they will seek another solution and then rightfully reject what we have said.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.12.9":["(Verse 9) And Abram, setting out, went into the desert; and famine came upon the land. And in the present and in many other places, for desert, it is written in Hebrew. Therefore, we should take note of this.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.12.15-16":["(Vs. 15 and 16) And the princes of Pharaoh saw her, and they praised her to Pharaoh, and she was brought into Pharaoh's house. And Abram benefitted because of her, and he had sheep, and cattle, and donkeys, and male and female servants, and mules and camels. Although the body of holy women does not see blemish, but the will does, and Sarai can be excused because, in the time of famine, she could not resist being alone in foreign places, with her husband conniving; however, the ugly necessity can also be excused in another way: that according to the book of Esther, whatever pleased the king among the women, for six months she was anointed with myrtle oil, and for six months she was in various perfumes and women's treatments, and only then she entered the king's presence. And so it happened that Sarai, after pleasing the king, while she was being prepared for a year to enter the king's presence, and Pharaoh gave many gifts to Abraham, and Pharaoh later was struck by the Lord, she still remained untouched by his concubinage.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.13.4":["(Chapter 13, Verse 4) And Abram went up out of Egypt, he and his wife, and all that he had, and Lot with him, into the wilderness. Now Abram was very rich in livestock, in silver, and in gold. And he went on his journey from the South as far as Bethel. It is said that Abram went up from Egypt in a splendid manner. But there is a contradiction to this idea in what follows: how could he have become very rich upon leaving Egypt? This is explained by the Hebrew word used, which means Abram was heavily burdened, that is, βαρύς σφόδρα: for he was burdened by the weight of Egypt. And although they may seem to be riches of cattle, gold, and silver, yet if they are of Egypt, they are burdensome to a holy man. Finally, not as we read in the Septuagint, 'He went back from where he came into the desert unto Bethel,' but as it is written in Hebrew, 'He went on his journey by way of the south unto Bethel.' Therefore, he departed from Egypt not to enter the desert that he had left in Egypt, but to come by way of the south, which is opposite to the north, to the house of God, where his tabernacle had been in the midst of Bethel and Ai (also called Gai).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.13.13":["(Verse 13) And the men of Sodom were wicked and sinners before God exceedingly. This addition, \"before God,\" is superfluous in the Septuagint interpreters, since the inhabitants of Sodom were wicked and sinners among all people. However, a person is said to be a sinner before God, who can appear righteous among people, just as it is declared in the proclamation of Zachariah and Elizabeth that they were both righteous before God (Luke 1:6). And in the Psalter it is said: 'No living creature shall be justified in your sight.' (Ps. 142:2)\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.13.14-15":["(Verse 14 and 15.) Lift up your eyes and look from the place where you are, towards the north, south, east, and west; for all the land that you see I will give to you and to your offspring. He set the four regions of the world: east, west, north, and south. But what is read in all the Scriptures, let it suffice to say once, that the sea is always considered to be in the west: because the region of Palestine is situated in such a way that the sea is in the western part.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.3":["(Chapter 14, Verse 3) And the king of Bale, which is Segor. All these agreed in the valley of Salsam, which is the Dead Sea. Bale in the Hebrew language is called κατάποσις, which means swallowing. Therefore, the Hebrews report that in another place in the Scriptures it is called Salisa, and again it is said to be μόσχον τριετίζουσαν, which means a three-year-old calf, because it was indeed swallowed up by the third movement of the earth (1 Samuel 9:4; Isaiah 15:5). And from that time, since Sodom and Gomorrah, Adama and Seboim were destroyed by divine fire, it is called that little one. Indeed, Segor is transferred to a small place called Zoara in the Syriac language. The Valley of Salt, as it is written in this same book, in which there were previously pits of bitumen, after the wrath of God and the rain of sulfur, was turned into the Dead Sea, which is called by the Greeks λίμνη Ἀσφαλτῖτις, that is, the lake of bitumen.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.5":["(Verse 5.) And the giants settled in Astaroth Carnaim, and strong nations along with them: and Ominaeos in the city of Save, before they came to Sodom. Four kings who came from Babylon killed the giants, that is, the strong ones of Arabia, and the Zozim in Hom, and the Emim in the city of Save, which is still called by that name today. But the Zuzim and the Emim are interpreted as terrifying and horrifying: for this reason, the Seventy [translators], conveying the sense rather than word for word, translated them as the strongest nations. Furthermore, Baem, for which they said, 'with them', they thought it was written with He (), guided by the similarity of the elements, while it is written with Heth. Baem () is written with three letters: if it has the middle He, it is interpreted as 'in them'; but if it has Heth, as in the present case, it signifies a place, that is, 'in Hom ()'.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.7":["(Verse 7) And they returned and came to the well of judgment, which is Kadesh. It is said by anticipation that it was afterwards so called. But it signifies the place at Petra, which is called the well of judgment because there God judged the people. And they struck all the region of the Amalekites and the Amorites who dwelt in Hazazon-tamar. This town is what is now called Engedi, fertile with balsam and palm trees. Moreover, Hazazon-tamar, in our language, means the city of palm trees. Thamar, indeed, is said to mean 'palm'. However, it is worth noting that shortly after it says: 'And they set their battle line against them in the valley of Salt, which is called in Hebrew, the valley of Seddim (), which Aquila interprets as 'of the plains', and Theodotion as 'of the groves', signifying pleasant woods.'\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.11":["(Verse 11.) And they took all the cavalry of Sodom and Gomorrah. For cavalry in Hebrew has Rachus (), which means substance.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.13":["(Verse 13) And he who had fled, reported to Abram the passerby. And he himself was sitting by the oak of Mamre, the Amorite, the brother of Eschol, and the brother of Aner, who were allies of Abram. Concerning this, which we have set forth to the passerby, it is written in Hebrew as Ibri (), for this indeed signifies a passerby. But what he says: by the oak of Mamre, the Amorite, we read better in Hebrew as by the oak of Mamre, the Amorite, the brother of Eschol, and not the brother of Aner, as the Septuagint translated, but of Aner, so as to show that Mamre and Eschol, and Aner, were Amorites and brothers, allies of Abram.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.14":["(Verse 14) And he pursued them as far as Dan, to the city of the Phoenicians, which is now called Paneas. But Dan is one of the sources of the Jordan. For the other is also called Jor, which means 'stream'. Therefore, the Jordan is the name given to the two springs that, not far from each other, join to form a single stream.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.14.18":["(Verse 18.) And Melchizedek, king of Salem, brought forth bread and wine; and he was the priest of the most high God. And he blessed him. Because our work, either the Hebrew Questions or the Collection of Traditions, is once for all, therefore let us infer what the Hebrews think of this. They say that he is the son of Shem, Noah's son; and by calculating the years of his life, they show that he lived until Isaac, and that all the firstborn of Noah, until Aaron assumed the priesthood, were priests. Furthermore, Jerusalem is called the king of Salem, which was previously called Salem. However, blessed Apostle to the Hebrews (VII, 3), mentioning one without a father or mother, refers to Christ, and through Christ to the Church of the Gentiles. For all the glory of the head is referred to the members, because he, having a foreskin, blessed the circumcised Abraham, and in Abraham the Levites, and through the Levites Aaron: from whom later the priesthood. From this he wants to gather that the priesthood of the Church, having a foreskin, blessed the synagogue with the circumcised priesthood. But as he says: You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek (Ps. CIX, 4): our mystery is signified in the word of the order: not by offering irrational victims through Aaron, but by offering bread and wine: that is, the body and blood of the Lord Jesus.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.15.2-3":["(Chapter 15, Verses 2, 3.) Lord God, what will you give me? And I go without children: and the son of my household, this Damascus Eliezer. And Abram said: Behold to me you have not given seed: and the son of my household will be my heir. Whereby we have, and the son of my household: in Hebrew it is written, Uben Mesech Bethi: which Aquila translated, the son of the one who gives drink to my house: that is, the son who gives drink to my house. But Theodotius, and the son of my servant: that is, his son, who is in charge of my house. And as for what he says, this is it: I die without children, and the son of my steward, or overseer, who manages and distributes all the food of my household, is called Damascus Eliezer, and he will be my heir. Moreover, Eliezer means 'my God is my helper'. They say that Damascus was founded and named after him.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.15.6":["Now Abraham believed God when he went out from his homeland into a land he did not know (Gen. 12 ff.); when he trusted that Sarah, ninety years old and barren, would give birth; and when, having heard God's promise that in Isaac his seed would be called, he offered that very Isaac as a victim, and yet did not doubt the Lord's promise. Rightly is faith reckoned as righteousness to such a man, who, having gone beyond the works of the Law, won the favor of God not by fear but by love.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.15.7":["(Verse 7.) I am God, who brought you out from the land of the Chaldeans. This is what we said a little while ago is written in Hebrew: who brought you out from Ur of the Chaldeans, that is, from the fire of the Chaldeans.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.15.10-11":["(Verses 10, 11.) And he placed them opposite each other: but he did not separate the birds. However, birds descended upon the carcasses and the divisions thereof, and Abram drove them away. It does not pertain to the present work to explain the sacrament. We only say this, because the Hebrew version has for these words: And birds descended upon the carcasses, and Abram drove them away. For he deservedly often delivered Israel from narrow straits.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.15.12":["(Verse 12) But when the sun was setting, a deep sleep fell upon Abram. The word for deep sleep in Hebrew is Thardema (), which means a descending, and we translated it as a deep sleep.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.15.16":["(Verse 16) But in the fourth generation they will return here. There is no doubt that these will be the ones who are descended from Abraham. It is asked how it is written in Exodus: In the fifth generation the children of Israel went forth from the land of Egypt (Exodus 15:18). On this chapter, we have published a small volume.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.16.2":["(Chapter 16, Verse 2) Behold, the Lord has made me barren, so go in to my maidservant; perhaps I shall obtain children by her. Take careful note that the procreation of children is written in Hebrew as building. For it is read there: Go in to my maidservant, that I may perhaps build through her. And see, lest perhaps this is what is said in Exodus: God blessed the midwives, and they built houses for themselves (Exodus 1:20).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.16.7":["(Verse 7) And the angel of the Lord found her by a fountain of water in the desert, by the fountain on the way to Shur. Then the Egyptian woman hurriedly went on the way to Shur, which leads through the desert to Egypt.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.16.11":["(Verse 11) And she called his name Ishmael; for God has heard my affliction. Ishmael means, God hears.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.16.12":["(Verse 12) This will be a rustic man. His hand will be against everyone, and everyone's hand against him, and he will dwell against the face of all his brothers. In Hebrew, Phara () is written for rustic, which means onager. It signifies that his descendants will dwell in the wilderness, that is, the wandering Saracens, in uncertain lands, who attack and are attacked by all the nations to which the desert is adjacent.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.17.4":["(Chapter 17—Verse 3 and following) And the Lord spoke to him, saying: Behold, my covenant is with you, and you shall be the father of a multitude of nations. It should be noted that wherever we read Testament in Greek, in the Hebrew language it is the word covenant or pact, that is, Berith ().\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.17.5":["(Chapter 17, Verse 5) And your name shall no longer be called Abram, but your name shall be Abraham, for I have made you the father of many nations. The Hebrews say that God, from his name, which is the Tetragrammaton among them, added the letter He to Abraham and Sarah: for he was first called Abram (), which means lofty father, and later he was called Abraham (), which means father of many: for what follows nations is not part of the name, but is understood. It is not surprising, therefore, that when it seems that the letter Α has been added among the Greeks and us: we have called it the Hebrew letter He added; for the language of that tongue is to write with He (ה‎), but to read with A (א‎): just as, on the contrary, the letter A is often pronounced as He.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.17.15":["(Verse 15) And God said to Abraham: You shall not call your wife Sarai, but her name shall be Sarah. Those who think that the first Sarah was written with one 'R' and then another 'R' was added to it are mistaken. And because 'R' is the hundredth number among the Greeks, they suspect many silly things about her name: when in fact, whatever way they want to understand the composite word, it should have a Hebrew, not Greek, origin, since the name itself is Hebrew. However, no one calling someone in another language takes the etymology of the word from the other language. Therefore, Sarai () was first called by Sin, Res, Iod: with Iod removed, that is, the I element, the letter He was added, which is read as A: and she was called Sara (). However, the reason for the change of name is that she was previously called 'my princess', the mother of the household of only one. For it follows: I will give you a son from her, and I will bless him, and he will be among the nations, and the kings of the peoples will come from him. And notably, not as we read in Greek: God said to Abraham. Your wife Sarai shall not be called by her name Sarai: in Hebrew it is said: you shall not call her name Sarai, that is, you shall not say to her, you are my princess: for she shall be the princess of all nations. Some suspect, mistakenly, that before she was called leprous, and afterwards princess: since leprosy is called Sarath in our language, which seems to have some similarity; but in Hebrew it is completely different. For it is written with Sade, and Res, and Ain, and Thau: which differs greatly from the previous three letters, that is, Sin, Res, and He, with which Sara is written, it is clear.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.17.17":["(Verse 17.) And Abraham fell upon his face and laughed. And he said in his heart, Shall a child be born unto him that is an hundred years old? and shall Sarah, that is ninety years old, bear? And after a little while: And thou shalt call his name Isaac. Different opinions, but there is one etymology why he is called Isaac. For Isaac is interpreted as laughter. Others say that Sarah laughed, therefore he was called laughter, which is false. Others indeed say that Abraham laughed, which we also approve. After Ishmael was born, Isaac was born, and then we read about Abraham and Sarah laughing. However, it should be known that in the Old Testament, four people were called by their names before they were born, without any disguise: Ishmael, Isaac, Solomon, and Josiah. Read the Scriptures.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.17.18-21":["This answer does indeed seem to have some weight; but it is at once crushed by the authority of the Scripture that follows. For it is written: \"But Abraham said to God: Let this Ishmael live in your sight\" (ibid., 17:18 ff.); and God answered him thus: \"Behold, Sarah your wife shall bear you a son, and you shall call his name Isaac, and I will establish my testament with him, for an everlasting testament, and with his seed after him. But concerning Ishmael: Behold, I have heard you, and behold, I have blessed him, and I will increase him, and I will multiply him exceedingly. He shall beget twelve nations, and I will make him a great nation; but my testament I will establish with Isaac, whom Sarah shall bear to you at this time, in the coming year\". From these words it is evident, by the very speech of God as well, that Ishmael was begotten according to promise. But this too is resolved thus: the promise is properly fulfilled in the giving of the testament, and it is one thing to bless, to increase, to multiply exceedingly, which is written of Ishmael, and another to make an heir through a testament, which is said of Isaac: \"I will establish my testament with him, for an everlasting testament, and with his seed after him\". And in what follows: \"But my testament I will establish with Isaac, whom Sarah shall bear to you\" (Gen. 17:19). And just as gifts are one thing and the substance another, legacies one thing and the inheritance another (for we read that gifts were handed over to the sons of Abraham's concubines, but to the son of Sarah was left the inheritance of the whole substance), so, as we have said, blessing and legacies are one thing, the testament another. And this too can be said: that concerning Ishmael either the angel or God spoke after he had been conceived, whereas concerning Isaac God made the promise before he was conceived in Sarah's womb.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.18.6":["(Chapter 18, Verse 6) And he said to him: Hurry, mix three measures of wheat flour. Because three measures are explicitly mentioned here, and the measure is uncertain: therefore I added that in Hebrew it has three sata, that is, three amphoras: so that we may understand the same mystery here and in the Gospel, where a woman is said to have fermented three measures of wheat flour (Matthew 13:33).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.18.10":["(Verse 10) However, he said, 'Returning, I will come to you at this time and in this hour, and Sarah will have a son.' For 'hour,' we read 'life' in Hebrew, so that the order or sense is: I will return to you in the time of life, as if he were saying, if I live, if life accompanies me. This, however, is anthropopathically expressed, like other things.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.18.12":["(Verse 12.) But Sarah laughed within herself, saying: After I am waxed old, shall I have pleasure? It is much differently read in Hebrew: And Sarah laughed within herself, saying: After I have withered, shall I have pleasure? Also note that where we have translated as pleasure, it is written as delight in Hebrew. Symmachus translated this passage as: After I have grown old, shall I have youth?\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.18.20-21":["He it is who also speaks in Genesis: The cry of Sodom and Gomorrah has been multiplied, and their sins are exceedingly great; I will go down, therefore, and see whether they are fulfilling it according to their cry which has come to me; and if not, that I may know. What he said is this: If they persist in their fury, punishment will not be lacking to the sinners; if they cease from their madness, they will become most worthy of my knowledge.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"GEN.18.32":["(Verse 32) And he said, 'Is it, Lord, if I speak?' Which is written in Greek, μήτι, Κύριε, ἐὰν λαλήσω. Secondly, Abraham spoke to the Lord, which does not seem to clearly indicate what he is saying. Therefore, in Hebrew it is written more explicitly: 'Please do not be angry, Lord, if I have spoken.' For since he seemed to be questioning, he qualifies what he seeks by beginning with a plea.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.19.14":["(Chapter 19, Verse 14) And he spoke to his sons-in-law, who had married his daughters. For afterward, it is said that the two daughters of Lot were virgins (about whom he himself had said long ago to the Sodomites: Behold, my two daughters, who have not known man), and now the Scripture mentions that he had sons-in-law; some think that those who had husbands remained in Sodom, and the ones who were virgins left with their father. And since the Scripture does not say, the Hebrew truth must be explained, in which it is written: Lot went out and spoke to the fiancés who were to receive his daughters. Therefore, the virgin daughters had not yet been joined in marriage.\n\n(Verse 21) And he said to him: Behold, I am amazed by your face. In Hebrew it is: Behold, I have received your face: that is, I accept your prayers. Symmachus, interpreting according to the sense, says, 'I was displeased with the sight of your face.'\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.19.28":["(Verse 28.) And behold, a flame was rising from the earth like the vapor of a furnace. Regarding this, we read in Hebrew: Behold, a rising (ἀναθυμίασις) of the furnace, which we can call vapor, smoke, or ashes.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.19.30":["(Verse 30.) And Lot went up from Segor and settled in the mountain, and his two daughters with him. For he was afraid to stay in Segor. It is asked why, when he first preferred the mountain of Segor for his escape, and wanted to be saved in it as his dwelling, he now flees from Segor back to the mountain? We will answer that the Hebrew conjecture about Segor is true, that it was frequently destroyed by earthquakes, first known as Bale, and later Salisa: and Lot feared, saying, If this place is often destroyed while the other cities still stand, how much more now, in a general destruction, will it not be able to be saved? And because of this occasion of unfaithfulness, they also gave rise to sexual relations with their daughters. For he who had seen other cities being destroyed and this one remaining, and himself being saved by the help of God, should not have doubted what he had heard granted to him. Therefore, the excuse that is given for the daughters, that they believed the human race had perished and therefore slept with their father, does not excuse the father. In fact, the Hebrews, as it follows:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.19.35":["(Verse 35.) And he did not know when she slept with him, and when he got up from her, they stabbed him from above, as if unbelievable, and something that nature cannot comprehend, to have intercourse with someone who is unaware.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.19.36-11":["(Versed. 36 seqq.) And the two daughters of Lot conceived by their father, and the firstborn son was born and she called his name Moab. He is the father of the Moabites to this day. And the younger, she also gave birth to a son, and she called his name Ammon, that is, son of my people. He is the father of the Ammonites. The name Moab means 'from the father' and has its etymology in it. But Ammon, whose name is given as if a cause, is derived as son of my race, or as it is better in Hebrew (), son of my people, so it is derived partly from the meaning of the name, partly from the language itself: Ammi, from whom the Ammonites are called, means my people.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.20.12":["(Chapter 20, verse 12.) Truly my sister is from my father, but not from my mother. That is, her father is Aran, not her mother. But because it says in Hebrew: truly my sister is the daughter of my father, and not the daughter of my mother: and it sounds more likely that she should be called Abraham's sister, we say this in her defense, that at that time such marriages were not yet prohibited by law.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.21.9-12":["I do not think that we can find where Ishmael persecuted Isaac, but only this: that when the son of the Egyptian woman, who was the elder by birth, was playing with Isaac, Sarah was indignant and said to Abraham: \"Cast out the bondwoman and her son: For the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\" (Gen. 21:10). And surely simple play among infants does not deserve expulsion and disinheritance. But the Apostle, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, and taught at the feet of the master Gamaliel (who once restrained in council the Pharisees raging against the Lord), understood from the words of Sarah, who said: \"for the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\", that that play had not been simple. But because perhaps Ishmael, as the elder by birth, and circumcised at a time when he could already understand and feel what he suffered, was claiming the birthright for himself, Scripture called the quarrel of the little ones play. Hence Sarah too, not bearing these words, and not enduring the habit of the bondwoman's son, who from an early age claimed the birthright for himself, broke out in speech: \"Cast out the bondwoman with her son: For the son of the bondwoman shall not inherit with my son Isaac\". And when it seemed hard to Abraham (for more is always owed to the firstborn) that Ishmael should not only cease to be the first, but should not even receive an equal portion with his younger brother, God, who willed that the free woman be within and the bondwoman be driven out of doors, confirms Sarah's words and speaks to Abraham: \"Let it not be hard in your sight concerning the boy and the bondwoman. In all that Sarah shall say to you, hear her voice: for in Isaac shall your seed be called\".\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.21.9":["(Chapter 21, Verse 9) And Sarah saw the son of Hagar the Egyptian, whom she bore to Abraham, playing. What follows, with his son Isaac, is not present in the Hebrew. Therefore, this is interpreted in two ways by the Hebrews: either that she made idols out of play, according to what is written elsewhere: The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play (Exodus 32:6); or that she claimed the rights of the firstborn against Isaac, as if he were older, through play and jest. Indeed, Sara, upon hearing this, could not bear it; and this is confirmed by her own words, saying: Cast out this bondwoman and her son. For the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with my son Isaac.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.21.14":["(Verse 14) And he took bread and a skin of water, and gave it to Hagar, putting it on her shoulder, along with the child, and sent her away. When Isaac was born, Ishmael was thirteen years old. And after he was weaned, he was expelled from the house with his mother. However, among the Hebrews, there are different opinions, with some claiming that the time for weaning is set at the fifth year, and others claiming the twelfth year. Therefore, to choose a shorter age, we have calculated that Ishmael was expelled with his mother after eighteen years, and it is no longer possible for him to have sat on his mother's lap as a young man. So it is true that the Hebrew language has the word 'infant' to refer to every child in relation to their parents. It is not surprising that a foreign language has its own peculiarities, since even today in Rome all children are called 'infants'. Therefore, Abraham placed bread and a skin of water on Hagar's shoulder and, having done this, he gave the child to his mother, that is, he handed him over to her, entrusted him to her care, and thus sent him out of the house. As for what follows:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.21.15-16":["(Verse 15, 16.) And she put the boy under a tree, and went and sat opposite him, as if shooting an arrow. She said, 'I will not see the death of my child.' And she sat opposite him. And immediately it happened: The boy cried out, and wept, and God heard the voice of the boy from the place where he was. And the Angel of God called to Hagar from heaven, and so on, let no one move. In Hebrew, after what is written, 'I will not see the death of my child,' it is read that Hagar herself sat opposite the boy, and raised her voice, and wept, and God heard the voice of the child. For when the mother was weeping and waiting miserably for the death of her son, God heard the boy, of whom He had promised to Abraham, saying: 'But I will also make your slave woman's son into a great nation' (Gen. XVII, 20). Otherwise, the mother herself mourned not her own death, but that of her son. Therefore, God spared her, for whom there had been weeping. Finally, it is said in what follows: 'Rise up and take the boy, and hold his hand' (Gen. XVII, 18). From this it is clear that the one who is held is not a burden to his mother, but a companion. And when someone is held by the hand of a parent, their concerned affection is shown.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.21.22":["(Verse 22.) And Abimelech and Phicol, the commanders of his army, said to his servants, 'Except Abimelech and Phicol, the third name mentioned here is not found in the Hebrew text.'\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.21.30":["(Verse 30) And he said, 'You shall take these seven ewes from me, that they may be a witness for me that I have dug this well.' Therefore he called that place Beersheba, because both of them swore an oath there. Where this is read, Beersheba, in Hebrew it is called Beersheba. But there are two reasons why it is so named: either because Abimelech received seven lambs from the hand of Abraham, for seven are called Sheba, or because they swore there, for an oath is also called Sheba in the same way. But if we read this name above this cause, we shall know that it has been said by prolepsis: just as Bethel and Galgala, which were certainly called by different names until the time they were so named. But it should be noted both from the preceding and the present place, that Isaac was not born at the oak of Mamre, or in Aulon of Mamre, as it is stated in Hebrew: but in Gerar, where the town of Beersheba is still located today. This province, not long ago, was called Salutaris, after the division of the garrisons of Palestine. The Scripture is a witness of this matter, which says: And Abraham lived in the land of the Philistines (Gen. XXI, 34).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.22.2":["(Chapter 22, Verse 2.) And God said to him: Take your beloved son, Isaac, whom you love, and go to the high land, and offer him there as a burnt offering on one of the mountains that I will tell you. It is difficult to translate the Hebrew language into Latin. Where it is now said, go to the high land, in Hebrew it has Moriah, which Aquila translated as 'the visible', that is, clear, and Symmachus, 'the vision', that is, sight. Therefore the Hebrews say that this mountain is the one on which the temple was later built, in the area of Ornan the Jebusite, as it is also written in the Chronicles: 'And they began to build the temple in the second month, on the second day of the month, in the mountain of Moriah' (2 Chronicles 3:1). This is interpreted as \"illuminating\" and \"shining\" because there is the Dabir, that is, the oracle of God: both the Law and the Holy Spirit, who teaches people the truth and inspires prophecies.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.22.3":["(Verse 3) And he went to the place which God had told him about, on the third day. It should be noted that from Gerar to Mount Moriah, that is, the site of the temple, is a three-day journey, and consequently it is said that he arrived there on the third day. Therefore, some people mistakenly think that Abraham lived at the oak tree in Mamre during that time, when in fact the journey from there to Mount Moriah is barely a full day's journey.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.22.12":["We have heard enough on how God does not know the sinner, so we ought to consider now how the just man is known by Him. God said to Abraham: 'Leave your country, your kinsfolk.' [Gen 12:1] Abraham accordingly came into Palestine; he was in Abramiri; he sojourned a long time in Gerara. When his son Isaac was born, he had received the promise: 'In your descendants all the nations of the earth shall be blessed.' [Gen 22:18] He took Isaac and offered him to God, and a voice from heaven was heard to say: Spare thy son. Straightway, at the very moment that he offered his son, what does God say to Abraham? 'I know now that you fear the Lord, your God.' [Gen 22:12] Have you just now known Abraham, Lord, with whom You have communicated for such a long time? Because Abraham had such great faith in sacrificing his own son, on that account God first began to know him. Why have we said all this? Because it is written: 'For the Lord knows the way of the just.' Let us put it another way: The way, the life, and the truth is Christ; [John 14:6] let us walk, therefore, in Christ and then God the Father will know our way.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"GEN.22.13":["(Verse 13.) And Abraham lifted up his eyes, and behold, behind him was a ram caught in a thicket by its horns. Emisenus Eusebius has spoken a silly thing in this place: Sabech, he says, is called a goat, which with straight horns is raised high to pluck the leaves from a tree. Again Aquila has interpreted as a bush, which we can call a thicket or briar: and to interpret the force of the word, dense and intertwined twigs. And Symmachus also expresses the same opinion, saying, 'And it appeared that a ram, after this, was caught in a net by its horns.' However, some have interpreted it better in this particular place, namely the Septuagint and Theodotion, who translated the word as 'Sabech,' saying, 'In the thicket Sabech with its horns.' For σύχνεων, that is, the net, which Aquila and Symmachus have used, is written with the letter 'Sin.' But here the letter 'Samech' is used. From this it is clear that the interpretation is not of thickened branches, and like the word 'Sabech' intertwined like a net with intertwined branches, but rather the name signifies a thicket, which is thus expressed in Hebrew. But in my diligent investigation, I frequently found that the letter συχνεῶνα is often written as Samech ().\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.22.14":["(Verse 14) And Abraham called the name of that place, The Lord will see: so that they say to this day, In the mount the Lord will be seen. For because he hath seen in this place, in the Hebrew tongue it is said, He shall be seen. And this hath passed into a proverb with the Hebrews, so that when they are in distress and desire the Lord's aid, they say, In the mount the Lord will be seen: that is, as He pitied Abraham, so will He have pity on us. Wherefore, even now, they are wont also to blow the horn for a sign when the ram is given.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.22.20":["(Verse 20) And they reported to Abraham, saying: Behold, Milcah has also borne children to your brother Nahor: Uz his firstborn, and Buz his brother, and Camuel the father of the Syrians, and Chesed. Uz was the firstborn of Nahor, brother of Abraham, by his wife Milcah, the daughter of Haran. From his descendants came Job, as it is written at the beginning of his book: There was a man in the land of Uz, whose name was Job. Therefore, some wrongly assume that Job is of the lineage of Esau, since that which is written at the end of his book, Because it was translated from the Syrian language, and he is the fourth generation from Esau, and the rest of what is contained there, is not found in the Hebrew volumes. Secundus was born to Melcha Buz, whom the Seventy wanted to translate as Bauz. And from this lineage comes Balaam the diviner (Numbers 24:3 et seq.; Joshua 13:22), as the Hebrews report, who is called Eliu in the book of Job: at first a holy man and prophet of God, but later, through disobedience and desire for gifts, while wanting to curse Israel, is called by the divine name. And it is said in the same book: And Eliu, the son of Barachiel the Buzite, was angry (Job 32:2), descending from this very Buzi lineage. Camuel is indeed the father of Damascus. For it is called Aran, which is written here for Syria: and it is read by the same name in Isaiah. Chased is also the fourth, from whom the Chaldeans, that is, the Chaldeans, were later called.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.9":["(Chapter 24, Verse 9) And the servant put his hand under the thigh of his master Abraham and swore to him concerning this matter. The Hebrews say that he swore at his consecration, that is, at his circumcision. But we say that he swore by the seed of Abraham, that is, by Christ, who was to be born from him, according to the Gospel of Matthew, which says: The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham (Matthew 1:1).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.22":["(Verse 22.) And the man took a gold earring, its weight was two drachmas. Bace (), which is written here instead of drachma, is half an ounce. Secel (), which is inaccurately called a shekel in Latin, has a weight of one ounce.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.43":["(Verse 43.) Behold, I stand by the fountain of water, and the daughters of the city will come out to draw water. And there will be a maiden, to whom I will say, 'Give me a little water to drink from your jar,' etc. In Hebrew it is written: Behold, I stand by the fountain of water, and there will be a young girl who will come out to draw water. And I will say to her, 'Give me a little water to drink from your jar,' and so on. For the young girl, which in Greek is called νεανὶς, we also read Alma, which is found in Isaiah as well. For in that place where it is written in our books: Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son (Isa. VII, 14), Aquila translated it as: Behold, a young maiden shall conceive, and bear. In Hebrew it is read: Behold, a young woman shall conceive, and bear. It should be noted that the word alma is never used except for a virgin, and it has an etymology, ἀπόκρυφος, which means hidden. For it is written in Job: Where shall wisdom be found? And where is the place of understanding? And it will be hidden from the eyes of all living (Job 28:21). When we say 'it will be hidden,' in Hebrew it is expressed differently due to the declension of the word, it is said 'Naalma.' To what is this similar? Even though it is declined in the masculine gender, it is written in the books of Kings, spoken by Elisha to Gehazi: 'And the Lord has hidden it from me' (2 Kings 4:27). Therefore, Alma, which means hidden, that is, virgin, seems to me to be of greater praise than a virgin who is guarded with excessive diligence. For a virgin according to the Apostle can be a virgin in body, but not in spirit. However, a hidden virgin has the addition of virginity, so that she is both a virgin and hidden. And she who is hidden, according to the idiom of the Hebrew language, is consequently also a virgin; but she who is a virgin does not immediately follow that she is hidden. This same word is also read in Exodus about Mary, the sister of Moses, as a virgin (Exodus II, 8). Therefore, the Jews show somewhere in the Scriptures where Alma is mentioned, which signifies only a young girl and not a virgin; and we concede to them that what is said among us in Isaiah: Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son, does not signify a hidden virgin, but rather a young girl already married.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.59":["(Verse 59.) And they sent away their sister Rebecca, and her belongings, and Abraham's servant, and the men who were with him. In Hebrew, it says: And they sent away their sister Rebecca, and her nurse, and Abraham's servant, and his men. For it was fitting that a virgin setting off for marriage without her parents should be comforted by the consolation of her nurse.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.62-63":["(Verses 62 and 63.) And he himself dwelt in the land of the south: and Isaac went forth to meditate in the field towards evening. The land of the south signifies Gerar, to which he had once been led by his father to be sacrificed. But when it says, And he went forth to meditate in the field, which in Greek is called ἀδολεσχῆσαι, in Hebrew it is read: And Isaac went forth to speak in the field, the evening already declining. That signifies, according to what the Lord alone prayed on the mountain, even Isaac, who in the type of the Lord, as a just man going out of his house, offered spiritual victims to God either at the ninth hour or before sunset.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.24.65":["(Verse 65.) And he took a theristrum, and covered himself. Theristrum is called a cloak, also a type of Arabian garment, with which the women of that province are veiled.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.25.1":["(Chapter 25, Verse 1) And Abraham added and took another wife, and her name was Keturah. And she bore him Zimran, Jokshan, Medan, Midian, Ishbak, and Shuah. And Jokshan begot Sheba and Dedan. And the sons of Dedan were Asshurim, Letushim, and Leummim. And the sons of Midian were Ephah, Epher, Hanoch, Abida, and Eldaah. All these were the sons of Keturah. And Abraham gave everything that he had to Isaac. But to the sons of the concubines whom Abraham had, Abraham gave gifts, and he sent them away from Isaac his son, while he was still alive, to the east, to the land of the east. Cetura is interpreted in the Hebrew language as 'joined' or 'bound'. For this reason, the Hebrews suspect that, with a changed name, she is the same as Hagar, who, after the death of Sara, went from being a concubine to a wife. And it seems that the advanced age of Abraham is now being excused, so that he is not accused of indulging in new marriages after the death of his old wife. Leaving aside what is uncertain, we say this: the sons of Abraham born from Cetura, according to Hebrew historians, occupied Troglodytis and Arabia, which is now called Idumea, up to the borders of the Red Sea. But it is said that one of the descendants of Abraham, who was called Apher, led an army against Libya, and there, after defeating the enemies, settled. And his descendants from the name of their ancestor were called Africa. This fact is testified by Alexander, who is called Polyhistor, and Cleodemus, nicknamed Malchus, who retell the barbarian history in Greek. And as for what he says, 'And the sons of Dedan were Asshurim, and Letushim, and Leummim': they think that Asshurim refers to merchants, Letushim to those who work with copper and iron, and Leummim to chiefs, meaning leaders of many tribes and peoples. Others claim that they are called Asurim, that they are Syrians, and that the regions of India have been occupied by most of the sons of Abraham from Keturah.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.25.8":["(v. 8) And Abraham died in good old age, an old man and full, and he was gathered to his people. A wrong addition is made by the Septuagint translators, that Abraham died lacking: for it is not fitting for Abraham to fail and diminish. Also, what we have proposed, an old man and full in good old age: in the Greek manuscripts it is written, full of days. While it seems to explain the meaning, because he has passed away full of light and the works of the day: nevertheless, it is more fitting for the spiritual interpretation if it is simply stated as full.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","But if we wish to understand something deeper as well — because of what is said by the Seventy: Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth —, and to take failing in a good sense, according to that saying: And failing, Abraham died in a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people; and from what Scripture relates of Isaac and Jacob, we shall see how they fail with failing from the face of the earth, and that they fulfil this precept: Who, having their conversation in heaven, and in the flesh not warring according to the flesh, because they know that those who live in the flesh cannot please God, do everything, as far as in them lies, that they may not be in the flesh but in the spirit; and withdrawing from the earth they say: He has raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ. But if anyone should set against our taking Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth in a good sense that which is written of Ishmael: These are the years of the life of Ishmael, a hundred and thirty-seven, and failing he died, and was gathered to his kind, we shall answer him, first, that Ishmael himself too was a son of Abraham, and received gifts and portions from his father, according to his measure; then, that it is written absolutely: Failing he died, and there is not added what is written of Abraham: In a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people. Or of Isaac: And the days of Isaac which he lived were a hundred and eighty-five years, and failing Isaac died, and was gathered to his kind, an old man and full of days. And also of Jacob: And Jacob ceased charging his sons, and lifting his feet upon the bed he failed, and was gathered to his people. From this we understand that it is one thing merely to fail, and another, along with failing, to have many virtues together.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"GEN.25.13":["(Verse 13) And these are the names of the sons of Ishmael, by their names, according to their generations. The firstborn of Ishmael was Nebaioth, and Kedar, and the rest, up to the place where it is said: They lived from Havilah to Shur, which is opposite Egypt as you go towards Assyria. He fell in the sight of all his brothers. Twelve sons were born to Ishmael, and the firstborn was Nebaioth, from whom the whole region from the Euphrates to the Red Sea is called Nebaioth until this day, which is part of Arabia. For both their families, towns and villages, fortified castles, and three of them, Cedar in the desert, and Duma in another region, and Theman to the south, and Cedema to the east, are celebrated with this name. But what we read in the final section of this chapter, according to the Septuagint, is that he dwelt opposite the face of all his brothers (Gen. XIII, 18): it is more accurate to say what we have put, he died in the presence of all his brothers, that is, he died in the hands of all his children, with his surviving children, and with no one dying before him. But to be called brothers for the sons, Jacob also shows to Laban, saying: What is my sin, because you have persecuted me: and because you have searched all my vessels? What have you found of all the vessels of your house? Let it be placed before my brothers and your brothers: and let them judge between us (Ibid. 35). For we cannot believe, as Scripture recounts, that Jacob, apart from his children, had any brothers with him.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.25.17":["But if we wish to understand something deeper as well — because of what is said by the Seventy: Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth —, and to take failing in a good sense, according to that saying: And failing, Abraham died in a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people; and from what Scripture relates of Isaac and Jacob, we shall see how they fail with failing from the face of the earth, and that they fulfil this precept: Who, having their conversation in heaven, and in the flesh not warring according to the flesh, because they know that those who live in the flesh cannot please God, do everything, as far as in them lies, that they may not be in the flesh but in the spirit; and withdrawing from the earth they say: He has raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ. But if anyone should set against our taking Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth in a good sense that which is written of Ishmael: These are the years of the life of Ishmael, a hundred and thirty-seven, and failing he died, and was gathered to his kind, we shall answer him, first, that Ishmael himself too was a son of Abraham, and received gifts and portions from his father, according to his measure; then, that it is written absolutely: Failing he died, and there is not added what is written of Abraham: In a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people. Or of Isaac: And the days of Isaac which he lived were a hundred and eighty-five years, and failing Isaac died, and was gathered to his kind, an old man and full of days. And also of Jacob: And Jacob ceased charging his sons, and lifting his feet upon the bed he failed, and was gathered to his people. From this we understand that it is one thing merely to fail, and another, along with failing, to have many virtues together.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"GEN.25.21":["(Verse 21.) And Rebecca his wife conceived, and the sons within her were moved. Concerning this movement, the Seventy Interpreters rendered it as ἐσκίρτων, which means they were playing or kicking; which Aquila translated as they were breaking in the womb. But Symmachus rendered it as διέπλεον, which means they were carried on the surface like a ship.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.25.25":["(Verse 25) And the first came out red, all over like a hairy garment; and they called his name Esau. And after that came his brother out, and his hand took hold on Esau's heel; and his name was called Jacob. And Isaac was threescore years old when Rebekah bare them.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","Set, he says, (or turn) your face against Mount Seir; and he says 'turn' significantly: from one prophecy to another prophecy; and 'face': not of the body but of the mind — of which the Apostle also speaks: But we all, with unveiled face beholding the glory of the Lord —; and Mount 'Seir', which means 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is to be taken as the opposing power that presided over the nation of the sons of Esau and had raised itself up against the people of 'Iuda' [Judah], that is, of 'confession' and of the true faith — but if a prudent reader objects, how in this place 'Seir', that is, 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is understood in a bad sense when Elijah too was called a hairy man, I shall answer briefly: it is written of Esau that he was red and shaggy like a hide, but of Elijah only that he was a hairy man; in Esau, who, bloodthirsty and bloodstained, is compared to a shaggy hide, it is a sign of mortal works and of death itself; in Elijah, an increase of manliness, for which reason both his dwelling in the wilderness and the austerity of his way of life showed him to be not only a man but a most valiant man; moreover Jacob, who had supplanted Esau and received his birthright, because he dwelt simply at home and was not a hunter like the giant Nimrod, is for that reason called smooth and sleek —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"GEN.25.30":["(Verse 30) And Esau said to Jacob: Give me a taste of that red stew, for I am famished. That is why he was called Edom. Red or tawny is called Edom in the Hebrew language. Therefore, from the fact that he sold his birthright for red food, he obtained the name Edom, which means tawny.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.4":["Moreover, the example that has been taken from Genesis is contained thus in its own book: \"And in your seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed\" (Gen. 26:4). Interpreting this of Christ, the Apostle says: \"It is not written 'of seeds,' as in many, but as in one, 'and your seed,' which is Christ\". And this we ought to observe in almost all the testimonies that have been taken from the old books into the New Testament: that the evangelists or apostles trusted to memory, and, setting out only the sense, often changed the order, and sometimes either took words away or added them. But no one doubts that it was not in Isaac and Jacob, or in the twelve patriarchs, and the others who descend from the stock of Abraham, that all the nations were blessed, but in Christ Jesus, through whom all the Gentiles praise God, and a new name is blessed upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.26.12":["(Chapter 26, verse 12) And Isaac sowed in that land, and in that year he reaped a hundredfold. Although Isaac sowed in a foreign land, I do not think that such fertility of barley would have been to him. Therefore, I think the Hebrew version is better, and even Aquila translated it: And he reaped in that year a hundredfold estimated, that is, ἑκατὸν εἰκασμένον. Although the same letters are used for both estimation and barley, the estimations are read as Saarim (), and the barleys as Sorim. But Scripture, however, seems to me to have shown in it the multiplication of all virtues, in that it has increased a hundredfold the type of fruits.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","And the full and perfect number is in the number of ten decades, which also multiplies the sowing of Isaac with a hundredfold crop; but because he was on earth and could still say: I am a sojourner and a stranger, as all my fathers were, he is related to have sown barley and reaped a hundredfold crop: for the grain of wheat had not yet died in the earth, to bear many fruits and to satisfy the Jewish people's hunger for hearing the word of God with that bread which came down from heaven; but how the hundred cubits were not only toward the eastern gate but also toward the gate of the north, the following testimony will show.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"GEN.26.13":["(Verse 13.) And the Lord blessed him, and he became a great man, and he walked, going forth, and he was greatly magnified, until he became exceedingly great. But the abundance of barley, I do not know if it can make anyone glorious.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.17":["(Verse 17.) And Isaac departed from there and came to the valley of Gerar, and he dwelt there. The valley has a stream in Hebrew. For Isaac could not dwell in the valley after he became great. However, he dwelt by the stream, of which it is written: He shall drink from the brook by the way (Psalm 110:7). Also, during the time of famine, Elijah drank from that stream. But because Elijah was not perfect like Christ, therefore that stream dried up for him (1 Kings 17:7). Our Lord, indeed, was also handed over in the river: consecrating our regeneration and the sacrament of Baptism.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.18-22":["I think this same thing is signified also by what is in Genesis, that wells are dug by the servants of Isaac which had been made by Abraham and which the Philistines had stopped up with earth. As long as Abraham lives, his wells are not closed; but when he is dead and the wells are stopped up, if servants dig, the Philistines oppose them, and there is strife. But if Isaac himself comes and digs a well and finds water, the Philistines have no power to oppose him. See Peter and Paul, and you will not doubt about the wells and rivers of Christ.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"GEN.26.19":["(Verse 19) And the servants of Isaac dug in the valley of Gerar and found there a well of living water. And for this valley, a stream was written; for never is a well of living water found in the valley.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.21":["(Verse 21.) And they dug another well, and they quarreled over it. And he called its name Enmity. Concerning enmity, because Aquila and Symmachus translated it as τὴν ἀντικειμένην, and ἐναντίωσιν, which means opposing and contrary, in Hebrew it is Satana (). From which we understand Satana to be interpreted as the opposite.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.22":["(Verse 22.) And they dug another well, and they did not quarrel over it: so he called its name Broad Places; or, in Hebrew, Rehoboth, to prove what we have said above, that he himself built the city of Nineveh and its broad places (Genesis 10).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.26":["(Verse 26.) And Abimelech went to him from Gerar, and Ahuzzath his counselor, and Phicol the commander of his army. Regarding Ahuzzath his counselor, in Hebrew it means his group of friends: it signifies not so much a single person as a crowd of friends who came with the king, among whom was also Phicol the commander of his army.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.26.32-33":["(Verse 32 and 33.) And the servants of Isaac came and told him about the well that they had dug, and they said to him, 'We have found water.' So he called it Shebah. I don't know how it is translated in the Septuagint: 'And the servants of Isaac came and told him about the well that they had dug, and they said to him, 'We have not found water.' So he called it Oath.' What is the etymology behind calling it Oath just because they did not find water? On the contrary, in Hebrew, which Aquila and Symmachus agree with in interpretation, this signifies that they found water, and therefore the well itself was called Saturation, and the city was called Beersheba, which means well of saturation (Gen. XXI): for although above we asserted that Beersheba was called so from the word of the oath, or from the number seven of the sheep, which is called Sabee, now because water was found, Isaac turned slightly the letter to the name of the city, which was thus called alluding, and instead of the harsh sound of the Hebrews Sin, from which Sabee begins, he placed the Greek sigma, which is the Hebrew Samech: otherwise, according to the law of allegory, after so many wells at the end of virtues, it does not at all agree that Isaac did not find water.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.27.11":["(Chapter XXVII, Verse 11) Behold, Esau my brother is a hairy man, and I am a smooth man. Where it is written that he was hairy, in Hebrew it is written Seir (). Therefore, afterwards the mountains of Seir and the region in which he lived were called Seir: we have spoken of this before and above.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.27.15":["(Verse 15) And Rebecca took the garments of her elder son Esau, which were greatly desired by him at home. And in this place, the Hebrews tradition holds that the firstborns, who had fulfilled the duties of the priests, had a priestly garment, which they wore to offer sacrifices to God before Aaron was chosen for the priesthood.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.27.36":["(Verse 36.) And Esau said, rightly is his name called Jacob: for he hath supplanted me these two times. Jacob is interpreted supplanter. And therefore, because he had deceived his brother by art, he alluded to the name. Therefore, he was called Jacob before, because he had taken hold of his brother's heel in his birth.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.27.40":["(Verse 40.) And you shall serve your brother, and it shall come to pass when you shall have removed it, and shall have taken away his yoke from your neck. This signifies that the Edomites will serve the Jews, and that a time will come when they shall throw off the yoke of servitude from their neck and resist their dominion. However, according to the Seventy Interpreters, who said: 'But it shall come to pass when you shall have removed it, and shall have taken away his yoke from your neck,' it seems that the decision is uncertain and not yet complete.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.28.19":["(Chapter 28, verse 19) And he called the name of that place Bethel, but previously its name was Luz. From what he had said above: How awesome is this place, it is nothing but the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven: now he gives the place a name, and he calls it Bethel, which means the house of God. The place was previously called Luz, which means nut or almond. Therefore, some foolishly think that the Hebrew word Ulam is the name of a city, when Ulam actually means previously. Therefore, the order of the reading is as follows: And he called the name of that place Bethel, and before it was called Luz, a city. All the ancient scriptures are full of the word 'ulam', or 'Elam', which signifies nothing else but before, or previously, or threshold, or gateposts.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.29.27":["(Chapter 29, Verse 27) Complete therefore this week, and I will give you this also. After Jacob was deceived by Laban and took Rachel as his wife instead of Leah, it is said that Laban told him to fulfill seven days after the wedding of the first sister, and then he could have Rachel, for whom he would serve another seven years. Therefore, contrary to what some think, Rachel did not become Jacob's wife after another seven years, but after seven days from the wedding of the first wife. And afterward he went in also unto Rachel, and he loved also Rachel more than Leah, and served with him yet seven other years. (Gen. XXIX, 30).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.29.32":["(Verse 32.) And she conceived and bore a son, and called his name Reuben. I desire to also mention the etymologies of the names of all the patriarchs for the sake of brevity in reading.\n\nAnd he called, he said, his name is Ruben, saying: Because the Lord has seen my humility. Ruben () is interpreted as the son of vision.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.29.33":["(Verse 33.) And she conceived, he said, another son and said: Because the Lord has heard me, since my husband hated me, and also gave me this one: and she called his name Simeon. From the fact that she was heard, she gave him the name Simeon. For Simeon indeed means hearing. But from the third, it follows:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.29.34":["(Verse 34) And she conceived again, and bore a son, and said: Now my husband will be with me, because I have borne him three sons: therefore she called his name Levi. When we read, 'My husband will be with me,' Aquila interpreted it as, 'My husband will be attached to me,' which is the Hebrew word (), Illave. And among the Hebrew doctors, it is translated differently, so that they say, 'My husband will follow me,' that is, I have no doubt about the love of my husband for me: he will be my companion in this life, and his affection will lead me to death, and he will follow me: for I have borne him three sons.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.29.35":["(Verse 35.) And she conceived and bore a son, and she said: Now I will confess to the Lord, and for this reason she called his name Judah. Judah means confession. Therefore, the name confession is derived from the confession. However, here confession is understood as thanksgiving or praise, as often in the Psalms and in the Gospel (Matthew 11:25): I will confess to you, Lord, Father of heaven and earth, that is, I thank you or glorify you.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.5-6":["(Chapter XXX.—Verses 5 and 6) And Bala conceived and bore a son to Jacob: and Rachel said: The Lord judged me, and heard my voice, and gave me a son: therefore she called his name Dan. She expressed the reason for the name, that because the Lord had judged her, a son of the handmaid, she would give the name of judgment: Dan, indeed, is interpreted as judgment.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.7-8":["(Verse 7 and 8) And she conceived again, and bore a son. And Rachel said, God has judged me, and has also heard my voice, and has given me a son. Therefore she called his name Naphtali. The meaning of the name Naphtali is explained differently here than it is written in the Book of Hebrew Names. Hence also Aquila says, 'God has joined me, and I have joined together,' which in Hebrew is written Naphtule Elohim, Naphtalethi. From the conversion or from the comparison, since both signify conversion or comparison, he named the son Nephthalim. But what follows.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.10-11":["(Verse 10 and 11.) And Zilpah, the maid of Leah, bore Jacob a son, and Leah said, 'In fortunate circumstances,' and she called his name Gad. Where we have placed ourselves, in fortunate circumstances, and in Greek it is said ἐν τύχῃ, which can be translated as 'event.' In Hebrew, it is Bagad (), which Aquila interprets as 'girdle' or 'belt.' But we can say 'in readiness.' For Ba can sound like the preposition 'in,' and gad means 'coming.' Therefore, from the event or readiness, the son of Zilpah, Gad, was called. Sequitur:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.12-13":["(Verse 12 and 13.) And Zilpah, Leah's maidservant, gave birth to a second son for Jacob. And Leah said, 'I am blessed, for women will call me blessed.' And she called his name Asher, which means 'riches'. The riches are added with a play on words, that is, πλοῦτος. The etymology of the name Asher is explained by the authority of Scripture, which says, 'I am blessed, and women will call me blessed.' And because she is called blessed by people, she named her son blessed. Therefore, Asher is not called riches but blessed, only in this particular context. For in other cases, according to the ambiguity of the word, riches can be called thus.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.17-18":["(Verse 17 and 18.) And God heard Leah, and she conceived and bore Jacob her fifth son, and she said, 'God has given me my wages because I gave my maid to my husband.' And she called his name Issachar. The Septuagint Interpreters have given the etymology of this name, it is wages. Not that, as many wrongly read with an added pronoun, it is to be understood as written, which is wages, but the whole name is interpreted, it is wages. For it is said that Sachar, the son of Ruben, bought the entrance which was owed to Rachel, for a price. It follows:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.19-20":["(Verse 19 and 20.) And Leah conceived again and gave birth to her sixth son for Jacob. And Leah said, 'God has endowed me with a good endowment. Now my husband will dwell with me, because I have borne him six sons.' And she called his name Zebulun. Wherever we have settled, he will dwell with me.' And the Seventy translated it as 'he will love me.' In Hebrew, it is written as Jezbuleni. And the meaning is that I have borne Jacob six sons, so now I am secure, for my husband will dwell with me. Therefore, my son is called \"Habitaculum\". However, in the book of Numbers, Zabulon is translated as \"fluxus noctis\" (night's flow) wrongly and violently.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.21":["(Verse 21.) And after these things she gave birth to a daughter, and named her Dinah (). She is involved in a case, which the Greeks more significantly call δίκην. For there was a cause of contention in Shechem. After the sons, the names of the parents are to be given. Leah (), is interpreted as laboring. Rachel (), a sheep, whose son Joseph (), is called the addition because his mother had desired another to be added to her.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.32-33":["(Verse 32 and 33.) I will pass through all your flock today: separate from it all the variegated and discolored animals: and all the animals of one color will be lambs and the variegated and discolored ones will be goats, they will be my reward: and my justice will be answered to me the next day, when my reward comes before you: everything that is not variegated and discolored among the goats and lambs, will be considered as theft to me, and so on. The meaning of this passage has been greatly confused among the Septuagint Interpreters, and until today I have not been able to find any clear explanation from our scholars regarding what is meant in this passage. I will serve you, Jacob, he said, seven more years, do what I demand. Separate all the speckled and spotted, both the sheep and the goats, and give them into the hands of your sons. And from the entire flock, the white and the black animals, that is, of one color, give them to me. So if anything speckled or spotted, which are of one color, is born, it will be mine; but if anything of one color is born, it will be yours. I ask for a not difficult thing. Nature makes it so that white offspring are born from white animals, and black offspring are born from black ones: my justice will be with me, as long as God looks at my humility and labor. He eagerly seized the opportunity given by Laban, and, doing as Jacob requested, separated Jacob and his sons with a three-day journey, so that no deceitful child would be born from near the flock. Therefore, Jacob devised a new strategy and fought against the natural order of white and black livestock with his cunning. For he made three rods, of poplar, almond and pomegranate, even though the Seventy had storax, walnut and plane tree rods, partly stripping the bark, to create a variety of rod colors, so that wherever he left the bark on the rod, the ancient color would remain; but where he removed the bark, a bright color would be revealed. So Jacob observed that at the time when the flocks were mating, and after the heat of the day, when they were thirsty and eager to drink, he would place the discolored rods in the troughs. And as the rams and goats came to drink, driven by their intense desire, he would make them mate, so that from this double longing, as they eagerly drank and were mounted by the males, they would conceive offspring resembling the shadows of the rams and goats as they ascended from above in the mirror of the water. For when men and women have experienced those of varied color in the bedding, it is not surprising that this is the nature of women, that they give birth to offspring resembling those they have seen or imagined in the heat of extreme pleasure; this is said to also happen among herds of mares in Spain. And Quintilian, in a controversy where a matron was accused of giving birth to an Ethiopian, argues in defense of her that this is the nature of conceptions, as we have mentioned above. But when the kids were born, and various lambs of different colors from white and solid color herds, Jacob separated them and made them be far from the original flock. But if any were born of one color, that is, white or black, he would give them into the hands of Laban's sons, and he would place the peeled rods in the troughs where the water was poured out, and they would come to drink opposite the animals, so that they would conceive at the time when they came to drink.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.30.41-42":["(Ver. 41 and 42.) And the flocks conceived against the rods that Jacob had set before the flocks in the gutters, so that they conceived by the rods; but the sheep that were weak, he did not put the rods. And Laban's flocks were weak, but Jacob's were strong and healthy. This is not found in the Septuagint Interpreters, but instead of the weak and healthy ones, they translated something else, I don't know what, which does not make sense. But what the Scripture says is this: Jacob, wise and clever, observed justice and fairness even in a new way. For if all the ewes and goats were producing various offspring, there would be some suspicion of deceit, and openly Laban, envying this situation, would oppose it. Therefore, he managed everything in such a way that he himself would benefit from the result of his labor, and Laban would not be completely deprived. If ever the sheep and goats were bred in the early season, because the spring offspring is better, he would place rods in front of them so that diverse offspring would be born. But whatever sheep and goats were bred late and were seeking a male, he would not place objects in front of their eyes so that animals of a single color would be born. And whatever was first born belonged to him, because it was different and varied: whatever came after, belonged to Laban: for from one colored cattle, both black and white, were born. But in the place where it is written, that they conceive in rods: in Hebrew it has Jeamena (). I cannot express the force of the Hebrew word without going around it. Jeamena properly means the extreme heat in sexual intercourse, by which the whole body trembles, and is near the end of pleasurable satisfaction.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.31.7-8":["(Chapter XXXI, verses 7 and 8) And your father deceived me and changed my wages ten times, and God did not allow him to harm me. If he said, 'Your wages will be the spotted flock,' then every flock was born spotted. And if he said, 'Your wages will be the solid-colored flock,' then every flock was born solid-colored. Because of this, he changed my wages ten times. The Seventy Interpreters have stated, driven by some unknown opinion, that 'moni' in the Hebrew language means 'number' rather than 'lambs.' Finally, the meaning is further confirmed by the fact that Laban always changed the condition of each offspring. If he saw mixed livestock being born, he would say after the birth, 'I want mixed offspring in the future.' Again, when he saw single-colored animals being born (because Jacob had heard this, he did not place rods in the canals), he would say that the future offspring should be of a single color for him. And what more? Laban changed the condition of his flock or Jacob up to ten times. And whatever color he had intended to be born as, it turned into the opposite color. And so that no one may think that in ten years twenty births are incredible, read Virgil (Georg. II), in which it is said:\n\nThe pregnant cattle twice. However, it is said that the nature of Italian and Mesopotamian sheep is the same.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.31.19":["(Verse 19) And Rachel stole her father's idols. Where we now read idols, it is written in Hebrew Theraphim, which Aquila interprets as μορφώματα, that is, figures or images. This is done so that we may understand what the word Theraphim means in the Book of Judges (17:5).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.31.21":["(Verse 21) And he crossed the river and came to the mountain of Gilead. Not that at that time it was called Gilead; but in anticipation, as we have often said, it is called by that name by which it was later called.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.31.41":["(Verse 41.) And you changed my reward to ten lambs. The same mistake as above: The word 'numerus' should be read instead of 'agnis'.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.31.46":["(Verse 46.) And Jacob said to his brethren, 'Gather stones.' And they gathered stones and made a heap, and they ate there upon the heap. And Laban called it Jegar-sahadutha, but Jacob called it Galeed. The heap is called Galeed in the Hebrew language and Jegar-sahadutha in the Syriac language, both meaning 'witness heap'. Jacob called the heap 'Galeed' in Hebrew, and Laban called it 'Jegar-sahadutha' in the language of his people. For he was a Syrian, and had changed the ancient language of his parents to the language of the province in which he dwelt.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.1-2":["(Chapter XXXII.—Verse 1 and 2.) And the angels of God met him, and when Jacob saw them, he said: These are the camps of God, and he called the name of that place Mahanaim. Where these camps were placed, in Hebrew it means Mahanaim (); so that we may know, if it is ever mentioned in another place without interpretation, what place it signifies. And beautifully, as he goes to meet his enemy brother, he is received by choirs of accompanying angels.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.2":["(Chapter XXXII, Verse 2) And Sara died in the city of Arboc, which is in the valley: this is Chebron, in the land of Canaan. This that is placed here, which is in the valley, is not found in authentic codices. The name of the city Arboc has also gradually been corrupted by scribes and readers. For it should not be assumed that the Seventy Interpreters translated the name of the Hebrew city in a barbaric and corrupted manner, differently than it is said in its own language. Indeed, 'Arboc' signifies nothing at all. But it is said to be 'Arbee' (that is, four) because there Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob were buried, and also the first of the human race, Adam, as will be more clearly demonstrated in the book of Jesus.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.6":["(Verse 6) Hear us, O Lord, you are our king from God, bury your dead in our chosen sepulcher. For a king is called a prince in Hebrew, or a leader. For a leader is called not a king, but a duke.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.9-10":["(Verse 9 and 10.) And Jacob said, O God of my father Isaac, Lord, who said to me, Return to your land and I will bless you: I am less than all your mercies, and all your truth, which you have shown to your servant. For because of what we have done, I am less, something else I do not know, which disturbs the meaning, is contained in the Greek and Latin codices.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.16":["(Verse 16.) And Abraham heard Ephron, and Abraham weighed out to Ephron the silver, which he had spoken of in the hearing of the sons of Heth. In Hebrew, as we have written here, his first name is spelled Ephron and his second name is Ephran. For after he was overcome by the price, in order to sell the burial place for silver, even though Abraham was urging him, the letter vau, which is read as o by them, was removed from his name: and he was called Ephran instead of Ephron, with the Scripture signifying that he was not a man of consummate and perfect virtue who could sell the memories of the dead. Therefore, let it be known that those who sell graves, and are not compelled to accept a price, but extort it even from those unwilling, should have their name changed and their deserved fate be undone, since even the one who unwillingly receives it is secretly condemned.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.27-28":["(Verse 27 and 28.) And he said to him: What is your name? He said, Jacob. And he said to him: Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but your name shall be called Israel: for you have wrestled with God, and have prevailed with men. Josephus, in the first book of Antiquities, thinks that Israel was called so because he wrestled with an angel. I carefully examined this, but I could not find it at all in Hebrew. And why should I seek the opinions of individuals, when the one who gave the name explains the etymology himself? He said, 'Your name will no longer be Jacob, but Israel will be your name.' Why? Aquila interprets it as 'because you have begun with God,' Symmachus as 'because I will start towards God,' the Septuagint and Theodotio as 'because you have prevailed with God.' Sarith (which is derived from the word Israel) means 'prince.' Therefore, the meaning is: Your name will not be supplanter, that is, Jacob, but your name will be prince with God, that is, Israel. For just as I am a prince, so you, who were able to wrestle with me, shall be called a prince. But if you were able to wrestle with me, who am God or an angel (for many interpret it differently), how much more so with humans, that is, with Esau, whom you should not fear! However, that which is interpreted as 'Israel' in the book of Numbers, 'a man who sees God' or 'a mind that sees God', seems to me to have been interpreted not so truly, but rather forcefully, in the common speech. For here Israel, as it is written in these letters, is spelled Iod, Sin, Res, Aleph, Lamed, which means Prince of God, or Direct of God, that is, εὐθύτατος Θεοῦ. But the word for \"seeing\" God is spelled Aleph, Iod, Sin, so that it is written with three letters and pronounced Is (), while \"seeing\" is spelled with three letters, Res, Aleph, He, and pronounced Raa (). And El () is formed by two letters, Aleph and Lamed, and it means God, or strong. Although, therefore, they are of great authority, and the shadow of their eloquence oppresses us, who have transformed the man, or rather the mind perceiving God, into the Israel: we are rather led by the authority of Scripture, and of the Angels, or of God, who called him Israel, than by any worldly eloquence. That which also follows afterwards:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.32.29-30":["(Verse 29 and 30.) And he blessed him there and called the name of that place Face of God: for I have seen God face to face, and my soul has been saved; in Hebrew it is called Phanuel (), so that we know that it is the same place, which in the other sacred volumes of Scripture, as it is written in Hebrew, is read as Phanuel in Greek.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.33.1-2":["(Chapter 33, verses 1 and 2.) And he divided the children over Leah and over Rachel, and over the two maidservants, and he placed the maidservants and their firstborn sons: but Leah and her youngest sons, and Rachel and Joseph the youngest. And he himself went before them. No, as most people think, he did not make three groups, but two. In conclusion, where we have it, he divided, Aquila placed, halfed, that is, halved: so that he would make one group of maidservants with their little ones, and another of Leah and Rachel, who were free, with their sons: and he made the maidservants go first and the free women second: but he himself went before both groups, to meet his brother and pay homage.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.33.17":["(Verse 17) And Jacob built a house there for himself, and he built shelters for his livestock; therefore he called the name of that place Tabernacles. Where we have tents, it is read in Hebrew as Sochoth. Furthermore, until today, there is a city beyond the Jordan with this name in the region of Scythopolis, about which we have written in the book of Joshua.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.33.18":["(Verse 18) And Jacob came to the city of Shechem, which is in the land of Canaan, when he came from Mesopotamia of Syria. A confusion arises, as to how Shechem is called the city of Salem, when Jerusalem, where Melchizedek reigned, was previously called Salem. Therefore, it seems that both cities have the same name, which we can also find for various places in Judea: that the same name of the city and place is in different tribes; or certainly we can say that the Shechem which is now called Sichem, translates to \"consummated\" or \"perfect\" in our language, and that the one which was later called Jerusalem is transferred to \"peaceful\". For both meanings of the word, with a slight difference in accent, this word is used. The Hebrews say that it refers to the limp of Jacob's thigh, where he became weakened and was healed. Therefore, they consider this city the same as the one that received the name of the cured and perfected.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.34.20":["(Chapter 34, Verse 20) And Emor and his son Sichem came to the gate of their city, and they spoke to the men of the city, saying, these men are peaceful with us. Where now the Septuagint translated it as 'peaceful', Aquila interpreted it as 'consummated' or 'perfected', for which in Hebrew it is read () Salamim. From this it is clear that what we said above about Salem is true.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.34.25":["(Verse 25) And they entered the city diligently, and they killed every male. Because it is read in Greek as ἀσφαλὼς, which means diligently: it is written in Hebrew as Bete (), which means boldly and confidently.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.6":["(Chapter 35, Verse 6.) And Jacob came to Luz in the land of Canaan, which is Bethel. Here it is clearly confirmed that Bethel is not Ulammaus, as stated above, but Luz, that is, the almond tree mentioned before.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.7":["(Verse 7.) And Jacob came to his father Isaac in Mamre, the city of Arba, which is Hebron. Arba was formerly a great city with fields surrounding it, while Hebron itself is situated on a mountain. This city is also called Mamre, as it has been called since ancient times by Abraham's friend.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.8":["(Verse 8) And Deborah, the nurse of Rebekah, died and was buried near Bethel. If the nurse of Rebekah named Deborah is dead, as the Septuagint interpreters also translated here, and the Hebrew word is Meneketh: we cannot know why they put 'substantiam' there, 'nutricem' here.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.10":["(Verse 10) And God said to him, Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel shall be your name: and he called his name Israel. For a long time, his name was not given to him by an angel, but it was predicted that it would be given by God. Therefore, what is promised to happen there is taught to be fulfilled here.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.16":["(Verse 16.) And it came to pass, while he was approaching Chabratha in the land of Canaan, to come to Ephratha, Rachel gave birth to the Hebrew word () Chabratha, in the following, where Jacob speaks to Joseph: But when I came from Mesopotamia in Syria, your mother Rachel died in the land of Canaan, on the way to Hippodrome, to come to Ephrata (Gen. 48): I do not know what they wanted, the Septuagint interpreters translated Hippodrome: and immediately there, where it is said in Hebrew: And they buried her on the way to Ephrata, this is Bethlehem (Gen. 35:19), again the Septuagint put Hippodrome instead of Ephrata: since surely if Chabratha is Hippodrome, Ephrata cannot be Hippodrome. But Aquila translated it this way: And it came to pass καθ᾽ ὁδον τῆς γῆς, that is, in the journey of the earth, when he entered into Ephratah. But it is better if it is translated, in the chosen time of the earth, when he entered into Ephratha. Moreover, it signifies the springtime, when all things burst into flower, and the chosen time of the year: or when those passing by along the way pluck and choose from the nearby fields whatever comes to hand, enticed by various flowers. Indeed, Ephrata and Bethlehem are two names for the same city, with similar meanings. For they both translate to 'fruitful' and 'house of bread', respectively. This is because of the bread that is said to have come down from heaven (John 6).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.18":["(Verse 18.) And it came to pass, when her soul departed, for she died, that he called her name, 'The Son of my Sorrow'; but his father called his name Benjamin. In Hebrew, the similarity of the name is echoed; for the son of my sorrow, which the dying mother gave him as a name, is called Benoni (). However, the son of the right hand, that is, of strength, which Jacob changed, is called Benjamin. Hence, those who think Benjamin () is interpreted as 'Son of Days' are mistaken. For when the right hand is called Jamin (), and it ends in the letter n: the days are indeed called Jamim (), but they end in the letter m.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.35.21":["(Verse 21.) And Israel advanced and pitched his tent beyond the tower of Eder. The Hebrews believe that this place is where the temple was later built, and the tower of Eder signifies the tower of the flock, that is, the congregation and assembly. This is also testified to by the prophet Micah, saying: And you, O cloudy tower of the flock, daughter of Zion (Micah 4:8), and so on. At that time, Jacob set up his tents across the place where the temple was later built. But if we follow the order of the way, there is a place near Bethlehem where the shepherds were, where even the King of the Angels sang at the birth of the Lord, or where Jacob fed his flock, giving the place its name, or what is more true, a certain prophecy was already then indicating the future mystery.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.4":["(Chapter 36, Verse 4) And Ada gave birth to Esau Eliphaz. This is Eliphaz, whose name is mentioned in the book of Job.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.19":["(Verse 19.) These are the sons of Esau, and these are their chiefs; he is Edom. And these are the sons of Seir. Esau and Edom and Seir are the names of one man; and the reason why they are called by different names has been explained above. And what follows:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.20":["(Verse 20.) And the inhabitants of Chorraea, and the rest. After he enumerated the sons of Esau, he goes on further and explains who were the princes from the race of the Chorraeans who were before Esau in the land of Edom, which in our language is interpreted as free. Let us read diligently Deuteronomy (II, 21), where it is written more clearly how the sons of Esau came and killed the Chorraeans, and possessed their land as inheritance.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.22":["(Verse 22.) And the sons of Lotan were Hori and Homam, and Lotan's sister was Timna. This is Timna, of whom it was said above. And Timna was the concubine of Eliphaz, the firstborn son of Esau, and from her was born Amalek. Now it is worth noting that the Horites, because the firstborn sons of Esau took their concubines as wives. And as for the names that are mentioned, Teman and Kenaz and Amalek, and so on: we will learn later the regions of the Edomites from which these names were derived.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.24":["(Verse 24) He is Ana, who found Jamin in the desert, while feeding the donkeys of his father Zebeon. Much and varied is debated among the Hebrews about this chapter: for indeed, both the Greeks and our own are silent on this. Some believe that Ajamim () is a name for the seas. For the same letters are used to write seas as are used in the present discourse. And they say that he, while feeding the donkeys of his father in the desert, found gatherings of water, which are called seas in the Hebrew language: namely, that he discovered a lake, the discovery of which is difficult in the wilderness. Some people believe that hot springs near the border of the Punic language, which is adjacent to Hebrew, are signified by this word. There are those who think that wild donkeys have bred with female donkeys through this act, and thus the fastest offspring known as Jamim are born. Many believe that he himself was the first to ascend the herds of female horses with donkeys in the desert, so that new animals contrary to nature, mules, would be born from there. Eagle translated this passage as follows: He is Ana, who discovered it with the mimics. And Symmachus likewise translated the mimics, which signifies a plural number. However, the Seventy and Theodotion translated it equally, saying: the mimi, which indicates a singular number.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.36.33":["(Verse 33.) And Jobab, son of Zerah from Bozrah, reigned in his place. Some suspect that this is the same Job, as it is added at the end of his own book. However, the Hebrews assert that he was descended from Nahor, as has already been said.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.37.3":["(Chapter 37, Verse 3) And Israel loved Joseph more than all his sons, because he was the son of his old age: and he made him a coat of many colors. As for the coat of many colors, Aquila interprets it as a long-sleeved tunic, that is, a talar garment. Symmachus, a tunic with sleeves, or because it reached down to the ankles, and was distinguished by the wonderful variety of the craftsman's hands: or because it had sleeves: For the ancients used more often to wear woven garments.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.37.28":["(Verse 28) And the Ishmaelites sold Joseph for twenty silver coins. In Hebrew, silver coins are used instead of gold coins. For the Lord should not be sold for a lesser metal than Joseph.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","Who fixed for him thirty pieces of silver. Joseph was not sold, as many think, for twenty pieces of gold, according to the LXX translators, but, according to the Hebrew truth, for twenty pieces of silver. For the servant could not be more precious than the master.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"GEN.37.36":["(Verse 36.) Now the Midianites sold Joseph in Egypt to Potiphar, an eunuch of Pharaoh and the chief of the royal executioners. In many places the Scriptures mention the chief of the executioners as the chief of the cooks, for μαγειρεύειν in Greek means to cook. Therefore, Joseph was sold to the chief of the army and the warriors, not to Potiphar, as it is written in Latin, but to Potiphar the eunuch. The question arises, how he is later said to have a wife. The Hebrews report that Joseph was bought by this person because of his excessive beauty for a disgraceful service, and after the Lord's men were dried up, he was later chosen according to the custom of the Hierophants as the high priest of Heliopolis; and his daughter was Aseneth, whom Joseph later took as his wife.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.5":["(Chapter 38—Verse 5) And she added, and gave birth to a son, and called his name Shelah; but she was in Chezib when she gave birth to him. The Hebrew word here is used in place of a geographical name, which Aquila translated as a thing, saying: And she called his name Shelah. And it came to pass that she lied in childbirth after she gave birth to Shelah. For after she gave birth to Shelah, her childbirth ceased. Therefore, Chezib is called not a place name, but a deception. And elsewhere it is written: 'The work of the olive tree will fail, that is, it will not produce fruit.' (Habakkuk 3:17).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.12":["(Verse 12.) And Judas, having been comforted, went up to those who were shearing his sheep: himself, and his shepherd Hirah the Odollamite. For the shepherd is read as a friend. But the word is ambiguous, because with the same letters both names are written: but the friend Ree is read as the shepherd Roe.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.14":["(Verse 14) And he sat at the gate of Enaim, which is on the road to Thamna. The Hebrew phrase Enaim (), is translated as 'in the eyes.' Therefore, it is not a place name, but rather a description. He sat at a crossroads or a junction where a traveler must carefully look to choose the right path.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.26":["(Verse 26.) But Judas acknowledged and said: she is more righteous than I, because I did not give her to my son Selom. In Hebrew, it says, she has been justified by me: not that she was just, but that in comparison to her, I have done less evil, not seeking base immorality, but seeking children.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.28-30":["That too which is read in the same book about Tamar and her two little ones (Gen. 38), namely that the first, who is called Zerah, put out his hand, and the midwife tied a scarlet thread on it, and then, as he drew his hand back inside, the later one, who is called Perez, stretched out his hand, fits the present place: that Israel showed its hand in the work of the Law, and drew it back defiled with the blood of the prophets and of the Savior himself. But afterward the people of the Gentiles broke forth, on whose account it is said to have been destroyed many times, and the middle wall which had stood between the Jews and the Gentiles to have been broken down, so that there might be one flock and one shepherd, and glory and honor and peace to everyone who works good, to the Jew first and to the Greek.\n— Commentary on Galatians","This indeed will become clearer if we take an example from Genesis, where twins are born of Tamar, with whom the patriarch Judah lay: It came to pass, it says, when she was giving birth, and had twins in her womb; in the very birth one put forth his hand, and the midwife tied scarlet on his hand, saying: This one will come out first. But when he had drawn back his hand, at once his brother came out, and she said: Why has the wall been divided for your sake? And she called his name Perez, which is interpreted division. And after this his brother came out, on whose hand was the scarlet, and she called his name Zerah, which in our language means seed, or rising. The elder people, then, in whom was the rising and the seed before the Church was born from the Gentiles, showed its hand in works, and afterwards drew it back, hearing through Isaiah: For your hands are full of blood. And when he had drawn back his hand and ceased from the works of justice, his brother, the people of the Gentiles, came out. And for his sake the hedge was divided, and the Lord and Savior destroyed, so that he might be able to advance, the partition which was in the middle and the wall separating the two peoples; and he made one flock, and created the two in himself into one new man, making peace. Hence too the midwife, speaking prophetically, says to Perez, the younger people: Why has the wall been divided for your sake? If you have understood the example from Genesis, go up, you who desire to be saved, by no means through the old people, which has its hand drawn back, but through the new, in which Christ is the way, in whom Jesus is the gate, through whom we advance to the Father; for he himself dissolved the middle partition and wall, that is, the obscurity of the ancient prophets, and opened all the mysteries of the old law, and, having scattered the difficulty of advancing, revealed the way to the eyes of all, so that whoever wishes to go on may be hindered by no obstacle nor be frightened by the fog of obscurity.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"GEN.38.29":["(Ver. 29.) And behold, his brother came out, and said, why is the wall divided because of you? And he called his name Phares. Concerning the wall, Aquila and Symmachus translated it as division, which in Hebrew is called Phares (). Therefore, he received the name of division because he divided the curtain of the second (also called placenta). Hence the Pharisees, who had separated themselves as if they were righteous from the people, were called the divided ones.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.38.30":["(Verse 30.) After this, his brother came out, who had the scarlet thread in his hand, and his name was called Zara. This name means 'east'. Therefore, either because he appeared first, or because many righteous people were born from him, as it is written in the book of Chronicles (1 Chronicles 2 and following), he was called Zara, that is, 'east'.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.40.1":["(Chapter 40, Verse 1) After these words, the chief of the king of Egypt's wine cellar sinned. Where we have placed 'chief of the wine cellar' in Hebrew it is written 'Masec', that word which we have recently read in the name of Abraham's servant, whom we can call the cupbearer according to the custom of the common people. Nor should this office be considered lowly, since it is of the utmost dignity even among barbarian kings to offer a cup to the king: Indeed, poets have written about Ganymede and Jove, that he entrusted his beloved to this very duty.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.40.10":["(Verse 10) And behold, a vine was before me, and in it there were three branches, and it was sprouting three shoots. The words in Hebrew, which are called Sarigim, signify three scourges and three branches or shoots.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.40.16":["(Verse 16.) And it seemed to me that I was carrying three baskets of wheat on my head. For the three baskets of wheat, he has three measures of flour in Hebrew.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.2":["(Chapter 41, Verse 2) And behold, seven cows were coming up from the river, attractive to look at, well-fed and well-built, and they were grazing in the meadow. In Genesis it is written as Achi (Ἄχα), which is neither a Greek nor Latin word. Even in Hebrew it is corrupted: it is said to be Ahu (אָחוּ), which means 'in the marsh'. But because the Hebrew letters Vau and Yod are similar and only differ in size, the Seventy Interpreters translated Ahu as Achi and, according to their custom of expressing double aspiration, they joined the Hebrew letter Heth with the Greek letter Chi (χ).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.16":["(Verse 16) And Joseph answered Pharaoh, saying: Without God, salvation will not be given to Pharaoh. In Hebrew, it is different: Without me, God will answer peace to Pharaoh. Finally, Symmachus translated more openly in his own way: Not I, but God will answer peace to Pharaoh.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.29":["(Verse 29.) Behold, seven years of great abundance are coming in all the land of Egypt. I wonder how the Hebrew word () Sabee, which we translated above as abundance or satiety, in reference to the well that the servants of Isaac dug, can now be translated most accurately as oath when we use the Seventy (Septuagint) translation. Both oath and seven and satiety and abundance can be understood as the context and order demand. But also in subsequent places, wherever abundance is mentioned, the same word is written in Hebrew.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.43":["(Verse 43.) And the herald cried out before him, and he set him over all the land of Egypt. Aquila translates it as: And he cried out in his presence with adgnition. Symmachus, interpreting the Hebrew speech, says: And he cried out before him 'Abrech' (). Therefore, it seems to me that the title 'herald' or the act of adgnition in greeting or adoring Joseph can be understood not so much as the Hebrews transmit it, saying 'tender father,' which is transferred in this speech; for 'Ab' means father, and 'Rech' means tender or most tender; and Scripture indicates that in wisdom indeed he was the father of all, but in age he was a young man and a boy.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.45":["(Verse 45) And Pharaoh called Joseph's name Saphaneth Phanee, and he gave him Aseneth, the daughter of Potiphera, priest of Heliopolis, as his wife. Although this name sounds like the revealer of hidden things in Hebrew, because it is Egyptian, it must have its own meaning in that language. Therefore, it is interpreted in the Egyptian language as Saphaneth Phanee, or as the Seventy wished to translate it, Psomthom-Phanech, the Savior of the world, because he delivered the world from impending famine. It should be noted, however, that the Lord once took the daughter of His owner as a wife, who was the high priest of Heliopolis in that place. For it is not right for the priests of that idol to be without eunuchs, so that the suspicion regarding what we have already said may be confirmed.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.41.50-10":["(Verse 50 and following) And Joseph had two sons before the years of famine came, whom Aseneth, the daughter of Potipherah, the priest of On, bore to him. And Joseph called the name of his firstborn son Manasseh, for God has made me forget all my toil and all my father's house. And the name of the second he called Ephraim, for God has made me fruitful in the land of my affliction. Note, however, regarding the question that will be posed shortly about Joseph's sons, that before the time of famine, when Jacob entered Egypt, Joseph had only two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim. Manasse, calling him because he has forgotten his labors. For in Hebrew, forgetfulness is called oblivio. Ephraim, because God has made him fruitful. And from this word, the increase is transferred into our language.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.43.11":["(Chapter 43, Verse 11) And bring gifts to the man, some resin, and honey, incense, and resin, and turpentine, and nuts. Or, as Aquila and Symmachus translated, almonds. Therefore, we have included this chapter to know where in our codices the term 'incense' is found, in Hebrew it is 'Necotha' (), which Aquila translated as storax. From which the house of 'nechota,' which is read in Isaiah (39:21), is understood to be the most obvious cell for incense, or storax, as different fragrances are stored in it.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.43.32":["(Verse 32) And they drank, and were intoxicated with it. The idiom of the Hebrew language is to equate drunkenness with satiety, as it says there: 'He shall be satisfied with the abundance of rain, with the produce of his land he shall be intoxicated.' (Psalm 65:11) Undoubtedly, this refers to a land irrigated by rain.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.44.2":["(Chapter XLIV - Verse 2) And put the silver of each one in the mouth of the bag, and put my silver cup in the mouth of the youngest one's bag. For bag, it has a peronem or follem in Hebrew. For cup, which we also read in Isaiah (LI, 17), Aquila translated as scyphum, Symmachus as phialam.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.45.17":["(Verse 17.) But Pharaoh said to Joseph, tell your brothers to do this: load your vehicles and go to the land of Canaan. As for the vehicles, which were interpreted as 'carriages' by the Septuagint and Theodotio, the remaining animals transported them.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.45.21":["(v. 21) And he gave them food along the way. The word Seda, which here everyone with one voice translates as 'provisions' (ἐπισιτισμὸν in Greek), is also found in the Psalter. For where our translation reads, 'I will abundantly bless her widow' (Ps. 132:15), (although in most manuscripts it is written as 'widow,' which is χήρα in Greek, some read it as 'prey,' θήραν), in Hebrew it has Seda, which means 'provisions.' Furthermore, the word 'θήραν' can more often refer to hunting than to crops, although it is the custom of the Egyptians to also call crops 'θήραν', which is now corrupted to 'atheran'.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.46.26-27":["(Chapter XLVI, Verse 26 and following) Therefore all the souls that entered Egypt with Jacob, and those that came out of his thighs, apart from the wives of Jacob's sons, were sixty-six souls; and the sons of Joseph, who were born to him in Egypt, were nine souls. Therefore all the souls that entered Egypt with Jacob were seventy-five. It is without doubt that except for Joseph and his sons, sixty-six souls that came out of Jacob's thighs entered Egypt. For thus also, gradually through each calculated number, it is proven, and it is found in the Hebrew volumes. But what we read in the Septuagint: And the sons of Joseph, who were born to him in Egypt, were nine souls: let us know that in Hebrew, instead of nine, there are two. For Ephraim and Manasseh were born to him before Jacob entered Egypt, and before the time of famine. They were born of Aseneth, the daughter of Potiphera, in Egypt. But also that which we read above: And the sons of Manasseh became, whom the Syrian concubine gave birth to him, Machir, and Machir gave birth to Gilead; and the sons of Ephraim, the brother of Manasseh, Suthalaam, and Thaam; and the sons of Suthalaam, Edem, it is added: indeed, what we read afterwards is described as if it had happened by anticipation. For at that time when Jacob entered Egypt, Ephraim and Manasseh were not old enough to be able to have children. From this it is evident that all the souls who entered Egypt from the loins of Jacob were seventy, but afterwards sixty-six entered, and they found (Al. gave birth to) three souls in Egypt, namely Joseph with his two sons, and the seventieth was Jacob himself. In order that we do not seem to speak against the authority of Scripture, even the Seventy Interpreters translated in Deuteronomy (X, 22) that Jacob (also called Israel) entered Egypt with seventy souls. Therefore, if anyone opposes our opinion, they will make Scripture contradict itself. For the Seventy Interpreters themselves, who said that seventy-five souls, including Joseph and his descendants, entered Egypt through πρόληψις, only mentioned in Deuteronomy that seventy entered (some say 'entered'). But if the opposite is opposed to us, how is it said in the Acts of the Apostles (VII) , in the sermon of Stephen to the people, that seventy-five souls entered Egypt; the explanation is easy. For Saint Luke, who is the author of the history itself, should not have written anything contrary to that Scripture, which had already been made known to the Gentiles, when he published the volume of the Acts of the Apostles. And certainly, at that time, the authority of the Seventy Interpreters was considered greater than that of Luke, who was unknown, and despised, and not held in high esteem by the nations. However, it is generally observed that wherever the holy Apostles or Apostolic men speak to the peoples, they often make use of testimonies that had already been spread among the nations: although many report that Luke the Evangelist, as a proselyte, was ignorant of the Hebrew letters.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.46.28":["(v. 28) But Judah was sent ahead of him to Joseph, to meet him in the city of Heroum in the land of Ramesse. In Hebrew, there is no city called Heroum, nor a land called Ramesse, but only Gesen. Some Jews assert that Gesen is now called Thebaid. And what follows afterwards: He gave them the best land in Egypt, in Ramesse, which they believe to be the village called Arsenoites in ancient times.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.47.9":["It is asked how the present age has been called evil. For heretics are accustomed to seize occasions from this passage to assert that there is one creator of light and of the age to come, and another of darkness and of the present age. We, however, say that it is not so much the age itself, which runs on by day and night, by years and months, that is called evil, as, homonymously (ὁμωνύμως), the things that are done in the age: just as the day is said to have enough of its own evil (Matt. 6), and the days of Jacob are written to be few and very evil (Gen. 47). Not that the span of time in which Jacob lived was evil, but that the things he endured exercised him through various trials. Indeed, at the very time when he was serving for his wives and was struggling with many hardships (Gen. 29), Esau was at rest; and so the same span of time was good for one and evil for the other.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"GEN.47.31":["(Chapter 47, Verse 31) And he said to him, swear to me. And he swore to him: and Israel worshipped against the top of his staff. And in this place, some falsely simulate that Jacob worshipped the top of Joseph's scepter, because he honored his son and worshipped his power, when in Hebrew it is read very differently: And Israel worshipped, he said, at the head of the bed: which means, after his son had sworn to him confidently about the request he had made, he worshipped God against the head of his bed. Indeed, as a holy and devoted man dedicated to God, oppressed by old age, he had his bed positioned in such a way that he himself, in the posture of one lying down, was ready for prayer without any difficulty.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.48.1":["(Chapter 48, Verse 1) And it came to pass after these things, that one told Joseph, Behold, thy father is sick: and he took with him his two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim. And from this, what we said above is demonstrated, that Joseph had only two sons in that place, Ephraim and Manasseh. For if many years later, when Jacob his father died, he led only two sons for the blessing, surely at that time, when his sons were unable to beget children, being young and nursing infants, he could not have grandchildren from them at the entrance of his father and brothers.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.48.2":["(Version 2.) And Israel, strengthened, sat upon the bed. The reason why the Seventy Interpreters have rendered the same word differently, I do not know; but I boldly say this one thing, that the word itself, Meta, which they translated here as bed, in the place where we have said above that Jacob worshipped on it, they have rather called a rod than a bed.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.48.5":["(Verse 5.) And now behold, your two sons, who were born to you in the land of Egypt before I came to you in Egypt, are mine, Ephraim and Manasseh, just as Reuben and Simeon shall be mine. But any children born to you after them shall be yours. They shall be called by the names of their brothers. If anyone doubted that seventy persons, the children of Israel, had entered Egypt, and that at the time when Jacob entered Joseph had not nine but only two sons, this is confirmed in the present chapter. Indeed, Jacob himself speaks, saying that he had two sons, not nine. And what he says, 'Ephraim and Manasseh shall be mine, just as Reuben and Simeon,' that means, just as Reuben and Simeon were two tribes and were called by their own names, so Ephraim and Manasseh shall be two tribes, and they shall produce two peoples, and thus they shall inherit the land of promise, just like my sons. But the remaining sons, he says, whom you will bear after my death, showing that they were not yet born at that time, shall be yours.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.48.6":["(Verse 6.) They will be called by the names of their brothers in their inheritance. No, he said, they will not receive land separately, nor will they have their own possessive cords like the other tribes, but they will be mixed in with the tribes of Ephraim and Manasseh, like appendages of the people.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.48.22":["(Verse 22.) And I gave ((Or, do)) to you Sicima, the special one, over your brothers, which I received from the hands of the Amorites with my sword and arrow. Sicima, according to Greek and Latin custom, is declined. Otherwise, in Hebrew it is called Sichem, as John the Evangelist also testifies (IV, 5): although erroneously, it is read as Sichar, the error has prevailed: and now it is the city of Neapolis, the city of the Samaritans. Therefore, because Sichem () is translated into Hebrew as shoulder, he cleverly alluded to the name, saying: And I will give you one shoulder. For it is written specially, that is, in Hebrew. And what he says that he possessed it in a bow and sword: the bow here, and the sword, he calls righteousness, through which he deserved to be delivered from danger as a stranger and sojourner, by slaying Shechem and Hamor. For, as we read above, he feared lest the neighboring cities and castles should rise up against him for the destruction of the federal city (Gen. 34): and the Lord did not permit them to harm him. Certainly, you should understand it this way: I will give you Sichem, which I acquired with my own strength, that is, with money, which I earned with much labor and much sweat. And what he said, surpassing your brothers, shows that it was given to Joseph without any obligation. For indeed, Joseph is buried in the same place, and his tomb can still be seen there today.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.3":["(Chapter 49, verse 3) Reuben, my firstborn, you are my strength and the first of my sons, tough in bearing and tough in insolence and injury, like water that does not boil. You went up on your father's bed, then you defiled it when you went up. In Hebrew it is written: Reuben, my firstborn, you are my strength, and the first in power among my sons. Greater in carrying, and greater in strength. You are poured out like water, do not exceed. For you have ascended the bed of your father and defiled the couch in his ascent. The meaning here is: You are my firstborn, greater among the children, and you were supposed to receive, according to the order of your birth, the inheritance that was rightfully due to the firstborn, and to receive the priesthood and the kingdom. This is clearly demonstrated by the burden you carry and your mighty strength. However, because you have sinned, and like water that cannot be contained in a vessel, you have been poured out by the force of pleasure. Therefore, I command you not to sin further and to be among your brothers, enduring the punishment for the sin that you have committed, which is the loss of your status as the firstborn.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.5-6":["(Verses 5 and 6) Simeon and Levi, brothers, carried out the wickedness of their own invention. Let not my soul enter into their counsel, nor let my liver be involved in their assembly, because in their anger they killed men, and in their self-will they hamstrung oxen. By necessity, we are compelled to go further and revisit those things which are in disagreement with the Hebrew truth. For it is written there: Simeon and Levi are brothers, weapons of injustice are their tools. In their secret they conspired against me, so that my soul should not come in: and in their meeting they have cast down my glory, I shall never be cast down. For in their anger they slew a man, and in their self-will they undermined a wall. (Gen. XXXIV). However, it signifies that it was not according to their own counsel that Sichem and Emor killed the allied men, but rather against the law of peace and friendship they shed innocent blood, and with a kind of madness and cruelty, they tore down the walls of the city where they were hosted. Where it follows and says:\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.7":["(Verse 7) Cursed be their fury, for it is stubborn; and their anger, for it is harsh. I will divide them in Jacob and scatter them in Israel. For Levi did not receive his own inheritance, but had a few cities to dwell in among all the tribes. As for Simeon, it is written in the Book of Joshua (19) that he did not even receive a portion of land as his own, but received something from the tribe of Judah. And in the Chronicles (1, 4) it is more explicitly written that he multiplied and did not have a place of possession, so he went out into the wilderness. Certain people interpret the prophetically killed men, the Apostles, and the paralyzed bull by the Pharisees as Christ.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.8":["(Verse 8.) Judah, your brothers will praise you. Because Judah, confession or praise, is subsequently written: Judah, your brothers will confess to you: or, your brothers will praise you.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.9":["(Verse 9.) The sons of your father shall adore you. The lion's cub of Judah, my son, you have risen, bowing down like a lion and like a lion cub. Who will awaken him? Although there is a great mystery about Christ, it is prophesied according to the literal interpretation that kings from the lineage of David through Judah will be born and that all tribes will adore him. For it does not say, the sons of your mother, but the sons of your father. And what follows, you have risen from the lineage, my son, in Hebrew is, you have risen from captivity, my son, to show that he will lead captive peoples; and according to a more sacred interpretation, he has ascended on high and led captivity captive (Psalm 68). Or what I think is better, captivity signifies passion, ascent signifies resurrection.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.10":["But since we have once begun to expound tropologically as well: The Lord will visit, at the coming and passion of the Savior, that is, on the day of the victim of his Son, upon the high priests and priests of the Jewish people and upon the royal house. For up to that time the kings of Judah from the stock of David continued, according to the prophecy of Jacob: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up, and he will be the expectation of the nations. For after the Lord's victim the kingdom was taken from the Jews.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Of this king Jacob too speaks in the blessing of the patriarch Judah: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he will be the expectation of the nations. Therefore, after the prince failed from Judah, and the leader from his thighs, and Herod, a foreigner and a proselyte, took the rule, we understand that he has come for whom the kingdom was laid up, and he will be the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"GEN.49.11":["(Verse 11.) He ties his young donkey to the vine, and the colt of the donkey to the choice vine. In Hebrew it says, He ties his young donkey to the vine, and in Sorec, his donkey's colt to the choice vine: which colt of the donkey, that is, the Gentile people whom Jesus has taken over, that is, the Apostles' vineyard, who are from the Jews, he has joined together. And in Sorec, that is, the chosen vine, he has tied the donkey, over whom he has taken control, the Church gathered from the nations. And what he says, my son, he addresses to Judas himself, because Christ will do all of this. But it should also be known that where we read: Alligating to its vine its foal, can be read as 'its foal' in Hebrew, its city, with the same meaning demonstrated by other words in the Church, of which it is written elsewhere: A city that is set on a hill cannot be hidden (Matt. 5:15). And the streams of the river make the city of God glad (Psalm 46:5).\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.16-18":["(Verses 16 and following.) And he shall judge his people, as one of the tribes of Israel. Let Dan be a serpent by the way, an adder in the path, that biteth the horse heels, so that his rider shall fall backward: thy salvation, O Lord, I will wait for. Samson was a judge in Israel, from the tribe of Dan. This therefore he saith: Now seeing in the spirit Samson thy Nazarite nourish his hair, and triumph over his enemies being cut down; which in the likeness of a serpent and ruler obstructing the ways, he permitteth none to pass through the land of Israel: but also if anyone, presuming on his own strength, relying on the swiftness of a horse, shall wish to invade it like a spoiler, he shall not be able to escape. However, the whole thing is spoken metaphorically of a serpent and a knight. Therefore, seeing your mighty Nazarene, who himself died for the prostitute and, dying, killed our enemies, I thought, O God, that he was the Christ, your Son. But because he died and did not rise again, and was led captive to Israel, another Savior of the world must be awaited by me and my race, to whom the promise has been given, and he will be the expectation of the nations.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.19":["(Verse 19.) Gad the robber will rob him, and he himself will rob a plant. We are interpreted according to the Hebrew. But where we put the robber, it is written Gedud (), to allude to the name Gad, which can be more significantly expressed as εὔζωνος, that is, girded or ready. But the whole story is that after fourteen years, when Reuben and half of the tribe of Manasseh had returned to the sons whom he had sent across the Jordan to take possession, he found a great war against the neighboring peoples, and after defeating the enemy, he fought bravely. Read the book of Joshua (Joshua 13) and Chronicles (1, 5). I am aware that there are more mysteries in the blessings of the patriarchs, but they are not relevant to the current work.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.21":["(Verse 21.) The tribe of Naphtali, a spreading vine, producing beauty in the next generation. In Hebrew it is written: Naphtali is a fruitful land, producing words of beauty, signifying that hot springs are born within the tribe itself, or that the region around the Sea of Galilee, watered by the flow of the Jordan River, is fertile. However, the Hebrews interpret it as prophesying an irrigated land and the words of beauty referring to Tiberias, which was considered to have knowledge of the Law. Furthermore, when they placed the irrigated field and the seventy twigs, which is called a loose branch, in Hebrew it is read as Ajala Sluaa (), which can also be translated as a released stag, showing the abundance of temporary crops and the swiftness of fertile land. But it is better if we refer everything to the teaching of the Savior, especially what He taught there, as it is also written in the Gospel.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.22-24":["(Verse 22 and following) The son of Joseph grew up, the son of Joseph grown tall above the fountain of the daughter, walking with a composed stride on the wall. And they provoked him, and contended with him, and were angry against him, having arrows. He sat in the strength of his bow, and the chains of his hands were broken, from the hands of the mighty Jacob. From there will come the shepherd, the stone of Israel from the God of your father: and the rest. Because the Seventy Interpreters disagree in many places, we have expressed their interpretation as it is in Hebrew. And the meaning of the chapter is this: O Joseph, who is called thus because God has increased you for me, or because you will become greater among your brothers (for indeed Ephraim was the strongest of the tribe, as we read in the Books of Kings and Chronicles); O, I say, my son Joseph, who is so handsome that a crowd of Egyptian girls watches you from the walls, towers, and windows, your brothers envied you and provoked you to anger, wounded by arrows of envy and spears of jealousy. But you have set your bow and weapons of war in God, who is a mighty warrior; and your chains, with which your brothers bound you, have been loosed and broken by him, so that from your seed may be born Ephraim, strong and steadfast, like an unconquerable and unyielding stone, ruling over the ten tribes of Israel.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis"],"GEN.49.27":["(Verse 27.) Benjamin the ravenous wolf, in the morning he will still eat, and in the evening he will give food.\n\nHow clear is the prophecy about the Apostle Paul, it is evident to all that in his youth he persecuted the Church, but in his old age he preached the Gospel. However, it is written in Hebrew as follows: Benjamin, a ravenous wolf, in the morning he will devour the prey, and in the evening he will divide the spoils. The Hebrews interpret this as referring to the altar where sacrifices were offered and the blood of the victims was poured out at its base, which was located in the territory of the tribe of Benjamin. They say, therefore, that this signifies that the priests in the morning sacrifice the victims, and in the evening divide those things which have been conferred upon them by the people according to the Law, placing the bloody wolf, the voracious wolf, as an interpretation upon the altar, and the division of spoils upon the priests, who serve the altar and live off the altar.\n— Hebrew Questions on Genesis","Indeed, of the apostle Paul, who was of the tribe of Benjamin, it is said according to the Hebrew: Benjamin a ravening wolf: in the morning he shall eat, and at evening he shall divide the spoils (Gen 49:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then the wolf and the lamb fed together, Paul the persecutor and Ananias the disciple (Acts 9). He is the wolf of whom it is written: Benjamin, a ravening wolf, in the morning shall eat the prey, and at evening shall give food (Gen 49:27); or, as it is said in the Hebrew, shall divide the spoils. His teaching was the food of believers, and he triumphed over his vanquished adversaries in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Where we translated behind your back, Benjamin, the Seventy rendered Benjamin has been moved in mind, always fitting ἔκστασιν, that is, ecstasy of mind, to Benjamin, the churchman. Hence also in the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm it is said: There is Benjamin, a youth, in ecstasy of mind. And in the blessings of Jacob, under the person of Benjamin, of which tribe the apostle Paul was, we read it written: Benjamin is a ravening wolf; in the morning he will eat the prey, and at evening he will give food. For he who in the beginning persecuted the Church afterwards bestowed the nourishment of the Gospel on believers throughout the whole world. Hence also Saul, who was of the tribe of Benjamin, prophesied in ecstasy of mind all day until evening among the band of the prophets.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"EXO.1.22":["Who would not say that beautiful Nineveh, a soul beautiful by nature, beguiled by the luxury and pleasures of this age, has come to womanish delights, and, her manliness lost, has languished into a woman? For if the soul of the righteous man, coming to the perfect man and keeping the firmness of its creation, and cleaving to God, is made one spirit with him, why should not, on the contrary, the soul that loves the world become one with the world, and, reduced to womanly softness, lose its manly firmness? I think that for this reason in Exodus Pharaoh commands that every male born to the Hebrews be thrown into the river, and every female be kept alive. For the Egyptian king, who says elsewhere: The rivers are mine, and I made them, could command nothing other than this, that whatever is strong and manly in the Hebrews, and in those who pass through this age, be thrown into the waters and carried down by their streams into the sea. And on the contrary, whatever seemed womanly and soft and comely in this age, that should be kept alive, should grow up and beget. And consider at the same time that the Egyptian ruler cannot kill the men of the Hebrews, nor those who have already come out of infancy, but only those whose age is still tender, and their body soft, and their progress just beginning; he knows that the females cannot be reared unless the males have been killed. So he wishes to drown in the whirlpool of his river whatever is strong and manly in the Hebrews, so that what is womanly may grow up alone more freely.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"EXO.2.23":["When Ahaz, who is interpreted κατάσχεσις, that is, holding, or possession, whom we read was a most ungodly king, had died, this weight, or word, comes upon the Philistines. For while he lived and reigned among sinners, neither a weight against the foreigners nor the word of God could come to the Prophet. Indeed I remember having said this above too, when, Uzziah having died, Isaiah saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up. And in Exodus we read (Exod 2:23) that after the king of Egypt died, the children of Israel groaned from their works, and cried out, and their cry went up from their works, and the Lord heard their groaning. For unless what was said were mystical, they ought rather to have cried out while he was reigning, when they were slaving at mud and bricks.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EXO.3.6":["But concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken by God, who says to you: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead but of the living. To prove the truth of the resurrection he could have used other, much plainer examples, among which is this one: The dead shall be raised, and those who are in the tombs shall rise again, and in another place: Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall arise, some to life, others to reproach and everlasting confusion. It is asked, therefore, what the Lord meant by bringing forward this testimony, which seems ambiguous or not sufficiently pertinent to the truth of the resurrection: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and why, as though by bringing it forward he had proved what he wished, he at once added: He is not the God of the dead but of the living. The crowds standing around, too, recognizing the mystery of this matter, marveled at the teaching of his answer. We said above that the Sadducees, confessing neither angel nor spirit nor the resurrection of bodies, also preached the destruction of souls. They received only the five books of Moses, rejecting the oracles of the prophets. It would therefore have been foolish to bring forward testimonies from a source whose authority they did not follow. Moreover, to prove the eternity of souls he sets down an example from Moses: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, and at once adds: He is not the God of the dead but of the living, so that, once he had proved that souls endure after death (for it could not be that he was the God of those who did not subsist at all), the resurrection of the bodies too might consequently be introduced, the bodies which together with the souls did good or evil. The apostle Paul pursues this passage more fully in the last part of the first letter to the Corinthians.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"EXO.3.14":["But they devised malice against the Lord, building up most impious heresies, and returned to the state of the Gentiles, so as to be without the knowledge and the yoke of God; or they returned to nothing, not that they ceased to be, but because, in comparison with him who speaks to Moses: Go, say to the people of Israel: He who is has sent me, all who think against the Lord are said not to be. According to what we read in Esther, in the Seventy only. Do not hand over your scepter to those who are not: there is no doubt that it means idols. For if God is truth, whatever is contrary to truth is a lie, and is called nothing.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"EXO.4.13":["So Moses too, to whom the Lord had said: Come, I will send you to Pharaoh king of Egypt (Exod 3:10), and he said: I beseech you, Lord, I am not worthy; provide another whom you may send, or as is read in the Hebrew, Send whom you are going to send (Exod 4:13), answered not out of contempt but out of humility, because he who had been instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians had heard nothing about his lips being cleansed. And Isaiah, not by his own merit but by the grace of the Lord, by which he was cleansed, offers himself for the ministry.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EXO.4.22":["From this point the Prophet relates what the Lord has spoken: that the people of Israel, whom he had created as servants under the common law, he changed into sons, and said: Israel is my firstborn son. Then too the Lord in the Gospel promises the Apostles that if they do his will he will no longer call them servants but friends (John 15:15). But if Israel should grow proud on hearing that he is the firstborn, let him understand that he is called firstborn for this reason, that second sons are signified from among the nations. For he is not called only-begotten, which would exclude other brothers, but firstborn, to show that others too will follow; and yet, according to the mysteries of the Scriptures, it is not the firstborn who receive the inheritance but the second. Cain was the firstborn, but the gifts of Abel pleased God. Ishmael was the firstborn, but Isaac received the inheritance. Esau was the firstborn, but Jacob the supplanter snatched away the father's blessing. Reuben was the firstborn, but the blessing of the seed of Christ is nevertheless transferred to Judah. Thus they were first according to the order of calling, and were called the head; we, the second, who were called the tail, have been turned into the head and are called sons of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EXO.14.15":["It should be noted also that in the Scriptures a cry is to be understood not as the emitting of a loud voice but as the greatness of knowledge and of doctrines. For in Exodus too the Lord answered Moses: \"Why do you cry to me\" (Exod. 14:15)? although no voice of Moses at all had gone before. But Scripture called a cry his heart, pierced with compunction and groaning tearfully for the people.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"EXO.20.5":["For we ought not to take as fathers, whose iniquities and sins are now repaid to us, those from whose seed our bodies were born. Otherwise Moses himself, who had said that the sins of the fathers are to be repaid to the sons to the third and fourth generation of those who hate God (Exod 20), interprets his own sentence, saying: The sons shall not die for the sins of the fathers: but each shall die in his own sin (Deut 24:16). Ezekiel approves the same sentence, that that parable is by no means to be spoken: The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons are set on edge (Ezek 18:2), but that only his teeth are set on edge who has eaten, and that the sinning soul perishes, and does not pay for another's sins. From this we understand that those are to be taken as fathers, whether in the good sense or in the contrary, to whom each is added when he has departed from this life.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Now divine Scripture warns that what was said in Exodus, I am the Lord your God; a jealous God, who render the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation for those who hate me, and show mercy to thousands for those who love me and keep my precepts, and again, The Lord descended in a cloud and stood beside Moses, and Moses called on the name of the Lord, and the Lord passed before his face, and he called on him, saying: Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much mercy and true, and keeping righteousness and mercy to thousands, taking away iniquities and injustices and sins, and he will not cleanse the guilty, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and upon the sons of the sons to the third and fourth generation, ought to be taken thus, as a parable and a proverb, so that it sounds one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; which we also said above on the parable of the two eagles. Hence the Lord in Psalm 77 (78): I will open, he says, my mouth in parables, I will utter propositions from the beginning; and in the Gospel he sets forth the parable of the sowing, and of the darnel, and of the mustard seed, which, though it is the least of all seeds, rises up into a great tree, in such a way that he presents one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; and we, up to the present day, think that the two testimonies of Exodus which we set down above are not a parable, but set out a simple statement. And although we would not dare to say anything, nor would the earthen vessel speak against the potter, 'why have you made me thus or thus', yet we suffered a hidden stumbling block, because it seemed an injustice on God's part that one should sin and another pay for the sins. For if he renders the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, it seems unjust that one should sin and another be punished; but from what follows, for those who hate me, the stumbling block of the threat, or of the precept, is resolved: for they are not punished to the third and fourth generation because their fathers transgressed, since it is rather the fathers, who were the sinners, who ought to have been punished, but because they proved to be imitators of their fathers and hated God, by an inherited evil, the impiety growing from the root into the branches as well. At this place the heretics who do not receive the Old Testament are accustomed to say against the Creator: How good and righteous is the God of the Law and the Prophets, who, resting and silent at the sins of the fathers, repays those who have not sinned; nay rather, how great is the cruelty in him, that he should extend his wrath even to the third and fourth generation! To them we shall reply that even in this the clemency of God the Creator is shown: for it is not a mark of ferocity and severity to hold back wrath until the third and fourth generation, but a sign of mercy to defer the punishment of sin; for when he says, The Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much compassion, and adds, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and the sons of the sons, he indicates this: that he is of such great mercy that he does not punish at once but defers the sentence of punishment. But if the vengeance on sinners is deferred to the third and fourth generation, what more will he do with the righteous and the holy? There follows: And keeping righteousness and mercy to many thousands for those who keep his commandments and do his precepts. It is written in Proverbs: As a sour grape is harmful to the teeth and smoke to the eyes, so is iniquity to those who practice it; from which it is clear that it is not the teeth of others that ache and are set on edge, but the teeth of those who have eaten the sour grape. And this is the sense of this passage: just as, if anyone should wish to say, The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge, it is ridiculous and has no logical consequence, so it is unjust and perverse that the fathers should sin and the sons and grandsons be tormented. There are those who expound what is written in Exodus, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, in such a way as to refer the statement to the human soul, saying that the father in us is the light prick of the senses and the incitements of the vices; the son, if the thought has conceived the sin; the grandson, if you carry out in deed what you have thought and conceived; and the great-grandson, that is, the fourth generation, if you not only do what is evil and wicked but glory in your crimes, according to what is written: The impious man, when he has come into the depth of evils, despises. God therefore by no means punishes the first and second goads of thoughts, which the Greeks call προπαθείας [pre-passions, the first movements of the mind], without which no human being can exist, but only if someone has resolved to do what he has thought, or has been unwilling to correct by repentance the very things he has done; whence it is written: No human being is without sin, not even if his life be of a single day. But the years of his life are numbered; and in another place: Who will boast that he has a chaste heart?; and again: The stars too are not clean in his sight, and against his angels he has devised something perverse. But if that sublime nature is not free of sin, what is to be said of human beings who, surrounded by fragile flesh, must say with the Apostle: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death?, and that word, that when we have done all things we must say, We are unprofitable servants; we have done what we ought to have done, and: Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain. Unless the Lord guards the city, he who guards it has kept watch in vain? Now in proof of this point, that the first impulse of thoughts, or rather the slight prompting of the mind, is by no means punished by God, but only if you complete in deed what you have conceived in the mind, that passage of Genesis must be brought forward: Ham sinned in mocking his father's nakedness, and the sentence was received not by him who laughed but by his son Canaan: Cursed, he says, be Canaan; a servant he shall be to his brothers. For what righteousness is there in this, that the father should have sinned and the sentence be pronounced against the son? Likewise what the Apostle sets down in the opposite direction, that the woman is saved if her son remains in faith and holiness and chastity, would seem not to have the righteousness of a sentence, namely that if the sons and grandsons are good, the parents are saved; for how many parents are holy and have wicked sons, and, on the contrary, how many are sinners and beget holy sons? Therefore all that we said above is to be taken according to this sense: that the sins of parents and begetters are punished in the branches, not in the root. Let it suffice for the present to have said this about the proverb, or the parable: that the Law and the Prophets, that is, Exodus and Ezekiel, or rather God himself, who spoke both here and there, should by no means seem to disagree in their statements, or to correct here what he had said badly there. But if anyone can find either a better sense or another one, which removes the stumbling block of testimonies contrary to one another, it is rather his opinion that is to be accepted.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The Lord threatens in the law that he will requite the sins of the fathers upon the children to the third and fourth generation; not by an equality of judgment, so that some sin and others are punished, but by the greatness of his clemency, since he always awaits repentance; and what is committed in the first generation he does not rebuke and correct until the third and fourth generation has come. Something of this kind the apostle Paul also says to the Romans: Or do you despise the riches of his goodness and forbearance and patience, not knowing that the kindness of God leads you to repentance? But according to your hardness and impenitent heart you are storing up wrath for yourself on the day of wrath and of the revelation of the just judgment of God, who will render to each one according to his works. What he says, then, is this: I waited a long time for them to repent, and for that reason I was unwilling to punish them when they sinned, so that at some time they might turn and receive healing. But because they do the same things a third and a fourth time, I am compelled to change my sentence and to correct the offenders with blows.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"EXO.20.12":["For God said: Honor father and mother, and whoever curses father or mother, let him die the death. But you say: Whoever says to father or mother: Whatever gift is from me will profit you; and he has not honored his father or his mother. Honor in the Scriptures is understood not so much in greetings and the paying of courtesies as in alms and the offering of gifts. Honor, says the Apostle, widows who are truly widows, and here honor is understood as a gift; and in another place: Presbyters are to be honored with double honor, especially those who labor in the word and teaching of God. And by this commandment we are bidden not to close the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain, and that the laborer be worthy of his wages. The Lord, considering either the infirmities or the ages or the poverty of parents, had commanded that sons should honor their parents also by supplying them with the necessities of life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"EXO.21.6":["We must also inquire what difference there is between \"age\" and \"age of the age,\" or \"ages of ages,\" and where it is used for a short span of time, and where for eternity: for in Hebrew \"age,\" that is, OLAM (עלם), when it has the letter VAV placed in it, signifies eternity, but when it is written without VAV, the fiftieth year, which they call the Jubilee. For this reason too that Hebrew who, because of his wife and children, loving his master, is subjected to servitude with his ear pierced, is ordered to serve \"for ever\" (in sæculum) (Exod. 21), that is, up to the fiftieth year. And the Moabites and Ammonites (Deut. 23) do not enter the church of the Lord up to the fifteenth generation, and \"for ever\": because every hard condition was dissolved at the coming of the Jubilee.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"EXO.21.24":["You have heard that it was said: An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth; but I say to you not to resist evil. He who says: An eye for an eye, does not wish to take away the other eye too, but to preserve both. Our Lord, taking away requital, cuts off the beginnings of sins. And in the law there is retribution, in the Gospel grace; there the fault is corrected, here the beginnings of sins are taken away.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"EXO.31.13-14":["These things are said to those who were led out of Egypt into the desert, so that, freed from the vices of the Egyptians, they might more easily fulfill the precepts of God in the wilderness and do his judgments and keep the sabbaths, which were given as a sign between the one who gave them and those to whom they were given, as Scripture says: The Lord spoke to Moses, saying: Command the children of Israel and you shall say to them: See that you keep my sabbaths, for it is a sign between me and you throughout your generations, that you may know that I am the Lord who sanctifies you; and keep the sabbath, for it is holy to you: whoever violates it shall surely die; everyone who does work on it, that soul shall be put to death from the midst of its people. So the sabbath and circumcision were given as a sign of the true sabbath and the true circumcision: that we may know that we must rest from earthly works in the perfect and true sabbath, and that we should circumcise not the foreskin but the heart. Hence, working for six days, we rest on the seventh day, so that day and night we may do nothing else than recognize that all that we live is owed to the Lord, and, as the week comes round again, consecrate ourselves wholly to his name, so that through the sanctification of the day we may remember the Lord who sanctifies us.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"EXO.32.10":["Let us speak first according to the Hebrew: the prophets of Israel are like foxes, daily committing thefts of domestic fowl, who dwell, according to Aquila and the Seventy, 'in the deserts', according to Symmachus and Theodotion 'in broken walls' and 'ruins'; who cannot go up from the opposite side, nor set up a wall for the house of Israel — we read that Aaron ran to meet the fire that was devouring the people of Israel, and stood in the midst, and set up a wall for the safety of the people; to Jeremiah also it is said that he should not run to meet the Lord, nor stand against his wrath by perseverance in prayer: for just as a wall is set against an enemy, and one is accustomed to meet an adversary from the opposite side and to confront one who is coming on, so the sentence of God is broken by the prayers of the saints; whence also it is said to Moses: Let me go, and I will blot out this people: for in the one to whom it is said: Let me go, it is shown that he has the power of holding back —. These cannot stand in battle on the day of the Lord, so as to fight for the people with prayers and to resist the sentence of God with supplications.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Wherefore in indignation I said to Moses: I will not feed you; let me alone and I will destroy them. But when, in anger, he says, let me alone, he invites entreaty, and shows that he can be held back by him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"EXO.33.20":["The Hebrews say that Isaiah was put to death for two reasons: because he called them princes of Sodom and people of Gomorrah, and because, though the Lord said to Moses, You cannot see my face (Exod 33:20), he dared to say, I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up (below, Isa 6:1); not considering that the Seraphim covered the face and feet of God, or their own, for in the Hebrew it reads ambiguously, and that Isaiah writes that he saw only his middle parts. Man, then, cannot see the face of God. But the Angels even of the least in the Church always see the face of God (Matt 18:10). And, Now we see in a mirror and in a riddle, but then face to face (1 Cor 13:12): when we have advanced from men to Angels and can say with the Apostle: But we all, with unveiled face beholding the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord (2 Cor 3:18). And yet no creature sees the face of God according to the proper character of his nature; and he is then discerned by the mind when he is believed to be invisible.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EXO.34.6-7":["Now divine Scripture warns that what was said in Exodus, I am the Lord your God; a jealous God, who render the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation for those who hate me, and show mercy to thousands for those who love me and keep my precepts, and again, The Lord descended in a cloud and stood beside Moses, and Moses called on the name of the Lord, and the Lord passed before his face, and he called on him, saying: Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much mercy and true, and keeping righteousness and mercy to thousands, taking away iniquities and injustices and sins, and he will not cleanse the guilty, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and upon the sons of the sons to the third and fourth generation, ought to be taken thus, as a parable and a proverb, so that it sounds one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; which we also said above on the parable of the two eagles. Hence the Lord in Psalm 77 (78): I will open, he says, my mouth in parables, I will utter propositions from the beginning; and in the Gospel he sets forth the parable of the sowing, and of the darnel, and of the mustard seed, which, though it is the least of all seeds, rises up into a great tree, in such a way that he presents one thing in the words and holds another in the sense; and we, up to the present day, think that the two testimonies of Exodus which we set down above are not a parable, but set out a simple statement. And although we would not dare to say anything, nor would the earthen vessel speak against the potter, 'why have you made me thus or thus', yet we suffered a hidden stumbling block, because it seemed an injustice on God's part that one should sin and another pay for the sins. For if he renders the sins of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, it seems unjust that one should sin and another be punished; but from what follows, for those who hate me, the stumbling block of the threat, or of the precept, is resolved: for they are not punished to the third and fourth generation because their fathers transgressed, since it is rather the fathers, who were the sinners, who ought to have been punished, but because they proved to be imitators of their fathers and hated God, by an inherited evil, the impiety growing from the root into the branches as well. At this place the heretics who do not receive the Old Testament are accustomed to say against the Creator: How good and righteous is the God of the Law and the Prophets, who, resting and silent at the sins of the fathers, repays those who have not sinned; nay rather, how great is the cruelty in him, that he should extend his wrath even to the third and fourth generation! To them we shall reply that even in this the clemency of God the Creator is shown: for it is not a mark of ferocity and severity to hold back wrath until the third and fourth generation, but a sign of mercy to defer the punishment of sin; for when he says, The Lord God, compassionate and merciful, patient and of much compassion, and adds, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons and the sons of the sons, he indicates this: that he is of such great mercy that he does not punish at once but defers the sentence of punishment. But if the vengeance on sinners is deferred to the third and fourth generation, what more will he do with the righteous and the holy? There follows: And keeping righteousness and mercy to many thousands for those who keep his commandments and do his precepts. It is written in Proverbs: As a sour grape is harmful to the teeth and smoke to the eyes, so is iniquity to those who practice it; from which it is clear that it is not the teeth of others that ache and are set on edge, but the teeth of those who have eaten the sour grape. And this is the sense of this passage: just as, if anyone should wish to say, The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons have been set on edge, it is ridiculous and has no logical consequence, so it is unjust and perverse that the fathers should sin and the sons and grandsons be tormented. There are those who expound what is written in Exodus, rendering the iniquities of the fathers upon the sons to the third and fourth generation, in such a way as to refer the statement to the human soul, saying that the father in us is the light prick of the senses and the incitements of the vices; the son, if the thought has conceived the sin; the grandson, if you carry out in deed what you have thought and conceived; and the great-grandson, that is, the fourth generation, if you not only do what is evil and wicked but glory in your crimes, according to what is written: The impious man, when he has come into the depth of evils, despises. God therefore by no means punishes the first and second goads of thoughts, which the Greeks call προπαθείας [pre-passions, the first movements of the mind], without which no human being can exist, but only if someone has resolved to do what he has thought, or has been unwilling to correct by repentance the very things he has done; whence it is written: No human being is without sin, not even if his life be of a single day. But the years of his life are numbered; and in another place: Who will boast that he has a chaste heart?; and again: The stars too are not clean in his sight, and against his angels he has devised something perverse. But if that sublime nature is not free of sin, what is to be said of human beings who, surrounded by fragile flesh, must say with the Apostle: Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from the body of this death?, and that word, that when we have done all things we must say, We are unprofitable servants; we have done what we ought to have done, and: Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain. Unless the Lord guards the city, he who guards it has kept watch in vain? Now in proof of this point, that the first impulse of thoughts, or rather the slight prompting of the mind, is by no means punished by God, but only if you complete in deed what you have conceived in the mind, that passage of Genesis must be brought forward: Ham sinned in mocking his father's nakedness, and the sentence was received not by him who laughed but by his son Canaan: Cursed, he says, be Canaan; a servant he shall be to his brothers. For what righteousness is there in this, that the father should have sinned and the sentence be pronounced against the son? Likewise what the Apostle sets down in the opposite direction, that the woman is saved if her son remains in faith and holiness and chastity, would seem not to have the righteousness of a sentence, namely that if the sons and grandsons are good, the parents are saved; for how many parents are holy and have wicked sons, and, on the contrary, how many are sinners and beget holy sons? Therefore all that we said above is to be taken according to this sense: that the sins of parents and begetters are punished in the branches, not in the root. Let it suffice for the present to have said this about the proverb, or the parable: that the Law and the Prophets, that is, Exodus and Ezekiel, or rather God himself, who spoke both here and there, should by no means seem to disagree in their statements, or to correct here what he had said badly there. But if anyone can find either a better sense or another one, which removes the stumbling block of testimonies contrary to one another, it is rather his opinion that is to be accepted.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"LEV.13.24-25":["We read in Leviticus, if indeed we read with open eyes, and the veil that is placed upon the law does not shut out the gaze of the inner eye, that leprosy is wont to arise in the weal and scar of a burn, and to change the color of the hair, and that a new foulness is added to the former disfigurement of the scar. This is so for this reason: that no one, secure about repentance because after sin he can say: I will endure the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, should sin and need cautery, and, once healed, be wounded again.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"LEV.16.21":["What follows, To be ready, is to be read in two ways: either so that \"to be ready for every good work\" is understood; or else, joining what he says, to obey for every good work, with what precedes and ending it there, to be ready is, as it were, another precept of its own and a special one, in keeping with what is written in Leviticus: \"The goat that is sent away, bearing the curses of the people, is to be handed over into the hands of a ready man\" (Lev. 16:21). If, then, anyone is ready to hold the ἀποπομπαῖον (the scapegoat), and lead it out into the desert, and there destroy it, and, so far as in him lies, banish it by the lot of the curse, he, when he has obeyed, will also be ready for every good work.\n— Commentary on Titus","And that which is said in a sacred manner in Leviticus, where in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, Aaron offers for the people the scapegoat, and that a living one, and, laying his hands upon its head, calls down upon it all the sins of the people of Israel, and hands it over into the hands of a prepared man, and sends it into the desert, this we understand of ourselves, and, preparing ourselves at the command of the true priest, we remove evil from the midst of the Church.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"LEV.23.40":["He who dwells in tabernacles of this kind, and hastens to go from the tabernacles to the courts, and from the courts to the house, and from the house to the temple of the Lord, ought to celebrate the solemnity of tabernacles with the most beautiful wood of wisdom, of which it is said in Proverbs: It is a tree of life to all who draw near to it, and to those who lean upon it, as upon the Lord of firmness. And with the branches of palms, in which the sign of victory and the reward of virtue are contained; and with the boughs of the thickest tree, which the Jews understand as the myrtle, because of the mortification of vices and lusts. Hence myrrh too is offered as a gift to the Lord and Savior by the magi. And with the branches, he says, of the willow and of the poplar, which some call one tree; and the very name of the wood, which in Greek is called ἀγνὸς, indicates chastity. Physicians, and those who have written on the natures of trees and herbs, say that if anyone drinks the flower of the willow, or of the poplar, mixed with water, all heat in him grows cold, and the vein of lust is dried up, and he can no longer beget children.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"NUM.12.3":["Through this Moses, the servant of God, deserved to receive the testimony of Scripture, which says: \"Moses was gentle, beyond all men upon the earth\" (Num. 12:3). Upon the earth, he says. For he could not be beyond those who saw God face to face, because we are often compelled to do many things through the weakness of the flesh.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"NUM.15.38":["For they broaden their phylacteries and enlarge their fringes; they love also the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues and greetings in the marketplace and to be called rabbi by men. Woe to us wretches, to whom the vices of the Pharisees have passed! The Lord, when he had given the commandments of the law through Moses, added at the end: You shall bind them on your hand, and they shall be unmoved before your eyes; and the sense is: Let my precepts be in your hand, that they may be fulfilled in deed; let them be before your eyes, that you may meditate on them day and night. The Pharisees, interpreting this badly, used to write the decalogue of Moses, that is, the ten words of the law, on little strips of parchment, folding them up and binding them on the forehead and making as it were a crown for the head, so that they might always move before their eyes, as the Indians and Babylonians do to this day; and whoever has this is judged among the peoples to be, as it were, religious. Moses had also commanded another thing: that they should make violet-blue fringes on the four corners of their cloaks to distinguish the people of Israel, so that, as circumcision in their bodies gave a sign of the Jewish nation, so their clothing might have some distinction. The superstitious teachers, courting popular favor and pursuing gain from silly women, used to make large fringes and bind very sharp thorns in them, so that, namely, as they walked and sat they might now and then be pricked and, as by this reminder, be drawn back to the duties of God and the ministries of his service. Since, therefore, the Lord had said: They do all their works in order to be seen by men, what he had charged in general he now divides into parts. Those little slips of the decalogue they called phylacteries, because whoever had them would have them as it were for his own guarding and protection, the Pharisees not understanding that these things are to be carried in the heart, not on the body; otherwise bookcases and chests too have books, and do not have the knowledge of God. This, among us, superstitious little women do to this day with little Gospels and with the wood of the cross and things of this kind, women who have indeed a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge, straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel. Of this kind was the fringe, small and short, prescribed by the law, which that woman also who suffered a flow of blood touched on the Lord's cloak; but she was not pricked by the superstitious thorns of the Pharisees, but rather was healed at its touch.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"NUM.23.10":["But what is said by Balaam, Let my soul die the death of the righteous, has this sense: that he desires to die to the world and to sin, and to live with the souls of the righteous, whose life is Christ, and who can sing, I will please the Lord in the land of the living; he is not indeed the God of the dead, but of the living; and if Balaam, translated into our language, means 'a vain people', which is most true, it is clear that the formerly vain people of the nations desired to have fellowship with the souls of the righteous Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, who are called εὐθεῖς, that is, 'upright' and 'righteous'; whence also the book of Genesis received its name from their designation.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"NUM.24.7":["And he makes an apostrophe to Gog himself: 'Are you not he of whom I spoke in ancient days, by the hands of my servants the prophets of Israel?': that is, of Moses — who says in the book of Numbers, at least according to the Seventy: A man shall come forth from his seed and shall rule over very many nations, and Gog his kingdom shall be lifted up, and his kingdom shall increase, but according to the Hebrew I found it written thus: His king shall be taken away because of Agag, and his kingdom shall be removed, because the kingdom of Israel was destroyed in Saul on account of Agag king of Amalek; and in another place: He will cleanse the land of his people —; Joel too describes the peoples to be gathered in the valley of 'Iosaphat', that is, 'of the judgment of God'; and Isaiah, and all the prophets, in whose hands and good works the word of the Lord comes about.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"NUM.24.8":["That what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Out of Egypt I have called my son. Let those who deny the truth of the Hebrew volumes answer where this is read in the LXX translators. And when they have not found it, we shall tell them that it is written in the prophet Hosea, as the copies too can prove which we have lately published. But we can also relieve this passage in another way, for the sake of the contentious, whose custom the apostle Paul denies that he or the Church of Christ has; and we bring forward a testimony from Numbers, where Balaam says: God has called him out of Egypt, his glory is like that of the unicorn.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"NUM.34.3-5":["For which in the book of Numbers it is written thus: It will begin from the wilderness of Sin, which is beside Edom, and will have as boundaries toward the east the sea. And they will go around the southern quarter by the Ascent of the Scorpion, so that they pass Senna and come into the south as far as Kadesh-barnea, whence the borders will go out to a village named Addar, and will extend as far as Asemona. And the boundary will go around from Asemona as far as the torrent of Egypt, and will be ended by the shore of the Great Sea. Which briefly signifies this — lest on the occasion of Ezekiel we seem to pass over to a very broad exposition of Numbers —: that the boundary goes around from the wilderness of Sin, which is beside Edom, and from the Red Sea, and, through the Ascent of the Scorpion and through Senna and Kadesh-barnea and the court of Addar and Asemona, reaches as far as the torrent of Egypt, which flows into the sea beside the city of Rhinocorura; but this boundary of the southern quarter, that is, the meridional, begins from Tamar — which is a city in the wilderness, which Solomon too built with wondrous works, and today it is named Palmyra, and in the Hebrew tongue it is called 'thamar', which in our language means 'palm' — up to the waters of contradiction of Kadesh, which beyond doubt is in the desert, and the torrent entering the Great Sea, this sea which stretches along the shores of Egypt and Palestine, and this is the southern quarter toward the south.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"NUM.34.7-9":["In the book of Numbers, then, in which the whole land of promise is divided by four quarters in brief speech, this is the description from the north, that is, from the northern quarter: Moreover, on the northern side the boundaries will begin from the Great Sea, reaching as far as the Highest Mountain, from which you will come into Hamath, up to the borders of Sedada. And the borders will go as far as Zephrona and 'the village of Enan' (or 'Aser-Enan'). And the Hebrews say that the northern quarter begins from the Great Sea, which stretches along the shores of Palestine, of Phoenicia, and of the Syria which is called Coele, and of Cilicia, and extends through Egypt to Libya. But as to what he says: Reaching — the boundaries — as far as the Highest Mountain, the same Hebrews assert that it signifies either Mount Amanus or the Taurus — which seems truer to us —. From which, he says, you will come into 'Emath', which is now named 'Epiphania', its name having been changed by Antiochus, the cruelest of tyrants, for he had the surname Ἐπιφανής [Epiphanes, the Manifest]. Up to the borders, he says, of Sedada, which place is also set down in the present reading of Ezekiel. And the borders will go as far as Zephrona, which city they today call Zephyrium, a town of Cilicia. And what follows: And 'the village of Enan' (for which in the Hebrew is written 'Aser-Enon' or '-Enan'), the present passage also contains.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"NUM.34.10-12":["For which in the book of Numbers I have found it written thus: Thence they shall measure the boundaries toward the eastern quarter from 'the village of Enan' (that is, 'Aser-Enon') as far as Sephama, and from Sephama the boundaries will go down to Rebla, opposite the spring; thence they will come toward the east to the sea of Chinnereth, and they will extend as far as the Jordan, and at the last they will be closed by the sea. Above he had said: And the boundary shall be from the sea up to Eser-Enon, the boundary of Damascus: from the end of the northern quarter, then, that is, from the court of Enan, the boundaries extend according to the book of Numbers as far as Sephama, which the Hebrews name Apamea, and from Apamea the boundaries will go down to Rebla, which is now called Antioch of Syria; and so that you may know that this Rebla signifies the city which is now the most renowned in Coele-Syria, there follows: Opposite the spring, which it is evident signifies Daphne, from which spring the aforesaid city enjoys most abundant waters — this place was planted by the military hand of Gnaeus Pompey, who first subjected Judea to Roman rule, and from him — following the fable of the poets — it received the name of Daphne, on account of the laurels and cypresses, trees which are very frequent in that very place too —. Thence, he says, the boundaries will come toward the eastern quarter to the sea of Chinnereth: as far as the lake of Tiberias; and it is called a sea although it has sweet waters, according to the idiom of the scriptures, which call gatherings of waters seas. And the boundaries, he says, will extend as far as the Jordan, and at the last will be closed by the sea: either by the Dead Sea or, as others think, by the tongue of the Red Sea, on whose shore Aila is situated, where a legion and garrison of the Romans is now stationed. Let this have been said according to the book of Numbers.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"DEU.5.32":["He who walks in the one royal way does not labor, the way about which God commanded through Moses: You shall walk in the royal way, and you shall not turn aside to the right or to the left (Deut 5:32). There is one way of truth, which says in the Gospel: I am the way, the truth and the life (John 14:6); and many ways of falsehood, in which Jerusalem is now accused of having walked. God, knowing the difference between these ways, speaks above to those who stray: Your ways are not as my ways (Isa 55:8); and they, confessing to the Lord, say: You have made our ways turn aside from your ways (Ps 43 [44]:19). Having come to know, then, the one royal way, let us see which are the ways to the right and to the left, by which we are forbidden to go. The royal way is the tempered one, having in itself neither more nor less. For example, the straight and royal way is prudence. We turn aside to the right if we are wiser than we ought to be wise, and love cunning instead of prudence; for the serpent was more prudent than all the beasts in paradise (Gen 3), and the sons of darkness are more prudent than the sons of light (Luke 16). And we turn off to the left when we are foolish and are less wise than is needful; of whom it is said: The fool has said in his heart, There is no God (Ps 13 [14]:1). Piety too and true religion is a royal way. He turns aside to the right who is superstitious, and deserves to hear: Be not overly righteous (Eccl 7:17); to the left, he who neglects the worship of God and is numbered among the kids and the he-goats. Liberality too and stewardship is a very great virtue, from which he turns aside to the right who is stingy and does not give what is needful, not only to others but not even to himself; to the left, he who devours his substance with harlots and says with Israel: Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die (Isa 22:13). Fortitude also and constancy is a royal way, from which he turns aside to the right who is rash and stubborn, to the left he who is fearful and timid. Hence the holy man, desiring to walk by the straight way, prays: Lead me, Lord, in the straight way (Ps 138 [139]:24). And in another place: Make known to me, Lord, the way in which I should walk, for to you I have lifted up my eyes (Ps 142 [143]:8). Of a way of this kind Scripture also speaks elsewhere: Seek the good way and walk in it, and you shall find purification for your souls (Jer 6:16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"DEU.6.8":["For they broaden their phylacteries and enlarge their fringes; they love also the first places at table at dinners and the first chairs in the synagogues and greetings in the marketplace and to be called rabbi by men. Woe to us wretches, to whom the vices of the Pharisees have passed! The Lord, when he had given the commandments of the law through Moses, added at the end: You shall bind them on your hand, and they shall be unmoved before your eyes; and the sense is: Let my precepts be in your hand, that they may be fulfilled in deed; let them be before your eyes, that you may meditate on them day and night. The Pharisees, interpreting this badly, used to write the decalogue of Moses, that is, the ten words of the law, on little strips of parchment, folding them up and binding them on the forehead and making as it were a crown for the head, so that they might always move before their eyes, as the Indians and Babylonians do to this day; and whoever has this is judged among the peoples to be, as it were, religious. Moses had also commanded another thing: that they should make violet-blue fringes on the four corners of their cloaks to distinguish the people of Israel, so that, as circumcision in their bodies gave a sign of the Jewish nation, so their clothing might have some distinction. The superstitious teachers, courting popular favor and pursuing gain from silly women, used to make large fringes and bind very sharp thorns in them, so that, namely, as they walked and sat they might now and then be pricked and, as by this reminder, be drawn back to the duties of God and the ministries of his service. Since, therefore, the Lord had said: They do all their works in order to be seen by men, what he had charged in general he now divides into parts. Those little slips of the decalogue they called phylacteries, because whoever had them would have them as it were for his own guarding and protection, the Pharisees not understanding that these things are to be carried in the heart, not on the body; otherwise bookcases and chests too have books, and do not have the knowledge of God. This, among us, superstitious little women do to this day with little Gospels and with the wood of the cross and things of this kind, women who have indeed a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge, straining out the gnat and swallowing the camel. Of this kind was the fringe, small and short, prescribed by the law, which that woman also who suffered a flow of blood touched on the Lord's cloak; but she was not pricked by the superstitious thorns of the Pharisees, but rather was healed at its touch.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"DEU.7.6":["Often considering with myself what the word περιούσιον might mean, and asking the wise men of this age whether they had perhaps read it anywhere, I could never find anyone to explain to me what it signified. Therefore I was compelled to have recourse to the old instrument, from which I supposed the Apostle too had taken what he had said. For a Hebrew of the Hebrews, and as to the law a Pharisee, he was surely setting down in his Epistle what he knew he had read in the Old Testament. So in Deuteronomy I found: \"For you are a holy people to the Lord your God, and it has pleased the Lord your God in you that you should be to him a people περιούσιον out of all the peoples that are upon the face of the earth\" (Deut. 7:6). And in the hundred and thirty-fourth Psalm, where we have, \"Sing psalms to his name, for it is sweet: for the Lord has chosen Jacob for himself, Israel for a possession (in possessione) for himself\" (Ps. 134:3–4): for what is \"for a possession\" (in possessione), in the Greek it is written εἰς περιουσιασμὸν, which Aquila and the Fifth Edition indeed expressed as εἰς περιούσιον. But the Seventy and Theodotion, translating περιουσιασμὸν, made a change of syllable, not of sense. Symmachus, then, for what is περιούσιον in the Greek, SGOLLA (סגלה) in the Hebrew, expressed ἐξαίρετον, that is, \"choice\" (egregium), or \"special\" (præcipuum); for which word, using the Latin language in another volume, he rendered \"peculiar\" (peculiarem).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"DEU.8.3":["Who answering said: It is written: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. The testimony is taken from Deuteronomy. And the Lord answered thus because his purpose was to overcome the devil by humility, not by power. At the same time it must be noted that, had the Lord not begun to fast, the devil would have had no occasion to tempt, according to that saying: Son, as you come to the service of God, prepare your soul for temptation. But the Savior's answer itself also shows that it was a man who was tempted: Not by bread alone does man live, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. So if anyone does not feed on the word of God, he does not live.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"DEU.19.15":["And according to what he commands in another place: Be my witnesses, and I am a witness, says the Lord God, and the servant whom I have chosen, the Father with the Son and the Holy Spirit is a witness for us, so that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may stand. And it seems to me that the sentence stands rather in this way, and that the truth is confirmed under these three witnesses, rather than through the letter. For there were two witnesses against Susanna, and against the Lord Savior himself, and yet the word did not stand in their mouth. Against Naboth, too, almost the whole city bore witness, and the agreement of most wicked witnesses had not the firmness of truth but the conspiracy of a crime.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"DEU.21.10-13":["We have understood who the virginal bridegroom is; but because this bridegroom, or husband, not only received a virgin as his bride, but also, in Hosea, took a harlot as his wife, for this reason it is written in Deuteronomy: If you go out against your enemies, and the Lord your God delivers them into your hand, and you lead them captive, and see among the number of the captives a beautiful woman, and love her, and wish to have her as your wife; you shall bring her into your house, and she shall shave her hair and pare her nails, and put off the garment in which she was taken, and sitting in your house she shall weep for her father and her mother for one month, and afterward you shall go in to her and sleep with her, and she shall be your wife. The husband of such a woman, that is, her lord, is not a virginal one; rather, he takes as his wife a harlot from the filth of the Gentiles. This we can say also of every soul of the believers. If it has believed in the Lord from a tender age, it has the Lord as a virginal bridegroom. But if it has come to the truth of the faith from the Jews or the Gentiles, and from the most filthy dregs of the heretics, and has passed from darkness to light, it will indeed have a bridegroom, but not a virgin one; and of such people it is said: In Egypt your breasts were crushed, and there you were deflowered.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"DEU.21.22-23":["Because it is written: Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree: that the blessing of Abraham might come among the Gentiles in Christ Jesus, that we might receive the promise of the spirit through faith.\n\nBefore we discuss the sense and the words of the Apostle, it seems right to go over briefly the testimony of Deuteronomy, from which the Apostle also took these words (Deut. 21:22, 23), and to compare it with the other editions. The Seventy translators, then, rendered this passage thus: \"But if there be in anyone a sin and a judgment of death, and he has died, and you hang him on a tree, his body shall not sleep upon the tree, but burying you shall bury him on that day; because cursed by God is everyone who hangs on a tree: and you shall not defile your land, which the Lord your God will give you for an inheritance\". Aquila: \"And when there is in a man a sin unto a judgment of death, and he has been slain, and you hang him upon a tree, his carcass shall not remain upon the tree, but burying you shall bury him on that day, because he who is hanged is the curse of God: and you shall not defile your ground, which the Lord your God will give you as an inheritance\". Symmachus: \"But if there be in a man a sin bringing a judgment of death, and he has been slain, and you hang him upon a tree: his corpse shall not stay the night upon the tree, but with burial you shall bury him on that same day, because he was hanged on account of blasphemy of God: and you shall not defile your land, which the Lord your God will give you for an inheritance\". Theodotion: \"And because there will be in a man a sin unto a judgment of death, and he will die, and you will hang him on a tree, his carcass shall not sleep upon the tree, because with burial you shall bury him on that same day, because the one hanged is the curse of God: and you shall not defile your adama, which the Lord your God shall have given you as an inheritance\". Now ADAMA (אדמה) is the word in the Hebrew language for \"earth\" or \"ground\". But in the place where Aquila and Theodotion translated alike, saying: \"because the one hanged is the curse of God\", the Hebrew has it thus: CHI CALALATH ELOIM THALUI. These words Ebion, that heresiarch, half-Christian and half-Jew, interpreted thus: ὅτι ὕβρις Θεοῦ ὁ κρεμάμενος, that is, \"because the one hanged is an insult to God\". I remember that in the Dispute of Jason and Papiscus, which is written in the Greek language, I found it thus: λοιδορία Θεοῦ ὁ κρεμάμενος, that is, \"a curse of God is he who is hung up\". The Hebrew who instructed me in some measure in the Scriptures used to tell me that it can also be read thus: \"because God was hanged shamefully\". We have gathered these things together for this reason: because it is a most notorious question, and the Jews are accustomed to throw it at us as a disgrace that our Savior and Lord was under the curse of God. First, then, we must observe that not whoever has hung on a tree is cursed before God, but he who has sinned, and on account of a crime has been sentenced to death and raised up on a cross: he is cursed not because he was crucified, but because he fell into such guilt that he deserved to be crucified. Next we must set against them this point: that further on the cause of the gallows is set out more fully, Scripture relating that he was crucified on account of a curse and blasphemy of God. Symmachus translated this more plainly, saying: \"because he was hanged on account of blasphemy of God\". Lastly, let us ask them whether, if Ananias and Azarias and Misael, refusing to worship the idol of Nebuchadnezzar, had been hanged on a tree (Dan. 3), and Eleazar too, a man of ninety under Antiochus king of Syria, and the glorious mother with her seven sons (2 Macc. 7), they would have reckoned them cursed, or most worthy of every blessing? Surely, if Haman had not himself mounted, as he deserved, the cross that he had prepared for Mordecai (Esther 7), I think Mordecai would have ascended it not as one cursed, but as a holy man. By these and other similar examples it is proved that he is cursed who has committed a crime worthy of the gallows, not he who has been crucified through the iniquity of judges and the power of enemies, or the clamor of the mob, or envy of his virtues, or the anger of a king. Naboth too was once condemned to death by the whole city of Jezreel at the letters of Jezebel (1 Kings 21); but his blood, as a type of Christ, is avenged many ages later, the Lord saying to Hosea: \"Call his name Jezreel, for yet a little while, and I will avenge the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu.\" (Hos. 1:4). So much against the Jews. But to bring the discussion back to ourselves, I cannot know why the Apostle, in what is written: \"Cursed by God is everyone who hangs on a tree\" (Deut. 21:23), has either taken something away or added something. For if he was once following the authority of the Seventy translators, he ought, as it was published by them, to have added the name of God as well. But if, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, he thought that what he had read in his own language was most true, he ought not to have taken up either \"everyone\" or \"on a tree\", which are not found in the Hebrew. From this it seems to me either that the ancient books of the Hebrews had it otherwise than they have it now; or that the Apostle (as I have already said before) set down the sense of the Scriptures, not the words; or, what is rather to be supposed, that after the passion of Christ the name of God was added by someone, both in the Hebrew codices and in ours, so as to brand with infamy us who believe in a Christ cursed by God. With a bold foot, therefore, I advance to this contest, to appeal to the books: that in no place is it written that anyone is cursed by God, and wherever a curse is set down, the name of God is never added. \"Cursed are you by all the beasts\" (Gen. 3:14), is said to the serpent. And to Adam: \"Cursed is the ground in your works\" (ibid., 17). And to Cain: \"Cursed are you upon the earth\" (Gen. 4:11). And elsewhere: \"Cursed be Canaan the boy, he shall be a servant to his brothers\" (Gen. 9:25). And also in another place: \"Cursed be their fury, because it is bold, and their anger, because it is harsh\" (Gen. 49:7). It would be long if I were to list all the curses that are written in Leviticus, and in Deuteronomy, and in Joshua son of Nun; and yet in none of them is the name of God added: so much so that even Satan himself, when he was promising concerning Job that, if he were pressed by great distresses, he would blaspheme, expressed this by the better word, saying: \"If he does not bless you to your face\" (Job 1:11). And in the books of Kingdoms Naboth is reported to have been stoned for this reason: \"because he blessed God and the king\" (1 Kings 21).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"DEU.22.10":["Do we ask where we have read of the ox and the ass together? In Deuteronomy it is written: You shall not plough with a calf and an ass together (Deut 22:10). And in this same Isaiah: Blessed is he who sows upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread (Isa 32:20). Ebion ploughs with the ox and the ass together, worthy, for the lowliness of his understanding, of the poverty of his name; for he so receives the Gospel as not to abandon the ceremonies of the Jewish superstitions, which went before in shadow and image. But blessed is he who sows in the words of the Scriptures, of the old as well as of the new Instrument, and treads the waters of the letter that kills, so as to reap the fruit of the life-giving spirit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"DEU.23.3":["We must also inquire what difference there is between \"age\" and \"age of the age,\" or \"ages of ages,\" and where it is used for a short span of time, and where for eternity: for in Hebrew \"age,\" that is, OLAM (עלם), when it has the letter VAV placed in it, signifies eternity, but when it is written without VAV, the fiftieth year, which they call the Jubilee. For this reason too that Hebrew who, because of his wife and children, loving his master, is subjected to servitude with his ear pierced, is ordered to serve \"for ever\" (in sæculum) (Exod. 21), that is, up to the fiftieth year. And the Moabites and Ammonites (Deut. 23) do not enter the church of the Lord up to the fifteenth generation, and \"for ever\": because every hard condition was dissolved at the coming of the Jubilee.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"DEU.24.1":["It was said: Whoever puts away his wife, let him give her a bill of divorce; but I say to you that everyone who puts away his wife, and the rest. In a later part the Savior explains this passage more fully: that Moses ordered a bill of divorce to be given on account of the hardness of heart of the husbands, not granting separation but taking away murder. For it is much better that discord, however grievous, should come about than that blood should be shed through hatred.\n— Commentary on Matthew","He says to them: Because Moses, in view of the hardness of your heart, permitted you to put away your wives; but from the beginning it was not so. What he says is of this kind: Can God be contrary to himself, so as to have commanded one thing before and then break his own sentence by a new command? That is not to be thought; but Moses, when he saw that, because of the desire for second wives who were richer or younger or more beautiful, the first wives were being killed or leading a wretched life, preferred to allow discord rather than that hatreds and murders should go on. And at the same time consider that he did not say: God permitted you because of the hardness of your heart, but Moses, so that, according to the Apostle, it is the counsel of a man, not the command of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"DEU.27.15":["We read in Deuteronomy: Cursed is everyone who makes a graven and a molten thing, the work of the hands of the craftsman, and sets it in a hidden place. The graven and the molten thing, I think, are perverse doctrines, which are adored by those by whom they were made. See how Arius carved for himself the idol of a creature, and adored what he carved. Behold how Eunomius cast a false image, and bows his neck to his own casting. And Scripture says pointedly: And he will set it, he says, in a hidden place. For they too have their orgies, and to certain disciples, as if to the perfect, they hand on hidden mysteries, which, if they come forth to the light, are at once convicted of being fictions. Their carving and their casting, therefore, will profit them nothing. Carving, which refers to stones, is understood of those doctrines which display their folly at first sight. Casting is found where there seems to be some reasoning of secular wisdom, and, as if with a kind of gold, so with the disciplines of the philosophers and the splendor of eloquence, the idol has been cast.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"DEU.27.26":["For whoever are of the works of the Law are under the curse. For it is written: Cursed is everyone who does not continue in all the things that are written in the book of the Law, that he may do them.\n\nI have this custom: whenever something is taken by the apostles from the Old Instrument, I go back to the original books and examine carefully how it is written in its own place. And so I found this very passage set down in Deuteronomy by the Seventy translators thus: \"Cursed is every man who does not continue in all the words of this Law, that he may do them: and all the people shall say, So be it\" (Deut. 27:26). In Aquila, however, thus: \"Cursed is he who does not establish the words of this Law, that he may do them: and all the people shall say, Truly\". Symmachus: \"Cursed is he who does not make firm the words of that Law, that he may do them, and all the people shall say, Amen\". Theodotion, moreover, translated it thus: \"Cursed is he who does not raise up the words of this Law, to do them, and all the people shall say, Amen\". From this we understand that the Apostle, as in other places, set down the sense of the testimony rather than the words; and we hold it uncertain whether the Seventy Translators added \"every man\" (omnis homo) and \"in all\" (in omnibus), or whether it was so in the old Hebrew and was afterwards deleted by the Jews. Now what spurs me to this suspicion is this: the word \"every\" (omnis) and \"in all\" (in omnibus), as though necessary to his sense for proving that whoever are of the works of the Law are under the curse, the Apostle, a man of Hebrew learning and most learned in the Law, would never have brought forward unless it were found in the Hebrew volumes. For this reason, reading through the Hebrew volumes of the Samaritans, I found that CHOL, which means \"every\" or \"all\", is written there and agrees with the Seventy translators. In vain, therefore, did the Jews remove it, so as not to seem to be under the curse if they could not fulfill all the things that are written, since the more ancient letters of another nation too testify that it was set down.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"DEU.32.11":["And Deuteronomy, in which something of the kind resounds: As the eagle protects her nest, and has longed for her young: spreading her wings, she has taken them up and carried them on her shoulders (Deut 32:11). Those who have written on the natures of living creatures say that in all wild beasts, beasts of burden, cattle and birds there is an inborn affection toward their offspring and young, but that the love of eagles is the greatest, which place their nests in high and inaccessible places, lest a snake harm the brood. And that an amethyst stone is found among its young, by which all poisons are overcome. If this is true, the affection of God toward his creatures is rightly compared to eagles, who protects his children with every guard, lest the dragon and ancient snake, the devil and Satan, creep up on the new brood, so that at the name of the stone which is laid in the foundations of Zion all the snares of the adversaries may be broken.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"DEU.32.22":["Of this perpetual fire we read in the song of Moses too: A fire is kindled from my fury, and it shall burn down to hell below. It shall devour the earth and its produce: it shall burn the foundations of the mountains, and my arrows shall consume them (Deut 32:22). The sense of this testimony is that the fire and vengeance of God always burns for sinners, and pursues them even to the lower world. Such was that rich man too in the Gospel (Luke 16), clothed in purple, who, while Lazarus rested in Abraham's bosom, was tormented with eternal burnings. The same flame will also burn the earth, that is, our flesh, and its produce, that is, the desires of the flesh, and the foundations of the mountains that raise themselves against the knowledge of God. The darts of God will also destroy the mountains themselves, so that, humbled in punishments, they cease to be mountains.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JOS.1.1":["Having finally finished with the Pentateuch of Moses, as though freed for a great advantage, we set (our) hand to Jesus son of Nave, who the Hebrews call Joshua ben Nun, that is, Joshua son of Nun, and to the book of Judges, which they call Sopthim, to Ruth also and Esther, which they extol by the same names. And I admonish the reader, that he, being careful with Scripture, might preserve the forest of Hebrew names and (their) separations divided into parts, so that our work and his effort might not be wasted. And that in the first place, which I often testify, let him know me not to coin the new in rebuke of the old, as though my friends are accused, but rather to offer, for my part, to men of my language, those things of ours which still delight, like the copies of the Hexapla for the Greeks, which require great expense and work, so they might have our edition, and anywhere the readings of the ancient scrolls are doubtful, comparing this this to them, they might find what they seek, especially when among the Latins there are as many versions as there are books, and everyone has, according to his own judgment, either added or subtracted whatever seemed right to him, and he indeed may not have been able to be certain what differed. From which may scorpion cease to rise against me with bow-like wound, and poisoned tongue desist from slandering a holy work, either accepting, if it has pleased, or condemning, if it has displeased, and remember these verses: \"Your mouth has abounded in malice, and your tongue constructed deceits; sitting, you have spoken against your brother, and against the son of your mother imposed a scandal. These things you have done and I was quiet; you wrongly thought that I might be like you: I will accuse you and stand before your face\" [Psalm 49.19-21]. For what advantage is it to the listener for us to sweat at work and to work at criticizing others, for Jews to lament that the opportunity has been taken away from them for falsely accusing and insulting Christians, and for men of the Church to despise, indeed to tear apart, that from which enemies are tortured. If only what is old in the interpretation pleases them, which things are also not displeasing to me, and they think of receiving nothing further, why are they reading or not reading those things which are either added or cut out by the asterisks and obeli? For what reason have the churches accepted the translation of Daniel by Theodotion? Why are Origen and Eusebius Pamphilou admired for having treated entire editions similarly? Or what foolishness was it, after they had spoken true things, to set forth things which are false? And from where in the New Testament are they able to prove the received testimonies, which are not supported in the books of the Old (Testament)? Thus, we say, I may be seen to be not altogether quiet to accusers.\nOtherwise, after the falling asleep of Paula, whose life is an example of virtue, and these books, which I was not able to deny to Eustochium the virgin of Christ, we have decided \"while spirit yet rules these limbs\" to incline to the explanation of the Prophets, and to resume, in a kind of return home, a work long unfinished, especially when the admirable and holy man Pammachius demands the same in letters, and we, hurrying on to the homeland, need to pass by the deadly songs of the sirens with deaf ear.\n— Prologue to Joshua"],"JOS.5.2-7":["For since Christ was to spring from his seed (in whom the blessing of all the nations had been promised, and many ages were to pass between Abraham and Christ), God, providing that the offspring of beloved Abraham should not be mingled with the other nations and his family little by little become uncertain, marked the Israelite flock with a kind of brand of circumcision, so that, living among the Egyptians, among the Assyrians, Babylonians and Chaldeans, they might be distinguished by this seal. Accordingly, for forty years in the desert no one was circumcised: for they were living alone, without any admixture of another nation; as soon as the people had crossed the bank of the Jordan and the Jewish swarm had poured itself into the land of Palestine, circumcision, now necessary, provided against the confusion that would come from mingling with the nations. But that the people is written to have been circumcised a second time by Joshua (Jesu) their leader (Josh. 5) signifies both that circumcision, which had been reasonably practiced in Egypt, ceased in the desert, and that believers were to be cleansed by the Lord Jesus Christ with a spiritual circumcision.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"JOS.7.25-26":["But that the valley of Achor, in which Achan was killed for the theft of the things that had been consecrated to God, is interpreted 'confusion' and 'tumult', and not, as some think, διαστροφὴ, that is, perversity, Joshua himself interprets when he speaks to Achan: Because you have troubled us, may the Lord trouble you on this day. Hence that place was called Emec Achor, that is, the valley of confusion.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"JDG.5.1":["(Judges 5:1) And Deborah and Barak son of Abinoam sang. Barak is said to have been a man of Deborah the prophetess. Barak himself is also called Barak son of Lapidoth. For Lapidoth means lightning, and Barak, thunder. Lightning, therefore, because before he avenged Israel against the Canaanites, there was a lightning shining among the people, by merit and prerogative of his deeds: but after he avenged Israel against the Canaanites, he was called Thunder, that is, striking.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.4-5":["(Judges 5:4-5) Lord, when you went out from Seir and passed through the regions of Edom, the earth trembled, the heavens and clouds poured down with water. It is said that the Lord went out from Seir and passed through Edom because the Edomites refused to receive his law, so he gave the law of Israel on Mount Sinai. The earth trembled, the heavens and clouds poured down with water. The earthquake of Mount Sinai is described. And when it says, 'The heavens and clouds poured down with water,' it means that in the presence of the Lord, the heavens, as if in fear, emitted sweat, which is said to have dripped down like water. Similarly, the mountains flowed from the presence of the Lord. And what follows, 'Sinai from the presence of the Lord God of Israel,' must be understood: it trembled and smoked.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.6":["(Judges 5:6) In the days of Sangar, the son of Anath, in the days of Jahel, the paths remained empty, and those who entered through them walked along crooked roads. The paths are said to have remained empty because during the time of Sangar, the leader of Israel, the paths had been taken over by enemies of Israel, and they did not dare to freely ascend to the house of the Lord to pray; instead, those who entered through them secretly ascended through crooked streets, out of fear of the enemies. Concerning which enemies Sangar is said to have not fully freed them (Judg. 3:31). But what is inserted a little above, in the days of Jahel, is to be understood, because when Sisara was killed by Jahel, the roads were opened, and they went into the house of the Lord without any fear, who previously went there by winding paths. And that Jahel is mentioned before Debbora here, it is said that Debbora did this for the sake of humility, because Jahel, the wife of Aber the Cinaean, killed Sisara.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.7":["(Judges 5:7) However, the strong ones in Israel ceased, they paused until Deborah, a mother in Israel, arose. These strong ones are the learned teachers and preachers of the law, who are said to have paused, that is, to not have been present, until she arose to teach Israel through preaching, exhortation, and counsel, and to deliver them from their enemies. Deborah also provokes these strong ones in the previous verses to bless the Lord together with her, saying: 'You who willingly offered your lives for the peril of Israel, bless the Lord!' (Verse 2). Hi, strong men, that is to say, the learned ones, are said to have offered their souls to danger, that is, to have submitted themselves to the law of God and his service, and to have sacrificed their body and soul in meditations on the law day and night.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.8":["(Judges 5:8) The Lord has chosen new wars, and he himself overturns the gates of the enemy. If a shield and a spear appeared among forty thousand Israelites, it is to be understood that he said this as if by swearing an oath, that among forty thousand Israelites neither a shield nor a spear could be found. Therefore, the Lord is said to have chosen new wars, and to have overturned the gates of the enemy because he has made the weak strong and victorious. But it is explained in the previous verse that the Israelites had been despoiled of their weapons by Jabin, king of Canaan, where it is said: And the children of Israel cried to the Lord. He had nine hundred chariots armed with scythes, and for twenty years he severely oppressed them (Judg. IV, 5). He severely and forcefully oppressed them, because it is said that he despoiled them of their weapons: as it is written in the book of Samuel: When the day of battle came, there was no sword or spear found in the hand of all the people who were with Saul and Jonathan, except for Saul and his son Jonathan (1 Sam. XIII, 22).\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.9-10":["(Judges 5:9-10) My heart loves the leaders of Israel. It should be noted that in Hebrew, not leaders, but teachers is read. This is spoken by Deborah in the person of God, that God loves the teachers who study the law of God. Of whom it follows: You who offered yourselves willingly among the people, bless the Lord. Of whom it has also been said above in that place where it says: You who willingly offered your lives from Israel, etc. You who ride on bright donkeys. In Hebrew, female donkeys. And you sit in judgment. In Hebrew, sitting on judgment. And you walk on the road, speak where the chariots collide, and the army of the enemies is suffocated, there the righteousness of the Lord, the strength of Israel, is proclaimed. The people of Israel who ride on donkeys are called ascenders; but the female donkeys on which they ride are called the teachers of the tribes of Israel, on whose doctrine the rest of the people are said to ascend like on donkeys, that is, to find rest. They are called asses themselves, that is, they walk like an ass in the law, sitting on judgment, that is, on the law. They are also said to sit on judgment, that is, on the law, and to walk in the way under the law, because they would by no means exceed the path of the law. Therefore, Deborah, provoked the people who rested on these asses, that is, on these teachers, to bless the Lord, and she said: Speak, that is, praise. And as if they were asking: where and when will we praise the Lord? she responded: Where the chariots collided, and the army of the enemy was drowned, there let the justice of the Lord be recounted, the strength of merciful Israel; as if she said: Although God is always and everywhere to be praised; nevertheless, he is now to be praised and blessed by us, who overturns our enemies, just as he did with the Egyptians in the Red Sea.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.11":["(Judges 5:11) Then the people of the Lord went down to the gates and obtained the leadership. The people of the Lord went down to the gates, that is, to the gates of the house of the Lord, because with the Lord blessed and praised at the place of victory, they descended into the house of the Lord to praise the Lord there, offering prayers and sacrifices to Him. But as for the tribe of Issachar, that they were the teachers of the other tribes is shown in the Book of Chronicles, where it says: Of the sons of Issachar, there were men who had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do, two hundred chiefs; and all their brethren followed their command (1 Chronicles 12:32). And the reason these scholars are called asses is shown in the book of Genesis where it says: Issachar, a strong donkey lying down between the borders, saw that rest was good and the land was pleasant, and he bowed his shoulder to bear the burden and became a servant under tribute (Gen. XLIX, 14, 15). The donkey is called strong in the Book of Genesis because of the strength of the law, since it carried the law on its shoulder and gave tribute, that is, imparted the teaching of the law to the other tribes. In the Book of Judges, they are called donkeys, because they were weakened and humiliated, and remained weak, oppressed by the yoke of the nations, and only satisfied with the law of God.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.12":["(Judges 5:12) Rise, rise Deborah; rise, speak the Song. It is noteworthy that in Hebrew it is read 'exspergisce,' that is, 'exspergisce' in the spirit of prophecy. Therefore, by the voices of the aforementioned teachers, Deborah is urged to be 'exspergisce' in the spirit of prophecy and praise, and not to cease from praising God. Hence, it follows: Speak the Song. Rise, Barak, and take your captives, son of Abinoam. In Hebrew, it is said: 'Praedare praedam, tuam fili Abinoem.' This statement also warns the same wise men of Barak to plunder the remnants of their enemies, and to bring them to utter destruction, and to commendably acquire the name of victory for themselves.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.13":["(Judges 5:13) Then the remnants of the people were saved. This sentence is to be connected to the previous sentence, which says: Where the righteousness of the Lord is told, the strength of Israel's mercy; because then the remnants of the people were saved. The Lord fought in the strongholds. In Hebrew it is written: The Lord fought for me in the strongholds. And this is the voice of all the people of Israel; for He fought for them against the Egyptians and against the other enemies of Israel. Unde et sequitur.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.14":["(Judges 5:14a) From Ephraim, he destroyed them in Amalek, that is, Joshua, who was from the tribe of Ephraim, destroyed the strong ones, that is, the enemies of Israel, in Amalek, as it is read in the book of Exodus: 'And Joshua defeated Amalek and his people with the edge of the sword, etc.' (Exodus 17:13). And that the same Joshua was from the tribe of Ephraim is shown in the book of Numbers, where it is said: 'From the tribe of Ephraim, Hoshea the son of Nun' (Numbers 13:9), whom Joshua also calls in the following passages, saying: 'Moses called Hoshea the son of Nun, Joshua.' And after him, that is, Joshua, rising from the tribe of Benjamin, Saul destroyed the people of Amalek, as commanded by the Lord, which Deborah prophesied about Saul in the spirit of prophecy. And this is said according to the figure of speech called prolepsis, that he himself would destroy Amalek, as you have in the Book of Samuel (1 Samuel 14:48). But the phrase 'O Amalek' is not found in the Hebrew text, but the Latin interpreter added it for the sake of clarity.\n\n(Judges 5:14b) From Machir, the leaders descended, and from Zebulun, those who led the army to fight. In Hebrew, it is written: From Machir, the leaders descended, and it is understood, to fight, that is, to overthrow the prince Sisera. And from Zebulun, it is understood, they descended, who led the army. And from the tribe of Levi, and from the tribe of Issachar, it is understood, to fight, that is, to praise and humbly beseech the Lord. Of which, it follows.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.15":["(Judges 5:15) The leaders of Issachar were with Deborah. In Hebrew: the princes, that is, the teachers of the Law, were with Deborah to praise the Lord, and Barak followed in the footsteps of the age. It is to be understood that a certain part of them remained with Deborah to praise the Lord, and a part followed Barak to exhort and vanquish Sisera. Who, as if into a precipice, gave himself to danger. Barak, indeed, believing the words of the Lord, gave himself to danger, throwing himself into the torrent Kishon, pursuing and vanquishing the army of Sisera. For in this place a raging torrent is understood as the abyss. Ruben, on the other hand, is divided within himself, and a dispute of noble men is found. It is written in the Book of Judges against itself. For the voice of Deborah is heard, rebuking the tribe of Ephraim, because they did not obey the command of the Lord and did not go with their brothers to fight against the enemies of the Lord. But he, being terrified by fear and unbelieving in the words of the Lord, separated himself from them and, because of that, was found to be proud, that is, arrogant. And so their reproof follows in the spirit of prophecy, saying:\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.16":["(Judges 5:16) Why do you dwell between two boundaries, to hear the hissing of flocks? It is not read in Hebrew, 'between two', but rather 'between your boundaries', namely the boundaries of your neighboring tribes, to hear the hissing of flocks; as if to say: You desire to preserve the flocks with which you abound, and fear losing them, and you take greater pleasure in hearing their hissing than in striving with your brothers to conquer the enemies of Israel. And because you do this, you proudly resist the command of the Lord. And so it follows the same thing as above: after the division of Reuben, a dispute of the magnanimous is found: because he did evil, despising the authority of the Lord and being proud.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.17":["(Judges 5:17) Gad remained beyond the Jordan. And this is set forth as a reproach because while Reuben remained, Gad remained with him of equal mind. And Dan was free with ships. For seeing that the tribes of Reuben and Gad did not go with their brothers to battle, being terrified by fear, he took flight by being free with ships. Asher dwelt on the seashore and stayed in the ports. Likewise, this is set forth as a reproach because Asher, while dwelling on the seashore, stayed in the ports, choosing instead to flee rather than fight with his brothers.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.18":["(Judges 5:18) Zebulun and Naphtali offered their lives unto death in the region of Morome. In Hebrew, it is 'super regionem Morome'. The meaning is that while the other tribes were unwilling to go, these two tribes offered their lives unto death. In the region of Morome. For Morome is interpreted as 'exalted': they are said to have offered their lives for the sake of the exalted region, that is, for God, and therefore they are praised, while the other tribes are criticized.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.19":["(Judges 5:19) Kings came and fought. The kings of the land of Canaan said, who were subject to King Jabin. The kings of Canaan fought. It repeats the same thing in Tanach. Tanach is indeed a province where the Kishon River is. Near the waters of Megiddo. Megiddo is a city situated on the Kishon River. And what is said, Near the waters of Megiddo, is understood near the Kishon River, the city of Megiddo. Yet they took nothing as plunderers. This is said in mocking, implying that not only did they not obtain any spoils, but they were also unable to save their own lives.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.20":["(Judges 5:20) There was a battle fought against them from heaven. Because in the forty thousand men of Israel a shield and spear could not be found, therefore the Lord fought for them from heaven with hailstones and fire against their enemies. Just as it is also written in the book of Joshua: The stars remained in their order and fought against Sisera (Joshua 10:11). The stars are understood to be the angels who fought against Sisera from heaven.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.21":["(Judges 5:21) The River Kishon swept away their corpses. For there is the place of their downfall; and it is as if one were to ask, where? and the answer is: In the River Kishon, that is, in the Red Sea. Hence it follows: The river Kishon, the river of Kishon. It calls the River Kishon the Red Sea. For Kishon means of old, because ancient miracles were performed there, when the children of Israel passed over and the Egyptians perished. And because the Red Sea vomited forth the dead Egyptians, so that the Israelites saw their dead bodies on the shore of the sea, and the same bodies were carried away by the fish and sea creatures: therefore the River Kishon swept away their corpses, so that the fish and sea creatures of the Red Sea might eat the bodies of the Canaanites, in place of the bodies of the Egyptians taken from them. Trample, my soul, the mighty ones. This is what Deborah, representing all of Israel, says, that they may crush the necks of their strongest enemies with one mind, that is, the strongest ones: as it is also written in the book of Joshua about the five kings of the Canaanites (Josh. 10:24).\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.22":["(Judges 5:22) Then the hooves of the horses fell off, with a powerful force to those fleeing, and headlong to the bravest of the enemies who were rushing. 'Their hooves,' it is read in Hebrew; for it is reported that the hooves of their horses fell off from hailstones from the heavens, that is, from their angels. So the meaning is: If the hooves of the horses fell off from touching the stones, what do you think happened to the riders?\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.23":["(Judges 5:23) Cursed be the land of Meroz, said the Angel of the Lord. In Hebrew it is written: Curse Meroz, said the Angel of the Lord. The Angel of the Lord is understood by the Hebrews to be Michael, who commands Deborah to have the people of Israel curse Meroz, that is, the power of the Angel that presided over the Canaanites. For Meroz is interpreted as secret. Therefore, he is commanded to be cursed, that is, anathematized, because while the other angels fought for Israel, he not only did not fight, but also persevered in their adversity. Curse the inhabitants of Meroz, says the Lord's angel. Curse them bitterly because they did not come to help the Lord. They did not come to help the strong Lord.\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.5.24":["(Judges 5:24) Blessed among women, Jael, the wife of Heber the Kenite. Among women, namely Sarah, Rebecca, Rachel, Leah, and almost all women. Let her be blessed in the temple, the Lord is understood, so that her memorial may be there, just like Judith. And the reason why she is blessed is given, namely because she acted wisely and prudently, in that she gave milk to the one asking for water. Therefore, she gave milk, so that the fleeing enemy would find a stronger faith in her. And if it is asked why he did not give wine, it must be answered that the house of Rechab do not drink wine, as you have in the prophet Jeremiah (Jer. XXXV, 14).\n— Commentary on the Song of Deborah"],"JDG.6.32":["And where the Seventy put Jareb, we, following Symmachus, have translated \"avenger\"; for Aquila and Theodotion rendered \"judge\". That Jareb signifies avenger and judge, the name of Gideon shows: when the worshipers of Baal demanded him for punishment, because he had overthrown the grove and the altars of Baal, his father answered: Let Baal avenge himself, or let Baal judge him; and he was called, it says, Jerubbaal, that is: let Baal avenge himself.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"JDG.16.21":["But because there follows: Lay bare your shame, the millstone too is understood figuratively by the Hebrews, namely that, after the manner of a harlot, she lies open to the lust of the victors. And what is written in the book of Judges about Samson (Judg 16), that he was condemned by the Philistines to the mill, they wish to signify this: that, being the strongest of men, he was compelled, for the sake of offspring, to do this with foreign women.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1SA.2.30":["Yet it must be noted that where things are seemly and honorable, and in accordance with the merit of those who undergo them, God professes that he does them himself; but where they are sad and not worthy of God, he says indeed that they happen, but not by him, as in the present passage: Therefore fathers shall eat sons in the midst of you, and sons shall eat their fathers; for he did not say: 'I will make fathers eat their sons in the midst of you, and sons eat their fathers'; but what was seemly and did not seem unworthy of the majesty of God, he says he does himself, for there follows: And I will execute judgments in you, and I will winnow all your remnants into every wind. Something of the sort we shall be able to find also in the curses of Deuteronomy, and especially in that passage: Those who glorify me I will glorify; but those who despise me shall be brought to nothing; for those who glorify him he himself glorifies, but those who despise him will not be despised or brought to nothing by the Lord — for this is what would have followed for him to say —, but they will be brought to nothing absolutely, not by God but for their own deserts and sins.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1SA.9.7-8":["Nor let anyone be troubled by what we read in the first book of Kings (1 Samuel): that Saul, wishing to go to Samuel, said to his servant that he could not go to him because he did not have a price to offer for the prophecy, and that the servant answered: Behold, there is found in my hand the fourth part of a shekel of silver, and I will give it to the man of God, and he will tell us our way; for it is not written that Samuel accepted it, or that they offered it to him; on the contrary, they are fed by the prophet and invited to a meal. But suppose that he did accept it: it should be reckoned rather as offerings for the tabernacle than as payment for prophecy. For a shekel contains twenty obols, and the fourth part of a shekel is five obols. So our priests too, if they wish to sell prophecy, and to set out in their chairs the doves which the Lord overturned with a whip, let them accept five obols, not the prices of country estates.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"1SA.21.1-6":["But he said to them: Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and those who were with him, how he entered the house of God and ate the loaves of the presentation, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but for the priests alone? To refute the false charge of the Pharisees he recalls the old history, when David, fleeing Saul, came to Nob and, received by Ahimelech the priest, asked for food; and he, since he had no common loaves, gave him consecrated ones, which it was not lawful to eat except for the priests and Levites alone, and he asked only this, whether the young men were clean from women; and when David answered, since yesterday and the day before, he did not hesitate to give the loaves, judging it better (as the prophet says: I desire mercy and not sacrifice) to free men from the danger of hunger than to offer sacrifice to God. For the salvation of men is a victim pleasing to God. So the Lord sets this against them and says: If David too is holy, and Ahimelech the high priest is not blamed by you, but both transgressed the command of the law with an acceptable excuse, and hunger is the cause, why do you not approve in the apostles the same hunger that you approve in the others? Although even in this there is a great difference: these rub ears of grain in their hands on the Sabbath; those ate the levitical loaves, and the days of the new moons were added to the solemnity of the Sabbath, on which, being sought at the banquet, he fled from the royal court. Note that neither David nor his young men received the loaves of the presentation before they had answered that they were clean from women.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"1SA.21.4-6":["Let the bishop also be chaste, whom the Greeks call σώφρονα; and the Latin translator, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, rendered \"prudent\" (prudentem) instead of \"chaste\" (pudicum). Now if laymen are commanded to abstain from intercourse with their wives for the sake of prayer, what are we to think of a bishop, who is every day to offer to God unblemished victims for his own sins and those of the people? Let us read again the books of Kings, and we shall find that the priest Abimelech was unwilling to give of the loaves of the presentation to David and his young men before he asked whether the young men were clean from a woman: not, of course, another man's, but a wife (1 Sam. 21). And had he not heard that they had kept away from the marital act yesterday and the day before, he would by no means have granted the loaves which he had at first refused. There is as great a difference between the loaves of the presentation and the body of Christ as between the shadow and the bodies, between the image and the truth, between the patterns of things to come and those very things which were prefigured through the patterns. Just as, therefore, gentleness, patience, sobriety, moderation, abstinence from gain, and also hospitality and kindness ought to be found especially in the bishop, and to stand out among all the laymen, so too a purity of his own and (so to speak) a priestly chastity, so that he abstain not only from the unclean act, but also from the glance of the eye and the straying of thought, so that the mind that is to consecrate the body of Christ may be free.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"1SA.25.25":["For the fool will speak foolish things, for which in the Hebrew it is written: NABAL NABALA IDABBER (נבל נבלה ידבר). We say this in order to explain the words of Abigail, who says of Nabal the Carmelite: According to his name he is foolish (1 Sam 25:25).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2SA.1.16":["Hence his blood will come upon him, that is: he himself will be the cause of his own death, according to what David says to the man who announced the death of Saul and related that the king of Israel had been slain by him: Your blood be upon your head. Not by my sentence, but by the blood of Saul will your blood be shed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"2SA.12.14":["And what follows: And his reproach the Lord will restore to him, agrees with that sense which Nathan speaks to David: You who have made the enemies blaspheme the name of the Lord, on account of this thing, that is, on account of this sin by which you killed Uriah, that very blasphemy and reproach, by which the Lord was blasphemed through you, will be turned upon your head.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"1KI.1.1":["There are twenty-two letters among the Hebrews, as is also witnessed by the language of the Syrians and Chaldeans, which is for the most part similar to the Hebrew; for these twenty-two elements also have the same sound, but different characters. The Samaritans still write the Pentateuch of Moses in the same number of letters, only they differ in shapes and points (or \"endings\" apicibus). And Ezra, the scribe and doctor of the Law, after the capture of Jerusalem and the rebuilding of the Temple under Zerubbabel, is certain to have found (or \"invented\" repperisse) other letters, which we now use, when up to that time the characters of the Samaritans and the Hebrews were the same. In the book of Numbers this same total is also mystically shown by the census of the Levites and the priests. And we find in certain Greek scrolls to this day the four-lettered Name of God written in the ancient letters. But also the thirty-sixth Psalm, and the one hundred tenth, and the one hundred eleventh, and the one hundred eighteenth, and the one hundred forty-fourth, although written in different meter, are nevertheless woven with an alphabet of the same number. And in the Lamentations of Jeremiah, and his prayer, also at the end of the Proverbs of Solomon from that place in which he says \"Who can find a strong woman?\" are counted the same alphabet or sections. Furthermore, five of the letters among them are double: chaph, mem, nun, phe, sade. For they write with these one way at the beginning and in the middle of words, another at the end. From which also five are considered double books by most: Samuel, Malachim, Dabreiamin, Ezra, Jeremiah with Cinoth, that is, his Lamentantion. Therefore, just as there are twenty-two elements, by which we write in Hebrew all that we say, and the human voice is understood by their beginnings (or \"parts\" initiis), thus twenty-two scrolls are counted, by which letters and writings a just man is instructed in the doctrine of God, as though in tender infancy and still nursing.\nThe first book is called among them Bresith, which we call Genesis; the second, Hellesmoth, which is named Exodus; the third, Vaiecra, that is Leviticus; the fourth Vaiedabber, which we call Numbers; the fifth, Addebarim, which is designated Deuteronomy. These are the five books of Moses, which they appropropriately call Thorat, that is, the Law.\nThe second order is made of the Prophets, and begins with Jesus son of Nave, which is called among them Joshua benNum. Then they append Sopthim, that is the book of Judges; and they attach Ruth to the same, because the history narrated happened in the days of the Judges. Samuel follows third, which we call First and Second Kingdoms. Fourth is Malachim, that is Kings, which book contains Third and Fourth Kingdoms; and it is much better to say Malachim, that is Kings, rather than Malachoth, that is Kingdoms, for it does not describe the kingdoms of many nations, but only that of the Israelite people which contains twelve tribes. Fifth is Isaiah, sixth Jeremiah, seventh Ezekiel, eighth the book of the Twelve Prophets, which is called Thareasra among them.\nThe third order holds the Hagiographa, and begins with Job, the first book, the second by David, which is also one book of Psalms comprising five sections. The third is Solomon, having three books: Proverbs, which they call Parables, that is Masaloth, and Ecclesiastes, that is Accoeleth, and The Song of Songs, which they denote with the title Sirassirim. Sixth is Daniel, seventh Dabreiamin, that is Words of the Days, which we may call more clearly a chronicle (Gk here: χρονικον) of all of Divine history, which book is written among us as First and Second Paralipomenon; eighth is Ezra, which is also in the same manner among Greeks and Latins divided into two books; ninth is Esther.\nAnd thus there are likewise twenty-two books in the Old (Testament), that is five of Moses, eight of the Prophets, nine of the Hagiographa. Although some may write Ruth and Cinoth among the Hagiographa, and think of counting these books among their number, and then by this to have twenty-four books of the Old Law, which the Apocalypse of John introduces with the number of twenty-four elders worshipping the Lamb and offering their crowns, prostrated on their faces, and crying out with unwearying voice: \"Holy, holy, holy Lord God almighty, Who was and Who is, and Who will be.\"\nThis prologue to the Scriptures may be appropriate as a helmeted introduction to all the books which we turn from Hebrew into Latin, so we may be able to know whatever is outside of these is set aside among the apocrypha. Therefore, Wisdom, which is commonly ascribed to Solomon, and the book of Jesus son of Sirach, and Judith and Tobias, and The Shepherd are not in the canon. I have found the First Book of the Maccabees (is) Hebrew, the Second is Greek, which may also be proven by their styles.\nWhile these things may be so, I implore you, reader, that you might not consider my work a rebuke of the ancients. Each one offers to the Tabernacle of God what he is able. Some offer gold and silver and precious stones; others, linen and purple, scarlet and blue. It will go well with us, if we offer the skins and hair of goats. For the Apostle still judges our more contemptible parts more necessary. From which both the whole of the beauty of the Tabernacle and each individual kind, a distinction of the present and future Church, is covered with skins and goat-hair coverings, and the heat of the sun and the harmful rain are kept off by those things which are of lesser value. Therefore, first read my Samuel and Kings; mine, I say, mine. For whatever we have learned and know by often translating and carefully correcting is ours. And when you come to understand what you did not know before, either consider me a translator, if you are grateful, or a paraphraser, if ungrateful, although I am truly not at all aware of anything of the Hebrew to have been changed by me. Certainly, if you are incredulous, read the Greek and Latin books and compare (them) with these little works, and wherever you will see among them to differ, ask any one of the Hebrews, in whom you might place better faith, and if he confirms us, I think that you will not consider him a diviner, as he has similarly divined in the very same place with me.\nBut I also ask you, handmaidens of Christ, who have anointed the head of your reclining Lord with the most precious myrrh of faith, who have in no way sought the Savior in the tomb, for whom Christ has now ascended to the Father, that you might oppose the shields of your prayers against the barking dogs which rage against me with rabid mouth and go around the city, and in it they are considered educated if slandering others. I, knowing my humility, will always remember these sentences: \"I will guard my ways, so I will not offend with my tongue; I have placed a guard on my mouth, while the sinner stands against me; I was mute, and humiliated, and silent because of good things.\"\n— The 'Helmeted' introduction to Kings"],"1KI.10.22":["Jehoshaphat too had ships which he sent to Tarshish (1 Kgs 22:49), but they were broken at Ezion-geber. Solomon too had ships which went to Tarshish (1 Kgs 10:22) and returned after three years and brought the king silver and gold, ivory and apes. But because both kings had sinned, the one given to pleasure and a lover of women of foreign nations, the other joining himself with the king of Samaria, both of which things are referred to the pagans and the heretics, in whom there is nothing but the glitter of eloquence, and a sense built up by diabolical art, and dead speech, which is interpreted in the ivory teeth, and a likeness of human reason, which is perceived in the apes; therefore we read in the Psalms, With a violent wind you will shatter the ships of Tarshish (Ps 47 [48]:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"1KI.14.3":["The same thing is heard also in the third book of Kingdoms (1 Kings): that the wife of Jeroboam, when her son was sick, goes to Ahijah the man of God and brings him loaves and raisins and a little jar of honey. For it is told what she brought with her, and yet it is not written that the prophet accepted it, since indeed he both rebuked her and foretold the mourning to come; although it may be that those who had been accustomed to go to soothsayers (for there were many diviners and soothsayers in Israel) thought, by a bad custom, the same thing also of the prophets, and wished to offer to holy men what they had been accustomed to offer to diviners, and that Scripture only related what they wished; yet it did not add either that they dared to offer it or that the prophets accepted it.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"1KI.15.11-14":["Idolatry of this kind existed in Israel, the women especially worshipping Baal-peor for the size of his obscene member, whom we can call Priapus. Hence also King Asa took away the high places from the people, and priests of this kind, and deposed his mother from her august rule, as Scripture testifies, saying: And Asa did what was right in the sight of the Lord, as David his father, and he took away the effeminate from the land, and he purged all the filth of the idols which his fathers had made. Moreover he also removed Maacah his mother, so that she should not be chief in the rites of Priapus and in his grove which she had consecrated; and he overturned its cave, and broke the most foul image, and burned it in the brook Kidron. But the high places he did not take away; nevertheless the heart of Asa was perfect before God all his days.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"1KI.19.6":["And that it is written, according to the Seventy, that Elijah, fleeing Jezebel, found a ὀλυρίτην [made of olyra] loaf, 'baked under the ashes', is a sign of persecution and want; hence he is also fed by ravens, and, because he had found no food in Israel, he was fed with the nourishment of the nations, which were unclean.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"1KI.22.19-22":["A certain one interprets this passage thus: On the right and on the left we read of powers and strengths of God, which the Greeks call δυνάμεις. On the right the Seraphim and Cherubim, and all the angelic powers; on the left the contrary strengths, of which it is written: He sent upon them the wrath of his indignation, fury and wrath, and tribulation, a sending by evil angels; of which Micaiah too speaks in the book of Kings: I saw the Lord God of Israel sitting on his throne, and all the host of heaven stood beside him on his right and on his left. I think that it was a left-hand spirit which went forth, and stood before the Lord, and said: I will deceive Ahab, and I will go forth, and I will be a lying spirit in the mouth of all his prophets. To this left-hand and contrary power, which was apt for deceiving, and practiced in ruin, and had deceived many before, God says: You shall deceive and prevail; go forth and do so. That spirit also which choked Saul, of which his servants said to him: Behold, an evil spirit of God has stupefied you, was from the left-hand parts, which minister to the Lord for punishing those who have deserved to be tormented for their sins. For not only are men ministers and avengers of his wrath upon those who do evil, and not without cause do they bear the sword, but also the contrary strengths, which are called the fury and the wrath of God, which the prophet, deprecating them, says: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath. To such, so to speak, inquisitors and torturers the Apostle handed over sinners for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit might be saved; among whom is Satan too, to whom he handed over others that they might learn not to blaspheme. So much on why the caterpillar was called the power of God.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"2KI.1.2":["If they have called the householder Beelzebub, how much more those of his household. Beelzebub is the idol of Ekron, which in the book of Kings is called the idol of the fly. Beel is the same as Bel or Baal; zebub means fly. Therefore they called the prince of demons by the name of that most filthy idol, who is called fly because of the uncleanness that destroys the sweetness of the oil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"2KI.1.8":["Set, he says, (or turn) your face against Mount Seir; and he says 'turn' significantly: from one prophecy to another prophecy; and 'face': not of the body but of the mind — of which the Apostle also speaks: But we all, with unveiled face beholding the glory of the Lord —; and Mount 'Seir', which means 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is to be taken as the opposing power that presided over the nation of the sons of Esau and had raised itself up against the people of 'Iuda' [Judah], that is, of 'confession' and of the true faith — but if a prudent reader objects, how in this place 'Seir', that is, 'shaggy' and 'hairy', is understood in a bad sense when Elijah too was called a hairy man, I shall answer briefly: it is written of Esau that he was red and shaggy like a hide, but of Elijah only that he was a hairy man; in Esau, who, bloodthirsty and bloodstained, is compared to a shaggy hide, it is a sign of mortal works and of death itself; in Elijah, an increase of manliness, for which reason both his dwelling in the wilderness and the austerity of his way of life showed him to be not only a man but a most valiant man; moreover Jacob, who had supplanted Esau and received his birthright, because he dwelt simply at home and was not a hunter like the giant Nimrod, is for that reason called smooth and sleek —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"2KI.2.14":["That which, according to the Seventy translators, is asked in the volume of Kings, what is meant when Elisha says (2 Kgs 2:14), Where is the God of Elijah APHPHO (אפא), is set down more plainly in the present place: where the Seventy translated now, the Hebrew has APHPHO, which we have translated now also; and Aquila, wishing to preserve the Hebrew idiom, set down καίπερτοι, a conjunction which the Latin tongue cannot render.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2KI.6.17":["Let us seek other horses also, which the Lord mounts. In the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) we read that Elisha's servant rose early, and going out saw an army surrounding the walls of the city, and horses and chariots. And after his eyes were opened at the prophet's prayers: He looked, it says, and behold, the mountain was full of horses, and chariots of fire round about Elisha. Attend carefully to this: horses are seen, and chariots, and yet among so many thousands of horses and chariots there is no rider; of these horses he was the charioteer and the driver of whom the psalmist sings: You who sit upon the Cherubim, show yourself. With such horses and such a chariot Elijah too was snatched up to heaven.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"2KI.19.1-2":["For which in the volume of Kings one reads: He himself was covered with sackcloth and entered the house of the Lord, and he sent Eliakim the overseer of the house, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests covered with sackcloth, to Isaiah the prophet, the son of Amos (2 Kgs 19:1, 2). Here, because Isaiah was writing the history about himself, he did not call himself a Prophet but the son of a Prophet; there, however, because the writer of the history was another, he writes that he himself was a Prophet. This we read also of Matthew the Evangelist: that Matthew himself called himself a publican (Matt 9), while the other Evangelists were silent about the name of publican and set down only the apostolic dignity; and that in the pairings of the Apostles he is second in his own list and first in the others'.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2KI.20.4-11":["Here, as in a prophecy, the history is related in reversed order, which in the volume of Kings is read in better sequence. While Hezekiah was weeping with a great weeping, before Isaiah had gone out of the middle part of the court, the word of the Lord came to him, saying: Go back and say to Hezekiah, the leader of my people: I have heard your prayer and seen your tear: Behold, I have healed you. And Hezekiah said to Isaiah: What will be the sign that the Lord will heal me (2 Kgs 20:4 and 5)? To him the Prophet answered: This shall be the sign from the Lord that the Lord will do the word which he has spoken: Do you wish the shadow to advance so many lines, or to go back as many steps? And Hezekiah said to him: It is easy for the shadow to increase by ten lines, and I do not wish this to be done; but that it go back ten steps. And when, at Isaiah's calling upon the power of the Lord, the sign had been accomplished, Isaiah commanded that a lump of fresh figs be brought: and when they had brought it and laid it upon his wound, he was cured. The Prophet returns to the king at the Lord's command, so that he who had struck might himself heal; and Hezekiah is called the leader of his people, and son of David, whose works he followed, for he had done what was right according to all that David his father did: and his prayer is heard and his tears are seen, because he had walked before the Lord in truth and with a perfect heart, and had wept with a great weeping, and had done what was pleasing in his eyes. Fifteen years are added to his life, which he had not asked for, and besides, the security of the kingdom during his lifetime is promised.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2CH.24.20-22":["That upon you may come all the just blood that has been shed upon the earth, from the blood of Abel the just to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, who was killed between the temple and the altar. Amen I say to you, all these things will come upon this generation. Concerning Abel there is no ambiguity that he is the one whom Cain his brother killed. That he was just is proved not only from the Lord's present judgment but from the testimony of Genesis, where his gifts are related as accepted by God. We ask who this Zechariah son of Barachiah is, since we read of many Zechariahs. And lest a free license for error be granted us, there is added: Whom you killed between the temple and the altar. I have read different things in different writers, and I ought to set down the opinions of each. Some say it is Zechariah son of Barachiah, who is the eleventh among the twelve prophets, and the name of the father agrees in his case; but where he was killed between the temple and the altar Scripture does not say, especially since in his times there were scarcely even ruins of the temple. Others want it understood as Zechariah the father of John, proving from certain dreams of the apocrypha that he was killed because he had preached the coming of the Savior. This, because it has no authority from the Scriptures, is despised with the same ease with which it is proved. Others will have this to be the Zechariah who was killed by Joash king of Judea between the temple and the altar, as the history of Kings relates. But it must be observed that that Zechariah is not the son of Barachiah but the son of Jehoiada the priest. Hence Scripture also relates: Joash did not remember the good things that Jehoiada his father had done for him. Since, then, we have both the Zechariah and a place of killing that agrees, we ask why he is called the son of Barachiah and not of Jehoiada. Barachiah in our language means blessed of the Lord, and the justice of the priest Jehoiada is shown by his Hebrew name. In the Gospel which the Nazarenes use we find written son of Jehoiada in place of son of Barachiah. The simpler brethren, among the ruins of the temple and of the altar, or at the exits of the gates that lead to Siloam, point out red stones and think they were stained with the blood of Zechariah. We do not condemn the error, which comes down from hatred of the Jews and from the piety of faith.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"2CH.24.23-24":["In the book of Days (2 Chr 24) the history relates that when the year was completed the army of Syria went up against Joash king of Judah, and came into Jerusalem, and slew all the princes of the people, and sent all the spoil to the king of Damascus; it had come with few men, and God had delivered into their hand a very great multitude, because they had forsaken the Lord God of their fathers. This must be considered according to the anagogy and as a type of things to come, whether we may refer it to the time of the Lord's advent. For after the end of the acceptable year, in which the Gospel was preached by the Savior, the army of the nations went up from Damascus with few men against Judah and Jerusalem, who had forsaken the Lord; and they carried off all their riches of the Law and the Prophets, and sent them to the king of Damascus: namely, churchmen and men of the Gospel teaching, who, in comparison with the whole world, still unbelieving at that time, and with the Jews scattered through the whole earth, were few; and yet the Lord delivered Jerusalem into their hands, because they had forsaken the Son of God, who had been foretold beforehand through the prophets (Acts 9). Therefore I think that for this reason Saul too, who afterwards received the name of Paul from his virtue, went to Damascus because he was a zealot of the Law, and wished to fight against the believers from the nations; and that, overcome there, he followed those who lived in Damascus, so that from there he might go up again to Jerusalem to do battle with the Jews.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"2CH.32.23-31":["That this is not my opinion but the faith of holy Scripture, the words of Chronicles testify, which say after other things: This is Hezekiah who stopped the upper spring of the waters of Gihon, and turned them down to the west of the city of David. In all his works he accomplished prosperously what he wished; but in the embassy of the princes of Babylon, who had been sent to him to inquire about the portent that had happened on the earth, God left him, that he might be tested, and that all that was in his heart might be made known (2 Chr 32:30, 31). And he was left to temptation for this reason, because after so great a victory, and the sun's going back, and the congratulation of a most powerful kingdom, his heart was lifted up. Indeed, in the same volume it is written: Many brought victims and sacrifices to the Lord in Jerusalem, and gifts to Hezekiah king of Judah: and he was exalted before all the nations. In those days he was sick unto death, and he prayed to the Lord; and he heard him and gave him a sign; but he did not make return according to the benefits he had received: for his heart was lifted up, and there was wrath against him, and against Judah and Jerusalem (2 Chr 32:23, 24). And again holy Scripture says that the lifting up of his heart was softened by penance, adding: And afterward he was humbled because his heart had been lifted up, both he and the inhabitants of Jerusalem: and therefore the wrath of the Lord did not come upon them in the days of Hezekiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"EZR.1.1":["Whether it may be more difficult to do or not to do what you have requested, I have not yet established. For it is also not my desire to refuse your commands, and the greatness of weight imposed thus press upon the neck, so that before a falling under the bundle, there might rather be a lightening (of the load). The efforts of the envious agree with this, who consider all that I write reproof, with conscience occasionally fighting against them, publicly tearing apart what they read secretly, to such a degree that I am compelled to cry out and to say: \"O Lord, free my soul from crooked lips and a false tongue\" (Ps 119.2). It is the third year that you always write and write again, that I might translate the book of Ezra for you from Hebrew, as though you do not have the Greek and Latin scrolls, or whatever it is which is translated by us might not be something immediately spat upon by all. As a certain person says, \"For to strive without effort, and not to seek anything by wearying except hatred, is extreme insanity\" (Sallust, Jugurtha 3). Therefore, I implore you, my dearest Domnius and Rogatian, that, keeping the reading private, you will not bring the book forth into the public, nor throw food to the fastidious, and you will avoid the pride of them who know only (how) to judge others, and themselves (know how) to do nothing. And if there are any of the brothers whom we do not displease, give the text to them, admonishing that they transcribe the Hebrew names, of which there is a great abundance in this book, separately and with intermediate spaces. For it will profit nothing to correct the book, without diligence being preserved in the correction of the copiers.\nNeither should it disturb anyone that the book edited by us is one, nor should they be delighted by the dreams of the third and fourth books (which are) of the apocrypha, both because among the Hebrews the discourses of Ezra and Nehemiah are confined to one scroll, and those things which are not found among them, nor are of the twenty-four elders, are for throwing away. And if anyone sets the (version of the) Seventy interpreters before you, the variety of the texts of which shows them torn and perverted, nor indeed can it be asserted truth is diverse, send him to the Gospels, in which are set down many things as though from the Old Testament, things which are not found among the Seventy interpreters, like this: \"He will be called a Nazarene,\" and \"From Egypt I have called my son,\" and \"They will look on him whom they have pierced\" and many other things which we are saving for a more extensive work, and ask of him where they might be written, and when he has not been able to reveal (where), you must read from these texts which recently were edited by us, daily pierced by the tongues of the slanderous.\nBut so that I might come to a shortcut, certainly what I will introduce is the most reasonable. I have given, in what is translated by me, anything that is not found in the Greek or is found otherwise (than there). Which interpreter do they mangle? They may ask the Hebrews and their authors, whether they accept or reject the sense of my translation. Furthermore, it is another thing if, as is said, with eyes closed they want to slander me and not imitate the study and goodwill of the Greeks, who, after the Seventy translators, with the Gospel of Christ now shining, they both attentively read the Jewish and Ebionite interpreters of the Old Law, namely Aquila, Symmachus, and Theodotion, and have also dedicated (them) to the churches, through the labor of Origen in the Hexapla. How much more should Latins be grateful, having understood that the joy of Greece is to borrow anything from itself (?). For firstly, it is of great expense and of infinite difficulty to be able to have all of the texts; then also, those who have (them) and are ignorant of the Hebrew words will err more, not knowing which ones of the many will have said the truth. Which thing also happened recently to a certain very wise man among the Greeks, so that occasionally leaving the sense of the Scriptures, the error of some particular translator was followed. And we, who at least have a little knowledge of the Hebrew tongue, and our Latin does not lack style in any way, are both better able than others to judge, and to express those things of them which we understand in our language. Therefore, even if a serpent hisses, \"and the victor Sinon throws burning torches,\" with Christ helping, my speech will never be silenced, for (even my) severed tongue will stutter (something). Those who will, may read; those who won't, may throw away. They may scatter (?) the writings; they may slander the letters. Much more by your love will I be provoked toward study, rather than be deterred by their detraction and hatred.\n— Prologue to Ezra"],"EZR.2.3":["Whose image prefigured this in Zerubabel and Ezra, when the people returned and the city began to be built in its exalted state, and the religion of the Temple was observed, and the other things contained in the book of Ezra himself. But it was more fully and completely fulfilled in the Lord Savior and the Apostles, when the city was built on its highest point, of which it is written: A city set on a hill cannot be hidden (Matthew 5:14); and the Temple was established according to its order and ceremonies, so that whatever was done in the former people carnally, would be spiritually fulfilled in the Church.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Many thought this at the time of Zerubbabel, the son of Shealtiel, and Joshua, the son of Jozadak, the high priest, when Haggai and Zechariah prophesied during the time of the completion of the Temple under the priest Ezra, and the walls were rebuilt under Nehemiah, that those whom He had cast out in anger, wrath, and great indignation from Jerusalem, and had scattered throughout the whole world, He had afterwards made them dwell confidently and be a people of God; and that the Lord had been their God, and the other things that the Scripture contains. But how can this be adapted to that time: That I will make them dwell confidently, and I will strike for them an everlasting covenant, or I will establish for them an eternal testament, cannot be thoroughly approved: indeed, those whom we have read and holy history narrates were often taken captive not only by neighboring nations, but also by the Persians and Macedonians, and by the Egyptians and Romans, and until now they serve. Therefore, all things must be referred to the advent of the Savior: which we see fulfilled in our time of faith, and the election of the remnant has been preserved according to the Apostle (Romans 9).\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"EZR.6.7":["We must certainly understand by this day the time of the composition of the history, whether you prefer the view that Moses was the author of the Pentateuch or that Ezra re-edited it. In either case I make no objection. The question now is whether the words unto this day are to be referred to the time of publishing or writing the books, and if so it is for him to show, now that so many years have rolled away since that day, that either the idols hidden beneath the oak have been found, or the grave of Moses discovered; for he obstinately maintains that what does not happen so long as the point of time indicated by until and unto has not been attained, begins to be when that point has been reached.\n— The Perpetual Virginity of Blessed Mary"],"EST.1.1":["The Book of Esther stands corrupted by various translators. Which (book) I, lifting up from the archives of the Hebrews, have translated more accurately word for word. The common edition drags the book by knotted ropes of words hither and yon, adding to it things which may have been said or heard at any time. This is as is usual with instruction by schools, when a subject has been taken up, to figure out from the words which someone could have used, which one either suffered injury, or which one caused injury (to the text).\nAnd you, O Paula and Eustochium, since you have both studied to enter the libraries of the Hebrews and also have approved of the battles of the interpreters, holding the Hebrew Book of Esther, look through each word of our translation, so you may be able to understand me also to augmented nothing by adding, but rather with faithful witness to have translated, just as it is found in the Hebrew, the Hebrew history into the Latin language. We are not affected by the praises of men, nor are we afraid of (their) slanders. For to be pleasing to God we do not inwardly fear those caring for the minas of men, \"for God has scattered the bones of those desiring to be pleasing to men\" (Ps 52.6), and according to the Apostle, those like this are \"not able to be servants of Christ\" (Gal 1.10).\n— Prologue to Esther"],"PSA.1.1":["The Psalter is like a great house, which has one outer key on the door, but within, in its various chambers, has its own keys. Although the one broader key of the great door is the Holy Spirit, nevertheless each single chamber has its own little keys. If, then, someone were to throw the keys of the house down in confusion, and wanted to open a chamber, he cannot unless he finds the key: so the individual psalms are like individual little cells, each having its own keys. And so the great door of this house is the first psalm, which begins thus:\n\nBlessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly. Some think that the key of this psalm should be applied to the person of Christ our Lord: so that this blessed man, according to his humanity, is Christ. Good will, indeed, but it is inexperience. For if the blessed man is Christ, and Christ gave the law, how is it now said of Christ, But his will is in the law of the Lord? Then, how is he compared to another tree, and it is said, And he shall be like the tree which is planted beside the running waters? For if he is compared to a tree, and everything that is compared is less than that to which it is compared; then the tree will be greater than the Lord, who is compared to the tree. You see, therefore, that the psalm cannot be interpreted of the person of the Lord, but is spoken generally of the just man. Although many think it was said of Joseph, the one from Arimathea, who did not walk in the counsel of the Jews, and stood not in the way of sinners, and sat not in the seat of the Pharisees: nevertheless what others interpret specifically of him, we interpret generally of the just man.\n\nBlessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly. We read in Genesis that Adam was cursed, when it is said: 'Cursed is the earth in your works.' The first curse of man, therefore, is now dissolved by a blessing. As in the old law one blessing is set down: but in the Gospel eight blessings together are pronounced. Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly. Blessed man — not \"human being,\" but \"man,\" who has come to the perfect man, Christ. Who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly. He has described three general sins of men. For we either think, or do, or teach. First we think; then, when we have thought, we turn the thought into deeds: when we have done something, an increase (auxesis) has now been made, so that we teach others what we ourselves had done. Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly, who did not think evil: and stood not in the way of sinners, who did not do evil: and sat not in the seat of pestilence, that is, who did not even teach others. And sat not in the seat of pestilence. And stood not in the way of sinners. It is difficult for anyone not to sin. Indeed John the evangelist also says that whoever denies that he has committed sin is lying, and is a liar. If, then, we all sin, how does he now say, And stood not in the way of sinners? If we all sin, and no one is blessed except him who has not sinned; then, because we all sin, no one is blessed. But see what he says, And stood not in the way of sinners. He did not say, blessed is the man who has not sinned, but blessed is the man who has not persevered in sin. And stood not in the way of sinners. I sinned yesterday, I am not blessed. If I do not stand in the sin, but withdraw myself, I am now blessed. And sat not in the seat of pestilence. Just as there he said \"stood,\" so here also he said \"sat\": just as there the man who has not persevered in sin is blessed, so here the man who has not persevered in evil teaching and sat, is blessed. What then? You yourselves see how blessedness is granted in three ways: if we do not think evils, if we do not persevere in sin, if we do not teach the things that are evil. This is what the prophet also says: 'For three and four sins I will not turn her back, says the Lord.' And he says this eight times. Now Amos speaks this, that is: you thought, I pardoned: you did it, I pardoned: you did not do penance, I pardoned: were you obliged even to teach evils? This is what is said, For three and four sins I will not be angry with you, says the Lord.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.1.2":["And in his law he shall meditate day and night. He named three things which he ought not to do: not to walk in the counsel of the ungodly, and not to stand in the way of sinners, and not to sit in the seat of pestilence. He named three things which we ought not to do: he names two things which we ought to do. For it is not enough for us to flee evils, unless we attain to goods. But his will is in the law of the Lord. He did not say \"fear,\" but \"will\": for many act out of fear, but the fear of the one acting has no reward. But his will is in the law of the Lord, that he may wish to do what the Lord has commanded. And in his law he shall meditate day and night. Speech cannot unfold how much the mind conceives. But his will is in the law of the Lord. Someone may say: Behold, I wish to do the law of God, therefore I am blessed because I wish it. But see what follows: it is not enough to will the law of God, but to meditate in his law day and night. He shall meditate day and night. Someone may say: This human nature does not bear; for one must walk, and drink, and eat, and sleep, and the things necessary for life must be procured. How then shall he meditate on the law of God day and night, especially since the apostle says: 'Praying without ceasing'? Can I pray at the time when I am sleeping? Meditation on the law, then, is not in reading, but in doing. Indeed it is said in another place: 'Whether you eat, or drink, or whatever you do, do all in the name of the Lord.' If I hold out alms, I meditate on the law of God: if I visit the sick, my feet meditate on the law of God: if I do the things that are commanded, what others meditate with the mouth, I meditate with the body. The Jews, therefore, meditate with the mouth: our meditation is work. He named, then, three things which we ought not to do, he named two things which we ought to do: he who has done this, of what reward is he worthy?\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.1.3":["And he shall be like the tree which is planted beside the running waters, which shall give its fruit in its season, and its leaf shall not fall off. Many interpret it simply, and say thus: Just as a tree, if it is planted next to the waters, must needs be green, and is not dried up, because its roots have moisture whereby they live: so whoever meditates on the law of God, the meditation on the law itself makes greenness and life for him. This they interpret simply: but we, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, and reading that in paradise was planted the tree of life, and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, and that this tree of life was planted in paradise, and that from paradise itself goes forth a fountain which is divided into four heads... We read also in Solomon, if it please anyone to receive the book, for there he speaks of wisdom (Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God): therefore where Solomon says 'It is a tree of life to all who lay hold of her,' there it is said of wisdom. But if wisdom is the tree of life, and wisdom itself is Christ: you see, then, that he who is the blessed and holy man is compared to this tree, that is, to wisdom. You see, then, that this just and blessed man, who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly, who did not do those things, and did these things, shall be like the tree which is planted beside the running waters, that is, he shall be like Christ; since indeed 'he has made us sit together in the heavens, and reign together.' You see, then, that we shall reign with Christ in heaven. You see that this tree is planted in paradise, and we all are planted together with him.\n\nAnd he shall be like the tree which is planted beside the running waters. For from that fountain proceed all the rivers. Which shall give its fruit in its season. This tree does not give fruit at every time, but in its season. This tree does not give in the present age, but in the future, that is, on the day of judgment. This tree now has flowers, this tree now buds, and promises fruits to come. This tree has two things: it has both fruits, and it has leaves. The fruit has the meaning in the Scriptures; the leaves, the simple words. The fruit is in the meaning, the leaves indeed are in the words. Whoever, then, reads the holy Scriptures, if he reads according to the Jews, he understands only the leaves; but if he understands spiritually, he takes the fruit. And its leaf shall not fall off. Nevertheless the leaves also of this tree are useful. Indeed if anyone understands even according to the history, he has profit for his soul. We read in the Apocalypse of John (that the book is not received in these provinces, yet we ought to know that in all the West, and in the other Phoenician provinces, and in Egypt, the book is received, and is ecclesiastical: for the ancient ecclesiastical men also, among whom are Irenaeus, and Polycarp, and Dionysius, and the other Roman interpreters, among whom is also holy Cyprian, receive the book and interpret it) — we read there, then: 'And behold,' he says, 'I saw a throne set, and one lamb, and a tree,' he says, 'beside the river, and on each part of the bank the tree itself': that is, it was on this part, and it was on that part. 'And it had,' he says, 'fruits, that tree, and in the year, in each single month, it brought forth fruit twelve times. And it had,' he says, 'leaves, and its leaves were for the healing of the nations.' I saw, he says, one throne set. For although we believe in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and there is a Trinity, nevertheless the kingdom is one. I saw, he says, one throne set. And I saw, he says, in the sight of the throne one lamb standing. It is said of the Savior's assumption of a body. 'Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world.' And there went forth, he says, a fountain from beneath the midst of the throne. You see, then, that from the midst of the throne goes forth a fountain of graces. Nevertheless that fountain does not go forth from the throne, unless the lamb has stood over against it: for unless we believe the incorporation of Christ, we do not receive these graces. And a tree, he says, one lofty tree was set. He did not say trees, but one tree. If it is one tree, how is it on this part of the bank, and on that part of the bank? For if he had said, I saw trees, it could be that some were on one part, and others on another. But now one tree is said to be on each part. One river goes forth from the throne of God, that is the grace of the Holy Spirit; and this grace of the Holy Spirit is in the holy Scriptures, that is, in that river of the Scriptures. Nevertheless that river has two banks, both the old and the new testament; and on each part the planted tree is Christ. This tree, then, in the year, that is, in each single month, brought forth fruit twelve times. For we cannot receive fruits from that tree, except through the apostles. If anyone, then, comes to the tree through the apostles, he must needs receive fruits, reap fruit from the holy Scriptures, that is, the divine meaning dwelling in the letter. If anyone, then, comes to that tree through the apostles, as we have said, he takes its fruit; but if anyone has not been able to, he is still weaker, and is not yet a disciple, but is of the crowd, but is from outside, is of the nations. This man, because he cannot take the fruit, let him take the words, that is, the leaves for the healing of the nations; for it is written, And its leaves for the healing of the nations. If anyone is still of the nations, if anyone is not a disciple, and is still of the crowd, let this man receive its leaves, that is, let him receive the simple words as a medicine. Indeed it is written, And its leaves for the healing of the nations, that is, for medicine. Why have we said all this, and digressed to the Apocalypse? Because it is written, Which shall give its fruit in its season, and its leaf shall not fall off, and all things whatsoever he shall do shall prosper.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.1.4":["We have spoken of the blessedness of the just man, we have spoken of his reward, that he who has not done three things, and has done two things, is compared to the tree of paradise, that is, to Christ the wisdom. We have spoken, then, of the holy man; now on the contrary let us see what is said of the sinner and of the ungodly. Not so the ungodly. All those kinds of rewards, which we have said of the just, the ungodly shall not receive. Not so the ungodly. He did not say, not so the sinners: for if he had said \"of sinners,\" we were all estranged. Not so the ungodly. Between the ungodly and the sinner there is this difference: he who is ungodly denies God; he who is a sinner confesses and sins. Not so the ungodly. In some copies it is read a second time, Not so the ungodly, not so: but let us know that in the Hebrew it is set down once. We have spoken of the holy man and of his comparison, And he shall be like the tree which is planted beside the running waters. Now on the contrary it is said of the ungodly. Just as the just man is compared to a tree; so the ungodly is compared to dust. He who is just is compared to the tree of paradise; but he who is ungodly is compared to dust, which the wind casts forth from the face of the earth. Dust, although it is of the earth, nevertheless has ceased to be earth. But like the dust which the wind casts forth from the face of the earth. See what is said: so wretched will the ungodly be, that he is not even earthly dust. He seems indeed not to have substance, but he has his own substance. He has nothing solid; but what he has, he has for punishment. He is scattered hither and thither, he is never in one place; wherever the wind has dragged him, thither his rush is directed: so also the ungodly man who has once denied God, wherever the breeze of the devil has dragged him, thither he is led by error.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.1.5":["Since we have said what the just man is, to whom he is compared; what the ungodly man is, to whom he is compared, and we have spoken of the present age: now we ought to learn of the future and eternal. Therefore the ungodly do not rise again in judgment, nor sinners in the counsel of the just. We read in the Gospel according to John: 'Whoever believes in me shall not be judged; whoever does not believe in me is already judged.' Who then is to be judged, if both he who believes shall not be judged, and he who does not believe is already judged? Who shall be judged on the day of judgment? Let us seek, then, a middle one between the believer and the unbeliever, who is to be judged. He who believes shall not be judged. He who believes does not sin, he who truly believes does not sin, he who has true faith does not sin. Indeed when we sin, our mind wavers in faith; at that time when we sin, when we grow angry, when we disparage, when we commit murders, when we fornicate, where is faith? Therefore what he says, He who believes in me, shall not be judged; for he has no need to be judged, he is already blessed. But he who does not believe, is already judged; for he is already prejudged unto punishment. Who then is judged? He who believes indeed, but is overcome by sins: he who has good things, has also evil things: he who at the time when he believes does well, and at the time when he does not believe does ill. Let us see then now what is said. Therefore the ungodly do not rise again in judgment. They do not rise again to be judged, because they are already judged: For he who does not believe in me, is already judged. Nor sinners in the counsel of the just. He did not say, sinners do not rise again; but, they do not rise again in the counsel of the just. They do not deserve to rise again with those who are not to be judged. If they believed in me, they would rise again with those who are not to be judged.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.1.6":["For the Lord knows the way of the just, and the way of the ungodly shall perish. For the Lord knows the way of the just. And what is so great, if the Lord knows the way of the just? But God is said to know those whom he deigns to know. Indeed it is said of sinners: 'Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you.' The apostle says: 'If anyone knows, he is known.' For the Lord knows the way of the just. He who is a sinner, the Lord does not know him; he who is just, is known by God. We read in Genesis, at the time when Adam transgressed, at the time when he listened more to the serpent than to God, when he hid himself from the face of God, God came after the meal into paradise, and was walking about. See what is said. God seeks Adam, not at midday, but toward evening; for now Adam had lost the sun, because he had no midday. Toward evening, that is δείλινον, evening (deilinon), and he was walking. For God does not stand still for the sinner, but walks. For he knew that he was in paradise, and he was not ignorant of what had been done; but because Adam had sinned, God does not know him. And God said: Adam, where are you? We have spoken of this, how the sinner is not known by God: on the contrary we ought to know how the just man is known by God. God says to Abraham: 'Go forth from your land and from your kindred.' He came into Palestine, was in Abramium, was in Gerara a long time. When a son, Isaac, had been born to him, and he had received the promise 'That in your seed all the nations shall be blessed,' he took Isaac, and offered him to God, and a voice from heaven was heard, Spare your son. And immediately it is written there, at the time when he had offered his son, what does God say to Abraham? 'Now I have known that you fear the Lord your God.' You who had spoken for so long a time, have you now come to know Abraham? But because great faith had been shown in offering his son, therefore now for the first time God began to know him. Why have we said all this? Because it is written, For the Lord knows the way of the just. Let us speak also in another way. The way and the life and the truth is Christ: let us walk, then, in Christ, and God the Father knows our way.\n\nAnd the way of the ungodly shall perish. He did not say, the ungodly shall perish; but if they do penance, they too shall be saved. At the time when the apostle Paul persecuted Christ and his churches, he was ungodly. For if the ungodly shall perish, no place is given for penance. But he did not say, and the ungodly shall perish; but, the way of the ungodly shall perish: that is, the ungodliness shall perish, the ungodly man shall not perish, but the ungodliness itself; the man who was ungodly shall not perish, but, the man doing penance, the ungodliness shall perish.\n\nMay God, then, grant us this, that we do not do the three things, and that we do the two: that we may be compared to the tree of life, that we may not be ungodly, who are compared to dust: that we may not be sinners, since sinners do not rise again in the counsel of the just, so that the evil way may perish: and let us bless God, to whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1"],"PSA.5.1":["The fifth psalm is headed with this title: Unto the end, for her who obtains the inheritance, a psalm of David. Many think that the titles do not belong to the psalms, and by what reasoning they judge this, they do not know. If indeed these titles were not found in the Hebrew and Greek and Latin volumes, they would rightly think so; but now, since they are found in the Hebrew books — and especially this title which is set at the head of the fifth psalm — I am astonished that they should wish to say this, that there is anything in Scripture without a reason. For if 'one iota and one tittle shall not pass from the law,' how much more such words or even syllables?\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Unto the end, for her who obtains the inheritance, a psalm of David. Our inheritance is not promised at the beginning, but at the end of the world. The Jews thought they had obtained it at the beginning; we obtain it at the end. Whence the apostle John also says: 'Little children, it is the last hour.' Therefore in the last hour there is the end; at the end, the inheritance. Unto the end, for her who obtains the inheritance. Who is this who obtains the inheritance? It seems to me that it is the Church; for she is the one who obtains the inheritance. A psalm of David. He sings at the beginning what the Church receives at the end. Therefore the fifth psalm is spoken in the person of the Church. Others interpret it in many ways: many speak according to the history concerning the people of Israel, that it wished to return from Babylon into Judea. This they interpret: they have not interpreted Unto the end, nor For her who obtains the inheritance. We therefore, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, shall strive by your prayers to expound this psalm in the person of the Church.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Give ear to my words, O Lord. No one has this confidence except the Church. He who is a sinner has not dared to say, Give ear to my words, O Lord. He who is angry and curses has not dared to say, Give ear to my words, O Lord; but he wishes rather that God would close His ears.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Understand my cry. A cry in the Scriptures is not of the voice, but of the heart. Thus to Moses it is said, 'Why criest thou to me?' though Moses had not cried out before. Understand my cry. And the apostle: 'Crying in our hearts, Abba, Father.' Surely he who cries out does not cry in the heart, but cries with the tongue. And how does the apostle say, Crying in our hearts? When therefore our groaning and our conscience make supplication, God understands that cry. Whence Jeremiah also says: 'Let not the pupil of my eye be silent.' See what he says, Let not the pupil of my eye be silent. And sometimes the pupil of the eye cries out to God. Surely if it cries, the tongue cries, not the pupil of the eye. But just as we cry in our hearts when we entreat the Lord with groaning, so when we pour out tears to God, at that time the pupil of our eye cries out to the Lord.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.2":["My King and my God. He has truly dared to say, My King and my God, in whom sin does not reign in his mortal body. My King and my God. Because Thou reignest in me, and sin does not reign, therefore Thou art my God. Thou art my God, because the belly is not my god, because gold is not my god, because lust is not my god. Since Thou art virtue, and I desire to have virtues, therefore Thou art my God, that is, my virtue.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.3":["In the morning Thou shalt hear my voice. Some interpret it more simply, thus: I rise up at dawn to pray, and I entreat Thee. This interpretation too is valid. What then? Does He hear us in the morning, but not hear us in the evening? Will God not hear us in the middle of the night? But see what he says: As long as I am in the darkness of error, Thou dost not hear me; but when the sun of righteousness has come into my heart, then Thou hearest me. In the morning Thou shalt hear my voice. He did not say, at the third hour, he did not say, at the sixth hour, he did not say, in full light; but see what he says: In the morning, as soon as the darkness has begun to flee, Thou hearest; immediately, at the beginning of a good work, Thou hearest me, Thou dost not require the finishing of it. For to will is mine, to bring to completion is Thine.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","In the morning I shall stand before Thee, and I shall see. See the order. In the morning, not in the evening, not in darkness, but in the morning. In the morning I shall stand before Thee: I shall stand firmly, and I shall imitate Moses. In the morning I shall stand before Thee: before Thee I shall stand, not before another. But when it is morning, and I have stood, and I have stood before Thee, then I shall deserve to see Thee.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","And I shall see. Understood: I shall see Thee. Many read it badly, and say καὶ ἐπόψῃ με ('and Thou shalt look upon me'), but let us know that this is an error: it is rather said καὶ ἐπόψομαι, that is, 'I shall see,' so that the order may be as we have said: as the light of the virtues begins to rise in my soul, when I have begun to stand before Thee, not to sit, not to lie, but to stand, and Thou hast set my steps upon the rock, then through those steps I shall deserve to see Thee.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.4":["For Thou art not a God that willest iniquity. Therefore Thou wilt not hear me in full light, but in the morning; because Thou dost not will iniquity, O God. For when I have begun to withdraw from iniquity, immediately I shall deserve to be heard by Thee.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Neither shall the malignant one dwell beside Thee. When someone who is evil withdraws from us, let us rejoice: this happens in us which happens also in the Lord. For see what the Holy Spirit says. Neither shall the malignant one dwell beside Thee. See what he says, the malignant one shall not dwell beside Thee. 'For what communion has light with darkness? what agreement has Christ with Belial?' When therefore some withdraw from us, let us also say: 'They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us.' It is said to God: The malignant one shall not dwell beside Thee. For if God is fire, and a consuming fire, whoever is stubble, whoever is wood, flees from the fire, lest he be consumed by the fire.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.5":["Neither shall the unjust remain before Thine eyes. When it is said, Neither shall they remain, it is shown that at one time they did remain. For the unjust did at one time remain before the eyes of God, they remained while God awaited their repentance; but since they remained unjust, therefore they did not remain before the eyes of God.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Thou hatest all who work iniquity. Unhappy is he whom God hates. Who is that one whom God hates? He who works iniquity. If therefore we all sin, and whoever sins is held in hatred by God, then we are held in hatred by the Lord. But if we are held in hatred by the Lord, how have we been saved by grace? Thou hatest all who work iniquity. He did not say, who have worked iniquity; but, who work iniquity. Those who persevere in sin, they are held in hatred by the Lord: but those who have forsaken it, they are loved by the Lord. Thou hatest all who work iniquity. This is said of sinners, who persevere in their sins. Let us see what else follows.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.6":["Thou wilt destroy all who speak a lie. I see a new thing. He who works evil is held in hatred; he who lies, perishes. Let us see which is more, to be held in hatred by the Lord, or to perish. He who is held in hatred by God is indeed unhappy, because he is held in hatred by the Lord: but he still lives. But he who speaks a lie and perishes, has already ceased to be. Therefore he who speaks a lie is more unhappy than he who works iniquity: for he who works iniquity is held in hatred; but he who speaks a lie, perishes. 'For the mouth that lies kills the soul.' And how is it written, 'That God may be truthful, but every man a liar'? But if whoever speaks a lie already perishes, and kills his own soul, and yet we all lie, then shall we all perish? Therefore, from what he says here, Thou wilt destroy all who speak a lie, and from what follows in the psalm, and from the very order, we can understand that he is speaking of heretics. For he who works iniquity kills only his own soul; but he who is a heretic, and speaks a lie, kills as many men as he has led astray. Thou wilt destroy all who speak a lie. Let us see who these are who speak a lie.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","The man of blood and the deceitful the Lord shall abhor. Every heretic is a man of blood; for daily he sheds the blood of souls. The man of blood and the deceitful. He rightly said, the deceitful: he is both a murderer, and deceitful. Why is he deceitful? He falsely fashions his own words into the words of the Lord. The man of blood and the deceitful the Lord shall abhor. See what a heretic is: the Lord shall abhor him. Of sinners he says, Thou hatest all who work iniquity; of false teachers, The Lord shall abhor them.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.7":["But I, in the multitude of Thy mercy, will enter into Thy house. They in their iniquity go out from Thy house: I in Thy mercy enter into Thy house. I will enter into Thy house, which is the Church. I will worship toward Thy holy temple in Thy fear. That is the house which is also the temple.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.8":["Lord, lead me in Thy righteousness because of my enemies. I indeed desire to enter into Thy house, and I wish to enter by Thy straight way: but since my enemies set stumbling-blocks continuously along the paths, and lay snares in the road for me who desire to go into Thy house; therefore I ask Thee, that Thou wouldst direct my feet even unto the end in Thy way. It is mine to set my feet in Thy way: it is Thine to correct my steps. Because of my enemies direct Thy way in my sight: that is, make Thy way straight because of my enemies, who lie in wait for me. But what is this way? The reading of the Scriptures, that is, that I may not fall in Thy Scriptures, through which I desire to enter into Thy Church. For everyone who understands the Scriptures badly falls in the way of God.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.9":["For there is no truth in their mouth. The heretics are described; the man of blood and deceit is described, whom the Lord shall abhor. There is no truth in their mouth: they do not have Christ the truth in their mouth, because neither do they have Him in their heart. Their heart is vain. It reads better in the Hebrew: 'Their heart is ἐπίβουλον (treacherous).' For the heart of the heretics truly lies in wait for all whom it deceives. Their throat is an open sepulchre. The heretics are unhappy, who are whitewashed sepulchres, full of the bones of the dead. Their throat is an open sepulchre. Arius, Eunomius, and the other heretics have tongues for arrows, they have their jaws for a sepulchre. Their throat is an open sepulchre. Excellently he said, open: for whenever some deceived person wishes to enter, the heretic is ready, and receives him. Never do the heretics close their mouth, but they always open it. Their throat is an open sepulchre. With their tongues they acted deceitfully. One thing they held in the heart, another thing they promised in speech: they spoke piety, and concealed impiety: they speak of Christ, and hide the Antichrist: for they know that no one can be led on, if they bring forth the Antichrist. They bring forth light, in order to conceal the darkness, in order through the light to lead to the darkness. With their tongues they acted deceitfully.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.10":["Judge them, O God. Let them know that Thou art the judge, let them know that Thou dost regard human affairs: Thou who art to judge at the end, judge also in the present: Thou who art to judge in general, judge now in part: let those who are unwilling to feel Thee as Father feel Thee as judge. Judge them, O God. Why? for what cause? It follows: Let them fall from their thoughts. For if in the present Thou wilt render to them what they deserve, they will begin to withdraw from their evil thoughts. As long as they do not feel Thee as judge, they do not fear. Let them fall from their thoughts. Excellently he said, from their thoughts: for daily the heretics change their doctrines. In short, if a man of the Church, learned in the Scriptures, contends against them, and refutes them from the holy Scriptures, immediately they seek out some other new doctrine to devise. They do not seek how they may be saved, but how they may prevail. Therefore he said now, Let them fall from their thoughts: let them fall from their many thoughts, and let them have Thee as their one thought.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","According to the multitude of their impieties cast them out. He did not say from where He should cast them out: whether He should cast them out from heaven, whether He should cast them out from the earth: from where He should cast them out, the Scripture does not say; but it is a ὑπερβατόν (a transposition) and a ὕστερον πρότερον (a putting of the latter first). We ought therefore to read, According to the multitude of their impieties cast them out. What he says is this: cast them out from their many impieties for the sake of Thy mercy, that is, let them not be in their many impieties. It can also be interpreted otherwise: since they have been most impious, and have been unwilling to fall from their thoughts, and heap impieties upon impieties, and daily gather them, and have more impieties, therefore cast them out. From where? From her who obtains the inheritance, that is, the Church, which obtains Thy inheritance. Do Thou cast them out from there, so that, since they do not have the faith of the Church, they may not dwell in the Church either. For they have provoked Thee, O Lord. Thou, O Lord, by nature art sweet; but whoever are sinners and heretics turn the nature of Thy sweetness into bitterness by their own vice.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.11":["And let all rejoice who hope in Thee. Let those perish, let those of the Church rejoice. They shall exult forever. Here, forever, we ought not to speak of the present age, but of the age to come. In short, he did not say, they exult in this age; but, they shall exult, it is said of the future. For here there is sadness, but there is joy there. The heretics laugh in the present, but they shall mourn in the future: we, however, mourn in the present, that we may laugh in the future. They shall exult forever. What will be the exultation of the holy men? And Thou shalt dwell in them. Happy will they be, who shall deserve to have Christ as their guest. And Thou shalt dwell in them. Happy are they who shall be the tabernacle of Christ. And all who love Thy name shall glory in Thee. Whoever loves the Lord shall glory in the Lord. See what he says. All who love Thy name shall glory in Thee. Who love: he did not say, who fear, but, who love. For wherever there is fear, there is no love: 'for perfect love casts out fear.' For whoever love the name of the Lord, they shall glory. They shall glory: our blessedness is in the future; all that is promised is of the future. Let others reign, let others possess, let others glory: we are here wretched, that afterward we may be happy. Let us follow Christ our Lord. He who says that he believes in Christ, let him also walk as Christ walked. Christ the Son of God came not to be ministered unto, but to minister: He came not to command, but to serve: He came not that His feet should be washed, but that He might wash the feet of the disciples: He came not to strike, but to be struck: He gave not a blow, but received one; He crucified not, but was crucified: He killed not others, but Himself suffered: He was poor, that He might make us rich: He received the stroke for us, that we, being scourged, might not feel pain. As often as we are struck, let us offer the cheek, let us present the back, let us imitate Christ. He who is struck imitates Christ: he who strikes, the Antichrist. Why do I say all this? That in the present age we may glorify the name of Christ for what we endure for Christ's sake. While we speak, while we walk, we are suddenly snatched away. I myself, who speak to you today, know not what shall become of me tomorrow. If a slight little fever comes, where is that voice? where is the pride? suddenly it is turned into ash. 'Why doth earth and ash boast?' We are earth and ash. 'Earth thou art, and unto earth thou shalt go.' And earth boasts against its Maker. It follows: For Thou wilt bless the just. Thou wilt bless, in the future: therefore blessedness is deferred.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.5.12":["Lord, Thou hast crowned us as with a shield of good will. In the world one thing is the shield, another the crown; but with God He Himself is the shield, He Himself is also the crown. As a shield He protects, as God He crowns: He Himself is our shield, He Himself is also our crown. And this is what he says, Lord, Thou hast crowned us as with a shield of Thy good will: Thou hast protected us with Thy shield, and Thy protection was our crown, that is, our crown was Thy shield, it was our victory. For the crown is interpreted as victory.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Let us give thanks to God, and let us entreat Him, that in His good will He Himself may be our shield and our crown, that we may never withdraw from Him, that we may follow Him, and may say with Jeremiah: 'And following after Thee I have not grown weary.' To whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5"],"PSA.6.1":["'For her that obtaineth the inheritance' — since in the fifth psalm we discussed, interpreting that the psalm had been sung in the person of the Church, which has obtained the inheritance of Christ. But the sixth pertains properly to the resurrection of the Lord, which is the eighth day. For we have both a first day and an eighth day. For on the eighth day we received the kingdom of heaven. The eighth day is after the sabbath, the first at the beginning. It would take long, if we now wished to speak about the number eight: how there are eight beatitudes in Matthew; how in the hundred and eighteenth psalm each letter has eight verses; how there are fifteen psalms of degrees, how they are seven and eight; how Ecclesiastes too says, 'Give a portion to seven, and give a portion to eight'; how the eastern gate in Ezekiel has seven steps and eight steps. It would take long to tell how David too was the eighth of the sons of Jesse, who received the kingdom. This is what is now said, 'For the octave': for on the eighth we received the kingdom of heaven. Whence he says: 'O Lord, rebuke me not in thy fury, neither chastise me in thy wrath.' But since we cannot interpret both psalms equally together, it is enough for us to have discussed the title of the sixth psalm.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.1":["Names are imposed on individual things, so that from the names the things themselves may be known: so too the psalms have been prefaced with titles, so that from the titles the psalms themselves may be understood. The fifth psalm is prefaced with this title: 'Unto the end, for her that obtaineth the inheritance.' The sixth psalm is prefaced with this title: 'Unto the end, in hymns, for the octave, a psalm of David.' In the fifth it is said, 'For her that obtaineth the inheritance'; in the sixth it is said, 'For the octave.' But in the seventh — since it too was sung under the alleluia, since on that other Lord's day the sixth psalm was read, and we were unable, on account of illness, to interpret it — but now the seventh psalm has been read. It has, moreover, a title of this sort: 'A psalm of David, which he sang to the Lord for the words of Chusi the son of Jemini.' See the mysteries. Have these things, then, been written without a cause? In the fifth it is said, 'For her that obtaineth the inheritance'; in the sixth it is said, 'For the octave'; in the seventh it is said, 'For the words of Chusi the son of Jemini.' You see, then, how great are the mysteries woven together here.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7","Now let us come to the beginning of the seventh, which begins thus: 'A psalm of David, which he sang to the Lord for the words of Chusi the son of Jemini.' I know that many men, and certainly most learned ones, have interpreted this title in this way. At that time, they say, when Absalom rose up against David his father (I beg you to listen more patiently: for we wish to interpret the Scriptures, not to declaim) — at that time, therefore, when Absalom rose up against David, one of David's friends, by name Chusi, wished to go with David, but David sent him back to Absalom, that he might scatter the counsels of Ahithophel. At the time when Ahithophel gave counsel that Absalom should at once pursue David unprepared and kill him, Chusi then gave counsel and scattered the counsel of Ahithophel. And this Chusi himself sent a messenger to David, saying: Thus and thus is Absalom making ready; and David began to flee and to prepare himself. At the time when Chusi sent the words to David, David sang this psalm to the Lord, because the counsels of Ahithophel had been scattered. This they say, and indeed they have expounded it reasonably. But let us read the Scriptures carefully: for there it is written thus, that this Chusi is the son of Arachi. Then that Chusi is written with a different letter in Kingdoms: there Chusi is written with SAMECH, but here Chusi is written with the letter SIN. Then that Chusi is the son of Arachi; whence many of the Greeks, not knowing, read: 'Chusi the friend of David.' It is not so in the Hebrew, but it stands written thus: 'Chusi the son of Arachi, the friend of David.' You see, then, that the Greeks err even in the reading.\n\nIt is therefore most manifest, according to the rule of the Scriptures, that that Chusi is the son of Arachi. But this Chusi is called Jemini. Since we said that that Chusi was the son of Arachi, who is this Jemini? And we ought, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, to find the rule of this name. Where do we find 'Jemini' in the Scriptures? It is doubtful to no one that there is one tribe of the twelve, by name Benjamin. Now Benjamin is interpreted 'son of the right hand.' For at the time when Rachel died in Ephrata (we see the tomb with our own eyes, we do not seek examples elsewhere); at that time, therefore, when Rachel was dying, 'She bore,' it says, 'an infant, and the women — or she herself who was dying — called his name Benoni, that is, son of my sorrow.' But on the contrary Jacob the father turned the mother's sorrow into glory, and does not call him Benoni, that is, son of my sorrow, but Benjamin, that is, son of the right hand, that is, of my strength. Benjamin, therefore, is a name composed of two parts, of 'son' and of 'right hand': for BEN means 'son,' JAMIN means 'right hand.' Let us know, therefore, that the tribe of Benjamin is always called Jemini. We read in the book of Kingdoms, at the time when it is written concerning Saul: 'And behold,' it says, 'a man of the tribe of Benjamin, by name Saul; the son,' it says, 'of Cis, son of Abiel, son of Jethra, son of Jether, son of Gera, son of Jemini.' And immediately there he is called a Jeminean man, that is, of the Jeminean tribe, that is, of the tribe of Benjamin. Let us give yet another testimony, lest we be able to doubt. That Shimei, who on the Mount of Olives at the time when David was fleeing his son Absalom — Scripture says this: 'And behold,' it says, 'Shimei of the kindred of Saul, of the tribe of Benjamin. And he threw,' it says, 'stones at David, and said: Get out, get out, thou man of blood.' At the time when one of his captains, by name Abishai, says, 'I will go,' he says, 'and kill him,' what does David say to him? 'What is there between me and you, sons of Zeruiah? Behold,' he says, 'he who came forth from my loins fights against me: and do you marvel that the son of Jemini, that is, of the tribe of Benjamin, of the tribe of Saul, who was my enemy?' Why have I said all this? To show that Jemini is the tribe of Benjamin.\n\nWe have said who Benjamin is, we have said the name of the father: let us see who Chusi the son of Jemini is. Chus in the Hebrew tongue is interpreted 'Ethiopian,' that is, black and dark, who has such a soul as he has a body, of whom Jeremiah says: 'If the Ethiopian will change his skin, or the leopard his spots.' So this Chusi signifies his black and Ethiopian enemy, Saul. And he says this: As the Ethiopian cannot change his skin, so this man too cannot change his character. Twice he came into my hands, I could have killed him. Whose blood I could have shed, I wished rather to overcome him by kindness; but his malice cannot be conquered. For just as the Ethiopian's skin cannot be changed, so too his malice cannot be changed. Finally, what does he say? 'O Lord my God, if I have done this' — that is, if I have done him any evil. 'If there be iniquity in my hands, if I have rendered evils to them that repaid me.' See what he says: I rendered good things for evils, he evil things for good things. It is manifest, therefore, that the psalm is against Saul, and he gives thanks to God, and prays for his help, that he may not fall into the hands of Saul.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7","'O Lord my God, in thee have I hoped.' I have not hoped in my sword, I have not hoped in my strength: but I have hoped in thy help.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.2":["'Lest at any time he seize my soul like a lion.' This fits Saul, it does not fit Absalom. For how could he call Absalom a lion, of whom he says, 'Spare my boy Absalom'? If there he calls him a boy, and as it were a little child and one that knows nothing, how here does he proclaim him a lion? This, so far, according to the history. And there are many things that might be said, and the whole psalm can be interpreted historically in the person of Saul. But since it is our concern to interpret not only according to the history, but according to the spiritual understanding, we interpret this Chusi the Ethiopian as none other than the devil. It is asked how this Chusi is the son of Jemini, how the devil, an Ethiopian, is the son of the right hand. That he is an Ethiopian, is by his own fault; that he is the son of the right hand, is because he was created by God. Rightly is it said of him: 'Lest at any time he seize my soul like a lion.' 'Our adversary the devil, like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may seize.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.3":["'O Lord my God, if I have done this.' 'Behold, the prince of this world comes, and finds nothing in me.' 'If there be iniquity in my hands.' 'Who did no sin, neither was guile found in his mouth.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.4":["'If I have rendered evils to them that repaid me.' They say, 'Crucify, crucify such a one'; I say, 'Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.5":["'Let the enemy pursue my soul, and take it.' 'The last enemy, death, is destroyed.' 'And let him tread down my life into the earth.' Life cannot be trodden down. 'And let him bring down my glory into the dust.' 'If,' he says, 'I have rendered evils to my enemies for evils, may I not rise again.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.6-7":["'Arise, O Lord my God, in the precept which thou hast commanded, and a congregation of peoples shall surround thee.' This we say, this believing we speak. For the persons are always changing in the psalms. It would be a great matter now, if we wished to say in the psalms into how many and how great persons they change. 'Arise, O Lord my God, in the precept which thou hast commanded.' This we say: For us thou hast suffered, for us thou hast been crucified; arise and save us. We seem to do violence to the Scripture; but the things that follow free us from this reproach. For what follows? 'And a congregation of peoples shall surround thee.' Therefore arise, that a very great multitude may believe in thee. For when thou hast arisen, what else do we pray? Return to the Father. 'And for her sake return on high.' For whose sake? For the congregation of peoples. That thou didst die, thou didst die for us; that thou didst rise again, thou didst rise for us; that thou didst ascend to the Father, ascend for us. 'And for her sake return on high.' 'No man ascends into heaven, save he that descended from heaven, the Son of man who is in heaven.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.6":["'Arise, O Lord, in thy wrath.' Since they did not perceive thee through kindnesses, let them perceive thee through anger. 'Be exalted in the borders of my enemies.' He prays for his enemies, that he may be exalted in the borders of his enemies. For when they have ceased to be enemies, then thou, O Lord, wilt be exalted in them.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.8":["'Judge me, O Lord, according to my justice, and according to my innocence upon me.' This David cannot say; this fits properly the Saviour, who did not sin.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.9":["'Let the wickedness of sinners be consummated, and thou shalt direct the just.' My justice cannot be directed, unless their iniquity — namely, of the Jews — be fulfilled.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.12-13":["For all the sinners of the people shall perish by the sword, not, surely, by a bodily sword — since there are many and various ways to death besides the sword —, but by a spiritual sword, with which those are to be struck who have not repented, and whom the psalmist threatens, crying out: Unless you are converted, he will brandish his sword; he has bent his bow and made it ready, and in it he has prepared the vessels of death.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.7.12-15":["'Unless ye be converted, he will brandish his sword.' This passage admits a twofold understanding. For many think it is said of God, so that, for example's sake, it is said to sinners. And what does it say? 'Unless ye be converted, he will brandish his sword': that is, unless ye do penance, the Lord will strike you with his sword. But let us see whether the things that follow can be fitted to the person of the Lord. 'He hath bent his bow, and made it ready.' This can be said of God. 'And in it he hath prepared the vessels of death.' This is difficult, for the Lord does not have vessels of death, but vessels of life; but this too can somehow be understood of the person of the Saviour. But that which follows, 'Behold, he hath been in labour with injustice, and hath conceived sorrow and brought forth iniquity, he hath opened a pit and dug it out,' cannot at all be taken of God. We are compelled, therefore, by necessity, on account of the things that follow which are harsher and more arrogant, not to understand of God, 'Unless ye be converted, he will brandish his sword.'\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.12":["And so many say that this is said of the devil; that is, unless ye be converted, unless ye do penance, ye will be in his power. 'He hath bent his bow, and made it ready.' The devil always has his bow ready, he shoots arrows with which to strike us.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.13":["'And in it he hath prepared the vessels of death.' They fit the person of the devil. 'He hath made his arrows for them that burn.' Beautifully he said, 'for them that burn': for those whose hearts burn with lust and passions, these have been conquered by the devil. 'He hath made his arrows for them that burn.' Beautifully he said 'for them that burn'; he did not say 'for those about to burn,' that is, those who are about to burn with his arrows; but whosoever's hearts he sees burning, those he strikes. The Apostle too says that the devil has burning arrows.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.14":["'Behold, he hath been in labour with injustice: he hath conceived sorrow, and brought forth iniquity.' Such a mother, such a father is the devil: thus he conceives, thus he is in labour, thus he nurses, thus he exalts.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.15":["'He hath opened a pit and dug it out, and he is fallen into the ditch which he made.' The pit is the devil's own, which is also the ditch. 'He hath opened a pit': but since it cannot hold water, according to Jeremiah, therefore his pit has been turned into a ditch. 'He hath opened a pit, and dug it out.' All the conventicles of heretics are ditches of the devil.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.16":["'His sorrow shall be turned upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown.' Just as if someone throws a stone on high, and is not careful, and the stone comes down upon his head, he is wounded by his own wound; so too the devil is slain by his own pride, he exalts himself and falls in ruin. 'His sorrow shall be turned upon his own head.' The devil now wishes to lift up his head, but cannot. Why can he not lift up his head? Because his sorrow is turned upon his head, and presses him down. The devil (understand, O mystical reader, according to the rule of the Scriptures) is read in Genesis to be himself the snake, himself the serpent in paradise, himself the serpent who spoke to Eve, who reigns on the earth. He is called a snake, because he clings wholly to the earth. Other animals, though they walk on the earth, yet do not have their whole body lying on the earth; though they have feet on the earth, yet they have another part of the body upward. But the devil, that is, the serpent and the old snake, according to Isaiah, is wholly on the earth: he has both his head and the rest of his body level with the ground. Why do I say all this? Because other animals (understand, O prudent reader), though they cling to the earth, yet desire to withdraw from the earth: but the devil has his head and his tail and his whole middle part clinging to the earth. 'His sorrow shall be turned upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown.' See what he says: both upon his head, and upon the crown of the head. But since iniquity presses both his head and his crown, where is the devil's repentance? This is the devil's end: 'His sorrow shall be turned upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown.' Beautifully he said, 'shall come down': for the everlasting punishment will come to him from heaven.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.7.17":["He shall be condemned in this way. But we who have believed in Christ, who received the inheritance in the fifth psalm; who in the octave, that is, on the day of resurrection, say, 'O Lord, rebuke me not in thy fury, neither chastise me in thy wrath'; since Chus, that is, our Ethiopian, is himself the lion of whom it was said, 'Lest at any time he seize my soul like a lion'; who bent his bow and made it ready, and in it prepared the vessels of death that he might slay us; since he conceived sorrow and brought forth iniquity, opened a pit and dug it out, and fell into the ditch which he made, and was cast into gehenna; therefore his sorrow shall be turned upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown: since he has been thus punished according to his desert, let us say, 'I will confess to the Lord according to his justice, and I will sing to the name of the Lord most high.' Beautifully he said, 'most high': for as much as the devil has been humbled, so much the Lord has been exalted.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"PSA.8.3":["We can also say this: that those converted from idolatry, having deserted their ancient error, see new heavens and a new earth, by no means reckoning the elements to be gods, nor the things that are born of the earth. Now we confess that heaven and earth are the works of the hands of God: but at that time we venerated servants and the creature with the homage due to God; and David, perceiving this, sings in the psalm: I shall see the heavens, the works of your fingers (Ps 8:4): not that at that time, when he said this, he did not see the heavens; but that through the several increases of virtues, and the knowledge of things to come, he sees as new those which he already saw as old.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.9.1":["Joshua the son of Nun was fighting in the desert, and as Moses prayed he kept prevailing: the one man fought, the other by his prayer conquered. So too do I, according to that which is written, 'Open your mouth, and I will fill it': I indeed seem to be the one speaking, but there are others by whose prayers I speak. The ninth psalm, which has been sung by you to the Lord, is great in its verses, great in its mysteries. Since, then, we cannot discuss everything, it is enough for the moment to speak about the title. Since we cannot enter into the house (for great is the king's palace, nor are we able to know all the mysteries of the king), for the moment, as it were standing at the title of the house, in the very vestibule, let us point out whose house it is.\n\n'Unto the end, for the hidden things of the son, a psalm of David.' The phrase 'unto the end' does not pertain to the Jews, but to us who at the end have believed. They were the earlier son, we the latest. We therefore who are at the end ought to hear this psalm, because it pertains to those who are at the end. 'For the hidden things of the son.' What are these hidden things of the son? The mystery which throughout all past ages had been hidden has, at the end, been opened to us. 'For the hidden things of the son.' In the Hebrew it reads otherwise. For in the Hebrew it has ALAMOTH, which is translated 'for death': for AL is said for 'for,' MOTH is said for 'death.' Therefore it is said 'for death'; therefore 'for the death of the son' is what is written in the Hebrew. See, then, the Seventy translators: since they were translating for Ptolemy, a Gentile king, and it was harsh to say 'the death of the son,' they translated it 'the hidden thing.' They did not say 'for the death of the son': for they knew that it was being spoken of the Son of God. They saw that it was so written; and yet they were afraid to say that the Son of God would die.\n\nThis much, for the moment, has been briefly discussed from the psalm, as to what was said, 'For the hidden things of the son.' And this was said in the Old Testament; for in the Old Testament it is spoken of the son in a hidden way. Now in the Gospel let us read openly of the open things of the son.\n— Homily 4, on Psalm 9"],"PSA.9.13-14":["We often read in the Scriptures of double gates, of death and of life, of vices and of virtues, as in that place in the psalms: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may announce all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion. There is no doubt that he signifies the Church: the lofty Mount Zion, and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and the multitude of angels, and the Church of the firstborn, who are written in heaven. He who has been lifted up in the gates of Zion will not be able to fear the gates of death, of which the Lord spoke to Peter: Upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.9.15":["We ought to understand the whole psalm as being about the death of the Savior. Finally, that you may know it is spoken of the Savior, it is said of the Jews: 'In this snare which they stretched out, their own foot has been caught.'\n— Homily 4, on Psalm 9"],"PSA.9.17":["We ought to understand the whole psalm as being about the death of the Savior. Concerning the calling of the Gentiles: 'All the nations that forget God.'\n— Homily 4, on Psalm 9"],"PSA.10.8-10":["And lastly, against the devil: 'God is not before his eyes, he sits in ambush with the rich.' Unhappy the rich, with whom the devil sits. Finally, see what is said of the devil: 'He sits in ambush with the rich.' For whoever is rich, unless he has stripped the poor man, cannot be rich: for this reason he said, 'He sits in ambush with the rich.' Whenever the rich persecute Christians, let us say: 'The devil sits in ambush with the rich.' He lies in wait to seize the poor man, to seize the poor man while he draws him in: 'in his snare he will humble him.' This is the impious man, this is the devil. The poor man not only in riches, but also in spirit: 'for blessed are the poor in spirit.' 'In his snare he will humble.' Whom? The poor man is understood. See what is said. 'He will humble'; not, 'he will slay': but when he has humbled the poor man, the humbling of the poor man is the ruin of the devil. For what follows? 'He will bow himself down and fall, while he has dominion over the poor.' Does not the devil seem to you, in persecution, when he would kill Christians, when he would slay the martyrs, to be slain in the very act of slaying? 'He will bow himself down and fall, while he shall have dominion over the poor.' The martyr who is slain goes to heaven; that slayer is humbled and falls.\n— Homily 4, on Psalm 9"],"PSA.11.1":["What the phrase \"unto the end\" signifies — for which Symmachus rendered ἐπινίκιον (a song of victory), while Aquila and the sixth edition rendered τῷ νικοποιῷ (to the giver of victory) — has been discussed more fully above. Now let us return to the order of the tenth psalm: a number which is both an end and a beginning, in which every reckoning is rolled around, and to which the decalogue also — that is, the first law — is owed. The just man too, on account of the Lord's money doubled in the Gospel, received cities of this number to govern. And, to bring in something rather curious: from this the name of Jesus, that is, of the Savior, begins, and that ineffable name of four letters among the Hebrews takes its start from here, being written with ioth, he, vau, he; into which, through its tens, the hundredth number unrolls, and again the thousandth grows through ten hundreds, and as far as human reason can proceed, always this number is both an end and a beginning, according to that sense: \"I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end.\"\n\nIn the Lord I trust; how do you say to my soul: Flee away to the mountains like a sparrow? Those who eat the raw flesh of the lamb and follow the letter that kills would have this psalm understood of the person of David, when he fled from the face of Saul and was compelled to dwell in the deserts of Judah. And they say that it is spoken by him thus: Although Saul and all his army do not permit me to dwell in my homeland, and I flit or run to and fro, here and there, wandering and roving after the manner of birds and beasts, yet I have God as my protector, in whom trusting I shall not fear your snares. And in truth, if only the beginning be read, their exposition does not appear frivolous. But what follows — \"For they have destroyed the things which you have perfected\" — and the rest down to the end does not agree with this exposition. Therefore the spiritual man, who judges all things and is himself judged by no one, says that this psalm was sung by our Lord and Savior according to a twofold understanding: either he was speaking to the angels who ministered to him, or to the adverse powers, which desired to mock and terrify him. If to the angels, this is the sense: Why do you exhort me to return to heavenly things, and, leaving behind earthly and lowly things, to dwell in my mountains, because of the snares of the devil and all his army, which you behold stirred up against me? Why do you wish that I take up the wings of my deity, and desert human converse, and dwell secure in the heavens, when I have come for this purpose: that in the Lord — that is, having confidence in my own divinity — I may overcome my exulting adversaries? But if his speech is to the adverse powers, it is to be understood thus: In vain do you think that I am to be terrified by your voices and by the wedge of battle drawn up against me, since I have come for this purpose: that I may fight, and by fighting overcome — as is also shown by the very title of the psalm, according to Aquila and Symmachus. Furthermore, another (since this psalm has nothing so special that it cannot also be understood under the person of any just man) will indeed accept the holy intention of those who refer the psalm to Christ, but will also assert that the whole context can be understood from the person of the prophet — that is, of the just man. In the Lord I trust; how do you say to my soul: Flee away to the mountains like a sparrow? The crafty adversary both tempted the Lord and Savior in the wilderness, and wishes all the saints to depart from the land of Judah and to dwell in the desert of the virtues, that there he may more easily strike them down. But even his very counsel is deceitful: for he does not exhort that one take the wings of the dove — a bird gentle, simple, and domestic, said to lack gall, and which is offered for the Lord in the temple — but the wings of a sparrow, a bird chattering and roving, which after the rearing of its young knows nothing of the fidelity of wedlock; although Aquila and Symmachus rendered \"bird\" generally in place of \"sparrow.\" By the mountains, however, we can understand those of which it is said in another place, \"Draw near to the eternal mountains,\" and in the second psalm of the steps, \"I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains from whence help shall come to me\": and to which, after the abomination of desolation shall stand in the holy place, we ought to migrate.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.2":["For behold, the sinners have bent the bow, they have prepared their arrows in the quiver, that they may shoot in the dark the upright of heart. For which it is written in the Hebrew, \"For behold, the impious have bent the bow, they have set the arrow upon the string, that they may strike in the dark the upright of heart.\" Therefore, he says, you wish me to migrate to the mountains, because the sinners, or the impious, burning, have prepared arrows, which must be repelled with the shield of faith. And in their quivers they have darts to strike the just, not in the day, not in the faintly-lit night, when the darkness is tempered by the solace of the moon, but when the night is full, and the darkness is palpable — that is, ψηλαφητὸν σκότος (touchable darkness, psēlaphēton skotos). Know that for this very reason I do not heed your counsels: because the adversaries are prepared for battle, and desire to wound not so much me, whom they cannot overcome, as my just ones; and since I have come from heaven for their salvation, I ought not to leave them orphans. For behold, as you say, they have set their arrow upon the string, and they wish to capture my boys, the sons of my ripening youth, my little young men, with the sweet enticements of pleasures. For all the strength of the devil is in the loin: for which cause also, when Jacob wrestled with me in the struggle, I made his γλαφυρόν (sinew, glaphyron) wither — that is, dry up. Since, therefore, they wish to strike the upright of heart in the darkness, let my just man speak and say: \"Your word is a lamp to my feet, O Lord, and a light to my paths.\" But I, standing in the battle-line and placed in array, do not forsake the contest once begun, especially when you say that they have bent the bow, prepared arrows in the quiver, wish to wound my saints in the darkness, and yet have not yet been able to strike. For they have bent the bow, they have prepared arrows in the quiver, that they may shoot in the dark the upright of heart. Since, then, they are thus prepared, I will set myself in the midst, I will receive their arrows, that my servants may not grieve at my grief, may be healed by my wounds, may not suffer while I suffer, and may say: \"O Lord, you have crowned us as with a shield of your good will.\" But it can also be understood otherwise: that the heretical or adverse powers, through the dogmas of the heretics, have aimed their blasphemies, and desire to deceive my just ones with varied fraud. For they are archers who, through the occasion of their darkness — which they understand as hidden mysteries — wish to strike the upright hearts of the just. Therefore I and my saints must migrate to the sublime testimonies of the Scriptures, as it is also written in the Gospel: \"If he strike you on the right cheek, offer him the other also.\" He does not say, offer him the left, but the other — that is, the other right — because the just man has nothing left-handed in himself. If, he says, he confounds you in one testimony of the Scriptures, do you overcome him from another, and fulfill that which is written in another psalm: \"Deep calls to deep at the voice of your cataracts\": so that each Testament may be a defense to the other in turn, and in such a way the snare of the heretics may be broken by the opposing of the other right hand, and by migration to the mountains, and by the calling of deep to deep.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.3":["For they have destroyed the things which you have perfected. And this too those who above still speak either to any just man or to the Lord: Therefore you must return to the heavenly things, because both the adversaries are so bravely prepared for the fight, and whatever you build up, they demolish. For daily they undermine your precepts and the law written in the hearts of believers, and by persuading diverse things they overturn them. But what did the just man do? This is understood in various ways. Either concerning the person of the just man, in this manner: While they strive to destroy the laws of God, the just man has done nothing worthy of ruin, and in vain suffers their snares; or certainly, while they oppose and attempt to destroy God's commandments, the just man does this one thing only: that he implore the help of him who dwells in his holy temple, whose throne is in heaven, and whose eyes look upon the poor man, that he may rescue him from the snares prepared. But concerning the person of God in this manner: Although the adversaries are prepared for battle, and lie in wait in secret like a lion in his den, that in hidden places they may kill the innocent and destroy the temple of God, yet the Lord dwells in his holy temple, and never is either temple or throne wanting to him, because as some fall others rise.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.4":["Whence in what follows it is written: The Lord is in his holy temple, the Lord's seat is in heaven. By the seat — that is, the throne of God — let us, according to the custom of the Scriptures, understand the kingdom, as in that place: \"God shall give him the throne of David his father.\" And elsewhere: \"With justice is the throne of dominion prepared.\" And: \"When a just king shall sit upon the throne.\" The Lord is in his holy temple, the Lord's seat is in heaven. Before the bodily temple was built by Solomon, the prophet knows the spiritual temple of the Lord and his throne in heaven. Whereupon it is asked, how it is said elsewhere, \"Heaven is my throne.\" When the throne of the Lord is in heaven, his throne is encompassed by heaven, and the throne of the Lord is in a part of heaven; when heaven is the throne of the Lord, the whole heaven is turned into the throne of the Lord. From which, according to the ἀναγωγήν (higher spiritual sense, anagōgē), the Lord dwelling in his holy temple — that is, in the soul of the believer — dwells either in a part of it or in the whole. When we are still imperfect, and there is in us both something good and something evil, God dwells only in a part of our soul, that is, in heaven. But when we are consummate and perfect, we are wholly turned into a dwelling-place of God, and we are made heaven, his throne. But the temple of God, properly, is understood either as our Lord and Savior, who says, \"I am in the Father and the Father in me\": or as that man who was assumed by the Savior, or generally the Church, or specially each one of the saints. His eyes look upon the poor man, his eyelids question the sons of men. Against those who understand the wrath and fury of God simply, let us set forth the eyelids and eyes of the Lord. For if they understand those things ἀνθρωποπαθῶς (after the manner of human passions, anthrōpopathōs), they will be compelled to take these things likewise. But if it is absurd to place eyes and eyelids in the almighty Lord, they will equally be compelled to seek some spiritual interpretation for wrath also and fury. For it is exceedingly unfitting not to understand both those and these alike, either spiritually or carnally. Whence in this place it is to be perceived that the just man does not dread the assault of the adversaries, having God as his helper, who beholds and sees all things, and does not permit his poor man to be deceived by their frauds and wounded by their darts. The poor man: that one of whom it is said above, \"To you the poor man is left.\" And at the same time it is to be observed, that God opens his eyes, bringing an open judgment, to the lowly with help, though he be one and poor, and always watches over him with his regard. But where he is judge, and those who are judged are sons of men (for they do not deserve to be called sons of God), there he does not look with his eyes, but examines with his eyelids, in a manner half-closing them and dissembling, and unwilling to know what he knows. \"For if you, Lord, will mark iniquity, who shall endure?\" Varro relates that eyelids (palpebrae) are so called from the fact that they move and quiver (palpitent), that is, ὀνοματοποιῶς (by onomatopoeia, onomatopoiōs).\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.5":["The Lord questions the just and the impious: but he who loves iniquity hates his own soul. For which it is better written in the Hebrew: \"The Lord tests the just, but the impious and the one who loves iniquity — his soul hates him.\" So that the sense is joined with the foregoing: In vain are the adversaries prepared, that they may shoot in the dark the upright of heart, and so on; because the Lord is a just judge, and nothing can be hidden from him, and he does both: that he both prove his just ones by tempting them, and hate those who are impious. The impious and the one who loves iniquity — his soul hates him: that is, he hates them with his whole heart and his whole soul. But let us understand \"soul\" in accordance with the eyes and eyelids, according to that: \"My soul hates your new moons and feast days and sabbaths.\" Furthermore, according to those who say, with the Septuagint, The Lord questions the just and the impious, the sense is far different: that he himself is the judge of the just and the impious, and that the impious are to be judged in this, that by loving iniquity they hate their own soul. And in a certain way the last part of a syllogism is knit together, which is called the conclusion. For although a man may deny that he hates his own soul, yet from the fact that he loves iniquity, he is convicted of hating it. As Solomon also says in the Proverbs: \"He who rejects discipline hates himself.\" But if he who loves iniquity hates his own soul, contrariwise he who loves justice loves his own soul, according to that: \"He who possesses prudence loves himself.\" Whence also the Savior says: \"He who loves me keeps my commandments.\" For he himself is justice. Nor indeed is it to be thought, according to the Septuagint interpreters, that something contrary is now said — The Lord questions the just and the impious — to that which is written in the first psalm, \"Therefore the impious shall not rise again in the judgment\": because just as there is a difference among the just, so also among the impious; and just as the just, differing among themselves, possess mansions, so also the impious who are perfect in malice and iniquity and who fight with their whole mind against the Lord shall not rise again in the judgment; but other impious ones, who are sometimes understood as sinners, because they are of lesser malice, are reserved for God's judgment.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.6":["He shall rain snares upon sinners. There is no doubt but that the Lord does this even for the sake of the sinners themselves, so that those who were badly abusing their liberty, being ensnared in the Lord's snares, may walk in the right path as if under his bridle, and may be able to advance through him who says, \"I am the way.\" Whence also fishermen are sent, and hunters, in Jeremiah, that they may hold with their nets the fish that wander and toss about in the depths, and may capture unto salvation the beasts straying about on all the mountains and hills. Therefore it is not, as many suppose, punishments that are promised to sinners from this little verse and from those that follow, but remedies. Fire, brimstone, and the spirit of storms is the portion of their cup. It is said to Babylon: \"You have coals of fire, you shall sit upon them, and they shall be for a help to you.\" The brimstone rouses the fire of these coals into a flame; which flame is fanned by the rushing wind, by the whirlwind of the tempest, so that whatever is filthy in our gold, and whatever — according to the prophecy of Ezekiel — consuming the silver in us, the bronze, the tin, and the lead, may reduce us into gold. Whence also it is written in Isaiah: \"The Lord shall wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion, and shall cleanse the blood from their midst by the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning.\" And in Malachi: \"Behold, the Lord enters in like the fire of the smelting-furnace and like the herb of the fullers, and he shall sit smelting his people like gold and silver.\" The cup of sinners is full of fire, brimstone, and the spirit of tempest. \"A cup of pure wine full of mixture,\" from which \"all the sinners of the earth shall drink.\" I judge that from this cup of fire and brimstone Sodom had drunk, and therefore it is prophesied concerning her through Ezekiel: \"Sodom shall be restored to her former state.\" And in the Gospel: \"It shall be more tolerable for the land of the Sodomites.\" \"For the Lord will not take vengeance twice upon the same thing.\" Whence also Lazarus, who had received his sins in his lifetime, and had drunk the cup of the Lord's fury, in the bosom of Abraham drinks that cup of which it is written: \"I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.\" Jerusalem too had drunk the cup of punishments for her remedy; whence it is said to her: \"Rise up, rise up, Jerusalem, you who have drunk the cup of ruin, the goblet of fury.\" And Jeremiah received this same cup of pure wine from the hand of the Lord, that he might give it to Jerusalem to drink, and to all the nations round about; who, drinking, vomit, and fall down, and, their pride broken, are laid low in repentance.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.11.7":["For the Lord is just and has loved justices; his countenance has beheld equity. For which the Hebrew has: \"For the Lord is just and loves justice; their faces shall behold equity.\" The Lord, he says, is just for the causes stated above: because he both, leaving the just man in the present age to temptations, does not punish but proves him, and his soul hates the impious man who loves injustice. For it is one thing to sin overcome by the necessity of the flesh, another to pass over into the disposition of the sinner. Whence also such men, whose heart is impenitent, the soul of God hates. And because he rains snares upon sinners, and gives them to drink the goblet of torments which they deserve, God himself, being just, loves justices. For just as there is one true God, even if there are many gods who are so called by participation in him, and there is one only-begotten Son of God, while others are called so by adoption; so also there is one true justice, as is written in the beginning of the Proverbs, but from communication of it the Lord loves many things called justices. But in that he says, His countenance — that is, God's — has beheld equity, so that the sense is, He judges the things that are right; in the Hebrew, according to that understanding, it is said: Those very ones who shall be judged, both the just and the impious and sinners, shall behold the justice of God, and their faces shall contemplate the equity of the Lord, and they shall say, \"You are just, O Lord, and your judgment is right.\"\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.15.1":["The fourteenth psalm has been read at just the right moment; and it has come about in due order in such a way that it almost seems to have been read on purpose. The psalm was read in order: I think it has been brought about by God's dispensation that what was profitable to you should be recited today in the sequence of exposition. The fourteenth psalm has been read, which is titled A Psalm of David. But our David is Christ, as we have often said. Then we read in Exodus that on the fourteenth day the lamb is sacrificed. It is sacrificed on the fourteenth day, when the moon is full, when nothing is lacking to it of its light. See, therefore, that Christ is not sacrificed except in perfect and full light. Since, then, on the fourteenth day the lamb is to be sacrificed for you, now the prophet marvels, and asks:\n\nLord, who shall dwell in your tabernacle? You, who wish to dwell in the Lord's tabernacle. Or who shall rest on your holy mountain? See what is said. Who shall dwell in your tabernacle, or who shall rest on your holy mountain? Not first on the mountain, and afterward in the tabernacle; but first in the tabernacle, and so then on the mountain. A tabernacle is not a firm house, a tabernacle has no foundation; but it is moved this way and that, and it migrates along with the one who journeys on. That is why it is called παροικία (a sojourning), not a dwelling. Lord, who shall dwell in your tabernacle? Because it is a tabernacle, therefore it is called παροικία (a sojourning). Let us see, then, what the tabernacle is, and what the mountain is. We have said that the tabernacle has no foundation, but is an uncertain house: whereas a firm house has foundations. The tabernacle seems to me to be the Church of this world. The churches you now see are tabernacles: for we do not remain here as dwellers, but we are going to migrate elsewhere. For if 'the fashion of this world passes away'; and it is said in another place that 'heaven and earth shall pass away': if heaven and earth shall pass away, how much more now also the very stones of the churches, which we see? Now, therefore, the churches are called tabernacles, since we are going to migrate from these to the holy mountain of God. Who is this holy mountain of the Lord? It is said in Ezekiel against the prince of Tyre: 'You have been wounded,' he says, 'from the mountain of the Lord.' And who shall rest on your holy mountain?\n\nSince, then, we are going to migrate from the tabernacles to the mountain, we ought to learn who these are who are going to migrate to the holy mountain of God. This that he said, Who shall dwell in your tabernacle, or who shall rest on your holy mountain, belongs to one who is asking. What, then, the prophet asked, the Holy Spirit now answers. And what does he say to him? Do you wish to know, O prophet, who shall dwell in my tabernacle, or who shall rest on my holy mountain? Hear what follows: if you do these things which follow, you shall dwell on my holy mountain. You, therefore, who wish to dwell in the tabernacle, and to ascend the holy mountain of God, need not listen to my words. Hear what the Lord answered the prophet; do this that the Lord commanded, and you shall ascend the holy mountain of God.\n— Homily 5, on Psalm 14"],"PSA.15.2":["He who enters without stain, and works righteousness. That is why it is also said in the one hundred and eighteenth psalm: 'Blessed are the undefiled in the way.' At once, at the very beginning, it is said, Blessed are the undefiled in the way. As it is said there, undefiled in the way, so here too it is said, He who enters without stain. And he who enters is in the way. He who enters without stain. See what is commanded. He did not say, he who arrives at the end without stain; but he who is still on the journey, and has no stain.\n\nSomeone could say: I have no stain, I have done no evil. It is not enough for us not to do evil, unless we also do good. Accordingly there follows, And works righteousness. See what he says, And works righteousness. He did not say, works chastity; he did not say, works wisdom, works fortitude. And these indeed are excellent virtues. For example, wisdom profits us, fortitude profits us, so that we may resist persecutions; then temperance and chastity profit us, that we may not lose our souls. Righteousness alone is a great virtue, and the mother of all. Let someone say: How is righteousness greater than the other virtues? The other virtues delight the one who has them: righteousness does not delight the one who has it, but others. If I am wise, wisdom delights me; if I am brave, my fortitude delights me; if I am chaste, my chastity is my joy: but righteousness does not profit the one who has it, but the other wretched ones who do not have it. Suppose some poor man has a quarrel with my brother; suppose my brother is powerful, and oppresses another — that is, one who is a stranger to me, a poor and wretched man — through his power: what does my wisdom profit the poor man? what does my fortitude profit the poor man? what does my chastity profit the poor man? Righteousness profits him, because I do not accept the person of my brother, but I judge for the truth. Righteousness does not know a brother, does not know a father, does not know a mother; it knows the truth, it does not accept the person, it imitates God. That is why he said And works righteousness, lest he should seem to have excluded the other virtues. He who is angry for another's relief, who does not delight in the miseries of others, this man is righteous.\n\nLet us speak also of the other things that follow. He who speaks the truth in his heart. Many speak the truth with their lips, but not in their heart; who seem to speak truly, but the heart does not agree with the lips.\n— Homily 5, on Psalm 14"],"PSA.15.3":["He who has practiced no deceit with his tongue: he who has uttered in speech what he has pondered in his mind. Nor has done evil to his neighbor. Some think that a neighbor is a brother, or a fellow townsman, or a kinsman, or a blood relation. But our Lord teaches in the Gospel in that parable, where a certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho: a priest passed by, a Levite passed by, and had no pity: a Samaritan passed by, and had pity. And afterward the Lord asks, and says: Which of these was a neighbor? At once it is answered: He who did well. And the Lord adds: Go and do you likewise. All men, therefore, are neighbors to us, and we ought to do evil to none. But if we understand neighbors to be brothers and kinsmen, then are we permitted to do evil to strangers? But far be it to believe so. We are all men neighbors to men: for we have one Father. And has not accepted reproach against his neighbors. A great thing is said. Never, he says, has a neighbor murmured against him; never, he says, has he found any occasion to disparage him. This virtue exceeds the human condition, it is God's grace.\n— Homily 5, on Psalm 14"],"PSA.15.4":["In his sight the malignant man is brought to nothing. See what he says. In his sight the malignant man is brought to nothing. Even if he is an emperor, even if he is a prefect, even if he is a bishop, even if he is a presbyter (for in the church these are the dignities), whoever is evil is counted for nothing in the sight of the holy man. And at once there follows, But he glorifies those who fear the Lord. This holy man, who enters without stain, who despises the powerful who are evil, if he sees anyone fearing God, though he be poor, yet him he glorifies. He who swears to his neighbor, and does not deceive. And here too we ought to understand \"neighbor\" as above.\n— Homily 5, on Psalm 14"],"PSA.15.5":["He who has not given his money at usury. There are many things that might be said, but the hour shuts us out. But since the day before yesterday we spoke about the beginning of the catechesis (κατήχησις), and, with God's favor, you came out of Chaldaea with Abraham, and you remember what we had said: how you came out of Chaldaea, and came into the land of promise — nay rather, the land of promise is promised to you. And indeed, after Abraham came into the land of promise, adversaries were on this side and that, enemies held the land. The Lord came, and led him outside, and set him on a mountain, and showed him the whole land, and said: 'All this I will give to you and to your seed.' To him he promised it, to us he will give it in return.\n— Homily 5, on Psalm 14"],"PSA.16.1":["An inscription of a title, to David himself. In place of this the fifth edition put the Hebrew word itself, \"MACTHAM David,\" which Aquila translated as \"of the humble and simple David,\" and Symmachus as \"of the humble and unstained David.\" And Aquila himself again, in the fifty-fifth psalm and in the rest as far as the fifty-ninth, rendered this very thing so as to say \"of the humble and perfect David.\" From this we understand that one word signifies both \"simple\" and \"unstained\" and \"perfect,\" yet in such a way that humility is always set before each of them; for among the Hebrews it is compounded of two whole words, so that, just as we say \"almighty\" (omnipotens), enclosing two words in one utterance, so also the word MACTHAM in the syllable which is called MAC shows humility without ambiguity, while in the following one which is called THAM it signifies the three things we mentioned above. Now there are six psalms, unless we are mistaken, which are headed by this title: the fifteenth psalm, and the fifty-fifth, and the fifty-sixth, and the fifty-seventh, and the fifty-eighth, and the fifty-ninth. All of these refer to the dispensation and passion of our Lord and Savior; and although in their headings they have among themselves certain things added, or removed, or varied, yet in this one phrase they agree, namely, in \"the inscription of a title,\" which here stands alone by itself only in the fifteenth psalm. For in the remaining five certain historical notices too are prefixed, so that the prophecy concerning the Lord Savior might be shown not only in word but also in deed. And I think there are six psalms containing this mystery and headed with such a title for this reason, that the Savior, crucified for all on the sixth day of the world, dying and rising again, left us this as it were the title of his victory; so that we may inscribe upon our hearts these psalms which are marked with these headings, and press into them the words of God, and that our minds may be mindful of the Lord's death; so that, just as Jacob made a monument for Rachel and it remains to this day, so we may mark in our heart the memorial and remembrance of his passion. Nor indeed let that be thought contrary to our exposition which the Lord commanded in the Law: \"You shall not make for yourself a monument, things which the Lord your God hates\"; but to the singular number \"a monument\" he joined a plural of the neuter gender, saying, \"things which the Lord your God hates,\" that is, you shall not do such works as remain for your condemnation forever. For these things the Lord your God hates. If perchance, through our negligence, he who oversows tares should erect such monuments in the land of promise, the clear-sighted ones (dioratikoi), namely the teachers, command the children of Israel to overthrow them and undermine them and banish them from the land that flows with milk and honey. Such a monument Absalom too built for himself, an empty tomb, which remains to this day for the disgrace and testimony of the parricide. Into such a monument Lot's wife too was turned, because she wished to look not at what lay ahead but behind her back. After this it is asked why the Seventy translators wished to translate otherwise than it stands in the Hebrew and in the other editions. This can be understood from many other passages too, but especially from the heading of the ninth psalm. For whereas among the Hebrews it is headed LAMANASSE ALMUTH LABEN, which Symmachus translates \"the triumph of the youth of the son,\" but the fifth edition \"the triumph of the flowering youth of the son,\" the Seventy translators, unwilling to disclose so openly to King Ptolemy and to the Gentiles the passion and resurrection of the Savior, translated \"Unto the end, for the hidden things of the son\"; and by the very title showing that certain hidden things lay concealed which they were unwilling to translate. So now too, because humility, simplicity, spotlessness, and perfection pointed not to a man (for there is no one who would dare to head himself with such a title) but to the Son of God, therefore they chose to translate \"stelographia\" (stēlographian), that is, a thing worthy to be marked with an inscription; so that we might understand one thing shut up in the title, and another uttered aloud in the word. Now a title is also placed for brave men upon their tombs in memory of their former courage, and for those who fell for their fatherland, and wherever there are trophies and victories, and where public laws are inscribed. The interpretation of all of which is easier when applied to the person of the Lord Savior.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15","Preserve me, O Lord, for I have hoped in you; I said to the Lord: You are my God, for you have no need of my goods. If one apostle were to interpret this psalm, if two, the authority even of one and of two would be great. But now (a thing written of no other of the psalms) we have found concerning this in the Acts of the Apostles that Peter, together with the apostles of Christ—when Matthias had already been taken in as one of the twelve in place of Judas—expounding it lifted up his voice, and after many words said: \"Men and brethren, let it be permitted to speak boldly to you concerning the patriarch David, that he both died and was buried, and his tomb is among us to this very day. Therefore, being a prophet, and knowing that God had sworn to him with an oath that from the fruit of his loins one would sit upon his throne, foreseeing this he spoke of the resurrection of Christ, that he was neither left in hell, nor did his flesh see corruption. This Jesus God has raised up, of which we all are witnesses.\" Since then all the apostles alike have referred this psalm to the Lord Savior, we, who wish to be Christians, must surely follow their opinion. And no wonder that he who was equal to God and humbled himself, taking the form of a servant, now speaks with the words of a servant. Hence in the very title he is inscribed as humble and simple and unstained and perfect; because he who needed no one's help humbled himself so far as to entreat the Lord on our behalf, on behalf of the Church, that is, on behalf of his own body. And in very truth it is great humility—for God to pray for men, for the Lord to pray for servants, for the Holy One to pray for sinners: so that he not only says that he himself is clothed and given drink and visited in his servants, but even, when they have prayed and have been heard, confesses that he himself has prayed and been heard. Therefore, taking on a human body, he saw the hostile powers, he saw too the army of the devil, drawn up in a wedge-formation against him, doing battle; and therefore he now says: Preserve me, O Lord; for unless you preserve my body, the Church which hopes in you, they who guard her keep watch in vain.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.2":["But what follows—\"I said to the Lord, You are my God\"—he directs his speech to the apostles and to the disciples and to us, if indeed we deserve to be disciples, teaching what he has said to the Lord of the universe. What, then, does he say he has spoken to the Lord? \"You are my Lord\": that is, you who are the Lord of all have become peculiarly mine, that is, the Lord of my own. He was the Lord of all even in the Old Testament, since he had created all things; but he became peculiarly the Lord of the saints, saying: \"I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob.\" As, then, you are theirs, so you are now especially my Lord, because you deign to be the Lord of my own. And the reason why he is his Lord is added: For you have no need of my goods. For God almighty has no need of a man's goods, nor do our virtues contribute to God's advancement, since God receives no increase: but whatever we labor at and strive for, he receives and requires it of us for this reason, that he may restore to us what he has received. For that man too in the Gospel who from one mina had made ten minas not only received back what he had given, but was even granted the other mina, which another could not multiply. It is said also to Job: \"What does it profit the Lord, if you make your way blameless?\" Other masters, however powerful they may be, cannot yet say to their servants, \"We have no need of your works\": for indeed they make full use of their service. But God alone is a Lord who needs the service of his servants only for this, that he may have occasion to give more. And at the same time we are taught that if the Savior prays for us (for he did indeed pray also for the apostles in the Gospel, and therefore permitted those whom he had earlier urged to keep watch to sleep), we surely ought all the more to pray for ourselves: because, although we run, yet without him we cannot arrive at our goal, according to the interpretation of Symmachus and of the fifth edition, who said \"My good is not apart from you\"; so that this translation may be referred to that saying: \"Unless the Lord build the house, they labor in vain who build it. Unless the Lord guard the city, he keeps watch in vain who guards it.\"\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.3":["To the saints who are in his land, he made all his wishes wonderful in them. In place of which it is written in the Hebrew: \"To the saints who are in the land, and to my exalted ones, is all my will in them.\" These goods of which you have no need, O Father, my saints do need—the saints in whom I have made all my wishes to be wonderful. But I made my wishes wonderful not outside them, but in them: so that, while each one is corrected, and understands both who he was before and who he has now begun to be, my wonderful wishes may be brought about in them; yet only if, going out from Egypt and crossing the desert, they are able to enter the land of promise, which land is the beginning of the land of the living. Nor is this at once contrary to it—that wonders are said to be done only in the land of God—namely that in the field of Tanis, that is, in Egypt, wonders were done. For there is a great distance between the wonders done by Moses and those done by Jesus. There one flees from Egypt, here one enters into Judea; there lord Pharaoh is avoided, here those who are to serve are taken captive; there frogs and gnats and soot and hail and darkness are employed among the wonders, here command is given to the sun and to the moon; there God goes before the people in a pillar of cloud and of fire, here the Savior himself comes to meet them holding a sword and declaring that he will fight for his army. Do you see how the wonders that were in the land of promise are greater than those done in Egypt or in the desert? But we may understand the land of God as the Church, since to the saints who are in the Church all the wishes of God seem to be wonderful; for to those who are outside the Church nothing seems wonderful which by its own nature is wondrous. Let us therefore be in the land of God, and let us be transformed into the image of the Savior, and he will make all his wishes wonderful in us.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.4":["Their infirmities have been multiplied; afterward they made haste. Whose infirmities have been multiplied? Surely those of the saints who above are recorded as being in the land of the Lord. When they were foolish, unbelieving, straying, and serving various desires and pleasures, passing their lives in greed and envy, their infirmities were being multiplied. But when they were washed and were sanctified and, turned to repentance, abandoned their past sins, then they made haste. I have read in the Epistle of Barnabas (if anyone is pleased to accept testimony from it) that God chose apostles who were more wicked than every sin. And surely, if they were more worthless than all sinners, their infirmities were nonetheless being multiplied in them; but see how quickly they afterward withdrew from them. To Peter and to his brother Andrew it is said: \"Come and follow me.\" Likewise to James and John, who leave both their father and the boat and the net. Matthew rises up from the tax-booth, and at once is an apostle. Zacchaeus comes down from the tree, and is holy. Paul, persecuting the Church, suddenly believes, is washed, teaches, is subjected to persecutions, is beheaded. What is swifter than this conversion? The people of the Gentiles too, before they were converted to the Lord and heard through Isaiah, \"Drink this first, do it quickly,\" was in all infirmities—or, as Symmachus translated, \"hardships,\" or, as more fittingly Theodotion and the fifth edition, \"idols.\" But when the cults of idols had been multiplied, believing at the first word of the apostles, it was quickly converted to the Lord. Which indeed Aquila too, translating \"diaponēmata\" (laborious things), according to his custom signifies as idols. For in the Hebrew it says ASABOTH, the very same word that is placed also there: \"The idols of the Gentiles are silver and gold.\" Further, in that he says \"proikisamenōn,\" which the Seventy had rendered \"they made haste,\" he seems to me to show a lofty meaning: namely that the apostles, or the people of the Gentiles, after they were converted to the faith of Christ, and the Lord had forgiven them their sins (for this is what \"proikisamenōn\" means, that as by a \"proikia\"—that is, as a free gift—their vices were reversed), their \"diaponēmata,\" that is, their pains, were increased in them, since they knew in how great a sea's waters they had been and from what an abyss they had been freed; according to that which is written in Ecclesiastes: \"He who adds knowledge, adds sorrow.\" And Paul: \"I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God.\" But it can also be discussed otherwise, since \"strength is made perfect in weakness\"; and when an apostle is weaker, then he is stronger. We too, if we wish to come quickly to the Lord, ought to multiply our infirmities, so that, made weak in the flesh, we may be stronger in the spirit. I will not gather their assemblies out of bloods. Clearly he who speaks is the Savior. For who else could say, \"I will not gather their assemblies out of bloods\"? But \"their\"—understand it either of the nations or of the apostles. If of the nations, this will be the meaning: Although they are guilty of blood, and have perpetrated many crimes, yet because after their infirmities they hastened to me, their assemblies will be pure and utterly foreign to blood. If of the apostles, this will be the meaning: They will by no means offer me victims as in the old Law, but there will be the spiritual religion of the New Testament. But according to a higher understanding, this must be said: That the Lord will abhor the man of bloods and the deceitful one; and the blood of the wicked is required from the hand of the watchman who has refused to preach the coming wrath of God; and whoever shall scandalize the least, he will slay him; and those whose hands are full of blood—it is certain that they cannot be gathered into the synagogues of the Lord, who must first be purged by the spirit of burning. As for \"the synagogues of the Lord,\" let us understand either the churches in the present, or, as I think better, the various dwellings with the Father in the heavens, which he will not be able to enter who has been sprinkled with the gore of sins. Nor will I be mindful of their names through my lips. The saints, whose infirmities have been multiplied, and whose assemblies I will not gather out of bloods, because they hastened to me—I will by no means call them by their former names, but by new ones: that is, after I have washed them in my fountain, I will not call them, as before, sons of men, but sons of God. Or otherwise: I will change, he says, their names, and out of Simon I will make Peter, because upon his rock my Church is founded; out of the sons of Zebedee, sons of thunder, for I am about to entrust to them the sublime mysteries of my Gospel. And not only to these, but to Abraham too of old and to Sarah were their names changed; nor is it fitting that those whose life is different from their former manner of living should keep the same names. But another will interpret this—\"Nor will I be mindful of their names through my lips\"—thus: I will not name them lightly and as it were in passing, but I will hold them in the depth of my heart, I will keep them in my soul. So that, just as it is said to the unbelieving people, \"This people honors me with its lips, but its heart is far from me,\" so, on the contrary, the Lord names his saints not with the lips only, but holds them with the inmost affection of his mind.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.5":["The Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup: it is you who will restore my inheritance to me. Our Savior in the Scriptures is called both high priest and king. High priest, in this: \"You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.\" King, as in that place: \"But I have been established as king by him over Zion, his holy mountain.\" According, then, to the fact that he is a priest, the Lord is his portion. For it is written in the Law: \"To the sons of Levi you shall not give an inheritance in the midst of the children of Israel, for I am their portion, says the Lord.\" Hence from Jesus they received no portion in the land of Judah, but were dispersed throughout the tribes, that prophecy being fulfilled: \"I will divide them in Jacob, and scatter them in Israel.\" But if the Levites had no portion except the Lord, how much more does this one who is high priest, and who dares to say \"The Father abiding in me does his works,\" have the Father as his special and proper portion. But according to the fact that he is king, it is said to him: \"Ask of me, and I will give you the nations as your inheritance, and the ends of the earth as your possession.\" And note how elegantly the wording is measured in both cases. Of the Father it is said, \"You are the portion of my inheritance.\" \"You are,\" he says, not \"you were\": for the Son always remained in the Father, and the Father in the Son. But of the nations, \"You,\" he says, \"will restore to me my inheritance\": for they ceased to be the Lord's inheritance when their infirmities were multiplied, and their assemblies were being gathered out of bloods. Although some think that the lower verse too should be referred to God the Father, so that the meaning is: Because I emptied myself, taking the form of a servant, and came down from my majesty to the earth, restore to me my inheritance; that is, yourself, in whom I always was, and glorify me with the glory which I had with you before the world was made. For you will not give me any new possession, but you will restore my inheritance, namely the divinity and nature of God, which I seemed not to have for the reason that I had taken the form of a man. Further, in that he says \"and of my cup,\" it is doubtful to no one that in the Scriptures the cup is sometimes taken for a longed-for drink and joy, as in that place, \"How excellent is your inebriating cup,\" and elsewhere, \"I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.\" So that, just as our Savior is our food and drink (for indeed we feed on his flesh and drink of his blood), so of that man who was assumed by the Savior the food and cup is the Father, that is, the one common divinity with God the Father.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.6":["The cords have fallen to me in excellent places: for indeed my inheritance is excellent to me. In place of that one people, I have possessed the whole world as my inheritance; and from the world, not just anyone, but the strong. For this is what \"in excellent places\" means, which in Greek is said \"among the strong\": according to that, \"The Lord is my strength and my refuge and my deliverer,\" and in another place, \"The Lord is my strength and my praise.\" If, then, anyone is excellent and strong, he is of the Lord's inheritance. Hence that Theophilus, \"friend\" or \"lover of God,\" because he was such as to request that a Gospel be written to him, is called \"kratistos,\" that is, excellent, strong: which was more simply translated by the Latins as \"most excellent Theophilus.\" Further, the measuring-cord is used in the division of fields, and wherever possessions are distributed, or a census of earthly revenues is conducted, land is divided, as also in Joshua the son of Nun. An angel holding a measuring-cord is shown to the prophet who is about to build Jerusalem, so that first of all he may measure the extent of the city by reason and order, and thereafter build up what has been measured. And in the canticle of Deuteronomy we read: \"The Lord's portion became his people Jacob, the cord of his inheritance Israel.\" In the second book of Kingdoms too, when David overcame the Moabites, the Scripture records two cords set apart for putting to death, and two for keeping alive. But what it means that of the Moabites some are set apart for death, others for life, Orpah shows, and Ruth: of whom the one, turned back to the idols and to her ancient homeland, is destined for death; the other, following her mother-in-law—whose name means \"sweet\"—says, \"Your people is my people, and your God is my God.\" And so we too, if we wish to be of the Lord's inheritance and of his cord, let us be strong and robust, and let us say: \"I can do all things in Christ who has strengthened me.\" Let there be nothing weak in us, nothing feeble, nothing that the heir would not deign to receive. Since indeed Christ glories in his saints, and says: For indeed my inheritance is excellent to me. Do you wish to know that the Savior's inheritance was excellent? Consider Peter, look at Paul, all the apostles and saints, since indeed they believed throughout the whole world, and you will see the inheritance of Christ, and an excellent one. In place of which Aquila translated: \"For indeed my inheritance is most beautiful to me.\" So that it may be that which is said in the Song of Songs: \"You are wholly beautiful, my love, and there is no spot in you.\" And again: \"Behold, you are fair, my near one, behold, you are fair, your eyes are doves.\" You who are fair and strong, when you have become like him of whom it is sung, \"In your beauty and your comeliness,\" you will hear from the Bridegroom: \"Forget your people and your father's house, and the King will desire your beauty.\"\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.7":["I will bless the Lord who has given me understanding: moreover even until night my reins have rebuked me. He who speaks below, \"My flesh shall rest in hope, for you will not leave my soul in hell, nor will you give your holy one to see corruption,\" consistently now too says: I will bless the Lord who has given me understanding, moreover even until night my reins have rebuked me. For he who is Wisdom, how does he receive understanding? But because \"Jesus advanced in age and wisdom and grace before God and men,\" the Son was instructed not so much by the Father as the man was instructed by his own divinity. Hence too, concerning him who flowered from the root of Jesse, there is the prophet's oracle: \"The Spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him, the Spirit of wisdom and of understanding.\" Which indeed, for beating back the madness of the Arian heresy, Aquila and Theodotion translated better, saying: \"I will bless the Lord my counselor\"; so that the Father did not so much give the Son wisdom and understanding which he did not have before, as join his own counsel to the Son's judgment. So that, just as in Proverbs Wisdom says that she was a counselor to God when he rejoiced in the completed world, and she rejoiced in herself daily; so also that same Wisdom, in the fact that he assumed a human body, that he died and rose again, shows that he was not without the Father's counsel, but according to that saying, \"That I might do your will, my God, I willed it.\" But this too which follows, \"Moreover even until night my reins have instructed me,\" must be taken thus, that we understand \"reins\" for hidden thoughts and deep affection, which is properly an idiom (idiōma) of the Scriptures. For as far as is in my memory, neither in any of the philosophers nor among the rhetoricians, nor among the poets, nor even among the physicians themselves, who promise a knowledge of the nature of bodies, do I recall ever having read that \"reins\" are put for the understanding and for deep thought. But Scripture uses this custom, so that when it wishes to indicate something secret and hidden or mystical, it says, \"God who searches hearts and reins\"; so that, since the heart is secret, the reins are the more secret parts of the heart. In Wisdom too, which is inscribed with the title of Solomon, I have found written: \"God is the witness of his reins, and the true beholder of his heart, and the hearer of his tongue.\" In the book of the Patriarchs too, although it is counted among the apocrypha, I found it thus, that as the gall was created for anger, so the reins for cleverness and for shrewdness. But \"panourgia,\" that is, cleverness, may be taken either in a good or in a bad sense: in a good sense, according to Proverbs, \"That he may give,\" it says, \"shrewdness to the innocent, and to the younger boy sense and understanding\"; in a bad sense, as in the apostle, \"But I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his shrewdness, your minds may be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.\" What he says, then, is this: my instruction and deep thought and the inner affection of the heart was not only with me in the heavenly places, but even while I dwelt in the night of this world and in darkness, it was engaged with me in the man, and instructed me, and was never absent from me, so that whatever the weakness of the flesh could not do, divine thought and power might accomplish. Or at least thus: my reins instructed me, who possess the inner wisdom of divinity, by which I was taught, when the passion was imminent, not to yield to human frailty through the terror of the cross. Moreover, what else does this declare (since the Savior's life is our example) but that he, not only waking but even sleeping, was free from every sin, and remained pure from every phantasm of the flesh: so that the saints too, who desire to be like their Lord and Master, may guard their heart with all watchfulness, and be overcome not only when waking, but not even in sleep—that is, not only by day, but not even by night. This too must be noted, that he said, \"Moreover even until night my reins have rebuked me\": to show that before night came, or before the Savior himself began to be in the night, he was always instructed by his own wisdom. There is another interpretation: because divine Scripture always speaks of God's powers after the likeness of human limbs—for example, when it says he has feet, eyes, ears, hands, and other things of this kind—that here \"reins\" should be taken for the places of the seed; so that, just as the beginning of men and the element of human flesh is founded in the seed, so our judgments too, and the beginnings of our thoughts, have their reins: so that, before we handle anything either in deed or in word, silent thought may hold this in its own movement and consider it within itself. So that the meaning is: our Lord and Savior could not sin—not only in deed and word, but not even in thought or in the beginnings of thoughts, which they call \"propatheiai\" (pre-passions)—he whom the Word of God was daily instructing. Not that God is one and the man another; but so that one and the same Son of Man and Son of God may now, as man, pray, now, as Lord, hear.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.8":["I foresaw the Lord in my sight always: for he is at my right hand, that I may not be moved. In place of what we read in Latin, \"I foresaw,\" and what in Greek is written \"proōrōmēn,\" it stands better in the Hebrew SEBETHI, which we may render \"I set before myself.\" So that it means: I set the Lord before me always at my right hand, and I could be overthrown by no fraud of my enemies. And at the same time consider that it is in our power to set the Lord before our eyes. He who is just and is like the Savior places God at his right hand, and says, \"Because he is at my right hand, that I may not be moved.\" But he places him at the right hand for this reason, that he has before his eyes the one whom he follows as he goes before. But he who is a sinner casts the word of the Lord behind his back, and the Lord becomes for him at the left hand. And when the devil has looked at him on the left of the sinner, he at once moves to the sinner's right, so as to possess the place of God: and that is fulfilled which is written of Judas: \"Let the devil stand at his right hand.\" Why this is put not as in narration but as in prayer belongs to another place to discuss. For the Lord Savior, then, or through the Lord for his saints, God always stands at the right hand. But the just man has no left hand, and wherever he turns, \"the angel of the Lord encamps round about those who fear him, and will deliver them.\" Do you wish to know that the just man has no left hand? When Moses, in the type of the cross, lifted both his hands to heaven, it is not written, \"Aaron was at his right and Hur at his left,\" but that on this side stood Aaron and on that side Hur. In the book of Judges too, that leader Ehud, whose mystery is manifold and great, who had a sword upon his thigh, and had cut off desires, and had also run through with his blade his very fat and gross adversary, is for that reason written \"amphoterodexios,\" that is, having both hands as a right hand, whom in Latin we may call \"ambidextrous.\" Someone may object: If the just man has no left hand, how is Wisdom written to have both a right hand and a left? A diligent reader will very easily judge how great is the difference between the right hand of Wisdom and the left, when he has noticed what things are said to be in her right and what in her left. For it is said: \"Length of eternity and years of life are in her right hand, but in her left hand riches and glory.\" You see eternal time and perpetual life placed in Wisdom's right hand; but the carnal things which pass away, which flee off while they are thought to be held—that is, riches and glory—placed in her left hand, according to that, that on the day of judgment some will stand at the right, and others at the left: the lambs, that is, and the saints at his right, but the goats and the sinners at his left. But perhaps an inquisitive reader will object this too: how we can assert that God stands at the right of the just man, and the devil at the left of the sinner, when concerning our Lord it is read in Zechariah, \"And the devil was standing at his right hand to oppose him.\" Let him, then, who objects this read also what follows: that when the devil was standing at his right hand, Jesus had filthy garments. Rightly did he who had taken up our sins and was bearing our offenses have the devil at his right hand, not so much for himself as for the filthy garments. And at the same time let him consider that it is not said, \"The devil was standing as victor and triumphing,\" but as still set in the contest and in the battle-line and in doubt—indeed, when he had not yet begun to fight. For it follows, \"That he might oppose him.\" But the devil was opposing him, as we said, because he had filthy garments from our sins. But after he heard, \"May the Lord who chose Jerusalem rebuke you,\" he did not stand at the right, but went behind his back and under his feet, and at once Jesus the great priest receives the ankle-length robe (poderis), and is clothed in white vesture, and, royal dignity being joined to him—that is, Zerubbabel being coupled with him—builds the Church, and constructs the spiritual Jerusalem.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.9":["Because of this my heart has been glad and my tongue has rejoiced. This that he says, \"Because of this,\" must be joined to what precedes, so that he says the cause of his gladness, or exultation, was this, that he always had God before his eyes, and, with him standing at his right hand, was never moved; and so let the order be: My heart has been glad and my tongue has rejoiced, rendering a hymn to the Father for the salvation of men, because, while I stood and endured the death of the cross, the Lord was set at my right hand. This passage also tells against those who reckon that the members which are set forth in the Scriptures must always be understood bodily. For let them answer how a tongue by itself can rejoice, or how a tongue, as if perceiving and understanding, can have its own gladness. Since indeed he does not say, \"the tongue rejoiced,\" but \"my tongue rejoiced.\" Understand, then, of the tongue as of the other members too, namely that it has all its effects, according to that saying of Solomon: \"In the hand of the tongue are death and life.\" But in place of what is read in the Acts of the Apostles and in the common edition, \"My tongue rejoiced,\" it must be known that in the other editions, and among the Seventy themselves too, it is not \"tongue\" that is put, but \"glory,\" which in Hebrew is called CHABOD. For \"tongue\" is called LASON. And I think that, because the glory of the orator is in the praise of his speech, just as the glory of the physician is in the industry of the medical art, and the athlete's glory is his strength, and the glory of the other arts is to fulfill what they know: therefore the tongue, through which what is felt is uttered, has been put for \"glory.\" Or at least the glory of that man who was assumed by Christ must be understood, the one concerning whom it is said elsewhere, \"My glory, and the lifter-up of my head.\" Or his glory must be understood as the apostles, because they glorified him in the whole world: just as we call an ample flock the glory of the shepherd, and learned disciples the glory of the master, and strong armies the glory of the general. Further, as to what John says, \"We saw his glory, glory as of the only-begotten from the Father, full of grace and truth,\" and what is written in another place, \"Arise, my glory, arise\": let us understand that to be the Savior's glory which the apostles saw in the humility of his flesh, so that they gazed with keener eyes not on what appeared, but on what lay hidden in the body.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15","Moreover my flesh too shall rest in hope. For you will not leave my soul in hell, nor will you give your holy one to see corruption. It is superfluous to wish to treat of those things which have been explained by the apostles. We therefore say these things, which were passed over by them as being, as it were, of lesser importance: that he said, \"My flesh shall dwell in hope,\" for this reason, that the Savior descended to the dead with the hope of rising again at once. And he did not say \"shall dwell,\" or \"shall rest,\" as is read in the Latin manuscripts, but \"kataskēnōsei,\" that is, \"shall sojourn,\" so that the meaning is: It will tarry there a little while, and will see the underworld in passing, and his soul too will not be left in hell. \"For I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up again.\" Nor will it be granted by the Father to see corruption. But he said \"to see\" for \"to suffer,\" according to that idiom (idiōma) of the Scriptures: \"Who is the man that lives, and shall not see death?\" And elsewhere: \"He who, though he had not known sin, was made sin for us.\" And at the same time note that the Lord had both flesh and soul: the flesh which was laid in the tomb, and the soul which descended to the underworld. And as the flesh without the soul was perfect flesh, so also the soul without the flesh was a perfect soul, and lacked no function of its own nature which the human soul is wont to have apart from sin. This is against the new heresy. And at the same time, because some argue variously, from the fact that the Lord entered with the doors shut, that the same flesh did not rise which had died, let them hear what the Lord himself here recalls. Moreover my flesh too shall rest in hope. Surely, when the Savior had died and suffered, that flesh was laid in the tomb which had also been alive; that flesh, then, also rose again, which had lain lifeless and dead in the tomb. But if the same flesh rose, how do some argue that a certain spiritual and great flesh, and not our own, rose in the Lord? Nor do we say this in such a way as to deny that the body of Christ took on that glory which we believe that we saints too shall receive. For then this corruptible shall put on incorruption, and this mortal shall put on immortality. But as, before he suffered, when transformed and glorified on the mountain he had assuredly the same flesh which he had also had down below, though of another glory; so also after the resurrection there was in him flesh of the same nature which had been before the passion, but of a more glorified and more august appearance, that being fulfilled which Paul says: \"And so from now on we know no one according to the flesh; and even if we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we no longer know him so.\" I am compelled in this place to say that word of the apostle: \"O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God: how unsearchable are his judgments and untraceable his ways!\" God the Son, God the Word, so long as he was in the heavens, and men had not so sinned that he himself should descend to them, dwelt simple and uncompounded with the angels, thrones, dominions, and the other powers. But when he descended to men, namely those who were framed of soul and body, he too came compounded, lest the compounded ones should be unable to bear the simple. Again, because souls without bodies were being held in the underworld, souls which in their nature are simple, so, the flesh itself being left behind, it was not God the Word, not the compounded man, but the soul that descended to the underworld: so that, just as each place had need of him, he might so be perceived by the inhabitants of that region. I know that many, since the body of Christ is the Church, and we are its members, according to anagogy (anagōgēn) understand as the flesh of Christ those who are lower, and as the soul those who are more perfect; or at least as the flesh those who follow the literal history, as the soul those who ascend to spiritual understanding.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.10":["Moreover my flesh too shall rest in hope. For you will not leave my soul in hell, nor will you give your holy one to see corruption. It is superfluous to wish to treat of those things which have been explained by the apostles. We therefore say these things, which were passed over by them as being, as it were, of lesser importance: that he said, \"My flesh shall dwell in hope,\" for this reason, that the Savior descended to the dead with the hope of rising again at once. And he did not say \"shall dwell,\" or \"shall rest,\" as is read in the Latin manuscripts, but \"kataskēnōsei,\" that is, \"shall sojourn,\" so that the meaning is: It will tarry there a little while, and will see the underworld in passing, and his soul too will not be left in hell. \"For I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up again.\" Nor will it be granted by the Father to see corruption. But he said \"to see\" for \"to suffer,\" according to that idiom (idiōma) of the Scriptures: \"Who is the man that lives, and shall not see death?\" And elsewhere: \"He who, though he had not known sin, was made sin for us.\" And at the same time note that the Lord had both flesh and soul: the flesh which was laid in the tomb, and the soul which descended to the underworld. And as the flesh without the soul was perfect flesh, so also the soul without the flesh was a perfect soul, and lacked no function of its own nature which the human soul is wont to have apart from sin. This is against the new heresy. And at the same time, because some argue variously, from the fact that the Lord entered with the doors shut, that the same flesh did not rise which had died, let them hear what the Lord himself here recalls. Moreover my flesh too shall rest in hope. Surely, when the Savior had died and suffered, that flesh was laid in the tomb which had also been alive; that flesh, then, also rose again, which had lain lifeless and dead in the tomb. But if the same flesh rose, how do some argue that a certain spiritual and great flesh, and not our own, rose in the Lord? Nor do we say this in such a way as to deny that the body of Christ took on that glory which we believe that we saints too shall receive. For then this corruptible shall put on incorruption, and this mortal shall put on immortality. But as, before he suffered, when transformed and glorified on the mountain he had assuredly the same flesh which he had also had down below, though of another glory; so also after the resurrection there was in him flesh of the same nature which had been before the passion, but of a more glorified and more august appearance, that being fulfilled which Paul says: \"And so from now on we know no one according to the flesh; and even if we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we no longer know him so.\" I am compelled in this place to say that word of the apostle: \"O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God: how unsearchable are his judgments and untraceable his ways!\" God the Son, God the Word, so long as he was in the heavens, and men had not so sinned that he himself should descend to them, dwelt simple and uncompounded with the angels, thrones, dominions, and the other powers. But when he descended to men, namely those who were framed of soul and body, he too came compounded, lest the compounded ones should be unable to bear the simple. Again, because souls without bodies were being held in the underworld, souls which in their nature are simple, so, the flesh itself being left behind, it was not God the Word, not the compounded man, but the soul that descended to the underworld: so that, just as each place had need of him, he might so be perceived by the inhabitants of that region. I know that many, since the body of Christ is the Church, and we are its members, according to anagogy (anagōgēn) understand as the flesh of Christ those who are lower, and as the soul those who are more perfect; or at least as the flesh those who follow the literal history, as the soul those who ascend to spiritual understanding.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.16.11":["You have made known to me the ways of life; you will fill me with gladness with your countenance: delights are at your right hand unto the end. Because his flesh did not see corruption, and his soul was not left in hell, now after the resurrection, ascending to the heavens as victor, in the person of that man who had risen, he says, \"You have made known to me the ways of life\": that is, I have learned how men are freed from the underworld, by what means they may attain eternal life. And at the same time consider that he who is the Way and the Life said that he had learned the ways of life, that is, how men may attain to him who said that he is the Way and the Life. And I think that, just as he himself is the Truth, and, speaking to the Jews, \"If you abide in my word, you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free,\" he speaks as it were of another truth; so, since he himself is the Life, he speaks as it were of another life, and since he himself is the Way, he says that other ways were shown to him—or, as it is read in the Hebrew, \"the way\": namely, that the flesh which he had taken, and in his flesh the whole race of men, might rejoice that those things which belonged to the divinity of the Son were made known to it. For which reason he says that he rejoices, and has exultation with the Father (for this is what \"with your countenance\" means, according to that, \"You will make him glad with joy with your countenance\"), and full and perfect gladness and ineffable delight, when he shall sit at the Father's right hand, and shall have fulfilled, \"The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.\" The Son sits at the Father's right hand in the majesty of one reigning; and because he had seen his first martyr Stephen battling against the Jews, he rose from his throne and stood at the Father's right hand, as if he too were fighting for the martyr. This is the delight, this the pleasure at the end of the dispensation, or of the victory: for in place of \"end\" Aquila and Theodotion and all the editions, except Symmachus and the Seventy, translated \"victory\"; because he suffered, and rose again, and as victor pierced through to the heavens, for this reason: that he might set man at the right hand of the Lord. It must also be noted that in this place where we read, \"You will fill me with gladness,\" Theodotion said \"Seven gladnesses,\" and \"With your countenance is delight at your right hand. To the victor\"; and Aquila and Symmachus translated \"A fullness of gladnesses with your face,\" or \"in your presence,\" and the rest. It is asked, then, why Theodotion said \"seven\" for \"fullness.\" This indeed he will easily know who has at least a small acquaintance with the Hebrew tongue. For the word SABA, as we have also said in the book of Hebrew Questions, signifies four things: fullness and satiety, an oath, and seven. Since, therefore, it is ambiguous, Theodotion, knowing that there are diverse graces of the Spirit with God, thought that, for the fitness of the passage, \"seven\" ought rather to be translated than \"fullness,\" \"satiety,\" and \"oath\"; so as to show that with God there are the Spirit of wisdom and of understanding, the Spirit of counsel and of fortitude, the Spirit of knowledge and of piety, the Spirit of the fear of the Lord: which are truly the seven full gladnesses—not for others, but for him who has deserved to be delighted forever at the right hand of the Lord. For this reason some suppose that seven heavens were named by the philosophers, and seven wandering stars called. Further, Symmachus, where we read, \"Delight is at your right hand unto the end,\" says, \"Strength is at your right hand forever\": showing that he who is full of virtues will remain continually with God.\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15"],"PSA.18.4-5":["The pains, then, that seized the tower of the flock are the pains of the underworld and the pains of death, which surrounded indeed and hemmed in even the Savior, but could by no means seize him, as he himself says in Psalm 17 (18): The pains of death surrounded me, and the torrents of iniquity troubled me, and the pains of hell hemmed me in.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"PSA.22.1":["And about the ninth hour Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying: Heli Heli lema sabacthani, that is: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? He made use of the beginning of Psalm 21 (22); and what is read in the middle of the little verse: Look upon me, is superfluous. For in the Hebrew it is read: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Therefore they are impious who think that the psalm was spoken in the person of David, or of Esther and Mordecai, since even the evangelists understand testimonies taken from it as concerning the Savior, such as this: They divided my garments among themselves, and upon my vesture they cast lots; and another: They dug through my hands and my feet. Nor should you wonder at the lowliness of the words and the complaints of one forsaken, since, knowing the form of a servant, you see the stumbling block of the cross.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.22.6":["Now the worm, which in Hebrew is called THOLATH (תולעת), is the name given, for its lowliness and contempt, to the choir of the Apostles, imitating the Savior, who speaks in the psalm: I am a worm, and no man, a reproach of men, and the outcast of the people (Ps 21 [22]:7). For as a worm penetrates the earth, so the apostolic word penetrated the cities of the nations, and entered first into the hardest hearts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.22.29":["And when a hymn had been said, they went out to the Mount of Olives. This is what we read in a certain psalm: They have eaten and worshiped, all the fat ones of the earth. According to this example, whoever has been filled and made drunk with the bread and the cup of the Savior can praise the Lord and ascend the Mount of Olives, where there is refreshment from labors, solace for pain, and the knowledge of the true light.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.24.7-10":["This is he whom the angelic powers, seeing him ascend bloodied to the Father, command the other angels, and hear from them: Lift up your gates, O princes, and be lifted up, O everlasting gates, and the king of glory will come in. Who is this king of glory? The Lord strong and mighty, the Lord mighty in battle (Ps 23 [24]:7, 8). And again: The Lord of hosts, he is the king of glory (ibid., 10). We have set this down beforehand to show that the present passage too agrees with the other testimonies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Nor is it a wonder that Christ is called almighty, in whose person we read in the Apocalypse of John: Thus says the faithful witness, the beginning of the creation of God, who is, and who was, and who is to come, the Lord God Almighty. That too which we read in Psalm 23 (24): Lift up your gates, O princes, and be lifted up, eternal gates, and the king of glory shall enter, and which again is said by other angels who did not know the mystery of the flesh assumed: Who is this king of glory? The Lord of powers, he is the king of glory, is referred to Christ. For where we read the Lord of powers, in the Hebrew Sabaoth is written, which is rendered almighty by the LXX translators. From this we understand that wherever the Lord of powers is said of Christ, he must be understood as almighty.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.29.8-9":["Finally, in Psalm 28 (29), where we read: The voice of the Lord shaking the desert (Ps 28 [29]:8), in the Hebrew it is written: The voice of the Lord making the desert bring forth (According to the Hebrew), so that the Church, formerly a desert, may bring forth, and the stags may be perfected when the woodlands are opened and crushed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.29.9":["And there shall be, he says, plains, or woodlands, as folds for flocks. For plains, SARON (שרון) is put in the Hebrew. The whole region around Lydda, Joppa and Jamnia is suited to pasturing flocks. Of it it is also written in the Acts of the Apostles: But the woodlands shall be turned into sheepfolds (Acts 9), according to what is sung in the psalm: The voice of the Lord perfecting the stags, and he will lay bare the woodlands (Ps 28 [29]:9), or the thickets of the forests, so that robbers may cease to lurk there, and beasts and venomous animals, and places once of ambush and blood may pass into churches of the Lord, and flocks may be pastured there by him who laid down his life for his sheep. Of whom it is written: He will shepherd us forever (Ps 47 [48]:15). This voice of the Lord, perfecting the stags and laying bare the thickets of the forests, cries out through John in the desert: Already the axe is laid to the root of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire (Matt 3:10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.31.19":["And in another place it is said: How great is the multitude of your sweetness, O Lord, which you have hidden for those who fear you! Therefore the great mercy of God is hidden from those who are still set in fear and do not have the perfect love of God, so that, while they hear of the day of the Lord as cruel and incurable and full of indignation and fury, they may cease to sin.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.32.1":["But according to anagogy, those are called iniquities, that is, both ἀνομίαι and ἀδικίαι (injustices), which we committed before baptism and which have been forgiven us in baptism; but sins are those which we have done after baptism, of which it is also written in the psalm: Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.32.9":["But if we wish to take the bridle, as the LXX translated, as the word of God, let us understand by the bridle him who reins in from vices horses raging with lust, and mules barren and wanton, and restrains them, and does not allow them to rush headlong; of whom it is said: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding. With bridle and bit you will bind fast the jaws of those who do not draw near to you. Of this bridle James also speaks: We put bridles into the mouths of horses, and we turn about their whole body, so, that is, that they may walk on a straight road and be able to offer the Lord backs gentle for sitting. Such a bridle and such a word, wrought with a variety of gold and silver, prepares wild horses for the Savior to sit upon, and makes them holy and properly consecrated to his worship.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.34.10":["And the mother of the Lord, full of the Holy Spirit, prophesied: He has filled the hungry with good things (Luke 1:53), those who before did not have the food of the Lord: and the rich he has sent away empty, those who were given for his burial, and who spat out him whom the Prophets had promised. Of these the Psalmist sings in another place too: The rich have been in want, and have hungered (Ps 33 [34]:11), that is, the people of the Jews. But those who seek the Lord, that is, the crowd of the Gentiles, shall not lack any good thing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.36.6":["And Jerusalem, he says, shall be inhabited without a wall because of the multitude of men and cattle, or, with an abundance of all fruits. Some interpret men and cattle as two peoples, of the Jews and of the Gentiles: that those who were versed in the law and came to the faith of Christ are called men, while we, who after idolatry were as it were in the desert of the law and the solitude of the prophets, and have received his passion, ought to be called cattle. But others want all rational men, learned in the knowledge of the Scriptures, to be understood as men, and as cattle all the simple among the believers, and that of these it is said: Men and cattle you will save, O Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","For before the foundations of the house of God are laid in us, and we are built up as a temple for God, and hear from the Apostle: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you; whatever good work we seemed to have, whether as rational beings, who are called men, or as simple ones, who are called beasts — For men and beasts, he says, you will save, O Lord — has no wage with God; and there are wars and discords in us, and tribulation everywhere, and we are without the peace of Christ, which, as he went to the Father, he left to the apostles; and the Lord's saying is fulfilled in us: A man's enemies are those of his own household. For every brother supplants with supplanting; and every friend walks deceitfully, and a man mocks his brother and does not speak the truth; their tongue has learned to speak a lie.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.36.8":["For splendor and oil, and fatness and brightness, we read in the Hebrew Isaar, according to what is written in the psalms of the joy and happiness of the saints: They shall be inebriated with the plenty, or with the fatness of your house: for πιότης means fatness rather than plenty.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.37.25":["And David, who had reached the old age of Abraham, sang freely: I have been young, and have grown old, and I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread. How many martyrs in persecutions perished of hunger, and lacked grain and these nourishments of the body. Therefore he speaks of that bread which came down from heaven, which whoever eats will be able neither to hunger nor to thirst.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.37.35-36":["This Sihon, of whom it is written: Already the axe is laid to the roots of the trees, because he was an unfruitful tree, was cut down when the Lord struck him; his height is compared to the height of the cedars, of which we read: I saw the wicked man exalted on high and lifted up like the cedars of Lebanon; and I passed by, and behold, he was not; and I sought him, and his place was not found. And he fittingly says that he passed by, because from him who passes over from the world, and can say: Passing over, I will see this great vision, the cedar is taken away, and the place of pride cannot be found.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.39.6":["We too can point out idols painted on the walls of our temple, when we are subject to all vices and paint in our heart the consciousness of sins and various images, of which it is said in the psalm too: O Lord, in your city you will scatter their image, and in another place: Yet man walks about in an image. That is, there is no one among men who does not have some image, whether of holiness or of sin; for which reason it is said of the furious and the wrathful: Their fury is after the likeness of a serpent, like a deaf asp that stops its ears, which will not hear the voices of the charmers; and of wicked men: Generation of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come?; and of the shifty and deceitful: Go, tell this fox; and of lovers of women: They have become to me horses mad for females; each one neighed after his neighbor's wife; and also of the brutish and senseless: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding; and again: With bit and bridle bind fast their jaws, who do not draw near to you; and many things of this kind.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.39.13":["'When I make you, O Tyre, a city desolate forever, as are other cities that are not inhabited at all, and bring up the deep upon you, and many waters, or boundless multitudes of enemies, cover you — or certainly, because he is speaking to an island and to a city set on an island, he τροπικῶς [figuratively] compares the throng of adversaries to the waves of the deep —, and drag you down, with those who go down into the pit or into the ditch, to those who are in hell — of whom we also read in the Psalms: They shall go into the lower parts of the earth and shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be portions for foxes —, to the everlasting people, where there is weeping of eyes and gnashing of teeth, and set you in the uttermost land, in which the ancient dragon has been placed, and like the ancient wastes and deserts, where there is no remembrance of good things but punishments are everlasting, and do not cause you to be raised up in the land of the living — of which it is written: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land, and in another place: I will please the Lord in the region of the living —, then you will be reduced to nothing, or you will perish forever, and you shall be no more', according to what is written in another psalm: Spare me, that I may be refreshed before I depart and shall be no more: not that he who is in everlasting punishments ceases to be, but that he who does not live to God is said in the holy Scriptures not to subsist. Hence Esther too, speaking against idols: Do not hand over, she says, your scepter to those who are not: surely those to whom she begs that it not be handed over existed, but they were not, to God, since they had ceased to be to the virtues and to the living God.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.40.7":["The lion of the tribe of Judah is the Lord Jesus Christ, who loosed the seals of the book, not, properly, of one book, as many think, the psalms of David, but of all the Scriptures, which were written by one Holy Spirit; and therefore they are called one book. Of this Ezekiel testifies in mystical speech (Ezek 2) that it was written within and without: in the sense and in the letter. Of this the Savior too speaks in the psalms: At the head of the book it is written of me (Ps 39 [40]:8); not of Jeremiah, not of Isaiah, but in all holy Scripture, which is called one book.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In which was a rolled-up book, and he spread it out before me, and it was written within and without; there were written in it lamentations and song and woe. For 'rolled-up book' the Seventy translated 'the heading of the book', in the hand of the Lord who sits upon the throne, the hand which was sent forth and stretched out; of this book it is also said in the Psalms: In the heading of the book it is written of me — let us understand 'heading' as the beginning —. This book, rolled up and sealed, and written within and without, or before and behind, was of such difficulty that no one could, either in heaven or on earth or under the earth, open it or read it, except only the lion of the tribe of Judah, the root of David and of Jesse; the book which John in the Revelation said he had devoured, not whole but in its heading, that is, in its beginning. For it cannot be that human nature should devour all the treasures of this book.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.42.7":["If, then, we take the abyss in a good sense, let us say: when the waters of the most wicked way were scattered, your wise ones saw you, and, borrowing from your countenance the height of knowledge which they had — because they saw you and the mountains were in labor —, whatever they had previously thought about you they brought forth in the praises of their voice. And he beautifully calls the opinion of phantasy height, in accordance with Jesus son of Sirach, who says: The abyss and wisdom, who will search them out? Hence also, from the little mountain, that is, the assumption of a human body, which Daniel calls a stone cut from the mountain without hands, that is, without the work of marriage, Christ the abyss calls upon the Father, the other abyss, in the voice of his cataracts, that he may give to those who preach the good news the word with great power. Or certainly the abyss, the New Testament, in testimony of the little mountain by which the prince of Tyre was wounded, calls upon the abyss of the Old Testament, so that through the cataracts of Christ, that is, the apostles, the preaching may become firmer.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PSA.44.9":["God seems to reject us when he leaves us to temptation. Hence Job too: The visitation, he says, of the Lord has looked upon me. And not only do the just speak and say: But now you have rejected and confounded us, as is written in Psalm 43 (44), but the Lord and Savior himself, in the person of the man whom he had assumed: But you, he says, have rejected and despised, you have put off your Christ; you have overturned the covenant of your servant. That it is the person of the just in Psalm 43 (44) who say: But now you have rejected and confounded us, is proved from what follows: All these things have come upon us, yet we have not forgotten you, nor have we acted wickedly in your covenant; nor has our heart turned back.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"PSA.45.3":["But if he had no form nor glory, but his form was ignoble and failing beyond the sons of men, or, as it stands in the Hebrew, despised and the lowest of men, how is it said in the Psalms: Gird your sword upon your thigh, O most mighty, in your beauty and comeliness (Ps 44 [45]:4)? This is easily resolved. He was despised and ignoble when he hung on the cross and, made a curse for us, bore our sins, and said to the Father: God, my God, why have you forsaken me (Matt 27:46)? But he was glorious and comely in appearance when at his passion the earth trembled, the rocks were split, and, as the sun fled, the elements feared an eternal night.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.45.7":["To him the Psalmist had already said: You have loved righteousness and hated iniquity; therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows (Ps 44 [45]:8). For when fellows are named, understand the nature of the flesh, since God has no fellows of his substance. And because the anointing was spiritual, and by no means of a human body, as it was among the priests of the Jews, he is therefore said to have been anointed above his fellows, that is, the other saints. His anointing was fulfilled at the time when he was baptized in the Jordan, and the Holy Spirit came down upon him in the form of a dove and remained on him (John 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when he has washed, and cleansed her of the blood — the blood from which, by the touch of his fringe, he also healed the αἱμορροοῦσαν [woman with a flow of blood] who for twelve years had been running with gore —, it is not enough to wash with water and cleanse from blood, unless he also anoints with oil, according to which the Samaritan, which means 'guardian', first soothed the wounds of the half-dead man by pouring in oil, then checked them with the sharpness of wine; but our Lord was anointed with another oil, which would not soothe the pain of wounds but grant gladness, as the Holy Spirit says to him: Therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And above all kinds of ointments there is a spiritual ointment, which is called the oil of exultation, with which the Savior is anointed, and it is said to him: Therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows. And I think his fellows are those to whom John too speaks: And you have an ointment from the Holy One. And a little after: These things I have written to you concerning those who lead you astray; and the chrism which you received from him, let it abide in you; and you have no need that anyone teach you, but the chrism itself will teach you about all things, and it is true and is not a lie, and as it has taught you, abide in it. And lest perhaps those who have lost the chrism of baptism should despair of the chrism being restored, it is written in Leviticus that when the leper cast outside the camp has come to the priest, and his leprosy has been cleansed, the priest shall pour oil into his own left hand, and, having dipped his finger, sprinkle the oil seven times before the Lord, and touch with the same oil the ear of him who had been a leper, and his right hand, and his right foot, and whatever remains of the ointment he shall put entirely on his head. And when he has duly completed all these things, then he shall offer a holocaust for him, and the man himself shall be called the anointed of God. I wish to say something, but I fear lest I give the careless an occasion of ruin: that in the holy Scriptures the same man is often found anointed. Indeed, David was anointed three times; which let us not understand of one who has sinned and is anointed again — for it suffices for the leper that, after the first ointment has been lost, he be anointed a second time —; but of one who advances day by day, and whose anointing is ever increased; and he passes from the oil of the leper to the oil of the people and of the saints, and from the oil of the people he arrives at the oil of the priests, and from the priests he rises to the chrism of the high priest, from the high priest also to the king, from the king to the patriarchs, and from the patriarchs he proceeds to Christ; and he is anointed with the oil of exultation, and whoever has been smeared with it is made one spirit with God, and where the Father and the Son are, there he too will be. But this is rare, and these are the prayers of believers. Besides, I do not know whether the effect follows. For it is said: God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows, that is, with that oil which those who are your fellows will be able to find either rarely or never.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PSA.45.9-13":["We read in Psalm 44 (45), which pertains especially to the union of the bridegroom and the bride, that is, to God the Savior and to the mysteries of the Church, that it is said among other things: The daughters of Tyre with gifts, the rich among the people shall entreat your face (Ps 44 [45]:13). And the bridegroom himself speaks again to the Tyrian bride: Hear, O daughter, and see, and incline your ear; and forget your people and your father's house, for the king shall desire your beauty (ibid. 11, 12). Hence in the description of her beauty too it is added: The queen stood at your right hand, in a vesture of gold, surrounded with variety (ibid. 10). And again: All the glory of the king's daughter is within (ibid. 14). And if the king desired the beauty of the penitent Tyrian woman, who has the ornaments of various virtues, how much more shall her hire and her trade belong not to those who remain in Tyre but to those who dwell in the sight of the Lord!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.47.1":["They also clap with their hands to the voice and sound of the instruments, so that all their works look for pleasure, and they do nothing except what they provide for the belly and for lust. Nor let the prudent reader at once object to us this, and how it is written: Let the rivers clap with their hands, and: All nations, clap your hands, and: Shout for joy to God our helper. For there they are said to clap not to the sound of instruments, but to have one accord in the praise of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.50.9-15":["But according to the Hebrew, God shows that he never wanted the victims of the Jews; which we read also in Psalm 49 (50): I will not take calves from your house, nor goats from your flocks: for all the wild beasts of the forests are mine, the cattle on the mountains and the oxen. I know all the birds of the sky, and the beauty of the field is with me. If I am hungry, I will not tell you: for the world is mine and the fullness thereof. Shall I eat the flesh of bulls, or shall I drink the blood of goats (Ps 49 [50]ff.)? And having rejected the ceremonies of the old Law, he passes on to the purity of the Gospel, and shows what he desires in their place: Offer to God the sacrifice of praise, and pay your vows to the Most High. And call upon me in the day of trouble, and I will deliver you, and you shall honor me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.51.5":["for who of men can boast that he has a chaste heart?, or: Into whose mind has the death of concupiscence not entered through the windows of the eyes and, to say little, a tickling of the soul? For the world is set in the evil one, and: From childhood the heart of man is set on evil, so that from the very beginning of birth the human condition is not without sin for even a single day; whence David too confesses in the psalm: 'For behold, I was conceived in iniquities, and in sins my mother conceived me, not by the iniquities of my mother or indeed my own, but in the iniquities of the human condition'; whence the Apostle too says: Death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over those who did not sin in the likeness of the transgression of Adam —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.51.7-13":["Notice in the present passage that someone can teach after sin, provided, however, that he has washed away his former vices with worthy repentance. Hence David too, after adultery and homicide, says in the psalm: You will sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed; you will wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow. Nor is he content with his own purity alone, but he adds: Restore to me the joy of your salvation, and strengthen me with a governing spirit. And when you have done this. I will teach, he says, the wicked your ways, and the impious will be turned to you.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"PSA.51.10-12":["But not to be long (for it is a commentary, not a dialogue, that is being written), I shall show briefly from the fiftieth psalm that three spirits are named, the prophet saying: \"Create a clean heart in me, O God, and renew a right spirit in my inward parts. Do not cast me away from your face, and do not take your Holy Spirit from me. Restore to me the joy of your salvation, and strengthen me with a princely spirit\". By the princely spirit he means the Father, because the Son is from the Father, and not the Father from the Son. But by the right spirit, the spirit of truth and righteousness, he signifies Christ the Lord: \"For the Father has given all judgment to the Son\" (John 5:22), as David says: \"O God, give your judgment to the king, and your power to the king's son\" (Ps. 71:1). Further, he calls the Holy Spirit by his open name. And though these differ in names and persons, they are joined in substance and nature; and, because of the fellowship of nature, the same Spirit is said without distinction to be now of the Father, now of the Son.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PSA.51.11":["As for what follows: Her prophets πνευματοφόροι, that is, bearing the spirit, men who are despisers, let it disturb no one that, interpreting this of teachers, we call the same men both prophets and despisers, since the Apostle too commands: Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you were sealed in the day of redemption. And David says in Psalm 50 (51): Do not take your Holy Spirit from me. For unless the Holy Spirit, when grieved, were accustomed to flee his former lodging and to abandon his dwelling, Paul would never have commanded what I said above, nor would David after his adultery have dreaded lest he lose what he had received; of which it is also written to the Hebrews: How much worse punishments do you think he deserves who has trampled on the Son of God, and has held unclean the blood of the covenant, in which he was sanctified, and has done insult to the spirit of grace?\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"PSA.55.23":["The saints fill up their days, as Abraham did, who died full of days in a good old age (Gen 25). But sinners and the impious die in the half of their days, of whom the Psalmist also speaks: Men of blood and deceitful men shall not live out half their days (Ps 54 [55]:25). For they do not fulfill the works of virtue, nor are they eager to amend their faults by penance. Hence in the middle of life's course and in the darkness of their errors they will be led to Tartarus.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.58.3-5":["But the prudent one in mystic speech (Jer 14), or the whisperer, and, as Theodotion would have it, the enchanter, seems to me to be a man learned and practiced both in the Law and the Prophets and in the Gospel and the Apostles, who can heal each disturbance of the soul by his teaching and bring it back to a settled state of mind: while the fornicator recovers chastity, and the glutton frugality, and the man once greedy gives alms. Sift through all the synagogues of the Jews, and you will be able to find no teacher who enjoins holy things and, despising riches, teaches that poverty is to be pursued. Of such enchanters against serpents and asps, that is, against sinners who have abandoned the righteousness of God, David spoke in the Psalm: Sinners have been estranged from the womb; they have gone astray from the belly, they have spoken falsehoods. Their fury is after the likeness of a serpent: like the deaf asp that stops its ears, which will not hear the voice of the enchanters, nor of the sorcerer who charms wisely (Ps 57 [58]:4ff.). Every word of the Apostle is an enchantment against serpents and asps, which sinners and heretics do not hear, because they shut their ears so as not to hear the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.58.3":["On the other hand, David sings of sinners: \"For behold, I was conceived in iniquities, and in sins my mother conceived me\" (Ps. 50:7). And in another place: \"Sinners are estranged from the womb\" (Ps. 57:4). And in both directions, before the little ones were born: \"God loved Jacob, but Esau he held in hatred\" (Mal. 1:1, 2). Here the heretics find an opening, who claim that there are diverse natures, namely the spiritual and the animal and the χοϊκὴν (earthy), and that one is saved, another perishes, and another stands between the two; for, they say, neither would the righteous man ever be chosen before he had done anything good, nor the sinner be hated before his sin, unless the nature of those who perish and of those who are to be saved were different. To this it can be answered simply that this comes about from the foreknowledge of God: that the one whom he knows will be righteous he loves before he comes forth from the womb, and the one whom he knows will be a sinner he hates before he sins; not that there is injustice in God, either in his love or in his hatred, but that he ought not to regard them otherwise, because he knows that they will be either sinners or righteous; we, as men, judge only of present things, whereas he, for whom future things are already done, passes sentence on the end of things, not on their beginnings. And these things indeed have been said rather simply, and without a loftier discussion they can in some measure satisfy the reader. But those who strive to assert that God is unjust, after the words we set down a little earlier, \"sinners are estranged from the womb\", also bring in the rest that follows: \"They have gone astray from the womb, they have spoken falsehoods\". And they say: how did sinners go astray straight from the womb and speak falsehoods, when they could have neither speech nor understanding? Or what is this righteousness of the foreknowledge of God, to love and guard one before he is born, and to detest another? And they refer the causes of this to a former life, holding that each one, according to his merit, is assigned from his very first birth to good or to evil angels. And that whole passage about Jacob and Esau, which we have just mentioned, as it is written to the Romans (Rom. 9), they toss about in such a way that they cannot be answered without sweat, and even the hellebore of Chrysippus.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PSA.62.11-12":["And as for what he says, Two things there are that have befallen you, or these two things are against you: who shall be grieved over you?, and in place of two he names four, devastation and destruction, famine and the sword: like this is what is sung in the Psalter: Once God has spoken, these two things I have heard: that power belongs to God, and to you, O Lord, mercy: for you will render to each according to his works (Ps 61 [62]:12, 13). For there too God speaks once, that he is almighty, and the Prophet hears two things, that his omnipotence prevails on both sides, so that he both grants mercy to the penitent and renders to those who persist in sin the punishments they deserve. In the same way in this other place two things befell Jerusalem, each of which has two parts. For destruction follows ruin, or devastation; and death follows famine and the sword.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.65.2":["But that all flesh is to be taken not absolutely, of men and of beasts and of birds and of fish, but specifically of men, is shown by that scripture: Hear my prayer; to you all flesh shall come. For it is not all the flesh of irrational living creatures that is to come to the Lord, but that flesh which is to receive the gift of the Holy Spirit, and of which it is said: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.67.1":["May God have mercy on us and bless us. Let him not be our judge, but be merciful. May God have mercy on us. This is the voice of the apostles speaking to the people gathered from the nations: You have indeed believed at our voice; behold, this Church is gathered together in the name of God; therefore we say, May God have mercy on us and bless us, that by his blessing he may undo the first curse laid upon man. May he make his countenance shine upon us. Let us not see him sorrowful, but rejoicing; let us not see him sorrowful over our sins, but rejoicing in our virtues; let us not experience him as a judge, but as a Father. May he make his countenance shine upon us. What is the face of God? Surely his image. For the apostle too says that the Son is the image of the Father. Therefore let him make us shine with his image — that is, let him make his image, the Son, shine upon us, so that the Son himself may make us shine. For the light of the Father is the light of the Son. He who sees the Father sees also the Son; and he who sees the Son sees also the Father — where there is no distance between glory and glory, both are one glory.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.2":["That we may know your way upon earth. This is what we ask: that you may make your countenance shine, that is, your image, upon us — that image which has always been hidden with you, and was, as it were, in the secret and darkness of your mysteries, according to what is written: \"He made darkness his hiding-place.\" This is what we ask, that you make it shine upon us, so that the mystery which was hidden from the generations, as the apostle says, may be revealed in us. But why do we ask for all this? Why do we desire it? Unless you make your image shine upon us, we cannot know your way and your knowledge. Therefore it follows, That we may know your way upon earth: that your majesty, which is known in the heavens, we may hold known also upon earth. That we may know your way upon earth. \"I am,\" he says, \"the way and the life and the truth.\" That we may know your way upon earth. Let us know it upon earth. \"Your will be done, as in heaven, so also on earth\"; that the one whom the angels adore in the heavens, men too may adore upon earth. But someone may say that the way of God was known. \"For God is known in Judaea, in Israel his name is great.\" God was known, but the way of God was not known in Judaea. Since, then, the way of God the Father was not known among the Jews, therefore he now says: Among all the nations your salvation. Not in one people, but in all the nations. Among all the nations your salvation. Where we have in Latin \"salvation\" (salutare), in Hebrew it is called \"Jesus.\" And indeed the angel too speaks this: \"And you shall call,\" he says, \"his name Jesus: for he shall save his people.\" Among all the nations your salvation. Behold, what the apostles prayed for is fulfilled: among all the nations his salvation is known. Wherever human speech can be, there Jesus resounds.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.3":["Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let the peoples confess to you. The Jews? No: all peoples. Just as all the nations, so also all peoples. Let the peoples confess to you, O God. What should they confess? Surely their sins — or at least let them confess that your image is in you: let them confess you in the Son, in him let them see you, in him let them adore you.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.4":["Let the nations be glad and rejoice. Because they have been freed from the captivity of the devil; because those who before were bent down to adore idols, wood and stones, now with upright face look upon the heavens. Because you judge the peoples in equity. Wherever there is equity and judgment, there is not gladness, but the dread of sinners. And how did he say, May God have mercy on us and bless us; and now he says, Let the nations be glad and rejoice, because you judge the peoples in equity? It seems to be contradictory. For if they need mercy, how are they glad and rejoice, because you judge the peoples in equity? For wherever there is mercy, there is no judgment; but where there is judgment and the equity of judgment, there mercy is far off. But this is the sense. Let the nations be glad and rejoice: why should they be glad? Because you judge the peoples in equity: because you in no way show favoritism to one nation, but are the God of all. Not the Jews alone are pleasing to you, but the whole world is yours. Someone may say: You are doing violence to Scripture; it did not speak in this sense. Let Scripture interpret itself, which says, Because you judge the peoples in equity. Which peoples? And you direct the nations upon earth. The nations which before did not walk with upright foot, the nations which before did not walk on an upright path, you made them to walk in your way: that we might know your way upon earth, that we might walk in the way, not in the ways, that through one way we might come to you, from whom the way is born. For we do not go to the Father except through the Son. Someone may say: This is the doctrine of the Arians. So, then, one comes to the Father through the Son? I say this: that through the image we go to the face, and in the light we see the light, in the Son we see the Father. It is the mystery and the dispensation that speaks. But if anyone thinks it blasphemous that we have called the Son the way, and that through the way we go as it were to God, let us see what this very way says. \"No one comes to me, unless my Father draws him to me.\" So, just as the Son leads to the Father, so also the Father leads to the Son. These are human words. But both the Son leads to the Father, and the Father leads to the Son: and there is one nature, one substance.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.5":["Let the peoples confess to you, O God — he had already said this above, and in this little psalm he repeats these two verses again. For he had said above, \"Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you. Let the nations be glad and rejoice,\" and so on: and now again he repeats these very verses. It is as though the apostles lead the chant for all the nations. \"Often have they fought against me from my youth, let Israel now say: often have they fought against me from my youth.\" See how they lead the chant, and the throng of all the nations responds. So too now, when they say at the beginning \"Let all the peoples confess to you,\" and when they urge the peoples and say \"Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you\" — because they see them falling silent, therefore they rebuke them with the same words, and say, Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you. Once we have said it, and you did not hear; let us say it again, that you may sing.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.6":["But why have we said all this, both once and a second time, that the peoples should confess to God? Why do we say this? Why do we urge this? The earth has given its fruit. The earth — holy Mary — from our earth, from our seed, from this clay, from this mud, from Adam. \"You are earth, and into the earth you shall go.\" This earth has given its fruit: what it lost in paradise, it has found in the Son. The earth has given its fruit. First it gave a flower. He says in the Song of Songs: \"I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys.\" This flower, then, has been made fruit, that we might eat him, that we might feed on his flesh. Do you wish to know what this fruit is? A virgin from a virgin, the Lord from a handmaid, God from man, a son from a mother, fruit from the earth. See what this fruit himself says. \"Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it cannot bear much fruit.\" The earth has given its fruit, it has given the grain of wheat: which grain of wheat fell into the earth and died, and therefore brings forth much fruit. It was multiplied in the ear of corn: what had fallen as one thing, rose again with many. For the grain of wheat fell into the earth, and an abundant harvest sprang up. The earth has given its fruit. Therefore Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you: the earth has given its fruit.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66","What is this fruit? May God bless us, our God; may God bless us. God — the Father; our God — the Son. For he himself is our God. \"And you shall call,\" he says, \"his name Emmanuel\" — that is, God with us. Our God. May God bless us — the Holy Spirit. See the mystery of the Trinity comprehended in one little verse, The earth has given its fruit with many; in this fruit the mystery of the Trinity is contained. May God bless us, our God. He who is the God of all by nature has been made our God in a proper sense. May God bless us. May he drive out the old curse. The curse cast us out of paradise: may the blessing bring us back. May God bless us, our God; may God bless us.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.67.7":["And let all the ends of the earth fear him. He did not say, Let all the ends of the earth love him: \"for perfect love casts out fear.\" To fear belongs to beginners, to love belongs to the perfect. He who still fears is placed at the start, he does not have perfect and full faith; but he who loves God, all things turn out to good for him. This little sentence seems brief and simple: but let us fan it out, and in these simple words we shall discover immense mysteries. We shall find within what lay hidden without. \"But to those who love God all things work together for good.\" Job, the holy man, was tempted: he lost his sons and daughters, his house collapsed, he lost everything, he lost all things suddenly — neither father nor master; for nothing sound remained to him in his body, except only his tongue with which he might blaspheme. See the tempter, the devil. \"From the sole,\" he says, \"of his foot to the crown of his head he struck him with a most grievous wound\" — that is, with elephantiasis. From it worms flowed over his whole body, and gore, and rottenness. His tongue alone he kept intact for him, that he might be able to blaspheme his God. \"In all these things Job did not sin with his lips.\" See the greatness of the temptation, see the greatness of his virtue. See and consider that the apostle's saying is fulfilled: But to those who love God all things work together for good. At the time when he was losing his possessions, when he was losing his sons, the things that had happened did indeed seem to him to be adverse: but because he loved the Lord, those evils, which he brings upon him, work together to his good. Those worms of his body were preparing for him the crown of heaven. Before he was tempted, God had never spoken to him: but after he was tempted, God came to him, and speaks familiarly, as a friend with his friend. Let the plagues come, every kind of punishment, so long as after the plagues Christ arrives.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66","We have gone out of the psalm, but not uselessly; because we had said And let all the ends of the earth fear him, and we had said that to fear belongs to the little ones, but to love belongs to the perfect; and to prove this we had cited from the apostle, But to those who love the Lord all things work together for good. And let all the ends of the earth fear him. By no means Judaea alone, but all the ends of the earth. But according to the figurative sense (tropology), all the ends of the earth — not all the middles of the earth, not those who are in the middle of the earth, but those who are at the end of the earth, who leave the earth behind and hasten to go to heaven. This, then, which the apostles said at the beginning, let us say at the end. May God have mercy on us and bless us: may he make his countenance shine upon us, and have mercy on us.\n— Homily 6, on Psalm 66"],"PSA.68.1":["Let God arise, and let his enemies be scattered. This psalm is understood both in a special sense and in a general sense. In the special sense, of the Lord himself, that he should rise from the dead and scatter his enemies — that is, the devil and his army, or the Jews. But in the general sense, whenever we are in tribulation and distress, and we say, \"Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord? and help us\"; just as the apostles too, in the little boat, wake the Lord who was sleeping, saying: \"Master, save us, we perish.\" And let those who hate him flee from before his face. He did not say, let them perish, but, let them flee: because sinners cannot stand in the sight of God.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.2":["As smoke fails, let them fail: not that they should be reduced to nothing, but that they should rest from their sins. As wax flows away before the face of fire. Just as wax is melted, and does not lose its nature, but grows soft: so let these too not perish, but lay aside their hardness, and thus turn to repentance, and be saved. So let sinners perish from before the face of God: because sinners are not worthy to see God.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.3":["And let the just feast. Those who remain in their righteousness, who have no vices, let them be delighted in the Lord. Let them exult in the sight of God, and let them be delighted with gladness. It is great confidence, to exult in the sight of the Lord. Just as a steward who has badly handled and lost the substance entrusted to him by his master fears his master's presence; so also he who has managed it well exults in his master's presence.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.4":["Sing to God, sing a psalm to his name. \"Sing\" — in Scripture this always signifies contemplation (theoria): that is, consider the mysteries and the meaning of divine Scripture. But \"to make melody\" (psallere) Scripture signifies as a good work: for example, that the hearing should furnish its own work, and likewise the mouth, and the eyes, and the hands, and all the members should as it were agree, and thus strike the psaltery in the virtues. Make a way for him who ascends above the sunset: the Lord is his name. If the sun of iniquity does not set for us, the sun of righteousness cannot rise for us, whose healing is in his wings.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","And what follows: As far as the torrent of the west, we shall interpret from the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm, in which it is written: Sing to the Lord, sing a psalm to his name; make a way for him who ascends above the sunset. The Lord is his name. For unless evil works have set in us, Christ will not ascend above us. And when they have set, and we have Christ as our charioteer, then, as we advance and go on to better things, it will be commanded us in the same psalm: Sing to God, sing psalms to the Lord, who ascends above the heaven of heaven toward the east. Hence also in the mysteries we first renounce him who is in the west, and who dies to us together with our sins, and so, turned toward the east, we enter into a covenant with the sun of righteousness and promise that we will serve him.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.68.5":["God is in his holy place. God always dwells in his holy ones: wherever holiness is, there is the dwelling-place of God.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.6":["God makes those of one manner to dwell in a house: those who are of one manner, and are not changed. Since indeed the just man is not changed, because once for all he has resolved to remain in righteousness. But the sinner is changed: \"for the fool shall be changed like the moon.\" In the Hebrew, however, it has \"The Lord makes monks to dwell in a house\": that is, in those in whom sin does not dwell alongside. Who leads out the bound ones in strength. God himself by his own power leads out those who are bound by the devil in sins: like that woman in the Gospel, who had been bound eighteen years by the devil. Likewise those who provoke him, who dwell in the tombs. God is by nature sweet: but those who lead him to bitterness are sinners, and they make God bitter for themselves. He does not change his own nature, but the sinners themselves make God bitter for themselves. Who dwell in the tombs. \"Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, for you are like whitewashed tombs.\" Just as the holy man is a temple of God, so the sinner makes himself a tomb.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.7":["O God, when you went out in the sight of your people. This is according to the history, when God went before his people as they went out of Egypt. When you passed through in the desert. God does not linger in the desert, but passes through.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.8":["The earth was moved, for the heavens dripped down from before the face of the God of Sinai. The heavens dripped down: that is, the manna. Sinai is interpreted as \"temptation.\" Therefore God dwells in those who are tempted and overcome; but in those who are among delights, he does not dwell.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.9":["You will set apart a voluntary rain, O God, for your inheritance. He speaks of the law, which was given through Moses. And it was weakened, but you have perfected it. The law was weakened, because no one was able to fulfill it except the Lord, who said: \"I have not come to abolish the law, but to fulfill it.\" And according to the apostle: \"For what was impossible for the law, in that it was weakened, God sent his own Son,\" and the rest.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.10":["Your animals dwell in it; that is, in the inheritance. But by \"animals\" he meant those who live in God, that is, the holy ones. You have prepared in your sweetness for the poor man, O God. \"Blessed are the poor in spirit.\" You have prepared the rain, that is, the law.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.11":["The Lord will give the word to those who preach the gospel with great power. In the Hebrew it has \"the women who preach the gospel\"; that is, to those women who were made evangelists to the apostles, where he says \"Go, tell my brothers,\" and the rest. Or else it signifies the souls which have believed in Christ. But according to the Seventy translators he speaks to the apostles, because the Lord gave them the power to preach the gospel.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","The Hebrews assert, and there is no doubt about this matter among them, that the Holy Spirit is called in their tongue by the feminine gender, that is, RUA CODSA (רוח קדשה). And what is said in Psalm 67 (68): The Lord will give the word to those who bring good tidings with great power, they understand thus: The Lord will give the word to those women who bring good tidings with great power (Ps 122 [123]:3): namely, to those souls which have obtained the Holy Spirit. And also the verse: As the eyes of a maidservant are on the hands of her mistress: they interpret the soul as the maidservant and the Holy Spirit as the mistress. But also in the Gospel written according to the Hebrews, which the Nazarenes read, the Lord says: just now my mother, the Holy Spirit, took me. No one ought to be scandalized in this matter, that spirit is said among the Hebrews in the feminine gender, while in our language it is called in the masculine gender, and in the Greek speech in the neuter. For in the Godhead there is no sex. And therefore in the three principal languages in which the title of the Lord's passion was written, it is called by three genders, so that we may know that what is diverse is of no gender.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.68.12":["The king of the powers of the beloved. \"King,\" the Father; \"of the powers,\" that is, of the holy ones; \"of the beloved,\" of the Son. \"Behold my beloved Son, hear him.\" \"Of the beloved.\" If anyone loves someone, he always has him on his lips: therefore the Holy Spirit, because of the love which he has for the Son, frequently makes mention of him. And to the beauty of the house to divide the spoils. The apostles themselves divide in the churches the spoils, which they have taken from the idols.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.13":["If you sleep among the midst of the lots (cleri): that is, if you rest between the New and the Old Testament. The individual books too are called \"lots\" (cleri): that is, Genesis, Exodus, Judges, the Gospels, the Apostle. The wings of the dove covered with silver. You will find in the two Testaments the grace of the Holy Spirit. \"Covered with silver\": it signifies the clear word of divine Scripture. And the hinder parts of its back in the paleness of gold: that is, in the inner things the mysteries are understood spiritually.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","What we have understood of Jerusalem, let us understand of the state of souls, which were created pure by God and received gold in understanding and wisdom, and silver in speech and eloquence, so that what they have conceived in the mind they may unfold in words. Of silver we read: The utterances of the Lord are chaste utterances, silver tried by fire, proved for the earth, purged sevenfold. Of gold and silver alike Psalm 67 (68) speaks, in which it is written: If you sleep in the midst of the lots, the wings of a dove covered with silver, and the hinder parts (or the inner parts) of her back in the greenness (or paleness) of gold. All indeed the glory of the king's daughter is within, she who speaks in the Song of Songs: The king has brought me into his chamber. Whoever has slept and rested between the two testaments at once takes up the silvered wings of the dove, and will shine with the brightness of gold in the treasury of the heart.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.68.14":["While the heavenly one distinguishes kings over her. By \"kings\" he meant the holy ones, who reign over their own passions. These holy ones, then, are divided among the churches: for example, Paul among the Gentiles, Peter among the circumcision. They shall be made white as snow in Selmon. This is what he says: although they are holy, yet nevertheless in this world they are as it were a shadow, knowing in part. For Selmon is interpreted \"shadow.\"\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.15":["The mountain of God, a rich mountain. This is the mountain which in Ezekiel wounds the prince of Tyre. And by \"mountain\" they mean the Lord himself.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.16":["Why do you suppose the curdled mountains? This he says to the Jews, who think that Moses and the prophets are like the Savior. The mountain in which it is well pleasing to God to dwell. It signifies the fleshly presence of the Savior, that the deity dwelt in a body: for in no other did the Holy Spirit dwell continuously, except in the Savior, John saying, \"He upon whom you shall see the Spirit descending and remaining on him, this is he.\" For indeed the Lord shall dwell unto the end: in the body which he took from holy Mary.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.17":["The chariot of God is ten thousandfold manifold. Hyperbolically he speaks of the ministering powers and spirits, that they are innumerable, as he said in Daniel: \"Thousands of thousands ministered.\" And in Ezekiel he describes the cherubim as having four wings in four corners, four faces, whose charioteer is God. And \"cherubim\" is interpreted \"multitude of knowledge.\" And it signifies the face of the lion, the kingly, that is, to reign over the passions; the face of the man, the rational; the face of the calf, in work; the face of the eagle, in contemplation, that is, in theoria, which exalts us in the virtues. But that we may say all at once: the throne of God is in the holy ones. The Lord is among them in Sina, in the holy place: that is, God is in the holy ones when they are tempted, and after the temptation holiness follows. For it is impossible for anyone, however holy he may be, to pass through this world without temptation, according to Job: \"The life of man upon the earth is a temptation.\"\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.18":["You have ascended on high, you have taken captivity captive. You have ascended into the heavens: you have taken us captive, we who were held captive by the devil. You have received gifts among men. He said it well, \"you have received.\" From the Father he received: he receives as man, he gives as God; and what he received, he received for men, that he might give. For he himself is full, and lacks nothing. For indeed those who do not believe that the Lord dwells. And these too you have saved, who did not believe that it is possible for God to dwell in a man, that is, for the Savior to take flesh. The Lord God blessed. These who before did not believe that it is possible for God to dwell in a man, they themselves, believing, say: The Lord God be blessed.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","Now all this was done that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Behold, a virgin shall have in the womb and shall bear. Where the evangelist Matthew says: shall have in the womb, in the prophet it is written: shall receive in the womb. But the prophet, because he foretells things to come, signifies what is to be and writes shall receive; the evangelist, because he narrates history not of the future but of the past, changed shall receive and put shall have. For one who has is by no means going to receive. Something of the kind we read in the psalms too: Ascending on high he led captivity captive, he received gifts among men. The Apostle, setting down this testimony, did not say received, but gave, because there it was signified of the future that he would receive, here it is told of him who had already given what he had received.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.68.19":["Blessed be the Lord day by day. He who makes progress can bless God daily. For what does it profit me, if I blessed yesterday, and today I cannot bless? The God of our salvations will make our journey prosperous. Be secure, O sinner, do not doubt that you can daily bless God: for behold, he says, God will make our journey prosperous. Therefore God helps us, and works together with us. Of our salvations. He said it well in the plural, \"of salvations\": because however many sins we have committed, so many salvations also do we have. Diapsalma. In the Hebrew it has \"sela,\" that is, \"always and continually\": therefore God always helps us.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.20":["Our God is the God of saving, and the outcomes of death belong to the Lord: because he himself rose from the dead, and made us rise together with him. Otherwise, the devil is the entrance of death: but the Lord leads out.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.21":["But nevertheless God will shatter the heads of his enemies: of the devil, and all his powers. \"You have shattered the heads of the dragon in the water.\" As many as the sins are, so many heads does the devil have. The crown of the hair of those who walk in their own trespasses. For his head is so shattered, that there does not remain in him any power by which he may goad those who sin. The topmost of the head is the hair, and the crown of the hair: therefore he says this, that there should not remain in the devil even the least thing that is not shattered.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.22":["The Lord has said, From Basan I will turn back: that is, from the confusion of this world I will turn back the human race; that is, through my passion and cross, which these people think foolishness and confusion, I will turn back my people. Since indeed Basan is interpreted \"confusion.\" I will turn back in the depths of the sea: that is, from the deep vices and sins of this world, from the commotion and the waves I will turn back my people.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","Let us say, then, according to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], that the oars of the Tyrian ship are made from Bashan — of which it is also written in the Psalms: The Lord said: From Bashan I will turn back, I will turn back from the depth of the sea; Basan in our language means 'ignominy' —; those, then, who were in the depth of the sea and in the ignominy of sins, the Lord turns back, and turns them into oars, so that, sailing with the apostles, they may reach the land and the shores; and benches are made of ivory when they have mortified their bodies, or have devoted the use of their teeth to the praises of God; and cabins or storerooms from the islands of 'Chetthiim', which, according to the ἐτυμολογιὰν [etymology] of the Hebrew language, is rendered 'struck', so that it has been not so much killed as tested by the blows of the devil.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.68.23":["That your foot may be dipped in blood. Because his own foot was dipped in his own blood. \"I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me.\" And again: \"Who is this who ascends from Edom? his garments red from Bosor.\" Edom is interpreted \"blood,\" Bosor \"flesh\": it signifies our Lord himself crucified. The tongue of your dogs from the enemies, from him. By \"dogs\" he means the Jews themselves, who cried out: \"Crucify, crucify such a one: we have no king but Caesar.\" And \"from the enemies\": prompted by the demons to deny the Savior, and to blaspheme him. Otherwise: because you yourself came of your own will and suffered, and of your own will you were crucified by the Jews.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.24":["They have seen your entrances, O God: because in the sight of five hundred men and all the apostles and the cherubim and all the angels you ascended into the heavens. The entrances of my God, of my king who is in the holy place. Because you who are the God of all and the Lord of those who rule and the King of kings, in a special way you are my God, just as also the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob and the God of the Hebrews.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.25":["The princes went before, joined with those making melody. The apostles themselves went before, and those who through them believed followed: or certainly the churches. But he says \"of the timbrel-players\" because their body is dead to sin. And Miriam the sister of Moses and Aaron, holding a timbrel in Exodus, went before those making melody, and said: \"Let us sing to the Lord, for gloriously has he been honored.\"\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.26":["In the churches bless God. O you who glory in abstinence, do this in the churches, and do not defend heresy for your own selves. These things can be said to Tatian, who is the chief of the Encratites. The Lord from the fountains of Israel. The Manichaean cannot bless the Lord, because he does not bless from the fountains of Israel: that is, because he does not receive the Old Testament. From the fountains of Israel, not of Jacob: since indeed Jacob is interpreted \"supplanter,\" and is still in the wrestling; but Israel is perfect, seeing God with the mind.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.27":["There is Benjamin the young lad in ecstasy of mind. He means holy Paul: \"there,\" in the church: \"the young lad,\" the last of all the apostles, of the tribe of Benjamin.\n\nThe princes of Judah their leaders. The apostles are the princes of Judah, that is, of the Lord, just as Jacob says: \"Judah, may your brothers praise you.\" The princes of Zabulon, the princes of Nephthalim. Zabulon is interpreted \"flowing of the night,\" Nephthalim \"breadth\": when we have been freed from the passion of lust, that is, from that flowing, then we begin the broad spaces of the virtues.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","Hence too it is said in the sixty-seventh Psalm: Bless the Lord from the fountains of Israel. There is Benjamin the younger (Ps 67 [68]:27-28; Acts 9); Paul the apostle, in ecstasy of mind, who also said elsewhere: whether we are beside ourselves, it is for God (2 Cor 5:13): The princes of Zebulun, the princes of Naphtali, their leaders; because in these tribes were the little villages from which our leaders the Apostles believed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.68.28":["Confirm this, O God, which you have worked in us. This salvation, which you have granted us through your passion, confirm, and give aid; because we are men, and we cannot advance in the virtues, if we have not obtained your help.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.29":["To you the kings shall bring gifts. He says \"kings,\" those who reign over their own selves, according to what is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" These shall bring gifts to God. The virtues of the holy ones are gifts to God.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.30":["Rebuke the wild beasts of the reed, that is, the heretics, who compose and write against you, so as to deceive us. \"For there must also be heresies among us.\" For they are called \"wild beasts\" from the fact that they seize souls and kill them. But it can be understood also of the devil and his angels. The assembly of bulls among the cows of the peoples. It signifies the heretics themselves, because they can deceive no one, except the simpler ones and the little ones, that is, the lesser cows: for they cannot deceive the bulls, that is, the great ones. That they may shut out those who have been proved with silver. And for this reason they deceive the simpler ones, that is, the multitude, so as to come even to the holy ones, and shut them out from the kingdom of heaven. Scatter the nations that desire wars: that is, scatter the heretics, who attack the church.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.31":["Legates shall come out of Egypt. Egypt is interpreted \"darkness\": out of the darkness of this world. Ethiopia shall stretch out her hand before to God. Because we who were black with sins and passions went before the Israelite people, and believe in the Savior: just as she who had a flow of blood went before the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue, and is healed.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.33":["Who ascends above the heaven of heaven to the east. Because above he said, Who ascends above the sunset; here he says, to the east. For when the passions and the sun of iniquity set for us, the sun of righteousness rises for us: that is, the Lord ascends over us, and dwells in us. Behold, he will give to his voice a voice of power. \"Lazarus, come forth.\"\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67","And what follows: As far as the torrent of the west, we shall interpret from the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm, in which it is written: Sing to the Lord, sing a psalm to his name; make a way for him who ascends above the sunset. The Lord is his name. For unless evil works have set in us, Christ will not ascend above us. And when they have set, and we have Christ as our charioteer, then, as we advance and go on to better things, it will be commanded us in the same psalm: Sing to God, sing psalms to the Lord, who ascends above the heaven of heaven toward the east. Hence also in the mysteries we first renounce him who is in the west, and who dies to us together with our sins, and so, turned toward the east, we enter into a covenant with the sun of righteousness and promise that we will serve him.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.68.34":["Behold, he will give to his voice a voice of power; his magnificence is over Israel. The magnificence of God is in Israel, who sees God. And his power is in the clouds, that is, in the prophets and apostles. \"I have commanded my clouds, that they rain no rain upon Israel.\"\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.68.35":["God is wonderful in his holy ones. In this God is wonderful, because he so loved the human race that he works signs through his holy ones. In these God is more wonderful than in the rest of the creatures.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"PSA.73.5":["When you see a sinner abounding in riches, boasting of his power, rejoicing in health, delighting in his wife, surrounded by a crown of children, and that which is written being fulfilled: They are not in the labor of men, and with men they will not be scourged, say that in him the prophet's threat has been fulfilled: I will not visit upon your daughters when they have played the harlot and upon your brides when they have committed adultery.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.75.1":["For the end, do not corrupt, a psalm of Asaph, a song. In the Hebrew it does not have \"for the end,\" but has \"to the victor.\" And the Seventy translators did not err greatly, since victory is something perfected. Well did he say, For the end, do not corrupt. This phrase is interpreted in many ways. David says even to one of his friends: \"Do not corrupt him\" — that is, Saul. See what he says. And in another place we read that David blessed the Lord because his hands were kept — that is, that he should not slay Saul. And so we too, when we do not kill anyone, our hands are kept. Some say that this psalm was composed by David and sung by Asaph; others, however, that Asaph himself both composed and sang it. Therefore, according to the Seventy, he says this: O Lord, who have kept me from laying my hand upon your anointed Saul, keep me even to the end.\n— Homily 8, on Psalm 74","We will confess to you, O God. Here \"we will confess\" does not signify penance, but glory. And we will call upon your name. The name of God is Father: for previously he was unknown. For the Lord says: \"Father, I have revealed your name to men.\" For every son is the name of the father.\n— Homily 8, on Psalm 74","We will tell of your wondrous works. This the Church says, this the multitude of believers says. We will tell of your wondrous works. This verse is joined with the preceding ones, but the one that follows is spoken in the person of the Lord: for so the ancients interpreted it.\n— Homily 8, on Psalm 74"],"PSA.75.2":["When I shall have received the time, I will judge righteousness. For now he is not a judge, but he will be: for if he were now a judge, sinners would not be lifted up and obtain riches in the world. Does this scandalize you, that the righteous flee, and sinners persecute them? Does this scandalize you, that iniquity reigns in the world? Hear the Lord saying: When I shall have received the time, I will judge righteousness. For the time of judgment has been reserved. In the present there is no judgment, but a contest. \"For the Father does not judge anyone, but has given all judgment to the Son.\" But hearing this, do not be scandalized and say that the one who receives from the one who gives is lesser; but see what he says in the Gospel: \"For the Father does not judge anyone, but has given all judgment to the Son: because he is the Son of man.\" He renders the reasons why he received judgment: because he is the Son of man. You hear \"Son of man,\" and do you doubt why he received it?\n— Homily 8, on Psalm 74"],"PSA.75.8":["'In the ways', he says, 'of your sister Samaria you walked; therefore you must drink the cup of her whose sins you followed — and that a cup is often taken for punishments and torments, there are many examples, among which is this: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of pure wine, mixed. And he has tilted it from this; yet its dregs are not emptied: all the sinners of the earth shall drink; and in Isaiah it is called 'the cup of ruin' and 'the draught of fury'; and God speaks to Jeremiah, saying: Take the cup of pure wine from my hand, and you shall give drink to all the nations to which I shall send you; and they shall drink and vomit, and go mad; for just as certain cathartics are given so that the noxious humor that is in bodies may be purged out, so the Lord too gives a most undiluted cup of torments, to take away from sinners whatever there is of gall and bitterness and restore them to their former health — ; and the cup itself will be deep and wide: deep in the magnitude of the punishments, wide in the length of the captivity; but when', he says, 'you have been made drunk and seized by sorrow, and have drunk the cup to the dregs and drained it to such a degree that you devour even the fragments of the cup itself, then you will be weary of your former fornication and will tear the breasts which had been broken in Egypt and which were subdued and crushed by your lovers — or I will take away all your feasts and new moons, so that in place of solemnity and gladness you may have eternal lamentation and grief —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And he is the angel of the testament whom you seek, whom evil things do not please, who does not accept persons in judgement, who is changed by no mercy and no severity: For in the hand of the Lord there is a cup of pure wine, full of mixture, and he has poured it from this into this, that is, mercy tempers justice, and justice mercy.\n— Commentary on Malachi","Second on Mount Sinai, giving her in the betrothal gifts the justice and judgment of the law, and mercy joined to the law, so that when she sinned she would be handed over to captivity, and when she repented she would be called back to her homeland and obtain mercy. Of these betrothal gifts the psalmist sings in various places: Mercy and judgment I will sing to you, O Lord. And: Mercy and truth have met each other, and justice and peace have kissed each other. These two cups we read of in the seventy-fourth (75th) psalm: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of unmixed wine, mixed, and he has tilted it from this into that; from the cup of justice into the cup of mercy, and again from the cup of mercy into the cup of justice. If you mark iniquities, he says, O Lord, Lord, who will endure? For with you there is propitiation.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.76.1":["Before the cross gave light to the world, before the Lord was seen upon the earth, God was known in Judaea, and his name was great in Israel; but when the Savior came, his sound went out into all the earth, and his words unto the ends of the world.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.2":["And his place was made in peace: which in Hebrew is said \"in Salem.\" So it seems, according to the letter, to speak of Jerusalem, that is, Salem, which formerly was called Salem, and afterward Jebus, and then was called Jerusalem. This is that Salem in which Melchizedek was king. We read, \"Thou art a priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek.\" He did not say, after the order of Aaron, but of Melchizedek. What did he mean to say, Thou art a priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek? It is plain that in that passage it is spoken of Christ. But why does he say, after the order of Melchizedek? Aaron offered victims, Aaron poured out the blood of sacrifices; Melchizedek did none of these things, but offered only bread and wine. This, then, is what Melchizedek did: and therefore it is said, after the order of Melchizedek. In the Hebrew it has, \"And his shelter was made in Salem.\" See what he says: according to the tropology, there is no shelter of the Lord except where there is peace. Where there are quarrels and discord, there God is not a protector. Let us speak also according to the Septuagint. The place of God is nowhere except in the soul that has peace: therefore the soul that does not have peace, let it know that it is not the place of God. \"My peace I give unto you, my peace I leave with you.\" This is the Savior's inheritance. And his dwelling is in Zion. According to the letter we can speak of Jerusalem and Zion, since the temple was there; but according to tropology and anagogy we say that the dwelling of God is in Zion. Zion is interpreted \"watchtower\": therefore in whatever soul there is knowledge of the Scriptures and teaching, there God dwells.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.3":["There he broke the strength of bows. There. Where? In Jerusalem and Zion. Jerusalem is interpreted \"peace,\" Zion \"watchtower\": let us see, then, what is peace, and what is the watchtower. Let us interpret it in two ways: either the human soul, in which is the peace of God and contemplation; or else the Church, or else the holy Scripture. For there is peace, and there is the dwelling and the watchtower of God. There he broke the strength of bows: those burning arrows which the devil used to shoot. The shield, and the sword, and the battle. And the battle: that of which it is said in another place, \"Scatter the nations that desire wars.\" And what follows? Selah, which the Septuagint interpret \"diapsalma,\" and in Hebrew it is said \"always.\" See, then, that the Lord always breaks the shield and the sword and the battle.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.4":["Thou dost give light wondrously from the everlasting mountains. We interpret the mountains in two ways: in the Old Testament, the prophets; in the New Testament, the apostles. Of these mountains he says: \"I have lifted up mine eyes unto the mountains, whence shall come my help.\" Upon these mountains also is the city of God set, \"A city set upon a mountain cannot be hid.\" We were all sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death; and the Lord gave light to us from the everlasting mountains, that is, from the prophets and apostles. All the foolish of heart were troubled. This verse we can speak of the unbelievers.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.5":["They slept their sleep, and found nothing. Truly this life is a sleep, and riches too: for when we seem to hold them, at once we lose them. Just as that which is written in the prophet: \"As one who is thirsty sleeps, if he finds water he drinks; afterward, when he has awakened, the thirst itself remains,\" so also are riches in this world: while we hold them, we lose them.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.6":["They slumbered who mounted horses. Let us speak according to the tropology. Indeed Pharaoh mounted horses, and slumbered, and perished. And the Egyptians had horses, but they perished. Hence too it was commanded in the Law that a Hebrew should not have a horse. Indeed Solomon too did not have a horse from Jerusalem or from Judaea, but bought them for himself from Egypt. Horses are always sold in Egypt. \"These in chariots, and these in horses: but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God.\" Those, then, who mounted horses, slumbered and perished. Our Lord also has horses, and he has shining mountains: on the contrary, the devil too has dark mountains. As, then, there are shining mountains and dark mountains, so also are there good horses and again bad horses. We have spoken of the bad horses; let us speak also of the good horses. At that time when they came against Elisha to seize him, the servant went out and saw the army of the Assyrians round about, and Elisha said: \"Fear not, for there are more with us than with them.\" And there it is written: \"Lord, open the eyes of thy servant, that he may see.\" And when his eyes were opened, the servant saw the chariots and horses. These were the helpers. See, then, what he says, chariots and horses. There was no man upon them, but there were only chariots and horses. This is what he means to say: the multitude of angels. They themselves were both chariots and horses: but the rider was the Lord. Therefore he says in the canticle of Habakkuk: \"And thy riding is salvation.\" This is said to God. O that we too might be the horses of God, and that God might deign to ride upon us. Those contrary horses, then, slumbered, and their riders.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.7":["From then thy wrath. From what time is the wrath of God? When the people cried out to Aaron in the wilderness, saying: \"Make us gods whom we may follow: for this Moses, who brought us out of the land of Egypt, we know not what has become of him.\" Thou didst bring them out of the land of Egypt, and immediately they denied thee.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.8-9":["Whoever, then, is humble and quiet and trembles at the words of God, him the Lord looks upon; and of him may be understood what is prophesied under the name of the earth: The earth trembled and was quiet, when God arose to judgment (Ps 75 [76]:9, 10). For its inhabitants, for whom the earth is named μετωνυμικῶς, considering the judgment of God, keep quiet from evil works; and sitting at home, rest in an eternal sabbath, so as not to stir themselves to the servile work of sin, as the builders of the tower once moved their feet from the East and left the rising of the true light. Nor did they hear what is said to Cain: You have sinned, be quiet (Gen 4). But according to the Proverbs of Solomon: He who hears God shall dwell confidently, and shall rest without fear from every evil (Prov 1:33).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.76.8":["From heaven thou didst make judgment to be heard. Thou didst speak from heaven, all the people heard and trembled: and yet they did not believe in thee. The earth feared and was still. But Israel neither feared nor was still.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.10":["For the thought of man shall confess to thee. This is what is said in another place: \"From my hidden faults cleanse me, and from those that are alien spare thy servant.\" Alien to thee, which the devil sends in. And what follows? \"If they shall not have had dominion over me, then shall I be blameless.\" If evil thoughts do not overcome me, then shall I be clean in thy sight. And what follows immediately? \"And I shall be cleansed from the greatest offense.\" See what he says. If the thought does not overcome me and does not carry me through to the deed, I am freed from a great sin. For the thought of man shall confess to thee. \"For none is clean in the sight of God, not even if his life be of a single day.\" \"The stars are not clean in the sight of God,\" \"and against his angels he devised something perverse.\" Why do I say all this? Because if the stars are not clean, and if in the angels something perverse is found, how much more in the thought of men? Where are those who say: \"Depart from me, for I am clean\"? Every day, in what we suffer, we know what is in our thoughts, and we blush to say it. There are many who have not done evil works, there are others who have not even spoken them: but of those who have not sinned in thought, no man is found. Therefore now he says: For the thought of man shall confess to thee.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.76.12":["To the terrible one. To whom? To God. And to him who takes away the spirit of princes. When we see certain powerful men, for example, emperors, prefects, and men exceedingly powerful in this world, let us too say to God: Who takes away the spirit of princes. By \"spirit\" we can speak both of the soul, and we can speak also of pride. Let us understand it also in another way. Who takes away the spirit of princes. Let us here understand \"princes\" as the saints. For example, Solomon was a prince, the other saints too were princes, Judas the apostle also was a prince: and because they sinned, God took away from them his spirit. Let no one, then, say: I am a bishop, I am a presbyter or a deacon or a monk, I am a prince in this world: God is able to take away the spirit of princes. Indeed, that you may know that God takes away the spirit, the good spirit of God departed from Saul, and the malignant spirit choked him. It follows: \"And the evil spirit of God choked him,\" that is, from God. Does God, then, have an evil spirit? By no means: but God had departed for this reason, that afterward the evil spirit might choke him. Therefore the spirit is called God's evil spirit. Indeed, the holy David, knowing that he takes away the spirit of princes, prays to God and says: \"And take not thy holy spirit from me.\"\n\nTo the terrible one among the kings of the earth. This is understood also according to the letter. But may God grant to us also that we may be kings of the earth: kings of the earth, that we may rule over our flesh. Whence the apostle also says: \"Let not sin reign in your mortal body.\" And in another place it is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" Was the heart of Julian the persecutor in the hand of God? Was the heart of Saul in the hand of God? Was the heart of Manasseh in the hand of God? Was the heart of Ahab in the hand of God? And were the hearts of all the impious kings of Judah in the hand of God? You see that it cannot stand according to the letter. Kings, then, are the saints, and the heart of these is in the hand of the Lord. Let us therefore also ask God that we may be kings, and may rule over our flesh, that it may be subject to us. Whence the apostle also says: \"Therefore I chastise my body and bring it into servitude, lest by chance, while preaching to others, I myself should become a castaway.\" Let our soul rule, let the body serve: and at once Christ will come, and will dwell in us. Indeed, what does he himself say in the Gospel? \"I will come,\" he says, \"and will stand before the door, and will knock: and if anyone shall open to me, I will come in,\" he says, \"and will sup with him.\" Every day Christ stands before the door of our breast, he desires to enter. Let us open, he enters into us, and he is our guest and our fellow-diner: that is, he both dwells in us and eats with us.\n— Homily 9, on Psalm 75"],"PSA.77.1":["With my voice I cried out to the Lord: with my voice to God, and he gave heed to me. In the day of my tribulation I sought out God. See how he is in tribulation, and he beseeches God, God, God — and yet God is one. With my voice I cried out to the Lord. A cry is necessary at that time when he who cries is far off. So what this man says is this: since on account of my sins I am far from you, therefore I cry, that you may hear me. With my voice to God, and he gave heed to me. It is the same thing he says also in another place: \"To the Lord, when I was in tribulation, I cried, and he heard me.\" In the day of my tribulation I sought out God. He is speaking of a difficult thing. When we are in tribulation, we are cast down to the ground, we think of nothing else but our trouble; but this man in his tribulation is prompted to beseech God. For example: I have lost my son, my house has been burned, I have come to beggary — I do not seek what I have lost, but I seek the Lord. For if I find him, in him I find all things. \"Seek first the kingdom of God, and all these things shall be added unto you.\"\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.2":["With my hands by night before him: and I was not deceived. In the Hebrew it reads otherwise: \"My hand is stretched out by night, and does not rest.\" With my hands by night before him. Understand: I cried out. See, then, that our very works cry out to the Lord while we ourselves are silent. Therefore it is said: \"The word of the Lord came in the hand of the prophet Aggeus.\" It does not come to him in his mouth, that is, in speech, but in his hand, that is, in work. \"For the kingdom of God is not in speech\" but in the hand, that is, in work. With my hands by night: that is, I cried out, I cried out with my hands. Therefore it is also said to Moses: \"Why do you cry to me?\" And surely it is not written there that Moses cried to God; but because his heart cried \"Abba, Father,\" and his heart cried out, therefore he says, Why do you cry to me? Hence it is that it is said in Jeremiah: \"The pupil of my eye was not silent.\" See, then, that even the pupil of our eye is not silent, but as often as we shed tears, so often do we cry out to God with our hands. By night before him. Hence it is said: \"In the nights lift up your hands to the holy places, and bless the Lord.\" In the night of this world and in the darkness, while others hastened to their vices, I lifted up my works to you alone. And I was not deceived. Because I cried out thus, and cried out in work, I was not deceived. It is understood in two ways: either I obtained what I wished, or at least I was not deceived by the snares of the devil, which were eager to encircle me. But in the Hebrew it is written: \"My hand is stretched out by night, and does not rest.\" The hand is always stretched out to virtues, it is never drawn back for sins.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.3":["My soul refused to be comforted. Insofar as it depended on my sins, I could not comfort myself with having no hope. I remembered God, and I was delighted. Insofar as it lay in my sins, I could find no comfort; again I thought of God, and in his mercy I was delighted. In the Hebrew it reads otherwise: \"I remembered God, and I was troubled.\" I considered his gentleness, I considered his tenderness, I considered his purity; and seeing myself unclean, I was vehemently troubled. And I was exercised, and my spirit failed. In the Hebrew it reads better: \"I was speaking within myself, and my spirit failed\" — whether from despair, or from longing for God.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.4":["My eyes anticipated the watches. In the Hebrew it reads otherwise: \"I held in check the gaze of my eyes, I was struck dumb and did not speak.\" What he means is this: considering my sins, I did not dare to lift my eyes to heaven. \"For to the sinner God has said: Why do you declare my statutes?\" Therefore I was struck dumb, I was afraid, I could not speak. This is according to the Hebrew. My eyes anticipated the watches. Before anyone else was awake, I was awake. We read also in Daniel: \"And behold,\" he says, \"an ir,\" that is, a watcher. \"He shall not slumber nor sleep, who keeps Israel.\" Therefore the monk who does not keep watch cannot say this verse. I was troubled, and I did not speak. This is what the apostle says: \"Let not the sun go down upon your wrath.\" I was troubled, as a man; I did not speak, as a Christian. Anger began in my heart, but I did not bring the anger forth into words.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.5":["I thought upon the days of old, and I had the eternal years in mind. Insofar as it lay in my sins, I was despairing, and my soul had refused to be comforted. I began to think from the beginning of the world, when God made man, all the way to my own year, from Adam down to my own time.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.6-10":["Seeing and reading these things, such great mysteries, let us cry out with the Apostle and say: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God, how inscrutable are your judgments, and your ways unsearchable. The prophet, too, perceiving this and turning it over within himself, conjectures concerning the judgments of God: In the night I was exercised with my heart, and I swept my spirit, and I was saying: Will God cast off for ever, or will he not add to have mercy any more, or will he hold back his mercies in his wrath? And I said: Now I have begun; this is the change of the right hand of the Most High. And the sense is: This, that I thought the Lord was abandoning sinners for ever and, with wrath taking its place, restraining his mercies, I understood to have been done for this reason, that by the change of his right hand, which is the right hand of the Most High, he might change all things and have mercy on those whom he had previously cast away.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"PSA.77.6":["I meditated by night with my heart, and I dug over my spirit. I was not thinking of my anger, I was not thinking of my enemy; but my whole thought was of God. The night, which is wont to be either for rest or for lust, was for me a thinking upon the virtues. I meditated with my heart, and I dug over my spirit: that is, I dug like a field, that there I might cast the seed of the Lord's teachings.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.7-9":["Will God cast off for ever? This was my whole thought. God made man from the earth, and promised him eternal life: how then was he cast out of paradise, out of the kingdom of God? And will he no more be pleased to show him favor? Or will he cut off his mercy for ever, from generation and generation? That is, will he cast off both generations, both of the former people and of the later? Suppose he had cast off the people of the Jews: will he cast off the people of the Gentiles too? Or will he in his anger hold back his mercies? He could not have said it more beautifully. However long he restrains himself so as not to have mercy, yet his mercy conquers him.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.10":["And I said, Now I have begun. In the Hebrew it reads otherwise: \"And I said, It is my weakness\" — that is, what I suffer is not from the cruelty of God, but from my sins. This is the change of the right hand of the Most High. That the Lord should have mercy, that he should give his grace to the whole world, that he should not hold back his mercies in his anger — this is the change of the right hand of the Most High. Unless his right hand, that is, his Son, had been changed and had taken the body of a man, we could not receive mercy. And I said, Now I have begun: this is the change of the right hand of the Most High. \"Who, though he was established in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant.\"\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.11-12":["I was mindful of the works of the Lord: which he had done for Moses, which he had done for the saints. For I will be mindful of your wonders from the beginning: and I will meditate on all your works, and I will be exercised in your devices. My whole thought is of your virtues. I consider how you had mercy on those men, and about myself I do not despair.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.13-14":["God, your way is in the holy place. Whoever, then, is not holy, the way of God is not in him. What is the way of God? \"I am the way, and the life, and the truth.\" This the Savior says. The way of God, then, is the Son. But the way of God is only in the holy one. If we wish that Christ should dwell in us, let us be holy: for the way of God is in the holy one. What God is great like our God? For just as \"there are many gods, and many lords\" (this the apostle says. \"God stood in the assembly of the gods.\" \"I have said: you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High.\" And to Moses, \"I have made you a god to Pharaoh\"): they are called holy gods. Those are gods according to grace, but you are God by nature. You are the God who does wonders. He did not say, who has done; but, who does. Daily God does wonders, and works — both the Son, and the Holy Spirit. You are the God who does wonders. Yesterday a robber, today a Christian: yesterday a fornicator, today continent: yesterday you were seizing what belonged to others, now you hand over what is your own.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.15":["You have made known your power among the peoples. \"Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God\": \"the mystery which had been hidden from the ages\"; now you have made known your power among the peoples. You have redeemed with your arm your people. \"Lord, who has believed our report? and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed?\" You have redeemed with your arm your people: that is, in Christ. The sons of Jacob and of Joseph. Both peoples, both of the Jews and of the Christians: Jacob, in the people of the Jews, and Joseph, in the people of the Gentiles. Indeed, Joseph is interpreted in the Hebrew tongue as \"increase.\" Those, then, went before, and we have been joined on: therefore it is said \"the sons of Jacob and of Joseph.\"\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.16-18":["The waters saw you, O God, the waters saw you, and they feared. For the moment let us speak according to the letter; let us cling to the earth for the sake of the simpler folk. When the people passed through the Red Sea and through the Jordan, the waters understood their Creator, and the people did not understand. And the depths were troubled. \"The sea saw, and fled: the Jordan was turned back.\" The great sound of the waters. \"The clouds shook out the waters,\" it has in the Hebrew. The clouds gave forth their voice. And this they interpret of the time when God was seen on Mount Sinai. For your arrows pass through: the voice of your thunder in the wheel. And this they interpret of the lightnings, and they say: When God thunders, he makes a sound like a wheel. You see how I still cling to the earth and speak according to the letter that kills.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76","Your lightnings shone upon the world. Here what do you do, O Jew? Your way is in the sea, and your paths in many waters. Make even this to be said either of the sea or of the Jordan. And your footsteps shall not be known. This, according to the letter, how do you understand it? For the sake of the simpler folk we have spoken according to the letter: let us return, then, to the spiritual understanding. The waters saw you, O God. Let us speak from the Revelation of John. \"The many waters,\" he says, \"are many peoples.\" The waters saw you, O God. What waters? \"And let the waters that are above the heavens praise the name of the Lord.\" The waters saw you, O God. What waters? Pure minds and souls. The waters saw you, and they feared. The fear was not from hatred, but from faith. And the depths were troubled. Let us say something according to the spiritual understanding. In the beginning of Genesis it is written: \"And over the waters,\" it says, \"the Spirit was borne.\" And then it is written: \"Over the depths,\" it says, \"darkness was borne.\" See, then, what is said in the beginning of Genesis. This according to the spiritual understanding. Over the waters, it says, the Spirit was borne. Already at that time baptism was signified. For there could be no baptism without the Spirit. But over the depths, darkness. This is the abyss into which the demons beg not to be sent by Jesus. Over the depths, then, darkness. In the waters was cleanness, in the depths darkness. What the depths are, you understand while I am silent: since the demons dread to go into the abyss. The clouds gave forth their voice. The clouds are the prophets: who kept silence to the Jews, but spoke to us. The clouds gave forth their voice. For your arrows have passed through. The preaching of the word of Christ has run into the whole world. The voice of your thunder in the wheel. Thunder is properly a great voice. Indeed, at that time when God spoke, \"This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased,\" there was made a voice of thunder like a wheel. Indeed, therefore it is written: \"Deep calls to deep in the voice of your cataracts\": that is, the two testaments, the new and the old. The voice of your thunder in the wheel: the voice of your preaching in the wheel. Therefore it is called a great voice, because the preaching is great. The voice of your thunder in the wheel: in the whole world round about. It is not only heard in Judea: but you speak, and it is heard unto the age. This we have said in a general way: but let us speak also in particular of the inner man. The wheel stands upon the earth with only some small trace of contact, and it not only stands, but as it were runs on: it does not stand still, but touches and passes by: finally, when it is rolled, it always mounts up to higher things. So too the holy man, because he is in the body, has the necessity of thinking some things about earthly matters; and when he has come to food and clothing and the rest of such things, having food and clothing, with these he is content, touching the earth, and he hastens to higher things. In him who runs and hastens to higher things, in him is your word. We read also in the prophet: \"Holy stones are rolled upon the earth.\" See what he said: Holy stones are rolled upon the earth. For because they are wheels, therefore they are rolled upon the earth and hasten to higher things. Do you wish to know of more wheels? We read: \"And a wheel within a wheel\"; we read in Ezekiel: \"The wheels cling one to another.\" The two wheels are the new and the old testament. This wheel clings to that, and that to this. Yet see what is said in Ezekiel: \"And wherever,\" it says, \"the spirit was leading, thither the wheels also followed.\" This is said also in Ecclesiastes, at the consummation of the world: \"And the wheel,\" it says, \"shall be rolled over the cistern.\" There are many things that might be said about wheels: but the discourse hastens on to the rest.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76","Your lightnings shone upon the world: the apostolic preaching. The earth was moved and trembled. Unless our earth had been moved and troubled, we would not be Christians. The earth was moved and trembled. For the Spirit of the Lord rests upon no one, save upon him who is humble and quiet and trembles at his words.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.19":["Your way is in the sea: where the waves are, where the bitter water is, where the dragon dwells, where the little tiny fishes cling. Your way is in the sea. You who were in heaven came down to earth. According to the letter we can say: At the time when Moses led the people out, it was you who led them out. Your way is in the sea. Then we can also, according to the history, say it otherwise: The seas were a way for you. But that he did once; for the rest, daily he speaks to the sea: \"Be still, be silent.\" The Lord calms you, that we may be able to pass through this world. Your way is in the sea. He came, the fountain of life, to sweeten the sea, the bitter sea, the Dead Sea. Therefore, according to Ezekiel, there goes forth from the temple of the Lord a fountain of living water, and it goes to the Dead Sea, which had nothing living. For no little fishes could be in the Dead Sea, nothing lived in the Dead Sea. From the temple of the Lord, that is, from his bosom, the Savior went forth, and sweetened the Dead Sea and the bitter waters. Your way is in the sea. And your paths in many waters. Therefore your way is in the sea, that you might make a way, and your apostles might follow you. And your paths in many waters.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.77.20":["And your footsteps shall not be known: you led out your people like sheep. The good shepherd led out his people like sheep. By the hand of Moses and Aaron. This he did according to the letter: for the rest, daily he does it in us. By the hand of Moses, that is, in the law (and we know that the law is spiritual): in the spiritual law. And of Aaron: that is, in the priesthood. In your spiritual law, and in your spiritual priesthood, you led out your people. Let us understand this according to the letter of Moses and Aaron. Moses indeed was led out, and Aaron, but not led into the land of promise. Jesus succeeded him. See what is written. Moses died in the desert, Aaron died, Miriam died; and this is what is written in the prophet: \"And I will strike,\" it says, \"three shepherds in one month.\" They died: for they could not enter into the land of promise. They only saw over against the land of promise, and could not enter. The Jews saw the land of promise, and could not enter. For they died in the desert, and their corpses are in the wilderness, of those who died in the desert. We, the sons, under Jesus as leader, came to the Jordan, and entered into the land of promise, and came to Gilgal, and were a second time circumcised with a spiritual circumcision, and the reproach of Egypt was taken away from us. Then Jesus himself our leader holds the sword, and always goes before us, and fights for us, and conquers our adversaries, and for seven days we go around Jericho, that is, this world. We sing with the priestly trumpets, and we go around Jericho, that is, this world, and its walls fall, and we enter in, and we are the victors. Then we conquer the city of Ai: afterward we come to Jebus, we come to Hazor, we come to other cities; we conquer the adversaries whom under Moses we could not conquer. Let us give thanks, then, to our leader Jesus: for with him as leader, with him fighting, we conquer. He commanded the sun to stand still, and it stood still, and that great man was a type of this one: he was commanding, but the Lord was accomplishing it. He came forth the victor: he, as I have often said, slew five kings in the cave: he led us into the land of promise. To him let us give thanks; he is the son of Nun, which we read as Nave: he it is who was called Osee, which we read as Ause. For according to the Hebrew truth it is not said Ause, but Osee, that is, \"savior.\" Truly a savior: for he saved us, and led us out of the desert, and led us into the land of promise.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"PSA.78.1":["Divine Scripture commands that, when we have been invited to a rich man's banquet, we should with understanding put forth our hand to the dishes. And so for us too the banquet of Scriptures has been set out as a rich man's feast. We have come into a meadow; it has very many flowers: here the rose is red, there the lilies gleam white, there are flowers of every kind. Our soul is drawn this way and that, to gather from wherever it may the more beautiful flowers. If we pick the rose, we leave the lily behind; if we take the lily, the violets are still left for us. So too in the seventy-seventh psalm, which is mystical and wrapped up in very many sacred mysteries, whatever letter you look at, they are flowers, flowers of every kind, and we cannot choose them all. But let us choose all that we can: from a few we can also contemplate the remaining excellence of the flowers.\n\n\"The understanding of Asaph.\" It is headed with this title. The very title summons us to understanding. Asaph was one of the choir-masters, just as also were the sons of Korah, and Idithun, and the rest. Therefore this man too is one of the prophets from among the choir-masters. Since, then, the understanding is straightforward, and in the psalm a history is narrated, we are compelled of necessity to understand everything that is written according to that understanding. It is written: \"In the land of Egypt, in the field of Tanis: he split the sea and led them out; he set the waters as if in a wineskin, and led them out in a cloud by day, and all night in the illumination of fire,\" and so on. We see that a straightforward history is narrated. If, then, we understand it just as it is written, the title \"The understanding of Asaph\" has been prefixed for no reason. But if we understand it as the title indicates, we are compelled of necessity to have one thing in the letter and to find another in the spirit.\n\n\"Attend, my people, to my law; incline your ear to the words of my mouth. I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter propositions from the beginning.\" You see that it is well written above, \"The understanding of Asaph.\" Indeed, the very prophecy begins thus: \"Attend, my people, to my law\" — not Moses' law, but \"my law.\" \"Incline your ear to the words of my mouth.\" This is what he says in the Gospel: \"He who has ears to hear, let him hear.\"\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.2":["\"I will open my mouth in parables\": to you I will open what is closed to the Jews; to you I will open my mouth in parables. \"And without a parable,\" it says, \"he did not speak to them\"; but to his own disciples he unfolded the parables in private. \"I will open my mouth in parables\": what I speak to those others outwardly in parables, to you I will open up inwardly. \"I will utter propositions from the beginning.\" In place of \"propositions\" the Hebrew has \"riddles (enigmas).\" Therefore everything that is said is a riddle.\n\nThe riddle is not that which is said, but something else which is signified by the riddle. Let someone say to me: You do violence to Scripture; for since the title has \"The understanding of Asaph,\" you interpret this saying for me from the person of Christ. If we are Christians (and we are Christians, we who have received the baptism of Christ, and not only confess but also profess it) — because we are Christians, we ought to believe the evangelists. It is written in the Gospel according to Matthew: \"When,\" it says, \"our Lord and Savior had spoken in parables, and those men did not understand,\" and the rest of what is written in the Gospel. And afterward what did the evangelist add? \"All these things,\" he says, \"were done, that there might be fulfilled that which is written: I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter propositions from the beginning.\" You see, therefore, that the evangelist Matthew understands this from the person of Christ. For when he had introduced the Lord speaking in parables, and those men did not understand: \"This,\" he says, \"was done, that there might be fulfilled that which is written, I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter propositions from the beginning.\"\n\nIt seems to us that no question has remained, but attend carefully: for it is my concern not to declaim in the manner of a rhetorician, but to grasp the sense of the Scriptures. Indeed, what did the Lord and Savior himself say? \"Search the Scriptures, and there you will find how it is written concerning me.\" And he said another thing to the Jews: \"You err, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God.\" See what he says. \"You err.\" Why? \"Not knowing the Scriptures.\" But since you do not know the Scriptures, therefore you do not know Christ, who is the power of God and the wisdom of God. It is said, then, in Matthew: \"These things,\" it says, \"were done, that there might be fulfilled that which is written in Asaph the prophet.\" Thus it is found in all the old codices, but ignorant men removed it.\n\nIndeed, many gospels to this day have it thus: \"That there might be fulfilled that which is written through Isaiah the prophet: I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter propositions from the beginning.\" This is not spoken by Isaiah, but by Asaph. Indeed, that impious Porphyry too puts forward this very thing against us, and says: \"Your evangelist Matthew was so unskilled that he said, that which is written in Isaiah the prophet, I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter propositions from the beginning.\" Therefore let us say it simply: just as, in the Gospel, it is written — it is written in Matthew and John that our Lord was crucified at the sixth hour, while again it is written in Mark that he was crucified at the third hour. This seems to be a discrepancy, but it is not a discrepancy. It was an error of the copyists: in Mark too \"the sixth hour\" had been written, but many, in place of the Greek episēmon (the numeral sign), thought it was a gamma. For just as there it was an error of the copyists, so here too it was an error of the copyists, that in place of Asaph they wrote Isaiah. For not knowing (since the early church was gathered from unskilled Gentiles) — therefore, when they read in the Gospel \"That there might be fulfilled that which is written in Asaph the prophet,\" the one who first was copying the Gospel began to say: Who is this Asaph the prophet? He was not known among the people. And what did he do? So that while he corrected the error, he made an error. Let us say something similar, and in another place in the Gospel according to Matthew — when Judas brought back the thirty pieces of silver, and the priests were unwilling to accept it or to put it into the corban, since it was the price of blood; and from it they bought the potter's field for the burial of strangers. The price of Christ is our burial; and that field is called Akeldama, which is \"the field of blood.\" It is the field of blood of the Jews, but it is our burial. For we were strangers and sojourners, we had nowhere to rest. He was crucified and died, and we were buried together with him. \"That there might be fulfilled,\" it says, \"that which is written in Jeremiah the prophet. He brought back,\" it says, \"the thirty pieces of silver, the price that was written,\" and so on. \"As it is written,\" it says, \"in Jeremiah the prophet.\" This is written in Matthew. We searched in Jeremiah, we could not find this at all; but we found it in Zechariah. You see, therefore, that here too there was an error, just as there.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77","That there might be fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet, saying: I will open my mouth in parables; I will pour forth things hidden from the foundation of the world. This testimony is taken from Psalm 77 (78). I have read in some codices, and the studious reader may perhaps find the same thing, that in the place where we have set down, and where the common edition has, That there might be fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet, saying, there is written: through Isaiah the prophet, saying. Because this was nowhere found in Isaiah, I think it was afterwards removed by prudent men. But it seems to me that originally it was published thus, namely that it was written: through Asaph the prophet, saying (for Psalm 77 (78), from which this testimony is taken, is inscribed with the title of Asaph the prophet), and that the first copyist did not understand Asaph, thought it a copyist's fault, and corrected it to the name of Isaiah, whose name was better known. It should be known, therefore, that in the psalms and hymns and songs of God not only David but also the others whose names stand at their head are to be called prophets, namely Asaph and Jeduthun and Heman the Ezrahite and Ethan and the sons of Korah and the rest whom Scripture mentions. And as for what is said in the person of the Lord, I will open my mouth in parables; I will pour forth things hidden from the foundation of the world, one must consider it more attentively, and it will be found that the going out of Israel from Egypt is described there, and all the signs are narrated that are contained in the history of Exodus. From this we understand that all those things that are written are to be taken parabolically, and that it is not so great a letter that sounds out plainly, but hidden mysteries too. For this is what the Savior promises he will speak, opening his mouth in parables and pouring forth things hidden from the foundation of the world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.78.3-5":["\"How great the things we have heard and known.\" These little verses, from \"Attend, my people, to my law\" up to this verse, \"I will utter propositions from the beginning,\" are spoken from the person of Christ; the Savior himself speaks. Now the remaining verses, up to the end of the psalm, we must take as from the person of the apostles. When, then, the Lord says, \"Incline your ear to the words of my mouth\" — O apostles, what you have heard in secret, he says, proclaim upon the rooftops — what do the apostles say? \"How great the things we have heard and known, and our fathers have told us.\" These things, O Lord, which you speak to us, our fathers the patriarchs and the prophets have also spoken to us. \"And our fathers have told us.\" \"They were not hidden from their children unto another generation.\" Therefore they spoke, so that we, their children, might know. \"And he established a law in Israel. How great the things he commanded our fathers.\" He spoke these things carnally, but we understand them spiritually. \"That they might make them known to their sons\": this is, to us. Therefore he spoke to them, so that they might pass them on to us.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.6-7":["\"The sons who shall be born and shall rise up, and shall tell them to their sons.\" \"In place of your fathers, sons have been born to you.\" We are their sons. They told us, and we have told our sons: we are their sons. \"That they might place their hope in God.\" That they might now no longer believe in idols, but believe in God. \"And not forget the works of God.\" That they might see the order of creation, and from the creation understand the Creator. \"And seek out his commandments.\" Let them not despise them like the Jews, but seek them out like Christians.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.8":["\"That they might not become like their fathers.\" That they might not murmur, as certain ones murmured in the desert and perished; that they might not murmur thus like the Jews, and perish in the desert. \"A generation crooked and provoking.\" It is not I who say this: your own prophet says this about you. \"A generation crooked and provoking.\" \"An adulterous and most wicked generation seeks a sign, and a sign shall not be given to it, except the sign of Jonah.\" Why did he say \"crooked\"? \"Because they were turned into a crooked bow.\" For a bow is never drawn against the one who shoots it, but against the adversaries. But this generation, though it had been created upright by God, and was held in the hand of God like a bow, so that he might shoot arrows through it, was turned like a crooked bow, blaspheming against their Creator.\n\n\"A generation which did not direct its heart.\" To this day Israel is crooked. \"And its spirit was not directed with God.\" Since it did not receive the Son, therefore it did not receive the Holy Spirit. Indeed, the Holy Spirit, when it descended in the likeness of a dove, did not descend upon the crowds of the Jews, but upon the one Jesus. If, then, you wish, O Jew, to receive the Spirit, believe in Jesus: since the Holy Spirit rests upon Jesus.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.9":["\"The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow.\" O how great the mysteries, O how many the flowers! I say not a day, but a whole month cannot suffice for the understanding of this psalm. In individual words there are meanings. We have also a treasure in these earthen vessels. Many interpret this of the body and the Holy Spirit: that is, \"we have a treasure in earthen vessels.\" That understanding indeed exists too. But this one is much better, since we have a most precious treasure in earthen vessels, that is, in the rustic words of the Scriptures. \"The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, were turned back in the day of battle. They did not keep the covenant of God, and in his law they were unwilling to walk. And they forgot his benefits, and his wonders which he showed them.\" We read in the books of Kingdoms that under Rehoboam the son of Solomon, Jeroboam the son of Nabat tore the people away from the rest of the people, and led ten tribes into Samaria. But the tribe of Judah and Benjamin remained under the rule of Rehoboam; and many of the tribe of Levi, who dwelt in Jerusalem as priests and Levites, as is written in Chronicles, returned to the temple of God, that is, in Jerusalem. There were, then, three tribes, that is, in Judea: Judah itself the royal tribe, and Benjamin, and afterward the Levites, where they had come from various tribes to the temple. Those, then, who were in Samaria had a king from the tribe of Ephraim. For just as those who reigned in Judea had a king from the tribe of Judah and from the lineage of David, so also those who reigned in Samaria had a king from the tribe of Ephraim, and their kings were called Ephraim. Let us read Hosea the prophet, and we find his whole prophecy against Ephraim; and it is said there: \"Ephraim,\" it says, \"has become like a foolish dove.\" See what he says: \"a foolish dove.\" It has abandoned the temple, and dwells in the woods. For the dove always dwells in houses. But Ephraim, my dove, has abandoned the temple, has abandoned the house, and dwells in the woods, dwells in the deserts.\n\nThis much for now according to the letter; for we ought also to set forth the history. But now, after the letter, we ought as if by certain steps to ascend to greater things. Let us say, then, that the sons of Ephraim are all the heretics. For these too have departed from the house of God, and these too have forsaken David and the kingdom of David, and they dwell in the deserts. And they are indeed called doves, because they too read the Scriptures, but they understand them foolishly. There are many things that might be said. \"The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow.\" Look at all the heretics: who have forsaken Jerusalem, who have forsaken the temple of God, who dwell in Samaria — where did they make the golden calves? In Bethel and in Dan. And see what he says: they made golden calves in Bethel, in the house of God. In the house of God they became idolaters. And they made that thing in Dan, that is, unto their own judgment and unto their condemnation. They forsook the house of the Lord, and they think of nothing else but gold. Let someone say: Why did they make golden calves? Our treasure is stored up in earthen vessels. For the men of the church are rustic and simple, but all the heretics are Aristotelians and Platonists. Indeed, that you may know that all worldly eloquence is called gold — that is, since their tongue, as if for its splendor, is called gold: \"Babylon is a golden cup in the hand of the Lord.\" See what he says: \"Babylon,\" of confusion. Therefore this world is a golden cup. From this golden cup all the nations are given drink.\n\n\"The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow.\" Whomever of the heretics you see, they all have superstitions. \"They were turned back in the day of battle.\" They do not know him who said, \"My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you,\" but they are prepared for words. They have words; but let them hear that which is written: \"Scatter the nations that desire wars.\" Let someone say that it is our own opinion. Let us look at what follows.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.10-11":["\"They did not keep the covenant of God, and in his law they were unwilling to walk, and they forgot his benefits.\" They rose up against him who regenerated them in baptism. \"And his wonders which he showed them: before their fathers the wonders which he did.\" See the sense of the Scriptures, how from the sons of Ephraim it comes to past histories; so that it shows that what is written, \"I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter propositions from the beginning,\" was said parabolically.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.12-14":["\"Before their fathers the wonders which he did in the land of Egypt, in the field of Tanis: he split the sea and led them through; he set the waters as if in a wineskin, and led them down in a cloud by day.\" Let someone say: You do violence to Scripture: for it is said simply of the people of Israel, when they were led out of the land of Egypt, and therefore it is said according to the history; and we read that the ten tribes forsook God, and became idolaters, and that they followed the golden calves. Let us look at the time in which Asaph the prophet lived. Certainly at that time when David lived. But under David the ten tribes had not yet been separated. David reigned forty years; again Solomon another forty years; after eighty years, later under Rehoboam, the ten tribes were torn away. You see, therefore, that what is said is not said of the ten tribes, but it is prophesied concerning the heretics.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.12":["\"Before their fathers the wonders which he did.\" The heretics, then, having forgotten their father, the wonders which he did in the land of Egypt, in the darkness of this world, how he freed them — they were reborn in the church, and afterward, once they were born, the church displeases them. \"In the land of Egypt, in the field of Tanis\": where idolatry was, where the king of Egypt was, where the horsemen of Egypt were, where Jannes and Mambres the adversaries of Moses were, where idolatry was. We were all in Egypt, and we have been freed by the Lord.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.13":["\"He split the sea and led them through.\" The Lord walked upon the sea, and calmed the waves of the sea, and led us through. \"He split the sea and led them through.\" To this day, O faithful monk, when you are led out of Egypt, the sea is split, and thus you are led out. \"He set the waters as if in a wineskin.\" In the Hebrew it does not have \"wineskin,\" but \"heap.\"\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.14":["\"And he led them down in a cloud by day.\" \"Behold, the Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud.\" By the light cloud we ought to understand either, properly, the body of the Savior, because it was light and weighed down by no sin; or certainly by the light cloud we ought to understand holy Mary, weighed down by no human seed. Behold, the Lord comes into the Egypt of this world upon a light cloud, the Virgin.\n\n\"And he led them down in a cloud by day.\" Beautifully he said \"by day\": for that cloud was not in darkness, but always in light. \"And all night in the illumination of fire.\" \"And the darkness is not hidden from you: as the light is to you, so also is the night to you.\" \"And all night in the illumination of fire.\" \"Our Lord is a consuming fire.\" A consuming fire. He did not say what it consumes: he left the understanding to us. Those who have built upon the foundation of Christ wood, hay, stubble — to them the Lord is a consuming fire. Fire has a twofold nature: it both gives light and burns. If we are sinners, it burns; if we are righteous, it gives us light.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.15":["\"He split the rock in the desert.\" The rock has been split for us in the wilderness. The rock was struck, and the waters flowed: that rock which says, \"Whoever thirsts, let him come and drink\"; from whose belly rivers flowed. \"And he gave them water to drink as if from a great abyss.\" In the desert, where there was no water, an abundance of waters was made for us.\n\nThere are many things that might be said, and the psalm is boundless, and we are shut out by time. But let us pray the Lord that for us too he may split the sea, that for us too the rock may pour out waters, lest our corpses too perish and lie with the people in the desert. For you know that the corpses of our fathers lie in the desert to this day. Believe me: as often as I see synagogues, I always have in my mind that which is said by the apostle, that we ought not to exult over the olive tree whose branches were broken, but to fear; since if the natural branches were cut off, how much more should we, who are grafted in from the wild olive, fear lest we become like our fathers, \"a generation crooked and provoking, a generation which did not direct its heart, and whose spirit was not held faithful with God.\" Since they do not have the Spirit of God, therefore they are dead, and their corpses lie in the desert. I understand their synagogues to be nothing else but tombs in the desert. Read the old books. But let us pray the Lord that these tombs too may rise again. For if the children of Abraham have been made from stones, the children of Adam, who are dead, can rise again, if Jesus wills it. For although they are dead and lie in tombs, even if they are four days dead, even if they stink, Jesus can say to them: \"Lazarus, come forth.\"\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77"],"PSA.78.47":["For if they had remained in what they had begun, so as to be like the paradise of God, and had not ended in evil, so as to be like the land of Egypt, their planting would surely have remained. Something of the kind is sounded also by that verse concerning the Egyptians in the psalms: He killed their vineyards with hail, and their mulberry trees with frost. For as a most merciful God he killed and overturned every Egyptian planting, and the shrubs which, rooted in Egypt, bear blood-red fruits, so that those who planted badly may not drink, and may not eat fruit from them.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"PSA.78.67-68":["According to the ἀναγωγὴν [anagogy, the higher sense], we have discussed more fully in the prophet Hosea what Joseph, Ephraim, Samaria, Jezreel and Israel signify, and how they are opposed to the house of David and to Jerusalem, which signifies the Church — of whom Psalm 77 (78) also sounds: The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle, and again: He rejected the tabernacle of Joseph and chose the tribe of Judah; for the Lord chose the house of confession, the Church, and rejected the tabernacle of 'Joseph', which means 'increase'; and they, setting aside the law of God, added golden calves of their own worship —; and she is called 'Oolla', that is, 'tabernacle', not of God but of error and of demons;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.79.8":["Since we have become poor. Since you became poor for our sake, therefore we too have become poor. \"The prayer of the poor man, when he is in anguish, and pours out his prayer before the Lord.\" Since you became poor, though you were rich, we too have become poor.\n— Homily 12, on Psalm 78"],"PSA.79.9":["Help us, O God our Savior. In the Hebrew it has \"Help us, O God our Jesus.\" For wherever there is \"Savior,\" there the Hebrew has \"Jesus.\" Thus Gabriel, when he came to Mary, said, \"And you shall call him Jesus: for he shall save his people.\" Help us, O God our Jesus. See what he says: \"help.\" He who says \"help\" is himself also striving to be set free: for God does not help us while we sleep, but while we labor.\n\nFor the glory of your name, O Lord, deliver us: since your new name has been invoked over us, which is blessed among the nations. And be merciful to our sins for your name's sake. You are the Savior, you are Christ, and we are Christians: have mercy on us for your name's sake.\n— Homily 12, on Psalm 78"],"PSA.79.11":["Let the groaning of the fettered enter into your sight. This is what those who follow the teachings of Origen are accustomed to say: The groaning of the fettered — that is, of the angels, they say, who have been fettered in bodies. For just as, if fetters are put on the feet, they weigh down the body and do not allow it to run; so also our bodies enclose souls — that is, angels — and weigh them down upon the earth. And with what words do they prove this? \"Unhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?\" And that which he says, \"I desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ\" — to return and to be with Christ. See, he says, what the apostle says. Whoever says \"I desire to return\" says it precisely of that place where he had been before. Let the groaning of the fettered enter into your sight. But I, in plain rustic simplicity and in the simplicity of the Church, answer you thus — for so the apostles answered, so they spoke, not with rhetorical and diabolical words: \"For it is better to return and to be with Christ.\" For so it stands in the Greek; it does not have \"to be dissolved\" but \"to return.\" To return, and to be with Christ. Where Adam my father was, there I too long to be; from where he was cast out, I desire to return. In Adam we have all been cast out of paradise. Let the groaning of the fettered enter into your sight. For why should I not understand this to have been said plainly of the martyrs, who were shut up in prisons? For how many were even sent to the mines? But I say that God truly hears the voice of those fettered ones.\n\nAccording to the greatness of your arm possess the sons of those who have been put to death. All of us, if indeed we are worthy, are the sons of the martyrs. The apostle, when he was in prison, was fettered: all the apostles, when they were in prisons, were fettered: all the martyrs who were slain in prisons were fettered. Therefore it is now said, Possess the sons of those who have been put to death.\n— Homily 12, on Psalm 78"],"PSA.79.12":["Render to our neighbors sevenfold into their bosom. It seems to me that he does not ask against them, but for them: that is, give them your sabbath. Now \"sabbath\" is interpreted as rest. What he says is this: make them rest in their wickedness. Or else, otherwise: render it to them here, and do not render it to them in the time to come.\n— Homily 12, on Psalm 78"],"PSA.79.13":["But we your people and the sheep of your pasture. When he says \"sheep,\" he signifies innocence. Sheep have nothing but a shepherd. We will give you thanks forever. We will confess: either we do penance, or, what is better, we glorify. From generation to generation; among the first people, and among the second people. Although according to the historical sense it is said otherwise in Ezekiel, nevertheless according to the spiritual understanding we say this, since these two peoples — that is, the two rods — are joined together in Ezekiel, and become one kingdom. From generation to generation we will proclaim your praise. It is the voice of the apostles: Peter among the people of the circumcision, and Paul and Barnabas among the nations.\n— Homily 12, on Psalm 78","And lest we think that the peoples are the same as the sheep, we read in another place: But we are your people, and the sheep of your pasture. \"People\" refers to all who are rational, but \"sheep\" to those who, not yet using reason, are content with simplicity alone, and are called of the inheritance of God.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"PSA.80.1":["'Cherubim' in our language is 'multitude of knowledge', the knowledge of the mysteries of God, and his throne and resting place; hence also in the psalm it is said: You who sit upon the Cherubim, be made manifest; for the glory of the Lord always sits in the multitude of knowledge, and it is made manifest then: when all things are shown to be ruled by providence, and nothing to happen by chance, and no one to say: The Lord has forsaken the earth, and the Lord does not see; hence also the gaze of the Cherubim and their desire is to forget the things that are past and to stretch themselves forward to the things to come.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.80.13":["But those who after the milk of consolation have advanced to solid food will abound with the delights of truth, and with the knowledge of glory of every kind, which in Hebrew is called ZIZ (זיז). For this the LXX translated entrance; Symmachus fatness; Theodotion multitude. We have said this in order to open an ambiguous word of Psalm 79 (80), in which it is written: The boar from the forest has laid it waste, and the solitary wild beast has fed upon it (Ps 79 [80]:14). For where in our codices and in the Greek one reads μόνιος ἄγριος, that is, the solitary wild beast, in the Hebrew ZIZ SADAI (זיז שדי) is written: which Aquila translated παντόδαπον χώρας, that is, every kind of thing of the region, to signify that Israel was laid waste not only by the Romans but by all the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.81.1":["To the end, for the winepresses, of Asaph. In the Law it is written that there are three solemn feasts: Passover, Pentecost, and the Feast of Tabernacles. Before the Feast of Tabernacles, ten days before, there was a fast, and the fast lasted until evening. At that time they sounded the trumpets — that is, they fasted, and during the fast they sounded the trumpets — and after ten days came the Feast of Tabernacles. Attend carefully. There are three solemn feasts described in the Law: first Passover, then Pentecost, then the Feast of Tabernacles. Before the Feast of Tabernacles, a fast; and during the fast they sounded the trumpets. Notice what Scripture signifies. Lift up the veil that was placed before the eyes of Moses, and see that we cannot become tabernacles unless we first fast. But that very fast, unless it has the sign of victory that frightens off our adversaries, Amalek is not conquered. Since therefore there are three solemn feasts — Passover, Pentecost, and the Feast of Tabernacles — for that reason three psalms too have the title 'for the winepresses': the eighth psalm, and the eightieth, and the eighty-third. You see, then, how three psalms have been written in the psalter that are inscribed 'for the winepresses.' See what it says: the eighth psalm, and the eightieth, and the eighty-third.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80","And before we argue about the number, let us say what these winepresses mean to signify. For the winepresses. Winepresses are found nowhere except where there is a vineyard and an unbounded vintage. For the winepresses. Therefore the Lord and Savior says: 'I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me.' Therefore he transferred a vine out of Egypt and planted it. And he says in Jeremiah: 'I planted you a true vine, wholly chosen: how have you turned into bitterness for me, you strange vine?' And Noah drank wine and became drunk. It is said also by Solomon, said in a mystery: 'Drink, friends, and become drunk.' We read also in another place that Joseph, that holy man who went before as a type of the Savior, who was sold by his brothers, whom his brothers sold, and reigned in Egypt — just as our Lord and Savior too was sold by the Jews and reigned in the Egypt of this world. That Joseph, then, the holy man, that outstanding one, who overcame cruelty with love; who for this reason was sold into Egypt, that he might feed Egypt and his brothers who had sold him — that Joseph, then, invited his brothers. But see: 'And he became drunk at midday.' And is this true, and can it stand according to the letter, that a holy man became drunk? Noah too became drunk, but he had truly become drunk. Joseph was drunk, Noah too became drunk, and he became drunk in his own house. See the mystery. Let us first see what it signifies in mystery, and afterward let us discuss what it signifies in its plain sense. After the flood of this world Noah drank, and became drunk in his own house, and his thighs were uncovered, and his shame appeared. And the elder brother came and mocked; but the younger brother covered him. All this is said of the Savior, since he had drunk his passion on the cross. 'Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me.' He drank, and became drunk, and his thighs were uncovered: that is, the ignominy of the cross. The elder brother came, the people of the Jews, and mocked; and the younger, the people from the nations, covered the ignominy. Therefore 'cursed be Canaan, he shall be a servant to his brother.' Behold, that curse persists to this very day. We, the younger people, command the elder people of the Jews. As therefore the Lord became drunk in his passion, so too the saints are daily made drunk in the ardor of faith, and are made drunk with the Holy Spirit. You who yesterday were gathering gold, today throw it away: do you not seem mad in the eyes of those who do not know you?\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80","Finally the apostles too, because the Holy Spirit had descended upon them and filled them, and they were speaking in various tongues, for that reason were said to be full of new wine.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80","Many are the things that might be said about the mystery of wine and about drunkenness. To the end, for the winepresses, of Asaph. The eighth psalm, and the eightieth, and the eighty-third. No other number could have the winepresses except the eighth, and the eightieth, and the eighty-third. The eighth is the day of resurrection in the single number. Again, in eight decades, the eightieth, another and greater number. You see, then, that these winepresses are prescribed only on the day of resurrection, the eighth, and the eightieth. Someone may say: Why is 'winepresses' said afterward in the eighty-third? These winepresses pertain to the mystery of the Trinity. Therefore there are also three choir-masters. In the eighth it says, 'A Psalm of David'; in the eightieth, 'of Asaph'; in the eighty-third, 'of the sons of Korah.' The eighth is David, which is interpreted 'strong in hand.' For our first victory involves a fight and a struggle: therefore he is called 'David, strong in hand.' But the second, that is in the eightieth, 'of Asaph.' And Asaph is interpreted 'synagogue' (assembly). In the eighth, then, we conquer, and in the eightieth we are gathered together with the Lord. But in the eighty-third, 'of the sons of Korah.' Korah is interpreted 'Calvary' (the place of the skull). 'Go up, bald one; go up, bald one.' This the boys said also to Elisha, because he had suffered in the place of the skull. Many are the things that might be said as to how Elisha was mocked at Bethel by forty-two boys. These are the winepresses of which the Savior too says: 'Make yourselves friends of the unrighteous mammon, that they may receive you into the eternal tabernacles.'\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.4":["For it is a commandment for Israel, and a judgment of the God of Jacob. What is the commandment of God? To sing to God in victory: not to think it one's own, but the Lord's.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.5":["When he came out of the land of Egypt, he heard a tongue which he did not know. We too, when we came out of Egypt, heard a tongue which we did not know. Which of us knew the Gospel? which the Apostle? which the prophets? We came out of Egypt, and we heard a tongue which we did not know; we learned a tongue which we did not know.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.6":["And immediately there follows: He turned his back away from burdens. When we were in Egypt, we were building the cities of Pharaoh, we were carrying mud and brick, and our whole soul was seeking straw. We had no wheat, we had no heavenly bread that comes down from heaven: we were not yet receiving manna from heaven, the serpent had not yet been lifted up for us in the wilderness, the rock had not yet sent us water, we were not yet stretching out our hands and Amalek falling. We sat in the mud, and we did Egyptian works, we were building the cities of Pharaoh. He turned his back away from burdens. How great were the burdens we once had. Therefore our Moses says: 'Come to me, all you who are laden with sins, and I will refresh you.' His hands served in the basket. Whose? Israel's. The baskets of Egypt are one thing, and the baskets of Jesus another. Jesus too has his own baskets, and our Savior filled twelve baskets from the fragments. O wondrous thing, signifying immense mysteries! Five thousand of the people were dwelling in the wilderness, apart from the little ones and the women; in a deserted place there are found only as many as five loaves; for the place was a desert. I ask you, you who follow only the letter: in a deserted place there are found only five loaves — and how are twelve baskets found? If the place was a desert, surely it could not have twelve baskets. But all this is signified: that the Lord led his people out of Egypt into the desert of this world; he fed them with his loaves, and whatever was left over he stored up in twelve baskets — that is, in the twelve apostles; so that what had perished in the twelve tribes might be saved in the twelve apostles.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.7":["In tribulation you called upon me, and I delivered you. The prophets and the psalms are for this reason obscure, because suddenly, when you are not expecting it, the speaker is changed. Up to this point the prophet has been speaking: now suddenly the person of God is brought in, speaking to the people. In tribulation you called upon me, and I delivered you. Whoever you are that are in tribulation, be secure: call upon me, and you will be heard. I heard you in the hiding-place of the tempest. That tempest which was pressing upon you, and dragging you this way and that as if with waves: I was in the midst of the tempest. I proved you at the water of contradiction. According to the letter it is said of Moses and Aaron that at the water of contradiction they offended God. Indeed, for this reason they did not enter the land of promise. But to us God says: I proved you at the water of contradiction. Simon was proved at the water of contradiction, because in hypocrisy he received baptism. Whoever, therefore, receives baptism in hypocrisy, he is proved at the water of contradiction.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.8":["Hear, my people, and I will testify to you. God is speaking to us. Whatever he said to the people, he speaks also to us. Israel, if you will hear me. Why do you ask that I hear you? Let us see what he commands.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.9":["There shall be no new god in you. He whose god is his belly, a new god is his. As many vices as we have, as many sins, so many new gods do we have. I have been angered: anger is a god to me. I have seen a woman, and I have lusted: lust is a god to me. For each one, whatever he desires and reveres, this is his god as well. The greedy man has gold for his god. Nor shall you adore a strange god. Our God is virtue; the god of others, vices and sins.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.10":["For I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt. It is we who have been brought out of the land of Egypt — if indeed we have gone out, if we are in the wilderness, if we long to see the land of promise. If we have left Sodom, let us not look back to Sodom: we have been saved on the mountain. Let us not desire the flesh-pots of Egypt, let us not say: How well off we were in Egypt, when we ate flesh in the cauldrons, and the rest, and the cucumbers. Do you wish to receive the Lord's foods? do you wish to eat your Lord and Savior himself? Hear what he says. Open wide your mouth, and I will fill it. Open wide your mouths: he himself is both the Lord and the bread. He himself exhorts us to eat, and he himself is our food. As much as you open wide, so much will you receive: it is therefore not in my power, but in yours. If you are willing, you will receive me whole; if you are unwilling, you will receive at least a part.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.11":["And my people did not hear my voice. I stand before the door, I knock. I want you to open, and I will come in: and you are unwilling to open. And Israel did not attend to me.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.12":["And I let them go according to the desires of their heart: they shall go on in their own devices. Unhappy is he who is not corrected by God. If anyone sinning is not struck, he is wretched. Therefore he says: I let them go according to the desires of their heart. For this reason I let them go, because I often struck them and they did not accept discipline. I entreated, they did not hear; I struck, they did not feel the pain. I let them go in their own wishes, so that, sated with their vices, they might even so do their repentance.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.13-14":["If my people had heard me. O most merciful Father, O compassionate God! He entreated, and was not heard; as though angered, he lets go the wanton son. Again he sees him perishing, and grieves that he perishes. And what does he say? If my people had heard me, if Israel had walked in my ways, I would perhaps have humbled their enemies for nothing. Was it a great thing for me to deliver you from tribulation? could I not send forth my majesty and rescue you from danger? But because I have left you to free will, and have set the stadium before you, and I do not crown any but the one who has fought, for that reason I leave the victory to your own choice.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.15":["The enemies of the Lord have lied to him. In the Hebrew it better reads 'the enemies of the Lord shall deny him' — that is, the Jews, his enemies, shall deny him. But these enemies who are going to deny him — will they then perish? will there then be no remnant? And how did you swear to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, that their seed should be as the stars of heaven and as the sand of the sea? Why did you promise to the fathers, and not repay the sons? The enemies of the Lord shall deny him. But what does it say? And their time shall be forever. 'When the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel shall be saved.' In the future they will believe; only now they have not believed. Now let me speak to the Jews. For they are wont to applaud themselves and say: And their time shall be forever. Let us say: It is true, for some of the Jews are going to believe. Indeed we read also in the Apocalypse of John that twelve thousand from each of the tribes — that is, a hundred and forty-four thousand — are going to believe; and this is said to be of virgins. But where the number of virgins will be so great, how great will be the number of those who are not virgins? Therefore let us too say to the Jews: Your time shall be forever — that is, in the age to come. But to you, who today do not believe, what does it pertain? And their time shall be forever. Such as belongs to the enemies of God, such shall their time be.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.81.16":["And he fed them from the fat of the wheat. He led them into the land of promise: he fed them not with manna, as in the desert, but with the wheat that fell, that rose again. And he fed them from the fat of the wheat. See the mystery of the Scriptures. From the fat of the wheat. Does wheat have fat? does it perhaps even have entrails? But he wished to show the bounty and richness of spiritual grace, and therefore he named it 'fat.' And with honey from the rock he satisfied them. He himself is the wheat; he himself is also the rock, who satisfied the people of Israel with water in the desert. Spiritually, he satisfied them with honey and not with water: so that whoever have believed and received the food may feel honey in their mouth. 'How sweet are your words to my throat, above honey to my mouth!' Finally our Lord too, after the resurrection, for this reason ate the honeycomb, and was satisfied with honey from the rock. I say something new. He himself the rock, he himself ate the honey, that he might bestow honey and sweetness upon us: so that those who under the Law had drunk myrrh — that is, bitterness — might afterward in the Gospel eat the honey of the Gospel.\n— Homily 13, on Psalm 80"],"PSA.82.1":["God stood in the assembly of the gods. There are many postures. For frequently we sit, sometimes we stand, sometimes we lie down, sometimes we run, sometimes we walk. So too God is described according to the variety of human beings, and his posture is introduced as varied. If we are holy, and are like Moses, it is said to us: 'But you, stand here with me.' For this is what God says to Moses. For Moses was standing upon the rock: therefore God too was standing for him. But if we were holy first, and afterwards sinners, God no longer stands for us, but walks: that is, he who before had stood for us moves from his place. After we have been moved, he too is moved together with us. Finally, Adam too, as long as he was in paradise and kept the law, God stood for him. But after he transgressed, he heard the voice of God walking in paradise. Do you want to know that God was walking for him? What did he say to him? 'Adam, where are you?' He who had not fled from God while he stood, afterwards fled from him as he walked.\n\nWe have spoken of the one standing, we have spoken of the one walking: let us speak of the one sitting. Whenever God is introduced as sitting, he is introduced in two ways: either as a king, or as a judge. If as a king, one sees him as Isaiah did: 'I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up.' There he is introduced as a king reigning. But when he is introduced as a judge: 'The thrones were set, and the books were opened.' It is written in Daniel. What is said? The thrones were set, and the books were opened: that is, the Lord about to sit as a judge, and about to read over the works of all. The books which are now folded up shall then be opened. Everything that we do, that we speak, that we think — even for an idle word we are going to render an account: it is written in the books of God. Someone thinks there are books in heaven, where our sins are written. I think the books are our consciences, which shall then be opened, and each one shall see what he has done. 'Nothing is hidden, that shall not be revealed.' Therefore the thrones were set, and the books were opened. How many things are written in my book, and I dare not confess even to my brother nor to my friend. The angels know what I have done, the thrones know, the seraphim know: the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit know.\n\nWe have said concerning God that for some he stands, for others he walks, for others he sits either as a king or as a judge: but for others he sleeps. If ever he abandons us to temptations — even though we may be holy, nevertheless we are left to temptations, that we may be tested — at that time the Lord sleeps for us. Finally, what does the psalmist too say? 'Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord?' And when the apostles were in the boat, and the boat was being battered by the waves, the Lord was sleeping for them: for this reason the boat was being battered by the waves. Finally, they rouse him, and he awakes, and immediately the storm grows still.\n\nWe have spoken of the four postures of God: that for some he stands, for others he walks, for others he sits, for others he seems to sleep, and for others he wakes and rises up. God stood in the assembly of the gods. Because they were gods, therefore God stood for them. In this very assembly of ours God has different postures: for some he stands, for others he sits, for others he walks, for others he sleeps. Although he himself is unchangeable, according to our variety he is changed. See the dignity of man. God stood in the assembly of the gods. He grants us the name, that he may grant also the merit. And in the midst he judges the gods. Like a commander in the midst of the army he judges the gods. He judges the gods. A fearful thing, a thing full of terror. If he judges the gods, what does he do about the sinner? These two little verses the prophet spoke, as it were. But this which follows, How long will you judge iniquity? — God himself speaks to the judges, that is, to the leaders of the people. Specifically these verses which follow are spoken to the judges. If the judges of the world, it is plain: but if the judges of the church, let us understand it of bishops and presbyters. How long will you judge iniquity? He who judges iniquity is a judge of iniquity. He is the judge of iniquity of whom it is said in the Gospel, that he did not fear God, and did not respect man. How long will you judge iniquity? I have given you power over my flock and over the people of God: you ought to be judges, not wolves.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.82.2-4":["And you accept the persons of sinners: judge for the needy and the orphan, do justice to the lowly and the poor: rescue the poor and the needy, and the rest. When a case comes to you, you do not consider the cause, you do not consider the judgment, but the persons of those who have the cases. For instance, if a poor man comes having a just case, and a rich man comes having a bad case: you look at the persons, not the case. This so far according to one interpretation. But there is also another, more sacred interpretation. In the theaters one man frequently is accustomed to have different persons. Now he enters into a woman, now into a man, now into a king; and he who had come forward as a king, again comes forward as a slave. I have given the example, so that from the carnal we may be able to come to the spiritual. We too take on different persons. For when I grow angry, I take on the person of a lion: when I seize the goods of others, I take on the person of a wolf: but when I am cruel and kill, I take on the person of a cruel man. But just as those who are sinners have different persons in their sins: so on the contrary those who are holy, they too have different persons, but in the good. When I give alms, I have as it were the person of a merciful man: but when I judge well, I have the person of a good judge: but when I suffer wrong and am humble, I have the person of a humble man. Unhappy is he who has many persons in evil: happy is he who has different persons in the good.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.82.5":["They knew not, neither did they understand, they walk in darkness. Who? The bad judges. Those whom I was calling gods, by their own vice have been called bad judges. They knew me not, neither did they understand my judgment: they walk in darkness. Because they forsook the light, therefore they walk in darkness. I said, you are the light of this world: and you turned aside from the light, and became darkness. Because, then, they have done this, let us see what follows.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81","All the foundations of the earth shall be shaken. See what kind of recompense the evil princes have: because of bad judges the foundations of the earth are shaken. This so far we have said first according to the letter, that the shaking of the whole world comes because of bad judges. But let us say it also in another way. All the foundations of the earth shall be shaken. I will overturn the bad judges, who have set their foundations on earth, and not in heaven: who ought to have Christ as their foundation, whom the architect Paul laid, but they were unwilling, and set their foundations on earth.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.82.6-7":["To this we shall answer that those who can say: \"Do you seek a proof of him who speaks in me, Christ\" (2 Cor. 13:3)? and: \"But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me\" (Gal. 2:20), are not so much themselves the ones teaching as God in them, who says to the saints: \"I have said, You are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High\" (Ps. 81:6); and at once, of sinners: \"But you shall die like men, and shall fall like one of the princes\". Since, then, Paul speaks, and Peter, who do not die like men nor fall like one of the princes, it is manifest that they are gods. And those who are gods hand on the Gospel of God, and not of man.\n— Commentary on Galatians","But if we wish to say that the prince of Tyre is that same power to whom the city itself, or province, was entrusted by God, let us take up that testimony: I said: You are gods, and all sons of the Most High. But you shall die like men, and fall like one of the princes; for provinces were handed over to them to rule, as to judges, by the Emperor; and they, forgetting that their honor depended on the command of another, such as Heraclianus lately was in Africa, rose up with a tyrant's mind against their king and Lord, so that, scattered through the whole world, under the names of idolatry, they took to themselves the names of gods and, puffed up with pride, fell into the judgment and the snare of the devil, of which the Savior also spoke in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","For if you forgive men their sins. This which is written: I said, you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High; but you shall die like men and fall like one of the princes, is said to those who because of their sins deserved to become men instead of gods. Rightly, then, those too whose sins are forgiven have been called men.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.82.6":["I said: you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High. Let Eunomius hear, let Arius hear, who say that the Son of God is a son in the same way as we are. That we are gods, we are so not by nature, but by grace. 'For as many as received him, he gave them power to become sons of God.' Therefore I made man, that out of men gods might be made. I said: you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High. See how great the dignity is: we are called both gods and sons. I made you gods, just as I made Moses a god of Pharaoh: that after you have been gods, you may be made worthy to be sons of God. Consider the divine Scripture. 'There is no respect of persons with God.' He did not say: I said: you are gods, the kings and the princes, but all: to whom equally I have given a body and a soul and a spirit, equally I have bestowed both deity and adoption. We are all born equally, both emperors and the poor: equally we also die. For our condition is equal.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.82.7":["But you shall die like men. You see, then, that he who is a man dies. For God does not die. And Adam, as long as he kept the commandment and was a god, did not die. But after he tasted of the forbidden tree, immediately he died. Finally God says to him: 'On whatever day you taste, you shall die the death.' It has it better in the Hebrew: 'But you shall die like Adam.' Just as he was cast out of paradise, so we too have been cast out. And you shall fall like one of the princes. Since he had said 'all of you sons of the Most High'; one cannot be a son of the Most High, unless he too be most high. I said that you should all be most high, as I am most high. But you shall fall like one of the princes. Since we were most high, therefore we are said to have fallen. Where are they who say that the devil was made a devil? Where are they who say that an evil nature was created by God? According to the Hebrew two things are said here. But you shall die like Adam: just as your father was unwilling to hear the commandment of the Lord, and fell, so you too shall die. Therefore that was said concerning Adam. But this which follows, And you shall fall like one of the princes: who is this one of the princes, who fell? If you should wish to say Adam, it was already said in the earlier verses, But you shall die like Adam. And at the same time see the significance of the word. But you shall die like Adam. Since Adam died, we too die. But the devil, who was a prince, and fell, did not die. For angelic dignity cannot receive death, but only ruin. Therefore he said, you shall fall like one of the princes. 'How is Lucifer fallen, who rose in the morning?' Behold, both here he fell, and there he fell. He fell for this reason, because he always dwelt among the heavenly things. This is he to whom it is said in Ezekiel: 'You are the unsealing of the likeness.' See what he says, the unsealing of the likeness. He did not say, You are the seal of the likeness: but, the unsealing of the likeness. God had sealed you, and had made you like himself: but you afterwards unsealed yourself. After the likeness and image of God you too were formed. Finally it is said in that same prophecy: 'In the midst of the cherubim you were nourished, among the stones of jasper and carbuncle you were in the paradise of God. And you fell,' he says, 'and were wounded from the mountain of the Lord.' This prince of Tyre: now prince of Tyre from the time when he fell: since Tyre in the Hebrew tongue is called sor, that is tribulation. Therefore this prince too, who at first was in heaven, has now been made prince of Tyre, that is, of the tribulation of this world. You shall fall like one of the princes. When he says 'like one,' he shows that there are others also. Read the Apocalypse of John: when the dragon fell from heaven, he drew with him a third part of the stars.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.82.8":["Up to this point God has spoken to men, and again the prophet speaks. Arise, O God, judge the earth. Arise, because our boat is battered by the waves. Arise, O God, judge the earth: since the bad judges do not judge, but overturn your law, and tear your flocks. Do you arise, who are the true judge. Because, then, the bad judges have died like Adam, and have fallen like one of the princes: therefore do you arise, do you judge, do you save your creature. But the hireling, when he sees the wolf, flees, and leaves the sheep: he cannot grieve for another's as one grieves for father and mother. Let us say it also in another way. Arise, you who suffered for us, who died for us: arise, and save us. Let us say it also in another way. You who came humble and despised, come as a judge, and vindicate us. Arise, O God, judge the earth. See the significance and the properties of the words. He did not say, Judge heaven, but the earth. Therefore the earth is judged, not heaven. If, then, the earth is judged, and not heaven, then in heaven sins are not committed. But if sins were committed in heaven, he would say: Arise, O God, judge the earth and heaven. See the mercy of the prophet. He did not say, Slay the earth: but judge, judge and save. He did not say, Judge through angels, judge through your ministers. For they, if they judge, cannot show mercy, because they are executors of your sentence. But you, if you judge, can yourself show mercy. Arise, O God, judge the earth. An emperor can change his sentence, a prefect cannot. Therefore we entreat you as judge, not despising, but praying. For men and angels in comparison with you are cruel: you alone are the most gentle judge. Do you want to know that God is merciful, and every man is cruel? While the master of the house was sleeping, an enemy man oversowed tares. The servants want to pull them up, and the master of the house says: 'Let be, allow them to grow, lest while you pull up the tares, you uproot the wheat also together with them.'\n\nFor you shall inherit among all the nations. Therefore we entreat you as judge, because you have had mercy on all the nations. Why did he not say, You shall possess all the nations, but, You shall inherit? Wherever an inheritance is set, there death has gone before. Therefore we are called heirs and coheirs. He said heirs of Christ, because he died for us; coheirs, because he is going to reign with us. You shall inherit. For you sent your servants (let us return to the apostles), one to the circumcision, another to the nations. As long as you had sent Moses, as long as Isaiah, as long as Jeremiah, as long as the other prophets, God was known in Judea, in Israel his name was great. In a small land the knowledge of God's name was carried. You sent Peter, Peter the fisherman, who had left even his net, who from his work had a callused hand. You did not send an orator, you did not send a philosopher: you sent a rustic man, a fisherman. This fisherman, this rustic, went from Jerusalem to Rome: and the rustic captured Rome, which the eloquent could not capture. Again the apostle Paul went, from Jerusalem he preached as far as Illyricum, and preached where the Gospel had not been preached, lest he lay a building upon another's foundation. Then he says that he would go from the city of Rome to Spain. See Paul the persecutor, who in Judea had been a persecutor, preaching among the nations. Where are they who proclaim Alexander the Great, the Macedonian commander, that in a short span he conquered so many nations? He had an army, he had also huge crowds: and he could not do any such thing, but only some small thing. But this Paul, once a persecutor, who said 'Though unskilled in speech, yet not in knowledge,' who makes solecisms in speaking, carries the cross of Christ, and as though triumphing captures all. He subdued the whole world from the ocean as far as the Red Sea.\n\nSomeone may say: They did all this for the sake of gain. For this is what Porphyry says: Rustic and poor men, since they had nothing, worked certain signs by magic arts. But it is not a great thing to work signs. For the magicians in Egypt too worked signs against Moses. Apollonius worked them too, Apuleius worked them too: and they worked countless signs. I grant you, Porphyry, they worked signs by magic arts, that they might receive riches from the rich little women whom they had led astray: for this is what you say. Why did they die? why were they crucified? Others too worked signs by magic arts: but they did not die for a dead man, for a crucified man. These know that the man is dead, and they die without cause. Happy, then, is our victory, which was consecrated in the blood of the apostles. Our faith is not proven except through their blood. Let us therefore bless God, to whom is glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 14, on Psalm 81"],"PSA.83.1":["O God, who shall be like unto you? Since there is none like you, we seek no other author but you. Be not silent, neither be still, O God. We keep silent; do you speak on our behalf. It is the voice of the righteous man: he who says, \"Neither be still, O God,\" is confident of his own righteousness.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82","O God, who shall be like you? None shall be like you. Do not keep silent, nor be restrained, O God. It is a man of the Church who speaks, from whose belly flow rivers of living water. For he sees that the harvest is great and the laborers few: a vast multitude of believers, and only a modest teaching from the masters; many heretics, many adversaries, and few warriors to do battle for Christ. Do not keep silent, nor be restrained, O God. Why this?\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.2":["For behold, your enemies have made a noise. We can interpret this, according to the tropology, of the Church and of the heresies. But we can also interpret it, according to the letter, of the people of the Jews and of the other nations that were assailing Judaea round about. For behold, your enemies have made a noise. He chose to call the voice and the disparagement of his adversaries a \"noise.\" For behold, your enemies have made a noise. See what he says: they have made a noise — that is, just like a tinkling cymbal, so they made a noise in my ears. It is properly called a \"sound\": only the sound is heard, and the voice is not understood. Therefore what he says is this. They indeed were speaking, as though they wished to accuse me; but I, for my part, was hearing not a voice, as it were, but a sound. And they that hate you have lifted up the head. See what he says. Therefore he who raises up his head hates God. \"For God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.\" And they that hate you have lifted up the head. If ever you have something against someone, if your brother disparages you, and you are humble for Christ's sake, and he is proud on account of the devil, you imitate him who says, \"Learn of me, for I am meek and lowly of heart.\" But he who exalts himself imitates the one who says, \"I will ascend into heaven, I will set my throne above the stars of heaven, I will be like the Most High.\" And they that hate you have lifted up the head.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82","For behold, your enemies have made a din. The heretics speak out, and the men of the Church keep silent; they make a noise, and we are quiet; they blaspheme, and we do not bark back. For behold, your enemies have made a din. Beautifully did he say, they have made a din: for it is not speech adorned for building up, but only a disordered clamor for tearing down. And those who hate you have lifted up their head. So great is the multitude of the heretics, and so small the number of those who believe in you.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.3":["Against your people they have dealt maliciously in counsel. It is enough for anyone, if he be the people of God. And they have taken counsel against your saints. This needs no interpretation: to have read it is to have understood it.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82","Against your people they have devised a malicious plan. This they speak, that they may snatch away from your Church. They have plotted against your holy ones. Each day they think of nothing else, save how they may deceive the men of the Church and trip them up.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.4":["They have said: Come, and let us destroy them from the nation. Let there be no nation, for it is the people of God. And let the name of Israel be remembered no more. Let him be remembered no more who sees God with his understanding.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82","They have said, Come, let us destroy them out of the nation. I see a strange thing. So great is the multitude of the heretics, and such the diversity of their teachings, that their opinions differ among themselves, and yet they agree in this alone — in their hatred of us. For just as Herod and Pilate were enemies, and, acting against Christ, they made peace, and their concord was worse than their enmity had been; so these too fall out wickedly and league together yet more wickedly.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.5-8":["For they have taken counsel with one accord together. Unhappy are we, unhappy the people of God: it cannot have as great a concord in what is good as the wicked have in what is evil. Against you they have arranged a covenant: the tabernacles of the Edomites and the Ishmaelites. See the mysteries of the names. The tabernacles of the Edomites and the Ishmaelites, Moab and the Hagarenes, Gebal, and Ammon, and Amalek, and the foreigners with them that dwell at Tyre: for Asshur also is come with them, they are become a help to the sons of Lot. There are eleven who fight against the saints. Therefore, since they fought against the saints, they could not come to the perfect number, that is, to the twelfth. The tabernacles of the Edomites. They have no foundation at home, but tabernacles which quickly pass away. Edom is interpreted in two ways: either \"bloody\" or \"earthly.\" See therefore who they are that fight against the saints: the tabernacles of the Edomites, the earthly ones. And the Ishmaelites. The Ishmaelites are interpreted as \"those obedient to themselves\": that is, whatever the heart and the evil thought may have conceived, they straightway do it. Therefore it is said in Ezekiel: \"Son of man, speak against the prophets who follow their own heart, and whatever their thought impels them to, they do.\" But we ought not to follow our own thought, nor to be called Ishmaelites, that is, \"obedient to themselves,\" but Ishmael, that is, \"obedient to God.\" Moab, \"from the father,\" that is, those withdrawing from God the Father. The Hagarenes, \"proselytes.\" They who ought to be dwellers with God have been made proselytes. Gebal is interpreted \"empty valley\": ge means \"valley,\" and bal is interpreted \"empty.\" These, then, who are against the people of God, are not of the mountain, but of the empty valley. And Ammon is interpreted \"turbulent people\": that is, those who trust in a multitude, and not in simplicity. Amalek is interpreted \"licking the earth.\" For whoever fights against the people of God does not eat the heavenly bread, but licks the earth like a serpent. The foreigners. Where it says \"foreigners,\" the Hebrew has Philistines. And the Philistines are interpreted \"falling by drink\": that is, those who are made drunk from the cup of the devil, and being drunk have fallen. With them that dwell at Tyre. Tyre in the Hebrew tongue is called sor, which is interpreted \"straitness\" and \"tribulation.\" They who ought to have been sojourners in this world have become dwellers. These, then, who assail the people of God, dwell in Sor, that is, in the straitness and tribulation of their own soul. So then, these so many nations that fight against the saints — let us see whom they have as their prince, the Savior or the devil. What follows? For Asshur also is come with them. Asshur, Nebuchadnezzar, the adversary of God, who led the people of God into captivity. Although the nations may seem to have very many fighting on their behalf, yet they have Asshur, that is, the devil, as their prince, who are become a help to the sons of Lot. Lot is interpreted \"declining.\" The demons were formerly angels, and afterward fell with the devil, and became his satellites, as it is written in the Apocalypse of John, that the dragon fell, and drew with him the third part of the stars. So then, what does he say? These, who came with so many nations, and assailed your people, and Asshur came with them, and they have the devil as their prince, are become a help to the sons of Lot — that is, to the demons who declined from you: they do the same work that the demons also do.\n\nYou see that these are most difficult passages, you see that they are obscure, and that in each single phrase there are great mysteries, and we are compelled by necessity to dwell rather long on the Hebrew words that are full of mysteries. For we are not now airing out some rhetorical commonplace: but we are striving to interpret that which was spoken by the Holy Spirit. For unless we interpret it as we have said, what does it profit the churches of Christ to read \"the tabernacles of the Edomites, and the Ishmaelites,\" and the other names?\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.5":["Against your covenant they have made their arrangements. You spoke through the prophets; and they through Manichaeus, Marcion, Arius, and Valentinus.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.6":["The tents of the Edomites and the Ishmaelites. Since once we have begun, leaving behind the lowliness of the literal history, to raise our understanding to a higher manner of the spiritual meaning's exposition... For the history is a simple one, in which the Jewish people speaks against the other nations that were fighting against it. But whatever was done, was done as a type of us. Israel was attacked, and its victory profited us. Let us see, then, who they are that fight against the Church. The tents of the Edomites. The Edomites are interpreted as \"earthly.\" Beautifully did he say, tents: for they have no foundations nor houses, but tents, seats forever unstable. And of the Edomites and the Ishmaelites. The Ishmaelites are interpreted as \"as if hearing.\" Beautifully did he say, as if hearing: for they do not hear God, but pass on by.\n\nMoab and the Hagarenes, Gebal and Ammon. Each single name holds its single sacred meaning: for as many words, so many mysteries. Moab, which is interpreted, \"from the father\": for they are not in the father, but from the father, because they have forsaken their former father. And the Hagarenes, who are interpreted \"proselytes\" and \"newcomers,\" who were formerly citizens and afterward became strangers: for they are not of the household, but are aliens, seeing that they assail the house of the father.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.7":["Gebal, which is interpreted \"empty valley.\" They are not of the mountains, but of the valley: for they have no taste for the things above, but for the things below; they are not of heaven, but wholly of the earth. Therefore he said, empty valley, because there is also a valley that is not empty: for whoever is humble, and casts himself down for Christ's sake, that one is a valley and a plain — not empty, but good. Ammon, and Amalek. Ammon indeed is interpreted \"a false people.\" Therefore it is said of the heretics, a false people: for they do not follow him who says \"I am the truth,\" but they follow him who is the prince of lying. And Amalek, who is interpreted \"licking\": for they suffer nothing to remain standing in the Church, but lick up all its foundations. They do not eat the words of the Scriptures, but lick the earth like a serpent.\n\nAnd the foreigners with those who dwell in Tyre. In place of what we have as \"foreigners,\" in the Hebrew is written Philistim, which is interpreted \"the cup of the draught,\" because they are made drunk from the cup of Babylon, as Jeremiah says: \"The golden cup of Babylon, making all the earth drunk.\" See what he says, the golden cup of Babylon. Babylon is interpreted \"confusion\"; but the golden cup is the doctrines of the philosophers and the eloquence of the orators. For who has not been led astray by the philosophers? Who has not been seduced by the orators of this world? A golden cup they have — outwardly the splendor of eloquence; and inwardly poisons are stored, which could not be hidden unless they were concealed under the appearance of gold. You see indeed the honey of eloquence, and you do not suspect the deadly poisons. The foreigners with those who dwell in Tyre. Tyre is interpreted \"narrowness.\" For truly there is narrowness there, and not the breadth which is Christ, as the Apostle also says to the Corinthians: \"You are not straitened in us, but you are straitened in your own bowels.\"\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.8":["For Assur also came with them. They went before, and he follows. Let us see who this is that follows. Assur indeed is interpreted \"the devil\": truly so, because he is both the enemy and the avenger, as is written in the eighth psalm, \"That you may destroy the enemy and the defender.\" He himself makes us sin, and he himself accuses us for punishment. For Assur came together with them. They have the devil as their prince, they have him as their emperor: not Josiah the righteous king rules over them, but the Assyrian. They became a help to the sons of Lot. Lot is interpreted \"turning aside,\" which is understood, from God, like the Jewish people: because all the heretics agree with the Jews in blasphemies.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.9-10":["Since, therefore, they came against your people, let us see what the psalmist imprecates upon them. Do to them as to Midian and Sisera. You have read the book of Judges: this is that Midian whom Gideon assailed. And Sisera, and Jabin. Jabin and Sisera are those whom Deborah and Barak assailed. At the torrent of Kishon, they perished at Endor. Deborah and Barak slew Sisera, the commander of the army. Therefore what he says, see what it means. Lord, since they have such great pride, since they come with so great an army, since they have Nebuchadnezzar the prince of the Assyrians, since they are become a help to the sons of Lot, since they do the very thing that the demons do, since their pride has been raised up even to you: therefore I pray you, that to their disgrace they may be conquered not by a man, but by a woman. They are become as the dung of the earth. Who? Midian, and Sisera, and Jabin: these three are become as the dung of the earth. Midian is interpreted \"abandoning judgment.\" Whoever fights against your people does not consider that there will be a judgment to come. Sisera is interpreted \"the sight of a horse.\" These who fight against your people are not of your flock and of your cattle, but of the horses that rage madly after females. These horses, then, are always ready for battle. As Jabin. Jabin is interpreted \"understanding.\" Those who trust in their own wisdom, and not in the glory of God, are become as the dung of the earth. Those who gloried in their army, who had Asshur as their prince, and were saying, \"I will ascend into heaven,\" not only fell to the earth, but became dung upon the earth.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.9-12":["There are many things that might be said, but the hour shuts us out: for it is a long task to run through them one by one. For who could easily say, Do to them as to Midian and Sisera, as to Jabin at the torrent of Kishon. They perished at Endor, they became like the dung of the earth. Make their princes like Oreb and Zeb and Zebee and Salmana: all their princes who said, Let us possess for ourselves as an inheritance the sanctuary of God? They thought themselves to be men of the Church, and claimed the Church for themselves.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)","This history the eighty-second (83rd) psalm also recalls, where it records that among the other leaders of Midian there was also Salmana, saying: Do to them, O Lord, — no doubt meaning those who have entered into a pact or covenant against God —, as to Midian and to Sisera. And after the rest: Make, he says, their princes like Oreb and Zeeb, and Zebah and Salmana, all their princes who said: Let us possess the sanctuary of God by inheritance. For in this psalm too the leaders of the heretics are described, who have tried to claim the altar of God for themselves.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.83.11":["Make their princes. Whose? Of those who fight against your people. As Oreb and Zeeb, and Zebah and Zalmunna, all their princes. I think you have read the history in the book of Judges, that Gideon, who is called by another name Jerubbaal, fighting on behalf of the people of God, conquered these four kings, and slew them. And see what sort of men the princes of the Midianites are, who forsook the judgment of God. Oreb and Zeeb and Zebah and Zalmunna. Who thinks that in these words there are mysteries of the Savior? The philosophers read this, and mock; the rhetoricians read it, and now think it to be nonsense. And not only the rhetoricians, but the Jews also: they have not the key of knowledge, since a veil is placed before their eyes. Oreb is interpreted \"a hole,\" into which a snake enters; Zeeb is interpreted \"a wolf.\" See the names of the princes who are adversaries of Christ. Zebah, \"a victim,\" that is, those whom the wolf has strangled; and Zalmunna, \"perfected in malice.\" See, therefore, the mysteries of the names.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.12":["Who said: Let us possess the sanctuary of God by inheritance. They said it, but they did not accomplish it: for the Lord was fighting for his people.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.13-18":["My God, set them like a wheel. Let them not be stable, but forever uncertain: that they may have no seat, and be forever rolled about unstably. For just as a wheel is not of itself stable, so also the heretics do not stand firm in their opinion, but forever change their doctrines. Like stubble before the face of the wind. Eleven punishments he asks against them, because they have denied the mysteries of the eleven apostles. But let the prudent reader say: How will you divide one verse into two? And you do violence to the Scripture: you say eleven apostles, when we know that the Lord and Savior had twelve disciples. For this reason, then, we set down the number eleven, because Judas the traitor, who had been the twelfth, departed from the number. My God, set them like a wheel. You have one plague: count therefore more carefully the rest that follow. Like stubble before the face of the wind: behold the second. Like fire which burns a forest: behold the third. Like a flame burning the mountains: the fourth. So will you pursue them in your tempest: the fifth. And in your anger you will confound them: the sixth. Fill their faces with shame: the seventh. And let them seek your name, O Lord: the eighth. Let them be ashamed and troubled forever and ever: the ninth. And let them be confounded and perish: the tenth. And let them know that your name is Lord: the eleventh. You alone are the Most High over all the earth.\n\nThus far it is wrath: what follows is now mercy. And in your anger you will trouble them. He did not say, kill, but, trouble them, that they may return to you: behold the sixth. Fill their faces with shame: not with punishment, lest, shut out by death, they be unable to return to the Lord. Fill their faces with shame, not with punishment. Why then do you not kill them? The divine word explains: That they may seek your name, O Lord. For if they seek, they will also find. Those who have been led astray by the heretics will be turned back to the Lord: behold the seventh. Let them be confounded and perish. He who blushes for his sins is next to being without sin: behold the eighth. Let the prudent reader say: This is contrary to the interpretation you have given. And let them perish. For those who are confounded to this end, that they may be saved — how shall they perish? Let them perish, let them perish, he says, the heretics, and let the men of the Church be saved. It is as if you were to say, Let this adulterer perish — not that you wish the adulterer to perish, but that the adultery may perish in him; nor, when the adulterer is saved, is the adulterer saved, but the righteous and chaste man. That you may know this to be the meaning we have stated, the Holy Spirit himself speaks thus: Let them be confounded and perish. How let them perish? That they may know that your name is Lord. Let them perish in error, let them be saved in the truth. You alone are the Most High in all the earth. Not the conventicles of the heretics, not the aeons of Valentinus and his innumerable days, not Basilides who asserts three hundred and sixty-five gods; not, he says, the innumerable multitude of false gods, but you alone are the Most High in all the earth. To whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.83.13":["O my God, make them as a wheel. See the clemency of the prophet: he does not pray against them, but for them. See what he says. O my God — you who are the God of all, but properly my God: O my God, make them as a wheel. Those who have, as it were, a foundation in malice, let them have no foundation, but let them totter, and not stand firm in malice. As stubble before the face of the wind. As stubble: not as wheat, but as stubble. What he says is this: if there is among them anything that is stubble and chaff, let it perish.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.14":["As fire that burns up a forest. He did not say \"fruitful trees,\" but \"forest,\" which has no fruit. If anyone, then, is stubble, and has no wheat; if anyone is a forest, and has no fruit — let this perish. As a flame burning up the mountains: let the pride in them perish.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.15":["So shall you pursue them in your tempest, and in your wrath... What? will you kill them? No: you will trouble them, that they may be saved.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.16":["Fill their faces with shame, that they may be confounded. Why this? And they shall seek your name, O Lord. You see, then, that he prays not against them, but for their salvation.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.17":["Finally, in what follows also, see what he says. Let them be ashamed and troubled for ever and ever, and let them be confounded and perish. Let them perish according to that which is evil in them, and let that which is good be saved. Finally, see that \"perdition\" here does not signify abolition, but salvation.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.83.18":["What follows? And let them know that your name is Lord: you alone are the Most High over all the earth. Let him who wishes exalt himself, and let him be exalted: let us say, You alone are the Most High over all the earth. However much anyone exalts himself, we have God, than whom there is none higher.\n— Homily 15, on Psalm 82"],"PSA.84.1":["To the choirmaster, for the winepresses, a psalm of the sons of Korah. There are three psalms which are inscribed \"for the winepresses\": the eighth, the eightieth, and the eighty-third. The eighth is inscribed to David, the eightieth is inscribed to Asaph, the eighty-third is inscribed as of the sons of Korah. I give you a certain rule, so that you may know what you ought to observe in the Scriptures. Whatever psalm is inscribed as of the sons of Korah has nothing sad in it, but always signifies joyful things.\n\nFor since Korah and Dathan and Abiram acted against Moses, and were punished by the Lord, the sons of Korah, who did not follow their father, were blessed with eternal joy. And since Korah is interpreted \"skull,\" and it is plain that the skull signifies the place of the resurrection, whoever is a son of Korah is a son of the resurrection. And whoever is a son of the resurrection can have nothing that is sad.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts: my soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord. Then it is said, \"Blessed are they who dwell in your house.\" See that there are three things: the tabernacles, and the courts, and lastly the house. How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts. What are these tabernacles? Let us look at the Gospel, whether it may offer us some meaning. The Lord speaks, and says: \"Make yourselves friends of the unjust mammon, that when it fails, they may receive you into the eternal tabernacles.\" What is it that fails? This, that is, the unjust mammon. May they receive you into the eternal tabernacles. You see, therefore, that the unjust mammon fails in this world. Yet consider that this unjust thing prepares righteousness. In the Hebrew tongue riches are called \"mammon\": not gold, as some suppose. Of the unjust mammon. There is also a certain saying of the philosopher that is true: \"Every rich man is either unjust, or the heir of an unjust man.\" For this reason the Lord and Savior says that the rich enter with difficulty into the kingdom of heaven. Someone may say: How did Zacchaeus enter into the kingdom of heaven, who was rich? But he gave away his riches, and at once he exchanged his riches for the kingdom of heaven. He did not say, Whoever are rich shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven: but he said, that the rich enter with difficulty into the kingdom of heaven.\n\nHow beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts. Others desire possessions, others the riches of this world, others to hold office in the churches of this world, and to have some glory among men: for me this alone is my desire, to see your eternal tabernacles. Those tabernacles are beloved to me, where there is a gathering of virtues, not of vices. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord. This is my only desire, this is my only love, that I may see your courts. See the order. First he desires the tabernacles, tabernacles which have no foundation, tabernacles which are quickly changed. For a tabernacle is always being changed, and is carried from here to there. But the courts are indeed not the house, yet they have some foundations, and from the court we enter into the house. Therefore this man first desires the tabernacles, and afterward faints and melts with love that he may see the courts: and when he has been in the court, then he says: \"Blessed are they who dwell in your house.\"\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","Long ago, when we spoke about the eightieth psalm, we said that the title of the eighth psalm, and of the eightieth, and of the eighty-third psalm — that is, of this one — is inscribed \"for the winepresses.\" So we said that winepresses are like granaries, in which the crops are stored away. So let us see what he says.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)","How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts. These, then, are the winepresses in which the spiritual grain is to be stored. \"Make for yourselves friends out of the mammon of iniquity, that they may receive you into the eternal tabernacles.\" How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts. How beloved are the tabernacles through which a man is carried over from earth to heaven. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord. Happy is that thought which by day and by night desires the tabernacles of the Lord. He who is a sinner, when death comes, does not think about these tabernacles, but about punishments: he does not consider the kingdom of heaven, but dreads the flames of hell. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord. See with what love, or with what longing, the mind of the holy man is wounded for the courts of the Lord. Beautifully has he said: I gaze upon the doors, and what the house is like inside I do not know.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.2":["My heart and my flesh have exulted in the living God. It is difficult that both the heart and the flesh of anyone should agree together in common. For if, according to the apostle, the spirit fights against the flesh and the flesh against the spirit, how does he here say: My heart and my flesh have exulted? This no one can say, except him who is fixed with his whole mind in the love of God. My heart and my flesh. This is what he says also in another place: \"My soul has thirsted for you, how manifoldly my flesh for you.\" And my flesh. This is the flesh that desires the Lord, of which it is said in another place: \"And all flesh shall see the salvation of God.\" But that flesh, of which it is said \"All flesh is grass,\" does not desire the Lord. My heart and my flesh. Show me some abstinent man, who reckons the death of his flesh to be the gain of his soul. And these are the fastings and the afflictions of the flesh. Finally, what does the apostle also say? \"Whom I have handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved.\" Unless the flesh has died, the spirit cannot be made alive. For this reason the apostle also says: \"When I am weak, then I am stronger.\" Let each one consider himself, when he is wearied and worn out with fasting, how the soul flourishes, how the mind considers nothing else except God, how it always says: How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","My heart and my flesh have exulted in the living God. Listen, heretic, to what he says. How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of hosts: he is speaking of the kingdom of heaven. \"There are many dwelling-places with my Father,\" the Lord says in the Gospel. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord: these are the courts in which the saints will dwell for eternity, which the prophet desires. My heart and my flesh. You see, then, that both the soul and the flesh alike desire the kingdom of heaven. But if the flesh is dissolved into the air, if it will not rise again, as the heretics suppose, how does the flesh of the prophet desire the kingdom of heaven? My heart and my flesh: because they have labored together, together they await the recompense. If the labor is shared, why should the reward not be shared? I beg you to hear me with indulgence. The flesh labors more in the present age than the soul: because the soul commands, the flesh serves. One thing is the labor of the servant, another the delights of the one who commands. The soul desires the Lord: the flesh fasts, the flesh lies on the ground in the cold, in prison it is scourged in martyrdom, it is beaten, it is condemned — the injury is the flesh's. The soul indeed grieves: but grief does not reach it except through the flesh. But someone may say: How does the apostle say that \"flesh and blood shall not obtain the kingdom of God\"? Do you wish to know how it is written? Listen to the Lord speaking in Genesis: \"My spirit shall not remain in man forever, because he is flesh.\" Why? For what is this? You made the flesh, and do you accuse and condemn the flesh? But what God says is this: my spirit shall not remain in these men, because they do not live according to the spirit, but according to the vices and will of the flesh. Let the apostle too give his testimony: \"Whoever are in the flesh cannot please God.\" If all who are in the flesh cannot please God, how did Paul himself, who is speaking, please God? How did Peter or the rest of the apostles and the saints please him, whom we cannot deny to have been in the flesh? Just as the same apostle again says: \"But you are not in the flesh, but in the spirit.\" They were in the flesh: and how does he say, You are not in the flesh? Because you do not live according to the flesh, because \"our citizenship is in heaven.\" For we who in this age \"are pilgrims away from the Lord\" do indeed walk on the earth, but we hasten toward the things of heaven: because here we have no home of our own, but are strangers and pilgrims, just as all our fathers were.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.3-4":["For indeed the sparrow has found itself a home, and the turtledove a nest for itself where it may place its young. For the moment let us interpret it simply. See what he says. I desire, O Lord, your eternal tabernacles: my soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord: I desire to have some seat and little nest for my soul and my body. The birds which wander freely here and there, yet after they have flown, have a place and little nests where they may rest: how much more ought my soul and my flesh to prepare for themselves a place, where they may be able to rest? Now let us ascend to something higher. \"In the Lord I trust: how do you say to my soul, Migrate to the mountains like a sparrow?\" See therefore, that no one migrates to the mountains, except him who is a sparrow. And the turtledove a nest for itself. This is the turtledove, of which he says in the Song of Songs: \"The voice of the turtledove has been heard in our land.\" The turtledove a nest for itself. The turtledove is an animal of chastity, the turtledove does not dwell in low places, but always makes its nests for itself in the lofty trees. As therefore the sparrow and the turtledove, most chaste birds, make their nests for themselves in the higher places, so therefore the tabernacles and the courts and the house are not on this low earth, but on high, that is, in the kingdoms of heaven. Finally, see what he says. Blessed are they who dwell in your house. In the tabernacle we do not dwell, but we sojourn. And as long as we are in the court, we do not dwell, but we sojourn: for from the courts we pass into the house. But when we have come into the house, what is said? Blessed are they who dwell in your house. The house, which has a foundation: because from the house it is not permitted for us to go out, but from the courts it is permitted.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83"],"PSA.84.3":["For indeed the sparrow has found itself a house, and the turtledove a nest where she may lay her young. This is what the Lord says in the Gospel: \"The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air have nests.\" For indeed the sparrow has found itself a house. He gave a comparison from an everyday example. Because he had said, How beloved are your tabernacles, which my soul longs for, and my flesh desires the kingdom of heaven; he gives a comparison, and says: The birds are free, and although they wander through the air, yet they remember their nests, and return to them. If, then, sparrows seek out their nests, why should the human soul not seek the dwellings prepared for it by the Lord?\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)","Your altars, O Lord of hosts. What is to be understood by, For indeed the sparrow has found itself a house, and the turtledove a nest where she may lay her young: your altars, O Lord of hosts? What is the connection? It hangs upon what came before. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord: your altars, O Lord of hosts. John in the Apocalypse saw the altar of the Lord, beneath which were the souls of the righteous. For he says thus: \"I saw beneath the altar of the Lord the souls of those slain for the word of the Lord and the testimony of Jesus.\" They were beneath the altar of God, because they had been offered up in martyrdom for Christ; for the victim of the Lord and Savior deserved to be beneath the altar in heaven.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.4":["Blessed are they who dwell in your house, O Lord: that is, in the Church, whether the present one, or \"the church of the firstborn.\" Forever and ever they will praise you. See what kind of office the martyrs have: it is that of the angels too. What they do in heaven, then, this we imitate on earth. So when we are called to the psalms and neglect them, we despise the praises of God.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.5":["Blessed is the man whose help is from you. We indeed desire your tabernacles and courts and house: but this very thing, that we may be able to attain it, is not of our own strength, but of your help. He has arranged ascents in his heart. Who has arranged ascents in his heart? Blessed is the man whose help is from the Lord: he has arranged ascents in his heart. Whoever is holy is daily stretched out toward the things before, and forgets the things that are past. For this reason there are also said in the Psalter the fifteen psalms of the steps, and the first is said, \"To the Lord when I was in tribulation I cried out.\" And in the second, \"I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, from where my help shall come.\" And in the third, \"I was glad in the things that were said to me.\" And he always proceeds, and advances, and ascends to loftier things. What he says is this. He has arranged ascents in his heart. Happy is he who daily advances, who does not consider what he did yesterday, but what he may do and advance in today. The holy man places ascents in his heart, the sinner descents. As he who is holy daily advances, so he who is a sinner daily decreases. Blessed is the man who arranges ascents in his heart. See the sequence of the Scriptures.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","Blessed is the man whose help is from you. To begin is ours, to help is God's. He has arranged ascents in his heart. Daily he thinks how he may ascend, not how he may descend. Whoever is a sinner puts not ascents, but descents, in his heart. That is why Jacob too saw the angels ascending and descending. He has arranged ascents in his heart: what is his own — he thinks the good, but the accomplishment he leaves to God. It is ours to arrange, his to bring to completion.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.6-7":["In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. Where \"in the valley\" is said, it is necessary that we understand, by contrast, also the mountain. Therefore whoever arranges ascents in his heart, where has he been placed? In the valley of tears. Let us consider that we are in the valley: we are not on the mountain, we are not in paradise, in the height of paradise; but in the lowliness of the earth, the accursed earth, which brings forth for us thistles and thorns, which is the serpent's food, of which it is said to Adam, \"You are earth, and to earth you shall go.\" As long, therefore, as we are in the valley of tears, we ought not to laugh, but to weep. For this reason the Lord also says: \"Blessed are they who weep, for they themselves shall laugh.\" For the time being, therefore, we are in the valley of tears, and this world is a world of tears, not of joy. We ought not to laugh, since this world and this time is of tears, that other is of joy. In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. What is it that he says, In the place which he has set? In the place, that is, in the valley of tears. In the place which he has set: God has set us for the contest, that we may always fight in this. This place, therefore, that is, the valley of tears, is not of peace, is not of security, but of contest and of wars.\n\nFor indeed he who gave the law will give blessings. Someone may say: In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set for the struggle, that is, for the contest. Why has he set us for the struggle? Why has he willed us to fight? The psalmist himself has answered himself, and says: For this reason in this place he has willed us to fight, that he may be able to bestow crowns. For indeed the lawgiver will give blessings. This lawgiver, that is, our judge of the contest (agonotheta), for this reason has willed us to fight, that he may be able to bless us. See the sequence. But what are the blessings of this judge of the contest? They shall go from strength to strength. Here they conquer, that there they may be crowned. If here a man appears strong, there he becomes more robust. They shall go from strength to strength. Therefore, unless here we have had strength, there we cannot have greater strength. For he did not say, they shall go from weakness to strength: but, they shall go from strength to strength. Do you wish to be strong there? First be strong here. Do you wish to be crowned there? Fight here. They shall go from strength to strength. We can also say it another way. \"Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God.\" He who has had strength here, will have that same strength there also. They shall go from strength to strength. What gain will they have, when they have gone from strength to strength? what reward will they have? The God of gods shall be seen in Zion. \"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they themselves shall see God.\" It suffices for the victors, it suffices for those who have fought, the face of Christ: to see God is an infinite crown. The God of gods shall be seen in Zion. How great a joy it is, how great a happiness, to go from strength to strength, and to have this very reward, that we may be counted worthy to see Christ!\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. We read in the book of Judges, when the angel came and preached repentance to the people, saying, You have forsaken the Lord, and the Lord will forsake you; and when the people heard this they wept, and that place was called the Valley of Tears. We have recalled the old history, so that we might flee heresy. But by the valley of tears we can understand, spiritually, this present age. For we are not on the mountain, that is, in the kingdom of heaven, but in the valley, that is, in the darkness of this world; and by our error we have been cast out of paradise with Adam into the valley of tears, where there is repentance, where there are tears. In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. What did he say? The place which God has set for the contest: so that here we may contend against the devil, against sins, that in the heavens we may be crowned. Why, then, did he give a contest of fighting? Could God not have given us life without a contest? But he gave it as a president of the games; he gave a stadium, where we might wrestle against the vices: so that afterward he might deservedly crown us, not sleeping, but laboring. What follows?\n\nFor indeed he who gave the law will give a blessing: therefore he gave a place for contending, so that afterward he might give a blessing, he who had given us the law of the contest. They will go from strength to strength. You see that the sense fits together. They will go from strength to strength: therefore they fight here, so that there afterward they may be crowned. They go from strength to strength. He did not say, they will go from weakness to strength: for unless they have been strong here, they will not be stronger afterward. But someone may say, God is merciful. But see what follows: The Lord loves mercy and truth. He is indeed merciful, but also truthful. When you hear \"merciful,\" you ought not to be careless, because there follows, \"and truthful.\" Again, when you hear \"truth,\" do not despair, do not think God to be only cruel: because mercy tempers truth. For \"the cup in the hand of the Lord is full of mixed wine, and he has poured out from this into that\": that is, from mercy he has tempered truth, and from truth mercy. The God of gods will be seen in Zion. When they have gone from strength to strength, then the God of gods will be seen in Zion. To whom is glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.6":["In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. Where \"in the valley\" is said, it is necessary that we understand, by contrast, also the mountain. Therefore whoever arranges ascents in his heart, where has he been placed? In the valley of tears. Let us consider that we are in the valley: we are not on the mountain, we are not in paradise, in the height of paradise; but in the lowliness of the earth, the accursed earth, which brings forth for us thistles and thorns, which is the serpent's food, of which it is said to Adam, \"You are earth, and to earth you shall go.\" As long, therefore, as we are in the valley of tears, we ought not to laugh, but to weep. For this reason the Lord also says: \"Blessed are they who weep, for they themselves shall laugh.\" For the time being, therefore, we are in the valley of tears, and this world is a world of tears, not of joy. We ought not to laugh, since this world and this time is of tears, that other is of joy. In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. What is it that he says, In the place which he has set? In the place, that is, in the valley of tears. In the place which he has set: God has set us for the contest, that we may always fight in this. This place, therefore, that is, the valley of tears, is not of peace, is not of security, but of contest and of wars.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83","Let us see where he arranged the ascents. Listen to what he says: In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. We read in the book of Judges, when the angel came and preached repentance to the people, saying, You have forsaken the Lord, and the Lord will forsake you; and when the people heard this they wept, and that place was called the Valley of Tears. We have recalled the old history, so that we might flee heresy. But by the valley of tears we can understand, spiritually, this present age. For we are not on the mountain, that is, in the kingdom of heaven, but in the valley, that is, in the darkness of this world; and by our error we have been cast out of paradise with Adam into the valley of tears, where there is repentance, where there are tears. In the valley of tears, in the place which he has set. What did he say? The place which God has set for the contest: so that here we may contend against the devil, against sins, that in the heavens we may be crowned. Why, then, did he give a contest of fighting? Could God not have given us life without a contest? But he gave it as a president of the games; he gave a stadium, where we might wrestle against the vices: so that afterward he might deservedly crown us, not sleeping, but laboring. What follows?\n— Homily 63, on Psalm 83 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.84.9":["Our protector, look, O God, and look upon the face of your Christ. See what he says, And look upon the face of your Christ. And a man prays, that the Father may look upon the face of the Son. But see what he says. Since the apostle said, \"Do you seek proof of him who speaks in me, Christ?\" and again the Savior himself said, \"He who receives you receives me,\" therefore this is what he says: Look upon us: for in us you look upon your Son, who dwells in us.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83"],"PSA.84.10":["For better is one day in your courts above thousands. See what he says, \"one day.\" Someone thinks it should be understood thus: It is better for me to have a little in the kingdom of heaven, than here the whole, that is, in this world. This understanding, indeed, is not a bad one. But let us say: If it could happen that someone should be for one day in the kingdom of heaven, and afterward be cast down headlong, he would rightly say: Better is one day in your courts. But now, in that he says \"one day,\" this is what he says: The kingdom of heaven is one day; it does not have night and darkness, but always there is light. He therefore who has been for one day in the kingdom of heaven, is always there.\n\nI have chosen to be an outcast in the house of my God, rather than to dwell in the tabernacles of sinners. This is what the Savior also says about John: \"Amen I say to you, that among those born of women there is not a greater than John the Baptist.\" Then he says that he himself is an angel. And then he says: \"But he who is lesser in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.\" The sense there is this: John is greater than all men, and, if you wish to know, he himself is an angel; but this one who on earth is an angel, is the least in the kingdom of heaven, that is, he is lesser than the angels; but he who is lesser in the kingdom of heaven, that is, the angel, is greater than this one who on earth is greater than all. This, therefore, is now what the prophet says: I have chosen to be an outcast in the house of my God, that is, I have wished to be the least in the kingdom of heaven, rather than the first in this world. I have chosen to be an outcast in the house of my God, rather than to dwell in the tabernacles of sinners. Consider what he says, Rather than to dwell in the tabernacles of sinners. Tabernacles have no foundation, but they pass away: yet he who is a sinner never merely sojourns on the earth, but dwells in it and possesses it.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83"],"PSA.84.11":["For God loves mercy and truth. Two things God loves, mercy and truth. If he were only merciful, he would provoke us all to sins: if he loved only truth, no one would have hope of repentance. For this reason he seeks two things, and tempers the one by the other. If you are a sinner, hear that God is merciful, and do not despair, and do penance. But if you are righteous, do not be negligent, because God is clement: since God is also just, and loves truth.\n\nThe Lord will give grace and glory. He did not say, He will give glory and grace: but what did he say? The Lord will give grace and glory. First he forgives sins, and afterward he crowns.\n\nHe will not deprive of good things those who walk in innocence. It has it better in the Hebrew: \"in perfection.\" For innocence is not a perfect virtue: since he who is innocent, unless he also has prudence, is not of perfect and consummate virtue. For this reason he also says in the Gospel: \"Be prudent as serpents, and simple as doves.\"\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83"],"PSA.84.12":["O Lord of hosts. See what he says, O Lord of hosts. Therefore when we have any sins in us, God, who is the Lord of hosts, is not our Lord: because the Lord is not the Lord of sinners, but of the righteous. Blessed is the man who hopes in you. He hopes in the Lord, who has no conscience of sins, who lifts free eyes to heaven. This man hopes, who knows that he has not offended his Lord.\n— Homily 16, on Psalm 83"],"PSA.85.1":["Unto the end, a psalm of the sons of Korah. The eighty-fourth psalm is headed with this title. I have often said: wherever there is the title \"Of the sons of Korah,\" nothing is sorrowful, but always joyful. For the sorrow of the father is consoled by the joy of the sons. Read the psalms through carefully, then, and wherever you find the title \"Of the sons of Korah,\" you will find nothing sorrowful. I have often said: Korah, which is interpreted as \"the place of the Skull,\" must signify something joyful. So then, who is this Korah? He is the one who was going up from Jericho to Bethel. Bethel, \"the house of God.\" Note this carefully. Whoever is in Jericho, and wishes to go to the house of God, must go up. Therefore our Korah went up from Jericho — that is, Elisha — to the house of God, Bethel; and there come out from Bethel, that is, from the house of God, that is, from the synagogue, where formerly there had been the house of God, there come out forty-two boys, and they mock Elisha, our Korah, and say, \"Go up, baldhead, go up, baldhead.\" He comes to his own house, and is mocked by the little children of Bethel. But that most patient Elisha, who had come to Bethel for this very purpose, to save it, at length turned round upon them: he turned round, and gave orders that two bears should come out of the wood, and they killed forty-two boys. So too our Lord, that is, our Korah, when He had come to Bethel and wished to go up, mocked by the little ones, gives orders that two bears come out — that is, Vespasian and Titus — and they killed forty-two boys. Who are these forty-two boys? From the ascension of Christ up to the overthrow of Jerusalem there are forty-two years. Look carefully, and you will find it so. And so He gave them room for repentance for forty-two years, even after His resurrection, when He went up to Bethel. But because they kept mocking, \"Go up, baldhead, go up, baldhead,\" the two bears came out and killed them.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","You have blessed, O Lord, Your land. This is spoken of the coming of the Savior. This properly according to the history; but according to the tropology, after we have spoken of the history, we shall discourse afterwards. You have blessed, O Lord, Your land. This we have said of the coming of the Savior. The land which had offended You, which had been defiled with idolatries, was saved when You came. You have blessed, O Lord, Your land. Let the sinner speak, and then, having obtained pardon: You have blessed, O Lord, Your land. Although it brought forth thistles and thorns, yet it is Your creation, and therefore it was saved. You have turned away the captivity of Jacob: of those who have believed in Christ. You have turned away the captivity of Jacob. Whoever is in sins is held captive.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","To the end, a psalm for the sons of Korah. Korah is interpreted as \"Skull,\" the place of the Lord's passion. We are all sons of Korah, we who believe in his resurrection. What then do we say, and what do we sing with harmonious voice?\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)","You have blessed, O Lord, your land. It is better said in Greek, Εὐδόκησας Κύριε, that is, \"It has well pleased you, O Lord, in regard to your land\"; it has pleased you, this has seemed good to you, this you have decreed, that through your mercy you might fulfill what is said in the following verse: You have turned away the captivity of Jacob, you have forgiven the iniquity of your people, you have covered all their sins. But we can also understand it according to the Latin sense. You have blessed, O Lord, your land. The land, which before had been cursed, has been blessed by your coming. That which before had heard, \"Thorns and thistles shall you bring forth,\" now is called, as is written in the Song of Songs, \"the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys.\" The land, which before brought forth thorns, hears in Isaiah: \"There shall come forth a rod from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from his root.\" You have turned away the captivity of Jacob. The Lord therefore came to preach release to the captives. This is what is said in another place, \"He led captivity captive\": that is, we who before had been taken captive by the devil unto perdition, are now taken captive by the Savior unto salvation.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.2":["You have remitted the iniquity of Your people. Not on account of their works, but on account of Your mercy, You have saved Your people. You have covered all their sins.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","You have remitted the iniquity of Your people. No one remits anything except what is owed. You have covered all their sins. You have covered them with virtues, and therefore the sins do not appear. For example, You have covered iniquity with righteousness, You have covered unchastity with chastity, You have covered blackness with whiteness.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","You have forgiven the iniquity of your people. This we believe was accomplished in baptism. You have covered all their sins. This in penance, as is said in the thirty-first psalm: \"Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.\" In baptism our iniquities are forgiven us, but in penance the sins that had been committed are covered over: they are not dismissed, but covered over. Let us carefully consider the force of the word. For when the Lord and Savior comes, all our iniquities are utterly forgiven us in baptism. For when we were living without law, we were sinning without law — ἀνομία it is called in Greek, that is, sin without law; but when we have received baptism, it is by no means called ἀνομία, but sin is called sin. For sin is not imputed except to him who knows it to be sin: where there is transgression of a commandment, there is true sin. Do you wish to know how sins are covered over by virtues? Yesterday I was dissolute, today I am chaste: chastity covers over lust. Yesterday I was imprudent, today I am prudent, I repent of my error: prudence covers over imprudence. Yesterday I was seizing what belonged to others, today I distribute what is my own: almsgiving covers over greed. Blessed is he whose iniquities are forgiven in baptism: but the second is penance, which is like a plank after shipwreck. Therefore that man too can be called blessed: though he be delivered amid peril, yet because he is delivered in penance, he is to be called blessed.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.3":["You have softened all Your wrath, You have turned away from the wrath of Your indignation.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","You have mitigated all your wrath, you have turned away from the wrath of your indignation. See what penance avails: it holds off the imminent wrath of God.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.4":["Turn us, O God of our salvations. See what he says. You, for Your part, have turned away from wrath on account of Your clemency. Turn us, O God. You have turned away from wrath; turn us away from sins. Turn us, O God of our salvations. Why did he not say, \"of our salvation,\" but, \"of our salvations\"? If we sinned but once, we would need one salvation. We have sinned in many things, therefore we need many salvations.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","Convert us, O God of our salvations. He did not say, \"of our salvation\": because we have sinned much, we have need of many salvations.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.5":["Will you be angry with us forever? or will you extend your wrath from generation to generation? Your wrath has profited unto salvation: for this reason you were angry with the former people, that you might have mercy on ours.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.6":["O God, You, turning again, will bring us to life. As long, therefore, as the Lord does not turn to us, we are dead.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","You, being turned, will give us life, and your people will rejoice in you.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.7":["Show us, O Lord, Your mercy, and grant us Your salvation. That the Savior came down is a matter of God's mercy: the physician would not have come, had not very many been sick. Because very many were sick, therefore the physician came: because we were in need of mercy, therefore the Savior came.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","Show us, O Lord, your mercy, and give us your salvation. What in Latin is called \"salvation\" (salutare), in Hebrew is called \"Jesus.\" Therefore in Hebrew it is said thus: Show us, O Lord, your mercy, and give us your salvation: Unless you first be merciful toward the human race, your Jesus will not be given to us.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.8":["I will hear what the Lord God will speak in me. Many think, when Moses speaks and says, \"Thus says the Lord,\" and Isaiah, and the rest, and when it is said, \"The word of the Lord came to Isaiah\" or \"to Ezekiel the prophet,\" that the speech comes from outside, and that the prophet hears through these ears of his what is spoken by the Lord. It is not so. Indeed, what does our Lord Himself say to the Pharisees and the Jews? \"He who has ears to hear, let him hear.\" And Isaiah says: \"The Lord has added an ear for me.\" See what he says: The Lord has added an ear for me. Because I did not have that ear which is in the heart, He added it for me, that I might be able to hear the words of God. And so now, then, whatever the prophet hears, he hears in his heart. For just as we cry out in our hearts, \"Abba, Father,\" and that cry is a cry of silence, and the silence is heard by the Lord: so too the Lord speaks to our heart, which cries out, \"Abba, Father.\" Therefore, then, the prophet now says: I will hear what the Lord God will speak in me. Something similar the prophet Habakkuk also says: \"I will stand upon my contemplation, and I will see what the Lord will answer me, and what I shall answer to Him\" — and I will hear what the Lord God will speak in me. Therefore this also that he says, I will hear what the Lord God will speak in me, he speaks in the heart, he speaks in the understanding. Do you wish to know what the cry of the heart is like? Do you wish to know that a cry before God is not one of voice, but of tears? It is said in the Lamentations of Jeremiah: \"The pupil of my eye was not silent.\" For He will speak peace unto His people. This is properly said of the people of the Jews, who are to believe in Christ: that is, it is said of the coming of Christ and of the faith of the Jewish people, that is, of the apostles, and of all who believed the apostles. For He will speak peace unto His people. Peace is not spoken of except when there is war.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","And unto His saints, and unto those who are turned to the heart: that is, those who first followed the flesh, and afterwards are turned to the heart. That you may know that this little verse is spoken further of the Jews — that is, of those who believed in the Savior — see the examples we have mentioned. Take for me a Jew reading Leviticus, and all those sacraments that are written there, and how the leper enters into the synagogue, how a hen is taken up, and its neck is wrung; then it is told how the blood is to be sprinkled, it is told of the water, it is told also of the hyssop, it is told too how his garments are to be rent, and thus he enters into the synagogue, he who had been a leper. If we read according to the letter, what profit is it to us that we read? But if we have turned to the heart, if we have understood spiritually, we shall see that the leper cannot enter to the people of God unless his garments be rent, unless whatever had been shut up be uncovered. Then see that this leper does not enter into the synagogue except through blood and water and hyssop. Therefore you too, who up to this day have been a leper — your leprosy did not appear until you came to the priest. But because you came to the priest, the priest unstitched your garments, and that which seemed to be covered and sound, he showed to be leprous. He set all your sins and your leprosy before your eyes, and therefore he brings you back into the synagogue of God through blood and through water. Through blood, through the passion of Christ; and through water, through baptism. Therefore, after leprosy you will not be able to be healed except through the blood of Christ and through baptism. And when you have done this, that too is said: \"You shall sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: You shall wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.\" Up to this day you are in Egypt: as long as you do not come to the blood and to the water, you will not be able to be saved. Do you wish that the destroyer, who slew Egypt, may not slay you? Take hyssop, dip it in the blood, sprinkle your doorposts: and the destroyer, when he sees the blood on your forehead, will not be able to approach. Why have I said all this? Because it is written, Unto those who are turned to the heart. Therefore God will not be able to speak peace to His people, except to those who are turned to the heart.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me. The prophet prays on behalf of the people, and he who speaks within himself while God speaks, and he says, I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me. You see that God speaks not in the ears but in the heart; just as Zechariah too says: \"And the angel who spoke within me said to me.\" There the angel is understood to be our Lord, who announces the will of the Father, who in Isaiah is called the \"angel of great counsel.\" I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me: I will incline the ear of my heart, that I may hear what the Lord God speaks within me.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)","For he will speak peace unto his people, and upon his saints, and unto those who are converted to the heart. I see three kinds of people: the common people, the saints, and those who are converted to the heart. He speaks peace with his people: not with the people of the Jews, of whom he says in Hosea, \"You are not my people,\" but concerning all Christians who have believed in him. For all whosoever are in the Church are together called the people of the Lord. But those who are already foremost among them, and advance in spiritual deeds, are called saints of God, just as Paul writes in his epistles to the churches and to the saints who are in them. This third follows, And unto those who are converted to the heart. These are they who had been tripped up by the devil, and afterward, doing penance, are converted to the virtues. For at the time when they sinned, they lost their heart — that is, the prudence of the heart, which, being converted to better things, they receive again. Hear, O Montanus; hear, O Novatus; hear, O Maximilla and the rest among the Phrygians, you who say that there is no penance. The Lord speaks to his people, that is, to all Christians without exception, and to his saints who are foremost in the Church, and to those who are converted to the heart, that is, who do penance. Blessed indeed is he whom no sin has ever stained, in whom no wound has ever been fixed: yet the second blessedness is, after a wound, to heal the scar.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.9-13":["You have covered all their sins, and right up to the end the psalm is about the coming of the Savior, as I said. Mercy and truth. Nevertheless His salvation is near to those who fear Him, that glory may dwell in our land. For truly the glory of God dwelt in our land, and thereafter righteousness looked down from heaven. For the Lord shall give loving-kindness. What loving-kindness? Our land shall yield its fruit. Mary, our land, our flesh, yielded her fruit. Righteousness shall walk before Him. The virgin land gave forth its righteous fruit.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84"],"PSA.85.9":["Nevertheless His salvation is near to those who fear Him. I have shown the way of salvation, I grant mercy. Although you are not yet turned to the heart, still I wait, I grant room for repentance. And all this, indeed, that glory may dwell in our land. For this reason He calls you to repentance, that you, who were a leper, may have Christ as your guest.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","Nevertheless his salvation is near to those who fear him. See what he says. Those who fear the Lord have the Lord near, since fear does not yet possess perfection: \"For perfect love casts out fear.\" Therefore we who love the Lord have him not nearby, but within. \"For I will dwell in them, and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.\" That glory may dwell in our land. This is the land of which he says in another place, \"The earth has given its fruit.\" The fruit of our land is the bread of life, which was born for us in Bethlehem; for Bethlehem is interpreted \"house of bread.\" And this bread, which arose in Bethlehem, was made for us descending from heaven, into whose mysteries the angels desire to look.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.10":["Mercy and truth have met each other: righteousness and peace have kissed. O how splendid a friendship! Mercy and truth have met each other. Are you a sinner? Hear what it says: Mercy. Are you holy? Hear what it says: And truth. Neither, if you are a sinner, should you despair; nor, if you are righteous, should you be proud. Let us speak in another way. There are two believing peoples: one from the people of the Gentiles, and another from the Jews. To the Jews it was promised that the Savior would come: but to us, who were strangers to the law of God, it was not promised. Mercy, therefore, is in the people of the Gentiles, truth in the people of the Jews: because what was promised has come, that is, what was promised to the fathers is fulfilled in the sons. Righteousness and peace have kissed. See what it says: Righteousness and peace have kissed. It is the same as what it said above, Mercy and truth. Mercy is the same as peace, but truth is the same as righteousness. For if anything pertains to peace, it pertains also to mercy: and if anything to truth, it pertains also to righteousness. See, then, what it says. Righteousness and peace have kissed each other: that is, mercy and truth have made friendship, that is, the people of the Gentiles and of the Jews are under one shepherd, Christ.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","Mercy and truth have met each other: justice and peace have kissed. Mercy and truth have met. I see two things, which by coming together and meeting become one. Justice and peace have kissed. All this is fitted to the mystery of the Lord and Savior: this is the Son of God and of man, who is for us truth, mercy, peace, and justice, in whom the justice of the former people and the mercy of the latter are joined together into one peace. \"For he himself,\" says the Apostle, \"is our peace, who has made both one.\" This is the mystery which the Church desires, and cries out in the Song of Songs, saying, \"Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth.\" This is the kiss of which the Apostle Paul says, \"Greet one another with the kiss of peace.\"\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.11":["Truth has sprung up out of the earth. \"I am the way and the truth and the life.\" He who said, I am the truth, has sprung up out of the earth. What is this truth, which has sprung up out of the earth? \"There shall come forth a rod out of the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from his root.\" And in another place: \"For God has wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.\" What do you say, Manichaean, who deny that the Savior had flesh? Behold, the truth, the Savior, has sprung up out of the earth, that is, out of Mary. And righteousness has looked down from heaven. For it was righteous that the Savior should have mercy on His people. See what it says. O righteous judgments of God, and His ways past finding out! Truth has sprung up out of the earth, that is, the Savior. And again, And righteousness has looked down from heaven. Righteousness is the Savior Himself. How has He sprung up out of the earth? How has He looked down from heaven? He has sprung up out of the earth, because He was born a man: He has looked down from heaven, because as God He is always in the heavens. That is, He was indeed born of the earth: but He who was born of the earth is always in heaven. That is, He so appeared on earth that He did not leave the heavens. For God is everywhere. He looked down: because, as long as we were sinning, He turned His eyes away from us. What He says is this. It is righteous that the potter should have mercy on his works, that the shepherd should have mercy on his flock. We are His people, we are His creation. Therefore, then, He sprang up out of the earth, and looked down from heaven, that He might fulfill His righteousness, and have mercy on His work.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","Truth has arisen from the earth, and justice has looked down from heaven. The truth of the promise has arisen from the earth, and the sun of justice has looked down from heaven: that he might lavish his light upon men set in darkness and in the shadow of death.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)","Others separate it from what precedes and want it to be the proper beginning of this chapter, and a prophecy of the Lord's coming: that command is given to the clouds, of which it is written above (cf. Isa 5:6): I will command the clouds not to rain rain upon it, that is, upon the vineyard of Israel; and to which the truth of God reaches, that they should rain upon the world the righteous one, or righteousness; and that the earth should be opened and bud forth the Savior. Of it is sung in the Psalms: Truth has sprung from the earth, and righteousness has looked down from heaven (Ps 84 [85]:12); or, according to the Seventy: The earth has budded forth mercy and righteousness alike, so that sinners may obtain mercy and the righteous their rewards. And as for what follows: I the Lord have created him, or, I am the Lord who created you, he will not be scandalized at the word 'creature' who has read that he is a worm and a servant, and that he sprang from the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","We do not divide Jesus, nor can we make two persons in one person, but he who is called Jesus because he saved the world is himself also called the Rising, because in his days justice has risen. And it is sung in the psalm: Truth has risen from the earth, because, begotten from a virgin's womb at the consummation of the ages, he said: I am the truth, and he came at the last hour, according to the epistle of John, who says: Little children, it is now the last hour, in which he hired for a denarius the people of the nations, who were standing and doing nothing, and sent them to the vineyard.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.85.12":["And so, that you may know that \"righteousness\" does not sound of cruelty, but of mercy, see what it says. For the Lord shall give loving-kindness. For this reason He looked down from heaven, that He might have mercy on His works. And our land shall yield its fruit. Truth indeed has sprung up out of the earth: this of the past. Now it is said of the future, And our land shall yield its fruit. Do not despair, because He was once born of Mary: daily He is born also in us. And our land shall yield its fruit. We too can bring forth Christ, if we will. And our land shall yield its fruit: from which is made the heavenly bread, of which He says, \"I am the heavenly bread.\"\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","For indeed the Lord will give kindness. For this reason these kisses were given, that the Lord might give kindness. And our land will give its fruit. What was promised at that time, we now see fulfilled. Our land gives its fruit, when it is tilled with the plow of justice, when by fastings, when by abstinence and the other virtues it is uprooted from its old vices, so that it may render the grain of the gospel seed which it received into a hundredfold fruit. Peter and the rest of the apostles, because they cultivated their land with diverse labors, can confidently say, Our land has given its fruit. The martyrs too, when they are cut down, when they are crucified, their land will render its fruit.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.85.13":["All these things, indeed, we have said concerning the mercy of the Lord, since He came for this purpose, to save the human race. Nevertheless we ought to know that He Himself is to judge the living and the dead, He Himself will come to judge. Righteousness shall walk before Him. Do not be negligent: Righteousness shall walk before Him. And He shall set His steps in the way. See what it says, His steps: where there are no stones, where there are no thorns and thistles, where the way is level, where He can walk, where He cannot stumble. Let us, then, also make a way for the Lord in our heart: the way over which John labored, and cried out in the wilderness, that we should make a way for the Lord. Therefore now too this is said. And He shall set His steps in the way. Where He finds a way, He will walk. Although before we had thorns and thistles, although stones, it is said to us in Isaiah: \"And cast the stones out of the way.\" And He says this for the reason, that when He wishes to walk about in our way, He may not stub His foot. The stones which are in the way, and are commanded to be cast out, are our sins. Christ does not walk in our heart, if there be any sin: at once He stumbles against the stones. And He shall set His steps in the way. Let us make a way, and Jesus will set His steps in the way.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84","There are many things which might be said, but the hour compels the sermon to reach its end. Let us together pray to the Lord, that both my speech and your hearing may be turned into work. \"For not the hearers only are just before God, but the doers.\" For he who speaks or hears, and does not do, either listens or speaks unto his own condemnation. For what does he say in the gospel? \"If you were blind, you would not have sin.\" Let us turn that around in another way: If you were deaf, and did not hear, you would not have sin. But because both to speak and to hear without work are each of them sin, let us ask the Lord to set his steps upon the way — that is, that he may accompany us in the perils of this life, and, walking in our heart without the offense of our sin, may delight to dwell in us always. To whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 64, on Psalm 84 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.87.1-7":["We ought to know that what we have interpreted of the Church can be understood also of our soul. For if Zion is interpreted as a watchtower, and our soul in no other way but always considers higher things: the true church, the true temple of Christ is nothing other than the human soul. The Church of Christ is nothing else but the souls of those who believe in Christ. 'You,' he says, 'are the temple of God, and the Spirit of God dwells in you.' Its foundations are in the holy mountains: that is, the foundations of God are nowhere but in the holy mountains, in the doctrines of the believing soul. The Lord loves the gates of Zion. Our Zion has very many gates: which, if we open them, the bridegroom enters in to us, and sleeps with us. Then he himself says: 'Behold, I stand before the door and knock: if anyone opens to me, I will enter and dine with him.' Glorious things are spoken of you, O city of God. A city is not one building, but consists of many buildings: so the Church and our Zion, unless it has many virtues, will not be the city of God. I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon among those who know me. She who once was in the breadth of sins, afterward ascended into the remembrance of God. Will a man say to Zion, and a man is born in her? I speak to you a wondrous thing, but a true one. Our Zion, in which at times there are foreigners, and Tyre, and the Ethiopian: that watchtower, that harlot, that Rahab, that Babylon, she who spread apart her feet to everyone passing by, according to Ezekiel: that harlot, if she wills, suddenly becomes a virgin. She becomes a virgin, and conceives the Son of God, and gives birth. 'From the fear of you, O Lord, we conceived and were in labor; we brought forth the spirit of your salvation upon the earth.' See, then, that the harlot conceives from God and gives birth, and brings forth the Savior. We brought forth the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. Therefore our soul, that Rahab, that harlot, can conceive and bring forth the Savior. And a man is born in her. If we will, Christ is born daily: through each single virtue Christ is born. For if 'Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God,' whoever does a virtuous deed generates virtue. And he himself, the Most High, founded her. He who is born in you, he himself gives the foundation to your Zion. The Lord will tell in the writing of the peoples. It is clear that what the Lord speaks in Scripture, he speaks to no one as he does to Zion. And yet, when all these things have come to pass, when Christ has been born in her, the victory is not certain and secure, but we are always in danger. As it were the dwelling of all who rejoice is in you. He who is joyful, from that very cheerfulness is secure: but he who is secure is quickly deceived. But he who dreads ambushes can quickly escape: with the Lord granting it, to whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.1":["A psalm of a canticle, for the sons of Korah. The difference between a psalm and a canticle I have often stated. A psalm is spoken from the psaltery, but a canticle is uttered from the voice. A psalm has to do with the hands, that is, with works; a canticle with contemplation (theoria).\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86","Its foundations are in the holy mountains. He did not say beforehand whose, yet he said, Its foundations are in the holy mountains. The one who speaks is the prophet: the sons are of Korah. What Korah means, I have often stated: a skull, that is, the sons of the resurrection. Its foundations: whether of God, or certainly of the Church. And what are the foundations, if not the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit? These foundations, then, are not in the valleys, but are in the mountains: and not on any mountains whatever, but on the holy mountains. Paul says: 'As a wise architect I have laid the foundation'—that is, faith in the Trinity. Then he also says in another place: 'For he was awaiting the city having foundations, whose builder and maker is God.' Its foundations are in the holy mountains. He said, In the holy mountains, because there are also other mountains that are not holy. Then it is also said in Isaiah: 'Upon the dark mountain lift up a sign.' And in another place it is said: 'Who are you, corrupt mountain?' It is also said in Jeremiah: 'Give glory to the Lord your God, before your feet stumble against the dark mountains.' You see, then, that there are also dark mountains: and it is said to us by Jeremiah that we should give glory to God before our feet stumble against the dark mountains. For if our feet strike against those mountains, we cannot give glory to the Lord. Its foundations are in the holy mountains. Whom can we call the foundations? The apostles. In them were the foundations: where the faith of the Church was first laid, there too the foundations were laid.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.2":["And because each one builds upon the foundation—one gold, another silver, another precious stones—The Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tabernacles of Jacob. Does he love those gates which we see have collapsed? Does he love those gates, and does he love that Zion, which has been plowed like a field? And see what he says, The Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tabernacles of Jacob. See what he says: above all the cities of Jacob, that is of Judea, he loves this city alone. And how is it that we see the others in part still standing, while this one has utterly collapsed? Let us read the Apocalypse of John, let us also read Isaiah, where the city of Jerusalem is built, and its twelve gates are said to be constructed of precious stones, and the city itself descends from heaven, and its walls are said to be of gold, and its streets are said to be paved with emerald, and each of the gates is said to be constructed of a single precious stone. These are all the riches of the Lord, and does the Lord love these the way men do too? He forbids us from avarice, and does he himself build a city out of avarice? Therefore the Lord loves the gates of Zion, those twelve gates of Zion: it is clear that he wrote of the apostles. Above all the tabernacles of Jacob: above all the ancient saints.\n\nThis much we have said so far according to one figure. But let us speak also in another way. The Lord loves the gates of Zion. To me the gates of Zion seem to be the virtues. For just as the gates of death are the vices and sins, so the gates of Zion seem to me to be the virtues. Then he says in another place: 'You who lift me up from the gates of death.' But if the gates of death are sins, then the gates of Jerusalem are virtues. Above all the tabernacles of Jacob. See that Jacob, that is the old people, has no foundation, nor a house set upon foundations, but tabernacles which quickly pass away.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.3":["Glorious things are spoken of you, O city of God. Therefore that which was said, Its foundations are in the holy mountains, we can also say of God. But we can also say this. Glorious things are spoken of you, O city of God. What is this city? Where were glorious things spoken? In all the prophets it was spoken of this city. Isaiah says: 'Arise, arise, O Jerusalem, put on the strength of your arm.' Again Isaiah himself: 'I am the strong city, the city that is besieged.' David too says: 'The rushing of the river gladdens the city of God.' And in another place: 'O Lord, in your city you will bring their images to nothing.' And the Savior spoke glorious things of this city: 'A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden.'\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.4":["I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon among those who know me. Since he said, Glorious things are spoken of you, O city of God, and yet we understand that very city as the Church gathered from the nations: therefore now it is said concerning the calling of the nations, I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon among those who know me. Whoever is a sinner, let him be secure: the Lord was mindful of Rahab. I said secure, if he returns to the Lord: for otherwise security without tears has no remedy. I will be mindful of Rahab: of that harlot Rahab, who received the spies of Jesus, who was in Jericho, where Jesus had come, and he sent two spies. Jericho, this world, which is overturned in seven days, wishes to kill the spies. And while it desires to kill the spies, the harlot Rahab alone received them. And she received them, not in a lower place, but in an upper room, on the housetop, that is, in the height of her faith, and she hid them in flax, that is, in tow of flax.\n\nThis much we have said so far according to the historical sense, and you see how from the history itself we gradually ascend to the mystical sacraments. Jesus the leader, who had led the people out of Egypt: Jesus, who is interpreted as Savior, after Moses had died and been buried in the land of Moab, in the land of Arabia, that is, after the Law was dead, desires to lead his people into the gospel, and sends two spies to Jericho: two spies, one to the circumcision, another to the nations, Peter and Paul. Jericho seeks to kill them; that harlot receives them, the Church gathered from the nations. She believes in Jesus, and those whom Jericho wished to kill, she herself saves on her housetop. And she places them on the housetop, in her lofty faith, and hides them in the flax of the reed. Though she is a harlot, yet she places them in flax. Flax comes to whiteness through much toil and diligence. You yourselves know that flax is born from the earth, and when it has been born in the earth, it is black, has no beauty, no use. First then it is taken from the earth, it is broken, then it is twisted, afterward it is washed, then it is beaten, then it is combed, and with much care and toil it scarcely comes to whiteness. Therefore this is said, that this harlot indeed received the spies in her flax, so that those spies might turn her flax into whiteness. What then? She gives them counsel, and says: 'Wait here three days.' It was not one day, not two, but three days. See what she says, Wait three days. She does not name three nights, but three days, because she had an enlightened heart. And then she says, and after three days: and what does she say? 'Do not,' she says, 'go through the plains, but climb up into the mountains.' For the faith of the Church is not established in the valleys, but in the mountains. Jericho then is afterward overturned by Jesus, and this harlot alone is preserved unharmed. Therefore this is said. I will be mindful of Rahab: that is, of her who received my spies, and I on the day of judgment will be mindful of her.\n\nI will be mindful of Rahab. What does Rahab mean? We have spoken according to the history: let us speak also according to the interpretation of the name and according to the anagogy. Rahab is interpreted in two ways: either it is interpreted as breadth, or certainly it is interpreted as pride. So see what he says. She who once walked in the broad and spacious way that leads to death, she who was proud and had a rush toward ruin, was afterward converted into humility. I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon. Babylon is interpreted as confusion. See what he says. Not only will I be mindful of Rahab, but also of Babylon, whatever soul is confounded by vices and sins. And of Babylon among those who know me. See what he says. Though someone may have been Rahab, though he may have been Babylon: yet I will be mindful, whoever will have known me. Therefore if we have been Rahab and Babylon, we too ought to be secure, and to say: God said, I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon. But see what follows. Among those who know me. They were Rahab and Babylon, but afterward they were converted to me. I will be mindful of Rahab and Babylon among those who know me. She who once was in the breadth of sins, afterward ascended into the remembrance of God.\n\nThus far he has spoken as if through riddles: but now he speaks more openly of the calling of the nations. For see what he says. Behold, the foreigners and Tyre and the people of the Ethiopians, these were there. He calls them foreigners to distinguish them from Israel: for we are the foreigners. And Tyre. Tyre is interpreted as tribulation, that is, sor. And the people of the Ethiopians: black, and wrapped up in the filth of sins. And the people of the Ethiopians. This is what is said: 'Ethiopia will stretch out her hand before God.' Behold, the foreigners and Tyre and the people of the Ethiopians. What is said is this: Those who before were strangers, those who before were in tribulation, who were set in the heart of the sea (for it is said of Tyre 'Tyre set in the heart of the sea'): those, then, who first were in the sea, and were beaten by the waves, were afterward found in the Church. And the people of the Ethiopians. We were once Ethiopians, Ethiopians in vices and sins. Why? Because sins had made us black. But afterward we heard 'Wash yourselves, be clean,' and we said 'You will wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.' We Ethiopians, then, were turned into whiteness. Behold, the foreigners and Tyre and the people of the Ethiopians, these were there. Where were they? Glorious things are spoken of you, O city of God. These were there: that is, in the city of God.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.5":["Will a man say to Zion, and a man is born in her? The Seventy translators rendered it thus: Will a man say to Zion? Many, then, expected, and did not understand what μήτη Σιὼν is: and they added a ρ, and they read μήτηρ Σιὼν ἐρεῖ ἄνθρωπος (mother Zion will a man say). And they say this because there follows, And a man is born in her: they expect that it also ought to be said in this way, Mother Zion will a man say. Because a son is said to be born, therefore they err in the word \"mother.\" Let us then speak first according to what is commonly said (for neither ought we to dismiss that interpretation), let us then say also ourselves: Mother Zion will a man say, and a man is born in her. These are they who had been born in the Church, the foreigners, Tyre, and the people of the Ethiopians: these are they who are born in the Church. They believe in the Church, and are born in the Church. For unless they have been baptized, they will not be sons of the Church. But those who have been baptized in the Church, they call the Church mother.\n\nLet us also speak according to the Hebrew truth. Will a man say to Zion, and a man is born in her? What he says is this. Who will be able to announce to Zion, who will be able to announce to her of men, that a man will be born in her, and he himself will save her? That is, no man can announce to her that Zion will be saved in the man who will be born in her. And a man is born in her. Who is this man? He himself, the Most High, founded her. Let the Arians answer: for they say that the Father alone is the Most High. Behold, here it is said of the Son, And he himself, the Most High, founded her. He did not say, The Son of God who was in the beginning with the Father. But what did he say? A man is born in her, and he himself, the Most High, founded her. But if he is called the Most High, how much more the Word of God?\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.6":["The Lord will tell in the writing of the peoples and of the princes, of these who have been in her. He did not say, who are in her; but, who have been in her. The Lord will tell. And how will he tell? not by word, but by writing. In whose writing? in that of the peoples. That is not enough, but he also says, of the princes. And of which princes? who are in her? He did not say this; but, who have been in her. See, then, how holy Scripture is full of sacraments. We read the apostle Paul, we read Peter, and we read him saying 'Do you seek a proof of Christ who speaks in me?' And what Paul speaks, Christ speaks. 'For whoever receives you, receives me.' Therefore our Lord and Savior tells us and speaks in the writings of his princes. The Lord will tell in the writing of the peoples, in the holy Scriptures: which Scripture is read to all peoples, that is, so that all may understand. What he says is this. So the apostles wrote, so the Lord himself spoke in his gospels, not so that few might understand, but that all. Plato wrote in writing: but he did not write among the peoples, but among the few. For scarcely three men understand him. But these, that is the princes of the Church and the princes of Christ, did not write for the few, but for the whole people. And of the princes, that is, of the apostles and of the gospels. Of these who have been in her. See what he says, Who have been, not who are: so that, apart from the apostles, whatever else is said afterward may be cut off, may have no authority thereafter. Therefore, however holy someone may be after the apostles, however eloquent he may be, he has no authority: because the Lord tells in the writing of the peoples and of the princes, of these who have been in her.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.87.7":["As it were the dwelling of all who rejoice is in you. The prophet speaks to the Church, and says to her: As it were the dwelling of all who rejoice is in you. Why did he not say, The dwelling of all who rejoice is in you, but said, As it were of those who rejoice? It is said in another psalm: 'When the Lord turned back the captivity of Zion, we became as those who are consoled'; and he did not say, consoled. So also in this place he says, As it were the dwelling of all who rejoice is in you. Why did he say all this? Because in the present age, however holy someone may be, however much he renounces the world, he does not have perfect victory. Then it is said in the gospel: 'Blessed are they who mourn, for they shall be consoled.' But if it was said 'Blessed are they who weep, for they shall laugh': it is clear that in the present age there is no certain joy. Therefore our joy is as it were joy. The dwelling is in you. See what he says: that someone may never withdraw from the Church.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"PSA.88.1":["At the very outset the title is most difficult, and although we have not yet begun the harbor of exposition, nevertheless the most obscure questions press upon us. \"A song of a psalm, for the sons of Chore, unto the end, for meleth, to be answered, of the understanding of Eman the Ezraite.\" You see that in the individual words there are individual questions. \"A song of a psalm\": there is no great difficulty. \"For the sons of Chore\": the sons of Caath, of the tribe of Levi. \"Unto the end.\" Attend carefully to what he said: \"unto the end,\" not at the beginning, not in the middle, but at the end is it promised: not at the beginning to the patriarchs, not in the middle to the prophets, but at the end to the apostles and to us is the joy of the song promised. \"For meleth.\" Meleth is a Hebrew word, and it is translated \"choir.\" Now it is called a choir when many sing together into one, and just as the strings of a lyre are indeed different strings, yet produce a single sound. Therefore, that you may know that meleth is translated \"choir,\" there immediately follows \"To be answered\": so that, as the prophet sings, the whole multitude of the choir may answer in the praise of God. For the mystery is prefigured of the Church gathered together from diverse nations, so that from diverse places and from diverse regions and manners one choir of the praises of God may be made. \"Of the understanding of Eman the Ezraite.\" Eman was one of the choir-masters, just as Asaph or the others were. But that he is called \"Ezraite,\" this is translated \"having obtained the help of the Lord\" (since indeed Ezras is translated \"helper\"), that is, having obtained aid from the Lord. Therefore this choir too, which sings together in praises, is upheld by the help of God. Let us run through the individual little verses, and because we are constrained within a brief span, let us fit the individual little sentences to the individual verses.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)","\"O Lord, the God of my salvation.\" This psalm is spoken in the person of the Savior, that is, in the person of that servant whom He deigned to take upon Himself. Whatever, therefore, is spoken humbly is narrated in the human person, because it profits human salvation. \"I have cried in the day, and in the night before thee.\" Though I cried by night, yet the awareness of virtues was light to me.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.2":["\"Let my prayer come in before thee.\" \"Into thy hands, Father, I commend my spirit.\" \"Let my prayer come in before thee. Incline thine ear to my petition.\" Where are the anthropomorphites, who suppose that God has bodily members? \"Incline thine ear, O God.\" It is the voice of the Son to the Father, speaking in our person. \"Incline thine ear.\" It is a mark of great weakness, if, when He wishes to hear, He needs to incline His ear, so as to hear more closely. See how wretched it is to say that the Father cannot hear the Son unless with head inclined. Scripture speaks with human frailty, so that we may more easily understand what is said.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.3":["\"For my soul is filled with evils.\" I bear the sins of many, therefore I grieve for all. \"My soul is filled with evils.\" I bear evils, that I may lay them down upon the cross. \"And my life hath drawn near to hell.\" Beautifully he said \"hath drawn near\": for He was not held fast by hell, but for our sake He drew near into hell. Finally, in another psalm it is said in the person of the Lord, \"For thou wilt not leave my soul in hell, nor wilt thou give thy holy one to see corruption.\"\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.4-5":["\"I am counted among them that go down into the pit.\" This my murderers supposed: that I, going down like the rest of men into the prison of the underworld, was to be held back in the lower pit; not knowing that I had gone down for this reason, that I might rather draw out with me those who were held fast in that very place. \"I am become as a man without help, free among the dead.\" He did not say, \"I am become without help,\" but \"I am become as though without help,\" because He had the Father as helper. \"Free among the dead.\" Other men are held in hell by the bonds of sins: \"There is no man without sin, not even if his life be of one day.\" For we are all held in transgression: and therefore whoever goes down into hell is held by the law of hell. \"For everyone is bound by the bonds of his own sins.\" I therefore was free by nature, but for a little while I became as it were a debtor because of the sins of men.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)","This can also be understood more deeply, as the Lord says of himself: I am reckoned with those who go down into the pit: I have become like a man without help, free among the dead (Ps 87 [88]:4). For he was truly reckoned among sinners and the unrighteous, so that he might go down to the underworld, which in many places of the Scriptures is called the pit, and set free those bound in prison.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.88.5":["\"Like the slain that sleep in the sepulchres.\" Beautifully he said \"like the slain\": for He had no wound, but for the salvation of men He received a wound, as Isaiah says, \"He himself bore our wounds, and grieves for us.\"\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.6":["\"They have laid me in the lower pit.\" I went down to the depths of hell, that I might free all from the bonds of hell. \"In the dark places, and in the shadow of death.\" He did not say \"in death,\" but \"in the shadow of death.\" Death was not inflicted on me by the Jews, but the shadow of death: because for the salvation of men I went down of my own will. Therefore I went down as light into the lower regions, that I might free the souls from the darkness of hell.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.7":["\"Thy fury is confirmed upon me.\" For this reason all thy fury came upon me, that it might depart from others. \"And all thy waves thou hast brought in upon me.\" Upon me the whole tempest thundered down, that calm might come to the world.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.8":["\"Thou hast put my acquaintances far from me.\" In the passion of the cross even the apostles fled from me, so much so that Peter himself said, \"I know not the man.\" You see that we run with brief sentences rather than with expositions, because the hour so compels. \"They have made me an abomination to themselves.\" The Jews, namely, saying, \"Crucify, crucify such a one: we have no king but Caesar.\"\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)","\"I was delivered up, and came not forth.\" What is this that he said, \"I was delivered up, and came not forth\"? The Jews delivered me up, and yet on account of my clemency I did not forsake them, but I dwelt among them with affection. I was delivered up to Pilate by them, and I came not forth from them, saying on the cross, \"Father, spare them, they know not what they do.\" Let us say it also otherwise. \"I was delivered up, and came not forth.\" In the Gospel it is written that, when they sought to cast Him down from the brow of the mountain, He escaped safe and free from their midst. This, then, is what He says: Could I not even then, when I was delivered up, have escaped the snares of men and the perils of death by my divinity? But because I had come for this, that I might suffer, therefore I willed nothing except that I might suffer. That I was delivered up was of my will, and that I came not forth was of my will. Let us say it also otherwise. I was delivered up as a man, and I came not forth from the majesty of my divinity. Let us say it also otherwise. I was delivered up by men, and I came not forth from the bosom of the Father. Let us say it also otherwise. I was delivered up as a man upon the earth, and as God I had not left heaven.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.9":["\"My eyes languished through poverty.\" I who was whole in my majesty was made weak for the sins of men.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)","\"I have cried to thee, O Lord.\" Not only with the voice, but with the heart: for thus indeed we cry to the Father. \"Crying in our hearts, Abba, Father.\" \"All the day I stretched out my hands to thee.\" This is the testimony which the apostle in the Epistle to the Romans applied in the person of the Savior. You see that we do no violence to the psalm, but follow the authority of the apostle. For if he applied the testimony of a single verse in the person of the Lord, why should we not wish to expound the whole psalm in this way? And if we should wish to interpret it otherwise, the sense of our exposition is dissolved by this evident sentence of the apostle. If we should wish to expound the whole psalm, we seem to be making delay for ourselves, whom now the hour compels to approach with pure and unstained conscience, in the unity of peace, to the flesh of the Savior, the true Lamb, that we may be worthily filled with the heavenly bread, through Christ Jesus our Lord. To whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.10":["\"Wilt thou show wonders to the dead?\" Not to those who, living, are dead, but to me who live in thee. \"Or shall physicians raise them up?\" and the rest. Not the spices of physicians, but thou, Father, raisest me up; nor they, but I will confess among the peoples.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.11":["\"Shall anyone declare in the sepulchre,\" and so forth. He shall not praise thee who is buried in the riches of the world, nor he who dwells in the ruin of sin.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.12-13":["\"Shall thy wonders be known in the dark, and thy justice in the land of forgetfulness? And I have cried to thee, O Lord, and in the morning my prayer shall come before thee.\" That is, because thou wilt glorify me by a morning resurrection.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.14":["\"Why, O Lord, dost thou repel my prayer,\" that this cup should not pass from me?\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.15":["\"I am poor, and in labors from my youth\": from the time of the flesh assumed. \"Being exalted\": that is, upon the cross. \"I have been humbled\": that is, in the sepulchre.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.16":["\"Thy wraths have passed over me.\" They passed, and did not remain.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.17":["\"They have surrounded me like water all the day.\" The Jews in the waves of persecutions. \"They surrounded me together.\" The Pharisees with the priests. Pilate with the princes and the peoples.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.88.18":["\"Thou hast put far from me friend and neighbor, and my acquaintances because of misery.\" Because of the confusion of the passion, even the apostles themselves were put far from him.\n— Homily 65, on Psalm 87 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.1":["The eighty-eighth psalm is prefaced with this title: The Understanding of Ethan the Ezrahite. Wherever \"understanding\" is placed first, what follows is mystical and obscure. Ethan is interpreted as \"strong,\" or \"robust\"; and Ezrahite as \"one helped,\" or \"one who has obtained help.\" I say this because we are dealing with the literal history. Rightly, therefore, is \"understanding\" placed first, so that this strong or robust man may obtain help from the Lord, and may be able to recognize the things that follow. But the Ezrahite either took his name from a place, or was of the tribe of Merari. Read the books of Numbers and Leviticus, and there you will find everything more fully.\n\nThe Understanding of Ethan. \"Understanding\" is placed first, so that the reader may be summoned to knowledge. For if the history were plain, and had nothing mystical about it, what need would there be for the reader to be reminded of \"understanding\" in the title? And so that Ethan, as we have said, \"strong\" or \"robust,\" and the Ezrahite, \"one helped\" or \"one who has obtained aid,\" is rightly summoned to understanding, so that he may be able to know the things that are to be said. For it follows in the psalm, The Lord has sworn to David his servant, unto eternity I will prepare your seed. The Jews say that the Lord promised with an oath that from the seed of David there would not fail among them a leader or prince, which even now their patriarchs also say: Behold, to this day the Lord keeps his oath to us. But if they truly think this, how is it that we see them subject to the Roman power, and serving the commands of emperors? But mystically it is promised to us concerning Christ. And I will build up your throne unto generation. Did God, then, lie, in that he promised with an oath and did not do it (since surely what God promised ought to be firm), if indeed to this day we see that this does not stand in the race of the Jews? But here understanding is necessary. Rightly, therefore, does that strong or robust man have necessary understanding as an aid or help, so that he may be able to perceive the mysteries of this psalm. Let us return, then, to the beginning of the psalm.\n\nYour mercies, O Lord, I will sing forever. He did not say \"mercy,\" but \"mercies.\" If there were one sin, there would be need of one mercy: since therefore the sins are many, the mercies too are very many. Your mercies, O Lord, I will sing forever. Since I have obtained mercy forever, I ought to sing forever, because my cause for praising is eternal. He who sings has let go of grief, has let go of fear, and has crossed over to joys, because he has obtained mercy. And what he says is this: Because my sins have been forgiven me through the mercy of God, therefore I will sing his mercy. And when he says \"forever,\" this is the meaning: because you have had mercy on me not for a short time, but forever, I too will praise you forever without ceasing, because you have set me free forever.\n\nFrom generation to generation I will proclaim your truth with my mouth. O the excellent order of the psalm! He did not begin first from truth, and afterward come to mercy: but first he obtained mercy, and so came to truth, when he had received the forgiveness of sins. For first, while I was a sinner, I did not dare to approach the truth; but when I obtained mercy, now secure I dared to preach the truth. And when he says \"from generation to generation,\" he said it beautifully: for he did not say \"in generations and generations,\" but \"from generation to generation.\" He does not, then, commemorate many generations, but only two, one in the circumcision, the other among the Gentiles: to the one Peter is sent, and to the other Paul is sent. And what I forgot to say at the beginning, I will now say: here the person of the Lord and Savior must be established. From generation to generation I will proclaim your truth with my mouth. He who spoke earlier through the patriarchs and prophets, afterward himself speaks in person, as it is said in the Song of Songs: \"Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth.\" So then what he says is this: he of whom I spoke through the prophets, from that same source I now also speak through my very self. So that he whom the world could not hear when he thundered, it might hear at least when he wailed as an infant.\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.2":["For you have said, Mercy shall be built up forever. I myself, who then spoke in the Old Testament through the patriarchs and through the prophets, now myself speak through my own self; for I have not come to loose the commandments of mercy which the law had built up, but to build upon them. As he himself says in the Gospel: \"I have not come to destroy the law, but to fulfill it.\" What then does Moses say? \"Give heed, O heaven, and I will speak, and let the earth hear the words from my mouth.\" Jeremiah too speaks thus: \"Thus says the Lord\"; and the rest of the prophets speak thus: because what they speak are the words of the Lord, and not their own, and what he says through their mouth, it is as though the Lord had spoken through an instrument. What then is the meaning of what is said, \"shall be built up,\" as if some great house or city were being built? Let us see where else something of this sort is written about building. Let us search in Genesis, what this saying means. \"And God took,\" it says, \"a rib from Adam's side, and built it into a woman.\" See what he said: \"he built.\" For building, as we said above, is not usually spoken of except in the construction of a great house. Since therefore that rib of Adam was fashioned into a woman, according to the apostle's authority, it signifies Christ and the Church; therefore he said, \"He built the woman from the rib.\" We have heard of the first Adam; let us come to the second, and let us see how the Church is built from his side. For the side of the Lord and Savior, hanging on the cross, is pierced by a lance, and from it came forth blood and water. Do you wish to know how the Church is built from water and blood? First through baptism, by water, sins are forgiven, then it is crowned in the blood of martyrdom. Since therefore it is clear that the Church of God is built by mercy, therefore it follows, In the heavens your truth shall be prepared. On earth is mercy, and in heaven is truth. Hear, O heretic, what is said. In the heavens the truth of God shall be prepared. If souls fell down from heaven on account of the sins they had committed, how then is the truth prepared in the heavens? If daily souls are cast down to earth, how does the Lord teach in the Lord's Prayer and say, \"Your will be done, as in heaven, so on earth\"? Do we then ask, on the authority of the Lord and Savior, that just as the soul without a body sinned in heaven, so in the body it might sin on earth; and that the soul which, free and in the heavens, could not keep righteousness, should now, confined by the straits of the body, keep it on earth? A strange thing I see: the soul sinned in heaven, and was thrust down to earth, so that what it lost in heaven, it might find on earth. It sinned without a body: how can it be that with a body it might more easily deserve God? Since indeed \"the flesh lusts against the spirit, and the spirit against the flesh.\"\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.3-4":["I have appointed a covenant to my chosen ones. Do you wish to know how to this day the Lord appoints his covenant? Let us act well, and we shall deserve to be sharers in the Lord's appointment of the chosen ones. I have sworn to David my servant. Let us see what it is that the Lord swore. This surely, which follows. Unto eternity I will prepare your seed. Come to our aid, O apostle, and interpret this little verse. For what does the apostle say? \"He did not say 'in seeds,' but 'in the seed which is Christ.'\" This is what is said in the Apocalypse of John: \"He who has the key of David, who shuts and no one opens, opens and no one shuts.\" This key the scribes and Pharisees held in the law, to whom the Lord says in the Gospel: \"Woe to you scribes, who hold the key of the kingdom of heaven,\" and the rest. O Pharisees, who hold the keys of the kingdom, and yet do not believe in Christ, who is the door and gate of the kingdom: to you indeed it is promised, but to us it is delivered: you have the flesh, but we have the spirit: nay rather, because you denied the spirit, you have lost the flesh too along with the spirit.\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.4":["And I will build up your throne from generation to generation. In two generations, as we have said, the throne of God is set, namely in the circumcision and among the Gentiles.\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.5":["And the heavens shall confess your wonders, O Lord. This is what is said in the eighteenth psalm: \"The heavens declare the glory of God.\" According to the mystical understanding, he calls the apostles \"heavens.\" For if it is said to the sinner, \"Earth you are, and to earth you shall go,\" why should it not be said to the holy or righteous man, \"Heaven you are, and to heaven you shall go\"? For of the saints, according to the apostle, \"the conversation is in the heavens.\" And when he says, The heavens shall confess your wonders, it is a sign of the repentance to be preached, which we have received, at the apostles' proclamation, in the confession of the Lord Jesus Christ; and if we follow their admonitions and examples, we too can be called heavens, when we imitate those whose conversation is in the heavens. For the prophet says in another place: \"I am a sojourner on earth and a pilgrim, like all my fathers.\" And this he was saying in Judea, that is, in his own homeland: and how does he say he is a pilgrim? But it is because the saints in the present age are pilgrims on earth, desiring to possess nothing earthly, whose whole possession is in heaven, where one has dwellings, where one has \"the city whose maker and founder is God.\" And therefore the prophet too, who is not of this world, says he is a pilgrim here, and by the will of his mind he hastens to his homeland, that is, to paradise, to the kingdoms of the heavens. Do not say, O heretic, because I have said that paradise is the homeland of the saints, that I am asserting that souls existed before they came here into bodies. Nor do I say this, but I say it according to the apostle, who affirms that we were all, in the transgression of Adam, cast down from paradise through sin. This the apostle says, that Adam fell even in us, who were afterward to be. Therefore now in Christ, that is, the heavenly Adam, we believe, so that we who through the sin of the first Adam fell from paradise, may now through the righteousness of the second Adam return to paradise. The heavens shall confess your wonders, O Lord. What wonders? That you made heaven? Or that you founded the earth? Or that you gathered the seas into one? For what is this that is great in God, for whom to have spoken all things is to have made them? These, then, are the great things in God, the wonders in God, that God became man, that he lay hidden in the womb of a virgin, that born of a virgin he lay in a manger, that he was wounded by men, he who healed the wounds of men, that he was struck with blows, that he was crucified, that he, immortal, suffered to die, and endured so great a punishment unjustly inflicted by men, he who suffers these things so that men might not have to endure punishment. The sun could not bear to see its Lord hanging on the cross, but hid itself, lest it be mingled with the sacrilege of the Jews. The earth too trembled, the rocks are split, all creation is troubled, because its Creator is slain. The veil of the temple is torn, because what had been shut in Judea is opened among all nations; the veil is torn, and the mysteries of the law are shown to the faithful: for to the unbelieving they are covered to this day. For when Moses is read, and the Old Testament is recited by the Jews every Sabbath, according to the apostle \"a veil is placed over their heart.\" For they read the law, but do not understand, because their eyes are darkened that they should not see. For they are like those of whom it is said: \"They have eyes and shall not see, they have ears and shall not hear.\"\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.6":["For who among the clouds shall be equal to the Lord? The clouds are the prophets, the clouds are the apostles, who pour showers of teachings upon the dry hearts of men. Who shall be like the Lord among the sons of God? All the saints are named with the appellation and title \"sons of God,\" because they are sons by adoption; but the Son of God, our Lord Jesus Christ, is alone the Son properly by nature. Of whom it is said, Who among the clouds or among the sons of God shall be like the Lord? That is, who of the angels or of the saints shall be able to be made equal to the Creator in glory or in power, since he himself gives glory to all, so that his praises may be without end: he whom every creature dreads and trembles at, whom the cherubim and seraphim, whom the four living creatures praise without ceasing, whom the dominions and principalities adore, whom all the earth venerates? To whom is glory and dominion unto ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 66, on Psalm 88 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.89.32-33":["But that we may know that visitation is brought by God, as upon the wounded and the sick, for this purpose, that as by cautery and most bitter potions they may recover health and be free of diseases, let us hear God speaking through the prophet: I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with stripes; but my mercy I will not scatter from him. He, therefore, who is loved is corrected; he who is neglected is left to his sins.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.90.1":["A prayer of Moses, the man of God. There are four psalms that are headed with the title \"prayer\": the sixteenth, and the one that follows it, and the eighty-ninth, and the hundred and first. In the hundred and first it says: \"A prayer of the poor man, when he is downcast, and pours out his prayer in the sight of God.\" This one: A prayer of Moses, the man of God. We read the song of Moses in Exodus, at that time when the people crossed the Red Sea, and when Pharaoh and all his army had been drowned, Miriam and Moses sang. Then we read one in Deuteronomy also, since he had written that song in Deuteronomy. If therefore these psalms and these songs belong to Moses, why are they not found in his own books? So we have already explained how the hundred and first psalm is inscribed, A prayer of the poor man: and it is another who wrote about him, and that poor man who intercedes for us is the Lord, \"who, though he was rich, became poor for our sake,\" and the rest. So too here is Moses, since from the very beginning of the human race he himself wrote the history: for he himself wrote how heaven and earth were made, how man too was made, about the creation of man; and this psalm speaks about the creation of man, what man is, and why he was born. He therefore who in Genesis wrote about the creation of men, is now himself also brought in by the Holy Spirit to discourse on what man is.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","Lord, you have been made a refuge for us from generation to generation. He who seeks a refuge, seeks it either from excessive heat and scorching, or because a beast is pursuing him, or because enemies are pursuing him, to kill him. We too, then, are in the heat of this world; and a beast pursues us too, of which we say, \"Do not hand over to the beasts a soul that confesses to you\"; and enemies pursue us too; and therefore we flee for refuge under your wings. From generation to generation: both in the past, and in the present, and in the future. In another sense, From generation to generation: both in the old people of the Jews, and in our own people of Christians.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","The eighty-ninth psalm has been read, whose title is this: A Prayer of Moses, the man of God. It is the beginning of the fourth book, for we ought to know that the psalter is divided into five books. From the first up to the fortieth is the first book; from the fortieth up to the seventy-first is the second book; from the seventy-first up to the eighty-eighth is the third book; from the eighty-eighth up to the hundred and fifth is the fourth book; from the hundred and fifth to the end is the fifth book. And therefore this is the opening of the fourth book: A Prayer of Moses, the man of God. The Hebrews think that not only this psalm, but also the other ten that follow, since they bear no title, were composed by Moses. And for that reason they think that those which are without a title belong to the same author as the one that has a title, because they seem to lie adjacent to the one that is inscribed with a title.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","A Prayer of Moses, the man of God. If all men were men of God, it would never have been added, A Prayer of Moses, the man of God. Do you wish to know that not all men are men of God? \"Man, when he was in honor, did not understand: he was compared to the senseless beasts of burden, and was made like unto them.\" It is a great thing, therefore, to be called men of God or servants of God. For when this is said of someone, it is a token not of lowliness but of dignity; nor is the condition of servitude shown, but the dignity of one who serves well is displayed. A Prayer of Moses, the man of God. By Moses the law was written down, by Moses the nature of the world is shown, by Moses it was told how man was fashioned in the image of God. What, then, is this that he says, A Prayer of Moses, the man of God? Moses indeed writes how man was made; he himself also writes how man fell. Now, therefore, he prays that the one who fell into death may be lifted up to life, and that the one who fell through sin by the devil's doing may again rise through Christ to glory. For this reason, then, Moses makes a prayer to God, and says:\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","Lord, you have become a refuge for us. A refuge is never spoken of unless there has been persecution: for unless someone else persecutes us, we have no need to flee to another for refuge. Lord, you have become a refuge for us. Since a great swelter of tribulations and heat wearies us, we take refuge under the shadow of your wings. Lord, you have become a refuge for us. Therefore we flee to your help, because a great beast persecutes us; and since we have many adversaries and enemies, be you at least our one refuge.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.2":["Before the mountains were made, and the earth and the world were formed. At this place a heresy creeps in: If he was made a refuge for us before the mountains were made and the earth and the world were formed, then souls existed before the world came to be. For what does Scripture say? Lord, you have been made a refuge for us: from generation, it says, to generation: from the beginning of the world, before the beginning of the world. This is what they say. And do not think, he says, that I am the one saying it. Before the mountains were made, and the earth and the world were formed, you, he says, have been made a refuge for us. But if you were our refuge before the world was made, then souls existed before the world came to be. You divide the sentence wrongly, O heresy. Why do you slander the Holy Spirit? Speak in the way the Holy Spirit spoke. It should therefore be read thus: Lord, you have been made a refuge for us from generation to generation: and the pause is up to this point. Then another fresh beginning: Before the mountains were made, and the earth and the world were formed, from age to age you are God. You are God before the mountains were made, you are God before the earth and the world were formed, from age to age you are God, you always are, you are eternal.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","From generation to generation. In this place the serpent hisses. For those who say that souls were made before the world and before they were put into bodies, say this: Lord, you have become a refuge for us from generation to generation, before the mountains were made or the world was formed — that is, before this world was created, you were our refuge — punctuating badly, understanding badly. For we ought to say it thus: Lord, you have become a refuge for us from generation to generation. And the pause of the sentence goes this far. Then, what follows, thus: Before the mountains were made, and the world was formed, from age to age you are God. This, then, is said, not that man's soul existed before the age, but that the Lord God is shown to have always existed and to be before the ages. Finally the following verse too speaks fittingly, And from age to age you are God. From eternity to eternity you abide, having neither beginning nor end.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.3-4":["Do not turn man away into humiliation. And you said: Turn back, sons of men. O Lord, that we might dare to ask this, that we might say, Do not turn man away into humiliation, you have given us confidence by saying: \"Return to me, sons, as you turn back, and I will heal your afflictions.\" And you said: Turn back, sons of men. For a thousand years before your eyes are like a single day that has passed by: do not turn man away into humiliation. And a watch in the night. What did he mean to say by this, And a watch in the night? We ought also to know this according to the literal history: the night is divided into four watches. Indeed in the Gospel too it is said: \"But in the fourth watch.\" One watch, then, is three hours: so the night is divided into four watches. Indeed soldiers have these four stations, when they keep guard and relieve one another. See, then, what he says. All of our eternity: that Adam our forefather, who lived nine hundred and thirty years; even that Methuselah, who lived nine hundred and sixty-five years; suppose he had lived a thousand: in comparison with your eternity, what was great is short. For what can be great that has an end? Our thousand years, before we had committed offense, was one day in your sight. Why do I say, one day? one watch of the night. Do not turn man away into humiliation. See what he says: You deigned for our salvation to come down, you poured out your blood for us: do not allow us to perish, and to die in humiliation. You yourself said: \"When I have been lifted up, I will draw all to myself.\" You have been lifted up, and you have lifted us up: do not abandon us, so that we be dragged down.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89"],"PSA.90.3":["Turn not man away into lowliness. Moses prays on behalf of man, and what does he say? Do not suffer to perish him whom you fashioned in your image. Turn not man away into lowliness. Consider not the vileness of the body, but the dignity of the soul. Let it suffice us that we once heard, \"You are earth, and into earth you shall go,\" and that because of the consciousness of sin we are brought low. Turn not man away into lowliness. Turn him not away, but turn him back: for you have said through all the prophets, Turn back, you sons of men. Let it suffice that you have sinned: do penance, that you may be able to have salvation. Turn not man away into lowliness. Send your Son, that he may lift the body of our lowliness on high, and, freed from the burdens of the earth, raise it through a new manner of life to heaven.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.4":["For a thousand years before your eyes are as yesterday's day, which has passed. Compared to the eternity of God, all the time of man is short: although it seems long to us, yet in comparison with eternity, because it is bounded by an end, it is reckoned as nothing.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","And as a watch in the night. The night has four watches, marked off in periods of three hours each, as it is said in the Gospel, \"In the fourth watch of the night\" Jesus \"came to them,\" which is understood to be about cockcrow. This, then, is what he says: a thousand years with you are to be compared not only to the shortness of a day, but to the span of three hours.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.5-6":["In the morning let it pass like grass, in the morning let it flower and pass away: in the evening let it fall, harden, and wither. O the mysteries of the Scriptures! In the morning let it pass like grass. Our Lord and Savior also speaks in the Gospel about the hay, which springs up in one day and withers in one day. Just as grass that is green and flowers seems in the morning to be, as it were, a flower and green: yet when the sun comes and scorches it, toward evening it wilts, as it were, and falls; so too all our life seems to be, as it were, a flower while it is green, and it withers. Ten years ago you saw a young lad: nothing more beautiful than he, he ran this way and that. If you look at him now, he seems in old age to be, as it were, dead. Though he lives in old age, yet his youth has perished. In the morning let it pass like grass, that is, our youth: for that is the morning. Let it pass like grass, in the morning let it flower and pass away. We flower in youth, we wither in old age. In the evening let it fall, harden, and wither.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","Their years shall be counted as nothing. What he says is this: since man from the beginning of the world lived nearly a thousand years, and a thousand years before God are as a watch in the night. Nay, I have said enough: the ages of men are as nothing in comparison with the divine eternity. Do you wish to know more fully how human life is nothing? Hear what follows. In the morning let it pass like grass. As the morning dew passes swiftly, so is the life of men. Our morning is youth. In the morning let it flower and pass. In youth and full age we flowered, but in old age we fall down, and the flower of our strength has withered. For which of us knows how our age passes by? While we scarcely know it, old age steals up on us from behind. In the evening let it fall, harden, and grow dry. Our evening is old age, which, when the flower of youth has been shaken off, is hardened by the manifold horror of diseases. \"For all flesh indeed is grass, and its glory is as the flower of grass: it withered and fell; but the word of the Lord abides forever.\" We still live, and part of us has already perished in old age: though the mind is the same, yet we who feel that we have lost the vigor of our former youth are in a manner become other men.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.7":["For we have failed in your anger, and in your wrath we have been troubled. Formerly we used to live nine hundred and thirty years, we had reached up to a thousand: behold, our life has been contracted to a brief span.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","In your anger we have failed. Because through our sins we have earned your anger, we have lost in Adam the immortality you had given, from whom sin has been handed down to us, as it were naturally, by hereditary right. And in your fury we have been troubled. Beautifully he said, troubled: for one who is troubled does not perish, but is shaken with terror, so that he may feel the power of him whom by sinning he had scorned, and may flee, humble and submissive, to repentance. And in your fury we have been troubled. Because we have given offense, we are thrown into confusion; but because, believing, we have received your Christ, we are consoled.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.8":["You have set our iniquities in your sight. Nothing is hidden from you: that is, neither does night hide our sins, nor do shadows envelop them: all things shine bright before you. Our age in the illumination of your countenance. It stands better in the Hebrew: \"Our faults in the illumination of your countenance.\" Whatever we do, whatever we think we are doing in secret, lies open in your eyes.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","You have set our iniquities in your sight. Nothing of our sins has been hidden from you: all the evils we have committed are manifest before you.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","Our age in the light of your countenance. All that we live, or that we do, is not hidden from your eyes: the very darkness is not concealed from you.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.9":["For all our days have failed. Our lifetime has run its course: and while we are unaware, it slips away, we perish; and this very thing that we are speaking is part of death, and we do not understand. Our years will be pondered like a spider. See what he says. Just as a spider casts out its threads, as it were, and runs this way and that, and weaves all day long, and the labor indeed is great, but the result is nothing: so too the life of men runs this way and that. We seek possessions, we prepare riches, we beget sons, we labor, we are raised up to kingdoms, and we do everything: and we do not understand that we are weaving a spider's web.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","For all our days have failed. So do the days pass and death draw near, that in a manner, while we know it not, our whole age and life itself is stolen away, scanty as it is. This very thing, which I now speak and you hear, is cut away from a part of our life; and although we believe there to be in it some advancing of the soul, yet because it uses up the span of hours, it is a loss of time and of age. And thus it comes about that where we think we have gain, there rather loss is brought upon us.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","Our years shall be pondered like a spider. He could have said nothing more beautiful than to describe human life and all our anxiety and pursuits, in which we run this way and that and lay up wealth, seek riches, build houses, beget children: and see to what a thing they are compared. Our years shall be pondered like a spider. For just as we see the spider draw out its threads on the walls and weave its webs, which are undone by a sudden wind; so every act of our life is scattered by the sudden onset of tribulation or of death.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.10":["The days of our years in themselves are seventy years. Where are the thousand years? We have been contracted into seventy years: but if more, eighty. But if we live longer, it is no longer life, but death. But if among the powerful, eighty years. For whom will you find to be an eighty-year-old and to be healthy? The Greek saying also says: \"Old age is itself a disease.\" And what is beyond them is toil and sorrow. Whatever is more is not life, but sorrow.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","This much we have said for the time being according to the literal history: but now let us speak according to the anagogical sense. And indeed every psalm admits of a figurative interpretation; and I could now run through it verse by verse, and give the spiritual understanding in each: but the hour shuts us out. Let me at least give you in a single verse, as it were, a taste of the spiritual understanding, so that whatever I here leave unspoken, you may consider to have been spoken here. The days of our years in themselves are seventy years. Seventy years are seven decades, and eighty years are eight decades. See, then, how the seventh and the eighth number is proclaimed. And just as we said, You have been made a refuge for us from generation to generation, both in the old and in the new testament: therefore now too he sets down both the seventh and the eighth number. This he says also in Ecclesiastes: \"Give a portion to seven, and give a portion to eight.\" Therefore there are also fifteen Psalms of Degrees, that is, seven and eight: which fifteen together lead us to the kingdom of heaven. And indeed, after the seventh Psalm of Degrees has been completed, what does Scripture call that psalm itself? \"A psalm,\" it says, \"of Solomon,\" who had built the old house. And what does it say? \"Unless the Lord builds the house, in vain have they labored who built it.\" What then does Solomon himself say, who is prefixed in the very title? I built the temple: but unless the eighth number builds, the house is built in vain. We read also in the Gospel according to Matthew: Blessed are the poor, blessed are the meek, blessed are those who hunger and thirst, and the rest, and there are eight beatitudes. Therefore our Lord enclosed the Gospel in the number eight. Indeed nowhere do we find eight beatitudes together except in the Gospel. In the number eight, that is, on the day of the resurrection. The days of our years in themselves are seventy years. Do you wish that I should meanwhile show you that the seventh and the eighth number contain very many mysteries? Let us read Ezekiel, and at one gate it is said: \"And let him enter,\" it says, \"through the gate of the east into the temple that is being built.\" In Ezekiel, in the last part. \"And the high priest shall go up,\" it says, \"by seven and eight steps.\" And could it be that at that gate there were both seven steps, and eight? But see what he says. The eastern gate, from which the light comes. Our Lord and Savior. There is indeed one gate, and no one enters through it except the high priest. And what is said there? \"This gate shall always be shut.\" And it is not opened except to the priest. So therefore both the new and the old testament was always shut: it was not opened except to the Savior. For he is the one \"who holds the key, who opens and no one shuts, who shuts and no one opens.\" So one gate has both seven and eight steps. And in the old testament indeed there are evangelical mysteries, and in the new testament there are mysteries of the law. Hear greater mysteries. At that time when Solomon, that is, our peacemaker who says, \"My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you\": for \"he himself is our peace,\" and \"the peace that surpasses all understanding,\" he himself built a temple to God. And see there what Scripture says. \"And there were,\" it says, \"seventy thousand stonecutters, and eighty thousand of those who carried burdens.\" See the number. The stonecutters who cut the stones, who prepare, as it were, the foundations of the buildings, who lift up the stones from the earth to build in the temple of God, are placed in the seventh number, in the prophets, in the patriarchs. For they, drawing out the human race, as it were, from the earth, prepared them for the temple of the Lord. But these others, that is, the eighty thousand, are the apostolic and evangelical preaching, these who together with the Lord the Savior and Solomon himself carried the heavy sins of the nations. Up to this point the mystery seems to be finished: hear greater mysteries. \"And those who were in charge,\" it says, \"of these works and of the temple, were three thousand.\" There can be none greater, even among those who preside over the works, than those who preach the Trinity. The days of our years in themselves are seventy years. There are many things that might be said, but the hour shuts us out.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","The days of our years in themselves are seventy years. See how gradually human life has been reduced. First in paradise we had received eternity, which we lost through disobedience: afterward the life of the ancients was extended to a thousand years: now, in the dregs of our time, one scarcely reaches seventy.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","The days of our years in themselves are seventy years: but if in strength, eighty years: and more than these is labor and sorrow. See what he says, More than these is labor and sorrow. This is what we said above, that where we think there is gain in our life, there we find loss and disadvantage. Where there is increase of years, there is old age: where old age, there diseases: where diseases, there torments: where torment, there the longing for death.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","For mildness has come upon us, and we shall be chastened. What is this mildness, through which we are chastened? \"Remember, Lord, David and all his mildness.\" What is this mildness? \"Learn that I am mild and humble of heart.\" Mildness has come upon us. He did not say, the mild one has come, but mildness has come, which is our Lord Jesus Christ; who, just as in another place he is called not wise but wisdom, so here too he is named not mild but mildness itself.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.12":["Make known your right hand, and those fettered in heart in wisdom. In some manuscripts it says \"the learned,\" in others it says \"the fettered.\" \"Learned\" means one thing, \"fettered\" means another. What then does he say: Make known your right hand? Why so long do you draw back your right hand, O God? \"Why do you turn away your hand into your bosom?\" it says in another psalm. What is being said is this: We are laid low in sickness, we lie in sins: send forth your right hand, and lift us up. Why so long do you keep your right hand in your bosom? Let your heart pour out your good Word: send forth your right hand, and set us free. Make known to us the mystery which was hidden from the generations. Make known your right hand. What are you stealing away by stealth, O Arius? He did not say, make your right hand: for God was never without his right hand. But what did he say? Your right hand, which always existed, and was in your bosom, make it known. Since we cannot know him remaining in his deity, let him take on manhood, and thus let us understand him. Make known your right hand thus, and those fettered in heart in wisdom: those who have the fetters of wisdom, that is, who are fettered in the heart through your wisdom. Do not let it trouble you that the passage is obscure. The prophets had wisdom: but that very wisdom, as it were, fettered them; they could not fly without Christ. See, then, what he says: Send forth your right hand, that is, your Son, and make him known to us; and let the prophets, who prophesied, as it were, the shadow, begin to preach the Lord more manifestly.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","Make your right hand thus known, and those fettered in heart in wisdom. Your right hand, with which you made the world, enlighten the world. \"For the arm of the Lord, to whom has it been revealed?\" Your right hand, with which you made man, let it itself take up and save the human body. And those fettered in heart in wisdom. It can be understood thus: those who before wandered freely, let them be fettered by your wisdom. For it is better to be well fettered than to have feet ill at liberty; it is better to stand well than to run badly. Just as that colt in the Gospel, which before was free, and unbridled went along over the precipices, afterward, when sat upon by the Lord, is reined in and is taught to walk well: so we pray you, that you make your right hand known to the world, so that the wandering and unstable may be fettered by your wisdom.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.13":["Turn back, O Lord, how long? We have sinned much, you turn away your face, our sins are not worthy to be seen by your eyes, and therefore we pray: Turn back, O Lord, how long? Behold, now so many ages have flowed by: the prophets have always proclaimed to the nations that you would come, and still you have not come. And direct the sons of men. We who are crooked, may we be set straight by you. And direct the sons of men. Well did he say, direct the sons of men. Direct them for this reason, because they are sons of men. For if they were your sons, they would have been set straight. For you yourself said: \"I said: you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High. But you shall die like men, and shall fall like one of the princes.\" And in another place: \"Sons of men, how long will you be heavy of heart?\" And in another place: \"My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh.\" Since they are men, therefore they are flesh. So because we are sons of men, and we have lost the state of being sons of God, and we have become crooked: therefore direct us, so that we may again be made your sons.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","Turn back, Lord, how long? Turn back, Lord, and look upon your image: do not show us your back, but your face. Turn back, how long? He who says how long shows that he desires, and cannot bear the delays of his desire.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.14":["We have been filled in the morning with your mercy. There are mysteries in what is said in the psalms, and all things are full of figures. Moses prays in the person of the human race, that the Son of God may deign to come to the lands of earth. And because he had said, Make your right hand thus known, now, because he feels himself heard, he adds and says, We have been filled in the morning with your mercy, when your Son on the third day rose again from the dead. And because in the evening he descended for our life into the darkness of death, that he might draw us out to the light of morning, in that hour we come to know the fullness of your mercy. Whence the twenty-first psalm is entitled with this heading: \"For the morning taking up.\" Your mercy indeed always appeared to the saints; but never was it so manifestly and so fully poured out as when the Redeemer of all rose again for the common salvation.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)","And we have exulted and been delighted. After the joy of the resurrection of our Lord, by which we now believe both that we have been redeemed and that we shall rise again, for the remaining days of our life, rejoicing in this confidence, we exult, and with hymns and spiritual songs we render praise to God through Jesus Christ our Lord: to whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 67, on Psalm 89 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.90.17":["And let the splendor of the Lord our God be upon us. The Apostle also says to the Hebrews that the Lord and Savior is the splendor of the glory of the Father. And let the splendor of the Lord our God be upon us. This is what is said also in another place: \"The light of your countenance has been sealed upon us, O Lord.\" The sign of the cross is our banner.\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89","This much we have said for the present about the psalter, and I reckon we have lingered longer than we should have. We realize that we have said much more than we ought to have said; but we cannot keep silent about the words of our Lord, we cannot leave a psalm we have begun. Whatever profits the soul is not too much; nothing is enough of what is good, if indeed it is good. Let us therefore ask our Lord and Savior that his splendor may shine forth in our hearts, that it may cast out all our darkness, that it may drive away from our hearts the dark thoughts and those of all sins, that it may illumine our darkness, that the shadows may flee, and that his splendor may dwell in our hearts. The temple of the Lord and the tabernacle and the holy of holies always had a burning lamp, always a lamp was shining in the temple of the Lord. For if the lamp is extinguished, the Lord does not dwell in darkness. Let us therefore say, we too: \"Your word, O Lord, is a lamp to our feet.\"\n— Homily 19, on Psalm 89"],"PSA.91.1":["Who dwells in the help of the Most High. \"Help\" is what Ezra means. Indeed, the prophet Ezra is called βοηθός (boēthos), \"helper.\" This is the one who led the people back from the captivity of Babylon and brought them into the land of promise. Whoever, therefore, dwells in Ezra, whoever dwells in Jesus (for the moment according to the tropological sense), who built for himself a house without hands, as the apostle says — that house which the Lord had built even for the midwives in Egypt: he shall abide under the protection of the God of heaven.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","The praise of a song, by David himself. First we ought to know this: that from the eighty-eighth psalm up to the hundredth—that is, eleven psalms—have no title in the Hebrew; and that they belong, as we treated most recently in the previous psalm, to the same person to whom that one belongs as well which is headed with this title: \"A prayer of Moses, the man of God.\"\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)","He who dwells in the help of the Most High. Ours it is to begin, God's it is to help; for He does not crown those who sleep, but those who labor. He shall abide in the protection of the God of heaven. In place of what we read, \"of the God of heaven,\" the Hebrew has saddai. Hence that which is said in Ezekiel, \"And I heard the voice of the Lord Saddai speaking,\" can be understood thus: that he heard the voice of the Lord, the God of heaven, speaking; for Saddai is interpreted \"God of heaven.\"\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.2":["He shall say to the Lord: You are my upholder and my refuge, my God. For many enemies pursue me: therefore you are my refuge. I will hope in him. He did not say, \"I hope\"; but, \"I will hope.\" For as long as we sin, we do not hope: if we cease to sin, we hope.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","My refuge is my God. Beautifully he said, \"refuge,\" for there are many persecutors, and you alone are a refuge. Many are the wounds, but you alone are the physician.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.3":["For he himself will free me from the snare of the hunters. There are many hunters in this world, who try to hunt down our soul. Indeed even that giant Nimrod was a great hunter in the sight of the Lord: and Esau was a hunter, because he was a sinner; and nowhere in the holy Scriptures do we find any holy hunter. Fishermen we find to be holy. For he himself will free me from the snare of the hunters. \"Our soul, like a sparrow, has been snatched from the snare of the hunters: the snare has been broken, and we have been set free.\" What is this snare that has been broken? \"The Lord,\" he says, \"will crush Satan under our feet speedily.\" And the apostle says: \"That you may be freed from the snare of the devil.\" You see, therefore, that this is a hunter, who desires to hunt our souls to their destruction. The devil has many snares, and he has diverse snares. Greed is a snare of the devil, slander is a snare of the devil, fornication is a snare of the devil. And from the harsh word. As long as we are in holiness, our soul is tranquil; but when we begin to think about sins, our soul is troubled, and is shaken as though by certain waves.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","I will hope in Him, for He Himself shall deliver me from the snare of the hunters. In this world there are many hunters who long to capture our souls, just as is said by the prophet: \"Our soul has been snatched away like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters.\" Now that hunters are taken in a sinister sense in the holy Scriptures, we have already said often. For that Nimrod, the giant, always a rebel against God, was a hunter. Esau too was a hunter. For He Himself shall deliver me from the snare of the hunters. We read in Proverbs: \"The eye of the harlot is the snare of the sinner.\" \"For whoever shall look upon a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.\" As many as are the sins, so many are the snares; as many as the snares, so many the hunters. The doctrines of the heretics are the snares of death. Finally there follows: and from the harsh word. You see that it is spoken concerning the heretics. In place of what we have, \"harsh,\" the Hebrew has \"hidden.\" For heretics always promise certain secret and great things, so that they may snatch away those things which are true and manifest.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.4":["With his shoulders he will overshadow you. But this is according to that passage of Deuteronomy: The Lord protects us with his wings like his own chicks, as an eagle does. For it is said there that he protects his chicks like an eagle. Although, therefore, we can say this, that God protects us like a father, and protects us like a hen protecting her chicks, lest they be seized by the hawk: nevertheless we can also say something else. With his shoulders he will overshadow you: he will be lifted up on the cross, he will stretch out his hands, and will protect us. And under his wings you shall hope. You shall look, he says, upon his crucified hands, and if the serpent bites you, you shall be healed. Although you walk in the desert of this world: if a scorpion strikes you, if a basilisk, if an asp, if the other venomous creatures, be secure concerning your healing: the serpent has been lifted up in the wilderness.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","His truth shall surround you with a shield. Our shield is round, that is, it hedges us in on every side. Not only is it a shield, but it is also a wall. \"Lord, as with a shield of your good will you have crowned us.\" Does someone crown with a shield? See what he says: Lord, as with a shield of your good will you have crowned us. What does he say? You will protect us, and make us conquer, and after the victory you will crown us. His truth shall surround you with a shield. Since he had said \"from the harsh word,\" and by the harsh word we can understand also that of the heretics, and of the philosophers, and of the Jews, and this, as it were, harsh word is not true, but is false. For it is turbid precisely because it is false; it is turbid precisely because it disturbs our souls through its lie. The Lord therefore, who is truth, and says \"I am the truth,\" his truth surrounds us with a shield, lest we be struck by the burning arrows of the devil. Therefore Christ, who is the truth, sets his shield against them, so that by the shield of truth he may overcome the lie.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","With His shoulders He shall overshadow you. Who shall overshadow? Surely the God of heaven, as a hen her chicks, and as an eagle its nest. Hence also in the song of Deuteronomy it is said that He carried the people of Israel upon His shoulders, and protected them as an eagle. It can also be understood of the Savior, that upon His cross He protected us with His wings. And under His feathers you shall hope. \"All the day long I have stretched forth my hands to a people that did not believe and that contradicted me.\" The hands of the Lord raised to heaven did not ask for help, but were protecting us wretches.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)","His truth shall surround you with a shield: that is, you shall be encircled round about with truth.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.5":["You shall not fear the terror of the night. The devil is always in darkness: \"that he may strike,\" he says, \"the innocent in secret.\" You see, therefore, that whoever is simple and innocent is always struck in secret. You shall not fear the terror of the night. What he says is this: Although you may be in terror, which is night, nevertheless you shall not fear, because you have the shield of truth.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","Of the arrow that flies by day. To this sense there seems to be, as it were, a contradiction: Of the arrow that flies by day. The flying arrow of the devil seems to me to be the discourse of the heretics and philosophers. For these too promise the light of knowledge, and say that they have day; and they cannot deceive unless they promise light. But that light which they promise is from burning arrows. And therefore it is said, Of the arrow that flies by day.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","You shall not fear from the terror of the night. The just man has no terror of the night, but of the day. \"Come, children, hear me, I will teach you the fear of the Lord.\" Those who are the children of the prophets have no terror of the night, nor of the darkness, but of the light and of the day. \"That they may shoot in secret at the upright of heart.\" The devil does not send his arrows in the day, lest he be seen. For everything that is done in the light is made manifest by the light.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)","But someone may say: If the devil does not shoot in the day, how then in the following little verse does the psalmist say, From the arrow that flies by day? What is this arrow that flies by day? It is the teaching of the heretics: which, as it were through the day—that is, the law of God—flies here and there, while they anxiously gather up testimonies searched out against us, which by their own interpretations they corrupt away from the truth.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.6":["Of the business that walks about in the darkness. The arrow indeed flies as though by day, but the business is one that walks about in darkness.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","Of the assault and the noonday demon. It is better said in Greek, ἀπὸ συμπτώματος (apo symptōmatos). Symptosis means either an occurrence, that is, when we do not expect it, and something new happens. Or it is called symptoma, when many fall at the same time. See therefore what he says. Although many have been led astray, nevertheless you, who are holy, are not to be led astray. I give an example, so that the simpler ones may be able to understand what is said. If you go into a city as a monk alone, and begin to walk about, and hear a shout in the circus, and someone says to you: Come and watch, it is the circus; and you begin to say to him, It is not permitted, I cannot go: if he shows you countless thousands of men, and says to you, Two hundred thousand men are there: are all those then going to perish, and you alone will be saved? — you ought to understand that it is a symptoma of the devil; that is, you ought to know that many are falling. And the noonday demon. Many indeed say that there are also noonday demons, and this is said among the common folk. But for my part I say plainly that a demon has power over us at the time when we sin. Whether we sin in the morning, the demon enters into us: whether at evening, whether at night, at whatever hour we sin, the demon enters into us. But if we do not sin at noon, he does not enter into us. You see, therefore, how frivolous is what is said among the common folk. What then does he say, Of the noonday demon? Because Abraham received the angels at noon, and Joseph invited his brothers to a meal at noon. For both Abraham and Joseph were holy, and therefore their banquet was in clear light and full sun, therefore they feed at noon. And I too, if I have full knowledge of the Scriptures, although it be the third hour, nevertheless I feed you at noon. Indeed even the bride in the Song of Songs says: \"Where do you feed, she says, where do you lie down at noon?\" You see that the bridegroom does not feed, does not recline at the third hour, but at noon. Where do you feed, where do you lie down, where do I find you, where do you take your delight, where can I find you, O my bridegroom? Do you wish to find me? At noon, in perfect knowledge, in good works, in clear light. Since therefore we have our noon, therefore the devil too transforms himself into an angel of light, and he himself pretends that he has light, that he has noon. When the heretics promise, as it were, certain mysteries, when they promise the kingdom of heaven, when continence, when fasts, when holiness, when renunciation of the world, they promise noon. But since it is not the light of Christ, it is not noon, but the noonday demon.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","And from the business that walks about in the darkness. He did not say, \"standing,\" but \"walking about\"; because heretics are never stable in their opinions, but are always changing and walking about. From the onslaught and the noonday demon. We read in Genesis that Joseph fed his brothers at noon; and in the Song of Songs it is written: \"Where do you lie down, where do you feed? At noon.\" Therefore, just as the saints have a noon and a light where they may feed and lie down, so too the devil, transfigured into an angel of light, has his ministers transfigured, as it were ministers of righteousness. Therefore when you see a heretic preaching, and saying, \"This says Moses, this is written in the prophets, this in the Gospels,\" does it not seem to you—if you are ignorant of the treachery of his mind—that you are hearing an angel of the Savior speaking? But the simple think there is a certain noonday demon who at noon can more readily assail men. We, however, shall interpret the chiefs of the heretics as the noonday demons, who, feigning themselves angels of light, preach the doctrines of darkness. Do you wish us to say something elegantly? Arius, or the rest of the heretics who came forth from Alexandria, are called noonday demons.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.7":["A thousand shall fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand. The individual words of the Scriptures are individual mysteries. Those rustic words which are thought so by the men of the world are full of mysteries. \"For we have this treasure in earthen vessels\": we have the treasure of the divine meaning in the most worthless words. A thousand shall fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand. At whose side? At the side of him to whom it had been said, His truth shall surround you with a shield, you shall not fear the terror of the night. Who is this? He of whom it was said above, Who dwells in the help of the Most High, he shall abide under the protection of the God of heaven. From him, therefore, who dwells in the help of the Lord, a thousand shall fall at his side, and ten thousand at his right hand. The right hand of the holy man is spoken of: the left is not spoken of, but the side. For see what is said: And ten thousand at your right hand. And how had you said it? A thousand shall fall at your side. For when the right hand is spoken of and the left is not named, in place of the left the side is named. For it could not be just that the just man should have a left hand. \"Whoever,\" he says, \"strikes you on the right cheek, offer him the other as well.\" He did not say, \"and the left\": for it is not a left cheek that is offered, but another right one. Therefore I say plainly that the just man has two right hands. Indeed even that Ehud, who is written of in the book of Judges, is said to have two right hands for this reason, because he was just, and he killed that very fat king. A thousand shall fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand. There are more who lie in wait for our right hand, there are not more who lie in wait for the left: therefore at the side a thousand shall fall, and at the right hand ten thousand. For where the greater battle is, the greater victory. Few lie in wait for the side, more lie in wait for the right hand.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","A thousand shall fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand. \"At your side\" and \"at your right hand.\" He did not say, \"at your left,\" because the just man has no left, just as it is written in the Gospel: \"Whoever shall strike you on the right cheek, offer him the other also.\" He did not say, \"offer the left\"; since indeed the lambs shall stand at the right, and the kids at the left. A thousand shall fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand. By the \"side\" of the just man we can understand the weaknesses of the body; but by the \"right hand,\" the integrity of the soul. By the labors of the body—that is, by fastings and chastity—a thousand shall fall; but at the right hand, where through the freedom of the soul there is pure and holy doctrine, there shall fall the very great multitude of the enemy. But to you they shall not draw near. Imagine the fortified wedges of the enemy coming against the just man, concerning whom it is said, But to you they shall not draw near: that is, they shall collapse before they can reach as far as you.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.8":["Nevertheless with your eyes you shall consider, and you shall see the retribution of sinners. You shall see the corpses of the dead, and you shall marvel at the majesty of the Lord and Savior. And you shall see the retribution of sinners. The ruin of sinners is their destruction. What he says is this: You who have made the Lord your refuge look with your eyes, secure, upon the retribution of the heretics, since you have guarded yourself from their traditions.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.9":["These things have been said in the person of the Lord to the just man; but now the person is changed, and out of the contemplation of the just man an answer is made to the Lord: For you, O Lord, are my hope. For he who says these things is the just man. You have made the Most High your refuge. Again the person is changed: and for this reason the psalms are obscure, because very often the persons are changed. For while the just man calls upon the Lord and says, For you, O Lord, are my hope, he hears from the Lord, You have made the Most High your refuge. And therefore the following verse is fitting for you:\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.10":["Evils shall not come to you, and the scourge shall not draw near to your tabernacle. He did not say, \"shall not sound,\" but, \"shall not draw near to your tabernacle\": for it cannot come to pass that the scourge of the devil should not sound for the just man. For if the Lord Himself was scourged, and was vexed with tribulations and temptations, which of the just shall not be scourged? But let us see what this is, \"shall not draw near to your tabernacle.\" This is it: it sounded indeed from without, and by fleshly passions it tried to disturb; but the tabernacle of the soul, or the mind, which is the tabernacle of faith, it did not overthrow.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.11-12":["For he has given his angels charge concerning you, to guard you in all your ways. This passage the devil interpreted concerning the Lord. For at the time when he tempted the Lord, \"If,\" he says, \"you are the Son of God, cast yourself down. For it is written concerning you, that he has given his angels charge concerning you, to guard you in all your ways. In their hands they shall bear you up, lest perhaps you dash your foot against a stone.\" The devil said nothing more than this: For he has given his angels charge concerning you, that in their hands they may bear you up, lest perhaps you dash your foot against a stone. He left out the middle part. For here it is said, For he has given his angels charge concerning you, to guard you in all your ways. This he did not say, To guard you in all your ways: for he knew it was against himself. For the Lord could have answered him: If the Lord guards me in all my ways, then I am guarded from you too. He was silent, therefore, about what he knew was against himself, and set down what was in his favor.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90","For He has given His angels charge concerning you, that they may keep you in all your ways. In their hands they shall carry you, lest ever you dash your foot against a stone. If ever the heretics interpret certain things from the Scriptures against us, we ought not to be disturbed: because the devil too used this testimony against the Lord and Savior, saying, \"If you are the Son of God, cast yourself down; for it is written that He has given His angels charge concerning you,\" and the rest. The devil interprets the Scriptures like a devil: for he was applying this psalm to the person of the Lord, which is spoken not of the Lord, but of any just man. But I grant you, O devil, that it be written concerning the Lord Himself, \"in their hands the angels shall carry you\": why did you not say what follows, \"Upon the asp and the basilisk you shall tread\"? O the cunning of the crafty, shape-shifting enemy! What seemed to pertain to the frailty of the Savior and to the temptation of the man, he spoke; but what he understood to be against himself, with his ancient craftiness he kept silent. You err, O devil, and by your deceit you ensnare your very self. In this place your lie profits the Church: although you did this in order to tempt, you have shown that the children of God, all the just, are carried by the hands of the angels—that is, by their help—at the command and bidding of the Lord.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.91.11-13":["He has commanded his angels concerning you, that they bear you up in their hands, lest perhaps you strike your foot against a stone. This we read in Psalm 90 (91). But there the prophecy is not about Christ but about a holy man. So the devil interprets the Scriptures badly. Surely, if he really knew it was written of the Savior, he ought also to have quoted what follows in the same psalm against himself: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon. Of the help of the angels he speaks as though to a weak man; of his own trampling, like a shifty evader, he is silent.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.91.13-16":["Truly the devil, like a devil, interprets concerning the Savior what is not concerning the Savior. For if the whole psalm is about the Savior, as you interpret it, O devil, then it is about him too what is said: I will protect him, because he has known my name. That is, I the Father will protect my Son. Let me interpret it, devil, as you understand it. Let us see whether the sense holds. I will protect him, because he has known my name: that is, I the Father my Son. He shall cry to me, and I will hear him: that is, on the day of the cross. I am with him in tribulation, I will rescue him and glorify him: that is, I will make him rise again. With length of days I will fill him. That is, he shall live forever. And let us understand this concerning the Son of God, With length of days I will fill him. But this that follows, how does it hold with respect to the Savior, And I will show him my salvation? The salvation of God is the Son of God. If therefore it is said about the Son, how can it hold? For the salvation of God is the Son of God. You see, therefore, that you have interpreted badly, O devil. But grant that it be interpreted as you say. You said, For he has given his angels charge concerning you, that in their hands they may bear you up, lest perhaps you dash your foot against a stone. Say also what follows. Why are you silent? Upon the asp and the basilisk you shall walk, and you shall trample under foot the lion and the dragon: Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 20, on Psalm 90"],"PSA.91.13":["The rest of what follows pertains to the same exposition. But that the just and those dear to God have the power to trample upon the ancient and twisted serpent, and to press down his whole army with the heel of the foot, we find most evidently in the Gospel. For thus the Lord says to the apostles: \"Behold, I have given you power to tread upon serpents and scorpions.\" Which power we too shall be able to understand as given to us, when we know ourselves to be girded against them by an apostolic way of life. Let us therefore ask the Lord that He who gave power to the just may give also to us, still little ones, the help of righteousness: that we may be imitators of the apostles, and may be aided by their merits, in Christ Jesus our Lord. To whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)","There is need, therefore, of the lifting up of the eyes and of spiritual understanding, that we may see the opposing powers which have lifted up their horn against us, and of which the Apostle speaks: Our wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of this world, of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. These four adversaries, who were always fighting against the saints, Christ crushed at his coming. And of these the psalmist says over again: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.92.1":["The ninety-first psalm is prefaced with this title: A psalm of a song for the sabbath day. No one can keep the sabbath day unless he has first passed through six days. For six days we labor; on the seventh day we rest. Therefore we cannot sing to the Lord except on the sabbath day. As long as we do the works of this world, that is, during the six days, we cannot sing to the Lord. And so it is said in Leviticus: \"You shall do no servile work on the sabbath day.\" Whoever, then, is in the sabbath and in the Lord's rest ought not to do servile work, that is, work that pertains to this world; but he ought to do that work which pertains to the sabbath. Do you want to know that on the sabbath the priests work in the temple of the Lord? On the sabbath no one is permitted to cut wood. Indeed, one man who was found doing so in the wilderness was stoned and killed. Likewise it is not permitted to kindle a fire, nor even to do any work at all. But you see that all these things, which no one in the world is permitted to do on the sabbath, are permitted to the priests alone: for they both cut wood, and lay on the fire, and perform other ministries, and offer up sacrifices. Why have I said all this? To show that it is written in the law that we too must cease even on the sabbath day from the works of the world, and do those things that pertain to God. So then, it is a psalm of a song for the sabbath day, when we do not work for the world, but work for God. Let us see, therefore, what we ought to sing on the sabbath, when we cease from the works of the world.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","It is good to give praise to the Lord, and to sing to your name, O Most High. He did not say, it is good to sing, and afterwards to give praise; but see the order: It is good to give praise, and it is good to sing. First do penance and wipe away your sins with tears, and afterwards sing to the Lord. It is good to give praise to the Lord: not to men, but to God: confess your sins to him who is able to heal. And to sing to your name, O Most High.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","It is good to give praise to the Lord, and to sing psalms to your name, O Most High. Why did he not say first, \"It is good to sing psalms,\" and afterward \"to give praise\"? But because this was the order of true confession, therefore he spoke thus: it is good first to do penance, and for a person to confess his sins to the Lord; and when he has confessed his sins, then to sing psalms to him, when by doing penance he has earned the free voice with which he can burst forth in the praise of God without the confession of sin.\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.92.2":["To proclaim your mercy in the morning, and your truth throughout the night. Understand carefully what is said: To proclaim your mercy in the morning and your truth throughout the night. Each little verse refers to a corresponding one. It is good to give praise to the Lord — to proclaim your mercy in the morning. If you give praise to the Lord, you proclaim in the morning the mercies of the Lord. And to sing to your name, O Most High. Refer this verse to the one that follows: And your truth throughout the night. For when we give praise to the Lord, we hope for mercy; but when we sing, we practice the virtues. To proclaim your mercy in the morning. Where are those who understand the scriptures according to the letter? Can we not proclaim the mercy of God at midday? And surely it belongs above all to Christians and monks to confess their sins in the night. And how does he now say, To proclaim your mercy in the morning? What he says is this. We cannot give praise to the Lord and obtain his mercy unless a bright light has begun to shine in our heart. Unless the darkness has gone before and the morning has come, we cannot obtain the mercy of the Lord. But then you proclaim the mercy of the Lord in the morning, when the sun of righteousness has risen in your heart. And your truth throughout the night. The truth of the Lord is not proclaimed except toward the night: in the day it cannot be proclaimed. For the weak cannot bear things that are great, nor can they endure things that are precious. Hence it is said also in another place concerning God: \"He made darkness his hiding place\" — that is, the Lord made darkness his hiding place. And so the truth of the Lord is proclaimed in the night: it is wrapped up, as it were, in a certain darkness of words. It is spoken in riddle and in parables: that those who see may not see, and those who hear may not hear. We read in Hosea, and it is said to the prophet: \"Go and take to yourself a wife, a wife of harlotry.\" The Jews hear it and the gentiles, and they laugh. Why do they laugh? Because the truth is placed in the night, not in the day. Do you want to know that the truth of God is in the night, and, as it were, in darkness, and is set in parables? Moses went up onto Mount Sinai: and he entered, it says, into the whirlwind and into the gloom and into the darkness, and there he spoke with the Lord. For the people of Israel could not see the mysteries of God, because it had not entered into the gloom of God.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","To announce your mercy in the morning, and your truth throughout the night. Why is mercy sung in the morning, and truth in the night? Pay careful attention, for the things that are being said are obscure. First he said, \"It is good to give praise to the Lord\"; then praise follows upon confession; and thus the one who after penance earns the right to praise will also begin to announce the one whom he praises. But what does it mean that he said mercy is announced in the morning, and truth is sung in the night? Wherever the mercy of the Lord is, there is light, there is morning, there is the hour in which the sun rises and the darkness of blind night will perish. And your truth throughout the night. Where there is truth, there is justice; where there is justice, there is judgment; where there is judgment, there is no mercy; where there is no mercy, there is the peril of sinners, there is night and not day, as the prophet says, \"What good is the day of the Lord to you? For this is darkness, and not light\" — on which day the examination is to be conducted according to the merit of each one. Whence in another little verse the prophet says that the day is to be feared even by himself... \"...will endure it?\"\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.92.3":["On the ten-stringed psaltery, with a song upon the harp. I say it plainly: as often as we lift up our hands without wandering thoughts and disputings, we sing to the Lord on the ten-stringed instrument. On the ten-stringed psaltery, with a song upon the harp. Our harp is body and soul and spirit: they are composed of all the strings.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.4":["For you have delighted me, O Lord, in your making, and in the works of your hands I will exult. A Christian, whatever he sees, is always built up. Nothing tears down a monk except sin. Whatever you look upon is your own edifice. You see the sky, you see the sun, you see the stars, you see the moon: this builds you up. For you ought to think and say: If the sun and the moon and the sky and the stars serve God, why should I not serve? You see the earth, you see the animals and all the things that are upon the earth: bring this too to bear upon your soul, and build yourself up, and say to your soul: All things run in their own season; consider indeed spring and summer, autumn and winter — do these seasons ever change? No, according to the ordinance of God all things serve. The sky serves, and the earth serves: and I, unhappy man, do not serve. Let us come to the little animals: I leave aside now the elephants, I leave aside the lions, I leave aside the various animals, I come to the little animals. Look at the bee, look at the ant: see the body, and search out the wisdom. The wisdom is greater than the size of the body. Bees and ants consider that winter is coming: and the monk and the Christian do not consider that judgment is coming. Those creatures consider that they may be endangered by hunger, if they have not labored in the summer to have something to live on in the winter: we do not consider that without work we will be tormented in hell. Why have I said all this? Because it is now written, For you have delighted me, O Lord, in your making. Whatever I look upon delights me: for I understand the maker, and I bless God. And in the works of your hands I will exult. I see a tree, and I consider how the bark of the tree is, as it were, clothed like a tunic, how it grows green like a bud. Then I see how there is, as it were, a blossom, how the blossom falls, and the blossom itself turns into fruit. I see nature little by little, through each single day and into each single season, working for me and growing into my food. I see how God in all things labors for me, so that nothing may be lacking to me: and therefore I exult in you, O Lord.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","On the ten-stringed psaltery with a song upon the harp. For you have delighted me, O Lord, in your making, and in the works of your hands I will exult. This cannot be said except by a learned man, one who knows that by the providence of God each single thing has been created; who is not scandalized by the work of creation, but understanding the reason of each individual thing, glorifies the Creator and says, \"For you have delighted me, O Lord, in your making, and in the works of your hands I will exult.\" Hear, O Manichaean, hear, O Marcion, Valentinus, and the rest of you heretics, who dare to disparage the Creator, and see that in the very thing over which you will suffer scandal, the prophet is delighted and exults in the works of the Creator, knowing that if anything should seem to be flawed, it has come not from nature, but from the will.\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.92.5":["How magnified are your works, O Lord. At this point Marcion and Manichaeus burst out. For what do they say? If God made all things, and made them for the benefit of men, what need was there to make the bedbug and the flea? I answer you briefly: so that your frailty, O man, might be shown. For you who place your thought in heaven — look, you are bitten by a bedbug and you shudder. Why are you lifted up into heaven, and why does your thought soar into heaven? Behold, you are wounded by a bedbug. How magnified are your works, O Lord. Small bodies indeed, but great design. Each has its own uses. I marvel at the Lord in the elephant just as much as in the ant; I proclaim him in the camel just as much as in the gnat. Look at the gnat, how it is like the point of some tiny body, and yet see how in that very point of a tiny body all its members are distinct. It has six feet, it has two eyes, it has a mouth and a belly, it has also a trumpet of voice, it has wings too. If you look at the members, you do not find the body; and yet God is magnified, because without a body there are all these members.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","Your thoughts have been made exceedingly deep. Whatever I may think, I cannot find it out. \"Your judgments are an abyss.\" \"O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God.\" I too will say with Ecclesiastes: \"I said, I will become wise: and it was made far from me, much farther than it was; and it is deep — who can find it out?\" What Ecclesiastes says is this: Before I put my thought to the work, I did not see the magnificence of God. I said, I will become wise, that is, I said, I ought to seek out the reasons of the individual causes; and it was made far from me, much farther than it was, that is, the wisdom which before, not searching, I was ignorant of, afterwards I began to seek, and I could not find it out.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","How magnified are your works, O Lord: your thoughts have been made exceedingly deep. Whence the apostle too says, \"O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God!\" Therefore, then, because your thoughts have been made exceedingly deep, and his works, which neither human sense nor reason can attain, in another place it is said, \"All your works are in faith.\" See what he says, \"Your works are in faith.\" If the creature stands firm in faith and not in reason, how much more the Creator himself and the Founder of all things? When therefore it is said, \"And all your works are in faith,\" I too, who am speaking, am in faith — since indeed I am a part of his works. I too stand firm in faith and not in reason: for I cannot know by reason how I move my steps, how the tongue makes its sound, how thought arranges itself, the will commands, and the body serves the will, how the soul, which is immortal, is joined to this body which is mortal, how my soul runs to and fro here and there, and that vast breadth of the world does not contain a thing which is comprehended by the body. Why do I say all this? Because I heard that among the brothers there was a contention — nay rather, a disputation — over the faith. I say a disputation, not a contention: for where there is contention, there is sin; but where there is disputation, there is a pious desire for knowledge. For each one was not seeking to prove the things he did not know, but they brought their ignorance to my knowledge: for they did not wish to teach what they did not know, but to know what they were ignorant of. Therefore their question is not foreign, which was raised concerning the mercy of the Lord. It is said in Isaiah concerning the Lord Savior, \"Who shall declare his generation?\" When it is said, \"Who?\" every creature is excluded. For he did not say, \"Which of men shall declare his generation?\" but generally, \"Who?\" — that is, not an angel, not an archangel, not a cherub, not a seraph. For if that which is spoken of was hidden in God from the heavenly creatures, what wonder if it seems unknown to us who were made from clay? It was reported to me, brothers, that certain brothers among themselves in disputing had asked how the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are both three and are one. You see from the question how perilous disputation is: mud and an earthen vessel disputes about the Creator, and cannot arrive at the reason of its own nature; and it curiously seeks to know about the mystery of the Trinity, which the angels in heaven cannot know. For what do the angels say? \"Who is this King of glory? The Lord of hosts, he is the King of glory.\" Likewise in Isaiah it is said, \"Who is this who ascends from Edom, his garments white?\" See what they say: they proclaim his beauty, and about his nature they are silent. Therefore let us too speak simply. Do you wish to know the nature of God? Do you wish to know what God is? Know this: that you do not know. And do not be saddened in this, that you do not know: because the angels too did not know, and likewise every creature. But someone may say: Do I then believe what I do not know? How am I a Christian, who do not know how I am a Christian? I will speak simply, before we bring forward anything from the Scriptures. O Christian, why do you think you know nothing? If you know this very thing, that you do not know, do you not rather seem to yourself to know more? The pagan man sees a stone, and thinks it a god; the philosophers see the sky, and think it a god; others see the sun, and think it a god. But you — see how much greater knowledge you have over these, you who say: A stone cannot be a god, the sun cannot be a god, which runs at the command of another. See that in the very thing in which you say you know nothing, you know more; but the pagans, in that which they say they know, do not know, because the things they have come to know are false. And at the same time consider what you are called: faithful indeed, not rational. In short, when baptism has been received, we say this: He has become faithful, or, I have become faithful. I believe what I do not know: and therefore I know, because I know myself not to know what I do not know. But someone may say: This is not to explain, but to flee the explanation; and we knew that we knew what we did not know — you, teach us to know what we are ignorant of. Is it not better piously to profess ignorance than rashly to lay claim to knowledge for oneself? On the day of judgment I am not condemned because I say, \"I did not know the nature of my Creator,\" but if I should say something rashly: rashness has punishment, ignorance earns pardon. Let us say something from the Scriptures, not following reason, but the authority of the Lord Savior. For what does he say, about to ascend to heaven, speaking to the apostles, to whom he preaches as master and Lord? No one knows how to speak about his own nature as does he who is himself God: let it suffice for us to know about the Trinity what the Lord has deigned to explain. For what does he say to the apostles? \"Go, baptize all nations in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.\" I hear three names, and one is spoken: for he did not say, \"in the names,\" but \"in the name.\" He says three names: and how does he say one, \"In the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit\"? The name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit is one: but it is the name of the Trinity. For what is said is, in the name of the Father who is God, in the name of the Son who is God, in the name of the Holy Spirit who is God: the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit are one name of divinity. But you ask of me how three are called by one name? I do not know, and I freely profess that I do not know, what Christ did not wish to make known. This alone I know, that in this I am a Christian, that I confess one God in Trinity. But if I should say that there is one person of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, I shall be called a Sabellian, and I begin to be not a Christian but a Jew: because the Jew too says there is one God, but because he does not know the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit. Therefore he does not hold the mystery of the Trinity. Therefore if we so say \"one God\" that we exclude the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit from the mystery of the Trinity, we become Jews. I speak freely, not from myself, but from the judgment of the Savior, of the scandal that arises in the mind of the hearer: How are three one? How are the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit not divided in divinity? Whenever I say \"persons,\" I beg you, do not think that I am speaking of persons of men: I do not speak of persons in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit as though persons of men, but according to distinctive properties. I speak of the person of the Father, because he is the Father, and of the Son, because he is the Son, and of the Holy Spirit, because he is the Holy Spirit. The Father is not the Son, nor is the Son the Father, nor is the Holy Spirit the Father or the Son: for they are divided by properties, but united by nature. See, this very scandal the apostle Philip once bore, when he said to the Lord, \"Lord, show us the Father, and it is enough for us.\" To whom the Lord in answer said, \"Philip, so long a time I am with you, and do you not know the Father? He who sees me, sees the Father also.\" That the Father himself is the Son, this is impious to say. In that you see the Son, understand the Father: for he cannot be called Son, unless he has had a Father. When Father is said, it is not said unless he has had a Son.\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)","There are many things that might be said: but let it suffice for the faithful to have heard a few things about the mystery of the Trinity. I beg you, let there rather be a contest among us in the monastery over how we may seek to triumph over the adversary, how we may fast, how we may bewail our sins, how our thought may not drag us captive into lust, how we may have patience against every injury, how we may not resist a brother doing us an injury, but may conquer him in the humility which we have learned from Christ, \"who, when he suffered, did not threaten.\" If ever a thought should come and add, \"What is God? and what is the reason of the Trinity?\", let it suffice for us to believe that he is. And we ought not rashly to seek the reason, but to fear, and always to pray to the Lord; and in this matter let us show him our knowledge alone, that day and night we may sing praises to him. To whom be glory and dominion for ever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.92.6":["The foolish man does not know, and the stupid man does not understand these things. Whoever is not a Christian, and in the works does not understand the creator, that man is foolish.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.7":["When sinners spring up like grass. You have seen emperors, you have seen prefects, you have seen armies, you have seen victories and triumphs. Yesterday it was, and today it is not. And all who work iniquity have appeared, that they may perish forever and ever. Yesterday it appeared like a flower, today the flower itself does not appear. Yesterday the grass was green, today it is dried up. Where is that beauty? Nothing is good except what is eternal: nothing is good except what is everlasting. Whatever has an end is not even to be counted among good things. For what does it profit me, if yesterday I was fed, and today I die of hunger? For what does it profit me, if a few days ago I was a king, and today I die in prison? Everything that is past and has an end is nothing. Each one of us has come from the world to this life. One left his mother behind, another his sons, another his wife, another his parents. Let us remember our infancy, let us remember our youth; let us remember that we once had riches, and each one according to his means had what he could. Behold, we have passed over to this life. Where are all those things? Thought about, they cause pain rather than pleasure. Nothing therefore is good except what is everlasting.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.8-9":["But you, O Lord, are the Most High for evermore. For behold your enemies, O Lord, for behold your enemies shall perish, and all who work iniquity shall be scattered. If they perish, how shall they be scattered? For he who has once perished cannot be scattered. He ought to have said: Behold, they shall be scattered, and they shall perish. What he says, then, is this: Your enemies, O Lord, shall perish; when all things have been made subject to you, everyone who was your enemy afterwards becomes a friend, that is, the man will not perish, but the enemy will perish. He who was an enemy will be a friend, and thus the enemy will perish. For behold your enemies shall perish, and all who work iniquity shall be scattered. Just as, when the saints are together, it is pleasing; so, if sinners are together, it is, as it were, a most wicked gathering. For the more of them there are together, the worse they are. Indeed, at the time when the tower was being built against God, they were scattered for their own benefit. Their agreement was evil: but the scattering profited even those very ones who were scattered.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.10":["And my horn shall be exalted like that of the unicorn. The horn is always set in the kingdom. \"In you we will toss up our enemies with the horn.\" Indeed, no animal is offered in sacrifice to the Lord in the temple except one that has a horn. Three things are offered to the Lord in the temple: the bull is offered, the ram is offered, the he-goat is offered. Three are offered, and all are horned. For unless someone has a horn with which to toss up his enemies, he is not worthy to be offered to God. For this reason the Lord too is called a horn by those who believe in him; and on the horns of his cross he tossed up his enemies. There he confounded the devil, and all his army. Christ indeed was crucified in the body, but there in truth he was crucifying the demons. It was not a cross: but it was a triumph, it was a battle-standard. For this reason he mounted upon the cross, that he might lift us up from earth on high. I think that this cross of the Savior is the ladder which Jacob saw. On that ladder the angels were descending and ascending; on that ladder, that is, the cross, the Jews descend, and the gentiles ascend. And my horn shall be exalted like that of the unicorn. Let others have many horns: I have one horn alone. \"But far be it from me to glory, save in the cross of the Lord: through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world.\"\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91","And my old age in plentiful mercy. Our old age needs the oil of God. For just as our bodies, when they grow weary (I speak rather plainly for the sake of the simpler brothers, who cannot understand more loftily) — just as our bodies, when they grow weary, are not restored except by the anointing of oil; just as the light that is in a lamp, unless you put in oil, is extinguished: so also the light of my old age needs the oil and mercy of God in order to grow. Indeed the apostles too go up onto the Mount of Olives, so that, because they were weary and their lamps needed the oil of the Lord, they might be illumined by the oil of the Lord. Hence the righteous man also says: \"But I am like a fruitful olive tree in the house of God.\" And in another place it is said: \"Your sons are like young olive shoots around your table.\" Was there no other mountain from which the Lord might ascend to the kingdoms of heaven, except the Mount of Olives? Was there not a loftier mountain in Galilee, Tabor? What need was there for the Lord to ascend into the kingdoms of heaven from the Mount of Olives? But see what the scripture teaches you. Unless you put oil into yourself and into your lamps, you will not be able to ascend into the kingdoms of heaven. You ought to be on the Mount of Olives: not in the valley of the Olives, but on the Mount of Olives. Let someone say: What is this valley of the Olives? The devil too has his olive trees: he has philosophers, he has heretics; they too have oil, and they too promise the light of knowledge. But those olive groves lead down into the valley: \"But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head.\" Let us, then, ask the Lord that our old age and all our toil and all our darkness may be illumined by the oil of the Lord. I attend carefully in the scriptures to where I may first read of old age. Adam lived nine hundred and thirty years, and yet he is not called old. Methuselah lived nine hundred and sixty-nine years, and he is not called old. I come all the way to the flood, and after the flood through nearly three thousand years: and I find no one who is called old. Abraham is the first to be called old, and he was certainly of much less age than Methuselah; but he is called old for this reason, that his old age had been anointed with plentiful oil. Indeed it is written there: \"And Abraham died,\" it says, \"nourished in a good old age; full,\" it says, \"of days.\" For this reason in a good old age, because he was full of days: for all the time of his life was days, and not night. This very thing that is said of Abraham is said also of Isaac, is said also of Jacob. For those who had served the Lord together are rightly called together the old men of the Lord.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.12":["Like the cedar of Lebanon he shall be multiplied: a sweet-smelling wood, the wood with which the roofs of the temple are covered. Like the cedar of Lebanon he shall be multiplied: a wood that does not rot.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.13":["Planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of our God they shall flourish. In one place we are planted, in another we flourish: here we are planted, in the kingdom of heaven we shall flourish. \"I,\" he says, \"planted, Apollos watered, but the Lord gave the increase.\" Planted in the house of the Lord, that is, in the church, not in the walls, but in the doctrines: \"For the kingdom of God,\" he says, \"is within you\": whoever has been planted in the house of the Lord, who has sent forth his roots here, there brings forth flowers. Planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of our God they shall flourish. These are the eternal tabernacles. What are these courts? The various dwellings in heaven. And at the same time see what he says: Planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of our God they shall flourish. Here is the house, there is the court. Surely, according to the order, he ought to have said first the court, and so the house. But see what he says. Here we may seem to be in the house: but when we have migrated to the kingdoms of heaven, in comparison with the angels and the other powers, we are not in the house, but we are in the court: we are at the beginning, not in perfection. For we shall not be angels, but as it were angels. Do not let it seem to you too small a thing, O man, if you shall be like an angel.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.92.14":["They shall still be multiplied in a plentiful old age. Happy is he who grows daily, who grows old in age, and grows old also in virtues.\n— Homily 21, on Psalm 91"],"PSA.93.1":["The title of the present psalm is this: A psalm of praise of David, on the day of the sabbath, when the earth was inhabited. On the sabbath day is our rest, and the inhabiting of the earth is ours. 'And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us.' Our earth, therefore, has been inhabited. Let us see, then, what this dwelling accomplishes, and with what praises it is extolled.\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)","The Lord hath reigned, he is clothed with beauty: the Lord is clothed with strength, and hath girded himself. For he hath established the round world, which shall not be moved. These three little verses are spoken by the prophet concerning the Lord and Savior; but the rest that follow are directed to the Lord himself. The Lord hath reigned, he is clothed with beauty. The Lord hath reigned, he who before had served. He is clothed with beauty. What does Isaiah say? 'We saw him, and he had no comeliness of his own: a man laid under a stroke, and knowing how to bear infirmity.' The Lord hath reigned, he is clothed with beauty. What he says is this: He who before had been an object of horror in the lowliness of the flesh, now shines resplendent in the majesty of the Godhead. For whatever in him is unshapely, let it be reckoned to our condition; but whatever there is of beauty will be fittingly applied to his own majesty. And he hath girded himself. This that he says, he hath girded himself, must signify some mystery. When we are ungirt, our tunic is let down all the way to the feet: unless it has been drawn up by a belt, the whole garment hangs down. So then, we too, because we could not bear the full strength of the Lord, he girded his majesty with flesh. This is what is said in another place: 'Thou shalt not be able to see my face; thou shalt see my back parts.' It is also said of Elijah in the book of Kings that he stood on Mount Sinai in a cleft, and thus saw the Lord passing by. See how, set in the cleft, he is said to have seen the back parts of the Lord and Savior. If the Lord and Savior is interpreted as the rock... 'And Christ,' says the apostle, 'was the rock.' For when we are set in the cleft of the rock, we behold God through the flesh. Why have I said all this? Because we embrace the following verse also with the same exposition: he is clothed with beauty, and hath girded himself. Just as the Lord and Savior is seen through the cleft of the rock, so too through the girdle his majesty is beheld.\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)","For he hath established the round world, which shall not be moved. If we understand this world to have been so established by God that it is disturbed by no commotion ever, how is it said elsewhere, 'Heaven and earth shall pass away'? But if heaven and earth shall pass away, how is it said here, He hath established the round world, which shall not be moved? The οἰκουμένη (oikoumenē) in this place, which in our tongue is rendered 'the inhabited world,' seems to me to be the Church, of which it is said in the Gospel: 'I and my Father will come, and will make our abode with him.' Rightly, therefore, is it called a habitation, which has as its inhabitant the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit. What, then, we have understood concerning the inhabited world—that is, concerning the round earth established forever—let us understand also concerning our soul. For if we have as our inhabitant the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, our earth shall not be moved forever.\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.93.2":["It has been said concerning the Lord's reign and concerning the beauty of his garment, it has been said concerning his strength, it has been said concerning the round world which shall not be moved: now the prophet's speech is directed to the Lord himself, reigning and thundering. And what does he say? Thy throne is prepared; from then thou art, from everlasting. This that he said, He is clothed with strength and hath girded himself, comes down from the lowliness of the flesh: but for the rest, thy kingdom has always been. Thy throne is prepared: always prepared, never beginning. From then. From when? From what time? It has no definition, because it has no beginning either. However often you say, 'from then,' the time never gives out, but you leave it in uncertainty. Because when you say 'then,' it is fitting that you be asked: When? You see, therefore, that the prophetic speech is set down without a definition, since you perceive that it admits of a question. From then thou art, from everlasting. Beautifully he said, thou art; he did not say, from everlasting thou didst begin, but, from everlasting thou art. Thou art who ever art, who saidst to Moses: 'Go, and say to the children of Israel: He who is hath sent me to you.'\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.93.3-4":["The floods have lifted up, O Lord, they have lifted up their voices; from the voices of many waters, wonderful are the surgings of the sea. As many words, so many meanings: as many little verses, so many sacraments. The floods have lifted up their voices. This is what is said in another place: 'The rush of the river maketh glad the city of God.' And in another place: 'The rivers shall clap their hands together.' The floods seem to me to be those which water the whole world, the apostolic and prophetic dignities. The floods have lifted up their voices. Proclaiming the praise and glory of the Lord, announcing his judgments through the whole world. 'Their sound went out into all the earth, and their words unto the ends of the round world.' From the voices of many waters. The voices of many waters have furnished us with three mysteries. Let us see what those mysteries are. From the voices of many waters, wonderful are the surgings of the sea, wonderful is the Lord on high: thy testimonies are made exceedingly credible. If we understand the sea mystically as this world... for it is bitter and full of tempests, in which those who sail behold many wonders of the Lord. On account of which he says: Wonderful are the surgings of the sea. For we see many, lifted up by swelling waves, suddenly collapse; but others we see, oppressed by the billows, again raised up to the heights. For nothing in this world is found firm, nothing stable. Where you think there is calm, there is shipwreck: where the appearance of quiet is shown, there the assault of the tempest and the whirlwinds lie hidden; and unless we cry out, 'Master, save us,' we suddenly perish. Wonderful is the Lord on high. Whoever has been able to withstand firmly the billows of this world's sea, dwelling steadfastly upon the rock, he will be able to understand how wonderful is the Lord on high. 'For the exalted one dwells in the exalted places, and the holy one in the holy'; as he also says elsewhere, 'Be ye holy, as I also am holy, the Lord your God.' But the man whom the consciousness of his sins humbles, and who cannot lift up his eyes to heaven together with Jesus, in his sight the Lord is not wonderful on high, but terrible to those beneath.\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.93.5":["Thy testimonies are made exceedingly credible. This is what is said in the seventeenth psalm: 'The testimony of the Lord is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones.' To what little ones? 'Father, I give thee thanks, because thou hast hidden these things from the wise and prudent of the world, and hast revealed them to little ones.' Holiness becometh thy house, O Lord. See what he says. Whoever seems to himself to dwell in the house, if he has not holiness, is a stranger to the house, because the house would not have its ornaments. Holiness becometh thy house, O Lord. If, according to the apostle, we are the temple and house of God, then the holiness of our manner of life ought to be the ornament and adornment of the Church; but if sins and vices are caught in us, we are the disgrace and foulness of the Lord's house, not its beauty. Holiness becometh thy house, O Lord. The house of the Lord is the Church. And the Church, according to the mystical understandings, was already prefigured in the flood by the ark, in which the clean animals, at the Lord's command, were multiplied the more. And although, on account of the unclean and the clean, beasts of diverse kinds dwelt there, nevertheless the clean animals became the Lord's house, which by their uneven number already prefigured the sacrifices of virginity: from which Noah, delivered from the perils of the flood, offered to the Lord a worthy holocaust from the clean animals. There are many things that might be said, but the hour shuts us out, and let it suffice to have said a few things concerning the praises of the Lord. To whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 70, on Psalm 92 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.1":["We are always discussing the title, so that from the title the psalm too may be understood. \"A psalm of David, on the fourth day of the week.\" The fourth day of the week is in the middle of the week, as if in the heart of the week — that is, in the middle of the seven days. For it is the fourth day, and the fourth day has an equal number of days on either side. For on one side it has the first, second, and third day; and on the other side it has the fifth, sixth, and seventh day. You see, therefore, that the fourth day — that is, the fourth day of the week — is made firm on either side by a double trinity. And at the same time it must be considered that the fourth number, in its power, is the tenth. How so? For if you count and make an ordered sum — one, two, three, four — the number ten is produced. You see, therefore, that the fourth number produces the tenth. And consider from this that the four Gospels are reckoned within the Decalogue, so that whatever was said in the Decalogue is fulfilled in the four Gospels, so that the old Law is not at variance with the dignity of the Gospel. So much, for the moment, we have said about the title; now let us come to the psalm.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"The Lord is the God of vengeances; the God of vengeances has acted freely.\" He who for so long lay hidden and did not appear among the people, afterward acted freely. He who before was unknown, afterward triumphed on the cross. \"The Lord is the God of vengeances.\" If the Lord is the God of vengeances (\"Vengeance is mine, and I will repay, says the Lord\"), why do you, O man, desire to avenge yourself? You have the Lord as your avenger. The Apostle says this too: \"If your enemy is hungry, feed him; if he thirsts, give him drink: for in doing this you will heap coals of fire upon his head.\" This does not seem to belong to kindness, but to cruelty. For if I do him good so that God may do him evil, this does not come from kindness, but from a cruelty of mind. For what does the Apostle say? \"If your enemy is hungry, feed him.\" A dutiful utterance. \"If he thirsts, give him drink.\" Up to this point it seems to be mercy. But what he adds — \"for in doing this you will heap coals of fire upon his head\" — does not seem to belong to mercy, but to cruelty. For you seem to give bread and to give water to drink for this reason: that he may be tormented for ages. And does the Apostle say this — does the preacher of mercy teach cruelty? It must therefore not be taken so, but in another sense. If your enemy strikes you, offer him the other cheek as well; if he does you evil, do him good: for if you do this, you will heap coals of fire upon his head. What he says is this: you will cleanse him from sin; for his cruelty will be overcome by your patience. If you aim an arrow at a stone, and the stone is hard, not only is it not driven in, but it rebounds: so your enemy, if he has struck you and has not been struck back, overcome by your patience will return to better things.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","Deus ultionum Dominus, Deus ultionum libere egit — \"The God of vengeance is the Lord; the God of vengeance has acted freely.\" Whether you say the Father, or the Son, or the Holy Spirit, according to the principle of the Trinity which we set forth in the Gospel, he is both God and Lord. \"The God of vengeance has acted freely\": he who was despised in his lowliness will afterward take vengeance in his majesty.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.2":["\"Be exalted, you who judge the earth.\" You judge the earth, because heaven does not deserve to be judged. Listen, heretic, to how the Lord judges the earth. If there is a fall in heaven, why is there not also a judgment in heaven? If souls sinned in heaven, why is the earth alone judged? \"Render their recompense to the proud.\" All sins indeed must be guarded against, because all are contrary to God; but they are so in various ways: proud sins are enemies to God. For \"the Lord resists the proud, but to the humble he gives grace.\" The devil is the prince of the proud. \"Lest,\" he says, \"puffed up in pride, he fall into the judgment of the devil.\" For everyone who exalts his own heart is a sharer with the devil, who used to say: \"By my strength I will act, and by the wisdom of my understanding I will remove the boundaries of the nations\" — and the rest, which it would be tedious to enumerate, in which the prophet describes the pride of the devil. The other vices earn the mercy of the Lord, because by their humility they are made subject to God: pride alone, which lifts itself up beyond its strength, resists God. The adulterer or the fornicator has not dared to lift his eyes up to heaven; with a downcast spirit he hopes, in supplication, for the mercy of the Lord; but the man whom his conscience makes turn away, it humbles upon the earth, yet it lifts him up to heaven. Pride and the appetite for glory, when they raise a man up, then abase him, because they make God the enemy of his sin.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.3-4":["\"How long shall sinners, O Lord, how long shall sinners glory?\" The sinner who beseeches God earns pardon; but he who glories in his wickedness is proud: and pride has God for its enemy. This which he says, \"how long?\" — it is to be understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (\"in common,\" carried over), so that we may recognize it thus: \"How long shall sinners glory? How long shall they utter and speak iniquity? How long shall they speak, all who work injustice?\" Human impatience therefore marvels at the patience of God, and says: I, a sinner, cannot endure sinners; how can you, who are just, bear with them? \"How long, O Lord, shall sinners glory?\" He did not say, \"How long shall sinners sin?\" He has a great sin who sins, and moreover glories in his sin. \"Shall they utter and speak iniquity?\" All sins are cloaked with speech: but pride betrays its iniquity, while it glories in its own wickedness.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.3":["\"How long, O Lord, the sinners — how long shall the sinners glory?\" Human impatience does not want God to have patience. Wretched are we, who want God to be patient toward us and impatient toward our enemies. Whenever we sin, we desire him to be patient; but whenever someone offends against us, we do not want God to be patient toward that man. \"How long shall the sinners glory?\" It is not enough for them to sin, but they even glory in their sins. The first misfortune is to sin; the second misfortune, or rather the last, is not to do penance. These sinners, therefore, not only do not bend the neck, but freely glory after their sin.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93"],"PSA.94.4":["\"They will utter and speak iniquity.\" He speaks of this properly concerning the heretics. \"All who work iniquity shall speak.\" See the sign: he who speaks evil must necessarily do evil. \"For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.\" Wherever the conscience is wounded, there sin is easily committed in the mouth.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93"],"PSA.94.5":["\"They have humbled your people, O Lord.\" Always the people of God are humbled, the people of God are oppressed. \"They have humbled your people, O Lord.\" The people of God are humbled, and imitate God who was humbled and meek of heart.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"Your people, O Lord, they have humbled, and your inheritance they have harassed.\" Who? The proud.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.6-7":["\"They have slain the widow and the stranger, and murdered the orphans: and they said, The Lord shall not see.\" Although this is plain according to the literal history — for always whoever needs protection is easily oppressed, and the more lowly anyone is, the more does the one who oppresses him offend God — nevertheless let us also interpret it according to the tropology. For I had said that this is said of the heretics: \"They have slain the widow and the stranger.\" That widow — that is, the soul of the sinner, which has ceased to have God as its husband. A stranger is one who is not a resident — that is, one who does not dwell. He who had recently believed, being suddenly scandalized, perishes. \"And they murdered the orphans; and they said, The Lord shall not see.\" Do we wonder that they are called orphans, where there is a widow? For just as the widow is the soul of the sinner, so he calls those orphans who have lost having God as their father. \"And they said.\" What did they say? \"The Lord shall not see, nor shall the God of Jacob understand.\" This everyone says who oppresses the saints. As they say this, see what the Holy Spirit answers.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"The widow and the stranger they have slain.\" The plain sense is clear: but if we should wish to interpret according to the anagogy, every day the proud devil with his satellites oppresses and harasses the humble in the Church. \"Saying, The God of Jacob will not see, nor understand.\" They took the Lord's patience for ignorance.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.8-9":["\"Understand, you who are foolish among the people, and be wise at last, you fools. He who planted the ear, shall he not hear?\" He who is a potter, is he ignorant of his own vessel? He who makes another hear, shall he then himself not hear? He who gave me eyes, shall he then himself not see? He who is the author of my understanding, shall he himself not understand? This passage tells especially against those who are Anthropomorphites, who say that God has the members that we too have. For example, God is said to have eyes: \"The eyes of the Lord look upon all things\"; \"the hand of God makes all things.\" \"And Adam heard,\" it says, \"the sound of the Lord's feet walking in paradise.\" These things they hear simply, and refer human weaknesses to the majesty of God. But I say that God is wholly eye, wholly hand, wholly foot. He is wholly eye, because he sees all things; wholly hand, because he works all things; wholly foot, because he is everywhere. So see what he says: \"He who planted the ear, shall he not hear?\" He did not say, \"He who planted the ear, does he then himself not have an ear?\" He did not say, \"Does he then himself not have eyes?\" But what did he say? \"He who planted the ear, shall he not hear? He who formed the eyes, does he not consider?\" He took away the members, he bestowed the functions.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"Understand, you who are foolish among the people; and you fools, at last be wise. Shall he who planted the ear not hear? Shall he who fashioned the eye not consider?\" By an argument from nature he rebukes and convicts the sinners. O you who suppose that mortal things do not pertain to the Lord, you who, along with Epicurus, say that God's care is kept apart from human affairs, listen to the argument from nature: because you are little children, I will offer you little examples. He who gave hearing to man, shall he himself not hear? And at the same time, see what he says. He did not repay like with like: for he did not say, \"Shall he who planted the ear not himself have an ear?\" or \"Shall he who fashioned the eye not himself have an eye?\" But because in God there are no members, therefore he speaks in such a way that we, as often as we read such things about God as are frequently found in the Scriptures, may understand that an operation is signified, not members. In Solomon we read: \"In the hand of the tongue are death and life.\" Does the tongue then have a hand? But in order that we may come more easily to the understanding of the sense above, we have brought forward this testimony: for by the same reasoning by which you would give the tongue a hand, by that same reasoning you are going to give God all members.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.10":["\"He who chastises the nations, shall he not reprove?\" He who teaches others, does he then himself not know? \"He who teaches man knowledge.\" It is understood: does he then himself not have knowledge?\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"He who instructs the nations, shall he not reprove?\" This is what he says: he who gave the law, gave it for this purpose, that he might instruct those who hear, or punish those who despise. \"He who teaches man knowledge.\" He did not add, \"Shall he himself not have knowledge?\" But what did he say?\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.11":["\"The Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain.\" We read in a secular poet: \"O the cares of men, O how much emptiness there is in things!\" Both the Gentiles and the philosophers lift this little verse to heaven. And what do they say? Nothing could be said more prudently. Behold, our rustic Hebrew, so many ages before, spoke this. And what did he say? \"The Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain.\" Do you want to know that the thoughts of men are vain? A father and mother rear their son: they promise themselves happiness from him, they send him to his studies, they educate him, he comes all the way to young manhood, they arrange that he should even serve in the army. When they have thought of everything over thirty years, one little fever comes and carries off all their thoughts. I have an enemy, and the day of judgment is thirty days off; by day and by night I think of nothing else except what I shall answer him. I am on my bed, and my thought is answering my absent enemy. The enemy is not there, and my words are answering the enemy: by day and by night I am wrapped in thought. But when the day of judgment has come, all those things that I had thought perish, and whatever God has said, that I speak. For this reason the Lord too says in the Gospel: \"When you stand before the judges, do not think what you shall answer: for the Lord will give you in that hour what you shall answer.\" \"The Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain.\" One thought alone is happy: to think about the Lord.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"The Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain.\" Men marvel at the philosophers and poets who say: \"O the cares of men! O how much emptiness there is in things!\" Behold, the psalm of David, many ages before, spoke the same sentiment. \"The Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain.\" As long as we are men, we have vain thoughts. \"I said, You are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High.\"\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.12-13":["\"Blessed is the man whom you shall instruct, O Lord.\" Happy is he who has God as his teacher. \"And whom you shall teach out of your law: that you may soften for him the evil days.\" See what he says. \"Blessed is the man whom you shall instruct, O Lord, and whom you shall teach out of your law\" — that is, out of your Scriptures. But of what use is this, that he should be taught out of your law? \"That you may soften for him the evil days.\" You see, therefore, that knowledge and practice and works drawn from the Scriptures soften for us the evil days. \"Until a pit be dug for the sinner.\" When you are instructed out of the law of the Lord, one thing is divided for you into two: for it softens for you the evil days, and it digs a pit for the sinner.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"Blessed is the man whom you shall instruct, O Lord, and shall teach him out of your law.\" You see, then, that the law is to be learned, so that the things which are obscure may be more diligently understood. For the apostle says that \"the law is spiritual.\" And David: \"Reveal my eyes, and I will consider the wondrous things of your law.\" This, then, is what he says. \"Blessed is the man whom you shall instruct, O Lord, and shall teach him out of your law.\" Why do you teach him? Render the reasons. \"That you may soften it for him from the evil days.\" Therefore you teach him, so that in the future you may spare him: therefore in the present you rebuke him, so that in the future you may not condemn him. For the judge's sentence is an evil to the one who endures the evils: not that the sentence is evil, but that the one suffering it, while he feels the punishment, feels a just and good sentence as evil. \"Until the pit be dug for the sinner.\" Who is it that digs the pit for the sinner? Let us see what the prophet says elsewhere. \"He opened a pit and dug it out, and he fell into the trench which he made.\" God, therefore, does not make the pit, but the sinner, so that he may fall into it. \"The blind,\" he says, \"if he offers guidance to the blind, both fall into the pit.\"\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.16-17":["\"Who will rise up for me against the evildoers? Or who will stand with me against those who work iniquity?\" When he says \"Who?\", he signifies either something rare, or none at all. For the prophet speaks thus in the person of a weak man. \"Who will rise up for me against the evildoers?\" Moses came, and could not make me better: the prophets came, and did not free me from the snares of sinners. Who then will rise up for me? Beautifully did he say \"will rise up\": as of one sleeping, as of one lying down. \"Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord?\" \"Master, save us, we perish.\" Who will rise up for me against the multitude of demons? Who will furnish help? \"Who will stand with me against those who work iniquity?\" Beautifully did he say, \"Who will stand with me?\" For unless the Lord stand with us, who lay in his passion, unless he rise up who slept in death, we cannot overcome our adversaries. For this reason Stephen, doing battle against the assaults of the Jews, sees Jesus standing at the right hand of the Father. He saw him standing, because he was fighting on behalf of his martyr. \"Unless the Lord had helped me.\" Unless Christ had risen from hell, my soul would have descended to the netherworld.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.16":["\"Who will rise up for me against the evildoers?\" The psalms are obscure, and they are always changing the speakers; and for this reason they are obscure, and it is of exceeding difficulty to know in each verse who is speaking. \"Who will rise up for me against the evildoers? or who will stand with me against those who work iniquity?\" These verses the Savior speaks. \"I am holy, I was lifted up on the cross, I died, I rose again, and I ascended a victor to the heavens. Who, do you suppose, among the saints, is there who will rise again with me?\" So what he says is this: For this reason I rose again, that others too might rise with me. \"Who will rise up for me?\" When he says \"Who,\" he provokes the rest to rise. For just as we read in Isaiah, \"Whom shall I send to this people, and who will go for us?\" — as Isaiah himself offers himself, so the Lord too provokes us, saying, \"Who will rise up for me against the evildoers?\" Whoever rises again, will rise for me, is of my army. \"Against the evildoers\": against the devil and his army. \"Or who will stand with me?\" See the order: he did not say first \"who will stand,\" and \"who will rise\"; but \"who will rise,\" and \"who will stand.\" For first we rise, then we stand. \"Or who will stand with me against those who work iniquity\" — as a general might stand and say, \"Who is coming in my army?\"\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93"],"PSA.94.17":["\"Unless the Lord had helped me, my soul had almost dwelt in hell.\" For this reason, O Lord, I rise again, because you were among the dead and rose again. My soul would have been held fast among the dead, if your soul had not risen again from the dead.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93"],"PSA.94.18":["\"If I said, My foot has slipped.\" Considering my frailty and human weakness, I did not think my foot stood on firm footing: wherever I set my foot, I feared a fall. Again I thought of your name, and I stood with firm foot.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"If I said, My foot has slipped: your mercy, O Lord, was helping me.\" The confession of weakness quickly earns God's help, and by however much pride gives offense, by that much is he appeased by humility. \"If I said, My foot has slipped\": if I confess my sins, if I say that my foot has stood on slippery ground, if I do not trust in my own strength, if I acknowledge that I am not, though fallen, strong, at once \"your mercy helps me.\"\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.19":["\"According to the multitude of my sorrows in my heart, your consolations have gladdened my soul.\" This very thing the Apostle too speaks of, namely that according to the labors and miseries which we suffer in this world, we have a reward with God in the heavens. The greatness of the tribulations is the greatness of the rewards. As many wounds as we suffer, so many crowns do we also deserve. This is what he says: The more I did penance, so many times did you console me. I shed one tear, I deserved one consolation: I shed ten tears, I deserved ten consolations. However great the weight of penance was, so great also was the number of consolations.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"According to the multitude of my sorrows in my heart, your consolations have gladdened my soul.\" This is what he said above: he pursues the same sense, the same intention. \"According to the multitude of my sorrows in my heart, your consolations have gladdened my soul.\" As great a sorrow as you saw in my heart from the groaning of repentance, so great a measure of your mercy will you minister: for the greatness of the sorrow became the material of consolation.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.20-21":["\"He who fashions toil in the commandment.\" No one is crowned while sleeping, no one at ease possesses the kingdom of heaven, no one with a stuffed belly can worthily discourse about fasting. You see, then, what he says: \"He who fashions toil in the commandment.\" All the commandments of the Lord have toil: without toil we cannot possess the kingdom of heaven. Do you want to know that it is so? \"If you wish,\" he says, \"to be perfect, go, sell all that you possess, and give to the poor, and come, follow me.\" He who wants, he says, to attain the kingdom of heaven, let him pray day and night, keep vigil, fast, lie on a rush mat, not on a feather cushion, not on silk. Penance does not love luxuries. \"For I ate ashes,\" he says, \"like bread, and mingled my drink with weeping.\" \"He who fashions toil in the commandment: they will hunt the soul of the righteous.\" See how it seems not to be consistent. For what is the logical connection: \"He who fashions toil in the commandment: they will hunt the soul of the righteous\"? But what the fleshly ear does not understand, the spiritual ear understands. What then does he say? \"He who fashions toil in the commandment\": therefore, he says, we toil in the commandments of the Lord day and night, we keep vigil, we pray, we fast, because we have many adversaries who hunt the soul of the righteous. See what he says: they hunt the soul of the righteous, not of the unrighteous. For they do not want to deceive those who have been deceived, but the righteous. Whence it is said in Habakkuk: \"His food is choice.\" \"And they shall condemn innocent blood.\" Some are accustomed to say: That man who was killed would not have been killed, unless he were a fornicator, or had had some sin; the collapse would not have overtaken him, unless he had been a sinner; the shipwreck would not have overwhelmed him, unless he had been a sinner. See, then, what holy Scripture says: \"And they shall condemn innocent blood.\" As long as we are in this world, we suffer all things in common. The righteous man and the sinner are shipwrecked alike, they have equal flesh, and equal conditions of the flesh. The righteous man and the sinner perish alike: the righteous man and the sinner have a similar end, but different rewards. One is led to Gehenna, another is led to the kingdom of heaven. \"And they shall condemn innocent blood.\" Your conscience is enough for you, O righteous man: and even if you are condemned, let your blood be innocent, and it will cry out to the Lord.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93","\"Shall the seat of iniquity cling to you?\" You who judge righteousness, can you have iniquity for a neighbor? \"You who fashion sorrow in a commandment?\" The commandments of God have the sorrow of repentance; but that sorrow itself is good, because it furnishes the medicine of sinners. For unless with sweat and toil, we shall not do the commandments of God. Let no one, then, suppose that he will be crowned amid delights. \"The widow who is in delights, while living, is dead.\" What the apostle said of one person, let us apply to all: for the apostle's care was not for widows alone, since he stood forth as the teacher of the whole Church. Now, because he did not name the virgin, nor the monk, nor the churchman, ought a man for that reason not to hold himself back from delights? For if the widow is a human being, I think the rest are human beings too: who, on account of the common frailty, can incur through delights that which the widow dreads. Listen, then, to what he says, \"You who fashion sorrow in a commandment.\" For it is commanded us that we fast, it is commanded us that we be sober, that to the one striking our cheek we offer the other, that to the one taking away our tunic we let go our cloak also. All these commandments are not accomplished without toil. \"For on account of the words of your lips I have kept hard ways.\" You see, then, that no one is crowned amid delights. \"For narrow and strait is the way that leads to life.\" For whatever virtues there are, they are not accomplished without difficulty. With immense toil we reach the summits of a mountain: how much more into heaven? This is what is said in the Gospel: \"The kingdom of heaven suffers violence.\" It suffers violence, because that which is not subject by nature is made subject to us through grace: so that from where the angels fell, men may ascend: so that where bodies did not fall, souls together with their bodies may go. Let someone say: How then will these same bodies of ours go into heaven? If Christ reigns, we reign with Christ: what he already possesses, he promises to us after the resurrection. If the firstfruits reign, the remnant also will reign. The flesh which reigns in Christ, why should it not reign in those who believe in Christ? \"For when Christ shall appear, then we also shall appear with him in glory.\" If the flesh is adored by the angels in heaven, why should it not reign in men, who have merited the dignity of the kingdom? It is said elsewhere: \"And adore his footstool, for it is holy.\" Some suppose it was said of the earth, \"his footstool\": but to me it seems that, in comparison with the divine majesty, the incarnation of the Savior is rightly named a footstool on account of the subjection of its lowliness; but because it is Christ's, it is adored. For nothing is lacking to the divinity: for whatever is οἰκονομία (the dispensation) in Christ is not added to him, but profits us: he is taken up into glory, so that the grace of our future hope may be shown to us, who have reigned with him. To whom is glory and dominion for ever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 71, on Psalm 93 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.94.22":["\"And the Lord has become a refuge for me.\" This passage tells against the Arians. For since they contradict the Lord the Savior, because he is said to be \"made,\" behold, we say, \"The Lord has become a refuge for me.\" No one doubts that this is interpreted of the Father. But if it is said of the Father, \"The Lord has become a refuge for me,\" it must be understood in this sense: the Lord who always was, has become a refuge for me. So too the Savior who always was, has become a Savior for me.\n— Homily 22, on Psalm 93"],"PSA.96.1":["The ninety-fifth psalm is headed with this title: When the house was being built after the captivity, a song of David. The whole mystery is in the title: the whole sacrament of our life and our salvation is in the title. When the house was being built after the captivity, a song of David. The Jews understand this passage thus: When, after the Babylonian captivity, under Ezra and Zerubbabel and Jeshua the son of Josedech, the temple was built again, a song of David. David, they say, knew in the spirit, five hundred years beforehand, that the temple was to be built again. So they say. But if this is so, O Jew, as you interpret it, what does it mean, what follows: Sing to the Lord a new song? And then, Declare his glory among the nations? What is this new song? Who are these nations? You see, then, that according to the letter that kills it cannot stand at all. Therefore it must be interpreted mystically: When the house was being built after the captivity. Would that my house too might be built after captivity! Many of us have been taken captive, many have been led away into Babylon, many have borne the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar on their own necks; many have been cast into his furnace and have burned, many have lost the temple of the Lord, and in them the house of God has been overthrown. The unhappy Judas, while he was an apostle, was the house of God. The devil came, and Judas took the morsel, and at once Satan entered into him, and his house was overthrown, and it was not restored, because he did not do penance. But if anyone does penance after sin, his house is rebuilt after captivity. Let us therefore pray, we also, to the Lord — first, indeed, that our house may not be overthrown; that the Chaldean and the Assyrian may not come and overthrow the temple of Christ within us. But if it has been overthrown, then, as in a shipwreck, by a second consolation, by means of a plank we can be saved. When the house was being built after the captivity. Daily this house of Christ is being built up in those who do penance. Indeed, he did not say, When the house had been built, lest it should seem only to have been, to be a thing of the past; but was being built, to show that this is happening daily. Thus far we have spoken concerning our soul. But we can also put it another way: when the Church of Christ was being built up after its ruin. Let us see, then, what follows after this title.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95","Sing to the Lord a new song. Happy penance! Though you have fallen, yet if you do penance, you are called the new house of Christ. Sing to the Lord a new song. A new house deserves a new song. Sing to the Lord a new song. Who are to sing? Sing to the Lord, all the earth. If it is said of the temple at Jerusalem, O Jew, how is all the earth summoned to praise? Sing to the Lord, all the earth. This passage defeats both the Jew and the Novatian. Sing to the Lord, all the earth: not Jerusalem, but the whole world. Here the Jew is slain. Sing to the Lord, all the earth. The Novatian is slain. How? For the Novatian says: There are certain sins for which we ought to do penance — for instance, for lying, for perjury, for theft; but the man who has committed fornication, the man who has done murder, that man cannot do penance. Hear what it says: Sing to the Lord, all the earth. Now all the earth — the adulterer too, and the murderer — and all sins are earthly. But if all sins are earthly, whatever sin you have, do penance, and in all things you shall be saved.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95","Before we discuss the Gospel, it seems to us that a few things should be said about the title of the psalm, and after our discussion of the title let us come to a few short verses; so that the understanding which we had spoken of concerning the entrance-hall we may recognize likewise in the whole doorway of the house. This is the title: When the house was being built after the captivity, of David himself. Whose house was being built after the captivity? Which of us, do you think, has been taken captive and, in the storm of captivity, torn down, so that after the captivity he might be built up again? If God has shut up all men under sin, and all things are found in unbelief: wherever there is sin, there is captivity; where there is captivity, there is ruin, and there building is necessary. We ought therefore to consider our houses more carefully, lest perhaps they have collapsed in some part, and need to be built after the captivity; and we must come to their aid with all toil and skill, so that they do not utterly collapse, that there may be fulfilled in us what is written in Exodus: 'Because the midwives feared God, they built themselves houses.' See what it says: Because they feared God, they built houses. Therefore a house cannot be constructed except by the fear of God. If then those women built houses by the fear of God, who had not sinned but rather acted in accord with God's will: what ought we, who have been taken captive, to do? Listen, sinner: we ought indeed to fear God, that we may not sin; but the second plank after shipwreck is repentance. When the house was being built after the captivity. When the house was being built: it did not say, has been built, because repentance has no end. For the righteous man, once dead, leaves repentance behind; but wherever there is sin, the conscience always gnaws. Therefore it is said, was being built, because it is not confined by time: however long you live, there you always run into it.\n— Homily 72, on Psalm 95 (Alternate Series)","Sing to the Lord a new song. The old man is put off, and the new is raised up: therefore a new song is sung. Sing to the Lord, all the earth. The earth which has turned with its whole mind to the Lord sings out His praises, in the confession of the Lord, with the sweetness of a new voice.\n— Homily 72, on Psalm 95 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.96.2":["Sing to the Lord, bless his name. The name of the Lord is Savior, from the fact that he was made for us. Sing to the Lord. Proclaim day from day his salvation. What does it mean to say: Proclaim day from day his salvation? The simple man understands it thus: On all days praise the Lord — that is, let day succeed to day — that is, you have praised today, praise tomorrow also. The sense indeed seems simple; but to me it seems that something else of the mystery lies hidden. Proclaim day from day his salvation. The Savior cannot be praised except in the day. For it ought to have said: Praise the Lord day and night, if it were speaking of this ordinary day. For we should have to say this, if we understand it according to the letter. If then we praise the Lord in the day, can we not again praise him in the night? See, therefore, what it says: Whenever you praise the Lord, always praise him in the light — not in the darkness of sins, but in the light of virtues. Let the sun of Christ always rise in your soul, that a new light may always be born in you. Proclaim day from day his salvation. Let us put it another way as well. There are two days, not three, not four, not five: there are two days, the Old Testament and the New. In the one Christ shines, and in the other Christ shines. Do not praise him only in the Old Testament, lest you be Jews; do not praise him only in the New Testament, lest you be Manichaeans. Praise him day from day, that is, in the Old and in the New Testament: which two days make one light. For this reason it is also written in Leviticus: 'Every animal that divides the hoof and chews the cud is clean.' That is, if it has two hooves and chews the cud, it is clean. If therefore it has one hoof, it is not clean. The Jew has one hoof, and therefore is unclean. The Manichaean has one hoof, and therefore is unclean. And because it has one hoof, it does not chew over its food, and what it has once sent down into the belly it brings back up again to the throat, and chews it, and makes it fine, so that what had been coarse may be sent down again fine into the belly — this, indeed, pertains to the speaking of the divine mysteries. Every animal that divides the hoof and chews the cud is clean. The Jew has one hoof: for he believes in only one testament, and does not chew the cud. For he reads only the letter, and considers nothing, seeks nothing within. But the churchman divides the hoof and chews the cud, that is, he believes in both testaments, and in both testaments he often searches; and whatever lies hidden in the letter, he brings forth in the spirit. Why have I said all this? Because it is written: Proclaim day from day his salvation. For this reason the apostles too are sent two by two; for this reason we have not one eye but two; for this reason we have two ears, for this reason two nostrils, for this reason two lips, for this reason two hands, two feet. We have nothing single in us except what is shameful: and the members of our body bear witness to the mysteries of the two testaments.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95"],"PSA.96.7":["Bring to the Lord, you families of the nations, bring to the Lord glory and honor. If it is said of the temple at Jerusalem, what does it now mean: Bring to the Lord, you families of the nations, bring to the Lord glory and honor? The multitude of the nations, the calling of the nations, is the gathering of the Church. Bring to the Lord glory and honor. When we honor the Lord in our body, the Lord is glorified in us. Bring to the Lord glory to his name. The glory of the servant is the glory of the master. 'He who receives you receives me.' Happy the servant, on whose account the master is glorified.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95"],"PSA.96.8-9":["Take up victims, and enter into his courts. 'A holy victim, living, pleasing to God.' Take up victims. Bring victims yourselves. What victims? Be yourselves the victims. Virginity is a whole-burnt-offering to Christ: all chastity, whether in virginity or in widowhood or in continence, is a victim to Christ. I speak a new thing: the victim of chastity carries itself. Take up victims, and enter into his courts: adore the Lord in his holy court. I see many courts, I see one court. Take up victims, and enter into his courts: behold, many courts. Again, Adore the Lord in his holy court: behold, one court. See, then, that one cannot come from one court to many courts; but from many courts one comes to one court. Do you wish to know this very mystery in another passage as well? That merchant, who had many pearls, sold the many that he might buy the one. And in Micah it is said: 'Stand,' he says, 'in the ways of the Lord, and inquire,' he says, 'after the way of the Lord.' Unless we have stood in the many ways, we cannot find the one way. What are these many pearls, what are the many ways, what are these many courts — that we may find the one pearl and the one way and the one court? Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, Moses, Joshua the son of Nun, Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, the twelve prophets, David, Solomon — these were courts. These are our courts: first we enter into them; and from those courts we afterward come to the court of the Gospel, where Christ is found.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95"],"PSA.96.10":["Let all the earth be moved before his face. See what it says. Heaven is not moved before the face of God; but whoever is earthly, he looks upon the Lord, and is moved, and trembles. Say among the nations that the Lord has reigned. Unless the earth has been moved and has withdrawn from its earthly works, the Lord will not reign among the nations.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95","For he has set right the round world, which shall not be moved. Nothing is ever set right except what was formerly straight and afterward became crooked. So therefore both we, and the whole human race, understand God by nature. For there are no nations that do not by nature understand their Creator. For though they venerate stones and wood, yet they understand that there is something greater than they themselves are, and in their very error they show that they have wisdom — that is, there is no nation that does not by nature understand God. Indeed the heathen worship idols, that is, they venerate stones and wood; and if perchance they have started a quarrel, and if some oath has come up between them, they do not say, These stones see, this wood sees: but, God sees, and God hears. See, then, what it says: For he has set right the round world, which shall not be moved. For this cause Christ came, and set right the human race, which before had been made crooked, that it might not be moved forever. For his cross is the pillar of the human race: upon that pillar his house has been built. I say the cross — not the wood, but the passion. Moreover, this cross is both in Britain, and in India, and in the whole round world. Indeed, what does he say also in the Gospel? 'Unless you take up my cross, and daily follow me.' See what it says: Unless your soul has been so prepared for the cross, as mine was for you, you cannot be my disciples. Happy is he who carries in his breast the cross, and the resurrection, and the place of Christ's nativity, and the place of the ascension. Happy is he who has Bethlehem in his heart, in whose heart Christ is daily born. And indeed, what does Bethlehem mean? House of bread. Let us too be a house of bread, of him who came down from heaven. Daily Christ is crucified for us: we are crucified to the world, and Christ is crucified in us. Happy is he in whose heart Christ daily rises again: if daily he does penance for his sins, even the light ones. Happy is he who daily ascends from the Mount of Olives to the kingdoms of heaven, where the olives of the Lord are fruitful, where the light of Christ is born, where are the olive groves of the Lord. 'For I am like a fruitful olive tree in the house of the Lord.' Let us too, then, kindle our lamp from that olive, and at once with Christ we shall go to the kingdoms of heaven. For he has set right the world, which shall not be moved. The inhabited world (oikoumene), in which he himself dwells; for it is better said in Greek, oecumene, than in Latin. Let us pray that our oecumene too may be set right, in which the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit may dwell. For unless our oecumene has been inhabited, it cannot be set right. There are many things that might be said. But since today is the day of the dedication feast (encaenia), and the dedication feasts are always in stormy weather, in rains, in winter. Indeed it is written in the Gospel: 'And the Lord was walking,' it says, 'at the dedication feast (encaenia) in Jerusalem, in the porch of Solomon, because it was winter and rains.' See what it says. At the dedication feast he was walking in the porch, because there was storm and rains. When someone wishes to renew himself to Christ, when the house is being built after captivity, if anyone should wish to renew his house, and to renew it to Christ; if anyone should wish to forsake the world, and to join himself to Christ: at once the storm, at once the winter, at once the rain rises up. But Christ — nay, in Christ the poor little ones, seeing the dedication feast, seeing the storm, seeing the floods, seeing the winter, leave the floods and flee under the porch of Solomon, that is, they are sheltered by the peace of Christ. For Solomon is interpreted \"peaceful,\" he who said, 'My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you.' Let us too, then, walk in the porch of Solomon, let us be sheltered by his majesty, let us have his help, and let us say that word from the Song of Songs, that 'the winter is past, and the rain is gone, it has gone away' in Christ Jesus.\n— Homily 23, on Psalm 95"],"PSA.97.1":["A Psalm of David, when his land was established. We read in the books of Kingdoms and of Chronicles that David was a warlike man, that he subjected all the surrounding nations, and reduced them under his own power. This for the moment according to the letter, so that we understand it in this way: A Psalm of David which he sang to the Lord, when, his adversaries having been conquered, he restored peace to his land, that is, to Judaea. This according to the history. But if David is interpreted as \"strong in hand,\" and no one is strong in hand except him who conquered all nations, our David, to whom the demons cried out, \"Why have you come to torment us before the time, Son of David?\" — then this psalm was composed by him, when his land was established. And the holy presbyter said excellently, that this land which is established is our bodies. For truly at that time ought we to sing to God, when our land has been set in order. But let us also speak of it in another way; for it does no harm, if we understand the same thing in two or three ways. For it is written in Solomon: \"You shall take these things,\" he says, \"upon the tablet of your heart threefold.\" It does no harm to say something different, when it is said with one spirit. When his land was established. This land, before our David conquered all, did not have peace, but everywhere there was discord. One nation worshipped Jupiter, another Mercury, another Juno, and all the nations had different idols. The standard of the cross was raised up, and all the land was established.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","The title of the psalm is prescribed thus: \"When his land was restored.\" When it says \"was restored,\" it is shown that it existed before, and afterward did not exist, and again was established. What then is this land of David which was restored? David's land is holy Mary, the mother of the Savior. \"Who was made to him of the seed of David according to the flesh.\" What was promised to David was fulfilled in Mary's virginity and childbearing, where a virgin is begotten of a virgin. And thus was accomplished what is said in the Gospel, \"The Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us\": so that what had been promised to David might be restored to us through holy Mary.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)","\"The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice.\" Let the earth rejoice: let it have one king, which before was held by many. \"Let the many islands be glad.\" It is said in a certain prophet: \"Speak to the inhabitants of this island,\" that is, to the inhabitants of Jerusalem. You see, then, that Jerusalem is called an island. Before David's land was restored, therefore, only the one island of Judea rejoiced; but now, David's land having been restored, it is said, \"Let the many islands be glad.\" By the many islands we can mean the various provinces or cities of the believing peoples, or we shall interpret them as the various churches. For just as islands are beaten this way and that by the waves of the sea, so also the churches are indeed beaten by the various storms of temptations, but are not overturned.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)","The Lord has reigned, let the earth exult. Let the whole earth, and all the world, which had before been subjected to demons and idols, rejoice now that the Lord reigns. The Lord has reigned. The Lord the Creator who made you, he himself has reigned. O you who before were subjected under the lord the devil, now you are subjected under the Lord the Creator. Let the many islands be glad — the holy presbyter spoke well of our souls, which are battered this way and that by various thoughts as if by various waves. But let us also call the churches islands. And indeed it is said in another place too: \"Many islands shall be converted to me.\" Do you wish to know that the churches are called islands? It is said in the prophet in the person of the Lord: \"Speak,\" he says, \"to the inhabitants of this island.\" Let the many islands be glad. For just as islands are set in the midst of the sea, so also the churches are set as it were in the midst of the sea of this world, and thus they are battered by persecutions as if by diverse waves. But these islands are battered daily, yet they are not overthrown. They are indeed in the sea, but they have Christ for their foundation, who cannot be moved.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96"],"PSA.97.2":["Cloud and darkness are round about him. Two things are round about the Lord, cloud and darkness. I think this is the cloud of which it is said in the Gospel: \"And a bright cloud overshadowed them\" — at that time when the Lord was transfigured, and the apostles fell down, and a bright cloud overshadowed them. I think these are the clouds to which the Lord commanded that they should not rain rain upon Israel. I think these are the clouds of which it is said in another place: \"Your truth reaches even to the clouds\" — that truth of the Lord which says in the Gospel: \"I am the way and the truth and the life.\" The truth of God is Christ. The truth of the Lord reaches even to the clouds. The clouds are the apostles and the prophets; to these it was commanded that they should not rain rain upon Israel. For from the time — according to the history, as it is written in the book of Judges — that the fleece was dried up, the rain has run out over the whole world. What is said is this: Israel was dried up, and the rain ran out over the whole world. Cloud and darkness are round about him. \"The Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud.\" See what he says. The Lord comes, the Lord and Savior, into this Egypt, in which we are: into the place of darkness, where Pharaoh is, the Lord comes. He comes only upon a light cloud. What is this light cloud? It seems to me to be holy Mary, weighed down by no human seed. This light cloud came into the world, and carries with it the Creator of the world. And what is said there in Isaiah? \"The Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud: and the idols of Egypt shall be shattered.\" The Lord comes, and the idols of Egypt were shaken and struck and destroyed. This cloud destroyed the Serapeum in Alexandria; not the emperor, a mortal man, but this cloud which came into Egypt. Cloud and darkness are round about him. We have spoken of the cloud: let us speak of the darkness. The Lord is in darkness. The Lord is either in light, or in darkness. He is in light for beginners: for to beginners he speaks more simply; but those who are perfect, to them he speaks mystically. And indeed to the apostles he did not speak as to the crowds, but he spoke inwardly. And what does he say? \"He that has ears to hear, let him hear.\" So this is what he means, and darkness is round about him: that is, and mysteries are round about him. Therefore it is said also in Exodus: \"And all the people,\" he says, \"stood below; but Moses alone entered into Mount Sinai, into the darkness.\" For not all the people of God could know the mysteries which Moses alone could know. It is said of God: \"He made darkness his hiding-place.\"\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","Justice and judgment are the setting-right of his throne. What does he mean, the setting-right of his throne? He ought to have said, the stability of his throne. When therefore it is said, the setting-right of his throne, it is shown that whatever is set right was crooked before it was set right. The judgment of God, therefore, before it comes to judge, seems as it were crooked: now the judgment seems to be unjust and crooked. For when wicked men are made rich and the holy are poor, does not the judgment of God seem to be crooked? But when he shall come to judge, so as to render to each one according to his works, at that time his throne shall be as it were set right. And indeed, do you wish to know that it is spoken of the judge?\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","\"Cloud and darkness are round about him.\" No one doubts that this is of the Lord Savior, round about whom was cloud and darkness — the flesh which he deigned to assume; although he is said to have appeared, in the Gospel of John. \"And he appeared to us.\" He appeared indeed to the apostles, but from the Jews he was concealed. To those he is shown on the mountain, from these he is concealed in the valley of humility: those he builds up with beatitudes, to these he speaks in parables, so that seeing they might not see, because of their unbelief. \"Cloud and darkness are round about him.\" He appeared as he willed, not as his nature demanded. \"And he made darkness his hiding place.\" If God is light, how can light dwell in darkness? There darkness signifies our knowledge and weakness: because we cannot gaze upon his majesty. For if human eyes cannot direct themselves at the rays of this sun which was created, which is our fellow-servant, how much more, in the Sun of Justice, are there darkness and gloom round about him, so that he cannot be understood or seen by us? This is what we read of holy Moses: \"And he entered into the darkness of the cloud,\" that he might see God, whom he could not see without a cloud. This he says also in Isaiah: \"The Lord shall come into Egypt on a light cloud,\" that is, in holy Mary. On a light cloud, because he was not weighed down by human seed.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)","\"Justice and judgment are the correction of his throne.\" When it says \"correction,\" it is shown that before it was crooked. \"Justice and judgment are the correction of his throne.\" Before the Lord came in the flesh, there was inequality, because God was known only in Judea. He came, and all the earth acknowledged the one and the same Creator whom Judea had known: and the correction of his throne was made.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.3":["A fire shall go before him. Let him who is holy not fear this fire; let him who is a sinner fear it. This fire purges the holy, consumes sinners. A fire shall go before him. \"Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a burning fire.\" I think these are the fires, that is, his angels. These angels, therefore, that is his fire, shall go before him. These angels — whom do they consume? Whoever is wood, hay, stubble. But he who is gold, silver, and precious stones shall indeed be cast into the fire, but shall be found the cleaner.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","\"Fire shall go before him.\" The Lord is pure, \"he dwells holy among the holy\": we cannot be joined to him, unless fire first has burned up our vices. \"For of what sort each one's work is, fire shall prove.\" \"And it shall inflame his enemies round about.\" God does not inflame his enemies as enemies, but as friends: for he burns away the vices of his enemies, that out of enemies he may make friends.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.4":["His lightnings have shone upon the world. The philosophers say that lightnings are generated from the collision of clouds. For unless the sound is first heard, the lightnings are not seen. This is said also of the flint-stone: that unless they are struck together, the fire in the middle does not come forth. This we have said according to the natural history, and we are still speaking to little ones. We have therefore stated the nature of things, so that from the nature of things we may bring forth something spiritual. The stone which is commonly called flint, if it is alone, cannot send forth fire: but if it is struck together with another stone, the fire in the middle is struck out. Suppose for me that Jeremiah, Isaiah, Elijah are a cloud: and that Paul too, and Peter, and the rest of the apostles are a cloud. These clouds, therefore, when they have been struck together among themselves, send forth the fire in the middle, and it gives light to the world. His lightnings have shone upon the world; the earth saw and was shaken. The lightnings of the apostles shone in the whole world, and the earth was shaken. They spoke, and the whole world heard the thunder of their voice. They had two things together: both a mighty voice, and the splendor of lightnings. These lightnings enlightened those who believed, and burned up those who did not believe.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","\"His lightnings gave light to the world.\" This is what is said in Jeremiah: \"The Lord who made the world in his wisdom, who brought clouds from the end of the earth, made lightnings into rain.\" \"His lightnings gave light to the world.\" The philosophers who are accustomed to dispute about the natures of things say that, unless clouds coming against each other are dashed together, fire cannot slip out of them; but when they have been driven, by a certain contest among themselves, into thunders, then lightning slips out of them. Which likeness we can also understand from fired stones. We have said this so that we may more easily see something similar to the mystery of the Savior. We have our own clouds, the prophets and the apostles; whence also elsewhere it is said, \"Your truth reaches even to the clouds.\" If his truth reaches even to the clouds of this air, how is it said elsewhere, \"Truth has arisen from the earth\"? Therefore what it says is this: \"Your truth reaches even to the clouds.\" It certainly signifies the prophets and apostles: which clouds, unless they have been dashed together among themselves, cannot give their splendor; but if in one course they come to thunder together, lightnings fall in the whole world. Do you wish to know how the saints are called clouds in the Scriptures? It is said in Isaiah: \"I will command the clouds not to rain rain upon them.\" Moses was a cloud, and therefore he spoke: \"Let my speech be awaited like rain.\" The epistles of the apostles are our spiritual rains. For what does Paul say in the epistle to the Hebrews? \"For the earth, drinking in the rain that often comes upon it,\" and the rest. And again: \"I planted, Apollos watered.\" \"It saw, and the earth was moved.\" In the whole world the earth felt the lightning words of the Lord, and was moved — the earth which, before it was moved, was hard, and was not able to receive the word of the Lord. The Lord looked upon it, \"who looks upon the earth and makes it tremble.\" What is this, \"makes it tremble\"? \"Upon whom else shall I rest, except upon the humble and quiet one, who trembles at my words?\"\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.5":["The mountains flowed away like wax. To me these mountains seem to be the powers of the demons. Or certainly the mountains are proud men, whoever they be. This fire does not consume the humble, but whoever are mountainous. And indeed, whenever a lightning-bolt is sent forth, it strikes with difficulty those who are in the valley, but always those who are on the mountain.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","\"The mountains flowed like wax before his face.\" By \"mountains\" he means the lofty powers. Those who before were raised up to heaven through pride, at the coming of the Lord were humbled; and those who were hardened by too great a boasting of vanity, afterward were softened by the heat of divine wisdom.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.6":["The heavens have announced his justice. These are the heavens of which it is said also in the eighteenth psalm, \"The heavens declare the glory of God.\" The heavens announce his justice: the earth does not announce it. Whoever is heaven does not fear the just God: whoever is of the heavens does not fear to announce his justice: whoever is holy and is heaven, he does not fear the just God. But the sinner seeks a merciful God.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","\"The heavens announced his justice, and all peoples saw his glory.\" In these two little verses two things are signified: the heavens announce the justice of the Lord, and all peoples see his glory. For unless someone has become heaven, he cannot announce the justice of the Lord. For if to the sinner it is said, \"You are earth, and into earth you shall go,\" why should it not be said to the just one, \"You are heaven, and into heaven you shall go\"? For we do not bear the image of the earthly, but the image of the heavenly: since \"our citizenship is in the heavens.\" If anyone, therefore, walks upon the earth, although in the flesh yet not according to the flesh, that one can freely announce the justice of the Lord. For if someone less knowing should suffer a scruple, and say, \"What is it that we see the just in this life pressed down by labors and want, and sinners flourishing in riches and every comfort?\" — unless the churchman has been instructed in the divine Scriptures, how can he show the justice of God in cases of this kind? But if he labors in the meditation of the law day and night, he will easily answer, that the brief lowliness of the present time is to be compensated afterward by eternal rewards. For it is much better for someone, for the sake of the Lord, in the small span of his life, to endure voluntary poverty, that he may in the future enjoy eternal riches, than here to abound in luxuries, and afterward be tortured with intolerable punishments. \"And all peoples saw his glory.\" Those who before were scandalized at the cross of the Lord, because by themselves they could not recognize him, afterward, when the heavens announced the justice of God, glorify God.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.7":["\"Let all be confounded who worship graven images.\" If the prophet is blessed, being blessed he never knew how to curse; and therefore in that which he says, \"Let them be confounded,\" it is not against them, but for them: for he prays that they may be confounded in their error, and be converted to the true and only God, the Creator.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.8":["\"The daughters of Judea rejoiced because of your judgments, O Lord.\" What does this sense intend, \"The daughters of Judea rejoiced,\" and not \"the sons\"? Let us understand first what \"Judea\" is interpreted to mean: and when we have known the interpretation, let us see what are the daughters of the interpretation. \"Judea,\" then, is interpreted \"confession.\" Whatever soul confesses God is a daughter of Judea. Therefore we call all the souls of believers the daughters of Judea, which rejoice at the judgments of God. For unless the soul has been faithful, it does not rejoice at the judgments of God. \"My house has collapsed, I have lost my riches in shipwreck, my son has died to me, my servant has fled and carried off my goods, I who before was rich am now consumed by want\": unless I am a daughter of Judea, I do not rejoice in the judgments of God. The soul of blessed Job was a daughter of Judah, which in the losses of household things thus consoled itself, saying: \"The Lord gave, the Lord has taken away, blessed be the name of the Lord: naked I came forth from my mother's womb, naked also shall I go\" — let there be understood, \"into the earth.\" First, made poor from rich, he lost his substance together with his sons: pressed down alike by want and bereavement, at last he came to the utmost misery, struck with the royal disease and an incurable wound: incurable to men, but curable with God. What human medicine could not heal, patience and faith healed. He sat upon the dunghill, and in his soul he walked about in paradise. With his whole body rotting, his tongue alone was preserved to him — the tongue that was to blaspheme the Lord. See the craft of the ancient enemy. He tore away the substance of the just man by hostile plunder, crushed his sons with ruin, struck the man himself with a grievous wound and the horror of his whole body: he left him nothing except his tongue and his wife, so that the one might tempt and the other blaspheme. The devil was mindful of his former craft, by which he once deceived Adam through the woman: so also he assails this man through his wife, reckoning that he can always deceive men through the woman, not considering that one man was cast down through a woman, and now through a woman the whole world has been saved. Eve comes into your mind, but consider Mary: she cast us out of paradise, this one leads us back to heaven. Let us return to the order of the psalm.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.97.10":["\"You who love the Lord, hate evil.\" If the Lord is good... For what do we read? \"The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep.\" And again in another parable: \"Friend, if I am good, why is your eye evil?\" \"You who love the Lord, hate evil.\" Two loves cannot be contrary to each other in one man. Just as there is no consent of Christ with Belial, of justice and iniquity, so it cannot happen that one soul love both good and evil. \"You who love the Lord, hate evil\": the devil. The love and the hatred of each consists in deed. \"For he who loves me will keep my commandments.\" But concerning the part of the devil, what is said? \"But through the envy of the devil death entered into the world; therefore they imitate him who are of his part.\" Or certainly it is to be understood simply: You who love the things that are good, hate the things that are evil. You cannot love good, unless you hate evil.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)","\"The Lord guards the souls of his saints.\" A most beautiful order: he who loves good and hates evil, what does he deserve from the Lord, except that he be guarded by him? \"He will free them from the hand of the sinner.\" Here a question arises: If the Lord guards the souls of his saints, and frees them from the hand of the sinner, how are the martyrs crushed in persecution? how did the impious Nero, with sentence given, condemn Peter and Paul to death on one day, if the Lord guards the souls of his saints? Attend diligently to what he says. \"The Lord guards the souls of his saints\": he said \"souls,\" not \"bodies.\" \"Do not fear those,\" he says, \"who kill the body, but cannot kill the soul; but fear him who can kill both body and soul in Gehenna.\" How, then, does the Lord guard the souls of his saints? In martyrdom. \"Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.\" In martyrdom, therefore, blood is poured out, so that the soul may be freed from temptations: so that it may leave the brief life, and migrate to the eternal: so that it may leave persecution, and go to the One who crowns us, our Lord Jesus Christ. To whom is honor and glory unto ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"PSA.98.1":["Sing to the Lord a new song. The new song is the Son of God crucified, which had never before been heard. Let a new thing have a new song. Sing to the Lord a new song. He indeed is a man who suffered — but you, sing to the Lord. He suffered indeed as a man, but he saved as God. Sing to the Lord a new song. A new song deserves a new name. Therefore it is said elsewhere too: 'And a new name,' he says, 'shall be given to you.' A new name deserves a new song. And in the Apocalypse it is said: 'To him that overcometh,' he says, 'I will give a pebble, and I will write upon it a new name.' The new name is that of the Christians. Sing to the Lord. Why? For what has he done? Why does he deserve a new song? Because he has done marvelous things. He worked signs among the Jews: he healed the paralyzed, he cleansed the lepers, he raised the dead. This other prophets also did. He turned a few loaves into many and fed a countless multitude. This Elisha also did. What then did he do that is new, so that he deserves a new song? Do you want to know what new thing he did? God died as though a man, that men might live; the Son of God was crucified, that he might lift us up to heaven. Because he has done marvelous things. Do you want to know that he has done marvelous things? The widow's son lay in the upper room: Elisha came and drew himself together, and placed mouth to mouth, and hands to hands, and feet to feet. For if he had stretched himself out, the widow's son would not have come back to life: therefore he drew himself together, that he might give life. Since he was in the form of God, therefore he took the form of a man, therefore he drew himself together, that he might make us higher.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97","His own right hand has saved for him. What he says is this: He saved men — not another's work, but his own; that is, what he had made for himself, he himself saved for himself. He had made man for salvation, who by his own fault had perished: this One died, that he might save man for himself by his own right hand. Here 'right hand' is put for power, and 'arm' for strength. And his holy arm. Although both 'arm' and 'right hand' may be said to be Christ — 'The right hand of the Lord has done valiantly.' 'And the arm of the Lord, to whom has it been revealed?' — nevertheless, since here it is said of Christ, the right hand and the arm of the Lord is his power.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.2":["The Lord has made known his salvation. He did not say 'he showed,' but 'he made known.' What he says is this: what before they had known of God, and by their own fault had forgotten. Therefore God, coming by his grace, made manifest to them this thing which they had lost. So this is what he says: Him whom Adam had known, whom Seth had known, whom Enoch had known, who called upon the Lord and hoped in him, and whom Noah had known, and whom afterward the human race had forgotten — he came again into the world, that men who afterward had forgotten might again come to know him. The Lord has made known his salvation. In the Hebrew it reads: The Lord has made known his Jesus. For wherever 'salvation' (salutare) is said, in the Hebrew 'Jesus' is put. In the sight of the nations he has revealed his righteousness. By no means in Judea alone, but in the sight of the nations. For it was just that the Savior should save his creature. He has revealed his righteousness — which had been obscured by the unbelief of men.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.3":["He has remembered his mercy. 'He has shut up all things under sin, that he might have mercy on all.' And his truth to the house of Israel. He remembers mercy: he fulfills truth. He has remembered his mercy: that is, toward the people of the nations. And his truth to the house of Israel: fulfilling by this work what he had promised to the patriarchs.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97","All the ends of the earth have seen the salvation of our God. Not Israel and Judah alone saw it, but all the ends; and at the same time, according to the mystical senses, the ends of the earth. As long as we are in the midst of the earth, we cannot see God. When therefore we as it were leave the earth and are on the summit, then we deserve to see God. Do you want to know how the ends of the earth see God? We read in Ezekiel: 'And those wheels,' he says, 'which are turned, were called gelgel,' which means 'revelation, revelation.' For GEL means 'revelation,' GELGEL 'revelation, revelation.' Those wheels then hear 'revelation, revelation': because the wheel touches the earth as it were only at some small point, and the whole of it hastens toward heaven. Therefore it is said also in another place: 'Holy stones are rolled upon the earth.' And what is said in Ezekiel? 'Wherever,' he says, 'the spirit went, thither the wheels also followed.' Let us too follow the Holy Spirit, and let us be called wheels. And what is said there? 'And they went not,' he says, 'backward, but always went forward.' And at the same time consider what he says. 'They did not turn back,' he says, 'but always went forward.' For they forgot the things that were past, and reached out toward the things before them. 'And they were full,' he says, 'of eyes.' For all of those wheels were full of the light of God. It is not the time now to discuss the wheels and the cherubim and the living creatures in Ezekiel.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.4":["Rejoice in God, all the earth. Not Judea alone, but all the earth — rejoice in the Lord. Send forth the standards of the victorious army. Sing and exult and make melody. Sing — in thought; but make melody — that is, with all your members. Let the hand make melody in almsgiving, let the foot make melody by going about in good work.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.5":["Make melody to the Lord on the harp. Let all your strings have voice. There cannot be a harp if one string is missing. What does it profit you if you are chaste, and yet greedy? What does it profit you if you are both chaste and generous in almsgiving, and yet envious? What does it profit you if you have six strings whole and one broken? If one string is lacking, the perfect sound of the harp cannot exist.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.6":["On beaten trumpets and with the sound of the horned trumpet. We read in the book of Numbers that there are two kinds of trumpets: one kind of trumpet beaten out of silver, and another kind of trumpets made of horn. So here both are named: On beaten trumpets and with the sound of the horned trumpet. What is said is this. The trumpet beaten out of silver is the word of God. 'The words of the Lord are chaste words: silver tried by fire, purified sevenfold.' Again, in 'the sound of the horned trumpet,' here the man of God is brought in reigning. In the scriptures 'horn' is properly said for kingship and for power, as it is written there: 'He has raised up a horn of salvation for us.' And in another place it is written: 'In you we will toss our enemies on the horn.' So see here what he says. Have two trumpets, both the silver and the horned: the silver, that you may have the word; the horned, that you may have power. Do you want to know that 'horn' is always put in a good sense? We read in Leviticus: 'Let no animal be offered except whatever is horned.' Three kinds of animals are offered: the ox, which has horns, and the ram, which has horns, and the he-goat, which has horns. Do you suppose it is said without reason, that no animal be offered except what is horned? So we too, as long as we have a horn, deserve to be a victim of God. But if our horn has been struck off, we are as it were feeble, and we cannot come among the priests of God.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.7":["Let the sea be moved, and the fullness thereof. Let the sea be moved, and let the bitter waters be changed into sweet. In fact, Marah too was changed and made into a sweet taste. Nothing else made that bitter water sweet except the wood of the cross, which had been cast into it. It seems to me that the bitter sea is the law of Moses. This is what is called Marah, that is, 'bitter.' This bitter water of the old law received the wood of the cross, and was made sweet.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.98.8":["The rivers shall clap their hands together. Let us ask the Jews, who understand according to the letter. Do rivers have hands? Do rivers have voices? Do rivers have feet? Do rivers have a belly? Someone may say: It is not written about a belly, about feet. But I say to you: from one member we understand the rest as well; for we cannot understand hands without feet, without a belly, without the other members. The rivers shall clap their hands together. The rivers, which have drunk from the fountain of Jesus. 'They have forsaken me,' he says, 'the fountain of living water.' The rivers themselves run from the fountain of Christ. He is the fountain, we are the rivers — if indeed we deserve to be rivers. Christ is the fountain, and the saints are the rivers; but those who are lower are little streams. Others, however, are torrents. Who are the torrents? Those who have waters for an hour, and when temptation comes are dried up. The rivers shall clap their hands together. It is not one river, there are many rivers: as many as the saints are, so many also are the rivers. But these rivers have no dissension among themselves: since they are the rivers of Christ, therefore they have concord among themselves. The rivers shall clap their hands together. They clap with the hand: the works of the saints are the praise of God. Christ is praised not by voice, but by work; he does not hear the voice, but the works. The rivers shall clap their hands together. Therefore he himself says in the Gospel: 'He who thirsts, let him come and drink; and rivers,' he says, 'shall flow from his belly.' The mountains shall exult in the sight of the Lord. What likeness is there between rivers and mountains? But since he speaks of the perfect saints, he did not say, 'the little streams shall clap their hands together,' but, 'The rivers shall clap their hands together.' See the order. Let the sea be moved, and the whole world: the rivers clap their hands together, his saints: the mountains no less, who have attained to the highest righteousness. Let us say it also in another way. Let the sea be moved, which before had been unmoved — let it be moved at the coming of Christ. Let the sea be moved: we have already said, this is the law of Moses. The rivers clap their hands together: this is the prophets. The mountains shall exult before the sight of the Lord: this is the apostles.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97","They also clap with their hands to the voice and sound of the instruments, so that all their works look for pleasure, and they do nothing except what they provide for the belly and for lust. Nor let the prudent reader at once object to us this, and how it is written: Let the rivers clap with their hands, and: All nations, clap your hands, and: Shout for joy to God our helper. For there they are said to clap not to the sound of instruments, but to have one accord in the praise of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.98.9":["Because he comes to judge the earth. By no means did he come only lowly in the flesh, but he comes glorious in his majesty. Because he comes to judge the earth. He did not say 'to destroy,' but 'to judge.' He comes to judge, that he may separate the tares from the wheat: he comes, that he may separate the bad little fish from the good.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97","He shall judge the world in righteousness, and the peoples in equity. Righteousness is set down, and equity. If righteousness is set down, and afterward equity, it is properly called εὐθύτης (euthytēs), 'straightness,' which makes the crooked straight. So he comes to judge in his righteousness, that what is crooked may in him be made straight. To whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 25, on Psalm 97"],"PSA.99.1":["The Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry. Three psalms have the same beginning: the ninety-second, the ninety-sixth, and the ninety-eighth. But although they have the same beginning in the opening verse, at the end they are different. For how does it read in the ninety-second? 'The Lord has reigned, he has clothed himself in beauty.' But in the ninety-sixth: 'The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice'; and in the ninety-eighth: 'The Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry.' The sequence seems as it were to be contrary to itself. For it ought first to have said 'The Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry,' and afterwards to say 'The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice,' and last of all 'The Lord has reigned, he has clothed himself in beauty.' But the different order gives us some hint toward understanding. 'The Lord has reigned, he has clothed himself in beauty.' The Lord has reigned, and he has clothed himself with the patriarchs and the prophets and the believing people. He has clothed himself in beauty: the patriarchs and the prophets were as it were the garment of Christ. That is the loincloth which is written of in Jeremiah: that is the loincloth which Jeremiah had about his loins. Do you wish to know that the saints are, as it were, a loincloth, the garment of God? Afterwards God himself speaks to Jeremiah: 'Just as you,' he says, 'placed that loincloth about your loins, so I too have joined my people to myself.' As, then, a garment is about a man, so the people is about God. But because this loincloth, because this beauty with which the Lord had been clothed, was exposed across the Euphrates in a cleft of the rock, and there rotted away, and was led off into captivity by the Assyrians—what does the Lord do? He is not naked, he cannot be without a loincloth, he cannot be without a garment: the former people having been lost, he makes himself a garment out of the people of the Gentiles. And what is said? The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice. Let the earth rejoice, the whole earth, that is, the entire people of believers. Then, do you wish to know that the second loincloth is spoken of concerning the people of the Gentiles? The Lord, he says, has reigned, let the earth rejoice: let the many islands be glad. Not one island, Judaea, but many islands, that is, the whole world. Let someone say to me: Give me an example from the Scriptures where Judaea alone is called one island. Everything we say we ought to affirm from the holy Scriptures. 'For in the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word stand.' The word of the one speaking does not have as much authority as the Lord's command. We read in the prophet Ezekiel: 'And you,' he says, 'son of man, speak to the inhabitants of this island'—that is, of Judaea. Since, therefore, one island has been forsaken, now it is said: Let the many islands be glad. Therefore concerning the loincloth it is said of the former people: The Lord has reigned, he has clothed himself in beauty. Of the second people, gathered from the Gentiles: The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice, let the many islands be glad. Now in the third it is said: The Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry. Whether they be of the Jews, or of the Gentiles, whoever do not believe, let them be angry. 'Why have the nations raged, and the peoples devised vain things?'\n\nThe Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry. The Lord suffered, the Lord was crucified, the Lord died, the Lord rose again, the Lord ascended victorious to heaven. The Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry. Who are these peoples? Let us give an example from the Gospel. 'A certain man,' he says, 'a householder, went to receive for himself a kingdom in a far country.' What is farther than heaven from earth? He left the earth, he went to the heavens, to receive for himself a kingdom. 'And his citizens,' he says, 'who hated him, sent an embassy, saying: We will not have this man to reign over us.' But he received the kingdom, and came to those wicked citizens. He came to those who did not want to have him as king. And now the Holy Spirit says to the wicked citizens: Whether you will or will not, the Lord has reigned, let the peoples be angry. O the mercy of the Holy Spirit: he did not say, let them perish, but, let them be angry. He wished to signify the fault, not the punishments. They are angry, and the Lord entreats. And what does he say? 'Come to me, all you who are burdened.' I have received the kingdom from the Father—rather, the man whom I took up. I give as God, I receive as man.\n\nWho sits upon the cherubim, let the earth be moved. As long as the earth is unmoved, it cannot be healed. He is not speaking of the earthquake by which we see lands shaken, and the whole race of mortals afraid; but he speaks of our earth, which, as long as it is unmoved, cannot be healed: but when it is moved and trembles, then it will recover health. 'Upon whom,' he says, 'shall I rest, except upon the humble and the quiet, and him who trembles at my words?' Since the earth of the one trembling is moved, therefore he who sits upon the cherubim begins to sit upon the earth of the trembling one. Happy is he who is the throne of God, happy is he in whom God ever sits. There are different postures: for either we sit, or we stand, or we walk, or we lie down. Unhappy is he for whom God lies down: for he lies down with the one lying down. Just as he weeps with the one weeping and laughs with the one laughing, so he lies down with the one lying down. Just as he thirsts with the one thirsting, and hungers with the one hungering, and is naked with the one naked, so too he lies down with the one lying down. He, then, who is wounded and lies down—he too is in a manner said to lie down with him. But he who walks—he also walks with him. He who walks seems indeed to be better than he who lies down; yet he has not yet come to the happiness of him who stands. Finally, when Adam hid himself from the face of God, God was walking to him after midday. Let someone say: If Adam had offended, and had been cast out of paradise, then whoever has God walking to him is in perfect unhappiness. And I say: If Adam had been utterly slain, and no place of repentance had been given to him, God would have lain down for him. But now, because a place of repentance is given to him, therefore God walks to him. Moses hears from God: 'But you, stand here with me.' And Stephen the martyr, when he was fighting, saw Jesus standing at the right hand of the Father. But God is presented as sitting in two ways. If he sits in us, we are happy; but if, while we walk, he sits in others, we are unhappy. As it says, 'The books were opened, and thrones were placed,' in Daniel. And thrones were placed. There he is set as it were a judge. Although, therefore, unhappy is he who is judged, yet happy is he for whom he sits. So, then, here too God is said to sit upon the cherubim. Cherubim is interpreted 'multitude of knowledge': whoever has a multitude of knowledge, he is the throne of God. Multitude of knowledge is not knowledge alone, but also works: for that is true knowledge which is confirmed by works. Otherwise, in vain does someone promise knowledge by word, when he destroys it by his deed.\n— Homily 26, on Psalm 98"],"PSA.99.4":["And the king's honor loves judgment. Whoever is a sinner fears the judgment of God: for he does not want to know a judge, but a merciful one. But whoever is holy, and hears God, and glorifies him in his body, this man does not fear the judge, but loves him. For example, if some most severe judge should come, and is said to be cruel and as it were drinking blood—if anyone is a murderer, he fears such a judge as this. But he who has done no evil, and hates the things that are evil, not only does not fear the judge, but even loves him. For what does he say? It is well done, that such a judge should come: there were so many robberies, so many vices are practiced, there are so many murderers: it is well done, that such a judge should come. For whoever is holy does not fear the judge, but loves him. At that time is fulfilled, And the king's honor loves judgment.\n\nYou have prepared uprightnesses. What he says is this: You made all men upright, but by their own fault they have been corrupted. You created man upright. And see what he said, uprightnesses: not one, but many. For everything that you made is upright.\n— Homily 26, on Psalm 98"],"PSA.99.5":["And adore the footstool of his feet, for it is holy. What is this footstool of the feet of Jesus, which is holy? We read in another place: 'Heaven is my throne, but the earth is the footstool of my feet.' And now it is said, Adore the footstool of his feet. If the earth is the footstool of the feet of Jesus, and it is said, Adore the footstool of his feet: is the earth therefore to be adored? How is it that we read in the Apostle that, except for the Creator, we ought not to adore the creature? To adore is said in Scripture in two ways: for there is said an adoration as toward God, and there is said to adore as it were to honor. When we say to adore toward God, adoration is properly set toward God. But when it is said of a man—for example, Sarah adored Abraham, and Elijah adored Ahab, that most impious king—it is not said that Elijah so adored Ahab as God, but here adoration is set as a greeting. Now, therefore, let us see this that he says, And adore the footstool of his feet, in which sense he says it. Do we adore the footstool of the feet of God as God, or do we adore and greet it as a man? We read in the Lamentations of Jeremiah, we read also in another prophet: 'How the Lord,' he says, 'has sanctified the footstool of his feet.' And there the footstool of his feet is called Jerusalem, or the temple: this, then, according to the history. We read also in another place, And adore the footstool of his feet. If the feet do not stand except on the footstool, then this which is said 'And adore where his feet have stood' is said also of the footstool. Therefore according to the letter we can say, for example, where he was born, where he was crucified, where he rose again. This we say for beginners. But otherwise the footstool of the feet of Jesus is the soul of the believer. Happy is he in whose breast the feet of Jesus are daily placed. Would that his feet were placed in my breast too, would that his footprints might ever cling in my heart, would that I too might say with the bride: 'I will hold him, and I will not let him go.' The bridegroom is quickly offended, he ever loves cleanness: wherever he sees filth, he quickly withdraws. And adore the footstool of his feet, for it is holy. Not every footstool of his feet, but whatever is holy: as many as persevere in holiness. For example, Adore the footstool of his feet: adore Peter and Paul and the rest of the apostles—that is, honor them. For it is holy. Adore those who persevere in holiness; but those who were a footstool and are not holy, do not adore them. For example, Judas the traitor was a footstool of God: but since he is not holy, do not adore him. I have read in a certain man's book: 'The assumption,' he says, 'of the human body—that is, the man whom God deigned to take up from Mary—this,' he says, 'he calls the footstool of his feet.' Although indeed the man was taken up, and in comparison with God every creature is the footstool of his feet, yet this very footstool has been joined to God and to him who sits upon it. See what a thing I dare to say. I adore the footstool that once was, just as I adore the throne. 'For even if we had known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him so no longer.' He had, perhaps, a footstool before his death, before his resurrection, when he ate, when he drank, when he suffered our affections. But after he rose again and ascended victorious to the heavens, I do not understand one who sits and another that is the footstool, but the whole in Christ is a throne. You ask and say: Why, or on what account? How it is, I do not know, and yet I believe that it is so. Do you wonder if I am ignorant of the mystery of the divinity, when I do not know myself? Do you ask me how both the divinity and the incarnation are one, when I do not know how I live? To have understood God is to have believed; to know God is to honor him. It suffices me to know what is written, it suffices me to know what I believe: more than that I neither will nor desire. For if I should wish to know more, I begin to lose even this which I believe. We are called faithful, not rational. Finally, it is said even by the Lord: 'Do you believe that I can do this?' And it is said to the woman: 'Your faith has made you whole'—not reason, not inquiry, not why or on what account it was done. She believed that it might be done, and it was done because she believed.\n— Homily 26, on Psalm 98"],"PSA.99.6":["Moses and Aaron among his priests, and Samuel among those who call upon his name. Many err, and think that blessed Samuel was a priest. He was not a priest, he was a Levite. Finally, even his garment was not made as for a priest, but as for a Levite. For it is written: 'And his mother made for him,' it says, 'an ephod bad garment.' Ephod means garment, but bad means linen. Now, none had the linen garment except the Levites. But the priests of God had it of five kinds, that is, of gold, of hyacinth, of fine linen, of purple, of scarlet. Whoever, then, were of the priestly line had these garments. But the Levites had the ephod bad, that is, the linen garment. This very thing, then, is now said. Moses and Aaron among his priests, and Samuel among those who call upon his name. They are priests, this one is a Levite. Yet with God it is not rank that is sought, but works. Finally, what Moses did, Samuel also did. Just as Moses withstood God, that he should not strike the people, when he said to him: 'Let me alone, that I may strike this people.' See the power of Moses. What does God say to him? Let me alone: you do me violence, your prayers as it were hold me fast, your prayers restrain my hand. I send the arrow, I send the darts: and your prayers protect the people. Let me alone, that I may strike this people. And at the same time consider the mercy of God. When he says, Let me alone, he shows that if Moses does not let him alone, God does not strike. If, then, you do not let me alone, I will not strike: let me alone, and I will strike. In another manner, therefore, what does he say? Do not let me alone, and I will not strike. Let us see whether Samuel too did this very thing. We read in the books of Kingdoms that he stayed the wrath of God coming upon the people, since even in the time of the harvest he made rain and thunders and lightnings. And what, he says, is written there? 'And there came,' it says there, 'stones of hail, and they struck the Allophyli.' See how wise the fire, how wise the hail. Where Samuel was, the lightnings do not dare to come: for they see the prophet of God, they see the Levite. The hands of Samuel threatened the lightnings. He was praying, and the lightnings could not come. But why have I said all this? Because Moses and Aaron and Samuel, under different names, performed the same virtues. And let us bless the Lord: to whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 26, on Psalm 98"],"PSA.101.1":["Mercy and judgment I will sing to you, O Lord. Let sinners hear of mercy, those who despair of their salvation, those who are doing penance; let those who scorn hear of judgment, who say: \"The Lord is merciful, let us sin, he pardons us.\"\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.2":["I will sing psalms and understand in the unspotted way. That a person should understand what he is singing, and that his mind should contemplate the psalm itself, so that his thoughts do not wander outside, and he seem indeed to sing psalms with his body, while his mind is without fruit. In the unspotted way: that as I walk in this world I may act justly, and have no spot. When will you come to me? But when I have received understanding, and have acted justly, then with confidence I say to the Lord: \"When will you come to me?\" And \"you will come\" is to be understood of his second coming.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100","I walked about in the innocence of my heart in the midst of my house. Nothing so pleases God as simplicity and innocence. And so among the other winged creatures the Holy Spirit was well pleased in none but the dove, on account of its simplicity, and among the four-footed beasts in the sheep, on account of its meekness.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.3":["I set no unjust thing before my eyes. I put nothing before the Lord, nor did I consent to iniquity; but I was wholly turned to God. I have hated those who commit transgressions. Whether it was my father, or my mother, or brother, or sister, or friend: nevertheless, if he had turned aside from the fear of the Lord, I held him in hatred, and I did not approach him at all. I did not set kinship or friendship before devotion to God.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.4":["A depraved heart did not cling to me. There was in me nothing difficult or anything perverse, but fairness and justice. As the malignant one turned aside from me, I did not know him: that is, if he was a friend to me or a neighbor, whether a bishop or a presbyter, or one established in any rank whatever: nevertheless, if he perverted his ways, I so fled from him that I made no mention of him at all.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.5":["Him who secretly disparaged his neighbor, this one I persecuted. So far ought we not to consent to the disparager, that we should even, as far as it lies in us, pursue such a person, providing for what is useful in both cases: that we neither listen willingly, nor permit him to sin. For so great is the distance between the fornicator and the disparager: that he who fornicates kills only himself, but he who disparages plunges to ruin both himself and the one who listens.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100","With the proud of eye and the insatiable of heart, with such a one I did not eat. If anyone was proud or greedy, with such a one I did not eat at all.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.6":["My eyes were upon the faithful of the earth, that they might sit with me. He did not say the rich, or emperors, or bishops, or presbyters, or deacons, but the faithful: with these I sat. A bishop, a holy man, can also say this: that I did not ordain as presbyter the man who was obsequious to me, or my kinsman, but the one whom I knew to be faithful. And again the same man can say: that I held all my clerics and laity to be faithful. This little verse can also be spoken in the person of the Lord. He who walked in the unspotted way, this one ministered to me. For I did not ordain those who were my kinsmen, but the just and the faithful.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.7":["He who speaks unjust things has not prospered in the sight of my eyes. For I so turned aside from him who disparaged his neighbor, that I neither believed such a one nor listened to him.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.101.8":["In the mornings I slew all the sinners of the earth. Well did he say, the sinners of the earth: for sinners are earthly. This is what he means, that the promptings of the devil, which he was suggesting to me, I cast out of my heart, and did not take up. For when evil thoughts are cast out, the demons are slain. The Jews, hearing this verse, and following the letter that kills, and desiring to shed blood, if anyone has transgressed against the law, put him to death. Since indeed they are not content with the slaying of the prophets: but they even crucified the Lord himself. That I might destroy out of the city of the Lord all who work iniquity. Would that we too may destroy out of the city of the Lord those who work iniquity. The city of the Lord is the church of the saints, the congregation of the just. That I might destroy: that is, I will reprove and rebuke him who sins, that he may do penance, and destroy iniquity out of his heart. For he did not say, that he may be cast out.\n— Homily 27, on Psalm 100"],"PSA.102.1":["Prayer of the poor man, when he is in anguish, and pours out his supplication before the Lord. He speaks of a poor man, not of this world, but of the one about whom it is written: 'Blessed are the poor in spirit.' When he is in anguish: when he calls to mind his sins, both past and present. Pours out: with his whole heart, not with his lips. But who is able to pour out his prayer before the Lord? Who is able not to be stung by his own conscience?\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.2":["Turn not away your face from me. If anyone prays with a pure heart, and is not accused by his own conscience, he can say this. For the sinner does not dare to say, turn not away your face from me: but rather he says: 'Turn away your face from my sins.'\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.3":["For my days have failed away like smoke. For example, I was a little child, and while I know it not, I came into the age of youth, and again into full age, and while I know it not, I have grown old: and my days have failed away like smoke. But some people think themselves to be everlasting, and they persevere in their sin. And my bones are withered like a dry twig for kindling. By 'bones' he means strength itself. If therefore what is strong in me has withered and been made weak: how much more the flesh, which is weak by nature?\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.4":["I am smitten like grass, and my heart is withered. Just as it is also written elsewhere: 'Man, his days are like grass.' For such is the life of men: it is green, and quickly it withers. Because I have forgotten to eat my bread. He did not say, I had no desire to eat, or I despised eating. To such a degree did the memory of my sins come upon me, and such penance did I do, that I even forget to eat, having my longing set on God, and considering him alone.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.5":["By reason of the voice of my groaning my bone has cleaved to my flesh. Let those who do penance learn how they ought to do penance. For I so dried myself up, that I was without flesh, and my skin clung to my bones. He said 'flesh' for 'skin.'\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.6":["I have become like the pelican of the wilderness. There are said to be two kinds of these birds. One lives in the waters, and its food is fish; and one lives in the wilderness, and its food is venomous creatures, that is, serpents and crocodiles and lizards. Now these birds are called in Latin 'onocrotali.' To that one therefore have I become like, which lives in the wilderness, which eats venomous creatures: that is, my food was to me as it were poison. I have become like the night-raven in its dwelling-place. We know that the raven is black, and has nothing white in it. He wished to say, by way of hyperbole, that I have become like the night-raven. If therefore it is black in the day, how much more in the night? So therefore have I become, considering myself because of my sins.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.9":["For I have eaten ashes like bread. I dipped my bread in ashes, and so ate it. If therefore the prophet says this, what should we do? And I mingled my drink with weeping. Nothing was sweet to me, neither bread, nor drink: but I longed only for the heavenly bread, that is, Christ.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.10":["Because of the face of your wrath and indignation. And all these things I did, considering your coming and the judgment to come. Because lifting me up you have dashed me down. Let those say this who were once holy, and fell: for having been exalted in their virtues, afterward they fell. Since indeed the saints say: because lifting me up you held me.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.11":["My days have declined like a shadow. If my days, how much more my nights? that is, if the light, how much more the darkness? And I have withered like grass. For as grass, so man passes away, and is dried up.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.13":["You, rising up, will have mercy on Sion. From this little verse it is manifest that this psalm is spoken in the person of Sion, and not in the person of the prophet. Now Sion is she who is set upon a watchtower, and looks out upon the things which come from afar. This Sion therefore, who is set on high and on a lofty place, has fallen. Therefore 'let him who stands take heed lest he fall.' Because it is the time to have mercy on her, because the time has come. Either it is the time of mercy on account of penance, or it signifies according to the coming of the Savior. Let him therefore be confident who does penance, since the time of salvation has come: for the Lord is merciful.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.14":["For its stones have pleased your servants. The stones are the saints: but the earth, that is, sinners, stand in need of the Lord's mercy.\n— Homily 28, on Psalm 101"],"PSA.102.25-26":["And David sings in the psalm: In the beginning, O Lord, you founded the earth, and the heavens are the works of your hands. They shall perish, but you remain; and they shall all grow old like a garment; and like a cloak you shall roll them up, and they shall be changed (Ps 101 [102]:26). From this it is shown that the perishing of the heavens signifies not destruction but a change for the better. Of these it is said: There shall be a new heaven and a new earth, which I will make to remain in my sight (Rev 21:2). For if it is written of the saints, We shall all indeed sleep, but we shall not all be changed (1 Cor 15:51), and in the titles of four psalms we read, For those who shall be changed: how much more is this to be believed of heaven and of the sun and the stars, when the moon shall receive the light of the sun, and the sun shall glow with sevenfold brightness? Hence the whole creation too groans and travails, awaiting the revelation of the sons of God, that it may be changed for the better (Rom 8). And not only the souls of men, which are immortal, but their bodies too are to be changed into a glorified substance. For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal put on immortality (1 Cor 15:53). And since heaven and earth grow old, it follows that the things contained within the circle of heaven, and men, who are the inhabitants of the earth, also die like these: not unto their destruction, but unto the abolishing of their old baseness and the renewal of the glory to come; when the righteous shall shine like the sun (Matt 13:43), and, the past and old things departing, all things shall have been made new.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But those who consider newness to be a change for the better, and not the destruction of the elements, use this example: In the beginning, O Lord, you founded the earth, and the heavens are the works of your hands. They shall perish, but you remain, and they shall all grow old like a garment, and like a cloak you shall roll them up, and they shall be changed (Ps 101 [102]:26, 27). In this it is clearly shown that perdition and destruction mean not an abolition into nothing, but a change for the better.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.103.1":["Bless the Lord, O my soul, and all that is within me, bless his holy name. It is said also in another place: \"Know that the Lord himself is God.\" And now it is said, And all that is within me, bless his holy name. If we have said, Bless the Lord, O my soul, and the Lord is Lord, what name of the Lord does he seek here? If he is called Lord, the Lord, why does he say, And all that is within me, bless his holy name? The coming of the Son is the name of the Father. Before the coming of Christ, God was known, but the Father was unknown. Indeed, he himself says in the Gospel: \"Father, I have revealed your name to men.\" We cannot linger over each individual little verse, since the psalm is long; but we ought to fit brief little remarks to the individual verses, expounding the sense rather than weaving together the words.\n— Homily 29, on Psalm 102"],"PSA.103.2":["Bless the Lord, O my soul, and do not forget all his rewards. The Lord has bestowed many rewards upon us, he has bestowed great benefits upon us: for he made the heaven, the earth, all the elements for us. The sun itself and the moon and the stars and all the elements run and run back again for our sake. O wretched creatures that we are: the elements serve us, and we do not serve the Lord. It is a great thing, therefore, that God made for our sake all the things we see. For a house is always made for the sake of the one who dwells in it. If we are the dwellers of the world, and the world is the house of men, and the house has been made for the sake of the dweller: it is a great thing, therefore, that he made for our sake all the things we see, that he made us to be born, to live, to move, to understand our Creator, that he set us apart from the beasts, that they look downward, bent toward the earth, while he made us stand upright to gaze upon heaven. And these things indeed are great: but greater is the fact that he was born for our sake. \"Who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal to God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, made obedient even unto death, and death on a cross.\" Since we were mortal because of our sin, he himself deigned to die for us mortals, so that he might make us alive.\n— Homily 29, on Psalm 102"],"PSA.103.3":["Who heals all your infirmities. Our soul has many infirmities. As many sins, so many infirmities. Indeed, there was also that woman in the Gospel, who for eighteen years had a spirit of infirmity, and could not lift her head upright... See what he says. When someone has a spirit of infirmity, he is bent over, he looks downward, he gazes at the earth, he cannot see heaven.\n— Homily 29, on Psalm 102"],"PSA.103.4":["Who redeems your life from destruction. \"You were bought,\" he says, \"with a price: do not become the servants of men.\" What is a greater price than for the Creator to pour out his own blood for the creature?\n— Homily 29, on Psalm 102","Who crowns you in mercy and compassions. Who crowns you. When he has redeemed, then he crowns. And he crowns, not because of your merits, but because of his own mercy. \"O Lord, you have crowned us as with a shield of your good will.\" See what he says, O Lord, you have crowned us as with a shield of your good will. Does anyone crown with a shield? Surely he who crowns, crowns with flowers, crowns with gold, crowns with the other kinds of crowns. Does anyone crown with a shield? But the Lord's shield is a crown: for it protects and defends, and thus it crowns. And here, therefore, what is there signified by a shield, here is signified by mercy; and the shield, while it protects, is mercy.\n— Homily 29, on Psalm 102"],"PSA.103.5":["But those who have their trust not in their own strength but in God, and always await his mercy, change their strength and go from strength to strength, and take on wings like the eagle, and hear: Your youth will be renewed like the eagle's (Ps 102 [103]:5); they run to the Lord and do not labor in their desire for him; they walk and never fail. We have often said that the old age of eagles grows green again by the changing of their feathers, and that they alone can look at the radiance of the sun and gaze with flashing eyes upon the splendor of its rays, and that by this test they prove their young, whether they are true-born. So too the saints grow young again, and, having put on an immortal body, do not feel the labor of mortals, but are caught up in the clouds to meet Christ, and, according to the Seventy, never hunger, because they have the Lord present as their food.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.104.1-2":["You have put on confession and beauty. \"Confession\" is understood in two ways. Either it refers to the glory of God: that is, you have put on glory, just as in the Gospel the Savior himself says, \"I confess to you, Father,\" that is, I glorify you. Or it means that we confess our sins to the Lord: for in the very act of confessing our sins to God, we glorify him. And you have put on beauty. Notice the order: first someone confesses, and thus he receives loveliness and beauty, and afterward light. For this is what follows: Clothed with light as with a garment. Now this is said in a special way of the Lord, that all things belonging to him are in the light, just as in Ezekiel the Cherubim are written to be \"full of eyes.\" For example: I look at the sky and the earth, and I understand the creator. I see the trees in their season putting forth leaves, sending out blossoms, bearing fruit, and I understand the creator. I see the ant, and every tiny creature, and I understand the creator. Therefore the whole creation of God has eyes, and is full of understanding.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.1":["This psalm contains the created world itself praising and blessing the Lord its creator through the prophet; just as he also says in the eighteenth psalm: \"The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims the works of his hands.\"\n\nBless the Lord, O my soul. The prophet rouses himself to bless God. But to bless the Lord means this: whoever praises the Lord blesses himself. O Lord my God, you are exceedingly magnified. You who are the God of all are in a special way my God: because I am not a slave of sin, because I have deserved to be called your servant. You are exceedingly magnified. When I look upon the sky, the earth, the birds, the four-footed beasts, the serpents, and your whole creation, I marvel, and I magnify the creator.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.2":["Stretching out the heaven like a skin. This means that from the beginning God stretched out the heaven, just as when someone opens a scroll and again rolls it back up, as is written elsewhere: \"And the heaven shall be rolled up like a scroll.\"\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.3":["You who cover its upper parts with waters. Just as it is written in Genesis, that there are waters above the firmament, and likewise beneath. You who walk upon the wings of the winds. He signifies that God is present everywhere.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.4":["You who make your angels spirits. What he passed over in Genesis, he indicated in this verse. For although God created all things, he made no mention anywhere of the angels. And he said, You who make your angels spirits, for this reason: because the angels do not have a body, but are wholly light. And your ministers a burning fire. He signifies their purity, and that they are not subject to sins. In another sense: for example, someone is holy, and a good angel, an angel of light, is sent to him. But if he is a sinner, an angel of wrath is sent to him, to set him on fire and torment him.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.5":["You who founded the earth upon its own stability. And truly it is marvelous how great a mass of earth hangs by the power of God. It shall not be moved for ever and ever. For it stands unmoved, by the providence of God.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.6":["The deep, like a garment, is his clothing. As is written elsewhere: \"And he made darkness his hiding-place,\" and again: \"The judgments of the Lord are a great deep.\" He signifies the unspeakable governance of God, and that we cannot comprehend the wisdom of God. For just as we cannot gaze with these eyes into the deep, so neither can we gaze into the greatness nor into the wisdom of God. But according to the Hebrew we ought to understand \"his clothing\" in this way, that \"of the earth\" is to be supplied; so that the sense is that this very deep surrounds the whole earth — the thing they call the ocean. For around the whole earth there are waters, and the earth is like an island.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103","The waters shall stand above the mountains. Not, as some suppose, that the waters stand above the mountains because of winter and the rains; but the mountains were covered by the waters, and the earth did not appear. But according to the figurative interpretation, the waters are to be taken for doctrine, and the mountains for the saints.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.7":["At your rebuke they shall flee. And truly at God's command the water withdrew, and the earth appeared, as is also written in Genesis: \"And God gathered the waters into one gathering.\" At the voice of your thunder they shall be afraid. For the thunder is the voice of the Lord. For he gave the command, and the waters were separated.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.8":["The mountains ascend, and the plains descend. He signifies the rush of the waters, because after the manner of mountains the waters rise up on high, and again are brought low.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.9":["You have set a boundary which they shall not pass. For it is marvelous to hear the roaring of the sea, and its rush, and the waves lifted up as high as heaven, and coming as if to cover the whole earth; and yet at the shore they lay down their rush, and return to themselves, and do not overstep God's command — while men do not keep God's command. Neither shall they turn back to cover the earth, as happened under Noah, the flood.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.10":["You who send forth springs in the valleys. Perhaps someone says: If all things were made for the service of man, what use is it that waters run in the desert? This too was done for our salvation and by God's providence, so that the four-footed beasts might have nourishment for our service. In another sense: because God made all things by his dispensation, and all things are his, and he feeds even the brute animals. The waters shall pass through the midst of the mountains. Since the things that were on the mountains needed to grow green from the rains of the Lord, therefore God sent the waters. But according to the figurative interpretation, it signifies the holy works of the just.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.12":["Over them the birds of heaven shall dwell. Remember the parable of the mustard seed, and the birds that dwell in it. From the midst of the rocks they shall give voice. Each thing in its own tongue praises the Lord. In the Hebrew it has \"of the trees.\"\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.13":["From the fruit of your works the earth shall be filled. In another sense: from all your benefits the earth shall be filled. In another sense: from your works, that is, your apostles, many have been filled.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.14":["Bringing forth hay for the cattle. Attend carefully, since the cattle eat hay. And grass for the service of men. Since he created all things for his own glory.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103","That he may bring forth bread out of the earth. He speaks of the Lord, who deigned to become man, as it is written: \"I am the living bread, who came down from heaven.\"\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.15":["And wine gladdens the heart of man. This is the wine which the Lord promised to drink after the resurrection (anastasis).\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103","That he may make the face cheerful with oil. The face of God makes the just cheerful; but of sinners \"let it not fatten the head.\" And bread strengthens the heart of man. The bread which came down from heaven.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.16":["The trees of the field shall be filled. Since the Lord waters even the trees, so that they may give us fruit.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.17":["There the sparrows shall build their nests. By \"sparrows,\" let us understand all birds — the whole from a part.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103","The house of the heron is their leader. The heron is a bird of very great size: and it is said to conquer even the eagle, and to have the eagle itself as its food. And it is not like the other birds, which have fixed nests, and go to their own places at evening; but this one sleeps wherever the darkness overtakes it. But according to the figurative interpretation, the monk has no cell, but wherever he finds himself, there he stays; and his battle is with the devil who reigns in this world, just as the heron too conquers the eagle, the king of the birds, as it is written: \"Though you be exalted like the eagle, from there I will drag you down, says the Lord.\" For the eagle is the devil, according to Ezekiel.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.18":["The high mountains for the stags. This animal kills serpents and eats them. Therefore it fittingly dwells in the mountains, this one that slays the intelligible serpent — the one who was wiser in paradise than all the beasts, who deceived Eve. The rock is a refuge for the hedgehogs. He who is timid has the rock as his refuge: and the rock is Christ.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.19":["He made the moon for seasons. They say that when the moon waxes, all fruits also grow, and when it wanes, the fruits again diminish. Therefore he said fittingly, He made the moon for seasons. Or let us understand the moon in another way as the Church: for as the Church advances, we advance with it; and when it suffers persecution and is diminished, we too are diminished with it and suffer persecution. The sun knew its setting. If the sun sets, do we suppose ourselves to be everlasting? In this, moreover, it is a thing to marvel at, how so great a light and so great a brightness, which covers the whole world with its light, sets in a moment. But let us pray that the sun of righteousness may not set upon us.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.20-21":["You have made, says David, darkness, and it became night; in it all the beasts of the forest will pass through. The lions' whelps, to snatch and to seek their food from God. The lion is hard to find by day, but he always prowls in the nights, to snatch from the Church of Christ, so that, according to Habakkuk, he may be sated with choice foods.\n— Commentary on Nahum","And not only will they lie open to the lion's bites, but the lion's whelp will seize them and lead them to his den, fleeing the light and making for the darkness. For everyone who sins loves the darkness and hates the light; of these we read in the psalm too: You have set darkness, and it became night; in it all the beasts of the forest will pass through. The lions' whelps roaring to seize their prey, and to seek their food from God. For the lion's whelp seeks not the prey which he already possesses, which is in his own power, but to snatch from the Church of Christ; of him it is written in Habakkuk: His foods are choice.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.104.20-23":["The Word of God, the true sun, has risen for us, and the beasts have gathered together, withdrawing into their dens: let us go forth as men to our work, and let us labor until the evening, as is mystically sung in the psalm: \"You have set darkness, and it became night. In it the beasts of the forest will pass through, the young lions roaring to seize their prey and to seek their food from God. The sun has risen, and they have gathered together, and in their dens they have slept. Man shall go forth to his work, and to his labor until the evening\" (Ps. 103:20 ff.).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PSA.104.20":["You have set darkness, and it became night: in it all the beasts of the forest shall pass through. And according to the letter it has its own order: for night was made for this reason, that men might rest from their works, and that the beasts might go out to their feeding. But according to the figurative interpretation, when the sun of righteousness has set upon us, all things are dark to us, and then the beasts pass through against us, and the roaring lion, wishing to seize and devour us.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.21":["The young lions roaring, that they may seize, and seek from God their food. He said fittingly, from God: since indeed he desires to deceive the saints — for example, Judas, David, Solomon, Peter — as it is written: \"His food is choice.\"\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.22":["The sun arose, and they were gathered together. Let not the one who has fallen be saddened, but let him do penance: the sun of righteousness rises upon him, and all the beasts and his sins flee from him, and he becomes such as he was before. For the sun of righteousness has arisen again.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.23":["Man shall go forth to his work, and to his labor until the evening. But after the sun of righteousness has arisen, we go forth again to our labor until the evening, that is, until the consummation. Therefore we ought to work righteousness at every time.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.24":["How magnified are your works, O Lord. The prophet comes into an ecstasy of mind. Not finding how he might praise the Lord, he said: How magnified are your works, O Lord. Your works surpass human understanding. You have made all things in wisdom. In our Lord Jesus Christ (for he himself is the wisdom of God), according to the apostle who says that in him all things were created. The earth is filled with your possession. And truly all the works of the Lord are full of wisdom. For when we see the ant know when winter is coming, and store up food for itself; and again the gnat, so tiny, having eyes, a belly, and all its members, just as we have; and the flea, and the rest: and then we see the elephant, so great a beast, having eyes, nostrils, feet, a belly, just as we have in those same places: and likewise the bee, how it makes honey and wax: are these things not worthy of wonder and full of wisdom?\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.25-26":["But to me, as I read that passage in the psalms: This great and wide sea, there are creeping things without number, small animals with great. There the ships pass, that dragon which you formed to mock him, it seems worthy of the goodness and clemency of God to destroy by his threat all the bitterness and saltiness of the sea, and to humble the dragon that reigns in the waters, and to dry up the whirlpools of malice in which the small creeping things swim, of which there is no number; for the things that dwell with the dragon are not worthy of number.\n— Commentary on Nahum","Of these fishers, who go down to the sea and are in ships, Isaiah too prophesies: They shall fly in the ships of foreigners, and together they shall plunder the sea. For in the churches gathered from the number of the nations the apostles flew through the whole world, to plunder the sea and to carry off the spoils of the dragon who reigned in the sea, and of whom it is written: This sea, great and spacious in its arms; there are creeping things without number, small creatures with great. There the ships shall pass through; this dragon whom you formed to mock him. The sea is broad and spacious for the small and great living creatures that dwell with the dragon, and whose number is beyond count.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.104.25":["This great and spacious sea with its arms. Is this not a matter of wondrous wisdom, how in the sea there are creeping things beyond number, and fish both greater and smaller: and how they are in the deep, and there draw their life, where, if a man should fall in, he dies, while they themselves, if they come onto the land, die?\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.26":["There the ships shall pass through. Which of us is a strong ship, able to flee from this world, and not be submerged, nor strike upon a rock — but let him have understanding to steer him, that he may be able to be saved?\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103","This dragon which you formed to mock him. The Jews say: God made a great dragon, which is called Leviathan, and it is in the sea; and when, they say, the ocean recedes, the dragon turns. But let us say: This is the dragon who was cast out of paradise, who deceived Eve, and was given into this world to mock us. For how many monks and clerics has he cast down!\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.27":["All things wait upon you, that you may give them food in due season. Because all things live by God's nod.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.29":["You will take away their spirit, and they shall fail. He signifies the consummation of the world, when at the command of the Lord all things are destroyed, as it is written: \"Earth you are, and into the earth you shall go.\"\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.30":["You will send forth your spirit, and they shall be created. On the day of consummation. If the Spirit creates, therefore he is God: for he creates in the same way as the Father also does.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.31":["Let the glory of the Lord be for ever. If the glory of the Lord is for ever, then we too, who glorify, shall be everlasting. The Lord shall rejoice in his works. For it is the Lord's joy, when we, his creation, bless him on the day of resurrection.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.32":["Who looks upon the earth, and makes it tremble. For our earth shall tremble on the day of judgment. And it signifies the earthly sinners. Who touches the mountains, and they smoke. If the mountains smoke, how much more the earth? The saints smoke, since we shall all be saved through fire. For it would not have smoke, unless there were fire.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.33":["I will sing to the Lord in my life; I will make music to my God as long as I am. Just as he also said elsewhere: \"Let me be, that I may be refreshed before I depart, and be no more.\" Let me be for a little while, that I may do penance for my sins: since in hell there is no one who can confess his sins.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.34":["May my speech be pleasing to him. May it be pleasing to him, so that one may worthily be able to praise and bless God. He spoke in the optative mood, not the imperative. But I will delight in the Lord. Blessed are those who say this after the judgment.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.104.35":["Let sinners fail from the earth. He did not say that the sinners should perish, but that sin itself should, so that it may not appear. And with sin not appearing, of course there are then no more sinners, but all shall be found to be just. And the wicked, so that they shall be no more. So that they may be no longer wicked, but just.\n— Homily 30, on Psalm 103"],"PSA.105.1":["Confess to the Lord and call upon his name. Confess and say: because we are sinners, but you are merciful, and you have mercy on us. And call upon his name. So that we may not fashion idols in our hearts, but we will call upon our Lord, and he himself will be our redemption. Proclaim his works among the nations. Let the Jews blush, who say that only within Israel were his wonders and his works accomplished.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.2":["Sing to him and make music to him. He who understands the holy Scriptures, and meditates continually on the law of the Lord, and whose mind contemplates heavenly things, this man sings to God. But he who has within himself all the virtues — that is, is a worker of good work — this man, composing as it were a lyre out of the virtues, makes music to the Lord. Tell of all his wonders. In the one hundred and sixth psalm he says: 'Those who go down to the sea in ships, doing their work in many waters: they themselves have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep.' If we cross over in this bodily ship of ours unharmed, and nowhere does the storm overwhelm us, nor do we strike against a rock and shatter our ship, we are able to tell of the wonders of God. And truly it is a great miracle to pass through this world without stain.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.3":["Be praised in his holy name. This is a prophecy concerning the people of the gentiles: that is, that we should be and be called Christians from the name of our Lord Jesus Christ. Let the heart of those who seek the Lord rejoice. Let the monks be glad, for the hearts of those who seek the Lord rejoice. If of those who seek, how much more of those who find him? For those who find him are unshaken.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.4":["Seek the Lord and be strengthened. Before we sought the Lord, we were weak; but now, seeking him, we are firm and strong. But if we also find him, how much more perfect shall we be? Seek his face always. To the Hebrews indeed it was written, that three times in the year they should appear before the Lord. And what righteousness was this? And what great thing did anyone do, to appear three times in the year in the sight of God? There he was speaking to little children; but here the prophet tells those who believe in God to seek him always, just as it is also written in the gospel, that we should pray without ceasing.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.5":["Remember his wonders which he did. In Egypt, and in the Red Sea, and in the desert.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.6":["The seed of Abraham, his servants: the sons of Jacob, his chosen ones. If someone is seed, he is not yet also a son, according to what is written in the gospel: 'If you were the sons of Abraham, you would do the works of your father.' Therefore, that in which they glory, they are not; and if they are seed, they are servants set under fear, not having the perfect love which casts out fear. We are the sons of Jacob, of him who supplanted his brother, and took away his birthright. 'But many are called, and few are chosen.'\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.13":["And they passed from nation to nation. Many think that he speaks of the captivity of the Jews. It is not so. For he speaks of Abraham. He went out from the land of the Chaldeans, and came into Mesopotamia, and from Mesopotamia into the land of promise, and again into Egypt. And from a kingdom to another people. We pass through many kingdoms, in order to reach the land of promise.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.14":["He did not permit man to harm them. Pharaoh, that he should harm Abraham in the person of Sarah, nor Abimelech Isaac in the person of Rebecca. And he rebuked kings for their sake. He says the same thing, Pharaoh and Abimelech.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.15":["Touch not my anointed ones, and do no malice among my prophets. He speaks of the patriarchs themselves. Let the Jews therefore blush, who say that unless someone is anointed with royal ointment, he cannot be called anointed (christ). For they say this, that our Lord is not the Christ, because he is not anointed with royal ointment. Behold, before the law the patriarchs, not anointed with royal ointment, are called anointed ones (christs). But they are anointed ones (christs) who are anointed with the Holy Spirit. Therefore our Lord too is rightly called Christ. Which they deny, going against the Scriptures.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.16":["And he called famine upon the land. Very often it happens that those things which are thought to be adverse, these are brought about by the providence of God for our benefit. For unless the brothers of Joseph had been jealous of him, and had sold him, how would he have gone down into Egypt? If the famine had not come about, how would the patriarchs have gone down into Egypt, and been recognized by their brother? How would Pharaoh have been struck with ten plagues? How would they have gone out of Egypt? How would waters have come out of the rock in the wilderness? How would the mysteries which were done in the desert as a figure have come to be? For we understand them spiritually. If Judas had not betrayed the Lord, how would we have been saved? If the Jews had not crucified him, and had not been unbelieving, how would we have believed? Joseph therefore went into Egypt, to prepare lodging and food for his brothers.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.17":["He sent a man before them. He speaks of Joseph himself. Joseph was sold as a slave. A well-born and holy man is sold.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.18":["They humbled his feet in fetters. Because he refused to consent to the Egyptian mistress. They humbled his feet in fetters: but they could not humble his righteousness. For when he was sixteen years old, he was loved passionately by the mistress, and though set in such an age, which is more given over to pleasure, he refused to consent to iniquity, but rather feared the peril of his soul. The iron passed through his soul. By the iron he means sin itself, which slays the soul. In another sense: because he was in peril, accused by the mistress of such a crime. For the mistress burned for him, as it is written: 'All the adulterers, their hearts are like an oven.'\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.19":["Until his word should come. He speaks of the word of the Lord himself, who set him free. The utterance of the Lord inflamed him. For unless he had been inflamed by the Holy Spirit, he could not have conquered lust. For the Spirit of the Lord inflamed him, and extinguished the fire of lust.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.20":["The king sent, and loosed him. According to the letter, Pharaoh sent, and loosed him. In another sense: if anyone is tempted by lust, unless the Lord appears and looses him, he cannot escape sin.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.23":["And Jacob was a sojourner in the land of Ham. For the just man never dwells in the land, but is a sojourner of the earth.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.105.24":["And he increased his people exceedingly. For they went down into Egypt in seventy-five souls, and six hundred thousand went out of Egypt.\n— Homily 31, on Psalm 104"],"PSA.106.1":["Alleluia, alleluia. We ought to hold to this rule, wherever two alleluias are set at the head of a psalm: let us understand that the one belongs to the end of the previous psalm, and the other to the psalm that follows.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105","Give thanks to the Lord, for he is good. O you who sin grievously, and despair of your own salvation, and think that because of the greatness of your sins you cannot obtain pardon: I command you — that is, the prophet does — give thanks to the Lord, for he is good. Great are your sins: but great is the Lord, who has mercy on you. Give thanks, therefore, to the Lord. Do penance; do not despair of your salvation: the Lord is kind. Give thanks to the Lord, you who have great sins. Do not trust in your own strength, but in the mercy of the Lord. For his mercy endures forever. See what he says — forever. 'But in hell, who shall give thanks to the Lord?' For there is no one who can do penance in hell. While you are in this world, I urge you to do penance. Give thanks, therefore, to the Lord, because it is only in this world that he is merciful. Here he can have mercy on the penitent: for there he is a judge, he is not merciful. Here he is merciful, there he is a judge. Here he stretches out his hand to the one who falls: there he sits as judge. And I have said all this against those who say that there is penance even in hell.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.106.2":["Who shall tell the mighty acts of the Lord? No one is worthy to proclaim the mighty acts of the Father, except the Son. No one can tell the might of the Father, except him who is himself also mighty: the mighty acts of the Mighty One he alone who is in the bosom of the Father, he who is himself mighty, can proclaim and tell. Shall he make all his praises heard? Before our Lord in the Gospel proclaimed the praises of the Father, they had not been heard in the world. Everything that is written in the old Law, if it be understood spiritually, this can be understood of the Father.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.106.3":["Blessed are they who keep judgment, and do justice at all times. David speaks rightly. He too had done judgment, he had done justice: but because he did not do it at all times, therefore he fell. For what does he say? Blessed are they who keep judgment, and do justice at all times. But blessed are they who do it at all times. For what does it profit a man to be in continence for twenty years, and afterwards to fall? — according to what is written in the prophet: 'The justice of the just man shall not profit him on whatever day he goes astray: it shall not free him, nor save him. And the sin of the sinner shall not harm him on whatever day he turns and does penance.' Neither ought the just man to be secure, nor ought the sinner to despair of his salvation: for in both of them there is both fear and hope. He who does justice. Justice is our Lord: for our Lord and Savior is called the justice of the Lord. Blessed therefore is he who does justice, that is, who makes Christ. How then do we make Christ, that is, justice? If we act justly, justice is conceived in us, and justice is brought forth from us, and we are the mother of justice. Happy and blessed is he who at all times is both father and mother of justice.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.106.4-5":["Remember us, O Lord, in the good pleasure of your people. What blessed David says is this: I know, O Lord, the old Law; the perfect blessedness is not in the old Law, but in the Gospel; I know that the former people offended you, and that a new people is being created, which will serve you: I know that your Son will come, and will save the whole human race, and those who are chosen will believe in him: therefore, then, I ask, since I was born earlier under the old Law, that you would reckon to me a reward together with the new people. This seems to be our opinion. Let us read the very verses, and we shall find the meaning to be that which we have said. Visit us with your salvation. That is, in your Son, when he shall come. That we may see in the goodness of your chosen ones. That is, give us a portion with the apostles: for we indeed went before them in time, but they go before us in spiritual things. That is, that we may see your goodness, which you are about to work with your chosen ones. That we may rejoice in the joy of your nation. We are the Lord's nation, if indeed we are his nation. 'For a people, he says, that shall be born, which the Lord has made.' That we may be praised with your inheritance. That is, that we too who were made under the old Law may have a portion together with the Gospel. For Abraham our father saw your day, and was glad. Let us too, then, see, and rejoice with your inheritance.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.106.6":["We have sinned with our fathers. As sons we have followed our fathers: we have done what our fathers also did. 'For he has shut up all under sin, that he might have mercy on all.' We have acted unjustly, we have committed iniquity. Note carefully what he says: We have sinned with our fathers, we have acted unjustly, we have committed iniquity. Did he say, we have acted impiously? It is one thing to be a sinner and unjust, another to be impious. The sinner is frequently overcome by the weakness of the body: but the impious man denies God. Therefore, then, because I am not impious, but a sinner, therefore I dare to pray for my sins.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.106.7":["Our fathers in Egypt did not understand your wonders. They saw, and they did not see: they saw with the eyes of the flesh, and with spiritual eyes they did not see. He did not say, they did not see: but, they did not understand. For just as those men saw the wonders, and did not understand: so also in the Gospel, if anyone indeed sees and hears carnally, but does not understand spiritually, he does not see. Blessed therefore are we who have not seen and yet have believed, more than those who saw and did not believe. They saw the Red Sea, they saw Pharaoh drowned, and understood only this, which they saw. But we, who have not seen him, understand better. In Pharaoh we understand the devil, in his army we understand the demons, in the sea we understand baptism. They understand Marah to be bitter waters, and they pass through Marah: but we daily see the baptism of heretics to be bitter waters. They hang up the bronze serpent in the desert. Let the anchorites hear, let those who dwell in the wilderness hear. But we, what have we done? We too have hung up the true and ancient serpent in the wilderness. Although the apostle does not interpret this of the Lord in the way we suppose. But what does he say? On the cross of the Lord the devil was hung up and fastened. The Lord indeed was crucified, but the devil died. This one fulfilled the mystery, and appeared as victor: but the other did not appear, but was spiritually slain, just as the apostle also signifies.\n— Homily 32, on Psalm 105"],"PSA.107.1":["Give thanks to the Lord, for he is good, for his mercy endures for ever. Therefore confess to him your sins, because he is good and forgives sins. But if he were not good, he would not say to you, \"Give thanks\"; and, \"Give thanks to him, for his mercy endures for ever.\" This is mercy: for the rest, above there is justice. \"For his mercy endures for ever.\" As long as you are in this age, give thanks.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.2":["Let them say so who have been redeemed by the Lord, whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy. He is speaking properly about the calling of the Gentiles: for this psalm holds this mystery. \"Who have been redeemed\" — in the Lord's passion. \"Let them say so who have been redeemed\": for he himself is the savior and the redeemer and the price. \"Let them say so who have been redeemed\": he himself redeemed, he himself gives the price, his own blood. But why did he give as the price his own blood? That he might redeem us from the hand of the enemy. For we were held captive in the hand of the devil: and for this reason he came and redeemed us, that he might set us free from the hand of the devil. \"Whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy.\" \"That you may destroy the enemy and the defender.\" \"Whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy.\" \"An enemy man oversowed the tares.\" And the enemy is the devil. \"Whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy.\" He is an enemy, and yet he is the possessor. Therefore he holds us in his hand: not because he loves, but because he hates. \"Whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy.\" Great is the majesty and great the power of the Lord's blood, which loosed the hand of the devil. For just as there are certain animals which grip something enclosed within themselves (this both the doctors and the natural philosophers say), so that unless it be broken, it is not parted from them, nor does it let go what it holds shut inside; but if oil be applied, at once it is dissolved, and what had been gripped tight is scattered and divided into parts: so too the blood of Christ loosed the most hardened hand of the devil. He was unwilling to let us go: the Lord poured out his blood like the oil of mercy, and through it he set us free. \"From the regions he gathered them.\" See how great is the hand of the devil. \"Whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy.\" But these whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy — they were being held in all regions in the hand of the enemy. \"From the regions he gathered them\": throughout the whole world the hand of the devil possessed us.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.3":["\"From the rising of the sun and from its setting, from the north and the sea.\" He seems indeed to have named four climes: the east, the west, the south, and the north — that is, the four climes of the world. But the psalm does not contain it so. For here it has: \"from the rising of the sun and from its setting\" — behold, two climes; \"from the north,\" that is, from the region of the septentrion — behold, three climes. \"And the sea.\" The sea, surely, is in the west. So then see that he named the west twice, and the south not even once. Why then did he name three climes, and refuse to mention one? We read in Habakkuk: \"God will come from the south, and the Holy One from the shady mountain.\" \"God will come from the south.\" It is said of the Savior: because God was born in the south. For by the situation of Jerusalem, Bethlehem is set to the south: therefore it is said, \"God will come from the south.\" But if \"God will come from the south,\" how is it now said, \"From the rising of the sun and from its setting, from the north\"? The south wind, the auster and the notus, is passed over in silence. \"From the rising of the sun and from its setting.\" \"Amen, amen, I say to you, that many will come from the east and the west, and will recline in the bosom of Abraham: but the sons of this generation will be far off.\" \"From the rising of the sun and from its setting\" — the foreigners, surely. \"And from the north\": \"The north wind is the most hardened wind, but by name it is called the right hand\"; \"From the north,\" he says, \"evils will blaze forth over the earth.\" The sea is read of in Numbers and in Exodus and in all holy Scripture; and wherever you hear \"the sea,\" according to the climes it is always understood as the west. For by the situation of Jerusalem this great sea, as it is called, is in the west. See therefore the mystery of the Scriptures: in this place the west is named twice, and the notus not even once. Why is this said? Because in this psalm the people of the Jews is not written as to be called, but only that of the Gentiles.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.4-5":["\"They wandered in the wilderness, in a waterless place: they did not find the way of the city of habitation, hungering and thirsting.\" And that you may know that it is written properly about the calling of the Gentiles and about the driving-out of the Jews, in what follows of this very psalm it is written: \"He turned rivers into desert\" — namely, of the Jews. \"They wandered in the wilderness, in a waterless place.\" Do you wish to know that we too were before in a waterless place? \"They wandered in the wilderness, in a waterless place.\" We have said what the waters were, we have said what the rivers were. \"They did not find the way of the city of habitation, hungering and thirsting.\" Let us set forth how the people of the Gentiles was in a desert where there were no waters, that is, in a waterless place, and how \"they did not find the way of the city of habitation, hungering and thirsting.\" Of those of whom it is said \"they did not find,\" it is plain that they sought. \"They did not find the way of the city of habitation.\" Therefore they sought a way, a way by which they might come to the city: hungering they sought, and thirsting. \"Their soul failed within them.\" \"They failed, searching with searching.\" They failed, they sought, and did not find, and in their hunger and thirst \"they did not find the way of the city of habitation, hungering and thirsting.\" There were many who kept promising that way which would lead to the city. Socrates promised, Plato promised, Aristotle promised. The woman with the issue of blood, who flowed with blood, sought many physicians, and by none could be healed. \"They did not find the way of the city of habitation.\" Their teaching produced hunger for the truth rather than fullness. The woman with the issue of blood spent all her substance on physicians: hungering and thirsting her soul failed within her. But because she had lost all her substance — that is, the Church gathered from the Gentiles, because her soul failed within her — she cried out to the Lord when she was in tribulation. The touch of the Lord's hem was the cry of one believing.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.6":["\"And they cried out to the Lord when they were in tribulation.\" \"To the Lord,\" he says, \"when I was in tribulation I cried out, and he heard me.\" Then are we heard by the Lord, when not a mere voice but tribulation cries out to the Lord.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.7":["\"And he led them into the right way.\" Because \"they did not find the way of the city of habitation,\" therefore \"he led them into the right way.\" So he shows that before they did indeed find ways, but crooked ones and not right. \"And he led them into the right way.\" The Way led them into a way. The Lord himself and Savior led them into the right way. The Way led them into the right way: he made them walk in himself, being himself both the leader and the way. \"And he led them into the right way\": that by this way, that is, by the Lord the Savior, they might go into the city of habitation, into his Church, which had been founded upon himself, both the rock and the way and the leader. And at the same time it is to be considered that in this psalm it is said a third time: \"Let them confess to the Lord his mercies, and his wonders to the sons of men. Because he satisfied the empty soul,\" and so on. Afterward it is written: \"Let them confess to the Lord his mercies, and his wonders to the sons of men: because he shattered the gates of bronze.\" And afterward this same thing is said: let the prudent reader find it. Though some say it is even written a fourth time: so that what is offered to the Trinity may be gathered into one in the Gospels.\n— Homily 33, on Psalm 106"],"PSA.107.23-24":["But there are on the contrary good ships too, of which it is said in the same psalms: Those who go down to the sea in ships and do business in many waters, they have seen the works of the Lord and his wonders in the deep (Ps 106 [107]:23). For those who are not idle in the waves of this world, but work, and carry the Lord's merchandise, and hasten to come to the harbor of rest, they see the works of the Lord and his wonders in the deep, when they have come to deep knowledge and, searching all things, that is, even the deep things of God, have beheld his wonders.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","That the world is called the sea (to pass over many things), I am content with a single testimony of the Psalm: They who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters, these have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep (Ps 106 [107]:23). For those who do the work of God in this world, and say with the Prophet, I have come into the depth of the sea, see his wonders in the deep; and, delivered from temptations and straits, they say that they have heard a terrible and hard vision.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Of this sea we also read in the psalms: They who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters, they have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep. Into this sea of the world, in which the number of the saints is hemmed in and set in tribulation, the apostles went down, and first of all the Lord, that he might free us from the bitter waves. Into this sea is cast the dragnet of the gospel, which is woven of the testimonies of the Scriptures, so that it may draw out into the free air many fish overwhelmed by the whirlpools of this world, that they may be able to sing praises to God. These are they who have gone down to the sea, and in the ships of the churches do many works, and behold the wonders of God in the depth of the riches of his wisdom and knowledge, and in the Holy Spirit, who searches even the deep things of God; to whom the Lord says: Come after me, and I will make you fishers of men.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.107.33-36":["These rivers, before the earth was moved, lay hidden, and while they were in the veins of the earth they offered no drink to the thirsty. But after the world and all the earth were shaken at the coming of Christ, they suddenly burst forth, and then was fulfilled: He made rivers into a desert, and the outgoings of waters into thirst, a fruitful land into saltness, because of the wickedness of those who dwell in it. He made the desert into pools of water, and the land without water into springs of water, and made the hungry dwell there, and they established a city to dwell in. For after the Lord came into the world, and that was fulfilled which he said in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world, that those who did not see might see, and those who saw might become blind, then the land of Israel, from which rivers formerly flowed and watered the whole people of Judea, dried up, and its springs were stopped. But the whole world, which was deserted and barren and did not have the waters of the Lord's preaching, was turned into pools of water, and as many teachers as it sent out, so many springs it also had. Nor is it enough for them, that is, for the springs and rivers, to water the peoples of the world; but in each province, gathering into one people those who were hungry and suffered famine of the Word of God, they built up the Church, which is called a city to dwell in, and which the rush of the river makes glad.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PSA.108.1":["A song of a psalm of David. A \"song\" always refers to the mind, but a \"psalm\" refers to bodily things. To put it more plainly, a song refers to contemplation (theoria), whereas a psalm refers to practice (praxis), that is, to action. Since, then, it is written in the title, \"A song of a psalm,\" rightly and consistently he says in the second verse, \"I will sing and I will make melody in my glory.\"\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107","\"My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready.\" I am ready not only in deed, but also in heart and mind. \"My heart is ready,\" in the mind; \"my heart is ready,\" in deed, that is, in practice. Since I am ready not only in heart and mind but also in deed, therefore in the second place I say, \"I will sing and I will make melody in my glory.\" \"I will sing\": that is, in my heart. \"And I will make melody.\" Just as a psaltery is composed of many strings, and if one string is broken the psaltery is ruined, so too in our actions, if we transgress a single commandment, our psaltery is broken. Therefore he said, \"I will sing and I will make melody in my glory.\" In other manuscripts it reads \"in your glory\"; but it is better to read according to the Hebrew \"in my glory.\" What he says is this: \"I will sing and I will make melody in my glory.\" This, O Lord, that I make melody to you, that I sing to you — I am doing no favor to you, but to myself. For in another psalm he says: \"For you have no need of my goods.\" What, therefore, I do, I do for myself. For the fact that I serve you is my glory. We fast: do we thereby bestow anything on God? We do this for our own sins. We lie in sackcloth: what do we thereby furnish to the Lord? This alone do we furnish to him, that we may save our own souls; for this is what he himself desires. Just as a good and merciful physician, if he has healed someone, rejoices himself, and so does the one who has been healed, so too our Lord.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107","And these things indeed we say according to the Jews. But according to the higher sense, it is the Lord who sings these things. \"My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready.\" I am ready here, I am ready in the future: I save both on earth and I save in heaven; that is, I save both the angels and men.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.2":["\"Arise, O psaltery and harp.\" O psaltery, O harp, you were made and created to make melody to God: arise and make melody, why do you lie idle? O monk, you who stand upright in body, why does your soul lie idle, and you do not make melody to the Lord? \"Cursed is the man who does the work of God negligently.\" If you are a psaltery, if you are a harp, why are you deaf, and do not glorify God? \"I will arise at daybreak.\" God cannot be blessed and praised in the darkness, but he is blessed in the light. I say a wondrous thing: even if we rise in the night, we bless God in the light. For a Christian it is never night: for a Christian the sun of righteousness always rises.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107","\"Arise, O psaltery and harp.\" The Lord speaks to his own body: O psaltery and harp, you who descended into hell and lay there, arise and praise the Lord. And you see what he says. It is not he himself, who moves the harp and psaltery, that praises; but the harp and psaltery themselves, they themselves bless the Father. For he is God, and is blessed and praised by others; but his body, and the man whom he took upon himself, this itself praises the Lord. And so that you may know that the prophet speaks in the person of the Lord, and that I do not say this of myself, see what follows.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.3":["\"I will confess to you among the peoples, O Lord.\" Understand \"the peoples\" as the Jews. \"And I will make melody to you among the nations.\" He speaks of us. For by \"the Jews\" he means those who first believed from among the Jews. And notice the order: first he praises God among the peoples, and afterward he praises God among the nations. For first they believed from among the Jews, and afterward they believed from among the Gentiles.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.4":["\"For your mercy is great above the heavens.\" There is no creature that does not stand in need of God's mercy. All of us stand in need of God's mercy. Gabriel, Michael, the Seraphim, the Cherubim, the Powers, the Dominions, are indeed holy, but they stand in need of the mercy of their creator. I do not disparage the angels, but I proclaim the creator; since the angels themselves gladly have their creator proclaimed. And we can say it another way: \"For your mercy raises up from the earth to the heavens. And your truth reaches even to the clouds.\" Where are the Jews, where are those like the Jews, who understand only according to the letter? How is the truth of God \"even to the clouds\"? The Son of God says, \"I am the way and the life and the truth\": and he descended to the earth, and dwelt among men. And how is your truth \"even to the clouds\"? The sense is plain, that he speaks of the apostles and of the saints, that they should not rain their rain upon the Jews, but upon the Gentiles, as it is written: \"I will command my clouds not to rain rain upon Israel.\" In these clouds, then, is the truth of God. But if the truth of God is in these clouds, how does Montanus say, and how do the Cataphrygians say, that what was not revealed to the apostles has been revealed to us?\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.5-6":["\"Be exalted above the heavens, O God.\" God is not exalted except above the heavens, he is not exalted above the earth. When we are earthly, we do not exalt God; but if from earthly we have been made heavenly, then we exalt God. And our Lord, when he was on earth, did not exalt us. Someone may say: You speak rashly, since he himself says in the Gospel: \"When I have been lifted up, I will draw all things to myself.\" See what he says: When I have been lifted up into the heavens, then I will draw all things to myself. This sense is indeed pious, but I understand something else. When I have been lifted up, when I have been crucified, then I will draw all to myself. \"That your beloved may be delivered.\" O wondrous understanding! for this reason the Lord is crucified and lifted up, that his beloved may be delivered. \"That your beloved may be delivered,\" he says, \"be exalted above the heavens, O God.\" What we asked for has come to pass: he has been exalted, and we have been delivered.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.7":["\"God has spoken in his holy one\": whether in the temple, or in each one of the saints, or in a prophet. He is heard in Moses, he is heard in an apostle; and more truly I say, he has spoken in his Son. What, then, has God spoken? These things that follow. \"I will be exalted, and I will divide Shechem.\" For this reason, he says, I am crucified, that I may divide Shechem. \"Shechem\" is interpreted in our language as \"shoulders.\" And \"shoulders\" are interpreted as \"works.\" The sense, then, is this: for this reason I am crucified, that I may divide my works among my saints; for this reason I am crucified, that I may lay my sweet yoke and my light burden upon the necks of men. You have done, O Lord, what you promised: you have been exalted, and you have divided Shechem. \"And I will measure out the valley of tabernacles.\" Truly our Lord and Savior entered down into this valley of tears, into the place of our lowliness, that is, into this world. However holy a man may be in this world, he does not yet know as he ought to know. \"For we know in part, and we prophesy in part.\"\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.8":["\"Gilead is mine, and Manasseh is mine.\" Our Lord and Savior rejoices, possessing greater riches. \"Gilead\" is interpreted \"migration\": he rejoices, therefore, when the people migrated from among the Gentiles and came to him. \"Manasseh\" is interpreted \"from forgetfulness.\" See, then, what he says. That son who received the substance and consumed it, and had forgotten me, now has remembered me; and he is called Manasseh, that is, \"from forgetfulness,\" because he has not forgotten, but has returned to his father. And \"Ephraim\" is interpreted \"fruitful.\" \"Ephraim,\" he says, \"is the receiving of my head.\" He has here put \"receiving\" for \"crown\": for that which is placed upon the head is a crown, and in the Scriptures the crown is called by various names. In another place it has: \"His truth will surround you with a shield.\" And in another psalm: \"Who crowns you in mercy and compassions.\" For just as there he put \"shield\" for \"crown,\" so too here he has put \"receiving\" for \"crown.\" See, then, what he says. You have become Gilead, you have become Manasseh, that is, you have migrated to the Lord, and after forgetfulness you have returned to your father: be Ephraim, be fruitful, have good works, and afterward you will be the crown of the Lord.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107","\"Judah is my king.\" See in how great glory he is who does penance: for \"Judah\" is interpreted \"confession.\"\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.9":["\"Moab is the pot of my hope.\" A great mystery is recognized in few words. I beg you, pay careful attention: since it profits both me who speak, and you who hear. \"Moab\" is interpreted \"from the father.\" \"AB\" means \"father,\" \"MO\" means \"from.\" Who is this one \"from the father\"? He who lost his father, who abandoned his father, who did not return to his own father, and is not worthy to be called Manasseh. Who is this Moab? An enemy of the Lord, of whose lineage, even to the fourth generation, and the tenth, and even forever, he shall not enter into the church of the Lord. Who is this Moab? He who is born of an unclean coupling in a dark cave. For from Lot and from his daughters two were born, Moab and Ammon, and Moab is the elder son. See, then, how a mystery is hidden in the letter. In darkness, in a cave: not in the sun, not in the light. \"Moab,\" then, is interpreted \"the devil,\" who has forgotten his father. In fine, Absalom too, who persecuted his own father, is interpreted \"the devil.\" How, then, is this Moab \"the pot of the Lord's hope\"? \"Pot\" (lebes) is interpreted \"cooking-pot\" (olla). How is a cooking-pot the hope of the Lord? Is it, then, credible that Moab, who is interpreted \"the devil,\" should be the pot of the Lord's hope? How is he the hope of the Lord? The Apostle says: \"Whom I have handed over to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme.\" The devil is, as it were, the Lord's torturer-examiner. Those who do not walk rightly toward God are handed over to the devil. Why? That they may perish forever? And where then is the clemency of God? where is the merciful father? where is he who makes his sun rise over the just and sinners, and rains upon sinners and the just? What he says is this: I hand over, he says, sinners to the devil, that, being tormented by him, they may be converted to me. It is said also in Jeremiah: \"What do you see, Jeremiah?\" \"A boiling pot,\" he says. See, then, that into this pot flesh is cast. This is written in Ezekiel. \"And I cast,\" he says, \"flesh into the pot\": that it may become soft, and the hard may be converted to the Lord, and from a heart of stone may become a heart of flesh, so that where before it could not be written upon because of hardness, afterward it may be written upon because of softness. \"I will kindle,\" he says, \"this pot, and I will boil down the flesh and the bones, until they lose all moisture and filth.\" And see what is said afterward. \"And let them learn,\" he says, \"that I am the Lord.\" Great mercy, great sacrament. The flesh is cast into the pot, that it may be boiled down and may learn that he is the Lord. There are many things that might be said about Moab and the pot, but for now it would be too long.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107","\"Into Idumea I will stretch out my shoe.\" \"Idumea\" is interpreted \"earthly and bloody.\" The merciful God wills to possess not only heavenly things, but also earthly ones. \"The foreigners have been made friends to me.\" He speaks of us, who have believed from among the Gentiles.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.10":["\"Who will lead me into the fortified city?\" No one, O Lord, for you are the one who says: \"I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me.\" \"For all have turned aside, together they have become useless: there is none that does good, there is not even one.\" Who, then, will lead me into the fortified city? No one, O Lord: since even Peter, who had promised to die for you, afterward denied you. What is this fortified city? Hell, where they are shut in, from which it is not permitted to go out. And truly from this city no other victor came out except the Lord. And truly this city is fortified. Moses could not come out of this city. And Jacob likewise entered into this city, and did not return. Do you wish to know that he did not return? In fine, he says to his sons: \"Mourning I will descend to the netherworld.\" But he descended to the netherworld, because paradise had not yet been opened by the thief. This city is fortified against going out, but it is open for entering in. \"Who will lead me even into Idumea?\" Where is the place of punishments and of blood. For \"Idumea\" is interpreted \"bloody.\" The place of the slaying of souls, where many souls are slain. For no one descended a victor into hell, except you alone, O Lord.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.11":["\"You who have cast us off.\" Was it not you, O God, who have cast us off? He did not say, \"Who cast us off,\" but \"Who once cast us off.\" \"And will you not go out, O God, in our powers?\" These things are to be read interrogatively: \"And will you not go out, O God, in our powers?\" You who suffered for us, why do you not help us, but allow us to be trampled by the most cruel devil? \"And will you not go out, O God, in our powers?\" See what he says. He did not say, \"you did not go out,\" but \"will you not go out, O God, in our powers.\" This he means to say: When we labor, then God helps us. \"For it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who has mercy.\" See what he says: Is it not of him who lies idle, nor of him who sleeps, but of God who has mercy? But see what he says: It is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who has mercy. If, then, we do not will and run, God will profit us nothing. It is ours to will and to run, and afterward God has mercy. For a sleeping athlete loses the victory.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.12":["\"Give us help from tribulation.\" He did not say, \"Give us help from delights,\" but \"from tribulation.\" \"To the Lord, when I was in tribulation, I cried out, and he heard me.\" He did not say, \"when I was in delights I cried out,\" or \"sleeping I cried out.\" Do you wish to be heard by the Lord? Be in tribulation: cry out, and you will be heard. If, then, anyone is not in tribulation, he cannot call upon the help of the Lord. \"For vain is the salvation of man.\" \"Cursed is the man who has his hope in man.\" There is no need to interpret this: this saying is plain to all. Someone hoped in an emperor, hoped in a prince, in a father, or a mother: in the course of some affair the spirit of the one in whom he hoped was suddenly taken away, and his salvation vanished. He did not say, \"vain is the salvation of that man in whom he hopes,\" but \"the salvation of him who hopes in man.\"\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.108.13":["\"In God we will do valiantly.\" Let us have hope in nothing except in God alone. Let us not say: If this should happen to me, or that, whence shall I have the means to live? I answer you: what is greater, if persecution should come, whence do you have the means to live? For a Christian there is always persecution, always nakedness is set before him. No one, then, ought to make arrangements about this life, let no one fear, let no one say: If I grow old, whence shall I have the means to live? if I become sick, whence do I live? You have Christ, and do you fear? If he feeds the birds of the sky, do you doubt that he can feed you? The devil feeds his own men, and shall Christ not feed his own servants? The devil makes the judges of this world and the rich, and can Christ not make it so that you do not beg? The devil gives gold and gems, and can Christ not give bread? Let us cast all anxiety out of our hearts, and let us say: \"In God we will do valiantly.\" He himself will be our valor, he himself our bread, he himself will be our ruler. \"And he himself will bring our enemies to nothing.\" He himself crowns us, he himself fights alongside us: or rather, he himself conquers in us. For what do we say? \"In God we will do valiantly.\" For when we are strong, he himself will bring our enemies to nothing. He did not say \"he will fight,\" or \"he will slay,\" but \"he will bring to nothing.\" He will bring them to nothing for this reason, that, if he should wish, he may make them strong, and glorify them. Let us, therefore, bless God: to whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"PSA.109.1":["To the end, a psalm of David. When it says \"to the end,\" let us understand that it is spoken not of the present, but of the future. And if it is spoken of the future, then the prophecy is about Christ.\n\nO God, do not keep silent about my praise. That is: Judas betrayed me, the Jews persecuted me and crucified me, and they thought that they would destroy me utterly, to the end. But you, O God, do not keep silent about my praise. All the churches, day by day, praise the Lord, and the prayer of the Lord has been fulfilled, when he said: O God, do not keep silent about my praise. See of what dignity the priests are. The priests speak: and in the priests God does not keep silent about the praise of his Son.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.2":["For the mouth of the sinner and the mouth of the deceitful man has been opened against me. Let us see how the mouth of Judas the betrayer was opened against the Lord. He comes to the Lord in order to betray him, and he says: Rabbi — that is, master. Behold, the mouth of the sinner and the mouth of the deceitful man. I in my clemency wish to save him, and I offer him a kiss, and I wish to overcome his malice; yet he persists in doing evil, so as to betray me. In a particular sense it is understood of Judas; but in a general sense of the Jews. In that time in which they were saying, \"Crucify, crucify such a man,\" the mouth of the sinner was opened against the Lord. And that you may see that he speaks of all the Jews, you will recognize it from what follows. For what does he say? They have spoken against me with a deceitful tongue. I was being crucified for them, and they were mocking me. My wounds were their medicine, and I seemed to them a thing of derision.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.3":["And they have surrounded me with words of hatred. Not that they might believe, but that they might war against me. And they have warred against me without cause. \"Without cause\" is understood in two ways. That is, without reason: for I had not sinned, and I did not deserve that they should make war upon me. And what worthy cause was there that they should war against me? Because I had healed their sick? because I had raised their dead? because I had done other signs? See therefore that they warred against him without cause. And it is understood in another way as well: They wished to extinguish and blot out my name, and yet they were not able. When someone persecutes another, and gains nothing, and does not fulfill his desire, he persecutes without cause.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.4":["Instead of loving me, they disparaged me: but I kept praying. In return for the benefits which I had conferred upon them, they were doing me insults. But what was I doing? I kept praying. These are the weapons of the Lord: and these are our weapons too, prayer. So then, whenever anyone persecutes us, and hates us, let us say: Instead of loving me, they disparaged me. But what was I doing? I kept praying. That I might overcome them? Far from it: for whom did the Lord pray that he might overcome? But what did he say — but I kept praying? What was I saying? \"Father, forgive them: for they know not what they do.\"\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.5":["And they set against me evils in return for goods. And set upon the cross, I kept praying for those who were crucifying me, while they were saying: \"Aha, you who destroy the temple, and in three days build it up. Let him save himself. Let the Father who loves you come now and set you free.\" I rose again from the dead for their sakes, and they say: \"His disciples came by night, and stole him away.\" And hatred in return for my love. Consider, O Christian, consider, O monk: if the Lord received his persecutor and betrayer with a kiss, and prays for those who were crucifying him, what ought we to do for our brothers?\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.6":["Set a sinner over him. Since I prayed for them upon the cross, and they saw my wounds — I gave them room for repentance and they do not repent, and they refuse to have me as king — let them have the devil as king. They will not have Jesus as king: let them have Barabbas as king, Barabbas, which is interpreted \"son of the father,\" that is, of the devil. This Barabbas, who was he? A seditious man and a robber. And truly they have Barabbas as king even to the present day, a seditious man and a robber, who slays the Jews themselves. Barabbas the robber, who delights in blood, before the Lord was crucified, before the Jews crucified him, was shut up in prison. They denied the Lord, and Barabbas was released. For from that time Barabbas began to have power over them; and that demon, who had been cast out of the man, and out of that generation, and was walking about in the desert, and could find no place, brought with him seven other demons worse than himself, and entered into the synagogue, and the last state of that man became worse than the first. In short, the synagogue of the Jews, before it crucified the Lord, was indeed a sinner, but it had only one demon. It crucified the Lord, and eight demons entered into it at once. So then was fulfilled what he said: Set a sinner over him.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108","And let the devil stand at his right hand. Because he stood at their right hand, therefore the synagogue had a withered right hand, as it is written in the Gospel. But let us pray that the Lord may stand at our right hand, and the devil at our left. It is written in Zechariah: \"And he showed me Jesus the great priest standing before the angel of the Lord, and Satan stood at his right hand, to fight with him.\" If, then, the devil stands at the right hand of the Lord, how does he now say of Judas, And let the devil stand at his right hand? But see what he says. The devil stood at the right hand of Jesus to oppose him: that is, he wished to be an adversary to his right hand. But here he does not say that he wishes to fight, but that he had already conquered Judas. There the devil desires to fight against the right hand; but here he has gained mastery over the right hand of Judas. But this too must be considered. At that time he stood at the Lord's right hand to oppose him, when Jesus had filthy garments. For so it is written there as well: \"And Jesus had filthy garments.\" As long as the Lord Jesus has filthy garments, so long does the devil stand at his right hand: as long as he bears our sins, in our sins the devil opposes him. But when it is said in Zechariah, \"Take away his filthy garments and clothe him with the changes of raiment,\" immediately Jesus says to the devil, \"The Lord rebuke you, Satan, may he rebuke you who has chosen Jerusalem.\" All this we have said for this reason, because it is written, And let the devil stand at his right hand.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.7":["When he is judged, let him come forth condemned. Let him come not into judgment, but into condemnation. For where there is judgment, there is doubt: but where there is condemnation, the sentence is already plain. And let his prayer be turned into sin. The repentance of Judas was made a worse sin. How was the repentance of Judas made a worse sin? He went and perished by hanging; and he who became the betrayer of the Lord, this same man came to be his own slayer. In view of the clemency of the Lord I say this, that by this he offended the Lord more, because he hanged himself, than because he betrayed the Lord. His prayer ought to have turned into repentance, and it was turned into sin. You see a heretic praying, you see a Jew, you see a Manichaean: though they fast, though they pray, yet their prayer is turned into sin.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108","But they said: What is that to us? You see to it. And throwing down the pieces of silver in the temple he withdrew, and going away he hanged himself with a noose. It profited him nothing to have repented, since by that repentance he could not correct the crime. If ever a brother so sins against a brother that he is able to amend what he has sinned, it can be forgiven him; but if the works remain, repentance is taken up in vain by the voice. This is what is said in the psalm about this same most unhappy Judas: And let his prayer become sin, so that not only was he unable to amend the wickedness of the betrayal, but to the former crime he added also the charge of murdering himself. The Apostle too says something of the kind in the second letter to the Corinthians: Lest the brother be swallowed up by excessive sorrow.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.109.8":["Let his days be few. His days were made few, but his nights increased: for he was cut off in the middle of his days. The sun withdrew from him, and behold, darkness was multiplied for him. And let another receive his episcopate. This too is interpreted more plainly in the Acts of the Apostles. But it was not only spoken of Judas in that time, it is spoken even to this day. If Judas lost his apostleship, let priests and bishops guard themselves, lest they too lose their own priesthood. If an apostle fell, a monk can fall more easily. The power does not perish, though the man falls and perishes. Nevertheless the Lord gives out his denarii at interest: if that man does not double them, his money is taken away and given to him who has. The Lord's money cannot be idle.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.9":["Let his children become orphans, and his wife a widow. See that the things which are spoken are mysteries. Whom do you suppose to be the children of Judas? The Jews. And they are called Jews, not from that holy Judah, but from this betrayer. From that holy one, we are the spiritual Jews; but from this betrayer, those are the carnal Jews. In short, there are two apostles who are called by the name of Judas. There is the eleventh apostle, who is called Judas of James. And there is the twelfth and the last, who is called Judas Iscariot. From that Judas of James, then, we are called Jews; but from this Iscariot, those are called Jews. That Judas our father is called \"of James,\" that is, Judah the son of the supplanter: for we have supplanted the people of the Jews, and have received their birthright. But those are called \"Iscariot,\" which is uncertain whether it signifies his father or his village. But however you interpret it, it is interpreted \"wages\" and \"price.\" Therefore this Judas, who was going to betray the Lord and to receive a price, received his name from the price. Though he returned the price to the priests of the Jews, yet they condemn him, since they refuse to receive the price of blood. See the disagreement of the Pharisees and the misfortune of Judas. If you bought the blood of Jesus rightly, why did you not take back your money? But if you refuse to take it because it is the price of blood, you show that you yourselves have shed the blood unjustly and wickedly. But see what they do. That very money, he says, they do not put into the corban, but they buy a field with it, and they call it Aceldama, which is interpreted \"the price of blood.\" Why do they buy it? For the burial of strangers. Blessed are we, for whom a field is bought from the price of the Lord's blood.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108","And his wife a widow. The synagogue, who first was made the wife of God, of whom he said, \"And I gave her a writing of divorce, and again I took her back, and I said to her, Turn to me\": that synagogue, then, received her divorce from the Savior, and was made the wife of Judas the betrayer. In short, what did she do? She received no dowry, but gave a dowry to her husband. And again that most wretched Judas, who betrayed the Lord, will not receive an evil dowry; but he returns to the harlot, his wife, the evil dowry which he received from her. And what does he say? You displeased your former husband, he repudiated you and cast you out, and gave you a writing of divorce: I refuse to have you as wife. You gave me a price, and you deceived me: and you who killed your Lord, you have killed me too. For I cannot find in Scripture that Judas had either a wife or children.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.10":["Let his children be carried away wandering, and let them beg. Consider carefully: for indeed the things that follow are difficult. From that time in which the Lord was crucified, from then on the Jews are ever moved about, they do not stand in their own place. In short, it is said of Cain also: \"And Cain, he says, went out from the face of God, and dwelt in the land of Nod.\" Now Nod is interpreted \"commotion.\" Because Cain had gone out from the face of God, rightly he did not stand still, but dwelt in the land of Nod, that is, in commotion. So then these too, since they crucified their God and Lord, are carried away wandering. For they are not in their own places, but are scattered throughout the whole world. Here he speaks of a begging for spiritual riches. For they do not have prophets, they do not have the law, they do not have priesthood, they do not have sacrifice, but they have truly been made beggars.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.11":["Let the moneylender search out all his substance. You gave him money, O Lord, you gave to him as also to the others. But what did he do? He did not bind it up in a napkin, nor bury it in the earth, nor was he negligent: but when he had the Lord's talent and money, he took a price from the enemies of the Lord, and sold his own Lord. Therefore I say to you, O Lord, exact from him the interest of your money. And let strangers plunder his labors. The Lord manifestly proved to be a moneylender, and what happened? Let strangers plunder his labors. Whether we understand it generally of all the Jews, because they were laid waste by the Romans: or truly in a particular sense, that Judas was plundered by the demons, and the people itself.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.12":["Let there be no helper for him, nor let there be any who will pity his orphans. In that time the people of the Jews had no helper. Answer me, O Jews. You were in Babylon: there you had prophets, Daniel, Ezekiel: and indeed you were idolaters. For seventy years you were in captivity, and afterward you came back into your own land. Behold, now it is four hundred years: why has no prophet been sent to you? In that time you worshiped idols, and God had pity on you. But now you do not worship idols: why has the Lord forsaken you? Behold, it is four hundred years. But then you forsook the Lord, and worshiped idols: whereas now you have crucified and slain the Lord. And therefore there will be no helper for them.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.13":["In one generation let his name be blotted out. O prophecy, O mystery of prophecy! In one generation let his name be blotted out. After the passion of the Lord, forty-two years passed, and that people was blotted out. Indeed the full age comes in the fortieth year. So then, after the passion of the Lord, in the forty-second year Jerusalem was blotted out. See therefore that in one generation his name was blotted out. But in this too is the clemency of the Lord. They crucified him, and he gave them forty-two years for repentance. I say it plainly: he slew them after forty-two years for this reason, because of those who crucified the Lord, none of them remained. Judas is cursed, so that in Judas the Jews may be cursed.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.14":["Let the iniquity of his fathers come into remembrance in the sight of the Lord. They sinned in the desert, he forgave them: now he reckons against them what they sinned then, since they have crucified the Lord. Or let us interpret it otherwise. He reckons against them the cruelty of their murders, and forgives them nothing. And let the sin of his mother not be blotted out. Who is the mother of Judas? Jerusalem, who rejoices in blood, who slew the prophets and her own Lord himself.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.16":["Because he did not remember to show mercy. See how great the clemency of the Lord is. He sees him coming with the officers, he sees him coming with clubs, and he offers him a kiss. That man comes to betray, and the Lord gives a kiss; so that he who did not fear his master might at least be overcome by clemency.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108","And he persecuted a man needy and a beggar. \"Who, though he was rich, for our sake became poor.\" The Lord calls himself needy and a beggar: and who glories in his riches? You have consolation, O poor: for the Lord is poor with you. And to put to death one pricked at heart. What is it that he said, \"one pricked at heart, to put to death\"? What he also said in the Gospel: \"My soul is sorrowful even unto death.\" And again when he said: \"Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me.\" Otherwise: this grieved me, and I was made sorrowful for those persecuting me, because they refused to repent. I was hanging on the cross, and washing away their filth with my blood: and they themselves refused to repent. These were my tears, this my compunction, that those who persecuted me I could not save.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.22":["Deliver me, for I am needy and poor. The Lord calls himself poor, that he might console the poor. With men he was made poor, that no one of the poor might despair of salvation. And my heart is troubled within me. See what he says: And my heart is troubled within me. This is the same thing which he says elsewhere: \"I was troubled, and did not speak.\" That I am troubled, belongs to a man: but to overcome the trouble and the anger, this belongs to great virtue. Therefore the apostle also says: \"Be angry, and do not sin.\" Are you angry? You are a man. Does the anger not remain in you? You are a Christian.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.23":["I am shaken off like locusts. I indeed had come to protect my people, and I said: \"Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kill the prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often did I wish to gather your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings.\" I had come like a hen to protect them: but they received me with a most hostile mind. I had come as a mother: and they slew me as if I were their murderer. I am shaken off. What is this? They persecuted me, they cast me out. They persecuted me from Nazareth, I came to Capernaum: they persecuted me from Capernaum, I came to Bethsaida, and from there they persecuted me. I came to Jerusalem, I was unwilling to withdraw from my people; and from there they persecuted me, and so they treated me like locusts. And what did I do? Was I angry, did I defend myself, did I curse them, and abandon them? Not one of these things did I do. But what did I do? I prayed for them.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.24":["My knees are weakened from fasting. Take consolation, O monk, in fasting: since the Lord too did this. I say that when a monk fasts, he becomes stronger through fasting; and when his knees are weakened by fasting, then he is most of all strengthened. And my flesh is changed on account of oil. I seem to be saying a difficult thing, and I interpret it according to the letter, that I may edify my hearers: for even the letter edifies me. It is good to fast, it is good to flee from delicacies. Before I come to the spiritual understanding, I linger in the letter. He who says that he does not eat oil, is far from the rest of the delicacies. All this I have said, because he said, And my flesh is changed on account of oil. This I have interpreted otherwise. But what he says is this. Oil is an image of brightness; oil is, as it were, the food of light. So then the Jews too received oil from me, they received life, they received light. Let no one suppose that he says \"oil\" for mercy: in the Hebrew it signifies intelligible oil. It is changed not into joy, but into sadness; not that I might be found more beautiful, but that I might appear more disfigured. Therefore he says: \"We saw him, and he had no beauty.\" Why? because his flesh was changed on account of oil. He had not the delights of the body, but of the spirit. And we too, then, when we fast, when our faces are pale, when we appear disfigured, let us recognize that we then appear more beautiful to Christ. Such soldiers Christ loves, who give themselves to fasting. Our provision and our food is fasting. Why? because in fasting is victory, and in victory is triumph. In short, the apostle too glories not in fullness, but in weakness. \"For when I am weak, then I am stronger.\"\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.25":["And I have become a reproach to them. For they called me a Samaritan, and said: \"By Beelzebub, the prince of demons, he casts out demons.\" \"Is not this the son of Joseph the carpenter? are not his brothers and sisters all with us?\" And again: \"You who destroy the temple, and build it in three days.\" And again: \"Let us see whether Elijah comes to save him.\" He was calling out \"Eli, Eli,\" which is \"My God, my God\"; and they thought it was Elijah. See the untutored Jews. Did the Son of God ask for the help of Elijah? They saw me, and they shook their heads. Why did they shake their heads? because they had already moved their feet as well.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.26":["Save me according to your mercy. He says also in the Gospel: \"I have power to lay down my soul and to take it up again.\" And again: \"No one takes it from me.\" And how does he ask for mercy? He himself asks for mercy, and he himself bestows it; he himself as man asks, and as God bestows.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.27":["And let them know that this is your hand, and you, O Lord, have made it. What is it that he says, And let them know that this is your hand, and you, O Lord, have made it? Let the Jews understand that they did not prevail against me by their own strength: but my will and your will was done, that I should suffer. And I willed to suffer; therefore I say, according to what I am as man: \"My God, I willed to do your will.\" And your will was also mine, that I should suffer. It was not, then, their treacheries and strength that brought it about: but I and you willed that I should suffer. For you struck your shepherd, and the sheep were scattered. This is written in Zechariah: \"I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.\" That I should suffer, was my will and yours. What you willed, I too willed. The scandal had to come, but woe to him through whom it comes.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.28":["They will curse, and you will bless. To this day this happens: they curse in the synagogue, and the Lord blesses in the church. Those who rise up against me, let them be confounded: but your servant will rejoice. They rise up against me: but I, your servant, will rejoice. But why \"your servant\"? because \"He who, though he was in the form of God, did not count it robbery to be equal with God: but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant.\"\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.29":["Let those who disparage me be clothed with shame. It profits us greatly to understand what we say. The clemency of God appears in this, in his saying \"let them be clothed.\" He does not say, let them perish; he does not say, let them be blotted out forever: but what does he say? let them be confounded, that is, let them understand their error, and be confounded, and stand in repentance. He who is clothed with shame is near to salvation: for if anyone is truly confounded, he begins to repent. Consider, O monks, if you ever have anything against your brother, you ought always to pray to God for him. The Lord is crucified, and he prays for those crucifying him. But you, if you hear an evil word, do not hold on to anger. \"Let not the sun set upon your wrath.\" And let them be covered with their confusion as with a double cloak (diplois). The diplois is a kind of cloak: indeed, in the Hebrew it is called ME'IL. It is called diplois for this reason, because the cloak is double. Therefore I say to you, O Lord: my enemies are the Jews, they have persecuted me, they have crucified me, they have done me every evil, they have blasphemed me: be gracious to them, since I, who have suffered, pray for them. And what do I ask? Let them blush and be confounded, and so be saved. They are naked, they have lost my tunic. Give them confusion, give them disgrace, and let them have a double cloak: that is, let them not have a single confusion, but a double one, that they may repent the more. He does not speak against the Jews, but he speaks for them. For he is the Creator both of them and of us: he has not rooted them out altogether. Do you wish to know? Peter was of them, Paul was of them, and all the apostles were of them. We have been grafted into their root; we are the branches, they the root. We ought not to curse the roots, but we ought to pray for our roots. We who have been grafted into the root, let us beseech the Lord, that just as the branches are saved, so also the root may be saved. Perhaps someone may say: You pray for the Jews, for blasphemers? I pray to the Lord for them: for if he himself has pity on his persecutors, how much more will he have pity on me?\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.30":["I will confess to the Lord exceedingly with my mouth. This is said after the resurrection, and it is sung of the future church. And in the midst of many I will praise him. For not in Judea alone, but in the whole world, in the church I will praise you.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.109.31":["Because he stood at the right hand of the poor man. The devil stood at the right hand of Judas: but for me the Lord stood at my right hand. That he might save my soul from those who persecute it. My soul, he says, he will save. And elsewhere he says: \"My soul is sorrowful even unto death.\" He who is sorrowful, had a faculty of feeling. For a soul without feeling has no perception, a soul without feeling has neither perception nor pain: for where there is pain and sadness, there is feeling. If then he had sadness, if he had pain, then he had also feeling: for feeling is pain.\n\nIf, then, they should wish to say to us: We do not say that he had feeling, for this reason, lest he seem to have sin; let us answer them: Did he have a body like us, or did he not have one? If they say, he had one, let us answer them: Then he had also the sufferings of our body. Everyone understands what I say. But if they deny that he had the passions or lusts of the body, then we say to them, that he did not have a body either. And let us say to them: Just as he had a true body like ours, and yet did not have the sin of the body; so also he had a true soul, and did not have the sin of the soul. Let us therefore confess that he had a true body and a true soul. For if the Lord did not take upon himself all the things that are man's, he did not save man. But if he took a body, and did not take a soul: then he saved the body, but he did not save the soul. Yet we wish rather that our soul be saved than our body. The Lord therefore took upon himself both body and soul, that he might save both, that he might save the whole man, just as he also created him. If, then, they say that the Lord did not take on feeling for this reason, that he might not sin, and they suppose this, that whoever has had human feeling cannot conquer it, let them tell us: Did John the Baptist have feeling, or did he not? In that time, in which he was in the desert, did he sin? Assuredly they say that he did not sin. Indeed, our Lord too says: \"Amen I say to you, there has not been among those born of women a greater than John the Baptist.\" Did Peter have feeling, or did he not, and the rest of the holy apostles? If, then, the apostles conquered human feeling, did the Lord fear to take on feeling, lest he be conquered by it? And while they wish to glorify the Lord, they disparage him. How do they disparage him? by making the Lord seem to fear what the apostles did not fear. Let us therefore give thanks to almighty God, and say: In your feeling you had no sin. And: \"The prince of this world came, and found nothing in you.\" So then grant to us, that just as in your own feeling you conquered the devil, so also in our feeling conquer the devil; that, just as you created us perfect, so also perfect you may save us. For you are the Creator, you are the master, you are the Lord, you are also the one who suffered for us: who live and reign for ever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 35, on Psalm 108"],"PSA.110.1":["The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand. A Psalm of David. It is David who speaks, a prophet, a holy man, a king. So what does the king say? What does the king and prophet say? The Lord said to my Lord. The Savior expounded this passage in the Gospel, saying: \"If Christ is the son of David,\" he says, \"how does David in the Spirit call him Lord?\" You see, then, what he is saying. If he is David's son, how do you say it — how does David in the Spirit call him Lord? Someone may say: So then he is not the son of David? He was saying this about the Pharisees, who confessed Christ to be merely the son of David. To the Jew and to Photinus I say this very thing: If he is the son of David, how does David call his own son Lord? But the one who confesses him to be Lord and God, and the Son of God — for instance the Manichaean, who denies that he is the son of David — before that man I prove that he is the son of David. To us, then, the one who is the Son of God, he himself is also the son of David: not one son here and another son there; I do not make two persons out of God and man; but the very one who is the Son of God, he himself is also the son of David. The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand. In the Hebrew the first name of \"Lord\" is written with those letters with which it is written only of God. But the second name of \"Lord\" is written with those letters with which men are accustomed to call one \"lord\": just as one is called \"lord\" by a servant, and kings are called \"lords.\" See, then, what he says. The Lord said to my Lord: to this Lord, who is commanded to sit. God does not sit; it is the assumption of a body that sits. To this one, then, it is commanded that he sit — the one who is man, who was assumed. This we say against the Arians, and against those who say: Greater is the Father, who bids him sit, than the one who is bidden. All the Gospels ring out concerning the person of the man, and now no proof is needed: because everything whatsoever that seems lowly is referred to the man, is referred to the flesh. Now let us speak about the psalm.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109","Now when the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them, saying: What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? They say to him: David's. He says to them: How then does David in the Spirit call him Lord, saying: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet. Those who had gathered to tempt Jesus, and were striving to catch the truth with a deceitful question, provided the occasion for their own refutation, and they are asked about the Christ, whose son he is. The question of Jesus profits us against the Jews even to this day. For these too, who confess that the Christ is to come, assert that he is a mere man and a holy man of the line of David. Let us therefore, taught by the Lord, ask them: If he is a mere man and only the son of David, how does David call him his Lord, not in the uncertainty of error nor by his own will, but in the Holy Spirit? Now the testimony that he set down is taken from Psalm 109 (110). David's Lord, therefore, he is called not according to that by which he was born of him, but according to that by which, born of the Father, he always was, going before the very father of his flesh. The Jews, to make sport of the truth of the question, invent many frivolous things, asserting that it was Abraham's home-born servant, whose son was Eliezer of Damascus, and that the psalm was written in his person, because after the slaughter of the five kings the Lord God said to his lord Abraham: Sit at my right hand until I make all your enemies the footstool of your feet. Let us ask them: How did God say to Abraham the words that follow: With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the morning star I begot you; and: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, and let us compel them to answer how Abraham was begotten before the morning star and was a priest according to the order of Melchizedek, for whom Melchizedek offered bread and wine, and from whom he received tithes of the spoil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.110.2":["The Lord shall send forth the rod of your power out of Sion. \"The rod.\" In the Hebrew it has not so much \"rod\" as \"scepter,\" that is, the emblem of royal power. The Lord shall send forth the rod of your power out of Sion. \"For out of Sion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord out of Jerusalem.\" Rule in the midst of your enemies. He did not say, Kill your enemies. But what? Rule in the midst of your enemies. Make your enemies, who had been strangers, begin to be yours. Rule in the midst of your enemies. Your enemies, of whom it is written in the sixty-seventh psalm: \"The tongue of your dogs from the enemies, from him.\" These are the enemies, who were in another's power. Now, then, the psalmist prays for this: rule over your enemies, deign to be the Lord of your enemies.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109"],"PSA.110.3-4":["The Jews, to make sport of the truth of the question, invent many frivolous things, asserting that it was Abraham's home-born servant, whose son was Eliezer of Damascus, and that the psalm was written in his person, because after the slaughter of the five kings the Lord God said to his lord Abraham: Sit at my right hand until I make all your enemies the footstool of your feet. Let us ask them: How did God say to Abraham the words that follow: With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the morning star I begot you; and: The Lord has sworn and will not repent: You are a priest for ever according to the order of Melchizedek, and let us compel them to answer how Abraham was begotten before the morning star and was a priest according to the order of Melchizedek, for whom Melchizedek offered bread and wine, and from whom he received tithes of the spoil.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.110.3":["With you is the beginning in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints. The little verse is obscure. Let us see the day of the Lord's power, so that when we have known the day, then we may know what the \"beginning\" is, and how this beginning is with him and has been with him; how this very beginning is in the day of his power, in the splendors of the saints. This beginning, then, which is with the Lord in the day of his power, is with the Lord for this reason: that the saints may shine. With you is the beginning in the day of your power. \"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. This was in the beginning with God.\" The Father is a beginning, but the Son too is a beginning. For a beginning does not have a beginning: for if it should have another beginning, then it itself ceases to be a beginning. Whatever, therefore, we grant to the Father, this we grant also to the Son. For if the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father, and all things of the Father are the Son's, and all things of the Son are the Father's, then the beginning of the Father is the beginning of the Son. Therefore what he says is this: At that time when you were suffering and were saying \"Father, into your hands I commend my spirit,\" and you were speaking as it were out of the affection of a man, you were not making request as though weak. For with you was the beginning, with you was the divinity: but you besought help for this reason, that having received help you might give it to your saints, and they might shine. As for this that is said, \"having received you might give\" — let it be a stumbling-block to no one: he speaks according to the man. For if he assumed the body of a man, it is necessary that he also take on the words of a man. Christ is man, and truly man: we say this according to the assumption of flesh. If, then, he is man according to the body, you see the body, and you are not scandalized: and do you marvel if he speaks with the words of a man? But if the lowliness of the body scandalizes you, and the cross, and the injuries, and the blows, and the scourges, and every outrage of the cross — if this scandalizes you, return to the beginning, and you are not scandalized. For the Father says to the Son: From the womb before the morning star I begot you. Do you marvel if our Lord and Savior, who truly assumed a man, speaks according to the man, when God the Father himself, who did not assume a man, speaks as it were as a man, \"From the womb before the morning star I begot you\"? I have no womb: but I cannot otherwise show myself to be a Father, unless I have spoken with the words of a man. From the womb before the morning star I begot you. \"From the womb,\" from my substance. \"From the womb.\" Hear, Eunomius, hear, Arius: if the Lord is a creature, if he is a thing made, does a craftsman ever beget his work? does a craftsman ever call his work a son? A carpenter-craftsman made a bench: did he beget it from his womb? does he say to it, \"From my womb I begot you, O bench\"? By no means does he say this. But what does he say? \"I made you with my hand.\" But when a \"womb\" is spoken of, a son is signified, and a son not adopted. For adopted ones too are called sons. \"For as many as received him, he gave them power to become sons of God.\" There he gave power: but here he begot from the womb, from his own nature, from his own inward parts, from his own substance. \"From the womb,\" that is, from the marrow of his divinity. The whole of whatever the Father is in divinity, he gave to the Son whom he begot. From the womb before the morning star I begot you. \"Before the morning star.\" This figure is called in Greek synecdoche (συνεκδοχή), which the grammarians call \"the whole from a part\" (ἀπὸ μέρους τὸ πᾶν). So when it says \"before the morning star,\" it names one creature, and it indicates all creatures. When it says \"before the morning star,\" let us understand it thus, as though it had said, before the moon, before the sun, before every creature. From the womb before the morning star I begot you. \"Before the morning star.\" Before that morning star which is seen in the world, the true light was begotten. Hence he says in another passage of the psalm: \"In your light we shall see light.\" The speech is to the Father: O Father, in the light of the Son we shall see the light, the Holy Spirit. From the womb before the morning star I begot you.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109"],"PSA.110.4":["The Lord has sworn, and he will not repent. \"The Lord has sworn.\" In what he says, \"he has sworn,\" it is not to the one who was begotten before the morning star, but he swore to the one who after the morning star was born of the Virgin. You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedech. It is superfluous for us to wish to interpret this little verse, since the holy Apostle argued about it most fully to the Hebrews. For this Melchizedech himself is without father, without mother, without lineage. And there he interprets most diligently why \"without father, without mother, without lineage\"; and it has been said by all churchmen that he is called \"without father\" according to the flesh, and \"without mother\" according to God. Let us therefore interpret only this: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedech. Let us say only this, why he said \"according to the order.\" \"According to the order\": by no means will you be a priest according to the Jewish victims, but you will be a priest according to the order of Melchizedech. For just as Melchizedech, king of Salem, offered bread and wine, so you too will offer your body and blood, the true bread and the true wine. This Melchizedech gave to us these mysteries which we have. He it is who said, \"Whoever shall eat my flesh and drink my blood\": according to the order of Melchizedech he handed down to us his sacrament.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109"],"PSA.110.5":["The Lord at your right hand. I see a new thing. It is said at the beginning: The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand; and now it is said to him: The Lord at your right hand. If he himself sits at the right hand of the Father, how in turn does the Father sit at the right hand of the Son? For when it is said, The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, it is said most plainly that the Son sits at the right hand of the Father. But when it is said, The Lord at your right hand, this is said, that the Father sits at the right hand of the Son. All this is said for this reason, that the Son may be equal to the Father. There are many things that might be said. But to sinners the devil stands at the right hand. Yet that discussion is another matter. Whoever is a sinner, the devil stands at his right hand. But someone says: How is it that in Zechariah it is said to stand also to Jesus the son of Josedech? \"And the devil was standing at his right hand.\" How is it written in Zechariah that the devil stands at the right hand of the Savior? You see that there is a question here. But at that time when the devil was standing at the right hand of the Savior, \"Jesus,\" it says, \"had filthy garments.\" He was bearing our sins, he was wrapped in our vices: therefore the devil was standing at his right hand. He shall shatter kings in the day of his wrath. \"All these things have been handed over to me.\" \"In a moment of time,\" it says, \"he showed him all the kingdoms of the earth.\" The devil shows the kingdoms of the world. And what does he say? \"All these things have been handed over to me: if, falling down, you will adore before me, all will be yours.\" In the day, then, of war, in the day of contest, he shall shatter kings. \"Of his wrath.\"\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109"],"PSA.110.6":["He shall judge among the nations. See the differences: kings are shattered, nations are judged. He shall fill up the ruins. Our Lord ascended upon the cross, and many ruins came about. \"This one is set for the ruin and resurrection of many, and for a sign that shall be contradicted.\" He shall fill up the ruins: of those who do not believe, who did not believe the scouts, the apostles. For Moses too sent scouts into the world; and because the people did not believe the scouts, therefore they fell in the desert. Even to this day the bodies of those who do not believe fall in the desert of this world. He shall crush the heads in the land of many. He shall fill up the ruins of those who do not believe; he shall crush the heads of those who are doubtful, of those who do not have perfect faith. He shall crush the heads in the land of many, not of all: for if he had said \"of all,\" none of us would have hope. \"Of many\" is said, so that others may be left.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109"],"PSA.110.7":["He shall drink from the torrent in the way. Who shall drink from the torrent in the way? This one, to whom it was said, The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand: this one who heard, You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedech: this one who shattered kings in the day of his wrath, who judges the nations, shall fill up the ruins, shall crush the heads in the land of many: what else will this one do? He shall drink from the torrent in the way. Let us first look at the way, so that afterward we may look at the torrent in the way. The way of this world is meant, through which he walked. Indeed it is written in the Gospel: \"Be agreeing with your adversary, while you are in the way\" — that is, while you walk in this world, and you go along the way to him who is the true Way. Hence it is said also in the one hundred and eighteenth psalm: \"Blessed are the unspotted in the way.\" Blessed are those who in the way of this world are unspotted, and in this world. Therefore the priest according to the order of Melchizedech, since he came into the way of the world, and walked in our way, he too drank from the torrent that was in the world. A torrent does not have natural waters, it does not have waters that always flow: but it has waters from storms, from rains, from whirlwinds, from tempests. A torrent is never in the mountains, but always in the valleys, in steep places. It takes in foreign waters, and gives birth to them, and swelling it rushes on. He shall drink from the torrent in the way. A torrent never has clean water, but always turbid water. This is said because the waters of this world are always turbid, always full of storm. Do you wish to know how he drank from the turbid torrent? He himself said: \"My soul is sorrowful even unto death.\" And the Gospel says: \"And he began,\" it says, \"to be sorrowful and troubled.\" Our Lord too, then, drank from the torrent of this world the turbid waters, the sorrowful waters, the waters that have no gladness. He took the cup, and filled it from the torrent of the world; and because the waters were turbid, he says: \"Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me.\" He drank, then, from the torrent of this world: but he did not drink as though in his own home, but in the way, hastening to go elsewhere. He shall drink from the torrent in the way. For this reason he drank from the torrent, because he was in the way. He shall drink from the torrent. If the Lord drank from the torrent of this world, how much more the saints? Do you wish to know that the saints always drink from the torrent? He says also in another place: \"Our soul has passed through the torrent.\" He shall drink from the torrent in the way. We have spoken about the torrent; let us speak also about the testimony, \"Our soul has passed through the torrent.\" Do you wish to know of yet another torrent? Elijah the holy prophet, when Jezebel was persecuting him, when he received food, and passing on for forty days went into the wilderness: \"He was,\" it says, \"in the desert, and drank from the torrent of Chobar.\" But hearing of the torrent of this world, do not despair: frequently this torrent fails. It seems indeed to be swelling, it seems to have many waters: but it quickly fails, if you endure. As for Elijah, he did indeed drink water in the desert, at that time when he was in sorrow, when he was in distress... He had left Judea: see how great his fear had been: he had left Judea, and had gone on to Mount Sinai. For Jezebel was persecuting him, and therefore he had drunk from the torrent of Chobar. But because he persevered to the end, therefore the water of the torrent failed. And it was said to him by the Lord, when the water of the torrent had failed: \"Go,\" it says, \"to the widow in Sarepta of the Sidonians,\" and the rest. Behold, Elijah too drank from the torrent. But the Lord and Savior himself also drank the cup from the torrent of Cedron. For thus the Scripture reports, when they came and seized him. \"They came,\" it says, \"and seized him at the torrent,\" and the rest. You see how lowly is the place of the torrent, how rugged. \"Cedron\" in the Hebrew tongue is interpreted \"darkness.\" You see, then, that the torrents of this world are in darkness. The Lord, therefore, is handed over: he is not handed over up above on the Mount of Olives, he is not handed over in the temple: but he is handed over at the torrent, and the torrent of darkness, that is, Cedron. For all who hate the light love the darkness. He shall drink from the torrent in the way. Because, therefore, the Lord drank from the torrent and tasted death, therefore the Father exalted him, and gave him a name which is above every name. Next it is now said, He shall drink from the torrent in the way; and because he drank from the torrent in the way, therefore he lifted up his head. And what does he say? \"When I have been lifted up, I will draw all things to myself.\" He is lifted up, and he lifted us up. His head has been exalted: as for the rest, the heads of the dragon have been crushed, and shattered in the sea. Therefore he lifted up his head. He did not say, \"Therefore his head has been exalted,\" that is, exalted by another: but the one who had humbled himself, he himself exalted. For he himself says in the Gospel: \"I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up again.\" Therefore he lifted up his head. He exalted himself, who is the head of all. Or else, when it says \"he lifted up his head,\" he did not exalt himself. Therefore he lifted up his head: for he was always on high. But when it says \"he lifted up his head,\" he exalted our head, we who were lying down below on the earth; he exalted our head, we who, bent over, could not look up to heaven, but to the earth. Do you wish to know that he himself lifted up the head? That woman too, whom the devil had bound, he himself set free, and lifted up her head, she who was always bent over: and she adored him. To whom is glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 36, on Psalm 109","But if anyone should object the brook Cherith, from which Elijah drank, and another torrent, from which the Lord drank on the way, for so it is written: He will drink from the torrent on the way, this must be said: that whoever is in Egypt and on the way of this age, though he be Moses and Aaron, though he be Jeremiah and Elijah, must nevertheless drink of the temptations of Egypt and of the wilderness; hence also the Word of the Lord, who had assumed flesh for this, that he might drink from the torrent, considering his majesty, said: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Again, seeing that he was in Egypt, and that the waters could not be cleansed unless he himself had drunk, he says: Nevertheless not as I will, but as you will.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PSA.111.1-2":["In the council of the upright, and in the great congregation, are the works of the Lord. He says \"of the upright,\" meaning those who before were sinners, but now are righteous. For is it not a thing to marvel at, when we see that the elephant and the camel, and likewise the fly and the gnat, have the same power of movement, the same breath of life, and the same members?\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110"],"PSA.111.1":["Confess to you, O Lord, with my whole heart. If David were not simple and had no guile, he would not have said: I will confess to you, O Lord, with my whole heart. Not with the lips only, but with the whole heart.\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110"],"PSA.111.5":["He has given food to those who fear him. In famine he fed Elijah; in the desert he rained down manna for the people of the Jews: and that was fulfilled which is said, He has given food to those who fear him. This is according to the letter. But he gave as food the bread that came down from heaven: and if we are worthy of it, we are fed upon it. For how many martyrs were consumed by hunger, and are now with the Lord?\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110"],"PSA.111.9":["Holy and terrible is his name. Holy to the holy, terrible to sinners.\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110"],"PSA.111.10":["The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom. Those who do not fear the Lord have no wisdom in them: or those who have no wisdom have no fear.\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110","His praise endures unto the age of the age. Since in the title \"alleluia\" was written beforehand, rightly does the psalm end in praise: for indeed \"alleluia\" is interpreted, Praise the Lord.\n— Homily 37, on Psalm 110"],"PSA.112.1":["Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. I have often said that everything interpreted in the person of the righteous man refers to Christ. For if the saints are a type and figure of the Savior, who alone is truly holy — for example, Isaac is a type of the Lord the Savior, and Joseph, David, Solomon, and the rest of the saints — then this blessed man too, who is now being described, does indeed refer to a righteous man, and is truly spoken of a righteous man; but this righteous man is a type of the truly Righteous One. Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. For now we have divided up the psalm: let us now speak about the righteous man. Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. The one hundred and eleventh psalm is an alphabetical one. And indeed the one hundred and tenth is itself also arranged under the alphabet. The first little verse is written from ALEPH, Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. The second from BETH, In his commandments he shall greatly delight. The third GIMEL, His seed shall be mighty upon the earth, and so on all the way to the twenty-second letter, thau. Let us therefore, aided by your prayers, expound the individual verses.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111","Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. It is the beginning of the psalm: it does not yet say, Blessed is the man who loves the Lord. For to fear belongs to men who are beginning, but to love belongs to the perfect: since 'perfect love casts out fear.' Therefore he speaks of the beginning, that is, of a holy man who is just starting out. Let us then look at the order of the psalm. Blessed is the man who fears the Lord. He who fears the Lord, what does he do? Does he hear the words of God? Is he learned in the law? This is not what he says. What does he say? In his commandments he shall greatly delight. He who fears the Lord, who is blessed, gladly performs His commandments. In his commandments he shall greatly delight. See what he says. He did not say, he performs his commandments; for many act out of fear, and have no reward. For he who fears hell, and for that reason does not commit fornication, does not have as great a reward as the one who acts out of love for God. In his commandments he shall will, that is, he wills, he desires to perform His commandments. Just as someone, if he is in the heat, thirsts: so does he desire the commandments of the Lord. In the commandments of the Lord he shall greatly will. An intensification (auxesis): he does not merely perform His commandments, but wills them: and he wills them not in passing, but exceedingly.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111"],"PSA.112.2":["The third verse is: His seed shall be mighty upon the earth. Because I have performed the commandments of God with my will, I am worthy to become a father of saints. Let us say it briefly, lest the discourse be drawn out too long. The generation of the upright shall be blessed. May the Lord grant that you also who hear me may be a generation of the upright.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111"],"PSA.112.3":["Glory and riches are in his house. Glory and riches. Do we suppose that the glory and riches of the world are meant? Is it for this that a man does the will of God and His commandments, in order to obtain the riches of this world? And surely he does not perform the commandments of God unless he has despised the riches of the world. Glory and riches are in his house. The glory of believers, as the apostle says: 'Are you not my glory?' And riches. The riches of which the same apostle speaks, 'that you have been made rich in all knowledge.' And his righteousness endures unto the age of the age. He did not say, righteousness endures unto the age, and has an end: but, it endures unto the age of the age.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111"],"PSA.112.4":["Unto the upright of heart a light has risen in the darkness. We were once in darkness, we were sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death: but a light has arisen for us, according to Isaiah. Merciful and compassionate and righteous. See the two things he says, Merciful and righteous. If you are a sinner, do not despair of pardon: He is merciful. But if you are proud, and put your confidence in the clemency of God, do not be negligent: He is also righteous. Merciful and compassionate and righteous. See what the prudence of the psalmist has done: righteousness is single, mercy is double. Merciful and compassionate and righteous.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111"],"PSA.112.5":["Delightful is the man who shows mercy and lends. Because the Lord is merciful and compassionate, let the righteous man too imitate his Lord: let him also be merciful. Delightful is the man who shows mercy and lends. Does someone suppose that he is speaking about gold and about silver and about money? The righteous have so far been without anything to lend to others that they themselves received their food from others. So then, if I have nothing to give, am I no longer righteous? If I do not give alms, shall I not be counted in the number of the righteous? How many of the saints gave alms, yet fell from their holiness. The perfect gave no alms: they had nothing from which to give. Not to give alms is the crime of one who possesses: but as for the one who has nothing from which to give alms, he is free. However much he was willing to give, that much he gave, because he gave by his intention. Nevertheless the saints too have something from which to give alms. Peter also says: 'Silver and gold I have; but what I have, this I give to you, in the name of the Lord Jesus rise up and walk.' Which is greater, to give money, or to make a crippled man whole? Delightful is the man who shows mercy and lends. What are these alms? What is this that the righteous man lends? The second verse shows it. He shall order his words in judgment. He did not say, he shall order his gold and his silver or his money in judgment: but, he shall order his words in judgment. This is mercy, if a holy man teaches others who are not holy. He shall order his words in judgment. We have understood, then, what are the riches that are lent. But this that he says, he shall order his words in judgment, is that which is said in the Gospel, that we should not cast our pearls before swine: that is, that he may know what to bestow on each one, that each may know what he is able to receive, and what he is not able. Whence the holy man says: 'In my heart I have hidden your sayings, that I may not sin against you.' I did not give them to whomever I pleased: I did not send your holy thing to dogs, nor did I give pearls to swine. Great, therefore, is the danger for teachers, in being able to pour out the words of the Lord.\n— Homily 38, on Psalm 111"],"PSA.116.1":["I have loved, because the Lord will hear the voice of my prayer. He did not say, I shall love: but, I have loved. He did not promise that he would do it, but he testifies that he has done it. I have loved. Why have I loved? Because the Lord will hear the voice of my prayer. \"I have loved\" belongs to time that is past; but in \"will hear\" it belongs to the future. For he did not say, I have loved because the Lord has heard me; but, I have loved because the Lord will hear me.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.2":["Because he has inclined his ear to me. Since we are small and lowly, and cannot stretch ourselves up and lift ourselves high, for that reason the Lord inclines his ear and deigns to hear us. And so, since we are human beings and cannot become gods, God became man and inclined himself, according to that which is written: \"He inclined the heavens, and came down.\"\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114","And in my days I will call upon him. Here the question is asked: how is it that he calls upon him in the days, and does not call upon him in the nights? And how does he say in another place, \"At midnight I rose to give praise to you,\" since the saints are more accustomed to pray in the nights? And in another psalm it is said: \"In the nights lift up your hands to the holy places, and bless the Lord.\" How then does he say, And in my days I will call upon him? He did not say, I called; but, I will call, in the future. For while we are in this world, we call upon him in the night; in the world to come we call upon the Lord in the day. Therefore he said, in my days: for to sinners it is night, but to me it is day.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.3":["The sorrows of death surrounded me. It seems to me that the sorrows of death are foul thoughts. When the soul is thinking, and desires to sin, when it is in travail with sins, it is near to death. The perils of hell found me. O how unhappy and wretched and pitiable we are, because sin is always seeking us! But if sins are always seeking us, let us make haste that they may not find us. Now this is the difference between the sorrows of death and the perils of hell: the sorrows of death are evil thoughts, the perils of hell are evil deeds. That I may put it thus: one thing is in contemplation, another in action, that is, in practice. For there is the sorrow and the travail, but here is the peril that is near to death.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114","I found tribulation and sorrow. He says a strange thing. He does not say, I found rest: he does not say, I found abundance. Now if he had said, tribulation found me, sorrow found me, there would be no question. But now what does he say? I found tribulation and sorrow. As one seeking, so he found: for nothing is found except what is sought. The holy man does not seek rest, but tribulation: for he knows that \"tribulation works patience, and patience trial, and trial hope, and hope does not confound.\" Something of the same kind is said also in Jeremiah: \"Tribulation,\" he says, \"and misery I called upon.\" \"Because,\" he says, \"at your bitter word I shall laugh\": that is, in this world I want nothing else but tribulation, that in the world to come I may have rest and happiness. And therefore, he says, I now endure bitterness, that afterward I may enjoy sweeter things. And the people of the Lord, going out from Egypt, came to Marah, which is interpreted \"bitter,\" and from Marah to Sinai, which is interpreted \"temptation.\" And again Jeremiah: \"I sat alone, because I was filled with bitterness.\"\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.4":["O Lord, deliver my soul. Neither the Greek nor the Latin can express the peculiar force of the Hebrew. For \"O\" the Hebrew has ANNA. Now anna is interpreted \"I beg\" and \"I entreat.\" And what he says is this: I entreat you, Lord, deliver my soul. For it is not the feeling of one wondering, but of one entreating: for he is set in enormous peril.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.5":["The Lord is merciful and just, and our God has mercy. Prick up your ears, sinner: the Lord is merciful. But do not be careless: for there follows, and just. If you are a great sinner, listen, the Lord is merciful: if you are careless, listen, he is also just. But it seems to be as it were equal, the Lord is merciful and just: for instance, he is merciful in ten parts, and just in ten parts, and I speak thus for the sake of the simpler folk. Let someone say: If my sins are weighed, I cannot have as many good things as I have evil ones: but now it is said that he is merciful and just, merciful toward sinners, just toward the good: my sins begin to weigh me down, and he conquers in his mercy. Therefore he put \"just\" once, and \"merciful\" a second time. Merciful. He could also have spoken of the past. And our God, he says, has mercy: that he may always show him to be merciful.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.6":["The Lord keeps the little ones. If anyone is great, and exalts himself, and is proud, this man the Lord does not keep; if anyone thinks himself great, on this man the Lord has no mercy: but if anyone humbles himself, on this man the Lord has mercy, and the Lord keeps him. Since he says: \"Behold, I and the children whom the Lord has given me.\" And again: I was humbled, and he saved me. We understand \"I was humbled, and he saved me\" in two ways: either I sinned, and he saved me; or, when I was proud, he humbled me and saved me.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.7":["Turn, my soul, into your rest. When it is said, Turn into your rest, then he had a rest before, which he lost. For no one turns, except him who goes back to where he was before. For God created us good, and left us to our own free will, and placed us all with Adam in paradise. But because by our own free will we fell from that blessedness, and came into the valley of tears; for that reason the just man exhorts his soul, to turn back there whence it came down. Turn, my soul, into your rest. For this place is a place of tribulation, it is the land of warriors. This place is a place of tears, where it is not permitted to walk in safety. For wherever anyone may be, there is peril: if in the desert, there are scorpions: if in the cities, the eye of the harlot is the destruction of a man: if on the sea, there are robbers and pirates. All these things I speak mystically. For it is not now a peril that a holy man should be killed by robbers: since indeed the martyrs conquered by their own blood. Turn, my soul, into your rest: because the Lord has done good to you. For the fact, he says, that you turn, O soul, into paradise, is not because you are worthy, but it belongs to the mercy of the Lord. That you went out of paradise was brought about by your own fault: but that you return, was of the mercy of the Lord. Let us too say to our soul: Turn into your rest. Our rest is Christ our God. If ever we are in straits, and a thought begins to lead us captive into sin, let us say: Turn, our soul, into your rest.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.8":["Because he has rescued my soul from death. He has not yet returned into paradise, and already he says that he has returned into safety. He said, Turn, my soul, into your rest; and as though secure he says, Because he has rescued my soul from death. When did he rescue my soul from death? My God suffered for me, my God was crucified for me, and I say: \"O death, where is your victory? O hell, where is your sting?\" My eyes from tears. I wept, that I might laugh: I mourned, that I might rejoice forever. He who does not weep in the present, will weep in the world to come. For \"blessed are those who mourn, for they themselves shall be comforted.\" But on the contrary we say: \"Woe to you who laugh, for you shall mourn.\" My feet from falling. Because my feet did not stand upon the rock, therefore \"my feet were almost moved, my steps were almost overthrown.\" My feet have been freed from falling. Why? Because my soul has been turned into its rest.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.9":["I will please in the sight of the Lord in the land of the living. I seem, he says, to men to be just, though I have no good works: I seem to men to be holy, though I have no good words. Now this is what David says, this is what the saints say, this is what the apostles speak: for a sinner does not say this. What does the just man say? Though I have no good words and works, yet I seem holy, and am praised by men: but I know my own conscience, I know with what sort of thoughts my heart is tossed, I know how great the lusts are that come upon me, and \"I see another law fighting against my mind and leading me captive in the law of sin.\" Therefore I say: \"Wretched man that I am, who will free me from the body of this death?\" And therefore I say: \"From my hidden faults cleanse me, and from those of others spare your servant.\" From those of others. It is not mine, that which I bring to birth: I do not want to think evil things, and I think them: I do not want to think evil, and as though unwilling and captive I am dragged into an evil thought. But since it is not in my power either to think or not to think the things that are evil, therefore I say: These things indeed that come into my heart are another's: but since a kind of necessity is imposed upon me, therefore I say, From my hidden faults cleanse me. The sins indeed are another's: but because I take them up, therefore I say, In the sins of others spare me your servant. Now why have I said this? Because the prophet said, I will please the Lord. He did not say, I please; but, I will please: for here, in what I do, I cannot be perfectly just. Therefore the apostle also says: \"In part we know and in part we prophesy,\" and \"now we see through a mirror in an enigma.\" Therefore we have now used a long discourse: because in the present world no one can be perfectly just, neither David, nor the apostles, nor any of the saints. \"For no one is clean from sin, not even if his life be of one day.\" Since indeed the apostles and the saints did not dare to say, we are holy; nor did they dare to say, I please, but I will please. He who has promised for the future confesses concerning the past that he has not done it. I will please the Lord. Where will I please? In the land of the living. For this is the land of the dead: the land of the living is another. Since David too was in this land, and truly in the land of promise; when he says, I will please in the land of the living, he confesses that this land is not of the living, but of the dead. Do you wish to know what that land of the living is? The Lord says in the Gospel, \"The God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob,\" and they were dead. And what follows? \"God,\" he says, \"is not the God of the dead, but of the living.\" Therefore the just man too, what does he say? I will please the Lord in the land of the living: in the land of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. That is, when I am truly in the bosom of Abraham, then I will please the Lord.\n— Homily 39, on Psalm 114"],"PSA.116.10":["I believed, therefore have I spoken. In the Hebrew the one hundred and fourteenth psalm and the one hundred and fifteenth are one psalm. So then, what did he say? I believed, therefore have I spoken. What did you believe? What did you speak? The earlier psalm ended thus: \"I shall please before the Lord in the land of the living.\" So the prophet says: I believed, therefore have I spoken. What did I believe? That I shall please before the Lord in the land of the living. This I believed, and I spoke. But what did I speak? But I was humbled exceedingly. O the great understanding of the divine utterance! I believed, he says, that I shall please the Lord, that I shall be an angel, that I shall be in the heavens: and yet I was not proud, I was not lifted up, but I was humbled exceedingly. That I shall be in the land of the living, that belongs to the Lord's mercy. But I have come to know my own self, that I am earth and ashes: therefore I say, But I was humbled exceedingly. And what is earth and ashes?\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115","Let us interpret it also in another way. I believed, therefore have I spoken. When a master teaches a disciple, unless he himself has first believed what he teaches, he cannot teach another. So he says that first he believed, and thus teaches another. But I was humbled exceedingly. But when I had obtained the grace of God, I was not lifted up into pride, knowledge did not puff me up: but what did I do? I was humbled exceedingly. Yet I who taught by word, was able to teach also by humility. I said in my ecstasy, Every man is a liar. I was teaching, he says, my disciple: and considering the heavenly contemplation, and pondering more divine things, I understood that everything that man knows is lies. For what truth can there be in this world, when our life upon the earth is a shadow? If, then, he who teaches a disciple confesses that every man is a liar: how much more ought the disciple not to exalt himself, and to reckon himself to be something?\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.11":["But I said in my ecstasy: Every man is a liar. In the Hebrew it stands otherwise: \"I said in my ecstasy: Every man is a lie,\" which is said ZECAM. I shall please the Lord in the land of the living: but I know my own self, that according to the condition of the body I am nothing: for there is no truth in our substance, but it is a shadow and as it were a lie. I speak of the bodily substance, not of the soul. And what he says is this. I shall please the Lord in the land of the living: but when I ponder human life, and consider its various errors, I do not find truth in this world. Since indeed \"we know in part, and we prophesy in part.\" Therefore I say: Everything that we see, everything that we understand, is a lie. In what way, then, is it a lie? He called it here a lie, as it were a shadow, as it were an image; just as it is said also in another place: \"Yet man passes away in an image.\" By the same understanding it is now also said, Every man is a lie: that is, every man is a shadow. But this we say according to the Hebrew truth. But let us speak also according to the Seventy translators. For someone may say: What is it to me, what does the Hebrew have? I follow the Church. I said in my ecstasy: Every man is a liar. Does David speak the truth, or does he lie? If it is true, Every man is a liar, and this that he says, Every man is a liar, is true, then he too lies: for he too is a man. But if he too lies, then this that he said, Every man is a liar, is not true. However you turn it, it comes out to the contrary. And since he too is a man, then he too lies: or if he lies that every man is a liar, then he lies in another manner. You see, then, that these are the syllogisms of the philosophers. Indeed, according to this proposition the syllogism of Aristotle is woven. In fact you say — speak to another — : If you lie, and you speak the truth that you lie: then you lie. See how the proposition turns to the contrary. For what does he say? O you who speak with me, if you lie, and you speak the truth about this very thing, that you lie: then you lie, and in the same utterance it comes about that he both speaks the truth and lies. Therefore it has been read in the Apostle: \"See that no one deceive you through philosophy and the vain deceit of men, according to the elements of the world.\" For the philosophers found out by their deceit and their own art how in the same utterance a person may both speak the truth and lie. But let us say that David spoke the truth, that every man is a liar. As long as we are men, we lie: but when we have become gods, we cease to lie. \"For where there are jealousies and contentions among you, are you not men, and do you not walk according to man?\" \"I said: you are gods, and all of you sons of the Most High. But you shall die like men, and fall like one of the princes.\" If, then, anyone is holy, he is made a god; and when he has become a god, he ceases to be a man, and now he does not lie. For what does the Apostle also say? \"But let every man be a liar, but God be truthful.\"\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115","Let us interpret it also in another way. I believed, therefore have I spoken. When a master teaches a disciple, unless he himself has first believed what he teaches, he cannot teach another. So he says that first he believed, and thus teaches another. But I was humbled exceedingly. But when I had obtained the grace of God, I was not lifted up into pride, knowledge did not puff me up: but what did I do? I was humbled exceedingly. Yet I who taught by word, was able to teach also by humility. I said in my ecstasy, Every man is a liar. I was teaching, he says, my disciple: and considering the heavenly contemplation, and pondering more divine things, I understood that everything that man knows is lies. For what truth can there be in this world, when our life upon the earth is a shadow? If, then, he who teaches a disciple confesses that every man is a liar: how much more ought the disciple not to exalt himself, and to reckon himself to be something?\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.12-15":["For the sin of the soul blood alone is worthily offered, and the blood that is worthily offered is not that of calves, not of rams, not of he-goats, but one's own, as the prophet says and asks: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? And afterwards answers: I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord. Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints. Yet even this very blood we do not give, but give back. And what is there alike? When the Just One has died for sinners, the Son of God for men, shall we, sinners and men, die for the confession of his name?\n— Commentary on Micah"],"PSA.116.12":["What shall I render to the Lord for all the things that he has rendered to me? Since I have said, I shall please the Lord in the land of the living, and this very thing I believed, therefore have I spoken, and I was humbled exceedingly, and I said, Every man is a lie, every man is a shadow, every man is an image: for such great benefits, that I shall please the Lord in the land of the living, what have I worthy to render? I am earth, I am ashes: he promised me that I shall please him in the land of the living: he made me a master: I believed, therefore have I spoken. What have I worthy, that I may render to him for such great benefits? Whatever I shall give him is his own, and I give it back to him rather than bestow it. I have nothing else that I may render to him, unless I pour out my blood for him, unless I stand forth a martyr for him. This is the only worthy recompense, to render blood for blood, so that we who have been freed by the Savior may gladly pour out our blood for the Savior.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.13-15":["Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? By the cup in the divine Scriptures we understand the passion: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; and in the psalm: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? I will take the cup of salvation and call upon the name of the Lord; and at once he adds what this cup is: Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.116.13":["I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord. In the Hebrew it stands thus: \"I will take the cup of Jesus.\" This very thing the angel also interprets: \"And you shall call his name Jesus, for he himself will save his people.\" I will take, then, the cup of Jesus. What is this cup of Jesus? \"Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me,\" and \"Are you able,\" he says, \"to drink my cup?\" And immediately there follows: \"My cup indeed you shall drink.\" But why did I say this? So that we may understand the cup to be the sufferings of martyrdom. A great thing is martyrdom. How is it a great thing? Because he renders to the Lord what he received from the Lord. Christ suffered for him, and he suffers for his name. Since we have said that the martyr has nothing else to render to the Lord, the Lord too reckons it to him as an equal, since he sees that his servant has nothing else to render to him. For what is comparable? God suffered for men, the Lord for the servant, the just for the sinner: what equality is there? But since the servant has nothing else to render to the Lord, God, as one merciful, accepts martyrdom as an equal. It is as if some rich man had fed a certain poor man, and it should come about that the same rich man came with his household, and came by chance into the house of the poor man, and the poor man had nothing to set before him and his household and his horses, but only said to him, Enter into the house of your client, and showed his goodwill, since the poor man has nothing else to give and to do more: like that widow who contributed two mites.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115","I will take the cup of salvation, or of Jesus. And this very martyrdom, he says, is not of my own strength, but of the grace of God: therefore I cannot drink it, unless I have called upon the name of the Lord. Jesus conquers in his martyr; he himself is crowned in his martyr.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.15":["Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints. Let the Jews now answer, how they say that every human body is unclean. Indeed in the Law, among the Jews, if anyone should die, whether a prophet, or a saint of whatever kind, if anyone should touch him, even if a priest, he is unclean until evening. This is written according to the letter in the Law. Let us say to them: If the body of a dead man is unclean, how is it now said, Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints? If the body of a dying saint is precious, how is it unclean? Therefore this is said here of the evangelist, this is proclaimed of the martyr.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.16":["O Lord, for I am your servant. And I have already said in the one hundred and fourteenth psalm, where it has \"O Lord, deliver my soul,\" that in the Hebrew it has ANNA, which is translated I ask or I beseech. And here in the Hebrew it stands thus: \"I ask, O Lord, for I am your servant.\" I am your servant. It seems to be a thing of humility, what is said. It is a thing of great dignity and merit, to be the servant of the Lord, and not the servant of sin. Because I do not have, he says, men as masters, because I do not have sin as master: for sin does not reign in my mortal body: therefore I am your servant. A great merit. Indeed it is said of Moses: \"But not so my servant Moses.\" And Abraham and Isaac and Jacob are called servants of the Lord. Therefore the Apostle also begins thus: \"Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ.\"\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115","You have broken my bonds: to you I will sacrifice a victim of praise. Truly it is of great happiness so to go to the Lord that one has no defiled conscience. Whoever, being holy, dies his own death, although he be holy who has performed works of mercy, wrought signs, cast out demons, does not go to the Lord secure: his conscience shudders when he sees the Lord. But the martyr, even if he has sinned after baptism, yet washed by a second baptism, the baptism of martyrdom, goes secure to the Lord. You have broken my bonds. \"For each one is bound fast by the chains of his own sins\": but in my martyrdom you have broken the bonds of my sins, and out of the bonds you have plaited for me a crown of victory. \"For where sin abounded, grace superabounded.\" To you I will sacrifice a victim of praise. After martyrdom, where does he sacrifice? In the land of the living. I will sacrifice, he says, to you a sacrifice. What kind of sacrifice? Surely not a goat, or a calf? Those were Jewish things, and they have passed away. I will sacrifice to you a victim of praise. This is the praise of the martyrs. For just as the martyrs praise the Lord purely in the land of the living, so too the monks, who day and night sing psalms to the Lord, ought to have the same purity as the martyrs: since indeed they too are martyrs. For what the angels do in the heavens, this the monks do on earth.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.17":["You have broken my bonds: to you I will sacrifice a victim of praise. Truly it is of great happiness so to go to the Lord that one has no defiled conscience. Whoever, being holy, dies his own death, although he be holy who has performed works of mercy, wrought signs, cast out demons, does not go to the Lord secure: his conscience shudders when he sees the Lord. But the martyr, even if he has sinned after baptism, yet washed by a second baptism, the baptism of martyrdom, goes secure to the Lord. You have broken my bonds. \"For each one is bound fast by the chains of his own sins\": but in my martyrdom you have broken the bonds of my sins, and out of the bonds you have plaited for me a crown of victory. \"For where sin abounded, grace superabounded.\" To you I will sacrifice a victim of praise. After martyrdom, where does he sacrifice? In the land of the living. I will sacrifice, he says, to you a sacrifice. What kind of sacrifice? Surely not a goat, or a calf? Those were Jewish things, and they have passed away. I will sacrifice to you a victim of praise. This is the praise of the martyrs. For just as the martyrs praise the Lord purely in the land of the living, so too the monks, who day and night sing psalms to the Lord, ought to have the same purity as the martyrs: since indeed they too are martyrs. For what the angels do in the heavens, this the monks do on earth.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.116.18":["I will pay my vows to the Lord in the sight of all his people. What are the prayers of the martyrs? What are the prayers of the saints? \"I will hold you,\" he says, \"and I will not let you go.\" I will adore, he says, the Lord, whom, reading in the Gospel, I found crucified, and in the heavens I see reigning with the angels. In the sight of all his people. Read Daniel.\n— Homily 40, on Psalm 115"],"PSA.118.9":["From all of which neither the teaching of spiritual parents — whom we understand to be teachers — nor princes will be able to deliver us, unless the sons give their assent and assist the parents' entreaties by their own efforts; for the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the iniquity of the wicked shall abide in him: Each shall die for his own sin, and shall be saved by his own righteousness; and in vain do the Jews say: Abraham is our father, since they do not have the works of Abraham; but if there is confidence in anyone, let us trust in the Lord alone: Cursed, for, is every man who puts his hope in man, however holy they may be, however much they may be prophets; we read: Do not trust in men, and again: It is good to trust in the Lord rather than to trust in princes, not in the princes of the world only but in the princes of the churches, who will deliver only their own souls, if they are righteous, but the sons and daughters whom they have begotten in the Church they will not be able to save, if these are negligent; and yet the Apostle says: If anyone's work is burned up, he himself will be saved, yet so as through fire.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.118.19-20":["Which are the gates that are opened by the angels, so that there may enter, not the people of the Jews, which has been cast off, but the righteous nation, which from faith has received the name of the faithful? Surely those of which the holy man speaks: Open to me the gates of righteousness; having entered them, I will confess to the Lord (Ps 117 [118]:19). But no one will be able to enter these gates except one who has been freed from the gates of death, as the Psalmist said: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may declare all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion (Ps 9:15). For when we have been snatched from the gates of death, then we shall be able to sing all the praises of the Lord in the gates of the daughter of Zion. And as I consider the gates of death to be sins, of which it is said to Peter: The gates of hell shall not prevail against you (Matt 16:18), so I consider the gates of righteousness to be all the works of the virtues; and he who has entered them will find one gate, of which it is said: This is the gate of the Lord, the righteous shall enter by it (Ps 117 [118]:20). And as through many pearls one comes to the one pearl, so through many ways and gates we come to him who says that he is the way and the gate, through which we enter to the Father.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.118.22-23":["Jesus says to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes? By various parables and different discourses the same things are woven together. For those whom above he had called workers and vinedressers and farmers, he now calls builders, that is, masons. Hence the Apostle too says: You are God's tillage, God's building. These masons, then, just as the vinedressers receive the vineyard, so received the stone, to place it either in the foundations, according to the architect Paul, or in the corner, so that it might join together two walls, that is, both peoples; and this stone, rejected by them, has become the head of the corner; and this was done by the Lord, not by human powers but by the might of God. Of this stone of help Peter too speaks confidently: This is the stone which was rejected by you builders, which has become the head of the corner; and Isaiah: Behold, he says, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a stone, chosen, precious, a cornerstone, and whoever believes in it will not be confounded.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.118.25-26":["Moreover, what the following words mean: Hosanna to the son of David, I remember that I said many years ago, in a short letter to Damasus, then bishop of the city of Rome, and now I will touch on it briefly. In Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Savior, among other things we read this too: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord, this is wonderful in our eyes; this is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it; and at once there is joined: O Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper well; blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord; we have blessed you from the house of the Lord, and the rest. Where the LXX translators have: O Lord, save me, in the Hebrew we read: Anna adonai osi anna, which Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: I beseech you, Lord, save me, I beseech you. Let no one, then, think that the word is composed of two words, namely a Greek and a Hebrew one, but that it is wholly Hebrew and signifies that the coming of Christ is the salvation of the world. Hence there also follows: Blessed is he who is to come in the name of the Lord, the Savior too confirming this very thing in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him. Further, by what is joined: Hosanna (that is, salvation) in the highest, it is clearly shown that the coming of Christ is the salvation not only of men but of the whole world, joining earthly things to heavenly, that every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven and on earth and in the lower world.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.118.26":["For I say to you: You will not see me henceforth until you say: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. He speaks to Jerusalem and to the people of the Jews. Now this verse, which the little ones too and the sucklings used at the entry of the Lord Savior into Jerusalem, when they said: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, Hosanna in the highest; he took from Psalm 117 (118), which was plainly written about the coming of the Lord; and by what he says he wants this understood: Unless you repent and confess that I myself am he of whom the prophets sang, the Son of the almighty Father, you will not see my face. The Jews have a time of repentance given them; let them confess him blessed who comes in the name of the Lord, and they will behold the face of Christ.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.120.1":["Let us come to the psalm that was read after the hundred and eighteenth psalm. The hundred and eighteenth psalm, as we have often said, is a lettered one — that is, it is an alphabetic psalm: for it begins from aleph all the way to the last Hebrew letter, which in Hebrew is called THAU, and each set of verses begins with a single letter. There are twenty-two Hebrew letters, and eight verses begin with each single letter. For example, eight verses begin with the letter called ALEPH. Then again, when those are finished, another eight verses begin with the second letter, called BETH. And in the third place, when those are finished, another eight verses begin with the letter called GIMEL. So then the hundred and eighteenth psalm is an alphabetic psalm. If we want to read a letter, we cannot read it unless we first know our letters: so in the same way we cannot recognize Scripture unless we first know the alphabet. So then the hundred and eighteenth psalm is an alphabetic one, a moral one, and it instructs our life. And because there is not now time to speak about the whole psalm, let us speak now about the first of the psalms of ascents, which begins thus: In my tribulation I cried to the Lord, and he heard me.\n\nNow they are called psalms of ascents, as we have often said, because by certain steps we climb up to greater things. There is not now time to discuss all the steps, and to say how through the individual psalms the steps are made, and how we come to the heights. Meanwhile let us now speak about the first of the ascents, leaving aside the mystery of why there are fifteen steps: for to say the same things often is a weariness to the hearers. Hence the apostle too [is read] to have spent fifteen days with Peter in Jerusalem, so that he might join together both Testaments, both the number seven and the number eight. There are many things that might be said. In my tribulation I cried to the Lord, and he heard me. Whoever is still on the lowest step, let him look up to the highest, the fifteenth step. Whoever is on the fifteenth is already close to the temple. And this is that temple whose ruin we now see; for it offended its Lord. And what did the Lord say? 'Arise, let us go from here.' And what does he say in another place? 'Your house shall be left to you desolate.' The Lord went out, the temple fell down. This temple, then, had fifteen steps round about it. We see some signs of it: count them, and you will see that it is as we say. So then the Levites and the priests, each one according to his order, stood on the steps. For example, the one who was high priest stood on the first step, that is, on the fifteenth step above. On the second step stood the priests, and among the priests themselves there was a difference of rank. On the third step in turn stood the lesser ones. On the fourth in turn stood the Levites. And among the Levites there was much difference: whoever was last, he was on the very lowest step. Consider that this earthly temple is a figure of the heavenly temple. 'For star differs from star in brightness: so also is the resurrection of the dead.' Happy the one who deserves to be on the fifteenth step in the heavenly Jerusalem and in the temple. Yet because that is a great thing, and I think it belongs to the apostles and the holy martyrs, let us pray that we may deserve to be at least on the last step of the temple of the Lord. They stand indeed on different steps, but they sing one psalm in praise of the Lord. The places are different, but the praise of the Lord is one. This which we have said about the heavenly Jerusalem, I think, is meanwhile practiced daily by us who are established in this present age. One of us is on the first step: let him not despair of the second. He who is on the second step, let him not despair of the third. Happy the martyrs, many of whom deserved to ascend from the very lowest step to the first step. He who lives in this present age cannot at once climb from the first to the highest. So then we ought not to be content with the first step; we ought always to strive toward greater things. Neither let him who is on the lowest despair of the first; nor ought he who is on the first to be secure: for he can fall to the very last. I think this is what Jacob's ladder also signifies, when he fled Esau his brother. 'And he came,' it says, 'to Bethel, and slept there, and put a stone at his head.' See our ascetic; he was fleeing a most cruel man, he was fleeing his brother, and he found help in a stone. That stone is Christ; this stone is a help to those who suffer persecution: but to the unbelieving Jews a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense. 'And Jacob saw there,' it says, 'a ladder set up from the earth all the way to heaven; and in heaven,' it says, 'the Lord leaning upon it (epestērigmenon autēi). And he saw,' it says, 'angels ascending and descending.' See what he says. He saw angels climbing up; he saw Paul climbing up. He saw angels descending: Judas the traitor was falling down. He saw angels ascending: holy men going from earth to heaven. He saw angels descending: the devil and the demons and his army cast down headlong from heaven. Great is the difficulty of climbing from earth to the heavenly places. We fall down more easily than we climb up. We fall down easily: in ascending there is great toil, there is great sweat. If I am on the first step, how much remains for me all the way to heaven? If I am on the second, if on the third, if on the fourth, if on the tenth, what does it profit me unless I reach the top? Suppose that ladder of mine has fifteen steps: I have come all the way to the fourteenth: unless I hold the fifteenth, what does it profit me that I have come to the fourteenth? If I come to the fifteenth, and fall down, the greater the ascent, the greater the fall. And at the same time consider the sacred meaning: that it is great toil and great sweat to climb from earth to heaven. Do you want to know what those steps are? What is the first step? I think the first step is fasting, for it is still near the earth: we withdraw from the earth, we climb up: yet we still think about earthly and bodily things. Someone may say: What does fasting profit me? I seek that my soul be pure before the Lord: how many do not fast, and yet are holy. I do not say this. If Paul the apostle, the vessel of election, chastised his body and brought it into subjection, lest, having preached to others, he himself should be found a castaway: if he says 'But I see another law resisting the law of my mind and leading me into captivity,' and afterward says 'Unhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?' — if he fears the snares of the body, are we secure? If he says 'When I am weak, then am I stronger' — weakness of the body is health of the soul. Why do I say all this? Because abstinence is the first step. Let us not at once suppose that, if we have fasted, we climb up to the kingdom of heaven: fastings do not lead us to the kingdom of heaven, but without fastings we do not climb up to the kingdom of heaven. By themselves they do not profit: together with other things they profit. Why have I said all this? Because it is as it were a foundation and a support (hypostērigma) for those ascending to greater things. But the second step is to renounce the world, to seek nothing of the world, to despise earthly things. Someone may say: And is doing this the second? I say, the second. It is nothing to let go of earthly things: to let go of the vices of the soul, that is a great thing. For if I have fasted, that is, if I am on the first step: if I have let go of my possessions, that is, if I am on the second step: what does it profit me, if I am quarrelsome, if I am irascible, if I am a slanderer, if I am envious? It is nothing great to let go of earthly things: the vices are greater, which are to be let go. But someone may say: It is difficult, it is great, it is hard. And it is great for this very reason, because it is difficult. The athlete of Christ is not crowned unless he has striven lawfully. Let it cause despair to no one: the ascent is arduous, it is difficult, despair is born. Do not despair, O man: on the fifteenth step the Lord is above, he watches you, he gives help. If you are on the first step, and the distance (diastēma) between the first and the fifteenth seems great to you: do not look at the steps, but look to the Lord. And see what he says. He did not say, I saw the Lord standing on the fifteenth step: for we could not reach the Lord standing and upright. But see what he says: he saw him leaning upon the ladder. See what he says: from the fact that he was standing, he bent himself and humbled himself, so that we might be able to ascend. The Lord is bent down, he humbled himself for your sake: ascend secure.\n\nThis man, then, who is now on the first step, is not in tribulation: for he has already withdrawn from tribulation, he has already set his foot on the first step. For he does not say, Now I am in tribulation, now I cry; but because he is on the first step, he reports what he suffered. And what does he say? In my tribulation I cried to the Lord, and he heard me. This little verse seems to have nothing within it, it is a small verse: but it has three meanings glued together in it equally. For what does he say? In my tribulation — behold, one thing: I cried: and after the crying, what? he heard me. In one verse first the tribulation, then the crying, then the being heard. He did not say, When I was secure, when I was glad, I cried: but, when I was in tribulation, I cried. 'You ask,' it says, 'and are not heard, because you ask carelessly.' In my tribulation I cried to the Lord, and he heard me. In what did he hear me? He made me stand on the first step. But in comparison with the fifteenth step, what is it to be on the first step? I have let go of earthly things, I have not yet climbed to the heights. Therefore I ought not to be secure: on the remaining steps I ought to beg for the help of God.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"PSA.120.2":["O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue. He says this about all heresies: he says it about the Jews, he says it about the Arians, he says it about the Manichees, he says it about the rest of the heresies. When the Arian says: The Father is greater, the Son lesser; what do we say? O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue. When the Manichee, and those like the Manichees, say: The Lord did not rise in the truth of a body, and — so that you may know it was not a true body — he entered with the doors shut: what do we say? O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue. The wounds of the tongue are greater than those of the sword. The sword kills the body, it does not kill the soul: the tongue kills the soul. The tongue is an evil thing, a great evil: 'it is indeed a small member, but it boasts great things.' How many evils the tongue has, read the epistle of the holy James. The tongue has nothing in the middle: either a great evil, or a great good. A great good, it confesses Christ to be God; a great evil, it denies Christ to be God. So then let no one suppose and say, Meanwhile I have not sinned in deed: if I have sinned, I have sinned with the tongue. What is a greater sin than blasphemy against God? and yet it is the tongue that sins. Whence did the devil fall? because he committed theft? because he committed murder? because he committed adultery? These indeed are evils: but the devil did not fall on account of these, but he fell on account of his tongue. For what did he say? 'I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, and I will be like the Most High.' So then let not monks suppose that they ought to be secure, and say, Meanwhile we do not commit great crimes in the monastery: I do not commit adultery, I do not commit theft, I do not commit murder, I do not commit parricide, and the rest of the great vices. In the tongue too there are great vices. It is a great crime, when I slander my brother; with my tongue I kill my brother. 'For he who hates his brother is a murderer.' See what Solomon says: 'In the hand of the tongue are death and life.' See what he says, in the hand of the tongue. Do you see how great an evil the tongue is? and it has a hand too. For what does he say? In the hand of the tongue. It is a great vice to slander the brethren, to be unable to keep silent, to go round the cells, to slander others. I am a sinner, that man is a sinner: what is that to you? Consider yourself, lest you fall. Why do you exult in another's ruin? if I fall, will you therefore stand? Although it be my ruin, a brother's ruin ought to be your fear, not your exultation. He has fallen: are you glad, or are you sad? answer me. A brother has fallen: either you are glad, or you are sad. If you are glad, is anyone glad at another's ruin? but if you are sad, why do you go about? why do you tell others? Let God see your sadness, and let the brother himself feel it, let not the ears of the brethren hear it. O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"PSA.120.3-4":["These are the coals which, as a remedy for the tongue and for lying, laying waste vices and sins, the prophet, when questioned, longs for: What shall be given to you, and what shall be added to you against a deceitful tongue? The sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals; and we shall be able to find many things of this kind in the holy Scriptures, of which we have often spoken.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","And in the psalm the deceitful tongue, full of lies, is said to need purging by the fire of coals: What shall be given you, or what shall be added to you, against a deceitful tongue? The sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals. Of these coals of the altar, the desolating coal is taken up with the tongs of the two testaments, and purges the unclean lips of Isaiah, so that he can prophesy the word of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.120.3":["What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, to a deceitful tongue? The Holy Spirit answers the prophet, who says, O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue: What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, to a deceitful tongue? He answers him and says: You who say, O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue, what do you want given to you? And after it has been given to you, what do you want in turn added to you to a deceitful tongue? See what he says. He did not say, against a deceitful tongue: but, to a deceitful tongue. If he had said, against a deceitful tongue, he would have seemed to speak about an adversary. See what the sacred meaning is: the prophet — or rather the holy man established on the first step — I speak rather simply for the sake of the simpler ones — what does he say? O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue: for example, from my adversary who slanders me. But the Lord answers something else, and what does he say? He did not say, what shall be given to you or what shall be added to you against a deceitful tongue? that is, he did not say, what do you desire to be done against your adversary? But what did he say? What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, to a deceitful tongue? He did not say, against the tongue: but, to the tongue. See what he says, to a deceitful tongue: to your own tongue, not your adversary's. For if your adversary slanders you, it does no harm: you kill yourself, if you slander others. For example, if a brother slanders me, what harm has it done me? if he says I am a murderer, if he says I am an adulterer, if a criminal, if a liar, what harm has it done me? For it is one of two things: either he speaks the truth, or he lies. If he speaks the truth, that which he said does not harm me; but the fact that I truly did it. But if he lies, his lie does not harm me, but it kills the soul of the one speaking. We are unhappy, we are wretched. When we have someone as an enemy, while we suppose we are acting against him, we are acting against ourselves. We slander him, we go about here and there, we tell everyone about him: do we suppose we harm him? We do not harm him, but we kill our own souls, because we lie. 'For the mouth that lies kills the soul.' What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, to a deceitful tongue? Like a good physician, who says: For this infirmity, for this wound, what do you want laid upon you? what plaster do you want laid upon you? Just as if someone were to say to some wounded man: What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you, for the wound that pains you? The Lord asks, not because he is ignorant, but so that this man who was asking may know how he ought to ask.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"PSA.120.4":["The sharp arrows of the mighty one, with the coals that lay waste. The arrows of the mighty one. This man now answers the Lord as to what he asks. Because the Lord had said, What shall be given to you, or what shall be added to you? now this man says what he asks. O Lord, this I ask: the sharp arrows of the mighty one, with the coals that lay waste. See what he says. The poison of my tongue and my slander nothing can cast out, except your arrows, which make a desolation of my tongue. My tongue swells, it has poison within: unless it is pricked and struck by your arrows, the poison does not come out. We ought to give an example. If ever there is a wound in the body, and in the wound itself the flesh is already putrid, and there is a canker, and there is pus within: unless it is opened, the pus does not come out. And if the pus has come out, unless there is a cautery, the pus is born again. See then what he says. Because my tongue swells, and has pus, and has poison, first wound it with your arrows, so that the pus may be able to come out: then apply your coals and fire, so that whatever is evil may burn away, and make it a desert. See then what he says, The sharp arrows of the mighty one, with the coals that lay waste. Would that this coal that lays waste were given to us. Would that we too might say: 'Was not our heart burning within us on the way, when he opened to us the Scriptures?' Isaiah says to Babylon: 'You have coals of fire, you shall sit upon them, these shall be for you a help.' And the apostles, before they received the Holy Spirit, still had in their tongue certain human swellings: therefore the Holy Spirit descended in the form of fire, and sat as it were as fire upon their tongues, so that whatever of pus they had, the Holy Spirit might boil away. Let us give another example too. Isaiah, when he had seen the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, what does he say? 'Woe is me, wretched that I am, for I am pricked with compunction, because I have unclean lips, and I sit in the midst of a people that has unclean lips.' See what he says, Woe is me, wretched, because I have unclean lips. And afterward what does he say? Because he had unclean lips, therefore one of the seraphim is sent, and takes a coal of fire from the altar, and touches his lips and his tongue, and purges it. And what does he say? 'Behold, this has touched your lips, and has cleansed your tongue.' And at once what does he say? 'Whom shall I send to this people, and who shall go?' O the mysteries of the Scriptures! As long as he had a deceitful tongue and unclean lips, it is not said to him, Whom shall I send, and who shall go? His lips are cleansed, and at once he is sent to speak. So then he who has unclean lips cannot prophesy, cannot be sent into the service of God. With the coals that lay waste. Would that this desolation too be granted to us, so that it may take away whatever is evil from our tongue: so that where there are thorns, where there are briars, where there are nettles, the fire of the Lord may be sent, and burn up everything, and there may come to be the desert of Christ.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119","But if anyone is a sinner, and groans that he dwells in the tents of Kedar, there are sent to him sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals, so that first he may be pricked by the words of God and say: I was turned about in misery while the thorn is fixed in me. And after he has been pricked, then let the desolating coal be sent to him through the Seraphim, that is, the burning word of God, to burn out not only the lips, which alone Isaiah had unclean, but also all the parts of his members, and to bring him back to a solitude of sins.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PSA.120.5-6":["Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. This the holy man says, who does not desire the body, who does not seek the earth, who longs for heaven. Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. When we are sick, when we are feverish, when we suspect we are dying, when we throw about our arms, when we are afraid, when we beg for a respite, we cannot say this little verse. This man was healthy, he was not sick, and what does he say? Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. For as long as we are in the tabernacle of this body, we are pilgrims away from the Lord. Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. My sojourning: he did not say, my dwelling. My sojourning: for in the present age we do not have a dwelling, but a pilgrimage. Hence the apostle too says: 'As strangers and pilgrims.' 'I am a stranger and a pilgrim.' Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. What he says is this. Unhappy am I, as long as I am in this body. Which of us can say this? If we are eighty years old, we fear to die; if we are a hundred and are sick, we still beg for a respite and a span of time. Why do we do all this? because the consciousness of our sins gnaws at us. For we know that if we depart from the body, we do not go to Christ, but to gehenna. What does the apostle say? 'I desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ.' Give me the security that after death I shall be with Christ, and even now I desire to die. So then this man, because he is holy, therefore says: Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged. I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar. Kedar in our language is interpreted as darkness. See then what he says. In what kind of place are we? in what kind of world are we? I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar. Long have I been in darkness, long have I lived in darkness, long have I been in the body of this death. My soul has long been a sojourner. See what the holy man says. Even if I have lived ten years, my soul has long been a sojourner. For what happiness is it, in comparison with the heavenly things, to be among earthly ones? There are many things that might be said, but the sermon already hastens to its end.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"PSA.120.6-7":["With those who hated peace I was peaceable; when I spoke to them, they attacked me without cause. Unhappy we, we cannot restrain our tongue, we cannot help slandering the brethren. If someone offends us, we as it were present a peaceful face, but for the rest the heart has poison. We are called monks, and although we are not such as we ought to be, yet we are so called. At the third hour we pray, at the sixth hour we pray, at the ninth, we hold the lamp-lighting, at midnight we rise, then at cockcrow we pray. See at what hours we are always roused for the service of God. See what he says: the third hour, the sixth, the ninth, the lamp-lighting, midnight, cockcrow, early morning. All this we do, and we do not consider that if our soul holds enmities against a brother, we pray in vain. For in the Gospel what is said? 'If you offer your gift at the altar, and there remember that your brother has something against you.' See what he says. He did not say, if you have something against your brother; but, if your brother has something against you. He seems to lay upon us something as it were unjust: for if he had said, if you have something against your brother, it would have been in our power what we had. But now what does he say? If you remember that your brother has something against you. I answer him: Lord, this is not in my power, whether my brother has something against me: I have nothing against him. If he has something against me, what does it concern me? and why do you refuse to receive my gift and say, If you offer your gift at the altar, and there remember that your brother has something against you: leave, he says, your gift, and go be reconciled to your brother, and so you shall offer your gift? Behold, I have nothing: if he has, what does it concern me? The Lord answers you, and what does he say? Wicked servant, I understand your mind: you have nothing against your brother? you have nothing? do you love him? why then do you not want him to be saved? if you love him, go, ask him not to have anything against you, so that he may be saved. But you show that you do not love him, because you do not want him to be saved. Go, ask him, cast yourself at his feet, entreat him day and night: say, I have left my gift at the altar, on your account I cannot offer the victims. He is pricked with compunction, and what does he say? If this man who asks me does not offer his gift: I who am asked, if I do not forgive, what shall I do? Therefore the apostle too says that we should give our enemies to eat and to drink. And what does he say? 'Doing this,' he says, 'you heap coals of fire upon his head.' Someone may say: Enemies do this; do I therefore do good to my adversary in order to heap coals of fire upon his head? But something else is meant. If he has done you evil, and you have done him good, you heap coals of fire upon his head: that is, you cleanse his vices, and you boil away his malice, so that he may turn to repentance. See then what this holy prophet says. With those who hated peace I was peaceable. He did not say, who did not want peace: but, who hated peace. They hated peace, and I loved those who hated peace. Therefore the apostle too says: 'As far as in you lies, having peace with all men.' Let us entreat our brother, if he is our enemy; let us entreat him even twice, and three times, and seven times. 'Lord, if my brother sins against me, how often shall I forgive him? seven times? I say to you, not seven times, but seventy times seven' — that is, four hundred and ninety times. If we wanted to reckon it up, from morning until evening we could not commit so many sins, four hundred and ninety. Yet what does the Lord say? If he sins against you seventy times seven in a day, and turning back asks you, forgive him. But you say: He does not turn back with his whole heart, he lies. Let us leave the judgment to God: he asks me, he entreats me: he does not say this truly — God knows. I hear a voice, Christ understands the heart: I accept what I hear, let Christ accept what he knows. Then see your own reward: he lies, you accept it as true; his is the killing, yours is the salvation.\n\nWhy have I said all this? Because in monasteries and especially in communities these vices are wont to exist. We do not blush, we do not grieve: we have left our mothers, we have left our parents, we have left our brothers, we have left our sisters, we have left our wives, we have left our children, we have left our fatherlands, we have left our homes, we have left the little rooms in which we were born and reared, we have left the little servants with whom we were reared, we have come into the monastery: and we have left all these things for this purpose, that over some trivial, frivolous matter we should pick a quarrel with the brethren in the monastery. We have left possession, we have left fatherland, we have left the world: and over a reed pen we pick a quarrel in the monastery. If I hear a bitter word, I cannot bear it, it is in my heart, it is inscribed in my very marrow for ten or fifteen days, it cannot be taken from my heart, I speak to my brother who had done me injury, and I promise peace with my lips, and hold poison in my heart. Often even my eyes and the countenance of my mind are an indication. When our heart is wounded against someone, we cannot look at him with straight eyes: the face itself and the eyes are as it were a mirror of our soul. What does Scripture also say? 'And they could not speak anything peaceable to him': the tongue could not lie about what the heart held otherwise. This, then, I say to your very selves and to myself; for I do not preach for the purpose of making myself, as it were, secure. The occasion of this little verse has compelled me to say these things. The tongue is a great evil. O Lord, deliver my soul from unjust lips and from a deceitful tongue: not from another's tongue, but deliver me from my own tongue. Another's tongue does me no harm: my own tongue is my enemy. O Lord, deliver my soul. Deliver my soul from my tongue: my tongue is a sword, it kills my soul. I suppose that I harm my enemy, and I do not know that I am killing myself. Let my adversaries fight when I speak to them, and let me speak peaceable things. Let them have a hostile mind, but let us have a peaceable mind. Since indeed it is written in Paralipomenon: 'And the children of Israel went out,' it says, 'to fight with a peaceable mind.' Does anyone go to battle, and have a peaceable mind? But the arms of Christians are peace. 'The peace of Christ, which surpasses all understanding.' Blessed are the peacemakers: not only those who make peace between quarrelers, but those who make peace within themselves. Blessed are the peacemakers. If others quarrel, and I intervene in the middle, and make peace between them, and I myself do not have peace in my mind: what does it profit me, that they have made peace? 'Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God.' Blessed are the peacemakers, who indeed speak peaceable things to those who hate peace. Blessed are the peacemakers. Christ is peace. For if Solomon is interpreted in Christ, and Solomon is interpreted as peaceable: then Christ our Lord is peace. Going to heaven he left us a standard, saying: 'My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you.' Let us keep what Christ has given, let us have peace, let us keep peace, and it keeps us in Christ Jesus: to whom is glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"PSA.123.2":["The Hebrews assert, and there is no doubt about this matter among them, that the Holy Spirit is called in their tongue by the feminine gender, that is, RUA CODSA (רוח קדשה). And what is said in Psalm 67 (68): The Lord will give the word to those who bring good tidings with great power, they understand thus: The Lord will give the word to those women who bring good tidings with great power (Ps 122 [123]:3): namely, to those souls which have obtained the Holy Spirit. And also the verse: As the eyes of a maidservant are on the hands of her mistress: they interpret the soul as the maidservant and the Holy Spirit as the mistress. But also in the Gospel written according to the Hebrews, which the Nazarenes read, the Lord says: just now my mother, the Holy Spirit, took me. No one ought to be scandalized in this matter, that spirit is said among the Hebrews in the feminine gender, while in our language it is called in the masculine gender, and in the Greek speech in the neuter. For in the Godhead there is no sex. And therefore in the three principal languages in which the title of the Lord's passion was written, it is called by three genders, so that we may know that what is diverse is of no gender.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.124.7":["The fourth punishment of those in discord is that they are caught in the snare, which is set not in heaven but on the earth; the saint glories in having escaped it, and says: Our soul, like a sparrow, has been snatched from the snare of the hunters; the snare is broken, and we are set free. This is the broken snare of which the Apostle speaks to believers: God will crush Satan under your feet swiftly. And again we read in David: Beside the path they have set a stumbling block for me. For they cannot deceive any of the simple among believers unless they put forward the name of Christ, so that, while we think we are finding Christ, we go on to the Antichrist.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.126.5-6":["The harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few. The great harvest signifies the multitude of the peoples, the few laborers the scarcity of teachers. And he commands them to ask the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest. These are the laborers of whom the psalmist speaks: Those who sow in tears shall reap in joy; going they went and wept, carrying their seeds, but coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves. And to speak more plainly: the great harvest is the whole crowd of believers; the few laborers are the apostles and their imitators, who are sent to the harvest.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.127.1":["And someone may say, as Epicurus and Aristippus and the Cyrenaics do, and the rest of the beasts called philosophers. But reflecting carefully within myself, I find that not, as some mistakenly believe, everything is done by chance, and that fortune plays a varied role in human affairs, but rather that everything is done by the judgment of God. For even if a swift runner were to think that he determines his own course with his feet, he does not. Nor let the strong trust in their own strength, nor let the wise esteem riches and wealth as being gathered by prudence; nor let the eloquent and learned think that they can find favor with the people through eloquence and learning; but let them consider that everything is done by God's disposition. And unless He governs all things according to His will, and builds the house, in vain do those labor who build it. Unless He keeps the city, in vain do those watch who keep it.\n— Commentary on Ecclesiastes"],"PSA.128.1-3":["Blessed are all who fear the Lord. Those who fear the Lord, what reward do they have? What crown? Let us see what praise they have, what reward. Blessed are you, and it shall be well with you. I have already spoken about blessedness; I am asking what this blessedness is: the one who fears the Lord, what blessedness does he have? Your wife like a fruitful vine on the sides of your house; your sons like young olive shoots around your table. I think that, with Christ favoring us, we all fear the Lord. Then do we not have this blessedness? For it is written that whoever fears the Lord has this blessedness: Your wife like a fruitful vine on the sides of your house, your sons like young olive shoots around your table. So then, is the man who has one son, and the man who has two, not blessed? For what does it say? Your sons like young olive shoots around your table. Unless we have so many sons that they encircle our table, will we not have the blessedness of the one who fears the Lord? Isaac did not have this blessedness: for he had two sons, one of them utterly wicked, Esau, for whom it would have been far better had he never been born. But he had one son, Jacob: so then did he not have the blessedness? because one son could not encircle the table. Virgins, then, do not fear the Lord? For what is said, that the one who fears the Lord should understand blessedness? Your wife like a fruitful vine on the sides of your house; your sons like young olive shoots around your table. John did not have this blessedness, both Johns, the evangelist as much as the baptist: for each of them was a virgin. Paul did not have this blessedness, who says: 'I wish, moreover, that all were as I am.' You see, then, that wife and sons must be understood spiritually; otherwise, unless we understand them spiritually, the angels do not have this blessedness, because they do not have sons. Let us return, therefore, to the order of the reading.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.1":["Blessed are all who fear the Lord. Just as no one can say 'The Lord is my portion' except the one who has no other portion in the world, so the one who fears anything else outside the Lord does not fear the Lord. Indeed, the Savior himself says: 'Do not fear those who can kill the body, but fear him who can kill both body and soul in hell.' You see, then, that whoever does not fear the Lord fears some other thing outside the Lord. The one who fears the Lord, what signs does he have? Those who walk in his ways. There are many ways, and many ways lead to one way. Indeed, it is also said in Jeremiah: 'Stand in the ways of the Lord, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord, and you will find the way, and you will walk in it.' See what it says, Stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord, and you will find the one way, and walk in it. See how through many ways we come to one way. This is also said in the gospel in other words, under another meaning of the parable, concerning that merchant who had many pearls, who sold them, and bought one most precious pearl. What it says is this: Stand in the ways, that is, have very many pearls. Stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord. Beautifully it says eternal, lest we should think that at the coming of Christ the law and the prophets were brought to an end. But see what it says. Stand in the ways, in the law and the prophets. Stand in the ways; do not so believe in the one way that you abandon the many ways; but stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord. Ask the paths of the Lord, what Jeremiah said, Isaiah, Ezekiel, what the rest of the prophets said. Ask the paths. It is better said in Greek τρίβους (tribous): for a path (semita) is so called from the fact that it is worn (trita) by the heel of those walking. And what is said is this: Ask the paths, that is τρίβους (tribous), which the foot of the Lord wore down: seeing that the Lord too walked in them. But when you have asked for the eternal paths of the Lord, and through those paths you have found the one way, which says 'I am the way and the life and the truth,' walk in it. See the mysteries of the scriptures: see in the simple words of the scriptures very many sacred meanings, in the ways and paths, in the law and the prophets. It did not say, walk, but, stand: do not abandon the law nor the prophets. Yet when you have found those ways, through these ways walk in this one way.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.2":["You will eat the labors of your hands. Many, not understanding, have interpreted this passage otherwise: 'You will eat the labors of your fruits.' Indeed the Latin copies have it thus: You will eat the labors of your fruits. But we read it written thus in the Hebrew: You will eat the labors of your hands. For who eats the labors of fruits? Surely it ought to be understood the other way around, that someone eats the fruit of his labors, and not the labors of his hands. For it is understood the other way around. For it can rather be said that someone eats the fruit of his labors, and not the labors of fruits. Since, therefore, it is written thus in the Hebrew, You will eat the labors of your hands, I think that in Greek too the Seventy translators translated this passage well. For in the Hebrew tongue the hand is called IAD; but in this passage it does not have IAD, but CAPH. And CAPH is translated καρπός (karpos)—not καρπός in the sense of fruit, but some part of the hand: seeing that καρπός in Greek means the joints (ἄρθρα, arthra). This we have said concerning the translation. You will eat, therefore, the labors of your hands. 'Whatever a man sows, this also will he reap.' Behold the man, and his works. So then we too will eat the labors of our hands. In the hands, works are understood. You will eat the labors of your hands. Someone may say: So then, are we going to eat after the resurrection? For this is said of the blessed man, You will eat the labors of your hands. I say this plainly, that man is composed of two substances, that is, of soul and body. The soul is immortal, but the body is mortal. What is mortal must of necessity have mortal food also; but what is immortal, that is, the soul, which is immortal, must have immortal food also. Do you want to know that the soul too has its own food? The Lord and Savior says it, when he was hungry. What does he say? 'My food is to do the will of him who sent me.' Do you want to know what food the soul has? The prophet too says to us: 'Taste and see that the Lord is sweet.' For just as the body, unless it receives its food, dies: so also the soul, unless it receives spiritual food, dies. Why do I say all this? Because some are accustomed to say: I do not need the holy scripture, it is enough for me only to fear God. And we have said this for this reason: as there are foods for the body, so the holy scriptures are foods also for our soul.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.3":["Your wife like a fruitful vine on the sides of your house. Solomon says that he wished to take wisdom as his bride. He says also in another place concerning wisdom: 'Love her, and she will embrace you: encompass her, and she will keep you.' Let us too, therefore, take this wife; let us hold her in our embraces, let us never let her go from our bosom, let her never flee from our hands. If this wife is always in our embrace, we will make from her sons: not daughters, but sons. Indeed, it is said here too: Your sons like young olive shoots around your table. Could it not have said, your daughters? or could it not have said τὰ τέκνα σου (ta tekna sou), so that both men and women might be understood? But see what it says, your sons. He who has wisdom as his wife does not make from her daughters, but sons. He has nothing soft, nothing dainty, nothing tender, nothing effeminate in his mind. Do you suppose it is for some other reason that the saints are written to have had not daughters, but sons? Let us read Genesis: for there the order of the generations is preserved. Holy Abraham had Sarah as his wife, had Hagar as his concubine, had afterward Keturah. From two wives he begets sons, he makes no daughters. Let us come to Isaac. Isaac too makes two sons, he has no daughters. For he was holy, and had in himself nothing soft and tender and effeminate, but all manly and firm. Blessed Jacob has twelve patriarchs as sons; and he has one daughter by the name of Dinah, which is translated cause, quarrel, strife. Indeed he had one daughter, and twelve sons; and on account of one daughter such great wars arise in Shechem. Jacob had never said: 'I am afraid, you have made me hateful.' He speaks to Levi and Simeon: 'Levi and Simeon, let my soul not come into their counsel.' Why? because as men they wished to plead the cause of a woman. Why have I said all this? Because the saints do not have daughters, but have only sons. I tell you a new thing, new to those who do not know it; but for those who read the scriptures, and read them diligently, not new, but the very thing they know. Jacob had twelve sons as patriarchs: Reuben, Simeon, Judah, Levi, Issachar, Asher, and the rest up to twelve. Read in the account of the generation, and you will never find that any of those twelve great patriarchs made a daughter, but all of them sons. I will tell you one, lest perhaps you forget. Asher had a daughter, Sarah. Jacob had twelve sons and one daughter: so among the twelve patriarchs one patriarch had only one daughter. Why have I said all this? So that we ought to understand sons and daughters in the scriptures spiritually. Your wife like a fruitful vine on the sides of your house. Like a fruitful vine. A fruitful vine has very many clusters, so also our wife, that is, wisdom our wife, who is the bride. Indeed, it is written in another place: 'Come my sister, my bride.' Lest in the fact that it had said, bride, you should understand something shameful, sister is joined, so that all shameful love may be excluded. Come my sister: for the love is holy, and therefore I call you sister. And my bride: therefore I call you bride, so that I may have a wife, and from you make many sons, many sons like clusters from a vine. Just as a vine has various clusters, and there is indeed one root, but it spreads its tendrils far and wide, here and there, and the tendrils themselves have their own fruits, and in the fruits themselves are the clusters, and one cluster is larger, another smaller, some grapes are larger, others smaller; so also from wisdom, which is our wife, provided that we never let her go, many clusters are born, that is, very many virtues, holy thoughts, splendid works. Your sons like young olive shoots around your table. We read in another place: 'And wine gladdens the heart of man.' We read also in the Song of Songs: 'Come, brothers, drink and be inebriated.' And wine which gladdens the heart of man. This fleshly wine does not gladden the heart of man, but overwhelms it, and makes it mad. Indeed, it is written that those who are powerful should not drink wine. The apostle too writes that we ought not to drink wine and eat flesh. Again it is said to us: And wine gladdens the heart of man. But it speaks of a spiritual wine, which, if anyone drinks it, he is immediately inebriated. All of you, that you might let go of your possessions and your parents and your children and your kinfolk, drank wine, and being inebriated for Christ began to be mad. Does it not seem to you to be drunkenness, does it not seem to you to be spiritual madness, to despise delights, to seek out squalor, to flee the cities, to pursue the deserts, to know no affections, to ignore the nearness of the flesh, and to know Christ alone? Indeed, the apostles were inebriated with this wine, and therefore it is said of them: 'They are full of new wine.' For they had not been inebriated from the old law, but from the gospel. Noah too, I think, was inebriated in this way with this wine. Your sons like young olive shoots. To nothing else are our sons compared except to young olive shoots. It did not say, to the old olive tree, but to the young olive. Just as there too, concerning the drunkenness of the apostles, it does not speak of old wine, but of new wine, so here too it says young olive shoots: young olive shoots, which are wholly green in the youth of the gospel, young olive shoots full of light. Oil has this nature, that it seasons food, that it provides fuel for light, that it restores an anointed body wearied by a long journey, as it were, to its former toil and to health. Indeed, whoever is weary, and is anointed, as it were recovers his former vigor. Do you think that wine and oil are named here without a reason? It could not have said honey, nor other aromatic spices, nor another tree, but only the vine and the olive. These are the remedies of our life, the vine and the olive, wine and oil. In wine, severity; in oil, mercy. Indeed, that man who was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho, who had been wounded, the Samaritan, that is, the guardian, poured into him nothing except wine and oil. Wine, the severity of the precepts, astringent wine; so that whatever there is of bad humor, it may burn out and consume. But since human frailty cannot bear the truth—'for if you should mark iniquities, Lord, Lord, who shall endure it?' for if the Lord should be only just, we cannot live—therefore to his justice, that is, to the wine, the oil of mercy has been added.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.4":["Behold, thus shall the man be blessed who fears the Lord. We have already spoken much, let us hasten to the end.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.5":["May the Lord bless you out of Zion. Out of this Zion? And how does another prophet say 'Zion shall be plowed like a field'? The Lord also says: 'Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kill the prophets, and stone those who are sent to you.' Indeed, when the Lord had drawn near to Jerusalem, and had seen it, he wept. But when he wept over it, surely he wept over it because it was about to fall, as though about to perish. Indeed, what does he say? 'Rise, let us go from here.' And in another place, what does he say? 'Your house shall be left to you desolate.' So then, in what it says, May the Lord bless you out of Zion, it does not mean this Zion, which we see to be desolate. The gates themselves and the field itself and the ruins themselves signify the wrath of God. Even if we do not know it, the very eyes cry out the wrath of the Lord. May the Lord bless you out of Zion. Zion is translated watchtower, Jerusalem is translated vision of peace. If our soul has a watchtower, if it is on high, if it thinks not of earthly things, but of things sublime: if our soul has the vision of peace, that is, if Christ, who is true peace, dwells in it, he himself will bless us. If Christ dwells in us, we too will be Zion, and we too will be Jerusalem.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.128.6":["And may you see the sons of your sons. I am speaking to you. If by my discourse I instruct you, I have seen my sons: if you instruct others, I have seen the sons of my sons. Let us pray, therefore, to the Lord, that we may have sons, may have grandsons, and may always have much offspring. Let us hasten to make such sons: this union is holy. I tell you a new thing: without the embrace of a wife many such sons are born. Peace upon Israel. Israel is translated, a man seeing God. See, therefore, what is said. Peace upon Israel: upon those who have a mind seeing God. If my mind has the vision of God, if I always see Christ, I will not see others. Whoever has Christ in himself, has peace in himself. 'My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you.' Although there are many persecutions, although there are many who persecute, yet in whomever Christ dwells, that one always has Christ.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"PSA.129.1":["They often attacked me from my youth — let Israel now say — they often attacked me from my youth. Two things are said: both that they often attacked me, and that they attacked me from my youth. If I, for example, at forty years of age, in the middle of my life, should begin to serve the Lord, I cannot say, \"They have attacked me from my youth.\" But what do I say? \"They have slain me from my youth, they have wounded me from my youth, they have trampled me from my youth.\" But the one who begins from boyhood to serve the Lord, and is often attacked by the devil and his angels, and yet is not conquered — what does this man say? \"They often attacked me from my youth: and indeed they could not prevail against me.\" \"An athlete is not crowned unless he has competed lawfully\" — unless he has taken a blow, unless his face is bruised, unless blood has been spilled from his face. The bruise earns the crown, the pain earns the joy. They often attacked me from my youth. Let the virgin of the Lord speak this little verse, let the monk speak it, who has begun from a tender age to serve the Lord. But I, who have begun to serve the Lord at forty or fifty years of age, how can I say, \"They often attacked me from my youth\"? Let the virgin therefore say this, let the monk who is whole in body and spirit say it. And what does he say? Why did they often attack me from my youth? Let someone say: O monk, how did the demons and the devil attack you? Those who assailed me seemed indeed to be men: but I understood the devil within the men. When men persecute us, therefore, let us not be angry at the persecutor, but let us feel compassion for them and lament them: for it is another who persecutes within the persecutors. If a man possessed by a demon, who has a demon, should come and strike me with his fist and hit me with his heel, do I get angry at him? He strikes with his fist, and I weep and lament the one striking: for I see that he is more grievously struck by the one working within him than I am by his hand. As often, therefore, as we suffer anything and endure any temptations, let us understand that it is that one, and those, who breed temptations for us within men. Let someone say: Prove this very thing from the Scriptures: for we ought not to say anything unless it is furnished and confirmed from the Scriptures. Judas was a traitor, but Satan entered into him, and he himself betrayed within the traitor. Potiphar's wife at the time when she seized Joseph her servant — the mistress the servant, the very rich woman the poor little man, the citizen the foreigner — she was holding Joseph, but the holy man understood, in the hands of the woman, that it was the devil holding him back. Why do I say all this? So that when we suffer something from men, we may not be angry at men, but may lament them, and may see that plotter who works within them.\n— Homily 43, on Psalm 128"],"PSA.129.2":["They often attacked me from my youth: and indeed they could not prevail against me. They attacked me with base thoughts, they attacked me with anger. I gave in to the thoughts: if I gave in to the temptations, if I did something that the thought was driving me to, if anger overcame me and I did injury to my brother, I cannot say \"and indeed they could not prevail against me\": for they did prevail against me.\n— Homily 43, on Psalm 128"],"PSA.129.3":["Upon my back the sinners built. Carefully observe what the Scripture says. The holy man is an athlete, and \"he is not crowned unless he has competed lawfully.\" For you know that there are many kinds of athletes: for there are some athletes, but others are like all-in wrestlers (pammacharii), some contend at the discus, and others are runners, that is, those who run in the stadium. Indeed, the Apostle too says that he runs, so that he may reach the crown. And what does he say in another place? \"Forgetting the things that are behind, I stretch myself forward to the things that are before.\" See, therefore, what the Scripture says. Upon my back the sinners were building. Thus I always ran, and ran so hard, that my adversary might never get ahead of me, but might always be found behind my back. He never came before my eyes, for he could not bear the light of my eyes; but he always pursued that part which is blind, which has no eyes. Upon my back the sinners were building. Whenever anyone disparages his brother, that man is building behind the back. Upon my back the sinners were building: but I did not see their snares. They were building behind my back: and I with my eyes was gazing on paradise. They were contriving snares against me, and I was longing to see the crown.\n— Homily 43, on Psalm 128","They prolonged their iniquity. What did he mean by \"They prolonged their iniquity\"? If I sin, and after my sin I do penance, my iniquity is short, not long. But if I sin, as often as I sin, so often does the cord of my sins grow longer. Whence it is also said in another place: \"The cords of sinners have surrounded me.\" See what he says: The cords of sinners have surrounded me. And in another place it is written: \"Each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins.\" See what he says: Each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins. These cords of sins cannot be woven unless I have had many sins. If I commit one sin, it is not so much a cord as it is, so to speak, a single thread. But if it is a single thread, it is quickly broken, and cannot hold and bind. But if there are many sins, and they have prolonged their iniquities, out of many sins, as out of many threads, a cord is made, and it binds, and is not quickly broken. Why have I said all this? Because we read in this little verse, They prolonged their iniquity.\n— Homily 43, on Psalm 128"],"PSA.129.4":["The just Lord has cut the necks of sinners. See what he says. He did not say, \"he has cut down sinners\": but if he had said this, no one could be saved, for we are all sinners. But what does he say? The just Lord has cut the necks of sinners: those who are both sinners and proud; since \"God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.\" Therefore he cuts the necks of sinners.\n— Homily 43, on Psalm 128"],"PSA.132.1":["Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness. Many suppose, from what is written in the Gospel — \"Have mercy on us, Son of David,\" which the blind men who were sitting in Jericho said — that this David, whose name means \"strong of hand,\" is referred to the Lord Savior. But if in this psalm we should choose to interpret it, as they judge, it does great harm to our understanding. For if this that is said, Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness, they refer to Christ, then how does Christ say, as though about someone else, \"Until I find a place for the Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Jacob: behold, we heard of it in Ephrata, we found it in the fields of the wood\"? And how is it said, \"For the sake of David your servant, turn not away the face of your Christ\"? The order does not stand. For if David, as they think, is Christ, then we begin to understand it thus: For the sake of Christ your servant, turn not away the face of your Christ. This I say against those who in this passage interpret David as Christ. Not that we deny that David may be interpreted as Christ according to the character of the passages: but in this passage the sense does not admit it. And I will tell you why they go astray: because it is written, Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness; and the Lord says in the Gospel, \"Learn from me, for I am gentle and humble of heart\" — with pious sense indeed, but in their error they here interpret David as Christ, and refer his meekness to Christ. Let us therefore interpret this psalm of David, so that he who is born of David may truly be understood as Christ. Then, see the order. For since the psalm is long, and we have already said many things, we ought briefly to touch upon the meanings, not to dispute at greater length: so that the prudent hearer may, from the things we have said, understand for himself even those things we do not say.\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131","Concerning David too (although many judge that it was prophesied of our Lord, which we also do not deny), the Holy Spirit sings, as a type of the one to come: \"Remember, O Lord, David, and all his gentleness\" (Ps. 131:1). Whose gentleness shone forth more than all toward Saul, Absalom and Shimei (1 Sam. 24; 2 Sam. 15)? When the one wished to kill him; the other, plotting a revolution, strove to cheat him of his rule; the other, throwing stones at him and tossing up dust, shouted and said: \"Go out, go out, you wicked man\" (ibid., 16:7).\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PSA.132.2":["As he swore to the Lord, he vowed a vow to the God of Jacob. In the Hebrew it does not have cos, that is, \"as,\" nor is that the sense; but it has os, that is, \"who,\" and the sense is: Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness, who swore to the Lord, vowed a vow to the God of Jacob. And this I think was done not so much by a fault of the seventy translators as by an error of the copyists.\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131"],"PSA.132.3":["If I shall enter into the tabernacle of my house. David says this: I seek no other rest but Christ, who is to be born of my seed, that is, whom you promised would be born of my loins.\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131"],"PSA.132.4-5":["If I shall give sleep to my eyes, and slumber to my eyelids, And rest to my temples, until I find a place for the Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Jacob. Many think this is said of the Church: but to me it seems to be said not so much of the Church as of holy Mary.\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131"],"PSA.132.6":["Then it is said: Behold, we heard of it in Ephrata, we found it in the fields of the wood. Let us read the holy Scriptures, and both by day and by night let us sift each single syllable and letter. Let someone say concerning Ephrata: here he said Bethlehem. For we read in Genesis: \"He came into Bethlehem, this is Ephrata.\" This is said of Jacob, when his wife Rachel died, that is, in Ephrata. But let us see why Ephrata received its name. For it is written in Chronicles that Miriam, the sister of Moses and Aaron, had for her husband Hur — not Ur, but Hur. And afterward the lineage is described, and it is said: \"Miriam, the sister of Moses and Aaron, this is Ephrata.\" This is written in the books of Chronicles: seek and you will find. And so this Ephrata too received its name from that Ephrata. Perhaps it seems to someone not to be true, because it is new: but I refer him to the authority of the book.\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131","Behold, we heard of it in Ephrata. In the Hebrew it does not have \"it,\" but has \"him\": that is, that one who is to be born of the seed of David. We heard of him in Ephrata: which the sense is about Mary. But we can also understand it simply, since Ephrata is called Bethlehem, and Christ was born in Bethlehem. Happy therefore the place, which so long a time before was sung by the voice of the prophets. All holy places indeed are venerable, both where he was born, and where he was crucified, and where he rose again, and where he ascended a victor to the heavens; but this place is properly more venerable. See how great is the mercy of God. Here a little one is born, an infant is laid in a manger: \"for there was no room for them in the inn.\" O monk, the Lord is born on earth, and has no cell of his own: there was no room for him in the inn. The whole human race had a place: and the Lord, who was to be born on earth, had no place. He found no place among men, he found none in Plato, none in Aristotle; but in a manger, among the beasts of burden and the dumb animals, and among all who are simple and innocent. Whence the Lord also says in the Gospel: \"The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air nests where they may rest: but the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head.\"\n— Homily 44, on Psalm 131"],"PSA.133.1":["Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity. He says two things: good and pleasant. Martyrdom is good, but it is not pleasant: for it has its tortures and punishments, and there is always pain in torments, and certainly in pain there is no pleasantness. Behold how good and how pleasant. Again, pleasure is pleasant. For example, to eat sweet foods seems to be pleasant: for it drives one to lust. Here therefore he has set down two things, and what seemed to him to be contrary — it is good and it is pleasant — he has joined in a single phrase: Behold how good and how pleasant. Pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity: brethren in mind, not in body. Esau and Jacob were brothers; and because they dwelt together, it was for them a cause of discord. Therefore what he says here, brethren, he does not say brethren in body, but spiritual brethren: the brethren of whom the Savior also spoke, and said to the women: 'Go, announce to my brethren'. Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity. This psalm properly suits communities and monasteries. For although it may be understood also of churches, yet there, because of the diversity of purpose, there seems not to be so great a concord. For what brotherhood is there there? Surely one is hastening to go to his house, another to the circus, another in the church is thinking about interest on loans. But in a monastery, just as there is one purpose, so there is also one mind. Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity. Truly good, truly pleasant. We have left one brother behind, and behold how many we have found. My worldly brother (what I say of myself, I say of each one) does not love me so much as he loves my property. But spiritual brethren, who indeed neglect what is their own, do not seek what belongs to others. So too we read in the Acts of the Apostles, that 'of the multitude of believers there was one heart and one soul. And, it says, they had all things in common'. Rightly did they have all things in common, because they possessed Christ in common. We have said much, but let us hasten on to the rest.\n— Homily 45, on Psalm 132"],"PSA.133.2":["Like ointment upon the head, which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron. Oh, if there were time for us to discuss each detail, a day could not suffice us. We read in Exodus how the priestly ointment is prepared; then we read how there was another ointment, with which the kings were anointed. There was also another ointment with which the prophets were anointed. And why do I say more? All the ointments had their own distinctions and mysteries. Do we marvel about holy persons? A leper, after he has been cleansed, after he has been sprinkled with hyssop and with the blood of a bird, and one bird is released into the wilderness, he too has his own proper ointment. Nothing is sanctified except through ointment. Therefore the young maidens also say in the Song of Songs: 'Thy name is ointment poured out. After thee we will run in the fragrance of thy ointments'. Like ointment upon the head, which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron.\n\nBeautifully did he say, upon the head. Which runs down upon the beard. The beard is a mark of manhood: for nature distinguishes woman and man by this sign. The beard therefore is a mark of manhood. The head then is called the divinity, that is, the Godhead; but the beard indicates the man. 'That, he says, we may arrive at the perfect man' who is Christ. See therefore what he says, like ointment. Like the blessing of ointment from the head, that is, from Hermon of the divinity, it ran down upon the beard, it ran down upon the perfect man, who is Christ; and from that beard, after the ointment itself had run down upon the hem of his garment, so the blessing and the dew of Mount Hermon ran down upon Mount Zion. Ointment therefore is here put for blessing. 'Thou hast loved, he says, righteousness, and hated iniquity: therefore God, thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows'. See what he says. Thou hast loved, he says, righteousness, and hated iniquity. It is not enough to love righteousness, but on the contrary see what he says: And thou hast hated iniquity. For these virtues what did he receive? Therefore God, thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows. This is said to Christ: therefore thou art anointed, that thou mightest anoint the others. Thou art anointed with the oil of gladness above thy fellows, that is, more than thy apostles. For thou didst have the fountain of ointment: they had drops. Like ointment upon the head. May the Lord also bestow upon us this ointment of the Godhead, which came upon the beard, that is, upon the perfect man Aaron, upon Aaron the priest. Now Aaron is interpreted 'mountain-man'. You see that this priest is not from the valleys, but is from the mountains. The beard, he says, of Aaron. That the ointment came from the head upon the beard, it came from the Word, God, to the man whom he deigned to take up. And what does this anointed beard, and this perfect man, profit us? Let us see what benefits he has. Which runs down upon the hem of his garment. If we are the garment of Christ, we clothe his nakedness with our faith. Behold, naked he hangs upon the cross, a scandal to the Jews, foolishness to the Gentiles; and yet by our faith and word and confession he is clothed. Noah lay naked in his house, and his shame appeared: the elder son mocked, and the younger covered him. And why do I say all this? To show that we are the garment of Christ. I tell you a new thing: rather, not new, but from the Scriptures. We are the garment of Christ: and when we have clothed him with our confession, we in turn put on Christ himself. For the Apostle also says that Christ is our garment. For when we are baptized, we put on Christ. And we are clothed, and we clothe. Do you wish to know how we clothe the Lord? We read in Jeremiah: 'Take unto thee, he says, a girdle, and go, he says, to the Euphrates, and put it in a hole of the rock. And I did, he says, what the Lord had commanded me. And after some days I returned to the Euphrates, and behold, he says, the girdle had rotted, so that it was fit for no use. And the Lord spoke, he says, to me'. Attentively take heed. 'As, he says, thou hadst a girdle about thy loins, so also I had this people about me, saith the Lord'. Why have I said all this? To show that believers are the garment of Christ. Which runs down upon the hem of his garment: which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron. There is not time to speak of the priestly garments. Indeed the high priest has eight kinds of garments. It is long now to speak of the ephod, of the tunic: it is long to speak of the breastplate, of the manifestations, of the little chains, of the girdle, of the breeches, of the fringes, of the pomegranates, of the little bells, with which virtues the priest ought to enter the temple of the Lord and into the holy of holies. And we therefore, if we wish to be of this priestly garment, either we are the shoulder-piece, which is interpreted the ephod, that we may have good works, and may carry the commandments of Christ upon our shoulders; or surely we ought to be the inner tunic, which tunic is close-fitting, and clings to the body, is not loose, is not spread out, but clings wholly to the body. You see therefore that whoever is inward is also the inner tunic, and is joined more nearly to the body. But if anyone is the breastplate, which is called λόγιον (the rational thing), that one is nowhere else but upon the breast. Take heed to the order of Scripture: whoever is the breastplate, and is the λόγιον, and is reason, is set upon the breast. Yet it can happen that someone has prudence, that he has the λόγιον, and does not have speech. Whoever therefore is a true priest ought to have the λόγιον, ought to have the δηλώσεις, that is, the manifestations, that is, that whatever he has conceived in his mind he may bring forth, and may teach all. And all these things are in vain, if chastity be not guarded and adorned: therefore there is the girdle, and it girds the loins, binds tight and mortifies. Then it is said that he should have the breeches, so that whatever is shameful and filthy in us may, as it were by this girdle, be cleansed and mortified. Then it is said that we ought to have the robe (podere), which adorns as it were our feet. And on that robe itself are various flowers: there are pomegranates there, there are also fringes there. I think that woman too who had an issue of blood touched this fringe, and was healed. Then consider this also, that at the very extremities of the feet, among the pomegranates, there were also the little bells; and the high priest did not enter into the holy of holies unless those little bells sounded. And those little bells and those pomegranates are the diversities of virtues, which each have their own voices; and the very going forth of the holy of holies is vocal. For whether we eat, or whether we drink, we do all things in the name of the Lord. We promised smaller things, and have said greater ones.\n— Homily 45, on Psalm 132"],"PSA.133.3":["Like the dew of Hermon, which runs down upon Mount Zion. The dew of Hermon. We read a certain apocryphal book, that at the time when the sons of God descended to the daughters of men, they descended upon Mount Hermon, and there entered into a covenant, how they might come to the daughters of men, and join them to themselves. It is a most manifest book, and it is reckoned among the apocrypha, and the ancient interpreters have said some things about it: but we say this, not for authority, but for remembrance. Like the dew of Hermon, which runs down upon Mount Zion. I read in a certain man's book, who confirms his own heresy from this apocryphal book. For what does he say? The sons of God, he says, who were descending from the heavens, and came upon Hermon, and lusted after the daughters of men, are, he says, angels descending from the heavenly places, and souls that desired bodies: since indeed the bodies are the daughters of men. Do you see how, without your knowing it, the doctrine of the Manichees arises? For just as the Manichees say that souls desired human bodies, and were joined by the pleasure of the bodies: so these too, who say that the angels desired bodies, that is, the daughters of men, do they not seem to you to say the very thing that the Manichees also say? It is long now to speak against them: but I wished only to point out from what volume, as it were, they confirm their doctrine by occasion of this text. Hermon in our tongue is interpreted ἀνάθεμα (anathema), that is, condemnation. The dew of the Lord therefore was formerly upon the people of the Jews, who were afterwards anathematized; and that blessing, which formerly descended upon the anathematized people, afterwards descended upon Mount Zion, that is, upon the Church. We, if indeed we are of the Church — and by Church I mean the truth of doctrines, the confession of Christ. We are in the building: and how many holy ones are in the desert, how many of us are not in the Church; and those who are not in the Church dwell within the Church of Christ — if therefore we are in the Church, if we have the faith of the Church, the faith of the apostles, the faith of Christ, the truths of the doctrines, we are mountains of Zion. We do not wish to be valleys of Zion, but mountains of Zion. For Zion has its valleys, it has also its plains. Whoever is a sinner is a valley of Zion, he is not a mountain of Zion. Someone may say: You are speaking from your own opinion. Let us call Isaiah as a witness, at the time when Zion had sinned, after many visions, where it is said, A vision against Idumaea, a vision against Moab, a vision against Edom and the sons of Ammon, at the last is placed: 'A vision of the valley of Zion'. For because it had descended from the height of faith, therefore it collapsed from a mountain into a valley. We therefore, first let us flee the valleys of Zion, let us come to the plains, from the plains let us come to the hills, from the hills let us come to the mountains, let us follow Christ upon the mountains. For our cousin, like a roe and a fawn of the harts, leaps over the hills, and springs across the mountains. Whence also, according to the truth of the ascension, he did not ascend into the heavens after the resurrection from a valley, but from a mountain. Unless we are mountains in virtues, we cannot ascend to heaven. For there, that is, in the mountains of Zion, in the virtues, in the confession of faith, in the truth of the doctrines, there is the blessing and life for evermore. Our life is Christ. He who confesses Christ receives life unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 45, on Psalm 132"],"PSA.134.1":["Behold now, bless the Lord, all you servants of the Lord. This is the last psalm of the Song of Ascents. The first of the gradual psalms had been: 'To the Lord, when I was in tribulation, I cried, and he heard me.' In the second he had climbed higher: 'I lifted up my eyes to the mountains.' In the third: 'I rejoiced in the things that were said to me.' In the fourth: 'To you I have lifted up my eyes, you who dwell in heaven.' See how little by little he ascends through the steps, and arrives at loftier things. It would take long, if we wished to recite the openings of the individual psalms, that is, of the steps. He comes to the thirteenth psalm—nay rather, he comes to the twelfth—and after all the virtues he set humility. For in vain do we have virtues, if the virtues themselves are not crowned by the humility of the Lord. For what did he say? 'Lord, my heart is not exalted, nor are my eyes lifted up.' Since therefore he had shown himself to be humble, he asks for the coming of Christ. And what does he say? 'Remember, Lord, David, and all his meekness.' And again in another place: 'For the sake of David your servant, turn not away the face of your Anointed.' Since he had received Christ, after the faith of Christ he gathers the church. And what does he say? 'Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity.' But since the brethren are gathered together in unity, and the church of the Lord is gathered, what does the very last psalm say? Behold now, bless the Lord, all you servants of the Lord. Since it is good and pleasant for brethren to dwell together in unity, and you are gathered together as one: Behold now, bless the Lord. Now bless, after the coming of Christ, because you are gathered in him: now bless, in the last psalm of the steps, because you are gathered at the summit of the virtues. Therefore before you reached the fifteenth step, that is, the height, you could not bless the Lord. So now I say to you, behold now, bless the Lord. When 'behold' is said, in a way the thing is pointed out with the finger. Behold now, bless the Lord. Who are to bless the Lord? All you servants of the Lord: you who are not servants of sin, but servants of the Lord. Do we think it a small thing to say, 'I am a servant of the Lord'? It is the highest virtue to deserve this. Just as a man claims a kind of rank who says, 'I am a servant of the emperor,' and no one dares approach the emperor's servant: so it is an infinite dignity to say that one is a servant of the Lord. And so the apostle too glories in the servitude of the Lord, and writes at the beginning of his epistles: 'Paul, a servant of Christ.' And of Moses it is said: 'And not as Moses my servant, to whom I spoke face to face.' See what he says. To the rest I spoke in a vision and in a riddle: but not as to Moses my servant, to whom I spoke face to face. Let us take an example. Let us suppose there is some very rich master: he has a household, he also has servants who wait upon him: surely the servants who are out on the country estates do not have as much confidence as those who serve him daily? Great is the dignity of the one who sees the master's face and serves him. In short, the other servants, who do not see their master's face, if they wish to obtain something, ask a fellow-servant. Why have I said all this? Our Lord Jesus Christ himself also has a large household: he has those who serve him, as it were, before his face, and he has others out in the fields. Monks and virgins I take to be those who serve their Lord before his face; but the laity, who are as it were in the field, belong to the household. In short, those who are in the fields ask these others to entreat the master, if they have any need. As great, therefore, as is the dignity of those who daily see the master's face, so great is their punishment, if they neglect the master's service. For bad servants, when they are in the field, are wont to fear the master more, because they do not see him: but if they begin to see him daily, they grow contemptuous, and familiarity breeds pride. And so we too ought not, out of habit, to despise the goodness of our Lord. Bless the Lord therefore, all you servants of the Lord, you who are not servants of sin, but servants of the Lord: all you servants of the Lord, who have the Lord alone as God: all you servants of the Lord, over whom anger does not command, over whom lust does not reign, over whom other sins do not reign. Those over whom the vices have no dominion are ruled by the Lord. All you servants of the Lord.\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133","It seems to have stopped here: but see the increase. It is not enough only to be a servant of the Lord, unless the servants themselves also stand. For there are some who stand, others who fall, others who sit, others who lie down. Therefore those who fall, who sit, who lie down—they cannot praise the Lord: you who stand, praise him. But someone may say: Others stand too, there are many churches, there are many congregations. Who then stand? Do the Arians stand? They name Christ—the Eunomians too, and the other heresies: do they then stand? So then, lest he should seem to grant them license to praise the Lord, see what he says. You who stand in the house of the Lord. So that, as the apostle says: 'until I come,' he says, 'that you may know how you ought to order the house of God, which is the church.' The church does not consist in walls, but in the truth of doctrines. The church is there, where the true faith is. Yet fifteen and twenty years ago the heretics possessed all the walls of the churches. For twenty years ago the heretics possessed all these churches. But the true church was there, where the true faith was. Therefore bless the Lord, you who are servants of the Lord. 'Praise is not comely in the mouth of a sinner.' Therefore praise, you who are servants of the Lord: you who both are servants, and stand. You who stand. 'He set,' he says, 'my feet upon a rock.' And to Moses: 'But you, stand here with me.' 'God stood in the assembly of the gods.' And you who stand, not just anywhere, but in the house of God. 'In the courts of the house of our God' is not found in the Hebrew volumes: but it is in the following psalm. For how does it run in that psalm? 'Praise the name of the Lord, praise the Lord, you servants: you who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.' Since therefore this psalm has, 'You who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God,' for this reason someone thought that in the psalm which precedes as well, at 'You who stand in the house of the Lord,' he ought to add, 'in the courts of the house of our God.' But what 'in the courts of the house of our God' signifies, and what the difference is between the house and the courts, will be discussed when the following psalm is read.\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133"],"PSA.134.2":["In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. 'This world is set in the power of the evil one,' and 'our struggle is not against flesh and blood, but against the rulers of these darknesses.' Whence the apostle also says: 'The night is past, and the day is at hand.' Therefore this present age is the night: but the age to come is the day. Whence the apostle says: 'The night is past, and the day is at hand. Let us walk honestly, as in the day,' he says. As we are to live in the day, that is, in the age to come, so let us now live. In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. Whether we will or not, we are in the nights: as long as we are in this age, we are in the nights. The night has darkness: and the nights of these darknesses are the sins of men. See, therefore, what he says: In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. 'While,' he says, 'you are in this age and are in the nights, lift up your hands.' Do not let them down, but lift them up: lift them up with Moses. If you lift up your hands, Amalek is conquered: but if you let them down, Joshua is overcome. And at the same time consider the mystery. If we lift up our hands, Jesus conquers. If we lift up our hands in good works, through our good works Christ overcomes the devil. And hands are interpreted as works. Whence it is written: 'The word of the Lord came by the hand of Haggai,' 'The word which came by the hand of Jeremiah the prophet.' Surely the word of the Lord does not come by the hand, but comes in the mouth. But see the mysteries of Scripture. God does not come to speech, except to good works. For a murderer too confesses Christ, and yet the Lord does not come to him, because his hands do not deserve the Lord's coming. Whence it is written in another place: 'In the hand of the tongue are death and life.' Does the tongue then have a hand? Tell me, why is it said, 'In the hand of the tongue are death and life'? See what he means. God does not hear the tongue, not words, but works: so that whoever says something may also do it. It is commanded us, therefore, in the last psalm of the steps, In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. 'Lift up,' he says, 'your hands, because Jesus too lifted his up on the cross.' In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. Why did he wish to add, and to say, 'unto the holy place'? Heretics too seem to lift up their hands, and pagans seem to lift up their hands, and Jews seem to lift up their hands. For they too give alms: if they see a poor man, they hold out their hand. When a Jew gives alms, does he not seem to you to lift up his hand? He does indeed lift up his hand, but he does not lift it up unto the holy place, because he does not confess Christ. But you who are in the house of the Lord, you who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God: you who are servants of the Lord, to whom I had said, 'Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity': lift up your hands in such a way that you lift them up in the holy, that is, that you confess Christ, and that whatever you do, you do for Christ. Good deeds differ according to the character of the one doing them. For example, a Christian gives alms, a pagan also gives, a Jew also gives, and another Christian, a holy man, gives too. You may find some among Christians who give alms for this reason, that they may be praised by the people. Whenever a poor man asks, they look about this way and that; and unless they see a witness, they give no money. If he is alone, the hand is more closed; he does not give willingly. O you Christian, a poor man asks you to give him money: why do you not give in secret, but give in public? If you seek God as your witness, why do you look for human eyes? Your almsgiving seems to be almsgiving among the ignorant, but before God it is an injury: for you do an injury to your brother, since you wish to put the beggar on display while others look on. When, therefore, we give, let us not give as if from what is our own, but from what is Christ's: we ought not to give as to a beggar, but as to a brother. We give carnal things, he gives spiritual things: the poor man gives more than he receives. We give bread, which is consumed that very day; he for the bread will repay us the kingdoms of heaven. So this is what I say: that whatever we do, we should do in the holy; that what we do, we should do for Christ's sake. Moreover there are many pagans who give alms: if they see someone with a hand cut off, if they see him with a cancerous and rotten foot, at once their soul grieves: the human soul is overcome by natural pity. And what does such a one say? 'Look, if I were like this, wretch that I am, what would I do? And it may happen that I become like this: who will give to me? So I too ought to give to this man: so that someone may give to me as well, if I become like him.' This man indeed gives the poor man bread; but in the poor man he pities himself, not the other. This mercy is not the mercy of Christ, but mercy for oneself. Everything, therefore, that we do, let us do for Christ's sake, let us do in the holy. 'Whether we eat or whether we drink, let us do all things in the name of Christ.' We read the Scriptures, we learn the psalms by heart, we hold fast the gospels, we expound the prophets: we ought not to do this in such a way as to boast among the brethren, but so as to please Christ, so that his word may resound in our mouth. Men are wont, and monks are wont, and even poor little women are wont to have this rivalry among themselves, to learn more of the Scriptures by heart; and they think themselves the better in this, if they have learned more by heart. He has learned more who does more: for what you learn by heart, I do: my works retain the Scriptures more than your speech, which resounds emptily. In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place. Lift up your hands, lift up the banner of the cross. In the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place: and when you have lifted them up unto the holy place, and have done the things that are holy, let Christ be, as it were, the healing balm of your good works. And bless the Lord. Whence the apostle too says: 'In every place lifting up pure hands.' And bless the Lord. Whatever you do, refer it to the Lord. Let the hands be lifted, let the mouth resound with Christ. In the nights lift up your hands: give alms to the poor man. And bless the Lord: give thanks to the Lord, who gave to you the means from which you might give to your brother. You should give thanks to Christ more, when you have given, than the brother who received from you gives thanks to you. The poor confer a great benefit on us. Our sins, which we can no longer wash away in any other way, almsgiving extinguishes. For what is written? 'As water extinguishes fire, so almsgiving extinguishes sins.' Almsgiving accomplishes the same as baptism. As baptism forgives us our sins, so almsgiving too forgives us our sins. As water extinguishes fire, so almsgiving extinguishes sins. For the fires of gehenna are prepared for sins: but almsgiving extinguishes them.\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133"],"PSA.134.3":["May the Lord bless you out of Sion, he who made heaven and earth. You bless the Lord, and the Lord blesses you. Out of Sion: from the watchtower, from the church, from true doctrines, from pious faith. May the Lord bless you out of Sion. These little verses the Holy Spirit speaks, or certainly the prophet speaks in the Spirit. And what does he say? O you who through the fifteen steps have climbed to the last, who have done this and that, who bless the Lord, who in the nights lift up your hands unto the holy place and bless the Lord—receive, you too, your reward. May the Lord bless you out of Sion. What you have given, receive back. You gave alms, you performed good deeds: from God you obtain a reward and a blessing. May the Lord bless you out of Sion. Out of Sion: from the heavenly Jerusalem, in the mother of the firstborn, where the joys to come are, where the archangels are, where the other powers are, where the apostles, where the prophets, where the saints, where the martyrs, where the multitudes of angels and of saints follow, wherever the Lamb goes. In short, John too saw the holy city let down from heaven, built up with gems, having twelve gates. It was all golden: it had golden walls, and it had streets of various gems. In this city Christ reigns, in this city the inhabitants themselves are both inhabitants and gates, they themselves are both houses and inhabitants. Do you wish to know that they are houses? Christ dwells in them, Christ walks about in them. 'I will dwell,' he says, 'in them, and I will walk about.' See how great the holy soul is: it contains Christ, whom heaven does not contain. See what he says. 'I will dwell,' he says. But it can happen that someone dwells in cramped quarters. 'I will dwell, and I will walk about in them.' Wherever he walks about, surely the house is spacious, where there is room to walk. 'You are,' he says, 'the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you.' Let us prepare our temple, that Christ may come and dwell in us: that our soul too may be Sion, that it may be a watchtower, that it may be set upon the heights, that it may be above, not below. We read in the prophet Zechariah: 'And the angel,' he says, 'who spoke in me, said to me.' If an angel spoke in the prophet, how much more does he who dwells in us have us both as his houses and as his conversation-partners?\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133","May the Lord bless you out of Sion, he who made heaven and earth. If you are holy, hear what he says, 'who made heaven': if you are a sinner, 'who made earth.' He made both heaven and earth. For the moment let us speak according to the letter. He said this to distinguish God from the idols. 'The gods,' he says, 'who have not made heaven and earth, let them perish from the earth.' 'But the Lord made the heavens.' This he says to distinguish God from the idols. This much for now according to the letter. But according to the spiritual understanding, just as it is said to the sinner, 'You are earth and unto earth you shall go,' so also to the holy one it is said, 'You are heaven and unto heaven you shall go.' Why have I said all this? To prove that the saints are heaven. 'The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims the works of his hands.' 'And let the waters that are above the heavens praise the name of the Lord.' 'The heavens of heavens are the Lord's.' Who made heaven and earth. Although we are earthly, although we walk upon the earth, yet our citizenship is in the heavens: there we have our commonwealth. Who made heaven and earth. Although you are a sinner, do not despair: the Lord is almighty. Many from earth become heaven, and many from heaven become earth. Unhappy Judas was heaven, and became earth. The apostle Paul, at the time when he was persecuting the church, was earth: he confessed, and became heaven. Neither should he who is heaven be complacent; nor should he who is earth despair of life. Jacob, at the time when he was fleeing his brother into Mesopotamia, came to Luz: and there, it says, he placed a stone for himself at his head. Under his head the stone was Christ. He never placed a stone at his head, except at the time when he fled his persecutor. When he remained in his father's house, as long as he was in his father's house and had the comforts of the flesh, he did not have a stone at his head. He departed from his home naked, he departed with a staff; and at once, that very night, he found a stone, and placed it at his head. Because he had such a pillow on which to take his rest, see his vision. 'He saw,' it says, 'a ladder reaching from the earth to heaven; and he saw,' it says, 'angels descending and ascending.' He saw angels coming from heaven to earth, and others ascending from earth to the heavens. Do you wish to know that that stone, which was at Jacob's head, was Christ the cornerstone? 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner.' That stone which is written of in the book of Kingdoms, Ebenezer. That stone is Christ. And Ebenezer is interpreted 'stone of help.' 'Jacob arose,' it says, 'in the morning.' And what did he say? 'This,' he says, 'is the house of God.' And what did he do? 'He took,' it says, 'oil, and anointed the stone.' Unless we understand the mysteries of the Scriptures, what reason was there that he should anoint the stone with oil?\n— Homily 46, on Psalm 133"],"PSA.135.4":["Often considering with myself what the word περιούσιον might mean, and asking the wise men of this age whether they had perhaps read it anywhere, I could never find anyone to explain to me what it signified. Therefore I was compelled to have recourse to the old instrument, from which I supposed the Apostle too had taken what he had said. For a Hebrew of the Hebrews, and as to the law a Pharisee, he was surely setting down in his Epistle what he knew he had read in the Old Testament. So in Deuteronomy I found: \"For you are a holy people to the Lord your God, and it has pleased the Lord your God in you that you should be to him a people περιούσιον out of all the peoples that are upon the face of the earth\" (Deut. 7:6). And in the hundred and thirty-fourth Psalm, where we have, \"Sing psalms to his name, for it is sweet: for the Lord has chosen Jacob for himself, Israel for a possession (in possessione) for himself\" (Ps. 134:3–4): for what is \"for a possession\" (in possessione), in the Greek it is written εἰς περιουσιασμὸν, which Aquila and the Fifth Edition indeed expressed as εἰς περιούσιον. But the Seventy and Theodotion, translating περιουσιασμὸν, made a change of syllable, not of sense. Symmachus, then, for what is περιούσιον in the Greek, SGOLLA (סגלה) in the Hebrew, expressed ἐξαίρετον, that is, \"choice\" (egregium), or \"special\" (præcipuum); for which word, using the Latin language in another volume, he rendered \"peculiar\" (peculiarem).\n— Commentary on Titus"],"PSA.135.19-20":["A certain writer sets down as the spiritual wings of the earth, by which we fly up to heavenly things, four kinds of believers: the house of Aaron and the house of Levi and the house of Israel and those who fear the Lord — of whom the psalmist sings: House of Israel, bless the Lord; house of Aaron, bless the Lord; house of Levi, bless the Lord; you who fear the Lord, bless the Lord —, understanding by Aaron the priesthood, by Levi the temple-keepers and ministers, by Israel the whole people, by those who fear the proselytes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.136.1":["Confess to the Lord, for he is good. And yet, even though heretics say that there is no repentance, still the prophet urges us to confess our sins. For God is good. Blessed, therefore, is he who confesses his sins, just as the apostle also says: \"I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God.\" If an apostle confesses, how much more the sinner? And again: \"The just man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech.\" If, then, the just man is, how much more the sinner?\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.2":["Confess to the God of gods. He means these gods, of whom it is written: \"I have said, you are gods.\" And again: \"God stood in the synagogue of the gods.\" Those who cease to be men, and abandon their vices, and are made perfect: these are gods, and sons of the Most High.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.3":["Confess to the Lord of lords. This is referred to the Son, and \"to the God of gods\" to the Father. To the Father, then, and to the Son we confess: for the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.4":["Who alone does great wonders. First he said, \"Confess to the God of gods\"; and again, \"to the Lord of lords.\" And since he named two persons, now he says, \"Who alone does great wonders,\" so that he may not speak of two gods. For it is written: \"One God, from whom are all things; and one Lord Jesus, through whom are all things, and we through him.\"\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.5":["Who made the heavens with understanding. These are the heavens which \"declare the glory of God,\" who have put on the image of the heavenly One: and there are heavens who have not put on the image of the earthly one. With understanding, in wisdom: for Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God, just as Wisdom herself also says in Proverbs: \"When he made the heavens, I was with him,\" and the rest.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.6":["Who established the earth upon the waters. Although the earth is said to hang upon the waters, that is, to hang in the sea, yet he founded and established our earth in baptism. He did not say, \"he lifted up,\" or \"he exalted,\" or \"he made it ascend into the heavens.\" For baptism forgives sins, and frees us as if from prison: but it cannot grant the kingdoms of heaven. For unless someone enters through works and faith, he cannot be secure.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.7":["Who made the great lights. According to the letter he speaks of the stars. Although we behold them as small, yet they are great. For just as with us, if we climb a high mountain, and see a man in the valley below, he seems to us as if something small: so also the stars are great, but from their height they seem small to us. For the star that is seen in Britain appears everywhere as well. But he says this to those to whom he said: \"You are the light of the world.\"\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.13":["Who divided the Red Sea into divisions. The Hebrews say that the sea was divided into twelve divisions, that is, one way for each tribe. And it is called \"Red\" from the blood.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.15":["And he shook off Pharaoh and his army in the Red Sea. Well did he say, \"he shook off\": for he shook off his sins, and he did not do penance; therefore he died. Just as he also says to Jerusalem: \"Shake off your dust.\"\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.17":["Who struck the great kings. First he struck Pharaoh, who is understood to be the devil, and thus his army.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.23":["For in our humility he was mindful of us. He did not say, \"in wisdom\": he did not say, \"in riches\": he did not say, \"in learning\"; but, \"in humility he was mindful of us.\" For although these too are virtues; yet unless someone has humility, he is not received by God. \"For God resists the proud, but to the humble he gives grace.\"\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.24":["And he redeemed us from our enemies. He did not say, \"he freed,\" but \"he redeemed,\" that is, with his own blood.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.136.25":["Who gives food to all flesh. Consider for me the elephant, the camel, the ox, the stag, and these great four-footed beasts, how each one has on its face eyes, nostrils, mouth, feet. But consider also the tiny creatures, that is, the fly, the ant, the flea and the rest, and in these tiny ones consider also the eyes, and the nostrils, and the mouth, and the feet, and all things in like manner as in the greater ones, and glorify the Lord: and to all these he furnishes food. Otherwise, as it is written: \"And I will take away from them the heart of stone, and I will give them a heart of flesh,\" that is, a soft one, with which they may be able to receive the law of God. For unless someone turns from his hardness, and becomes soft, he cannot receive food, and the bread which came down from heaven, so that he may be able to say: \"Give us this day our supersubstantial bread,\" that is, the bread which is of your substance. In the Hebrew Gospel according to Matthew it has thus: \"Give us this day our bread for tomorrow,\" that is, the bread which you are going to give us in your kingdom, give us today.\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PSA.137.1":["By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down and wept, when we remembered Sion. If someone is not sick, he does not know how much health is worth. And again, a lame man does not know how much he is worth who is sound of foot. Likewise the blind man does not know the one who sees. All this has been said because the psalm says: \"By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down and wept, when we remembered Sion.\" Babylon is interpreted as \"confusion\": therefore Babylon is this world. So the sinner who fell from paradise, and came into the valley of tears — that is, into Babylon, into this world — says (or rather the prophet, in the person of the one who fell, says): \"By the rivers of Babylon.\" Now, the rivers of Babylon. When you see someone who is beautiful for a moment, and being sick he loses his beauty, and his flower withers — this passes like a river, and the waters that follow one another, coming and receding, perish, and again others rise up, and these too perish: so also in this world, because for a moment or for some short time we are either enriched, or we are strong, or we are beautiful, and after a little while it passes like water, and perishes. There, then, we sat down and wept, when we remembered Sion. Granted that it was indeed by our own fault that we fell from paradise, nevertheless we call to mind our former happiness, and we do not forget it. And that man who went down from Jerusalem to Jericho — which is interpreted \"moon,\" that is, that which is always changing (and this is understood to mean this world) — fell among robbers, and the Samaritan takes him up, and heals him with oil and wine, mingling severity with mercy.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.2":["On the willows in the midst of it we hung up our instruments. The willow has such a nature: it is indeed a barren tree, and it is said that if anyone takes a drink from it or eats of it, he does not beget children. There, then, we hung up our instruments. Now the instrument is the body of man. For just as an instrument is put together out of many pipes, yet sends forth one melody by its tuning, so too we have our instrument, the sense of touch: through it, that is, through works, we render back to God melody and song and hymn. Likewise through hearing, and through smell, and through taste, and through sight, and through all these faculties, as though from one instrument, we render back hymn and song to the Lord. It is said, again, that the willow has no root; but if a branch is cut off, and is planted beside the water, it at once grows green. So too the sinner, although he has sinned, although he has fallen: nevertheless, if he is beside the waters — that is, if he reads the holy Scriptures, and hears the divine word from a holy man — his soul grows green, and is turned to repentance.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.3":["For there they that led us away captive asked of us the words of songs. The demons, who deceived us and led us away captive, now, as though mocking us and boasting, say to us: \"Give us back a word about your faith, and just as when you were in Jerusalem, sing a hymn to God.\"\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.4":["How shall we sing the Lord's song in a strange land? They answer, those who have sinned and been deceived, and they say: \"How shall we sing you the Lord's song in a strange land, set as we are in our sins, we who have fallen from Jerusalem into Babylon?\" Therefore no one can praise the Lord unless he is holy. For the sinner ought not to sing to the Lord, but to mourn his sins.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.5":["If I forget you, Jerusalem, let my right hand be given over to forgetfulness. Therefore, because he has sinned and fallen, he calls to mind his former way of life and Jerusalem, and he sets before himself this resolve: that unless I shall always be mindful of you, and always hold you in mind, let my right hand be given over to forgetfulness — that is, my works. He did not say the left hand, but the right: for the left hand is always in forgetfulness. Do you wish, then, always to be in remembrance of the Lord? Do works of the right hand.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.6":["Let my tongue cleave to my jaws, if I do not remember you. That is, let me have neither works nor speech, if the memory of you is not continually turned over in my heart.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.7":["Remember, O Lord, the children of Edom, in the day of Jerusalem. Edom is interpreted as \"earthly\" and \"bloody.\" Who then is this, if not the devil? See, then, what he says. This is spoken in the person of Adam: \"Lord, remember how the serpent, the devil — who is understood to be Nebuchadnezzar — deceived me and dragged me off, and cast me out of paradise. Remember and repay him, and let him feel the punishment which he by his own malice brought upon me, dragging me and leading me into Babylon and to the Assyrians.\"\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.8-9":["Their infants will be dashed to pieces before their eyes: their houses will be plundered, and their wives will be violated. This is what David prophesies in the spirit: Wretched daughter of Babylon, blessed is he who will repay you your recompense, which you have paid out to us. Blessed is he who will take and dash your little ones against the rock (Ps 136 [137]:8). So great will be the devastation of the city and the savagery of the victors that not even innocent age will be spared, that all the wealth of the houses will be plundered, and the chastity of wives will be violated before their husbands' eyes.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.137.8":["Wretched daughter of Babylon. The daughter of Babylon is the soul which is always in motion and in confusion, and has no stability, but is in vices and in sins — which is truly wretched. \"Blessed is he who shall repay you your recompense which you have repaid to us.\" Blessed is he who shall repay her what she herself has done. For example: she cast me out of paradise; I, through abstinence, bring her back there. She deceived me into fornication; I, through chastity, bring her back into Jerusalem, and out of vices into virtue. Therefore I do this not as an enemy, but as a friend.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136"],"PSA.137.9":["Blessed is he who shall take hold of and dash your little ones against the rock. The \"little ones\" are said to be thoughts. For example: I have seen a woman, I have desired her: at once, if I do not cut off that desire, and, as it were, take it by the foot and dash it against the rock while the desire is small, it cannot afterwards be cut off when it has grown. Blessed, then, is he who cuts it off at once, and dashes it against the rock. And the Rock is Christ.\n— Homily 48, on Psalm 136","And would that we too might be stirred up by the Lord, and that this power might be given to us, that we should desire neither the silver nor the gold of worldly eloquence and wisdom, but should kill the sons of the heretics, and of all who have been deceived, with spiritual arrows, that is, with the testimonies of the Scriptures: and that those who are nourished with the milk of error may be slaughtered without any mercy, so that they may perish by a merciful cruelty, and that we may have pity on no infancy, and may be worthy of that beatitude: Blessed is he who will take hold of his little ones and dash them against the rock!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PSA.138.1":["I will confess to you, O Lord, with my whole heart. As the wounds are, so too is the poultice. For just as the body has various wounds, so too the soul has its passions and its wounds; and against sin we ought to perform a corresponding repentance. If someone confesses for all his sins, that man confesses to the Lord with his whole heart. For example, someone has committed fornication, and he confesses for the fornication: but since he is either greedy, or wrathful, or a slanderer, or a blasphemer, and has very many vices, that man does not confess with his whole heart. But if someone performs repentance for all the vices and passions of his soul, that man can say: I will confess to you, O Lord, with my whole heart. For you have heard all the words of my mouth. This little verse is not found in the Hebrew. But as for what it says, this is the meaning: Therefore I will confess to you, O Lord, with my whole heart — that is, for all my vices and sins, since I have confessed to you not for one vice alone, and you have heard me.\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137","And in the sight of the angels I will sing psalms to you. See the advancement of the penitent, that after confession and repentance he is counted worthy to sing psalms with the angels. But he who sings psalms no longer performs repentance, but gives thanks, and blesses.\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137"],"PSA.138.2":["I will worship toward your holy temple. If this is the temple which now is seen to be deserted, in which one ought to worship: then the Jews ought not to worship, since the temple has been overthrown. But by \"temple\" he means the holy and heavenly Jerusalem.\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137","Upon your mercy and your truth. Let the sinner hear \"mercy,\" but let the just and proud man hear \"truth.\"\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137"],"PSA.138.3":["You will multiply strength in my soul. You will multiply: that is, that the soul is subject to many anxieties.\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137"],"PSA.138.4":["Let all the kings of the earth confess to you, O Lord. The kings of the earth are those who reign in sin. Therefore let those very ones confess, who reign in sin. For he is not speaking of the kings of this world. For it is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" Do you suppose that the heart of the most impious Julian was in the hand of God? God forbid. Or of Nero, or of the persecutors Maximian and Decius? God forbid. But he is speaking of those who reign in sin, because those who conquer the vices and passions of the soul and reign over sin, their heart is in the hand of God. Because they have heard all the words of your mouth: because they have kept your commandments.\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137"],"PSA.138.5":["And let them sing in the ways of the Lord. The philosophers of this world did not sing in the ways of the Lord, but we sing in the ways of the Lord, that is, in Moses and Isaiah and Jeremiah: and from these ways we come to that way which says, \"I am the way.\"\n— Homily 49, on Psalm 137"],"PSA.139.16":["Which Scripture also attests in the hundred and thirty-eighth (139th) psalm: Your eyes saw my imperfect being. And the sense is: Before I was made, before I was shaped into limbs, while I was still contained in the seed, your eyes saw me.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PSA.140.1":["Snatch me away, O Lord, from the evil man. The devil cannot deceive us so much by himself as he can through the instrument of a man; and it is from this that the prophet prays to be delivered.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.2":["Who have devised iniquities in their heart. He is speaking about the heretics.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.3":["The venom of asps is under their lips. For they seem to have words of friendship, yet they are full of death-dealing poison.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.5":["Beside the path they have set a stumbling-block for me. That is: by bringing forward testimonies from the Scriptures, which seemed to have some semblance of reason, they were trying to deceive me.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139","This is what we read in the psalm: Beside the path they have set a stumbling block for me. For unless one has seen the path of God, that is, has heard the name of Christ, he will not enter upon it. Therefore the heretics too, under the name of Christ, have set snares beside the path, so that he who believes that he walks in Christ, of whom we read that he himself is the way, may tread also in the snares of those who have contrived madness in the house of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"PSA.140.6":["I said to the Lord: You are my God. Unless a man is holy, he cannot say this little verse; he who is not reigned over by sin, he it is who says, 'The Lord is my portion.'\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.8":["Do not hand me over, O Lord, from my desire to the sinner. Scripture says that 'if anyone looks upon a woman to lust after her, he has already committed adultery with her in his heart.' So this is what the prophet is saying: Because of my concupiscence, do not hand me over to the devil; rather, it is well for me to be judged in the deed, and not in the thought.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.9":["The head of their compassing about, the labor of their own lips shall cover them. Just as a serpent, when it sees someone coming to strike it, makes a coil of its whole body and hides its head in the middle, where it knows its life resides: so too these heretics, by the discourse of the philosophers and of Aristotle and the rest, seem to hide themselves, and as it were to shield and defend themselves. But the man of the Church, as it were with a rod — that is, with the cross — unwinds the coil, and finds the hidden head, and strikes it there; that is, he brings forth a testimony from the Scriptures.\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.140.13":["The upright shall dwell with your countenance. And what is the countenance of God, if not Christ? 'Who thought it not robbery to be equal with God.'\n— Homily 50, on Psalm 139"],"PSA.141.1":["O Lord, I have cried to you, hear me. Moses was standing with the people, and Pharaoh was pursuing behind his back, and on every side there was distress for him. At that time he wept to God, and immediately God said to him: 'Why do you cry out to me?' While he was praying in silence, he was heard; yet Scripture does not record that he spoke, but only that he wept. And the blood of the martyrs always cries out to the Lord, and it is heard. And the Lord spoke to Cain: 'The blood of your brother cries out to me.' And in the Apocalypse of John the souls of the righteous beneath the altar were saying to the Lord: 'How long, O Lord, will you not vindicate us?' And at the same time let us consider that the souls of the righteous are an altar to the Lord. Attend to my voice, when I have cried out to you. That is, see and consider, O Lord, what I ask of you: for I do not ask of you a bodily desire, or gold, or any such thing of this world, but that I may obtain mercy. Therefore he says, attend, that is, listen carefully, because I ask nothing that is foreign.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.141.2":["Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight. That is, let my prayer come straight to you, having no filth or faults or diabolical thoughts. Now incense is the prayers of the saints. And this we know from the Apocalypse, when the twenty-four elders held censers and said: 'These are the prayers of the saints.' The lifting up of my hands an evening sacrifice. The lifting up of the hands points to the Lord, and in the lifting up of the hands we behold the Creator. But notice that he did not say, the stretching out of my hands. For stretching out is not lifting up; but one stretches out both behind his back and to the side, that is, into faults, just as the Lord too says: 'When you stretch out your hands to me, I will not hear you.' The evening sacrifice, of the eleventh hour: that is, in the Gospel I lift up my hands. And again the Apostle: 'Lift up your hands to prayers.' And when Moses lifted up his hands, Jesus was winning; but when he lowered them, he was being beaten. If I understand the Scriptures according to the history and according to the letter, Moses holds his hands downward: but if spiritually, he lifts his hands on high.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140","For there shall come out of Zion one to rescue and to overturn iniquities from Jacob, when from the east and the west incense is offered to the name of the Lord in every place, and a pure sacrifice, by no means in the victims of the Old Testament, but in the holiness of evangelical purity; of which incense we read elsewhere too: Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight. And what this incense is, the words that follow show: The lifting up of my hands an evening sacrifice.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PSA.141.3":["Set, O Lord, a guard to my mouth. 'Death and life are in the hands of the tongue.' And again: 'Amen I say to you, that for every idle word which you have spoken, you shall render an account on the day of judgment.' This therefore the prophet asks, that everything he speaks may not be idle, but holy and worthy of God. And set a door of enclosure to my lips. He asks for a door of enclosure like a wall around the whole house, so that he may be overcome in nothing. For Jeremiah says: 'Death has entered through your windows.' Now we have five windows: that is, sight, hearing, taste, smell, and touch. If therefore I have looked at a woman to lust after her, I have already committed adultery with her in my heart; and death has entered through my window, that is, through sight. If the lyre or the organ or the reed-pipe as it were dissolves me, death has entered through my hearing. But if I touch something soft, and am unstrung by the softness of the body, death has entered through my touch, and the rest likewise. Therefore this the prophet begs for, a door of enclosure, that is, that his whole house may be fortified, so that the adversary may have no place by which to enter and to lord it over men. For it is said in the epistle of St. Peter: 'Our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion seeking whom he may devour.' The devil does not quickly fight against anyone by great faults, but by small ones; so that he may somehow or other enter, and lord it over the man, and afterward drive him into greater faults. Not through fornication, not through avarice, but through lesser faults does he enter.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.141.4":["Incline not my heart to words of malice, to make excuses for excuses in sins. O most wretched human race, who excuse our sins, saying, I was overcome by nature: when it is in our power both to sin and not to sin. We always excuse ourselves, and say: I did not want to sin, my flesh overcame me. That woman came to me, she sent for me, she touched me, she said that to me, she called me; and instead of doing penance and saying, Lord, I have sinned, we make excuses and join sins to sins. We all have an equal body, but different labors. 'God is no respecter of persons.' Do you wish to know that we and the saints have an equal body? Paul the Apostle says: 'But I see another law fighting against the law of my mind, and taking me captive in the law of sin.' And again after this: 'Therefore I chastise my body, and bring it into subjection, lest, preaching to others, I myself should be found reprobate.' And again: 'Wretched man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?' We have, therefore, different labors; and as much as each one labors, so much reward also does he receive.\n\nWith men that work iniquity. From the common sense it is understood: Incline not my heart with men that work iniquity. And I will not communicate with their chosen ones. Just as the Lord has his chosen saints, so the devil too has his own chosen ones. Understand for me the leaders of the heretics, and you do not doubt about their chosen ones. For if he had said the chosen ones, and not with the qualification 'their,' he would now be excluding the chosen of God. 'I will communicate' is understood in two ways: either I will consent, or I will become a second, that is, unclean. For you know that Scripture calls two things unclean. The Apostle says: 'That he might create both, that is, two men into one new man': two, the interior and the exterior; that is, making the unclean holy. And again Noah brought into the ark with him seven and seven of the clean, but two and two of the unclean.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140","We read in the prophet David: To make excuses in sins, because many put forward, as it were, just pretexts for their sins, so that what they commit by will they may seem to sin by necessity.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"PSA.141.5":["The just shall correct me in mercy, and shall rebuke me. 'Whom the Lord loves he corrects, and he chastises every son whom he receives.' Blessed is he who is corrected here: 'the Lord will not requite twice for the same thing.' It is a great wrath of the Lord, when here he is not angry with us: for he reserves us like a calf for the slaughter. For to Jerusalem he says: 'Many are your sins and many your iniquities, therefore I will not be angry with you.' That is, when you were only an adulteress, I was jealous over you as one who loves you; but when you had many lovers, I despised you, and I will not be angry with you. Just as a man is jealous over his wife when he loves her; but if he is not jealous, he hates her, and does not attain to the words of him who says: 'I will visit their iniquities with a rod.' And again: 'And I will not visit their daughters when they have committed fornication.' The just shall correct me in mercy, and shall rebuke me. Set here some teacher, or father, or physician. If a physician sees rotten flesh in a body, and says, What is that to me? know that he is cruel. But if he cuts it away, and applies the cautery, he is merciful: because he saves the man. Likewise a teacher too, if he lets the boy go, and does not demand discipline of him, he hates him. But if he demands it, and like a physician heals him; what is thought to be harshness is clemency. And Ahab is reproved by the Lord concerning Naboth, when he killed him, and took his vineyard, and shed innocent blood. Elijah the prophet was sent to him, who said: 'You have killed, and moreover you have taken possession.' At once, stricken in conscience, he bowed down his neck and his eyes: and this an impious king, and clothed in purple. And afterward Scripture says that he went and put on sackcloth beneath the purple. And God, seeing this, said: 'Because Ahab has reverenced me, I will not bring evil upon him.' See therefore how much the sackcloth availed, and the fasting, and how much blood a little tear washed away. So then one ought to wear sackcloth and to fast in such a way that no one sees. 'Shall correct' is understood in two ways: either he will reprove, or he will teach.\n\nBut let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head. 'My people, they who call you blessed lead you astray.' The praise of heretics and their oil, with which they anoint the heads of men and promise them the kingdoms of heaven, fattens the head. Now every fat thing Scripture reproves, as it says: 'And Jacob ate, and was filled, and the beloved kicked back, he grew thick, he grew fat, he grew broad.' But the manna of the angels is thin.\n\nFor my prayer also is still in their good pleasures. This he says: They indeed were raging; but I was praying for them, that they might be converted.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140","The opposing powers too have the smoothness of a counterfeit oil, promising sweet things through the heretics: things which are turned into bitterness, and which the prophet detests, recalling: But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PSA.141.6":["Their judges have been swallowed up, joined to the rock. As elsewhere he says: 'Blessed is he who shall take hold of and dash your little ones against the rock.' 'And the rock was Christ.' Now the little ones are the lesser and small thoughts before they grow up. And now these very heretics, although through Aristotle and Plato they seem to despise the simplicity of the Church, yet when they have come to the Scriptures they are at once swallowed up by the rock, that is, by Christ, and are converted to him. For 'swallowing up' is said for victory, as the Apostle says: 'Death is swallowed up in victory. Where, O death, is your victory? where, O hell, is your sting?' Now he calls that very swallowing up a victory, the victory which it had in swallowing.\n\nThey shall hear my words, for they have prevailed. The Lord himself says: Those who have come to me have been overcome, and have been delighted by my words, and have been converted to me in my Church.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.141.7":["As the thickness of the earth. Everything thick is near to the earth: and the soul that sins clings to the earth. For the Lord from the beginning made all things thin. By his own fault, therefore, each one is made thick, and is drawn down to the earth. Let us give an example. As a wound in the body, swelling, is thick, and is full of pus, when the physician comes he cuts it, and heals it and makes it thin, then it is brought back to its own nature, as it was also created. Therefore he says: As the thickness of the earth has been broken up over the earth.\n\nAnd the Savior — Our bones are scattered beside hell — in the twenty-first psalm says: 'All my bones are scattered.' If we understand according to the letter, it does not stand: since he was only crucified. When you see Arius, Eunomius outside the Church, understand the bones of the Lord to be scattered beside hell. The bone, which is strong: those very greater ones, who seem to be something through their much talking and philosophy and contention, are led astray by the heretics.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.141.9":["Keep me from the snare which they have set for me. Always either heretics or demons stretch snares for us. For faults are near to virtues. They have set a snare for me in almsgiving, if I stretch out my hand so that men may see, and, as though doing a good deed, I come into a fault and into sin. But if I give a tunic to a brother so that another may see, they have set a snare for me. Always nets are stretched for stags and snares are set, where the hunter has caught their tracks. Now the stag is taken for the saints: because it is lofty, and has great horns, and kills serpents. Therefore it is said elsewhere: 'Who made my feet like those of stags.' For it is said to be of such power that with the breath of its nostrils it draws serpents out of their caverns and thus devours them. Those very serpents, therefore, that is, the demons, set stumbling-blocks for us in our very tracks where we walk: in the virtues, in almsgiving, that we may do it before men for the sake of vainglory: in fasting, in prayer, in wearing sackcloth, and showing it to men. For he who wears sackcloth ought to mourn his sins, not to boast and show it to men. And from the stumbling-blocks of them that work iniquity. That is, that I may not stumble.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.141.10":["The sinners shall fall into his net. Above he said, Keep me from the snare which they have set for me, concerning many; but now concerning one, The sinners shall fall into his net. Just as the Lord casts a net, and catches a multitude of fish, and has the apostles as fishermen gathering and bringing to him, that is, those who believe through them: so also the devil has demons, who are subject to him, who both deceive men and bring them to him.\n\nI am alone until I pass over. Jeremiah says: 'I sat alone, for I was filled with bitterness.' That is, I was in penitence. Alone: clean, not a second. And Moses says: 'Passing over, I will see this great vision.' Unless he passes over, that is, all the faults, he cannot see the great vision. Now the vision was great, because the bush was burning, and was not consumed: the thorns were burning, that is, the sins. By this fire Isaiah too is cleansed: for this is the coal which touched his lips, which was upon the altar. Of this coal the prophet David also makes mention: 'The sharp arrows of the mighty one with desolating coals.' This coal is a desolator: it makes solitude, and purges of sins.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"PSA.142.1":["The intelligence of David when he was in the cave, a prayer. This title is prescribed according to history, indeed, when David was fleeing from the face of Saul in the desert of Engaddi, hidden in a cave; and Saul entered, unaware, as if on his own account. But since it has in its heading \"The intelligence of David,\" we ought to understand the psalm spiritually. This psalm is indeed taken also as referring to the Lord, the true David; and Saul is understood as the devil, the cave as this world, and the fact that the devil does not send anything good into this world, but dung, and whatever is rotten. Now this world is understood as a cave, because it does not have perfect light in comparison with what is to come; but the Lord, coming into this world, shone forth like some light. Therefore the Apostle also says: \"Who is the light of his glory.\" Therefore, just as David entered into the cave, hiding himself from Saul, so too the Lord entered into this world, and suffered persecution.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.142.2":["I pour out my prayer in his sight. He said it more expressively: I pour out my whole heart in the sight of the Lord — just as someone might confess his wound to a doctor, and tell him all the sufferings he has, so that the doctor may thus apply the cure to him. I declare my tribulation before him. This is the tribulation which the Apostle enumerates, which, when counted with the rest, produces hope, and hope does not confound.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.142.3":["When my spirit was failing within me. If the evil spirit that was choking Saul does not fail from us, the Lord cannot know our paths. And what are the paths of the righteous? Righteousness, good works, an upright life, chastity, self-restraint, and other things like these.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141","In this way in which I was walking, they have hidden a snare for me. The vices are near to the virtues. In whatever way anyone wishes to walk, in that way they set snares: whether in fasting, or in almsgiving, or in the other virtues.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.142.4":["I looked to the right hand and saw: and there was no one who knew me. If we make this psalm refer to the Lord, the Lord himself is speaking. All my disciples abandoned me, and fled. One had remained, Peter, who had promised himself, saying, \"If I must die for you, I will not abandon you\": and he too denied me. Therefore he said, I looked to him on the right hand, and he did not say on the left, because Peter did penance.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141","Flight has perished from me. When he was seized by the Jews. And there is no one who seeks after my soul. For they all were shouting: \"Crucify, crucify.\"\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.142.6":["Attend to my supplication, for I have been humbled exceedingly. Unless someone has been humbled, he is not heard by the Lord. For what does it profit anyone to pray with words and to keep his neck held erect? But if we understand it of the Lord: \"Learn from me, for I am gentle and humble of heart.\"\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141","Deliver me from those who persecute me, for they have been made stronger than I. Those who fight against us are stronger than we are, that is, the princes of this world.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.142.7":["Bring my soul out of custody, that it may confess to your name. That is, out of this body. To confess, that is, to glorify, as he says: \"I confess to you, Father.\" The righteous await me, until you reward me. The Lord says: The apostles await me, until I rise again from the dead.\n— Homily 52, on Psalm 141"],"PSA.143.1":["O Lord, hear my prayer. The words of a humble man, full of humility and mercy. Give ear to my supplication in your truth. These are the words of one who trusts. In your truth, that is, in your Christ. 'I am the way and the life and the truth.' Hear me in your justice. Here too, in your Christ. That is, I do not trust in myself, but I ask for your truth and your justice, that it may have mercy on me.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.2":["And enter not into judgment with your servant. See, he has shown most plainly that he awaits the mercy of Christ, saying: And enter not into judgment with your servant. Here it is as though someone judges between God and the prophet, just as it is also written in the fiftieth psalm: 'That you may be justified in your words, and prevail when you are judged.' And God enters into judgment for this reason, that he may punish justly. Something of this sort was done also by Cyrus, king of the Persians and Medes, to the king of the Armenians, when he had violated his friendship. He conquered him, and took him captive; and he made that man's own kinsmen, and his wife, and his sons judge between them, so that he might justly either show mercy or punish. For in your sight shall no living man be justified. 'The God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob: the God of the living, and not of the dead.' Therefore not even the patriarchs themselves will be justified in the sight of God. 'For the stars are not clean in his sight.'\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.3":["For the enemy has persecuted my soul. This needs no interpretation: no one doubts that it is the devil. He has humbled my life on the earth. This is what he means. Before this I had wings, and I flew: but now the enemy has persecuted me and seized me, and bound my feet and my hands; just as a bird is caught by a man, and seems as it were to be dead, because it does not have the free use of its wings to fly. So too, he says, the enemy has bound me with the awareness of my sins.\n\nHe has placed me in dark places, like the dead of the world. Like the dead of the world: not yet dead. Why? Because he stands in repentance. But if anyone who sins does not do penance, this man is dead to the world, and not like one dead, but perfectly dead. This, then, the enemy supposed—that I would not do penance for my sins, which he himself suggested to me that I should commit.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.4":["And my spirit was in anguish over me. 'Do you first tell your sins, and you shall be saved.' And again: 'If you only sigh for your sins, you shall be saved.'\n\nMy heart within me was troubled. Because of my conscience, because I sinned.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.5":["I was mindful of the days of old. I was mindful of my former days, how I used to have confidence: but now it is night for me.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.6":["I stretched out my hands to you. Bound by the enemy, I can do nothing else except do penance, and lift up my hands to you. My soul is as earth without water to you. As earth without water thirsts and seeks the rain, so too my soul longs for and thirsts after you, its God.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.7":["Hear me speedily, O Lord, my spirit has failed. My soul has failed, my flesh has failed: my spirit alone remained in me, and it too has failed: but hear me speedily, and have mercy on me.\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.8":["Make known to me the way in which I should walk. Make your Christ known to me, who said, 'I am the way.'\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.143.10":["Your good Spirit shall lead me into the right land. Here the most impious Arians set before us and say: 'Good Master, what good shall I do, that I may be saved? And he says: Why do you call me good? No one is good, except God alone.' Therefore if the Son is not good, but the Father is, the Father is greater than the Son. Therefore according to your reasoning, most impious Arian, who is greater, the Son or the Holy Spirit? You certainly say, the Son. Here, then, he says: Your good Spirit shall lead me into the right land. Therefore if the Spirit, who is lesser, is good, as you say: how much more is the Son good, who is greater, Christ. But the Gospel has it thus: 'And one of the scribes coming and tempting him said: Good Master. And he said, Why do you call me good' and do not confess it? Just as if someone tempting his bishop, whom he despises, should say to him, Bishop; and the other should answer, I am no bishop to you, depart from me. But have you not read elsewhere: 'The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep'? But what is better than the Son, who put on flesh for our sake and suffered, was nine months in the womb of the Virgin, and coming down from his majesty was in blood, was in the cradle, suffered to grow little by little, and to be beaten, and to be struck on the cheek, and to be crucified? What is better than this?\n— Homily 53, on Psalm 142"],"PSA.144.1":["Blessed be the Lord my God, who teaches my hands for the fight. He who interprets briefly gains this: that he himself has no praise, and yet whatever there is in the interpretation, those who have heard retain it. Blessed be the Lord my God, who teaches my hands for the fight, and my fingers for war. Let us lift up our hands in holy things without thoughts and disputes, and let us lift them up in every place; and when we have lifted up our hands, our prayer to God is a weapon against the devil. Who teaches my hands for the fight, and my fingers for war. This is our lyre, this our harp: on this we sing to God.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.2":["My mercy and my refuge, my helper and my deliverer: my protector. With how many names is God called! As many benefits, so many names. Who subdues my people under me. This can be said in the person of Christ, that is, from the assumed man. But it can also be said in the person of the apostles, that which he says: Who subdues my people under me. This too the holy father in the monastery says, and gives thanks to God, and says: Blessed be the Lord my God, who subdues my people under me. For it is not subjected to me, but subjected to you; therefore it serves me, that it may serve you.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.3":["Lord, what is man, that you have made yourself known to him? or the son of man, that you take account of him? This very thing is said also in the eighth psalm. 'What is man, that you are mindful of him? or the son of man, that you visit him?' Lord, what is man, that you have made yourself known to him? He speaks of the frailty of the body and of the human condition: and what does he say? If you look at the flesh, what is man? If at the spirit, man is a great thing. Let us not despise the flesh, but let us despise the works of the flesh. Let us not despise the flesh, which reigns with Christ in heaven. 'Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God': not flesh and blood, but the works of the flesh. Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God. And how do they reign in Christ? and how do we sit with Christ in the heavenly places? Lord, what is man, that you have made yourself known to him? or the son of man, that you take account of him? He gives thanks to God, and this is what he says. As regards the human condition, man is nothing. 'Vanity of vanities, all is vanity, said the preacher.' If all is vanity, then heaven too, then the angels too. If heaven and the angels are vanity, how much vanity is man? And if the angels are vanity, if heaven is vanity, why then did God work vanity? Vanity in comparison with God: but in itself not vanity. Lord, what is man, that you have made yourself known to him? That you have made yourself known to him. Great is the happiness of man, to know his Creator. In this we differ from beasts and from wild animals, that we understand our Creator, and they do not understand. But even the very posture of the body seeks out its Maker. For the animals look toward the earth, and there is the belly where also is the eye. But our eyes are lifted up toward heaven: so that even if the mind should be blinded, still with unwilling eyes we may see heaven.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.4":["Man is made like to vanity: his days pass away like a shadow. Truly our days are like a shadow. I was a little infant, I was a boy, I was a young lad, I was a youth, I was a man, that is, of perfect age, I was of middle age. While I know it not, I am an old man. Death creeps up upon old age. I am changed daily, and I do not perceive that I am nothing. Not for even a single moment of an hour do we stand in our own state, but we are always either growing or diminishing. Therefore man is changed by moments, and while he knows it not, he perishes. I, an old man, recall what I was as a little infant, what I did, how I played, how I ran hither and thither: now I see myself weighed down with excessive burden. His days pass away like a shadow. Our life is a shadow: it is seen, and it has no substance. But that which has no substance quickly passes away. Since therefore man is so wretched, and since he is thus infirm, what do we pray for? what do we ask? what does the prophet say here? what does he pray for?\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.5":["Lord, bow down your heavens, and descend. You will not be able to save this vileness of man, unless you yourself put it on. Lord, bow down your heavens, and descend. Your straying sheep will not be able to be healed, unless it is carried back on your shoulders. Touch the mountains, and they shall smoke. 'Amen I say to you: if you shall have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you shall say to this mountain, Pass over, and it shall be transferred.' There it was being said concerning the lunatic. Concerning that mountain, which could not be transferred by them, it is written in Jeremiah: 'Give glory to the Lord our God, before your feet stumble against the dark mountains.' Why have we said all this? Because now too there follows, Touch the mountains, and they shall smoke. The mountains that reign, the mountains that are raised up through pride, who say 'The rivers are mine, and I made them.' Touch the mountains, and they shall smoke. He did not say, they shall be set ablaze; but, they shall smoke. Let them have the token of punishment: not of blaze, not of splendor.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.6":["Flash forth the lightning, and you shall scatter them. See what he says. He did not say, you shall destroy them: but, you shall scatter them. We read in Genesis, at that time when they were building the tower, God says: 'Come, let us descend and confound their tongue' lest at some time they should have agreement among themselves; but let them be scattered abroad, it profits them.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.7":["Send forth your hand from on high. 'Lord, who has believed our report? and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed?' Send forth your hand from on high. Lord, bow down your heavens, and descend. This is said to the Son: Bow down your heavens, and descend. And how does he say, Send forth your hand from on high? If he descended, how does he send his hand from on high? But although he may be on earth, still he sends his hand from heaven: for he does not withdraw from heaven. Rescue me and free me from the many waters. Since you descended, since you bowed down the heavens, and sent forth your hand from on high, and deigned to assume human flesh, and many believed in you, in place of one water many waters began to be. You may see diverse heresies scattered throughout the whole world. Rescue me and free me from the many waters. This the catechumen says: Free me from the many waters, and grant me the living fountain. For there is one baptism: 'one faith, one baptism.' Free me from the many waters. For those many waters do not bring forth your sons, but the sons of strangers. Whence it follows: Free me from the many waters, and from the hand of the sons of strangers. For they are not your sons who are born in the many waters, but they are the sons of strangers.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.8":["Whose mouth has spoken vanity. They promise themselves knowledge, and they raise up their mouth against their Creator. 'They have set their mouth against heaven.' Whose mouth has spoken vanity. Their knowledge is the knowledge of vanity. And their right hand is the right hand of iniquity. Both are condemned, both mouth and hand, both knowledge and works. Whose mouth has spoken vanity: this to knowledge. And their right hand is the right hand of iniquity. The right hand, in work. They say that they have a right hand, but they are wholly left-handed. It is the right hand of iniquity, and not of equity. Whence to such a left hand it is said: 'Let the devil stand at his right hand.' He who has such a right hand, and the right hand of iniquity, the devil stands at his right hand.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.9":["O God, I will sing to you a new song. A new man, a new song. O God, I will sing to you a new song. 'Sing to the Lord a new song.' How beautifully he did not say, I sing, but, I will sing. In the future he made his promise; for in the present he knows there is a battle, not a song. An army never sings, except when it has conquered. Therefore since in the present I fight, I have no leisure to sing. When I shall have conquered, then I will sing. On the ten-stringed psaltery I will make music to you. Then I will stretch out both hands, and on such strings I will sing to you. My prayer is your psaltery.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.10":["Who gives salvation to kings. To what kings? 'The heart of the king is in the hand of God.' Even of Julian the persecutor, and of Nero, and of Decius? The heart of the king is in the hand of God. Those who reign over their own body, who subject their body and reduce it to servitude, lest, while preaching to others, they themselves be found reprobate. These are the kings of whom wisdom says in Proverbs: 'She gives a kingdom to kings.'\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.11":["Rescue me and free me from the hand of the sons of strangers. The little verses which were spoken above are repeated again. Whose mouth has spoken vanity, and their right hand is the right hand of iniquity. Whose sons are like new plantings established in their youth. From the hand of the sons of strangers. Let us see what sort the sons of the heretics are. Whose sons are like a new planting in their youth. From the hand of the sons of strangers. Attend diligently to what is said about the heretics.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.12":["Their daughters are adorned, decked round about like the likeness of a temple. Their storehouses are full, belching forth from this into that. Their sheep are fruitful, abounding in their goings-out: their oxen are fat. There is no ruin of the wall, nor passage, nor outcry in their streets. They called blessed the people who have these things. This they said. What do I say? Blessed the people whose God is their Lord. We shall therefore expound each point briefly for your prayers. Whose sons are like a new planting in their youth. Their planting is not old, but is new. For it is not from the old law, it is not from the prophets, it is not from the apostles; but it is from new masters. Their sons are like a new planting in their youth. In their youth: they are always young. Although there are many old heresies, still, since they change daily, and daily find new things, the heresies themselves are new. Although they are old, still, since daily they renew their doctrines, they are new. For they are not content with the error of the old masters, but daily they themselves discover new things. Whose sons are like a new planting in their youth. What is said about the Church, about the one who fears the Lord? 'Blessed are all who fear the Lord, who walk in his ways.' What is said about them? 'Your sons are like new olive plants round about your table.' About the table of Christ are those sons, they are not far from the table of Christ. There daughters are not spoken of, but only sons. But the heretics have both daughters, and have the frailer sex too, the delights of pleasure. Their daughters are adorned. The speech is adorned, and the sense is filthy. In the holy scriptures thoughts are always called sons: words, daughters. See therefore what he says. Their thoughts, that is, are new sons. But their words, that is of the heretics, which they have from the wisdom of the world, are adorned: for they have adorned words, elegant speech. Their daughters are adorned. They do indeed seem to be adorned: but because they are daughters, and not sons, they have no strength. Decked round about like the likeness of a temple. Beautifully he said, the likeness of a temple: they do not have the true Church, but the likeness of a church.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.13":["Their storehouses are full, belching forth from this into that. They are ready for contending. If you hold them fast on one testimony, they pass over to another. If you hold them fast in the scriptures, they flee to Aristotle. If you hold Aristotle fast, they pass over to Plato. Their storehouses are full, belching forth from this into that. Properly that is called a storehouse which has riches laid up in it. The heretics have storehouses: for they have prepared words with which they contend. But the men of the Church are simple, they have nothing prepared. They open their mouth, and it is filled by the Lord, according to what is said: 'Open your mouth, and I will fill it' and 'Do not consider what you shall answer them, for your Father will give you in that hour what you should say.' You see that we do not have storehouses prepared, but our riches are sudden. Let someone say: Give a testimony, how the saints do not have storehouses. 'Behold,' he says, 'the birds of heaven, for they have neither storehouses nor barns: and your heavenly Father feeds them.' Those birds are especially the monks: they have no storehouses, they have no pantries, but they have the very Lord of pantries and storehouses, Christ himself. They have no barns, but the Lord of barns. They have not the riches of the devil, but they have the poverty of Christ. What does the devil say? 'All these things are mine: if falling down you will worship me, I will give them to you.' What does Christ say to his own? 'He who will not sell all his things which he has and give to the poor, cannot be my disciple.' The one promises kingdoms and riches, that he may kill: the other promises poverty, that he may save. We do nothing great, if we leave our own things: Christ left the Father and the kingdoms of the heavens for our sake. Let no one think that I have blasphemed, because I said, he left the Father. I speak according to the dispensation, according to that in which he is sent, according to what he says: 'The Father, who sent me, is greater than I.' God is not sent, God is not subjected, does not serve: but the dispensation is sent. If God is everywhere, and all things are in God, how is God sent to anything, since all things are God's? Christ therefore for our sake was made poor. Why have we said all this? Because now it was read, Their storehouses are full, belching forth from this into that. Beautifully he said, belching forth: from excessive fullness. They belch from fullness, and perish: we are saved by fasting.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143","Their sheep are fruitful. They have many sons: for the number of heretics is great. Their sheep are fruitful. Whence it is said also in Hosea the prophet: Lord, give to them. What will you give them? A barren womb and dry breasts. About whom is this said? About the masters of the heretics, who applaud themselves in the number of their disciples. Their sheep are fruitful, abounding in their goings-out. He did not say, in their goings-in; but, in their goings-out. For they do not go into the Church, but they go out of the Church. They went out from us, but they were not of us: for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us. Abounding in their goings-out. Daily they go out of the Church. Abounding in their goings-out. What is said about the Church? All her glory, the daughter of the king, is from within.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.14":["Their oxen are fat. Pharaoh too saw in a dream fat Egyptian oxen. Their oxen are fat. The Israelite peoples are lean, the Egyptians are fat. For you may see that the masters of the heretics do nothing else, except pursue riches. They pursue riches, and they despise the poor Christ. Their oxen are fat. Beautifully he says, fat oxen: whose God is the belly, and whose glory is in their confusion.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143","There is no ruin of the wall, nor passage. Does it not seem to you to be obscure what is said, There is no ruin of the wall, nor passage, nor outcry in their streets? You will find heretics unschooled with difficulty: for all the heretic masters are equipped with worldly knowledge. They do not have the apostolic net, but the little chains of dialectic. There is no ruin of the wall, nor passage. If ever they are in disputations, when they begin to dispute with you, their words are so brief, so enclosed with artful speech, that it is difficult to escape from there. For when they have bound you with their syllogisms, and have shut you in, and have woven and built up the syllogism for you like a wall, you cannot fall down, you cannot pass through, you are held enclosed. There is no ruin of the wall, nor passage. And when they have held you there as though enclosed in a certain ring, then you will not be on the narrow way that leads to life, but on the broad one that leads to death. Nor outcry in their streets. Jesus cries out, and calls to himself, and says: Let him who thirsts come and drink. The outcry of Jesus is on the narrow way; in the streets of the heretics it is not.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.144.15":["They called blessed the people who have these things. Many, not knowing, think such people blessed. But for us there is Christian simplicity: we do not want Platonic eloquence, but we want the apostolic simplicity of fishermen. This one holds the net, has feet and hands muddied from the work; this one has a calloused hand from the work. Blessed the people whose God is their Lord. Blessed is the people, who instead of all riches have Christ. Those people have sons and daughters, and storehouses, fruitful sheep, oxen fat and sleek and comely. But we do not have a multitude, but we know that saying in the Gospel: Wherever two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst. Fear not, little flock: for it has pleased God to dwell in you. Let not the monks say: Behold, such great multitudes, the cities are full, the whole world: is no one then saved, except him who is in the monastery? Behold, such great multitudes: are we then alone saved? When you have thought this, hear: Fear not, little flock, for it has pleased the Father to dwell in you. And now it was read in the Gospel, that one soul is saved, so that two thousand swine perish. Why do I say all this? Because more precious is the soul of one saint, than infinite multitudes of sinners. Out of the whole world he chose twelve apostles: out of the twelve one departed, eleven remained. The cross came, ten fled: one Peter remained, one with the One. And that one fled, and would that he had fled: and he denies. Therefore let us say, the whole human race perished. Since it had perished, therefore the Lord, being crucified, said: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was not a man with me. At that time the psalm was fulfilled: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed. There is none who does good, there is none, even to one. He who had promised, saying If it be necessary for me to die, or to go into prison, I will not deny you, denied. Why do I say all this? Because the monk ought not to look to the multitude of sinners, but to the manner of life and the fewness of the saints. All Judea was being led into captivity. Nebuchadnezzar had come, so many thousands of men were being led captive into Babylon. Jeremiah alone was there who confessed God, and he himself was being cast into the pit of mud; and yet more precious was the soul of that one man, than of the whole people. Do you wish to know what one man does? Jesus the son of Nave, when the whole earth was inhabited, was alone. Surely there were infinite multitudes, and he alone was. Alone he commands the sun and the moon, and it stands still; a man bids, and heaven hears. Heaven hears him, because he himself was hearing the Lord. Jeremiah was in captivity; but there were also many multitudes. And what does he say? I sat alone, because I was filled with bitterness. How were you alone in the city? But therefore alone, because I had no companion of my purpose.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"PSA.146.1":["Praise the Lord, O my soul. This psalm has a heading: Of Haggai and Zechariah. And this we must know first of all, that it is not found in the Hebrew, but in the Septuagint translators. But let us speak also according to the common edition, Of Haggai and Zechariah. Since the restoration of Jerusalem is here spoken of — The Lord shall reign forever, thy God, O Sion, unto generation and generation — they suppose it to be said, as it were, concerning the restoration of Jerusalem, which took place under Haggai and Zechariah.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145","Praise the Lord, O my soul. He did not say, praise the body, but, praise, O my soul; not that he condemned the body, but he condemned the works of the flesh. Praise the Lord, O my soul. The soul exhorts itself. Just as if David were to say, Praise the Lord, O David; so too the soul itself says to itself, Praise the Lord, O my soul.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.2":["I will praise the Lord in my life. See what it says: it exhorts itself, and it itself answers. What does it say? It says to me, Praise the Lord, O my soul: I myself answer, I will praise the Lord in my life. At present I cannot praise, I am weighed down by sins. As often as I open my lips in the praises of God, so often does the consciousness of my sins glue them shut. I will praise the Lord in my life. Beautifully he said, I will praise, not I praise. When I shall have been with my Lord, then I will praise my Lord in my life. Now I am in death, I cannot praise. I will praise in my life. Our life is Christ: let us praise the Lord in life. I will sing to my God as long as I shall be. See what it says. When we do something righteously, we are: but when we sin, we cease to be. There are many things that might be said, but the discourse hastens to its end.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.3":["Put not your trust in princes, nor in the sons of men, in whom there is no salvation. This he says generally to all: Put not your trust in princes, not in the emperor, not in prefects, not in the judges of the world. Put not your trust in princes. Who are the princes? The sons of men. Who are the sons of men? Those in whom there is no salvation.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.4":["His spirit shall go forth, and he shall return into his earth. But when he shall have returned into his earth, what will happen? In that day all his thoughts shall perish: all the confidence of princes. All their thoughts shall perish. Put not your trust in princes. Many have trusted in princes. Today he was, tomorrow he is not. Today the army goes before him, and in the evening he lies in the tomb. After such great magnitude, such great glory, no intervals of time intervene: in the same moment is the working of Christ. In the sons of men, in whom there is no salvation. Who are the princes? The sons of men. If the men themselves are nothing, what are their sons? His spirit shall go forth. Whose? Man's. His spirit shall go forth. Here he puts spirit for soul. Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit. His spirit shall go forth, and he shall return into his earth. It is not the spirit that shall return into his earth, for the spirit is not of the earth: but the spirit shall go forth, and the man shall return into his earth. In that day all their thoughts shall perish. We ourselves, who are not princes, how many things do we daily ponder, and say: Tomorrow I will do this, I will pull down my barns, and again tomorrow I will build them. It is said to me: Fool, this night thy soul shall be snatched away: and the things which thou hast prepared, whose shall they be? This is said concerning those who trust in princes. But concerning the holy man, what is said?\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.5":["Blessed is he whose helper is the God of Jacob. These trust in chariots, and these in horses: but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God. They have more helpers: but they are princes, but they are men, but they collapse. We have one, who is able to save. His hope is in the Lord his God. Let us see what the three young men say in the Babylonian furnace, when Nebuchadnezzar summons them, and compels them to worship Bel: what do they answer Nebuchadnezzar? O king, we have no need to answer thee concerning this word. God indeed is able to deliver us from the burning furnace: but even if he shall not deliver us, know, O king, that we do not worship the image which thou hast set up. See their faith. We believe, they say, that he is able to deliver us: but if perchance our sins should hinder it, nevertheless we believe also in him who is unwilling to deliver. For we do not believe in this life, but in the life to come: we do not believe in him so that we may not burn here, but so that through these fires we may not pass over to other fires. Set us aflame therefore, prepare the furnace: this burning, this consuming, is our purgation. Blessed is he whose helper is the God of Jacob. See the significance of the words. His helper. And he could not work any sign there because of their unbelief. God is our helper. He delivers and labors alongside those who are willing and who labor: those who sleep and lie idle and are unwilling he does not deliver. His hope is in the Lord his God. Excellently he said, hope: even if in the present he does not deliver, nevertheless there is hope concerning the future.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.6":["Who made heaven and earth. This God who made heaven and earth, the sea and all the things that are in them. In comparison with heaven and earth and the sea, we men, according to the condition of the flesh, are as if ants and fleas. He therefore who made heaven and earth, can he not deliver the man whom he made? Who keepeth truth forever. If a lie oppresses us, let us not be saddened: the Lord keepeth truth forever. Someone or other has lied against us, and the one lying is believed more than we who speak the truth. We ought not to despair: the Lord keepeth truth forever. Beautifully he said, keepeth. He keeps it, and holds it in his treasury: he will give back to us what he has preserved. Who keepeth truth forever. Christ is truth: let us speak truth, and truth preserves truth for us.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.7":["He executeth judgment for them that suffer wrong. Even if it comes late, do not despair: coming he shall come, and shall save. He executeth judgment for them that suffer wrong. Let this alone be in our conscience, that we suffer unjustly. Let us hold this in our conscience, that we do not suffer on account of our sins, and that we do not do that of which we are accused. He giveth food to the hungry. And according to the letter it is profitable; and even if it be understood simply, it edifies the hearer. Behold the birds of the air, for they neither sow nor reap, and your heavenly Father feedeth them. He giveth food to the hungry. To the hungry he gives, not to those who belch. He therefore who as a monk has nothing, let him confidently receive: he who has and is belching, let him not receive. You know that you are hungry, you know that you have nothing: if you are hungry and have nothing, you rather confer a benefit, if you receive, than is bestowed on you. But if you have and are not hungry, you ought not to take away the food of the hungry, so that you, being sated, may receive. Receive what you may put into your belly, not what goes into your purse: receive a tunic that may cover your body, not one that may fill your chests. He giveth food to the hungry. This according to the letter. Let us speak also in another way. Let us hunger for Christ, and he himself gives us the heavenly bread. Give us this day our daily bread. Those who say this, hunger: those who desire bread, hunger. He who says, Give us this day our daily bread, surely speaks as one hungering. He giveth food to the hungry. Someone supposes that he speaks of the heavenly bread from the mysteries. And this indeed we receive, since it is truly the flesh of Christ, and truly the blood of Christ. But let us speak also in another way. The bread of Christ and his flesh is the divine word and the heavenly teaching. Whoever shall have received this bread and been sated, what will happen? What? The Lord looseth them that are fettered. If this bread shall have refreshed our soul, that is, the divine word and the heavenly teaching, at once our feet, which had been bound, are loosed. The Lord looseth them that are fettered. Lazarus too was bound; and he said: Loose him. The Lord looseth them that are fettered. And the daughter of Abraham had been bent over and bound for eighteen years; she is loosed, and looks up to heaven. See what is said there in the Gospel. And behold, it says, there was a certain woman, who had a spirit of infirmity for eighteen years: and she could not, it says, lift up her neck. Because she had a spirit of infirmity, she could not lift her neck upward. The Lord looseth them that are fettered. These same fetters are also works: since indeed the same sins, which take our necks captive, also bind our feet. Whoever is fettered cannot run. Whoever is fettered cannot run in the stadium of Christ: for the feet are weighed down by fetters, and are pressed to the ground, and fasten us to the earth. The Lord looseth them that are fettered. See the order. He giveth food to the hungry: first we hunger, then we receive food. But when we shall have been sated, our feet, which had been fettered, are loosed. And when they shall have begun to walk freely, what follows?\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.8":["The Lord enlighteneth the blind. See what it says. If he were speaking of these blind men, he ought to have said, The Lord enlighteneth the blind. But he did not say this: but according to the Hebrew and Greek truth, what did he say? The Lord maketh the blind wise. If he were speaking of these eyes of the flesh, he ought to have said, The Lord enlighteneth the blind: but now indeed he says, The Lord maketh the blind wise. If ye were blind, he says, ye should have no sin. Our heart has been blinded by the darkness of sins. Foolishness and stupidity are the darkness of our eyes. When therefore we hungering shall have been refreshed, and our feet shall have been freed, then also the eye of our heart will begin to see the light which it had once lost, and to have wisdom. If one shall have been made wise, what follows? The Lord raiseth up them that are cast down. And if you were lying down, be secure: the Lord stretches out his hand. The Lord loveth the just. I see two things: he loves, and he guards. He who loves, loves it as his own: but he who guards, guards him who is set in danger. The Lord loveth the just. Whoever is just and perfect, he is loved; but whoever is a stranger, and has not come to the servitude of the Lord, he is preserved.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.9":["The Lord keepeth the strangers: he will take up the orphan and the widow. And according to the letter it is profitable. He will take up the orphan and the widow. O martyr, you who are held fast to shed your blood for me, do not be anxious about your children, do not be anxious about your wife: they have me in your place. Let us speak also according to the spirit. He will take up the orphan and the widow. He will take up the orphan, who had lost God his father through sins. And he will take up the widow. The widow, that is, that soul which had lost Christ her husband. But see what it says. The orphan and the widow: he did not say, he will guard: he did not say, he will save: but, he will take up, if she return to the father, if she come back to the husband. And the way of sinners he will destroy. He did not say, sinners he will destroy: according to the first psalm, And the way of the ungodly shall perish. If the way of the just is Christ, the way of sinners is the devil. And the way of sinners he will destroy. The way, therefore, of all sinners is the devil, him he will destroy: but us he will save, who walk along it, and are unlearning to walk.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.146.10":["The Lord shall reign forever. When the way of sinners shall have perished, then the Lord shall reign forever. He did not say, unto the age: but, unto the ages. As long as the way does not perish, Christ does not reign in us: as long as the way of sinners does not perish, Christ does not reign in us. As long as our feet walk in the way of the devil, Christ does not reign in us. I grow angry without cause with my brother: I walk in the way of the devil, Christ does not reign in me. I disparage my brother: I walk in the way of the devil, Christ does not reign in me. In whatever sin I shall have walked, I walk in the way of the devil, Christ does not reign in me. My mind is empty, and is dragged off in different directions. I am in one place, and my mind wanders through the whole world. It holds angels in the world, it holds kings, it holds armies, it holds the world: one monk it cannot hold. Why do I say all this? Because of those who wander through the whole world, and cannot sit in one place. Thy God, O Sion, unto generation and generation. Thy God, O Sion. We have frequently said that Sion is interpreted as a watchtower, or the Church, or the soul contemplating God. Thy God, O Sion, unto generation and generation. In the first generation, of the Jews; in the second, from the Gentiles. In all the scriptures and in all the psalms, generation and generation is said, so as to signify two peoples. But we can also say it in another way: The Lord shall reign forever, thy God, O Sion, in this generation and in the generation to come. Let Christ reign in us: let not sin reign in our body. The heart of the king is in the hand of God. Let us be kings: let us rule over our body, and subject it, and let our heart be in the hand of Christ. To whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 55, on Psalm 145"],"PSA.147.1":["Praise the Lord, for a psalm is good. The one hundred and forty-sixth psalm is headed with the title \"Alleluia.\" Those who do not know the Hebrew language, when they see \"alleluia\" set at the head of a psalm, are wont to ask what it means. This psalm, then, has \"alleluia\" not only in its title, but also at the very opening of the psalm. For where it says \"Praise the Lord,\" in the Hebrew it says \"alleluia.\" For God among the Hebrews has ten names: he is called Sabaoth, he is called Saddai, he is called Eloim, he is called El, he is also called Iao, he is called Eser Iaia. Among the other names there is one name which is called \"Ia.\" Now \"Ia\" is interpreted \"invisible.\" Where therefore we say \"praise,\" in the Hebrew it says \"allelu.\" So here it says \"allelu Ia,\" just as if one were to say \"allelu Sabaoth,\" \"allelu Saddai,\" \"allelu Eloim\": so therefore even now it says \"allelu Ia.\" Finally Theodotion, who is one of the translators, wishing to declare the truth of the interpretation, says \"praise Ia.\" Why have we said all this? So that we may know that in this psalm \"alleluia\" is set at the head. Praise the Lord. This interpretation, once we have gone out, will be pondered in your hearts; but for now the discourse will be extended further.\n\nPraise the Lord, for a psalm is good. He did not say \"a good psalm,\" but absolutely, \"a psalm is good\": that is, it is a good thing to sing psalms. But to sing psalms not with the voice, but with the heart. How many have a good voice, and, because they are sinners, sing badly! He sings well who sings in his heart, who sings to Christ in his conscience. Let praise be pleasant to our God. Let the praise be pleasant, not with the voice, but with the conscience. Finally, Noah too offered victims, and the Lord smelled the odor of sweetness. There are many things that might be said: but we ought briefly to touch on the highest points, so that we may point out the meaning rather than broader words.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.2":["The Lord who builds Jerusalem shall gather together the dispersions of Israel. How Jerusalem is built, how the dispersion is gathered together, has already been said. Since therefore these two little verses have been discussed, and we ought not to build upon another's foundation, let us pass on to what remains. Let us therefore see how Jerusalem is built: for in what follows the prophetic word has described it.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.3":["Who heals the contrite of heart. You see how Jerusalem is built: the contrite of heart are healed. A contrite and humbled heart God does not spurn. And who binds up their bruises. Do you crush your own heart, and the Lord will bind up your bruises. That Samaritan who was coming down from Jerusalem to Jericho, when he saw the wounded man lying in the road, bound up his wounds. This is the one of whom now too it is said, And binding up their bruises. This is said of those who do penance. But of those who do not do penance, it is said: There is no way to apply a poultice, nor oil, nor bindings.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.4":["Who numbers the multitude of the stars. What is the connection, according to the letter, between \"And binding up their bruises\" and \"who numbers the multitude of the stars\"? But because he healed the contrite of heart and bound up their bruises, those who before had been contrite, and afterward were healed, he turns into stars. Who numbers the multitude of the stars. The multitude. For many were those who sinned: many are those who have been cured. Who numbers the multitude of the stars. He does not number the wounded, but he numbers those who have already been turned into stars: God numbers none but those who are worthy of his number. And calling them all by their names. Because he speaks of Lucifer and of Orion, of those things which we see in the sky, this indeed can be understood: for God is wonderful, who made heaven and earth and all things that are in them. But let us understand it in such a way that what happens every day may profit us. And calling them all by their names. Do you wish to know how stars are made out of the contrite? It is written in Genesis that God led Abraham out, and showed him the stars, and said to him: So shall your seed be. And calling them all by their names. From Abram he calls him Abraham: he was called Abram in Chaldea, and in heaven he is called Abraham. At that time when he was made a star, he is also called Abraham. He who before was Saul, afterward is called in heaven Paul. John and James, sons of thunder: thunder is surely in heaven. And so the stars are numbered, the countless saints are numbered: and Christ alone cannot be numbered.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.5":["Great is our Lord, and great is his power, and of his wisdom there is no number. Great is his power: Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God. And of his wisdom there is no number. Christ is wisdom: and the one wisdom cannot be numbered.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.6":["The Lord who lifts up the meek. What are those stars? The Lord who lifts up the meek. The meek are turned into stars. But the just shall shine like the stars of heaven: Daniel says it. But bringing down sinners even to the ground. See the difference of things. Angels fall down to the earth: men go to heaven. The Lord who lifts up the meek. Meekness calls up to heaven: pride drags down to the earth. But bringing down sinners even to the ground.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.7":["Sing to the Lord in confession. This David fulfilled: he who had offered us examples of righteousness, offered us also examples of penance. For he taught us how we ought to do penance, himself doing it. See, holy prophets, that not only their righteousness has profited us, but also their sin. Sing psalms to our God upon the harp. Let no string of the virtues be broken; let all the strings of the virtues sound together. Sing psalms to our God upon the harp. The harp has many strings: if one is broken, the sound cannot be produced. So too the holy man, however holy he may be, if one virtue is lacking, cannot send forth a tuneful sound.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.8":["Who covers the heaven with clouds. This heaven, which we behold, cannot be covered with clouds. But there is another heaven, and there are other clouds. And your truth reaches even to the clouds. Then does the truth of God reach even to those clouds, which are quickly dissolved when the wind blows? The truth of God is Christ. I am the way and the life and the truth. Is then the truth of Christ in these clouds, which have not in themselves the substance of truth? Can it then be that truth is there, where the wind has power to scatter? I will command, he says, the clouds not to rain rain upon them—that is, upon the people of the Jews. The clouds are the prophets: the Lord commanded them not to rain rain upon Israel. The prophetic word has been turned toward us. These clouds, if they are dashed against one another, make thunder and lightnings. Who covers the heaven with clouds. Why did he say, He covers the heaven with clouds? If the clouds are prophets and apostles, how then is the heaven covered by apostles and prophets? The heaven is the divine mysteries. Since therefore the mysteries, whatever are divine, are called heaven: whoever is unworthy, these clouds protect the sacraments from him. If anyone is unworthy, he cannot receive the sacraments: they are covered by the clouds. Who prepares rain for the earth. The earth needs rain: our earth, unless it receives rain, the fountain of doctrines, will bring forth no fruit. When the earth has been moistened by a fountain of such doctrines, what follows?\n\nWho brings forth hay on the mountains. If he were speaking of hay alone, surely hay is born more in the valleys than on the mountainous places: for wherever the rain is more abundant, there hay is born more. From the mountains the waters flow down into the valleys, and grass is born more abundantly.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.9":["And who gives to the beasts their food. This Scripture which we read, which we handle, which we have in our hands, is called the mountains; in the Scriptures Abraham is called a mountain, Isaac is called a mountain, Jeremiah a mountain, the holy prophets are mountains. These mountains have both grain and hay. If you are a man, you receive grain from them: if you are cattle, you receive hay. Men and beasts you will save, O Lord. If you are a beast, you receive hay; if you are a man, you receive grain: according to your faith you are saved. Who brings forth hay on the mountains, and gives to the beasts their food. If you are a man, in the holy Scriptures you receive spiritual understanding: if you are a beast, you receive it according to the letter, in the Jewish manner. I see a great thing. God, who has been praised with such great acclamations, is brought down to lowlier things, and it is said: And to the young ravens that call upon him. If he were speaking of birds, he would rather name eagles or vultures. And to the young ravens that call upon him. No other bird is named, but the young of the ravens; and not the ravens themselves, but the young of the ravens. The raven is not saved, but the young that is born of it. We are the young of ravens: for we are born of idolatrous parents. We read in Solomon that the eye which mocks its father and the word of its mother, let the ravens dig it out of the valleys. He did not say \"the young of ravens,\" but the ravens themselves. For he who is a raven cannot be saved. The raven is sent out from the ark, and does not return. In the flood it is with the rest: after the flood it is cast out in the time of prosperity—that is, at that time when there is calm, it cannot be in the ark of God. If ever there is a storm, if ever a flood, the raven is in the midst: calm returns, and the raven flees. Who are these ravens? Who are the young of ravens? I think that the ravens of black color, who always pursue the corpses of the dead, who always with a most raucous voice provoke quarrels, are demons; and whoever have served the demons, like ravens, they too take on the color of ravens. Therefore our parents, as we have already said, who served idols and demons, were ravens. For they are not found except where there are corpses and filth and foulness and the corpses of the dead. But we who are born of ravens do not await corpses, but the dew. For the young of ravens are said to live on the dew. For so the natural philosophers say, that the young of ravens live on the dew. Do you wish to know yet another mystery about ravens? Elijah, which is interpreted \"God the Lord\": Elijah—that is, our Lord and Savior—suffers persecution from the Jews; and is fed by ravens, the people of the Gentiles. And to the young ravens that call upon him. If you call, you are a young raven: if you are silent, you are a raven. And to the young ravens that call upon him. We read in the prophet: And everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. If therefore we take it simply of ravens, then will the ravens too be saved? Answer, Jew: answer, you who in the Jewish way follow only the letter. You say that these ravens are truly ravens, you do not interpret spiritually, but carnally: for you say it is written of ravens. If then it is written of the young of ravens, and here it is said, And to the young ravens that call upon him: and it is written, Everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved: then will the young of ravens too be saved in the resurrection? We therefore, the young of ravens, call upon him and are saved. Finally the apostle Paul too wrote to the church of the saints, and wrote also to those who call upon the name of the Lord.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.10":["He will not take pleasure in the strength of the horse. We promised brevity, and, compelled by necessity, we have disputed at greater length. We have lied, but the lie is most useful. Would that Herod had lied and had broken his oath! And so we too promised lesser things, and, as you prayed, have offered greater things. The hour compels me to be silent: the greatness of the mysteries compels me to speak. He will not take pleasure in the strength of the horse. I hear \"horse,\" and a neighing horse: I cannot pass it by. It is written in the psalter: The horse is a false hope for safety; and in another place: The horse and its rider he has thrown into the sea. It is even commanded to the king of Israel that he should not rear horses. Finally Solomon, who brought chariots out of Egypt, was overcome by pleasure. He will not take pleasure in the strength of the horse. The horse and its rider he has thrown into the sea. They have become to me as maddened horses after women, each one neighed after his neighbor's wife—Jeremiah says it. Over such horses, then, God has no pleasure. We have spoken of the horse. The horse and its rider he has thrown into the sea. We have said who the horses are: let us say who the riders are. I think that the horses are sinful men, and the riders are demons who ride upon them. If ever therefore we see some brother a persecutor: him who is called a brother persecuting us and being an enemy: let us know that he is a horse. And he is a horse: but the rider is the devil, who strikes us with his lance. This one runs, and that one strikes. But the horse itself too is driven by the heels, is pierced, and against its will goes mad.\n\nNor is he well pleased with the legs of a man. See what he says: he did not say, in the head of a man: he did not say, in the hands of a man: but, in the legs, which are below. Nor is he well pleased with the legs of a man. The legs here, like the thighs also, are always taken for lusts. Finally in Ezekiel too God is seen from the loins upward as amber (electrum), from the loins downward as fiery. Whatever is above, that is gold: whatever is below, that is prepared for Gehenna. Nor is he well pleased with the legs of a man. Let us speak according to the letter. Since he had said, He will not take pleasure in the strength of the horse, and had, as it were, described a warrior man: it seems to me here, where he said \"not in the legs,\" that he is not naming the legs, but the greaves. We see therefore that whatever covers the legs does not please God, but that which is above. It does not please God in strength, it does not please him in the legs of a man: but it pleases him in his own fear. Might does not please, fear pleases. He destroys the one who wages war, he raises up the one who prays, he exalts the humble, he casts down the proud.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.11":["The Lord is well pleased with those who fear him. One fear casts out many fears. Is it not better to fear one, that you may not fear many, than to fear many, that you may not fear one? And with those who hope in his mercy. They hope not in their own works, but in the mercy of the judge. Praise the Lord, for a psalm is good; for in those who hope in his mercy is salvation alone. To whom is glory forever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"PSA.147.12":["Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem; praise your God, O Zion. Just now we heard the holy presbyter preaching that, because of its transgression of the law, the Jewish people has been forsaken; and how is it that now it is said, Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem; praise your God, O Zion? Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem. Jerusalem, \"vision of peace.\" Praise your God, O Zion. Zion is interpreted \"watchtower.\" You see, then, what he says: Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem. Wherever there is a vision of peace, wherever there is contemplation of God, there let there be praise of God. Praise the Lord, then, O Church, when you have begun to believe in him and to have peace, and have begun to see peace: this is Jerusalem, \"vision of peace.\" You who were formerly of the idols have been made the servant of God: praise, then, your God. But when you have begun already to have knowledge, and to be a watchtower — this is Zion — praise the Lord: that is, you praise Jerusalem, you praise Zion, you soul of the Church, you Church. Let not the heretics praise, let not the synagogue praise, but you praise.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.13":["For he has strengthened the bars of your gates. Is it, then, of this Jerusalem that he speaks? Of this Zion? Praise your God. Why? For he has strengthened the bars of your gates. There are no gates: and how, then, are the bars kept safe? He has blessed your children within you. How has he blessed, he who said, Your house shall be left to you desolate? For he has strengthened the bars of your gates. The Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tabernacles of Jacob. We said the gates of Zion are the prophets. Since, then, we know the gates of Zion are called the prophets, let us see what the bars of the gates are. For truly the prophets are the gates of the Church: we cannot enter the Church except through the prophets. Manichaeus wanted to enter without the gates, and did not enter. Marcion does not receive the Old Testament, and without the gates of the Old Testament he could not enter into the Gospel. We, then, receive the gates, the prophets, and through the gates we enter. But let us see what the bars of the gates are. Many robbers, many thieves long to enter through the gates. All who came before me were thieves and robbers. Oh, if only the Lord would grant to me too that I might be a bar of the gates of Zion! If any of the heretics wished to enter through them into the arrangement of the Gospel, I would stand in the gates, and I would keep him out. For he has strengthened the bars of your gates. He has strengthened them. Give me some churchman learned in the heavenly Scriptures: let Eunomius come, let Arius come, wanting to take away something from the prophets against us — does he not stand like a bar, does he not conquer like a bar? For he has strengthened the bars of your gates. Beautifully he said, he has strengthened: so that when you see a churchman disputing, you should not think that it is he who disputes, but he who strengthens the churchman. He has blessed your children within you. As many as received him, he gave them power to become children of God.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.14":["Who has set your borders as peace. Where has peace been set, which was overthrown by war? And he fills you with the fat of the wheat. Does wheat have fat? Answer, O Jew, does wheat have fat? If, then, wheat does not have fat, and you are compelled by necessity to understand it spiritually, then according to this verse the rest too must be understood. And he fills you with the fat of the wheat. Unless, he says, the grain of wheat shall fall, it alone shall be saved; but if it itself shall fall, it makes many to be saved. Our Lord, the grain of wheat, fell into the earth, and multiplied us. But this grain of wheat is very rich; it has marrow, it has fat, it has richness. And he fills you with the fat of the wheat. Happy is he who understands the fat in this wheat. We read the holy Scriptures. I hold the Gospel to be the body of Jesus; the holy Scriptures I hold to be his teaching. And when he says, He who has not eaten my flesh and drunk my blood — although it can also be understood in the mystery, yet truly the body of Christ and his blood is the word of the Scriptures, is the divine teaching. If ever we go to the mystery — he who is faithful understands — if a little crumb falls, we are in peril. If ever we hear the word of God, and the word of God and the flesh of Christ and his blood is poured into our ears, and we are thinking of something else, into how great a peril do we run? And he fills you with the fat of the wheat. The divine word is very rich; it has in itself all delights. Whatever you wish is born from the divine word: just as the Jews hand down that the manna, when they ate it, tasted in the mouth according to each one's desire. For instance, he who ate the manna, if he desired apples, or a pear, or grapes, or bread, or meat — according to the kind and the desire of the one eating, so too was the taste in the manna. So too in the flesh of Christ, which is the word of teaching, that is, the interpretation of the holy Scriptures, as we wish, so also do we receive the food. If you are holy, you find refreshment; if you are a sinner, you find torment. And he fills you with the fat of the wheat. He leaves no remnants. Who has set your borders as peace. In another psalm we read: And his place was made in peace. My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you. My peace: not as the world has peace, since indeed the peace of the world is our war. My peace I give to you. Have war with the world, and you will have my peace. Who has set your borders as peace. He, then, who is not at peace with his brother, this man is not within the borders of Jerusalem. Let someone say: What am I to do? My brother does not want to be reconciled with me — am I then outside the borders of Jerusalem? As much as it lies in you, he says, having peace with all men. With those who hated peace, I was peaceable. You, keep the peace: if another is angry, he has war, and through his war peace is born for you. Do you want to know how there can be both war and peace? The wretched Judas was coming to the betrayal, and the Lord offers him peace by a kiss: he creates war out of the kiss.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.15":["Who sends forth his word to the earth. Answer, O Jew: if he was speaking of wheat, And he fills you with the fat of the wheat, how does he now say, Who sends forth his word to the earth? In the beginning was the Word. This Word is sent forth in the flesh. And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us. Who sends forth his word to the earth. At that time was fulfilled, The earth has given its fruit. Who sends forth his word to the earth. He is speaking of the preaching of the Gospel, of the teaching of the apostles. Then follows: Swiftly runs his word. For into all the earth his word was heard, and into the whole world the apostolic teaching. One poor man, already old, preached from Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, and swiftly runs his word. Since, then, it had come from Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, and his word had run swiftly, next it follows, and that very word which had said, From Jerusalem all the way to Illyricum, says: But I wish to go even to the Spains. This the apostle Paul speaks. See the swiftness of the word. He is not content with the east: he longs to run to the west.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.16":["Who gives snow like wool, who scatters mist like ashes. He sends his crystal like morsels. See how great is the difficulty, how obscure are the things that are said. Before the face of his cold, who shall stand? Whose? Nothing is said. Let us see, then, from the beginning what is said. And he fills you with the fat of the wheat: he sends forth his word to the earth, swiftly runs his word. Suddenly, what follows? Who gives snow like wool. The word of the Lord, which runs swiftly, is given as it were like snow, and that snow is as it were like wool. See the mercy of the Lord. The food of the Lord is turned for us into a garment. John had said of the Lord the Savior: Behold the Lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world. And in another place Isaiah: Like a sheep he was led to the slaughter. Of this lamb, then, and of this sheep, this wool is as it were like snow. Isaiah says: If your sins be like scarlet, they shall be made white like snow. You shall sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: you shall wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow. Who gives snow like wool. Take away, O Lord, my filth, take away whatever vice there is. Grant your snow and your purity to Christian minds. Unless you give us the snow of your cleansings, we cannot have your garment. Christ is our garment. If we wish to have the garment of Christ, let us be clean like snow. He scatters mist like ashes. The Latin translated badly, \"mist\": for what is called ὁμίχλη in Greek, in Hebrew has \"hoarfrost.\" And so it follows thus: He scatters hoarfrost like ashes. He teaches us, and makes for us a garment like snow; and when he has clothed us with his garment, then he grants us nourishment. He scatters hoarfrost like ashes. See what he says, like ashes. Do you wish to be clean? Do penance. I ate my bread like ashes, and mingled my drink with weeping. Do you not see how he scatters his hoarfrost like ashes? A great change of things: out of ashes snow is made, out of penance one is made clean.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.17":["He sends his crystal like morsels. As his loaves are broken and become morsels, so too this crystal is broken and turned into morsels. Crystal has this nature: it forms whenever waters are frozen — what we call ice. Between crystal and water, then, there is the same difference as between water and ice: for water is poured out, as it were, very broadly, whereas crystal is, as it were, compacted into itself, and reduced into one form. Whenever water freezes, it makes crystal. Since, then, we cannot grasp the flesh with the whole word, it is broken, so that we may grasp it through those parts. The matters are obscure, and the difficulty of interpretation does not allow us to speak more broadly. Before the face of his cold, who shall stand? Whose? Some say the crystal's, but others say God's. To me, however, it seems that Before the face of his cold, who shall stand? is said of God. Of God I say it, Before the face of his cold, who shall stand? What torments are said in the holy Scriptures to be prepared for the sinner? Nothing is hotter than Gehenna, and yet it itself is cold. Gehenna is of fire: but if anyone has grown sufficiently cold, he is cast into the fire of Gehenna. It is written that in the last days the love of many shall grow cold. Whoever, then, has grown cold in this way, this man is cast into the fire. May the Lord grant to us that cold may never enter our hearts: we do not sin unless love has first grown cold. Then, what does the apostle also say? Fervent in spirit. Our God is a fire. If, then, God is fire, he is fire for this reason: to cast out the cold of the devil. The north wind, the coldest wind; but by name it is called \"the right.\" Beautifully he said, is called: it has taken to itself a name, though it is on the left. If, then, anyone's soul has grown cold, like the north wind, the coldest wind — if ever the warmth of love grows cold in our heart, if it has sinned, if it has grown cold, if it has died... Attend diligently to what I say: if it has grown cold, if it has died. To grow cold belongs to the dead, to be warm to the living.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.147.18":["He shall send forth his word, and shall melt them. Of that one of whom he had said, He sends forth his word to the earth: He shall send forth his word. Who shall send it forth? God the Father. And the Word was with God, and the Word was God. We have named the Father, we have named the Son: we cannot believe in a twofold number, unless the Trinity be completed. His spirit shall blow, and the waters shall flow. You see that where the Father and the Word of God are, at once the Holy Spirit is there too. If anyone, then, has grown cold and died, God will send his word, and will melt him. May the Lord grant that our cold too may be melted, that that crystal may be dissolved and become soft. He shall send forth his word, and shall melt them. Give me a sinner who does not look to God, who has no warmth, but is wholly cold, and dead: if at the word of God he is pierced with compunction, and begins to do penance, and the hardest things of his heart are softened, at that time is fulfilled, He shall send forth his word, and shall melt them. The Father sends forth, the Word is sent, the Holy Spirit is joined. His spirit shall blow. Fervent in spirit. The cold is not driven out except by the warmth of the Holy Spirit. Where are the Eunomians, the Arians, where are the Macedonians, who say: We do not number the Holy Spirit with the Father and the Son? God the Father sends, the Word is sent: and yet there is no full healing, unless the Holy Spirit shall have blown. His spirit shall blow, and the waters shall flow. The waters. What before was hard shall become soft. He who before could give righteousness to no one, this man is now a disciple. But since we have spoken at length, and the things that follow are plain, let us pray the Lord that our hardness too may soften, that our sins too may be purged, that we may be made as it were fire, that the cold of the devil, whatever there is of it in our hearts, may be driven out, that we may grow warm with the Holy Spirit. For there is also an evil warmth. All the adulterers, their hearts are like an oven. But that warmth is driven out by the warmth of the Holy Spirit. To whom be glory unto the ages of ages. Amen.\n— Homily 57, on Psalm 147"],"PSA.148.1":["This psalm has two alleluias in its title; and many think this is because they belong to the title of the psalm itself. But we ought to know that one alleluia belongs to the end of the preceding psalm, while the other alleluia is the beginning of the following psalm. And we ought to take up this rule and understand that all the psalms which have alleluia at their beginning have it also at their end. Many, however, suppose that it is the beginning of another psalm. It is not so: rather, that psalm which has alleluia at its beginning is the very one that has alleluia at its end as well. Why have I said all this? So that you may know why in this psalm two alleluias have been placed at the front.\n\nPraise the Lord from the heavens, praise him in the heights. For the praise of God the nature of men alone does not suffice: let the heavens too be joined in. Praise the Lord from the heavens. He did not say, Praise, O heavens; but, You praise who are in the heavens, that is, you who dwell in the heavens, thrones, dominions, powers, seraphim, cherubim, and every name that is named, not only in the present age, but also in the age to come. Praise the Lord from the heavens. You who are in the heavens, you who are in the heights, praise him in the heights. You lofty ones, praise the Lofty One. He did not say to the lowly and to those placed below, Praise; but to you who are on high.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.2":["Praise him, all his angels. Do you see in what rank the angels are? First the thrones are named: thrones, seats. Let us not suppose these to be chairs on which God sits: but certain powers among the heavenly beings on which God ever sits, as it were in his own chariot. Praise him, all his angels. All his angels. He who is an angel, let him praise: he who is a demon cannot praise. Praise him, all his hosts. Since we have summoned the angels to the praises of God, and I, a man, cannot know the name of all the hosts, I say in general terms: Praise him, all his hosts. So far concerning the invisible things. Praise him, you who are invisible, whom the human eye cannot behold. Now he comes to the creatures which we discern with our eyes.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.3":["Praise him, sun and moon. Look, you pagans: look, you Manichaeans. The sun praises, it is not praised. Praise him, sun and moon. You call the sun a god. See after how many others it praises. Praise from the heavens, praise in the heights, praise, you angels, praise, all you hosts. Your god is placed as one that praises in the fifth rank. Praise him, sun and moon. He did not say, moon and sun; but, sun and moon. What is greater in light praises first. See the order of the visible things. Praise him, O sun: after the sun, the moon: after the moon, all the stars. All the stars: not only the morning star, not only the greater stars, but let the lesser ones as well alike praise the Lord. Let someone say: How do the sun and moon and stars praise God? In this, that they do not depart from their office and service. Their service is the praise of God. You have great honor, O human soul. The sun and moon and stars run their course for you, they serve for you. The angels, thrones, dominions have no need of the sun, nor of the moon, nor of the stars: for they are above the sun and moon in the heavenly places. The sun therefore and the moon and the stars are for our sake, they serve us. The apostle too says that the very creation groans together and is in travail for the deliverance of the sons of God. But when the sons of God shall have been taken up, then the creation itself also is set free from this servitude. Let someone say: How does the sun serve, and how is it set free? how the moon? how the stars? The sun is set free, that it may not look upon the Jews, nor upon the pagans, nor upon those who blaspheme their Lord. It sees parricides, it sees murderers: and yet it serves the command of him who ordered it. For he makes his sun to rise upon the just and the wicked. Praise him, all you stars and light. Let someone say: What is light without the sun, without the stars, without the moon? We can say, between night and day, or between day and night, there is that which shines apart from the sun: though many think that this very brightness belongs to the sun. He says therefore, Praise him, all you stars and light, so that just as he had said of the invisible things, Praise him, all his hosts, so also of the visible things he may say, Praise him, everything that shines.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.4":["Praise him, you heavens of heavens. We speak of several heavens. Indeed even Paul says that he was caught up into the third heaven. See therefore how great is the splendor, that there should be a heaven of heaven. And the rest, which follow, he said concerning the visible things.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.5":["For he spoke, and they were made. For God to have commanded is to have made: his command is the fashioning. For he spoke, and they were made: according to that which is written in Genesis, And God said, and God made: that is, God the Father spoke, God the Son made. Let someone say: Then greater is he who commands, and lesser is he who is commanded. This the Arians say, this the Eunomians say, and this the Macedonians say. I answer you according to your own way of thinking, O heretic. You say: Greater is the Father who commands, and lesser is the Son in that he is commanded by him. If this be according to human reasoning, answer me: which is greater, to command, or to make? I say, Let a house be built: and another builds the house. To speak is no great thing: to make is difficult. Greater therefore is he who makes than he who gives the order. But this too is impious: for the Son is not greater than the Father. For just as it is impious to believe this of the Son against the Father, so also it is impious to believe that of the Father against the Son. For he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created. One nature commands and makes: God commands, God makes. The painter orders that the painter should paint, and the painter paints what he had ordered to be painted.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.6":["He established them for ever, and for age upon age: he set a decree, and it shall not pass away. Great is the honor of man, and great his unhappiness. The sun runs its course for you, the elements serve you: and you do not know your Creator. He set a decree, and it shall not pass away. He set a decree for the sun: behold, through so many years the decree of God has never passed away. So far has it not passed away, that its course has even been grasped by human observation. He set a decree, and it shall not pass away. He set a decree for the moon, that through thirty days it should wax and wane: does it ever change its courses? In heaven the decree of God is kept, and on earth it is not kept. He set a decree for the sea, that it should not go beyond its shores: the ocean comes swelling with its waves, and returns into itself, and remembers the decree of God. The whole world serves God, and remembers his decree: and man alone does not remember. Therefore we say also in the Lord's Prayer: Your will be done, as in heaven, so on earth. As in heaven the angels serve you, and the creation serves, so let man too serve. O unhappy race of men! God has come down to you, because you are unwilling to ascend to heaven. It is not enough for you that you do not receive him, but you kill: you kill, you crucify, you blaspheme, you scourge. It is not enough for you that you kill: but you do not even do penance for having killed. He spoke of the invisible things, he spoke also of the visible things, but of the visible heavenly things: now of the earth as well. See the order of praising. First the invisible things praise, then the visible: and the visible things themselves are divided into two, into the heavenly and the earthly. He spoke of the heavenly things, now he speaks of the earthly.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.148.7-13":["Praise the Lord from the earth. Since the angels have praised, the heaven has praised: let the earth also praise. Let us see which things of the earth praise first. Dragons and all deeps: fire, hail, snow, ice, the spirit of storms, which do his word: mountains and all hills, fruit-bearing trees, and all cedars: beasts, and all cattle, serpents and feathered fowl. After such great things, what is said? After the dragons, after the serpents, after the fire, after the hail, after the beasts and all the cattle? O man, you who seem to yourself so great, it is said of you: and not of the simple man, but of those men who are the greatest among men. Kings of the earth, and all peoples: princes, and all judges of the earth. Then there follows: Let young men and maidens, old men with younger ones praise the name of the Lord: for the name of him alone is exalted. And the rest that follows. Oh, how many mysteries are contained in these words! The heavenly things have praised, the invisible things have praised, the visible things have praised: now he descends to earthly things, and to the praise of God first the dragons and the deeps are stirred up. Where sin abounded, grace has superabounded. You who were first a dragon, who had the venom of the serpent: O you who were first a deep, and darkness was borne over the deep. See what he says. Where dragons are, there is venom: where the deep is, there is darkness. Do not despair therefore, O you who were a dragon, who were full of darkness, and did not dare to praise God; do penance, and at once you will be converted. Although in the Hebrew it does not have \"dragons,\" but has THANNINIM, which is rendered \"sea-monsters.\" Now the sea-monster (cetus) is said to be of boundless size. Indeed the sea-monster itself is said to be in the deeps. Let us then speak first according to the letter. See what he says. Praise, you things that are in the sea, and praise, you things that are outside the sea: dragons and all deeps. The whole from a part, by way of synecdoche (synekdochikōs). Then follows fire. See the concord of discord: water and fire together praise the Lord. Dragons and all deeps, fire. All things that are born from water are also born from fire, that is, from moisture and heat: neither can fire alone generate without moisture, nor moisture without fire. Nothing is so contrary as moisture to heat, and heat to moisture; yet by the will of God these things, which are diverse among themselves, come into concord in our birth. Hail, snow, ice, the spirit of storms, which do his word. There are many things that might be said, and we cannot linger over each one. Hail, snow, ice, the spirit of storms. All the things that we see in the world serve us. Serpents and deeps, fire, hail, snow, ice, the spirit of storms seem to be without reason: but they serve us. Yesterday there was a storm, it seemed to us to be against us: there was cold, there was damp, there were rains. This which for an hour seems to us to be against us, is for us: for it brings forth fruit for us. For see what he says: these things and those, which do his word. Blush, O man: serpents and deeps, snow, rains and storm do the word of God, and you do not do it. Mountains and all hills. Neither are mountains changed into hills, nor valleys into mountains: they keep their own nature. Fruit-bearing trees, and all cedars. You see a tree as if dry, its leaves lost: and at once, when the spring has come, it sends forth a bud for you, it brings forth fruit for you. The tree brings forth fruit for you, and you bring forth no fruit for God. And all cedars. Imitate the cedar: cedars strive toward the heights, as if they hasten to go to heaven. And you, imitate the irrational trees. Beasts, and all cattle. They change nothing of their nature: all stand in their own order, except man alone, to whom all things are made subject. Serpents, and feathered fowl. Neither do those things which are of the earth go to heaven, nor do those things which are in heaven come to earth: they serve you. Let someone say: You speak according to the literal sense, you do not speak allegorically: if you speak according to the literal sense, why do first the deeps, and snow, and hail, then the fruit-bearing trees, and the other irrational things praise the Lord, and last of all man? Let us read Genesis, and according to the order of the birth and the creatures, so shall we also see all things praise. For there it is written that first those living creatures were made which are in the water: then were made those which are on the earth: then the flying things were made: and afterward he made man alone. Therefore, as each was first made, so let it praise the Lord. But there is another sense. O man, all these things which I have named, for whose sake were they made? Beasts, serpents, fruit-bearing trees, feathered fowl: all these praise God in this, that they do not change their nature. For he set a decree, and it shall not pass away. Imitate therefore the things that serve you. They were made, that they might serve you: you were made, that you might serve God. They serve you: imitate the things that serve you, and serve God, just as they too serve you, and through God serve. To whom is glory for ever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 58, on Psalm 148"],"PSA.149.1":["Psalm 149 has been read, which has at its beginning: Sing to the Lord a new song. The holy presbyter has said that both the ninety-fifth psalm and the ninety-seventh have the same beginnings. The ninety-fifth psalm is headed with this title: A Psalm of David, when the house was being built after the captivity. The house of Christ is built after the captivity, that is, after repentance. Since, therefore, he said in the first one, Sing to the Lord a new song, so that a new people might sing a new song — and he put it well — let us run through the rest.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.2":["Let Israel rejoice in him who made him. Let that man rejoice who sees God with the mind: for Israel is interpreted as \"one seeing God with the mind.\" And let the sons of Zion exult in their king. Let the sons of the Church, the sons of the new people, exult in their king: in Christ, who reigns over them. You see how lightly we touch upon each of the highest points.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.3":["Let them praise his name in the choir. Wherever there is a choir, there diverse voices are gathered together into one song. For just as diverse strings produce the one sound of a song, so also diverse voices — that is, when they have been gathered together at once — make the choir of the Lord. Let them praise his name in the choir: where there is dissension, where there is envy, there is no choir. Let them praise his name in the choir: let them sing praise to him on the timbrel and the psaltery. Let this choir have a song, and have a timbrel and a psaltery. Let us crucify our bodies to Christ, and with such a timbrel let us sing to God. Let them sing praise to him on the timbrel and the psaltery. A timbrel does not have flesh, but a skin: and we, as long as we are fleshly, are not a timbrel. It is not a timbrel unless it has no flesh, unless it is stretched taut: a timbrel cannot be shrunken, but stretched out. And let sins not shrink us up, but let righteousness stretch us wide. Let us run through the rest. Let them sing praise to him on the timbrel and the psaltery. What we say in the choir, let us say this on the psaltery. The psaltery properly is a kind of musical instrument, which is like a lyre. It has a resemblance to a lyre, but it is not a lyre. Between the psaltery and the lyre there is this difference: the lyre is struck below, whereas the psaltery is struck above, which in the common word is called polyphthongon (many-toned). This, then, is the psaltery. Here, therefore, it is enjoined upon us that in the new song we should not sing to the Lord from below on the lyre, but from above, that is, on the psaltery.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.4":["For the Lord is well pleased with his people, and he will exalt the meek in salvation. \"Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the earth.\" If, therefore, it is said there, Blessed are the meek — that is, the gentle — for they shall possess the earth; and here it is said, And he will exalt the meek in salvation; and these meek shall possess the earth by inheritance: therefore this earth is not below, but is above. For what does he say? He will exalt the meek in salvation. For if these meek were to possess the earth that is below, he would not say, He will exalt.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.5":["The saints shall exult in glory. In what glory? \"Arise, my glory; arise, psaltery and lyre: I will arise at dawn.\" In glory. \"For the Spirit was not yet given, because Jesus was not yet glorified\" — that is, he had not yet been crucified. Scripture says this, The Spirit was not yet given, because Jesus had not been glorified, that is, he had not been crucified. The saints shall exult in glory. Why do I say all this? Because it is said, The saints shall exult in glory. This, then, is what I say: the saints shall exult in the cross. \"But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ.\" Behold, in the voice of the apostle the prophecy is fulfilled. Let the saints exult in glory. Let them rejoice in their beds. \"I will wash my bed every night; with my tears I will drench my couch.\" That belongs to the sinner, that belongs to the penitent; but this which is said, They shall rejoice in their beds, pertains to the saints, pertains to the just. Which of us rejoices in his bed? Who does not fight with his own flesh? When I have lain awake on my little bed, I do not rejoice, but I fight: I do not sigh for the kingdom of heaven, but I fight with the flesh. The burning of the flesh I drench with tears, and I quench it. For this reason it is said, I will wash my bed every night; with my tears I will drench my couch. My little bed, which burns with lust, is quenched with tears. Happy are those of whom it is said, They shall rejoice in their beds.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.6":["The exaltations of God are in their throat. Surely he who cries out does not cry out in the throat, but on the lips: that is, he does not cry out in the throat, but cries out on the lips. And how does he say here, The exaltations of God are in their throat? \"Crying out in our hearts, Abba, Father.\" The voice that cries out to God is not of the lips, but is of the heart. Finally, the Lord says to Moses: \"Why do you cry out to me?\" And certainly Moses had said nothing. And two-edged swords are in their hands. These who exult in their beds — it is said of the saints, it is said of perfect men — what else do they have? And two-edged swords are in their hands. Two-edged swords. The swords of the saints are two-edged. We read in the Apocalypse of John, which is read in the churches and is received — for it is not held among the apocryphal scriptures, but among the ecclesiastical — it is said there, then, of the Lord Savior: \"And a sword, he says, sharp on both sides went forth from his mouth.\" You see, therefore, that these saints received those two-edged swords, which they have in their hands, from the mouth of the Lord. The Lord, therefore, gives a sword from his mouth to his disciples: a two-edged sword, that is, the word of his teaching. A two-edged sword: both according to history and according to allegory, both according to the letter and according to the spirit. A two-edged sword: which both kills adversaries and defends its own. A two-edged sword. This two-edged sword has two edges: it speaks both of the present age and of the age to come: and here it kills adversaries, and in heaven it opens the kingdoms of heaven. Why do the saints have these swords?\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.7":["To execute, he says, vengeance upon the nations. Oh, if only I too might have that two-edged sword, that I might execute vengeance upon the nations, that I might slay the various peoples, that I might slay Arius, that I might slay Eunomius, that I might slay Manichaeus, that I might slay all the heresies! And see what he says, To execute vengeance upon the nations. That they may be killed? that they may perish? He does not say this. Rebukes among the peoples. Rebuke, that the peoples may be saved, that those who have been led astray by evil teachers may be saved. But the peoples cannot be set free unless the teachers have been bound. Whence it follows:\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.8":["To bind their kings in fetters. Do you wish the peoples to be set free? bind the teachers. To bind the kings. He did not call them teachers, but kings: for they reign by the miseries of those people. To bind their kings in fetters. He put it well, in fetters: lest the teachers should run about too much, lest they deceive many, but let them be bound and stand still. And their nobles in iron chains. Their nobles. You may see them saying among the heretics: This man is learned, that one works signs, this one casts out demons. The saints of the Lord, who have the two-edged swords — that is, of two edges, both of the Old and of the New Testament — bind those teachers, and shackle their feet, so that they cannot run. And their nobles in iron chains. They bind, they do not kill; they do not slay, but they bind. They fetter the hands, and lead them captive to the Church in iron chains. These chains are made only from the testimonies of the scriptures, when I join testimony to testimony. If you wish that these chains not be broken, let them not be single, but woven together from this side and that. \"A threefold cord is not broken.\" But why is all this done?\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"PSA.149.9":["That they may execute upon them the judgment that is written. What is the judgment that is written? That those who at first had denied God may afterward return through repentance. The judgment that is written: that they may loose their captives, that those whom they had bound, those whom they had led bound out of the Church, they may afterward set free in the Church. That they may execute upon them the judgment that is written. I speak in simpler words. That they may execute upon them the judgment that is written: that is, that they may so judge them as it has been set forth in the holy scriptures. This is the glory for all his saints. Truly it is not glory where there is gold, where there is silver, where there are jewels, where there are silken garments. Whoever has these swords, what else does he need? See what he says, This is the glory for all his saints. Let us ask the Lord that we may have this glory: that a sword going forth from the mouth of our Savior may arm our hands, and that we may be able to say: \"Blessed be the Lord my God, who teaches my hands for the fight, and my fingers for the war.\" He who has this broadsword does not fear the sword of the world. He who has this sword does not fear the sword of the world. I tell you a new thing. Frequently the sword of this world grants that sword: frequently it happens that the sword of the world bestows that sword upon us. For if we do not fear this one, we receive that one. Let us therefore give thanks to him who has this sword, and let us bless him for ever and ever. Amen.\n— Homily 59, on Psalm 149"],"JOB.2.10":["I think that Job too, when he was speaking to his wife as to one of the foolish women, spoke of bodily riches with regard to her, who thought so: \"If we have received good things from the hand of the Lord\" (Job 2:10); and again, of the straits and afflictions and temptation, which bring victory: \"Why should we not endure evil?\" Whereas, surely, evil and good are placed not in riches and afflictions, but in virtues and vices, as the righteous man says in the psalm: \"Who is the man who wants life, desires to see good days? Keep your tongue from evil, and let your lips not speak deceit. Turn away from evil, and do good\" (Ps. 33:13 ff., and 36:27). Properly, evil is called that which must be avoided; good, that which we ought to do.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"JOB.40.8":["I think that it was for the pressing out of such wine and oil that Job suffered many things, and that, after he had made the wine and the oil, he heard from the Lord: Do you think that I answered you otherwise than that you might appear just? As if he were speaking to the grape and the olive: Do you suppose that I pressed and afflicted and crushed you for any other reason than to press wine and oil out of you?\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"JOB.40.16":["When Jerusalem was born of an Amorite father and a Hittite mother and poured forth from the womb, her navel was not cut, the navel by which fetuses are nourished in the belly, like trees and shrubs which are nourished through their roots by the hidden moisture of the earth — and just as the seed-bed of men is signified in the loins, so the genitals of women, by a decent expression, according to the custom of the Scriptures, are called the navel, on the testimony of Job who, under the figure of the dragon, speaks of the devil: His strength is in his loins, and his might in the navel of his belly; for this dragon, the ancient serpent who is called the devil and Satan and deceived the whole world, has his strength against men in the loins, against women in the navel —, and this signifies of Jerusalem that she did not at once receive the Law, nor were the beginnings of her shameful birth cut away from her, but she first lived a Gentile life; for which the Seventy translated: On the day on which you were born, they did not bind your breasts, since Holy Scripture has this custom, that for the heart it names the little breast, or the breast, and the breasts, as it sees fit for each place — the priests, in whom there ought to be doctrine, and from whose mouth they seek the Law, receive the little breast; John reclines upon the breast of the Lord, to draw the streams of wisdom from the richest fountain; the virgin's two breasts in the Song of Songs are like two fawns, twins of a gazelle, which feed among the lilies, until the day breathes and the shadows are removed; a careful mother, as soon as her baby girl is born, binds her breasts, so that they may refrain from tender swelling and keep their virginal beauty, but when she has come to the age of puberty, it is said of her: Will a bride forget her adornment, or a virgin her breast-band? —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PRO.1.3-4":["And because he rains snares upon sinners, and gives them to drink the goblet of torments which they deserve, God himself, being just, loves justices. For just as there is one true God, even if there are many gods who are so called by participation in him, and there is one only-begotten Son of God, while others are called so by adoption; so also there is one true justice, as is written in the beginning of the Proverbs, but from communication of it the Lord loves many things called justices.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 60 (PSALM 10)","To confess that we are imperfect, and have not yet apprehended, nor yet obtained, this is true wisdom in man: know thyself to be imperfect; and, if I may so speak, the perfection of all who are righteous, so long as they are in the flesh, is imperfect. Hence we read in Proverbs: \"To understand true righteousness.\" For if there were not also a false righteousness, the righteousness of God would never be called true.\n— Against the Pelagians 1.14","But \"panourgia,\" that is, cleverness, may be taken either in a good or in a bad sense: in a good sense, according to Proverbs, \"That he may give,\" it says, \"shrewdness to the innocent, and to the younger boy sense and understanding\"; in a bad sense, as in the apostle, \"But I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his shrewdness, your minds may be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.\" What he says, then, is this: my instruction and deep thought and the inner affection of the heart was not only with me in the heavenly places, but even while I dwelt in the night of this world and in darkness, it was engaged with me in the man, and instructed me, and was never absent from me, so that whatever the weakness of the flesh could not do, divine thought and power might accomplish.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 61 (PSALM 15)"],"PRO.3.16":["Someone may object: If the just man has no left hand, how is Wisdom written to have both a right hand and a left? A diligent reader will very easily judge how great is the difference between the right hand of Wisdom and the left, when he has noticed what things are said to be in her right and what in her left. For it is said: \"Length of eternity and years of life are in her right hand, but in her left hand riches and glory.\" You see eternal time and perpetual life placed in Wisdom's right hand; but the carnal things which pass away, which flee off while they are thought to be held—that is, riches and glory—placed in her left hand, according to that, that on the day of judgment some will stand at the right, and others at the left: the lambs, that is, and the saints at his right, but the goats and the sinners at his left.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 61 (PSALM 15)"],"PRO.3.18":["'He is like a tree planted near running water, that yields its fruit in due season, and whose leaves never fade.' [Psalm 1:3] There are many who interpret these words very simply to mean that just as a tree, if planted near water, will take root and grow and not wither away because it has enough moisture, so in like manner one who meditates on the law of God will derive strength and life from his meditation. This is their simple interpretation. But we shall combine spiritual things with spiritual things [1 Cor. 2:13] and read of the tree of life that was planted in Paradise, the tree of life and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. This tree of life was planted in the Garden of Eden and in Eden there rose a river that separated into four branches [Gen 2:9]... Likewise we read in Solomon - if one accepts that book as Solomon's, for he speaks there of wisdom (Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God [1 Cor. 1:24]) then, as I was saying, where Solomon says: 'She is a tree of life to those who grasp her,' [Prov 3:18] he is speaking of wisdom. Now, if wisdom is the tree of life, Wisdom itself, indeed, is Christ. You understand now that the man who is blessed and holy is compared to this tree, that is, he is compared to Wisdom. Consequently, you see, too, that the just man, that blessed man who has not followed in the counsel of the wicked - who has not done that but has done this - is like the tree that is planted near running water. He is, in other words, like Christ, inasmuch as He 'raised us up together, and seated us together in heaven.' [Eph. 2:6] You see, then, that we shall reign together with Christ in heaven; you see, too, that because this tree has been planted in the Garden of Eden, we have all been planted there together with Him.\n\n'He is like a tree planted near running water.' Indeed, it is from that fountainhead that all rivers take their rise. 'That yields its fruit in due season.' This tree does not yield fruit in every season, but in the proper season. This is the tree that does not yield its fruit in the present day, but in the future, that is, on the day of judgment. This is the tree that bears blossoms now, that buds forth now, and promises fruits for the future. This tree bears twofold: it produces fruit and it produces foliage. The fruit that it bears contains the meaning of Scripture; the leaves, only the words. The fruit is in the meaning; the leaves are in the words. For that reason, whoever reads Sacred Scripture, if he reads merely as the Jews read, grasps only the words. If he reads with true spiritual insight, he gathers the fruit.\n\n'And whose leaves never fade.' The leaves of this tree are by no means useless. Even if one understands Holy Writ only as history, he has something useful for his soul. We read in the Apocalypse of John (a book which, although rejected in these regions, we ought nevertheless to know, because it is accepted and held as canonical throughout the west, and in other Phoenician provinces, and in Egypt, for the ancient churchmen, including Irenaeus, Polycarp, Dionysius, and other Roman expounders of Sacred Scripture, among whom is holy Cyprian, accept and interpret it): 'Behold, I saw a throne set up, and one Lamb and a tree alongside a river, and on both sides of the river was that tree.' [Revelation 22:1-2] This means that the tree 'was both on this side and on that side of the river. 'And this tree,' he says, 'bore fruit and was yielding its twelve fruits for the year according to each month. And it had lean's, too, and the leaves for the healing of the nations.' [Revelation 22:2]\n\n'I saw,' he says, 'a single throne set up.' We believe in the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit, that is true, and that they are a Trinity; nevertheless the kingship is one. 'I saw a single throne set lip, and I saw a single Lamb standing in the presence of the throne.' [Revelation 5:6] This refers to the Incarnation of the Savior. Scripture says: 'Behold the lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world' [John 1:29] 'And there was a fountain of water coming forth from beneath the middle of the throne' [Revelation 22:1] Notice that it is from the midst of the throne that there issues forth a river of graces. That river does not issue forth from the throne unless the Lamb is standing before it, [Revelation 7:17] for unless we believe in the Incarnation of Christ, we do not receive those graces.\n\nA tree, he says, one lofty tree had been set up. He did not say trees, but only one tree. If there is but one tree how can it be on both sides of the river? If he had said, I saw trees, it would have been possible for some trees to be on one side of the river and other trees on the other side. Actually, one tree is said to be on both sides of the river. One river comes forth from the throne of God - the grace of the Holy Spirit - and this grace of the Holy Spirit is found in the river of the Sacred Scriptures. This river, moreover, has two banks, the Old Testament and the New Testament, and the tree planted on both sides is Christ. During the year, this tree yields twelve fruits, one for each month, but we are unable to receive the fruits except through the apostles. If one approaches the tree through the apostles, he must receive the fruit; he gathers the fruit from the Sacred Scriptures; he grasps the divine meaning abiding within the words. If, therefore, one comes to this tree through the apostles, he gathers its fruit just as we have said. If, indeed, he cannot pluck the fruit, it is because he is still too weak; he is not yet a disciple, but belongs to the throng; he is an outsider, a stranger from the nations. Because he cannot pluck the fruit, he plucks only words, the leaves for the healing of the nations, for it is written: 'and the leaves are for the healing of the nations.' One who belongs to the nations, who is not a disciple, who is as yet only one of the crowd, gathers only leaves from the tree; he receives from Scripture plain words for a healing remedy. Briefly, then, the Scripture says: 'and the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations'; in other words, the leaves are medicine. Why have we digressed on the Apocalypse? Simply became of that tree 'that yields its fruit in due season, and whose leaves never fade. Whatever he does, prospers.'\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1","What this tree of life is, Solomon explains more plainly, who, discoursing of the wisdom of God, says: It is a tree of life to all who come to it, and for those who lean upon it, it is as firm as upon the Lord (Prov 3:18); nor is there doubt that it signifies the Word of God, who is himself the way and wisdom, and says of himself: I am the life (John 11:25). And of whom the Prophet sings: In wisdom you have made all things: the earth is filled with your creation (Ps 103 [104]:24); and the apostle Paul: Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1:24).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PRO.6.26":["I will hope in Him, for He Himself shall deliver me from the snare of the hunters. In this world there are many hunters who long to capture our souls, just as is said by the prophet: \"Our soul has been snatched away like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters.\" Now that hunters are taken in a sinister sense in the holy Scriptures, we have already said often. For that Nimrod, the giant, always a rebel against God, was a hunter. Esau too was a hunter. For He Himself shall deliver me from the snare of the hunters. We read in Proverbs: \"The eye of the harlot is the snare of the sinner.\" \"For whoever shall look upon a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.\" As many as are the sins, so many are the snares; as many as the snares, so many the hunters. The doctrines of the heretics are the snares of death.\n— Homily 68, on Psalm 90 (Alternate Series)"],"PRO.7.11-13":["These stones the heretics do not imitate, who do everything for the sake of glory and gain, and pray at the corners of the streets, leaving the straight path. For every corner breaks a straight line; and therefore the harlot in Proverbs, however we may wish to take her, whose feet do not rest at home, but she either wanders out of doors or lies in wait at every corner of the streets, when she has seen a foolish young man — for she does not invite one whom she has perceived to be prudent and mature with age, of whom it is written: A man's grey hairs are his wisdom —, at once seizes him, and kisses and flatters him, and leads him to the brothel, and invites him to intercourse.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"PRO.8.22":["According to the Seventy, those who said, Lord God, possess us, pray for this, that after peace has been restored to them they may become the possession of God. This indeed we read also of Wisdom, which according to the Hebrew speaks in Proverbs: God possessed me, the beginning of his ways (Prov 8:22), although some copies wrongly have creature for possession. Then it follows: But before all the hills he begot me. For how can begetting be fitted to a creature, when it rather suits possession?\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Now there came to this tower the first power, or the first principality, he who had said: I am alpha and Ω, the beginning and the end, the first and the last. And he who, in the person of the man he had assumed, says in Proverbs: The Lord created me in the beginning of his ways for his works, or, as it is written in the Hebrew: The Lord possessed me: for canani means not he created me, but he possessed me and held me.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"PRO.9.8":["In the same way the wise are named in two senses: both those who are of full and perfect virtue, and those who are beginning and are set on the way of progress. Of the perfect it is said: \"I will send wise men to you\" (Luke 11:49). Of the unwise: \"Rebuke a wise man, and he will love you\" (Prov. 9:8). For he who is of full and consummate virtue does not need reproof. According to this sense the other virtues too are to be understood: namely, that brave and prudent, pious, chaste, righteous and temperate are taken sometimes in the full sense, sometimes loosely.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PRO.13.8":["And also those poor of whom it is written in Solomon: \"The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches\" (Prov. 13:8). But the poor man does not bear the threat. For he cannot hear the future terror of punishments, poor in faith, poor in grace, not having spiritual riches, nor the knowledge of the Scriptures, which are compared to gold and silver and precious stone.\n— Commentary on Galatians"],"PRO.18.17":["Confess to the Lord, for he is good. And yet, even though heretics say that there is no repentance, still the prophet urges us to confess our sins. For God is good. Blessed, therefore, is he who confesses his sins, just as the apostle also says: \"I who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God.\" If an apostle confesses, how much more the sinner? And again: \"The just man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech.\" If, then, the just man is, how much more the sinner?\n— Homily 47, on Psalm 135"],"PRO.20.13":["Or certainly this is to be said, that the word of the Gospel's teaching is called rain, and the showers which the spiritual clouds pour upon the good earth, the clouds to which the truth of God reaches. This rain and these showers Moses promises in the Song of Deuteronomy (Deut 32:1, 2): Let the earth hear the words of my mouth: let my speech be awaited as the rain, and let my words come down as the dew; so that those who have sown in tears may reap in joy (Ps 125 [126]). And let those who have sown in righteousness and in the spirit reap the fruit of life everlasting, and receive the bread of the Gospel's teaching, of which it is written in Proverbs and in Ecclesiastes: Open your eyes, and be filled with bread (Prov 20:13); and again: Cast your bread upon the water, for in the multitude of your days you will find it (Eccl 11:1). For it is not to be believed that those who eat are commanded that, to eat this bread by which bodies are nourished, they must open their eyes and so be filled with the bread which in Habakkuk the poor man eats in secret (Hab 3). Rather, he exhorts us to the bread of the teaching of God, which we cannot eat unless we have opened the eyes of our heart. Of this Paul too often writes to those who are nourished by the words of faith and truth (2 Cor 9). And the teacher is commanded to cast the bread of his teaching upon every water, and to pour spiritual grace upon all, and to know that if he does what has been commanded, he will receive rewards in the last time. And it would seem unjust enough that, while he who gives alms makes for himself friends of the mammon of iniquity, who may receive him into the eternal tabernacles (Luke 16), he who bestows spiritual foods, and gives his fellow servants their rations in due season, should not find them after many ages, which Ecclesiastes calls the multitude of days (Eccl 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PRO.21.1":["The kings of the earth are those who reign in sin. Therefore let those very ones confess, who reign in sin. For he is not speaking of the kings of this world. For it is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" Do you suppose that the heart of the most impious Julian was in the hand of God? God forbid. Or of Nero, or of the persecutors Maximian and Decius? God forbid. But he is speaking of those who reign in sin, because those who conquer the vices and passions of the soul and reign over sin, their heart is in the hand of God.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 49 (PSALM 137)","Who gives salvation to kings. To what kings? \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" Even of Julian the persecutor, and of Nero, and of Decius? The heart of the king is in the hand of God. Those who reign over their own body, who subject their body and reduce it to servitude, lest, while preaching to others, they themselves be found reprobate. These are the kings of whom wisdom says in Proverbs: \"She gives a kingdom to kings.\"\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 54 (PSALM 143)","And in another place it is written: \"The heart of the king is in the hand of God.\" Was the heart of Julian the persecutor in the hand of God? Was the heart of Saul in the hand of God? Was the heart of Manasseh in the hand of God? Was the heart of Ahab in the hand of God? And were the hearts of all the impious kings of Judah in the hand of God? You see that it cannot stand according to the letter. Kings, then, are the saints, and the heart of these is in the hand of the Lord. Let us therefore also ask God that we may be kings, and may rule over our flesh, that it may be subject to us. Whence the apostle also says: \"Therefore I chastise my body and bring it into servitude, lest by chance, while preaching to others, I myself should become a castaway.\" Let our soul rule, let the body serve: and at once Christ will come, and will dwell in us.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 9 (PSALM 75)","And it is now said: Horns in his hands, in the same way as we read written elsewhere: The heart of the king is in the hand of God, meaning that the mind and the ruling part of the heart of a holy man — who is going on to the kingdom of heaven, who while still placed on earth reigns over his body without sins — does not wander outside, but is set in the keeping of God.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"PRO.21.22":["Therefore the Lord, who dispenses all things by reason and does all things by true judgment, divides crushing upon the strong enemy, so as to bring misery upon the fortress which raises itself up against the knowledge of God. Of which we also read in Proverbs: The wise man entered strong cities and destroyed the fortress in which the ungodly trusted. This he does indeed against all worldly strength, but properly against the heretics, who try to strengthen the falsity of their dogmas with arguments and sophisms and the dialectical art. But the wise man destroys it, and, helped by the aid of God, shows every fortress to be most vain, so as to bring misery upon it, and, when its pride has been bowed down, he can say with the Apostle: Wretched man that I am, who will deliver me from the body of this death?\n— Commentary on Amos"],"PRO.22.20-21":["We read in Proverbs: But you, describe them threefold, that you may answer words of truth which are proposed to you, and we are commanded to understand the utterances of truth, that is, the holy Scriptures, threefold: first, according to the letter; second, in a middle way, through tropology; third, more loftily, so that we may know all mystical things. According to the letter is this: Neither let us commit fornication, as some of them committed fornication, and twenty-three thousand fell in one day, and: Do not murmur, as some of them murmured, and perished by the destroyer; in the middle way and according to tropology, when we withdraw from the letter and climb a little to higher things, as the Apostle says: It is written: You shall not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treads out the grain, and immediately there follows: Is it for oxen that God cares? Or did he speak surely for our sake?; but the last, that is, the third, sublime and sacred understanding, according to that word of the same Apostle: For this reason a man shall leave father and mother and shall cleave to his wife. This mystery is great; but I speak in Christ and in the Church.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PRO.23.1":["Divine Scripture commands that, when we have been invited to a rich man's banquet, we should with understanding put forth our hand to the dishes. And so for us too the banquet of Scriptures has been set out as a rich man's feast. We have come into a meadow; it has very many flowers: here the rose is red, there the lilies gleam white, there are flowers of every kind. Our soul is drawn this way and that, to gather from wherever it may the more beautiful flowers. If we pick the rose, we leave the lily behind; if we take the lily, the violets are still left for us.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 11 (PSALM 77)"],"PRO.26.4-5":["We, however, say this: that, just as in Proverbs it is written in the same place: Do not answer a fool according to his folly, lest you become like him, and again there is set down what seems to us to be contrary: Answer a fool according to his folly, lest he seem wise to himself, and both agree according to the difference of times and persons, since the fool is both despised because he does not receive wisdom, and foolish pride is knocked down by another folly, according to what the Apostle also says: I have become foolish; you compelled me, so in this place too we ought to ask why above it was said twice: The living creatures did not turn back when they advanced, and now once: The living creatures ran and returned; and there would be a problem were it not followed by: In the likeness of flashing lightning, which in the Hebrew tongue is called 'bezec', and which Symmachus translated: As the appearance of a ray of lightning.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"PRO.27.16":["The north, of which Jeremiah wrote: From the north evils will blaze up upon all the inhabitants of the earth. And of which Solomon makes mention: The north wind is a harsh wind, but by name it is called the right. And God stretches out his hand, the hand that inflicts punishments, for this reason: that both the north may feel the penalties, and those who are in its land, to which, according to Zechariah, the black horses go, of which it is said: There were black horses, they were going into the land of the north. And fittingly, whoever has moved his feet and withdrawn from the East, of which the same prophet says: Behold a man, the East is his name, and has looked toward the west, goes at once toward the north, which is not the right, but by name is called the right. This indeed is understood bodily as well: whoever stands in the east and turns himself, looking toward the west, has the north on his right; those who have grown cold from its blast call it the right, though it is the right only in name, but in reality and in deed is rather on the left.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"PRO.30.8-9":["Wherefore Solomon prays to God: Give me neither riches nor poverty. But appoint for me what is necessary and sufficient for my living, lest, being full, I become a liar and say: Who sees me? or, being in want, I steal and forswear the name of the Lord (Prov 30:8, 9). And if he prays for this, to have neither riches nor poverty but only the necessaries of living, of which the Apostle also says: Having food and clothing, with these we are content (1 Tim 6), it is clear that riches and want, health and sickness, pleasure and torments, are neither good nor evil, but become good and evil according to the difference of those who bear them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PRO.30.17":["Something of this kind I think Solomon too says in the Proverbs: The eye that mocks its father and despises the old age of its mother, let the ravens of the valleys dig it out, and the young eagles eat it (Prov 30:17). For as soon as the mind of the heretics has mocked the Creator, the Father, and despised the old age of the Church, its mother, it is dug out by foul and unclean birds, which are referred to the opposing powers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"PRO.31.4":["We read in another place: \"And wine gladdens the heart of man.\" We read also in the Song of Songs: \"Come, brothers, drink and be inebriated.\" And wine which gladdens the heart of man. This fleshly wine does not gladden the heart of man, but overwhelms it, and makes it mad. Indeed, it is written that those who are powerful should not drink wine. The apostle too writes that we ought not to drink wine and eat flesh. Again it is said to us: And wine gladdens the heart of man. But it speaks of a spiritual wine, which, if anyone drinks it, he is immediately inebriated. All of you, that you might let go of your possessions and your parents and your children and your kinfolk, drank wine, and being inebriated for Christ began to be mad.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 42 (PSALM 127)"],"ECC.1.6-7":["All things heavenly and earthly, and whatever falls under human understanding, roll on their own wheels; the moon runs through the yearly circuit of the sun in each month; the Morning Star, which is itself the Evening Star, while it gleams ruddy in the east and in the west and tempers the darkness of night with a small light, completes the same course in two years; and the four other stars which are called wandering, and whatever shines in the sky, and the varieties too of crops and trees and herbs, run on their own wheels through the four seasons; and we see nothing that was not before: Circling in circles the spirit goes, and returns into its circuits. All torrents go into the sea, and the sea is not filled. Why? Because they return to their springs from the womb of the abyss.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ECC.3.2":["But what is the time in which the barren woman shall bring forth? As I suppose, that of which Isaiah speaks: In an acceptable time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have helped you. Paul too, understanding this as foretold of the time of Christ, says: Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation. I think that this very thing is also shown mystically by what is written in Ecclesiastes: A time to bring forth, and a time to die; because at the time when the people of the Gentiles was born of the barren woman, the synagogue lost her children.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"ECC.3.7":["What we read in Ecclesiastes: A time to keep silent and a time to speak, is borne out in the prophetic word as well: that it belongs to the same wisdom both to keep silent and to speak according to the time, and to give fellow servants their rations in due season; whence Isaiah too says to the unbelieving people: 'I have kept silent; shall I keep silent forever? says the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ECC.7.10":["Those to whom he had said above: House of Israel, pass over, all of you, and see, from what sort of vices they are bidden to pass over to better things, the following words describe: Who come to the evil day, preparing for themselves by their own vice an evil day; not that any day is evil, but that each one prepares evil for himself, according to what we read in Ecclesiastes: Do not say, The former days were better for me than these, for you do not ask about this in wisdom; for it is foolish to lay it on the times, since it is in our power to make for ourselves either a good day or an evil one.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ECC.10.16-17":["Of these we read in Ecclesiastes too: Woe to you, O city, whose king is a youth, and your princes eat in the morning. Blessed is the land whose king is a son of the freeborn (Eccl 10:16). Such a young king was Rehoboam the son of Solomon, who followed the counsels of the young men (1 Kgs 12): not that he was young in age, but in wisdom. Otherwise, he is related to have received the kingdom at forty years and more. And on the contrary, Solomon was twelve years old when he took up the rule, and because he had wisdom, he was therefore not called a youth. For there was in him breadth of heart and amplitude of wisdom as great as the sand on the shores of the sea. Hence the Apostle too writes to Timothy: Let no one despise your youth (1 Tim 4:12). For he who is young in age is an old man in maturity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ECC.11.1":["Or certainly this is to be said, that the word of the Gospel's teaching is called rain, and the showers which the spiritual clouds pour upon the good earth, the clouds to which the truth of God reaches. This rain and these showers Moses promises in the Song of Deuteronomy (Deut 32:1, 2): Let the earth hear the words of my mouth: let my speech be awaited as the rain, and let my words come down as the dew; so that those who have sown in tears may reap in joy (Ps 125 [126]). And let those who have sown in righteousness and in the spirit reap the fruit of life everlasting, and receive the bread of the Gospel's teaching, of which it is written in Proverbs and in Ecclesiastes: Open your eyes, and be filled with bread (Prov 20:13); and again: Cast your bread upon the water, for in the multitude of your days you will find it (Eccl 11:1). For it is not to be believed that those who eat are commanded that, to eat this bread by which bodies are nourished, they must open their eyes and so be filled with the bread which in Habakkuk the poor man eats in secret (Hab 3). Rather, he exhorts us to the bread of the teaching of God, which we cannot eat unless we have opened the eyes of our heart. Of this Paul too often writes to those who are nourished by the words of faith and truth (2 Cor 9). And the teacher is commanded to cast the bread of his teaching upon every water, and to pour spiritual grace upon all, and to know that if he does what has been commanded, he will receive rewards in the last time. And it would seem unjust enough that, while he who gives alms makes for himself friends of the mammon of iniquity, who may receive him into the eternal tabernacles (Luke 16), he who bestows spiritual foods, and gives his fellow servants their rations in due season, should not find them after many ages, which Ecclesiastes calls the multitude of days (Eccl 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ECC.11.2":["The seven shepherds I take to be all the patriarchs and prophets and holy men who served the hebdomad, that is, the old instrument. But the eight bites of men, or eight principal men, and, as Symmachus rendered it, christs, are all those of the New Testament who, from the apostles down to this age, have bitten the Assyrian and torn him with their teeth. Hence also in Ecclesiastes the command is given: That we should give portions to seven, let us give also to eight. And in the temple of Ezekiel, which is interpreted as the Church and the heavenly Jerusalem, one goes up by seven and by eight steps. And in the psalter there are fifteen steps, by which, through the hebdomad and the ogdoad, we climb to sing praises to God. On the eighth day the spiritual circumcision is celebrated, and in the temple of the Jews the sabbath is destroyed, and some psalms are inscribed for the eighth.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"SNG.1.2":["Mercy and truth have met each other: justice and peace have kissed. I see two things, which by coming together and meeting become one. Justice and peace have kissed. All this is fitted to the mystery of the Lord and Savior: this is the Son of God and of man, who is for us truth, mercy, peace, and justice, in whom the justice of the former people and the mercy of the latter are joined together into one peace. \"For he himself,\" says the Apostle, \"is our peace, who has made both one.\" This is the mystery which the Church desires, and cries out in the Song of Songs, saying, \"Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth.\" This is the kiss of which the Apostle Paul says, \"Greet one another with the kiss of peace.\"\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 64 (PSALM 84)","Or, according to the Seventy, he did not wish to save his people through angels and prophets and other holy men, but he himself came down to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, to carry back the sick sheep on his shoulders, and to find the drachma that had been lost, and to run joyfully to meet the prodigal son as he returned. For this reason the bride says in the Song of Songs: Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: not through the Patriarchs, Moses and the Prophets, but let him assume my body, let him dwell in my flesh, let the Word become flesh, and so let him kiss me, dwelling in me, that he may be Emmanuel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"SNG.1.3":["Like ointment upon the head, which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron. Oh, if there were time for us to discuss each detail, a day could not suffice us. We read in Exodus how the priestly ointment is prepared; then we read how there was another ointment, with which the kings were anointed. There was also another ointment with which the prophets were anointed. And why do I say more? All the ointments had their own distinctions and mysteries. Do we marvel about holy persons? A leper, after he has been cleansed, after he has been sprinkled with hyssop and with the blood of a bird, and one bird is released into the wilderness, he too has his own proper ointment. Nothing is sanctified except through ointment. Therefore the young maidens also say in the Song of Songs: \"Thy name is ointment poured out. After thee we will run in the fragrance of thy ointments.\"\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 45 (Psalm 132)"],"SNG.1.5-6":["And when they have fallen down, and have brought victims to the Lord from there, from beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, they will be able to say: I am black and beautiful, because the sun has discolored me. For bodies that languish in shade and idleness cannot bear the temptations and the heats of the world; but those which are made ready for wrestling and for contests, dried by the sun, overcome the injuries of the world and obtain the blessing of the Holy Spirit, who says to the righteous man: The sun shall not burn you by day, nor the moon by night.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"SNG.1.7":["Because Abraham received the angels at noon, and Joseph invited his brothers to a meal at noon. For both Abraham and Joseph were holy, and therefore their banquet was in clear light and full sun, therefore they feed at noon. And I too, if I have full knowledge of the Scriptures, although it be the third hour, nevertheless I feed you at noon. Indeed even the bride in the Song of Songs says: \"Where do you feed, she says, where do you lie down at noon?\" You see that the bridegroom does not feed, does not recline at the third hour, but at noon. Where do you feed, where do you lie down, where do I find you, where do you take your delight, where can I find you, O my bridegroom? Do you wish to find me? At noon, in perfect knowledge, in good works, in clear light. Since therefore we have our noon, therefore the devil too transforms himself into an angel of light, and he himself pretends that he has light, that he has noon. When the heretics promise, as it were, certain mysteries, when they promise the kingdom of heaven, when continence, when fasts, when holiness, when renunciation of the world, they promise noon. But since it is not the light of Christ, it is not noon, but the noonday demon.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 20 (Psalm 90)"],"SNG.1.9":["Let us set out examples of horses. The Bridegroom speaks in the Song of Songs: To my cavalry among the chariots of Pharaoh he has likened you, my near one. Not that Christ compares the Church, or the Word of God the soul which he calls his bride, to the chariots of Pharaoh, but that every soul, however holy and perfect it may be, compared with God is as it were a chariot of Pharaoh, and a beast of burden. Hence Moses too says to the Lord: But I am ἄλογος, that is, irrational. And David: I have become like a beast of burden before you. Not that he is a beast of burden absolutely, but that he is a beast of burden before God.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"SNG.1.15":["So that it may be that which is said in the Song of Songs: \"You are wholly beautiful, my love, and there is no spot in you.\" And again: \"Behold, you are fair, my near one, behold, you are fair, your eyes are doves.\" You who are fair and strong, when you have become like him of whom it is sung, \"In your beauty and your comeliness,\" you will hear from the Bridegroom: \"Forget your people and your father's house, and the King will desire your beauty.\"\n— Homily 61, on Psalm 15 (Alternate Series)"],"SNG.2.1":["The earth has given its fruit. First it gave a flower. He says in the Song of Songs: \"I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys.\" This flower, then, has been made fruit, that we might eat him, that we might feed on his flesh. Do you wish to know what this fruit is? A virgin from a virgin, the Lord from a handmaid, God from man, a son from a mother, fruit from the earth. See what this fruit himself says. \"Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it cannot bear much fruit.\" The earth has given its fruit, it has given the grain of wheat: which grain of wheat fell into the earth and died, and therefore brings forth much fruit.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 6 (PSALM 66)"],"SNG.2.4":["He who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me, and the rest. Because he had said before: I have not come to send peace but a sword, and to divide men against father and mother and mother-in-law, lest anyone should put filial devotion before religion, he went on, saying: He who loves father or mother more than me; and in the Song of Songs we read: Set charity in order in me. This order is necessary in every affection. After God love your father, love your mother, love your children; but if a necessity comes such that the love of parents and children is weighed against the love of God, and both cannot be kept, hatred of one's own is devotion toward God. So he did not forbid loving father or mother, but pointedly added: He who loves father or mother more than me.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"SNG.2.8":["We therefore, first let us flee the valleys of Zion, let us come to the plains, from the plains let us come to the hills, from the hills let us come to the mountains, let us follow Christ upon the mountains. For our cousin, like a roe and a fawn of the harts, leaps over the hills, and springs across the mountains. Whence also, according to the truth of the ascension, he did not ascend into the heavens after the resurrection from a valley, but from a mountain. Unless we are mountains in virtues, we cannot ascend to heaven. For there, that is, in the mountains of Zion, in the virtues, in the confession of faith, in the truth of the doctrines, there is the blessing and life for evermore.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 45 (PSALM 132)"],"SNG.2.15":["Moreover, according to the Seventy the sense here is this: all the heretics, because of their deceitfulness and wickedness, are like foxes, of whom the Savior speaks: Foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky nests; and of Herod it is written: Tell this fox; and of these same foxes, which deceive the little and simple ones and lay waste the vineyard of Christ, Solomon speaks: Catch us the little foxes that destroy the vineyards; and in the psalm it is sung concerning those who are tripped up by their deceitfulness: They will go into the lower parts of the earth, they will be handed over into the hands of the sword, they will be the portions of foxes.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"SNG.3.4":["But otherwise the footstool of the feet of Jesus is the soul of the believer. Happy is he in whose breast the feet of Jesus are daily placed. Would that his feet were placed in my breast too, would that his footprints might ever cling in my heart, would that I too might say with the bride: \"I will hold him, and I will not let him go.\" The bridegroom is quickly offended, he ever loves cleanness: wherever he sees filth, he quickly withdraws.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 26 (PSALM 98)"],"SNG.4.16":["Of these two winds the Church speaks: Arise, O north wind, and come, O south wind, so that, as the north wind, a most cold wind, which is interpreted as the devil, withdraws, the south wind, a warm wind, may come; seeking him, the bride says: Where do you pasture, where do you lie down, at noon?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"SNG.5.1":["Indeed, it is written in another place: \"Come my sister, my bride.\" Lest in the fact that it had said, bride, you should understand something shameful, sister is joined, so that all shameful love may be excluded. Come my sister: for the love is holy, and therefore I call you sister. And my bride: therefore I call you bride, so that I may have a wife, and from you make many sons, many sons like clusters from a vine. Just as a vine has various clusters, and there is indeed one root, but it spreads its tendrils far and wide, here and there, and the tendrils themselves have their own fruits, and in the fruits themselves are the clusters; so also from wisdom, which is our wife, provided that we never let her go, many clusters are born, that is, very many virtues, holy thoughts, splendid works.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 42 (PSALM 127)","We read also in the Song of Songs: \"Come, brothers, drink and be inebriated.\" And wine which gladdens the heart of man. This fleshly wine does not gladden the heart of man, but overwhelms it, and makes it mad. Indeed, it is written that those who are powerful should not drink wine. The apostle too writes that we ought not to drink wine and eat flesh. Again it is said to us: And wine gladdens the heart of man. But it speaks of a spiritual wine, which, if anyone drinks it, he is immediately inebriated. All of you, that you might let go of your possessions and your parents and your children and your kinfolk, drank wine, and being inebriated for Christ began to be mad. Does it not seem to you to be drunkenness, does it not seem to you to be spiritual madness, to despise delights, to seek out squalor, to flee the cities, to pursue the deserts, to know no affections, to ignore the nearness of the flesh, and to know Christ alone?\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 42 (PSALM 127)"],"SNG.5.10":["Of him the bride too says in the Song of Songs: My kinsman is white and ruddy, chosen out of thousands (Song 5:10). White in the fullness and purity of his virtues, ruddy in his passion, of which we shall read later: Who is this who comes up from Edom, tawny are his garments from Bozrah; chosen out of thousands in the resurrection, so that he who was the firstborn of all creation might become the firstborn from the dead.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"SNG.5.13":["In the Song of Songs the bride praises her bridegroom: His cheeks are like bowls of spices. By the cheeks is understood speech, which, uttered by the Lord, brings forth various ointments; and so great will be the fragrance of its good odor that the Lord makes himself a scourge woven from the testimonies of the scriptures, and casts out of the temple those who sell and buy, and says to them: It is written, My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations; but you have made it a house of trade.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"SNG.6.2":["Let us turn the Jewish cauldrons, in which the flesh of the victims was cooked, into bowls of spices before the altar of the Lord, of which the bride says to the bridegroom: My kinsman has gone down into my garden, to the bowls of spices, to feed in the gardens and to gather lilies. The garden and paradise into which the bridegroom goes down to the bride is the reading of the holy scriptures, from which he plucks lilies and violets and roses and various spices, to fill the bowls of believing souls and to pour out libations to the Lord from them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"SNG.6.11":["Let us say also that according to the LXX it is called ῥοῶν, not a single tree of pomegranates, that is, of the Punic apple, but a place planted with these trees, of which, according to the spiritual understanding, the bridegroom says in the Song of Songs: I went down to see in the produce of the torrent whether the vine had flowered, whether the pomegranates had flowered. For the Savior went down to the torrent of this world and its turbid waters, from which Elijah too, in a type of him, is described as having drunk, so that after the flowers of the vine and of the pomegranate he might receive the fruit of both, and, making his Church drunk, might hear from her: You shall give me to drink of the perfumer's wine, of the wine of my pomegranates. A drink of this kind not only drives away the heats of the stomach, but is also said to heal a corrupted belly and to benefit the other inner organs. For nothing is more beautiful than this fruit; in its redness it signifies the modesty of the Church, in the order of its seeds the ranks and members of the whole body distributed through their several offices.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"SNG.8.2":["Let us say also that according to the LXX it is called ῥοῶν, not a single tree of pomegranates, that is, of the Punic apple, but a place planted with these trees, of which, according to the spiritual understanding, the bridegroom says in the Song of Songs: I went down to see in the produce of the torrent whether the vine had flowered, whether the pomegranates had flowered. For the Savior went down to the torrent of this world and its turbid waters, from which Elijah too, in a type of him, is described as having drunk, so that after the flowers of the vine and of the pomegranate he might receive the fruit of both, and, making his Church drunk, might hear from her: You shall give me to drink of the perfumer's wine, of the wine of my pomegranates. A drink of this kind not only drives away the heats of the stomach, but is also said to heal a corrupted belly and to benefit the other inner organs. For nothing is more beautiful than this fruit; in its redness it signifies the modesty of the Church, in the order of its seeds the ranks and members of the whole body distributed through their several offices.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"WIS.3.13":["That this can also be understood of the teachers of contrary dogmas is evident, who glory in the multitude of their peoples, and in those children whom they have reared for destruction, in order to lead them out of the Church and lead them in to the murderer. For the devil slaughters as many as the sons the heretics have begotten in error. Of a soul of this kind it is said: Blessed is the barren woman who is undefiled, who has not known the bed in sin. For blessed is the churchman who, in comparison with the heretic, has not begotten children in error.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"SIR.27.25":["But since our hope is certain and the vows of the Jews are empty, let us now weave the present and last chapter according to the former understanding, and bring forward a testimony from Jesus son of Sirach: He who throws a stone on high throws it upon his own head. For since Zion and Jerusalem are set on high, whoever disparages Zion and Jerusalem and throws at her the stones of insults hurls them upon his own head, and his reproach will return upon his own head: And his pain and his iniquity will come down upon his crown.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"HOS.1.1":["If in the explanations of all the prophets we need the coming of the Holy Spirit, so that they may be opened by the revelation of him by whose prompting they were written; and if in Isaiah and in the Apocalypse we read of a sealed book, which the scribes and Pharisees, who boast that they know the letters of the law, cannot read, because it is sealed, and no one could be found who could unlock its mysteries except the lion of the tribe of Judah, whom God the Father has sealed: how much more, in the explanation of the prophet Hosea, must the Lord be prayed to, and must we say with Peter: Explain this parable to us. Especially since he who wrote it himself testifies at the end to the obscurity of the volume: Who is wise, and he will understand these things, understanding, and he will know them? This we ought to take not as something impossible but as something difficult, according to that saying of the Gospel: Who, do you think, is the faithful and prudent servant whom the Lord has set over his household? For who would not at once, at the very front of the book, be scandalized and say: Hosea, first of all the prophets, is commanded to take a harlot as his wife, and he does not object? He does not even pretend to be unwilling, so as to seem to do a shameful thing against his will; but he carries out the command gladly, as though he had longed for it, as though he regretted having given himself to chastity for a long time; whereas we read that holy men, at the Lord's command, refused things more honorable and without sin. Moses is sent to Pharaoh and appointed leader of the Israelite people; and yet, not from weakness but from humility, he answered: Provide another whom you may send. Jeremiah says that he is a boy, so as not to rebuke Jerusalem in her sin. Ezekiel is commanded to make one loaf baked under the ashes from every kind of pulse and seed, and to cook it in human dung; and he says: By no means, Lord, for nothing unclean has ever entered my mouth. And Hosea, hearing from the Lord: Take a fornicating wife, does not knit his brow, does not testify to his grief by pallor, does not show his shame by a changed blush on his cheeks; but he goes to the brothel and leads the harlot to his bed. And he does not initiate her into the chastity of a matron; rather he proves himself a voluptuary and a profligate. For he who cleaves to a harlot becomes one body with her. Hearing these things, then, what can we answer except that prophetic word: Who is wise, and he will understand these things, understanding, and he will know them? Hence we too must say with David: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law, so that the king may bring us into his chamber, and take away from us the veil which was placed before the eyes of Moses in the reading of the Old Testament. For as soon as the Lord had said: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit; and saying this he breathed his last, the veil of the temple was torn, and all the secrets of the Jews lay open. And the fountain that went forth from the house of David entered the sea of the wilderness; and the true Elisha seasoned the barren and deadly waters with the salt of his wisdom and made them life-giving. And the Marah of the law, which is interpreted bitterness, was sweetened by the wood of the gibbet. For we understand who Judah the patriarch is, chosen to be king, and we do not wonder why a holy man goes in to Tamar as to a harlot; why Samson, which is interpreted sun, loved Delilah, which is translated poor, and, mocked on her account and dying, slaughtered thousands of his adversaries; why Salmon begot the just man Boaz from Rahab the harlot, Boaz who, covering Ruth the Moabitess with the wing of his cloak, carried her over from lying at his feet to the head of the Gospel. What reason there is that, though David had so many wives, he made no other the successor of his kingdom except the one who was born of Bathsheba; so that not only harlots but even adulteresses seem to please God. Hence in this very prophet too we read that, joined first to a harlot, he is coupled secondly to an adulteress, the Lord saying to him: Go again, love a woman beloved of a friend, or loving evil things, and an adulteress. This is the woman, harlot and adulteress, who in the Gospel washed the Lord's feet with her tears, wiped them with her hair, and honored them with the ointment of her confession; and when the disciples were indignant, and above all the betrayer, that it had not been sold and its price distributed for the food of the poor, the Lord answered: Why do you trouble the woman? She has done a good work on me; the poor you will always have with you; but me you will not always have. And lest we should think what she had done a light thing, and that the pistic nard, that is, the most faithful ointment, was to be referred to something else and not to the Church, he gives us an occasion for understanding; and he promises great rewards to great faith, saying: Amen, amen I say to you, wherever this Gospel is preached in the whole world, what she too has done will be told in memory of her. This is the harlot of whom the Lord speaks to the Jews: Amen I say to you, harlots and publicans will go before you into the kingdom of God. For you were unwilling to receive the Son of the householder, the Lord of the vineyard, who planted it, when he was sent to you. This harlot received with kindness my two spies, most valiant young men, of whom I sent one to the circumcision and the other to the Gentiles; she hid them with zeal, lifted them up onto the roof, and covered them with stalks of flax. That flax, cut down by persecutions and washed in baptism, changes its color, and from dark is turned to white. Nor is it surprising if we recall that these things were done as a figure of the Lord Savior and of the Church gathered from sinners, since he himself says in this same prophet: I will speak to the prophets; I have multiplied visions, and in the hands of the prophets I have been likened; so that whatever the prophets are commanded to do is to be referred to my likeness. His wife is an Ethiopian, against whom Aaron and Mary, the carnal priesthood of the Jews and the prophecy that serves the letter, murmur and offend God; she it is who says in the Song of Songs: I am dark and beautiful, daughters of Jerusalem. Otherwise, if we contend that all the things which are commanded for a reason, to be done as a likeness, really happened, then Jeremiah too, girded with a loincloth, a woman's garment, went among innumerable nations, the Assyrians and the Chaldeans, the peoples most hostile to the Jews, to the Euphrates, and there hid his loincloth, and, returning after a long time, found that it had rotted so as to be of no use. How could he go out and travel so far when Jerusalem was besieged, with siege works built all around it, ditch, rampart and forts? He who, when he once wished to go out to Anathoth, his little village situated at the third milestone from the city, was both seized at the gate and led back to the princes, and, beaten as a traitor, was thrown into prison. If that was in figure, because it could not happen, then this too is in figure, because if it happened, it would be most shameful. But you will answer: When God commands, nothing is shameful; and we will say: God commands nothing except what is honorable, nor by commanding shameful things does he make honorable what is shameful. But because we know that God wills nothing except what is honorable, he commanded this, which is honorable. I have spoken of these things at greater length at the beginning, so as first to solve the most difficult question, and, passing with God's help the rock set in the very mouth of the passage, to run down into safe waters. For the rest, I am not unaware, dearest Pammachius, that it has been passed over by many churchmen; and of those who attempted to expound it, among the Greeks I found Apollinaris of Laodicea, who, though in his youth he had left brief little commentaries both on this prophet and on others, touching the senses rather than unfolding them, was asked afterwards to write more fully on Hosea; that book has come into our hands, but he too, through excessive brevity, cannot lead the reader to a perfect understanding. Origen wrote a small book on this prophet, to which he gave this title: Περὶ τοῦ πῶς ὠνομάσθη ἐν τῷ Ὠσηὲ Ἐφραΐμ; that is: Why Ephraim is so called in Hosea, wishing to show that whatever is said against him is to be referred to the person of the heretics. And another volume, ἀκέφαλον καὶ ἀτέλεστον, which lacks both head and end. I have also read a very long treatise of Pierius, which he poured out on the opening of this prophet on the day of the vigil of the Lord's passion, in an extempore and eloquent discourse. And Eusebius of Caesarea, in the eighteenth book of the Εὐαγγελικῆς ἀποδείξεως (the Demonstration of the Gospel), discusses certain matters concerning the prophet Hosea. Hence about twenty-two years ago, when, at the request of your holy and venerable mother-in-law, or rather your mother, Paula (for the one is the name of the flesh, the other of the spirit; she always burned with love for monasteries and for the Scriptures), I was at Alexandria, I saw Didymus, the most learned man of his time, and heard him often; and I asked him to complete what Origen had not done, and to write commentaries on Hosea; and he dictated three books to me, at my request, and five others also on Zechariah. For on that prophet too Origen wrote only two volumes, expounding barely a third part, from the beginning of the book up to the vision of the four-horse chariots. I say these things so that you may know whom I have had as forerunners in the field of this prophet; whom, however, to confess it to your prudence simply and not proudly — as a certain one of my friends is always hissing — I have not followed in everything; so that I might be a judge of their work rather than its translator, and might say what seemed right to me on each point, and what I received from the teachers of the Hebrews, barely one or two of them. Such a one is now a rare bird even among themselves, since all devote themselves to pleasures and money, and take more care of the belly than of the breast; and they think themselves learned in this, if in the shops of physicians they disparage the works of everyone. But now it is time that, setting down the words of the prophet, we discuss what is written.\n— Commentary on Hosea","The word of the Lord which came to Hosea son of Beeri. LXX: likewise. The Word of the Lord (which in the beginning was with God the Father, and the Word was God) came to Hosea son of Beeri, so as to make the prophet too a god, as the Savior says: If he called them gods to whom the word of God came, and the scripture cannot be broken, him whom the Father sanctified and sent into the world you tell: You blaspheme; because I said, I am the Son of God. So that, just as God makes gods, and stands in the synagogue of gods, and in the midst he judges between gods; and, though he himself is the true light, which enlightens every man coming into this world, says to the apostles: You are the light of the world, so too the Savior himself may make his prophet a savior. For Hosea, in our language, means savior, a name which Joshua son of Nun also had before his name was changed by God. For he was not called — as is wrongly read in the Greek and Latin manuscripts — Ause, which means nothing at all; but Hoshea, that is, savior; and Lord was added to his name, so that he might be called savior of the Lord. This savior, then, is the son of Beeri, that is, of my well; such wells Abraham, Isaac and Jacob dug, and the foreigners were always trying to fill them in. Between a well and a pit, that is, a cistern, there is this difference: a well has perpetual waters, flowing from a living spring; a cistern, which keeps water cool, holds waters that are external and brought in from outside. Hence God speaks through the prophet Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and dug for themselves cisterns which cannot hold water. Of this fountain the psalmist cries out to God: With you is the fountain of life; in your light we shall see light. Hence some think that Beeri is interpreted my light; but the former is the true translation.\n\nIn the days of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, Hezekiah, kings of Judah; and in the days of Jeroboam son of Joash, king of Israel. LXX: likewise. Azariah, who is also called Uzziah, of the stock of David, reigned in Jerusalem over the two tribes, which were called Judah, for fifty-two years; his son Jotham succeeded him in the kingdom, and he too reigned for sixteen years; after him his son Ahaz reigned, likewise for sixteen years. After Ahaz his son Hezekiah reigned for twenty-nine years, in whose sixth year the ten tribes, which were called Israel, were captured by Shalmaneser king of the Chaldeans and settled in the mountains of the Medes. From this it is evident that, while Hosea, Isaiah and Joel, and Amos and Obadiah, and Jonah and Micah were prophesying, who were his σύγχρονοι (contemporaries), the kingdom of the ten tribes came to an end, which from the first king, Jeroboam, to the last, Hoshea, lasted two hundred and fifty years. At the time when Uzziah began to reign over Judah, Jeroboam, the great-grandson of Jehu, was reigning over Israel, Jehu to whom the Lord had promised that his offspring would reign to the fourth generation, because he had struck down the two impious kings of Judah and of Israel. We say this to show you briefly that the prophet Hosea prophesied both before the captivity of Israel and after its captivity, and that, treating it as near as well as present and past, he both announced it as coming, and mourned it as it fell upon them, and, for the correction of Judah, went back over it once past; and this we shall try to prove in the prophet himself according to the history. Uzziah is interpreted strength of the Lord; Jotham, consummation and perfection of the Lord; Ahaz, power; Hezekiah, command of the Lord. These reigned among the people of Judah, whose name is confession. Moreover, in Israel, which had made idols for itself and had been separated from the people of God, reigned Jeroboam son of Joash, which is interpreted χρονισμός, that is, temporality, or delay; because he loved the world and thought that to linger long in it was eternal life, delighting not at all in things to come but in things present.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.2":["It is asked why Ezekiel refused easier things, while Hosea was at once joined to a harlot and did not object, or rather reply, that he had a chaste body and ought not to be stained by union with a prostitute, as the Apostle says: He who cleaves to a prostitute becomes one body with her; from this it is shown that it was a figure of the Synagogue or of the Church, not truly carried out according to the letter, as we discussed more fully when expounding that prophet himself.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","The beginning of the Lord's speaking in Hosea. LXX: The beginning of the word of the Lord to Hosea. Because, as we said above, Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, in whose reigns they prophesied, are set at the head of the title of other prophets too, he therefore now says that among all these the Lord spoke first in Hosea, and afterwards to the rest. But it is one thing for the Lord to speak in Hosea, another to speak to Hosea. In Hosea, he does not speak to Hosea himself, but through Hosea to others; but when he speaks to Hosea, he is signified to address his discourse to Hosea himself. Others, however, do not want Hosea to have been the first of all the prophets on the ground of what is said: The beginning of the Lord's speaking in Hosea; but hold that it shows that the Lord spoke these things which follow first to Hosea.\n\nAnd the Lord said to Hosea: Go, take for yourself a wife of fornications and children of fornications, for the land, fornicating, will commit fornication away from the Lord. LXX: And the Lord said to Hosea: Go, take for yourself a wife of fornication and children of fornication, for the land, fornicating, will commit fornication behind the Lord. The Hebrew word zanunim means not a fornicating woman and fornication, as most suppose, but many fornications. From this it is shown that this woman, whom the prophet takes in marriage, had committed fornication not once but quite often, so that the more sordid she is, the more patient is the prophet who married such a wife. And as for what is added: Take for yourself also children of fornications; for it is understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (from what is common, i.e. understood with both parts): Take for yourself a wife of fornications, and take for yourself children of fornications. Both can be understood: that he both receives the children whom the fornicating woman had earlier conceived from fornication, and himself begets offspring from the harlot, who are therefore to be called children of fornication, because they were begotten of a harlot. Nor is the prophet to be blamed, to follow the history for the moment, if he converted a harlot to chastity, but rather praised, because he made a good woman out of a bad one. For he who remains good is not himself polluted if he is joined to one who is bad; but he who is bad is turned to good if he follows the examples of the good. From this we understand that the prophet did not lose his chastity by being joined to a fornicating woman, but that the fornicating woman took on the chastity which she did not have before; especially since blessed Hosea did not act for the sake of luxury, nor of lust, nor of his own will, but obeyed the command of God, so that what we read done carnally in him we might show to have been done spiritually in God, who took to himself the Synagogue, that is, the people of the Jews, enslaved to fornication and lust. To her the Lord speaks through Ezekiel: And you, harlot, hear the word of the Lord. For her breasts were broken in Egypt, and she was wallowing in blood, and had been polluted up to the crown of her head, so that there was no part of her body and limbs which did not bear the stain of shame. Such a woman the Lord covered with his cloak and joined to his embraces; he gave her honey and oil and fine flour to eat, and clothed her in the most precious garments; he placed ornaments of gems on her neck; he adorned her ears with gold and most precious stone; he also furnished bracelets for her arms, so that she might practice good works. And nevertheless, despising this generosity and goodness of her husband, forgetful of her former shame, she followed her lovers, the Chaldeans and Assyrians and Egyptians, who are of great flesh. Of the type of the Savior and the Church we spoke in the little preface: that he took for himself a fornicating wife, who previously served idols. But if anyone contentious, and especially of the Gentiles, refuses to accept that it is said figuratively, and mocks the prophet for being joined to a fornicating woman, let us set against him what Greece is wont to praise and the schools of the philosophers sing in chorus. On what ground do they extol that most learned man Xenocrates, who made Polemon, a most dissolute youth, drunk and crowned with ivy among harp-girls and pipers and shameless women, obey wisdom, and changed a most shameful young man into the wisest of philosophers? Why do they raise Socrates to heaven, who transferred Phaedo, after whose name a book of Plato is called, from a brothel, where through the cruelty and avarice of his master he served the lust of many, into the Academy? And whatever they answer about these masters of philosophy, we apply to the defense of the prophet. Let this be said against the heathen and those who are like the heathen. But to our own, who nevertheless wish to accept the truth, let us briefly show this, from what is said: For the land, fornicating, will commit fornication away from the Lord: that it is not so much that the prophet was joined to a harlot as that the whole human race withdrew from fellowship with the Lord. Also, since \"all the earth\" is not added, it can be taken here as Judea, or properly Samaria and Israel, that is, the ten tribes, which, at the time when these things were being said, had withdrawn from the Lord.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.3-4":["And he went and took Gomer daughter of Diblaim; and she conceived and bore him a son. And the Lord said to him: Call his name Jezreel, for yet a little while and I will visit the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu; and I will cause the kingdom of the house of Israel to cease. LXX: And he went, and took Gomer daughter of Diblaim; and she conceived and bore a son. And the Lord said to him: Call his name Jezreel, because yet a little while and I will avenge the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Judah; and I will cause the kingdom of the house of Israel to cease. The prophets make their promises about the coming of Christ and the calling of the Gentiles many ages later in such a way as not to neglect the present time, lest they seem not to teach an assembly called together for another purpose about the things that are at hand, but to trifle about things uncertain and future. And so this Gomer too, daughter of Diblaim, who is taken as wife by Hosea, and conceives by him, and bears him a son named Jezreel, which is interpreted seed of God, in vengeance for whose blood he overturns the kingdom of Jehu (or, as an error has wrongly prevailed, of Judah), is to be referred to the calling of the nations in such a way as to fit that time in which she is recorded to have borne a son. And not to keep the reader's eagerness waiting with a long treatment: these two women, of whom one is called Gomer and is a harlot, and bears three children, first Jezreel, second, a girl, who is called: Without Mercy, and third a male, who is himself also called: Not My People; and the other woman, who is hired for fifteen pieces of silver, and a cor, and half a cor of barley, and is called an adulteress, are referred to Israel and to Judah, that is, to the ten tribes which were in Samaria under king Jeroboam, who was of Ephraim, and to Judah, which reigned in Jerusalem from the stock of David. These are the two women who in Zechariah are said to have the wings of a hoopoe, or of a kite, or of a heron, and to go into the land of Shinar, where Babylon was founded. These women Oholah and Oholibah signify under the name of two sisters; these are shown in the two rods which Ezekiel joins into one rod. And because we are writing commentaries, not broad volumes, reserving for each section its own explanation in its place, let us now discuss only the present section.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.3":["Gomer is interpreted τετελεσμένην, that is, consummated and perfected; others think that θώρακας, that is, breastplates, is signified. There are some who suppose it means measure, or bitterness, which they would say rightly if it did not have the letter gimel. Diblaim means παλάτας (cakes of pressed figs), of which there is a very great abundance in Palestine, and which the prophet Isaiah is commanded to apply to the ulcer of king Hezekiah. It is a mass of rich dried figs, which they shape after the manner of bricks, tread and press together, so that they may remain long unspoiled.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.4":["Therefore Israel too, consummated and perfected in fornication, a daughter of pleasure, which seems sweet and pleasant to those who enjoy it, is taken as wife by Hosea as a type of the Lord Savior; and from her a son of God is first begotten, that is, Jezreel; and this is the metropolis of the ten tribes, in which Naboth was killed, on account of whose blood Jehu is raised up, who destroyed the house of Ahab and Jezebel. But because Jehu himself too, the avenger of just blood, walked in the ways of Jeroboam son of Nebat, who made Israel commit fornication and set up golden calves in Dan and Bethel, his kingdom too is said to be overturned; under his great-grandson Jeroboam Hosea began to prophesy; when he died, his son Zechariah succeeded to the kingdom; and in the sixth month of his rule Shallum, born of another stock, killed him. For this reason it is now said: Yet a little while and I will visit the blood of Jezreel, that is, the slaughter of my people, upon the royal house of Jehu, which at that time ruled over Israel. Nor is it surprising if the house of Jehu is overturned, since even the kingdom of the house of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes, was to be utterly destroyed after not many years. For from Zechariah son of Jeroboam, whose ancestor was Jehu, to the ninth year of Hoshea, under whom the ten tribes were led into captivity, forty-nine years are counted. And once Zechariah, who was the last of the stock of Jehu, was killed, the kings of Assyria at once took Reuben and Gad and the half tribe of Manasseh, which were beyond the Jordan; and then many cities of Samaria, and then all Naphtali, and at the last all the remaining tribes. In place of Jehu, in the common edition Judah is read; but this seems to me to have grown up not through the fault of the Seventy translators but through the ignorance of the copyists, who, not knowing Jehu, wrote Judah, which was more familiar. The type of the seed of God, and the vengeance for his blood, is referred to the Lord's passion, on account of which both the house of Judah and the kingdom of all Israel are said to be overturned.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.5":["And on that day I will break the bow of Israel in the valley of Jezreel. LXX: likewise. When I have avenged the blood of Jezreel upon the house of Jehu, and have destroyed the kingdom of Israel through the victory of the Assyrian, then on that day and at that time I will break all the strength of the army of Israel in the valley of Jezreel. We said above that Jezreel, which is now near Maximianopolis, was the metropolis of the kingdom of Samaria; near it are very broad plains, and a valley of exceeding vastness, which stretches for more than ten thousand paces. In this valley, when battle was joined, Israel was cut down by the Assyrians, that is, the ten tribes, which, on account of Jeroboam of the tribe of Ephraim, who first made a schism among the people, were called Ephraim. Sometimes, on account of Joseph, who was the father of Ephraim, it is called Joseph; sometimes Samaria, which was itself the other metropolitan city of the ten tribes, and which was afterwards called Augusta by Augustus Caesar, that is, Σεβαστὴ; in it the bones of John the Baptist are laid to rest. After the division, then, of the two and the ten tribes, because of the very great part of the multitude which followed Jeroboam, the ancient name Israel remained with the ten tribes. And on account of the tribe of Judah, which reigned in Jerusalem, the other tribes were called Judah; and at the same time the truth of the type is also unfolded. For just as, on account of the blood of Naboth, which was shed in Jezreel, the house of Ahab was destroyed, so that the prophecy of Elijah might be fulfilled; so, on account of the blood of the true Jezreel, that is, of the seed of God, the kingdom of the Jews was destroyed. In all the prophets indeed, but especially in Hosea, the ten tribes are referred to the heretics, whose multitude is very great; but the two tribes, which are called Judah, hold the person of the Church, and reign under the stock of David. The bow of the heretics, therefore, of whom it is written: The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle, will be broken in the valley of the seed of God, about whom they held lowly and earthly opinions.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.6-7":["According to the type, we say that those who, on account of the blood of the seed of God, are called: Without Mercy, and dared to say: His blood be upon us, and upon our children, serve the Romans to this day; but that the house of Judah, namely those of the Jews who confessed the Lord, were saved not by the strength of an army but by the preaching of the Gospel. What we have interpreted of Israel and Judah, both according to the history and according to the type, let us refer to the conventicles of the heretics and to the Church of the Lord Savior: that, while they are left without mercy and lose their kingdom, the Church has prevailed by the power of her God.\n— Commentary on Hosea","For οὐκ ἠλεημένη, that is, without mercy, in some copies is found οὐκ ἠγαπημένη, that is, not beloved. But the truer copies are those which have without mercy; especially since, to distinguish it from Israel, on whom he has no mercy, God adds: But on the house of Judah I will have mercy.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.6":["And she conceived again and bore a daughter; and he said to him: Call her name: Without Mercy; for I will no more have mercy on the house of Israel, but with forgetting I will forget them. And on the house of Judah I will have mercy; and I will save them in the Lord their God; and I will not save them by bow and sword, and by war, and by horses, and by horsemen. LXX: And she conceived again and bore a daughter, and he said to him: Call her name: Without Mercy; for by no means will I any more have mercy on the house of Israel, but opposing I will oppose them; but on the sons of Judah I will have mercy, and I will save them in the Lord their God; and I will not save them by bow, nor by sword, nor by war, nor by horses, nor by horsemen. After the bow of Israel was crushed and broken in the valley of Jezreel, and the kingdom of the ten tribes was forsaken, so that they were led into captivity, there is no longer born a Jezreel, that is, the seed of God, nor a son of the male sex; but a daughter, that is, a woman, of the frail sex, and one exposed to the insults of the victors; and she is called: Without Mercy. For she was led away captive for this reason, because she did not have the mercy of God. And the indignation of the Lord must be considered, in that he says he will by no means have mercy any more on the house of Israel, but will blot them out of his memory with an eternal forgetting; for to this day they serve the kings of the Persians, and their captivity has never been released.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.7":["But to the house of Judah he promises mercy, and says that he saves them in the Lord their God, either in himself who speaks; or the Father saves in the Son, according to what is written: The Lord rained from the Lord. For when Israel was handed over to the Assyrians, he saved them from the hand of Sennacherib, not by bow and sword and war and by a multitude of horsemen, but by his own strength, when he sent an angel and struck down from the army of Sennacherib king of the Assyrians in one night one hundred and eighty-five thousand.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.8-9":["And she weaned her who was Without Mercy; and she conceived and bore a son; and he said: Call his name Not My People, for you are not my people and I will not be yours. LXX: likewise. He who was called the seed of God, turned into a female and, because of the weakness of his strength and the offense of God, led into captivity, since he did not have the mercy of the Lord, is said to have been weaned not as a son but as a daughter; for the man had already lost his vigor. Whoever is weaned withdraws from the mother, no longer feeds on the parent's milk, and is sustained by food from outside. So also Israel, cast off by the Lord, hemmed in by the straits of captivity, and in Babylon sustained by unclean foods, is called not the people of God, and is struck with the eternal sentence of a foreign nation, so that he is called Not My People and is disowned forever. This we can rightly understand both of the whole people of the Jews, which, handed over to captivity because of the offense against the seed of God, lost its kingdom and its province and is called not the people of God, and also in the person of the heretics. But if some contentious interpreter will not accept what we have said, but understands that the harlot named Gomer, daughter of Diblaim, bore the first and the third as males and the second, the middle one, as a female, wanting the Scripture to mean just what is read, let him answer how he explains that passage in Ezekiel where he is commanded by the Lord to bear the iniquities of the house of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes, and for three hundred and ninety days to sleep continually on one side, the left, although in the LXX one hundred and ninety are written, and to sleep so that he never wakes nor changes his side, unless perhaps, sated with sleep, he opened his eyes a little to take the most filthy food of ash-baked bread, made of wheat and barley, and bean and lentil and millet, and baked on human dung. For the nature of things does not allow that any man should sleep always on one side for three hundred and ninety days. And again he says: You shall take up the iniquities of the house of Judah, and for forty days you shall sleep on the right side. These days, however, will be reckoned as years, during which Israel and Judah will be pressed down by siege and a very long captivity, so that, conquered and motionless, they cannot turn themselves from one side to the other. If he cannot prove that these things, and others like them which we read in the holy Scriptures, were done, but contends that they signify something else, then this harlot woman too, and the other woman, the adulteress, who were either joined to the prophet or kept by the prophet, indicate not a shameful union of lust but the mysteries of things to come.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.1.10-11":["And the number of the sons of Israel will be like the sand of the sea, which is without measure and will not be numbered. And it will be in the place where it will be said to them: You are not my people, it will be said to them: Sons of the living God. And the sons of Judah and the sons of Israel will be gathered together; and they will set over themselves one head and will go up from the land, for great is the day of Jezreel. LXX: And the number of the sons of Israel will be as the sand of the sea, which will not be measured nor can be numbered. And it will be in the place where it was said to them: You are not my people, they will be called sons of the living God. And the sons of Judah and the sons of Israel will be gathered together; and they will set over themselves one rule, and will go up from the land, for great is the day of Jezreel. We have read of the casting off of the ten tribes, and of Israel, not to be measured and no longer the people of God, condemned by perpetual indignation. Now we learn how the sons of Judah and the sons of Israel are gathered together, and set over themselves one head or rule; and go up from the land, and in the place where it was formerly said: Not my people, are called sons of the living God; and that this happens because great is the day of Jezreel. To one who is in doubt and wavering among various opinions there comes to mind that passage of Paul the Apostle writing to the Romans: What if God, wishing to show his wrath and to make known his power, endured with much patience the vessels of wrath fitted for destruction, so that he might show the riches of his glory upon the vessels of mercy which he prepared for glory; us whom he also called, not only from the Jews but also from the Gentiles, as he says in Hosea: I called not my people, my people, and: Not Obtained Mercy, Obtained Mercy. And it will be in the place where it was said to them: Not my people are you, there they will be called sons of the living God. But Isaiah cries out on behalf of Israel: If the number of the sons of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant will be saved. For he is completing and cutting short the word in equity, because the Lord will make a shortened word upon the earth. And as Isaiah foretold: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we would have become like Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah. What then shall we say? That the Gentiles, who did not pursue righteousness, have laid hold of righteousness, but the righteousness that is from faith; but Israel, pursuing a law of righteousness, has not attained to the law of righteousness. Therefore the blessed Apostle, taking up the testimony of the prophet Hosea and expounding it of the calling of the Gentiles and of the faith of those of the Jews who were willing to believe, has cut away for us every difficulty of interpretation, asserting that it was fulfilled in the times of Christ; namely, that in Israel the twelve tribes are chosen, that is, the whole people of the Jews, and in Judah those of the Gentiles who confess the rule of the Lord. But if someone who is a stranger to the faith of Christ and does not accept the authority of the New Testament, but is of the number of the circumcision, replies that the sons of Judah and the sons of Israel denote the two tribes and the ten, of which we have often spoken, then, yielding even on this point, we will show that it does no harm to our faith. But after the number of the sons of Israel has been scattered like the sand of the sea throughout the whole world, and the multitude of the people has surpassed all reckoning, then Israel, who is a captive even today, and who was formerly called Without Mercy and Not My People, will have leave to enter into concord with the two tribes, that is, Judah and Benjamin, a great part of which believed in Christ, so that faith may join those who are separated in body, and they may set over themselves one head and prince; of whom Ezekiel wrote: And one prince in their midst, David my servant, and the dead will be raised as from hell, namely Judah and Israel, who were dead in unbelief. And all these things will come to pass because great is the day of the seed of God, which is interpreted as Christ. From this it is plain that the blood of Naboth the Jezreelite went before as a type precisely so that the truth might be fulfilled in Christ. For it is in him and not in the other that great is the day of Jezreel, of which it is said: This is the day which the Lord has made: let us exult and rejoice in it. Of the third interpretation which we have undertaken, explaining Israel as the heretics and Judah as the men of the Church, this is the sense: that after the Lord has come in his glory to reign, those who were formerly called not his people will be called sons of the living God, when they have been united to Judah, that is, to the Church of God, and have had one head, Christ, and have gone up from the land, that is, from earthly senses and the lowliness of the letter, and have taken up the great day of the seed of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.1":["Say to your brothers: My People, and to your sister: Obtained Mercy. LXX: Say to your brother: My People, and to your sister: Obtained Mercy. Since great is the day of Jezreel, in which Judah and Israel had one prince, and it will by no means be said to Israel: Not My People; but on the contrary they will be called sons of the living God. Therefore, O men of the tribe of Judah, do not despair of the salvation of the ten tribes, but daily, by word and by prayer and by letters, call them to repentance, because he is called your brother and your sister: brother from the fact that he is called My People; sister from the fact that he is named Obtained Mercy. Otherwise: you who believe in Christ, and are as much from the Jews as from the Gentiles, say to the broken branches, and to the former people which was cast off: My People, because he is your brother; and Obtained Mercy, because she is your sister. For when the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel will be saved. This same thing is commanded us, that we should not utterly despair of the heretics, but call them to repentance, and desire their salvation with the affection of kinship.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.2-3":["The Synagogue is both of these, and if she persists in fornication and adultery, God will take away from her the garments and ornaments which he had given. Of these Ezekiel writes: On the day when you were born, they did not bind your breasts, and you were not salted with salt: and I passed by you, and found you naked, and disfigured, and spattered with blood. And a little after: I clothed you with fine linen and delicate stuffs, and placed ornaments about you, and gave bracelets about your hands, a necklace about your neck. These things her most generous husband gave her then, when he found her in Egypt, defiled by the lust of idolatry and spreading her feet for all. And now he threatens that, if she will not return to her husband, she will be without God and husband, just as she had been before in Egypt. Lest we always stretch out our volumes with a threefold explanation, let it suffice to have pointed this out now and then: that what has been said fits both the Jews who deny Christ and the heretics who abandon the faith of the Lord, whose fornication is properly between the breasts; and it dwells in the heart, in the craft of idols and of various dogmas; they will return to the day of their birth, so that, if they do not repent, they will be compared to the heathen.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.2":["Judge your mother, judge, since she is not my wife and I am not her husband; let her put away her fornications from her face, and her adulteries from between her breasts, lest perhaps I strip her naked and set her as on the day of her birth. LXX: Be judged with your mother, be judged, because she is not my wife and I am not her husband; and I will put away her fornication from my face, and her adulteries from between her breasts, so that I may strip her naked, and restore her as on the day of her birth. Up to this point the discourse has been directed to the people of Israel, that is, to the ten tribes. Now another section begins, and the sons, that is, the people, are commanded to enter into judgment against their mother, who bore them, who, made a wife from a harlot, did not abandon her former ways, and again played the harlot with her lovers. And see the clemency of the husband. She has already been repudiated, already cast off; he has already said to her: She is not my wife and I am not her husband; yet he commands the sons to speak, not at all to the wife of their father whom he has put away, but to their mother, who bore them. And let them speak calling her to repentance, so that she may put away her fornications from her face, and her adulteries from between her breasts. A fornicatress is one who couples with many. An adulteress is one who, deserting one husband, is joined to another.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.3":["And I will make her like a wilderness, and set her like a pathless land, and kill her with thirst. LXX: And I will make her as a desert, and set her as a land without water, and slay her in thirst. If she will not be converted to better things, I will do to her what I did in the wilderness, so that, led into captivity, they may fall in a foreign land, suffering thirst for all good things, and be unable to return to their homeland. Or at least let them hear in the Gospel: Your house will be left to you desolate. And the Lord will send them not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water; but a famine of hearing the word of the Lord. Of which Isaiah also speaks: They will be like a garden that has no water. The heretics who have been cast off by the Lord, if they do not return to their former home, will suffer such want of all things that even what they seem to have, though false, will be reduced to nothing.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.4":["And on her children I will have no mercy, since they are children of fornications, for their mother has played the harlot. She who conceived them is confounded, for she said: I will go after my lovers, who give me bread and my waters, my wool and my flax, my oil and my drink. LXX: And on her children I will have no mercy, since they are children of fornication, for their mother has played the harlot. She who bore them is confounded; for she said: I will go after my lovers, who give me my bread and my water, and my garments and my linens, and my oil and all things that are necessary to me. This I will do besides to her to whom I said: not my wife, and I am not your husband, and I will make her like a wilderness, and make her like a pathless land. For when I led her out of Egypt, the parents were slain and their sons entered the land of promise. But now the children of the fornicating mother will perish with their harlot mother, since they are children of fornication, and evil ones begotten of evil ones. To them it is said in the Gospel: Brood of vipers, a brood which came to such shamelessness that it heard through Jeremiah: You have got the face of a harlot; you are shameless.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.5":["Is not this the mark of a hard brow and of a harlot's shamelessness, to glory in one's own crime and say: I will follow my lovers; I will go to the idols which have supplied me with what is necessary both for food and for clothing? All the things which the prophetic word describes the Jews received spiritually from the Lord. And because they denied the Son of God, choosing for themselves Barabbas, the author of robbery and sedition, and crucifying the Son of God, therefore to this day they follow demons, and ascribe the benefits of God to those who have destroyed the souls of their worshippers. The heretics have bread and waters: their bread is the bread of mourning, and their waters are muddy, waters which choke and kill the baptized. They have also wool from scabby sheep, and flax that remains in its blackness, and oil, of which the prophet says: Let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head, and the drink of the waters of Egypt, of which Jeremiah cries out: What have you to do with the way of Egypt, to drink the water of Gihon? And: What have you to do with the way of the Assyrians, to drink the waters of the rivers? We run through all these things briefly, so as to pass on to the rest.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.6-7":["And also in Hosea, where it is said of Jerusalem committing fornication: Behold, I will close her ways with thorns, and I will block up her paths, and she will not find her track, and she will pursue her lovers, and will not overtake them, and she will seek them, and will not find them; and she will say: I will go and return to my former husband, for it was better with me then than now. Notice that unless the ways had been closed and the paths fenced off, and unless the Lord had destroyed her ways, the fornicating soul could never have said: I will go and return to my former husband.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But if, following the sense above, we wish to take Now the daughter shall be blocked with a blockade as said of the Church, let us bring forward that example from Hosea, in which the adulteress speaks: I will go after my lovers, who gave me my bread, and my water, and my garments, and my linens, my oil, and all things that profit me. And afterwards God, wishing to hinder her most wicked thought, does not let her fulfill what she desires, but blocks her ways, so that she may not overtake her lovers and commit fornication further. For he says: Therefore, behold, I will block her ways with stakes, and I will hedge up her ways, and she will not find her path; and she will pursue her lovers, and will not lay hold of them, and she will seek them, and will not find them, and she will say: I will go and return to my former husband, because it was well with me. Notice that the Lord's work has succeeded: for the adulteress, not finding her way and not able to go where she wished, returns, bound by necessity, to her former husband, and confesses that it is better for her in her former husband's house than it once had been with her lovers; and Israel is schooled by tribulations and blows.\n— Commentary on Micah","Because of this, behold, I will hedge up your way with thorns, and I will hedge it with a wall, and she will not find her paths, and she will follow her lovers and will not overtake them, and she will seek them and will not find them, and she will say: I will go and return to my first husband, for it was well with me then, more than now. LXX: Therefore, behold, I will hedge up her way with stakes, and I will block her ways; and she will not find her path. And she will pursue her lovers, and will not overtake them; and she will seek them and will not find them; and she will say: I will go and return to my first husband, for it was better with me then than it is now. The harlot had said: I will go after my lovers, who have supplied me with abundance of all things. The Lord answered: I will hedge up your way with thorns, or with stakes, so that you cannot go where you desire, and I will set a hedge-wall, or a wall, in the way, and you will not find your paths which you had often trodden with your foot, so that you may not overtake those whom you were following with such zeal, and, compelled by the need of things, you may return to your husband; and say that saying from the Gospel: How many hired servants in my father's house have bread in abundance, but I perish here with hunger. I will arise and go to my father and will say to him: Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you, I am no longer worthy to be called your son, make me as one of your hired servants. From this we understand that by the providence of God evils often befall us, so that we may not have the things we desire, and, oppressed by the various calamities and miseries of this world, we may be compelled to return to the service of God. And by the lovers of Jerusalem and of the Jewish nation, according to the history of that time, let us understand the Assyrians and Chaldeans and Egyptians and the other nations, with whose idols she played the harlot, and from whom, in time of war and when evils pressed upon her, she hoped for help in vain. According to the spiritual understanding, the heretics follow these lovers, and, often deserted by them, return under the weight of their evils to the bosom of mother Church. For through all scourges and torments Israel is instructed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.8":["And she did not know this, that I gave her grain and wine and oil, and multiplied silver for her, and gold, which they made into Baal. LXX: And she did not know that I gave her grain and wine and oil, and multiplied silver for her; but these she made into things of silver and gold for Baal. He is answering what came before, for she had said: I will go after my lovers, who give me my bread and my water, wool and flax, and my oil and my drink; all of which she had received so that she might turn them to the worship of God. But she, the bread that strengthens and the wine that gladdens the heart of man, and the oil that enlightens every man coming into this world, and the silver of which we have often said: The words of the Lord, chaste words, silver tested by fire, proved for the earth, purified sevenfold; and the gold of which we read: If you sleep in the midst of the lots, the wings of a dove covered with silver, and the hinder parts of her back in the greenness of gold, she turned into idols, and made Baal, which is interpreted 'higher' and 'devouring', while she either thinks that she has greater dogmas than those of the Church, or is devoured in the very knowledge of false opinion. But that, according to the letter, Jerusalem abounded in gold and silver and all riches, and made idols to Baal, the demon of the Sidonians, or, as some more rightly judge, the Babylonian one, from whom he is also called Bel, Ezekiel sets out more fully in his volume, and the whole choir of the prophets relates.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.9":["Therefore I will return and take my grain in its time, and my wine in its time, and I will set free my wool and my flax, which covered her disgrace. LXX: Therefore I will return and take away my wheat in its time, and my wine in its time, and I will take away my garments and my linens, so that they may not cover her disgrace. The punishment is heavier when, at the time of harvest and vintage, the hoped-for crops and the wine are taken away, and are, as it were, snatched out of the hands that already held them. But if at the time of the threshing floor and the vat and the winepress, when the earth makes up for past barrenness with new crops, there is a want of all things, what must we reckon of the rest of the year, when the old stores too are being saved? Now the wool and the flax, or the garments and the linens, are set free, so that they may no longer cover the harlot's disgrace; that is to say, so that she may be stripped of God's help, and the protection of all the angels may depart from her. Hence the Apostle too says that the creation is set free from the bondage of corruption into the liberty of the glory of the sons of God. Many have received the gold and silver of wisdom and eloquence, out of which they might make the lampstand of seven lamps of purest gold, and the golden table of the showbread, and the mercy seat and the cherubim gleaming with the splendor of gold, and the silver bases of the pillars, and the wheat of the word of God, and the wine, the joy of the Holy Spirit; and garments too and linens with which believers might be clothed in Christ; all of which they turned to the worship of idolatry, composing various dogmas of error, and, being deceived, they deceived others. All these things God takes away, so that those who did not perceive the giver from abundance may perceive him from want.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.10-12":["These things, both according to the history and according to tropology, unhappy Judea has suffered: all her shame has been uncovered in the eyes of the Gentiles, and no one could rescue her from the hands of God. All the ceremonies have ceased, the festivity has been turned into mourning; all that she thought was given her by the demons, she now knows has been taken away because she offended God. First the Assyrians and the Chaldeans devoured her, the Medes and the Persians and the Macedonians; at the last the most savage beast tore her apart, the empire of the Romans, whose name is passed over in silence in Daniel, so that the dread of those who are to be devoured may be made greater. What we have said of Judea, refer to the heretics, who, promising doctrine and knowledge at first sight, having gone out from the Church, are deserted by God; and all their disgrace is set in the sight of their lovers, whom they had deceived before; and, handed over to the beasts which the prophet shuns when he says, Do not hand over to the beasts the soul that confesses you, they will be left to their bites.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.10":["So then, once the garment and the linen have been set free, so that they might no longer cover the disgrace of the fornicating woman, all the shame of Jerusalem, or the folly through which she worked shame, will be uncovered in the sight of her lovers, so that those who desired her veiled may despise her exposed. And when she has been handed over to her lovers the Assyrians, or to the demons whom both she herself and the Assyrians serve, no one, he says, will be able to snatch her from my hand; for the weakness of the demons is proved in this, that those whom they had received abounding in all things they cannot free when they are crushed by evils.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.11":["And handed over to Babylonian slavery, she will by no means be able to celebrate the three solemnities of Passover and Pentecost and Tabernacles, nor to keep the joy of the new moon, that is, of the calends, nor of the sabbath, nor all the festivals, which he comprises under one name.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.12":["And now I will uncover her folly in the eyes of her lovers; and no one will rescue her from my hand and I will make all her joy cease, her solemnity, her new moon, her sabbath, and all her festal times; and I will spoil her vine and her fig tree, of which she said: These are my wages, which my lovers gave me; and I will make her into a forest, and the beast of the field will eat her. LXX: And now I will uncover her uncleanness in the sight of her lovers; and no one will rescue her from my hand; and I will turn away all her gladness, her solemnities and her new moons, and the sabbaths, and all her festivals; and I will destroy her vine, and her fig trees, of which she said: These are my wages which my lovers gave me; and I will make her a testimony, and the beasts of the field will eat her. What follows, The birds of the sky and the creeping things of the earth, must be marked beforehand with an obelus. For 'forest', too, which in Hebrew is called iar, whence Kiriath-jearim also is interpreted 'town of the woods', the LXX translated 'testimony', through the deceptive likeness of the letters res and daleth. For if daleth is read in place of res, it means 'testimony', provided only that the letter iod does not come before it.\n— Commentary on Hosea","The vine too and the fig tree and the abundance of all things will be spoiled: in the vine understand gladness, in the fig sweetness and pleasantness, which are taken away by the evils of a most grievous slavery; and they are taken away for this reason, that she judged them given to her not by God, as to a wife, but by her lovers, as to a harlot, for the wages of lust. Then she will by no means have fruit-bearing trees, but everything will be turned into forest. And since he had once taken the metaphor from the woods, he carries it through in the rest, so as to call the enemies, by whom all things are to be devoured, beasts.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.13":["And I will visit upon her the days of the Baalim, on which she burned incense and adorned herself with her earring and her necklace, and went after her lovers, and forgot me, says the Lord. LXX: And I will avenge upon her the days of the Baalim, on which she sacrificed to them; and she put her earrings and necklaces about herself, and went after her lovers; but me she forgot, says the Lord. He keeps up the character of the harlot, who adorns herself with gold and gems to please her lovers, and whatever beauty she does not have by nature she procures by art. These earrings, with which her ears had been adorned by the teaching of God, and these pearls which hung precious from her neck, so that the bridegroom and husband said to her, Your neck is like necklaces, she cast before the feet of swine, and gave what is holy to the dogs. And what we read in Proverbs was fulfilled: Like a golden earring in a sow's nostrils, so is beauty to a most wicked woman. Now she did all these things so as to follow her lovers and leave her husband. And so great was her desire for pleasure and lust that she lost all memory of her marriage and forgot that she had been a wife. Therefore, on those very solemnities on which she burned frankincense to the demons, she will be visited with blows and chastised with punishments. Baal in the singular number, Baalim in the plural: the same idols are named in the masculine gender. For wherever we read the syllable im at the end of a Hebrew word, it is in the plural number, masculine gender; but where oth, plural number, feminine gender. Therefore we understand seraphim and cherubim as plural number, masculine gender; but Sabaoth, which is interpreted 'of armies', or 'of hosts', or 'of powers', as plural number, feminine gender. And so the Baalim too are of the masculine gender and plural number, although some wrongly read τῇ Βαὰλ (feminine article) and ταῖς Βααλεὶμ (feminine plural), in the feminine gender. But how the heretics deceive their lovers, and adorn themselves with charm of speech and the arrangement of words, so as to make falsehood look like truth, and abandon conjugal chastity, and burn incense to the Baalim, that is, to the idols which they have fashioned out of their own heart, we see daily. For they have no care for the simple rusticity that does not seek a harlot's ornaments, but for artful and elegant falsehood, so as to please their lovers, the devils and demons.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.14-15":["For this reason God says of Jerusalem too: Behold, I will lead her astray, and I will make her like a desert, and set her like a land without water, and I will speak to her heart; and I will give her possessions from there, and the valley of Achor to open understanding (Hos 2:14, 15). Let us consider the order of each thing: he leads her astray, and makes her deserted and without water, so that she may suffer thirst for the virtues. After she has said, My soul has thirsted for you, how many ways my flesh for you (Ps 62 [63]:2), then he will speak to her heart and console her as she mourns. And he will give her a possession from there, that is, from the repentance and sorrow which works salvation. And the humility of her troubled heart (for this is what the valley of Achor means) opens her understanding, so that she may not be ignorant of her Creator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Therefore, behold, I will allure her; and I will lead her into the wilderness, and I will speak to her heart. And I will give her her vinedressers from the same place, and the valley of Achor to open up hope. LXX: Therefore, behold, I will lead her astray and will make her like a desert; and I will speak to her heart and give her her possessions from there, and the valley of Achor to open up her understanding. Where we said to open up hope, and the LXX rendered to open up her understanding, Symmachus interpreted εἰς θύραν ἐλπίδος, that is, into a door of hope, Theodotion διανοῖξαι τὴν ὑπομονὴν αὐτῆς, that is, to open up her patience, or her expectation. After the disgrace of Jerusalem, or of Judea the harlot, has been uncovered in the eyes of her lovers, and every solemnity has ceased, and drought or hail has spoiled the vineyard and the fig tree, and, reduced to woods and fruitless trees, she has been devoured by beasts, and the Lord has repaid her with tortures and torments, because she had burned frankincense to the demons, the Baalim, and she thinks that she can by no means rise again any more from the ashes and embers, then, that is, at the coming of Christ his Son, he will open the hope of salvation, and give room for repentance, and will caress her; for this is what I will allure her means; so as to soften her former pains, after the greatness of her punishments, by the promise of prosperity. And I will lead her, he says, into the wilderness, that is, I will lead her out of evils, just as I had earlier led her out of Egyptian slavery; and I will speak to her heart soft words, words of comfort, so as to temper sadness with joy, according to the idiom of the Scriptures, in which words Shechem too spoke to the heart of Dinah, and Joseph in Egypt to his brothers, who were sad and afraid, so that grief might be changed into joy.\n— Commentary on Hosea","These things the circumcision and our own Judaizers refer to the reign of a thousand years, things which we see fulfilled in the beginning through the apostles as vinedressers and the many thousands of believers from Israel, and being fulfilled daily in those who are willing to believe. And where we said, I will allure her, and the LXX interpreted, I will lead her astray, they refer it to the time of the Antichrist, so that those who did not receive the truth of Christ may receive that one's lie, and afterwards, when Christ comes, may be saved.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.15":["And what follows: And I will give her her vinedressers from the same place. He who had set forth beforehand the caressing and the wilderness in the likeness of the past history of those who went out of Egypt, in which Moses and Aaron arose as princes from the same nation of the Jews, now too promises that he will give her vinedressers from the same place. That the vineyard is understood as Israel, all the scripture of both the Old and the New Testament bears witness: The vineyard of the Lord of Sabaoth is the house of Israel. And: You transferred a vineyard out of Egypt. And in the Gospel: The householder let out his vineyard and did not receive the fruits. And when his son had been killed, at the last he let it out to other vinedressers. This, then, is what the prophetic word promises: that the princes of this vineyard, going out from the nations and from the captivity of enemies, or of vices, should be of the very race of the Jews, that is, the apostles; and that the place of tumult and valley of troubles — for this is what Achor is interpreted — should be changed into a door of hope, or to open up hope and patience, in that she has suffered punishments and torments for this reason, that through them she might come to prosperity. But that the valley of Achor, in which Achan was killed for the theft of the things that had been consecrated to God, is interpreted 'confusion' and 'tumult', and not, as some think, διαστροφὴ, that is, perversity, Joshua himself interprets when he speaks to Achan: Because you have troubled us, may the Lord trouble you on this day. Hence that place was called Emec Achor, that is, the valley of confusion. And at the same time we understand this too: that at the beginning of the holy land, near Jericho, when the people came out of the wilderness, beside the streams of the Jordan, in Israel's first victory, grief was changed into joy, and hope was opened there, where there had been despair. So that, once those have been punished who sinned against Christ and committed sacrilege, those of them may be saved who have detested the blaspheming Jews and, as far as was in their power, have slain them.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And she will sing there according to the days of her youth, and according to the days of her coming up from the land of Egypt. LXX: And she will be humbled according to the days of her infancy and according to the days of the coming up from the land of Egypt. In the place where we put she will sing, and the LXX translated she will be humbled, in the Hebrew is written anatha, which Symmachus interpreted 'she will be afflicted', Theodotion 'she will answer', Aquila 'she will obey', that is, ὑπακούσει; we take it rather from the Hebrew κατὰ λέξιν (word for word), that is, 'she will lead the singing'; so that, since he had once set down the being tossed about and the leading out into the wilderness, and the vinedressers from the same place, and the valley of Achor, touching in brief speech on the whole history of those who went out of Egypt and journeyed to the holy land, he might even now keep the likeness of the history. So that, just as at that time, when they were going out of the land of Egypt, after Pharaoh had been drowned in the Red Sea, Miriam took up a timbrel and, leading the singing for the others, exulted and said: Let us sing to the Lord, for he has been gloriously magnified; horse and rider he has cast into the sea, so now too, according to the days of her youth, or of her adolescence, when she went out of the land of Egypt, she may sing and rejoice, and sing together of the kingdom of Christ and her own salvation among the choirs of the Church. And notice that when we go out of Egypt and pass over to better things, we are said to go up, because Jerusalem is set in the mountains; and he who had wished to go down from it into Jericho was wounded. But to those who seek the help of Egypt, that is, of this world, it is said: Woe to those who go down into Egypt for help. The interpretation of Aquila and Theodotion, of whom the one put ὑπακούσει, that is, 'will hear', the other ἀποκριθήσεται, that is, 'will answer', is on our side, in that while some lead the singing, others answer, singing together. But she will be humbled and she will be afflicted, which the LXX and Symmachus translated, does not suit a time of gladness, unless perhaps she will imitate Paul, who, after he was called as an apostle, bewails his former sins and says that he is unworthy of the calling of an apostle, because he persecuted the Church of God; so that the humiliation and the affliction are to be taken as the awareness of a past wound, not as pain at present evils.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.16-17":["And it will be on that day, says the Lord, she will call me My husband, and will no longer call me Baalim; and I will take away the names of the Baalim from her mouth, and she will no longer remember their name. LXX: likewise. That Ninus, the son of Belus, was the first to reign over all Asia, all histories, both Greek and barbarian, relate; he founded among the Assyrians the city of Ninus, named after himself, which the Hebrews call Nineveh. His wife Semiramis, of whom many marvelous things are told, built the walls of Babylon. Of her the famous poet bears witness, saying: \"Where Semiramis is said once to have girded the city with walls of baked brick.\" He fought in a strong contest against the great Zoroaster, king of the Bactrians, and came to such glory that he enrolled his father Belus as a god; he is called Bel in Hebrew, and in many prophets, and above all in Daniel according to Theodotion, he is called by this name as the idol of Babylon. The Sidonians and Phoenicians call him Baal; for between the consonant letters beth and lamed the same vowel letter ain is placed, which, according to the peculiar character of that language, is read now Beel, now Baal. Hence Dido too, the Sidonian of royal race, when she had received Aeneas as a guest, pours a libation of wine to Jupiter from that bowl which Belus and all those descended from Belus were accustomed to use. We have learned the origin of the demon, or rather of a man consecrated into a demon; for all idols have grown out of the error concerning the dead. Let us hear the rest: in the Hebrew and Syrian tongue Baal is interpreted ἔχων, that is, 'having'. If we wish to say 'having me', let us say Baali; and among both nations wives call their husbands Baali, that is, 'my husband', which is understood as 'having me'; and the sense is: he who has me in marriage. In the same language of the Hebrews a man is called is, whence the wife too, who was taken from the man, is called Issa in Genesis, as virago, as it were, from vir, man.\n\nWhat God says, then, is this: since with both words, that is, Issi and Baali, 'my husband', or 'my spouse', is named, I so hate the names of idols that even what can rightly be said I would by no means wish to be said, because of the ambiguity and the likeness of the word, but wish to be called Issi rather than Baali, lest while she says one thing she should remember another, and, naming her husband, think of an idol. Forgive the obscurity, which arises in three ways: either from the difficulty of the matters, or from the inexperience of the teacher, or from the excessive slowness of the learner. For in expounding a Hebrew prophet I ought not to play with little oratorical declamations, and to sing in narrations and epilogues in the Asiatic manner, but, with the help of your prayers and with an incredible zeal for learning, to open what is closed. Finally Aquila, a diligent and painstaking translator: She will call, he says, me My husband, and will no longer call me Having me, that is, Baal.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.18":["And I will strike a covenant for them in that day with the beast of the field, and with the bird of the sky, and with the creeping thing of the earth; and the bow, and the sword, and war I will crush out of the land; and I will make them sleep confidently. LXX: And I will arrange a covenant for them in that day with the beasts of the field, and with the flying things of the sky, and with the creeping things of the earth; and the bow, and the sword, and war I will crush out of the land; and I will make them dwell in hope. When all the names of the hostile religion have been taken away from the people that confesses the Lord, and she calls me, he says, My husband, and by no means Baali, that is, my idol, then I will strike a covenant and concord for them with the beasts of the field, and the birds of the sky, and the creeping things of the earth. Of this time Isaiah also speaks: The wolf will dwell with the lamb; and the leopard will lie down with the kid. The calf, and the lion, and the sheep will abide together, and a little child will drive them. The calf and the bear will feed, and their young will rest together; and the lion will eat straw like the ox, by no means desiring flesh and blood, but feeding on clean and simple foods. When it was revealed to Peter, that he might receive Cornelius from the nations, and he was commanded to eat all the beasts and to know that nothing is unclean which is received with thanksgiving, concerning which he afterwards hears: What God has cleansed, do not call common. Therefore at the coming of the Lord the Savior, after the triumphs of his resurrection and his ascent to the Father, the two walls were joined by the cornerstone, by him who made both one. And he called her who was called Without Mercy, Obtained Mercy; and him who was called Not My People, his own people; and when all things have been made peaceful, the bow and the sword will be crushed, and war. For the instruments of fighters will not be necessary when those who would fight are lacking. Israel will be joined to the nations, and that saying of Deuteronomy will be fulfilled: Rejoice, you nations, with his people. Known for in Judea is God, in Israel his name is great. And: His place has been made in peace, and his dwelling in Zion, that is, in the Church, in which he broke the powers, the bow, the shield, the sword and war; and when these have been broken and crushed, the believers will sleep confidently and will rest under one shepherd. Or: they will hope in those things which eye has not seen, and ear has not heard, and which have not entered into the heart of man, which God has prepared for those who love him.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.19-20":["And I will betroth you to me for ever; and I will betroth you to me in justice and judgment, and in mercy and in compassions; and I will betroth you to me in faith, and you will know that I am the Lord. LXX: likewise. O how great is the clemency of God! The harlot who has played the harlot with many lovers, and for her crime was handed over to the beasts, after she returns to her husband, is by no means said to be reconciled to him, but to be betrothed. And see what difference there is between the union of God and that of men: a man, when he has taken a wife, makes a woman out of a virgin, that is, not a virgin; God, even when joined to harlots, changes them into virgins, according to that which is said to the adulteress through Jeremiah: You have not called upon me as lord, or father, or prince of your virginity. Hence the Apostle too speaks to the Corinthians who believed after fornication and the worship of idols: For I have betrothed you to one husband, to present you as a chaste virgin to Christ. We ask why he repeated the word betrothal a third time. For first he says: I will betroth you to me for ever. Second: I will betroth you to me in justice and judgment, and in mercy and in compassions. And not content with this end, he adds a third as well: I will betroth you to me in faith, and you will know that I am the Lord. First he betrothed her in Abraham, or in Egypt, so that he might have an everlasting wife. Second on Mount Sinai, giving her in the betrothal gifts the justice and judgment of the law, and mercy joined to the law, so that when she sinned she would be handed over to captivity, and when she repented she would be called back to her homeland and obtain mercy. Of these betrothal gifts the psalmist sings in various places: Mercy and judgment I will sing to you, O Lord. And: Mercy and truth have met each other, and justice and peace have kissed each other. These two cups we read of in the seventy-fourth (75th) psalm: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of unmixed wine, mixed, and he has tilted it from this into that; from the cup of justice into the cup of mercy, and again from the cup of mercy into the cup of justice. If you mark iniquities, he says, O Lord, Lord, who will endure? For with you there is propitiation. This harlot, then, who first had been joined by the vow of the bridegroom in everlasting embraces, so that she should never withdraw from the marriage bond, because she withdrew and played the harlot in Egypt, is taken again through the lawful rites; and because she transgressed this, and the prophets, the friends, as it were, of the bridegroom, who had been sent to her, were killed, last of all came the Son of God, the Lord Jesus; and when he had been crucified and had risen from the dead, she is betrothed by no means in the justice of the law, but in faith and the grace of the Gospel, so that when she has known the Only-begotten, she may know the Father also. For he himself says to Philip: Have I been with you so long a time, and you have not known me? Philip, he who has seen me has seen my Father also; how do you say: Show us the Father? Do you not believe that I am in the Father, and the Father is in me? Whoever has been betrothed in faith and has believed the Gospel will at once know that he himself is God, believing in him whom he had formerly denied.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.21-22":["And it will be in that day: I will hear, says the Lord, I will hear the heavens, and they will hear the earth, and the earth will hear the wheat and the wine and the oil, and these will hear Jezreel, and I will sow her for myself in the land, and I will have mercy on her who was Without Mercy; and I will say to Not My People: You are My People; and he will say: You are my God. LXX: And it will be in that day, says the Lord: I will hear the heaven, and the heaven will hear the earth, and the earth will hear the grain, the wine, and the oil, and these will hear Jezreel, and I will sow her for myself upon the land, and I will love her who was not loved; and I will say to Not My People: You are My People; and he will say: You are the Lord my God. In that day and at that time, when I have betrothed you to me in faith, and you have known that I am the Lord, I will hear the heavens, which tell the glory of God, and the heavens will hear the earth, so as to water it with heavenly rain; and the earth, from which truth has sprung, and into whose field the householder goes out to sow his seed, will hear the wheat and the wine and the oil, of which we spoke above; and all these will hear Jezreel, that is, the seed of God, so that it may be understood that the abundance and fertility of all things are granted on account of the seed of God, who is Christ, because the seed of God is sown in the earth, to bring forth manifold fruits, a hundredfold, sixtyfold and thirtyfold.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Concerning the hearing of heaven and earth, and of the grain, wine and oil, and of Jezreel, some hold this view: that after Christ has come, all things will run in their own order, and the whole creation will serve the advantage of men, as it was founded in the beginning. All these things the Jews and our Judaizers await bodily, after the Antichrist, at the end of the world.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.2.23":["And that harlot who had been joined to God, and had borne three children, two males and one female, the first Jezreel, the second Without Mercy, the third Not My People, may see that because of the seed of God the names of things have been changed, and that in the faith of Christ she who was Without Mercy has obtained mercy, and he who was formerly called Not a People has been called the people of God. From this we perceive that everything that has been said can be referred both to the ten tribes, which are called Israel, and which under the name of the harlot bore three children, and to the whole race of the Jewish name.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.3.1":["And the Lord said to me: Go again, love a woman loved by a friend and an adulteress, as the Lord loves the sons of Israel; and they look to other gods, and love the husks of grapes. LXX: And the Lord said to me: Go again and love a woman who loves evil things and an adulteress, as the Lord loves the sons of Israel; and they look to other gods, and love cooked cakes with grape-husks. To the former woman the prophet is joined as a type of the ten tribes, or of the whole Jewish people playing the harlot, and at length he received back into salvation the children who had been cast off and tormented; here he is ordered to love an adulterous woman again. When it is said: again, does it show that he had loved a fornicatress before, who as an adulteress either loves those evil things or is loved by a friend and neighbor? For the Hebrew word Re is read either as evil or as friend. And lest we should think that the prophet's love for the adulteress signifies anything other than God's love for the sons of Israel, he added: as the Lord loves the sons of Israel. Therefore the fact that the prophet loves the adulteress, and yet is not joined to her in marriage nor united with her in fornication, but only loves the woman who does wrong, is a type of God, who loves the most wicked sons of Israel; and they, though they are loved by the Lord, look to other gods and idols, and love the husks of grapes, which have no wine; and they have lost their former grace, like the demons, who, having fallen from their own dignity and possessing nothing of their ancient grace, are dry and withering in an old dryness. Hence for husks, which in Hebrew is called asise, Aquila translated παλαιὰ, that is, old things; Symmachus ἀκάρπους, that is, barren; and they are not only husks, but old husks, so that they bear witness to ancient offenses. And it is to be noted that this adulteress signifies the present time of the Jews, who, without God and the knowledge of the Scriptures and the grace of the Holy Spirit, are loved by the Lord, who awaits the salvation of all and opens the door to those who repent; and nevertheless they love useless things, loving the traditions of men and the dreams of the δευτερωσέων (of the 'secondary teachings', the Jewish traditions), and by no means have grapes and wine and winepresses full of must, but old husks which have been thrown away. For the pemmata, which the LXX translated, and which are eaten with raisins or grape-husks, we can say in Latin flat-cakes, or pastries, which are offered to idols and are called in Greek πόπανα (cakes). But the sons of Israel in the present passage are called the twelve tribes; above, however, the ten which were in Samaria and possessed the metropolis, Jezreel.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.3.2-3":["And I dug her for myself for fifteen pieces of silver, and a cor of barley, and half a cor of barley, and I said to her: Many days you will wait for me; you will not play the harlot, and you will not be for a man; but I also will wait for you. LXX: And I hired her for myself for fifteen pieces of silver, and a gomor of barley, and a nebel of wine, and I said to her: Many days you will sit for me; and you will not play the harlot, nor will you be for another man, and I for you. For gomor, in the Hebrew is written omer, which all the translators, except the LXX, have rendered cor, a word Greek and especially Palestinian, which holds thirty modii. And for nebel of wine, in the Hebrew is read lethech seorim, which the other translators rendered ἡμίκορον of barley, that is, half a cor, which makes fifteen modii. And what is added in the common edition: Nor will you be for another man; another is not found in the Hebrew, but simply you will not be for a man. For if another is put, it is understood that she is had by her own husband. But when it is said absolutely you will not be for a man, we understand that she is joined to no one at all, and is without marital intercourse. Now the measure of the cor we read in Ezekiel, and in the Gospel. So he dug her for himself, that is, the adulteress, or hired her for fifteen pieces of silver. When he says: I dug, he points to the vineyard which was planted by the Lord and is set down in many places of the Scriptures, signifying the people of the Jews. But if we read: I hired, it is the price of an adulteress, not so that she may sleep with the prophet, but so that, content with her wage, she may cease committing adultery and not be joined here and there to others. She is also dug, or hired, for a cor of barley and half a cor of barley, that is, for forty-five modii; and having received this wage, she heard from the Lord: Many days you will wait for me, or you will sit for me; you will not play the harlot nor be for a man. That is: neither will you shamefully prostitute yourself to other lovers, nor will you be lawfully joined to me, the man by whom you were hired. And lest you think a wrong has been done to you because I said: you will wait for me, I will repay like for like: and I also will wait for you. As the fifteenth day of the month Nisan was dawning, the firstborn of the Egyptians were struck, and the people of Israel was led out of Egypt and hired for a wage into the service of God. Finally, for the firstborn of Israel, which did not feel the common plague, take five shekels of silver each, which are contributed to the gifts of the temple. Most of our writers refer the fifteen to the hebdomad of the law and the ogdoad of the Gospel, that is, the Sabbath and the day of the resurrection, on which spiritual circumcision is practiced. But how an adulterous woman who is fed on barley and is estranged from her husband should receive both testaments as her wage, they cannot explain. On the forty-fifth day the people came to Mount Sinai, and at once on the next day they were commanded to sanctify themselves from intercourse with women for three days, and to prepare themselves to receive the law of God. When the three days had passed, on the fiftieth day Moses went up the mountain and received the Decalogue. So the people of the Jews, which was once hired for fifteen pieces of silver and forty-five modii of barley, has sat since the coming of the Lord the Savior, and awaits the coming of its husband. And when he says: You will not play the harlot, and you will not be for a man, he shows this: that in the meantime she does not now serve idols, and yet does not have God, but has been stripped both of her lovers and of her husband. And because she has no husband, she does not eat the food of men, grain and pulse, but the barley of irrational beasts of burden, chewing over the cheapness of the letter which kills, and not having the spirit which gives life. Hence also in the law, the woman who is accused of adultery by her husband, in the drink of ἐλεγμοῦ, that is, of conviction, when she is proved guilty of sin, receives barley meal; for she who has imitated horses and mules, which have no understanding, is sustained on the food of horses and mules. For half a cor of barley the Seventy translated a nebel of wine, which is not found in the Hebrew at all. And we can say: that in the nebel of wine, that is, a full and finished measure, the adulteress was made drunk and afflicted with full punishments. Hence Jeremiah too gives the cup of unmixed wine to drink to the nations and to Jerusalem. The husband also sits, or rather waits for the repentance of the adulteress, so that after the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, and Israel has believed last, so that what had been the head is turned into the tail, and the tail is turned into the head, then there may be one flock and one shepherd.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.3.4-5":["The present section some of the Jews interpret of the Babylonian captivity, when the temple was desolate for seventy years, and there were no altar and victims and priesthood, and afterwards under Zerubbabel they returned to their former seats. Others, like us, defer it to the future time; and what is the cause of so great an offense, that they have been abandoned for so long a time, especially since they do not worship idols, they cannot find any other than the killing of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.3.4":["For many days the sons of Israel will sit without a king, and without a prince, and without sacrifice, and without altar, and without ephod, and without teraphim. And after this the sons of Israel will return and will seek the Lord their God and David their king; and they will tremble before the Lord and before his goodness in the last of days. LXX: For many days the sons of Israel will sit without a king, without a prince, without sacrifice, without altar, without priesthood, without manifestations, — which in Greek are called δῆλοι —. And after this the sons of Israel will return, and will seek the Lord their God, and David their king; and they will be astonished before the Lord, and at his good things on the last day. For ephod and teraphim the Seventy translated ἱερατείαν, that is, priesthood, and δήλους, that is, manifestations. Now it should be known, as we have often said, that ephod signifies a priestly garment, which in Exodus and Leviticus is called by the LXX ἐπωμὶς, that is, the shoulder-piece, and by Aquila ἐπένδυμα, that is, the over-garment. Teraphim, however, properly means μορφώματα, that is, figures and images, which we can say, in the present passage at least, are the cherubim and seraphim or the other things which were ordered to be made for the adornment of the temple. But since the Seventy translated δήλους, for which Aquila rendered φωτισμούς (illuminations), and these very things are in the λογείῳ, that is, in the rational, we understand this: that in the breast and heart of the high priest there ought to be ἀλήθεια and φωτισμοί, that is, truth and teaching, so that he may not only know the right faith, but may also be able to bring forth with his mouth what he knows. This the apostle Paul also writes to Titus, teaching what kind of man should be ordained bishop: For a bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God; not proud, not quick to anger, not given to wine, not a striker and not greedy for shameful gain; but hospitable, kind, sober, just, holy, continent, embracing the faithful word which is according to doctrine, so that he may be able both to exhort in sound doctrine and to refute those who contradict. Ephod and teraphim we also read of in the book of Judges, which the Levite had made for himself, who afterwards set out with six hundred armed men of the tribe of Dan. From the passion of the Lord the Savior up to the present day, almost the four hundredth year is being completed, and how much time remains until the day of judgment neither the angels know nor the Son. He says that he does not know for this reason, because it is not expedient for us to learn. These, then, are the many days in which the unhappy Synagogue and adulterous woman is fed on barley, and has sat huddled, because she cannot stand with Christ. Without a king, of whom the Father spoke: I have raised up a king with justice. And he himself says in the psalm: But I have been appointed king by him over Zion, his holy mountain. Without a prince: either the Lord the Savior himself, or at least the high priest, of whom it is written: You shall not curse the prince of your people. And without sacrifice, and without altar. For since the temple was overthrown, and Jerusalem was burnt, the Jewish nation can carry on neither sacrifice nor priesthood. And without ephod and without teraphim, that is, the instruments of the priestly dress. Of this king Jacob too speaks in the blessing of the patriarch Judah: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he will be the expectation of the nations. Therefore, after the prince failed from Judah, and the leader from his thighs, and Herod, a foreigner and a proselyte, took the rule, we understand that he has come for whom the kingdom was laid up, and he will be the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.3.5":["This is the blindness which has happened in part to Israel, so that the fullness of the Gentiles might come in, and then all Israel would be saved; and after a long time they will return and will seek the Lord their God, and David their king, who was born of the stock of David, and whose name is interpreted strong of hand. For he himself released the captivity of his people, and gave release to those in bonds. And when the sons of Israel see him, who was denied by his own brothers, reigning in the majesty of the Father and in his own, they will tremble and be astonished before the Lord and before his Good One, he who, a good son, was born of a good father; or to the good things of the Lord, of which the saint speaks: I believe I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living; for this land which we sinners inhabit is the land of the dead.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.1-2":["Hear the word of the Lord, sons of Israel, for the Lord has a judgment with the inhabitants of the earth. For there is no truth, and there is no mercy, and there is no knowledge of God on the earth; cursing, and lying, and murder, and theft, and adultery have flooded in; and blood has touched blood. LXX: Hear the word of the Lord, sons of Israel, for the Lord has a judgment against the inhabitants of the earth; for there is no truth, nor mercy, nor knowledge of God upon the earth. Cursing, and lying, and murder, and theft, and adultery have been poured out upon the earth, and they have mingled blood with blood. From the beginning of the prophet up to this place, under the description of a harlot and of an adulteress, after whose most grievous punishments and very long abandonment there comes afterwards a restoration to the former, or to a better, state, the sins of the ten tribes, or of the two, and of all in common, are enumerated. Now the discourse turns again to Israel, that is, to the ten tribes, setting forth that it is not without cause that an angry God threatens and inflicts such grievous punishments; lest perhaps the sentence passed against those who had not sinned should seem to have been passed unjustly, not from the truth of judgment but from the power of God. Hear, says the prophet, the word of the Lord, O sons of Israel; for the Lord wishes to enter into judgment with his people and to set forth the causes of his indignation. There is no truth, and there is no mercy, and there is no knowledge of God on the earth. For neither can truth be sustained without mercy, and mercy without truth makes men negligent; hence the one is mingled with the other, and whoever does not have these will consequently not have the knowledge of God either. But on the contrary, in place of truth there is lying, and in place of mercy cursing, murder, theft, adultery. He did not say \"are\", but, to show the abundance of the offenses, he added: have overflowed; and in place of the knowledge of God, which is not on the earth, blood has touched blood, or they have mingled blood with blood, so as to add sins to sins and heap new ones upon the old. And rightly are those who are inhabitants of the earth, and not sojourners, summoned to judgment, because from the face of the north evils blaze up upon the inhabitants of the earth. And in the Apocalypse of John woe, woe, woe is pronounced upon the inhabitants of the earth. But whoever can say with the prophet: I am a stranger with you, and a pilgrim, as all my fathers were; and passes through this world as a sojourner and a pilgrim, follows truth and mercy and the knowledge of God, so as not to be overwhelmed by the flood of cursing and of lying, of murder and of theft, and of adultery and of blood.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.3":["Because of this the earth will mourn, and everyone who dwells in it will languish; with the beast of the field and with the bird of the sky; and even the fish of the sea will be gathered up. LXX: Therefore the earth will mourn and will be diminished with all who inhabit it: with the beasts of the field, with the serpents of the earth, and with the fowls of the sky, and the fish of the sea will fail. Because there is no truth, and there is no mercy, and there is no knowledge of God upon the earth; but, over against these, cursing and lying, and murder and theft and adultery have flooded in, and blood has touched blood. Therefore the earth will mourn with its inhabitants, and will languish, so that it has no beasts of the field and birds of the sky, and the fish of the sea will fail. For when the captivity of the ten tribes has come, once the inhabitant has been taken away, the beasts too and the birds of the sky and the fish of the sea will fail, and even the mute elements will feel the wrath of the Lord. Whoever does not believe that this happened to the people of Israel, let him look at Illyricum, let him look at the Thracian provinces, Macedonia and the Pannonias, and all the land that stretches from the Propontis and the Bosphorus to the Julian Alps, and he will find it proved that, together with men, all the living creatures also fail which were formerly nourished by the Creator for the uses of men. But if we wish, as some think, to interpret the beasts as savage men, and the birds of the sky as those who are lifted up into pride and despise all human things, and the fish as those who are irrational, and so dull that they have no understanding at all, and do not see the free air and the sky; then this is the act of a Lord not so much angry as merciful, that the things which are evil should be taken away from the earth.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.4":["Nevertheless let no one judge, and let no man be reproved; for your people are like those who contradict the priest; and you will fall today, and the prophet too will fall with you. LXX: So that no one is either judged, or anyone reproved; but my people are like a priest who will be contradicted, and he will be weakened by day, and the prophet too will be weakened with you. According to the Seventy translators, this which we have set down, so that no one is either judged, or anyone reproved, must be fitted to the preceding section. But let us follow the Hebrews. The sons of Israel who dwelt in the land, having been summoned to the judgment of God to hear the causes of the Lord's indignation and to recognize the past sins for which they were to be handed over to their enemies, now, because they persevere in their crimes and despise God with a shameless brow, hear: There is no need for you to come to judgment, to be reproved for your outrages; for you are of such shamelessness that not even when convicted do you have shame and modesty; but you contradict me, as if, like a disciple against his master, the common rabble, which does not have the dignity of the priesthood, were contradicting the priest.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.5-6":["By night I have made your mother silent.\n\nMy people has fallen silent, because it had no knowledge; because you have rejected knowledge, I too will reject you, so that you may not serve as priest to me; and you have forgotten the law of your God, I too will forget your sons. LXX: I have likened your mother to the night, and my people has been likened to one not having knowledge; because you have rejected knowledge, I too will reject you, so that you may not serve as priest to me; you have forgotten the law of your God, and I too will forget your sons. He calls them mother and sons, not because the mother is one thing and the sons another; but just as the Lord, speaking to the people of the Jews, said: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kill the prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often I wished to gather your sons, as a hen gathered her chicks under her wings, and you would not? Not that Jerusalem was one thing and her people another; for he was not speaking to the rubble of the city and its timbers and stones apart from the people. So the multitude of the people and the whole crowd of the Hebrew nation to which he speaks is called mother; and the sons are either the individuals of the people, or those scattered through the towns and villages. In the night, then, and in the darkness of captivity and of grief and of pressing distress Israel is handed over, and its people falls silent in eternal silence; because it did not have knowledge of the law, nor did it keep the commandments of God, and it received back what it had done. For she herself rejected the law of God, and therefore lost the priesthood forever, worshiping the golden calves in Dan and Bethel; and because she forgot the law of God and gave herself over wholly as a slave to Egyptian idols, therefore the Lord too will forget her sons, handing them over to eternal captivity: For whoever does not know will not be known; and in the psalms we read: They did not know, nor did they understand; they walk in darkness. All the things that are said to the ten tribes we can refer to the heretics, who have left the kingdom of David and Jerusalem, that is, Christ and the Church; and therefore they are covered with eternal night, and do not have the knowledge of God, and are rejected by the Lord, so that they may not serve as priests to him, and he never remembers the sons whom they have begotten, because they have become alien sons to him.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.5":["And because you are such, therefore today you fall, that is, you are led into captivity, and you lose the kingdom of Israel. Now as for his saying: today, it either signifies the present time, or that you will be led into captivity not by fraud and ambush but in broad daylight; and so great will be your weakness that even the prophets, who used to prophesy a lie to you, will fall with you and experience captivity. Here we ought to understand by the prophets either the false prophets, or at least every prophetic grace. For as long as the ten tribes had not been taken captive, they had both the prophet Elijah and Elisha, and the other sons of the prophets, who prophesied in Samaria. Hence also the prophet Amos, who was of the tribe of Judah and of the little village of Tekoa, is compelled to return to his own country, so as not to prophesy in a foreign kingdom and in Samaria.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.7-9":["Concerning the heretics the understanding is easy: that the more numerous they are, the more they sin against God and glory in their peoples; and therefore they deceive the unhappy, so as to eat the sins of the people, and through sweet words to devour the houses of widows. For when they see some people sinning, they say: God seeks nothing else but the truth of faith; if you guard it, he does not care what you do. For by saying these things they lift up their souls in their iniquities, so that they not only do not repent nor are humbled, but rejoice in their crimes and walk with neck held high. Hence both the people and the priest, both those who have been taught and those who taught, will be bound by an equal judgment.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.7":["According to their multitude, so have they sinned against me; I will change their glory into disgrace, and they will eat the sins of my people, and will lift up their souls to their iniquities; and it will be, as the people, so the priest; and I will visit upon him his ways, and I will repay him his thoughts. LXX: According to their multitude, so have they sinned against me; I will turn their glory into disgrace; they will eat the sins of my people, and in their iniquities they will take their souls, and it will be, as the people, so also the priest; and I will avenge upon him his ways, and I will repay him his thoughts. As many men as Israel had, so many altars did it build to demons, in whose victims it sinned against me. Therefore their glory, in which they gloried for themselves and preferred idols to God, I will change into disgrace, so that both priests and peoples are taken captive.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.8":["For indeed the priests eat the sins of my people, the priests of whom it is written: Who devour my people as a meal of bread. And they eat the sins of my people for this reason, that they consent to the crimes of those who offend; for when they have seen them sinning, they not only do not reprove them, but praise and extol them, and proclaim them blessed. Of these Isaiah too speaks: My people, those who call you blessed lead you astray, and trip up the paths of your feet. Of these the psalmist also cries out: For the sinner is praised in the desires of his soul, and the unjust man is blessed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.9":["Hence the people and the priest alike will bear the sentence of an indignant God; because he will visit not only their works, which are called ways, in which they walk, but also the thoughts with which they deliberated in order to do such things. For not only the work, but also the thought of an evil work will pay the penalty.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.10-11":["The ten tribes too played the harlot with the idols of Jeroboam son of Nebat; and they forsook their God, not keeping what he had commanded when he said: You shall worship the Lord your God and serve him alone, but this is to be read ἐμφατικῶς (emphatically): Fornication and wine and drunkenness take away the heart. For just as wine and drunkenness make him who has drunk no longer master of his mind, so fornication and pleasure pervert the sense and weaken the spirit, and make of a rational man a brute animal, so that he pursues eating-houses and brothels and the dens of lusts. And when the heart has thus been moved from its place, it thinks wood and stones are gods and worships the works of its own hands.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.10-12":["The heretics are never satisfied with their error, nor do they cease from the foulness of fornication, and daily, by not keeping the law and the holy Scriptures, they forsake the Lord, they rave and become drunk; and having lost the judgment of the mind, they worship idols which they have fashioned from their own heart, and are possessed by the spirit of fornication.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.10":["And they will eat and will not be satisfied; they have played the harlot and have not ceased, since they have forsaken the Lord in not observing; fornication and wine and drunkenness take away the heart. My people has inquired of its wood, and its staff has declared to it. For the spirit of fornications deceives them, and they have played the harlot away from their God. LXX: And they will eat and will not be filled; they have played the harlot and will not be set straight, because they have forsaken the Lord, so as not to observe; the heart of my people has taken up fornication and wine and drunkenness; they inquired by auguries, and by their rods they made announcement to it; for they have been led astray by a spirit of fornication and have played the harlot away from their God. Pleasure is insatiable, and the more of it is taken, the more hunger it creates in those who use it. On the contrary, blessed are those who hunger and thirst for justice, for they shall be satisfied. As justice satisfies, so iniquity, having no substance, mocks with deceit those who eat empty things, and leaves the bellies of those who devour it empty. They have played the harlot and have not ceased. Their strength fails in fornication, and the desire to fornicate does not rest.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.12":["Hence the prophet too, as if astonished and full of wonder, declares: My people, which was once called by my name, has inquired of wood and of rods; a kind of divination which the Greeks call ῥαβδομαντείαν (divination by rods). Hence we also read in Ezekiel that Nebuchadnezzar mixed his rods against Ammon and Jerusalem, and the rod came out against Jerusalem; and the cause of this madness is the spirit of fornication, which deceived them, so that they played the harlot away from their God. Now fornication signifies idolatry, according to what we read in Jeremiah: And they committed adultery with wood and stone, and I said: After they have played the harlot with all these, return to me; and she did not return with her whole heart, but in falsehood. And again: You have forsaken me and said: I will go upon every high hill, and I will pour myself out in my fornication under every leafy tree. And in the psalm it is said: You have destroyed all who play the harlot away from you. For the beginning of fornication is the invention of idols.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.13-14":["It is a grave displeasure, after you have sinned, not to merit the wrath of the Lord. Israel had played the harlot away from the Lord his God and been led astray by a spirit of fornication; therefore his daughters and brides also play the harlot and are left in their crime without any punishment, so that what the sons and the wife had done to their true parent and husband, they may feel in their own children and wives, and from their own pain understand the pain of God, who is so angry that he does not strike the offenders at all. The Apostle too, writing to the Romans, testifies to this in mystical speech: For, saying that they were wise, they became fools; and they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of the image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed beasts, and of serpents. And because they had played the harlot with idols, they are struck with such a sentence: For this reason God gave them up to the desires of their heart, to uncleanness, so that they dishonor their bodies among themselves, who exchanged the truth of God for a lie; and they worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator, who is blessed for ever. Amen. But that we may know that visitation is brought by God, as upon the wounded and the sick, for this purpose, that as by cautery and most bitter potions they may recover health and be free of diseases, let us hear God speaking through the prophet: I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with stripes; but my mercy I will not scatter from him. He, therefore, who is loved is corrected; he who is neglected is left to his sins. And so great was the number of fornicators in Israel that the vengeance was one that despaired of amendment. For what is more shameful than for men to be joined to the worship of harlots, and with the effeminate to offer the victims of their own lust? But what he adds: the people that does not understand will be beaten; for which the Seventy translated: And the people that understands clung to the harlot; this signifies that Israel is beaten in captivity and afflicted with various blows, so that through pain it may receive discipline. Concerning the heretics the understanding is easy: their daughters and brides play the harlot, that is, the souls which they have begotten in error and betrothed to their own dogmas. Men of this kind are unworthy of God's correction, since all their worship is fornication, and the shameful are mingled with the shameful; and therefore they will be beaten, so that at some time through blows they may understand what God they should seek. When you see a sinner abounding in riches, boasting of his power, rejoicing in health, delighting in his wife, surrounded by a crown of children, and that which is written being fulfilled: They are not in the labor of men, and with men they will not be scourged, say that in him the prophet's threat has been fulfilled: I will not visit upon your daughters when they have played the harlot and upon your brides when they have committed adultery.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.13":["On the tops of the mountains they sacrificed, and on the hills they burned incense; under the oak and the poplar and the terebinth, because its shade was good. LXX: On the summit of the mountains they sacrificed, and on the hills they offered victims; under the oak and the poplar and the shady trees, because the shelter was good. In the law it is commanded that no sacrifice be made to the Lord except in the place which the Lord God shall choose; and that a grove and trees not be planted beside the altar, lest, namely, a promiscuous and pleasure-loving religion overturn the austerity of the one and true religion. On the contrary, Israel sacrificed on the mountains and burned incense on the hills, loving the high places of the earth because it had forsaken God on high, and seeking the shade it had lost the truth. This is what we read of the several kings: Nevertheless he did not take away the high places; the people still offered victims and sacrificed on the high places, which in Hebrew is called bama. The heretics promise themselves loftiness of dogmas, and they sacrifice under the oak and the poplar and the terebinth, unfruitful trees, which have not the fig and the vine under which the saint is said to rest. But at times they take up for themselves the terebinth, which according to Isaiah has no leaves, so that they may seem to imitate the example of Abraham.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.14":["Therefore your daughters will play the harlot, and your brides will be adulteresses.\n\nI will not visit upon your daughters when they have played the harlot, and upon your brides when they have committed adultery; since they themselves consorted with harlots and sacrificed with the effeminate; and the people that does not understand will be beaten. LXX: Therefore your daughters will play the harlot and your brides will commit adultery, and I will not visit upon your daughters when they have played the harlot and upon your brides when they have committed adultery; because they themselves also mingled with harlots and offered victims with the initiated; and the people that understands clung to the harlot. The word cadesoth, which Aquila translated ἐνηλλαγμένων, Symmachus ἑταιρίδων, the Seventy τετελεσμένων, Theodotion κεχωρισμένων, we have rendered 'effeminate', so as to lay open the sense of the word to the ears of our own people. These are they who today at Rome, serving the mother not of the gods but of demons, are called Galli, because the Romans, in honor of Attis — whom the harlot goddess had made a eunuch —, made over men of this nation, cut off from lust, as her priests. And the men of the nation of the Gauls are unmanned for this reason: that those who had captured the city of Rome may be struck with this disgrace. Idolatry of this kind existed in Israel, the women especially worshipping Baal-peor for the size of his obscene member, whom we can call Priapus. Hence also King Asa took away the high places from the people, and priests of this kind, and deposed his mother from her august rule, as Scripture testifies, saying: And Asa did what was right in the sight of the Lord, as David his father, and he took away the effeminate from the land, and he purged all the filth of the idols which his fathers had made. Moreover he also removed Maacah his mother, so that she should not be chief in the rites of Priapus and in his grove which she had consecrated; and he overturned its cave, and broke the most foul image, and burned it in the brook Kidron. But the high places he did not take away; nevertheless the heart of Asa was perfect before God all his days. But it must be known that in the present passage he calls cadesoth the harlots, ἱερείας, that is, priestesses, made over to Priapus. In other places, however, we read cadesim for men cut off from lust, as Isaiah says: And mockers will rule over them, for which in the Hebrew is written: And cadesim will rule over them, which we have rendered 'effeminate'. But Aquila, translating ἐνηλλαγμένους, that is, changed, wished to show this: that they had changed their nature and from men had become women. Symmachus called them ἐταιρίδας, properly harlots. The Seventy, τετελεσμένους, that is, consecrated and initiated, to show that they were worshippers of idols. Theodotion, κεχωρισμένους, that is, separated from the people, men who seemed to themselves to have something more than the common crowd. We have discussed the word briefly; now let us return to the sense of the section.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.15-16":["Concerning the heretics the understanding is ready at hand, to whom, or of whom, it is said: If once you play the harlot, heretic, at least you, churchman, do not sin, nor go into Gilgal, the conventicles of the heretics, where the sins of all are revealed, and like swine they wallow in the mire. Nor think that you are going up to the proud and arrogant fictions of false dogmas. For there is not the house of God, but the house of the idol. Nor swear by the name of Christ, whose majesty you have polluted by mingling it with idols. For like a cow stung by the gadfly, the heretics have been struck by the burning arrows of the devil and have abandoned the knowledge of the law; therefore they will be pastured on the broad and spacious way that leads to death, and the patience of the Lord, the good shepherd, will nourish them for destruction.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.15":["If you play the harlot, Israel, let Judah at least not sin; and do not go into Gilgal; and do not go up to Beth-aven, nor swear: The Lord lives; for like a wanton cow Israel has turned aside. Now the Lord feeds them like a lamb in a broad place. LXX: But you, Israel, do not be ignorant, and Judah, do not go into Gilgal, do not go up to the house of ὢν, and do not swear: The Lord lives; for like a cow stung by the gadfly Israel has gone mad. Now the Lord feeds them like a lamb in a spacious place. For 'house of ὢν', in certain copies, and especially in Theodotion, 'house of iniquity' is read, which Aquila and Symmachus translated 'house of ἀνωφελοῦς', that is, useless, which profits nothing and by another word is called an idol. Now it is Bethel, which formerly was called house of God; after the calves were set up in it, it was called Beth-aven, that is, house of uselessness and house of the idol, which we have expressed as it is read in the Hebrew. But it seems to me that the reason why both the people of Israel in the wilderness made for themselves the head of a calf to worship, and Jeroboam son of Nebat fashioned golden calves, was this: that what they had learned in Egypt, that ἄπιν (Apis) and μνεῦιν (Mnevis), who are worshipped under the figure of oxen, are gods, they might keep in their own superstition. Let us state the sense of the section: If once, O Israel, you have been deceived by error and have mingled yourself with harlots, so that whoever filled the hand, either his own or the king's, by offering and giving him gifts, became a priest of the high places; at least you, Judah, who possess Jerusalem, and have Levites according to the law, and perform the ceremonies of the temple, ought not to follow the examples of your former sister Oholah in fornication, and worship idols along with God. Do not go into Gilgal, of which we read in this same prophet: All their wickedness is in Gilgal; in which Saul was anointed king; in which the people, coming out of the desert, first encamped and were purified by the second circumcision. From that time the error of a contrary religion grew up in a celebrated place. And do not go up to Beth-aven, that is, the place which was once called Bethel, because after golden calves were set up there by Jeroboam son of Nebat, it is called not House of God but house of the idol. For which I wonder why the Seventy translated 'house of ὢν', unless perhaps, by a common error, in place of the letter iod in the middle, which is flanked on either side by the letters aleph and nun, they supposed uau, which differs only in size. Nor swear: The Lord lives. For I do not want mention of my name to be made by your mouth, which is polluted by the remembrance of idols.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.16":["For as a wanton cow, casting off the yoke of the law, so Israel, that is, the ten tribes, has turned aside from the service of the Lord. For 'wanton cow' the Seventy translated παροιστρῶσαν, one stung by the oestrus and the asilus, which is commonly called the tabanus. Of this Vergil also tells in the third book of the Georgics: \"whose Roman name is asilus, the Greeks turned it oestrus, so calling it; fierce, sounding harshly; terrified by it, whole herds scatter through the woods; the air, shaken, rages with bellowing, and the woods and the bank of dry Tanagrus\". Since, therefore, Israel has gone mad and, struck by the spirit of fornication, has raved with incredible fury, therefore not long afterward, but while I prophesy, while breath rules these limbs, the Lord feeds them like a lamb in a broad place. Because he had taken the metaphor from the wanton cow, or from the gadfly, he keeps it in what remains, so that he calls the captivity among the Assyrians, and the dispersion of the people of Israel in the very broad land of the Medes, the pasturing of a flock and of lambs, as in a very broad plain and in a spacious land.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.17-19":["Ephraim is a partaker of idols; let him go; their banquet is set apart; in fornication they have played the harlot; his protectors loved to bring disgrace; the spirit has bound him in its wings; and they will be put to shame by their sacrifices. LXX: Ephraim, a partaker of idols, has set stumbling blocks for himself, he has provoked the Canaanites; playing the harlot they have played the harlot, they have loved disgrace from their roaring; you are a whirlwind of the spirit in its wings, and they will be put to shame by their altars. Ephraim, the tribe from which came Jeroboam son of Nebat, who first set up the golden calves in Bethel and Dan, is king of the ten tribes. O Judah, then, to whom I said above: If Israel plays the harlot, let Judah at least not sin, hear my counsel, do not spurn the words of the prophet: since Ephraim is once for all a friend and partaker of idols, let him go, lest you follow his impiety, whose worship and religion and food are set apart from your banquet. For they serve idols once for all, and sacrifice to demons, and play the harlot daily; and they love their fornication; indeed, its princes and protectors, that is, the kings, loved to bring disgrace upon the people, that is: by the vice of the princes the unhappy people took up the worship of idols, whose unclean spirit has bound Israel in its wings and does not allow him to fly about freely. They will therefore be put to shame in their sacrifices, and in their confusion they will take on the disgrace of the princes. Symmachus, for what we have translated: the spirit has bound him in its wings, rendered it into Greek in this way: as if someone were to bind the wind in the wings of the wind; that is, so as to assert that both the princes and the people, or rather both the demons and Israel, are joined together, the vain with the vain and the empty with the empty. For wind and spirit among the Hebrews are called by one word, rua.\n— Commentary on Hosea","This same thing can also be referred to the heretics, and it is said to Judah, that is, to the churchman: since Ephraim, which is interpreted καρποφόρος (fruit-bearing), promises himself a false richness of doctrine and fruitful dogmas, and is once for all a friend of demons, let him go and despise him, especially since their sacrifices are set apart from your sacrifices. For this is what he says: their banquet is set apart; they have played the harlot once for all, and their princes have deceived the unhappy people; and in place of the worship of God they have steeped them in the disgrace of idols, and the spirit of the devil has bound them in its wings, they who are carried about by every wind of doctrine and cannot remain with a steady foot in the Church. They truly will be put to shame in their sacrifices, because their bread is the bread of mourning. And what is said, he has provoked the Canaanites, can in the same sense be referred to the heretics as well: that most of the heretics have invented things so foul and unspeakable, and have such unclean sacrifices, that idolatry is inferior to them. Or at least, because Canaanite is interpreted merchant or μετάβολος, that is, one who transfers, all who make the house of the Father a house of trade, and seek gains from the peoples, and transfer the truth of the Church into a lie, are to be called Canaanites.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.4.17":["That which the Seventy set down: he has provoked the Canaanites, is not found in the Hebrew. Yet it can be interpreted thus, so that we say that Israel had such zeal in the worship of idols that he did not imitate the Canaanites, that is, the heathen, but provoked them to imitate his own error.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.1-2":["According to tropology, the people is accused so that it may not think itself free of the crime if it was led astray by kings and priests, whom we understand as the princes of the heretics. The princes themselves are also accused, because like a snare they caught the people and, though set as watchmen in the Church, led them into error. And on Mount Tabor, lofty and most beautiful, which is interpreted coming light, they laid ambushes, so as to drag all whom they had deceived down into the depth and sacrifice them to demons, and turn aside the victims into the abyss, lest they should ever come to their senses, lest they should look up to the Church, the house of God. Some think that Tabor is interpreted pit, that is, cistern; and that it fits the present sense that the heretics have dug a pit and fallen into the ditch which they made.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.1":["Hear this, O priests, and attend, house of Israel, and house of the king; listen with your ears, for the judgment is for you; since you have become a snare to the watchtower and a net spread over Tabor, and you have turned aside the victims into the depth. LXX: Hear this, O priests, and attend, house of Israel, and house of the king; perceive with your ears, for the judgment is against you, because you have become a snare to the watchtower; and like a net spread over Itabyrium, which those who take the prey have fixed fast for the hunt. The priests of the ten tribes are summoned to judgment, and the kings: not that the priests are of the tribe of Levi, but that they are called priests by the people. Hence too the priests of Baal, the four hundred and fifty prophets whom Elijah, and afterwards Jehu son of Nimshi in the presence of Jonadab son of Rechab, killed, were called priests; but Israel too, that is, the people, is summoned to judgment; and no one is excepted, so that priests and people and kings, who had led the people astray and appointed the priests, may hear together what they have done and why they are handed over to their enemies. I set you, he says, as watchmen and princes among the people, and placed you on the lofty summit of dignity, so that you might guide the wandering people. But you have become a snare, and are to be called not so much watchmen and princes as hunters. For you spread a net over Mount Tabor, which the Seventy rendered Ἰταβύριον (Itabyrium), having this custom, that they inflect Hebrew names in the Greek tongue; just as Edom, that is, Esau, and Seir they always translate Idumaea. Now Tabor is a mountain in Galilee, situated in the plains, round and lofty, and it is bounded evenly on every side. Of this mountain we read in the psalm too: Tabor and Hermon will exult in your name. This mountain the Seventy also rendered Itabyrium in Jeremiah; on it birds are commonly caught with snares.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.2":["And the victims, he says, you have turned aside into the depth, so that no one should repent, nor the slain lift up his head. Both the kings and the priests prevented the people from going to the temple at Jerusalem; therefore they are said to have turned aside the victims into the depth.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And I am the instructor of them all.\n\nI know Ephraim, and Israel is not hidden from me, for now Ephraim has played the harlot, Israel is defiled; they will not give their thoughts to return to their God, for the spirit of fornications is in their midst, and they have not known the Lord. LXX: For I am your teacher. I have known Ephraim, and Israel has not departed from me, for now Ephraim has played the harlot, Israel is stained; they have not given their thoughts to return to the Lord their God, for the spirit of fornication is in them. But the Lord they have not known. O priests and kings, who have deceived my people; and you, O people, who have been deceived: you have sinned so grievously that you have not only slain the victims, but have sent them into the depth of hell. Do not think that by the greatness of your crime you have departed wholly from me. I am your teacher, or rather your instructor, who desire to amend, not to punish; and to save, not to destroy.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.3-4":["Hence the Lord is not ignorant of the princes of perverse dogmas, nor of the people; not that they are worthy of his knowledge, but that nothing escapes him of what they do in secret, of whom some have deceived and others have been deceived. Nor will they give their thoughts to repent, they who are always advancing in perdition. For the spirit of fornication with which they played the harlot in the Church and departed from the true marriage moves in their midst; therefore they have not known the Lord.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.3":["I, he says, know Ephraim, that is, Jeroboam, by whom the people was seduced, and all the kings who followed him both in rank and in crime; and Israel is not hidden from me, that is, the people of the ten tribes, because when the king played the harlot, Israel was defiled. The king first began to play the harlot away from the worship of God, desiring to adore golden calves, and the people willingly followed, and they accomplished an equal impiety with equal zeal.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.4":["Finally, they will not return to the Lord, because what they wanted they have found, and the spirit of fornication, which according to the Apostle works in the sons of unbelief, possesses their captive hearts; therefore, while he reigned, they did not know the Lord, or rather they forgot their Creator.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.5":["And the arrogance of Israel will answer in his face; and Israel and Ephraim will fall in their iniquity, Judah also will fall with them. LXX: And the injury of Israel will be humbled into his face; and Israel and Ephraim will be weakened in their iniquities, Judah also will be weakened with them. The word gaon the Seventy and Symmachus translate as injury, Aquila and Theodotion as pride. Whatever, therefore, Israel has done, whether acting proudly against the Lord or venerating idols to the injury of the Creator, will answer in his face, so that he may not go away unpunished, but that his insolence may be humbled; and that the people and the kings may fall together in their iniquity, or be weakened, so that those who were strong in crime may be compelled, now weak, to return to the Lord. And this will befall not only Israel and Ephraim, that is, the ten tribes and their kings, so that they are led into captivity; but Judah also, that is, the two tribes which reigned in Jerusalem, will follow the footsteps of the captives, so that those whose crimes they imitate they may imitate also in punishment. The heretics have pride as the mother of their iniquity, while they constantly boast that they know higher things and rave in insult against the Church. But their arrogance will be weakened, and the people and the teachers will fall together; Judah also, who seems to be in the house of God, and dwells in the Church not in mind but in body, and holds in error the same opinion as the heretics, in vain lays claim to the name of churchman, because he too must be punished with the heretics. We run quickly over what is plain, so that we may linger over the more obscure.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.6-7":["The heretics suppose that they appease God by a multitude of sacrifices; and churchmen, that by alms they redeem the sins in which they persist; whereas every sacrifice blots out past crimes, not present ones; therefore they do not find the Lord, who is taken away from such men and withdraws far off. These, indeed, have transgressed against God, and have begotten strange sons not to Christ but to the devil; therefore at all times their works are accursed, and all that they do the rust of bloody color lays waste, because they are next to blood and slaughter. Now rust is properly said to come down in the night dew and to stain the grain, still milky in the ear, with the color of vermilion or of red ochre; and fittingly, according to the ἀναγωγήν (anagogy), it lays waste the lots of the heretics, of whom it is said: Their lots will not profit them.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.6":["With their flocks and with their herds they will go to seek the Lord and will not find; he has been taken away from them; they have transgressed against the Lord, for they have begotten strange sons; now a month will devour them with their portions. LXX: With sheep and calves they will go to seek the Lord and will not find him; he has turned aside from them, because they have forsaken the Lord; because they have begotten strange sons for themselves, now rust will devour them, and their lots. Not only Israel and Ephraim will go with flocks and herds to seek the Lord, but Judah also, of whom it is written above: Judah also will fall with them; all of them having this one contest, that they strive to appease with victims him whom they have offended by their transgressions; and they do not find him whom they seek, because he too has departed from those who depart from him. Especially since he says elsewhere also: Shall I eat the flesh of bulls, or drink the blood of goats? And again: I will not accept calves from your house, nor goats from your flocks. And in Isaiah: Burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of lambs, and the blood of goats and bulls I do not want.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.7":["For they have transgressed against the Lord, because, playing the harlot with idols, they have begotten sons not to God but to demons. Others think that what is meant here is what is said in Ezra, a long time afterwards, when they took foreign wives, begetting children from them, and afterwards were compelled to put them away. But here it is better to take as strange sons those who were begotten in the error of idols, or those whom they led through the fire and consecrated to idols. Since, therefore, they have done this, not long afterwards, not after endless spans of years, nor, as I was formerly accustomed, foretelling things to come long after, but now and at this present time the Assyrian and the Chaldean will come; and they will devour them with their portions, or lots, that is, with the possessions which they received in the division of the land by the measure of the line. Where we said: a month will devour them, the Seventy translated rust; whereas rust, that is, ἐρυσίβη, is certainly called hasil, as they themselves also said in the prophet Joel: What the locust left, the rust has eaten, that is, hasil. But month is called hodes; accordingly Aquila translated new moons, that is, Kalends; Symmachus and Theodotion month; and the sense is: month by month the enemy will come and will lay everything waste. Let us read the books of Kings and Chronicles, and we shall find that under King Pekah, who reigned over the ten tribes, Tiglath-pileser king of the Assyrians came and carried a great part of the people of Samaria away to the Assyrians; at which time among the Greeks it was the second year of the first Olympiad; and among the Latins — Rome not yet founded — Amulius was reigning at Alba in his twentieth year, he whom Romulus afterwards drove from the kingdom.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.8-9":["Some, following the Seventy, who said house of ὢν, have interpreted Beth-aven as the city of the sun; and, calling Christ the sun of justice, they would have his city be the Church. This displeases me; for the house of the idol, which Aquila translated useless house, cannot be referred to the Church even according to tropology. But let us say this: that the heretics, who in Gibeah and Ramah promise themselves lofty knowledge, are bidden to howl in the house of the idol, and are not before the face of Benjamin, which is interpreted son of the right hand, but behind his back, where he has no eyes. For all the boasting of Ephraim, which is interpreted fruitfulness, will shortly be in desolation; and when the day of judgment, he says, and the time of rebuke has come, I will show by deeds that my words are not empty.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.8":["Blow the horn in Gibeah, the trumpet in Ramah; howl in Beth-aven, behind your back, Benjamin; Ephraim will be in desolation in the day of rebuke; among the tribes of Israel I have shown faithfulness. LXX: Blow the trumpet upon the hills, sound upon the high places, proclaim in the house of ὤν; Benjamin has fallen out of his mind, Ephraim has become a desolation in the days of rebuke; among the tribes of Israel I have shown faithful things. The months one by one, or the rust, will devour Ephraim and Israel and Judah with their portions. Therefore I command you who hear to sound not with a loud voice but with a clear horn; for clear hearing is needed, so that all who are round about may hear. Blow the horn in Gibeah, whose etymology the Seventy translated as hills; and the trumpet in Ramah, which is interpreted high places; now these are two cities in the tribe of Benjamin, neighbors to one another, that is, Gibeah, in which Saul was born, and Ramah, which is next to Gibeah, situated at the seventh milestone from Jerusalem, and which the king of Israel tried to seize, in order to shut off the going out and the coming in of the tribe of Judah. In Gibeah, therefore, and in Ramah, sound out with a clear horn and trumpet; of these the horn is a shepherd's instrument and is made of curved horn; hence it is properly called in Hebrew sophar, in Greek κερατίνη. The trumpet, however, is made of bronze or of silver, and sounds in wars and at solemn festivals. Over Beth-aven, which was once called Bethel, and is in the tribe of Ephraim, where the golden calf was, there is need not of blaring and sounding but of howling, because captivity is near. And he said beautifully that Beth-aven is behind the back of Benjamin; for where the tribe of Benjamin ends, not far off, in the tribe of Ephraim, this city was founded.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Where we translated behind your back, Benjamin, the Seventy rendered Benjamin has been moved in mind, always fitting ἔκστασιν, that is, ecstasy of mind, to Benjamin, the churchman. Hence also in the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm it is said: There is Benjamin, a youth, in ecstasy of mind. And in the blessings of Jacob, under the person of Benjamin, of which tribe the apostle Paul was, we read it written: Benjamin is a ravening wolf; in the morning he will eat the prey, and at evening he will give food. For he who in the beginning persecuted the Church afterwards bestowed the nourishment of the Gospel on believers throughout the whole world. Hence also Saul, who was of the tribe of Benjamin, prophesied in ecstasy of mind all day until evening among the band of the prophets.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.9":["For this reason, then, I say and command: Blow the horn in Gibeah, and the trumpet in Ramah, and howl in Beth-aven, because the royal house of Ephraim, or Ephraim himself, that is, the dominion of Israel, will now be in desolation, and captivity is close at hand. In the day of the rebuke and punishment of the ten tribes of Israel I have shown my words to be faithful, the words I threatened through the prophets, so that what I had foretold in word I might prove in deed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.10":["The princes of Judah have become like those who take up the boundary; upon them I will pour out my wrath like water. LXX: The princes of Judah have become like those who move boundaries; upon them I will pour out my onrush like water. When Ephraim and Israel had been led into captivity and their land reduced to a wilderness, the princes of Judah, who ought to have wept, and to have exhorted their own people to withdraw from idolatry lest they too should suffer the like, began to rejoice and be glad that the land of those tribes lay open to them to possess more widely; and they became like those who take up the boundaries of Israel, passing beyond their own borders and desiring to extend their kingdom and possession into the places that once were Israel's. Therefore the Lord says: Upon these very princes of Judah too the Babylonians will come, and will thus overwhelm them like an onrush of water, not by their own strength but by my indignation. He says also to the princes of Judah, that is, of the Church, that they ought not to exult and to reckon the perdition of the heretics their own salvation, but rather to lament that those have perished. Hence also the apostle Paul teaches churchmen not to glory over the breaking of the Jewish branches, but rather to fear lest they too be broken. And in another place: Who, he says, is made to stumble, and I do not burn? Otherwise, upon such princes, who glory in the miseries of others and think they stand precisely because others fall, the Lord will pour out his wrath like water. For wrath the Seventy translated ὅρμημα, that is, onrush; and some think it is to be taken as a blessing, according to what we read elsewhere: The onrush of the river makes glad the city of God. But it is more correctly taken as wrath — as all except the Seventy have translated —, especially since the word pouring out goes with wrath, as the prophet says to the Lord: Pour out your wrath upon them, and let the fury of your wrath take hold of them. The princes of Judah move the boundaries which their fathers set when they exchange the truth for a lie and preach something other than what they received from the apostles.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.11":["Ephraim suffering wrong, broken in judgment, because he began to go after filth. LXX: Ephraim has oppressed his adversary, has trampled on judgment, because he began to go after vain things. If Ephraim is impious, and on account of his impiety will be in desolation, how is it now said: Ephraim suffering wrong, broken in judgment? For he who suffers wrong and is broken has his judgment unjustly overborne, all the more since a just cause is set down why he was handed over to captivity. For there follows: because he began to go after filth, that is, after idols, which are compared to filth. What he says, then, is this: Ephraim is oppressed by the Assyrians, first by Pul, then by Tiglath-pileser, afterwards by Shalmaneser; not that those who oppressed him were just, and that he was therefore handed over to them, but that those who were once my people, since I abandon them, are handed over to punishment; and in this there seems to be wrong, not in the eyes of God, who passes a just sentence, but of those who endure the torments, while they are yielded up to adversaries worse than themselves. And the Lord gives the reason why in this respect they endure wrong, and are handed over to worse men, and justice is not kept toward them: because Ephraim, that is, Jeroboam, began to go after idols and to forsake God; for it was he who fashioned the golden calves. According to the translation of the Seventy, Ephraim, that is, Jeroboam, oppressed his adversary Rehoboam, that is, the tribe of Judah, and oppressed him in this, that, having deserted Jerusalem and the temple, he followed the images of Egyptian filth. Concerning the heretics the sense is plain: that by their false sophisms and by dialectical art they often oppress churchmen; but when they have done this, they follow not the cleanness of the true faith but the filth of falsehood; and, handed over to the devil and his angels, they seem to themselves to suffer wrong, and that the truth of judgment has been broken in their case. In another way: Israel is laid waste, and all his labors are plundered when the Assyrian comes, and he loses God as his protector; and he is broken in judgment for this reason, that he followed idols.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.12":["And I am like a moth to Ephraim, and like rottenness to the house of Judah. LXX: And I am like a disturbance to Ephraim, and like a goad to the house of Judah. In the Hebrew language a moth is called As; recob is rottenness and decay; of which the one consumes garments, the other wood; for which the LXX translated ταραχὴν, that is, tumult or disturbance, and goad. And it must be noted that the moth is referred to Ephraim, and rottenness to the house of Judah. Just as, therefore, the moth consumes garments, and rottenness or decay consumes wood (both of which happen over a long time), so God, giving the ten tribes, and afterwards the two, room for repentance over a long time, and calling them to salvation, while they treasure up for themselves wrath in the day of wrath, becomes as it were a moth and rottenness; not that God is a moth or rottenness, or a disturbance or a goad, but that to those who suffer punishment all these things seem so. First, therefore, Ephraim and Israel were consumed, then the house of Judah also followed; not Judah himself, but his house; otherwise there is reserved from the race of Judah the one for whom it was laid up. And he was the expectation of the nations. But when the heretics, who are understood as Ephraim, have been punished, Judah too, that is, those who remain with the Church and are held fast by either the errors or the vices of the heretics, will be subject to a like sentence.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.13":["And Ephraim saw his sickness, and Judah his bond; and Ephraim went to Assyria and sent to the avenging king; and he will not be able to heal you, nor will he be able to loose the bond from you. LXX: And Ephraim saw his infirmity, and Judah his pain, and Ephraim went to the Assyrians, and sent envoys to King Jareb; and he will not be able to free you, nor will he make the pain cease from you. \"Envoys\" is not found in the Hebrew. And where the Seventy put Jareb, we, following Symmachus, have translated \"avenger\"; for Aquila and Theodotion rendered \"judge\". That Jareb signifies avenger and judge, the name of Gideon shows: when the worshipers of Baal demanded him for punishment, because he had overthrown the grove and the altars of Baal, his father answered: Let Baal avenge himself, or let Baal judge him; and he was called, it says, Jerubbaal, that is: let Baal avenge himself. Ephraim, therefore, perceiving his infirmity, and Judah his bond, because he had been bound together with the ten tribes in sin, sought help not from God, who was able to loose them, but from the king of the Assyrians. We read that under King Menahem, who ruled over the ten tribes, Israel sent gifts to the Assyrians; and that Judah, under King Ahaz, begged the protection of Tiglath-pileser, king of the Assyrians, who, since God opposed them, were not able to free them, nor to loose the bond of captivity. We can also refer the bond, for which in the Hebrew is written mezur, and which Aquila rendered ἐπίδεσιν or συνδεσμὸν, that is, a binding together or a conspiracy, to that time when Rezin and Pekah son of Remaliah laid waste many thousands of men of the tribe of Judah, in that Judah in vain begged against the two kings the help not of God but of the Assyrians. Some, according to tropology, refer Ephraim and Judah to the heretics and to churchmen; because both they and Judah, bound together by the bonds of sins, according to what is written: Each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins, sent to Assyria and to the avenging king, that is, to the devil, of whom we read: That you may destroy the enemy and the avenger. And because they did not entreat the true helper or judge, therefore he made them remain in the pain of sickness and in the bonds of their offenses. I have read in someone's commentaries that King Jareb is interpreted as Christ. And because there follows: He will not be able to heal you, he argued from this that Christ cannot heal the heretics or the sinners among churchmen in the time of judgment, where there is no mercy, according to what is written: But in hell who will confess to you? And that he cannot heal or free them is by no means through his own weakness, but through the desert of those who have asked for help too late. Just as the Lord too is said not to have been able to do signs in his own country; and the cause why he was not able, he explains: Because, it says, they did not believe in him. Let him have said these things; let us interpret the avenging king in a bad sense. In place of what we have expounded as Jareb, that is, avenger, others wrongly read Iarim, with the letter mem, which is translated \"forests\"; whence Kiriath-jearim is interpreted \"village of forests\".\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.14-15":["To the heretics too, and to a negligent Church, God is turned into a panther and a lion; and he will take from them the prey which they formerly snatched from the Church, so that those who had perished while free may be saved by being captured; and he will by no means dwell in the conventicles of the perverse, but will return to his place, of which he says: I am in the Father and the Father in me, and he will spurn and despise them, until they fail in their impiety and seek through repentance him by whom they had been abandoned. Others think that the place of God is heaven, to which God, offended by those who dwell on earth, returns, and will make them perish who, by the greatness of their sins, have turned the most merciful Lord into the rage of wild beasts against themselves.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.14":["For I am like a lioness to Ephraim, and like a lion's whelp to the house of Judah. I, I will seize and go; I will carry off, and there is none who may rescue. Going, I will return to my place, until you fail and seek my face. LXX: For I am like a panther to Ephraim, and like a lion in the house of Judah. And I will snatch, and go, and carry off, and there will be none who may rescue; I will go and return to my place, until they perish and seek my face. While they go to Assyria and send to the vindicating or avenging king, who will not be able to heal them nor to loose the bond that binds them, I will show that, when I oppose, all the help of men is vain; for I will be like a lioness to Ephraim, and like a lion's whelp to the house of Judah. For lioness, which in Hebrew is called sohel, the Seventy rendered panther, which is said alike in Greek and in Latin, and can be taken both as the name of a beast and as \"every beast\"; so that whatever is savage in beasts you may recognize in the indignation of God. Nothing is swifter than the panther, nothing stronger than the lion; in the panther is signified the swift destruction of the kingdom of Samaria by the Assyrians; and in the lion is shown the most mighty kingdom of the Chaldeans, which some time afterwards came against Jerusalem and Judah. And because he had said that he was a lioness or a panther, and a lion, he keeps the metaphor, and says: I, I will seize and go; I will carry off, and there is none who may rescue. It is asked: if, when God seizes, carries off and holds, no one can snatch from his hands, according to what is written: No one can snatch out of the hand of my Father, how was Judas snatched from the hand of God by his betrayal? To which we will briefly answer that no one can snatch from the hand of God; but that he who is held can, by his own will, fall out of the hand of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.5.15":["And as for what follows: Going, I will return to my place, by the place of God we ought to understand his magnificence and majesty; so that he no longer, by way of dispensation, descends to men, is angry, has mercy, forgets, becomes like a panther, is turned into a lion, is changed into beasts; but disregards human affairs and allows those whom he formerly protected to be subjected to their enemies, so that they may waste away, and fail, and perish, and afterwards seek the face of the Lord, and say: Make your face shine, and we shall be saved. And: Show us, O Lord, your mercy, and grant us your salvation.\n— Commentary on Hosea","In their tribulation they will rise early to me: come, and let us return to the Lord, for he has seized and he will heal us; he will strike and he will cure us; he will make us live after two days; on the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight; we shall know and we shall follow, that we may know the Lord. LXX: In their tribulation they will watch early for me, saying: let us go and return to the Lord our God, for he will scourge and he will heal us; he will strike and he will cure us; he will make us whole after two days; on the third we shall rise again and we shall live before him and we shall know; let us pursue, that we may know the Lord. Therefore God has handed Ephraim and Judah over to captivity, and there is none who can snatch from his hand, and he will return to his place until they fail; and they will seek his face, so that him whom they had not felt as gracious and present, they may seek again as angry and absent; and in their tribulation, when the light of repentance has risen for them, they may rise early to him, according to what we read in Isaiah: In tribulation I remembered the Lord. And in the first of the psalms of ascents: To the Lord I cried when I was in tribulation, and he heard me.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.1":["And when they have risen early to the Lord, what will they say? Come, and let us return to the Lord. They are not content with their own salvation, but urge one another on to return to the Lord whom they had left, whom they had deserted because of their sins, by whom they had been abandoned. For he has seized and he will heal us; he who had said above: I, I will seize and go, will strike and heal us. For what we have said, \"he will cure\", all alike translated μοτώσει (will dress with lint); now properly μότα is the name for the little linen pads that are inserted into wounds, to eat away the putrid flesh and draw out the pus; and it belongs to the art of physicians to heal great wounds over a long time and to restore health through pain.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.2-3":["The Lord therefore strikes and cures us; for whom the Lord loves he rebukes, and he chastises every son whom he receives; not only does he cure, but he makes alive after two days, and on the third day, rising again from hell, he raises up with himself the whole race of men. And when he has cured those who were struck, and made alive those who were cured, and raised up those who were made alive, then we shall live in his sight, we who, while he was absent, lay dead. And we shall live in his sight; we shall know him and follow him with all zeal, that we may know the Lord, by whose rising on the third day we have risen. By these words is explained what we have often already pointed out, that both Israel and Judah, that is, the ten and the two tribes, will then have one shepherd, and David as king, when they have believed in the Lord who rises again; and that the Jews vainly promise themselves dreams of a thousand years, since the salvation of all has been promised on the third day, on which the Lord rose again from hell. The Hebrews interpret the second day as the coming of their Christ, and the third day as the judgment, when they are to be saved. Granting this, let them answer us what the first day is, that is, the first coming of the Savior. And when they cannot answer, let us conclude that the first day is, according to what they themselves would have, the coming of the Savior in humility, the second in glory, the third in the guise of one who judges. But those who accept the second and the third testify that they have lost the first, because a second and a third cannot be so called without a first.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.3":["His going forth is prepared like the dawn, and he will come to us like the early rain, and the late rain to the earth. LXX: We shall find him prepared like the morning, and he will come to us like the early and the late rain to the earth. Come, he says, and let us return to the Lord, for he who has seized also will heal us, will strike and cure us, and will make us live after two days, and on the third day will raise us up; he himself and no other is prepared for us like the morning and the dawn. Of whom too is the title of the twenty-first (22nd) psalm: For the morning taking up; although in the Hebrew it is written: For the morning hart, because, having slain death and the twisting and ancient serpent, he longs to climb to the mountains; and he himself, the darkness scattered, rises for us as the sun of justice, to illuminate our blindness. And beautifully he says: his going forth is prepared. Of which, according to tropology, we read in the eighteenth (19th) psalm: And he, like a bridegroom, goes forth from his chamber. He is not only called morning, and dawn, and daybreak; but he will come to us like the early rain, and the late rain to the earth. We receive Christ as the early rain when the foundations of faith have been laid in us, and we shall receive him as the late rain when, the crops being ripe, we shall gather eternal fruits and be stored away in the Lord's granaries. Therefore the Jews, who did not receive the early rains and cast their seed without rains, will not receive the fruit of the crops in the last time. This is the rain of which the Lord makes a promise, saying: I will give you the early and the late rain. According to allegory, the early rain is given when we know in part; the late rain is given when that which is perfect comes. And the Lord is always prepared for those who rise early, who can say: I will rise at daybreak. And: O God, my God, to you I watch from the light. My soul has thirsted for you.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.4-5":["We perceive these same things to have been said also to the heretics, and to the true Judah, who is to suffer the like: that the Lord calls them to mercy and desires that they return to salvation. But they pursue the delights and refreshments of this age, like a cloud and dew that quickly passes away; to whom it is said in the Gospel: Fool, this night they require your soul of you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be? And that rich man clothed in purple, who despised Lazarus lying before his gates, learned that all he had enjoyed had passed away like a cloud and dew. But God always slays the prophets of the heretics, when he threatens them with eternal punishments, and takes them away from the true life, and leaves them to the death of their crimes. But let us love that cloud which is everlasting, and which protects us from the heat of this world, sitting upon which the Lord came into Egypt and broke all the images of the Egyptians. Let us love that dew of which Moses speaks: Let my words come down like the dew. And of which Isaiah says: The dead will rise again, and those who are in the tombs will be raised up; for the dew that is from you is their health. There are some who think that the prophets who were slain are holy men, because on account of a sinful people even prophets were slain and handed over to their enemies.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.4":["What shall I do to you, Ephraim? What shall I do to you, Judah? Your mercy is like a morning cloud, and like the dew passing away in the morning; for this reason I have hewn them by the prophets, I have slain them by the words of my mouth, and your judgments will go forth like the light. LXX: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? What shall I do to you, Judah? But your mercy is like a morning cloud, and like the dew passing away in the morning; therefore I have cut down your prophets, I have slain them by the words of my mouth, and your judgment will go forth like the light. When he says: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? What shall I do to you, Judah? he shows the affection of a parent toward lost sons. According to what we read in Isaiah: What is there that I ought to have done further for my vineyard, and have not done for it? And in Micah: My people, what have I done to you, and in what have I been troublesome to you? Answer me, for I brought you out of the land of Egypt, I freed you from the house of servants, and I sent before your face Moses, and Aaron, and Miriam. What then shall I do to you, Ephraim, what shall I do to you, Judah? Your mercy, with which I have always had mercy on you, has passed away like a morning cloud, and like the dew rising in the morning, which is dried up when the sun has risen.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.5":["For already captivity is near, already I see you led in chains to the Assyrians and the Babylonians; I have hewn you by the prophets and threatened you with terrible words, I have brought out the scalpel, fire and cautery; so that you who despised me when I was merciful might fear me when offended; and I have slain the negligent by the words of my mouth, so that I might punish sinners by the terror of words before captivity was at hand. And all these things I have done so that the truth of the judgment with which I am about to judge you might appear, and no one might doubt that you have justly suffered what you suffer. For what is I have hewn them by the prophets, the Seventy translated: I have cut down your prophets, understanding the false prophets themselves to have been slain by the Lord; so that those who had been the cause of error, by promising prosperity, might, when slain, be turned into an occasion of salvation. And the sense is: Lest you should say: We believed the prophets; I have slain even them, so that every occasion of sinning might be taken away from you. We read in the book of Kings that three hundred prophets of the Baalim were slain under Elijah, and another countless multitude under Jehu, who overthrew the house of Ahab.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.6-7":["Daily God calls to repentance both those who are outside the Church and those who sin while dwelling in the Church, and says to them: I desire mercy and not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings. But they offer sacrilegious bread, and give alms, and appear to pursue humility; these, then, if they are truly done, I interpret as burnt offerings. But since they have abandoned the knowledge of God, in vain, with the head of faith cut off, do they boast that they have the other members; for they have transgressed the covenant of God in the Church, just as Adam transgressed in paradise; and they show themselves imitators of their ancient parent, so that, as he was cast out of paradise, so these too may be cast out of the Church.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.6":["For I desired mercy and not sacrifice, and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings; but they, like Adam, have transgressed the covenant; there they have dealt treacherously against me. LXX: For I desire mercy and not sacrifice, the knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings; but they are like a man passing over the covenant. What follows in the Seventy: There Gilead despised me, a city that works vain things, and the rest, must be fitted to the following section; let us discuss what we have set down: I have hewn them by the prophets, I have slain them by the words of my mouth, I have threatened grievous things, so that I might have mercy on the penitent, so that I might stretch out my hand to those who have fallen and are rising. For I do not delight in sacrifices and victims, and in a multitude of burnt offerings. My victims and burnt offerings are the salvation of those who believe, the conversion of sinners.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.7":["But they have imitated Adam, so that what he had done in paradise, passing over my covenant and law, these did on earth. And there, that is, in paradise, all have dealt treacherously against me, in the likeness of the transgression of Adam. For it is no wonder if what went before in the parent is condemned in the children also.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.8-9":["Gilead, a city of those who work an idol, supplanted by blood, and like the jaws of robber men (or: of a robber; for it can be read either way). LXX: There Gilead despised me, a city that works vain things, and troubles the water, and your strength is that of a pirate man. We read that in Ramoth-gilead Jehu was anointed king, he who mingled blood with blood, and overthrew the house of Ahab, and before sunrise ordered heaps of the heads of his sons to be set up; in this city across the Jordan, in the possession of the tribe of Gad, an idol was consecrated; it was inhabited by priests, for it too was a city of fugitives. Therefore, the more celebrated it was and the greater its authority, because it had been assigned as the portion of the priests, so much the more was it, for Israel dwelling across the Jordan, the beginning of idolatry and of all evils, so that those who had sinned first were the first to be taken captive by the Assyrians. And since that province itself is full of robberies, he compares them to robbers: as those lie in wait for travelers, so the priests lay in wait for the simplicity of the people. Moreover, according to tropology, Gilead is interpreted 'migration of testimony'; and it despises God when it twists the testimonies of the Scriptures into perverse dogmas, and all its works are vain, and it troubles the waters of the Church, and out of the purest springs makes muddy and filthy streams, which stain rather than cleanse the baptized. And all the strength of this city is like that of a pirate man, while it imitates the devil, who in the sea of this world, through which ships pass, lies in wait for those who strive to reach the harbor. Finally, Symmachus translated more plainly, saying: And your jaws like those of a man lying in ambush. Of pirates of this kind we read in Job: There is no delay for pirates. For although in the present world they seem to vaunt themselves, and to trouble the waters, and to work vain things, and to practice piracy, yet there is no delay of the punishments, which will swiftly overtake them.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.9":["A partner of the priests who kill on the way those going from Shechem, because they have wrought wickedness. LXX: The priests hid the way of the Lord, they killed Shechem, because they wrought iniquity. Symmachus translated this passage thus: The fellowship of the priests on the way were killing Shechem. Theodotion in this way: The priests hid the way, they were killing on the back. Aquila: The partnership of the priests on the way were slaying shoulders; and when we sought their meaning according to the history from a Hebrew, it was explained to us thus: The priests of Bethel, or rather the fanatics of Beth-aven, at the times of Passover and Pentecost and Tabernacles, when one had to go through Shechem, which today is called Neapolis, to Jerusalem, where alone it was lawful to sacrifice victims, used to post robbers along the road to lie in wait for those going, so that they might worship the golden calves in Dan and in Beth-aven rather than God in Jerusalem and in the temple. But when he says: The partnership and fellowship of the priests, it signifies their conspiracy and their agreement in the bad sense. But if we read, as we have translated: partners of the priests, it must be referred, he says, to Gilead, which works an idol; and it is supplanted by blood, because it followed the impiety of the priests and is given over to robberies and blood. This is what they may say; let us say that the heretics shut off the way, so that we may not go from Shechem, that is, from good works, to Jerusalem, that is, into the Church. These are like the jaws of robber men, and they kill those who desire to go along the way of this world to the truth. Shechem is interpreted ὦμοι, that is, shoulders; in shoulders we understand work, and all the false priests hide the way, and kill men with evil works, so that they may not reach Jerusalem. And that the shoulder signifies work, this shows: Put your heart on your shoulder, that is, turn what you understand into works. And of Issachar we read that he put his shoulder under to labor, and is a farmer.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.10-11":["But in the house of the heretics we see a horrible thing daily, the teachers first playing the harlot, and the people who are led on by them being defiled. Judah too, that is, the Church, is commanded that it too should prepare for itself, because of its sins, a harvest, or a vintage, when the time of judgment comes. But it is pardoned, and the Lord promises it forgiveness, because whom he loves he rebukes, and he chastises every son whom he receives, so that, tested and purged, he may store him in his treasuries. Some refer what is said: Judah, begin your vintage, or, set a harvest for yourself, to the good sense, so that, Israel having been punished, this one receives the fruits of its works, according to what is written: Those who sow in tears will reap in joy. To us the former sense is more pleasing.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.10":["In the house of Israel I have seen a horrible thing; there Israel has been defiled by the fornication of Ephraim; but you also, Judah, set a harvest for yourself, when I shall turn back the captivity of my people. LXX: In the house of Israel I have seen horrible things, there the fornication of Ephraim; Israel and Judah have been defiled; begin your vintage when I shall turn back the captivity of my people. Of this crime and terrible horror Jeremiah too speaks: The heaven was astounded at this, and the earth shuddered exceedingly. For what is more horrible than that the ten tribes suddenly went over to the worship of idols? Hence it is said to their metropolis: Take away your calf, Samaria, in which Ephraim first played the harlot, that is, Jeroboam of the tribe of Ephraim; and while he played the harlot, Israel was defiled, namely the people of Samaria, which for the most part held the common name of Israel.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.6.11":["Hence the word is turned to Judah also: You too, Judah, set a harvest for yourself; and the sense is: Do not think yourself secure because Israel is led away captive; you too, prepare your crops to be reaped; for not much later you will be led captive to Babylon, and the time of your reaping will come. And when the Chaldeans have reaped you, I will turn back again the captivity of my people, and under Cyrus king of the Persians and Artaxerxes I will bring my people back. And note how pointedly the captivity of Judah and its return are prophesied together; but of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes, there is silence even now, and if ever something favorable is said, it is put off to the coming of Christ.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.1":["When I wished to heal Israel, the iniquity of Ephraim was uncovered, and the malice of Samaria, because they have wrought a lie; and the thief has entered, the robber plundering outside. LXX: likewise. Israel often received many wounds of idolatry, and above all that one when in the desert they cast the head of a calf and said: These are your gods, Israel, who brought you out of the land of Egypt. Hence I, who prefer the repentance of the sinner to his death, and who afterwards say in the Gospel: The healthy have no need of a physician, but those who are sick, have tried to heal the wounds of my people. And while I was treating these with every art, so that the wretched people might be cured, suddenly Jeroboam of the tribe of Ephraim arose, to make the golden calves, and the malice of Samaria, which followed the impious king, was uncovered; for both king and people wrought a lie, that is, an idol. For just as an image is contrary to God, so a lie is contrary to the truth. And the king himself entered like a thief to the people of Israel, and like a robber plundered the unhappy people of God of their help. And the sense is: When I wished to blot out the old sins of my people on account of their ancient idolatry, Ephraim and Samaria found new idols. But this too can be said: that when the Lord and Savior, after the shedding of his blood and his Church gathered from Jews as well as from Gentiles, wished to heal the sins of the people and to bring them back to repentance, suddenly Ephraim, who promise an abundance of false dogmas, and the people of Samaria, who say that they keep the commandments of God, rose up and wrought the idol of false dogmas; and through them the thief and robber, the devil, entered the Church; or else the very doctrine of the heretics entered, like a thief and a robber, of whom the Savior too says in the Gospel: All who came before me were thieves and robbers. Thieves lie in wait and deceive by hidden fraud; robbers boldly carry off what belongs to others. For those who steal, steal by night and in darkness. Hence he says pointedly that the thief entered secretly, and that the robber plunders outside. For they cannot strip those whom they have taught of the garment of Christ unless they have led them out of the Church and made them walk in the perverse way of their doctrines. By the thieves and robbers who came before the Lord we ought to understand not Moses and the prophets, who are always praised by the mouth of the Savior, but the false prophets; and afterwards the heretics, who were not sent by the Lord but came of their own will.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.2":["And lest perhaps they say in their hearts that I have remembered all their wickedness, now their devices have surrounded them; they have been done before my face. LXX: That they may sing together as those who sing in their hearts: I have remembered all their wickednesses; now their thoughts have surrounded them, they have been done against my face. Lest perhaps, he says, they say in their hearts: God has brought old sins back upon us, and we are paying for the offenses of our fathers; they ate the sour grape, and our teeth were set on edge; for that reason I will recount in the following discourse what they have done now and at present, while I look on, and what they do daily; and I will show them their devices, or the thoughts with which they have most zealously pursued evil, and the things they have done in my presence, not fearing my face. But what we read in the Seventy: that they may sing together as those who sing in their hearts, is to be referred to this: that the thief entered, or the robber plundered outside, for this reason, lest, remaining in their former riches and garments, they should reject agreement with the thief and robber; but that, when they have been plundered, they should sing together with them and be made of one heart. Therefore let them receive what they have done, and all their thoughts or works will not merit my regard. The heretics too cannot plead old sins against God, since daily they add new impiety to their ancient works, and perish with the lost, and are bound by their own errors, and, though they think they are hiding from the Lord, are not able to escape his eyes.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.3":["In their wickedness they made the king glad, and in their lies the princes. LXX: In their wickednesses they made the kings glad, and in their lies the princes. He sets out what they did before his face: In their wickednesses they made King Jeroboam glad, and in their lies the princes who had been set over the people under Jeroboam. The heretics too, in the wickedness of their works, have made the king, the devil, glad, and in the lies of their perverse dogmas, the princes, no doubt those of this world, whose false wisdom God destroys. Among the heretics we can call king the one who first invented the heresy, and princes those who, set over the peoples of the heretics, claim for themselves a false priesthood. And at the same time it must be noted that the opposing powers and the rulers and princes of this darkness rejoice in our sins.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.4":["All are adulterers, like an oven heated by the baker; the city rested a little from the mixing of the leaven, until the whole was leavened. LXX: All are adulterers, like an oven burning for baking, to the scorching of the flame, from the mixing of the leaven, until the whole was leavened. Those who in their wickedness made the king glad, and in their lies the princes, are all adulterers; and like an oven they were heated by Jeroboam with the fire of idolatry, so as to bake the bread of impiety; and he, when he had sent the burning of error into their souls, like a furnace and an oven first heated with fire, rested a little, so as not to force the people but to leave them to their own will, until the whole lie was leavened; for whatever is done by compulsion is quickly undone, but what we seize upon by our will endures. Therefore here too, since he had taken the metaphor from an oven, which is heated for baking loaves, he keeps it in the rest, so as to show, in the mixing of the leaven, the consent of the whole people; by which, namely, both king and people fell with equal ardor into idolatry. No one doubts that the hearts of the heretics are heated with the fire of the devil, so that the bread of the Antichrist may be baked in them; therefore at first they rest quiet in the Church and speak in secret, and promise all things peaceable, so that the cancer may creep little by little into the peoples, and the leaven of their doctrine; which the Lord too understood when he says: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees; when it has swollen in the hearts of the deceived, then they break out into open madness; and in them is fulfilled what is said by the apostle John: They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.5-6":["Now let us pass to the spiritual understanding: Unhappy peoples who are led astray by the king, the devil, and by his princes; or who have taken up other solemn feasts from the prince of a heresy and from his leaders, leaving the Church and trampling on the truth of the faith, are accustomed to shout and say: This is the day of our king; for example, of Valentinus, of Marcion, of Arius and of Eunomius. Those set over them, hearing this, are not made drunk with wine, lest the sin be thought a light one, but they rave from the wine of which Moses writes in the song of Deuteronomy: Their wine is the fury of dragons, and the incurable fury of asps; for these eat the food of impiety and are made drunk with the wine of iniquity. Of this the Apostle also says: Do not be drunk with wine, in which is debauchery. And in Proverbs we read: Let princes not drink wine, lest they forget wisdom and be unable to judge rightly. Hence, both peoples and leaders having been deceived, the prince stretches out his hand to the mockers, or the pestilent, such as were the sons of Eli, and of whom we read in the first psalm: He has not sat in the chair of the pestilent; of whom it is said: Cast out the pestilent man from the council, and strife will go out with him; whose hearts were kindled, so that they might dash down those whom they had deceived. For this, according to the Seventy, is what cataracts signify, which do not lift up on high but dash down below. And what he says: All night Ephraim was filled with sleep, shows that the heretics, sleeping, do not see the light of the sun of justice. For those who sleep, sleep at night, because their sense is weighed down. And of these we read in the psalms: They have slept their sleep and found nothing.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.5":["The day of our king: the princes began to rage from wine; he stretched out his hand with the mockers, because they applied their heart like an oven when he lay in wait for them. All night he slept baking them; in the morning he himself was kindled like a fire of flame; all were heated like an oven and devoured their judges; all their kings have fallen; there is none among them who cries to me. LXX: The day of your kings. The princes began to rage from wine; he stretched out his hand with the pestilent, because their hearts were kindled like an oven, while they were cast down headlong all night. Ephraim was filled with sleep; morning came; he was set on fire like a flame of fire. All were heated like an oven, a burning fire, and they ate up their judges. All their kings have fallen; there is not among them one who calls on me. An obscure passage, and one needing the close attention of the reader, so that we may first learn the history. Israel and the city of Samaria rested a little, having received into themselves the heat of error, until the whole lump became like leaven, and rose and burst out; and the people, swelling up, shouted at the doors of King Jeroboam and said: This is the day of Jeroboam our king, this is the feast day which our ruler has appointed for us; this we celebrate, this we sing, on this we exult and play, on this we worship the golden calves. When the people shouted, the princes were not angry, as some think; but they too began to rage from wine and to lose the understanding of their mind, to forget God and to dash themselves against the wood of idols. When the king saw this, namely that the people were shouting and saying: This is the day of our king, and that the princes, like drunken and frenzied men, did not know what they were saying, he stretched out his hand to the mockers, he joined his assent to those who mocked him with empty praises.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.6":["These mockers, when their king lay in wait for them and was leading them away from their God, offered him their heart like an oven, for him to kindle them and make them burn with the flames of idolatry. For he assented to the people for this reason: because he understood that they had turned to error with their whole mind. And what follows: All night he slept baking them; in the morning he himself was kindled like a fire of flame, signifies this: after he sent fire into the oven of their heart, and saw them raging, and that there was no one to resist his will, he slept all night, that is, he was secure; he kept himself in darkness while they were being baked and were making the bread of impiety. Hence afterwards he rose in the morning, and showed the flame of his crimes in open madness, so that they passed over, no longer by stealth but shamelessly, from the worship of God to the ceremonies of idols.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.7":["What more? All, like an oven, were heated with the fire of idolatry, and devoured their judges, so that even one who could have been good by nature and could have remembered the religion of the Lord, seeing both princes and people subject to the calves and thinking them gods, is himself also devoured by the crime. Finally, all the kings of Israel fell, and walked in the ways of Jeroboam son of Nebat, who made Israel sin, and none was found who abandoned the idols and returned to God. These things we have spoken according to the tradition of the Hebrews, more boldly than knowingly, leaving the credit for what is said to its authors.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Their hearts are heated by various passions: anger, love, avarice; and they devour their judges, either whatever virtues they may be able to have in their mind, or the senses by which they distinguish evil from good. Or else it must be said that the leaders of the heretics are devoured by their own peoples, so that they themselves are devoured by the assent of those whose houses they devour for the sake of base gain. All the princes of the heretics have fallen; although they cry to the Lord, there is none who calls on his name. For everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved. Moses and Aaron among his priests, and Samuel among those who call on his name; they called on the Lord, and he heard them, he who does not hear the kings and princes of the heretics, because there is none among them who cries to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.8-9":["Ephraim himself was mingled among the peoples; Ephraim has become a loaf baked under the ashes, which is not turned over; strangers devoured his strength, and he did not know it. Yes, and grey hairs were spread over him, and he was unaware of it; and the pride of Israel will be humbled before his face, and they have not returned to the Lord their God, and they have not sought him in all these things. LXX: Ephraim was mingled with his peoples; Ephraim has become a loaf baked under the ashes, which is not turned over. Strangers devoured his strength, but he did not know it; and grey hairs blossomed on him, and he was unaware of it; and the insolence of Israel will be humbled before his face, and they have not returned to the Lord their God; and they have not sought him in all these things. The kingdom of the ten tribes became like all the nations, because they withdrew from the Lord; and, like a loaf baked under the ashes which is not turned over, that is, which does not repent, the Assyrians and the Chaldeans devoured its strength, and whatever strength it could have, they swallowed up. And such was its madness that it did not know it had been devoured; or at least it was unaware of the cause for which it had been handed over to those who devoured it. Finally, right up to old age, that is, right up to the last captivity, it persisted in the error it had begun.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.8":["But, to pass to another sense: though Ephraim ought to teach the peoples and draw them to his own likeness, he himself is mingled with the peoples and becomes like them; according to what was said above: As the people is, so will the priest be. Or Ephraim is mingled with the peoples and the nations, so that all heretics differ in nothing from the error of the Gentiles. And he who was once a leader in the Church has become a loaf baked under the ashes, surrounded on every side by unclean ash and the heat of the fires, so that he did not return to the Lord but persisted in the error he had begun.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.9":["Demons devoured his strength, for these are the strangers and the enemies of all Christians, and he did not know it, thinking his adversaries friends, and judging those who devoured him to be his table-companions; yes, and grey hairs were spread over him, or rather they blossomed, that is, he went astray for a long time; and nonetheless he was unaware of his old age and his decrepitude, of which it is written: What grows old and ages is near to destruction. And if it is said of a just man and a churchman: A man's grey hairs are his wisdom, why should it not be said of an unjust man and a heretic: A man's grey hairs are his folly? Of this old age Daniel spoke to the elder: You who have grown old in evil days. Hence also in the book of the Shepherd — if, however, anyone cares to accept the reading of it — the Church appears to Hermas first with a grey head, then as a young girl and a bride adorned with her tresses.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.10":["Hence the pride of Israel will be humbled, not after a long time, but now and in the present; for this is what he means by before his face; and it was being humbled because it had lifted itself up and trusted not in God but in the size of its army, and because God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble; for pride, that is, gaon, the Seventy, after their custom, translated ὕβριν, that is, outrage. And because he had said above: Ephraim has become a loaf baked under the ashes which is not turned over, and this seemed ambiguous and did not make clear enough what he meant, he now puts it more plainly: They have not returned to the Lord their God; and they have not sought him in all these things. For if they had returned to the Lord their God, they would surely have heard God speaking through Jeremiah: Return to me, and I will return to you. And though they had done so much, they did not seek him whom they had lost through their own fault.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And though the pride of the heretics is humbled, or the insolence which they daily offer to churchmen, they do not return to the Lord; but in all these things they by no means seek him, because they have been heated like an oven, and they have not sought him; they have devoured their judges, and the kings fell together; nor is there anyone who cries to the Lord. All these things they suffered so that they might seek the Lord, whom they refused to seek.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.11-12":["And what he says: I will strike them according to the report of their assembly, can also mean this: just as by a common counsel they all fashioned idols, so, when I am angered, they will all be laid waste together. The teachers of contrary dogmas, who have abandoned Christ, the Wisdom, and have gone out from the Church, we can rightly call a foolish and heartless dove, since, desiring earthly things, they have been handed over to the Assyrians. And when they have set out from the Church, the Lord spreads his net, woven from the testimonies of the Scriptures and the skilful discourse of wisdom, so as to bring down to low places those who lift themselves up against the knowledge of God and, like a bird, fly to the heights; and to correct them by threats and by the hearing of punishments, so that, corrected, they may not perish for ever.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.11":["And Ephraim has become like a dove led astray, without heart. They called upon Egypt, they went off to the Assyrians; and when they have set out, I will spread my net over them, I will bring them down like a bird of the sky, I will strike them according to the report of their assembly. LXX: And Ephraim was like a foolish dove without heart. He called upon Egypt, and they went off into the Assyrians as they will go. I will cast my net over them, I will bring them down like the birds of the sky; I will correct them in the hearing of their tribulation. The Lord commands in the Gospel that we be simple as doves and shrewd as serpents, so that, imitating the simplicity of doves and the shrewdness of the serpent, we may neither be able to harm others nor suffer snares from others; but by simplicity and prudence may show ourselves balanced men, because prudence without goodness is malice, and simplicity without reason is called folly. Ephraim therefore has become like a dove led astray, in Hebrew photha; by Aquila and Symmachus it is called θελγομένη or ἀπατωμένη, that is, enticed or deceived; and by the Seventy foolish or senseless; for ἄνους can express both. And he is fittingly called a dove led astray, or foolish, because there is also a dove that is wise, which says in the psalms: Who will give me wings like a dove, and I will fly and be at rest? Whose wings are silvered, and the hinder parts of whose back are in the greenness of gold. But Ephraim, a foolish dove and without heart, is shown to be of so brutish a mind that, calling upon Egypt, he went on to the Assyrians. For he who begged the help of the Egyptians is led away captive by the Assyrians. Egypt is a staff of reed: whoever leans on it, it will at once crush him, and, broken and shattered, it pierces the hand of the one who rests upon it.\n— Commentary on Hosea","The question is asked why Ephraim is compared not to other birds but to the dove. The other birds hasten to protect their young even at the peril of their own life; and when they see a hawk, a snake, a raven or a crow approaching their nest, they fly about hither and thither, and hurl themselves at the attacker with their bites, and tear it with their claws, and testify to a parent's grief with plaintive cries; the dove alone does not grieve for its young when they are taken away, and does not look for them; and to it Ephraim is rightly compared, because he does not perceive that his people is being laid waste piece by piece, but is careless of his own safety.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.12":["And so that God might show that by changing places we cannot escape his eyes, and that we are always ruled by the power of God: When, he says, they have set out into the Assyrians, even there I will spread my net, and if they have been lifted up like birds, I will bring them down from there. But I will bring them down, not to destruction, but to strike them as sons; and I will strike them not with the greatness of punishments, but with fear, so that, hearing of the punishments of old, they may be corrected by terrors alone.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.13-14":["Woe to them, because they have withdrawn from me; they will be laid waste, because they have transgressed against me. I redeemed them, and they have spoken lies against me, and they have not cried to me in their heart, but they howled on their beds. LXX: Woe to them, because they have withdrawn from me; they are manifest, because they have acted impiously against me; but I redeemed them; yet they have spoken a lie against me, and their hearts have not cried to me, but they howled on their beds. While I was spreading my net to catch them, and to bring them down from their pride like a bird of the sky, and to strike them in the hearing of their distress, they withdrew and leapt away from me; for this is what ἀπεπήδησαν means, which the Seventy used in their translation. And therefore they will be laid waste, because they have transgressed against me. Where we said: they will be laid waste, in the Hebrew is written sod laem, that is, devastation to them. Symmachus translated it destruction; Theodotion, misery. Moreover, in the common edition we read it in two ways; for some manuscripts have δῆλοί εἰσιν, that is: they are manifest; others δείλαιοί εἰσιν, that is: they are fearful or wretched. They will be laid waste, therefore, and will be wretched, and always fearing and dreading, because they transgressed against God, worshiping the golden calves and abandoning him who redeemed them from Egyptian slavery and led them out with a lofty arm. But they spoke lies against the Lord, saying of the idols: These are your gods, Israel, who led you out of the land of Egypt, and they did not cry to the Lord in their heart, but wallowed in the fornications of idolatry. Or, because lust and luxury follow the worship of demons, those who worshiped demons consequently wallowed like pigs in the mire of lusts. And he fittingly calls the songs of those who serve idols not hymns to God but howling. Concerning the heretics the interpretation is easy: they have everlasting woe, because they have withdrawn from God; and they are wretched, because they have abandoned their Creator, who redeemed them with his own blood; and they themselves speak lies against him, composing the impious dogmas of falsehood; and they do not cry out in their hearts, but always howl in their conventicles, which he fittingly calls beds, and the lairs of wild beasts. Such men cannot say: Every night I will wash my bed, with my tears I will water my couch; but they wallow in the filth of lusts, they give themselves to debauchery, and whatever they say, and think they are saying in praise of God, is the howling of wolves and the din of raving Bacchantes. Rarely does a heretic love chastity, and whoever pretend to love purity, like Mani and Marcion, and Arius and Tatian, and the restorers of the old heresy, promise honey with a poisoned mouth. For the rest, according to the Apostle, the things they do in secret it is shameful even to speak of.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.14-15":["They ruminated on wheat and wine, they withdrew from me.\n\nAnd I taught them and strengthened their arms; and they devised malice against me; they returned so as to be without a yoke; they have become like a deceitful bow; their princes will fall by the sword, from the fury of their tongue; this is their derision in the land of Egypt. LXX: On wheat and wine they were cutting themselves; they were taught in me, and I strengthened their arms, and they devised evils against me; they turned to nothing, they became like a bent bow; their princes will fall by the sword because of the unskilfulness of their tongue; such is their derision in the land of Egypt. Because of the abundance of all things, he says, they fell. Ezekiel too records that this happened in Sodom and Gomorrah, so that they ruminated on nothing but food and luxury; for which the Seventy translated: On grain and wine they were cutting themselves, after the example of the prophets of Baal, who, in the presence of Elijah, prayed for rain by gashing their limbs. And at the same time, to show that they were like beasts of burden, he did not say: they were eating, but: they were ruminating; and therefore they withdrew from the Lord, who says: I taught them, I gave them strength, and they raised their necks against me; not that they could do anything and harm their Creator, but, what alone they were able to do, they devised evils against me.\n— Commentary on Hosea","On wheat and wine, and the false mysteries of the body and blood of Christ, who says in the Gospel: Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone. And in another place: I am the true vine. And: Unless you drink my blood. Over this wheat and wine, then, the heretics are cut asunder, and build diverse tabernacles for themselves; or they are cut off from the body of the Church, and pretend to meditate on the law of God and to ruminate on it. But in doing this they withdraw from the Lord, who taught them in the Church and gave them strength with which to fight against the adversaries.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.15-16":["But they devised malice against the Lord, building up most impious heresies, and returned to the state of the Gentiles, so as to be without the knowledge and the yoke of God; or they returned to nothing, not that they ceased to be, but because, in comparison with him who speaks to Moses: Go, say to the people of Israel: He who is has sent me, all who think against the Lord are said not to be. According to what we read in Esther, in the Seventy only. Do not hand over your scepter to those who are not: there is no doubt that it means idols. For if God is truth, whatever is contrary to truth is a lie, and is called nothing. This fits the heretics, who, instructed from the holy Scriptures, turn the words of the law and the prophets and the Gospel against the Lord; and they are like a deceitful or perverse bow. A deceitful and perverse bow is one that strikes the one who aims it, and wounds its own master. Or they have become like a bent bow, always ready for battle and contentions, to the subversion of their hearers. Hence their princes, that is, the heresiarchs, are struck by the Lord's sword because of the madness of their tongue, with which they blasphemed the Lord; doing in a Church falsely so called the same thing they did at the time when they dwelt in the Egypt of the world, that is, when they were Gentiles. For all the questions of the heretics and of the Gentiles are the same, because they follow not the authority of the Scriptures but the sense of human reason.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.7.16":["And as they were from the beginning, before I called them through Abraham and afterwards through Moses and Aaron, when they were without the yoke and the knowledge of the law and were mingled with all the nations, so even now they have returned to their former state, so as to be carried without yoke and bridle over precipices, and to be changed into a deceitful bow; so that the bow which God had bent against the adversaries they themselves turned against their own Lord, and shot against him the arrows of blasphemies. Hence their princes, who deceived the unhappy people, will fall by the sword because of the fury of their tongue, with which they dared to call the golden calves gods, so that they did in the land of promise what they had learned in Egypt, worshiping the Egyptian Apis and venerating all their monstrosities. For so also in the wilderness, when they had come out of Egypt, they derided the Lord, saying: These are your gods, Israel. And: Would that we were in the land of Egypt, where we sat by the pots of meat, and so on.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.1-4":["The devil, who longs to set his nest above the stars of heaven, we very often read called an eagle: If you are exalted like an eagle, from there I will bring you down, says the Lord. The eye too that mocks its father and despises the old age of its mother will be dug out by ravens, and the young of eagles will devour it; losing the clear light of its vision through the devil and the demons. Hence it is commanded in the law that we should not eat the eagle. This eagle, then, comes upon the conventicles of the heretics, which were once the house of the Lord; and it comes because they have transgressed his covenant and abandoned the law of God; and in vain they call upon him and say: You are my God, and we know you, we who are called Israel, seeing God, and are called by the Christian name. In vain, he says, they do these things, since they have set up kings for themselves, and acted against my will, and have as princes my adversaries, whom I do not know, because they do not deserve knowledge of me. Their silver too and their gold, and whatever they could possess by nature, words and thoughts, they turned into idols which they fashioned from their own heart; and they turned them, not in order to perish; but because they turned them, therefore they perished. For they did not do it with this purpose, that they might perish; but because they did it, therefore they perished.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.1":["Let there be a trumpet in your throat, like an eagle over the house of the Lord; because they have transgressed my covenant and violated my law. They called upon me: My God, we know you, Israel. Israel has cast away the good; the enemy will pursue him. They have reigned, and not from me: they have been princes, and I did not know it. Of their silver and gold they have made idols for themselves, that they might perish. LXX: In their bosom like earth, like an eagle over the house of the Lord, because they have transgressed my testament and acted impiously against my law. They called upon me: God, we know you; because Israel has turned away from good things. They pursued the enemy, they reigned for themselves and not through me; they have been princes, and they did not make it known to me; of their silver and their gold they have made idols for themselves, that they might utterly perish. What we have translated: let there be a trumpet in your throat, for which in the Hebrew is written alechcha sophar, Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated likewise. The Seventy alone said: in their bosom like earth, and what this means is uncertain. For some think that bosom is taken in two ways: both as the lower part of a garment, from the genitals to the feet; and as the colpus of the sea, that is, a gulf, for example of the Adriatic and the Ionian and the Propontis; and that the false teachers, who followed the blasphemy of Egypt, keep in their bosom all things that serve lust, and earthly things; or at least that in the harbor and refuge of their voyage they acquire no precious merchandise, but earthly things. But let us, following the Hebrew truth, weave on the sequence of the explanation we began. The prophet is commanded, and it is said to him: Let there be a trumpet in your throat, that is, raise your voice so that it is like a trumpet, that very many may hear, because very many have sinned. And when you have raised your voice, say this with a shout: like an eagle over the house of the Lord; and the sense is: Nebuchadnezzar will come with all his army so swiftly, so quickly, that he will imitate the flight of an eagle hastening to its prey; and he will come not to some other place but to Jerusalem, in which the temple of God stands, to destroy and overturn it. Of this eagle Ezekiel too speaks more fully: of great wings and feathers and talons, which has the course for entering into Lebanon, that is, into the temple of God, according to the prophet Zechariah, in whom it is written: Open your gates, Lebanon, and let fire devour your cedars. But this, O prophet, which I say and command you: Let there be a trumpet in your throat, so that you may cry aloud and say, like an eagle over the house of the Lord, that the king of the Chaldeans is coming, I command for no other reason than that they have transgressed my covenant and abandoned my ceremonies.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.2":["Hence in the time of necessity and distress, when the captivity comes, they will call upon me and say: My God, we know you, Israel; we who are called Israel know you and acknowledge you, and we keep the ancient name of Jacob, who pleased you, so that we are called Israel.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.3-4":["To them the Lord replied: How are you called Israel, when Israel has cast away the good, that is, his Lord, from whom he was called Israel? Therefore, because Israel cast away the good, the enemy, that is, the Assyrian, will pursue him and seize him, who, having abandoned me as king, asked for a king for themselves, as the other nations also had, and acted against my will. Finally, Samuel for this reason sets before them the harsh commands of a king, and says that their sons and daughters will serve kings, so that they might return to the Lord, the most merciful king; but they became princes without the will of God. And this crime was not enough for them, unless they doubled the outrage with a greater impiety, turning into idols the silver and gold which they had received for riches and adornment. Therefore Saul became king not by the will of God, but by the error of the people. And because he did not have the root of piety, as soon as he began to reign he was inflamed with impiety. What he says: They have reigned, and not from me; they have been princes, and I did not know it, can also be taken of Jeroboam son of Nebat, and of the other princes who succeeded him in power. Nor, because God, angry with Solomon, willed his kingdom to be divided, did Israel therefore do well in accepting a king. For according to the precepts of the law it ought to have asked the Lord whether he wished this to be done. For of the Savior too it is said that the Son of man must indeed be betrayed, but woe to him through whom he is to be betrayed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.5-6":["God casts off the calves of the heretics and of Samaria, who say that they keep the precepts of the law; which calves cling to the ground and work in the earth, not in the spirit, nor lift their eyes to heaven; and therefore the fury of the Lord is kindled against them, and he wonders what this great perversity is, that they will not abandon the idols which they have fashioned for themselves, and love heretical filth instead of the cleanness of the Church. These calves Israel, who pretends that he sees God, did not receive from the other nations; but he himself forged for himself out of the holy Scriptures the depravity of his understanding, and he is the craftsman of his own god, which will quickly perish and will imitate spiders' webs, which are broken at a light touch.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.5":["What he had said above: Of their silver and their gold they have made idols for themselves, that they might perish, he now explains more plainly: Your calf is cast off, Samaria, my fury is kindled against them; either against the calves, because they had made two; or against the inhabitants of Samaria, who worshiped them. Moreover, as for what we read in the Seventy: Cast off your calf, Samaria, he exhorts its inhabitants, not of one city but of all the ten tribes, which are called Samaria — for otherwise there were no calves in the city of Samaria, but in Dan and in Bethel — to cast off the calves against which God is angry; or ἀποτρίψεται, that is, let it rub them away; so that it may little by little rub away and cleanse those which it had worshiped for a long time. And when they do not listen, he turns to others, and speaks as though to a third person: how long will they be unable to be cleansed? What madness is this, he says, that, while I give room for repentance, they refuse to turn to health?\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.6":["Your calf is cast off, Samaria. My fury is kindled against them. How long will they be unable to be cleansed? For it too is from Israel; a craftsman made it, and it is not God, for the calf of Samaria will become spiders' webs. LXX: Cast off your calf, Samaria; my fury is roused against them. How long will they be unable to be cleansed in Israel? And this a craftsman made, and it is not God, because your calf, Samaria, was leading astray. In the place where we have put spiders' webs, in the Hebrew is written sababim, with the letter iod as the penultimate; not, as some falsely think, sababum, that is, with uau, which the Seventy and Theodotion translated πλανῶν, that is, leading astray and deceiving; Aquila, wandering or turned about; Symmachus, inconstant or unstable, that is, ἀκαταστατῶν; the Fifth edition, ῥεμβεύων, roving and fluctuating. We have learned from a Hebrew that sababim properly denotes the threads of spiders flying through the air, which perish even while they are seen, and are dissolved into atoms and into nothing. And to these the calf of Samaria is rightly compared, which at that time the people worshiped because of the greatness of its value, since it was of gold.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And because he had said: Cast off your calf, Samaria, he explains who this calf is: for it too is from Israel; not from others, he says, from the nations, have you received the calf, as Baal and Ashtaroth from the Sidonians, as Chemosh from the Moabites and Moloch from the Ammonites; but you yourselves, and your king Jeroboam, made in Israel what you had learned in Egypt. Or what sort of god is that which is formed by the hand of a craftsman? Finally, as the threads of spiders dissolve into the wind, so the calf of Samaria will be reduced to nothing.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.7":["For they will sow the wind and reap the whirlwind; there is no standing stalk in it, the shoot will make no flour, and if it should make any, strangers will eat it. LXX: For they have sown things corrupted by the wind, and their overthrow will receive them; a sheaf without the strength to make flour, and if it should make any, strangers will eat it. He compares the calf of Samaria to spiders' webs; therefore he keeps the metaphor in what follows, so that what he had called spiders' webs he compares to wind and whirlwind, and to stalks that do not stand; and if they do stand, they have no flour; and if, he says, they should make flour, it will be devoured by others. And since the sense is common both to the heretics and to those in Samaria who fashioned idols, it must be discussed in common. These men sow the wind, or seeds corrupted by the wind, which have no pith, which the Greeks call ἐντεριώνην, and therefore, sowing empty things, they receive what is void and empty; nay rather, sowing in the flesh, they reap corruption from the flesh, and are carried about by every wind of doctrine. And when they have sown the wind, they reap whirlwinds and storms; and first of all, no stalk, that is, no straw, will come from these seeds, nor will they be able to have any appearance of a fruitful crop. But if it should rarely happen that they seem to have something like the Church's dogma, that very shoot and ear will make no flour. Into three measures of this flour the woman of the Gospel puts leaven, so that the spirit by which we perceive, and the soul by which we live, and the body by which we walk, may be brought together into one Holy Spirit, according to the Apostle: In him we live, move and are. But if it should rarely happen to the heretics that their sowing too makes flour, from this flour will be made a loaf baked under the ashes, which is not turned over, and which strangers will eat. Hence he now also says: And if it should make flour, strangers will eat it. But we ought to understand as strangers those of whom it is written: Strange sons have lied to me. And in the eighteenth (19th) psalm: From my hidden sins cleanse me, O Lord, and from strangers spare your servant. If they have not had dominion over the righteous man, then he will be unblemished and will be cleansed from the greatest sin.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.8":["Israel has been devoured; now he has become among the nations like an unclean vessel. The Seventy translated \"useless\" for \"unclean\"; the rest likewise. The unclean, or useless, vessel the Hebrews call a chamber pot, which we are accustomed to use for receiving and throwing away excrement. To this uncleanness he compares the idolaters and the heretics, who are mingled with the Gentiles while they do not keep the truth of God, and, having been made a vessel unto honor, have been turned into a vessel of dishonor. For what is more unclean than a demonic spirit and the dogmas of the heretics, which have mingled them with the pagans? Such was Jeconiah, enslaved to idols, of whom God speaks through Jeremiah: Jeconiah is dishonored, like a vessel of which there is no use. On the contrary Paul, who could say: Or do you seek a proof of Christ who speaks in me? is called a chosen vessel, of gold and of silver, because he had wisdom and eloquence, with which he preached the Gospel of Christ. And when he says: has been devoured, or swallowed up, it signifies that, mingled with idols and with the nations, he has lost the proper name of Israel and of Christian.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.9-10":["As for the heretics, we do not doubt that, going to the Assyrians according to the error of their mind, they think that they are ascending, not descending. Hence it is said to them through Isaiah: What has happened to you now, that you have all gone up onto empty roofs? The prince of these Assyrians is the great mind. Hence Ephraim has sprouted within himself, judging in his presumption that he has grown. Or he has become a solitary wild ass, so as to enter not the planted fields of the Church but the deserts of the devil. He has loved gifts in return for his error, promising himself rewards, or doing everything for the sake of shameful gain. Or at least he has given gifts and payments to his lovers, the demons, and when he has done this, he will be handed over to the Gentiles. For just as the nations worship bodily images, so these men think to be gods the idols which they have fashioned out of their own heart, and therefore they will be reckoned among the number of the nations.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.9":["For they themselves have gone up to Assyria, a wild ass alone by himself. Ephraim have given gifts to lovers; moreover they have hired the nations for pay; now I will gather them, and they will rest a little while from the burden of the king and of the princes. LXX: For they themselves have gone up to the Assyrians; Ephraim has sprouted within himself, they have loved gifts; therefore they will be handed over among the Gentiles; now I will receive them, and they will rest a little, that they may anoint kings and princes. Israel, he says, has been devoured, and has become like a useless, or unclean, vessel, of which no potsherd remains in which water could be drawn, or some small bit of fire. And because he has become an unclean vessel, therefore they have gone up to the Assyrians, imitating the solitary wild ass; not at all as sheep fed by the Lord, but wickedly abusing their freedom, and led away into captivity; and the prophet bewails them and says: Ephraim have given gifts to the Assyrians and have hired their lovers for pay; of whom Ezekiel writes: To all harlots payments are given, but you have given payments to your lovers, and the contrary has happened in you.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.10":["And when they have given presents to the Gentiles, and have hired the nations for pay to help them, they will be gathered for battle, and, bound, will be led away together. And because they love to offer gifts to their adversaries, therefore for a little while they will obtain the favor of not paying tribute to the king and the princes, until they come to the Assyrians, where they will by no means pay taxes and tribute as free men, but will be reduced to the utmost slavery.\n— Commentary on Hosea","But if, he says, now and in the present age they repent, and I receive them, they will cease to set up over themselves the little king and his princes; so that we may understand the little king to be the devil, in distinction from the great king, who can have no fellowship with Belial, but, as soon as he has been received, drives the little king and his princes out of the heart of believers. According to the Hebrew, however, those who had before been scattered, and had hired the nations for pay, will be gathered into the Church of God, and will rest from the burden of the king, about whom the Apostle rebukes those cut off from the Church, saying: Without us you reign; and would that you did reign, and from the princes whom they have, set up in the synagogues of the devil.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.11-13":["Now the Apostle teaches that there is one altar in the Church, and one faith, and one baptism; deserting this, the heretics have fashioned for themselves many altars, not for appeasing God, but for a multitude of offenses. Therefore they do not deserve to receive the law of God, since they had earlier despised those laws which they had received. And if they have said anything from the Scriptures, it is to be compared not with the divine words, but with the thoughts of the pagans. These men sacrifice many victims and eat their flesh, deserting the one victim of Christ; nor do they eat the flesh of him whose flesh is the food of believers. Whatever they do, feigning the order and rite of sacrifices, whether they give alms, or promise chastity, or feign humility and deceive all the simple with pretended blandishments, the Lord will accept nothing of sacrifices of this kind.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.11":["For Ephraim has multiplied altars for sinning; altars have become for him an offense. I will write for him my manifold laws, which have been reckoned as foreign; bring victims, bring; they will sacrifice flesh and eat it, and the Lord will not accept them. LXX: For Ephraim has multiplied altars, for sin, altars are beloved to him. I will write for them a multitude; his ordinances have been reckoned as foreign, beloved altars; for if they have sacrificed victims and eaten flesh, the Lord will not accept them. Led, he says, into captivity, they will rest for a little while from the burden of the king and the prince. And they will suffer this because Ephraim, their prince, has multiplied altars, not on which to sacrifice to the Lord, but on which to join sins to sins; and these altars, that is, places of sacrifice, will be turned for him into an offense, so that the more of them there are, the more his crimes are multiplied.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.12":["Then, as he had said above: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? What shall I do to you, Judah? as though hesitating and seeking with what remedy he might heal the sick man, and by what counsels he might draw the sinner back to salvation. I will write for them again, he says, the laws which I had given before through Moses. But what profit is there in writing still others, when he has despised those which he received before? Is it not contempt of God when, though I commanded that there should be one altar in Jerusalem, they fashioned idols on all the mountains and hills with which to provoke the Lord?\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.13":["They also made altars for this reason, not to please me, but so that, when many victims had been sacrificed, they might eat their flesh, according to what the Lord says in the Gospel: Amen, amen, I say to you, you seek me, not because you saw signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled. For they put all their zeal for victims into devouring the sacrifices, not into pleasing God through them; nor will the Lord accept those which they sacrificed not to him, but to their own belly and gullet.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Now he will remember their iniquity and will visit their sins; they will be turned back into Egypt.\n\nAnd Israel has forgotten his Maker and has built shrines, and Judah has multiplied fortified cities; and I will send fire upon his cities, and it will devour his dwellings. LXX: Now he will remember their iniquities, and will avenge their injustices; they have turned back into Egypt, and Israel has forgotten him who made him, and they have built shrines, and Judah has multiplied walled cities; and I will send forth fire upon his cities, and it will devour his foundations. Between ἀνομίαν, that is, iniquity, and sin there is this difference, that iniquities are before the law, sin is after the law; and for those who persist in their offenses, the Lord will remember their iniquity, which they had committed before the law; but their sins he will not remember, but will take vengeance on them. And he will both remember their old iniquities and visit their former sins for this reason, that they have gone back into Egypt, either asking for help or worshiping the same gods by which they had earlier been led astray, ἄπιν (Apis) and μνεῦιν (Mnevis).\n— Commentary on Hosea","But according to anagogy, those are called iniquities, that is, both ἀνομίαι and ἀδικίαι (injustices), which we committed before baptism and which have been forgiven us in baptism; but sins are those which we have done after baptism, of which it is also written in the psalm: Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered. All of these will be imputed to the heretics, so that both the old iniquities and the new sins are brought back upon them. For those who had gone out of Egypt through the confession of Christ have gone back into Egypt through faithlessness.\n— Commentary on Hosea","What we read according to the LXX translators, ÷ and in Assyria they ate unclean things :, is not found in the Hebrew, and therefore it must be marked beforehand with an obelus. We can, however, say that the Israelites, longing for Egypt, were taken captive by the Assyrians and there ate unclean things, according to Ezekiel, who describes them as having eaten food offered to idols in Chaldea, food so polluted with the filth of idols that it is compared to human dung. The heretics too, whose princes are the Assyrians — of whom we have often spoken — eat unclean things among them, while they are defiled by their filth.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.8.14":["For Israel has forgotten his Maker and has built shrines on the high places, consecrating whole hills and mountains, and shady trees, to Baal and Ashtaroth and other idols. Judah too, understanding that Israel had departed from the love of God and that their sins had been visited, was not converted to the Lord, but trusted in the fortification of his cities, which the Lord says he will destroy, devouring them down to his foundations. His: there is no doubt that it signifies Judah; although some read their foundations, that is, of the cities, in place of his.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Israel has forgotten his Maker, and, having despised the Creator, has fashioned another god for himself. Judah too, that is, the churchman, in evil works, or in a perverse interpretation of the holy Scriptures, has built himself fortified cities, not with the help of God, but with skillful falsehood; and the Lord says that he will set them on fire with the fire of his spirit, and will devour his βάρεις, that is, great houses built in the manner of towers, and will overturn the foundations badly laid, so that they may not be able to set up sacrilegious shrines against God. Some take the cities fortified by Judah in a good sense, and they try to temper what seemed contrary to this sense, I will send fire upon his cities, and it will devour his dwellings, in such a way that, after what is perfect has come, what is in part is destroyed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.1-2":["It is said also to the heretics, that they should not exult and rejoice and think themselves like the other nations. For those did not believe in God; but these, under the name of God, worship idols and play the harlot away from their God; and they multiply for themselves many threshing floors and countless winepresses, and they eat the wheat from which the bread of mourning is made, and drink the wine of Sodom, which is trodden out with the gall of asps. And because they prepare for themselves many winepresses and many threshing floors, therefore they will not eat from the true and only threshing floor, nor drink from the winepress which the Lord Jesus trod; but whatever they think they have will be corrupted by falsehood.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.1":["Do not rejoice, Israel, do not exult like the peoples, for you have played the harlot away from your God; you have loved a payment upon all the threshing floors of wheat. The threshing floor and the winepress will not feed them, and the wine will deceive them. LXX: Do not be glad, Israel, and do not rejoice like the peoples, for you have played the harlot away from your God; you have loved gifts upon every threshing floor of wheat. The threshing floor and the winepress did not know them, and the wine has deceived them. Those who have departed from God, when they have come into the depth of sins and have despaired of their salvation, will despise everything. Finally Israel too, departing from the law of God and worshiping idols, says that he is one nation out of many nations, rejoicing and congratulating himself that he has departed from the knowledge of God and is a nation mingled with the rest; and therefore he now rebukes them and says: Do not be glad, do not rejoice, and do not think that you are such as the other nations are. For one who does not know God is punished in one way, one who departs from God in another, because the servant who knows the will of his master and does not do it will be beaten with many stripes.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.2":["You have reckoned many threshing floors and winepresses as the payment for your fornication, so that you might enjoy an abundance of all things; therefore the threshing floor and the winepress will not produce wheat and wine, and the winepress will deceive them, or will deny its wine, with which they thought they would be made drunk. We read that under Ahab king of Israel, and Elijah the prophet, there was a most severe famine in Samaria, so that mothers fed on the corpses of their own sons. At that time, according to the letter, the threshing floor and the winepress did not feed them, and the wine deceived them, and they wasted away in want.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.3-4":["They will not dwell in the land of the Lord who have departed from the Church, and have returned in mind into Egypt, and eat the sacrifices of the Assyrians, that is, of demons; nor do they pour out to the Lord the wine with which they have been made drunk unto luxury, and neither the things they offer nor they themselves who offer please him. The sacrifices of the heretics are the bread of mourning and of tears; for all that they do will be turned into weeping. Nor will they be able to hear: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted; but on the contrary they will hear: Woe to those who laugh, for they shall mourn. Whatever they offer, they offer not to God but to the dead, namely to those who have contrived wicked heresies; and whoever eats of their victims will be defiled. The blind will be led into the pit by the blind. Whatever they do, they do for the sake of delights, to deceive the people, to devour the houses of widows. We can call the bread of mourning the deadly words with which they speak iniquity against the Lord; and this bread does not enter into the house of God; for the conventicles of the heretics are called not the house of God, but dens of robbers.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.3":["They will not dwell in the land of the Lord; Ephraim has returned into Egypt, and among the Assyrians he has eaten what is polluted. They will not pour out wine to the Lord, and their sacrifices will not please him; like the bread of mourners, all who eat it will be defiled, because their bread, their own soul, will not enter into the house of the Lord. LXX: They did not dwell in the land of the Lord; Ephraim dwelt in Egypt, and among the Assyrians he ate what is unclean; they did not pour out wine to the Lord, nor did their sacrifices please him, because it is to them the bread of mourning; all who eat them will be defiled; because the loaves of their souls will not enter into the house of the Lord. Not only did the threshing floor and the winepress fail to feed them, and the wine lie in the land of Israel, when for three years and six months everything perished, but the inhabitants themselves also will depart from the land of the Lord; and they will be led into a foreign land, so that they may not dwell in the holy land which they have polluted with their fornications. Ephraim has returned, he says, into Egypt, and among the Assyrians he has eaten what is polluted. From this place some have added above: and among the Assyrians he has eaten what is polluted, which is not found in the Hebrew, and about which we have already spoken.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.4":["But when they are in Chaldea without a temple and without an altar, they will not pour out wine to the Lord, but to demons; and they will not please him, those who pour out to foreign gods, and who are held in captivity and among the Assyrians eat things sacrificed to idols, and are like the bread of mourners. For it is not lawful to eat of the sacrifices of mourners, and if anyone eats, what was offered becomes unclean. The Greeks call the suppers of mourners περίδειπνα; we can call them parentalia, from the fact that they are offered for dead parents. And not only he who has offered, but also he who has eaten of food of this kind will be unclean; for their bread, that is, the food which they offer, will not enter into the house of the Lord, which has been destroyed, which has been burned with Babylonian fire; but it will be for their own soul. And the sense is: they provide for their own gullet and their own pleasure; but polluted things do not please me.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.5-6":["It is said also to the heretics: When the solemn day comes, what are you going to do? For solemn day Aquila has translated time. From which it is manifest that it does not mean a festal day, but the time of retribution; for at once there follows: The days of vengeance have come, the days of your retribution have come. Behold, you have been laid waste by many enemies: the Assyrians and the Chaldeans have slaughtered you, you have fled to the world, and have been made like the other nations; there Memphis will bury you, which is interpreted from the mouth; and the sense is: you will receive according to your blasphemies, and what you have spoken you will feel in your punishments; the desirable things, that is, the dogmas which you had composed for yourselves with crafty eloquence, which is what silver signifies, the nettle will possess, which will consume you with eternal burning; and the bur or thorn will be in your tents; for thorns spring up in the hands of those who have been made drunk with the Babylonian cup. And in the Gospel we read that shameful thoughts and the cares of this world, and the vices that cling to us, are called thorns, which choke the grain as it rises among the grasses.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.5":["What will you do on the solemn day, on the day of the festival of the Lord? For behold, they have set out from the devastation; Egypt will gather them, Memphis will bury them; the nettle will inherit the desirable things of their silver, the bur will be in their tents. LXX: What will you do on the day of assembly and on the day of the solemnity of the Lord? Therefore behold, they will go from the misery of Egypt, and Memphis will receive them, and Michmash will bury them; destruction will possess their silver, thorns will be in their tents. When the day of captivity comes, he says, and the most savage enemy falls upon you, what is my solemnity? What victim do I hold as propitious? That he avenges me on my enemies, and puts a limit to wrong, and scourges the impious sons. What then will you do on the day of the festival of the Lord? Answer. And while they are silent, he answers himself, or rather he beholds with divine eyes what they are going to do.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.6":["Behold, he says, when misery and devastation pressed upon them, and the Assyrian and the Chaldean were eager to bind them as captives, they fled to Egypt. There Memphis will bury them, which at that time was the metropolis of Egypt, before Alexandria, which was formerly called No, received both the greatness of a city and its name from Alexander of Macedon. But what is said in the Seventy: Michmash will bury them, is not found in the Hebrew; but rather mamad, which is called desirable. From which it is clear that they were deceived by the similarity of the letters daleth and chaph, and in place of mamad, which all have translated desirable, they thought it was Michmash, a city of Egypt. We can also take what is said: For behold, they have set out from the devastation; Egypt will gather them, Memphis will bury them, of the tribe of Judah; when, after Gedaliah, whom Nebuchadnezzar had set over the land, was killed by Ishmael, the remnant of the people fled over to Egypt with Jeremiah the prophet, and there, when the Chaldeans afterwards pursued them, they were either taken captive or buried. And by the desirable things of their silver, which the nettle possesses, we shall understand country houses and all the adornments of country houses, which are bought at the price of silver. And what follows, the bur will be in their tents, signifies a long devastation, so that where once there were houses, there burs and nettles and thorns spring up.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.7":["The days of visitation have come, the days of retribution have come; know, Israel, that you are a foolish prophet, an insane spiritual man; because of the multitude of your iniquity, and the multitude of madness. LXX: The days of vengeance have come, the days of your retribution have come, and Israel will be afflicted like a raving prophet, a man who had the spirit; because of the multitude of your iniquities, your madness has been multiplied. In this place too there is the usual error: for where we have translated: know, Israel, that is, Israelites, and in the Hebrew is read iadau, the Seventy translated: and will be afflicted, taking the letter iod for vau, and reading res for daleth; of which the one means knowledge, the other affliction or malice. Therefore the days of visitation have come, of which he had said above: What will you do on the solemn day, on the day of the festival of the Lord? The days of retribution have come. O Israel, now recognize your own words, you who used to call the prophet who told you true things, and prophesied by the Holy Spirit, a fool and insane, according to what the princes said to Jehu in Ramoth-gilead: Why did this madman come to you? Therefore, because of the multitude of your iniquities, in which you have long run riot in wickedness, recognize that it is not my prophet, but you, who are a madman, you who labored to this end, that you might trample on my words. For madness Aquila rendered ἐγκότησιν, which in Latin we can call either wrath or the memory of a grievance. The day of vengeance and retribution some interpret as the day of judgment, when Israel will be afflicted, who now boasts that he sees God, and is not ruled by the Holy Spirit, but is carried about in various directions by a demonic one, not knowing what he says, and calling the Son of God a creature, denying that the Holy Spirit is God; and again asserting that the good God is one, the creator of the world another; and their madness is manifold, because their iniquities too were very many. Whom we have called a foolish prophet, the Seventy have translated a false prophet. And lest, by often repeating the same things, we seem to distrust the reader's prudence, we briefly advise that whatever is said in this prophet about Israel and about Ephraim is to be referred to the heretics, who, truly raving, speak lies against God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.8-9":["The watchman of Ephraim with my God, the prophet, has become a snare of ruin over all his ways; madness in the house of his God; they have sinned deeply, as in the days of Gibeah; he will remember their iniquity and will visit their sins. LXX: The watchman of Ephraim with God, a prophet; a crooked snare over all his ways; they contrived madness in the house of God; they have been corrupted according to the days of the hill; he will remember their iniquities; he will take vengeance upon their sins. God gave princes for this purpose, that they might correct the people when it transgressed, and draw it back to the right road. Whence he also says to Ezekiel: I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel. Therefore Jeroboam was given among the people as a watchman, and as a prophet with my God, that is, with God, who speaks these things to Hosea. But he, according to what is written above: You have become a snare to the watch and a net spread out over Tabor; and you have turned aside victims into the depth, is even now called a snare of the people of Israel, because all fall in his snare, especially since he has set up, or contrived, madness in the house of God, that is, made the golden calf in Bethel, for this is interpreted house of God; and has sinned so deeply in wickedness, and has thus been sunk in the abyss of impiety, that he surpasses the crime which was once committed in Gibeah, when they killed the wife of the Levite returning from Bethlehem by unlawful intercourse.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Searching the ancient histories, I cannot find that any have rent the Church and led peoples astray from the house of the Lord, except those who had been appointed by God as priests and prophets, that is, watchmen. These, then, are turned into a crooked snare, placing a stumbling block in all places, so that whoever enters by their ways falls, and cannot stand in Christ, and is drawn away by various errors and carried by crooked paths to the precipice. These are the watchmen of Ephraim, who have contrived madness in the house of the Lord, that is, in the Church, or in the holy Scriptures, interpreting them perversely, or at least in each one of the believers, who is most rightly named the house of God. Therefore they have been corrupted and have perished according to the days of the hill, when they spoke iniquity against the heights and went up on vain roofs. God will remember their iniquity, by which they acted wickedly against their neighbor, leading him out of the Church, and he will visit their sins, by which they have sinned against their own souls. This is what we read in the psalm: Beside the path they have set a stumbling block for me. For unless one has seen the path of God, that is, has heard the name of Christ, he will not enter upon it. Therefore the heretics too, under the name of Christ, have set snares beside the path, so that he who believes that he walks in Christ, of whom we read that he himself is the way, may tread also in the snares of those who have contrived madness in the house of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.9":["We can also take the days of Gibeah as that time when, in place of God, they chose for themselves a king from the city of Gibeah, that is, Saul. And he says that they have now sinned much more greatly by choosing Jeroboam and worshiping idols than at the time when they chose Saul; for here idolatry too was joined to schism, but there the worship of God remained among the people. Therefore he will remember their iniquity, he who is now thought, because of his patience, to have forgotten, and he will visit their sins, and the wounds which have long festered.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.10":["Like grapes in the desert I found Israel; like the first fruits of the fig tree at its top I saw their fathers; but they went in to Baal-peor and were estranged unto confusion; and they became abominable like the things they loved. LXX: As a grape in the desert I found Israel, and as an early fig on the fig tree I saw their fathers; but they went in to Baal-peor, and were estranged unto confusion; and they became abominable like the beloved. For which in other copies we read: and the beloved became as abominable, which agrees better with the truth. When the whole world was a desert and did not have the knowledge of God, I found, he says, the people of Israel like a grape in the wilderness; and how he found it, he says: Like the first fruits of the fig tree, at its top I saw their fathers. Therefore the people was found in Abraham and Isaac and Jacob. And note the exactness of the language: the parents are seen, the people is found; and in both there is the vine and the fig tree, under which he who trusts in the Lord is said to rest. But they, when they had been brought out of Egypt, committed fornication with the Midianites, and went in to Baal-peor, the idol of the Moabites, whom we can call Priapus. Indeed, Baal-peor is interpreted (that is, the idol of lust) as having in its mouth, that is, at its top, a skin, so as to display the shamefulness of the male member. And because they went in to Baal-peor, therefore they were estranged from God, made over as slaves to their confusion, that is, to the idol; so that they worshiped that for whose sake they had departed from God, according to what is written: For by whatever one is overcome, of that he is also the slave; and just as those who serve the gullet have their belly as their God, so those who serve lust have Baal-peor as their God. And they became, he says, abominable, like the things they loved, according to what is written in the psalms: Let those who make them become like them, and all who trust in them, so that they are called not only idolaters, but idols. Moreover, the Lord says in his passion: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me. And in the psalm: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed. When the whole world was held in sin, the nations did not know God, and Israel had rejected him whom it had known before, the Lord found, first in the apostles and in apostolic men, Israel, the Christian people, and seeing God with the mind; and he was satisfied with their sweetest fruits, the grape and the fig, which, if they are found in the desert and when the time is not yet ripe, are of greater delight because of their rarity. But they, that is, Israel, who take to themselves the Christian name — for it is not to be understood of the fathers — went in to the idol Baal-peor, which has a skin in its mouth. For whatever the heretics speak is deadly, and separated from the living word of God. Or they went in to lust; for it is difficult to find a heretic who loves chastity; not that he ceases to put it forward on his lips, but that he does not keep it in his conscience, saying one thing and doing another; whence they have both been estranged from God, and have glory in their confusion, and have become abominable, they who were formerly loved in their fathers. But if we wish to read: they became abominable like the beloved, which however is not found in the Hebrew, we say that the Gentiles, who are abominable, have become so, in the same way as the heretics also, who were formerly loved in their fathers; that is, so that both the one and the other are equally abominable and guilty of the fault.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.11-13":["But let us say according to tropology that Ephraim, that is, the heretics, has departed from the Church like a bird, and has all his glory in birth and in the womb and in conception, if they have begotten many sons, whom the Lord threatens, even if they have been reared, to be punished as well, and not by any other whatever, but by the Lord himself, since they have begotten sons of fornication; and a true woe will be upon them when God has departed from them. And at the same time he unfolds what Ephraim was: when he was in the Church, he says, he was beaten by the temptations of this world as Tyre is by the waves of the sea, and yet he could suffer nothing adverse, because he had the foundation of Christ, upon which a house that is built cannot be overthrown. But now he leads out his sons to the slayer, that is, to the devil. And he said finely: he leads out, that is, he makes them go outside from the Church. We can call the sons of Ephraim the worst thoughts and the dogmas contrary to the truth, which the Lord slays with the spirit of his mouth, and he does not suffer them to have such children, and he abandons them to eternal destruction. But what we read in the Seventy: My flesh is from them, Ephraim, as I saw, has offered his sons for a hunt, we understand thus: If Christ is the head of the body, that is, of the Church, we are all members of Christ and of the Church. He, then, who departs from the Church tears the body of Christ; therefore Ephraim was the flesh and a member of the Lord the Savior. But he offered his sons for a hunt, and for the hunt of those of whom it is written: Our soul has been snatched like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters. And he led out his children from the Church to slaughter, or to be wounded, so that they are wounded by those who shoot burning arrows, so as to strike and burn at the same time.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.11":["Ephraim, that is, the ten tribes, has flown away like a bird into captivity and has departed from its place. And he called it a bird to show its swift passage into Babylon. But if we read, like a bird their glory has flown away, we say this, that the help of God has departed from them and flown away. And what follows, from birth and from the womb and from conception, can be understood in two ways: that the glory which has flown away from Ephraim departs also from their birth and from their womb and from their conception, that is, it deserts their sons and abandons their descendants. Or at least we say that Israel had all his glory in his multitude, and in this he thought himself greater than Judah his brother, because he was set over ten tribes, the other over two.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.12-13":["Ephraim has flown away like a bird; their glory from birth and from the womb and from conception. And even if they rear their sons, I will make them childless among men. But woe to them also when I depart from them. Ephraim, as I saw, was Tyre, founded in beauty; and Ephraim will lead out his sons to the slayer. LXX: Ephraim has flown away like a bird; their glory in birth and in travail and conception; for even if they have reared their sons, they will be without sons among men, for woe to them also; my flesh is from them. Ephraim, as I saw, has offered his sons for a capture; and Ephraim, to lead out his sons to slaughter. In this place the translators greatly disagree among themselves. For in the place where we said: woe to them when I depart from them, the Seventy and Theodotion translated: woe to them, my flesh is from them; and seeking the reason why there is such great variety, I seem to myself to have found this: My flesh is said in the Hebrew tongue basari; again, if we say: my departure, or my turning aside, it is said basori. The Seventy and Theodotion, therefore, in place of what is my departure and my turning aside, rendered: my flesh. Again, where we have put: Ephraim, as I saw, was Tyre, the Seventy translated θήραν, that is, a hunt, or a capture; Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion, the hardest rock, that is, flint, which in the Hebrew tongue is called sur; and if we read Sor, it means Tyre. But the Seventy translators, thinking because of the similarity of the letters res and daleth that it was not res but daleth, read sud, that is, a hunt, or a capture; whence Bethsaida too is called the house of hunters. We have spoken of the diversity of the translators; let us return to the sense.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.12":["Whence the Lord says that even if they rear their sons and gather together a multitude of children, they will be handed over to death; and then a true woe will fall upon them, when God has departed from them.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.13":["And consequently he sets forth what Ephraim, who is now deserted, once was: so beautiful, he says, was he, and so walled about with the help of God, as Tyre is girded by the sea, or at least like a very hard crag which, fixed in the earth, scorns all storms and does not care about whirlwinds and winds. But he himself, that is, Ephraim, or Tyre, which was founded in the fullness of the sea, will lead out his sons to captivity. Many refer this section to the times of Hazael, who besieged Samaria and afflicted it with famine for a long time, so that the besieged thought it lighter to perish by the sword than by starvation.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.14":["Give them, O Lord; what will you give them? Give them a womb without children and dry breasts. LXX: likewise. If we wrongly abuse the things that God has given as a blessing, and they are turned to the opposite of the purpose for which they were given by him, it is expedient that they be taken away from us. Finally, the tongue was given for praising the Lord God and for speaking the things that are good. If anyone abuses it for blasphemy, against him the psalmist prays to the Lord: Let the deceitful lips be made dumb, which speak iniquity against the righteous in pride and in abuse. And in another place: May the Lord destroy all deceitful lips and the boastful tongue. Since, therefore, Ephraim gloried in the womb, and in conception, and in childbirth, and in the multitude of his peoples, the prophet prays to the Lord and says: Give them, O Lord. And he answers himself: What will you give them? And at once he adds: Give them a barren womb and dry breasts, so that they may have no grounds for pride, so that in the very thing in which they are accustomed to glory, in that they may be confounded. That this can also be understood of the teachers of contrary dogmas is evident, who glory in the multitude of their peoples, and in those children whom they have reared for destruction, in order to lead them out of the Church and lead them in to the murderer. For the devil slaughters as many as the sons the heretics have begotten in error. Of a soul of this kind it is said: Blessed is the barren woman who is undefiled, who has not known the bed in sin. For blessed is the churchman who, in comparison with the heretic, has not begotten children in error. And in another place we read: It is better to have no children, with virtue. For from an unlawful union the seed will perish; and though they be of long life, they will be counted as nothing, and their old age at the last will be without honor; for the fruitful multitude of the ungodly is useful for nothing; nor indeed ought we to think that he prayed for a barren womb and dry breasts in a bodily sense.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.15":["All their wickednesses are in Gilgal, for there I held them in hatred. LXX: likewise. In Gilgal Saul was anointed king, while Samuel announced the wrath of God to the people. There, he says, I held them in hatred, and by asking for a man as their king they withdrew from my rule. Or because Gilgal is a place of idolatry: there they committed all their crimes. But since Gilgal is interpreted revelation, or κυλισμοὶ, that is, wallowing-places, he says that all the wickedness of the heretics is to be revealed at that time when God gives them a barren womb and dry breasts, and they see their own shame. And those who boasted through pride that they had climbed to the heights will be rolled down to the earth, or dragged down to hell. Truly the heretics are worthy of God's hatred, who speak a lie against God, of whom he says in what follows:\n\nBecause of the malice of their devices I will cast them out of my house. There follows: I will not again love them; all their princes are departing, or disobedient. The Seventy have the same. And of the heretics indeed there is no doubt that they have been cast out of the house of God, and that he will not again love them as long as they remain in error, and that all their princes are departing from God, or disobedient, like Valentinus, Marcion and the rest. We can call the princes of the heretics demons, who truly departed from the Lord and are called princes, according to what the Lord says in the Gospel: The prince of this world will come and will find nothing in me. And the Apostle says that we fight against the powers and principalities and the rulers of this darkness. But it is asked according to the history how he cast them, that is, the ten tribes, out of his house, since they were not in the house of God? But we shall call the house of God either the holy land, into which they were brought, or the false name of Israel, or the fact that prophets were sent to them as to the people of God. But that he does not again love them, and that all the kings of Israel were departing from God, is evident; for to this day they remain in captivity. Others think that what is written, I will cast them out of my house, refers to the kingdom of Judah, because they too were to be led into captivity. But how can I will not again love them be fitted to them, since afterwards they were brought back to Jerusalem; and all their princes are departing, since we read that David, Asa, Jehoshaphat, Hezekiah and Josiah were righteous kings? Hence we must pass on to the times of Christ, because at his coming they were cast out of the house of God, and are in no way saved as Israel, but as the Christian people. Hence also the Lord made himself a whip of cords and cast them out of the temple, because they had made his Father's house a house of trade.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.9.16-17":["Ephraim is stricken; their root is dried up; they will bear no fruit at all; and even if they beget, I will slay the most beloved of their womb. God will cast them away because they did not hear him; and they will be wanderers among the nations. LXX: Ephraim has suffered pain in his roots; he is withered, he bears no fruit at all; for even if they beget, I will slay the desirable things of their womb. God will reject them because they did not hear him; and they will be wanderers among the nations. He takes a metaphor from a tree, which, if its roots have withered, cannot bear fruit, and if it produces a little, will at once dry up in the very blossom. He speaks, moreover, of Ephraim, whose root has withered because he lost God, in whom he had been founded, or did not deserve to have his fathers Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, in whom he had sent down his root; and therefore he does not produce the fruit of righteousness; and even if he should produce it: I will slay, he says, the most beloved of his womb, according to what he had said above: If they rear their sons, I will make them childless among men; hence also God cast them away and made them go as captives. And they will be wanderers among the nations. We can say this same thing also of all the Jews: because their princes departed from God, stirring up the people to demand him for death, therefore he cast them out of his house and will not again love them any more. He struck their root and dried it up, and they will bear fruit no more; and even if they do, and seem to meditate on holy Scripture and the law, and bring forth from their own heart, like most beloved sons, something of knowledge and doctrine, with the Lord opposing, they will be cut down. For the God of all the prophets cast them away, because they did not hear him; and they will be wanderers among the nations, having no altar, no seat, no city of their own. Hence David too says in the psalm: Do not slay them, lest at any time my people forget: scatter them by your power. And in another place: According to the multitude of their impieties cast them out, for they have provoked you, O Lord. Of this tree we also read in the Gospel: Already the axe is laid to the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire. That the heretics cannot bear the fruits of the virtues, because they have lost the Lord, in whom, according to the Apostle, they ought to have been rooted and founded, no one doubts; and even if they do bear them, and beget some by the fruitfulness of their womb, with the Lord opposing, they will die. Or, because their fruits are all the things they invent and beget from their own heart, these will wither and perish; and it will be plain to all that a dry root cannot bring forth crops. These will be cast away, or rather have been cast away, by God, because they did not hear him saying: Do not move the boundaries which your fathers set. And therefore they will be wanderers among the nations, passing over now to these opinions, not to those; since what they have once found does not please them, but they always exchange the old for the new and imitate the errors of the heathen.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.1":["Israel is a leafy vine, its fruit was made equal to it; according to the multitude of its fruit it multiplied altars, according to the richness of its land it abounded in images. LXX: Israel is a leafy vine, abundant fruit in it; according to the multitude of its fruits it multiplied altars, according to the good things of their land they will build monuments. For leafy vine, Aquila translated ἕνυδρον, which we can call watery, or ἔξίτηλον, because it loses the flavor of wine. Symmachus has ὑλομανοῦσαν, one that has grown entirely into leaves. Vines that have not been pruned by the vinedresser run riot into shoots and leaves, and the moisture that they ought to have changed into wine they squander on the empty ambition of foliage and leaves; a vine of this kind is ruinous to farmers. Such was Israel, growing in the multitude of its peoples, and not rendering fruit to God the farmer. Let us also put it another way: the leafy vine, or according to the Seventy εὐκληματοῦσα, that is, having good layers and fruitful shoots, bore many clusters, and the fruitfulness of the grapes equaled the size of the branches; but she who before was such, before she offended God, afterwards turned the abundance of fruits into a multitude of offense, so that the more peoples she had, the more altars she built, and surpassed the abundance of the land by the number of idols. For images, the Seventy rendered στήλας, which we call statues or monuments, which properly belong to demons or to dead men. So also the heretics, while they were planted in the Church and growing in the house of God, were called the vineyard of Sorek and bore most abundant fruits; but afterwards, the more numerous they became, the more altars they multiplied for themselves, so that instead of the one altar that is true they built many altars for their error, and according to the richness of their land they abounded in images. The land of the heretics is fruitful: receiving from God keenness of understanding and of talent, so that they might turn the goods of nature to the worship of God, they made idols for themselves out of them. For no one can build a heresy unless he is of ardent talent and has gifts of nature which were created by God the artificer. Such was Valentinus, such was Marcion, whom we read to have been most learned. Such was Bardesanes, whose talent even the philosophers admire. These, then, turned the good things of their land into monuments of the dead, because all their doctrine refers not to the living but to the dead, both to those whom they worship and to those whom they deceive.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.2":["Their heart is divided; now they will perish, he himself will break their images, he will lay waste their altars. LXX: They have divided their hearts; now they will perish; he himself will dig under their altars; their monuments will be afflicted. The Hebrews hand down a tale of this kind, confirming their surmise by the authority of the Scriptures: as long as both the kings and the people alike worshiped the golden calves and were in agreement in impiety, captivity did not come. The last king of the ten tribes was Hoshea, of whom it is written that he did indeed do evil in the sight of the Lord, but not like the kings of Israel who were before him; in whose ninth year Shalmaneser, king of the Assyrians, took the people of Israel, and led them away to the Assyrians, and made them dwell by the river Gozan in the cities of the Medes. It is asked, therefore, why they were not taken captive under the worst kings, but under one who had begun in some part to turn to better things? To this they adduce the following: at first the people excused itself and said: We obey the commands of kings, and we cannot resist their tyranny; we worship the calves that we are compelled to adore. But in the days of Hoshea it was ordered by that same king that the calves should not be worshiped with such zeal, but that whoever wished should go to Jerusalem and sacrifice to God in the temple; this decision, they say, the people opposed. And this is what he now says: their heart is divided, that is, the heart of the king and of the people, and with no excuse remaining, now they will perish and be handed over to eternal captivity; for as soon as the people separated from the king, destruction came. And what follows: he himself will break their images, he says of God: and he will lay waste their altars; not that God himself did this with his own hand, but that his will was fulfilled through enemies. That the hearts of the heretics are divided among themselves, and that they fight with contrary opinions, even they themselves do not deny, since they hold different views. Hence they will be destroyed, and the Lord will break, or dig under, their images or altars, which they have fashioned from their own heart; and he will lay waste the monuments by which each of them is called by his own name; and they have called their names upon their lands, so that they are said to belong in no way to the Church of Christ, but to this man or that.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.3-4":["The disciples of contrary dogmas too, when their lies have been crushed and their altars and groves overthrown, will say too late: We do not have the kings who formerly ruled over us, by whose deceptions we did not fear the Lord; for what did it profit us to follow those whose help we do not feel in our need? They will say these things seeking some excuse, so that they may seem to have erred not through themselves but through very bad teachers. Hence also the LXX translated their words as false excuses, which the prophet shuns, saying: Do not incline my heart to words of malice, to make excuses in sins. We gladly applaud our own vices, and, overcome by pleasures, we plead the weakness of the flesh or the harsh commands of our elders; hence the words and visions of the heretics will be useless. And they will strike a covenant, not at all with God, but with bitterness, which, when the day of judgment has come, will spring up over the furrows of their field; so that those who sowed in joy may reap in tears; those who laughed may weep; those who had consolation may mourn.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.3":["For now they will say: We have no king; for we do not fear the Lord, and what will a king do for us? Speak words of a useless vision, and you will strike a covenant, and judgment will spring up like bitterness over the furrows of the field. LXX: Therefore now they will say: We have no king, because we did not fear the Lord, but what will a king do for us? Speaking words, lying pretexts; he will draw up a testament, judgment will spring up like grass upon the wilderness of the field. After God has broken the images of Israel, and laid waste their altars or statues, and the final captivity has come, they will say: We have no king. And lest they think that the sentence is put off for a long time, he added: Now they will say, when they are laid waste, when they perceive that Hoshea, their last king, has been taken from them; but the king has been taken from us for this reason, because we did not fear the Lord, the true king; for what could a man as king profit us?\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.4":["Say what you will, sigh after your old errors, promise yourselves prosperity, which will be turned into its opposite; you will strike a covenant, not at all with God, but with a lie. And after the covenant, which the Seventy translated testament, there will spring up for you not a fruitful crop of grain, not even barley, the food of beasts of burden, not various pulses, not vines that sweat out their fruit into must; the trees will not put forth fruits that change the moisture of the earth into various flavors; but bitterness will spring up for you, or rather the judgment of bitterness, or ἄγρωστις, which in Latin we render as grass. For it is a kind of plant like a reed, which at each of its joints sends a shoot upward and a root downward, and these shoots and twigs in turn are the seedbeds of another plant, and so in a short time, unless it is dug out to its deepest roots, it makes whole fields like thickets of briars. Indeed, even if some dry part of it, provided it has a joint, falls upon cultivated ground, it fills everything with grass. We have said these things according to the LXX translators; but in the Hebrew ros is written, which is translated as bitterness, that is, the judgment of bitterness, of which the Lord also speaks in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world; and of others it is written that they will receive greater judgment.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.5":["The inhabitants of Samaria worshiped the cows of Beth-aven, for its people mourned over it; and its temple-keepers exulted over it in its glory, because it departed from it. For indeed it too was carried into Assyria, a gift to the avenging king; shame will seize Ephraim; Israel will be put to shame in his own will. LXX: With the calf of the house of ὢν those who dwell in Samaria will sojourn, for its people mourned over it. And as they provoked him, they will rejoice over its glory, because it was carried away from it; and binding it for the Assyrians, they bore gifts to King Jareb in the house. Ephraim will receive shame, Israel will be put to shame in his own counsel. What Beth-aven is, for which the LXX translated house of ὢν, and who King Jareb is, which is interpreted avenger, we have discussed more fully above. In Beth-aven, then, that is, Bethel, the inhabitants of Samaria worshiped the golden cows; and he called them in mockery not calves of the male sex but cows, that is, females, so that, plainly, Israel worshiped not only calf gods but cow goddesses. And to show that by the cows of Beth-aven the one calf in Bethel is to be understood, he did not add, the people mourned over them, but over it, that is, over the golden calf. But if the people mourned, why did its temple-keepers exult over it? The Hebrews relate that the golden calves were taken away by the priests by theft, and that bronze gilded ones were put back in their place. When, therefore, the people mourned in the time of need and distress that the golden calves too, among the other gifts, had been sent by the king of Israel to the Assyrian kings, and above all to King Sennacherib, the temple-keepers exulted, because their fraud could in no way be proved or detected. And this is what he says: its temple-keepers, that is, the calf's, exulted over it in the glory of the people, that is, in the calf, which they held as their glory; because it had departed from it, that is, from the people, and had been carried over to the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Hosea","According to the spiritual understanding we must labor at how we may fit everything to the heretics. The cows of Beth-aven, or of the house of ὢν, which is interpreted toil, were worshiped by the heretics, who boasted that they dwelt in the keeping of the commandments of God, that is, in Samaria; and on the day of judgment, when judgment will spring up like bitterness upon the furrows of the field, the people will mourn over it, that is, over the calf, and over the perverse dogma which it thought to be God. But of the temple-keepers too he did not say, will exult, but, exulted, referring it to past time, over their glory, with which they had once fabricated their glorious things, because the people had departed from God, or because the very glory of God had departed from the people, by whom it had been abandoned.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.6":["And that we may know, they say, that this is what is meant, the following verse shows it clearly: For indeed it was carried into Assyria, a gift to the avenging king. And at once there follows: Shame will seize Ephraim, and Israel will be put to shame in his own will, or in his own counsel. For the fraud of the gilded calves, once detected, is reported to the king of Israel by letter, and the very thing by which they had thought to please is that by which they are put to shame most of all; and they offend those to whom they had sent the gifts, who think that this was accomplished not by the theft of the priests but by the fraud and counsel of the kings. We read in the book of Kings that Menahem, king of Israel, sent to Pul, king of the Assyrians, a thousand talents of silver, that his hand might be with him, that is, that he might give him help; and some think that among these the golden calves too were carried off. For Jareb in the present place Symmachus translated ὑπερμαχητῆ, that is, to the protector and defender.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And whatever the heretics say and compose in fine language, they send as gifts to their king, the devil, referring everything to him; whence shame will seize them for ever, and they will be put to shame in their own wills. A certain man has left it written in his commentaries, both above and in the present place, that King Jareb, that is, the avenger, is to be understood as Christ. This displeases us altogether. For it is impious that what according to the history will be understood of the Assyrian king should according to tropology be referred to Christ.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.7-8":["Samaria has made her king pass away like foam upon the face of the water, and the high places of the idol, the sin of Israel, will be destroyed. The bur and the thistle will come up upon their altars; and they will say to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us. LXX: Samaria has cast out her king like brushwood upon the face of the water, and the altars of ὦν, the sins of Israel, will be taken away. Thorns and thistles will come up upon their altars; and they will say to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us. For foam, which the LXX and Theodotion translated φρύγανον, that is, brushwood, dry grasses and withered twigs, which are got ready for the furnace and for burning, Symmachus put ἐπίζεμα, wishing to indicate the upper waters of a boiling pot, rising into foam and bubbles, which the Greeks call πομφόλυγας. As, then, the foam that is upon the water quickly dissolves, so the kingdom of the ten tribes will swiftly be ended; and the high places will perish, that is, bamoth, of which it is written: But still the people were sacrificing and burning incense on the high places. Now these high places are those of ὦν, or auen, that is, of the idol, which is interpreted useless; and this idol and useless thing is the sin of Israel.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.7":["But the Samaria of spiritual wickedness too, which had divided itself from the people of God, made its king pass swiftly away, namely the speech of the heretics and their doctrine, like foam, or brushwood, upon the face of the water: the one, even while it is seen, suddenly dissolves; the other is easy to lift from the surface of the waters and cast into the fire. Such are the heretics, swelling with foaming words and mixing precepts destined for burning into the baptism of Christ and into his words.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.8":["But once the idol and its high places have been scattered: The bur and the thistle will come up upon their altars. A sign of utter desolation, so that not even walls and the last traces of buildings are left. At that time they will say to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us. This the Lord says is to be fulfilled in the last time of the Jewish captivity. Whatever, then, is now said against the ten tribes, or against all Israel, let us know that it can also be transferred τυπικώς (typologically) to the whole people, so that when the Romans had taken Jerusalem and overthrown the temple, or when the day of judgment comes, as others suppose, they say, fearing with great horror, to the mountains: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us, preferring to die rather than to see the things that bring death.\n— Commentary on Hosea","All these will pass away, and the grand words on which they labored, which is what ὦν is interpreted, will at once be scattered, the words in which Israel sinned; and so great will be the desolation of that most wicked doctrine that thorns and thistles will come up upon their altars. That thorns and thistles spring up where there is no cultivation of the fields is plain to all. These are the thorns that choke the seed and do not let it grow, which spring up in the hand of the drunkard, and which Israel produced instead of the grape. For the Lord waited for it to produce grapes, and it produced thorns, or the wild vine, which has the likeness of the grape and twists the mouths of those who eat it with its bitter taste. Therefore, when the time of judgment comes and all things have been overthrown, they will say to the mountains, which they formerly thought lofty, and to their former teachers: Cover us; and to the hills: Fall upon us. But because with the mountains he put cover, and with the hills: fall, something more sacred must be explained. To the mountains, that is, to the saints, who have a true and not a feigned height, they will say: Cover us. For blessed are those whose iniquities are covered. And to the hills, which have no natural height, which they once thought had something of loftiness, they will speak: Fall upon us; for the mountains will cover, and the hills will fall; and these things will happen out of fear and incredible dread, through which both mountains and hills will be humbled.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.9-10":["From the days of Gibeah Israel has sinned; there they stood. The battle against the sons of iniquity will not overtake them in Gibeah. According to my desire I will chastise them; peoples will be gathered against them when they are chastised for their two iniquities. LXX: Since the hills have been, Israel has sinned; there they stood. War did not overtake them on the hill against the sons of iniquity; he came to chastise them, and peoples will be gathered against them when they have been chastised in their two iniquities. From the day on which Benjamin shamefully and cruelly killed the Levite's wife in the city of Gibeah, all Israel has sinned against me; not because it avenged the wrong and punished the crime with blood, but because it leapt to battle out of a husband's grief and would not avenge the sacrilege against its own God, in that it disregarded the ephod and teraphim in the house of Micah, which were worshiped as idols. Here, then, Israel stood; there it checked its step, so as to walk no further in the ways of the Lord. Therefore the battle, or captivity, will not overtake them on account of Gibeah, as they themselves suppose; for there they did well to pursue the sons of iniquity; but I will chastise them, he says, and instruct them with the whole will of my mind, and I will gather against them a multitude of peoples, because they committed two iniquities, avenging a man and neglecting the wrong done to their God. Or two iniquities, because they sinned first in the idols of Micah, second in the calves of Jeroboam. Or at least we can call the two calves of Samaria, in Dan and Bethel, the two iniquities, of which Jeremiah too speaks: My people have done two most evil things; they have forsaken the fountain of living water, and dug for themselves broken cisterns, which cannot hold water. These two iniquities broke out against two commandments of the Decalogue, in which it is said: I am the Lord your God; you shall have no other gods besides me.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.9":["The Seventy interpreted Gibeah as hills. From the days of the hills, then, Israel has sinned, when it abandoned the mountains of the Church and went down to the hills, or heretical slopes, thinking itself more learned than the Church is and to have found something more sublime: there they stood, that is, they persisted in error. And what follows, The battle will not overtake them on the hill, some have interpreted thus: because they have begotten sons of iniquity, and, withdrawing from the Church, have begun to be on the hills, when persecution comes the battle will not overtake them, since the devil is unwilling to attack his own. Others thus: Since from the days of the hills Israel has sinned, and stood there and could walk no further, must it not be overtaken by battle on the hills? Ought not churchmen to fight against it, to destroy them over the sons of iniquity? If they are overtaken and overcome, they will no longer be able to beget.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.10":["And at the same time the Lord promises that he will chastise and instruct them, so that, when the teachers have been overcome, their own disciples, whom they had deceived before, may be gathered against them and see the chastisement on account of the two iniquities: because they both abandoned the Church, the fountain of the Lord, and dug for themselves broken cisterns, namely the caves of the heretics, which cannot hold the waters, that is, the doctrine of the Savior and the mystery of baptism.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.11":["Ephraim is a heifer trained to love threshing, and I passed over the beauty of her neck; and I will mount upon Ephraim; Judah will plow, Jacob will break up the furrows for himself. LXX: Ephraim is a heifer — or a cow; for what in Hebrew is called egla, that is, μόσχος (calf) and δάμαλις (heifer), signifies both —; Ephraim, then, is a heifer taught to love contention; but I will come upon the beauty of her neck; I will lay upon Ephraim and I will keep silent about Judah; Jacob will be strengthened for himself. This passage, or rather everything that follows this section, is wrapped in great obscurities. Hence let both we who try to explain it and the prudent reader with us pay attention, so that, if not the truth, which is most difficult, we may at least be able to track down a conjecture of what is probable. The divine word has this custom, to express the truth of the history through tropology and metaphor. Ephraim, then, is like a cow or a heifer which from her youth has learned to tread the threshing floor and to drag iron wheels over the heaps of grain, so that the chaff may be separated from the wheat; and she has not only learned it, but through excessive habit has begun to love what she was taught. And I, he says, passed over the beauty of her neck. The Hebrew word abarthi, that is, I passed over, especially when it is said by God, always signifies blows and adversities. Indeed the destroyer too is recorded to have passed through in Egypt. Because, then, Ephraim, the cow or heifer, loves to tread the threshing floor, I, he says, passed over the beauty of her neck, and, laying a yoke on it, I tamed the swelling muscles of her neck. Why should I mention the yoke of the law? I myself mounted upon her, and while I was laboring thus, Judah, that is, the two tribes, began to cleave the fields with the plowshare and to turn the earth back into furrows. And while Ephraim bore the yoke and Judah plowed, Jacob broke up the furrows for himself. Here by Jacob, as distinct from Israel and Judah, let us understand the twelve tribes, in that they began to break the clods with hoes and to crumble the earth, so that, softened, it may receive the seed, and after a little while a fruitful crop may sprout. For threshing, or threshing floor, the Seventy translated contention, and the sense is: Because Ephraim will not receive the yoke of the law, I will pass over and mount upon the beauty of her neck, so that the contentious and wanton cow may learn to labor, which she does not want. But Judah will plow of his own accord, because he has the temple; and he abides in the law, so that all the twelve tribes vie with one another in preparing the fields for sowing. And what follows according to the same Seventy, I will lay upon Ephraim and I will keep silent about Judah; Jacob will be strengthened for himself, can have this sense: On Ephraim, who is contentious and will not bear the burdens of the law, I will lay captivity. Judah, however, I will leave for a little while and say nothing about him; but whoever, whether of Ephraim or of Judah, keeps my precepts will be strengthened for himself and will be called Jacob. According to ἀναγωγήν (anagogy) this can be said: that Ephraim, who was instructed in the law of God so as to tread the threshing floor of the Scriptures and to meditate on it day and night, began to love contention, to throw off the yoke of the law, and to contend against the churchmen to the subversion of the hearers. Hence the Lord will either press down with the yoke the neck that is lifted up and promises itself lofty things, or, passing over, will himself trample it with his feet and mount upon him, so that he may know that he has a Lord. But Judah, that is, the churchman, will plow, persevering in the work he has begun. Or: I will keep silent, he says, about Judah. It is not, after all, the healthy who need a physician, but those who are sick. But Jacob, which is interpreted supplanter, who daily supplants vices and sins, and receives his brother's birthright, and is heir of his father's possession, and sleeps in Bethel, which is interpreted house of God, will break up the furrows and the clods, so that the earth may receive the scattered seed in its soft bosom, and may yield a hundred measures of barley, or, as it is in the Hebrew, a hundredfold. For it is not to be believed that the patriarch Isaac devoted himself to barley and not to wheat. To this day the churchman Jacob breaks into pieces the clods of the history and the hardness of the letter, and divides them spiritually, so that they can yield spiritual fruits. This indeed we read that the Lord too did, breaking into pieces the five loaves of the law, which the people could not eat whole, so as to give them through the hands of the apostles to believers to eat. And what he says according to the Seventy, Jacob will be strengthened for himself, shows that everyone who labors labors for himself, so as to gain everlasting fruits.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.12":["Sow for yourselves in righteousness and reap in the mouth of mercy. Renew for yourselves the fallow ground; but it is the time of seeking the Lord, when he comes who will teach you righteousness. LXX: Sow for yourselves in righteousness, gather for yourselves the vintage of the fruit of life; light for yourselves the light of knowledge, for it is time; seek the Lord, until the fruits of righteousness come to you. He keeps up the metaphor taken from farmers, once he has begun it. He had said that Ephraim, a trained heifer, loves and cherishes the threshing of the threshing floor, and that he had mounted upon her neck, and that Judah had plowed, and that Jacob had broken up the furrows, or the clods. Now he commands them to sow for themselves through repentance, and to sow in righteousness, that is, in the law, and to reap in mercy, that is, in the grace of the Gospel; for there it is: An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth; here we read: Whoever strikes you on the right cheek, offer him the other as well. And when you have sown in righteousness and reaped in mercy, renew for yourselves the fertile fallow lands. And he gives the reasons why they should sow, why reap, why renew the fertile fallow lands. It is the time, he says, of seeking the Lord, when he comes, Christ and Savior, who will teach you righteousness, which you now hope for in the law: For Christ is the end of the law unto righteousness for everyone who does good. Where we said, reap in the mouth of mercy, the Seventy translated, gather for yourselves the vintage of the fruit of life; and reaping suits seed better than the vintage does; but the fruit of life is he who is also the tree of life. And where we put, renew for yourselves the fallow ground, they rendered, light for yourselves the light of knowledge, so that from the works and commandments of the law we may deserve to have knowledge, according to what we read in a certain book: You have desired wisdom; keep the commandments, and the Lord will give it to you. For whoever turns the commandments into works sows in righteousness and will reap from it the fruits of life. Whence we read elsewhere too: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes. And Isaiah says to the Lord: Your precepts are a light upon the earth. And in another place: From your commandments I have gained understanding. Those too who have been separated from the Church and take to themselves the false name of Christians are commanded to repent and to receive both testaments: to sow righteousness in the Old, to reap mercy in the New; and to light for themselves the light of knowledge, or to renew for themselves the fallow lands, and to seek the Lord, who can teach them true righteousness, and to destroy the false teachers, from whom they learn not righteousness but iniquity.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.13-14":["We have escaped, after a fashion, from the rugged places; now, spreading our sails for the deep, let us cross the sea of allegory. Because you have trusted, O Ephraim, in the ways, or in the chariots, of yours, of which it is written: Some in chariots and some in horses; but we will be magnified in the name of the Lord our God; and you have trusted in the multitude of your mighty men, whom you have strengthened for yourself by false knowledge; therefore a tumult and noise will rise up among your people. For whatever the heretics say has no voice that unfolds meanings, but only tumult, clamor and noise. And all your fortresses, or the things that are walled, will be laid waste —, for they have been fortified and built not with the testimonies of the Scriptures, but with the art of dialectic and the arguments of the philosophers —; as Salmana was once laid waste by Gideon, the mother being killed upon her sons. This history the eighty-second (83rd) psalm also recalls, where it records that among the other leaders of Midian there was also Salmana, saying: Do to them, O Lord, — no doubt meaning those who have entered into a pact or covenant against God —, as to Midian and to Sisera. And after the rest: Make, he says, their princes like Oreb and Zeeb, and Zebah and Salmana, all their princes who said: Let us possess the sanctuary of God by inheritance. For in this psalm too the leaders of the heretics are described, who have tried to claim the altar of God for themselves.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.13":["You have ploughed impiety, you have reaped iniquity; you have eaten the fruit of lying. LXX: Why do you keep silent about impieties, and you have gathered the vintage of his iniquities; you have eaten lying fruit. I am compelled against my will to discuss rather often the peculiarities of the Hebrew language; for we do not, after the manner of the rhetoricians, repeat maxims, build up words, and rouse our hearers or readers by declamations to our own praises. But what is obscure, above all in a foreign tongue, we strive to explain to men. Above, where we translated: Judah shall plough, in the Hebrew is read ieros, with iod as the first letter; the Seventy, thinking it uau, translated: and shall I keep silent? Now too in the Hebrew is written arasthem, which we have rendered you have ploughed, for which the Seventy translated: why do you keep silent; by the same error as above, rendering silence for ploughing. And this is the sense of what is said: Over the neck of the heifer Ephraim, contentious and loving to thresh, I passed; and I went up, so that, while Judah ploughed and broke up the furrows, Jacob might tread the threshing floor and bear the heat of the sun. And I admonished them to sow in righteousness and to reap in mercy, and to make for themselves fallow ground; and to know that that is the time for seeking the Lord in which he will come who will teach us righteousness. While I commanded this and willed to receive from the glad fallow lands the fruits of righteousness and mercy, they ploughed impiety, because they acted impiously against the Lord, forsaking the Creator and worshipping idols; and they reaped iniquity, receiving from evil seed evil fruits, from which they made not only bread baked under the ashes, but deceitful and lying bread, which would deceive the one who eats it with an empty hope. Of this kind are the heretics, who plough with composed speech and protect or keep silent about impiety, lest it seem to be impiety, but be believed to be piety. Hence, because they said in their heart: There is no God, they are corrupt and have become abominable, they also reaped, or gathered the vintage of, iniquities. For as avarice is the root of all evils, so the root of all sins and crimes is impiety, and whoever has ploughed, or sown, it will reap iniquities. Those therefore who have ploughed impiety and reaped iniquities have eaten the fruit of lying, preaching to the deceived peoples all things that are false, so that they do not seek the true bread which came down from heaven, but the bread of lying, which chokes and kills those who devour it.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Because you have trusted in your ways, in the multitude of your mighty men,\n\na tumult will rise up among your people, and all your fortresses will be laid waste; as Salmana was laid waste by the house of him who judged Baal in the day of battle, the mother dashed down upon the sons, so has Bethel done to you from the face of the malice of your wickednesses. LXX: Because you have hoped in your chariots, in the multitude of your strength, destruction will rise up among your people, and all your walled places will depart; as the princes of Salmana from the house of Jerubbaal in the days of war dashed the mother down upon the sons, so will I do to you, house of Israel, from the face of your wickednesses. Therefore you have eaten the fruit of lying, and in all that you set before yourselves an empty hope has deceived you; because you have trusted, O Ephraim, in the ways of your idolatry; for these are your ways; and in the multitude of your mighty men, having your hope not in God but in the strength of an army.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.14-15":["As, therefore, Salmana was laid waste by Jerubbaal, whom some wrongly think to be Jeroboam son of Nebat, who ruled over the ten tribes, and was laid waste, as is contained in the Hebrew, by Arbel, which signifies the very same thing as Jerubbaal, but in a shorter and more eloquent expression; so has Bethel done to you, O Israel, from the face of your wickednesses, Bethel, where you set up the golden calf and worshipped the gods of Egypt. For Bethel, which is interpreted house of God, the LXX translated house of Israel, which is not found at all in the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.14":["And so a tumult will rise up among your people, which in Hebrew is called Saon, that is, the noise and roaring of a howling army; and at their shouting all your fortresses will be laid waste; and the places you thought fortified and safe will lie open to the enemy and will be laid waste just as Salmana, the prince of the Midianites, was laid waste and crushed, who was killed by the house of Jerubbaal. There is no doubt that he means Gideon, who, from the fact that he destroyed the temple of Baal and cut down the grove, and Baal could not avenge himself, obtained the surname Jerubbaal, that is, let Baal avenge himself; so that, as Salmana killed the sons in front of their mothers, the mothers themselves also being killed afterwards, so too your sons, O Ephraim, may be killed in your presence, you who are yourself also to be slaughtered. We ask where it is written that Salmana killed the mother upon her sons; we read in the book of Judges, where Gideon speaks to the prince of Midian: As your sword has made many mothers childless, so your mother will be without sons among women.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.10.15":["And we understand that the heretics pass away like the first light of dawn, and their king, the devil, or the heresiarch; whom in their infancy, — when they believed in the Church, and were little ones, and were reckoned under the name of Christ —, God loved; and he called them out of the tribulation and darkness of Egypt. And he called them through the apostles and the teachers of the Church. And when they had been called, he says, by my leaders, they withdrew from their face and worshipped Baal-peor, that is, they served their own vices and lust, and afterwards they sacrificed to the Baalim and to images which they had fashioned for themselves. For each of the heretics has his own gods, and whatever they have feigned they set up as a carved and a molten image.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And what follows, So has Bethel done to you from the face of the malice of your wickednesses, is properly fitted to the leaders of the heretics: that Bethel has done this to them, the one they themselves call Bethel, that is, the house of God, and a false Church; so that the sense is: So will your Church do to you, which you call the house of God; but from the time it is held by you, it must be called Beth-aven, that is, house of the idol, because of the multitude of your wickednesses.\n— Commentary on Hosea","As the morning passes, the king of Israel has passed away; because Israel was a child, and I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son; they called them, so they went away from their face. They sacrificed to the Baalim and offered sacrifice to images. LXX: In the morning they were cast away, the king of Israel was cast away; because Israel was a little child, and I loved him, and out of Egypt I called his sons. As I called them, so they themselves went away from my face. They sacrificed to the Baalim and burned incense to carved images. He sets out the same sense under different figures. He who had said above: Samaria has made her king pass away like foam upon the face of the water, because foam and bubbles floating on the surface of the waters are quickly dissolved, now puts the same thing under another likeness. For as the rising of the dawn, the first light and the beginning of the day, which is called morning, passes quickly between the nearness of night and of the sun, so that the night ends and the day grows bright, so the king of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes, will swiftly pass away.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.1":["That what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Out of Egypt I have called my son. Let those who deny the truth of the Hebrew volumes answer where this is read in the LXX translators. And when they have not found it, we shall tell them that it is written in the prophet Hosea, as the copies too can prove which we have lately published. But we can also relieve this passage in another way, for the sake of the contentious, whose custom the apostle Paul denies that he or the Church of Christ has; and we bring forward a testimony from Numbers, where Balaam says: God has called him out of Egypt, his glory is like that of the unicorn.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And he sets out the benefits that God conferred upon him. While he was a boy, he says, and a little child, and was held captive in Egypt, I loved him so much that I sent my servant Moses, and called out of Egypt my son, of whom I said in another place: Israel is my firstborn son.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Where we have said: Out of Egypt I called my son, the Seventy translated: Out of Egypt I called his sons, which is not found in the Hebrew; and no one doubts that Matthew took his testimony from this place according to the Hebrew truth. Therefore let those who disparage our translation produce the scripture from which the evangelist took this testimony and interpreted it of the Lord Savior, when he was brought back from Egypt into the land of Israel. And when they cannot find it, let them stop wrinkling the forehead, knitting the brow, curling the nose and snapping their fingers. The emperor Julian, in the seventh volume of what he vomited forth against us, that is, against the Christians, slanders this place, and says that what was written of Israel the evangelist Matthew transferred to Christ in order to mock the simplicity of those of the Gentiles who had believed. To him we will answer briefly: first, that Matthew published his Gospel in Hebrew letters, which none could read except those who were of the Hebrews. Therefore he did not do it in order to mock the heathen. But if he wished to mock the Hebrews, he was either foolish or unskilled: foolish, if he invented an open lie; unskilled, if he did not understand of whom these things were said. The volume itself clears him of folly, since it is composed prudently and in order; unskilled we cannot call him, whom we know from other testimonies of the Scriptures to have had knowledge of the law. It remains for us to say that the things which go before τυπικῶς (typologically) in others are referred to Christ according to truth and fulfillment; which we know the Apostle did with the two mountains, Sinai and Zion, and with Sarah and Hagar. For it is not that Sinai is not a mountain and Zion is not; that Sarah did not exist and Hagar did not exist, because the apostle Paul referred these to the two covenants. So, therefore, this which is written: Israel was a little child, and I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son, is indeed said of the people of Israel, which is called out of Egypt, which is loved, which at that time, after the error of idolatry, was called as an infant and a little child; but it is perfectly referred to Christ. For Isaac too was a type of Christ, in that he himself carried the wood of his coming death; and Jacob, in that he had as wives Leah, sore of eye, and Rachel, the beautiful. In Leah, who was the elder, we understand the blindness of the Synagogue, in Rachel the beauty of the Church; and yet those who were in part types of the Lord Savior are not to be believed to have done in his type all the things they are related to have done. For a type indicates a part; if it went before as a whole in the type, it would no longer be a type, but ought to be called the truth of history. These things we have said briefly, as is fitting in commentaries; now let us return to the rest.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.2":["And because Israel is indeed spoken of in the singular but understood in the plural, as are people, Ephraim and Judah, since in the singular number there is a great number contained in this number, he recalls the old history: that he called them through Moses and Aaron, who called them to go out of Egypt; but they, called by them, withdrew from their face, turning their backs on him and showing the hardness of their mind by the gesture of their body. Nor was it enough for them to despise those who called them, unless they also sacrificed to Baal and burned incense to carved images, or idols. We read that they first worshipped Baal under Ahab king of Israel, who took as his wife Jezebel, daughter of the king of Sidon, and brought the Babylonian and Phoenician idol over into Samaria. Therefore he joins in one discourse sins distant in time: how first they were called out of Egypt and named sons, then in the wilderness withdrew from God, worshipping Baal-peor rather than God, and afterwards in the holy land served the Baalim and Ashtaroth and the other idols.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.3-4":["Otherwise: I loved them so much, and was so merciful a shepherd, that I myself carried the sick sheep on my shoulders; but they did not know that I was healing them by my passion, and that I, who am the lover of all men, was drawing them to believe with the bonds of love, according to what is written in the Gospel: No one comes to me unless the Father who sent me draws him. And they thought my light yoke most heavy; and I inclined toward them, leaving the kingdoms of the heavens, so as to eat with them, having taken the form of a man; or I gave them my body to eat, I myself both the food and the guest at table.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.3":["And I was like a foster-father to Ephraim, I carried them in my arms, and they did not know that I was healing them. With the cords of Adam I will draw them, with the bonds of love. And I will be to them as one who lifts the yoke upon their jaws, and I inclined toward him that he might eat. LXX: And I bound Ephraim together, I took him up upon my arm; and they did not know that I was healing them in the corruption of men; I stretched them out in the bonds of my love. And I will be to them as a man giving slaps upon his jaws; and I will look upon him, prevailing over him. The Hebrew and the edition of the Seventy translators differ greatly from each other. Let us try, therefore, to weave the history according to the Hebrews, and ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy) according to the LXX. He who had said above: Israel was a child, and I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son, and afterwards added what wickedness he committed: They sacrificed to the Baalim and offered sacrifice to images, now relates with what love he loved Israel, according to what we read in Deuteronomy: The Lord your God carried you, as a man is wont to carry his little son, in all the way by which you walked, until you came to this place. And in another place: He spread out his wings and took him up, and carried him on his shoulders. I, he says, who was a father, became a foster-father, and I myself carried my little one in my arms, lest he be harmed in the wilderness; and lest he be terrified either by heat or by darkness, by day I was a cloud, by night a pillar of fire; so that those whom I had protected I might enlighten and heal with my light; and when they had sinned and made for themselves the head of a calf, I gave room for repentance, and they did not know that I was healing them, and closing over the wound of idolatry in the space of forty years, and restoring them to their former health.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Let us pass to the spiritual understanding, according to the Seventy translators only; lest, if we wish to expound both, according to the history and according to ἀναγωγὴν, we stretch out the size of the book. While they sacrificed to the Baalim which they had fashioned from their own heart, and, when I called, fled from my face, — for so it stands in the Seventy —, I, the most merciful Lord, bound the feet of Ephraim, that they might not flee farther from me; for this is what συνεπόδισα signifies. And I bound them with the testimonies of the Scriptures and the disputation of the teachers of the Church, so that he might hold them, bound through patience, in his arms, while they did not understand that the patience of God was the occasion of their salvation.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.4":["And I healed them because of the cords and bonds of love with which I had bound Abraham, Isaac and Jacob to myself. For in place of Adam, Aquila and Symmachus and the Seventy and Theodotion translated all men, so as to say: with the cords of men I will draw them, with the bonds of love. And what follows, I will be to them as one who lifts the yoke, for which Symmachus translated: and they thought that I was putting a yoke upon their jaw, is taken in two ways: either I took from them the yoke of all the nations round about, or they thought my law a most heavy weight of a yoke. And I gave him manna as food in the desert, for him to eat; for this is what he says: and I inclined toward him that he might eat; for which Symmachus translated: and I inclined food toward him. Not that God inclined toward him, but that he made the food of manna incline toward him.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Hence, in the corruption of men, that is, of the teachers who had deceived them, I extended the warmth of faith to those contracted by the cold of faithlessness, and bound them, resisting as they were, with the bonds of my love. And because they did not run of their own accord, but were dragged along bound with ropes, I struck their jaws a little with slaps, not punishing but correcting and amending. A judge tears the flesh, tortures with cords, torments with scourges and fire. But he who is a father strikes his wanton son with the palm of the hand. And he finely did not say: I will be beating them with slaps, but: as a man striking the jaws with the hand. And God strikes his straying sons with the threat of punishments, with the reading of the Gospel and the testimonies of the prophets. And when he has so struck on the jaw as to knock the bread and doctrine of the heretics out of the polluted mouth, then he looks upon him, while the beaten son says to him: Look upon me and have mercy on me. And again: Look and hear me, O Lord my God. And when he has looked upon him, he will prevail, or it will profit them, that is, he will overcome the adversaries and make servants out of runaways. Or he will give true and sweet food to those who formerly devoured the lies and most bitter food of the heretics.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.5-6":["According to the Seventy, Ephraim dwelt in Egypt, saying that he held the holy land and the Church of the Lord Savior; but by vices and sins and the perversity of his faith he always lingered in Egypt. Because, therefore, he dwelt in Egypt, the great mind, the Assyrian, will be his king; for he would not return to the Church, and, having lost power, that is, Christ, who is the power of God and the wisdom of God, he has always lived in languor; and he was weak, subject to all the demons and passions; therefore the sword, that is, spiritual knowledge or the word of a churchman, laying waste and subduing, will always be at work in his cities, which he impiously built against God; and the sword itself will rest in his hands, so that, slain by another, he cannot slay another nor lift his hand against the adversary. Finally they will receive, and will devour, according to their own counsels.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.5":["He will not return into the land of Egypt, and Assyria itself is his king, because they would not be converted. The sword has begun in his cities and will consume his chosen ones; and it will eat their heads, and my people will hang in suspense for my return. But a yoke will be laid upon them all together, which will not be taken away. LXX: Ephraim will dwell in Egypt, and Assyria itself is his king, because he would not be converted. And the sword has grown weak in his cities, and has rested in his hands, and they will eat of their own thoughts; and his people hangs from its sojourning, and God will be angry over his precious things, and will not exalt him. When he says: he will not return into the land of Egypt, he shows that he desires to return but cannot go. For Israel desired to return, begging for help from the Egyptians; but he was taken in possession by the Assyrian, who captured him and ruled over him by the right of the victor; and he suffered this because he would not be converted nor repent. Or at least let us say that he returned into the land of Egypt when in the holy land he worshipped the gods of the Egyptians; or it is to be taken in the sense in which it was said above: They called upon Egypt, they went away to the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.6-7":["And so the sword has begun in his cities, or will rush in, as Aquila translated, or will wound, as Symmachus rendered. And see how great is the weight of miseries: not that fields or possessions and the countryside are laid waste, but that the enemy enters the midst of the cities and consumes his chosen ones, or his arms, as Symmachus translated, which in Hebrew is called baddau. And when the sword has consumed the chosen and the princes, or the strength of the army, and has devoured either their heads or their counsels, so that they can find no protection at all, then the wretched common people, who would not return to me, will wait for my return to them. And they will repent late, while the enemies lay everything waste. And so, because great sins must be punished with great torments, he will lay upon those of the people who are left, — their king and princes having been cut down by the Assyrian sword, — the most heavy yoke of servitude, and he will lay it on them all together, which will not be taken away according to the letter, unless it is taken away spiritually in Christ.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.7":["But the unhappy people and the untaught crowd will sigh for their ancient homeland and will feel themselves to be captive, or will hang in suspense in their sojourning, not knowing what to do and ignorant where to turn. And God is angry over their precious things, namely the gold and silver which they had received from him, of which we have often spoken, and he will by no means set free him who has fallen by his own fault. This according to the LXX; and we will fit the same sense to the Hebrew as well.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.8-9":["To the heretics also, and to the people deceived by them, the Lord says that unless they repent they will be set as Admah and Zeboiim, so as to have no hope of salvation. Again, as a most merciful father, he says that he changes his sentence and repents of having said such things, so as to call them too to conversion and to repentance. I will not act, he says, in my fury, I will not destroy Ephraim. As far, he says, as lies in me, as far as I desire, if he corrects his error by the truth, if he loves me more than the leaders of heresy; God, indeed, am I, and not man: I will stretch out my hand to the fallen, I will call the wandering to salvation. And because I am holy, for that reason I will not enter the city, that is, the conventicles and cities of the heretics. Those who go out of their cities I gladly receive; into their cities I will not enter.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.8":["How shall I give you up, Ephraim, shall I protect you, Israel? How shall I give you up like Admah, set you as Zeboiim? My heart is turned within me, my repentance is troubled together. I will not execute the fury of my wrath, I will not turn to destroy Ephraim, because I am God and not man; the Holy One in your midst, and I will not enter the city. LXX: What shall I do to you, Ephraim, shall I protect you, Israel? What shall I do to you? I will set you as Admah and as Zeboiim; my heart is turned in him; my repentance is troubled together. I will not act according to the wrath of my fury. I will not abandon Ephraim to be destroyed, because I am God, and not man; holy in you, and I will not enter the city. In that place where we and the LXX translated: shall I protect you, Israel? in the Hebrew is written amaggenach, which Aquila translated ὅπλῳ κυκλώσω σε, that is: I will surround you with a shield. And while we thought this was to be understood in a good sense and signified protection, from the edition of Symmachus a contrary sense is set before us, for he says ἐκδώσω σε, that is: I will hand you over. From the translation of Theodotion too, not favorable but adverse things are shown: ἀφοπλίσω σε, which signifies: I will strip you, and take from you the ὅπλον, that is, the shield with which I had protected you before; and this sense better suits the Lord when he threatens. What he says, then, is this: since they would not be converted, and Assyria became their king, and the sword will devour cities and princes and people, and a yoke will be laid upon them which will not be taken away from them; and because the sentence seemed harsh, leaving them no room at all for repentance, now God speaks to Israel with a parent's affection: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? how shall I strip you of my help? What shall I do to you? By what art shall I correct you? With what remedy shall I be able to heal you? I will set you as Admah and Zeboiim, which are two of the five cities, as we read in Genesis: Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim and Bela, which is Zoar, and in the Syrian tongue it is called Zoara. I will therefore set you and turn you into a wilderness, and destroy you down to cinders and ashes, as I destroyed Admah and Zeboiim. And when he had pronounced a harsh, nay a cruel sentence, again he is overcome by mercy through a parent's affection, and softens the severity of the judgment with a father's tenderness. For he says: My heart is turned within me; my repentance is troubled together. As soon as I said that I would set Ephraim and Israel as Admah and Zeboiim, my bowels were moved.\n— Commentary on Hosea","But if we wish to read: How shall I give you up, Ephraim, shall I protect you, Israel? it is to be understood thus: What shall I do to you? Are you worthy of protection, you who have done such things? It must also be noted that where something is said against Judah, that is, the people of God, it is not Admah and Zeboiim that are set down, but Sodom and Gomorrah. For we read in Isaiah: Hear the law of God, you princes of Sodom; and: Give heed to the word of the Lord, you people of Gomorrah. In the Gospel too, of the city which has not received the apostles, when they shake off the dust of their feet, it is said that in the day of judgment it will be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city. And the prophetic word is directed to Jerusalem: Sodom has been justified by you. We are therefore given ground to suspect that Sodom and Gomorrah were the leaders in sin, and that Admah and Zeboiim followed their example; because the mighty will suffer torments mightily; and the servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it will be beaten with many stripes. Hence churchmen too, if they are held fast by the same crimes as the heretics, will be subject not to the torments of Admah and Zeboiim, which are the lesser, but to those of Sodom and Gomorrah, which are said to be of greater crimes.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.9-10":["This which he said, I will not enter the city, and what follows according to the LXX, I will walk after the Lord, some have interpreted so as to say that the people answered the Lord, and that the sense is: Because your heart is turned within you, and you have not dealt with us according to our sins, but imitate your own clemency, and do not punish our offenses, and promise that you, holy and merciful, will dwell in our midst, therefore I too will not enter the city of evil men, nor will I be of the number of sinners; but I will walk after the Lord our God. The Hebrews, however, explain it thus, in the person of God: I will not abandon you, I will not go to another nation; nor will I enter another city.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.9":["I repented of destroying for ever my people of old; therefore I will not act according to the fury of my anger, nor will I be changed from my clemency so as to destroy Ephraim. For I do not strike in order to destroy for ever, but in order to amend. My cruelty is the occasion of repentance and of mercy. God, indeed, am I, and not man. Man punishes for this end, that he may destroy; God chastises for this end, that he may amend. The Holy One in your midst, and I will not enter the city, that is: I am not one of those who dwell in cities, who live by human laws, who think cruelty to be justice, for whom the highest law is the highest malice; but my law and my justice is to save those who have been corrected. We can also say otherwise: since Cain the parricide first built a city in the name of his son Enoch, the Lord does not enter a city of this kind, which was built by crime and blood and parricide.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.10-11":["The Hebrews refer these things to the coming of Christ, whom they hope is yet to come. We show that they are already accomplished, because both from Egypt and from the Assyrians, that is, from the east and the west, and from the north and the south, they have come, and daily they come, who sit at table with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. And we can call sons of the sea, or of the waters, those who have been caught in the Lord's dragnet and drawn out of the sea of this world. And when they have been taken from death into life, they will be settled in their houses, which the Gospel calls barns, in which the chosen grain, separated from the chaff, is stored. It is said to be the nature of lions that when they growl and roar, all living creatures tremble, and, their step fixed, cannot move: so great is the terror, so great the dread. And so the Lord too, when he roars and thunders like a lion and utters his voice, all the birds and every winged creature will shudder, and they will go to their nests, that is, to their houses, in which the Lord will dwell with them. Let us also say it another way: when the true lion growls, the false lion, who according to the apostle Peter is our adversary, will at once fall silent, and every perverse dogma will be unable to open its mouth; and those who had before been taken captive by him, set free by the roar of the lion and its terrible threat, will follow the Lord their God. Then the sons of the sea, or of the waters, will fear, who had been begotten in the bitterness and in the salt waters of the heretics; and taking wings, they will fly like birds out of Egypt, and like a dove from the land of the Assyrians, and they will say: Who will give me wings like a dove, and I will fly and be at rest? So that those who had labored among the heretics may rest in the Church, and dwell in their houses, from which they had been led away by error. We know that Egypt, that is, Mesraim, is interpreted tribulation and distress; the Assyrians also, directing, or, as we judge better, rebuking. From these, therefore, the heretics will be set free when they begin to dwell in their houses and say to their evil parents: Your house will be left to you deserted.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.10":["They will walk after the Lord; he will roar like a lion, for he himself will roar; and the sons of the sea will tremble, and they will fly like birds out of Egypt, and like a dove from the land of the Assyrians; and I will settle them in their houses, says the Lord. LXX: I will walk after the Lord; as a lion he will roar, for he himself will roar; and the sons of the waters will tremble, and they will fly like a bird out of Egypt, and like a dove from the land of the Assyrians; and I will settle them in their houses, says the Lord. When the Lord promises prosperous things, the people will be converted to him, and will walk after the Lord, because the Lord will roar like a lion. Of this the prophet Amos also makes mention: The Lord will roar from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will utter his voice. And he roars when he says: as Admah, I will set you as Zeboiim. And when he has roared, then the sons of the sea will tremble, or of the waters, as the LXX translated. For the word maim, which is written with three letters, mem, iod, mem, if it is read maim, means waters; if meiam, it is understood of the sea.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.11.12":["Ephraim has surrounded me in his denial, and the house of Israel in deceit; but Judah went down as a witness with God, and faithful with the saints. LXX: Ephraim has surrounded me in falsehood, and the house of Israel and Judah in impiety; now God has known them, and the holy people of God will be called. The Hebrews hand down a tale of this kind: at the exodus of Israel from Egypt, when on one side the mountain, on another the Red Sea, and on another the army of Pharaoh hemmed them in, and the people were held shut in, while the other tribes despaired of safety and wished either to return to Egypt or to fight, Judah alone faithfully entered the sea, and for this reason he also merited to receive the kingdom. And this, they say, is what is now said: Judah, a witness of the words of God, his supporter and avenger, went down with God into the sea, and among the saints was most faithful, so as to believe the words of God who commanded. That is what they say. Let us follow the sequence of the explanation we began: that Ephraim, the royal tribe, and the house of Israel, the people that served the royal tribe, surrounded him, and surrounded him in denial or falsehood, since they deny the Lord and confess idols. But Judah, that is, the two tribes, which had the temple, the law, the prophets, and kept the precepts of the law, were witnesses, and walking with God, and faithful with the saints. By the saints we can mean either the angels, or the patriarchs and prophets and the others who served at God's command. For in comparison with that time at which these things were said, when Ephraim had gone utterly astray and Israel had been deceived into the worship of idols, Judah alone had remained to be occupied in the worship and testimonies of God, and could go down with him, or be strong with the strong one. For rad means both descent and strength; for which Aquila translated ἐπικράτειαν (mastery). According to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), the heretics surround God in falsehood, nay rather in denial. For whatever they speak is denial, nay rather falsehood; and they surround him in deceit, or in the impiety of the house of Israel, while they compose with artful speech everything that they feign, and speak impiety against God. But Judah, that is, the churchman, is not proud, is not puffed up with heretical swelling, but is humbled with God, and with the choir of the saints is faithful and strong, while he builds his house upon the rock, which no storm can shake. The LXX translated very differently: that Ephraim and the house of Israel and Judah surrounded God in falsehood and in impiety, and that God is of such great clemency that he does not cut off from them the hope of salvation, but knows them and is ready to call the people a holy people and the people of God, which is now perverted by impiety. According to tropology also: that the Lord wills both heretics and sinful churchmen to be saved, and all to be called by his name. But he who is truly holy does not surround God in falsehood but in truth, as the psalmist says: You are mighty, O Lord, and your truth is round about you.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.1":["Ephraim feeds on the wind and follows the burning heat; all day long he multiplies lying and devastation, and he has entered into a covenant with the Assyrians; and he was carrying oil into Egypt. LXX: But Ephraim, a most evil spirit, has pursued the burning heat; all day long he has multiplied vain and empty things, and he has made a covenant with the Assyrians; and he was trading oil in Egypt. Sacred history relates that Menahem, king of Israel, having made peace with Assyria, asked help of the Egyptians; and this is what is now said: that Ephraim feeds on winds, that is, deceives himself with vain hope, and follows καύσωνα, that is, the burning heat, and goes to the south, and does nothing else all day long but mock himself. And while he runs about this way and that, he prepares devastation and overthrow for his own cities. Is it not devastation and lying to have made a covenant with the Assyrians, and to have carried oil into Egypt? Συνεκδοχικῶς, the whole from a part: namely, that he sent gifts to the Egyptians. Although some think that oil is by no means produced in Egypt, and that the most precious oil was sent by Ephraim, whose land, Samaria, is most fertile in oil. Moreover, according to tropology, all heretics are held fast by the most evil spirit of the demons, of which it is written also in the Apostle: Against the spiritual things of wickedness in the heavenly places. And the unclean spirit, when it has gone out of a man and has found no rest, joins to itself seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and returns to its former house. With a most evil spirit, therefore, they follow καύσωνα, that is, dryness, or the burning wind, which is hostile to flowers and destroys everything that sprouts; all day long he pursues empty and vain things, nor is he content with his own error, but he multiplies many disciples, nay rather companions of his vanity and error. He also makes a covenant with the Assyrians, whose prince is the great mind, so that whatever he has invented he may seem to have feigned wisely, with that wisdom which is destroyed by God, which the Apostle too commands us to avoid, saying: See that no one catches you through philosophy and empty seduction, according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the world. But he also carries oil into Egypt, or buys the wisdom of Egypt, desiring to mix with the dogmas of the Church the oil of anointing with which the prophets and priests were anointed, with which kings too were anointed. This oil the saints also have, of whom it is said: Your sons like young olive shoots around your table. And the good olive tree into which our wild olive has been grafted. But although the heretics try to mix lying with the truth, oil cannot be combined with water and the other moist and liquid things. The truth is always on top, and lying below. All other kinds, that is, heresies, which do not have, as we have said, the oil of truth, can be mixed with one another, and from many be made one body. But the oil of these, which is carried down into Egypt and descends from the holy land to the realms of Pharaoh, the prophet detests, saying: Let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.2-5":["We can understand Judah as the churchman, who is rebuked by the Lord because he is not mindful of his former benefits toward him, but daily joins sins to sins; and he sets forth what those benefits are. When the Church, he says, was bringing you to birth in faith, you supplanted your brother, the Jew or the Gentile, and received his birthright; and in your strength you were directed with the angel, either overcoming the adverse powers, or strengthened by the blessings of the angel, who is himself God; and you prevailed in figure against the angel, so that you might prevail against men, and you were strengthened. And when you had won the victory, you wept and besought the angel of the Lord, and, remembering your old sins, you found him in Bethel, that is, in the house of God, which is the Church, or in the house of ὢν, of sorrow and tears and repentance. And that we might know who this Judah was, there, he says, he spoke with us, that is, with us Christians; and from that time to the present day we are reckoned by the name of Christ, and with him directing us we are corrected.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.2":["Therefore the judgment of God is with Judah, and visitation upon Jacob; according to his ways and according to his devices he will repay him. In the womb he supplanted his brother, and in his strength he was directed with the angel; and he prevailed over the angel and was strengthened; he wept and besought him; in Bethel he found him, and there he spoke with us, and the Lord God of hosts. The Lord is his memorial; and you will turn to your God; keep mercy and judgment, and hope in your God always. LXX: And there is judgment for the Lord with Judah, that he may punish Jacob; according to his ways and according to his devices he will repay him. In the womb he supplanted his brother, and in his labor he prevailed with God, and he was strengthened with the angel, and was able; they wept and entreated me; in the house of ὢν they found me, and there it was spoken to them. But the Lord God almighty will be his memorial, and you will turn in your God; keep mercy and judgment; and draw near to your God always. Since Ephraim once fed on winds and followed lying, and came to such madness that he wavered in doubt between two hostile nations, making peace with the Assyrians and carrying oil into Egypt, now all my judgment is with Judah, and visitation upon Jacob. Now he calls visitation scourges and punishments, so that he who repaid Ephraim what he deserved may repay Judah too, who sprang from the seed of Jacob, according to his ways and according to his devices; for he was not merely deceived by chance error and brought down by human frailty, but sought out and devised the things in which to sin and fall.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.3":["Moreover he sets forth how great benefits Judah, that is, Jacob, received, and the son is named in the father, and he recalls the ancient history, so that both the mercy of God toward Jacob and his hardness against God may be known. While he was still in Rebekah's womb, he supplanted his brother Esau, not indeed by his own strength, since he could not perceive it, but by the mercy of God, who knows and loves those whom he has predestined. And not only did he supplant his brother in the womb, but in his strength also he was directed with the angel, when at the torrent Jacob fought against the angel all night long. And because he was directed with the angel, he therefore received the name εὐθυτάτου, which in Hebrew is called isar, that is, of one directing, or directed.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.4-5":["And he prevailed, he says, against the angel; and by the blessing of him whom he had overcome he was strengthened. He also wept and besought him, that is, the angel, saying: I will not let you go unless you bless me. And when, on the advice of his father and mother, he was fleeing to Mesopotamia, he found the same angel in Bethel, who spoke to him; he spoke with us, that is, in the father he spoke to the sons also, and in Jacob he loved Judah too; and from that time down to the present the memory of his name, which was imposed on him by the angel and by God, endures.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.4":["Where the Hebrew has: he wept and besought him; in Bethel he found him, and there he spoke with us, we read in the common edition: they wept and besought me, in the house of ὢν they found me, and there it was spoken to them; ὢν is interpreted sorrow. If anyone, therefore, weeps, and repents, and entreats the Lord, he will find him in the sorrow of his heart, and when he has called upon him, he will hear him answering him.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.6":["Since these things are so, you too, O Judah, imitate your parent: weep and beseech the Lord of hosts, and turn to him. Keep mercy and judgment, and when you have done both, hope in your God always, advancing by good works to greater things.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Therefore, O churchman, you who are called Judah, and confessing, turn daily through repentance to your Lord; and if perhaps you have sinned, imitate the prophet who says: I have labored in my groaning; every night I will wash my bed, I will water my couch with my tears. And let it not suffice to have said this, but keep the commandments of God, show mercy to others, that you yourself may obtain mercy. Judge true judgment, so that in what you judge, judgment may be passed on you. And hope in your God always, or draw near to your God continually, so that, advancing in virtue at all times, you may draw near to your God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.7":["Canaan: in his hand is a deceitful balance; he has loved calumny; and Ephraim said: Yet I have become rich, I have found an idol for myself; all my labors will not find for me the iniquity which I have sinned. LXX: Canaan: in his hand is a balance of iniquity; he has loved to oppress by power; and Ephraim said: Yet I have become rich, I have found rest for myself; all his labors will not be found for him, because of the iniquities in which he has sinned. He had admonished Judah to turn to the Lord his God, and to keep mercy and judgment, and to hope in the Lord always, or to draw near to him continually. Now the discourse turns to Ephraim, that is, to the ten tribes, whom he calls Canaan, according to what Daniel says to the elder, who was certainly of the seed of Judah: Seed of Canaan and not of Judah, beauty has deceived you. And in Ezekiel we read it said to Jerusalem: Your father was an Amorite, and your mother a Hittite. And in Isaiah it is said to the tribe of Judah: Hear the word of the Lord, you princes of Sodom; perceive with your ears the law of our Lord, you people of Gomorrah. In the last little verse of Zechariah too we read: And there will no longer be a Canaanite in the house of the Lord. Now he says that Canaan, that is, Ephraim, has in his hand a deceitful balance, or an unjust one, though Scripture commands: Let your weights be just; and that he not only has an unjust and deceitful balance, but loves calumny and oppresses men by power.\n— Commentary on Hosea","Hence this very thing is also referred to the heretics. For Canaan can be interpreted: as if moving. And note that he said: as if moving, not moving. They are moving those whom they have deceived; as if moving, those whom they have tempted. But because these are founded upon the rock, they can be shaken by no whirlwind, nor change the footprint of their feet. In the hand of a Canaan of this sort, that is, in his works, there is a deceitful and unjust balance; for whatever the heretic speaks does not have the justice of God, and is full of deceit and frauds; whence they also love calumny, while they press down the innocent, or oppress them by power.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.8":["And lest we should think that some other Canaan is to be understood, he states more plainly who this Canaan is. Ephraim said: Yet I have become rich; and the sense is: it makes no difference whence I possess it, so long as I possess it. Very many suffer from this disease, of whom it is written: Riches gathered unjustly will be vomited up. For the ransom of a man's soul is his own riches. Hence we are commanded to make ourselves friends of the unrighteous mammon, who can receive us into the eternal tabernacles. But Ephraim, who glories and says: Yet I have become rich, I have found an idol for myself, or ἀνωφελὲς, that is, auen, which does not profit its possessor, has sweated with vain labor. Just as the belly is god to the glutton and the luxurious, so the avaricious man too worships the idol of gold, and says in his heart: I have found what I was seeking; but he will hear: Fool, this night your soul will be snatched from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be? And when once, I will not say the glitter, but the blindness of riches has seized his eyes, he says: all my labors will not find my iniquity, in which I have sinned. And the sense is: whatever I sin, if I have riches, it cannot be imputed to me by those who need my help, according to what is written: And he who does iniquitous things is blessed. For the rich have many friends.\n— Commentary on Hosea","The poor churchman is oppressed by the wordiness and the subtleties of the heretics, who, after they have deceived some, are accustomed to say: we have become rich, we have a very great multitude; a crowd of disciples follows us; we have found an idol, or refreshment, for ourselves. For heresies are put together chiefly for this reason, that they may devour the houses of widows, who are always learning and never arrive at the knowledge of the truth. And beautifully: I have found, he says, an idol for myself. For all the fictions of the heretics are idols and images of the Gentiles; nor do they differ much in impiety, though they seem to differ in name. And they are accustomed to say: whatever I do, whatever I perform, cannot be imputed to me; for I have my riches, the arguments of the philosophers; I have a multitude of people, and whoever beholds it will not think that I sin.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.9":["And I am the Lord your God from the land of Egypt; I will yet make you sit in tabernacles as in the days of festivity; and I have spoken upon the prophets, and I have multiplied visions, and in the hand of the prophets I have been likened. LXX: But I am the Lord your God, I brought you out of the land of Egypt; I will yet make you dwell in tabernacles, as in the days of solemnity, and I will speak to the prophets, and I have multiplied visions, and in the hands of the prophets I have been likened. You indeed have sinned so greatly that you rejoiced in crime, and thought the multitude of your sins to be riches, and said: I have become rich, I have found an idol for myself; all my labors will not be able to find my sins. But I, the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt when you were serving Pharaoh and building cities of mud and straw, still grant you room for repentance, and by the greatness of my promises I exhort you to return to me; for I will yet make you sit in tabernacles as in the days of festivity. He calls the day of festivity Tabernacles, in the seventh month, on the fifteenth day of the month, when the sons of Israel went out of Egypt. Just as, he says, at that time I set you free from Egypt, and you dwelt in tabernacles, hastening to the holy land and to the place of the temple, so now also I will bring you out of tribulation and distresses and the impending captivity, if only you do what I have commanded.\n— Commentary on Hosea","To those also who have been led astray by the heretics it is said that they should return to the Lord, who prefers the repentance of the sinner to his death; for he is the one who brought them out of the land of Egypt, that is, out of the darkness and error of the Gentiles. And lest perhaps, remembering their sin, they should return too slowly: I will yet, he says, make you sit in tabernacles, as in the days of festivity; so that what baptism does, repentance may do, and they may dwell in the tabernacles of the Savior, that is, in the Churches, of which it is said: Planted in the house of the Lord, they will flourish in the courts of the house of our God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.10":["For it is I who through all the prophets and various kinds of visions have been likened to men, and have called you to repentance. Is it not of human likeness when Moses, raising his hands on high, prays that Joshua may conquer Amalek, and the mysteries of the cross are shown in him? Is not God likened in the hands of the prophets when Jonah is in the deep three days and nights, that he may signify the Lord rising from the underworld on the third day? And we read of visions multiplied in all the prophets, when Ezekiel sees God sitting like a charioteer above the cherubim. And Isaiah: I saw, he says, the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and two seraphim around him crying to one another: holy, holy, holy, Lord God Sabaoth. And Habakkuk stood on his watchtower, to see horns in the hands of the Savior, in which his strength is hidden. Hence the psalmist too cries out: I will hear what the Lord God speaks in me. And that we may know that every prophecy in the holy Scriptures is called a vision: And all the people, he says, saw the voice of the Lord; hence the prophets also were formerly called seers.\n— Commentary on Hosea","And lest they think that the heresiarchs and leaders of their error have spoken by the spirit of God: it is I, he says, who spoke to the prophets, and not to your masters; I multiplied visions, and in the hand of my prophets, who are established in the Church, I have been likened.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.11":["If Gilead is an idol, then in vain were they sacrificing to oxen in Gilgal; for their altars too are like heaps upon the furrows of the field. LXX: If Gilead is not, then the princes sacrificing in Gilgala were false, and their altars like tortoises upon the desert of the field. Where we have translated 'to oxen', which in Hebrew are called surim, the LXX translated 'princes', who are called sarim, deceived by the likeness and ambiguity of the word. Again, where we have put 'heaps', which in Hebrew are called gallim and properly signify θῖνας, that is, mounds gathered together out of sand, which, chiefly in the desert and on the shores, are either increased or diminished as the wind blows, the LXX translated 'tortoises', for which Symmachus rendered 'heaps of stones', Theodotion 'hills'. And indeed, if you look at θῖνας, they have the likeness of great tortoises in a desert field, or on banks and shores, rising a little above the ground. What he says, then, is this: if in Gilead, of which it is written: Gilead, a city of those who work an idol, supplanted with blood, the gods are false and the religion perverse, and it is beyond the Jordan where two tribes dwell, Reuben and Gad, and the half tribe of Manasseh, then Gilgal too, of which we read in this same prophet: All their wickedness is in Gilgala, which is behind Beth-aven; whoever worship idols do not sacrifice oxen to the gods, but offer sacrifices to oxen, imitating the error of Samaria. For at the time when these things were prophesied, Gilead was in the kingdom of the ten tribes, and Gilgal under the rule of the two tribes, which were called Judah. Therefore both the ten tribes and the two were deceived by an equal error of idolatry, and their altars are like heaps and mounds gathered together of stones, or of sand. And since both these and those will have been led away into captivity, their once-altars, without worshippers, will have the likeness of tortoises or of mounds. But since Gilead is interpreted 'transference of testimony', and Gilgal 'wallowing place', we can say this: that the princes of the heretics transfer the testimonies of the truth into a lie, and whatever they worship is an idol, and their sacrifices have the likeness either of heaps gathered of stones or of tortoises. For just as θίνες and heaps are gathered here and there from stones and sand, so the heretics too compose images out of worldly wisdom and the subtleties of men, by fraud and falsehood. And when they have done this, they move with slow steps in one place, and cannot occupy the whole world. The tortoise, slow-footed and burdened, nay, weighed down by its own weight, does not so much walk as move, signifying the most grievous sins of the heretics, who sacrifice to their own errors in the mire and wallowing place of mud, adoring the works of their own hands, and, like oxen, toiling at everything for earthly crops.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.12-13":["He will not err who says that Jacob the supplanter, and Israel who sees the Lord, went before as a type of the Lord; and that Rachel, at first barren and beautiful, whom Jacob loved most, signifies the Church; but that Leah, with bleary eyes and fruitful, shows the mysteries of the Synagogue; and that he himself led the people of believers out of the darkness of this world, and came to the sweetest streams of the Jordan, that is, of baptism.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.12":["Jacob fled into the country of Syria, and Israel served for a wife, and for a wife he kept watch. But by a prophet the Lord brought Israel out of Egypt, and by a prophet he was preserved. LXX: And Jacob withdrew into the plain of Syria, and Israel served for a wife, and for a wife he guarded; and by a prophet the Lord brought Israel out of Egypt, and by a prophet he was preserved. It seems that, without reason and without prophetic order, after the idol of Gilead and Gilgal and the altars like heaps of stones, he suddenly chose to narrate the history of Genesis concerning Jacob; which is at once resolved by one who remembers having read above concerning Jacob: In the womb he supplanted his brother, and in his strength he was made upright with the angel; and he prevailed over the angel and was strengthened; he wept and besought him: in Bethel he found him, and there he spoke with us. This Jacob, therefore, was not strengthened by the angel in vain; but because he fought the whole night and overcame his adversary, so that from this he might learn not to fear his brother, for fear of whom he was fleeing into Syria to Laban his mother's brother; and he served seven years for a wife, Rachel; and for Leah he guarded the sheep of his father-in-law Laban for the same span of years.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.13":["And because he had once said both Jacob and Israel, he joins the father and the sons, and recalls the history that follows, when by the prophet Moses the Lord brought Israel out of Egypt, and the twelve tribes which were begotten of Israel were preserved, as the prophet led them out.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.12.14":["Ephraim provoked me to anger in his bitternesses, and his blood will come upon him, and his reproach his Lord will restore to him. LXX: likewise. Since, then, I had bestowed such great things on Ephraim that I brought him back rich and a lord, and the parent of many sons, who had been naked and an exile and alone, Ephraim deserted me, nay, provoked me to anger; and by his bitterness he made me bitter, who am sweet. Hence his blood will come upon him, that is: he himself will be the cause of his own death, according to what David says to the man who announced the death of Saul and related that the king of Israel had been slain by him: Your blood be upon your head. Not by my sentence, but by the blood of Saul will your blood be shed. And what follows: And his reproach the Lord will restore to him, agrees with that sense which Nathan speaks to David: You who have made the enemies blaspheme the name of the Lord, on account of this thing, that is, on account of this sin by which you killed Uriah, that very blasphemy and reproach, by which the Lord was blasphemed through you, will be turned upon your head. The heretics always provoke the merciful God to anger, and by the hardness of their heart they compel him who prefers the repentance of the sinner to his death to punish them; and their blood, by which they have shed both their own blood and that of many, will come upon their head; and the reproaches with which they have blasphemed the Lord, their Lord will restore to them, not that he is their Lord, but because he was once their Lord.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.1-2":["When Ephraim spoke, horror seized Israel, and he sinned in Baal, and died; and now they have added to sinning, and have made for themselves a molten image of their silver, as it were the likeness of idols; the whole is the work of craftsmen; to these they themselves say: Sacrifice men, adoring calves. LXX: According to the word of Ephraim he himself received justifications in Israel, and placed them for Baal, and died; and now he has added that he might sin, and they made for themselves a molten image of their silver, after the image of idols, molten works of craftsmen; to these they themselves say: Sacrifice men, for the calves have failed. Where the LXX translated: sacrifice men, for the calves have failed, and we rendered: sacrifice men, adoring calves, Symmachus translated: sacrifice; let men adore calves; so that the sense is: Sacrifice, that is, offer sacrifice to idols, and let the pause fall up to this point; then: let men, a rational animal, adore calves, dumb living creatures. When Ephraim spoke, then, that is, Jeroboam son of Nebat of the tribe of Ephraim, horror seized Israel, that is, the ten tribes. For 'horror', which in Hebrew is called rathath, and which Symmachus and Theodotion translated 'trembling', the LXX, intending I know not what, translated δικαιώματα, that is, justifications. And so great a horror seized Israel that he sinned and offended God in Baal, and died, losing him who says: I am the life. The soul, for, that has sinned, it shall die. And the Apostle: The widow, he says, who lives in pleasures is dead while she lives. And not only did he die in Baal, but he added sins to sins, so that from the silver which the Lord had given idols were fashioned, the works of men's hands. To these they themselves say, that is, the priests and princes who ought to have taught the people good things: Sacrifice men, adoring calves; which indeed is also said in the psalms: They sacrificed their sons and their daughters to demons. Where, following Symmachus and Theodotion, we rendered 'adoring', Aquila translated καταφιλοῦντες, that is, kissing. For those who adore are accustomed to kiss their own hand; which Job denies having done, saying: If I have kissed my hand, putting it to my mouth, let this too be reckoned to me as the greatest iniquity. But if, as some would have it, the demons say to the people: Sacrifice men, for the calves have failed, the gluttony of those is shown who are fed on the blood of victims and the smoke of burnt offerings; for, when the victims fail, they desire men to be sacrificed to them, in whose not only death but also gore they delight.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.1":["But when the heretics speak, nay, the princes of the heretics, that is, Ephraim, horror and trembling will seize the unhappy people; and he will sin in the idols which he has fashioned from his own heart, and he dies with the people whom he has seduced.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.2":["And it is not enough for him to have fallen, unless he turns the tongue which he had received for singing of God into images of idols, and with artful eloquence composes a dogma like the truth, which is nothing else but a contrivance of human depravity. And they instruct their disciples that they too should sacrifice men, that is, steal them from the Church of God and bring them in to the heretics, and kill those whom they have deceived. What follows: For the calves have failed, has this sense: Do not seek those whom you may seduce among the Gentiles, who are called brute animals; but seize those, sacrifice those, who, set within the Church, are reckoned by the name of Christ and are called men, according to that prophetic word: But choice foods.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.3":["Therefore they will be like a morning cloud, and like the morning dew passing away, like dust snatched by the whirlwind from the threshing floor, and like smoke from the chimney. LXX: likewise; changing only this, which is at the end: and like vapor from locusts, or from tears, because in most manuscripts we find ἀκρίδων (of locusts) set down, in others δακρύων (of tears). Because, he says, they sacrificed men in place of calves, and adored calves, therefore they will be like a morning cloud, and like the dew passing away in the morning, like dust snatched by the whirlwind from the threshing floor, and like smoke from the chimney. All these are seen for a time and suddenly vanish, according to what he had said: Samaria has made her king pass away like foam upon the face of the water. And again: As the morning passes, the king of Israel will pass away. And that a cloud or dew passes swiftly, and dust from the threshing floor, and smoke from the chimney, no one doubts, according to what is written: As smoke fails, let them fail. But we ask why the LXX, for 'chimney', which Theodotion translated καπνοδόχην, rendered 'locusts'? Among the Hebrews 'locust' and 'chimney' are written with the same letters, aleph, res, beth, he. If it is read arbe, it means locust; if orobba, chimney; for which Aquila translated καταράκτην, Symmachus 'opening'. Now a cataract is properly the name for an opening made in a wall, through which the smoke goes out. But if someone contentious and unwilling to accept the Hebrew truth should seek the sense of 'locust', let him hear that Ephraim is compared to ἀτμῷ, that is, to a vapor, or a breath and a breathing, which comes out of the mouth of a locust so thin that it is not perceived; but if, on the contrary, he objects, why did he not liken Ephraim, who was to perish, to other things which are smaller, for example to the flea, which has all its members, head, eyes, feet, belly and the rest, which, though we do not see them with our eyes, we nevertheless perceive by sense, so much so that the mouth and teeth of the flea, which we do not see with our eyes, we feel by its bites. He must be answered that he compared the glory of those who were to perish to the vapor of the locust, or to a most thin breath, for this reason: because the locust is harmful, and so hostile to mortals that it causes famine and lays waste the tilled crops, so much so that it strips the bark from trees and vines as well, as we read more fully in the prophet Joel. And to this locust, and to the morning cloud and the dew and the dust, the heretics are compared, of whom it is also said in the catholic epistle: These are clouds without water. For they have the appearance of prophets and of apostolic clouds, to which the truth of God comes; but they do not have waters, that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit, as the Lord says in the Gospel: He who believes in me, as the scripture says, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. But this, he says, he said of the Spirit which those who believed in him were to receive. But concerning the tears, which in the Greek tongue have some likeness to the locusts, δακρύων καὶ ἀκρίδων, the error is manifest, since some supposed 'tears' in place of 'locusts'.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.4":["But I am the Lord your God from the land of Egypt. In place of which one reads in the LXX: But I am the Lord your God ÷ who make firm the heaven and create the earth, whose hand created all the host of heaven, and I did not show them to you, that you might walk after them. : And I brought you out of the land of Egypt. Since these words are not found in the Hebrew, and are rendered by none of the translators, and are not read in the ancient edition of the LXX either, they must be marked beforehand with an obelus; especially since their sense is plain. Let us therefore pass on to the rest, joining the preceding section to what follows:\n— Commentary on Hosea","But I am the Lord your God from the land of Egypt, and you shall know no God besides me, and there is no savior but me.\n\nI knew you in the wilderness, in the land of solitude, according to their pastures; and they were filled and were satisfied; they lifted up their heart and forgot me. LXX: But I am the Lord your God, and I brought you out of the land of Egypt; and you shall know no God but me, and there is no savior besides me. I was pasturing you in the desert, in an uninhabitable land, according to their pastures; and they were filled to satiety, and their hearts were lifted up; therefore they forgot me. He who had said above: Jacob fled into the region of Syria, and Israel served for a wife, and for a wife he kept watch; but by a prophet the Lord brought Israel out of Egypt, and by a prophet he was kept, now also recounts what he provided for them: I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, who commanded you through Moses: take heed lest perhaps you eat and be satisfied, and forget your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt; for there is no other God besides me, and there is no other who is able to save.\n— Commentary on Hosea","The heretics too the Lord brought out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage, and out of the iron furnace, who at first served King Pharaoh and his captains; and he commanded them in the Church to know no other God except him who is the creator of all things and knows how to save those whom he made.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.5-6":["I, who am the founder of all things, knew you, or fed you, in the wilderness and in an uninhabitable land, where there is want of all things, where there are no waters; I gave you manna from heaven, and brought forth springs of water from the hardest rock. And they, according to what is written elsewhere: The beloved grew fat, became thick, grew broad, and kicked, now also have eaten and been satisfied, and have lifted up their heart, and have forgotten him whose benefits they ought to have remembered. For through so great a waste of desert, where not only no crops and trees and vines, but not even grass is produced, and no waters temper the heat of the sun, Israel could not for forty years have reached the land of the Jordan, unless the Lord had provided everything.\n— Commentary on Hosea","He himself knew them and fed them in the land of solitude, so that they could say: The Lord pastures me and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture there he has set me, he has brought me up beside the water of refreshment. And he gave them the bread of angels, manna from heaven, which they had never eaten in Egypt, and water from the rock that followed them. Now the rock, according to the Apostle, is Christ; and they ate and were filled, and did not endure the food of the Lord. To them the same Apostle says: Already you are satisfied, already you have become rich; without us you reign, and would that you did reign, so that we too might reign with you. For they ate in the holy Scriptures the bread that came down from heaven, and said with David: The uncertain and hidden things of your wisdom you have made manifest to me. And being filled and satisfied, they lifted up their heart against the Creator, and invented another god for themselves, ascribing whatever they had drunk and eaten to their own merits, not to the mercy of God. Therefore they forgot God, who had commanded them to bind the words of the law between their eyes and on their hands, and on the fringes of their cloaks, so that they might never forget their God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.7-8":["And I will be to them like a lioness, like a leopard on the way of the Assyrians; I will meet them like a bear robbed of her cubs, and I will tear apart the inner parts of their liver; and I will devour them there like a lion; the beast of the field will rend them. LXX: And I will be to them like a panther and like a leopard on the way of the Assyrians; I will meet them like a bear in want of food, and I will tear apart the inner parts of their heart; and the whelps of the forests will devour them there, the beasts of the field will tear them apart. They were filled and satisfied; they lifted up their hearts and forgot me. But I, he says, will be to them like a lioness, or a panther, of which we spoke more fully above; and like a leopard on the way of the Assyrians, when they are led captive by the Assyrians; and I will meet them like a bear robbed of her cubs, or in want of food; and I will tear apart all their vital organs. Those who have written on the natures of beasts say that among all wild animals nothing is more savage than a bear when she has lost her cubs or is in want of food; and he threatens not only the ferocity of the panther, the leopard and the bear, but also that of the lion and of all the beasts that are bred in the woodland pastures; and he says he will be turned into all these when they go to the Assyrians, so that, when they have suffered hard things there, they may not ascribe their miseries to the strength of their enemies, but to the power and indignation of the Lord. And at the same time let us consider that he who in the Gospel says to believers: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you; for my yoke is sweet and my burden is light, now through the prophet becomes to the unbelieving and to those unwilling to repent a panther, a leopard, a bear and a lion; not only to the Israelites, who because of their idolatry were settled in the cities or mountains of the Medes, but also to the heretics, who because of the pride of their mind and the vanity of their false dogmas have forgotten their God, and have fashioned and followed foreign gods.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.9":["You have become a surfeit to me: I will by no means forgive your sins. For which Aquila translated, I have labored in bearing; Symmachus ἐκοπώθην ἱλασκόμενος, that is, I have grown weary of showing mercy; to show that he will by no means have mercy any longer, because it is one thing to kill the servants sent to them, another to kill the Son. This sense we read also in the prophet Hosea: Your destruction, O Israel; only in me is your help (Hos 13:9). Which is understood thus: You would perish, Israel, because you are saved not by your own merit but only by my help.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Your destruction, Israel; only in me is your help. Where is your king? Now above all they save you in all your cities; and your judges, of whom you said: Give me a king and princes; I will give you a king in my fury, and I will take him away in my indignation. LXX: Who will help your corruption, Israel? Where is this king of yours? And let him save you in all your cities; let him judge you, of whom you said: Give me a king and a prince; and I gave you a king and a prince, and I gave you a king in my anger, and I held him in my fury. Where the LXX translated I held, all the others rendered I took away. Unhappy Israel, and worthy of a perpetual curse, which has gone down into so great a depth of impiety that it is saved by the mercy of God alone! But in the Hebrew it can also be read in this sense: May you perish, Israel, because nothing is left to you except to be preserved by my clemency alone. In the LXX, however, the sense is different: Who will help your corruption, Israel? That is, which of those whom you thought your protectors will be able to bring help to your captivity and final servitude?\n— Commentary on Hosea","Every heretic is lost and given over to corruption; for whoever corrupts the temple of God, the Lord will destroy him; and he has help in nothing else but in the mercy of God alone, which he obtains by repentance.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.10-11":["Where is your king, of whom you said to Samuel: Appoint a king over us to judge us, as the other nations also have. And when he spoke against it, you answered: By no means; but there shall be a king over us, and we too shall be like all the nations, and our king will judge us, and go out before us, and fight for us. Let him, then, of whom you had promised yourself that he would fight your wars, now come to your aid in your need, and free all your cities from servitude. Where are your judges? Where are your kings? For you said: Give me a king and princes; and so I gave you Saul as king in my fury, so much so that in the days of harvest I displayed rain, contrary to the nature of the province of Judea. And I took away, he says, the king in my indignation, namely Zedekiah, so that the one I had given with fury I might take away with indignation. Others think that the king given in fury was Jeroboam son of Nebat, and the one taken away in indignation Hoshea, the last king of the ten tribes. This which we have expounded, I gave you a king in my indignation, and I took away the king, the Hebrews refer to the future tense. At that time, he says, when you were saying: Give me a king and princes, I answered you through Samuel that I would give you one in my fury and would take him away in my indignation.\n— Commentary on Hosea","His king and judges are the devil and the demons, or all the princes of perverse dogmas, who will not be able to free them in the time of need and distress, who were given in fury and will be taken away in indignation; not that the Lord wished them to have such kings, otherwise he would not take away those whom he had given of his own accord, but because he let them go after their own wills, so that, eating, and with their flesh grown fat, they might be nauseated and vomit it out through their nostrils, and begin to hate those whom they followed with such zeal.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.12-13":["The iniquity of Ephraim is bound up; his sin is hidden; the pains of a woman in labor will come upon him; he is an unwise son; for now he will not stand in the crushing of the children. LXX: A gathering of iniquity is Ephraim; his sin is hidden; pains as of a woman in labor will come upon him; this is your wise son, because now he will not hold out in the crushing of the children. Just as, if something is bound up in a bag, it is kept, and is not lost to him for whom it was tied up, so every iniquity by which Ephraim has sinned against God is bound up for him, and is reserved, hidden as in a purse. Finally, when the day of vengeance has come, and the final captivity, pains as of a woman in labor will come upon him, or will seize him. A woman in labor knows long before she gives birth, from the time she conceived, that she is going to give birth, and daily awaits the extreme torments and agonies that are to come. So too Ephraim, the unwise son, of whom he had said above: Ephraim is a foolish dove, not having a heart in the crushing of his children and his people. When the day of labor and of captivity has come, he will be able neither to stand nor to endure. Instead of the unwise son, in the LXX one reads by irony: This wise son, that is: the one you thought wise, so that by contrast he may be understood as unwise. But for all the heretics their iniquity is bound up, which they have spoken on high; and their sin is hidden, while they think they hide the poisons of their heart and hold secrets, which, when the day of labor has come, will be laid open with pain and wailing. This Ephraim is an unwise son, because he has abandoned the wisdom of God, of whom it is also written in Jeremiah: His end will be foolish; and in the crushing of his children, whom he has killed, whom he has slaughtered, he will not be able to endure the wrath of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.14-15":["And seeing the hard necessity of death, and that there is no man who lives and does not see death, the most merciful Father remembers the ancient sentence, that in Adam we all die. Or the prophet, understanding his own frailty and the condition of human flesh: Consolation, he says, is hidden from my eyes; and the sense is: I am not able to be consoled; whatever I conceive in my mind cannot soften my grief, as I see names most dear to one another severed by death; hell itself, he says, divides between brothers. Whatever, then, separates brothers is to be called hell; and above all the harlot woman, who, calling the unwise man to herself, says in the wealth of prudence: Touch gladly the hidden loaves, and the sweetness of stolen water; and the unwise man does not know that the earth-born perish with her and run into the depth of hell. For whatever is not permitted is desired the more, and what is sweet through rarity is turned into bitterness through constancy. And honey drips from the lips of the harlot woman, which for a time makes the jaws of the unwise man fat, but at the last is found more bitter than gall, and sharper even than a two-edged sword. Whoever is earth-born, and is not born from heaven, is killed by her embraces and bound with the bands of her couches; and the feet of folly lead down those who make use of her, with death, to hell. Now between death and hell there is this difference: death is that by which the soul is separated from the body; hell is the place in which souls are shut up, whether in refreshment or in punishments, according to the quality of their merits. We have said this to show that death does what the harlot woman does. For death divides brothers, and the woman does this too. Under brothers understand every bond of love, so that mother also is divided from daughter, and father from son, and brother from brother. That death is one thing and hell another, the psalmist also shows, saying: There is no one in death who is mindful of you; but in hell who will confess to you? And in another place: Let death come upon them, and let them go down alive into hell. Where we have translated: I will be your death, O death; I will be your bite, O hell, the LXX rendered: Where is your cause, O death? where is your goad, O hell? In place of which the Apostle put: Death is swallowed up in strife? Where, O death, is your strife? Where, O death, is your sting? And expounding the force of the testimony he added: Now the sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the law; but thanks be to God, who has given us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ. Therefore what he interpreted of the resurrection of the Lord, we neither can nor dare interpret otherwise. Death and hell can also be taken as the devil, who was slaughtered by the death of Christ, of whom it is also said in Isaiah: Death, growing strong, has devoured. And afterwards there follows: The Lord has taken away every tear from every face. But the two brothers divided from each other by death some understand, according to the history of that time, as Israel and Judah, so that what was then prefigured in part may now be perceived in the whole, and Israel and Judah are to be freed and redeemed together with the whole human race. In the place where the LXX rendered: where is your cause? and we said: I will be your death, Symmachus translated: I will be your stroke. The Fifth edition and Aquila: where are your words?, for in Hebrew it is written dabarach; they read dabar, that is, word, in place of deber, which is interpreted death, according to what we read in Isaiah: The Lord sent death into Jacob, and it came into Israel, that is, deber, in place of which we translated: The Lord sent a word into Jacob, and it came into Israel, that is, dabar. For sting also, which we rendered bite, Symmachus translated ἀπάντημα, that is, an encounter, and Theodotion and the Fifth edition a stroke and a shutting-in.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.14":["From the hand of death I will free them, from death I will redeem them. I will be your death, O death; I will be your bite, O hell. Consolation is hidden from my eyes, because he divides between brothers. LXX: From the hand of hell I will free them, from death I will redeem them; where is your cause, death? Where is your sting, hell? Consolation is hidden from my eyes, because he will divide between brothers. According to both understandings, both of Ephraim, that is, of the ten tribes, and of the heretics, who could not hold out in the crushing of their children when the pains as of a woman in labor came, the Lord promises that he will free them from the hand of death and redeem them from death. Now he calls the hand of death the works by which it kills, according to what is written: Death and life are in the hand of the tongue. And the Lord freed all and redeemed them in the passion of the cross and the shedding of his blood, when his soul went down into hell and his flesh did not see corruption; and to death itself and to hell he spoke: I will be your death, O death. For this is why I died, that you might die by my death. I will be your bite, O hell, you who devoured all in your jaws.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.15":["The Lord will bring a burning wind rising from the desert, and it will dry up its veins, and lay waste its spring, and he will plunder the treasure of every desirable vessel. LXX: The Lord will bring a burning wind from the desert upon them, and will dry up its veins, and lay waste its springs; he will parch its land and all the desirable vessels. I have read in the commentaries of a certain writer that the burning wind which the Lord will bring from the desert is the one that struck the house of Job at its four corners and made it fall upon his sons as they feasted, and that it is one of those winds which we read in the Gospel blow and come with whirlwind, rains and floods to overturn the house that is built upon the rock, or upon the sand. This by no means seems right to me; for it is not written in Job that the Lord brought the wind from the desert; rather, the name of the Lord is passed over in silence, so that the wind from the wilderness, which had come against the holy man of its own will, can be taken as a hostile power; and the winds that overturn the foundations of houses certainly cannot be referred to the good side. It remains that we understand the burning wind, which the Lord will bring rising from the desert, as the one of whom we also read in Habakkuk: God will come from the South, and the Holy One from Mount Paran, which is certainly situated in the wilderness and in the south. And in the Song we read: Where do you pasture, where do you lie down at midday? This burning wind, then, to dry up the veins of death and parch its springs, the Lord will bring rising from the desert; but from the desert of the human race, in which even the devil, seeking rest, could not find it. Or we understand the desert as the virginal womb of holy Mary, because without human seed it sprouted no shoot, but a rod, simple and most pure and fruitful in its oneness, brought forth that flower which says in the Song of Songs: I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys. And beautifully, both in Isaiah and in the present place, it is called a flower rising and a wind rising, because from the lowliness of the flesh he climbed to the heights, and led us with himself to the Father, saying in the Gospel: When I have been lifted up, I will draw all things to myself. He himself will rise like a root from an uninhabitable land, and death will by no means come upon him, but he will come upon death; for death found in him no path for its power, and this is what is said in Proverbs: It is impossible to find the tracks of a serpent upon a rock. And the rock itself speaks in the Gospel: Behold, the prince of this world will come, and will find nothing in me. He will dry up the veins of death and lay waste its springs. The veins and springs and sting of death are named sins by the Apostle; and when these have been dried up, death itself also will be dried up. And what follows: He will plunder the treasure of every desirable vessel, is taken in two ways: either because they are desirable to those who dwell in death, or by the desirable vessels that were held in the treasury of hell we understand the saints, whom, bound as they were, the Lord perhaps plundered and carried off from hell, and led with him into paradise like most precious vessels. For treasure the LXX translated land; there is no doubt that the land signifies death. And in the psalms we read: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living. And according to the Gospel: The meek will possess the land. By contrast we must take it that the land of hell is not the land of the living but of the dead, which is plundered and laid waste when, by the death of Christ, the souls bound in hell are set free. According to tropology, in those same commentaries — of which we spoke above — we read that the burning wind is understood as the devil and the individual heresiarchs. This does not please us; for the devil cannot dry up the veins of death and the springs of error, since he himself is the source and beginning of the dead. Therefore the burning wind is to be understood as the word of the Church, which parches all the dogmas of the heretics and brings them to nothing, and plunders and scatters those who had been gathered together in death by the doctrine of the heretics.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.13.16":["Let Samaria perish, because she has provoked her God to bitterness; let them perish by the sword; let their little ones be dashed and her pregnant women be ripped open. LXX: Samaria will be destroyed, because she has resisted her God; they will perish by the sword, and let their sucklings be dashed against the rock, and those with child be torn apart. We have often said that the ten tribes are called Samaria from the capital city Samaria, which is now called Augusta, that is, Sebaste, from the name of Augustus. And why the city was called Samaria we read in the book of Kings. The prophet, then, commands, and, to speak more truly, speaks in the optative mood, that Samaria should perish. For though God was preparing such great goods for her, she acts against God and follows rather the images of demons. Symmachus, however, did not say let her perish, but μεταμελήσει, that is, she will repent, and she will regret her error, that she turned the sweetest God into bitterness, so that her warriors perish by the sword, her little ones and sucklings are dashed to the ground, and her pregnant women and those with child are torn apart unto death. All this we must believe befell her in the time of captivity and distress, when they lost their homeland, and those who escaped the sword were led away into perpetual servitude. Concerning the heretics the understanding is easy: they are called Samaria because they boast that they keep the precepts of God, not that they had kept his laws, but because they say that they are this, after the likeness of the schism of the Novatians, who themselves too call themselves καθαροὺς, that is, clean, though they are the most unclean of all, denying repentance, through which sins are cleansed, according to what is written: You will wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow. And in Isaiah: Wash yourselves, be clean. Now by washing he does not mean baptism, but all repentance, which washes away the filth of sins. Let such a Samaria, then, perish, because whatever it says fights against its God and turns his clemency into cruelty, so far that those among it who are men and have come to the full age of malice are cut down by the spiritual sword. And those who are little ones and sucklings, let them be dashed against the rock, of whom we also read in the psalm: Blessed is he who will take and dash his little ones against the rock. Her pregnant women too, and those with child, who have conceived from evil seed, will be torn apart for this reason, that they may not produce most wicked offspring. Something of the kind is also set before us in the Gospel: Woe to those who are with child and to those who nurse in those days; that is, in the days of tribulation and distress. And the warriors of Samaria are killed by the sword, and the sucklings are dashed, and those with child are torn apart, so that, the evil seed perishing and its tares burned up, the wheat alone may remain, to be stored in the Lord's barns.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.1-3":["To every perverse dogma also the prophet speaks daily, and calls its followers to repentance, saying: Return to the Lord your God, you who have fallen, or have grown weak, having lost the health of the Lord; take words with you, the true confession of faith, and say: Take away the iniquity that dwells in our heart, and receive the good of faith. For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, but with the mouth confession is made unto salvation. The calves and victims, or the fruit of the lips, are to believe in the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and in the passion and resurrection of the Lord; and whoever has offered this to him will by no means hope in the Assyrian king, of whom we have often spoken. Nor will he mount upon the horse which the Lord commands by no means to be multiplied, the horse with which Pharaoh was drowned together with his cavalry. For every heretic, through pride, mounts horses which he himself has bred in his error. And by no means will they any longer say to the works of their hands, which they themselves have fashioned with artful eloquence: our gods. The glutton's god is his belly, the covetous man worships mammon, the heretic, the dogma he has invented. Whoever deserts all these, that is, Assyria and the horse and the works of his hands, will return to the Lord, and will appease his father, by whom he had been cast off.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.1":["Return, O Israel, to the Lord your God, for you have fallen in your iniquity. Take words with you, and return to the Lord; say to him: Take away all iniquity and receive the good, and we will render the calves of our lips. Assyria will not save us, we will not mount upon a horse; nor will we say any more: Our gods, the works of our hands; for you will have mercy on the orphan who is in you. LXX: Return, O Israel, to the Lord your God, for you have been weakened in your iniquities; take words with you and turn back to the Lord, say to him, so that you may not take up iniquity, but may receive good things; and we will render the fruit of our lips. Assyria will not save us, we will not mount upon a horse; by no means will we say any more: Our gods, to the works of our hands; he who is in you will have mercy on the orphan. Now that Samaria and its men are perishing, and its little ones and pregnant women have been slain, dashed down and ripped open, all Israel is called to repentance; so that he who has been weakened, or has fallen in his iniquities, may return to the physician and recover health, or that he who had fallen may begin to stand; and he is taught how he ought to repent.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.2":["Take, he says, words with you, that is, prayers and the confession of sins, and return to the Lord as much by words as by works; and say to him: Take away all iniquity, leave in us nothing of our sickness and of our former ruin, lest the revived seedlings of an evil seed sprout again; and receive, he says, the good. For unless you take away our evils, we cannot have a good to offer you, according to what is written elsewhere: Turn away from evil and do good; and we will render, he says, the calves of our lips. For the calves, which in Hebrew are called pharim, the Seventy translated fruit, which is called pheri, through the likeness of a deceptive word. But the calves of the lips are praises to God and thanksgiving: For a sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit. Therefore, fleshly victims being already rejected at that time, the offering acceptable to God is pure confession.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.3":["Those who say that they will render the calves of the lips, and will sing the praises of God with unceasing voice, also promise something else: that they will by no means have hope in the Assyrians, nor upon Egyptian horses, because a horse is deceptive for safety, and that they will no longer worship the works of their hands, the golden calves which they cast in Dan and Bethel; and therefore they say: By no means will we say to the work of our hands: our gods, for you will have mercy on the orphan who is in you; that is, on the people of Israel, of whom you had said: Israel is my firstborn son. And: I have begotten sons and exalted them, but they have despised me. And in another place: Foreign sons have lied to me. Now he is called an orphan because he has lost God as his father. A certain writer has explained the orphan as him who has withdrawn from an evil father, the devil, and who is therefore lifted up by the mercy of God.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.4-8":["Whatever we have interpreted according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy) of the coming of the Lord the Savior and of the conversion of the true Israel, this can be referred as much to heretics and Jews as to the Gentiles and to every perverse dogma, so that when they have repented they may obtain pardon. If, therefore, the fullness of the promise was fulfilled at the coming of the Savior, and is fulfilled daily in the Church, we must believe that it will be fulfilled more fully when, perfection having come, what is now in part will be done away. We must note what we have often said: that the salvation of Israel and of the return to God, and the redemption from captivity, are to be taken not carnally, as the Jews think, but spiritually, as is most truly proved.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.4":["I will heal their breaches, I will love them freely, for my fury is turned away from him; I will be like the dew. Israel will spring up like the lily, and his root will break forth like that of Lebanon; his branches will spread, and his glory will be like the olive, and his fragrance like that of Lebanon. Those who sit in his shade will be converted, they will live on wheat and will spring up like the vine. His memorial like the wine of Lebanon. Ephraim, what more have I to do with idols? I will hear him and direct him; I, like a green fir tree; from me your fruit has been found. LXX: I will heal their dwellings; I will love them openly, for my anger is turned away from them; I will be like the dew. Israel will flower like the lily and will send out his roots like Lebanon; his branches will spread, and he will be like a fruitful olive, and his fragrance like that of Lebanon; they will turn back and sit under his shade; they will drink and be inebriated with grain, and his memorial will blossom like the vine, like the wine of Lebanon, Ephraim. What more has he to do with idols? I have humbled him and I will strengthen him, I am like a dense juniper; from me your fruit has been found. To those who have turned to repentance and, like an orphan, acknowledge the father whom they had left, God answered: I will heal the breaches, or their dwellings, in which they had been wounded, or broken, or in which they had dwelt badly: I will love them freely; which the LXX translated ὁμολόγως, plainly and openly or without any doubt. Now the Lord loves those who love him, of whom he also says in another place: I love those who love me. For I, who before was angry with them because of the sins they had committed, will now have mercy because of my clemency.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.5":["And I will be to them like the dew; so that with my dew I may quench the Babylonian furnace and the oven of the blazing fire, I who also through the patriarch Isaac spoke to my servant Jacob: Of the dew of heaven shall your dwelling be. For just as the Lord becomes to believers light, way, truth, bread, vine, fire, shepherd, lamb, door, worm, and the rest; so for us who need his mercy, and burn with the fevers of sins, he is turned into dew, he to whom Isaiah says: For the dew that is from you is their health. And in the song of Deuteronomy Moses speaks: Let my words descend like the dew. And when the Lord has sprinkled us with his dew, and has watered the dryness of our breast with his rains, we will spring up, nay, we will flower like the lily, imitating the Lord the Savior, who says in the Song of Songs: I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys; and he speaks to his bride, who has no wrinkle or spot: As the lily in the midst of thorns, so is my nearest one in the midst of the daughters. And when we have grown in the Lord, we will send out our roots like the trees of Lebanon, which sink their root into the depths as far as they rise with their top into the air, so that they are shaken by no storm, but stand firm in their steady bulk.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.6":["The branches of these trees stretch this way and that, so that the birds of heaven may come and dwell in them. And lest perhaps we should think, because he had said: his root will break forth, or: he will send out his roots like Lebanon, that he was speaking of cedars and unfruitful trees, he compares the holy man, converted to the Lord, to the fruit-bearing olive, the man who says in another place: But I am like a fruitful olive in the house of God. Its fruit the five wise virgins prepared for themselves; by it the swelling of wounds is soothed, the limbs of the sick find rest, light is kindled in darkness, those who contend in the contest are anointed. This olive will have a fragrance like that of Lebanon, or of frankincense, which is a kind of incense: ὁμωνύμως (by the same name) among the Greeks and the Hebrews both the mountain and frankincense are so called; or at least like that of Mount Lebanon, which, most fertile and green, is covered with the densest foliage of trees, so that the olive can say: We are the good fragrance of Christ.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.7":["But those who have been converted to the Lord will receive the reward of their conversion, that they may sit in his shade and say: Under his shade I rested and sat, and his fruit is sweet in my mouth. And when they have sat in his shade, those who before had been dead will live; or, according to the Seventy, they will drink and be inebriated with wheat, that is, with an abundance of all things. That inebriation here signifies not the overturning of the mind but a plenty of all things, that little verse declares, saying: You have visited the earth and inebriated it. And the banquet of Joseph, at which he is said to have inebriated his brothers. And the Lord, speaking to the apostles: Eat, friends, and drink, and be inebriated, brothers. Or, because the Lord himself is the grain and the vine, whoever believes in him is said to be inebriated. Finally there follows: And it will flower like the vine, his memorial like the wine of Lebanon. Now we can call wine of Lebanon wine mixed and seasoned with incense, so that it has a most sweet fragrance; or the wine of Lebanon is the wine poured out to the Lord in the temple, of which we read in Zechariah under the name of Lebanon: Open your gates, O Lebanon.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.8":["Since, therefore, so great an abundance of all things is to come, O Ephraim, everyone who repents and who has begun to be mine, put away idols, despise images; for I am he who humbled you, and I will exalt you; or, I will hear and direct, and I will make him like a green fir tree, so that of him it may be said in the psalm, according to the Hebrews: The fir tree is its house. Or at least, I will be like a dense juniper, so that he may rest under my shade. Of ἀρκεύθοις, that is, of junipers, according to the Seventy translators, Solomon is recorded to have made the doors of the temple, because Christ, through whom we come to the Father, has this nature, that he always flowers, always bears new fruits, and never lays aside his greenness. This juniper, for those who rest under its shade, so that they may not be struck by the burning of this world and the heat may not strike their head, as it once struck Jonah's too, gives fruits, and provides not only rest to those who sleep and sit, but also fullness to those who eat.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"HOS.14.9":["Who is wise, and will understand these things? Understanding, and will know these things? For the ways of the Lord are right, and the just will walk in them, but transgressors will fall in them. LXX: Who is wise, and will understand these things, or understanding, and will know them? For the ways of the Lord are right, and the just will walk in them; but those who are impious will be weakened in them. When he says: Who is wise, and will understand these things? understanding, and will know them? he shows the obscurity of the book and the difficulty of its explanation. But if he himself who wrote it confesses that it is either difficult or impossible, what can we do, who, with bleary eyes darkened by the filth of sins, cannot gaze upon the brightest radiance of the sun, except say what is written: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How inscrutable are his judgments and unsearchable his ways! For who, without Christ teaching him, can know what Jezreel signifies, what his sister Not Obtained Mercy, what the third brother Not My People; who the adulteress is, who is to sit for a long time without the law of God; what the covenant with the beasts of the earth and with the birds of heaven is; who the David is to whom the people is to return, whose resurrection is on the third day and whose going forth is compared to the dawn; what the first and the last rain are; who he is whom the prophet says is to come, to show us righteousness, or in whose type Israel is led out of Egypt, and carried in arms, and led with the cords of love; who he is who slays death, and dries up its veins and parches its springs, and plunders the vessels that were kept stored in the treasury, and the rest, which it would take long to go over again? Hence whoever is holy and just will know that the ways of the Lord are right. Now we know that the ways of the Lord are the reading of the Old and New Testament, the understanding of the holy Scriptures. Whoever walks in these ways, unless he is converted to the Lord and the veil which was before the eyes of Moses has been taken away from him, will not be able to find the right road. But if he says with David: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law, he will walk in them, and will find Christ; and he will perceive that the Jews and the heretics, whom Scripture here names either transgressors or impious, stumble in them, and are weakened and fall; according to what is written: Behold, this one is set for the fall and for the rising of many in Israel.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"AMO.1.1":["But the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Judah is said to belong to that time when Uzziah, who is called by another name Azariah, trying to claim for himself a priesthood not lawful for him, was struck with leprosy on his forehead; of this earthquake we also read at the beginning of Amos: The words of Amos, who was among the shepherds of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake. In short, after this wicked king died, at whose impiety the whole earth was shaken, Isaiah saw the great vision which he wove into his book.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","The prophet Amos, who follows Joel and is the third of the twelve prophets, is not the same man whom we read of as the father of the prophet Isaiah. For that one is written with the first and last letters of his name, aleph and sade, and is interpreted strong and robust; this one, however, with ain and samech, and is interpreted a people torn away; the middle letters, mem and uau, are common to both. But among us, who do not have so great a difference of vowel letters, and of the letter s, which among the Hebrews is threefold, both these and other names seem to be the same, which among the Hebrews are distinguished by the diversity of their letters and by their own peculiarities. This prophet, then, whom we now have in hand, was from the town of Tekoa, which lies six miles toward the southern region from holy Bethlehem, which gave birth to the Savior of the world; and beyond it there is no village at all, not even rustic huts like ovens, which the Africans call mapalia. So great is the vastness of the desert, which stretches as far as the Red Sea and the borders of the Persians, the Ethiopians and the Indians. And because on the dry and sandy ground no crops whatever are produced, everything is full of shepherds, so that they may make up for the barrenness of the land by the multitude of their flocks. Of this number of shepherds was also the prophet Amos, unskilled in speech, but not in knowledge. For the same Holy Spirit who spoke through all the prophets was speaking in him. Hence we translate from the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Amos","The words of Amos, who was among the herdsmen of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel. But the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated: The words of Amos, which came to be in Accarim of Tekoa, which he saw on behalf of Jerusalem. And it should be known that this prophet prophesied, not on behalf of Jerusalem, which is not found at all in the Hebrew, but concerning Israel, that is, the ten tribes, which are called Israel and were in Samaria. And indeed Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated this likewise. The first, then, the prophet Hosea, speaks to the ten tribes, which are called Ephraim, Samaria, the house of Joseph and Israel. The second, Joel, utters his prophecy to Jerusalem and the two tribes, which are called Judah and Jerusalem and Jacob, making no mention at all of Israel. The third, Amos, preaches by no means to Jerusalem, which was ruled by the tribe of Judah, but to Israel in Samaria. This can be proved from his whole volume, and above all from what is written: Amaziah the priest of Bethel sent to Jeroboam king of Israel, saying: Amos has rebelled against you in the midst of the house of Israel. And a little after, Amaziah said to Amos: You who see, go, flee into the land of Judah, and eat bread there; and there you shall prophesy, and in Bethel you shall not again prophesy any more, for it is the king's sanctuary and the house of the kingdom. To whom Amos answered: I was not a prophet, nor the son of a prophet, but a shepherd stripping the berries of brambles; and the Lord took me as I followed the flock, and said to me: Go, prophesy to my people Israel; not, surely, to Judah and Jerusalem, as is wrongly found among the Greeks and Latins, but to Israel, that is, the ten tribes, which, because of the multitude of the people, had kept the original name. But in the place where the Seventy translated in Accarim, Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself: in nocedim, which Aquila rendered ἐν ποιμνιοτρόφοις, that is, among the herdsmen, Symmachus and the Fifth edition ἐν τοῖς ποιμέσιν, that is, among the shepherds. And I think that here too, because of the likeness of the letters daleth and res, they were deceived, and read nocerim as though for nocedim, and from there put down the word Accarim, although the letter nun at the beginning of the name leaves no excuse for the error. But up to the present I have not read that Accarim is Hebrew. And those who interpret it barrenness, that is, στείρωσιν, express Ekron, the city of the Philistines, rather than Accarim, which is not read anywhere at all. The words, therefore, of Amos, who was from the town of Tekoa, of the number of the shepherds, since the region itself is also a pastoral one, are contained in this volume, which he saw concerning Israel, not with the eyes of the flesh but with the gaze of the mind; for the prophets were called seers; otherwise, according to the letter, words are not seen but heard. Hence the people also saw the voice of God, and Moses, keeping silent, spoke to the Lord when he said to him: Why do you cry to me? In the New Testament also we read: What we have seen and heard, what we have seen with our eyes, and our hands have handled, of the Word of life. For the Word of God, which in itself is invisible, the apostles see and handle with their hands. Because the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us. And he saw these words:\n\nIn the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake. At the time when Sardanapalus reigned among the Assyrians and the cities of Cilicia, of whom the distinguished orator wrote: \"Uglier, he says, in his vices than in his name\"; and among the Latins Procas Silvius, whom Amulius succeeded in the kingdom after driving out his brother Numitor; and when Amulius had been killed, Romulus, having gathered a band of shepherds and robbers, founded the city of his own name. This, moreover, is Uzziah king of Judah, surnamed Azariah, who, having tried to claim for himself a priesthood not due to him, was struck with leprosy on his forehead, when not only the punishment of the man who had committed sacrilege but also the earthquake, which the Hebrews record happened at that time, showed the wrath of the Lord. Jeroboam, however, is not the son of Nebat, who made Israel sin, but the son of Joash, son of Joathan, son of Jehu, the great-grandson of that same Jehu, under whom Hosea and Joel and Amos prophesied. Uzziah, then, is interpreted κράτος Κυρίου, that is, the dominion or strength of the Lord; Jeroboam, δικασμὸς λαοῦ, that is, the judging or the cause of the people, although some, reading it with the Greek letter chi, suppose it to mean the division of the people; Joash, Κυρίου χρόνισμα, that is, the delay of the Lord, or temporality. The words of Amos, therefore, at the time when the people of Israel had been torn away from the Lord and were serving golden calves, or had been torn away from the kingdom of the stock of David, sang out with a clear voice like a blaring trumpet, which is what Tekoa is interpreted. And they sang concerning Israel, which was once the most upright of God, that is, εὐθύτατος Θεοῦ. In the days of Uzziah king of Judah, in which, because of the temple and the holy of holies, the strength of the Lord remained, and ruled by its own dominion the people who confessed him. But the name of Jeroboam, who had been the cause of the division of the people, signifies that Israel remained for a long time in the error of idolatry before the universal captivity shook it; and this, in the two years, shows the twofold distress of the ten tribes and of the two, so that, if they were willing to repent, they might not feel the earthquake thereafter.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.2":["And he said: The Lord will roar from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will give his voice. And the beautiful places of the shepherds have mourned, and the top of Carmel has been dried up. LXX: And he said: The Lord has spoken out from Zion, and from Jerusalem he has given his voice; and the pastures of the shepherds have mourned, and the top of Carmel has withered. It is natural that all craftsmen speak in the examples of their own craft, and that each one should bring forward likenesses from the pursuit in which he has spent his life: for example, he who is a sailor and a helmsman compares his sadness to a storm, calls a loss a shipwreck, names his enemies contrary winds; and again he calls prosperity and gladness a most gentle breeze and favourable winds, calm seas and waters like plains. On the other side, whatever a soldier says sounds of shield, sword, breastplate, helmet, lance, bow and arrows, death, wound and victory. In the mouth of the philosophers Socrates and Plato, Xenophon and Theophrastus, Zeno and Aristotle, the Stoics and the Peripatetics are always turning up. The orators extol to the sky with praises Lysias and Hyperides, Pericles and Demosthenes, the Gracchi, the Catos, the Ciceros and the Hortensiuses. The poets, if they are epic, celebrate Homer and Vergil in frequent speech; if lyric, Pindar and Flaccus. Why have these things been said? Clearly so that we may show that the prophet Amos too, who was a shepherd from among shepherds, and a shepherd not in cultivated places planted with trees and vines, or at least among woods and green meadows, but in the broad vastness of the desert, in which the ferocity of lions and the slaughter of flocks are found, used in his discourses the terms of his own craft, so as to name the terrible and fearful voice of the Lord the roaring and growling of lions. Comparing the overthrow of the Israelite cities to the solitude of the shepherds and the dryness of the mountains, he says: The Lord will roar from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will give his voice. Will roar is said in Hebrew iesag. And it is fittingly said that he will roar from Zion and give his voice from Jerusalem, where the temple and the worship of God were, in order to teach that God dwells not in the cities of Israel, that is, in Bethel and in Dan, where the golden calves were, nor in the metropolis Samaria and in Jezreel, but in the true religion, which at that time was practised in Zion and Jerusalem. When he roars from Zion and gives his voice from Jerusalem — for the law shall go forth from Zion, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem —, all the beautiful things of the shepherds and of the kings of Israel have mourned, and the top of Carmel, which once abounded in rich pastures, has been dried up. Carmelus it is called in Greek and in Latin, but in Hebrew it is named Chermel; and there are two mountains which are called by this name. One, in which Nabal of Carmel, the husband of Abigail, lived, toward the southern region. The other near Ptolemais, which was formerly called Acco, overhanging the sea, on which the prophet Elijah, on bended knees, obtained the rains. If, then, he is speaking of the Chermel in which Nabal lived, it suits rather the flocks of the shepherds, because it is near the wilderness. But if of the one which is close to the shore, it pertains to the kings and to the pride of the kings of Israel, who, he says, when captivity is impending, are to be laid waste like deserted mountains. Another interpretation: when the Lord, from Zion and Jerusalem, from the watchtower of the Church, which, set upon a mountain, cannot be hidden, and in which there is the vision of peace, has given his voice through the Old and the New Testament and through the teachers of the Church, and has sounded as it were a clear trumpet, then all the beautiful things of the shepherds, that is, the doctrine of the heretics, which seemed composed in fine language, and the knowledge of circumcision — for this is what Chermel means —, by which they promise themselves the knowledge of the true circumcision, will mourn and be dried up, and the thousands of the peoples, who because of their simplicity are called flocks, will wither with sudden drought.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.3":["'Happy the man who follows not the counsel of the wicked.' [Psalm 1:1] In Genesis, we read how Adam was cursed: 'Cursed be the ground because of you;' but the first malediction pronounced against man is absolved and replaced with a benediction. The Old Law lays down, as it were, only one condition of blessedness; the Gospel, on the other hand, announces simultaneously eight beatitudes. 'Happy the man who follows not the counsel of the wicked.' Happy the man, not any man, but the man who has reached the perfection of the manhood of Christ: 'Who follows not the counsel of the wicked.'\n\nHere, Scripture describes the three usual ways of committing sin: we entertain sinful thoughts; we commit sin in act; or we teach what is sinful. First, we entertain a sinful thought; then, after we have reflected upon it, we convert that thought into action. When we commit sin, moreover, we multiply sin by teaching others to do what we have done. 'Happy the man who follows not the counsel of the wicked' - who thinks no evil; 'nor has stood in the way of sinners' - who does no evil; 'nor sits in the company of the insolent' - who has not taught others to sin. He has not consorted with the scornful, 'nor has stood in the way of sinners.'\n\nIt is difficult for one not to sin. John the Evangelist says, in fact, that anyone who denies that he has sinned is a liar. [1 John 1:8] If, therefore, we all sin, what do the words mean, 'nor has stood in the way of sinners'? If we all sin, no one is happy, except, of course, the one who has not sinned. But we all sin, every last one of us, and so no one is blessed.\n\nConsider, however, just what the Scripture says: 'nor has stood in the way of sinners.' Scripture did not say happy the man who has not sinned, but rather, happy the man who has not persevered in sin. 'Nor has stood in the way of sinners.' Yesterday I committed sin. I am not happy. If, however, I do not remain in the state of sin, but withdraw from sin, I become happy once more. 'Nor sits in the company of the insolent.' Why does it say 'sits' in this verse and 'has stood' in the preceding one? For this reason: just as he who has not stood - persisted - in sin is happy, so he who has not sat - persisted - in evil doctrine is happy. What does that mean? You see yourselves that the three determinants of beatitude consist in not thinking evil, in not persevering in sin, and in not teaching evil. This is really what the Prophet Amos says: 'For three crimes and for four, I will not revoke my word, says the Lord.' [Amos 1:3] Moreover, he says this same thing eight times. Now, this is what he actually is saying: you have entertained sin, I have pardoned you; you have done evil, I have forgiven you; you have not repented of your sins, I have excused you: did you also have to teach evil? What the Scripture implies is this: For three sins and for four, I shall not be angered against you, says the Lord. [Isaiah 57:16]\n— Homily 1, on Psalm 1","Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Damascus, and for four, I will not turn him back, because they threshed Gilead with iron carts. LXX: And the Lord said: For three impieties of Damascus, and for four, I will not turn away from them, because they sawed with iron saws the women with child in Gilead. For the threshing sledges, which in Hebrew are called arsoth, and were translated by Theodotion iron wheels, which we have rendered iron carts, the Seventy translated iron saws. It is, moreover, a kind of cart which rolls on iron, toothed wheels underneath, so that, when the grain has been shaken out, it may crush the stubble on the threshing floors and break up the chaff into fodder for the beasts of burden, because of the scarcity of hay.\n— Commentary on Amos","We have spoken briefly about the causes of the different translation; now let us pass on to the sense of what has been said. First the word of God is directed against Damascus, in which Hazael reigned, who, at the time when Amos was prophesying, was pressing hard upon Israel, that is, the ten tribes, so much so that, after Ahab king of Israel had been overcome at Ramoth-gilead, he slaughtered and crushed the whole region which was called Gilead, and which had fallen as an inheritance to the half-tribe of Manasseh, like threshing sledges threshing grain on the threshing floors. This the prophet Elisha speaks of more fully in the volume of Kings, where we read it written: And he stood with him, and was troubled until his face was flushed; and the man of God wept. Hazael said to him: Why does my lord weep? And he answered: Because I know what evils you are going to do to the sons of Israel: you will set their fortified cities on fire, and you will kill their young men with the sword, and you will dash the little ones, and you will rip up the women with child. And Hazael said: For what am I, your servant, a dog, that I should do this great thing? And Elisha said: The Lord has shown me that you will be king of Syria. But as for what follows in the prophet:\n— Commentary on Amos","And what is said at the beginning: For three crimes of Damascus, and for four, I will not turn him back, and according to the Seventy: Shall I not turn away from him?, that is, from the people of Damascus, or Aram, that is, the Syrian, can have this sense according to the history: If he had persecuted my people once, or a second time, I would grant pardon; but now, since he has shown himself cruel a third and a fourth time, so that he crushed the captive multitude with iron carts, ought I not to chastise him with blows? Ought I not to turn the face of my clemency away from him? But according to tropology we can say this: the first sin is to have thought what is evil; the second, to have consented to perverse thoughts; the third, to have carried out in deed what you have resolved in your mind; the fourth, not to repent after the sin, and to be pleased with oneself in one's own offence. This all the heretics do, who not only think and do evil, but by their doctrine deceive all the simple, and, after the manner of the people of Damascus, which is interpreted drinking blood, drink the blood of those whom they have deceived. For sins of this kind, says the Lord, is it not right that I should chastise them with blows and turn my face away from them? So that those who do not see the truth of my dogmas may be overwhelmed with darkness by the turning away of my face. For they have crushed, or cut asunder, with iron carts the women with child of Gilead. These are the women with child of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Woe to those who are with child, and to those who give suck in those days. The women with child are the souls of believers, who at the beginning of faith can say: From fear of you, Lord, we have conceived and been in labour; and if they have not been cut up and pierced with iron by the carts and the iron and toothed wheels, of which the psalmist says: The sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows, they can also say what follows: And we have brought forth; we have made the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. And consider at the same time that they cannot cut up men of mature age, but only those who are still in the womb, or who cannot take solid food and are still nourished with the milk of infancy. These women with child, moreover, are of Gilead, which is interpreted transfer or migration of the testimony. For they have migrated from the Church into heresy, and have transferred the testimonies of God which they had received before to foreign borders.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.4-5":["Again, where in the Hebrew there is Benadad, and it is a single name, the Seventy translated the sons of Ader, deceived by the likeness of the letters res and daleth. In that place too where we said, from the house of pleasure, which in Hebrew is mebbeth eden, and all translated it likewise, the Seventy translated from the men of Charran, taking daleth, the middle letter of the name, for res, and, according to their custom, putting the Greek chi before ain, the first Hebrew letter. Cyrene also, which in Hebrew is called cira, they translated ἐπήκλητον, that is, called or named, because of the likeness of the word, since carae can be said for calling, and they understood the proper name as a common noun.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.4":["And I will send fire into the house of Hazael, and it will devour the houses of Ben-hadad. And I will break the bar of Damascus, and I will destroy the inhabitant from the plain of the idol. LXX: And I will send forth fire into the house of Hazael, and it will devour the foundations of the son of Ader. And I will break the bars of Damascus, and I will destroy the inhabitants from the plain of ὢν. He gives us to understand that Hazael, in the reigns of Uzziah and Jeroboam, had already died in Syria, and that his son Ben-hadad had succeeded him in the kingdom, from whom afterwards all the kings of Aram, that is, of Syria and Damascus, held the name Ben-hadad. Hence he fittingly did not say: I will send fire into Hazael, but into the house of Hazael, that is, into his royal house, over which his son Ben-hadad presided at that time.\n— Commentary on Amos","But the Lord will send fire into the house of Hazael, which they themselves call Hazael, that is, vision of God; a fire to consume the wood and stubble in them, and to devour the foundations of the son of Ader. For we ought to speak according to the Seventy as well, lest we seem to have set them out to no purpose: Ader is interpreted εὐπρέπεια, which we can call comeliness, because all the discourse of the heretics, being elegant and well composed and having the beauty of worldly eloquence, deceives its hearers the more easily.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.5":["And I will break, he says, the bars of Damascus, and I will destroy the inhabitant from the plain of the idol: that is, all the strength of the Syrians, who, as with a kind of cart and with bars, kept off the onsets of their adversaries. But the plain of the idol, which in Hebrew is called auen, and which both the Seventy and Theodotion rendered ὢν, Symmachus and the Fifth edition translated iniquity; Aquila ἀνωφελοῦς, that is, useless, to show the vain help of idols, since the people of Damascus is taken captive when the Assyrian comes. For we read that Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria, after killing Rezin king of Damascus, of whom Isaiah also speaks, carried away the whole people of Syria, which was called Aram, to Cyrene, and for this reason it is now said:\n\nAnd him who holds the sceptre from the house of pleasure and luxury, and the people of Syria will be carried away to Cyrene, says the Lord. LXX: And I will cut down the tribe from the men of Charran, and the people of Syria, the named one, will be led captive, says the Lord. So that we understand: I will break and I will destroy; and the people of Syria, last of all, will be carried away into the borders of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Amos","And by the bars of Damascus, as it were very strong and very firm bolts, let those be understood who are equipped with the art of dialectic and with the arguments of the philosophers. For example: he is called the door who conceived and brought forth a false dogma, like Arius in Alexandria; its bars and its very firm bolts are Euticius and Eunomius, who try to strengthen it with syllogisms and enthymemes, or rather with sophisms and pseudomena and sorites, which were wickedly invented by others. And when the bars of Damascus have been crushed and broken, he will also destroy the inhabitants from the plain of ὢν, which is interpreted labour or pain. For all the heretics have tried with excessive labour and with the pain of seeking to find some order and consistency for their heresy. And I will cut down the tribe from the men of Charran, which in our tongue is rendered holes. For they have no windows through which the light of truth may enter in to them, but certain holes and hidden passages through which harmful and unclean animals come in. And Aram will be led captive, a people lofty and exalted, which has been lifted up into pride and promises itself heavenly things, so that it may be taken captive for its own salvation and, according to the Hebrew, be carried away to Cyrene, where the calling of God is.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.6-8":["According to tropology it seems that it should be explained by us thus: Gaza, which in Hebrew is called Aza, means strength or dominion; Solomon, peaceful; the Idumean, bloodthirsty and earthly; Azotus, that is, Esdod, fire of the breast, or of the paternal uncle, although some, wrongly reading dor for dod, think it means fire of generation; Ashkelon is interpreted murderous fire, or hung up and weighed; Ekron, στείρωσις, that is, barrenness, and ἐκρίζωσις, which is interpreted uprooting. Gaza, therefore, which promises itself the strength and dominion of its own knowledge, is accused of having shut up in Idumea captives from the household of Solomon. These I take to be the teachers of the Jews, and all who follow the letter that kills and will not receive the spirit that gives life, but want whatever they interpret and understand to be earthly, and do not hear, with the disciples, the Lord expounding parables and saying to them: Those who have ears to hear, let them hear, especially since the Apostle understands a plain history as referring to the two testaments: one upon Mount Sinai, which is Hagar, and is in slavery with her children; the other upon Jerusalem the free, which is our mother. He says, then, that he will send fire upon the walls of Gaza and upon all its arguments, with which it strives to surround and defend its lies. And when he has sent fire upon the walls, its houses, or foundations, will be devoured, whatever it could have that was firm and built by the art of dialectic. From Azotus also, which itself too belongs to the foreigners, he will take away the inhabitant, whose breasts, because he follows the letter, are full not of milk but of fire, and who falsely claims to have the fire of the paternal uncle, or whose whole generation is to be consigned to the fires. And he will take away the sceptre, or the tribe, from Ashkelon, where the murderous fire is. For just as the fire of the Lord will save those whose hearts it has penetrated, so that fire which fell from heaven like lightning, which kindles the arrows of the devil, will kill whomever it breathes upon. He will move his hand also upon Ekron, where there is barrenness and uprooting. For whoever does not receive the spiritual understanding will hear through Hosea: Give them, Lord. What will you give them? Give them a barren womb and dry breasts. And so as to take in everything at once in a single word, he says that he will destroy all the remnant of the Philistines, either the city of Gath, in which is the winepress of the devil, or all the remaining Philistines, who are called falling by the cup. For when they have become drunk, they will fall down and wallow in their own vomit.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.6":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Gaza, and for four, I will not turn him back, because he carried away a complete captivity, to shut it up in Idumea. And I will send fire into the wall of Gaza, and it will devour its houses; and I will destroy the inhabitant from Azotus, and him who holds the sceptre from Ashkelon, and I will turn my hand upon Ekron, and the remnant of the Philistines will perish, says the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Gaza, and for four, I will not turn away from them, because they led captive the captivity of Solomon, to shut it up in Idumea; and I will send forth fire upon the walls of Gaza, and it will devour its foundations, and I will destroy the inhabitants of Azotus, and the tribe will be taken away from Ashkelon, and I will bring my hand upon Ekron, and the remnant of the foreigners will perish, says the Lord. The Lord threatens in the law that he will requite the sins of the fathers upon the children to the third and fourth generation; not by an equality of judgment, so that some sin and others are punished, but by the greatness of his clemency, since he always awaits repentance; and what is committed in the first generation he does not rebuke and correct until the third and fourth generation has come. Something of this kind the apostle Paul also says to the Romans: Or do you despise the riches of his goodness and forbearance and patience, not knowing that the kindness of God leads you to repentance? But according to your hardness and impenitent heart you are storing up wrath for yourself on the day of wrath and of the revelation of the just judgment of God, who will render to each one according to his works. What he says, then, is this: I waited a long time for them to repent, and for that reason I was unwilling to punish them when they sinned, so that at some time they might turn and receive healing. But because they do the same things a third and a fourth time, I am compelled to change my sentence and to correct the offenders with blows. Let us see, however, what the Philistines and their cities sinned, whom the Seventy always translated foreigners; for wherever in the Old Testament we read ἀλλοφύλους, that is, foreigners, we must take it not as a common name for all foreign nations, but properly as the Philistines, who are now called the people of Palestine. What did Gaza do to provoke the Lord to anger and vengeance? It carried away the complete captivity of the people of the Jews, and shut it up in Idumea, so as to sell to the Idumeans those whom it had taken from the Jews. For complete captivity the Seventy translated the captivity of Solomon, taking the Hebrew word salma, which means perfect and complete, for the name of Solomon. Accordingly Aquila translated ἀπηρτισμένην and ἀναπεπληρωμένην, Symmachus and Theodotion τελείαν, which signifies not Solomon but perfect and complete, so that none of the captives remained who was not handed over to the Idumeans.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.7":["Hence he says that he will send fire upon the walls and houses, or foundations, of Gaza, to devour and swallow up everything.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.8":["And because we read of five cities of the Philistines, which were struck with plagues on account of the wrong done to the ark, after Gaza he passes to Azotus, which is called Esdod, and again to Ashkelon, and with a terrible voice he threatens that he will destroy the inhabitants of Azotus, and him who holds the sceptre from Ashkelon, that is, the royal power, or the tribe of Ashkelon, so that no one may remain there, not even men of the last age, who support their trembling limbs with a guiding staff. And again he moves his hand to strike Ekron. And having enumerated four cities, he takes in the remaining cities of the Philistines in a single word: And the remnant of the Philistines will perish, by whom we must understand either the city of Gath, or all the villages which lie under the aforesaid cities.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.9-10":["Tyre means tribulation, or one who hems in; and every perverse doctrine strives to seize the truth, and to shut it up in earthly senses, and it did not remember the covenant of its brothers, that we ought to ascend from the letter to the spirit, from earthly things to heavenly ones; and that it is a precept for us to write out the sacred Scripture in our heart threefold. But God will send fire upon all the fortifications of Tyre, which will devour not only its walls but also its foundations. Concerning the captivity of Solomon, in place of which the Hebrew has consummate and perfect, we have spoken above.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.9":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Tyre, and for four, I will not turn him back, because they shut up a perfect captivity in Idumea; and they did not remember the covenant of brothers, and I will send fire upon the wall of Tyre, and it shall devour its houses. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Tyre and for four I will not turn away from her, because they shut up the captivity of Solomon in Idumea, and they did not remember the testament of brothers. And I will send forth fire upon the walls of Tyre, and it shall devour its foundations. The same three and four impieties or crimes which he had imputed to Damascus, to Gaza, and to Azotus, and to Ashkelon, and to Ekron, and to the remaining cities of the Philistines, he heaps upon Tyre as well, and he reproaches it for having shut up a consummate and perfect captivity in Idumea, concerning which we spoke above: And they did not remember the covenant of their brothers. We ask how the Tyrians are brothers of the Jews? He here calls brothers those who are friends and joined by close ties, because Hiram, the prince of Tyre, had friendship with David and Solomon, to such a degree that he, for the building of the temple and of the palace, brought cedar wood on rafts as far as Joppa; and they bestowed grain and oil on the Tyrians, and gave them twenty cities in Bashan as a gift; which he refused to accept, because they were full of grass.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.10":["Therefore he says that he will send fire upon its walls, and that it will devour its houses or foundations, which the prophet Ezekiel, in the vision of Tyre, records that Nebuchadnezzar king of the Chaldeans did, where he says: He will surround you with fortifications, and he will heap up a mound round about. And again: Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon made his army serve a great service against Tyre. Every head was made bald, and every shoulder stripped of hair, and wages were not paid to him, nor to his army, for Tyre, for the service with which he served me against her, because his army heaped up a mound for taking Tyre, which formerly was an island. And history relates that Alexander of Macedon also did this, so as to make out of the island a peninsula, which the Greeks call χεῤῥόνησον.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.11-12":["Whatever we have said about Esau and Jacob, let us refer to the Jews and the Christian people. For they, earthly and bloodthirsty, persecuted their brother Jacob, who supplanted them and took away their birthright, and they persecuted him with the sword, so that they confiscated the substance and possessions of the believers, as we read in the Acts of the Apostles; and they violated mercy and the rights of nature, and forgot their mother Rebekah, which means patience, who bore them together in Christ; and holding on to their ancient fury and wrath, to this day in their synagogues they blaspheme the Christian people under the name of Nazarenes, and provided only that they may kill us, they are willing to be burned with fire. But the Lord will send fire into Teman, into the deserted and dry places of Judea — which are by no means watered by the showers of the prophets —, and it will devour all their fortifications, or the foundations of its walls, so that, with every sense of the letter collapsing, the Church may be built up upon the foundations of Christ the Lord. In place of mercy the Seventy translated womb, and in place of fury, horror, and in place of indignation, onset, led by the ambiguity of the words, because rehem signifies both womb and mercy, aphpho both his fury and his horror; and ebrath is said both for indignation and for onset. Moreover armanoth, which we have translated houses, Aquila and Symmachus translated βάρεις, that is, houses, and Theodotion, inhabitants; the Seventy alone, both here and above, said foundations.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.11":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Edom and for four I will not turn him back; because he pursued his brother with the sword, and violated his mercy, and held his fury beyond measure, and kept his indignation to the end; I will send fire into Teman, and it shall devour the houses of Bozrah. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Idumea and for four I will not turn away from them, because he pursued his brother with the sword, and violated the womb upon the earth, and seized his horror for a testimony, and kept his onset for ever; and I will send forth fire into Teman, and it shall devour the foundations of its walls. Edom is the same as Esau, so called because of the cooking of the red lentil, for the food of which he lost his birthright. He is called by another name Seir, hairy and shaggy. Hence the mountains of the Idumeans are called the mountains of Seir, and from Edom, in the Greek language, it is called Idumea. He pursued his brother with the sword, namely the sons of Jacob, concerning which it is written more fully in the blessings of Isaac, with which he blesses first Jacob and afterwards Esau; so that the hatred of the brothers was preserved in their descendants, and the Idumean nation persecuted its brothers to such a degree that it did not even grant them passage into the holy land when they were coming out of Egypt; and not only did he pursue his brother with the sword, but he violated his mercy, or his womb, or, as Symmachus translated more plainly, his own bowels; so that he forgot his brotherhood, and hardened the bowels of mercy, and did not know that he was a brother, and despised the womb of Rebekah, who in one birth had brought forth twin infants. And what follows: And he held his fury beyond measure, and kept his indignation to the end, shows an ancient hatred, because they were never joined to one another in peace.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.12":["Hence he threatens punishment for their crimes, and says: I will send fire into Teman, which is a region of the Idumeans, and it lies toward the southern quarter, which is what Teman means; this province is called not only Teman but also Daron and Nageb, because it faces the east wind and the south wind and the south-west wind. And as for what he says: It shall devour the houses of Bozrah, it signifies, not another city, as some think, but Idumea, ὀχυρωμένην and fortified, according to that which we read in Isaiah: Who is this who comes from Edom, with garments dyed from Bozrah; this one beautiful in his robe, walking in the multitude of his strength?\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.13-15":["Now between the cruelty of Damascus and that of the sons of Ammon there is this difference, that they crushed Gilead with iron carts, or the pregnant women of Gilead with iron saws; but these ripped up the pregnant women of Gilead, to enlarge their border. In the people of Damascus, who drink blood, are understood the heretics, who not only have broken the people of God away from the Church, but have also crushed them and ground them with iron teeth, of whom it is written: Who devour my people as a meal of bread? In the sons of Ammon, which means people of grief, or my people, we understand the schismatics, who do indeed separate a deceived multitude from the Church of God, and break off and divide untrained souls which have lately conceived the word of Christ; yet they do not do this with the cruelty with which the heretics slaughter all whom they have deceived, but, remaining in the same rule of faith, they desire to enlarge their borders and to send down to posterity a name of glory. Therefore the Lord threatens that he will set on fire the walls of Rabbah, that is, of the multitude, in the day of howling and of battle, of commotion and of whirlwind, and that their king, the author of the schism, will go captive, and that the princes will submit their necks to the yoke of the Church.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.13":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of the sons of Ammon and for four I will not turn him back, because he ripped up the pregnant women of Gilead, to enlarge his border; and I will kindle fire on the wall of Rabbah, and it shall devour its houses with howling in the day of battle, and with a whirlwind in the day of commotion; and Melchom shall go into captivity, he and his princes together, says the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of the sons of Ammon and for four I will not turn away from them, because he cut up the pregnant women of the Gileadites, that he might enlarge his borders; and I will kindle fire upon the walls of Rabbah, and it shall devour the foundations with shouting in the day of battle, and it shall be shaken in the day of its consummation; and the kings shall go into captivity, their priests and their princes together, says the Lord. The divine word rebukes the sons of Ammon, who were begotten of the stock of Lot, and dwell in Arabia, where Philadelphia now is, because under Saul, king of Judea, Nahash the Ammonite, their prince, laid waste Jabesh-gilead, and ripped up its pregnant women, that he might enlarge his borders and subject all Gilead to his rule.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.1.14-15":["Hence he threatens that he will kindle fire on the walls of Rabbah, which was the metropolis of the Ammonites, so that its houses may be devoured amid the shouting and howling of the conquering army; in the day of commotion and of whirlwind, when, Nebuchadnezzar conquering, its captives will be led away, and the idol of the Ammonites, which is called Melchom, that is, their king, will be carried off to the Assyrians; and its princes, that is, its priests, will be led away together. Priests is not in the Hebrew, but princes. So the Seventy added priests, so that, if you wish to know who these princes are, you may hear: priests.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.1":["Now to prove that in the present place Moab is to be understood of the pride of the philosophers, of whom it is said: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the understanding I will reject, a few examples from the prophet must be unfolded: Amos says: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Moab and for four I will not turn away from him, because he burned the bones of the king of Edom to ashes — for truly, whatever there is in the world of perverse doctrines, whatever pertains to earthly knowledge and is thought to be robust, this is overthrown by the dialectical art and, like a fire, dissolved into ashes and embers, so that what was thought to be strongest is proved to be worth nothing —; Isaiah too rebukes the pride of Moab, saying: We have heard the insolence of Moab, he is exceedingly insolent; I have taken away his pride; Jeremiah also, prophesying against all the nations, speaks specifically to Moab: Because you trusted in your fortifications, and a little after: You had confidence in your glory, and again: How do you say: We are strong?, and then: Near is the day of Moab, that it should come, and its malice is exceedingly swift, and more plainly: Say: How has the glorious staff been broken, the rod of magnificence?, and again: The horn of Moab is broken; and these things are said so that, under the name of Moab, the foolish pride of the world that lifts itself up to heaven may be crushed.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Moab and for four I will not turn him back, because he burned the bones of the king of Idumea even to ashes. And I will send fire into Moab, and it shall devour the houses of Kerioth, and Moab shall die in the noise, in the blast of the trumpet, and I will destroy the judge from the midst of it, and I will slay all its princes with him, says the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Moab and for four I will not turn away from them, because he burned the bones of the king of Idumea into ashes; and I will send forth fire into Moab, and it shall devour the foundations of its cities, and Moab shall die in weakness with shouting, and with the voice of the trumpet; and I will destroy the judge out of it, and I will slay all its princes, says the Lord. Not only the sons of Ammon, but Moab too was begotten of the seed of Lot, who was the son of Abraham's brother. And so that God may show himself to be the Lord of all, and that all souls belong to his dominion, who is their maker, he avenges the injury done even to the king of the Idumeans, or rather the crime which was committed against him by the Moabites, in that they burned his bones even to ashes, and did not end their savagery and rage even at his death. The Hebrews relate that the bones of the king of Idumea, who was already buried, and who had gone up with Joram king of Israel and Jehoshaphat king of Judah against Moab, were afterwards torn up and burned by the Moabites in revenge for their grief.\n— Commentary on Amos","But just as one ought not to carry off the perfect captivity, or that of Solomon, and shut it up in Idumea, so as to make lowly and earthly men out of lofty and heavenly ones, which Gaza and Tyre are recorded to have done, so we ought not to burn the bones of the king of Idumea and dissolve them into ashes and embers. The Jews transfer the spiritual understanding into Idumean flesh, and the royal sense, which is engaged in the letter, and is most solid and most firm, they enervate and break down with certain genealogies and superfluous traditions, and reduce to dust; and not only do they do this, but all the heretics, who would have God, after the manner of a human likeness, sit on a high and lofty throne and set his feet upon the earth, so that, of course, they may not dangle; and have a nose with which to smell the odor of a good fragrance, eyes with which to see, hands through which to work, feet by which to walk, ears with which to hear, a mouth with which to speak, teeth with which to grind his food. Whoever reads that Judah went in to Tamar the harlot and begot two sons of her, if he follows the shamefulness of the letter and does not ascend to the beauty of the spiritual understanding, burns the bones of the king of Idumea. Whoever thinks that Hosea took a wife of fornication, and perceives nothing more in the saying than is contained in the simple words, burns the bones of the king of Idumea.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.2-3":["For this reason, therefore, they say, God declares that he will send fire into Moab, either the metropolis of the Moabites, from which the whole province was named, or the whole province, so that it might devour the houses of Kerioth, which is the name of a city; although the Seventy translated of its cities, that is, of Moab; and that it will perish in the noise and in the howling of the conquering army, of which the one is called in Hebrew saon, the other therua; and in the blast of the trumpet or horn, for this is what sophar signifies. And when Moab has perished, the counsel of its princes and judges will be vain, its cities and its leaders being overthrown alike.\n— Commentary on Amos","And for that reason the Lord sends fire into Moab, which means from the father, because he was indeed born of God, but has abandoned him. And it will devour its cities, or towns, which is what Kerioth means. Hence also Kiriath-jearim is turned into our language as village of the forests. And Moab will die in no other way than in shouting, and noise, and howling, and the blast of the horn, so that they may be overwhelmed by the lofty senses which in the holy books are compared to the blasts of the horn. Then the divine word destroys both the judges and the princes, and all who preside over earthly works, and it commands the teachers of the Church: Go up onto a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice, you who preach to Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.4":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Judah, and for four I will not turn him back, because he has cast away the law of the Lord, and has not kept his commandments; for their idols have deceived them, after which their fathers had gone, and I will send fire into Judah and it shall devour the houses of Jerusalem. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of the sons of Judah, and for four I will not turn away from them, because they have rejected the law of the Lord, and have not kept his precepts; and they have been deceived in their vanities, which their fathers followed, and I will send forth fire into Judah, and it shall devour the foundations of Jerusalem. To the other cities and nations mentioned above, Damascus, Gaza and Azotus and Ashkelon and Ekron, and the remaining towns of the Philistines, to Tyre also and Idumea, and to the sons of Ammon and Moab, he does not reproach that they cast away the law of God and despised his commandments; for they did not have the written law, but the natural one. Hence he says that they violated their own bowels and the bowels of mercy, and crushed the pregnant women of Gilead with iron carts, and carried off the captivity of Solomon, or the perfect captivity, and shut it up in Idumea, and did not remember the covenant of their fathers, and pursued their brother with the sword; and that they burst forth into such, I will not say cruelty, but rage, that they burned the bones of the king of Idumea, and would not allow death to be the last of all evils. But Judah, in which, at the time when these things were being said, there were the religion of God and the temple and the ceremonies, and which had received the law, and the precepts, and the judgments, and the testimonies, and the commandments — on the difference among which there is a fuller discussion in the eighteenth (19th) psalm and in the hundred and eighteenth (119th) —, is rebuked and convicted by the Lord; and he is going to receive fitting punishments, because he cast away his law and did not keep his commandments. And because he cast these away and despised them, his idols deceived him in due order and course. For he could not have been deceived by idols before, unless he had rejected the law of the Lord and had not done his commandments. These are the idols after which their fathers went in Egypt, fashioning images of the Egyptian ox, and adoring Baal-peor, and worshipping Ashtaroth and the Baalim.\n— Commentary on Amos","Whatever we have said about Judah is referred to the Church, in which there is the true confession, and the peace of the Lord, and the vision of truth. And in this it is reproved, that it has despised the law of God and has not done his commandments, and that each one, adoring his own vices and sins, has begun to have as God that by which he has been overcome, as the apostle Peter says: For by what one is overcome, of this he is also the slave. The miser worships gold, the glutton his belly, the lustful man his member and Baal-peor; the wanton woman, who, living in pleasures, is dead, adores venereal pleasures.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.5":["Hence the Lord threatens that he will send fire into Judah also, to devour the houses and the foundations of Jerusalem, not of that Jerusalem in which the vision of peace abides, but of those which have arisen in various cities under the name of Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Amos","Hence the Lord will send fire into Judah, and it will devour the most wicked houses and the vicious foundations, which have destroyed the glory of Jerusalem; and at the same time we learn that the Creator of all has care not only of Judah and Israel but of all the nations; and that, according to the Apostle, those who have sinned without the law perish without the law, and those who have committed offences under the law are judged by the law of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.6-7":["But we, who have shown in Hosea that under the name of Israel, and of Samaria, and of Ephraim, and of the sons of Joseph — of which tribe was Jeroboam, who separated the people from the kingdom of David and from Jerusalem and from the temple of God — the heretics are signified, let us understand now too, after Judah and Jerusalem, which is interpreted as the Church, that the prophetic discourse is directed at the heretics, who sell a holy and righteous man, and a poor one too, for money. The poor and righteous man is the churchman who does not have knowledge of the Scriptures, but, content with simplicity, does the commandments that have been enjoined, of whom it is written: The poor man does not endure threatening. And to the Galatians: Only that we should be mindful of the poor. All these things the heretics do for riches, and for sandals, with which they tread the dust of the earth; for because they cannot stand with bare feet upon the holy ground — on which Moses stood, and Joshua the son of Nun; whence the apostles too are commanded to go with bare feet, so that they may have nothing of death and of skins, which are referred to the flesh —; therefore they strike the poor on the head. And the Savior commanded his disciples that if by chance, since they were still dwelling on the earth, anything mortal had clung to their works, they should shake the dust off their feet.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.6":["Thus says the Lord: For three crimes of Israel, and for four I will not turn him back, because he sold the righteous for silver and the poor for sandals, they who crush the heads of the poor upon the dust of the earth and turn aside the way of the humble; and a son and his father went in to the girl, to profane my holy name; and upon garments taken in pledge they reclined beside every altar, and they drank the wine of the condemned in the house of their God. LXX: Thus says the Lord: For three impieties of Israel, and for four I will not turn away from them, because he sold the righteous for silver, and the poor for the sake of sandals, which tread upon the dust of the earth; and they struck the heads of the poor with the fist, and turned aside the way of the humble, and a son and his father went in to the same girl, to defile the name of their God; and binding their garments with cords, they made awnings beside the altar, and they drank wine from false accusations in the house of their God. He puts Israel, that is, the ten tribes, last for this reason: because, as we said before, almost everything that follows was written to it, so that the prophetic discourse might keep the order of the book within one continuous text. Their first crime, then, or impiety, enumerated in three offences or four, is that for money they sold a man, and a righteous man, who is the more admirable in this, that he was not overcome even by poverty so as to do anything unjust; and if indeed, compelled by the necessity of hunger, they had sold the poor righteous man for a price, there would be some excuse for the crime; but as it is, for the cheapest of things, sandals, with which they tread dust and dung, they sold the precious soul of a man.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.7":["These, according to the Seventy, strike the poor man on the head; according to the Hebrew, they crush the heads of the poor upon the dust of the earth, and they are lifted up into such pride that they are unwilling to walk with the humble. And lest this misdeed be thought small, a son and his father went in to one girl, to violate the holy name of God. Whatever, therefore, is done shamefully is referred to an injury against God, who says: Through you my name is blasphemed among the nations. This is what the Apostle also writes to the Corinthians: It is actually reported that there is fornication among you, and such fornication as is not even among the nations, so that someone has his father's wife. And you are puffed up, and have not rather mourned, so that he who has done this deed might be taken away from your midst. It often happens that a father defiles his son's wife, that is, a father-in-law his daughter-in-law; both of which are forbidden in the law: You shall not reveal the shame of your father, and you shall not uncover the shame of your daughter-in-law. And so the letter too has the greatest usefulness, if it is kept.\n— Commentary on Amos","And the heretics strike the heads of the poor, although this is not read in the Hebrew, because they have turned aside the way of the humble. The way of the humble is that which says: I am the way, and the life, and the truth, which calls us to walk along it, and says: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you. But all the chiefs of the heretics swell with pride, of whom the Apostle also speaks: Lest, being puffed up, he fall into the judgment of the devil; who says: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my nest, I will be like the Most High. Hence the proud heretics God resists, but to the humble churchmen he gives grace. The son and the father, too, went in to one girl, to violate and profane the holy name of God. That the people of the Jews is our father, we often read, as Paul says: For I do not wish you to be ignorant, brothers, that our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea, and all were baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea. And in another place: Ask your fathers, and they will declare to you, your elders, and they will tell you. And again: In place of your fathers sons have been born to you. Hence also, in the Church of the nations, it says: The sons of my mother have fought against me. We, therefore, are the sons, and the father is the people of the Jews. We sin and commit a crime when we go in with our parent to the observance of the sabbath, the injury of circumcision, the ceremonies of the abolished law; to whom the Apostle says: Behold, I Paul say to you, that if you are circumcised, Christ will profit you nothing. And again: Those who are justified in the law have fallen from grace. Whoever enters the Church in such a way as to keep the law within the Gospel, he goes in with his father to the girl, and commits debauchery, and violates the name of God. Hence those who say that it does the Jews no harm, after the coming of Christ, if they so believe in God as to keep the precepts of the law as well, defile father and son in one fornication.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.8":["And because every outrage, once it has passed the boundary of shame, adds crimes to crimes and always advances to worse things, the ten tribes even reclined upon garments taken in pledge beside every altar, which the Hebrews interpret thus: upon the garments of those who were unwilling to go to the idols, which they had extorted by force, they reclined, feasting in the idol's temple. But according to the Seventy, so great was their contempt for God that they stretched out with cords beside the altar their garments in which they sleep, or in which they commit fornication, and made παραπετάσματα, that is, coverings, so that no one could see them committing fornication in the temple; and, serving drunkenness and lust, they bought even the very wine that they drank, not from their own labor, but from false accusations; and they did this in the shrine of their god, so as to defile those whom they thought gods with shamefulness and debaucheries. So speak, and so expound, those who follow the simple history.\n— Commentary on Amos","But also, binding their garments with cords, they make coverings beside the altar, holding out the faith of Christ as a screen. For as many as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ, and they set their garments, which they have bound with the cords of sins, before the eyes of those who look, so that their outrages may not be seen, and so that whoever looks at the altar may not suspect the debauchery. This all the heretics do, committing fornication under the Christian name, and spreading the testimonies of the Scriptures over their errors and lies as a screen. Hence the apostles too spread their garments on the foal of the ass, so that the Lord might sit more softly, and might mount the road paved from the law and the prophets. On the contrary, the heretics do not spread their garments beneath the footsteps of the Savior; but they bind them beside the altar and hold them out before their sins, so that they may drink the wine of false accusation, or of the condemned, who are condemned from the very fact that they have gone out of the Church. This is the wine of Sodom, of the fury of dragons and of asps; whoever drinks it cannot be healed. And they drink the wine, not in the house of God, where the temple is and Jerusalem, but in the house of their god, whom they have fashioned for themselves with artful speech.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.9-11":["But I destroyed the Amorite from before their face, whose height was the height of the cedars, and he was strong as an oak; and I crushed his fruit above, and his roots beneath. It is I who made you go up from the land of Egypt, and I led you out in the desert forty years, that you might possess the land of the Amorite; and I raised up of your sons for prophets, and of your young men Nazirites. LXX: But I took away the Amorite from before their face, whose loftiness was as the height of the cedar, and he was strong as an oak; and I dried up his fruit above, and his roots below. I led you out of the land of Egypt, and led you around in the desert forty years, that you might possess the land of the Amorites; and I took of your sons for prophets, and of your young men for sanctification. You indeed sold the just man for silver and the poor man for sandals, striking his head, or crushing the heads of the poor in the dust, and you turned aside the way of the humble, so far that father and son went in to one girl and violated, or profaned, my holy name; and your garments, polluted by brothels and fornication, you bound with the chains of sins, so that you committed adultery in the holy place and, joining injustice to drunkenness, drank another's wine, and did this in the house of your God. But I, on the contrary, in return for your evils, which you paid back to me, bestowed good things on you: so that, as you were coming out of Egypt, I destroyed before your face Sihon king of the Amorites, who was as lofty and as strong as a cedar and an oak; and I crushed his fruit above, and his roots below. I led you out of Egypt, and through forty years I made you come to the holy land by a long circuit, that you might possess the land of the Amorite, of which we spoke above, which Moses divided to the sons of Reuben and Gad and to the half-tribe of Manasseh; and after such great benefits I added this as well, that I took prophets to myself from your sons, and from your young men, or chosen ones, made Nazirites, whom the Seventy translated as sanctified. Nor indeed, where the praises of God are spoken of, must the order of the history be kept; but it often happens that what was done first is told last, and what was done last is set at the beginning. This the seventy-seventh (78th) psalm and the hundred and fourth (105th), where the power of the signs, not their order, is described, will be able to teach us, and so will the titles of the psalms, of which let us set down only two examples, those of the third psalm and of the fifty-first (52nd), where what was done earlier is narrated last, and what we read as last is placed at the beginning. For in the volume of Kings we read that what concerns Doeg the Edomite, which is the title of the fifty-first (52nd) psalm, was written before Absalom rose against his father, which is noted at the head of the third psalm in its title. So too the Amorite was destroyed, or blotted out, last, which is now told first; and his making them go up from Egypt, and afterwards leading them out into the wilderness for forty years, we read was done at the beginning, things which are told last here, the order being changed.\n— Commentary on Amos","His roots too he cut down and broke in pieces, so that nothing of the evil tree might sprout afterwards; and the Lord himself made us go out from the world and, through forty years, a number which always belongs to affliction and fasting, to mourning and sorrow, come through tribulations and straits to the holy land, that we might first possess the land of the Amorite, and the region that was once his might become our possession; and that afterwards he might raise up prophets from our sons, all the holy men who received the prophetic spirit, of whom we read more fully in the first epistle to the Corinthians; and that from our young men, or chosen ones, he might take Nazirites and sanctified ones, who, offering their souls to God in sacrifice, do not touch wine, which can make men drunk and overturn the state of the mind, so that they may have the hair of Samson, in whose head, because the head of a man is Christ, his strength and victory resided.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.9":["Therefore, before God led us out of the land of Egypt, out of the iron furnace, he destroyed before our face the Amorite, who is called bitter, or λαλητὸς, that is, speaking, or famous and celebrated in frequent talk. Now this Amorite and king of the Amorites is called Sihon, an unfruitful and barren tree, not because it bears no fruit, but because it brings forth bad fruit, of which it is said: Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire. And of the false prophets we read: Who come in the clothing of sheep, and inwardly are ravening wolves; by their fruits you will know them, by their bad fruits, that is. Whether, then, it bears no fruit, or bears fruit but not good fruit, it is called an unfruitful tree. This Sihon, of whom it is written: Already the axe is laid to the roots of the trees, because he was an unfruitful tree, was cut down when the Lord struck him; his height is compared to the height of the cedars, of which we read: I saw the wicked man exalted on high and lifted up like the cedars of Lebanon; and I passed by, and behold, he was not; and I sought him, and his place was not found. And he fittingly says that he passed by, because from him who passes over from the world, and can say: Passing over, I will see this great vision, the cedar is taken away, and the place of pride cannot be found. His strength too, like that of the oak, a tree most hard and most strong. From its name Philo, the most eloquent man of the Hebrews, thinks that Esau was called δρύινον, that is, oaken and made of hard oak, although Esau can also be understood as ποίημα, that is, a thing made, so as to be referred to evil works. Of this strong and sturdy one he speaks in the Gospel: When a strong man armed guards his court, the things he possesses are in peace; but if one stronger than he comes upon him and overcomes him, he will take away all his armour, in which he trusted, and will distribute his spoils. And the Lord granted us that he should crush from above the fruits of this Amorite Sihon, whose name we said means an unfruitful tree, because they were bad, and take them away, lest anyone, thinking them good, should eat and perish.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.11":["Is it not so, sons of Israel, says the Lord.\n\nAnd you will give the Nazirites wine to drink, and you were commanding the prophets, saying: Do not prophesy. LXX: likewise. While I was bestowing such great benefits on you, so as to kill your adversaries and hand their land over to you, and to choose prophets and Nazirites from your sons and young men and consecrate them to my worship. Can you say that I did not do these things, and deny my clemency toward you, through which you live?\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.12":["You who broke out into such madness that you made my Nazirites drunk with wine, though they are commanded by the law not to drink anything that can intoxicate, and charged the prophets not to prophesy in my name. This is what Amaziah the priest of Bethel also commanded the prophet Amos, whom we now have in hand; and Jeremiah was ordered by the king not to speak the words of the Lord to the people, to such a degree that the king even burned his discourses in the fire. From this passage Tatian, the chief of the Encratites, tries to build up his heresy, asserting that wine must not be drunk, since it is commanded in the law that the Nazirites should not drink wine, and now those who give the Nazirites wine to drink are accused by the prophet. If they follow the letter in all things, and thrust Jewish fables upon the churches of Christ, then they ought also to let their hair grow, and not eat grapes, dried or fresh, and not go in over a dead father and mother; and if perhaps they have done these things, and have been overcome either by human frailty or by necessity, let them shave their heads, and let all the days of their consecration and their toil be void. But if they do not do these things, and cannot, after the manner of Jewish innkeepers, mix water with wine, shadows with the truth, the constraint of the law with the grace of the Gospel, let them understand that drunkenness to be the one by which the vigour of the soul is made drunk and overwhelmed with worldly cares, and understand that it is those who charge the prophets, saying: Do not prophesy, who, overcome by envy, forbid learned men to bring forth the word of doctrine. And while the Lord says: Go and say to this people, they on the contrary command that you not speak in the name of the Lord, above all if he who teaches looks not to shameful gain, glory and boasting, but to the profit of those who read and hear.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.13-14":["And the cart that is rolled is the one of which we read in Isaiah: The land of Moab shall be trampled down, as a threshing floor is trampled with carts. And in another place: I will make you, he says, like the new wheels of a cart with the teeth of saws, and you shall crush the mountains and grind the hills to pieces, so that, after you have crushed the mountains and hills, flight may perish from the swift, as Paul says: Do you not know that those who run in the stadium all run indeed, but one receives the prize. And in another place: You were running well; who hindered you, so that you should not obey the truth? Hence, fearing for himself as well, he says: Not that I have already received, or am perfect; but I run, if I may lay hold of that in which I also have been laid hold of by Christ. All these things he did, lest flight perish from the swift; and for this reason, when he had now reached the finishing mark and received the prize of victory, he said in security: I have fought the good fight, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith; for the rest, there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the just judge, will render to me on that day; and not to me only, but also to all who love his coming. If we flee with a quick step, sin will not be able to catch us; but if malice binds our feet, we shall speak with the prophet to God: Where shall I go from your spirit, and where shall I flee from your face? Of bad runners the Apostle speaks: It is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who has mercy.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.13-16":["Behold, I will creak beneath you, as a cart loaded with hay creaks, and flight shall perish from the swift, and the mighty shall not hold his strength, and the strong shall not save his soul, and he who holds the bow shall not stand, and the swift of foot shall not be saved, and the rider of the horse shall not save his soul, and the strong of heart among the mighty shall flee naked in that day, says the Lord. LXX: Therefore behold, I will roll beneath you as a cart full of stubble is rolled, and flight shall perish from the runner, and the mighty shall not hold his might, and the fighter shall not save his soul, and the archer shall not endure, and the swift of foot shall not be able to be saved, and the horseman shall not save his soul, and his heart is found among the powerful, and he shall flee naked in that day, says the Lord. While I was bestowing benefits on you, and leading you out of the land of Egypt, and killing the Amorite before your face so that you might possess his land, and raising up prophets from your sons and Nazirites from your young men, you were making my Nazirites drunk with wine and saying to the prophets: Do not prophesy. Therefore, just as a cart weighed down with a load of stubble or hay howls far off with creaking and noise, so I, no longer bearing your sins, and handing them over to the fire like stubble, will cry out and say: Flight shall perish from the swift, whom the Hebrews understand as Jeroboam the son of Nebat, who had earlier fled into Egypt. Here, however, let us take not the princes themselves but their houses and their offspring. And the mighty shall not hold his strength — the mighty one they interpret as Baasha, who was most ready for wars —, and the strong shall not save his soul — this one here they take to be Omri —, and he who holds the bow shall not stand, or endure, they think was said of Jehu the son of Nimshi, who struck Joram king of Israel with an arrow. And the swift of foot shall not be saved: they understand Menahem, who, hastening in vain, sent gifts to the king of the Assyrians. And the rider of the horse shall not save his soul: this one they interpret as Pekah the son of Remaliah, who, joined with Aram, that is, the Syrian, laid waste much of Judea under Ahaz its king. And the strong of heart, among the mighty, shall flee naked in that day, says the Lord. Hoshea alone, who was the last king of the ten tribes and tried to call the straying people back to the worship of God, will come out of the fire as if naked. And he calls him naked because under him the ten tribes were taken captive. This is what the Hebrews hold, and as it was handed down to us by them, so we have faithfully set it out for our own people.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.13":["But now let us return to our own. God threatens that he will roll a cart weighed down with hay or stubble beneath them, so that, since they have no grain to be stored up in barns, their hay and stubble may be burned up in the fire. This is the hay of which the prophet says: Let them become like the hay of the buildings, which withers before it is plucked up. And: All flesh is hay. And the stubble is that over whose burning the prophet laments so mournfully, saying: Woe is me. For I have become like one who gathers stubble in the harvest, and like grapes at the vintage, when there is no cluster to eat, the first-ripe. Woe is me. O soul, for the reverent man has perished from the earth, and there is none among men who does what is right: all are judged in blood, and the rest. This is the hay and stubble of which the Apostle also speaks: Wood, hay, stubble, each one's work will become manifest, because in the day of the Lord it will be revealed in fire.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.14":["There follows: And the mighty shall not hold his strength; not that he is mighty, but that he boasts of being mighty. Or he is the one who trusts in his own strength and not in the mercy of God, according to what is written: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject; not that true wisdom can perish and the understanding of the truth be rejected, but that the wisdom of those perishes who seem wise in their own eyes and trust in their own learning. The strong one too, or the fighter, who will not save his soul, is he who by no means possesses the armour of the Apostle: having a shield, but not of faith; girded about the loins, but not in truth; clothed with a breastplate, but not of righteousness; bearing a sword, but not of salvation. A fighter of this sort does not sanctify the battle, nor can he fight the wars of the Lord, since he struggles against the truth on behalf of falsehood. Such a fighter cannot say: Blessed be the Lord my God, who teaches my hands for battle and my fingers for war. My mercy and my refuge.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.15":["The heretics too have archers, who, bending the bow in vain, will not be able to withstand the arrow of the Lord, who speaks through Isaiah: But I am like a chosen arrow in the quiver of the Lord. These are the archers of whom David also sings: Behold, sinners have bent the bow, they have made ready their arrows in the quiver, to shoot in the dark at the upright of heart. And the swift of foot, he says, shall not be saved: he who, because of the sharpness of his wit, runs about through the testimonies of the Scriptures and strives to crush the truth with the eloquence of orators or the sophisms of dialecticians; and in this he is tripped and falls, because he trusts not in the Lord but in his own feet. The rider of the horse, too, shall not save his soul, he who does not know what was said through the prophet: The horse is deceitful for salvation. And he does not know that it is written: All who mounted horses have slumbered. This man will not save his soul, but as he perishes he will hear: Some in chariots, and some on horses, but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God. They were hindered and have fallen, but we have risen and stand upright.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.2.16":["And the strong also of heart among the mighty shall flee naked. This passage can be explained in two ways: either that he can escape for this reason, that he has stripped off the old man and the skin tunics of sins, and was weighed down by no burden; or, on the contrary, that because he is naked and has lost the garment of Christ, of which it is said in the Apostle too: Put on Christ Jesus, and in another place: If indeed, being clothed, we are not found naked, his strength will profit him nothing, but in the day of battle and contest he will flee from his pursuers and, unable to resist without the armour of the Apostle, will turn his back to the enemy.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.1-2":["Hear the word which the Lord has spoken concerning you, sons of Israel; concerning every kindred which I brought out of the land of Egypt, saying: Solely you have I known of all the kindreds of the earth; therefore I will visit upon you all your iniquities. LXX: Hear this word which the Lord has spoken concerning you, house of Israel, and against every tribe which I brought out of the land of Egypt, saying. Nevertheless, you have I known of all the tribes of the earth; therefore I will take vengeance upon you for all your wickednesses. The prophetic word is directed to the sons of Israel, and who these sons of Israel are the following verse shows: Concerning every kindred, he says, which I brought out of the land of Egypt, which we must take to refer not only to the ten tribes but to all twelve, adding Judah and Benjamin. For he brought them all out of the land of Egypt, and he says: Solely — or, as Symmachus translated, Only —, you have I known of all the kindreds of the earth. And because I, who am the Creator of all, have known you alone, and have held you as my peculiar people, therefore upon you alone I will repay all your sins: For the mighty will suffer torments mightily; but he who is least is worthy of mercy. And in Ezekiel we read: Begin from my saints. And the judgment of God is said to have its beginning from his own house. Let this, meanwhile, have been said according to the history. Moreover, since hearing in the holy Scriptures is taken to mean not only that which we receive with the ears of the flesh, but also that of which the Lord says: He who has ears to hear, let him hear, and since for this reason all the people also saw the voice of God, the word of the Lord is properly addressed to those who see the Lord with their understanding, whom he brought out of the land of Egypt and out of the power of Pharaoh — so that they should by no means serve with mud and straw, building the cities of Egypt —; and whom he has known out of all the tribes of the earth, as the Apostle says: But now knowing God, or rather being known by him. And in another place: He who is ignorant will be ignored. The Lord, then, does not know all, but those who are worthy of his knowing them, as he says in the Gospel: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you. For in that they are workers of iniquity, they are unknown to the Lord. Therefore, he says, because I have known you alone and have held you as mine, I will visit upon you all your iniquities. Whom the Lord loves, he reproves, and he chastises every son whom he receives. And he said well, I will visit, and not, I will strike, because the stroke of God is a visitation and a healing. And all your iniquities, he says, or your sins, I will visit, so that nothing may remain unstruck, and nothing fail to receive healing.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.3-6":["Will two walk together unless they have agreed? Will a lion roar in the forest unless he has prey? Will the lion's whelp give voice from his den unless he has seized something? Will a bird fall into the snare of the earth without a fowler? Will the snare be taken up from the earth before it has caught something? If the trumpet sounds in the city, will the people not be terrified? Will there be evil in the city which the Lord has not done, for the Lord God will not do a thing unless he has revealed his secret to his servants the prophets. The lion will roar, who will not fear? The Lord God has spoken, who will not prophesy? LXX: Will two walk together at all unless they have known each other? Will a lion bellow from his forest, having no prey? Will the lion's whelp give his voice from his den at all unless he has snatched something? Will a bird fall upon the earth without a fowler? Will a snare be set down upon the earth unless it has caught something? If the trumpet sounds in the city, will the people not fear? Will there be evil in a city which the Lord has not done, for the Lord God will not do anything unless he has revealed his instruction to his servants the prophets. The lion will bellow, and who will not fear? The Lord God has spoken, and who will not prophesy? I have read in the commentaries of a certain man, who wished to win assent to a difficult thing, that to the eight crimes, or impieties and sins, first of Damascus, second of Gaza and the other cities of Palestine, third of Tyre, fourth of Idumea, that is, Edom, fifth of the sons of Ammon, sixth of Moab, seventh of Judah, that is, the two tribes, eighth, which is the last, of Israel, that is, the ten tribes, as many threats are now given in return, and that the first answers to the first, that is, Damascus, the second to the second, that is, Gaza, and the rest to the rest. Whether this is true, he who wrote it will know for himself. Another, however, thinks that six examples are set forth, of two walking together on the road, and of a lion roaring in the forest, and of a lion's whelp giving its voice when it has seized something, and of a bird caught by a fowler, and of a snare which is fixed in the ground to catch prey, and of a trumpet sounding in the city, and that to these examples a likeness is given in return: as the things above have a cause and do not happen unless that cause has gone before, so no evil befalls in a city which has not been done at the Lord's command.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.3":["There are some who think that the first example contains a teaching: that two cannot walk together unless they have first agreed with each other in him who made both one, and the middle wall of the partition destroyed, and fashioned the two into one new man; and he made peace between them, and joined them with the bond of love, so that, when two are together, they may ask of the Father and obtain whatever they have asked. These are the two rods which are joined to each other in Ezekiel, and the two peoples, of the circumcision and of the Gentiles, of which the Lord speaks to Elijah: I have left for myself seven thousand men who have not bent the knee before Baal. And in another place God: I was found, he says, by those who did not seek me; I appeared to those who did not ask for me. But when the two have been brought together into one, they will cleave to the Lord, and one spirit will come to be: For he who cleaves to the Lord is one spirit. These two, then, who agree with each other and walk together in the way of Christ, can fear the assault of none of their adversaries.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.4":["But if they are separated by discord, at once the roaring lion will attack them, of whom the apostle Peter also says: Your adversary the devil, like a roaring lion, goes about seeking whom he may devour. This roaring lion does not move about in sown fields, not in vineyards, not in olive groves, nor among the fruit-bearing trees, which praise God, but where there are forests and untilled ground and wild beasts dwell. And not only will they lie open to the lion's bites, but the lion's whelp will seize them and lead them to his den, fleeing the light and making for the darkness. For everyone who sins loves the darkness and hates the light; of these we read in the psalm too: You have set darkness, and it became night; in it all the beasts of the forest will pass through. The lions' whelps roaring to seize their prey, and to seek their food from God. For the lion's whelp seeks not the prey which he already possesses, which is in his own power, but to snatch from the Church of Christ; of him it is written in Habakkuk: His foods are choice.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.5":["The third punishment of those whom discord has separated is that they lose the wings by which they were formerly borne on high, and fall headlong from heaven to earth, and are held by the fowler, who is better called ἰξευτὴς, because with clinging birdlime he catches free birds which of their own will come down to earthly things. Of these it is written in Proverbs: Not unjustly are nets spread for birds. For just is the ruin of sinners, who, though they had the wings of a dove and ought to have flown through the air, were pressed down to earthly things by the weight of their sins, and through their own vice stuck fast to the birdlime. Of this we read in the Apostle: He who cleaves to a harlot becomes one body with her. The fourth punishment of those in discord is that they are caught in the snare, which is set not in heaven but on the earth; the saint glories in having escaped it, and says: Our soul, like a sparrow, has been snatched from the snare of the hunters; the snare is broken, and we are set free. This is the broken snare of which the Apostle speaks to believers: God will crush Satan under your feet swiftly. And again we read in David: Beside the path they have set a stumbling block for me. For they cannot deceive any of the simple among believers unless they put forward the name of Christ, so that, while we think we are finding Christ, we go on to the Antichrist.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.6":["The fifth punishment is that of those who have abandoned the peace which surpasses all understanding, and which the Lord, as he went to the Father, left to the apostles, saying: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you: that, though set in the city of the Lord, they are terrified by the sound of the trumpet. For whatever is said in the holy Scriptures is a threatening trumpet, piercing the ears of believers with a great voice. If we are righteous, we are summoned by the trumpet of Christ to blessedness; if sinners, we hear what torments we are going to suffer. But the evil which the Lord does in the city is not the contrary of virtue, but affliction and torment, of which we read: Sufficient to the day is its own evil, that is, tribulation and distress. And in the prophet we read: I am God, who made light and darkness, who make peace and create evils. For just as darkness is contrary to light and day, so war too is contrary to peace; it is not evil in itself, but seems to be evil to those who endure it. And that we may know that in the holy Scriptures evil is sometimes taken not as the contrary of virtue but as affliction, punishments and distress, let us take one more example, from Jonah: And God saw their works, that they had turned from their most wicked ways, and he repented over the evil which he had spoken of doing to them. The evil there is surely the overthrow of the Ninevites, which God had threatened through the prophet, and not the evil contrary to virtue, which belongs to sin, on account of which he was threatening torments to sinners.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.7":["There follows: The Lord God will not do a thing unless he has revealed his secret, or his correction, to his servants the prophets; not that God reveals to the prophets everything that he does in heaven or has already done before, but what he is going to do on earth. He revealed to Noah his servant that he was going to bring on the flood. He revealed to Abraham and Lot his servants that Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim were going to burn. He revealed to Joseph the interpretation of the dream of the seven years of famine in Egypt, and to the prophet Jonah the overthrow of Nineveh, so that those who heard of the punishments to come might either repent and avoid the torments, or, if they scorned them, be punished more justly. So now too the Lord reveals through Amos his servant and prophet what he is going to do to the ten tribes, so that, if they are turned to better things and abandon their idols, they may be freed from the danger that threatens. And note that God, merciful and kind, always foretells what is to come, so that he may not be forced to inflict punishments. And what the heretics slanderously charge against the Creator, as though he were severe and savage, cruel, and nothing but a judge, because there is no evil in a city which he does not do, this we refer to the greatness of his mercy, in that he does not inflict punishments unless he has foretold them beforehand. And he who foretells does not wish to punish sinners.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.8":["The lion, he says, will roar, and who will not fear? The Lord God has spoken, who will not prophesy? The word who is taken in this passage not for the impossible, but for the rare. For he will not fear, and he will not prophesy, who because of his hardness has scorned to hear the Lord. And the sense according to the history is this: If at the voice of the lion all things tremble and the whole nature of living creatures is terrified, shall we, when God commands us to speak and announce to the people the punishments to come, not prophesy? Shall we not speak? I know that a certain man has left it written in his commentaries that the roaring lion is to be understood as the devil, roaring at those who are going to perish, but that God, who speaks through the prophets, is to be referred to the Lord the Savior; so that, as they hear the lion's roaring in the bad sense and are taken captive unto death, so the saints may hear the Lord commanding in the good sense, and be saved.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.9-10":["Make it heard in the houses of Azotus, and in the houses of the land of Egypt; and say: Gather yourselves upon the mountains of Samaria, and see the many madnesses in the midst of her, those who suffer calumnies in her inner chambers; and they did not know how to do what is right, says the Lord, treasuring up iniquity and plunder in their houses. LXX: Announce to the regions among the Assyrians, and to the regions of the land of Egypt; and say: Gather yourselves upon the mountain of Samaria, and see many wonders in the midst of her, and the oppression in her; and she did not know what things are to come before her, says the Lord, who treasure up iniquity and misery in their regions. We said above that the prophet Amos prophesies especially, indeed in the great part of the volume, to the ten tribes, which are called Israel and Ephraim, and Samaria. And so now too he is commanded to announce to Azotus and to the land of Egypt the things that follow. In place of Azotus the Seventy, intending I know not what, put the Assyrians, although Azotus in the language of the Hebrews is called Esdod, and the Assyrians, Assur. For the houses too, which are called armanoth, they translated regions, which are called sadoth, and the words have nothing of likeness. Say, he says, that all the nations which are in the neighbourhood should gather and see the crimes of Israel, lest they think the judgment of God unjust. As if he were saying: First see what they do, and then approve my sentence. And he fittingly added: Upon the mountains of Samaria, whether of the city, which is now called Sebaste and was then called Samaria, or of the whole province, which is situated in mountainous country: And see the many madnesses in the midst of her, that in place of God they worship calves and sacrifice their sons, and, not content with this crime, they bring calumnies against the poor in their inner chambers, and do not know at all how to do what is right, treasuring up for themselves not gold and money, which are sometimes gained by labour, but iniquities and plunder in their houses; so that, because they abandoned the worship of God and worshipped idols, he delivers them, God does, to a reprobate mind, to do what is not fitting. And since, once and for all, according to the laws of tropology, Samaria is referred to the heretics, who falsely claim to be the guardians of the commandments of God, the divine word commands that they preach among the nations which have no knowledge of God, in whose teaching there is a burning fire, and tribulation and anguish, that they may consider the false Samaria, and go up to the mountains of her pride, and see the many madnesses in the midst of the city, while each one fashions what he wishes and worships his own fiction: as Marcion a God who is good and idle; as Valentinus thirty αἰῶνας (aeons) and a Christ last of all, whom he calls ἔκτρωμα, that is, an abortion; as Basilides, who calls the almighty God by the monstrous name ἀβράξας (Abraxas), and says that this same name, according to the Greek letters, and the number of the yearly course, is contained in the circle of the sun, whom the heathen, under the same number in other letters, call Μείθραν (Mithras). And the Iberian follies marvel at Balsamus and Barbelo. Are these not madnesses, and many madnesses, when each one fashions whatever has fallen into his mind? They also suffer calumny in her inner chambers, that is, all the simple among the believers are oppressed, while under the name of truth they worship a lie; and they do not know at all how to do what is right, because they have lost the path of truth, nor do they believe in him who says: I am the truth; and they treasure up for themselves the dogmas of iniquity, and they seize and shut up in their houses those whom they have been able to deceive by false error. But if it please us to read Assyrians — which, however, is not found in the Hebrew —, let us say that these are the ones of whom it is written: I will bring it upon the great mind, the prince of the Assyrians, who said: By my strength I will do it, and so on, so that the heretics are taken captive by the Assyrian and held in the land of Egypt, from which, coming now to the Church, they have been set free.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.11":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: The land shall be afflicted and surrounded, and your strength shall be pulled down from you, and your houses shall be plundered. LXX: Therefore thus says the Lord God: Tyre, and your land round about, shall be deserted, and he will pull down your strength from you, and your regions shall be plundered. In place of Tyre, which in the Hebrew is written with two letters, sade and res, and is called Sor, as Aquila and the Seventy likewise translated it, the Hebrew who instructed me in the holy Scriptures interpreted tribulation; nor do we reject his opinion, because Symmachus too, who is not accustomed to follow the κακοζηλίαν (affectation) of words but the order of the understanding, says siege and encircling of the land. In place of siege, which he calls πολιορκία, strength was put by Theodotion, who thought that what should be read was not Sar and Sor, which means tribulation or Tyre, but sur, which properly refers to a very hard rock, which in Greek is called ἀκρότομος, and which we in Latin can call flint. Let us speak, then, of each in turn. The LXX said: Tyre, and your land all around. And the sentence seemed to hang unfinished; so they added on their own: Shall be deserted; nor is it an error of the translators, where, because of the ambiguity of the word, both tribulation and Tyre can be said. And the sense is: Upon the mountains of Samaria my people has committed many madnesses, practising calumny, and utterly ignorant of what is right, and treasuring up for itself iniquity and plunder; therefore it shall be afflicted, or crushed, and their land shall be surrounded by the Assyrian army, and there shall be pulled down from you, he says, O Samaria, O ten tribes, O once my people, your strength; and there shall be plundered in your houses what you gathered by calumny from the tears of the wretched. The heretics too will either be afflicted in the day of judgment, and all their strength will be worn down, and what they had gained from plunder will be plundered, so that the peoples whom they had deceived may be set free; or certainly they will be afflicted daily by churchmen, and surrounded by the testimonies of the Scriptures, and there will be pulled down from them the strength of the syllogisms and the subtleties of words by which they had confirmed their dogma. And their houses will be plundered, so that those who had been carried off from the Church may return to the Church.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.12":["Thus says the Lord: As if a shepherd should rescue from the mouth of the lion two legs, or the tip of an ear, so shall the sons of Israel be rescued who dwell in Samaria in the region of the couch and on the pallet of Damascus. LXX: Thus says the Lord: As if a shepherd should tear away from the mouth of the lion two legs, or the tip of an ear, so shall the sons of Israel be torn away who dwell in Samaria over against the tribe and in Damascus. At the beginning of Amos, where we discussed those little verses: The Lord will roar from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will utter his voice; and the beautiful places of the shepherds mourned, and the top of Carmel was dried up, we said that he used the language of his own craft, so that, because as a herdsman of flocks he had known nothing more terrible than the lion, he compared the wrath of the Lord to lions. According to this sense, then, now too he takes an example from what he had often seen, and so he tells that the small remnant of the ten tribes is to be rescued from the hands of the Assyrians: as if a shepherd, when all the other limbs have been devoured, should snatch two legs or the tip of an ear from the lion's jaws; and, having set down the example for the comparison, so, he says, shall the sons of Israel be rescued, namely the ten tribes that dwell in Samaria, in the region of the couch and on the pallet of Damascus. This seems to me to be explained thus: We read in Isaiah that Rezin king of Aram, that is, of the Syrians, who reigned in Damascus, and Pekah son of Remaliah, king of Israel, having made a league, came fighting against Judah; concerning whom the Lord commands Ahaz king of Judah and Jerusalem not to fear the two half-burnt firebrands, when, as a sign of full liberty, both the conception and the childbearing of a virgin are shown to him. The sons of Samaria, then, dwell in the region and quarter of the couch, resting on the help of the Syrians, and promising themselves victory on the pallet of Damascus, so that, as one who is weary is refreshed on a couch, so they might strengthen their broken forces by the help of a neighbouring nation. Hence also in the same Isaiah it is said, by a type of things to come, that a child is to be born who, before he knows how to call father and mother, will take the spoils of Samaria and the power of Damascus, which, that is, were fighting against Jerusalem with their forces joined. For the region of the couch and the pallet of Damascus the Seventy translated: Over against the tribe and in Damascus, so that according to tropology we may refer the tribe to Judah, and Damascus to the calling of the nations, out of whom one flock of the Lord has been made, whose sheep the savagery of lions often mangles, and from whose jaws scarcely two legs or the tip of an ear is carried off. In the leg is shown the way of teachings, in the ear the mysteries of the words. Hence also the apostles are commanded to walk with bare feet, without shoes and without any skin of a dead animal. And to believers it is said: He who has ears to hear, let him hear. In the Apocalypse of John too we read: He who has ears, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches. And more plainly through the prophet: Put, he says, your words in the ears of your heart. This path both the heretics and their masters the demons hasten to obstruct and to interweave with nets, so that we may fall in the way which is Christ. But if there comes a churchman and man of God, and a Samson, which means sun, he kills the lion, and going down into the pit of hell, which cools the waters, in the time of snow, of storm and of winter, he strangles the lion, so that with secure foot and safe ears we may be able to follow the Lord and to hear his words.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.13":["Hear and testify in the house of Jacob, says the Lord God of hosts, that in the day when I begin to visit the transgressions of Israel upon him, I will visit also upon the altars of Bethel; and the horns of the altar shall be cut off and shall fall to the ground; and I will strike the winter house with the summer house; and the houses of ivory shall perish; and many houses shall be scattered, says the Lord. LXX: Priests, hear: testify to the house of Jacob, says the Lord God Almighty, that in the day when I avenge the iniquities of Israel upon him, I will avenge also upon the altars of Bethel; and the horns of the altar shall be undermined and shall fall upon the ground. I will confound and strike the winged house upon the summer house, and the houses of ivory shall perish; and many other houses shall be added, says the Lord. What is put at the beginning of the section according to the Seventy, priests, is not found in the Hebrew, but in place of this word is read ares, which Aquila interpreted as pallet; and I think that the Seventy put the Hebrew word itself, which some, not understanding it, read in place of ares as ἱερεῖς, that is, priests. Now the prophetic word commands that they first hear, and then testify in the house of Jacob, that is, among the ten tribes, what the Lord has said.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.14-15":["When the Lord begins to visit the transgressions of Israel, which formerly beheld God with the mind, he will visit also upon the altars of Bethel, not the one altar which the Church has, but the very many altars of the heretics. For they have as many altars as schisms. He will cut off also the horns of the altar which in their pride they boast that they possess as the one altar. And their horns, that is, their arrogance, will fall to the ground; and he will strike the winter house, those heretics who toil in continence and fasts, in dry fasts and sleeping on the ground, among whom are Tatian and Mani; and the summer house, such as the Nicolaitans and the Eunomians, and the new followers of an old heresy, who for the gluttony of the belly admit every pleasure. And the houses of ivory will perish, which are put together with an artful tooth and the splendour of rhetorical polish. And many houses will be scattered, which are dispersed through the whole world. For when the one truth rises, many lies will be destroyed. For the winter house the Seventy translated οἶκον τὸν περίπτερον, which we have interpreted winged, because it has little doors over the windows, and as it were wings for keeping off the severity of the cold.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.14":["When I begin, he says, to visit the transgressions, or impieties, of the house of Israel, and to render to them what they deserve, and the time of captivity has come, then the altars of Bethel shall be destroyed, where the golden calves were, and the horns of the altar shall be cut off, the altar which was split apart when Jeroboam too stretched out his hand, and they shall crash to the ground, broken, as the Assyrian lays waste.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.3.15":["And I will strike, he says, the winter house with the summer house, which we can take simply according to the letter: that they were of such great wealth that they had double houses, winter ones and summer ones, of which some faced the north and others the south, so that according to the variety of the seasons they might secure a temperate measure of cold and heat and of the sky. We can call the winter house the kingdom of Israel, in which there was coldness of religion and of the worship of God, and various whirlwinds of the winds, and savage storm; and the summer house, Judah and Jerusalem, in which was the temple, and burnt offerings were offered morning and evening, and the warmth of religion flourished. And the houses of ivory, he says, shall perish, which can also stand according to the history. For we read that Ahab king of Israel abounded in such great luxuries that he made himself a house of ivory. For 'of ivory' we find in the Hebrew houses of tooth, that is, of elephant. Of these it is also written in the forty-fourth (45th) psalm: From the houses of ivory, out of which the daughters of kings have delighted you in your honour.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.1":["Hear this word, you fat cows, who are on the mountain of Samaria; who do calumny to the needy, and crush the poor, who say to your masters: Bring, and we will drink. The Lord God has sworn by his holy one that behold, days shall come upon you, and they shall lift you up on poles, and your remnants in boiling pots; and you shall go out through the openings, one against another, and you shall be cast into Armon, says the Lord. LXX: Hear this word, you cows of Basanitis, who are on the mountain of Samaria, who oppress the poor and trample the needy, who say to your masters: Give us, that we may drink. The Lord swears by his holy ones that behold, days shall come upon you, and they shall take you up in arms; and those who are with you, in pots, pestilent traders; and you shall be cast out naked, one facing another, and you shall be cast upon mount Remmam, says the Lord. In place of fat cows, the Seventy put of Basanitis; Aquila and Theodotion the Hebrew word itself, Basan; we, following the interpretation of Symmachus, who says αἱ Βόες εὔτροφοι, that is, fattened kine, have interpreted fat cows. Now he speaks to the princes of Israel and to all the nobles of the ten tribes, who gave themselves up to luxuries and plunder, that they should hear the word of God, and know that they are not ploughing oxen but fat cows from the herd, or such as are fed in the pastures of Bashan, which are places most fertile in grass; and by this he signifies that they were made ready not for agriculture but for sacrifice and for eating. You are fat cows on the mountain of Samaria, and you crush all the lowly, and you say to your masters, that is, to the shepherds, by whom we understand the kings: Give us and we will drink, that is, only give the command, and we will lay everything waste. But from the fact that he put: Give us and we will drink, and did not say: Give and we will eat, he signifies their drunkenness in wine and luxury, which overturn the state of the mind.\n— Commentary on Amos","Now the word of the Lord which the cows of Bashan are commanded to hear is, according to the tropology we have begun, addressed to the heretics, who, serving the belly and gluttony, are rightly called very fat cows, or cows of ignominy; for this is what Bashan means, that is, αἰσχύνη; and if we wished to call it confusion, we should be interpreting Babylon rather than Bashan. These fat cows, or cows of ignominy and dry, for Bashan is understood in this sense too, are on the mountain of Samaria, to which it is also said in Hosea: Take away your calf, Samaria. And again in the same prophet: For your calf was a deceiver, Samaria; and they are on the mountain of Samaria for this reason, that they are always lifted up into pride and promise themselves lofty things. Samaria also means watch, not because they keep watch over the words of the Lord, but because they boast that they are the watchmen of his precepts. Who do calumny to the needy and crush the poor. By the needy and the poor understand the churchman, who, content with the simplicity of the truth, does not seek the furniture of the heretics, and the brilliance of eloquence, and the riches of arguments. These cows say to their masters: Bring and we will drink. Their masters we can call either the princes of perverse dogmas, such as Valentinus, and Marcion, and Arius, and Eunomius, or those who through many books strengthen what others have wickedly invented. To these the cows of Basanitis say: Bring us and we will drink. For unless those give, these have nothing to devour, or rather nothing to drink so as to be drunk. But that we may know that waters and cups signify teaching, the Lord speaks to the Samaritan woman: Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again; but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever. Therefore whoever drinks of the waters of the Samaritan woman, that is, of the heretics, will always thirst and will not be able to cool the burning of a parched throat, as Isaiah cries aloud about this very thing: As one who is thirsty dreams that he drinks, and when he has risen he still thirsts, and his soul has hoped in vain; so shall all the nations be that fight against Jerusalem. For truly whoever drinks of the waters of the heretics, and fights against Jerusalem, the Church of God, drinks in his sleep, and his soul is mocked by empty images. And when he thinks himself satisfied, then he will have the beginning of thirst. Hence also it is said to the churchman: Drink waters from your own vessels, and your waters from the springs of the wells, and let them be for you alone.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.2-3":["And the Lord your God swears by his holy one against the cows that are slaves to fattening and feasts, or he swears by his holy ones, that days shall come upon them, days of judgment and of punishments; so that, carrying them in their arms, they may put them, and those who are with them, into cauldrons, or carry them; and either the cows themselves are pestilent traders, or those who come to carry them; and they are cast out naked, seeing one another, and are cast upon mount Remmam, says the Lord God. For we must speak also according to the LXX, lest we seem to have set them out in vain. When the day of judgment and of vengeance comes against the heretics, then they will be carried away in arms, that is, with their own arms, with which they had fought against the Church, or, conquered by the armour of God and overcome by his fighters, they will be put into cauldrons, so that those who were formerly pestilent traders may be burnt and boiled through; for they traded to this end, that they might hand over to death those whom they had deceived. These are they of whom it is said: The sons of Eli, sons of pestilence, who sat in the chair of the pestilent. And when they have been boiled through and burnt, they will come out naked, having none of the things which they formerly presumed for themselves. And they will see their ignominy, and will be cast upon mount Remmam, which means loftiness, so that they may be crushed in their own pride. Some interpret Remmam as the vision of someone, that is, ὅρασίν τινὸς. For they were not able to see all things, but promised themselves the knowledge of some part, so that they are cast down in that which they believed they knew. According to the letter, we can understand that, when the days of captivity come upon the cows of Samaria, the enemies overcome them in battle and crush them with their arms, and drive them, overcome in the encounter, into fortified cities, which are compared to boiling pots; so that, as Jerusalem, holding its peoples shut in and besieged, is likened to a boiling pot full of flesh, so also the cities of Samaria are compared to boiling pots, which by famine and pestilence force the peoples shut in them to go out, and to go into captivity seeing one another, and to be transferred to Armon, the mountains of Armenia, which border on the Medes and the Persians.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.2":["And so the Lord swore by his holy one, whether by himself, or by the Son, or by the temple, or by everyone who is holy and is called the temple of God, that a day is to come not far off, nor after many ages, but already imminent: the day of captivity and distress, in which the cows are lifted up on poles, and their remnants in boiling pots, for which both in the Hebrew and in Aquila is written in cauldrons of little fish. For the poles too, which in Hebrew are called sannoth, Aquila interpreted shields, Symmachus and the LXX arms, Theodotion alone δόρατα; following him, we have interpreted poles or spears. This signifies that they are taken in battle, and carried and borne away by right of victory, while he nevertheless keeps the metaphor of the cows that he had begun, so that, as he had said that they were fat cows, he tells that their flesh is to be carried on poles or shields. And as a boiling cauldron wraps up small fish all together, so also the cows of Bashan are to be overwhelmed, without any order, by the miseries of captivity.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.3":["And what follows: And you shall go out through the openings, one against another, can be explained thus: The way of captivity lies open to you, and when your cauldrons have been burnt out, you shall go out one against another; according to the idiom of the Hebrews, who, for what we say mutually or in turn, say woman and woman, that is, one against another. And you shall be cast, he says, into the places of Armenia which are called Armona. Finally Symmachus interpreted: And you shall be cast into Armenia, for which the Seventy translated mount Remmam, Aquila mount Armona, Theodotion mount Mona, the Fifth edition the lofty mount.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.4-5":["Come to Bethel, and act impiously; to Gilgal, and multiply transgression; and bring your victims in the morning, your tithes on three days. And sacrifice praise from what is leavened, and call for voluntary offerings, and proclaim them. For so you have willed, sons of Israel, says the Lord God. Therefore I also have given you numbness of teeth in all your cities, and want of bread in all your places; and you have not returned to me, says the Lord. LXX: You entered into Bethel, and acted unjustly; in Gilgal you multiplied acting impiously, and you brought in your sacrifices in the morning, your tithes on the third day. And they read the law outside, and called upon confessions. Proclaim that the sons of Israel have loved these things, says the Lord God. And I will give you numbness of teeth in all your cities, and want of bread in all your places; and you have not returned to me, says the Lord. First let us lay the foundations of the history: O wretched Israel, captivity is already near you, already the Assyrian army is pressing upon you; do what you please, act impiously; commit fornication freely with idols, so that the more shameless you have been, the more just my sentence upon your torments may appear. Come, he says, into Bethel, in which you set up the golden calf, and act impiously against God. Come to Gilgal, the place of idolatry, of which I spoke through Hosea: All their wickedness is in Gilgal. And again in the same prophet: In vain were they sacrificing oxen in Gilgal. And when you have come to Gilgal: Multiply transgression. For whatever you do there is transgression against God, to whom you have preferred idols. And bring your victims in the morning, so that there may be no delay in crime. Your tithes on three days, or, as Symmachus translates, your tithes on the third day. The explanation of this passage seems to us to be this: in Leviticus it is commanded in the law that certain sacrifices are not to be kept over to the second day, and that others are not to remain to the third day, and that if they do remain they are unclean. The sense, therefore, is: Slaughter unclean victims every day, and offer defiled sacrifices, and sacrifice praise from what is leavened, which according to the precepts of Moses is not offered to God at all. For 'praise' Aquila translated εὐχαριστίαν, that is, thanksgiving, which in Hebrew is called thoda. And call, he says, for voluntary offerings, which the Hebrews call nadaboth, that is, spontaneous. A freewill sacrifice, moreover, belongs to rejoicing, and in Latin we can call it a banquet. And when you have done this, proclaim your impiety to all, so that you may be seen not only to have done it, but also to have taught others. These things I command, and I speak in the imperative mood, in order to satisfy your will, because so you have acted and so you have willed, sons of Israel, says the Lord God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.4":["This we have said according to the Hebrew; let us pass on to the LXX translators, and discuss briefly what seems to us according to anagogy in each point; for the size of the books does not allow it, if we should wish to say both things in each edition. You entered into Bethel, that is, into the house of God, which is understood as the Church, and you acted impiously against the Lord, trampling on his precepts. And in Gilgal, which is interpreted revelation or wallowing-place, you multiplied impieties, claiming for yourselves the knowledge of the holy Scriptures, and while you are lifted up into pride, you have rolled down into the lowest depths of the mire. You also brought in your sacrifices in the morning, your tithes on the third day, transformed into an angel of light, and compressing the threefold understanding of the Scriptures — concerning which we have been commanded to write it threefold in our hearts — into the meaning of a single day. For we ought to understand holy Scripture first according to the letter, doing in ethics whatever is commanded; secondly according to allegory, that is, the spiritual understanding; thirdly according to the blessedness of things to come. But you, he says, despising the first and the second day, compose for yourselves certain spiritual fictions without a foundation, setting a roof on top of walls.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.5":["Nor are the heretics, of whom and to whom this is said, content with this limit of impiety, but they read the law of God outside, withdrawing from the Church, and tried to join confessions and testimonies to each of their dogmas, which they feigned with a perverse heart; or they sacrificed from what is leavened, concerning which it is said in the Gospel: Beware of the leaven, that is, the teaching of the Pharisees. And they did these things not by error, but by design; not by a chance will, but with the fullest affection for their forebears.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.6":["Wherefore I too have given you numbness of teeth, as the LXX translated, whom we have followed in this place because of the simplicity of the word, or cleanness of teeth, as Aquila and Symmachus translated, so as to show by clean teeth the greatness of the famine. And I caused want of bread, not in one city, but in all your cities, nor in one place, but in all your places. And though I have done this, not punishing, but granting an occasion for repentance, not even so have you returned to me, says the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos","Hence the Lord threatens vengeance upon them: I will give, he says, you numbness of teeth, which in Greek is called γομφιασμός. For if anyone, according to Ezekiel, eats a sour grape, his teeth will be numbed; so that those who make ill use of the testimonies of the holy Scriptures, and eat them unripe and without their sweetness, lose the strength of their teeth, so that they cannot grind hard food and pass into the belly what would profit the whole body. This numbness of teeth and want of food I will give both in all your cities and in all your places, so that you may suffer a famine of the word of God, and of that bread which came down from heaven, and of which it is written in the psalms: Man ate the bread of angels. All of which I did, not out of cruelty and savagery, as the heretics slanderously claim, the ferocity of a stern judge, but that you might be converted to me, according to that which is written: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not received discipline.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.7-8":["I also withheld the rain from you, when there were still three months left until the harvest. And I rained upon one city, and upon another city I did not rain. One part was rained upon, and the part upon which I did not rain withered. And two and three cities came to one city to drink water, and they were not satisfied; and you did not return to me, says the Lord. LXX: And I held back the rain from you three months before the vintage; and I will rain upon one city, and upon one city I will not rain. One part will be watered, and the part upon which I shall not rain will dry up. And two and three cities will gather into one city to drink water, and they will not be satisfied; and you have not returned to me, says the Lord. Not only did I give numbness of teeth in all your cities, and want of bread in all your places, but I withheld the rain from you when there were still three months left until the harvest, the rain which is called the latter rain and is most necessary of all to the thirsty fields and ploughlands of Palestine, so that, when the grass was swelling toward the harvest and the wheat was bringing forth its ear, it withered from excessive drought. He means the spring season at the end of the month of April, from which three months remain until the wheat harvest: May, June, July. For 'harvest' the LXX, after their manner, translated 'vintage'; and if we accept this, it is altogether unusual and impossible throughout all the regions of the East. For we have never seen rains at the end of the month of June, or in the month of July, in these provinces, and above all in Judea. Indeed, in the books of Kings, rains were stirred up at Samuel's prayer in the days of summer and harvest as a great sign and portent. And it would have been superfluous now to threaten a drought in the month of July, in which he had never given rains. He withheld the rain, moreover, so that they should endure not only want of bread, but also the burning of thirst and a scarcity of drink. For in these places in which we now live, apart from small springs, all the waters are those of cisterns, and if the divine wrath holds back the rain, the danger of thirst is greater than that of famine, which Scripture records also happened in the days of the prophet Elijah for three years and six months. And lest they should perhaps think that this had befallen cities and peoples by the law of nature, and the course of the stars, and the changes of the seasons, he says that he rains upon one city and its fields, and holds back the rain from another, so that two and three cities go to another city, and yet are not satisfied with draughts of water. And though he has done these things, not as punishment, but as medicine, he rebukes those who persist in crime: and not even so did you return to me, says the Lord. The Lord also withholds, or holds back, the spiritual rains from the heretics, and all the dew of divine wisdom, and commands his clouds not to rain rain upon them three months before the harvest, or the vintage, so that they cannot attain to the fruit of the mystery of the Trinity. And just as this sun, which we see, completes its yearly course, until it returns to its former goal, in twelve months, each of which rolls on for thirty days; and the moon, which in Hebrew is called iare, in Greek μήνη, has in both languages given its name to the months from its own name, and is lit by the rays of the sun on the side on which it is near to it, receiving more or less light according to the variation of the times; so too the Church, adorned with the splendour of the true sun, fills up the number of the twelve apostles. Hence also the tribes in Israel are called twelve, and as a testimony of eternal memory twelve stones are taken from the bed of the Jordan, to be set up in the place of the second circumcision. The Lord, then, rains upon one city, the Church of the true confession, and upon another he does not rain, the one which is in the conventicles of the heretics. And while the one receives an everlasting rain, the other is dried up by a continual drought, so that those who thirst, compelled by want, may come to the city of the Lord, from which goes forth a most abundant fountain that waters the torrent of thorns. This is the fountain which says through Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves broken cisterns, which cannot hold waters. This fountain, proceeding from one fountain, runs down in a threefold union; and for these fountains of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit the psalmist sighs after the manner of a thirsting stag, saying: As the stag longs for the fountains of waters, so my soul longs for you, O God. And when two and three cities have gone to the one city in which the waters of hope, faith and charity abound, they will not be satisfied, because they have come to seek the divine grace not by will, but by necessity.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.9":["I struck you with scorching wind and with mildew. The multitude of your gardens and of your vineyards, your olive groves and your fig groves, the caterpillar devoured, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. LXX: I struck you with burning heat and with mildew. You multiplied your gardens, your vineyards, and your figs, and your olive groves the caterpillar devoured, and not even so were you converted to me, says the Lord. Not only did I do the former things in order to correct you, but whatever was left over from the drought I struck with scorching wind and with mildew. For 'scorching wind' Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated ἀνεμοφθορίαν; the Seventy πύρωσιν, which we can call burning. 'Mildew', moreover, all of them likewise translated ἴκτερον, except Theodotion, who alone translated ὠχρίασιν, which signifies pallor. And when, in defiance of the wrath of the Lord, they multiplied gardens and vineyards and fig groves and olive groves, the caterpillar consumed everything, of which more is said in Joel. And not even through blows and the weight of evils were they willing to come to the Lord. By this saying let the heretics be confounded, who interpret as cruelty the discipline and, so to speak, the medicine of the Creator. The Lord, moreover, strikes the heretics spiritually with that burning of which Paul the Apostle speaks: It is better, he says, to marry than to burn. And the prince of the apostles: Beloved, do not be as strangers in the burning which has come upon you for a trial. And with mildew, which, when the bile is spread abroad, changes the redness of the blood into pallor and allows nothing in the body to be sound, so much so that even the sweetest honey seems bitter. And those who once heard in the Church: Taste and see that the Lord is sweet, and themselves said: How sweet are your words to my throat, above honey to my mouth. Let them be called παραπικραίνοντες (embitterers), and let their waters and rains not drip sweetness, nor be from the land of promise, which we read is flowing with milk and honey, but let them be called mara, that is, bitterness. These have multiplied gardens for themselves, into which Naboth was willing to die rather than have his vineyard changed. He who, after all, is weak, let him eat herbs. And because the heretics, always swelling with pride and falling into the judgment of the devil, promise great things to themselves, and feign images of good things, in order to mix poisons with honey, they also boast that they have vineyards and olive groves and fig groves; but their vine is the vine of Sodom; their olive groves are not of good olives, but of wild olives, which the Apostle commands to be grafted onto the roots of good olives. Their fig groves too are such as to fill a basket with the worst figs, which Jeremiah testifies cannot be eaten for their bitterness. When the Lord comes to these, he curses them with an everlasting drought, so that they may never bear fruit, lest by the greenness of their leaves they deceive those who pass by. And so that we may understand that the gardens and vineyards and olive groves and fig groves of the heretics are to be taken in the bad sense, he added 'your' to each word, to show that they belong not to God, but to the heretics: Your gardens, your vineyards, your olive groves and your fig groves, all of which the caterpillar has laid waste, the last of all punishments; for it does not fly away like the locust, and, running here and there, leave things half-eaten, but stays on the crops that are to perish, and with slow movement and sluggish bites consumes everything. And though they suffered these things, not even so were they willing to return to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.10":["I sent death upon you in the way of Egypt, I struck your young men with the sword, even to the captivity of your horses. And I made the stench of your camps rise into your nostrils, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. LXX: I sent forth death upon you in the way of Egypt, and I killed your young men with the sword, together with the captivity of your horses. And I brought out your camps in fire in my wrath, and not even so did you return to me, says the Lord. Israel is instructed by every kind of scourge and torment; hence also, at the time when they were asking help from the Egyptians, death is sent upon them, and their young men are struck with the sword, and the horses which they had multiplied for themselves against the precept of God a savage captivity shuts in, so that the stench of the camps and the foulness of a dying army fill the nostrils of the living. And though he did these things too, in order to rebuke the offenders and amend the erring, not even so did they return to him, says the Lord. He sends death in the way of Egypt not only at that time, but daily, so that whoever has trodden the Egyptian path may, as he dies, hear the Apostle teaching: For in that I died, I died to sin; but in that I live, I live to God. And in another place: If we have died with Christ, we shall also live together with him. And again: I always carry about the mortification of Jesus in my body. And once more: But I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me. By this death the Lord wishes to give us life, so that, dying to sin, we may live to God. And we die to sin when we mortify our members which are upon the earth: fornication, uncleanness, wantonness, idolatry and the rest. We read in Isaiah, at least according to the Seventy translators — otherwise the Hebrew has it differently —: I sent death upon Jacob, and it came upon Israel. Jacob is the name of the body being born; Israel, a name of blessing. For because he wrestled all night and conquered in the struggle, and at the rising of the morning star could say with the Apostle: The night is far spent, the day has drawn near, for this reason he received the name Israel, of one seeing God. Death, therefore, is first sent upon Jacob, so that we may mortify our members which are upon the earth, and through the mortification of the bodily members may come to the death of Israel, so that all the incitements of the passions in us may die. God strikes whatever is strong in evil and is lifted up in a perverse youth, lest it reach old age, and he hands the horses over to captivity, lest they slip down the precipices of hell; and he makes the stench of their camps rise into their nostrils, so that they may recognize their sins and perceive that they stink, and say with David: My scars have putrefied and are corrupted because of my folly. And though he did these things with the desire of one who heals, not even so did they return to him, says the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.11":["I overthrew you as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah, and you became like a brand snatched from the burning, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. LXX: I overthrew you as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah, and you became like a brand plucked out of the fire, and not even so were you converted to me, says the Lord. It is the last remedy, both for the ten tribes and for the heretics and for all sinners, that, after he has sent death in the way of Egypt, and struck their young men with the sword, and consumed their horses, and made the stench of their camps rise into their nostrils, and still they have not returned to him, he should overthrow them as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah; and that, when they have been overthrown because of the likeness of their crimes to those of Sodom and Gomorrah, and the divine fire has burned up the worst buildings in them, they themselves should be delivered like a brand snatched from the burning. And just as Lot, when Sodom perished, was saved, losing his substance and a part of his body, which we understand to be his wife, so these, all losing the riches of Sodom, may escape naked, according to what we read in the Apostle: If anyone's work which he has built upon it remains, he will receive a reward; but if anyone's work is burned, he will suffer loss, yet he himself will be saved, but only as through fire. He, then, who is saved through fire is snatched like a brand from the burning. And to men of this kind, whom the Savior addressed in the Gospel, rebuking them: If you were sons of Abraham, you would do the works of Abraham, John the Baptist says: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come? Bear, therefore, fruit worthy of repentance, and do not presume to say within yourselves, We have Abraham as our father. For I say to you that God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham? Therefore both Israel and all the heretics, because they had the works of Sodom and Gomorrah, are overthrown like Sodom and Gomorrah, so that they may be delivered like a brand snatched from the burning. And this is what we read in the prophet: Sodom shall be restored to its former state, so that he who by his own vice is among the Sodomites may, after the works of Sodom have burned up in him, be restored to his former state.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.12-13":["Therefore I will do these things to you, Israel; but after I have done these things to you, prepare to meet your God, Israel; for behold, he who forms the mountains, and creates the wind, and announces to man his speech, who makes the morning mist, and treads upon the heights of the earth, the Lord God of hosts is his name. LXX: Therefore thus will I do to you, Israel; nevertheless, because I will do thus to you, prepare to call upon your God, Israel. For behold, he who establishes the thunder, and creates the spirit, and announces to men his Christ, making the morning and the mist, and ascending upon the heights of the earth, the Lord God Almighty is his name. Where we translated 'after', in the Hebrew is written eceb, and Aquila translated ὕστερον, that is, afterwards, and Theodotion 'at the last', and the Seventy 'nevertheless'; in the Hebrew it can be read thus: Therefore I will do these things to you, Israel, afterwards, that is, a long time after, and in the last ages, so that the verse begins again from another beginning: And since I am about to do this to you, prepare to meet your God, Israel. And again, in the place where the Seventy translated: Prepare to call upon your God, and we, following Theodotion, have put: Prepare to meet your God, Symmachus and the Fifth edition translated: Prepare to oppose your God, which in Hebrew is said hechin lacerath Eloiah. For 'mountains' also, which in Hebrew are called arim, the Seventy alone rendered βροντήν, that is, thunder. And why they said 'spirit' and we 'wind', which in Hebrew is called rua, the reason is plain: because by this word both wind and spirit are named. And as for what follows: Announcing to man his speech, while all translate it as we have rendered it, the Seventy alone translated: Announcing to men his Christ, deceived by the likeness and ambiguity of the word. For if we read 'his Christ', which in Hebrew is said Messio, it is written with these letters, mem, sin, iod, heth, and uau, which is what the LXX supposed. But if it is as in the Hebrew, according to Aquila τήν ὁμιλίαν αὐτοῦ, according to Symmachus τὸ φώνημα αὐτοῦ, according to Theodotion τὸν λόγον αὐτοῦ, according to the Fifth edition τήν ἀδολεσχίαν αὐτοῦ, all of which they translate as meaning that it is 'his speech', it will be written with these letters, mem, he, which is read ma, that is, 'that' or 'what'. Then sin, iod, heth, which we read sia, that is, speech. And the O, which is written with the single letter uau, signifies αὐτοῦ, that is, 'his'; and all mixed together it is read masio, having, compared with the word above, one letter more, he, the second letter. We have spoken of the variety of interpretation, which will be tiresome to the careless and welcome to the studious; now let us pass on to the sense of what is written.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.12":["I gave numbness to your teeth, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. I withheld the rain from you, and I rained upon one city, and upon another I did not rain, and two and three cities came to one to drink water, and they were not satisfied, and not even so did you return to me, says the Lord. I struck you with scorching wind, and with mildew your gardens and vineyards and olive groves and fig groves, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. I sent death upon you in the way of Egypt, and I struck your young men with the sword, and I made the stench of your camps rise into your nostrils, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. I overthrew you as the Lord overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah, and you became as a brand snatched from the burning, and you did not return to me, says the Lord. Therefore I will do these things to you, O Israel, so that you who have despised what is past may at least be corrected by what I am about to bring upon you. And when he has said, I will do these things to you, he is silent about what he is going to do, so that while Israel hangs uncertain over each kind of punishment — which are the more terrible for this reason, that men suspect them all —, he may repent, lest God bring on what he threatens. But after I have done to you what I solemnly declare I will do, prepare to call upon the Lord your God. Everyone, after all, who calls upon the name of the Lord will be saved. Or: prepare to meet your God, so that you may receive with all eagerness the Lord who comes to you.\n— Commentary on Amos","But if we read according to Symmachus and Aquila: These things I will do to you, Israel, afterwards, and when I have done these things to you, prepare to oppose your God Israel, it is to be understood thus: In order to correct you I did the things which the preceding discourse describes; and because you were unwilling to return to me, I will do to you the things that are contained in my secret counsel. You killed the servants whom I had sent to you; last of all I will send my Son; but you, according to your custom, by which you have always resisted the will of God, prepare yourself to contradict and oppose your God, according to that which is written: Behold, this one is set for the fall and for the rising again of many, and for a sign which shall be contradicted. And he says this not to command what Israel ought to do, but to foretell what he will do of his own accord, as if rebuking and reproving him, so that, corrected, he may at least not do what has been foretold.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.4.13":["He it is who establishes the thunder, or makes firm the mountains, at whose voice the hinges of the heavens and the foundations of the earth are shaken. He it is who creates the spirit, which in this place we understand not as the Holy Spirit, as the heretics suppose, but as the wind, or the spirit of man, because no one knows what is in a man except the spirit which is in him; and the Spirit himself intercedes for us with unspeakable groanings. Or certainly by 'spirit' we ought to understand the soul, according to that which is written: You will take away their spirit, and they will fail, and they will return to their dust. And: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit, and saying this he breathed his last. And when he has created the spirit, he announces to men his own speech, or announces to man that man's speech, he who knows the secrets of the thoughts, and understands what the hidden mind ponders in silent thought, according to what we read in Jeremiah according to the Hebrew: The heart of all is perverse and unsearchable; who shall know it? I the Lord search the heart and test the reins. Which Scripture also attests in the hundred and thirty-eighth (139th) psalm: Your eyes saw my imperfect being. And the sense is: Before I was made, before I was shaped into limbs, while I was still contained in the seed, your eyes saw me. And Jeremiah hears from the Lord: Before I formed you in the womb, I knew you; and before you came forth from the womb, I sanctified you, and I set you as a prophet among the nations. And the evangelist: But Jesus, seeing their thoughts. But if we read: Announcing to man his speech, it is to be taken according to what was said above, where he says: The Lord God will not do a thing unless he has revealed his secret to his servants the prophets. And he who announces the thoughts of man and his speech, or his own, is the one who makes the dawn and the daybreak, and fills all things with clouds, and treads upon the heights of the lands, whose name is the Lord God Almighty. In place of this one reads in the Seventy: And announcing to men his Christ; on this pretext the heretics want the Holy Spirit to be created, from the order that follows: Creating the spirit, and announcing to men his Christ, so that the one is created and the other is announced among the peoples. To them we shall reply according to their own sense and the common edition: He who is the creator of all things, and establishes the thunder, or fashions the mountains, consequently brings the winds out of his treasuries, and, as the founder of the universe, promises his Son Christ to men. But after Christ has been announced, then the light of truth is opened to us, not to perfection, because now we see in part and know in part, and contemplate the things that are to come through a mirror and an image. Hence there follows: Making the morning and the mist, and ascending upon the heights of the earth. For the Lord is high in the high places, and he who is high does not dwell in low things; but the creator of the mountains ascends upon the mountains, in those who have their citizenship in the heavens, and, walking in the flesh, do not live according to the flesh but according to the spirit.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.1-2":["Hear this word, which I raise over you as a lament. The house of Israel has fallen; it will not again rise. The virgin Israel is cast down into her land; there is no one to raise her up. LXX: Hear this word, which I will take up over you as a lament. The house of Israel has fallen; it will not any more rise again. The virgin Israel is cast down upon her land; there is no one to raise her up. As far as concerns the order of the letter and the truth of the history begun, the ten tribes, which are called Israel, once led into captivity, by no means returned afterwards to their own land. And the people of Israel is called a virgin, not because it remained in the purity of virginity, but because it was once joined to the Lord like a virgin. Hence the prophet is also commanded to take up a lament over it, because it will by no means be restored to its former rank. But as far as concerns the spiritual understanding, the prophet takes up a lament over every Israel which used to discern God with the mind and afterwards ceased to serve him, according to that which is commanded to Ezekiel, that he should devour the book in which there was written, both within and without, lamentation, and song, and woe. Understand by within the Song of Solomon that follows, who says: The king has brought me into his chamber. And the forty-fourth (45th) psalm, in which it is written: All the glory of the king's daughter is within. But without is whatever is read in the letter, and is seen in the bark, and is not held in the marrow of the spirit. And so, both according to the letter and according to tropology, in all the books of the prophets a lament is written over those who repent after sins; a song over those who, stained by no filth of sins, are worthy of canticle and praise; woe over those who do not repent, but according to the hardness of their heart treasure up for themselves wrath in the day of wrath.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.2":["But if, as we have said, the lament is over those who repent, and repentance restores health to the wound, how is it said according to the Seventy: The house of Israel has fallen, it will rise no more. The virgin Israel has strayed in her land, there is no one to raise her up? This can be resolved thus: after the house of Israel has collapsed by its own will, it will by no means recover its former dignity; after the virgin Israel has strayed in her land, she will no longer be able to find one to raise her up. And consider the proper meanings of the words. He who is a house, and is counted in the crowd, is said to fall. But he who is of the number of the virgins, if he strays, even for a light sin, will not be able to be raised up; not that he is not raised up, but the virgin Israel is by no means raised up, and the Lord of Israel will by no means rise again. For the glory is not the same of him who has always followed the Lord and of him who has wandered from the flock and afterwards been carried back on the shoulders of the good shepherd. Hence the Lord also says through another prophet: I prefer the repentance of a sinner rather than his death. Repentance becomes better not when compared with the purest holiness and the Church of Christ — which has no wrinkle nor spot —, but when compared with death and the underworld. We say these things, not in order to take away, with Novatus, the hope of repentance, but in order to make more fearful, and therefore anxious, those who, with the door of repentance open, while they hope for the future, lose the present, and who, though they could have remained without a wound, carelessly receive a wound, so as afterwards to be tormented by pain. There are many mansions with my Father, and star differs from star in brightness; so also is the resurrection of the dead, the saints shining like the sun and the moon, the evening star and the morning star. But those who have repented after sin will be made equal to other stars, according to the diversity of their merits.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.3":["For thus says the Lord God: The city from which a thousand went out, there shall be left in it a hundred; and from which a hundred went out, there shall be left in it ten, in the house of Israel. LXX: likewise. The divine word gives the reasons why the house of Israel, falling, does not rise again, and the virgin Israel, straying, or cast down upon the land, has no one to raise her up. The city, he says, from which a thousand went out, there shall be left in it a hundred; and from which a hundred went out, there shall be left in it ten, in the house of Israel; so that where once there had been a multitude, because of the excessive devastation scarcely a tenth part remains. And lest, clinging wholly to the letter, we leave the mysteries of numbers untouched: that the number seven is holy the sabbath also proves, on which God rested from all his works. And he commands that no servile work be done on it, except only those things which pertain to the soul, and that we should not carry burdens on it. Hence also in the wilderness the man who had gathered wood on the sabbath day, things that are to burn, is condemned by the sentence of the Lord. And seven weeks complete the number of holy Pentecost, and the jubilee year of remission and of the sounding trumpets is woven from this number. In the seventh month also tabernacles are pitched, and the Hebrew, when he has served six years, is set free in the seventh year. Secular philosophy also knows this, and the books of the physicians, of whom Galen, most eloquent and most learned, wrote three books, κρίσεων καὶ τῶν κριτικῶν ἡμερῶν (On Crises and Critical Days), in which, showing the power of the number seven, he says that the most burning fevers are broken on the seventh day; or, if the quantity of noxious humour and phlegm has been so great that it has by no means been consumed by the heat of the first week, the last day of the second week is awaited, that is, the fourteenth. But if, to speak with Hippocrates, the νόσος (the disease) has overcome this one, they pass on to the twenty-first day, that is, to the end of the third week; the days having been so established from the beginning of the world that all labours and troubles come to rest at the seventh number. Finally, the captivity of the people of Israel and the overthrow of the temple were also completed in the seventieth year of desolation, and seven stars are said to be wandering, according to the number of the days. Of the mysteries of this number Cicero tells more fully in the Dream of Scipio; and the Timaeus of Plato is a most obscure book, which is not made plainer even by the golden mouth of Cicero. Just as, therefore, the number seven has its own mystery, so the number that is held in unity, and is enclosed by the majesty of the one God, is sanctified and perfect and, so to speak, the true number. Hence the Son says: I am in the Father, and the Father in me, and, wishing all to be one with the Father, he speaks to him: Father, grant that, as I and you are one, so these also may be one in us. The first blessedness, therefore, is to be in the first number, which is one and true; the second in the second, that is, in the decad; the third in the third, that is, in the hecatontad. For as the decad is completed by the tenth unit, so the hecatontad is built from ten decads. The fourth number, which is contained in the thousand, consists of ten hecatontads. When, therefore, someone has repented, from the thousand and the fourth number he scarcely returns to the hundred and the third number. Again, he who is in the hundred scarcely returns to the second number, that of the first decad; and so it comes about that the house of Israel which had fallen cannot rise again, and the virgin Israel which had strayed has no one in the land to raise her up, because he who has once departed from unity, and has lost that glory of the purest virginity — of which the Apostle says: For I am jealous for you with the jealousy of God, and I have resolved to present you as a chaste virgin to one husband, to Christ, in which there is no spot nor wrinkle —, will not be able to recover his former state and the blessedness of unity, and it will scarcely be granted him to return from a thousand to a hundred, and to go back from a hundred to ten. I have said these things briefly, lest I should seem in this section to have fled tropology altogether because of the difficulty of the numbers.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.4":["For thus says the Lord to the house of Israel: Seek me, and you shall live; and do not seek Bethel, and do not enter into Gilgal, and do not pass over to Beersheba; for Gilgal shall be led captive, and Bethel shall be useless. LXX: For thus says the Lord to the house of Israel: Seek me, and you shall live; and do not seek Bethel, and do not go into Gilgal, and do not pass over upon the well of the oath; for Gilgal shall be led captive, and Bethel shall be as though it were not. It is the custom of the Scriptures always to join glad things to adverse ones, and after God has threatened sad things, he calls to repentance those whom he has frightened, according to that which we read in Isaiah: Woe, sinful nation, a people full of offences, a most wicked seed, unjust sons. And when he had said: Your land is deserted, your cities consumed with fire, strangers will devour your regions before you, he speaks to them, promising better things: Wash yourselves, be clean; take away your wickednesses from your souls, learn to do good, seek judgment, judge for the orphan, justify the widow; and come, let us dispute, says the Lord. Just as, therefore, in Isaiah he supports with gentle speech those whom he had frightened with a severe voice, so also in this prophet, to those to whom he had said: The house of Israel has fallen, it will not again rise; the virgin Israel has strayed in the land, there is no one to raise her up. Now he speaks to them and says: To the house of Israel, that is, to the ten tribes, Seek me and you shall live, since in that you do not seek me, you are dead. And when you have sought, you will find; and when you have found, you will live.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.5":["And do not seek Bethel, in which was the golden calf, and Gilgal, the place of idolatry, of which I have also said above: All their wickedness is in Gilgal. And you shall not pass over to Beersheba. Beautifully he says: you shall not pass over to Beersheba, that is, to the well of the oath, where the tribe of Judah, whenever it went astray, had been accustomed to worship idols. But Israel raged in the worship of images to such a degree that, by no means content with its own idols, it passed over to those of others. Finally, Gilgal, he says, shall be led captive, and Bethel shall be useless, or not subsisting at all, when the idols in it have been overthrown. But of Beersheba he was altogether silent, because when the ten tribes were conquered, the city by the name of Beersheba, which was in the tribe of Judah at that time, was neither taken nor destroyed. And at the same time it must be noticed that the LXX translators in the present place translated the name Beersheba, saying: The well of the oath, and in later passages they set down the name itself: Your God lives, O Dan, and the way of Beersheba lives. And they set down the way of Beersheba because from Israel they went by a long journey to the farthest borders of Judah, which were in Gerar and adjoined the Egyptian wilderness, in order to worship idols. It is, moreover, the place in which Abraham dwelt; and from the fact that he and Abimelech, after seven sheep had been given, swore to a mutual covenant, it was called the well of the oath, or the well of the seventh, because of the number of the seven sheep. For Sabe signifies both. And according to the laws of allegory he commands the house of Israel, that is, those who promise themselves the knowledge of God, not to seek Bethel, and not to enter into Gilgal, and not to pass over, or go up, to the well of the oath, but rather to seek God and live in him. And they seek Bethel, which is interpreted house of God, who say: The temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord; and they trust in buildings, of which the Lord spoke to the disciples: Days will come in which there will not be left a stone upon a stone that will not be destroyed. And they enter into Gilgal who after the coming of Christ desire to be circumcised again. For in Gilgal the people was circumcised a second time. Hence the place itself also received its name, because the Lord took away from them the reproach of Egypt. And you shall not pass over, he says, to Beersheba, or to the well of the oath, lest you think those to be the borders of Judea which Scripture once, according to the letter, had promised, from Dan to Beersheba. Nor should you say any longer with the prophet: God is known in Judea, his name is great in Israel, but you should hear with the apostles: Their sound has gone out into all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world, because Gilgal also, that is, the circumcision of the flesh, will be led captive by the true circumcision of the heart. And Bethel, which you think the house of God, will not subsist, or, as I think better, returns useless, that is, auen, so that it is by no means called the house of God, but is called the useless house, or the house of the idol. Another interpretation: he seeks Bethel who follows only the letter that kills, and does not seek in the words the sense, which is God; and he enters into Gilgal who strives after greater revelations, promising himself the knowledge of things above; and he passes over, or goes up, to the well from which the Samaritan woman, desiring to draw waters that could not satisfy thirst, did not know him from whose belly proceed rivers of water springing up into eternal life.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.6":["Seek the Lord and live, lest perhaps the house of Joseph be burned like fire; and it will devour, and there will be no one to quench Bethel. LXX: Seek the Lord and live, lest perhaps the house of Joseph be kindled like fire; and it devour it, and there be no one to quench the house of Israel. Just as it is said in the person of God: Seek me, and you shall live, so the prophet speaks about the Lord, that they should seek him and live. For in seeking the Lord they begin to live; but if they have not sought him, and for that reason have not lived, at once the house of Joseph will be kindled like fire. By it we ought to understand, because of Jeroboam, who was of the tribe of Ephraim and of the house of Joseph, the ten tribes, which are called Israel and, from the greater part of the people, possessed the former name. But the two tribes which were ruled by the stock of David, who was of the tribe of Judah, were called Judah, and possessed Jerusalem, in which was the temple of God. And when the house of Joseph has been kindled, it will devour and consume Bethel, of which I have said above: Do not seek Bethel, and there will be no one to quench it, since it will have been set on fire by its own kings. In place of Bethel, in the LXX is read the house of Israel, since they translated the sense rather than the word; so that, as King Jeroboam succeeded, and all the kings who thereafter succeeded him in the rule, the ten tribes, which are called Israel, burn. This is the fire which is kindled, or shines out, and inflames the house of Joseph, so that Bethel is consumed; of which it is said elsewhere: Walk in the light of your fire, and in the flame which you have kindled. And since we have often referred the house of Joseph — because of Jeroboam, who separated the people of God from the stock of David, and made golden cows in Dan and in Bethel, and said: We have no part in David, nor inheritance in the son of Jesse — to the person of the heretics, who with polished speech have fashioned comely and beautiful and, so to speak, golden images, and adore the works of their own hands, and under the appearance of cows promise themselves an image of agriculture, it is said to them: Seek the Lord, and live, him who says: I am the way, and the truth, and the life, so that, after they have walked in him and found the truth, those who before were dead may then begin to live. And unless they do this, they will be burned by the ardours of the devil, and none will be able to be found among their princes, who themselves also have been kindled by the heretical fire, who could put out the flame that devours all things, and especially Bethel, which assumes for itself the false name of the house of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.7":["You who turn judgment into wormwood and leave righteousness on the earth; him who makes Arcturus and Orion, and turns darkness into morning, and changes day into night. Who calls the waters of the sea and pours them out upon the face of the earth: the Lord is his name. Who smiles devastation upon the strong, and brings plundering upon the mighty. LXX: Who makes judgment on high and has set righteousness on the earth; who makes all things, and transforms them, and turns the shadow of death into morning, and darkens the day into night. Who calls the water of the sea and pours it out upon the face of the earth: the Lord God Almighty is his name. Who divides crushing upon strength, and brings misery upon the fortress. In this place the common edition disagrees greatly with the Hebrew truth, as the prudent reader at once understands, without our reminder, from what we have set forth. Let us therefore expound first according to the Hebrews, and afterwards, if we have deserved it, with Christ opening the way, let us say what seems to us in the translation of the LXX. The house of Joseph, that is, the house of Ephraim, and for that reason the royal house, and Bethel, or, as the LXX translated, the house of Israel, that is, both kings and peoples, both worshippers and idols alike, will be overthrown, who by unjust judgment provoked God to anger. And they turned the sweetness of judgment into the bitterness of wormwood, which is a kind of most bitter herb, taking up iniquity and leaving righteousness.\n— Commentary on Amos","Let us speak also according to the LXX: God makes judgment on high, when he judges truth and renders to each according to his work; and everyone who is an imitator of God, and desires to be his son, and to be perfect, as his Father who dwells in heaven is perfect, makes judgment on high, and does not imitate that judge who did not fear God, and did not respect man, and by the perversity of his judgment did not lift his sentence up to heaven, but dragged it down to the depths. And as for what follows: And has set righteousness on the earth, we ought to take it according to this, that he gave us Christ, his righteousness, and did not cast it away but set it down on the earth, so that, all iniquity being overcome, he might make us heavenly from earthly. I think that the Gentile poet also stole from this place, who, discoursing on the simplicity and blessedness of country folk, added: \"Justice, leaving the earth, made her last steps among them.\"\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.8":["But what this righteousness is, the following verse shows: Him who makes Arcturus and Orion, and turns darkness into morning, and changes day into night. He of whom he had said above: Forming the mountains, and creating the wind, and announcing to man his speech, making the morning mist, and stepping upon the high places of the earth: the Lord God of hosts is his name, he it is who is the Creator of Arcturus, which in Hebrew is called Chima, and by Symmachus and Theodotion is rendered εἰς πλειάδα (the Pleiades), which is commonly called Boötes. And as for what follows, Orion, which in Hebrew is called Chasil, Symmachus translated it absolutely as stars, Theodotion as the evening star. But the Hebrew who instructed us in the holy Scriptures thinks that Chasil means splendour, and signifies in general the shining stars. But when we hear Arcturus and Orion, we ought not to follow the fables of the poets and the ridiculous and monstrous lies with which they try to defame even heaven and to set the reward of debauchery among the stars, saying: \"He scans Arcturus and the rainy Hyades and the twin Triones, and Orion armed with gold\"; but we ought to know that the Hebrew names, which among them are called otherwise, have been carried over into our language by the terms of the Gentile fables, since we cannot understand what is said except through those terms which we have learned by use and drunk in with error. Hence also in the book of Kings the Greeks translated the Hebrew Raphaim as titans, which is a most celebrated fable among the heathen, from which they write γιγαντομαχίας (battles of the giants) in praise of the gods; and the Typhoean shafts, and Mount Etna placed upon Enceladus, at whose movement Trinacria trembles. But this God, the Creator of all things, who makes Arcturus and Orion, changes night into day and day into night, and filters the most bitter waters of the sea, suspended by the heat of the upper air, and distils them into the sweet taste of the rains, like a medicinal cupping-glass, which by the heat of its upper round draws moisture and blood upward; from which we learn whence the rains come.\n— Commentary on Amos","But in saying: Making all things and transforming them, they comprehend Arcturus and Orion in one word, neglecting to carry over the proper sense of the names into the Greek language. And God transforms all things when he makes heavenly things from earthly, and bestows on men the likeness of angels; when the moon will glow with the brightness of the sun, and the sun will have sevenfold light; when man, animal and weak and corruptible, is transformed into one spiritual and strong and incorrupt, changing his glory, not his nature; when those who understand will shine like the splendour of the firmament, and what is written will be fulfilled: One is the glory of the sun, another the glory of the moon, another the glory of the stars. For star differs from star in brightness; so also is the resurrection of the dead; when every creature will be set free from the slavery of corruption into the liberty of the glory of the sons of God. This God who transforms all things also changes the shadow of death into light, when those who sat in darkness and the shadow of death saw a great light, and those who were sons of night and of darkness became sons of light and sons of the day. This is the God who also turns the day into darkness, the day of those who said: Crucify, crucify him, take such a one from the earth; when from the sixth hour, as a type of Jewish blindness, the day was turned into night. And not only according to the letter but also according to a higher understanding, the light which rose for them in the law and the prophets was turned into darkness, since they do not know what they read, what they hear, so that there is fulfilled concerning them what is written: Let their eyes be darkened, lest they see, and bend their back always. This God calls to himself the water of the sea and pours it out upon the face of the earth, making righteous men out of sinners. To expound this, for the sake of brevity let us set down only one example. Paul the Apostle, like a violent whirlwind and a savage tempest and the flood of a swelling sea, was persecuting the Church of God and striving to crush it. Being called by God, he was poured out upon the face of the whole earth, to preach the Gospel from Jerusalem as far as Illyricum, and to build not upon another's foundation, where there had already been preaching, but to press on as far as the Spains, and to run from the Red Sea, nay rather from ocean to ocean, imitating his Lord and the sun of righteousness, of whom we read: His going forth is from the highest heaven, and his course to its highest point, so that the earth gave out before him sooner than his zeal for preaching.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.9":["And as for what follows: Who smiles devastation upon the strong, he returns to the present time. And the order is: he who is the Creator of all things also threatens captivity upon Samaria, and brings plundering upon the mighty, since it turns judgment into wormwood and leaves righteousness on the earth. Where we have said, who smiles, Aquila translated ὁ μειδιῶν. And μειδίαμα is properly said of what we can call a smiling, when someone is angry and, with his lips parted a little, pretends to smile, so as to show the greatness of his anger.\n— Commentary on Amos","This God also divides crushing upon strength, so as to make weak those who are wickedly strong, and so that they can imitate the Apostle, who says: When I am weak, then I am stronger. For the sons of this world are more prudent than the sons of light in their own generation. The strength of the body is the weakness of the soul, and again the strength of the soul is the weakness of the body. Therefore the Lord, who dispenses all things by reason and does all things by true judgment, divides crushing upon the strong enemy, so as to bring misery upon the fortress which raises itself up against the knowledge of God. Of which we also read in Proverbs: The wise man entered strong cities and destroyed the fortress in which the ungodly trusted. This he does indeed against all worldly strength, but properly against the heretics, who try to strengthen the falsity of their dogmas with arguments and sophisms and the dialectical art. But the wise man destroys it, and, helped by the aid of God, shows every fortress to be most vain, so as to bring misery upon it, and, when its pride has been bowed down, he can say with the Apostle: Wretched man that I am, who will deliver me from the body of this death?\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.10":["They hated him who rebukes in the gate, and abhorred him who speaks perfectly. LXX: They hated him who reproves in the gates, and abhorred the holy word. These upon whom God smiles devastation, and brings plundering upon them, who are strong in evil and mighty to oppress, hated him who rebukes in the gate, that is, in court: either me, or the other prophets who have been sent to them; and him who speaks perfectly, or the immaculate word, as Symmachus translated, or the holy speech, as Theodotion and the LXX rendered. That the judgments of the people took place in the gate, according to the ancient custom of the Jews, we have both often read and frequently explained, so that neither would the farmer coming to a case be frightened by the throng of the city and its novel aspect, nor would the inhabitant of the city hasten far from the city and seek the conveyance of beasts of burden. This much let us say according to the letter. But it is a great sin to hate him who rebukes, especially if he rebukes you not out of hatred but out of love; if alone with you alone; if with another brother taken along; if afterwards in the presence of the Church; so that he may be seen to bring the charge not from a desire to disparage, but for your amendment. We often read in the Scriptures of double gates, of death and of life, of vices and of virtues, as in that place in the psalms: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may announce all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion. There is no doubt that he signifies the Church: the lofty Mount Zion, and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and the multitude of angels, and the Church of the firstborn, who are written in heaven. He who has been lifted up in the gates of Zion will not be able to fear the gates of death, of which the Lord spoke to Peter: Upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. The gates of virtue we learn plainly in another psalm: Open to me the gates of righteousness; having entered into them, I will confess to the Lord. This is the gate of the Lord; the righteous shall enter into it. Finally, Wisdom too, in the gates of the city, said boldly: As long as the innocent have held to righteousness, they will not be put to shame. Therefore I think that the gates of the city, that is, of the soul that believes in Christ, are the virtues through which Christ enters into those who believe. And since it is not written in which gates they hated him who rebukes, whether in the evil or in the good, let us refer it to both. The prophet reproves sinners standing in the gates of vices, and for that reason he is held in detestation by them. Or certainly the prophet himself stands in the gates and beginnings of the virtues, and is hated by those whom he rebukes; and it is not enough for sinners to hate him who reproves, or rebukes, in the gates, unless they also abhor the holy word that is brought forth from his mouth. For he who does not receive the prophet does not receive him who sent him. But if they abhorred the holy word, or him who spoke perfectly — moreover, the holy word is the Lord Jesus, of whom it is said: You will not give your holy one to see corruption —, all the Jews, saying anathema to Jesus, abhor the word of God, which is both perfect and holy and immaculate. For to the sinner piety is an abomination. And whatever we have said of the Jews can be referred both to the heathen and to the heretics, of whom the former abhor all the holy Scriptures, in which is the word of God; the latter, while they perversely understand what they read, set up an abomination in the house of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.11-12":["Therefore, because you plundered the poor man and took choice spoil from him, you shall build houses of squared stone and shall not dwell in them; you shall plant most lovely vineyards and shall not drink their wine. For I have known your many crimes and your strong sins, enemies of the just, taking a gift and pressing down the poor in the gate; therefore the prudent man shall keep silent at that time, for it is an evil time. LXX: Therefore, because you struck the poor with the fist, and received choice gifts from them, you have built houses of hewn stones, and you shall not dwell in them; you have planted desirable vineyards, and you shall not drink their wine; for I have known your many impieties and your strong sins; trampling the just, receiving exchanges, and turning aside the poor in the gates; therefore he who understands shall be silent at that time, for it is an evil time. Lest Israel should perhaps think that it was being handed over to the enemy for punishment on account of idolatry alone, he joins to it also the other things which it committed once the religion of the truth had been lost. You plundered the poor man, he says, so as to take from him who scarcely had what was necessary for his sustenance, and whatever you saw that was precious and beautiful you handed over to your own uses. Hence also from the price of the things which you seized and took from the poor man you built houses of squared stone, so that, while despoiling men, you might clothe walls with marble; and because you built them out of plunder, and prepared them not only for dwelling and shelter, which human frailty requires, but for beauty and delights, you shall not dwell in them, carried off by sudden captivity or by death. You also planted most lovely vineyards, providing for future drinking, and you shall not drink wine from them, because I have known all your crimes and sins, and strong sins, which have provoked my wrath. You are the enemies of righteousness, or of every just man, and you receive gifts and press down the poor in judgment.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.11":["We can say this very thing of the heretics as well, who plunder the poor man, or strike his head with a clenched hand: for this is what κατεκονδύλιζον means in Greek, according to what we read above, striking the poor upon the head. For the heretics strike none but the poor man, who cannot withstand the threat, and not in his other members, but in the ruling part of the heart and in the truth of faith. The poor, I think, are those who, content with a simple faith, are unable to answer the malice of the heretics. But whatever good work they have also prepared as gifts for God, unless they resist the adversaries, they will lose in the time of battle and contest; and these adversaries, by the composition and structure of words, build houses for themselves so that they may remain safe and secure. But they shall not dwell in them, when these have been destroyed and overturned by churchmen. And they not only build houses, but also plant most lovely and desirable vineyards, so as to counterfeit the mysteries of Christ; but they shall not drink wine from them, except that which is the incurable fury of dragons. For the Lord waited for these vineyards to bring forth fruit, and they brought forth not grapes but thorns or wild grapes, not judgment but a cry, with which they blaspheme against their God with insane mouth. And therefore they shall not dwell in their houses, and shall not drink the wine of the vineyards which they had planted, because the Lord has known their many impieties.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.12":["Here knowledge is not to be understood according to what we read elsewhere: The Lord knew those who are his, but in the sense that nothing is hidden from God, and that he knows all the secrets of sinners. I have known, he says, many impieties, which are not only many but also strong and oppressing, or trampling on righteousness itself, or on him who is just. And you receive, he says, exchange, for which all likewise translated ἐξίλασμα, that is, propitiation, and we said gift; but ἄλλαγμα, according to the idiom of the Scriptures, means a price, as we also read in the Gospel: Or what exchange shall a man give for his soul? They also turned aside the poor in the gates, or, as Symmachus translated, oppressed them, so as to sell a sentence for a price against the truth of judgment, falling into that which is written: Gifts blind the eyes even of the wise. These the heretics receive, so as to turn the severity of the Scriptures, which announce torments to sinners, into blessedness; and while they promise every prosperity to the rich, toward the poor alone they are fierce and severe.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.13":["Therefore he who is poor and prudent, when he sees that the judge has been bought, shall keep silent at that time, for it is an evil time. Or certainly it is to be understood thus: What use is it now, when your captivity is near, to enumerate your sins, when there is no longer any remedy and a hostile army surrounds the walls of your cities?\n— Commentary on Amos","When therefore a churchman, prudent and understanding, comes to know that there are many impieties in that which is called the house of God, and not only many but also strong, and such as can oppress righteousness, and that the madness of the teachers has gone so far that they take a price in judgment and do everything for gifts, and also shun the poor in the gates and disdain to hear them, let him be silent at that time, lest he give what is holy to dogs and cast pearls before swine, who would turn and trample them; and let him imitate Jeremiah, who says: I sat alone, because I was filled with bitterness. And that in the psalms: I am alone until I pass over.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.14-15":["All these things can also be fitted to the heretics, so that, abandoning the error which they have invented, they may return to the Church, and hate their former dogmas, and love the truth of the Lord in the Church, and exercise true judgment in the gates of vices and virtues, leaving the former and passing over to the latter; and that those who have been able to escape from the jaws of the devil may hope for mercy. But according to the LXX, with the order changed, the reading of the present section can be rendered more plainly thus: As you have said, We have hated evil things and loved good things, so seek good and not evil, that you may live; and as the Lord God Almighty is with you, so render judgment in the gates, that the Lord God Almighty may have mercy on the remnant of Joseph.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.14":["Seek good and not evil, that you may live, and the Lord God of hosts will be with you, as you have said: Hate evil and love good, and establish judgment in the gates, if perhaps the Lord God of hosts may have mercy on the remnant of Joseph. LXX: Seek good and not evil, that you may live; and so the Lord God Almighty will be with you, as you have said: We have hated evil things and loved good things; and render judgment in the gates, that the Lord God may have mercy on those who are left of Joseph. You say that God is with you because you are sons of Abraham; hear what follows: If you are sons of Abraham, do the works of your father. What are the works of your father Abraham? Love good and not evil. It is a great sin not only to do evil but also to love it. Many sin, and when the heat of pleasure is spent they are stung by their conscience, and they repent of their sin. But he who not only does not grieve that he has done what is evil, but even glories in his crime, fulfils that which is written: The sinner, when he has come into the depth of impiety, despises. Seek therefore good and not evil. For if you seek good, in the very act of seeking good you at once repel evil. But you would never seek good unless you first repelled evil, fulfilling the words of the psalmist, who says: Turn away from evil and do good. And when you have sought good and avoided evil, then you will live in him who says: I am the life. He seeks good who believes in him who speaks in the Gospel: I am the good shepherd. He repels evil who flees from him of whom it is written: The world is set in the evil one. And in the Lord's prayer he says: Deliver us from evil. And when you have sought good, he says, and not evil, and have lived, then the Lord God of hosts will be with you, as you had said that he was with you for this reason, that you were born of Abraham.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.15":["Nor is it enough to seek good and not to seek evil, unless you have ἐπιείκησιν (equity) in both, so that first you hate evil and then love good. He hates evil who is not only not overcome by pleasure, but hates the works of pleasure; and he loves good who does what is good not unwillingly, nor from necessity or fear of the laws, but because it is good, so that he has as the reward of his good work his own conscience and the love which he bears toward the good. Hence the Apostle too says: God loves, he says, a cheerful giver. For not every almsgiving pleases God, but only that which is offered with cheerfulness. And when you have hated evil and loved good, establish judgment in the gates, of which it was spoken above, so that, iniquity being driven out, truth may return. And if you do this, perhaps the Lord God Almighty will have mercy on the remnant of Joseph, those of the tribe of Ephraim and of the ten tribes who were able to escape captivity.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.16-17":["Therefore thus says the Lord God of hosts, the Ruler: In all the streets there shall be wailing, and in all the places that are outside it shall be said: Woe, woe. And they shall call the farmer to mourning, and to wailing those who know how to wail. And in all the vineyards there shall be wailing, for I will pass through the midst of you, says the Lord. LXX: Therefore thus says the Lord God Almighty: In all the streets wailing, and in all the ways it shall be said: Woe, woe. The farmer shall be called to mourning, and to wailing those who know lamentation, and in all the ways wailing, for I will pass through the midst of you, says the Lord. I commanded you to seek good and not evil, that you might live, and that the Lord might be with you. And again I added, lest you should say that you had been warned only once: Hate evil and love good, and restore judgment in the gates, you who hated him who rebuked in the gate, that the Lord may have mercy on the remnant of Joseph. And because you would not do it, trampling on my commandments, and you turned a retreating shoulder to me, therefore thus says the Lord God Almighty, who is the Lord of hosts. Everywhere lamentation, everywhere sadness. The farmers shall be called to mourning, and those who know how to lead the dirge, after the custom of the province, so as to stir up tears, shall be called, so that the wailing and mourning shall be, not in all the streets, as was said above, but in the vineyards, so that where there was once the matter of gladness, there may be the source of tears. And all these things shall come to pass, because I will pass through, he says, the midst of you. The Hebrew word I will pass through, which in their language is said eebor, whenever it is placed in the holy Scriptures in the person of God, is to be taken as a punishment, meaning that he does not remain among them but passes through and leaves them. Hence also in other places, according to the translation of Aquila, when God is angry, he calls his fury and wrath ἀνυπερθεσίαν (no postponement).\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.16":["All these things can be referred to the heretics, so that, because they would not do this and that which is just, there may be wailing in all their streets. Wide indeed and spacious is the way that leads to death, and each of the heretics and of the gentiles has his streets in their fables and fictions; to which it is fittingly added: And in all the places that are outside it shall be said: Woe, woe. For those who are in the Church will not hear woe, which is the last of punishments; but if perhaps they have sinned, a lamentation will be taken up over them. And it shall be said outside, or in all the ways: Woe, woe, for this reason, that they do not have the one way that leads to life, which is the king's way, but crooked and perverse ways, turning aside to the right and to the left, since they do not hear the Lord saying: Do not be overmuch just, and: Perverse are the ways which are on the left. And they fall into a double woe, of the flesh and of the spirit, of the present age and of the age to come; whereas, on the contrary, the churchmen hear: Rejoice, again I say, rejoice. But the farmer too is called to mourning — for the heretics have their own farmers, in whose fields thistles and thorns spring up —, and to wailing are called those who know how to wail, whether for their own sins or for those of others; although we can also take this in a good sense, that a churchman who is able to call others to repentance should imitate his Lord, who says: We have lamented, and you have not wailed; and that he should wail for the heretics, as Samuel once wailed for Saul, king of Israel. And the Apostle says that he mourns over those who have not repented.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.17":["And in all the vineyards there shall be wailing, for their vineyards are the vineyards of Sodom. And instead of the wine of gladness, which gladdens the heart of man, they have brought forth the wine of dragons and the incurable fury of asps. And they will suffer all these things, because the Lord will pass through their midst, so as not to dwell among them, nor to say: I will dwell in them, and walk among them, and: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.18-20":["Woe to those who desire the day of the Lord: to what end is it for you? That day of the Lord is darkness and not light; as if a man should flee from the face of a lion, and a bear should meet him, and he should enter the house and lean with his hand upon the wall, and a snake should bite him. Is not the day of the Lord darkness and not light? And gloom and no brightness in it? LXX: Woe to those who desire the day of the Lord: to what end is this day of the Lord for you? And it is darkness and not light. As if a man should flee from the face of a lion, and a bear should fall upon him, and he should go into the house and lean with his hands upon the wall, and a snake should bite him. Is not the day of the Lord darkness and not light? And gloom, and it has no brightness. Lest the Tekoite prophet, who was of the tribe of Judah, should seem to neglect the men of his own tribe altogether and to address his word to the ten tribes alone, the prophecy is now directed to both kingdoms, that is, to Judah and Israel; this can be understood from many other things indeed, but especially from what follows: Woe to you who are wealthy in Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria. Woe therefore to Judah and Israel, who say: Let the day come, let the foretold captivity come, provided that the restoration also which is promised through the prophets follows. For there is not so much evil in the injury of captivity as there is good in the things which the Lord promises after the captivity. To them the prophet replied that they were waiting in vain, after the seventy years of the Babylonian captivity, for what is to come a long time afterwards, at the coming of the Son of God; for desolation and poverty and innumerable miseries will follow them. For to those who flee, he says, from the face of Nebuchadnezzar the lion, Ahasuerus will come to meet them, under whom the history of Esther is told; or, when the empire of the Assyrians and Chaldeans has been destroyed, the Medes and Persians will rise up. And when you have returned in the reign of Cyrus, and at the command of Darius have begun to build the house of the Lord, and have put all your confidence in the temple, so that you rest in it and lean your weary hands upon the walls, then will come Alexander, king of the Macedonians, or Antiochus surnamed Ἐπιφανὴς (Epiphanes), who will stay in the temple and bite you like a snake, not outside in Babylon and in Susa, but within the borders of the holy land. By these things it is proved that the day which you long for is not one of light and joy, but of darkness and sadness. These things we have said briefly according to the history, so as not to leave the opinion of the Jews wholly untouched.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.18":["But no one doubts that all our writers understand the day of darkness as the day of judgment, of which Zephaniah also writes: Near is the great day of the Lord, it is near and exceedingly swift: the voice of the day of the Lord is bitter and hard. And Isaiah: Behold, the incurable day of the Lord is coming, of fury and wrath, to make the whole world a desert, and to destroy the sinners out of it. And at the same time the confidence of the proud is shaken off, who, in order to appear just before men, are accustomed to await the day of judgment and to say: Would that the Lord might come, would that it were granted us to be dissolved and to be with Christ, imitating the Pharisee who spoke in the Gospel: God, I thank you that I am not like other men, robbers, unjust, adulterers, or like this publican. I fast twice in the week, I give tithes of all that I possess. For by this very fact, that they desire the day of the Lord and do not dread it, they are judged worthy of punishment, because no man is without sin, and the stars are unclean before him. And he has shut up all things under sin, that he may have mercy on all. Since therefore no one can pass judgment on the judgment of God, and we are to render an account even for an idle word, and Job daily offered sacrifices for his sons, lest perhaps they should think something perverse against the Lord, what rashness it is to hear with the Corinthians: Without us you reign, and would that you did reign, that we also might reign with you. Certainly, even if their own conscience did not sting them, they ought to have imitated Paul, who says: Who is made to stumble, and I do not burn? And they ought to have been anxious for all, lest, as lovers of themselves, they should desire the torments of others so that they themselves might reign, as if someone wished his own country to be captured and his city overthrown, so that he alone might enjoy the friendship of the victors. We are accustomed to say in straits and tribulations: Would that I might depart from the body and be freed from the miseries of this age, not knowing that so long as we dwell in this flesh we have a place for repentance; but if we have departed, we shall hear that word of the prophet: But in hell who will confess to you? This is the sadness of the world, which leads to death, by which the Apostle does not wish him to perish who had committed fornication with his father's wife; by which also the unhappy Judas perished, who, swallowed up by excessive sadness, joined murder to betrayal, and a murder worse than all murders, so that where he thought there was a remedy, and the death of hanging an end of his evils, there he found the lion and the bear and the snake.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.19-20":["By these names there seem to me to be signified either various punishments or the devil himself, who is rightly called both lion and bear and snake. And when we think that we hear Isaiah saying: Go, my people, into your chambers; shut the door, hide yourself for a little while until the wrath of the Lord passes, and that we are as it were in our own house, as it were resting in hell, then the snake will bite us, which in the present place is called Nahas, and in Job is called Leviathan, about whose nature and manifold terror we learn more fully in that book itself. But in the gloom and darkness, which are the opposites of brightness and light, the diversity of torments is set forth.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.21-22":["I have hated and cast away your festivals, and I will not take in the odour of your gatherings. And if you bring me your whole burnt offerings and your gifts, I will not accept them, and I will not regard the vows of your fatlings. LXX: I have held in hatred and rejected your festivals, nor will I smell in your assemblies. And if you offer me holocausts and sacrifices, I will not accept them, and I will not regard the saving offering of your presence. This is said properly against the tribe of Judah, and against those who had migrated from Israel to the ceremonies of God, and nevertheless did not depart from the high places, and worshipped idols, and polluted the sacrifices of God by the greatness of their sins. For I never think that he would have said of the offerings of calves which they offered in Dan and in Bethel: If you offer me your whole burnt offerings and your gifts, I will not accept them. But God hates, and not only hates but also has cast away, the festivals of those who flee the lion and run into the bear, and enter the house and are bitten by the serpent, because they do not celebrate the festivals of God but their own festivals, as the Lord says: I have hated and cast away your festivals. And he does not accept the gathering and the odour of men of this kind, nor does he hold it for an odour of sweet fragrance, and he detests all their gifts, and does not regard their fattest vows. This indeed will happen not only to the men of that time, but to us as well, if we commit similar offences, and think that we offer to God what has been gained by plunder and perjuries and crimes, and that we redeem our sins, when we have read that Zacchaeus restored fourfold whatever he had seized, and of what he had gained honestly offered half his substance. For he could not offer as gifts to God what had been wrongly gained, unless he first restored it to its owners, and afterwards fulfilled what is written: Honour the Lord from your just labours, and: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches; for God does not accept vows from the hire of a harlot. On the contrary, the just man can say: Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight. This prayer was turned into sin for Judas the betrayer, for it did not have a good odour, but he said by his deed: My scars have putrefied and are corrupted, because of my foolishness. All these things we can say of the heretics as well, who, while they flee the lion, run into the bear, and having entered the house which they think is the Church of God, lean upon walls which they themselves have made, and are bitten by the serpent; whose light and day darkness and gloom snatch away, so that the darkness is palpable and their firstborn are slain. God hates their sacrifices and casts them away from himself, and whenever they have gathered under the name of the Lord, he detests their stench and shuts his nostrils. But in speaking of hating and casting away and not smelling, he speaks by a human likeness, so that we may come to know the disposition of God through our own words. And if they offer holocausts, or seem to fast, to give alms, to profess chastity, which are the true holocausts, the Lord does not accept these, nor does he deign to look upon their fattest victims. For he judges not the greatness of the sacrifices but the merits and motives of those who offer. Hence also the widow in the Gospel, who had cast two mites into the treasury, is preferred by the Savior to all those who believed that they were offering the fattest vows and victims full of marrow, giving little out of much, since she had offered all that she had. Are these things said more plainly and more truly, after the coming of the Lord, to the people of the Jews, who, though the temple and the altar have been destroyed, believe that they offer victims? God hates and casts away their festivals, and does not take in the odour of their gathering, when, gathered together, they say: Crucify, crucify such a one, and: His blood be upon us and upon our children. And if they offer holocausts in the synagogues, and gifts in the councils of Satan, and their fattest vows, the Lord does not regard them, just as he did not regard the gifts of Cain. They worship the one God well indeed, and offer rightly, but in this they are not regarded, that they do not divide their confession into the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit. But our gifts, that is, those of the Church, which we offer from our firstfruits, God regards, as once he regarded the sacrifices of Abel.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.23":["Take away from me the tumult of your songs, and the canticles of your lyre I will not hear. LXX: Remove from me the sound of your songs, and the psalm of your instruments I will not hear. The beautiful canticles of the Levites, with which they praised God, he calls a tumult and a confused sound, because praise is not beautiful in the mouth of a sinner, and they had been accustomed to offer these same things to idols as well. The prayer of the Jews also, and the psalms which they sing in the synagogues, and the composed praise of the heretics, are a tumult to the Lord and, so to speak, the grunting of a swine and the braying of asses, to whose cries the works of Israel are rather compared. But the psalm and the songs, the lyre and the instruments, take either according to the letter in the people of Israel, things which were once done as an image of things to come, or according to the spirit in us and in the heretics: if we direct them with good works, they are heard by the Lord; if with evil works, he shuts his ears and does not deign to hear the canticles of the ungodly.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.24":["And judgment shall be revealed as water, and justice as a mighty torrent. LXX: And judgment shall roll as water, and justice as an impassable torrent. As water, if it slides down a slope, lays bare what it had covered before and makes it lie open to the eyes of all, so the judgment and justice of God, which he once judged concerning his own people, will lie open to all and will be borne along like a most mighty torrent. Whatever it seizes, it drags with it, and it suffers nothing to withstand it. But according to the Seventy the judgment of the worst men rolls about like water, because it does not stand in one opinion but is carried about by every wind of doctrine, disapproving what it had approved, and thinking what it had praised before to be worth nothing. Their justifications are compared not to rivers and the purest springs, but to muddy and miry torrents, which have no waters of their own but waters gathered from rocks and brambles and crags. Whoever wishes to cross them will at once be swept headlong, and, his feet overturned, he will not be able to say: He set my feet upon a rock; for he treads upon sands, which have no foundation, and when he has been in peril he will say, according to the Hebrews: The torrent has passed over my soul. On the contrary, we read of the just, whose judgment does not roll like water and whose justice is not borne along like an impassable torrent: The thoughts of the just are judgments.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.25-27":["Did you offer victims and sacrifice to me in the desert for forty years, house of Israel? And you carried the tabernacle of your Moloch and the image of your idols, the star of your god, which you made for yourselves. And I will make you migrate beyond Damascus, said the Lord: the God of hosts is his name. LXX: Did you offer victims and sacrificial beasts to me in the wilderness for forty years, house of Israel, and you took up the tabernacle of Melchom, and the star of your god Repham, their figures which you made for yourselves. And I will carry you over beyond Damascus, says the Lord: God Almighty is his name. From this place we learn that all the victims and sacrifices which Israel offered in the desert it offered not to God but to Moloch its king, whose tabernacles they carried, and that they venerated the image of their idols and statues. And what this image or idol is, he shows in the words that follow: The star of your god, which in Hebrew is called chocab, that is, of Lucifer, whom the Saracens worship to this day. For this reason the Lord made them migrate beyond Damascus, that is, to the Assyrians and Chaldeans, the Lord whose omnipotence is shown from the fact that he is the Lord God of hosts.\n— Commentary on Amos","That we may understand it so, Stephen, the first martyr of the Gospel, worthy of his name, relates in the history of the apostles: And they made a calf in those days, and offered a victim to the image, and rejoiced in the works of their hands. But God turned, and gave them up to serve the host of heaven, as it is written in the book of the prophets: Did you offer sacrificial beasts or victims to me for forty years in the desert, house of Israel? And you took up the tabernacle of Moloch, and the star of your god Rhepham, the figures which you made to worship them, and I will carry you over beyond Babylon. Nor must the first martyr be thought to have erred because, where it is written in the prophet, Beyond Damascus, he said, Beyond Babylon. For he set down the sense rather than the word, since they were led beyond Damascus into Babylon, or beyond Babylon. But in the place where Luke put Μολὸχ and where in the Hebrew is written Melchechem, Aquila and the LXX translated Μελχὸμ, Symmachus and Theodotion, of your king. Likewise, where Repham is read in the Seventy, Aquila and Symmachus, transferring the Hebrew word itself, put Chion, Theodotion ἀμαύρωσιν, that is, obscurity. Again, for Sochoth Aquila translated συσκιασμοὺς, that is, tabernacles, Symmachus and the Seventy, tabernacle, Theodotion, vision. And this is to be observed in all the holy Scriptures: that the apostles and apostolic men, in setting down testimonies from the Old Testament, consider not the words but the sense, and do not tread in the same footprints of the expressions, provided they do not depart from the meaning.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.25":["We ask how they offered victims and sacrifice in the desert not to God but to their king, whom they call Lucifer? From the time when they transformed the gold into the head of a calf, saying: These are your gods, Israel, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, everything they did is shown to have been done not for God but for idols. And as for our reading that afterwards they offered certain things to the Lord, they did it not willingly but out of fear of punishment, and because of the slaughter of those who fell on account of the idols; but the Lord regards not the things that are offered but the will of those who offer. Finally, whenever there was occasion, they always returned in heart to Egypt, longing for the garlic and onions and cucumbers and the flesh of Egypt, and despising the manna that was given from heaven.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.26":["But whatever is said according to the letter against the people of the Jews, refer all of it to those who worship idols under the name of Christ, and who, inventing perverse dogmas for themselves, carry the tabernacle of their king the devil, and the image of their statues and idols. For they do not worship one idol, but according to the variety of their doctrine they adore different gods, and the star of their god. Since he is an angel of Satan, he transforms himself into an angel of light, and falls from heaven like lightning, and the antichrist imitates Christ. And he fittingly added: Which you made for yourselves. For they did not receive these things from God, but fashioned them out of their own mind.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.5.27":["Therefore the Lord will make them migrate beyond Damascus, so that they may not drink the blood of the Lord, but may pass over into Babylon and hear through the prophet: Babylon is a golden cup, making all the nations drunk. For Damascus, as we have often said, means drinking blood, or the blood of sackcloth, so that through repentance they may be called forth to drink the blood of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.1":["Now that Samaria is to be understood of the heresies is proved both in the prophet Hosea and in many other places, and especially by that testimony: Woe to those who despise Zion and who trust in the mountain of Samaria; they have gathered as a vintage the chiefs of the nations; for all heretics despise 'Sion' [Zion], which means 'watchtower' and is referred to the Church, and they trust in the mountain of Samaria, namely in the pride of perverse doctrines which they think sublime, and through these frauds of preaching they gather as a vintage and lay waste the chiefs of the nations, so that, by the marvel of their doctrines, they draw all the powerful among the Gentiles into heretical error.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Woe to you who are wealthy in Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria; you nobles, heads of the peoples, entering with pomp the house of Israel. LXX: Woe to those who despise Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria; they have gathered the vintage of the firstfruits of the nations, and have gone in for themselves. To Zion, as we said above, and to the mountain of Samaria, that is, to the two tribes, which were called Judah, and to the ten, which were called by the name of Israel, the prophet's word is directed; and especially to those who in each people are nobles and princes, and give themselves to delights after the example of that rich man clothed in purple, who rejoiced in feasting and was lifted up into such pride that he did not deign to look at Lazarus, full of sores, before his door, and did not grant him even what was going to be wasted from his table. These are the heads of the peoples, who trust in riches, and are wealthy in Zion, and have confidence in the mountain of Samaria, and enter with pomp the house of Israel, so that the body shows the swelling of the mind, and they seem like the litters carried in processions. But according to the laws of tropology and the LXX translators, Zion is referred to the Church, of which we read it written: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may declare all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion. But the mountain of Samaria, on account of the pride and boasting in the keeping of God's commandments, is understood of the heretics, who despise the Church, because God chose the weak things of the world, to confound the strong, and the foolish things, to destroy the wisdom of the wise and reject the understanding of the prudent. These have gathered the vintage of the firstfruits of the nations, so that under the Christian name they might bring them into their own winepresses, where the grape is not trodden but ruined, where not new wine is pressed out but poison. And they have gone in for themselves. He said well, for themselves; for they did not go in in the Lord, but went in for themselves. Otherwise, as regards God they have rather gone out, while they withdraw from the Church. And what follows, house of Israel, according to the Seventy is to be read at the beginning of the following section; according to the Hebrews, at the end of this one, which we have expounded.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.2-3":["Pass over to Calneh, and see, and go from there to Hamath the great, and go down to Gath of the Philistines, and to all the best kingdoms of these, whether their border is wider than your border. You who are set apart for the evil day, and draw near to the throne of iniquity, who sleep on beds of ivory, and wanton on your couches, who eat the lamb from the flock, and calves from the midst of the herd, who sing to the sound of the psaltery: Like David they thought they had instruments of song, drinking wine in bowls, and they are anointed with the best ointment, and they suffered nothing over the breaking of Joseph. LXX: House of Israel, pass over, all of you, and see, into Calneh, and pass on from there into Hamath Rabba, and go down into Gath of the foreigners, the best of all these kingdoms, whether their borders are larger than your borders, you who come to the evil day, who draw near and touch false sabbaths, who sleep on beds of ivory, and abound in delights on your couches, and eat kids from the flocks and suckling calves from the midst of the herds, who clap to the sound of the instruments; they thought them as things standing, and not as things fleeing; who drink strained wine, and are anointed with the first ointments, and suffered nothing over the breaking of Joseph. Pass over, all of you, to Calneh, which is now called Ctesiphon, and pass over, all of you. Who are these all? Surely those of whom he had spoken above: Nobles, heads of the peoples, and you who are wealthy in Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria. You therefore, pass over into the city of Persia, and observe carefully, and from there go on to Hamath the great, which is now named Antioch. And he calls it great to distinguish it from the lesser Hamath, which is called Epiphania. Hence even to this day, for those going to Mesopotamia, the first stopping-place is called Emmas, a corrupted word indeed, but one keeping traces of the former name; its region is called Riblah, in which, in the presence of Nebuchadnezzar, the sons of Zedekiah king of Judea were killed, and his eyes were blinded. And go down, he says, to Gath of the Philistines. You who dwell on mount Zion and on the mountain of Samaria, go down to the Philistines, who live in the plains, and to all the best kingdoms of these, which lie under different cities, Gaza and Ashkelon, Azotus, Ekron and Gath, and see whether their border is wider than your border, whether you or they hold the larger provinces; you, I say, you of the people of Israel, who are set apart for the evil day, namely for the day of captivity, and draw near to the throne of iniquity, going to the unjust judge, the Babylonian king.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.2":["All that we have said according to the truth of the history, let us now transfer, according to the LXX translators, to the cloud of allegory. House of Israel, you who have withdrawn from me, who trust in the mountain of Samaria, who have gathered the vintage of the firstfruits of the nations, pass over and see, and go on to the very many walls, for this is what Hamath Rabba means; and from there go gently down into the winepress of those who fall while drinking: for this is what Gath and the Philistines signify. And contemplate everything, and especially the best kingdoms, or the best cities out of all the kingdoms; and consider whether their borders are more than yours. For if we wish to treat it in the mind and to examine the philosophy of all the nations, of the Egyptians, the Indians and the Persians, we shall find their borders narrower than the borders of the holy Scriptures: Into all indeed the earth their sound has gone out, and their words to the ends of the world.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.3":["Those to whom he had said above: House of Israel, pass over, all of you, and see, from what sort of vices they are bidden to pass over to better things, the following words describe: Who come to the evil day, preparing for themselves by their own vice an evil day; not that any day is evil, but that each one prepares evil for himself, according to what we read in Ecclesiastes: Do not say, The former days were better for me than these, for you do not ask about this in wisdom; for it is foolish to lay it on the times, since it is in our power to make for ourselves either a good day or an evil one. These draw near to and touch false sabbaths. For just as there is what is called circumcision of the flesh and circumcision of the heart, and there are Jews openly and in secret, of whom the one kind is rejected and the other approved, so there are sabbaths of the Lord that are holy and rest upon truth, and others that are not holy and are false, which follow the idleness of the letter that kills.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.4-5":["Though you are about to suffer this, you now sleep on beds of ivory and wanton on soft couches, so as to couple lust with sleep. You eat not to drive away hunger and to sustain the human body, but for delights and luxury, so that whatever is tender and fat in the flocks and in the herds you make ready for your gullet; for you the lust of the genitals and of the throat, and a paunch seething with food, are not enough unless you soothe your ears with the songs of pipes and psaltery and lyre, so that what David did for the worship of God, devising the orders of the Levites and the varieties of instruments, you turn to pleasure and luxury.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.4":["And what follows, You who sleep upon beds of ivory, we can interpret thus: he who is an athlete of the Lord, and is trained for wrestling, and prepares himself against the adverse powers, sleeps on the bare ground, imitating Jacob; and he puts at his head the stone which the builders rejected, and which has become the head of the corner. But those who give themselves to delights and luxury, and do everything for the sake of the belly, sleep on beds made of the ivory of a dead animal, and cling to unclean bones; and because vices seem beautiful and delight with their present sheen, they rest on their couches and are weighed down by the heaviest sleep. These do not eat solid and athletic food, by which the strength of wrestlers is reinforced, but soft and tender food, and of the left-hand side, such as are kids from the flocks and fattened calves, very tender, indeed still fed on milk, for this is what μοσχάρια γαλαθηνὰ means.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.5":["They also clap with their hands to the voice and sound of the instruments, so that all their works look for pleasure, and they do nothing except what they provide for the belly and for lust. Nor let the prudent reader at once object to us this, and how it is written: Let the rivers clap with their hands, and: All nations, clap your hands, and: Shout for joy to God our helper. For there they are said to clap not to the sound of instruments, but to have one accord in the praise of God. After the voice of the instruments he added: They thought them as things standing, and not as things fleeing. For the things of the world and all bodies, according to Epicurus, flow and pass away moment by moment, and nothing stands firm in its own state, but all things either grow or decrease, and, in the manner of the waters of torrents, slide headlong. Hence we also read in secular literature: \"But meanwhile time flies, flies beyond recall.\" And in another place: \"Alas, Postumus, Postumus, the fleeting years slip by.\" For nothing is more fleeting than the world and the things of the world. While we hold them we lose them, and through infancy, boyhood, youth, and manhood, and the age that grows heavy, and ripe age, and the last years of old age, the seven stages into which Philo divides human life, we are changed, and we run, and unawares we arrive at the boundaries of death.\n— Commentary on Amos","This most beautiful sense, in which it is said: They thought them as things standing, and not as things fleeing, is not found in the Hebrew, but in its place is written: Like David they thought they had instruments of song. Hence it seems to me to have been added by the translators, who in the description of luxury brought forth a maxim of this kind against vices and pleasures, so that they did not translate what was written, but added from their own what seemed good to them, or rather changed it.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.6":["And you drink wine in bowls, not to sate thirst but to overwhelm the mind. And you are anointed, not with the purest oil to soothe the labours of the body, but with the most costly ointments. And while you abound in these delights, if you see any of the people perishing, you are moved by no pity over their slaughter, but you despise them as brute animals and as beasts perishing in their own gore. This same sense the prophet Ezekiel also sets down under the example of the shepherds: Who eat the milk of the sheep, and are clothed with the wool, and devour whatever is choicest, and do not heal the wounded, do not bind up the broken, do not seek the perishing.\n— Commentary on Amos","And what follows, Who drink strained wine, can properly be referred to the heretics, who approve some Scriptures and reject others, wishing to drink strained wines, although in the holy Scriptures nothing is muddy and miry, but all is most pure, from the water above. These are anointed with the first ointments who, not having the perfumer's art, without any knowledge of the Scriptures, and of the traditions and teaching of the apostles, claim for themselves the dignity of the priesthood, and say that they are the anointed of the Lord, and contaminate the purest oil with the dregs of their own understanding. And while they do these things to the slaughter of those whom they have deceived, they are tormented by no grief, but rejoice in the deaths of others and take delight in the blood of the wretched.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.7":["Therefore now they shall migrate at the head of those who migrate, and the faction of the wanton shall be taken away. The Lord God has sworn by his soul; says the Lord God of hosts: I detest the pride of Jacob, and I hate his houses, and I will deliver up the city with its inhabitants. And if ten men remain in one house, they too shall die; and his kinsman shall take him up, and shall burn him, to carry the bones out of the house, and he shall say to the one who is in the inner rooms of the house: Is anyone still with you? And he will answer: It is the end; and he will say to him: Be silent, and do not remember the name of the Lord. LXX: Therefore now they shall be captives from the beginning of the mighty, and the neighing of horses shall be taken away from Ephraim; for the Lord has sworn by himself, says the Lord God of powers: For I will abominate all the insolence of Jacob, and I hate his regions, and I will take away the city with all its inhabitants. And it shall be, if ten men remain in one house, and they die; and his household shall take them up, and shall try to carry their bones out of the house; and they shall say to those who are over the house: Is anyone still with you? And he will say: By no means; and he will say: Be silent, do not name the name of the Lord. For the reasons above which the prophetic word describes — of those who sleep on beds of ivory, and wanton on their couches, and eat lambs from the flock and calves from the midst of the herd, and sing to the sound of the psaltery, and drink in bowls, and are anointed with the best ointment, and moreover suffer nothing over the breaking of his people, which was begotten of the stock of Joseph — the Lord now threatens and says: Because they have done this and this, now they shall migrate at the head of those who migrate. And the sense is: The punishment is by no means put off into the future, nor is it prophesied of ages long after; what now presses upon them, what is now about to come, my word foretells: that they will go at the beginning of those who migrate, namely the princes and the powerful, to whom he had said above: Hear this word, you fat cows, who are on the mountain of Samaria. And again: Woe to you who are wealthy in Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria; you nobles, heads of the peoples, entering with pomp the house of Israel. You who are first in riches will be the first to bear the yoke of captivity, according to what is written in Ezekiel: Begin from my sanctuary. Not from the saints, as most people think, but from the overthrow of the temple, which was holy. The mighty indeed shall suffer torments mightily, and from him to whom more is entrusted, more will be required. And there shall be taken away, he says, the faction of the wanton, who had one accord in seizing pleasures, and practised revels and shared feasts; these will be taken away together, so that those whose luxury was one may have one punishment. For this the LXX translated, the neighing of the horse shall be taken away from Ephraim, which is not found in the Hebrew, and will be discussed at length by us when we begin to weave the tropology.\n— Commentary on Amos","We have drawn the slender lines of the history; now let us set our hand to the allegory. The princes of the heretics, who for the sake of their delights devoured my people and suffered nothing over the breaking of Joseph, will be the first to be led to punishment, and the neighing of the horse will be taken away from Ephraim, which in the holy Scriptures is taken in two ways: either for the pride and power of those who neigh, or for the greatness of lust. For pride and the conspiracy of the wicked, as there: Some in chariots, and some in horses, and: A horse is deceitful for safety. And the kings of Israel are commanded not to multiply horses for themselves. And in the book of Job the voice of the horse is compared to the blast of the trumpet. In Zechariah too we read, what is confirmed by the testimony of the Gospel and is referred to the presence of the Savior: Rejoice greatly, daughter of Zion, proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem. Behold, your king comes to you, just and a Savior, himself meek, and mounting upon a she-ass and the foal of a she-ass; and he will destroy the four-horse chariots from Ephraim, and the horses from Jerusalem. But for the greatness of lust and the unbridled desire for intercourse, as in Jeremiah, who, describing the luxurious and the adulterers, added: Each one neighed after his neighbour's wife. Such horses the coming of Christ and the wrath of God hamstring.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.8-9":["And the Lord swears by himself, since he has no one greater by whom to swear, that he detests all the insolences of the heretics and hates all their regions. For whatever they say is an insult, and worthy of the hatred of God. And he swears that he will take away the city and their meeting-places with those who dwell in them, namely both the people and the teachers, and that, even if ten men remain — men who, had they been in Sodom and Gomorrah, the fire would by no means have come down upon those cities — all will die that death which leads to Tartarus, of which Ezekiel writes: The soul that sins, it shall die.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.8":["The Lord has sworn by himself, or, as we read in the Hebrew, by his soul, according to what is written in Isaiah: Your new moons and sabbaths and feast days my soul hates, not that God has a soul, but that he speaks with human affections. Nor is it surprising if he is said to have a soul, since he testifies that he has the other members too, which are lower than the soul, feet and hands and belly, and the other limbs. But if those who deny that Christ had a human soul, and say instead that in the human body God was in place of the soul, should raise this against us, let them hear that in Christ the substance of a soul is shown, just as the members of his body also had substance. But in God the Father, just as the other things that are spoken of are not members, but by their names a diversity of operations is indicated, so also the soul is not substantial, but an affection of the inner mind and the seat of thoughts, through which God indicates his will. Has sworn, therefore, the Lord, the God of hosts, that is, Sabaoth — for which the Seventy translated, of powers — that he detests the pride of Jacob and hates his houses. Here, following the preceding section, where it is written: And they suffered nothing over the breaking of Joseph, take Jacob either as the ten tribes, or certainly as the whole house of the twelve tribes. And he has sworn that he will deliver up the city with its inhabitants, either Samaria, or certainly Jerusalem, or both in common. This we can refer to the time of the Lord Savior, after whose coming and passion God detested all the pride, or the insult, of Jacob, with which they cried out that he was the son of a carpenter, and a Samaritan, and had a demon. For this reason Jerusalem with its inhabitants was delivered up to the Roman armies.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.9-10":["And the wrath of God raged against them to such a degree that, even if ten men remain in one house, they too die, and a kinsman, or a neighbour, burns the corpses of the dead to carry the bones out of his house, because, for the crowds of the dying, whole bodies cannot be carried out. And when the one who has grown weary of carrying them away asks the one who is in the inner rooms of the house whether any remain whom he should hand over to him, and he answers: It is the end, I no longer have anyone to hand over to you for burial; then, before he can swear that he has none, the one who had asked, who was outside and had learned that there were none, commands him and says: Be silent, and do not remember the name of the Lord. He records this in order to show that not even when driven by the weight and necessity of evils are they willing to confess the name of the Lord, and that the name of God had come into such oblivion among Israel that Israel does not deign to hear his name even in a simple oath.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.10":["Their bones are buried by their kinsmen and their household, of whom it is said: Let the dead bury their own dead. And he who is outside, and does not enter the house of the dead, but rather casts the dead out, commands the one who burns the dead, and dissolves them into ashes, and crushes their bones, to be silent and not to stain the most pure name of God with a dead mouth. For God says to the sinner: Why do you declare my justices, and take up my covenant through your mouth? Hence we too must take care that we do not, being dead, bury the dead, but rather that, living, we lead forth to life those who are dead. If we do not do this, we are commanded and told, Be silent, because we are judged unworthy of the name of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.11":["For behold, the Lord will command, and he will strike the greater house with ruins, and the lesser house with rents. Can horses run on rocks, or can there be ploughing with wild oxen? For you have turned judgment into bitterness, and the fruit of righteousness into wormwood. You who rejoice in nothing, who say: Did we not by our own strength take horns for ourselves? For behold, I will raise up against you, house of Israel, says the Lord God of hosts, a nation; and they will crush you from the entrance of Hamath to the torrent of the desert. LXX: Therefore behold, the Lord will command, and he will strike the great house with ruins, and the small house with rents. Will horses give chase on rocks, will they keep silent toward the females? For you have turned judgment into fury, and the fruit of righteousness into bitterness. You who rejoice over no good word, who says: Did we not have horns by our own strength? For behold, I will raise up against you, house of Israel, says the Lord God of powers, a nation; and they will crush you, so that you do not enter into Hamath, and as far as the torrent of the west. Because you have leapt into such madness that even in the time of death, with evils hanging over you, you were unwilling to name the name of the Lord, therefore the Lord will command, and he will strike the greater house with ruins, and the lesser house with rents. If he commands, how does he strike? If he strikes, how does he command? But in commanding and giving orders to his ministers, he himself is seen to strike. Just as, when the Father commands and the Son performs, he who commanded himself does it, and that little verse is fulfilled: He spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created. For all things indeed were made through him, and without him nothing was made that was made. And in Egypt the Lord testifies that he himself killed the firstborn, who are reported to have been slain by the destroyer. So too in the present place the Lord commands, and he himself, through his ministers, strikes the greater house with ruins, that is, the ten tribes, which were called Israel, and the lesser house with rents, the two tribes, which were ruled by the stock of the house of David. And note the particular character of each. Israel, because it had sinned more greatly, is struck with ruins and handed over to perpetual captivity. But the house of Judah, in which the temple was, and which had sinned in part, is held in a captivity of seventy years, and is not struck with ruins but with rents. For a house that is rent can be mended; ruins call not so much for restoration as for building anew.\n— Commentary on Amos","Some, according to anagogy, think that the great house and the lesser house signify the Jewish people and the Church gathered from the Gentiles. For they are called great because of the fathers, and the law, and the prophets; we lesser, because we were without the covenant and the commandments of God, of whom we also read in the Song of Songs: My sister is little, and she has no breasts. This house, great and small, gathered into the one household of God, if it does not keep discipline and does not do the commandments of God, will be struck with ruins and rents. Whenever, therefore, the house of God, which is the Church, collapses and is torn, whether in persecutions or in heresies and schisms, it shows the hand of God striking; and if we wish to escape it, let us hear and follow the example of the comparison.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.12":["The ruins and rents of these two houses he compares to horses and wild oxen: the former cannot run on rocks; the latter are so untamed that they do not take the yoke on their necks, and, being forest cattle, because of their wildness they refuse to cleave the earth with the ploughshare. But you, though horses and wild oxen cannot change their nature, have changed the nature of God, so as to make the sweet one bitter and to turn the fruit of his righteousness into wormwood, which is a most bitter herb.\n— Commentary on Amos","Horses cannot give chase on rocks. The rock is Christ, who granted to his apostles that they too should be called rocks: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church. Whoever is on these rocks, the adverse powers, which are called horses, of which we spoke above, will not be able to pursue him, their courses being hindered and they falling on every single rock. Let us come also to the other comparison, according to our custom, discussing the edition of the LXX translators, lest, if we cling wholly to the Hebrew, we should seem to have mocked the diligence of the reader, and, by keeping silent about the common edition, to have set it out to no purpose. Or will they keep silent, he says, among the females: there is no doubt that he means the horses, of which he had said above: Will horses give chase on rocks. These horses, that is, the opposing powers, which go mad for the females, do not dare to approach when they see a manly spirit strengthened by the might of God. But when they see an effeminate mind, enervated by ointments and luxuries and turned to womanish softness, at once they go mad, cannot hold themselves back, and are wild with lust. There follows: For you have turned judgment into fury. He turns judgment into fury who judges in anger; and though the Lord says: You shall not accept a person in judgment, and in another place: You shall not pity the poor man in judgment, and: For the judgment is God's, such a man comes down to judge with an angry mind; indeed, though he does not know the case nor understand the truth of the judgment, he decides beforehand what sentence he ought to pass; the fruit of righteousness too, which is most sweet, he turns into bitterness. What we have said of one virtue, let us understand also of the rest, prudence, fortitude, temperance: whoever is angry will not be able to gather their fruits, and when he does gather them, they will be bitter. Hence it is said in Isaiah: Woe to those who call sweet bitter, and bitter sweet. This is said by those who in judging consider not the cases but the persons, and turn the fruit of Christ's righteousness, which is most sweet, into bitterness. Whoever, therefore, in judging is led either by kinship or by friendship, or, on the other hand, by hostile hatred or by enmities, perverts the judgment of Christ, who is righteousness, and turns his fruit into bitterness.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.13":["You who rejoice in nothing, either in idols and golden calves, which are nothing, as Esther says to the Lord: Do not hand over your sceptre to those who are not, or in nothingness and falsehood. You who think that by your own strength you took horns, and kings and power, with which to toss your enemies.\n— Commentary on Amos","Those who do this rejoice in no good word, or in vain, as Symmachus translated, that is, ἀλόγως, and, lifted up in pride, they say: Did we not have horns by our own strength? Whereas, on the contrary, the just man glories in the Lord and says: In you we will toss our enemies with the horn. And in the same place: For I will not hope in my bow, and my sword will not save me. Hence also in Exodus, according to the Hebrew and the edition of Aquila, we read: And Moses did not know that the appearance of his face was horned, he who could truly say: In you I toss my enemies with the horn. We read also in another place: And he will exalt the horn of his people, and: He has exalted the horn of his Christ, and of the horn of the altar, and of the clean and horned animals, which alone are offered to God, whose interpretation belongs not to this occasion.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.6.14":["Because, therefore, you have done these things, behold, I will raise up against you, O great house and lesser house, which are to be struck with ruins and rents; that is, O house of Israel and all the twelve tribes, the most savage nation of the Assyrians and Chaldeans, to crush and overthrow you from beginning to end, from head to tail; from the borders of your land which look toward the sun, to the torrent of the desert, or of the west, as the LXX translated, that is, from Hamath to Rhinocorura, between which and Pelusium the stream of the Nile, or the torrent coming from the wilderness, enters the sea. We said above that Hamath is Epiphania, which took its name from Antiochus, whose surname was Ἐπιφανὴς (Epiphanes). But those who think that the house of Israel here signifies the ten tribes will not be able to explain how it can be said in a threat against the ten tribes that they are to be crushed from Epiphania to the borders of Egypt, within which borders not the ten tribes only, but all twelve are enclosed, among which were also Judah and Benjamin.\n— Commentary on Amos","Because of these great sins and the utmost pride, which speaks iniquity against God and sets its mouth on high, the Lord God Almighty says that he will raise up a most savage nation, to crush and grind them down, or rather to forbid them to enter into Hamath, and as far as the torrent of the west. Hamath means wall, or walled. They were therefore forbidden by this most savage nation, to which the punishments had been entrusted, to flee for refuge to the fortified city, which is the heavenly Jerusalem, lest they enter and be saved; after the manner of that section which we read in Genesis, where God placed the Cherubim and the flaming sword which turns to guard the way of the tree of life, so that he who had been cast out of paradise might by no means enter there unworthy. And what follows: As far as the torrent of the west, we shall interpret from the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm, in which it is written: Sing to the Lord, sing a psalm to his name; make a way for him who ascends above the sunset. The Lord is his name. For unless evil works have set in us, Christ will not ascend above us. And when they have set, and we have Christ as our charioteer, then, as we advance and go on to better things, it will be commanded us in the same psalm: Sing to God, sing psalms to the Lord, who ascends above the heaven of heaven toward the east. Hence also in the mysteries we first renounce him who is in the west, and who dies to us together with our sins, and so, turned toward the east, we enter into a covenant with the sun of righteousness and promise that we will serve him. For the torrent of the west, Symmachus translated a valley of the plain, Theodotion the torrent of Arabia, Aquila the torrent which is in the level ground. By these words it is shown that those who are shut out from the walled city can by no means be fortified against sins, nor do they reach the torrent of the plain and the level ground, or of the desert, which is called the torrent of pleasure, according to what we read: You will give them to drink from the torrent of your pleasure.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.1":["These things the Lord God showed me: and behold, the maker of the locust at the beginning of the shoots of the late rain, and behold, the late one, afterwards the king's shearer. And it came to pass, when it had finished eating the grass of the land, I said: O Lord God, be merciful, I beseech you; who will raise up Jacob, for he is very small? The Lord had pity over this: It shall not be, said the Lord. LXX: Thus the Lord showed me: And behold, a brood of locusts was coming in the morning; and behold, one cankerworm, Gog the king. And it will be, if it has finished devouring the hay of the land, and I said: O Lord God, be merciful; who will raise up Jacob, since he is small? Repent, O Lord, over this; and this shall not be, says the Lord. The prophetic word foretells not only the things which are to come many ages later, but also things near at hand, which follow immediately upon the prophecy. For we men are accustomed to think much more about ourselves than about posterity, as Hezekiah says: Let there be peace in my days. This is so that those who see fulfilled in fact what was announced beforehand may be converted to the worship of God, in whose prophets is the truth of divination. The Lord therefore shows that Sennacherib king of the Assyrians, with a boundless multitude of his army, like a locust shearing everything, would come at the beginning of the late rain, when Israel was in need of the utmost mercy of God Almighty; and the maker and creator of this locust is the Lord. This locust comes at the beginning of the late rain, when all things are green and every field is in labour, and the blossoms of the various trees burst out into fruits of their own kind. And to show in one word what I mean, the blossoms of the trees and the blades of the crops promise an abundance of all things. These locusts, which were flying in early spring, were followed by a countless cankerworm, which came after the late rain and was called the shearer, or the shearing, of the king, because it laid everything waste and left nothing at all of green grass on the earth. This shearer, or shearing, Isaiah calls a sharp razor, which shaved off all the hairs and locks of the body of Israel. And when, he says, I was in doubt what this razor might be, he at once added: the king of the Assyrians. The razor, therefore, and the king's shearer is the army of the Chaldeans, which like the cankerworm ravaged everything, and consumed not only the crops but the trees, the hay and the stubble.\n— Commentary on Amos","The LXX translators seem to me to have understood the Hebrew word gozi, which means shearer or shearing, as Gog, taking the letter zai for uau, and to have compared the countless multitude of the cankerworm to Gog, a most savage nation, which is described as about to lay waste the land of Judea. But what Aquila meant to say with Behold, the late one after the king of Gaza, since Gaza is called Aza in the Hebrew word, I do not well understand, unless perhaps he set down the very word gozi, and it was gradually corrupted by error into Gaza. Let us speak also spiritually: those who after works of righteousness — of which it is said in the prophet: Sow for yourselves in righteousness, and break up fresh ground for yourselves; and do not sow upon thorns — have committed grave sins, upon them is brought the brood, or generation, of locusts coming in the morning, when, as the darkness of night passes, they begin to recognize their own sins; and because they have not repented, the cankerworm is brought on, which is called Gog, the king; and Gog is rendered in our language roof, a certain proud and arrogant strength.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.2":["And it came to pass, when the cankerworm that had come after the locust had finished eating the grass of the land, and the prophet understood what he was seeing, he turns to prayer and says: O Lord God, be merciful, I beseech you. I do not wish my words to be fulfilled in the overthrow of my people; I do not wish to have been chosen from the number of the shepherds for this, that I should announce the ruin of the ten tribes. For who will be able to raise up Jacob, except you alone? When you strike him down, there is no one to raise him up. Jacob is very small, and destroyed by the frequent incursions of enemies.\n— Commentary on Amos","And when it has consumed the hay and the stubble of our land, whoever are saints among the people will pray for pardon and will say: O Lord God, be merciful. For who else will be able to raise up Jacob? Which of the physicians can heal the αἱμοῤῥοῦσαν (the woman with the issue of blood), except you alone, at the touch of whose fringe health at once returns? For Jacob is very small, or of small number, because no traces of virtues, or only a few, have remained in them.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.3":["And as he prayed and poured out the tears of the inner man, the Lord had pity over this and answered: I will not destroy the whole race of Israel; a remnant will be saved. Or certainly it is to be taken thus: he beheld two things to come together, first the locust, then the cankerworm. For both he entreated the Lord, and he is heard in one only, so that the cankerworm should not lay everything waste to utter destruction; but the locust, flying about, devours some things and leaves others untouched.\n— Commentary on Amos","Repent, O Lord, over this which you threatened that you would do to your people. But the repenting of God in the Scriptures we ought to understand in the same way as his sleep and his anger: not that God repents or changes his sentence, he who speaks through the prophet: I am God, and I do not change, and to whom we say: But you are the selfsame, and your years will not fail; but that, when we are converted to better things, he too repents of his sentence, so that he neither pays the just man the rewards he promised, if he turns aside to iniquity, nor the sinner the punishments he threatened, if he is converted to salvation. It is said to him who is about to repent: Give no sleep to your eyes, nor slumber to your eyelids, that you may be freed like a little doe from the nets, and like a bird from the snares. If such a one has at first despised the commandments of the Lord, and afterwards, set in straits, begins to wake up, he rouses the Lord, who for him is sleeping, and says: Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord? On the other hand, those who have stored up wrath for themselves in the day of wrath will feel God angry. But if they choose repentance, the wrath will be turned into mercy, and according to our disturbances and our virtues the Lord will change in either direction, toward punishment or toward clemency.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.4":["These things the Lord God showed me, and behold, the Lord God was calling judgment to fire; and it devoured the great deep and ate up a part as well. And I said: O Lord God, be still, I beseech you; who will raise up Jacob, for he is very small? The Lord had pity over this: But this too shall not be, said the Lord God. LXX: Thus the Lord God showed me, and behold, the Lord God called judgment in fire, and it devoured the great deep, and ate up a part; and I said: O Lord God, cease; who will raise up Jacob, since he is very small? Repent, O Lord, over this; and this shall not come to be, says the Lord God. First the Lord showed the prophet the maker of the locusts at the beginning of the shoots of the late rain, and after the late rain the shearer king, or the king's shearer, and, to render word for word, the shearing of king Sennacherib, by which he sheared and laid waste everything of the ten tribes. Now the same Lord shows Nebuchadnezzar, or rather calls him and commands him to come against Judah and Jerusalem. And he calls him so that he may set the temple and Jerusalem on fire, and execute judgment in fire upon that which was once his people. And when the fire had come to judgment, to fulfil the Lord's command, it devoured the great deep, and ate up a part as well: all the cities of Judah, and the Lord's part, which was called his temple.\n— Commentary on Amos","But since, once and for all, following the prophet Hosea and the psalmist, who says: The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle, we have referred the ten tribes, which were called Israel, to the person of the heretics, and the two, over which Judah presided, to the Church and the sinners of the Church, who confess indeed the right faith, but because of the filth of their vices need flames to purge them; therefore the Lord now shows that he calls judgment to fire, so that the fire may prove of what sort each one's work is, and that may be fulfilled which is written: Walk in the light of your fire, and in the flame which you have kindled. Hence it is said also to Babylon: You have coals of fire, you will sit upon them, they will be a help to you. And in the psalm the deceitful tongue, full of lies, is said to need purging by the fire of coals: What shall be given you, or what shall be added to you, against a deceitful tongue? The sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals. Of these coals of the altar, the desolating coal is taken up with the tongs of the two testaments, and purges the unclean lips of Isaiah, so that he can prophesy the word of the Lord. The fire, then, called to judgment, first devours the deep, that is, all kinds of sins, wood, hay, stubble, and afterwards eats up a part as well, that is, it reaches his saints, who are reckoned as the Lord's own possession and in his portion; for it is time for judgment to begin from the house of the Lord. And in Ezekiel it is commanded to those who are to inflict the punishments: Begin from my saints. And in the Apostle we read: If anyone's work burns, he will suffer loss, but he himself will be saved, yet so as through fire.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.5-6":["And when we have all been in sin, and have lain prostrate before the truth of the sentence, the Lord will have mercy on us, and because we are very small, he will raise us up at the time of the resurrection, or he will raise up through virtues us who lay in vices; the Lord promising this and saying: But this too shall not be. And he said well: But this too, because above he had already said: This shall not be. Not, indeed, to the end will he be angry, nor for ever will he threaten. He has not dealt with us according to our sins, nor repaid us according to our iniquities. As far as the east is from the west, so far has he removed our iniquities from us. As a father has pity on his sons, so the Lord has had pity on those who fear him.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.5":["And when the prophet had seen this, he said to the Lord, not as above: Be merciful, I beseech you, but: Be still, or cease, so that by his prayers he might obtain that he whom he had already seen begin should cease, especially since there is no other who can raise up Jacob, fallen and very small and humbled, except the Lord, who is able to bring back into the land of Judah those taken captive and carried off into Chaldea.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.7":["These things the Lord showed me: And behold, the Lord standing upon a plastered wall, and in his hand a mason's trowel. And the Lord said to me: What do you see, Amos? And I said: A mason's trowel. And the Lord said: Behold, I will set a trowel in the midst of my people Israel. I will not again overlay it, and the high places of the idol shall be demolished, and the sanctuaries of Israel shall be made desolate; and I will rise up against the house of Jeroboam with the sword. LXX: Thus the Lord showed me: And behold, a man was standing upon a wall of adamant, and in his hand adamant. And the Lord said to me: What do you see, Amos? And I said: Adamant. And the Lord said to me: Behold, I will cast adamant into the midst of my people Israel. I will by no means again pass him by, and the altars of laughter shall be scattered, and the sacrifices of Israel shall be made desolate, and I will rise up against the house of Jeroboam with the sword. Before we discuss the adamant, which Symmachus and the LXX gave for what is written in the Hebrew as enach, it must be said briefly that Aquila rendered this word γάνωσιν and Theodotion τηκόμενον, of which the one means tinning and the other melting, so that that frying pan which we read of in Ezekiel, in which is signified the frying of the people hemmed in by siege, may be shown, through the word tinning, also in the ten tribes and in the reign of Jeroboam — who was the grandson of Jehu, under whom this prophecy is seen —. The Lord, then, is seen standing upon a wall tinned, or plastered, and in his hand tinning, or a mason's trowel, with which walls are usually overlaid, and receive not only beauty but also strength against the harm of the rains and the very frequent storms.\n— Commentary on Amos","We have said as best we could what has been handed down to us by the Hebrews according to the truth of the history; let us pass to anagogy, setting down a few words from Xenocrates, who wrote on the natures of stones and gems: \"Adamant is a stone of its own name, which in Latin we can call untamable, because it yields to no material, not even to iron. For if it is placed on an anvil and struck with a heavy blow of the hammer, the anvil and the hammer receive damage before the adamant is broken. And though fire tames all things and consumes all metals, it renders the adamant harder, so that the force of excessive heat does not blunt even the smallest corner of it. I have seen also in gold an adamant the size of a millet grain, and though the gold around it is worn away by long use and great age, the adamant alone is not worn down, and it cannot be diminished by any file, but on the contrary wears down the file, and furrows with lines whatever it touches. This stone, most hard and untamable, is dissolved by the blood of goats alone, and put into hot blood, it loses its strength. It is small and unsightly, having the colour of rust and the brilliance of crystal. And four kinds of adamant are described: the first is Indian, the second Arabian, the third Macedonian, the fourth Cyprian, possessing more or less hardness according to the character of the regions. It is said also, like electrum, to detect poisons and to resist the arts of sorcerers\". Such is the Lord and Savior, who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal to God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, made in the likeness of men, and found in bearing as a man; humbling himself, made obedient unto death, and the death of the cross. Of whom Isaiah writes: He has no form nor comeliness; we saw him, and there was no beauty, despised and the last of men, a man of sorrows, and knowing how to bear infirmity. He stands upon the wall of adamant, that is, upon his saints and apostles, to whom he granted that they too should be called adamants, and, overcome by no one, should say: Who will separate us from the love of Christ? Tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or the sword? And again: I am sure that neither death nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor strength, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature will be able to separate us from the love of God which is in Christ Jesus our Lord. Peter too was a most strong adamant: the gates of hell did not prevail against him. This man and Lord, who stands upon the wall of adamant, has in his hand the adamant, which, unless it is held by the hand of God and fortified by his help, loses all its strength, as the Lord says in the Gospel: No one can snatch out of the hand of my Father. And he is so robust that it is said to him: If you pass through fire, the flame will not burn you; and the more he is beaten by temptations, the stronger he becomes, and for the name of the Savior he rejoices amid the scourges. And though he can be overcome by no one, he is dissolved by the heat of deadly lust alone. For this is said to be the nature of the blood of goats and of the goat himself, that he is most hot for lust, and that what fire cannot tame, his blood alone dissolves.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.8-9":["The Lord therefore sets an adamant of this kind in the midst of his people Israel, and no longer passes him by, nor does he leave off, so that the altars, which deserve laughter and derision, are scattered, and he strikes with the sword and overthrows all the mysteries of the heretics and the house of Jeroboam, who first divided the people of God.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.8":["We read in Ezekiel that the Lord threatens that, when storm and hail arise, he will by no means plaster or clothe the wall of Israel, but will let it be broken up by the rains. Hence now too he of whom it is written, He shall be called the builder of the wall, himself the cornerstone and the builder of the house, says that he will lay down the trowel in the midst of his people Israel, and make it cease and lie idle, so that he will by no means again overlay it, and clothe and protect it with his help.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.9":["And when the Lord, the trowel ceasing, has withdrawn his protection and, so to speak, the clothing of the wall, then the high places of the idols, which in Hebrew are called bamoth, will be demolished, and the sanctuaries of the ten tribes will be made desolate, so that Dan and Bethel are destroyed, in which the golden calves were worshipped.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.10-13":["All that we have interpreted concerning Amaziah, and Jeroboam, and Israel, and Amos must be referred τροπολογικῶς (tropologically) to the heretics, whose priest Amaziah is accustomed at times to send to Jeroboam, the heretical king and patron of heretics, and to accuse holy men and heralds of the faith before him, and to order the teachers not to preach in Israel, lest they act against the king's will, because Bethel, that is, the house of God, and the false Church is the sanctuary of the king and the house of the kingdom. For they are accustomed to say: The emperor is in communion with us; and if anyone resists them, they at once bring the slanderous charge: So you act against the emperor? You despise the commands of the Augustus? And yet let us consider that many Christian kings who have persecuted the Church of God, and tried to establish the Arian impiety in the whole world, outdo Jeroboam king of Israel in wickedness. For he despised the commands of the false priest, and would give no answer to his prompting. But these, with their many Amaziahs as their priests, by hunger and want, by prisons and exiles, have put to death the prophet Amos and the priests of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.10":["And Amaziah the priest of Bethel sent to Jeroboam king of Israel, saying: Amos has rebelled against you in the midst of the house of Israel. The land will not be able to bear all his words. For thus says Amos: Jeroboam shall die by the sword, and Israel shall go captive out of his land. And Amaziah said to Amos: You who see, go, flee into the land of Judah, and eat bread there, and there you shall prophesy; and in Bethel you shall not again prophesy any more, because it is the sanctuary of the king and it is the house of the kingdom. LXX: And Amaziah sent the priests of Bethel to Jeroboam king of Israel, saying: Amos is making gatherings against you in the midst of the house of Israel; the land will not be able to bear all his words. For thus says Amos: Jeroboam shall die by the sword. But Israel shall be led captive out of his land. And Amaziah said to Amos: You who see, go out, withdraw into the land of Judah, and live there, and there you shall prophesy; but in Bethel you shall not again prophesy any more, because it is the sanctuary of the king, and the house of the kingdom. It is a proverb of Solomon, or rather a command: Do not rebuke the wicked, lest they hate you. And on the contrary he gave a precept about the good: Rebuke a wise man, and he will love you. In another place also: He who rebukes, he says, the ways of a man will have more favor than he who speaks what pleases. And so Amos too spoke to the people out of a desire for their amendment, and from the words of God threatened terrible things, so that they might repent, return to God and forsake their idols. Then the priest of Bethel, in which was the golden calf that Jeroboam the son of Nebat and the others who had succeeded him in the rule had set up, sends to Jeroboam, the grandson of Jehu, someone to report to him, saying: Amos has rebelled against you, or, is making gatherings and assemblies in the midst of your kingdom, Israel; and he says such great things that the land of your kingdom cannot bear his words. And he dares to send to the king of Israel as though he were a high priest, fearing that if the people should be converted to the worship of God, he would lose the glory of his priesthood. And though he had heard two things, the high places for idols will be demolished, or the altars of laughter, and the sanctuaries, or ceremonies, of Israel will be laid waste, and I will rise up against the house of Jeroboam with the sword, because the idols over which Amaziah the priest presided were to be utterly destroyed, and God would rise up against Jeroboam with the sword unless he repented, he sends no word about the wrong done to himself, so that he may seem to grieve only over the insult to the king.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.11":["For thus, he says, says Amos. You are wrong, slanderer. For in all his words the prophet does not speak as though from himself, but always sets at the head: Thus says the Lord. Therefore he makes out that the prophet Amos said what the Lord spoke, so as to provoke the king the more to vengeance. What are the things that Amos says? Jeroboam shall die by the sword. In this too you lie: for he did not say: He shall die; for if he had said this, it would seem that he did not admit repentance; but he said that the Lord will rise up against the house of Jeroboam with the sword, threatening punishment, not inflicting it. Nor does he say: Jeroboam shall die by the sword, but: I will rise up against the house of Jeroboam with the sword. For Jeroboam did not die by the sword, but his house, that is, his son Zechariah, was destroyed when God struck him. And Israel, he says, shall go captive out of his land; add the rest too: if he does not repent.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.12-13":["Moreover Jeroboam, despising the utterly empty messages of Amaziah, would give no answer to what he had sent him; whereupon Amaziah takes upon himself the authority of the priestly eminence, and speaks to Amos: You who see, go, flee into the land of Judah. The prophets, as we have often said, were formerly called seers, because they beheld with the eyes of the heart the things they said were to come. The prudent reader may ask why he calls him a seer, and by the prophetic name, the man whom he compels to leave the land of Israel. To him the answer must be either that he says it in mockery, κατὰ ἀντίφρασιν (by antiphrasis), since everything Amos says is a lie, or that he saw there were many among the people who listened to him gladly; for which reason he also reported to the king: Amos is making gatherings against you; he does not dare to do him an open injury, lest he seem to offend those who listen to him. Go, he says, into the land of Judah, in which you were born, where madmen are gladly listened to, and eat your bread there, or live there, or at any rate practise your art there, by which you may find yourself food, and prophesy there, because you have many there whom you are used to leading astray; but in Bethel, in which I am priest, you shall not prophesy any more, because it is the sanctuary of the king and the house of the kingdom. And this he says as a false priest, flattering the royal eminence, so that he does not say: It is the sanctuary of our God, and the house of the idol, but the sanctuary of the king and the house of the kingdom; for all who worship false gods have this custom, to ascribe their own pride to kings, so that what they themselves do may seem to have been done by the king.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.14-15":["And Amos answered, and said to Amaziah: I am not a prophet, and I am not the son of a prophet, but I am a shepherd plucking sycamores. And the Lord took me when I was following the flock, and said to me: Go, prophesy to my people Israel. And now hear the word of the Lord: You say: You shall not prophesy over Israel, and you shall not drip upon the house of the idol. Therefore thus said the Lord: Your wife shall commit fornication in the city, and your sons and your daughters shall fall by the sword, and your ground shall be measured with the line, and you shall die in a polluted land, and Israel shall go captive out of his land. LXX: And Amos answered, and said to Amaziah: I was not a prophet, nor the son of a prophet, but a herder plucking sycamines. And the Lord took me from the sheep, and the Lord said to me: Go, and prophesy over my people Israel. And now hear the word of the Lord: You say: Do not prophesy over Israel, and do not gather hearers against the house of Jacob. Therefore thus says the Lord: Your wife shall commit fornication in the city, and your sons and your daughters shall fall by the sword, and your land shall be divided with the line, and you shall die in an unclean land; but Israel shall be led captive out of his land. What Scripture records that the blessed apostles did, when the scribes and Pharisees warned them not to teach in the name of Jesus, and they answered, saying: We must obey God rather than men, we recognize that the prophet Amos also did: when the high priest of the idols ordered him not to prophesy in Bethel, he not only prophesies, and shows that he fears God who sends him more than the man who forbids him, but boldly and freely announces punishments to the very man who tried to forbid him and to hinder the word of God. Not only, he says, am I not a prophet, or, was not — of which the one belongs to humility, the other to truth — nor the son of a prophet, descended from a prophetic stock, but when I was a herdsman and was stripping blackberries from the brambles, the Lord took me as I followed the flocks. For herdsman, which in Hebrew is called boger, Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion and the Fifth edition translated βουκόλον, one who pastures herds of cattle, not sheep. The Seventy alone said αἰπόλον, which properly means a herder of goats, ἀπὸ τοῦ αἰπολίου, which indeed means a flock that lives on the heights, to show that flocks of goats always climb rocks and rough crags and higher places. But since he adds: And the Lord took me ἐκ τῶν προβάτων, that is, from the flocks, he seems rather to mean sheep than goats, although we read at the beginning of Leviticus that both sheep and goats are designated by the name of flocks. For thus the Lord speaks: If you offer ἀπὸ τῶν προβάτων, that is, from the flocks to the Lord, a lamb or a kid, and a sheep or a goat. Moreover, what he says, plucking sycamines, which Aquila translated searching sycamores, and Symmachus having sycamores, some explain in this way, holding that sycamines is the name of a kind of tree that grows in the plains of Palestine and bears wild figs; and these, if they are not plucked, produce very bitter little figs and are spoiled by gnats. To us, however, since the wilderness in which Amos lived produces no tree of this kind, it seems rather that he means brambles, which bear blackberries and relieve the hunger and want of herders. However the reader may wish to take it, this must be said: that the Lord took up a lowly and rustic herder, and sent him to his people Israel, and commanded him to go out from his own land and proceed to Samaria, and there prophesy the things that are to come.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.16-17":["According to tropology, Amaziah the priest means strong and rigid, because, rebellious and fierce, he forbids the churchman and true prophet to bring forth the words and the teaching of God, and prohibits him from correcting the erring people. His wife, the false Church, will lie open to the fornication of all, and his sons and daughters, whom he wickedly begot in error, will be struck by the Lord's sword; his ground too and all his possession will be the portion of demons, and he himself will die in a polluted land, which does not have God as its inhabitant but is contaminated by the many errors of a depraved religion; and all the people who take to themselves the false name of Israel will go captive out of their land, so as not to serve God, but to submit their necks to the yoke of heretics and demons.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.16":["Since, therefore, he says, when the Lord commands me to speak, you, Amaziah, say to me: You shall not prophesy over Israel, and you shall not drip upon the house of the idol, hear what the Lord threatens against you. For house of the idol, in the Hebrew is written beth isaac, that is, house of laughter, for Isaac means laughter; which the LXX translated house of Jacob, understanding one name in place of another, and not the thing. Again, where we have put: And you shall not drip, Symmachus translated: you shall not rebuke. Now that the prophets drip is an idiom of the Scriptures, because they do not bring on the whole wrath of God at once, but announce it in small drops by their threatening.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.7.17":["Because, therefore, you said to me: You shall not prophesy, your wife shall commit fornication in the city; which Symmachus translated better, πορνευθήσεται, not that she herself will commit fornication, but that, in the passive voice, violated by others, she will suffer fornication. And it is a great grief and an incredible disgrace when a husband, in the middle of the city, with everyone present, cannot prevent the outrage done to his wife. Your sons also, he says, and your daughters shall fall by the sword. The grief over a violated daughter is not so great as over a defiled wife, nor the grief over a slain wife so great as over slaughtered children. For a husband would more willingly have his wife killed than defiled. Nor is this enough for the outrage, unless his land too is divided with the line by the enemy, and receives new tillers. Amaziah himself also, who now glories in the power of the priesthood, will by no means die in his own land, but, led into captivity, will die in a land polluted with idols; and he will not die before he sees the people he had deceived enslaved and captive.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.1-2":["But if we choose to read a fowler's vessel instead of a hook for fruit, this must be said: that as a fowler, with birdlime or nets, draws down to the earth birds that fly through the air and range on high, so the Lord, through Sennacherib or Nebuchadnezzar, whom we now understand as the fowlers, seized, bound, carried away and killed his people, who were formerly free and lofty through their observance of the law. For not unjustly are nets spread for birds. And this is to be referred not only to the time of the Babylonian captivity, but also to the coming of the Lord the Savior, when they said: Away with such a one from the earth; crucify him, crucify him; and they lost the wings of the dove. And the end came upon them, and the final captivity, and God spared them no longer.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.1":["These things the Lord God showed me: And behold, a hook for fruit, and he said: What do you see, Amos? And I said: A hook for fruit. And the Lord said to me: The end has come upon my people Israel; I will not again pass him by any more, and the hinges of the temple shall creak in that day, says the Lord God; many shall die, in every place silence shall be cast. LXX: Thus the Lord God showed me: And behold, a fowler's vessel. And he said: What do you see, Amos? And I said: A fowler's vessel. And the Lord said to me: The end has come upon my people Israel; I will not again pass him by any more; and the ceilings of the temple shall howl in that day, says the Lord God. Many will be falling: in every place I will cast silence. What Jeremiah sees under the figure of the rod of watchings, or of the nut, because God kept watch over the sins of his people, lest, as though with eyes shut and blinking, he should not see their offenses, this Amos now beholds against Judah and Israel alike under the appearance of a hook, which in Hebrew is called chelub and is turned by the LXX into a fowler's vessel; whereas a fowler is properly called moces, as we read above in this same prophet: If a bird will fall upon the earth without a fowler. And the sense is: As the branches of trees are drawn down with a hook to pick the fruit, so I have drawn near the time of captivity.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.2":["And that we may know that this is what we say, God himself explains what the vision he had shown the prophet signifies: The end has come upon my people Israel. And what follows: I will not again pass him by any more, signifies this: that he will no longer pass over the iniquities of his people, nor neglect them, nor allow their crimes to pass unpunished.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.3":["And lest he seem to speak only of the ten tribes, he added: The hinges of the temple shall creak, or the ceilings, in that day, says the Lord. This, however, is to be taken ὑπερβολικῶς (hyperbolically): so heavy a weight of evils will press down that even the very hinges of the gates and the lofty ceilings will howl and feel an incredible devastation, when many will die and, the inhabitants being captured or killed, silence will be cast in every place.\n— Commentary on Amos","And the hinges of the temple howled, or the ceilings of the temple, collapsing upon the slain people. And in every place silence has been cast, the Jews having Moses and the prophets, but not having the Word of God; reading the letter and losing the spirit, when their table was made into a snare, and into a recompense, and into a ruin; and their eyes were darkened, so that they should not see; and their ears were stopped, so that they should not hear; and their back was bowed down, so that they should not look up to heaven, but, with that woman of the Gospel who had a spirit of infirmity, should always be bent toward the earth. And the wrath of God was poured out upon them, and the fury of his wrath seized them, so that their habitation became desolate, and there was no inhabitant in their tents; when this was fulfilled: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will send a famine upon the land; not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the word of the Lord; about which, if we reach that point, we must speak more fully later on. We, then, seeing that God did not spare the natural branches, let us fear to suffer the same things, and let us avoid the fowler's snares, and say to the Lord: Do not be silent, nor hold yourself back, O God, and do not keep silence from me, and I shall become like those who go down into the pit. Over the whole world the silence of the Jews has been cast: wherever they are, they mutter rather than speak; and though their blasphemy against the Lord the Savior reaches even to heaven, all interpretation of the Scriptures is mute and silent, and does not set its ears to hearing.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.4-6":["And this indeed we sometimes see done also by perverse teachers and rulers, who without the fear of God lord it over the clergy, of whom Jeremiah speaks: Their clergy will not profit them; who think that gain is godliness, and, sitting in the temple as money-changers, set out doves, not in cages but in chairs, as though they were teachers, selling the gifts of the Holy Spirit; who make the measure smaller and greater, so that to the poor they speak either little or nothing, but to the rich, and to those from whom they pursue gain, they speak in the manner of public orators at very great length. And for money they crush and trample the heads of the poor, and thrust into greedy throats, not the grain of the Lord, which strengthens the heart of man, but refuse and the vilest dust; selling even these things that are of no value, although the Lord commanded the apostles: Freely you have received, freely give.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.4":["Hear this, you who crush the poor and make the needy of the land fail, saying: When will the month pass, and we shall sell our wares, and the sabbath, and we shall open the grain, that we may diminish the measure and increase the shekel; and put in deceitful balances, that we may possess the needy for silver, and the poor for shoes, and sell the refuse of the grain. LXX: Hear these things, you who crush the poor in the morning, and oppress the needy from the land, who say: When will the month pass by, and we shall trade; and the sabbaths, and we shall open the granary, to make the measure smaller and the weight greater; and let us make an unjust balance, that we may possess the poor for money, and the needy for shoes, and trade in every sale. Lest the hook for fruit and the fowler's vessel should seem to have been shown in vain, and the captivity to threaten as near through these signs, the causes of God's offense are briefly described. O you, Judah, O you, Israel, hear what crimes you have committed: rising by night, instead of prayer and hymns to God, you have a zeal for oppressing, or crushing, the poor, so that, worn out with hunger and want, they may be removed from the land.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.5-6":["You who wait for the Kalends, to trade and to heap interest upon interest, and for the sabbaths, to open the granaries and give out grain, meaning to receive more back, and who turn the solemn feasts of God to your own profit for the sake of shameful gain; who make the measure smaller in selling goods, and the weights greater in receiving them, and the balance unjust, so that you may possess the poor man's money, and hold men so cheap that you fulfill what I said of you above: Because you sold the righteous for silver, and the poor for shoes; you have leapt into such a frenzy for gain that you sell to the poor not grain, by eating which men's bodies are sustained, but the refuse and sweepings of the grain, mixing dust and chaff with the wheat.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.7-8":["And he swears that he will not forget their works even to the end; for the proud God resists, but gives grace to the humble; but that he holds in memory all the sins they have committed, and brings them even to the end and the time of judgment. Hence the penitent pray: Do not remember our former iniquities; especially since against the proud the earth itself is moved, or troubled, and mourns, not the tenant and the stranger, but its inhabitant; and its consummation rises up like a river, and goes down like the stream of Egypt, so that he who repents rises with the climbing river, but he who persists in his offenses goes down like the river of Egypt and, entering the sea, is swallowed up. And by this he shows that the pride of Jacob, against which the Lord swears, is to be devoured by eternal punishments.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.7":["The Lord has sworn against the pride of Jacob: If I shall have forgotten all their works even to the end. Shall not the earth be moved over this, and every inhabitant of it mourn, and it rise up wholly like a river, and be cast out, and flow away like the brook of Egypt? LXX: The Lord has sworn against the pride of Jacob: If he will forget all your works to the end. And over these things shall not the earth be troubled, and every inhabitant of it mourn, and its consummation rise up like a stream, and go down like the river of Egypt? Scripture quite often records the Lord swearing. First, in Genesis, the angel of the Lord called Abraham a second time from heaven, saying: By myself I have sworn, says the Lord, because you have done this thing, and have not spared your dearest son, blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you like the stars of heaven, and like the sand that is on the shore of the sea. And in the hundred and ninth (110th) psalm: The Lord has sworn, and he will not repent; you are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek. Discussing this, whoever he is who wrote the epistle to the Hebrews says: Since he had no one greater by whom to swear, he swore by himself. And in this same prophet we read above: The Lord has sworn by his holiness, or by his holy ones, that behold, days will come upon you, and they will take you up with weapons, or with poles, and the rest. He, then, who had earlier sworn by his holiness, or by his holy ones, now swears against the pride of Jacob, so that if they do not believe him when he threatens, they may believe him when he swears, after the likeness of men, who take up an oath when their simple words are not believed. Nor is it surprising if God is said to swear, since he sleeps with those who sleep and keeps watch with those who keep watch, and is said to be angry with those who have stored up wrath for themselves in the day of wrath.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.9-10":["And it shall be in that day, says the Lord: The sun shall set at midday, and I will make the earth grow dark in the day of light; and I will turn your festivals into mourning and all your songs into lamentation; and I will bring sackcloth upon every back of yours and baldness upon every head; and I will make it like the mourning for an only son, and its last end like a bitter day. LXX: And it shall be in that day, says the Lord God: The sun shall set at midday, and the light shall grow dark upon the earth in the day; and I will turn your solemnities into mourning and all your songs into lamentation; and I will bring sackcloth upon every loin and baldness upon every head; and I will make it like the mourning for a beloved one, and those who are with him like a day of grief. By that day he means the day of captivity, when both peoples will be led away to the Assyrians and the Chaldeans; for whom, from the greatness of their sorrow, the sun will set at midday, and while the light is clear for all, darkness will fill everything; when their festivals and all their songs were turned into mourning and lamentation, and upon every back, or loins, they were girded with haircloth, and, according to the custom of mourners of old, they had baldness on their heads, which we read that Job also did at the deaths of his children. And so great will be the greatness of the mourning and sorrow that it will surpass the grief for an only son, dying, and very dear to his mother, and all things will be filled with lamentation and bitterness. We can also understand this passage of the Lord's passion, when the sun at the sixth hour drew back its rays and did not dare to look upon its Lord hanging on the cross; when, as darkness filled everything, their festivals and songs, with Vespasian and Titus prevailing, were changed into mourning and lamentation; when all things were filled with tears and remorse and haircloth, and their heads had baldness, which before, adorned with the long hair of the Nazirites, had grown their locks for the Lord. Then the first-born son of God, the people of Israel, which had laid its hand upon the Only-begotten and true Son of God, was handed over to eternal mourning; and its last end, or those who were with it, were filled with bitterness. Now therefore, while they have darkness, who drove back the sun of righteousness, we who sat in darkness and in the shadow of death have seen a great light, and their whole festival has been transferred to the mysteries of the Church, so that while they weep, we sing praises together to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.10":["They, instead of a belt, are girded with ropes and haircloth; to us, with the apostles, it is said: Let your loins be girded, and burning lamps in your hands. We are girded with the truth of Christ, fulfilling what is written: Stand, having your loins girded in truth. But they, instead of truth, are girt about with the lie of the devil. Our head, of which the Savior speaks: But even the hairs of your head are numbered, has perpetual hair, and in our head, who is Christ, we possess strength to kill the lion. But they slept on the knees of the harlot synagogue, and, shaved bare as the devil sheared them, lost the power of the head, losing together with their strength their eyes, of which it is written in Ecclesiastes: The eyes of the wise man are in his head, but he who is a fool walks in darkness. Their companions pass their days in mourning; our comrades are clothed with the garment of gladness.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.11-12":["Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will send a famine into the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the word of the Lord. And they shall be moved from sea to sea, and from the north to the east they shall go about seeking the word of the Lord, and shall not find it. In that day the beautiful virgins shall fail, and the young men, in thirst, who swear by the sin of Samaria, and say: Your god lives, Dan, and the way of Beersheba lives; and they shall fall and shall not rise again any longer. LXX: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord God, and I will send forth a famine upon the land, not a famine of bread nor a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of the Lord. And the waters shall be moved from sea to sea, and from the North to the East they shall go about seeking the word of the Lord, and shall not find it. In that day the beautiful virgins shall fail, and the youths, in thirst, who swear by the propitiation of Samaria; who say: Your god lives, Dan, and your god lives, Beersheba; and they shall fall and shall not rise again any more. 'Waters' are not found in the Hebrew; and 'propitiation', which we have rendered 'sin', and which is called asamath by the Hebrews, signifies an idol, which is the beginning of sins. Ancient history, Latin as well as Greek, and that of all the barbarian nations, relates that nothing is harder than famine, which often compels the besieged to feed on human flesh and to rage against their own nature, so that parents do not spare their little children, and marital affection tears apart the limbs of a wife once loved. If the famine of bodies does this, what must be said of the famine of souls? This famine, on the day of the Lord's resurrection, overwhelmed the people of the Jews, and joined to the famine was a most burning thirst, since they did not have the bread which came down from heaven, nor those waters which flowed from the belly of Jesus. The law has been taken away from them, and the prophets have fallen silent in eternal silence; they are moved from sea to sea, and from the British Ocean to the Atlantic Ocean, that is, from the west to the south, and from the north to the east, wanderers through the whole world, and they are not able to find the word of God. At this place we shall ask the Jews what day they think is signified, in which they are to endure a famine of hearing the word of God, especially since they read the Scriptures and follow the lowliness of the letter? To them we press home that what was foretold is a famine of spiritual understanding, in which Christ is discerned, and the passion and resurrection of the Lord are found. They go around the world and seek the word of the Lord and do not find it, because they denied the Word of the Lord, which came to pass in the hands of all the prophets, which in the beginning was with the Father, which was made flesh and dwelt among us.\n— Commentary on Amos","But the Lord sends a famine into the land upon those who savor earthly things; and a famine not of bread, and a thirst not of water, but of hearing the word of the Lord, when on account of the sins of the people teaching fails in the churches; and from sea to sea, that is, from salt and most bitter waves they come as far as the sea; not meeting with rivers, nor with most sweet and varied springs, but running back again to bitter things; and from the north to the east, desiring to forsake the north, which is the harshest wind and is called 'the right-hand wind' by the ignorant, and to reach the east, which they will not be able to find, because they do not go by a straight road but wander about by devious paths, and, not keeping to the king's road, are led along winding turns.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.13-14":["And when the virgins fail, the young men too will fail, who had previously conquered the world; and they will fail for this reason, because they swear by the idol of Samaria, which we always take as standing for the heretics, as this same prophet says: Woe to those who despise Zion, and trust in the mountain of Samaria. For the heretics despise the Church of the Lord and trust in the falsity of their own dogmas, raising themselves up against the knowledge of God, having split his people, and saying: We have no portion in David, nor inheritance in the son of Jesse. If anyone, therefore, swears by the sin of Samaria and says: Your god lives, Dan, and the way of Beersheba lives, he will fall and will not be able to rise again any longer. The tribe of Dan could not find a possession in the farthest borders of the Jews, as is written in the book of Judges, and Dan is interpreted 'judgment'. Beersheba, according to the variety of accents, is rendered into our language 'well of the oath', or 'well of plenty', and 'of the seventh'. Therefore the heretics too will thirst at the farthest borders of the holy Scriptures, despising the judgment of God and desiring the way of Beersheba, which was in the tribe of Judah; and, desiring to imitate many mysteries of the Church, they say that they are sated and filled. These the apostle Paul reproaches: Already you are sated, already you have become rich; and they swear in the name of the Lord; and, once fallen, since they consider the gods of the idols to be their god, they will not rise again any more. But those who are willing to repent, and do not say: Your god lives, Dan, and the way of Beersheba lives, will hear through Jeremiah: Shall he who falls not rise again, or he who has turned away not return.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.13":["In that time the beautiful virgins failed, and the young men, or the chosen ones and teachers — for this is what baurim means —, failed in thirst. They interpret the beautiful virgins as the synagogues, and the chosen ones as the masters of the people. And since these have failed, they teach that the curses of Deuteronomy have been fulfilled in the Jewish people.\n— Commentary on Amos","In that time the virgin souls will fail, which the Apostle calls uncorrupted; and he desires them to possess everlasting chastity, writing to the Corinthians: For I betrothed you to one husband, to present a chaste virgin to Christ. But I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his cunning, so your minds should be corrupted and fall away from the simplicity which is in Christ. And if all virgins were beautiful or good, he would never have said: The good virgins will fail; but he set down 'good' to distinguish them from bad virgins, the good being those who are holy in body and in spirit. The bad virgins are the five foolish ones, who did not prepare oil for their lamps. The good and comely virgins are those who had the light of the virtues and entered the bridegroom's chamber. But the virgins will fail, because they will not find the word of the Lord. From this we understand that when teaching is lacking in the churches, modesty perishes, chastity dies, all the virtues depart, because they have not eaten the word of the Lord; and whoever has eaten it, fattened with its food, will hear through Solomon: The righteous man eating satisfies his soul, but the souls of the ungodly hunger. And David, who had reached the old age of Abraham, sang freely: I have been young, and have grown old, and I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread. How many martyrs in persecutions perished of hunger, and lacked grain and these nourishments of the body. Therefore he speaks of that bread which came down from heaven, which whoever eats will be able neither to hunger nor to thirst.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.8.14":["These chosen ones and teachers swore by the idol of Samaria, that is, by the golden calves, and said: Your god lives, Dan, at the borders of the land of Judea, where Paneas now is, and where at that time the golden calf was worshipped, and the way of Beersheba lives, because they travelled there by a long and seldom-taken journey, on account of the kings of Judah, the righteous and the unrighteous alike. And because they did this, they will therefore fall and will not rise again any more, that is, they will not recover the state which they had had before.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.1":["I saw the Lord standing upon the altar, and he said: Strike the hinge, and let the lintels be shaken; for avarice is at the head of them all, and the last of them I will slay with the sword. There shall be no flight for them; whoever of these flees shall flee and shall not be saved. LXX: I saw the Lord standing upon the altar, and he said: Strike upon the mercy seat, and the porches shall be shaken; and dash apart upon the heads of all, and the rest of them I will slay with the sword. Whoever of them flees shall not flee away; and whoever of them has been saved again shall not be saved. Whoever, with Ezekiel and John the Baptist, has seen the heavens opened, and from whose eyes the veil that was laid over the face of Moses has been taken away, so that there may be fulfilled in him what is written: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes; he will see the Lord standing upon the altar and commanding the prophet, or, as many think, the angel who has been appointed to render the punishments of sins, to strike the hinges of the temple, or the mercy seat, and that its lintels, or porches, be shaken. And when, he says, the temple has been scattered and destroyed because of the wickedness of men, and my wrath has begun from my sanctuary, let each one know that he cannot escape, since avarice possesses all things; and that his last end is death, and that no defences can come to his aid for escaping the sentence of God. And as for what we read according to the LXX: That he should cut into the heads of all, fittingly are their heads divided who of their own accord divided themselves from him who is the head of all, and said: We have no king but Caesar, who cried out with impious voice: Crucify, crucify such a one, and: His blood be upon us, and upon our children. All these things we can also refer to the heretics, whose altar is struck, and all their initiations and sacrilegious mysteries are shaken, who have had avarice at their heads, the partridge crying out through them, and gathering what it did not bring forth, and making riches without judgment. Hence the Lord will also slay its last things, or remnants, so that the section begun may be completed, and its last end will be foolish. But when the Lord has struck the hinges and brought the sword upon them, there will be no one who can escape and avoid the Lord's impending sword. And at the same time this too must be noted: that, with the Lord standing upon the altar, first the mercy seat, or the hinges of the temple, is struck; then the porches are shaken; thirdly the heads of all are divided; fourthly those who are left are slain with the sword. For unless the Lord has trampled the pride of the heretics under his foot, and struck their perverse and wicked teaching with the spiritual blade, and divided among themselves their masters, who are understood by the heads, and slain them in a good sense, the disciples cannot be made alive, according to that which is written: I will kill and I will make alive; I will strike and I will heal. The masters are killed and struck, so that the disciples may be made alive; the heads are divided, so that the other members may be healed.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.2-5":["And these things, following the order of the history, we have interpreted as best we could, if indeed understanding has not failed us in some points. But David sets out the whole περιοχὴν (the whole tenor) of this passage, saying: Lord, you have proved me, and you have known me. And at once he adds: Where shall I go from your spirit, and from your face where shall I flee? If I ascend into heaven, you are there; if I descend into hell, you are there. If I take my wings at dawn, and dwell in the uttermost parts of the sea: even there your hand shall lead me, and your right hand shall hold me. In Deuteronomy also we read something similar: This commandment which I command you today is not above you, nor placed far off, nor set in heaven, so that you could say: Which of us is able to climb up to heaven, to bring it down to us, that we may hear it and fulfil it in deed? Nor is it placed beyond the sea, so that you should make excuses and say: Which of us will be able to cross the sea and bring it all the way to us, so that we may be able to hear and do what is commanded? But the word is near you, exceedingly, in your mouth and in your heart, that you may do it. For when the soul, loosed from the bodily chains, has, by reason of the thinness of its substance, the freedom to fly where it will, or where it is compelled to go, it will either be led to the lower regions, of which it is written: Let sinners be turned into hell, all the nations that forget God, and: In hell who will confess to you? And: Your glory has gone down into hell. Or else it will be lifted up to the heavenly places, where are the spiritual hosts of wickedness in the heavenly places; and if it wishes to claim for itself the knowledge of the true circumcision, for this is what Carmel is interpreted, and, scorning humility, to dwell on the mountains, even there it will not be able to escape the searching hand of God. But if, despairing of salvation, it tries to avoid the eyes of God and to reach the farthest limits of the salt waves, even there the Lord will command the crooked and ancient serpent, who is the enemy and the avenger, and it will bite it. Taken captive also by vices and sins, it will be punished with the sword of the Lord, and he will set his eyes upon it for evil and not for good, so that through tortures and punishments it may return to the Lord. And when Almighty God has touched their land and shaken it, and has made all earthly things melt, they, understanding their crimes, will be turned to mourning, and the Lord himself will ascend and descend in a twofold way. He will ascend upon the saints like the river of its consummation, that is, of the earth, so as to consume their earthly works. He will descend upon sinners like the flood of Egypt, so that by the Lord's onrush they may be thrust down into the bitterness of torments.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.2":["If they go down even to hell, from there my hand shall bring them out; and if they go up even into heaven, from there I will drag them down; and if they are hidden on the top of Carmel, from there I will search them out and take them away; and if they hide themselves from my eyes in the depth of the sea, there I will command the serpent, and it shall bite them; and if they go into captivity before their enemies, there I will command the sword, and it shall slay them; and I will set my eyes upon them for evil, and not for good. And the Lord God of hosts, who touches the earth and it melts, and all who dwell in it shall mourn, and the whole of it shall rise up like a stream, and shall flow down like the river of Egypt. LXX: If they are dug down into hell, from there my hand shall pluck them out; and if they go up into heaven, from there I will drag them down; if they are hidden on the top of Carmel, from there I will search them out and take them away; and if they plunge themselves away from my eyes into the depth of the sea, there I will command the dragon, and it shall bite them; and if they go into captivity before the face of their enemies, there I will give orders to the sword, and it shall kill them; and I will fix my eyes upon them for evils, and not for good things. And the Lord God Almighty, who touches the earth and shakes it, and all its inhabitants shall mourn, and their consummation shall rise up like a river, and shall go down like the flood of Egypt. He who had said above: There shall be no flight for them; he shall flee, and he who flees shall not be saved, now cuts flight itself into its parts, and testifies hyperbolically that even if they do this and that, they cannot escape. If they go down, he says, even to hell, from there my hand shall bring them out: not that anyone is drawn back from the lower world before the day of resurrection, but that even those placed in hell are in his power. We know that this was fulfilled upon Dathan and Abiram, whom the gaping of the earth swallowed alive. And if they go up, he says, even to heaven, from there I will drag them down: because both Enoch and Elijah, caught up with their bodies into heaven, are governed by the will of God. And observe the proper use of the words: one goes down to hell, one climbs up to heaven; we are brought out of hell, we are dragged down from the heavens. In the one there is extreme despair, in the other the greatness of pride.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.3":["If they are hidden on the top of Carmel, either by the borders of Phoenicia in the northern region, or toward the south on account of the vastness of the desert, where Nabal of Carmel once lived, from there, he says, I will search them out and take them away; and if in the depth of the sea, and, to speak of the prophet, in Tarshish, they try to avoid the eyes of God, there I will command the serpent, by which in this place he signifies Leviathan, or the sea-monster, so as to keep the metaphor, and to come to the enemies by way of the serpent and the sea-monster. And it shall bite them, that is, it shall devour them with its jaws.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.4":["And lest perhaps, from what he said above, we should understand anything other than captivity at the hands of the enemy, Scripture, after its custom, has disclosed what it had said before under riddles. And if they go into captivity before their enemies, there I will command the sword, and it shall slay them: so that, namely, they may not think servitude the extremity of their evils, but the enemy's blade may pierce them even as captives; and those who have been able to survive and escape death may not escape the gaze of God, but he will set his eyes upon them for evil and not for good, so that he may always visit them and drive them to repentance through torments.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.5":["And Almighty God says that he will do these things, at whose touch and nod the foundations of the earth are shaken, or melt, and every inhabitant of it is taken up with lamentation and mourning. He says these things to show the greatness of the divine power, lest perhaps he should indeed wish to do what he threatens, but his strength should not fulfil his will. But if the earth melts, or is shaken, and even insensible nature feels its Creator, how much more man, a fragile creature, the treasure of whose soul is shut up in earthen and bodily vessels? And as the river of Egypt runs down into the sea and is swallowed up, so also the land of Israel, of which he had said above: Who touches the earth and it melts, will go into captivity and will be devoured by the enemies; μετωνυμικῶς (by metonymy), the divine word signifying by that which holds, that which is held.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.6":["He who builds his ascent in heaven, and has founded his bundle upon the earth; who calls the waters of the sea, and pours them out upon the face of the earth: the Lord is his name. LXX: He who builds his ascent in heaven, and founds his promise upon the earth; who calls the water of the sea, and pours it out upon the face of the earth: the Lord Almighty is his name. The Lord God Almighty, who looks upon, or touches, the earth and shakes it, is himself the one who daily builds his ascent into heaven, and says in the Gospel: My Father works until now, and I work. And not only did he once build Eve from the rib of Adam as a type of the Church, but daily he builds believers and the members of his body, and lifts them from the earth to heaven, so that in them he himself may climb up. The Lord ascended into heaven with Enoch, he ascended with Elijah, he ascended with Moses, the place of whose burial could not be found on earth, because he had ascended into heaven. He ascended with Paul, who, a vessel of election, was changed from a persecutor into an apostle, and was caught up from lowly things to the heights, so that he ascended into the third heaven, and through the Holy Spirit and the Son came to the Father, and heard the unspeakable words of the mystery of the Trinity, which it is not lawful for men to hear. He, therefore, who daily ascends in the saints, has founded his bundle upon the earth, of which he speaks in the Gospel: Fear not, little flock, for it has pleased my Father to dwell in you. This bundle is bound tight by the one religion of the Lord. Hence religion itself has taken its name from binding fast, and from tying into the Lord's bundle. Moreover, according to the Seventy, he founds his promise upon the earth, so that all his promises, which the holy prophets sang with their mouths, may not have an empty sound and the hollow names of tropology alone, but may be founded on the earth. And when they have the foundations of history, then let them receive the summit of spiritual understanding: so that Christ was truly born of a virgin, truly raised the dead Lazarus, at his touch the αἱμοῤῤοῦσα (the woman with the issue of blood) was truly healed, at the coming of the Lord the blind truly saw, the lame ran, withered hands were stretched out, the leprosy was cleansed; although according to tropology the divine word is daily born of a virgin soul; daily those dead in sin and bound with the cords of vices are bidden to come out of the tomb of their crimes; daily the works of blood are stanched; the blind look upon the light in faithfulness to Christ; those who were lame before in faith run in the way of the Lord; and hands withered by avarice are stretched out to almsgiving; and the leprosy of Miriam, which defiles whatever it has touched, recovers its former purity. But this Lord also calls the most bitter waters of the sea, and has poured them out upon those who have turned their face to the Lord. And he calls the bitter waters for this reason: to make them sweet, and to bring forth the winds from his treasuries, and by his command to hang aloft the waters heavy with salt, and, straining them and boiling them with the heat of the upper air, to dispense them in rains and send them out upon the face of the earth, so that all dry places may be watered with showers, and where sin abounded, grace may abound the more.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.7-10":["The whole understanding of this passage which I have set forth, and of everything contained in this section, I will sum up in a brief discourse, up to the place where it is written: Who say, Evil will not draw near, and will not come upon us, so that we may be able to know what is said: I, the Lord God Almighty, from whom nothing can be hidden, who touch the earth and it shall be moved; who build my ascent in heaven; who call the waters of the sea and pour them out upon the face of the earth; whose name is the Lord; I who am the creator of all, formed all the nations from the same clay and begot them with an equal lot. In short, I have joined together the Ethiopians, the Philistines, the Syrians and the Jews, far apart as they are in places and in bodies, by the law of mortality, and at my will I move my servants hither and thither, and transfer them into all the provinces. Do not be lifted up into pride because I brought you out of Egypt and, as my peculiar people, did not allow you to serve Pharaoh; this same thing I did also for the Philistines, whom the Seventy translated 'foreigners', who in Hebrew are called caphthorim: I transferred them from Cappadocia and settled them in the regions of Palestine; the Syrians also, that is, Aram, I transferred from Cyrene; and so those who were made in an equal condition will be punished by an equal sentence of my judgment, and I will overturn all impious kingdoms, without respect of persons. But you, O sons of Israel, of whom I said: Israel is my firstborn son, and: Out of Egypt I called my son: I will strike you with the rod and will visit your sins; but I will not crush you for ever, and I will not take my mercy away from you; and, as though you were shaken and tossed in a sieve, I will cleanse and choose you, so that whoever is a little stone, and strengthened through repentance, may not fall out of my sieve, but whoever falls to the ground like dust may be struck with the sword; and the sinners of my people shall die, not because they sinned before, but because they persevered in their sins until death.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.7":["Are you not like the sons of the Ethiopians to me, sons of Israel, says the Lord? Did I not make Israel go up from the land of Egypt, and the Philistines from Cappadocia, and the Syrians from Cyrene? Behold, the eyes of the Lord God are upon the sinning kingdom, and I will crush it from the face of the earth; nevertheless, crushing, I will not crush the house of Jacob, says the Lord. LXX: Are you not like the sons of the Ethiopians to me, sons of Israel, says the Lord? Did I not bring Israel out of the land of Egypt, and the foreigners from Cappadocia, and the Syrians from the pit? Behold, the eyes of the Lord God are upon the kingdom of sinners, and I will take it away from the face of the earth. Nevertheless I will not take away the house of Jacob to the end, says the Lord. For Cyrene, Aquila and the Fifth edition put the Hebrew word itself, cir; the Seventy 'pit', that is, βόθρον; Theodotion 'wall'; Symmachus 'Cyrene', whom we too have followed in this place.\n— Commentary on Amos","But everyone who is Israel, who once saw God with his mind and was brought out of Egypt, if, desiring the vices of Egypt and the pleasures of the world, he returns not only into Egypt but into Ethiopia, in which whoever is, according to Jeremiah, cannot change his skin, is saved by the coming of Christ, and there is fulfilled in him what is written: Ethiopia shall hasten its hands to God. And when he has worshipped him, it will be said of him: Before him the Ethiopians shall fall down. And when they have fallen down, and have brought victims to the Lord from there, from beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, they will be able to say: I am black and beautiful, because the sun has discolored me. For bodies that languish in shade and idleness cannot bear the temptations and the heats of the world; but those which are made ready for wrestling and for contests, dried by the sun, overcome the injuries of the world and obtain the blessing of the Holy Spirit, who says to the righteous man: The sun shall not burn you by day, nor the moon by night. The Ethiopians, therefore, are turned into sons of God if they repent, and the sons of God pass over into Ethiopians if they come into the depth of sins. For God himself, the Creator of all, turns Cappadocians into Philistines, and those who dwelt in the cold of faith and lay exposed to the North, the harshest wind, he made to fall drinking, and, laying aside their pride, to feel the judgment of the Lord. The Syrians also, that is, the lofty and sublime, who are called Aram, he transferred from Cyrene, the weak wall, or the place which overhung the most bitter sea and was near the Syrtes; and what, according to the LXX, was held in the pit, he changed for the good, so as to make them lofty.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.8":["For the eyes of the Lord are upon all the sinning kingdoms, which the devil showed to the Lord, and of which the Apostle says: Let not sin reign in your mortal body, so that you obey its desires. Nevertheless, although he visits sinners with the rod, and crushes and strikes those who have remained in sin, he does not allow to perish for ever those souls which are called the house of Jacob, and supplant sin, and overcome in the contest, and seize the heel of the bloodstained brother.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.9-10":["So too the Lord has scattered the wretched house of Israel through the whole world, and shaken it in a sieve, and winnowed it with a shovel; and while the pebbles and little stones do not fall upon the earth, those will die by the sword who, on account of their filth and dust, are called the sinners of the people. And they will suffer this because they do not believe the prophecies of the prophets, nor think that the things which the Lord threatens through them will come. And though they promise themselves prosperity, they will afterwards endure evils; while, on the other side, the saints fear and do not sin, and therefore by no means die by the sword, because they said: Evils will draw near to us, and the punishments which our sins have deserved will come upon us; of which God speaks more fully in Jeremiah: I will take it up and speak against a nation and a kingdom, to take it away and destroy it; if that nation turns from its evils, I too will repent of the things that I had thought to do to it. God, therefore, is not changed, who is always immutable; but we change him by our conversion. He rages, he is angry, he threatens, and says that he will inflict punishments: If we repent, he too will repent of his sentence. Again, according to the same Jeremiah, he promises prosperity: If we grow slack through negligence, he too will repent of his pledge, and will change what he promised. Of this we can have an example in the Ninevites and in Jerusalem, of whom the one were set free from the punishments that hung over them, the other lost what had been promised to their fathers.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.9":["For behold, I will command, and I will shake the house of Israel among all the nations, as it is shaken in a sieve, and not a pebble shall fall upon the earth. All the sinners of my people shall die by the sword, who say: It will not draw near, and evil will not come upon us. LXX: For behold, I give command, and I will winnow the house of Israel among all the nations, as it is winnowed with a winnowing fan, and no crushing shall fall upon the earth. All the sinners of my people shall die by the sword, who say: It will not draw near, nor will evils come upon us. God, who has measured the waters with his hand, and the heaven with his palm, and encloses the whole earth in his fist, he himself, as befits his greatness, holding the edges of the earth with both hands, will shake it hither and thither like a sieve; so that, while the chaff and the filth of sinners fall to the earth, the pure grain may remain, to be stored in the granaries; or, as the LXX translated, he will hold the winnowing fan in his hand, and will cleanse his threshing floor, and will store the wheat in the granaries, but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire; of which he speaks through Jeremiah: What has the chaff to do with the grain, says the Lord? The same thing, under the figure of another parable, is shown by that net which is cast into the sea of this world and draws out fish of every kind, and, when the bad fish have been thrown away, only the good are kept.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.10":["This is the sword, and this is the broadsword and the sword (framea), of which the Lord also speaks to the Father in Psalm 21 (22): Deliver my soul from the sword and my only one from the hand of the dog. Of this broadsword it is also written in Amos the prophet: All the sinners of the earth shall die by the sword. This cannot stand at all according to the letter, for many sinners perish by shipwreck, others by poison; these are choked by water, those a fire consumes. But by this sword and this broadsword all sinners perish, and the Ethiopians of black color too, of whom Zephaniah testifies with his sacred mouth, saying: But you also, Ethiopians, shall be slain by my sword.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"AMO.9.11-12":["In that day I will raise up the tabernacle of David that has fallen, and I will rebuild the openings of its walls, and what had collapsed I will restore, and I will rebuild it as in the days of old, that they may possess the remnant of Idumea, and all the nations, because my name has been invoked upon them, says the Lord who does these things. LXX: In that day I will raise up the tabernacle of David that had fallen, and I will rebuild what had collapsed, and what has been undermined I will raise up, and I will restore them as the days of old, that the rest of men may seek me, and all the Gentiles upon whom my name has been invoked, says the Lord who does all these things. Where the authority of the apostles goes before, above all of Peter and James, whom the vessel of election calls pillars of the Church, there every suspicion of a differing explanation must be removed, and what is expounded by such great men, that must be followed. In the Acts of the Apostles, when a question had been raised among the apostles as to why Paul and Barnabas had received men from the Gentiles without circumcision and the observance of the sabbath, Peter answered as he ought; and James, approving his judgment, spoke thus: Men, brothers, hear me: Simon has related how God first visited to take from the Gentiles a people, for his name, and with this the words of the prophets agree; as it is written: After these things I will return, and I will build the tabernacle of David that has fallen, and its ruins I will rebuild, and I will set it up; that the rest of men may seek the Lord, and all the Gentiles upon whom my name has been invoked, says the Lord who does these things; known to the Lord from eternity is his work. This tabernacle of David, then, which had fallen — and it had fallen in those who say: It will not draw near, and evil will not come upon us, whom the Lord had shaken in his sieve and tested, and whose threshing floor he had cleansed with the winnowing fan of his majesty, and whoever had been sinners he killed with the sword —, he now says, according to the custom of the Scriptures, promising prosperous and joyful things after torments, after punishments, that he will raise up, and that in the resurrection of the Lord he will restore all things, so that what had fallen in the synagogues may rise in the churches, and believers may possess the remnant of Idumea, and all the nations; so that whatever is left of the bloodthirsty and earthly kingdom may be changed into heavenly kingdoms, and all the Gentiles who have forgotten the Lord may be converted and return to him.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.11":["And, not to put off with a long discourse the rule for these promises: both in this prophet and in the others, whatever is proclaimed about the building of Jerusalem and of the temple, and about the blessedness of all things, the Jews promise themselves with a vain expectation for the end of time, and they speak of it as to be fulfilled carnally. But we, who follow not the letter that kills but the spirit that gives life, prove that these things are already accomplished in the Church, and are fulfilled daily in individuals, who, falling through sin, are rebuilt through repentance.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.12":["But if we choose to read according to the LXX: That the rest of men may seek me, and all the Gentiles upon whom my name has been invoked, we ought to take as the rest of men those who believed from the Jewish people, and who, like little stones, did not fall through the sieve, nor were cast out with the dust and the chaff and the filth. For a remnant will be saved at the beginning of the faith, and at the end of the world, so that when the fullness of the Gentiles has come in, then all Israel may be saved. And the name of the Lord which is invoked upon the remnant and upon all the Gentiles is that of which the Savior says: Father, I have revealed your name to men.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.13":["Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and the ploughman shall overtake the reaper, and the treader of the grape him who sows the seed. And the mountains shall drip sweetness and all the hills shall be tilled. And I will turn back the captivity of my people Israel, and they shall build the deserted cities, and they shall dwell in them, and they shall plant vineyards, and they shall drink their wine, and they shall make gardens, and they shall eat their fruits. And I will plant them upon their own soil, and I will not uproot them any more from their land which I have given them, says the Lord your God. LXX: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and the threshing shall overtake the vintage, and the grape shall change its colour at the sowing. And the mountains shall drip sweetness, and all the hills shall be planted. And I will turn back the captivity of my people Israel, and they shall build the ruined cities, and they shall dwell in them, and they shall plant vineyards, and they shall drink their wine, and they shall make gardens, and they shall eat their fruit. And they shall be planted upon their own land, and they shall not be torn away any more from their land which I have given them, says the Lord God Almighty. When the tabernacle of David that had fallen has been raised up, and what had slipped and been overturned has been rebuilt, the abundance of all things is described: how those who before going went and wept, carrying their seeds, coming shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves, so that the threshing of the sheaves overtakes the vintage, or the ploughman the reaper, and the grape changes its colour and takes on its hue at the time of sowing. Or, as is truer, the treader of the grape overtakes the sowing, and so all things follow one upon another, so that no day is without grain, wine and joy. In that time the grape will be trodden in full winepresses, and new wine reddened with the blood of Christ and of the martyrs will be poured out, and a treader of the grape of this kind will be the seedbed of the word of God, so that their blood cries out in the world more than the blood of righteous Abel cried out. And whoever by the merit of his virtues has climbed up to the mountain heights will sweat honey, or rather will drip the sweetness of the word of God, of which it is written: Taste and see how sweet the Lord is, and: How sweet are your words to my throat, above honey and the honeycomb to my mouth. And those who are below the mountains, or rather second to the mountains, whom the bridegroom leaps over in the Song of Songs and calls hills, will be planted and will imitate the paradise of God, so that every fruit of teachings hangs in them.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.14":["Then, if anyone is a captive through unbelief, and has not yet believed in the name of the Lord, and is of the remnant of Israel, once his people, he will return to the faith of Christ, so that what he had heard in the prophets he may hold in the Gospel. And these mountains which will drip sweetness, and the hills which have been planted, after the Lord has turned back the captivity of his people Israel, will build cities that were before deserted, and will dwell in them, so that each city that has been built may say: I am a strong city, a city that is besieged, and the one of which the Lord speaks in the Gospel: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden. And in the psalms it is said: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God. They will also plant vineyards with Noah, and drink their wine, and be drunk, and they will hear from the Lord Savior: Drink, my friends, and be drunk. And they will drink the wine which he promised he would drink new with his apostles in the kingdom of the Father. This is the vineyard of Sorek, whose wine we drink daily in the mysteries: Not content with the happiness of these things, they will make gardens for their delight, and will water them, so that no kind of virtue may be lacking in them, they will eat their fruits; for he who has planted and watered will himself also eat.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"AMO.9.15":["And when the mountains and hills have done all these things, dripping sweetness, and building cities, and dwelling in them, planting vineyards, and drinking their wine, making gardens, and eating their fruits, then the Lord will plant the planters themselves upon their own land, of which it is said: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living, and in the Gospel: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land. And after he has planted them, and made them firm with a root driven deep, he will not uproot them any more from the land which he had given them. From this we understand that the Church, until the end of the world, is indeed shaken by persecutions, but can by no means be overthrown; tempted, not overcome. And this will come about because the Lord God Almighty, or the Lord her God, that is, the Church's, has promised that he will do it, he whose promise is a law of nature.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"MIC.1.1":["Micah, on whom I now desire to dictate commentaries, is third in the order of the twelve prophets according to the Seventy translators, sixth according to the Hebrew truth; and he follows the prophet Jonah, who succeeds Obadiah, and so it comes about that Amos is third, and Joel second after Hosea, who is first among all. Therefore, placed as it were in the heart of the volume, he must contain deep mysteries; and the word of God, which always descends to the prophets, descends to Micah as well, which means humility; to Micah of Moresheth, which to this day is a rather small village near Eleutheropolis, a city of Palestine. Moresheth, moreover, in our language means heir. Beautifully, therefore, humility, which is even the foremost among the virtues, is born of the hope of the Lord's inheritance. But humility not of the kind that comes from the consciousness of sins, but that which is placed among the virtues, according to which it is said: Humble yourselves under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation, and: He who humbles himself shall be exalted, and: Before destruction the heart of a man is lifted up, and before glory it is humbled. Hence the Lord too: Learn, he says, from me, for I am meek and humble of heart. Just as, therefore, among us names of good wish, and as it were for an omen of virtue, are given, for example: Victor, Castus, Pius, Probus, and among the Greeks one is called σώφρων (temperate) and εὐσεβὴς (pious), and common nouns are turned into proper names, so among the Hebrews Micah too, and Obadiah and Zechariah and the others like them, are given to children by their parents from the name of a virtue.\n— Commentary on Micah","The word of the Lord which came to Micah of Moresheth in the days of Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, kings of Judah: which he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem. Or according to the Seventy: Concerning the things which he saw concerning Samaria and Jerusalem. The word of the Lord, then, came to Micah after Hosea, Amos and Isaiah, who prophesied under Uzziah. From this we understand that Micah did not prophesy in the times of Uzziah, but in those of his son Jotham, after whom Ahaz reigned, Hezekiah succeeding to the rule of his father Ahaz, under whom the ten tribes were led into captivity by the Assyrians. As far, therefore, as concerns the history, according to the order of the captivity — because first Samaria, the metropolis of Israel, was taken, afterwards Jerusalem, the city of Judah —, the title of the prophecy is set down first concerning Samaria, second concerning Jerusalem. But as far as concerns the mystical understandings, since Samaria is always taken for heresies and Jerusalem for the Church, we say that the word of the Lord comes to Jerusalem and to the coheir of Christ, concerning perverse doctrines, and concerning the Church, if perhaps she has committed any sins, and that it weaves together the order of the whole volume. That Samaria, moreover, and the ten tribes, which were cut off from the stock of David under King Jeroboam, are taken in the person of the heretics, the whole of Scripture indeed bears witness, but above all the prophet Hosea, and this very book, which calls the heretics impious and the churchmen sinners. For it immediately follows: What is the impiety of Jacob? Is it not Samaria? And what is the sin of the house of Judah? Is it not Jerusalem? This, at least, according to the Seventy translators. How it is read in the Hebrew, however, we shall add a little later. But this too, that the heretics always trust in the supposed loftiness of their doctrines and despise the simplicity of the Church, is said in another place: Woe to those who despise Zion and trust in the mountain of Samaria; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. Moreover, Samaria fashioned for herself golden calves out of her own understanding, which had beauty indeed on the surface, but did not have the vital spirit, and she fashioned them in the house of God, which is the meaning of Bethel. For the people of Israel could not accept gods except those that were fashioned out of the Scriptures and in the house of God. But although they call their conventicles Bethel, yet after the fashioning of the idols it ceased to be Bethel, and is called Beth-aven, which means the house of the idol, for which the Seventy translated the house of Ὡν (On). So much for the title; now let us come to the opening of the prophecy, and, while the Lernaean beast rages, let us invoke the coming of the Holy Spirit. You, O Paula and Eustochium, pour out prayers to the Lord the Savior, that envy may not harm me, but that my mind, free, may think only of what it strives to explain, and may not feel the slaps of insults, which the Lord despised in his passion.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.2":["Hear, all you peoples, and let the earth attend, and its fullness, and let the Lord God be a witness to you, the Lord from his holy temple. LXX: Hear, all you peoples, and let the earth listen, and all who are in it, and let the Lord God be for a testimony to you: the Lord from his holy house. Since according to the history the sense is plain, I leave the understanding of it to the prudence of the reader. According to tropology, however, he calls together the peoples to hear, that is, the churches of the whole world, and the earth to listen, because the earthly doctrines of the heretics have been built up in it. That heresies, moreover, are numbered among the works of the flesh, which is always referred to the earth, the Apostle does not pass over in silence in writing to the Galatians, and the Lord in the Gospel shows to the prudent hearer: He who is of the earth speaks of the earth. And to distinguish them, he says again of the churchmen: But he who comes from heaven is above all, and: What he has seen and heard, to this he bears witness. If, however, what he says, Hear, you peoples, because of that saying of the Lord, He who has ears to hear, let him hear, sounds something more than what is brought in afterwards, Let the earth attend, we shall apply to the Church: Hear, all you peoples. But to the heretics, who have taken up an earthly doctrine: Let the earth attend, and all who are in it, so that, if these hear and those attend, they may not suffer the things which the word of the Lord afterwards threatens. And let the Lord be for a testimony to them, or, as it is read in the Hebrew, for a witness, or, as Symmachus more plainly translated it, testifying, and testifying from nowhere else than from his own house, which is the Church, or surely in the Son, that is, in our Lord Jesus Christ, who is truly the temple of God the Father, and from whose mouth the Father speaks, piercing the inward parts and the marrow of those who are willing to attend and to hear.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.3-4":["and the place of the Lord is every one in whom he finds lodging, or certainly the Son is the place of the Father, just as the Father too is the place of the Son, as the Lord Savior says: I in the Father, and the Father in me; Micah too signifies this same thing: Behold, the Lord will go forth from his place, and will come down upon the high places of the earth; and the mountains will be shaken beneath him, and the valleys will melt like wax before the fire, and like water that is carried down a steep place: for when the Lord Savior came down and set out from the Father, the high places of the earth and the mountains were shaken, and the lower parts of the valleys were melted;\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Tropologically, however, the Lord will go forth from his place, by which we can understand either the Son or all the saints. For the Son himself says: I am in the Father, and the Father in me, and of the saints: I will dwell and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. From these, then, he will go forth, not that he abandons them; for the word of the Lord also went forth from the apostles to those who heard, and yet it did not leave them; and places of this kind, that is, those which deserve to have God as their guest, rising as it were with Christ and sitting with him in the heavenly places, are set on high. Hence too he is said to come down to those who cannot hear his teaching on the mountain. And when he has come down, he will not go up upon the lowly and upon those who are placed below, but upon those who are called the high places of the earth, and who, understanding the majesty of the Lord as he comes, will be shaken. And although they are mountains, yet they will tremble at the presence of such a charioteer or rider. But the valleys, that is, souls set into χοϊκοῖς (earthy) bodies and not rising with the heavenly man, will not be able to bear his presence, but whatever is hard in them will be dissolved, and so they will flow, as if waters had no level ground and were borne headlong into the depth.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.3-5":["For behold, the Lord will go forth from his place, and he will come down and tread upon the high places of the earth, and the mountains will be consumed beneath him, and the valleys will be split like wax before the fire, like waters that run down headlong; for the crime of Jacob is all this, and for the sins of the house of Israel. What is the crime of Jacob? Is it not Samaria? And what are the high places of Judah? Are they not Jerusalem? LXX: For behold, the Lord will go forth from his place, and will come down, and will go up upon the high places of the earth, and the mountains will be shaken beneath him, and the valleys will melt like wax before the fire, and like water flowing down a slope; because of the impiety of Jacob are all these things, and because of the sin of the house of Israel. What is the impiety of the house of Jacob? Is it not Samaria? And what is the sin of the house of Judah? Is it not Jerusalem? O Samaria and Jerusalem, hear, and diligently attend to the Lord testifying to you from his temple and foretelling whatever he is going to do. Behold, the Lord will go forth from his place. For he who is meek and kind, and whose nature is clemency, is compelled for your sake to take on the character of cruelty, which he does not have. And he will come down and tread upon the high places of the earth. The descent of God, and the running down of his majesty to lower things, is to tread the earth and to crush all the powerful. The mountains, he says, will be consumed, or will melt, and the valleys beneath him, by which we are to understand princes and peoples. And just as wax does not endure the nearness of fire, and waters are borne headlong in a downward course, so all the pride of the impious, when the Lord comes, will be dissolved and will flow away. All this, moreover, will happen because of the crimes of the ten tribes, which he calls Jacob and Israel, and because of the transgression of Judah, since among the ten tribes Samaria was the metropolis, and in the kingdom of Judah Jerusalem fashioned the idols of the high places; this according to the letter.\n— Commentary on Micah","It can also be understood thus: that because of the impieties of Samaria and the crimes of the Jews the Lord went forth from what was once his place, and said to the Jews: Behold, your house shall be left to you desolate. And he came down from the heavens, and went up upon the high places of the earth, that is, upon those who, believing out of the lowliness of the Gentiles, deserved to be lofty. And the mountains were shaken, the doctrines of the philosophers and lofty kingdoms, and those who remained lowly were consumed and crushed by the coming of the Savior; and as the Church grew and the mountains rose on high, the idols fell down into the depth. The Lord, therefore, went forth from his place, and the Church was built from the Gentiles, so that the mountains might be moved beneath his feet and the depths of the valleys dissolved, because Jacob acted impiously, and Israel sinned, and all the tribes denied the Lord.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.5":["The Lord, therefore, will come, terrible for teaching, that is, for moving the mountains and for dissolving the lowness of the valleys, because Jacob committed impiety, and Israel sin. But the impiety of Jacob is the conventicles of the heretics, which are called Samaria. And the sin of Judah, that is, of him who confesses the Lord, is nothing other than Jerusalem, in which many crimes are found. That the house of Judah, moreover, is referred to Christ, whose Church it is, we have both often said, and let us set down this for the present: Judah, your brothers shall praise you; your hands shall be upon the back of your enemies.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.6-7":["But since we have once understood Samaria as the church of the heretics, which, separated from God, has become a conventicle of the people, the Lord himself threatens that he will make her a watch-hut for fruit, a field and a planting of a vineyard. For it is much better that a useless city be overthrown, and the stones with which it had been built be pulled down headlong, and that it be prepared for an orchard and a planting of vineyards, than that it remain in a most wicked building. For when her foundations have been destroyed and laid bare, — those by which she seemed to conceal her mysteries and to have firm doctrines on which she stood; and when all the idols which seemed to have outward show, and a beauty composed by artful speech, have been cut to pieces by the ministers of God, that is, by churchmen —, then in the place of the most wicked building the various fruits of the Church will spring up, and not only will they spring up, but they will also be guarded, and the vine of Sorek will be planted, from which may come the wine which the Lord promised that he would drink in the kingdom of the Father. And not only will her foundations, which before were pressed down by earth, be opened and brought out into the open, and the idols which she had fashioned for herself be cut to pieces, but the glory too and the riches which she seemed to have from her fornication and her error will be consumed by my fire, of which I said in the Gospel: I came to cast fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn, and they will be burned up and reduced to nothing, because they were not acquired from the truth of doctrines, but from the fornication of the soul, and were collected from error here and there. For the heretics do not have riches coming from a paternal inheritance, but every day they find something to worship, and fashion idols for themselves with an artful hand and a curious mind.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.6":["And I will make Samaria like a heap of stones in the field when a vineyard is planted, and I will pull down her stones into the valley, and I will lay bare her foundations, and all her carved images shall be cut to pieces, and all her wages shall be burned with fire, and all her idols I will lay waste; for they were gathered from the wages of a harlot, and to the wages of a harlot they shall return. For this I will lament and howl, I will go stripped and naked, I will make a lamentation like that of the dragons, and a mourning like that of the ostriches, for her wound is desperate, for it has come even to Judah, and has touched the gate of my people, even to Jerusalem. LXX: And I will make Samaria a watch-hut for fruit in the field, and a planting of a vineyard; and I will pull down her stones into the abyss, and I will lay bare her foundations, and all her carved images shall fall to pieces, and all her wages they shall burn with fire, and all her idols I will lay waste, for from the wages of fornication she gathered, and from the wages of fornication she destroyed. Because of this she shall lament and mourn, she shall go barefoot and naked, she shall make a lamentation like that of the dragons, and a mourning like that of the daughters of the Sirens; for her wound has prevailed, for it has come even to Judah, and has touched even to the gate of my people, even into Jerusalem. According to the order of the sins comes the order of the punishments. Samaria sinned first, and fashioned idols, and worshipped calves in place of the Lord; let her therefore also be the first to perish. I will destroy her when the Assyrians come, and I will make her like a heap of stones when a vineyard is planted, so that she is reduced to mounds. And I will pull down her stones into the valley. For she was situated in the mountains, where Sebaste now is, in which the bones of Saint John the Baptist are also laid to rest. And I will lay bare her foundations. For so great will the ruin be, and so great the overthrow of the city, that not only will the walls and buildings fall, but the foundations too will be laid bare down to the last stone.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.7":["And all her carved images, and all the riches that various kings had brought together into her, will be cut to pieces and burned with fire and reduced to nothing. For the wealth and the manifold furnishings, which had been gathered, as was thought, from the fornication of idols, will be carried off to another harlot, that is, to Nineveh, so that, just as in their own land they committed fornication with the idols which they made, so they may go to another land of idols and of fornication, that is, to the Assyrians. Thus far concerning Samaria.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.8-9":["And since the same wound is to reach Jerusalem — for she too sinned with a like error, forsaking her God and fashioning idols —, for that reason the prophet makes as it were a προσωποποιίαν (personification) of God, and under his own person brings in the feeling of one who laments, and says: For this I will lament and howl, I will go stripped and naked; for I have lost the ten tribes, and I will make a lamentation like that of the dragons, and a mourning like that of the ostriches. For just as dragons resound with a terrible hissing, according to the accounts of those who have written on natural things, at the time when they are overcome by elephants, and as ostriches are forgetful of their eggs and, as though they had not given birth, leave their young in the sand to be trampled by the feet of beasts, of which it is written more fully in Job, so I too shall go about without children, stripped and naked. And I will do this because her wound is desperate, that is, Samaria's. And the same sin, or rather the same punishment of sin, which had overthrown Samaria, will come even to Judah and even to the gate of my city Jerusalem. For as Samaria was overthrown by the Assyrians, so Judah and Jerusalem will be overthrown by the Chaldeans.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.8":["Because of this, when their field has been turned into a watch-hut for fruit and prepared for vineyards, and the stones with which the city had been built have been pulled down into the depth, and their foundations have been laid bare, and all the carved images cut to pieces and burned, and the wages which they promised themselves with vain hope, and whatever they seemed to worship as God, reduced to nothing, because they had procured every reward for themselves from the fornication of the soul, then, understanding their former error and returning to themselves, they will lament over the things at which they used to laugh, and they will mourn over the things in which, committing fornication, they formerly in some way rejoiced. And they will put off from their feet whatever deadly thing they had, and they will be barefoot, because the ground on which they are to stand is a holy place, and they will cast away all the garments of their fornication; and they will be naked, so that they may be clothed with the garment of Christ, and they will make a lamentation like that of the dragons. For the dragons too will one day lament, when they see the greatest dragon captured and hanging on the fisherman's hook, and the sea made desolate. And they will mourn like the daughters of the Sirens, for the songs of the heretics are sweet, and deceive the peoples with a pleasant voice. Nor can anyone pass by their songs unless he has stopped his ear and escaped as if deaf.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.9":["And this Samaria of such a kind will lament and mourn for this reason: because, wounded by the arrow of the Lord and receiving the wound of the word, she will recognize her error. And not only did she herself sin, but she also wished to bring her iniquity and her error into the gates of Judah. Hence it is said of her: It has come even to Judah, and has touched even to the gates of my people, even to Jerusalem. She touched the gates, he says, by which we understand the ears. But she could not enter the middle of the city; for if she had entered, she would have made Jerusalem a Samaria. Whenever we see some in the Church scandalized at the discourses of the heretics, and asking how to answer their questions, and yet not withdrawing from the Church, let us say: Samaria, or the wound of Samaria, has come even to the confessing people, even to the ears of the people of God, and even to the ears of Jerusalem. For what is said, it has touched even to the gates of my people, is to be understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (in common), so that this is understood as well: it has touched even to the gates of Jerusalem. Thus far against Samaria and against Jerusalem; let us look also at the rest that follows.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.10":["In Gath do not announce it, do not weep with tears; in the house of dust sprinkle yourselves with dust; pass over for yourselves, dwelling of Shaphir, confounded with shame; she who dwells in Zaanan has not gone out. The house of Ezel will receive mourning from you, the house that stood by itself, for she who dwells in Maroth has grown weak unto good; for evil has come down from the Lord to the gate of Jerusalem. The tumult of the chariot of astonishment for the one dwelling in Lachish is the beginning of sin for the daughter of Zion, for in you were found the crimes of Israel. Therefore he will give emissaries upon the inheritance of Gath; the house of lying, for a deception to the kings of Israel. Still I will bring an heir to you who dwell in Mareshah; the glory of Israel will come as far as Adullam. LXX: You who are in Gath, do not be magnified; you who are in Bachim, do not build out of a house of mockeries; sprinkle earth on your derision, you who inhabit its cities well; the inhabitress of Zaanan has not gone out. Mourn for the house next to her; she will receive from you a blow of pain, who began unto good, she who dwells in pains? For evils have come down from the Lord upon the gates of Jerusalem. The sound of chariots and horsemen, the dwelling of Lachish, is the chief of sin to the daughter of Zion, for in you were found the iniquities of Israel. Therefore he will give emissaries as far as the inheritance of Gath; vain houses, they were in vain to the kings of Israel, until I bring heirs to you, you who dwell in Lachish; the inheritance will come as far as Adullam. The Hebrew disagrees greatly with the translation of the LXX, and both my translation and theirs are wrapped in such great difficulties that, if ever we have needed the Spirit of God, and indeed in expounding his holy Scriptures we always need his coming, now most of all we long for him to be present, and to unfold what he spoke in the prophets, so that what he himself deigns to promise elsewhere may be understood of us too: Open your mouth, and I will fill it. Gath, as the history of Kingdoms also testifies, is one of the five cities of Philistia, near the border of Judea, and for those going from Eleutheropolis to Gaza it is even now a very large village; from there too came that Goliath of Gath whom David killed in battle. Since, therefore, the prophet, or rather the Lord through the prophet, had said: I will mourn and howl, I will go despoiled and naked; I will make a lamentation like that of dragons, and mourning like that of ostriches, for the wound of Samaria is desperate and incurable, and it has come even to Judah and has touched the gate of my people, Jerusalem, for this reason I still command with the voice of one weeping: Do not announce it in Gath, lest the enemies hear and rejoice; do not weep with tears, that is, let not even your grief burst out into sobs; hide your weeping, lest your adversaries exult; let your faces have no tears, though your breast have grief; do not go outside, but in the house of dust sprinkle yourselves with the crumbling ash of the ruins.\n— Commentary on Micah","Gath means winepress; those, therefore, who are in Gath, that is, in the winepress, thinking that they have gathered the vintage of the fruit of life and trodden the cluster from the vine of Sorek, are lifted up into pride, not knowing that the cluster of the land of Judea is not found in the territories of the Philistines. Do not, he says, be lifted up, you who are in the winepresses. For your vine is of the vine of Sodom, and your shoot of Gomorrah, and your grape is of bitterness, and you have a cluster of gall, and the fury of dragons is your wine, the incurable fury of asps. For even if you should bear fruit — since your vintage is not only of Sodom and Gomorrah, but also of Egypt and the other hostile nations —, the Lord will give your fruit to the blight, and your labors to the locust, and will kill your vineyards with hail, and your mulberry trees with frost. Let not the likeness of wine deceive you, and do not call the bitter sweet; taste your wine more carefully, and you will find, in place of the wine of Sorek, the fury of dragons and the venom of asps. Therefore do not be proud, but rather humble yourselves under the mighty hand of the Lord, and pass over to that winepress of which he who comes up from Edom, and ruddy from Bozrah, speaks in Isaiah the prophet: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there is no man with me. Again, since there are others too of foreign race, for the province of the Philistines has many regions and cities, who through evil works and thoughts contrary to God say: We have been destroyed, but let us return and build up what has been destroyed. Hence they are also called the borders of impiety, and the people with whom the Lord is angry. To them it is said: You who are in Bachim, do not build from a house of mockeries. Bachim in our language signifies mourning and weeping. In short, except for the Seventy, all translated κλαυθμὸν, that is, weeping. You, therefore, who are engaged in works and opinions of this kind, which are worthy of weeping, do not renew a most wicked building; and do not suppose your own understanding to be God's edifice, nor build upon sand, lest, when the storm comes and your house falls, the empty labor offer laughter to those who see it. Rather, understanding that your building is worthy of derision, sprinkle your heads with its ruins and dust, and repent, because you wished without counsel to build a house that would fall.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.11":["Pass over for yourselves, dwelling of Shaphir, which in the Syriac and Hebrew language means beautiful. For Samaria is shown even now, situated in the most beautiful and most fertile place of Judea. It is said to her, therefore: O you who dwell in a most fertile region, since you are confounded with shame, so pass over, so be led away into captivity, that because of the greatness of your evils not even your neighbor the Philistine may hear your voice. Moreover, what follows: She who dwells in Zaanan has not gone out, which means going out, or as Symmachus renders: The abundant dwelling has not gone out, is said of the same Samaria, which is at the very doors of the Assyrian captivity, and as soon as she is moved from her borders, enters hostile land. For the abundant dwelling is to be taken in the sense of what we have already said above, the beautiful dwelling. She, therefore, who dwelt in the going out, or in abundance, did not go out of her own will, but was led by force to the Assyrians. Hence the neighboring house, at the side, which is what Ezel means, namely the kingdom of Judah, will receive mourning from you, she who now for the time being, Samaria having been captured, stood firm and had God as her defender.\n— Commentary on Micah","There follows: She who dwells well in her cities has not gone out, dwelling in Zaanan. This seems to me to have this meaning: You who are proud in Gath and in Bachim, in vain do you try to build a house worthy of laughter; sprinkle yourselves with dust and repent, because you wished to press out a detestable wine and to construct a building contrary to God. But the Church of Christ, which dwells well, and, possessing churches in the whole world, is joined by the unity of the Spirit, and has the cities of the law, of the prophets and of the Gospel, and of the apostles, has not gone out of her borders, that is, out of the holy Scriptures, but keeps the possession she entered upon, because she dwells in Zaanan, which Symmachus, as we said above, rendered abundance; for she has the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, with whom are all spiritual graces and abundance of virtues. Hence it is said to her: Let peace be in your strength, and abundance in your towers. But you who dwell beside Zaanan, that is, beside the abundant Church, O you heretics, O contrary doctrines, mourn for yourselves, because you have built yourselves a house for derision, and have pressed the winepress into pride, and have built your house not in the Scriptures but in the neighborhood of the Scriptures, a house now worthy not of laughter but of mourning and tears. Hence it is also added: mourn for the house which is beside her, that is, beside Zaanan. But what is added, She will receive from you a blow of pain, is said to the same heretics who are commanded to mourn for the house neighboring the Church, because the enemy and avenger, the devil, to whom they are to be handed over for punishment, himself inflicts blows on them, and for their most wicked building wrings out of them the pain of the blows. These are inflicted for this reason, that, perceiving the sins they had committed, they may repent, and the dwelling of pains may be for them an occasion of good things. Or else the pain of the Church is indicated, because she mourns her one-time sons, and she herself is the cause of the heretics' salvation, if they are willing to return to their grieving mother.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.12":["And she received mourning, and was struck with fear and weakened in her good, she who dwells in Maroth, that is, in bitternesses; or as Symmachus renders: The dwelling provoking to bitterness, that is, ἡ κατοικία ἡ παραπικραίνουσα, which in Hebrew is called iosebeth maroth, on account of the captivity of the neighboring tribes, because evil came down from the Lord to the gate of Jerusalem; for the Assyrian, after laying waste Samaria, came also to Jerusalem, at the time when Rabshakeh was sent, hurling insults, about whom both the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) and Isaiah write more fully. There it is said that the Assyrian king sent from Lachish to Jerusalem, and afterwards, Lachish having been taken, passed on to attack Libnah.\n— Commentary on Micah","Moreover, as to what is brought in, For evils have come down from the Lord upon the gates of Jerusalem, the Marcionites and the Manichaeans too use this text, on the ground that the God of the law is the author of evils. Let us say that evils came down from the Lord in the way that the Savior also speaks in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven. For as there Lucifer, who rose in the morning, fell from heaven and was crushed upon the earth, he who used to send to the nations, so also these evils, which fell from the Lord and came to the gates of Jerusalem, were not evils before they fell, but because they fell headlong from the Lord, for that reason they became evils. And that we may know the snares of the evils: They came, he says, to the gates of Jerusalem, which, because they are strong and adamantine, are shut by the apostles, to whom the keys of Jerusalem have been entrusted; before them the enemies roam about, and whomever they see going out, they kill. If anyone, therefore, is of Jerusalem, which dwells well in her cities, he does not go out, since he dwells in abundance; but he remains always within, and does not go out of her gates, for whoever goes out of them is killed; and he is killed by those who lift themselves up in Gath, and dwell in Bachim, and build a house for derision. In short, the evils which came down from the Lord to the gates of Jerusalem have only the din of chariots and the tumult of horses, and a confused sound before the doors of Jerusalem, so that with the lance of their mouth they may kill whomever they see wandering.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.13":["Therefore, O Lachish, city given over to idols, the chariot and cavalry of the Assyrians will come to you too, because in you also were found the crimes of Israel, and you were the beginning of idolatry in Judah; for through you, as through a gate, the impiety of the ten tribes migrated into Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Micah","After this it is written: You who dwell in Lachish: she is the chief of sin for the daughter of Zion. Lachish is interpreted πορεία ἐστὶν, that is, walking. And so these too, who went up in their chariots, and had horsemen, and only din and empty sound, to whom she who dwells well in her cities said, These in chariots and these on horses, but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God; because they moved their feet, and were carried about by every wind of false doctrine, and wished to go out from the Church, which is interpreted watchtower, that is, Zion, were the chiefs of sin for the daughter of Zion. And in her, that is, in Lachish, were found the impieties of Israel, which separated the people of God from its former kingdom. She, then, who dwells in Lachish, that is, in a most evil walking, with a step ever wavering, is the chief of sin for the daughter of Zion; and the impiety of Israel is in those who always move their feet and are said to dwell in Lachish.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.14-15":["And not only upon Lachish will the tumult and the chariots come, but also upon Gath, the chief city of Philistia, of which I had said above: In Gath do not announce it; for the Assyrian will send his brigands, whom he calls emissaries, and will possess the house of idolatry and the city of lying, which was a stumbling to the kings of Israel. As for what follows: Still I will bring an heir to you who dwell in Mareshah, he has alluded beautifully to the name; for since Mareshah means inheritance, he called the coming of the enemies into her heirs; and Mareshah, that is, the inheritance, will come as far as Adullam, a city of Judah, which Adullam is glorious among the cities of Israel. In short, Symmachus too translated thus: Ἔτι κληρονόμον ἄξω καί σοι κατοικία Μαρέσα, ἕως Ὀδολλὰμ ἥξει τῆς δόξης Ισραὴλ, that is: Still I will bring an heir to you too, dwelling of Mareshah; as far as Adullam will come of the glory of Israel; that is, you who are glorious among the cities of Israel. And where gloriae is said, let it be the genitive case of the singular number, huius gloriae, of this glory, and not the nominative plural, hae gloriae, these glories. Or certainly let us understand it thus: The captivity of Israel which came to Lachish, and Gath, and Mareshah, will come as far as Adullam too. And of the glory of Israel should be read more pointedly, so that κατὰ ἀντίφρασιν (by antiphrasis) ignominy or devastation is meant. Moreover, as to our having interpreted above the city of the prophet, Moresheth, as inheritance, let the reader know that in the same little verse which we set down, Therefore he will give emissaries upon the inheritance of Gath, in the Hebrew, maraseth Geth is written for the inheritance of Gath. Thus far, according to the Hebrew, as best we could and as it seemed to us, certainly as we heard it from the Hebrews, we have steered our little boat as though among rocks and very sharp crags; whether it has entered port or still tosses on the open sea, it will be for the reader's prudence to judge. Now, by your prayers, let us go on to other waves, and, with shipwreck of the exposition threatening on this side and on that, let us escape if we can.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.14":["Emissaries too will be given as far as the inheritance of Gath: Gath most wicked, and a winepress of poisons, which is built against the house of God, where there are vain houses, which were built for derision. And these vain houses were made in vain for the kings of Israel: as far as the history is concerned, for those kings whose sins are written in the books of Kingdoms and of Chronicles; but as far as anagogy is concerned, for the chiefs of the heretics and the leaders of perverse doctrines; for to them the houses are vain and built in vain. And they last only so long, until the heirs who are to be brought in by the Lord hold them.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.15":["After this there follows: You who dwell in Lachish, the inheritance will come as far as Adullam. Adullam means testimony of the draught, or of their drawing, which in Greek is expressed more significantly, μαρτυρία ἀντλήσεως αὐτῶν. We read in Proverbs: If you are evil, you alone will draw up evils. She, therefore, who dwells in Lachish, that is, on a most evil road, will come to the testimony of her own draining, which she will drain and drink down according to the measure of her works. Or certainly it should be punctuated so that what is said, you who dwell in Lachish, is referred to what precedes, and the order and sense is: I will bring heirs to you from the Church, you who dwell in Lachish, because you too will be of the inheritance of the Lord, when you have drained and received what you deserve. At the end of this section I beseech the reader not to take necessity for choice, nor to judge a lengthy exposition to be verbosity, and to marvel rather that in places so rough I find anything at all, than that I leave out none of the things that have to be said.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.1.16":["Make yourself bald and be shorn over the sons of your delights; enlarge your baldness like the eagle, for they have been led captive from you. LXX: Glory of the daughter of Israel, make yourself bald and be shorn over your delicate sons; enlarge your baldness like the eagle, for they have been led captive from you. What was said by the Seventy, glory of the daughter of Israel, with their addition of the daughter, the Hebrews read at the end of the preceding section. But since you wished it so, and we have once undertaken it, the necessity lies upon us of interpreting the Scriptures as they are read in the Church, and nonetheless of not omitting the Hebrew truth. It is said, then, to Israel, for the present according to the letter, so that we take either the ten tribes in Samaria, or all Israel in common, that, since the people has been led captive and all Judea laid waste by the Assyrians and the Babylonians, they should take up mourning and weep for their sons. And as the eagle, which is the queen of birds, at a certain time loses its feathers and remains featherless, so let Israel too lay aside all its glory, with which it had formerly been surrounded, and mourn its sons, made subject to the power of enemies. That the eagle is accustomed to lose its plumage at a certain time is written both in the Psalter, Your old age will be renewed like the eagle's, and by the Comic poet in the Heautontimorumenos: She seemed, he says, truly, as the saying goes, an eagle's old age. But if we wish to take this same thing also of the present time of the Jewish overthrow, we shall see that all the grace with which they once flourished before God has completely departed from them. For where is the prophet? Where is the teacher of the law? Where the protection of angels? Where the victory hoped for by the few against the many? Jerusalem has been made bald and has lost all the hair of her former glory, and her sons, who cried out against the Lord, Crucify, crucify him, have been led into captivity. I have read in someone's commentaries that what is said, Make yourself bald and be shorn over the sons of your delights, can be taken of the human condition, so that the speech of God is directed to Adam, or to the heavenly Jerusalem: O human soul, O city once mother of the saints, you who were formerly in paradise and enjoyed the delights of diverse trees, and had most beautiful hair, now, because you have been cast down from the heights and led away into Babylon, and have come into the place of captivity, and have lost your hair, shave yourself and put on the garb of a penitent; and you who formerly flew like an eagle on high, mourn your sons, mourn your offspring, which has been led captive out of you.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.1-2":["Woe to you who devise what is useless, and work evil on your beds; in the morning light they do it, for their hand is against God; and they coveted fields and took them by violence, and seized houses, and they wronged a man and his house, a man and his inheritance. Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I devise evil against this family, from which you shall not withdraw your necks, and you shall not walk proudly, for it is a most evil time. In that day a parable shall be taken up over you, and a song shall be sung with sweetness by those who say: We have been laid waste with devastation; the portion of my people has been changed. How will it depart from me, when he returns who divides our lands? Because of this you will have no one casting the cord of the lot in the assembly of the Lord. LXX: They have become men who devise toils and work evils on their beds, and at once in the day they will accomplish them, because they did not lift up their hands to God; and they desired fields, and plundered orphans, and oppressed houses, and seized a man and his house, a man and his inheritance. Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I devise evils against this tribe, from which you shall not withdraw your necks, that you may not walk upright suddenly, for it is a most evil time. In that day a parable shall be taken up over you, and a lament shall be lamented in a song of those who say: In misery we have been made miserable; the portion of my people has been measured out by the cord, and there was no one to forbid him, that he might be turned back. Our fields have been divided; because of this you will have no one casting the cord in the inheritance. What we set at the end of the section according to the Hebrew, in the assembly of the Lord, for which the Seventy translated in the Church of the Lord, is, according to the common edition, the beginning of the following section. And therefore we shall discuss it there, if the Lord commands. Woe, therefore, to you, peoples of the Jews, who both devise evil and complete it in deed. And the beds given you for rest you pollute with debaucheries, and whatever iniquity you plot at night, as if it were not permitted to put it off, you hasten to fulfill as soon as the day has dawned, not considering that your hand is strong against God. And in order that Scripture might teach what it was that they devised at night and did by day, it sets it out part by part. They coveted, he says, fields and took them by violence, and houses, it is understood, they coveted, and those which they had coveted they plundered; and not only did they wrong men and their houses, but they also laid waste with rabid mouth their descendants, who deserved mercy because of their tender age.\n— Commentary on Micah","But this same thing can also be taken of the last captivity, that all things befell them because they crucified the Lord, yet in such a way that it is the edition of the Seventy translators that is expounded. For the glory of the daughter of Israel has been made bald and shorn over her once most delicate sons, and if any hair grew back afterwards, it was cut off in turn by another shaving and shearing. Then all their counsels were turned into toil, and what they devised, with mind and spirit asleep, brought them toils; and what they had done was thrown into confusion as soon as the light of Christ and vengeance appeared. For though they had read that Israel conquered when Moses lifted up his hands to the Lord, and was overcome by Amalek when Moses let his weary arms sink, they did not lift up their hands to the Lord, but, perpetrating every crime against the poor and the people of the Lord, they desired fields, and plundered the houses of orphans, and laid waste alike a man and his wife and his children and his substance.\n— Commentary on Micah","But if we wish to follow the third exposition, of which I spoke above, that the whole discourse is directed to the human soul, which, falling from paradise, has come into the captivity of this world, we shall see that all our thinking is toil and pain, and our beds are full of evils, and the very light that is seen is mixed with darkness, and what we plot at night we accomplish in gloom. For who of us lifts holy hands to God without anger and scheming? Who does not desire the estates of this world, forgetful of the possessions of paradise? You may see some joining field to field and boundary to boundary, and the lands of cities not sufficing for the small little body of a man.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.1-5":["I have expounded, as far as the narrow limits of my slender wit allowed, both the first captivity, by the Assyrians and Babylonians, and the second, by the Romans, because they crucified the Lord, and the third, the spiritual one, by which each one of us fell with Adam from paradise and lives in the captivity of this world; from which, when the Lord comes, he will raise up those who are crushed, and loose those in fetters, and lead back into his own possession those who were once the devil's captives, and the speech of the Psalmist will be fulfilled, who says: Ascending on high, he led captivity captive. According to this exposition, a fourth captivity can also be understood with regard to the Church, from which each one goes out through sin, and afterwards is led back into Jerusalem by Ezra, which means helper, that is, by the word of God. But if anyone, meditating on the law of the Lord day and night, has had greater zeal, greater talent, leisure and grace, and can say something more probable about the present section, I do not envy him, I do not scorn him; rather, I desire to learn from him what I do not know, and I will gladly profess myself his pupil, provided he teaches and does not disparage. For nothing is so easy as for a man at leisure and asleep to dispute about the labors and vigils of others.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.3":["Since, therefore, you have done these things and devised what is useless, I too, the Lord, will devise evil against this family; not that what I devise is evil, but that what I inflict seems evil to those who suffer it; and it will so press upon you that you cannot lift your necks, nor walk proudly, you, namely, whom the time of captivity had oppressed.\n— Commentary on Micah","Therefore the Lord devised evils against that tribe, by no means against the twelve tribes, but against the tribe of malice, befouled in crime, from which they could not lift their neck, nor walk upright. In short, to the present day they are subject to the Roman Empire, and are pressed down by the yoke of captivity, and do not lift their necks. As for what follows, ἐξαίφνης, that is, suddenly, it is not found in the Hebrew scrolls, and yet it can fit the present passage, so that we say: Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I devise evils against this tribe suddenly, from which they cannot lift their necks, and for this reason they will not be able to lift them, because the time is evil. For just as they worked evils against the Lord Jesus, so they will endure the evils of perpetual captivity, and will come into such great distress that all their songs and psalms will be turned into mourning.\n— Commentary on Micah","Therefore the Lord devises evils against us, from which we cannot lift our necks, nor walk upright, for it is a most evil time, according to the words of John, who says: The world is set in wickedness. The same thing is signified also by that daughter of Abraham in the Gospel, a noble soul, whom Satan had bound and bent over, and who could not be raised up on high before the coming of the Lord, nor look up at her Creator. Hence the Savior says: This woman, he says, a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan had bound, ought she not to have been loosed from this bond on the sabbath day?\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.4-5":["Then a parable will be spoken over you, and your miseries will be turned into a song: We have been laid waste with devastation, the portion of my people. My temple, he says, which alone I had more than the other nations, will be changed into a ruin. How will the Assyrian depart from me, when he returns to distribute my fields to himself by lot? Therefore, O family of Israel, against which I devise evil, you will have no part in the inheritance of the just.\n— Commentary on Micah","And the people will know how to say nothing else but this: In misery we have been made miserable. For the land of promise, which had earlier been divided by lot to two and a half tribes beyond the Jordan, with Moses casting the cord, and afterwards apportioned by Joshua son of Nun to the remaining tribes, has been divided among the nations by a Roman surveyor, and there was no one to forbid; rather, though it holds all the nations, there is no one of the Jews who possesses the ancient soil in its former freedom.\n— Commentary on Micah","Since, therefore, all our glory has been shorn, and we have enlarged our shaving, or our widowhood — for so it is found in some codices —, men have been sent to mourn us with Jeremiah, and to take up a parable over us, and to say with the Apostle: I shall mourn many who have sinned and have not repented. For who would not mourn, seeing human souls possessed like assorted household goods by demons and various vices? One demon, that of fornication, casts the cord; another, that of avarice; that one, of murder; this one, of perjury, stretches out the lines. The portion of the people of God has been divided by the cord, and the fields once of holiness and of paradise, with whose fragrance Isaac was delighted in his son Jacob, have been handed over to the Assyrians and to the Babylonian king. And while the foxes have holes and the birds of the air nests, The Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.6-7":["The Church, then, is commanded not to have sadness and anxiety over the affairs of the world and the losses that are wont to happen in this world, and it is said to her inhabitants: O you who are in the Church of the Lord, rejoice always, and whatever befalls you from the judgments of God, give thanks for it. Nor do I say this because you ought not to weep — for Blessed indeed are those who weep, for they shall laugh —, but I warn you of this, that you do not lament over the things that are of the world. If one of your kin has died, if the treasury has seized your property, if gout or any other disease whatever has oppressed your body, do not weep, do not shed tears, and do not consider the present but the future, and be weighed down rather because you dwell longer in this tabernacle of death. Rather beware of this, that you do not insult those who fall, and think another's ruin your own edification; let each one measure himself by his own strength, not by the weakness of another. Otherwise what justice is this, for the branches of the wild olive to insult the branches of the olive, which were broken off because of their unbelief, and to say: The house of Jacob provoked the Spirit of the Lord to anger, killing the prophets, worshipping idols, crucifying the Son of God? He who does this will not be free from reproach, and with the measure with which he judged he will himself be judged, and just as he speaks of the sins of the one who falls, so another will insult his ruin.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.6-8":["So much according to the Hebrew understanding; but the Seventy translators in this place do not cohere with themselves at all. For to what was set first, In the Church of the Lord do not weep, what consequence is there, that there should be added, for he will not cast off the reproaches which he speaks: The house of Jacob has provoked the Spirit of the Lord? And also what follows: If these are his devices, are not his words good with him, and have they walked upright? What bearing on the later part have the words that are said: And formerly my people stood out as an adversary against its own peace; they flayed off its skin, to take away the crushing of war? It seems to me, however, that in a place so very difficult the sense can be rendered or expressed thus, if only the prudent reader will acquiesce in our reasoning.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.6":["Do not speak, speaking; it will not drip upon these, confusion will not overtake them, says the house of Jacob. Is the Spirit of the Lord cut short, or are such his thoughts? Are not my words good with him who walks uprightly? And on the contrary my people has risen up as an adversary; you have taken away the cloak from over the tunic, and those who were passing by in simplicity you have turned into war. LXX: In the Church of the Lord do not weep with tears, nor let them lament over these things? For he will not cast off the reproaches, which he speaks: The house of Jacob has provoked the Spirit of the Lord, if these are his devices; are not his words good with him, and have they walked upright? And formerly my people stood out with enmities against its own peace; they flayed off its skin, to take away hope, the crushing of war. Do not speak, he says, speaking, for which Aquila translated: Do not drip, dripping; at times by a Hebrew idiom calling eloquence a dripping, from the fact that it flows and reaches the ears of those who hear, in the likeness of descending rain. Do not deceive yourselves, O house of Jacob, and say to one another for mutual consolation: God is good, the captivity which we fear will not come.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.7":["Will his generous mercy and his most clement Spirit, which has risen widely and abundantly upon all, be cut short and be severe in us alone? Or are his thoughts of such a kind as we see in men, so that he holds on to an old anger and is roused to vengeance by sudden fury? To this the Lord answered: I am indeed good, and my words sound clemency, but to those who walk on the right road.\n— Commentary on Micah","There follows: If these are his devices: are not his words good with him, and have they walked upright? That the Jewish people stumbled, he says, so that the fullness of the Gentiles might come in, belongs to the dispensation of God, so that afterwards, when Israel believes, all might be saved, and all might stand in need of the mercy of God. Hence the Apostle too, coming to the question of this place, proclaims that the depth of the wisdom and knowledge and of the judgments of God is an unsearchable abyss. If, then, it is God's devices and thoughts that the former branches of the olive should be broken off and others from the wild olive grafted in, you ought not to insult but to fear lest you fall, nor should you suppose that you please God in this, if you read his words, that is, his scriptures. The scriptures profit the reader only if what is read is fulfilled in deed. If, speaking of the scriptures, you can say: Or do you seek a proof of him who speaks in me, Christ? The Lord indeed will give the word to those who preach the gospel with great power, and upon a high mountain has he gone up who preaches the gospel to Zion, and he lifts up his voice in strength who preaches the gospel to Jerusalem. And in this way the words of God are good, if they are with him, that is, if God does not desert the preacher whose heart and lips agree. But he who confesses with his lips while his heart is far from God, and recounts his justices and takes up his covenant in his mouth, and is defiled with the filth of sins, with him the words of God are not good. This is to be said not only of the sinner, but also of him who does not have spiritual grace, that is, the grace of prophecy and of teaching and of interpretation, and of the greater charisms. This must be said, that if a man of this kind wishes to give the causes of the elements and the reason for his faith, and to explain why God, who is good and the Creator of all, came to the Jews alone and called the nations only in the last age, he does not have the good words of God with him, but out of his own unskillfulness defiles even those words of God which are good, words which walk upright and require upright ears. And these things indeed the Lord commands the people that succeeded, and the Church gathered from the Gentiles: not to insult the former generation, and so by insulting not be free from reproaches.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.8":["But he who, I do not say long ago, but yesterday, worshipped idols in insult to me, and, as far as lay in him, seized tyrannical arms against me; who stripped the unhappy people of God's help, and took away the cloak as if from over the tunic; who turned against me in war all the simple among the believers and those who rest in the authority of their elders, will not the Lord drip upon him, and will not confusion seize him? But as for what we have translated, And on the contrary my people has risen up as an adversary, since the word mul signifies both the contrary and yesterday, Symmachus rendered it more plainly, so as to say: One day before, my people stood out as an enemy, in order to resolve the question, namely that God was imputing to the people not past vices but recent ones, and such as seemed to have been committed as if yesterday.\n— Commentary on Micah","And he himself, who is the true judge and speaks without perturbation, relates how in his passion Israel was contrary to him, and did this not because it could harm its Creator, but because it perpetrated everything against its own peace. Hence too the word to Jerusalem: If you knew the things that are for your peace. And with their peace lost, they flayed off their own skin, that is, they took away from themselves the help of God by which they were protected, and left their flesh as it were bare, without skin and covering, so that whatever seemed beautiful while the mercy of God covered it from above, this, when that mercy withdrew, displayed ugliness to those who saw it. And when peace and the help of God withdrew, because they had resisted the Lord, of whom it is said, The Lord who crushes wars, the Lord is his name, they could not resist their adversaries, but were defeated in every contest, nor was there anyone to crush the wars springing up against them, in respect of both persons of the enemies, either the men who led them captive, or the strength of the adversaries who daily slaughtered their souls by blasphemy.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.9-10":["In the Lord I trust; how do you say to my soul: Flee away to the mountains like a sparrow? The crafty adversary both tempted the Lord and Savior in the wilderness, and wishes all the saints to depart from the land of Judah and to dwell in the desert of the virtues, that there he may more easily strike them down. But even his very counsel is deceitful: for he does not exhort that one take the wings of the dove — a bird gentle, simple, and domestic, said to lack gall, and which is offered for the Lord in the temple — but the wings of a sparrow, a bird chattering and roving, which after the rearing of its young knows nothing of the fidelity of wedlock; although Aquila and Symmachus rendered \"bird\" generally in place of \"sparrow.\" By the mountains, however, we can understand those of which it is said in another place, \"Draw near to the eternal mountains,\" and in the second psalm of the steps, \"I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains from whence help shall come to me\": and to which, after the abomination of desolation shall stand in the holy place, we ought to migrate.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"MIC.2.9":["You have cast out the women of my people from the house of their delights; from their little ones you have taken away my praise forever. Arise and go, for you have no rest here; because of its uncleanness it will be corrupted with a most grievous rotting. The translation of the Seventy — if indeed it is the Seventy's; for Josephus writes, and the Hebrews hand down, that only the five books of the law of Moses were translated by them and handed over to King Ptolemy —, differs so much in the present place from the Hebrew truth that we can neither set out the heads side by side nor explain their meanings together. Therefore let our translation be discussed first, and afterwards we shall come to them. The word is still against the people of God, to whom he had already said above: On the contrary my people has risen up as an adversary: from over the tunic you have taken away the cloak; namely that they had not only done this, but had also made the women, that is, the matrons once delicate, go into captivity, or, under a metaphor, the cities of Judea, which in Isaiah too he calls the daughters of Zion, because Zion was their metropolis. From their little ones too, he says, you have taken away my praise forever: no one remained among the people, all being either killed or captured, who sang my psalms; and even the few who survived in Babylon testify that they cannot sing. How, they say, shall we sing the song of the Lord in a foreign land?\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: The leaders of my people will be cast out of the houses of their delights; because of their evil devices they have been cast out. This can be taken both generally of the princes of the Jewish people, the priests and Pharisees, who after the passion of the Lord were cast out of the city of their delights, in which they had formerly wantoned because of their evil devices; and specially of the stock of David, because as soon as the Lord was born, the prince failed from Judah, and the leader from his thighs, when he came for whom it was reserved, and the expectation of the nations appeared. But the prophetic word also describes the princes of the Church who abound in delights and believe that they keep their chastity amid feasts and wantonness: that they are to be cast out of their spacious houses and sumptuous banquets, and dishes sought out with great labor, and cast out because of their evil thoughts and works. And if you wish to know whither they are to be cast out, read the Gospel: Into the darkness namely without, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. Is it not a confusion and a disgrace to preach Jesus crucified, the poor and hungry master, with stuffed bodies, and to set forth the doctrine of fasting with red cheeks and swollen faces? If we are in the place of the apostles, let us not only imitate their speech, but also embrace their way of life and their abstinence. Holy, certainly, is the ministry of the apostles also, to minister to widows and the poor. It is not right, they say, that we should leave the word of God and serve tables. But now the priest of Christ invites to dinner, I do not say the poor, I do not say the brethren and those who cannot invite in return, from whom, apart from the grace, episcopal hands could hope for nothing else, but soldiers and men girt with the sword, and judges, with centurions and squadrons of soldiers keeping watch before their doors. The clergy run about the whole city, seeking to provide for the judges what they either cannot find in their own praetoria or at least do not buy up once found. Nor indeed is it to be thought that this invective is directed generally at everyone, but rather that the prophetic word strikes those who are such, and threatens them with punishments and everlasting darkness, so that those who are not restrained by shame and modesty may repent at least at the threat of penalties.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: Draw near to the eternal mountains. By the eternal mountains we can understand either angels or prophets, of whom it is also written in the psalm: Its foundations are on the holy mountains, and in another place: I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, whence help will come to me. And he draws near to the eternal mountains who is not separated by his sins from the fellowship of the blessed, just as Moses too drew near to God, not in place but in merit. And to those who drew near to the eternal mountains the Lord himself spoke: I am a God who draws near, and not a God from afar. The mountains are called eternal to distinguish them from those which are not eternal, namely the princes of this world, the dark mountains; and when these have been lifted up like the cedar of Lebanon, they pass away with the world, and their place cannot be found.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.10":["Arise, therefore, and go into captivity, for you have no rest in this land, which has been polluted because of your crimes and cannot be cleansed unless it first keeps a long sabbath. And the reason I say to you, you have no rest here, is that your land is polluted and will be corrupted with a most grievous rotting, namely by captivity, either the Babylonian or the Roman, because it drank the blood of the Lord; for it can be understood according to either truth of the history.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: Arise and walk, for there is no rest for you here. We are commanded to think that there is rest for us in none of the things of the world, but, as if rising from the dead, to strive toward the heights, and to walk after the Lord our God, and to say: My soul has cleaved after you. But if we despise this, and refuse to hear him who says, Arise, you who sleep, and be lifted up, and Christ will enlighten you; we shall indeed slumber, but we shall be deceived, and we shall not find rest, for where Christ does not enlighten the one who rises, what seems to be rest is tribulation.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: Because of uncleanness you have been consumed with corruption. For what we have said, you have been consumed, it can, according to the Greek understanding, sound in the Latin language as you have been corrupted, so that the order is: because of uncleanness you have been corrupted with corruption. This is said to those who, serving the pleasure of the body and lust, corrupt not only their soul but their body as well, lovers of pleasures rather than lovers of God. He could also have said, because of uncleanness you have been corrupted, and the sense would have been complete even without with corruption. But now, because he says, You have been corrupted with corruption, he seems to me to have said it to distinguish it from a salutary corruption, of which the Apostle also speaks: And though our man who is outside is corrupted, yet he who is inside is renewed from day to day. He who always carries the dying of Jesus in his body, and corrupts the outer man, and subjects the flesh to the rule of the soul, is indeed corrupted, but not with corruption, because his corruption is salutary.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: You have fled with no one pursuing. This is said to those who because of uncleanness have been corrupted with corruption, that through the consciousness of their sins, even without punishments, they do not dare to resist the enemy and fight. Hence too those who are fearful in battle with the enemy, and would terrify the minds of their brothers, are cast out of the camp and driven from the battle line, and in the curses of Leviticus the word is directed to men of this kind: The sound of a flying leaf will pursue you, and you will flee, with no one pursuing. I know that I have read in the commentaries of a certain man expounding the beginning of John the Evangelist, All things were made through him, and without him was made nothing, that he referred what is said, nothing, to wickedness; and again that he interpreted wickedness itself as the devil, and by this step, as it were, understood the very nothing that was made without Christ to be the devil. If, then, wickedness, or the devil, is nothing, and those who have been corrupted with corruption fled with no one, that is, nothing, pursuing, the devil pursued them in the guise of nothing. But if this seems to anyone too forced, and argued against the simplicity of Scripture by an artifice of eloquence rather than by true interpretation, let him follow either the former exposition or one he has found himself.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.11-12":["LXX: The spirit has set a lie. It has dripped for you into wine and drunkenness, and it will be from the drop of this people; Jacob, to be gathered, will be gathered. It is not, as most suppose, the spirit stood false, but the spirit has set a lie that must be read, which in Greek is said πνεῦμα ἔστησε ψευδὲς, that is, τὸ ψεῦδος (the lie). For just as with putrid wounds, lest a cancer creep on and the dead body feed upon the living flesh, physicians arrest the wound and burn it with a cautery or with a καυστικῷ (caustic) powder, so the Spirit of God put an end to the lie, lest the people of God be any longer subverted by the voices of the false prophets. That spirit, in the New as well as in the Old Testament, wherever it is set down without an addition, is to be understood in a good sense, we have often said, and we shall now set it out in part, lest anyone be in doubt. But the fruit of the spirit is love, joy, peace, and: If we live by the spirit, let us also walk by the spirit, and elsewhere: But if by the spirit you put to death the works of the flesh, you will live, and in the Old Testament: Giving breath to the people which is upon it, and spirit to those who tread upon it; there is no doubt from what precedes that it signifies the earth. For those who tread on earthly works and put them under their feet surely deserve to receive not an evil but a good spirit. On the contrary, an evil spirit is always read with an addition, as there: But when the unclean spirit has gone out of a man, and in another place: He rebuked the unclean spirit, and: An evil spirit seized Saul, and other things like these. The Spirit of God, therefore, who put an end to the lie in the false prophets, will himself drip for you into wine and drunkenness: the wine that gladdens the heart of man, and the drunkenness with which Noah was made drunk, and of which it is said elsewhere: Eat, friends, and be drunk. Yet all this gladness and drunkenness, in comparison with the wisdom of God, which like a river waters the heavenly Jerusalem, is a drop and a very small droplet. Nor will those hesitate to speak of a drop of the wisdom of God in men who have read of the stone cut from the mountain without hands, and that the foolishness of God is wiser than men, and that the apostles themselves too knew in part and prophesied in part. From this wisdom, then, that is, from the drop of the Jewish people — for he came only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel —, Jacob was gathered: everyone who supplants Esau, and snatches away his birthright and blessing, and, before he is born, while still lying in the womb, seizes the heel of his hairy brother.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.11":["Would that I were not a man who has the spirit, and that I rather spoke a lie. I will drip for you into wine and into drunkenness, and this people will be the one upon whom it drips. Gathering I will gather Jacob, all of you; I will bring together into one the remnant of Israel alike. I will set him like a flock in the sheepfold, like cattle in the midst of the folds. They will be in tumult from the multitude of men; for he will go up opening the way before them; they will divide and pass through the gate and go out by it; and their king will pass on before them, and the Lord at their head. In this section too the Seventy translators differ much from the Hebrew. Therefore let us first expound according to what has been handed down to us by the Hebrews, and afterwards, if God wills, we shall treat of their translation. O Jewish people, to whom belong the promises, and whose are the covenants and the law, and from whom is Christ according to the flesh, to whom, when the Babylonians or the Romans were threatening, I said: Arise and go into captivity, since there is no rest for you in this land, which because of its uncleanness will be corrupted by a final devastation, do not think that I speak willingly and proclaim with joy what I see coming; I would wish myself to be anathema for my brothers, who are Israelites. Would that I spoke from my own mind and did not have the Holy Spirit; and that, counted rather among the false prophets, I alone might perish, and the things I say might not be true; and that so great a multitude might believe in the Son of God, and not be handed over to perpetual captivity. But since I am a prophet, and speak by the Spirit of God, and, sent by the Godhead, proclaim the truth, for this reason I will drip my word for you into undiluted wine, which will make you drunk and cause you to fall. And while I drip, and sing of the evil of the captivity to come, this people will receive my rains, that is, willing or not, it must endure what I say.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.12-13":["And lest you think that I am a prophet of evils only: now indeed the foretold captivity will come; but behold, in me speaks the word of God, which comes to all the prophets. When it is silent, the prophet does not speak; and now it says: I will come, and take a human body, I will be born of a virgin. Or thus: Because I came in the humility of the flesh and you did not believe me, I will come at the consummation of the world in my majesty with the angels and the other powers, and then I will gather all of you, Jacob; then I will bring together into one the remnant of Israel, and join them alike with the people of the Gentiles in my sheepfold; then I will hedge you about with a most firm wall, and so great will be the multitude of believers, and the tumult of the flock set among the folds, that the number of the cattle will be overcome by their abundance. And lest perhaps you think, because I said, I will set him like a flock in the sheepfold, and like cattle in the midst of the folds, that I am speaking of sheep, understand that these sheep are men. For there follows: They will be in tumult from the multitude of men. Tumult is the voice of very many, and the shout of an exceedingly great multitude uttered together, so that we may not suppose it to be the voice of one, but the common voice of all, praising the good shepherd, who has leveled every steep place and made it level with his own foot, so that he, the leader of their journey, is himself the door of paradise, and says, I am the gate; and as he divides the way and goes before, being both the way and the gate, the believing flock will pass through him. And this shepherd is king and Lord. Hence there follows: And their king will pass on before them, and the Lord at their head. But if we wish to take all these things of his first coming, and to refer the whole of Jacob and the remnant of Israel to the apostles and to that multitude from the Jews which was saved in the Acts of the Apostles, the expositor's explanation will not be far from the truth. For these the Lord truly gathered into his sheepfold, and set in the midst of the folds, and made a way before them, and led them into the Church, and he was their king before them, and is the Lord at their head forever.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.12":["LXX: With all, receiving I will receive the remnant from Israel. When, he says, the people of the Gentiles has believed, and the whole world has been brought to my faith, and the fullness of the Gentiles has entered, then the remnant of Israel too will be saved; not that remnant of which it is written in the book of Kingdoms, I have left for myself seven thousand men who have not bowed the knee to Baal, and of which Paul speaks, So also at this present time a remnant according to the election of grace has been saved, and of which this prophet testifies above, And it will be from the drop of this people; Jacob, to be gathered, will be gathered, but that remnant which, after all have been received, will be received by God at the end, and of which it is now said, With all, receiving I will receive the remnant of Israel, so that, according to what is written, God, shutting up all under sin, may have mercy on all.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: I will set their turning away like sheep in tribulation. The remnant from Israel, which I am to receive after I have received all, I will now in the meantime, because they have turned away from me, set in tribulation, and straiten, and make to sit without priest, without altar and without prophet, so that him whom they did not perceive through benefits they may understand through punishments.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: Like a flock in the midst of their resting place; I will set is understood. Not only, he says, will their turning away, by which they turned away from me, be set like a flock in tribulation, but after they have been afflicted and have completed the time of distress, they will be set in rest, that is, in their resting place. And then they will migrate from men and transcend the state of the human condition, and will fulfill what follows: They will leap forth from men. And not only will they leap forth and depart, but all to whom the word of God comes, and who, leaving human vices behind, imitate the way of life of the Godhead, and hear: I said, you are gods, and all sons of the Most High, they will leap forth from men, and will be borne, as it were, to heaven.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.2.13":["LXX: Go up through the division. The present place has as it were its own exordium and an apostrophe of prophetic speech to him who desires to be saved, who is commanded to go up through the division. This indeed will become clearer if we take an example from Genesis, where twins are born of Tamar, with whom the patriarch Judah lay: It came to pass, it says, when she was giving birth, and had twins in her womb; in the very birth one put forth his hand, and the midwife tied scarlet on his hand, saying: This one will come out first. But when he had drawn back his hand, at once his brother came out, and she said: Why has the wall been divided for your sake? And she called his name Perez, which is interpreted division. And after this his brother came out, on whose hand was the scarlet, and she called his name Zerah, which in our language means seed, or rising. The elder people, then, in whom was the rising and the seed before the Church was born from the Gentiles, showed its hand in works, and afterwards drew it back, hearing through Isaiah: For your hands are full of blood. And when he had drawn back his hand and ceased from the works of justice, his brother, the people of the Gentiles, came out. And for his sake the hedge was divided, and the Lord and Savior destroyed, so that he might be able to advance, the partition which was in the middle and the wall separating the two peoples; and he made one flock, and created the two in himself into one new man, making peace. Hence too the midwife, speaking prophetically, says to Perez, the younger people: Why has the wall been divided for your sake? If you have understood the example from Genesis, go up, you who desire to be saved, by no means through the old people, which has its hand drawn back, but through the new, in which Christ is the way, in whom Jesus is the gate, through whom we advance to the Father; for he himself dissolved the middle partition and wall, that is, the obscurity of the ancient prophets, and opened all the mysteries of the old law, and, having scattered the difficulty of advancing, revealed the way to the eyes of all, so that whoever wishes to go on may be hindered by no obstacle nor be frightened by the fog of obscurity.\n— Commentary on Micah","LXX: Before their face they divided and passed through the gate, and went out by it. And their king went out before their face. But the Lord is their leader. For this reason I said to you, Go up through the division, you who have risen with Christ and seek the things that are above: because the angels, or the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit, divided what seemed to stand in the way, and made a way for those who wished to enter; and because, the road being open, they took to the road, they not only entered the gate, but also passed through it. And they entered because their king too entered the same gate and opened the way for them, so that they might walk without any difficulty. For the Lord himself is both king and shepherd, both the way and the gate, and he says: I am the gate: whoever enters through me will be saved; he will go in and go out, and will find pasture. Of this gate it is prophesied elsewhere too: This is the gate of the Lord, the just will enter by it. But whoever has entered ought not to remain in the state in which he entered; let him go out to the pastures, so that in entering there is the beginning, and in going out and finding pasture, the perfection of the virtues. He who enters is still in the world, and understands the Creator from the creatures. But he who goes out transcends the whole creation, and, counting as nothing all things that can be seen, will find pasture above the heavens, and will feed on the word of God, and will say: The Lord shepherds me, and I shall lack nothing. This is said so that we might understand the testimony of the Gospel, He will go in and go out, and will find pasture, and what is now said through the prophet, And they passed through the gate, and went out by it; yet this passing through and going out cannot be granted without Christ our king, who is king and Lord. For immediately there is joined: But the Lord will be the leader.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.1-2":["LXX: And he will say: Hear these things, O princes of the house of Jacob, and the rest of the house of Israel. The sentence clings to what went before. For above he had said: But the Lord will be the leader, and he will say: Hear, O princes of the house of Jacob, and the rest of the house of Israel. For \"the rest of the house of Israel\", all except the Seventy translated \"leaders of the house of Israel\". The Lord, therefore, who made a way for his people, and went out before their face, and is himself the leader of the journey of the simpler people, whom he calls his flock, threatens those who are unwilling to follow him as he goes before, but are proud, and, as judges of the people, do not follow his footsteps, and he says: Hear, O princes of the house of Jacob, and leaders of the house of Israel. But what is it that compels them to hear? It is not yours, he says, to know judgment, you who hate the good, and seek evils; that is, you do not deserve to know the judgment of God, which is a great abyss, and the depth of his justice a perverse mind does not find. Or how can you know the judgment of God, you who hate the good and seek evil, who loathe the holy poor and honor rich sinners? And at the same time let us consider the force of the words: if not to love good things is a mark of sin, of how great a crime is it even to hate them? And on the contrary, if not to flee evil things is a fault, of how great an impiety is it even to seek them out diligently? After this the savagery of the judges is described, and their cruelty toward those subject to them.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.1-4":["And I said: Hear, O princes of Jacob, and leaders of the house of Israel. Is it not yours to know judgment, you who hate the good and love evil, who violently tear their skins from off them, and their flesh from off their bones, who have eaten the flesh of my people, and flayed their skin from off them; and they have broken their bones, and chopped them up as in a cauldron, and like flesh in the midst of a pot. Then they will cry to the Lord, and he will not hear them, and he will hide his face from them at that time, as they have acted wickedly in their devices. The word is plainly directed against the princes of Israel, and under the metaphor of lions or of robbers their cruelty is described: that they despoiled the poor, and killed them, and crushed their flesh and bones; and that, as in the midst of a pot, so in the city of Jerusalem they afflicted the wretched common people; and that on account of these things punishment is afterwards brought upon them in the day of captivity, whether by Nebuchadnezzar or by Vespasian and Titus. And they cry to the Lord, and he does not hear them, and he hides his face from them, because they acted wickedly in their crimes.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.2-4":["LXX: Tearing away their skins from them, and their flesh from their bones. As they have devoured the flesh of my people, and flayed the skins from them, and broken their bones, and chopped them up like flesh in a cauldron, and like flesh in a pot; so will they cry to the Lord, and he will not hear them, and he will turn away his face from them at that time, because they have acted most wickedly in their devices. It had not been enough for them to despoil the flock subject to them, but they also afflicted their bodies with harsh rule, and crushed their bones, so as to break and grind whatever perhaps had been in them. As, therefore, they despoiled my people, and stripped them of all the beauty and comeliness of their skin, and put their flesh and bones into the boiling pot which the Assyrian king kindled, handing my flock over to the devil and his angels, so they too, when the day of vengeance has come, will cry to the Lord and will not be heard, because they themselves did not hear those who asked; and they will stretch out their hands to the Lord, and God will turn away his face from them, because they too turned away their countenance from those who entreated them. And they will suffer all these things because they acted most wickedly in their pursuits and in their pleasures; and they were not kings but tyrants, not overseers but lions, not teachers of disciples but wolves of the sheep; and they glutted themselves with flesh, and grew fat, and, like a most fat victim of slaughter, were also made ready for the punishments of the Lord. Thus far against the wicked princes; the word that follows is against the false prophets and the very wicked teachers, who trip up the people of God with flattery, promising knowledge of the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.5-7":["Thus says the Lord concerning the prophets who lead my people astray, who bite with their teeth and preach peace; and if anyone does not put something into their mouth, they sanctify war against him; therefore night shall be to you instead of vision, and darkness to you instead of divination, and the sun shall go down upon the prophets, and the day shall be darkened over them, and those who see visions shall be confounded; and the diviners shall be confounded, and they shall all cover their faces, because there is no answer of God. But I am filled with the strength of the spirit of the Lord, with judgment and truth, to declare to Jacob his crime, and to Israel his sin. LXX: Thus says the Lord concerning the prophets who lead my people astray, and bite with their teeth, and preach peace over him, and it was not given into their mouth, they sanctified war against him; therefore night shall be to you instead of vision, and darkness shall be to you from divination, and the sun shall go down upon the prophets, and the day shall grow dark over them; and those who see dreams shall be confounded, and the soothsayers shall be mocked, and all these shall speak against them, because there is no one to hear them, unless I fill up strength in the spirit of the Lord, and judgment and power, to declare to Jacob his impieties, and to Israel his sins. We read that there were false prophets in Israel who for gifts preached peace, which had not been given to them, and if anyone had not given them presents, however holy he might be, they would announce to him that the wrath of God was going to come. Hence it is now said to them that they speak lies, and that their word is not prophecy but divination, and that they have not light but darkness and error. And when the things they had promised have been turned to the contrary, then they are covered with confusion, because there will not be, he says, an answer of God.\n\nNow not even the demons will receive power to deceive them by their fraud. The oracles will be mute, the unclean spirit will be silent and will not dare to mock. So much concerning the false prophets.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.5":["But if we choose to take what is said according to the Seventy as concerning the heretics, who are truly false prophets, and say, thus says the Lord, when the Lord has not sent them, we shall not err. For they deceive the people that was once God's with error, and they do this in order to eat them, either simply in receiving presents, or mystically in the slaughter of their souls; and they promise them peace and the heavenly kingdoms, and say, there is no need for you to live continently and holily; hold the faith which we teach, and you will obtain all the promises of the Lord. Speaking such things, they rather stir up the wrath of the Lord against them, and sanctify war against them.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.6-8":["Wherefore, O you heretics, who think that you have prophecy and imitate the Church of God, where you suppose there is vision, there will be night, and where you boast of prophetic utterance, there the unclean spirit will speak. For the sun of justice will set upon prophets of this kind, and they will see their own darkness and be confounded. And when they have been proved to be diviners rather than prophets, they will be laughed at over their dreams, and the peoples who had formerly been deceived by them will speak against them. Then the teachers themselves too will repent, and no one will hear them except I, whom they had offended. And because I am merciful, and do not wish the death of the sinner, but desire that he turn back and live, when I have heard them I will give them the power of my spirit, and I will fill them with my judgment and strength, so that those who formerly deceived the people with blandishments may afterwards, by declaring true things, deter them and call them back to the right way, and those who had been the cause of error may begin to heal the wounds which they had inflicted, and become an occasion of health. Notice in the present passage that someone can teach after sin, provided, however, that he has washed away his former vices with worthy repentance. Hence David too, after adultery and homicide, says in the psalm: You will sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed; you will wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow. Nor is he content with his own purity alone, but he adds: Restore to me the joy of your salvation, and strengthen me with a governing spirit. And when you have done this. I will teach, he says, the wicked your ways, and the impious will be turned to you.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.8":["Again the speech of the prophet speaking about himself is introduced: while they prophesy falsely and are covered with confusion and ignominy, I, what I speak, I speak moved by the Holy Spirit, and I speak the judgment of the Lord and the truth. And while the false prophets bite with their teeth and preach peace, I without any fear declare to Jacob his crime and to Israel his sin, because they either worshipped idols in place of God, or crucified the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.9-10":["The judges and princes of the house of Jacob and of the house of Israel, that is, of both the two and the ten tribes, not only did not do judgment, but abhorred it, and perverted all that is right, so that not even a little justice might remain in the city, because they built Zion with bloodshed, and Jerusalem with iniquity, with the plundering of the poor, the slaughter of the innocent, the killing of the saints.\n— Commentary on Micah","But if anyone transfers these things which are said of Jerusalem and of Zion to the Church (for she is the house of Jacob and the house of Israel, according to what is written in Isaiah: Jacob my servant, I will uphold him, and that word to Timothy: That you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church), he will see clearly that the princes of the house of Jacob, and the rest of the house of Israel, or, as is better written in the Hebrew: judges of the house of Israel, are none other than the bishops, presbyters and deacons, who, unless they have kept their heart with all watchfulness, abhor judgment and pervert all that is right; for when they judge according to the person, and in the hearing of a case it is not the merit of the cause that prevails but the power of the defendants, do they not abhor judgment and pervert all that is right? But it can also be taken in another way: that the princes of the house of Jacob and the judges of the house of Israel abhor and detest judgment, turning aside from the sentence of God the judge, and distorting every rule of the Scriptures with evil interpretations; these build Zion with bloodshed, and Jerusalem with iniquity. For whereas Scripture commands: Do not bring the ungodly man into the tabernacle of the just, and: The man of blood and deceit let the Lord abhor, these, ordaining as clergy any of their hangers-on whatever, and exposing their life as a scandal to the peoples, are guilty of the unbelief of those who are scandalized. Hence it is said that it is better for a man that a millstone turned by an ass be tied around his neck and that he be cast into the deep, than that he should scandalize one of the least of the Church.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.9-12":["Hear these things, O princes of the house of Jacob, and judges of the house of Israel, you who abhor judgment and pervert all that is right, who build Zion with bloodshed, and Jerusalem with iniquity. Her princes judged for gifts, and her priests taught for hire, and her prophets divined for money; and they leaned upon the Lord, saying: Is not the Lord in our midst? No evils will come upon us. Therefore, because of you, Zion will be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem will be like a heap of stones, and the mountain of the temple like the high places of the forests. LXX: Hear these things, O judges of the house of Jacob and the rest of the house of Israel, you who abhor judgment and pervert all that is right; who build Zion with bloodshed, and Jerusalem with iniquities. Her leaders judged for gifts, and her priests gave answers for hire, and her prophets divined for money; and they leaned upon the Lord, saying: Is not the Lord among us? No evils will come upon us. Therefore, on account of you, Zion will be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem will be like a watch-hut for fruit, and the mountain of the Lord like a grove of the forest. No one doubts that Jerusalem was overthrown on account of the crimes which are described in this section; and since earlier the threat had gone before against either the judges or the false prophets: Hear, O princes of the house of Jacob, and leaders of the house of Israel. And after a few little verses: Thus says the Lord concerning the prophets who lead my people astray, and the rest; now the prophetic word is woven generally, both against the judges and against the false prophets, and against the priests, and against those who promised themselves the knowledge of God, and it rebukes them because on account of their crimes Zion is to be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem is to collapse like a heap of stones, and the mountain of the temple is to become like the high places of the forests. We see fulfilled the things that were said; the eyes prove the utterance of the mouth; the sight is the witness of the prophecy. This very testimony is written also in the scroll of Jeremiah, where mention is made of the prophet Micah, and the devastation of Jerusalem is foretold.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.11":["And if any of the princes seemed to judge what was right, he rather sold his sentence, and judged for gifts. The priests also did not teach the people unless they had received a price; and whereas it is said to the saints: Freely you have received, freely give, they, bringing forth the answer of God when money had been received, sold the grace of the Lord for avaricious hire. And after these evils, not understanding their sin, as though they were buying back the love of God with crimes, they kept declaring that they were judges and priests and prophets of God, and in accordance with their impenitent heart they said: The Lord is in our midst, and no evils will come upon us.\n— Commentary on Micah","And although the prophet Malachi calls priests angels, and their mouth is the oracle of the Lord, they do not judge without the taking of gifts, though they read: Gifts blind the eyes even of the wise, and like a bridle in the mouth turn away reproof. And although it is said to the apostles: Do not possess gold and silver, nor let there be copper in your belts; and money, even when earned by labor, is to be shaken out of the hands of a holy man, they sell the words of the Lord and trade in doves in the temple. But the prophets of Jerusalem also divined for money, not knowing that prophecy is one thing and divination another; for divination is never taken in a good sense in the Scriptures. There shall be, he says, no augury in Jacob, nor divination in Israel. They seemed to themselves indeed to be prophets, but because they took money, their prophecy became divination. The apostle Peter: Silver, he says, and gold I have not; he certainly could have sold to Simon Magus what he was asking for, or rather, he could have pretended to be selling it, for the Holy Spirit can be neither sold nor bought; but he condemned the money offered, together with the one offering it. Now you may see that the prophets of Jerusalem do not have prophecy in their mouth, and rest upon the Lord, and say: No evils will come upon us, on whose account God's watchtower is cut by the enemy's plow; and the place once of peace becomes full of ruins, and the temple of the Lord is turned into briars and thorns, and is a dwelling of beasts. Nor let anyone be troubled by what we read in the first book of Kings (1 Samuel): that Saul, wishing to go to Samuel, said to his servant that he could not go to him because he did not have a price to offer for the prophecy, and that the servant answered: Behold, there is found in my hand the fourth part of a shekel of silver, and I will give it to the man of God, and he will tell us our way; for it is not written that Samuel accepted it, or that they offered it to him; on the contrary, they are fed by the prophet and invited to a meal. But suppose that he did accept it: it should be reckoned rather as offerings for the tabernacle than as payment for prophecy. For a shekel contains twenty obols, and the fourth part of a shekel is five obols. So our priests too, if they wish to sell prophecy, and to set out in their chairs the doves which the Lord overturned with a whip, let them accept five obols, not the prices of country estates. The same thing is heard also in the third book of Kingdoms (1 Kings): that the wife of Jeroboam, when her son was sick, goes to Ahijah the man of God and brings him loaves and raisins and a little jar of honey. For it is told what she brought with her, and yet it is not written that the prophet accepted it, since indeed he both rebuked her and foretold the mourning to come; although it may be that those who had been accustomed to go to soothsayers (for there were many diviners and soothsayers in Israel) thought, by a bad custom, the same thing also of the prophets, and wished to offer to holy men what they had been accustomed to offer to diviners, and that Scripture only related what they wished; yet it did not add either that they dared to offer it or that the prophets accepted it. The Apostle Paul: Those who serve the altar, he says, both partake of the altar and live. It is permitted to you, O priest, to live from the altar, not to live in luxury. The mouth of the ox that treads out the grain is not muzzled. We know these things, and yet the Apostle does not abuse this license; and having food and clothing he is content; night and day he labors with his own hands, so as not to be a burden to anyone. And he swears in his epistles that he has conducted himself holily and without avarice in the gospel of Christ, and he asserts this very thing not only of himself but also of his disciples, that he sent no one who either demanded anything from the churches or was willing to accept it. And if in some epistles he gives thanks, and calls the gifts of the senders the benevolence of God, he gathers them not so much for himself as for the poor among the saints who were in Jerusalem. Now the holy poor were those in Jerusalem who first believed in Christ from among the Jews and, cast out by their parents and kinsmen and relations by marriage, had lost both their possessions and all their household goods, which the priests of the temple and the people laid waste. If the poor are such, let them accept. But if, under the pretext of the poor, a few houses are enriched, and we eat gold in glass and earthenware vessels, then either let us change our clothes along with our treasures, or let the poor habit not seek the riches of senators. Little does it profit not to have a little linen cloth about the neck for wiping away sweat. What does it help to be μονοχίτωνας (wearing a single tunic), and to display poverty in our dress, when the whole crowd of the poor sighs for our purse?\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.3.12":["Since, therefore, they did not repent, and the whole people followed the vices of the princes and priests and prophets, Zion will be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem will be like a heap of stones, and the temple, which formerly shone with gold and silver, will collapse into utter ruins. These things are against the people of the Jews, upon whom the true captivity and the final ruins were imposed, on account indeed of their earlier crimes, but chiefly on account of the shedding of the Lord's blood. Hence too Zion has been plowed like a field, and Jerusalem turned into mounds of stones, and that temple, once noble and lofty, has been ground down into the last ashes.\n— Commentary on Micah","Therefore, because of us, who are such, who build Zion with blood, and Jerusalem with iniquity, who judge for gifts, who give answers for hire, who divine for money, and who, claiming besides a feigned sanctity for ourselves, say: No evils will come upon us. Let us hear the sentence of the Lord which follows: Zion and Jerusalem, and the mountain of the temple, the watchtower, and the vision of peace, and the temple of Christ, at the consummation and at the end, when charity has grown cold and faith has become rare, will be plowed like a field, and will be reduced to mounds, and will be like the high places of the forests, or a watch-hut for fruit, so that where once there were spacious houses and heaps of grain without number, there there may scarcely be a little hut keeping the appearance of foods which hold no refreshment for the soul.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.1-2":["On my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel, says the Lord God, there all the house of Israel shall serve me: all, I say, in the land in which they shall please me; and there I will require your firstfruits and the beginning of your tithes in all your sanctifications. In an odor of sweetness I will receive you, when I have led you out from the peoples and gathered you from the lands into which you were scattered, and I shall be sanctified in you in the eyes of the nations. And you shall know that I am the Lord. Of this mountain, on which all the house of Israel is to serve God, Isaiah and Micah sang with one voice: In the last days the mountain of the house shall be manifest on the summit of the mountains and shall be raised above the hills; and all the nations shall flow to it, and many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will teach us his ways and we will walk in his paths; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem; and again Isaiah: Go up onto a high mountain, he says, you who proclaim to Zion; lift up and exalt your voice, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. This mountain, Zion, we understand either as the Church, which means 'watchtower' and is set on the height of holy doctrines, or as the Lord Savior himself, in whom the firstfruits and the tithes and all worship are turned into an odor of sweetness, so that all the nations round about, seeing that the people of the Lord has been saved, may glorify God, and those who have been saved may know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"MIC.4.1-7":["It must also be known that the Jews and the heirs of their error refer both this section which we have now expounded and the one like it in Isaiah to the thousand-year reign of Christ and of the saints; and that what is said: All peoples shall walk, each in the name of the Lord his God, they interpret in this way: that each nation is to be tormented with its idol and sent into the fire of eternal punishment. But they are refuted by what follows: this is said not of the end of the ages but of the first coming of Christ, in which the remnant of her that halts is gathered, and the nations are saved first. Isaiah, then, set down a testimony of this kind: The word that came to Isaiah son of Amoz concerning Judah and Jerusalem: for in the last days the mountain of the Lord shall be manifest, and the house of God upon the summit of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills, and all nations shall come to it. And many nations shall go, and shall say: Come, let us go up into the mountain of the Lord, and into the house of the God of Jacob, and he will announce to us our way, and we will walk in it; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And he shall judge among many nations, and reprove a very great people, and they shall beat their swords into plows, and their hunting-spears into sickles, and nation shall not take up sword against nation, and they shall not learn to make war any more. Significantly it is said here that the word of God, going forth from Jerusalem, judges among many nations, and properly reproves the people of the Jews. For us, as sinners, he will judge according to the measure of our works; but them, as impious men and deniers, he will not judge, but will reprove them as condemned.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.1":["And it shall be in the last of days, there shall be the mountain of the house of the Lord, prepared on the top of the mountains, and lofty above the hills; and peoples shall flow to it; and many nations shall hasten, and they shall say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths, for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem; and he shall judge among many peoples, and rebuke strong nations even afar off; and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into mattocks; nation shall not take up sword against nation, and they shall not learn to make war any more; and a man shall sit under his vine and under his fig tree, and there shall be none to make him afraid, for the mouth of the Lord of hosts has spoken; for all peoples shall walk, each in the name of his god; but we will walk in the name of the Lord our God forever and beyond. In that day, says the Lord, I will gather her that halts, and her whom I had cast out I will gather, and her whom I had afflicted; and I will make her that halts a remnant, and her that had labored a mighty nation, and the Lord shall reign over them in Mount Zion from this time forth and even forever. LXX: And it shall be in the last of days, the mountain of the Lord manifest, prepared upon the top of the mountains; and it shall be lifted up above the hills, and peoples shall hasten to it, and many nations shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will show us his way, and we will walk in his paths; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem, and he shall judge among many peoples, and reprove strong nations even afar off, and they shall beat their swords into plows and their spears into sickles; and nation shall no more take up sword against nation, and they shall by no means learn to make war; and each shall rest under his vine, and each under his fig tree, and there shall be none to make him afraid, for the mouth of the Lord Almighty has spoken these things; for all peoples shall go, each his own way. But we will go in the name of the Lord our God forever and beyond. In that day, says the Lord, I will gather her that is crushed, and her that had been cast out I will receive, and those whom I had driven back; and I will make the crushed one a remnant, and the one driven back a strong nation, and the Lord shall reign over them in Mount Zion, from this time forth and even unto the age. Because the princes of the Jews abhorred judgment, and had perverted all that was right, and had built Zion with blood and Jerusalem with iniquity, and had not only done these things but had also judged for bribes, and the priests of Jerusalem had given answers for hire, and her prophets had divined for money, and on their account Zion had become like a plowed field, and Jerusalem had fallen into heaps of stones, and the mountain of the temple of God, made desolate, into the high places of the forests: therefore now, their house being forsaken and deserted, when the Son of God, going out of the temple, said: Arise, let us go hence, and: Your house shall be left to you deserted. The angels too, as Josephus relates, said: Let us pass over from these seats, and in place of Mount Zion the mountain of the Lord has been lifted up, of which it is said to the prince of Tyre. And you were wounded on the mountain of the Lord. But this mountain of the Lord was shown in the last of days, when the kingdom of heaven drew near. For indeed at the consummation of the ages, for the rejection of sins, our Savior appeared through his own victim, and came at the eleventh hour to hire laborers. And when his passion was accomplished, John says: It is the last hour; for in six thousand years, if five hundred years are allotted to each hour of the day, the time of the faith of the Gentiles will consequently be called the last hour. And manifest, he says, shall be the mountain of the Lord, prepared upon the tops of the mountains. He will be manifest who before had been hidden, and prepared not only on the mountains but upon the tops of the mountains, Moses and the prophets, who prophesied of him. For although all that they wrote is holy, yet in comparison with the prophecy in which they prophesied of the coming of the Savior, the rest is lowly, and by no means reaches up to the tops of the mountains. And it shall be exalted, he says, above the hills. He appeared indeed as a man, and took the form of a servant, humbled even unto death, and the death of the cross; but the Father exalted him, and gave him the name which is above every name; and the whole life of men, compared with his manner of life, is called plains and valleys. To this mountain, therefore, which is prepared upon the tops of the mountains and exalted above the hills, all peoples shall hasten, or, as it stands in the Hebrew, shall flow; that is, innumerable crowds shall gather after the manner of rivers. Now the peoples shall hasten when there believed in him alike Parthians and Medes, Elamites, and dwellers in Mesopotamia, Judea and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia, Phrygia and Pamphylia, Egypt and the parts of Libya which is near Cyrene, and visitors from Rome, Jews and proselytes, Cretans and Arabians. Do not those seem to you to have hastened to the mountain to whom it was said: Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men; and at once they followed the Savior? And again Scripture relates of James and John that, leaving the boat and their father and the waves of the world, they hastened to the mountain. And Matthew the publican, on hearing: Follow me, at once ran. That passage too in the Gospel, when great crowds followed him from Galilee and the Decapolis and Jerusalem, and Judea and beyond the Jordan, and he healed them, proves that the peoples were hastening.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.2":["And while the peoples hasten, very many nations also shall go to the mountain, namely the whole world believing in him, and they shall say, calling one another to the standard of the gibbet: Come, let us go up into the mountain of the Lord. There is need of an ascent for anyone to be able to reach Christ, to the house of the God of Jacob, the Church, which is the house of God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. Moreover, that Jacob signifies the Savior we have also said above: Jacob my servant, I will receive him. And they will say this also to those to whom they had spoken: Come, let us go up into the mountain of the Lord and into the house of the God of Jacob, and they will add: That they may show us his way; understand this either of the angels who preside over the churches, or of the holy Scriptures, which show beforehand the way of the Lord, and him who says: I am the way. And let us walk in his paths, namely in the apostles, through whom we have believed in Christ. For out of Zion went forth the spiritual law, passed over to the Gentiles, the word of the Lord from Jerusalem, who will judge among many peoples.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.3-4":["The Father indeed has given all judgment to the Son. And he will rebuke strong nations even afar off. For the Lord catches the wise in their craftiness and has known the thoughts of the wise, that they are vain. And he discerns among the peoples who are worthy of salvation and who unworthy, and at his coming all eagerness for war will be turned to peace. Swords will be changed into plows, spears will be forged into sickles, nation will cease to war against nation. No one will learn to fight, the need of fighting being laid aside. And so great will be the rest that not only in the cities but in the hamlets too and in the fields each one will be secure; and this will come to pass because the mouth of the Lord has spoken. And first, according to the letter: before a child was born to us, whose government is upon his shoulder, the whole world was full of blood; peoples fought against peoples, kings against kings, nations against nations. In short, even the Roman Republic itself was torn by civil wars, with Cinna and Octavius and Carbo fighting; Sulla and Marius, Antony and Catiline, Caesar and Gnaeus Pompey, Augustus and Brutus, and the same Augustus and Antony, in whose battles all the kingdoms shed blood. But after Rome, with a view to the rule of Christ, obtained a single rule, the world was made open to the journeys of the apostles, and the gates of cities were opened to them, and for the preaching of the one God a single rule was established. This saying too: They shall beat their swords into plows, and their spears into sickles, can also be taken tropologically, so that we say that at the faith of Christ anger and unbridled abuse have been laid aside, so that each one puts his hand to the plow and does not look back behind him, and, breaking the darts and spears of insults, desires to reap spiritual fruits, so that, others having labored, we may enter into their labors, and it may be said of us: But coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves. Now no one fights against another, for we read: Blessed are the peacemakers. No one learns to contend to the subversion of the hearers, but he sets silence on his mouth, and is silent, because the time is evil. And each shall rest under his vine, to press the wine that gladdens the heart of man, under that vine whose husbandman is the Father, and under his fig tree, plucking the sweet fruits of the Holy Spirit, love, joy, peace and the rest. And all these things will come to pass, according to both expositions, because the words of the Lord are true, and for him to have said is to have done.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.5":["All peoples shall go, each in his own way, but we will walk in the name of the Lord our God unto the age and beyond. While all the nations, they say, go according to their own error, we have hastened to the mountain of the Lord, and have said: Come, let us go up into the mountain of the Lord, and into the house of the God of Jacob and we will walk in the name of Christ our Lord, for he himself is the mountain who is God.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.6-7":["In that day, in which the sun of justice will shine, she who had before been crushed will be gathered, and she who had been driven out will be received; and the crushed one will be gathered for this, that she may be made a remnant, and the forsaken one a strong nation. Unless indeed the Lord of Sabaoth had left us a seed, we would have been as Sodom, and we would have been made like Gomorrah. But according to the Hebrew it is beautifully put: I will gather, he says, her that halts, and her who had walked badly, and to whom Elijah had said: How long do you halt? Whose foot, which had caused stumbling, had been cut off. Her whom I had cast out, and to whom I had given a bill of divorce, I will gather; and her whom I had afflicted with various captivities, or had handed over to the devil and his angels. And lest the careful reader at once object to us: How do you assert that she who halts has been gathered, and that she who had been cast out has been collected, when the Jews remain in unbelief? Let him hear that this is said of the first Church of Christ, which believed from among the Jews, to which the apostles too belonged, concerning which Luke writes in the Acts of the Apostles that in one day three thousand believed, and elsewhere five thousand, and of whom James says to Paul: You see, brother Paul, how many thousands there are of the Jews who believe, and all these are zealots of the law. But consider also the prophetic precision: he did not say, I will make her that halts wholly saved, but I will make her that halts a remnant, so that the remnant and the elect may be saved, and her that had labored a mighty nation, namely the Christian name, which not the sword, not fires, not torments shall overcome. Look at the faith and the passion of the martyrs, and you will not doubt about the mighty nation. And the Lord shall reign over them, that is, over many nations, and over the halting remnant, in Mount Zion, in the Church, in the watchtower, in the contemplation of the virtues, from the present age and even into the future. But if anyone wishes to understand what is said: I will gather her who was crushed, and the one cast out I will receive, and the rest, of the human soul, that before the coming of Christ it served various passions and vices, and like a straying and sickly sheep was torn by the bites of wolves, he will not err, provided he knows that she who had been crushed and afflicted will afterwards be under the reign of the Lord, and will live in Zion, and is to be carried back on the shoulders of the good shepherd to her former mountain.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.8-9":["Some think that the squalid or dark tower, and the daughter of Jerusalem, are to be understood of the heavenly Jerusalem, which is the mother of the saints, of which the Apostle also says: You have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, which remains squalid so long as her sons are not brought back to her, and king and counselor are not in her, and pains lay hold of her as of a woman in labor, because she has given birth in vain, seeing so many sons slain.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.8":["And you, tower of the flock, cloudy one of the daughter of Zion, even unto you it shall come; and the first power shall come, the kingdom of the daughter of Jerusalem. Now why are you drawn together in grief? Is there no king for you, or has your counselor perished, because pain has seized you like a woman in labor. LXX: And you, tower of the flock, murky daughter of Zion, to you it shall come, and the first principality shall enter, the kingdom from Babylon of the daughter of Jerusalem. And now why have you known evils? Was there no king for you, or has your counsel perished, because pains have seized you like a woman giving birth? The tower of the flock, cloudy or squalid, which in Hebrew is called ophel, we ought to take as no other than that of which Isaiah says: And I built a tower in the midst of it, that is, of the vineyard. Now the vineyard of the Lord is the house of Israel. As long as this tower has a winepress, that is, the altar, and a wall round about, namely the help of the angels, and the devil, the boar, does not enter the vineyard, it is not squalid, it is not dark, but, having received its name from the Lord who shines upon it, it is called a city which, set on a mountain, cannot be hidden. The tower, then, once of the flock and of the people of God, because the most wicked tenants killed the son of the householder, is now squalid and abandoned, and under the name Ariel cries out from the earth in Isaiah. And this tower belongs to the daughter of Zion, or, as Symmachus rendered it into Greek: She herself is the daughter of Zion, and even unto her shall come God, or the first power, which power is the kingdom of the daughter of Jerusalem. Now there came to this tower the first power, or the first principality, he who had said: I am alpha and Ω, the beginning and the end, the first and the last. And he who, in the person of the man he had assumed, says in Proverbs: The Lord created me in the beginning of his ways for his works, or, as it is written in the Hebrew: The Lord possessed me: for canani means not he created me, but he possessed me and held me. And the first power came, and the kingdom of the daughter of Jerusalem, so that after the first there might be a second as well; for just as he himself says with confidence: I am the light of the world, so he also grants to his disciples that they too should be called the light of the world, and he says to them: You are the light of the world. And calling himself the true vine in the Gospel, he says of believers through Jeremiah: But I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true. And though he is the living bread coming down from heaven, he granted to his disciples that they should be called bread. Hence the apostle Paul too says with confidence: For we all are one bread. In this way, then, the first power and the kingdom enters into Jerusalem, so as to make those who believe in her a power and a kingdom. But as for what is read in some books: And the first principality shall enter, the kingdom of the daughter of Zion, from Babylon, let us know that it has been added, since it is found neither in the Hebrew nor among the other translators. And it seems to me indeed that it was thinking of the Babylonian captivity, because the people, going out from there, came to Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.9":["There follows: And now why are you drawn together in grief? Or, as it is written in the LXX: And now why have you known evils, you to whom, he says, the Lord is to come, and the first power and the kingdom? Why now are you drawn together in grief, or why have you known evils? At once it is answered and said: Because there is no king for you, and your counselor has perished, because pain has seized you like a woman in labor. Or surely, though you have all these, by your own fault you do not deserve the help either of the king or of the counselor. As for his saying: Why have you known evils, it is to be taken in this sense: that everyone who deserves and suffers evils is said to know evils and to be ignorant of good things. According to what is written also in the first book of Kingdoms (1 Samuel): The sons of Eli, sons of pestilence, did not know God. And elsewhere: He who keeps the commandment shall not know an evil word. And to sinners: Depart from me, says the Lord, you who work iniquity, for I do not know you. On the contrary, of the Lord: Him who had not known sin he made sin for us, God the Father being understood. But the King and angel of great counsel is understood to be the Savior, who perished for an unbelieving people, which pains seized like a woman in labor; for Israel, thinking that it held dominion, was suddenly laid waste. And as a woman giving birth cannot escape pain, so it could not avoid or put off the impending captivity and the army surrounding the city. Let us read the Scriptures, and we shall never find that holy women, except Rachel, gave birth with pain; and she, because she was on the road, and in the hippodrome, that is, the course of the horses that are sold in Egypt, bore a son of pain, whom his father afterwards called son of the right hand. Eve, cast out of paradise and hearing: In pains you shall bring forth, is described as having given birth in pain. The wife of Phinehas, bent over and not raising herself, like that woman in the Gospel whom the devil had bound, gave birth after she had learned that the ark of God was taken and of the ruin of the people. But Sarah, because she was holy and the ways of women had ceased in her, says when Isaac was born: God has made laughter for me, for whoever hears will rejoice with me. The pains, then, that seized the tower of the flock are the pains of the underworld and the pains of death, which surrounded indeed and hemmed in even the Savior, but could by no means seize him, as he himself says in Psalm 17 (18): The pains of death surrounded me, and the torrents of iniquity troubled me, and the pains of hell hemmed me in.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.10":["Be in pain and toil, daughter of Zion, like a woman in labor, for now you shall go forth from the city, and dwell in the country, and come even to Babylon; there you shall be delivered, there the Lord will redeem you from the hand of your enemies. LXX: Be in pain, and act manfully, daughter of Zion, like a woman giving birth, for now you shall go forth from the city, and dwell in the plain, and come even to Babylon; from there he will deliver you, and from there the Lord your God will redeem you from the hand of your enemies. She who is commanded to be in pain, or to be in labor, and to whom is then added, act manfully, is not commanded in vain; but it is so that, bearing her pains patiently, she may go forth from the city, and dwell in the plain or in the country, and come even to Babylon, and when, enduring captivity for her sin, she has bravely borne what was inflicted, then the Lord may deliver her, and her God redeem her from the hand of her enemies, so that, after she has been delivered, she may say to those who mock her: Do not mock me, my enemy, for I have fallen and I shall rise again; for even if I walk in darkness, the Lord is my light. I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, and executes my judgment, and brings me out into the light, and I shall see his justice. And my enemy shall behold it, and shall be covered with shame. Hence in the psalms too it is sung with a fitting voice: Not forever will he be angry, nor to eternity will the Lord threaten. And that Be in pain, and act manfully pertains to the salvation of the one who is in pain and acts manfully, Joshua son of Nun too is witness, to whom the Lord says: Be strengthened, and act manfully. And the daughter of Zion is in pain and acts manfully, because she went forth from the city unshod and naked, and is to be led captive into the plain of Shinar, and to be in Babylon until Zerubbabel comes, and Ezra, which means helper, and delivers her from the hand of the Chaldeans. Since this is plain according to the letter, it seems to me to mean that the soul, driven out of the Church for sin, and handed over to the enemy and avenger for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved, goes forth from the city which the rush of the river makes glad, and dwells not on the mountain, where it had been before, but in the plain, in which the army of the Assyrians roams, and is in the confusion of its own vices; and that, after it has worn fetters, and dragged the mill, and made flour for the Babylonians, returning to itself it says: How many hired servants of my father have their fill of bread, and I perish here with hunger. And that afterwards, returning to the father's house, it is received by a most merciful father, and redeemed from the hand of a most harsh master. Let us give an example, so that what is commanded to the daughter of Zion, when it is said: Be in pain, and act manfully, like a woman giving birth, we may refer not to her punishment but to her benefit. Paul says to the Galatians: My little children, with whom I am in labor again, until Christ be formed in you. And he was in pain so long, and in labor so long, until he begot again through repentance those who had perished through sin. Suppose a physician says to a man in a fever, or to a wounded man: Be in pain and act manfully, endure thirst, bear the cautery, so that a surer health may follow.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.11-13":["And now many nations are gathered against you, who say: Let her be stoned, and let our eye look upon Zion. But they have not known the thoughts of the Lord, and have not understood his counsel, for he has gathered them like hay of the threshing floor. Arise and thresh, daughter of Zion, for I will make your horn iron, and your hoofs I will make bronze, and you shall break in pieces many peoples; and you shall slay to the Lord their plunder, and their strength to the Lord of the whole earth. LXX: And now many nations are gathered against you, of those who say: Let us insult, and let our eyes look upon Zion. But they did not know the thought of the Lord, and did not understand his counsel, for he has gathered them like sheaves of the threshing floor. Arise and thresh them, daughter of Zion, for I will make your horns iron, and your hoofs I will make bronze, and you shall break in pieces many peoples, and you shall vow to the Lord their multitude, and their might to the Lord of the whole earth. O Jerusalem, O daughter of Zion, who will come even to Babylon, and there will be delivered, and the Lord will redeem you from the hand of your enemies: now, meanwhile, many nations are gathered against you, who speak as of an adulteress and say: Let her be stoned, and let our eye look upon her. Or, as it is written in the LXX: Let us insult and rejoice, and let our eyes look down upon Zion; and they did not know the will and counsel of the Lord, that the nations were gathered against you for this very reason, that you might crush them like hay or stubble of the threshing floor. Arise, therefore, daughter of Zion, and with the iron horns, which I promise to give you, and with the bronze hoofs, which you are to receive, winnow and crush the peoples, and slay them to the Lord of the whole earth. For he delights in such a sacrifice and such an offering. The Jews, seeing that these things have not yet been fulfilled, promise this there, at the future coming of Christ, and say that all nations will serve the Jewish people, and that the very empire of the Romans, which they interpret under the name of Edom, is to be crushed by their hoofs and winnowed by their horns. How foolish this is, is easily proved from all the Scriptures; but that belongs to another time. We, therefore, who follow not the letter that kills but the spirit that gives life, say that against the daughter of Zion, which is interpreted as the Church, many nations of demons are gathered, and in the present age, which is set in the evil one, they insult her and rejoice in the slaughter of her sons, not knowing the thoughts of the Lord, nor recognizing his counsel. For if they had known, they would never have crucified the Lord of majesty. He will therefore gather them like sheaves of the threshing floor, so that whatever they seemed to have that is thorny and rough, empty and light, she may crush with her hoofs and winnow with her horns, and the pure grain that remains may be offered as gifts to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.4.13":["Now, as to what he signifies by saying: And you shall break in pieces many peoples, and you shall consecrate to the Lord their multitude, and their might to the Lord of the whole earth. Let us read Numbers, and Joshua son of Nun, and the first book of Kingdoms (1 Samuel), and we shall see how, from the subjugated nations, when all had perished by the edge of the sword, gold and silver, and a fixed number of the spoil, of men as well as of beasts, was consecrated to the Lord. In short, Achar too, who stole something from the accursed thing of Jericho, troubled the people, and from his sin a name was given to the valley, emec achor, that is, the valley of tumult, or of disturbances. And that you may know that, according to the LXX translators, who said: You shall consecrate to the Lord their multitude, and their might to the Lord of the whole earth, the consecration ought to be taken in a good sense: Theodotion rendered gifts for multitude; the Fifth Edition, profit, that is, ὠφέλειαν; Symmachus, gain, that is, τὸ κέρδος αὐτῶν.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.1":["Now you shall be laid waste, daughter of the robber; they have laid siege against us; with a rod they will strike the cheek of the judge of Israel. LXX: Now the daughter shall be blocked with a blockade; he has laid distress upon us; with a rod they will strike upon the cheek of the tribes of Israel. Not that one tribe strikes the cheek of another, but that others strike the tribes of Israel on the cheek. I did indeed promise you, O daughter of Zion, that a time would come when I would make your horn iron and your hoofs bronze, and when, the multitude of demons having been crushed, you would offer to the Lord of the whole earth whatever they had possessed before. But since this will come to pass then, when the fullness of the nations has come in and all Israel has been saved, now in the meantime be laid waste as your deserts require, or, as it stands in the Hebrew, be cut to pieces. For according to the Apostle you are by no means called the circumcision, but the concision; nor do I call you my daughter, but the robber's daughter, which in Hebrew is said bath gedud, that is, the daughter of the devil, who is ever girded for plunder. For you have made my house a den of robbers, you have fought against me, and your sons have laid siege against me, and against the Son, and against my Spirit. Or is it not an insult to the Trinity when, with rod and reed, at your doing, the Romans struck the head of the judge of Israel, saying: Prophesy to us, Christ, who is it that struck you? Or when one of your attendants struck him on the cheek, saying: Do you answer the high priest so? This is according to the Hebrew, and Aquila and Symmachus, and Theodotion, and the Fifth Edition agree with this interpretation. But according to the LXX the sense is much loftier, and it is understood as though from a beginning of its own. Now the synagogue is blocked with a blockade, and those who have been shut up in it will say: He has laid distress upon us; and, subject to the Roman powers, the tribes of Israel will be struck on the cheek. For the Lord has taken away from Judea and from Jerusalem the strong and the mighty, and the wise master builder, and the understanding hearer; and her ways are shut and hedged up to this day, and she cannot go out of captivity, but is pressed down by a most harsh rule. But if, following the sense above, we wish to take Now the daughter shall be blocked with a blockade as said of the Church, let us bring forward that example from Hosea, in which the adulteress speaks: I will go after my lovers, who gave me my bread, and my water, and my garments, and my linens, my oil, and all things that profit me. And afterwards God, wishing to hinder her most wicked thought, does not let her fulfill what she desires, but blocks her ways, so that she may not overtake her lovers and commit fornication further. For he says: Therefore, behold, I will block her ways with stakes, and I will hedge up her ways, and she will not find her path; and she will pursue her lovers, and will not lay hold of them, and she will seek them, and will not find them, and she will say: I will go and return to my former husband, because it was well with me. Notice that the Lord's work has succeeded: for the adulteress, not finding her way and not able to go where she wished, returns, bound by necessity, to her former husband, and confesses that it is better for her in her former husband's house than it once had been with her lovers; and Israel is schooled by tribulations and blows. Hence it is also said mystically to David, under the type of Christ: If his sons forsake my law, and do not walk in my judgments, if they profane my justices, and do not keep my commandments, I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with scourges; but my mercy I will not scatter from him. Therefore God has laid tribulation upon the daughter of Israel, and the angels who are set over the blows will strike her on the cheek. And lest perhaps you should not know who the daughter is that is blocked with a blockade and placed in distress, it is at once added and said: the tribes of Israel. But we are Israel, who discern God with the mind; to distinguish us from them the Apostle says: Behold Israel according to the flesh. For he would never call Israel fleshly unless he knew that there is also a spiritual one.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.2":["And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, are little among the thousands of Judah; out of you shall come forth for me one who is to be ruler in Israel, and his going forth is from the beginning, from the days of eternity. LXX: And you, Bethlehem, house of Ephrathah, are least, that you should be among the thousands of Judah; out of you shall come forth for me one who is to be prince of Israel, and his going forth is from the beginning, from the days of the age. In the Gospel according to Matthew, when the Magi had come from the east and Herod asked the scribes where Christ the Lord was to be born, they are related to have answered: In Bethlehem in the land of Judah, and, adding the prophet's testimony, they said: And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the princes of Judah; for out of you shall come forth a leader who will rule my people Israel. That this testimony agrees neither with the Hebrew nor with the Seventy translators is plain even if I say nothing; and I think that Matthew, wishing to reprove the negligence of the scribes and priests in the reading of divine Scripture, set it down in this way too, just as it had been said by them. But there are some who assert that in almost all the testimonies taken from the Old Testament there is an error of this kind, that either the order is changed or the words, and sometimes even the sense itself is different, since the apostles or the evangelists did not pick the testimonies out of a book but trusted to memory, which is sometimes deceived. Let us therefore expound the Hebrew: And you, Bethlehem, that is, house of bread, who are called Ephrathah, are indeed least among the cities of Judah; and, compared with so many thousands, you are scarcely a little hamlet; but out of this little hamlet Christ will come forth, who is ruler in Israel. And lest you think that he is only of the race of David, to whom I promised, saying: Of the fruit of your womb I will set upon my throne, the assumption of flesh does not hinder the divine majesty; for he was born of me before all ages, and the founder of times is not held within time. He it is to whom I also said in another psalm: Before the morning star I begot you. For in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. He was in the beginning with God. And therefore his going forth is from the beginning, from the days of eternity. That Bethlehem itself is Ephrathah, the book of Genesis shows, in which Scripture records: Rachel died, and was buried on the way to Ephrathah, that is Bethlehem. And in each of the two names it signifies a mystery: for it is called house of bread because of the living bread which came down from heaven; and Ephrathah, which means he sees fury, because of the madness of Herod, who, mocked by the Magi, was exceedingly angry, and sent and killed all the infants in Bethlehem and in all its borders from two years old and under, according to the time he had inquired of the Magi; and because of the great bloodshed a voice was heard on high, the lamentation and wailing of Rachel weeping for her children. We read, according to the Seventy translators only, in Joshua son of Nun, where the cities and towns of the tribe of Judah are described, this too written among the rest: Thaeco, and Ephrathah, that is Bethlehem, and Phagor, and Aetham, and Culon, and Tami, and Soris, and Caraem, and Gallim, and Baether, and Manocho, eleven cities and their villages, which is found neither in the Hebrew nor in any other translator; and whether it was erased from the old books by the malice of the Jews, lest Christ should be seen to have sprung from the tribe of Judah, or added by the Seventy, since we by no means know it clearly, what do we know for certain? Nonetheless we can also prove this very point from the book of Judges, that Bethlehem is in the tribe of Judah; for it is written: And there was a Levite man dwelling on the sides of the house of Ephraim, and he took for himself a concubine from Bethlehem of Judah, and his concubine was angry with him, and went away to her father's house in Bethlehem of Judah. And it is fittingly said, in Bethlehem of Judah, to distinguish it from that Bethlehem which is situated in Galilee, as I have found in the same book of Joshua. Ephrathah can sound in our language καρποφόρον, rich and fruitful, and show in a mystery the very same thing as house of bread.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.3":["Because of this he will give them up until the time in which she who is in labor shall bring forth, and the remnant of his brothers shall be converted to the sons of Israel. LXX: Therefore he will give them up until the time of her who brings forth: she shall bring forth, and the remnant of the brothers shall return to the sons of Israel. Because Christ, the ruler in Israel, came forth from Bethlehem which is Ephrathah, and his going forth had not been only at the time when he was seen in the flesh, but from the beginning of eternity, or from the beginning of the age. Because he himself always spoke through the prophets, and the word of God came to be in their hand, therefore he will give up the Jews and allow them to reign until the time of her who brings forth, when that saying is fulfilled: Rejoice, barren one who do not bear; break forth and cry out, you who are not in labor, for many more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband. For when the barren woman has borne seven, and she who had had many sons has been weakened, and through the trespass of the Jewish people the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel will be saved, and the remnant of his brothers will be converted to the sons of Israel; and Elijah the prophet, whose name means God the Lord, coming, will turn the heart of the fathers to the sons, and the heart of the sons to their fathers, and the last people will be joined to the ancient one, so that they may truly be called sons of Abraham, when they have believed in him whom Abraham saw and rejoiced. But what is the time in which the barren woman shall bring forth? As I suppose, that of which Isaiah speaks: In an acceptable time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have helped you. Paul too, understanding this as foretold of the time of Christ, says: Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation. I think that this very thing is also shown mystically by what is written in Ecclesiastes: A time to bring forth, and a time to die; because at the time when the people of the Gentiles was born of the barren woman, the synagogue lost her children. It can, however, be understood otherwise: the Lord will give up the temple and Jerusalem and the Jews until that time when the virgin shall bring forth; and after she has brought forth, and the child that was born has taken the spoils of Samaria and the strength of Damascus, when the people of the Jews has been slain, the remnant of Israel will be saved. And the brothers of Christ, that is, the apostles, will be converted to the faith of the prophets and patriarchs, who announced that Christ would come, and the prophecy of the psalm will be fulfilled: In place of your fathers sons have been born to you, and the rest that follows.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.4":["And he shall stand and feed in the strength of the Lord, in the loftiness of the name of the Lord his God, and they shall be converted, for now he shall be magnified to the ends of the earth. LXX: And he shall stand and see, and the Lord shall feed his flock in strength, and they shall be in the glory of the name of the Lord their God, for now they shall be magnified to the ends of the earth. After the sun of justice has gone forth from the height of the heavens and has come to their ends, and she who brings forth has brought forth, and the remnant of his brothers has been converted to the sons of Israel, then the Lord himself, who before was walking, and, together with those who were set on the road, did not yet have a fixed step, will stand and feed them in the strength of the Lord, so that they can say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me; by the waters of refreshment he has nourished me; he has converted my soul. And he feeds them not only in the strength of the Lord, but also in the loftiness of the name of the Lord his God, when he says to the Father: Holy Father, keep in your name those whom you have given me, that they may be one, even as we are. When I was with them, I kept them in your name; those whom you have given me I have guarded, and none of them has perished. And they shall be converted, or, as Symmachus has better rendered it, they shall dwell; for the Hebrew word iasubu signifies both. And they shall dwell in the Church of the Lord, because Christ has been magnified to the end of the earth. Or, according to the LXX, because they themselves shall be magnified with their shepherd to the uttermost part of the earth, so that into all the earth there may go out their sound, and their words to the ends of the world.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.5-6":["And we shall raise up against him seven shepherds, and eight principal men. And they shall feed the land of Asshur with the sword, and the land of Nimrod with its lances; and he shall deliver from Asshur when he comes into our land, and when he treads within our borders. LXX: And seven shepherds shall rise up against him, and eight bites of men; and they shall feed Asshur with the sword, into the land of Nimrod in its pit; and he shall deliver from Asshur, when he comes upon your land, and when he goes up upon your borders. Where we have put principal men, and in the Hebrew is written nesiche adam, Symmachus rendered christs (anointed ones) of men; Theodotion and the Fifth Edition, princes of men; Aquila, weighty, or established men, that is, καθεσταμένους. Again, where Aquila and I translated with its lances, so that of the land of Nimrod is understood, Symmachus turned it ἐντὸς πυλῶν αὐτῆς, that is, within its gates; Theodotion, in their gates; the Fifth Edition, ἐν παραξίφεσιν αὐτῶν, which we can render, with their daggers; but in the Hebrew is set baphethee.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.5":["And this one shall be peace: when the Assyrian comes into our land, and when he treads in our houses. LXX: And this shall be peace: when the Assyrian comes upon your land, and when he goes up upon your country. When the devil, who is interpreted εὐθύνων, that is, reproving and rebuking, comes upon the land and country of believers, and of those whom the Lord will feed in strength and in the loftiness of the name of the Lord his God, and treads them down with various tribulations, and like a proud man goes up upon the houses of our souls, that is, our bodies, and presses them down, and yet nothing separates us from the love of Christ; then the peace of Christ, or Christ himself, will be in us, and it will be said of the saint: The enemy shall gain nothing over him. Let us set out an example, so that what we are saying may become plainer. The Assyrian once came upon the land of Paul and went up upon his country, when he was in labors beyond measure, in blows more abundantly, in prisons exceedingly, in deaths often, when he received from the Jews five times forty lashes less one, was beaten with rods three times, was stoned once, suffered shipwreck three times, was a night and a day in the depth of the sea, in perils from robbers, in perils among false brothers, in perils from my own race, in perils from the Gentiles; but in all these he conquered because of him who had loved him. And therefore he was filled with peace, because he had been filled with insults. The Jews contend that all these things will come to pass according to the letter at the coming of the Christ whom they feign for themselves.\n— Commentary on Micah","Then, therefore, there will be peace, when the remnant of Christ's brothers has been converted to the sons of Israel, and the Assyrian has come into our land, and, helped by the Lord, we have swiftly crushed under our feet him who desired to tread our houses. For the Lord himself says: We shall raise up against him seven shepherds, and eight principal ones of men, or bites of men. And we shall raise up, I and my Son and the Holy Spirit, according to what is written in Genesis: Let us make man after our image and likeness. And at the beginning of Obadiah: Arise, and let us go up against her to battle. The seven shepherds I take to be all the patriarchs and prophets and holy men who served the hebdomad, that is, the old instrument. But the eight bites of men, or eight principal men, and, as Symmachus rendered it, christs, are all those of the New Testament who, from the apostles down to this age, have bitten the Assyrian and torn him with their teeth. Hence also in Ecclesiastes the command is given: That we should give portions to seven, let us give also to eight. And in the temple of Ezekiel, which is interpreted as the Church and the heavenly Jerusalem, one goes up by seven and by eight steps. And in the psalter there are fifteen steps, by which, through the hebdomad and the ogdoad, we climb to sing praises to God. On the eighth day the spiritual circumcision is celebrated, and in the temple of the Jews the sabbath is destroyed, and some psalms are inscribed for the eighth.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.6":["These seven shepherds, then, and eight bites of men, shall feed Asshur with the sword. For the word of God, living and effective, and sharper than any two-edged sword, was sent by him who came to send a sword upon the earth, so that two might be divided against three. And the land of Nimrod, which means temptation descending, they shall feed in its pit; for the land of the giant and hunter, and of him who is proud against the Lord, is not on the mountains but in pits. He fell like lightning from heaven, and is always busy among the beasts; and because he is a hunter, he roams the fruitless trees and the forests. So far, then, as I can recall in my memory, I have never read of a hunter in a good sense. Ishmael and Esau were hunters, and they went before as a type of the Jewish people: one of them is the son of the Egyptian woman, walking according to the flesh and living according to the flesh; the other lost his birthright for a dish of lentils, and, envying his brother's blessings, drives him off into Mesopotamia. The land of Nimrod, then, is reduced into its own pits: He who indeed digs a pit shall fall into it, and he who opened a cistern and dug it out shall fall into the pit which he made, and his pain shall return upon his head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his crown. And when the Assyrian had been pierced through by the seven shepherds and the eight bites, Christ delivered us from the hand of Asshur, who had come upon our land and desired to tread the borders of Israel. But as for what Symmachus says, And they shall feed the land of Asshur with the sword, and the country of Nimrod within its gates, this is to be understood: that the strong man is bound in his own house and the adversary is wounded. And according to Aquila and the Fifth Edition, that he is pierced through by the daggers and lances of the seven shepherds and of the eight christs of men.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.7-15":["But the Hebrews dream ravings of this kind: after the seven shepherds, whom they invent and choose at will, and eight princely men have conquered the Assyrians and have fed upon the land of Nimrod with their swords, so that this shall have happened when the Assyrian has first come into the land of Judah, then, they say, when Christ comes, all the remnant of Jacob that has been able to survive among the nations will be in blessing, like dew coming from the Lord and like rain upon the grass, and they will by no means hope in men and in the sons of men, but in God. And they will be in the midst of the nations and peoples bloodthirsty and cruel, avenging themselves on their former masters, like a lion among the beasts of the forests and like a lion's whelp among the flocks of cattle, and there will be no one who can resist their strength. Then your hand, O God, or O Israel, will be exalted over the Assyrians, and all your foes and enemies who now possess you will perish. In that day, namely, when you have been freed from the nations, I will take away your horses and four-horse chariots which are in the midst of your cities. Not that Israel had horses and four-horse chariots then, but the horses and four-horse chariots of the Assyrians which move about in the midst of your cities; and I will destroy all your cities, and the strongholds which you dedicated to idols; and I will take away the magicians and soothsayers from your land, and I will make all your carved images and statues perish, and you shall no longer worship the works of your hands. I will also pluck up and overturn all your groves, and your cities which you had dedicated to idols. And when I have done this to you, and have been so appeased that whatever evil is in you is taken away, then also in my fury and indignation I will avenge you on all the nations which were unwilling to hear my word. Let carnal Israel answer at this place: does it say that these things have happened, or that they are to come? If it recalls them as done, let it produce the history, let it give the authority of the ancient books; let it show that all the nations and the Assyrian were once subject to Israel. But if, mocking itself with vain hope, it thinks that what is said is to come, when Christ comes, what idols will then be taken away from Israel which it does not now worship? What groves will be cut down which it does not have? What cities will be overthrown which were overthrown long ago? What soothsayers will be removed? Though it does not have them, nor boasts of having them, yet for so long a time the daughter of Zion has been abandoned, and sits without an altar and without priests, and while others eat their produce, they themselves, with dry throats, promise themselves a future which they do not know.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.7":["And the remnant of Jacob shall be in the midst of many peoples, like dew from the Lord, and like drops upon the grass, which does not wait for man, and does not tarry for the sons of men. And the remnant of Jacob shall be among the nations, in the midst of many peoples, like a lion among the beasts of the forests, and like a lion's whelp among the flocks of cattle, which, when it has passed through and trampled and seized, there is none to rescue. Your hand shall be exalted over your foes, and all your enemies shall perish. And it shall come to pass in that day, says the Lord, I will take away your horses from your midst, and I will destroy your four-horse chariots, and I will ruin the cities of your land, and I will pull down all your strongholds, and I will take away sorceries from your hand, and there shall be no divinations in you. And I will make your carved images perish, and your statues from your midst, and you shall no longer worship the works of your hands. And I will pluck up your groves from your midst, and I will crush your cities, and I will execute vengeance in fury and in indignation on all the nations which have not heard. LXX: And the remnant of Jacob shall be among the nations, in the midst of many peoples, like dew falling from the Lord, and like lambs upon the grass, so that no one may be gathered nor be among the sons of men. And the remnant of Jacob shall be among the nations, in the midst of many peoples, like a lion among the beasts, in the forest, and like a lion's whelp among the flocks of cattle; as if it should pass through and, dividing, seize, and there be none to rescue. Your hand shall be lifted up over those who afflict you, and all your enemies shall perish. And it shall come to pass in that day, says the Lord, I will kill your horses from your midst, and I will destroy your chariots, and I will overturn the cities of your land, and I will take away all your strongholds. And I will take away your sorceries from your hands, and those who speak shall not be in you; and I will destroy your carved images, and your statues from your midst, and you shall no longer worship the works of your hands; and I will cut down the groves from your midst, and I will demolish your cities, and I will execute vengeance in anger and in fury on the nations, because they have not heard. When we have been delivered from Asshur, when he comes upon our land and goes up upon our borders, and delivered by the Lord, who raised up against him seven shepherds and eight bites of men, the remnant of Jacob, by which we understand the apostles and the first Church, the one from the Jews, shall be in the midst of many peoples like dew falling from the Lord. For the hearts of the peoples were burning with the fiery darts of the devil, and all the nations, committing adultery away from God, had hearts like an oven kindled with fire. Hence the dew falling from the Lord became the healing of the sick. And what we read concerning Hananiah, Azariah and Mishael, that the spirit of a whistling dew extinguished the furnace of fire, let us understand of all generally: that in the midst of all the nations the teaching of the apostles was like dew from the Lord. But what follows: And like lambs upon the grass, so that no one may be gathered nor be among the sons of men, let us take of those of the nations who were unwilling to believe, that the apostles and the remnant of Jacob are over them, like lambs cropping the grass and mowing down the herbage with their teeth. And they will do this so that those who were unwilling to be angels by receiving the spiritual dew may by no means be gathered among men, nor be reckoned in the rational part, but that it may be said of them: They are not in the labor of men, and they will not be scourged with men.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.8":["And the remnant of Jacob shall be in the midst of the nations like a lion among the beasts in the forest, and like a lion's whelp among the flocks of sheep. For it is the Lord Jesus of whom it is prophesied in Genesis: A lion's whelp is Judah; from the shoot, my son, you have gone up; lying down you slept like a lion and like a lion's whelp. And in another place: Lying down he rested like a lion, and like a lion's whelp; who shall rouse him? He gave this also to his apostles, to whom he had said: Go, baptize all nations in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, so that, just as no one can resist a lion among the beasts, or a lion's whelp among the sheep, so they, delivered from the hand of the Assyrian and from the hand of the devil, might seize beasts and cattle; for the Lord will save men and beasts. And they would seize, not to kill, but to divide, that is, to separate them from the unbelievers, and there would be none to resist them.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.9":["And as the lion and the lion's whelp thus range among the beasts and among the sheep, the hand of God is exalted over those who before afflicted either God or the remnant of Israel. And all his enemies will be destroyed, not that the enemies perish and cease to exist, but that they perish in that they are enemies. As it is written to the Thessalonians: Whom the Lord Jesus will kill with the spirit of his mouth. This killing signifies not abolition but the cessation of the most wicked life by which they had lived badly before. In short, he adds: And he will destroy him by the brightness of his coming. But he would never destroy him if killing meant abolition, since he would already have ceased to exist; so here too the consuming of the enemies is to be understood according to the Proverbs of Solomon, in which it is written: Destruction will lodge with the ungodly: it will not be perpetual, it will not reduce them to nothing, but it will lodge with them as long as it takes for the ungodliness that is in them to be consumed. For God created man that he should not perish, and he did not make death.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.10":["After this there follows: And it shall come to pass in that day, when your hand has been lifted up over your foes, O Israel, and all your enemies have perished, I will kill your horses from your midst, that is, from the ruling part of your heart, the wanton impulses that rush forth like horses when their bonds are broken, and your chariots, in which you pleased yourself in your vices, and, joining sins to sins, were carried along as if triumphing in four-horse chariots.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.11":["And I will destroy the cities of your land: for you did not build the city which the rush of the river of God makes glad, and which is set on the mountains, and the heavenly Jerusalem, but the one which Cain had built. Hence they are called cities of the land, built with earthly works. And I will take away, he says, all your strongholds, namely riches and the pomp of the world, and the eloquence of rhetoricians and the little snares of dialecticians, in which you trusted as in strongholds.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.12":["And I will take away sorceries from your hands, by which either you yourself were deceived by others, or, being deceived, you deceived others; and those who speak, or pronounce, shall not be yours. Now the world is full of speakers and pronouncers; they speak what they do not know, they teach what they have not learned; they are teachers, though they were not pupils before. When, therefore, God teaches man knowledge, false presumptions will cease, and perverse doctrine will be removed.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.13":["And I will destroy your carved images and your pillars from your midst. Our carved images are those which were made by the speakers, or which we fashion for ourselves. Hence it is commanded in the law that we should not make a carved image for ourselves, and that we should not set up pillars in our land; and you shall no longer worship the works of your hands. Unhappy human condition, full of folly and error! It knows from what sense its doctrines were composed, it is not ignorant that the idol was feigned by itself, and yet in place of God it worships the works of its own hands, and man bows down, so that, deceived, he may deceive.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.14":["There is added, in the promise which is directed to the remnant, that is, the ὑπόλειμμα of Jacob: And I will cut down the groves from your midst, and I will destroy your cities, so that he may pull down and consume all the trees of the forests, and the groves which it was forbidden to plant in the temple of God, and the badly built cities which he had earlier called the cities of the land.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.5.15":["And after he has done this for the remnant of Jacob, then they will turn to the nations which the apostles grazed down like lambs grazing the grass. And because they were unwilling to receive the dew of speech, I will execute, he says, vengeance in anger and in fury on the nations, because they were unwilling to hear. Hence the prophet also says: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your anger. Let this be what we have discussed according to the Seventy translators, since it is the spiritual understanding, and we had to join the sense to the earlier sections.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.1-2":["Hear what the Lord says: Arise, contend in judgment against the mountains, and let the hills hear your voice. Hear, O mountains, the judgment of the Lord, and you strong foundations of the earth, for the judgment of the Lord will be decided with his people, and with Israel: LXX. Hear what the Lord has spoken: Arise, be judged before the mountains, and let the hills hear your voice. Hear, O mountains, the judgment of the Lord, and you valleys, foundations of the earth, for the judgment of the Lord is against his people, and with Israel it will be judged. For 'strong foundations of the earth', which the LXX rendered 'and valleys, foundations of the earth', Symmachus and Theodotion translated 'and ancient foundations of the earth'; but the Fifth Edition set down the Hebrew itself, ethanim, foundations of the earth. The first utterance, then, is the prophet's: Hear what the Lord says. Then God speaks to the prophet: Arise, contend in judgment against the mountains, and let the hills hear your voice. Again the prophet, as he had been commanded, speaks to the mountains, and not only to the mountains but also to the strong foundations of the earth, and says: Hear, O mountains, the judgment of the Lord, and you strong foundations of the earth. And he gives the reason why he urges them to hear: because the judgment of the Lord will be decided with his people, and with Israel. For the mountains to which the prophet speaks, and for the strong foundations of the earth, the LXX translated hills and valleys, understanding, as it seems to me, that the people had done nothing worthy of the hearing of mountains, but only of hills, which are lower than the loftiness of the mountains, or of valleys sunk into the depths. Arise, he says, contend in judgment before the mountains, and let the hills hear your voice. He is commanded to arise who either sits, or lies, or sleeps, or is dead, according to what the Apostle says: Rise, you who sleep, and arise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. Arise from the dead, so that you may walk in newness of life, so that, leaving the earth, you may strive toward higher things. And contend in judgment against the mountains, by which I think nothing else is signified than the angels, to whom the care of human affairs has been entrusted, the Song in Deuteronomy agreeing on this very point: When the Most High divided the nations, when he scattered the sons of Adam, he set the bounds of the earth according to the number of the angels of God. These are ministering spirits, sent for service on account of those who are to possess the inheritance of salvation. And contend in judgment, so that, whether the mountains or the hills are found not to have cared for the peoples worthily, either it may be seen to be my fault, since I set such ones over them, or the blame may be taken from the people and referred to the princes. Let us read the Apocalypse of John the apostle, in which the angels of the churches are praised and accused for the virtues and vices of those over whom they are said to preside. For just as sometimes it is the bishop's fault, sometimes the people's, and often the teacher is answerable for the sin, often the pupil, and now it is the father's failing, now the son's, according as they are taught well or badly, so in the judgment of God the charge will be referred either to the angels, if they have not done all that belonged to their office, or to the people, if, while the angels did everything, they themselves scorned to hear. There are some who interpret the mountains and hills and strong foundations of the earth as Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and the rest of the patriarchs, before whom, as hearers called together to the judgment, the case of the people of Israel is to be argued. Others think the mountains, hills and valleys are angels, as we said above, who either serve God in the heavenly places, or preside over men upon this earth, or, being set in the underworld, are called the foundations of those who through their own fault became earthly; of these foundations of the earth we find it written elsewhere too: A fire is kindled from my fury, it will burn down to the underworld below; it will devour the earth and its foundations. The strong and ancient foundations of the earth — on account of which the earth has not passed away until now, and, hanging over the void, stands balanced — are the merits of the just, of whom the Apostle speaks: Built upon the foundations of the apostles and prophets. Just as, then, the apostles and the prophets and the whole choir of martyrs are the strong foundations of the earth, so, according to the LXX, the valleys and precipices, which in Greek are more expressively called φάραγγες, are the foundations of those who have received the image of the χοϊκοῦ (of the earthy one). The judgment of the Lord, therefore, will be decided with his people, and with Israel. He who, as God, could inflict punishments for the crimes of a sinful people does not wish to appear powerful, but just, and he summons the sinners to judgment, according to that prophetic word: Come, and let us judge, says the Lord; even now he presses the people of Israel, in the presence of the angels and of all creation, to answer if it has anything to answer, so that God may be justified in his words and may prevail when he is judged.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.3-4":["God, therefore, speaks to the people of Israel, and summons them to judgment, and grants them leave to dispute against him. My people, what have I done to you that I ought not to have done? Or how have I grieved you? although this is not found in the Hebrew. But a father grieves the son he has scourged, and the shepherd visits the iniquities of the sheep with the rod. In what have I been troublesome to you? Or, as is written more expressively in the Hebrew, with what toil have I oppressed you? Or will you interpret my benefits as an insult, and, longing for the melons and the meats of Egypt, will you grieve that you were brought out of the land of Egypt and freed from the house of bondage by my help, because I gave you as leaders Moses my friend, and Aaron my priest, and Miriam my prophetess?\n— Commentary on Micah","But we who long to contemplate the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, and truly have Abraham as our father, let us hear God, when we have sinned, disputing against us and reproving us by the greatness of his benefits. For we once served Pharaoh, and made mud and bricks for the Egyptian people, and he redeemed us who gave himself as a redemption for all, so that we who had been redeemed by the Lord, whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemies and whom he gathered from the regions, might say: For his mercy is forever. He also sent before our face Moses, the spiritual law, and Aaron, the great priest, not bearing the typical ephod and the truth, but having on his forehead the seal of holiness which God the Father sealed. And he sent Miriam, the prophecy of the prophets; and not only did he grant us this, but he also freed us from the hands of the enemies.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.5":["For the torrent of cords, or of thorns, Symmachus rendered the valley of thorns. Now there is a place near Livias, beyond the Dead Sea, six miles distant from Livias, where Israel once committed fornication with the Midianites. This place the prophet Micah mentions in the person of God, saying: My people, remember, I pray, what Balak king of Moab devised, and what Balaam son of Beor answered, from Settim to Gilgal. For which there too the Seventy translated ἀπὸ τῶν σχοίνων, that is, from the cords to Gilgal.\n— Commentary on Joel","My people, what have I done to you, and in what have I been troublesome to you, answer me, for I brought you up out of the land of Egypt, and from the house of servants I freed you, and I sent before your face Moses, and Aaron and Miriam. My people, remember, I pray, what Balak king of Moab devised against you, and what Balaam son of Beor answered him, from Shittim to Gilgal, that you might know the justices of the Lord. LXX: My people, what have I done to you, or how have I grieved you, or in what have I been troublesome to you? Answer me: for I brought you up out of the land of Egypt, and from the house of bondage I redeemed you, and I sent before your face Moses, and Aaron and Miriam. My people, remember what Balak king of Moab devised against you, and what Balaam son of Beor answered him, from Schoenis to Gilgal, that the justice of the Lord might be known. For justices or justice Symmachus rendered mercies, and where the LXX has Schoenis, all rendered the Hebrew itself, Settim. Now it is a place in which Balak king of the Moabites gathered an army against Israel, ὁμώνυμος (of the same name) with the trees which even today grow throughout the desert of Mount Sinai. For where in the LXX the ark of the covenant, or the altar and the tabernacle, and the other things which are reported to have been made of incorruptible wood are mentioned, in the Hebrew Settim wood is set down, which has the likeness of the tree that we commonly call the white thorn. Hence I think that the LXX too rendered σχῖνον, that is, the mastic tree, but that gradually, through the error of copyists, it came about that σχοῖνοι, that is, ropes, was read instead of σχίνοις, that is, mastic trees.\n— Commentary on Micah","But if this seems little to you, remember that time when Balak king of Moab hired Balaam the diviner for a fee against you, and see how, against his own will, the soothsayer, wishing to curse you, blessed you; from Shittim to Gilgal, surveying with his eyes the whole army of Israel and changing places, as if I could not go with him as he went and pass over with him as he passed over; and this I did so that my mercies and justices might be known to you, for I love you so much that, while I am cursed daily by the mouth of blasphemers, I did not allow you to be cursed by the enemy. The Hebrews expound this passage, in which it is said: From Shittim to Gilgal, that you might know the justices of the Lord, thus: From the time when you committed fornication in Midian until the time when Saul was anointed king at Gilgal, recall to memory what evils you have done, and how great the good things I have done for you, and you will know my mercy toward you. Let this be what God spoke to carnal Israel according to the history.\n— Commentary on Micah","For let us remember what he devised against us who wished to devour us and lick up our congregation: the true Balak, the devil. For Balak is interpreted ἐκλείχων, that is, licking up, king of the paternal water; since Moab, according to another etymology, is called paternal water. When, therefore, Balak was lying in wait for us, and lying in wait through his vain people, which is what Balaam is interpreted, God did not allow us to lie under his curses, but on the contrary there blessed us, compelled by the truth of the thing itself, the vain people of the nations, born of him who is in the skin — for Beor is interpreted in the skin — ever given over to the flesh and to the works of death; and the vain people, sprung from him who is wholly in the skin, answered on our behalf, always changing places, or standing upon thorns, or upon cords, so that we may follow the error of the common edition too. Now thorns, according to the Savior's words, are the cares of this world, and riches, and pleasures, in which the vain people is. And that it stands in ropes, namely in the bonds of sins — for with the ropes of his own sins each one is bound — Isaiah also is witness, saying: Woe to those who drag out their sins like a long rope, and iniquities like the strap of a heifer's yoke. If, then, it stands, it stands only on thorns and on ropes; but if it wishes to go around, it has no steady step, but, always wavering and tottering, it comes as far as Gilgal, which is interpreted κυλισμὸς, that is, rolling, or a wallowing-place. Whenever we see some rising up against us, and thirsting for our blood with greedy jaws, and, by an unforeseen dispensation of God, those who had come against us coming over to our side, let us say: Balaam came from the cords as far as Gilgal, that the justice of God may be known.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.6-7":["What worthy thing shall I offer to the Lord? Shall I bend the knee to God most high? Shall I offer him burnt offerings, and yearling calves? Can the Lord be appeased with thousands of rams, or with many thousands of fat he-goats? Shall I give my firstborn for my crime, the fruit of my womb for the sin of my soul? LXX: With what shall I lay hold of the Lord? Shall I receive my God most high? Shall I lay hold of him with burnt offerings, with yearling calves? Will the Lord accept, with thousands of rams, or with ten thousands of fat he-goats? If I give my firstborn for impiety: the fruits of my womb for the sin of my soul. God had summoned the people to judgment; the people, knowing its sin, does not wish to contend, but to entreat, and yet even in its very prayers it has no confidence. For there is nothing worthy that can be offered to God for sin, and no humility can wash out the stains of offenses, because it is impossible for the blood of bulls and of calves, and burnt offerings full of marrow, and the gore of rams and of fat he-goats, to wash away the filth of the soul. Shall I, he says, give my firstborn for my crime, as the king of Moab is described as having done? Or the fruit of my womb for the sin of my soul, as Jephthah did, offering his daughter for the rashness of a vow? We, therefore, who are of the people of God, knowing that there shall not be justified in the sight of him anyone living, and saying: I have become a beast before you, repenting for our sins, hesitate and say: With what shall I lay hold of the Lord? Shall I receive my Lord most high? How shall I be able to hold him as he flees? With how great purity shall I be able to prepare a lodging for the Trinity? Or shall I lay hold of him with burnt offerings, so as to offer my whole self to him as a burnt offering, or with yearling calves, so that, leaving milk and coming to solid food, I may be made worthy of the acceptable year of the Lord? If I offer a thousand rams, if ten thousand he-goats, and, understanding them spiritually, display in myself all the victims of Leviticus, and a thousand fall at my side, and ten thousand at my left, yet I shall be able to give nothing worthy with which to lay hold of God or receive him. If I give my firstborn for impiety, and the fruit of my womb for the sin of my soul, I shall indeed give whatever is first in me, but for my sin and impiety I shall offer nothing worthy to God. Hence David also prays and says: Wash me more and more from my iniquity, and cleanse me from my sin. For I know my iniquities, and my offense is ever against me. For the sin of the soul blood alone is worthily offered, and the blood that is worthily offered is not that of calves, not of rams, not of he-goats, but one's own, as the prophet says and asks: What shall I render to the Lord for all that he has rendered to me? And afterwards answers: I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord. Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints. Yet even this very blood we do not give, but give back. And what is there alike? When the Just One has died for sinners, the Son of God for men, shall we, sinners and men, die for the confession of his name?\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.8":["I will show you, O man, what is good, and what the Lord requires of you: surely to do judgment, and to love mercy, and to walk carefully with your God. LXX: It has been declared to you, man, what is good, or what the Lord requires of you, except that you do judgment, and love mercy, and be ready to walk with your God. Since you are in doubt, O people of Israel — or rather the whole human race, for I am by no means speaking to the people of the Jews, but my speech will run out generally to every man — how you can appease God for your sins, if you do not even have victims by which your impiety may be compensated, I will answer you what God requires; indeed, I have already shown it before in the law. For it is written in Deuteronomy: And now, Israel, what does the Lord your God require of you, except that you fear the Lord your God, and walk in all his ways, and love him; and serve the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul, and keep the commandments of the Lord your God and his justifications, whatever I shall command you today, that it may be well with you? The Lord requires of us, and, counting our salvation necessary, asks to receive what profits the giver: that we do judgment, that is, that we do nothing without reason and counsel, so that the mind first judges what it is going to do and afterwards completes it in deed; that we love mercy, and are merciful not as if compelled or out of necessity, since God loves a cheerful giver. Nor let us say: Go today, and come back tomorrow, and I will give to you. And when we have done judgment and loved mercy, what reward shall we receive? We shall walk with the Lord God, as Enoch, according to the faith of the Hebrew scrolls, walked with God, and pleased him, and he was not found, because God translated him. For you said: With what shall I attain to the Lord, or with what shall I lay hold of him? I promise you more: do judgment, and love mercy, and you will walk with your God. Or certainly, to walk with God is not a reward but a precept. For just as we are commanded to do judgment and to love mercy, so it is enjoined that we be prepared to walk with the Lord our God; we ought to sleep at no hour, to be secure at no time, but always to expect the householder's coming and to dread the day of judgment, and in the night of this world to say: I sleep, and my heart keeps watch. The word esne, which the LXX translated 'to be ready', and we 'to walk carefully', Theodotion expressed more significantly: καὶ ἀσφαλίζου τοῦ πορεύεσθαι μετὰ Ἐλωαὶχ, that is: And take careful heed that you walk with your God, or, as the Fifth Edition translated: καὶ φροντίζειν, namely to act carefully, and to have this care, that you walk with your God. For he who says that he believes in Christ ought so to walk as he also walked. And the apostle Paul: Be imitators of me, he says, as I am of Christ.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.9-12":["Let us therefore return to the beginning of the section. And, discussing it first according to the letter, let us, with the help of your prayers, strive to attain the spiritual sense. Hear, ten tribes of Samaria, what the Lord solemnly declares to you: Still there is fire, that is, iniquity, in the house of the ungodly Omri, and the treasures of ungodliness persist in the royal house. Do you wish to hear, one by one, the evils of which your city is full? Learn: a scant measure that provokes the wrath of God, a deceitful balance and differing weights, and men who sell their wares by one weight and buy by another. And if indeed the poor were doing this, their want could have defended the crime by necessity. But as it is, her rich men are full not so much of riches as of iniquity, because all riches, while they despoil others, are begotten by iniquity. Lying follows the amassing of riches, and the hand accustomed to storing up treasures possesses a deceitful tongue. Truth begets poverty, lying begets riches.\n— Commentary on Micah","Thus far we have set out what seemed right to us according to the Hebrew; now, returning to the translation of the LXX translators, let us discuss each point as best we can. The Samaritan tribe, which cut itself off from the people of God, is called to hear. And it is told: In vain you fashion idols, you put together golden calves with a craftsman's hand, and in imitation of Jerusalem you wish to build another metropolis; for who will be able to adorn the city? Can fire, which is kindled by the flaming darts of the devil, and the house of the wicked man, which according to its hardness and impenitent heart treasures up for itself wrath on the day of wrath, and of the revelation of the just judgment of God? And injury increases iniquity, so that it not only plunders from the house of God, which is the Church, but also lays waste what belongs to others with pride and haughtiness. Can he be justified who, without balance and weight, fraudulently gathers his riches from the testimonies of the Scriptures, riches which are treasures of iniquity? For although the Lord commands: There shall not be in your bag a great weight and a lesser, these men, for the sake of shameful gain, always respect persons in judgment, and in the same case judge rich and poor now one way, now another, not according to the matter but according to the difference of their means; and the inhabitants of their city, which they think they are adorning with depraved doctrines and perverse teaching, speak lies, and set their mouth on high, and despise the simplicity of the people of the Church.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.9":["The voice of the Lord cries to the city, and there will be salvation for those who fear your name. LXX: The voice of the Lord will cry to the city, and he will save those who fear his name. In the Hebrew this is the beginning of the next section, but with the Seventy translators it is the end of the preceding one; and it has this sense: Your God asks nothing else of you, O man, except that you do judgment, and love mercy, and be ready to walk with your God. For the voice of the Lord is heard in his city, the Church, and resounds daily in the holy Scriptures, so that not only those who love mercy, but also those who are lower and still fear the name of the Lord, may be saved by his teaching and clemency. But if it is the beginning of the following section, let us refer it, according to the history, as said to Samaria, the metropolis of the ten tribes, which was captured while Micah was prophesying, and let us say: The Lord rebukes Samaria and threatens her with the blows that are to come, so that the people of Judah, or those who fear the name of the Lord, hearing that others suffer punishments, may themselves be seized with fear and obtain salvation. For when the pestilent man is scourged, not only the wise man but even the fool will become more prudent. And you will refer this very thing to sinners in general and to the just, so that the torment of some may become an example to the rest. This indeed the Lord also explains in the Gospel concerning those on whom the tower in Siloam had fallen: that they were not the only sinners in the whole people, but that their punishment was meant to provoke the rest to repentance.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.13-15":["When your princes were doing this, I did not wish to overthrow you at once, but little by little I began to strike you and to warn you with various blows. I sent famine upon you, I sent thirst, I sent diseases, and devastation by the enemy round about; the crop did not bring forth grain, the pressed olive did not yield oil, barren vintages denied the wine. Against the iniquities, and the deceitful measures, and the fraudulent weights, I brought these punishments.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.13":["Hence the most merciful God does not strike them all at once, but strives little by little to admonish them through blows, saying: And I will begin to strike you with ruin on account of your sins; and the sense is: O city which the heretics wish to build, I will strike you so that you perish, not unto abolition, but in so far as you are a sinner.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.14":["There follows: You shall eat, and not be satisfied. For they read and do not understand; and feeding on the words of the Scriptures, they suffer a want of truth. And I will cast out, he says, you; and you shall lay hold, and shall not save; and whoever shall be saved shall be handed over to the sword. I will abandon you, he says, to your own judgment; and after you have sought many things and found nothing, understanding your error, you will see that you cannot be saved by all your doctrines. But whoever think themselves satisfied, and have not been cast out from themselves, nor have grasped the truth, will be handed over to the sword, and will be instructed by punishments.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.15":["You shall sow, therefore, O tribe, and O most wicked city, which the heretics build with fire, and iniquity, and insults, and a deceitful balance, and a fraudulent bag; you shall sow, and not reap; you shall press the olive, and not be anointed with oil; and the grapes, and not drink wine. Indeed it profits you, once your error is recognized, that you may not have disciples, that you may not anoint your head with the oil of sinners, that you may not make drunk with the wine of Sodom those who drink it.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.6.16":["Hear, O tribes, and who will approve it?\n\nStill there is fire in the house of the ungodly, treasures of ungodliness, and a scant measure full of wrath. Shall I justify an ungodly balance and the deceitful weights of the bag? By which her rich men were filled with iniquity, and those who dwell in her spoke lies, and their tongue is deceitful in their mouth. And I therefore have begun to strike you with ruin for your sins. You shall eat and not be satisfied, and your humiliation is in the midst of you; and you shall lay hold and shall not save; and those whom you save I will give to the sword. You shall sow and not reap, you shall tread the olive and not be anointed with oil, and the must, and not drink wine. And you have kept the precepts of Omri and every work of the house of Ahab; and you have walked in their wills, that I might give you up to ruin, and those who dwell in her to hissing, and you shall bear the reproach of my people. LXX: Hear, O tribe, and who will adorn the city? Is it fire, and the house of the wicked man treasuring up wicked treasures, and iniquities with injury? Shall the wicked man be justified in the balance, and deceitful weights in the bag, from which they have filled up the riches of their ungodliness, and those who dwell in her have spoken wicked things, and their tongue is exalted in their mouth. And I have tormented you with ruin on account of your sins; you shall eat, and not be satisfied; and I will cast you out into yourself, and you shall lay hold, and shall not save; and whoever shall be saved shall be handed over to the sword; you shall sow and not reap; you shall press the olive and not be anointed with oil; and the grape, and not drink wine; and the statutes of my people shall be scattered, and all the works of the house of Ahab; and you have walked in their counsels, that I might hand you over to ruin, and its inhabitants to hissing, and you shall receive the reproaches of the peoples. In this section the LXX disagree greatly with the Hebrew truth, and most of all at the beginning: Hear, O tribe, and who will adorn the city? And the statutes of my people shall be scattered. In place of which we have put, for the sake of the coherence of the discourse: And you have kept the precepts of Omri, although in the Hebrew too it is written: And the precepts of Omri have been kept, and all the works of the house of Ahab. For if Ammi had been written in the Hebrew, the LXX would rightly have translated it 'of my people'; but as it is, since Amri is written, with the letter res added, it denotes not the name of the people but the father of Ahab, of whom the history of Kings relates, and no one is in doubt that it is an error. In short, after the father's name the son is placed, and it is said: And all the works of the house of Ahab.\n— Commentary on Micah","But because you kept all the ceremonies of idolatry which Omri, the ungodly king, had established, and observed every work of the house of Ahab and Jezebel in place of my law, I was driven by your crime to give you and your inhabitants up to hissing, and that you should bear the reproach of my people, while, taken captive by the Assyrians, you are conquered as if you were the people of God, and on your account my name is blasphemed among the nations. It should be noted in the present passage that where we read: And you shall bear the reproach of my people, or, as the LXX translated: You shall receive the reproaches of the peoples, Ammi is written in the Hebrew for 'of my people'. If, then, Ammi means 'of my people', no one doubts that above, for Amri, 'my people' was wrongly translated.\n— Commentary on Micah","And the statutes of my people, or of Omri, shall be destroyed, and all the works of the house of Ahab, of those who stood forth as patriarchs and princes in the heresies. These we can refer either to the contrary powers or to the heresiarchs, such as were Marcion and Basilides, and recently Arius and Eunomius. And you walked in their wills, namely those of Omri and Ahab. And he says well, in their wills. For the teaching of wicked masters is not the teaching of God, but the inventions of their heart. And I will hand you over to ruin, so that you perish in so far as you are heretical. And your inhabitants to hissing, either so that you may follow the hissing of the good shepherd, according to Zechariah, who says: I will hiss for them and gather them, because I have redeemed them. Or surely to the hissing of the dragon, that is, to the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved, and that, being corrected, they may learn not to blaspheme. And they will suffer all this so that they may understand their error, that they have borne the reproaches and sins of all nations and of many peoples. I know that some have referred to the Church what we have interpreted of the heresies. But how the name of Omri and Ahab, princes of Samaria, can be referred to Jerusalem and Judah, under whose names the Church is interpreted, I do not well understand.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.1":["Woe is me, for I have become like one who gathers the clusters of the vintage in autumn. There is no cluster to eat; my soul desired the first-ripe figs. The holy man has perished from the land, and there is no upright one among men. All lie in wait for blood, a man hunts his brother to death. The evil of their hands they call good; the prince demands, and the judge is for requiting, and the great man has spoken the desire of his soul, and they have troubled it. He who is best among them is like a brier, and he who is upright like a thorn from the hedge. LXX: Woe is me, for I have become like one who gathers stubble in the harvest, and like clusters in the vintage, when there is no cluster to eat the first-ripe; which my soul has suffered. Woe is me, O soul, for the reverent man has perished from the land, and there is none among men to correct; all are judged in blood, each one afflicts his neighbor with affliction, they prepare their hands for evil; the prince demands, and the judge has spoken peaceful words, it is the desire of his soul. And I will take away their goods like a devouring moth, and walking upon a rule in the day of your watching. Having foretold the captivity both of the ten tribes and of the two — for the word of the Lord came to Micah of Moresheth concerning Samaria and Jerusalem — the prophet laments that no just man from the people is found in the land who could withstand the wrath of God and set himself in the midst like a wall. In vain, he says, have I spoken; in vain have I wished to seek, as it were, the last clusters of the city already stripped of its vintage and ruined, and, since there is no cluster to eat, at least to take as food the unripe figs, which the Hebrews call bechchora, that is, the green fruit of the fig; as if he were saying: Finding no bread, because of the greatness of the famine I sought out refuse and bran.\n— Commentary on Micah","Moreover, according to the Seventy, who disagree in certain points and at the end of the section translated altogether differently, the sense seems to me to be of this kind. The prophetic word, or the apostolic, laments over the human race in general, that the word has cast the seed of doctrines in vain, and that, instead of crops and grain, the late reaper has found scarcely empty stubble and hollow stalks, and has not been able to find even small clusters in the vineyard, and the rest up to the end of the section. For if he is blessed who speaks into the ears of one who listens, and the ear of the hearer is the desire of the wise man, and an understanding hearer is the joy of the speaker, on the contrary a bad pupil is the teacher's grief; and the words of Jeremiah also agree with this complaint: I have not profited, nor has anyone profited me. There are some who think this is said in the person of the Savior, complaining that in so great a multitude of believers, and in the whole world of the human race, he scarcely finds works worthy of his blood, he who also says in Psalm 29 (30): What profit is there in my blood, while I go down into corruption? Others, however, assert that it by no means befits his person to say: Woe is me, for I have become like one who gathers stubble in the harvest, he who said in the Gospel: Lift up your eyes and see the regions, for they are already white for reaping. And elsewhere: The harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few. Those, then, who wish it to be understood in the person of the Savior say that it is no wonder if he says: Woe is me, he who both wept over Jerusalem and shed tears at the death of Lazarus. But that too, I have become like one who gathers stubble in the harvest, they refer to the consummation of the age, which they also meaningfully interpret as the harvest; and they say that this prophecy can be fulfilled at that time when, iniquity having multiplied, the love of many will have grown cold, and the Son of Man, when he comes, will find faith rare upon the earth; for then, like stubble after the harvest and like clusters after the vintage, scarcely will any be found among them who have kept the faith amid the devastation of all; and they think that this utterance, spoken in the person of the man who was assumed, is confirmed by what follows: Woe is me, O soul, the soul of which he was speaking: My soul is sorrowful, even unto death.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.2-3":["The holy man has perished from the land, and there is no upright one among men; everywhere ambushes, everywhere fraud, innocent blood is shed, for avarice and lust brotherhood is disregarded; and they not only do evil things but also defend them, and, exchanging the names, they say that what is evil is good. The princes themselves do not receive gifts from those who offer them, but compel their subjects to give, and demand them. And the judge is for requiting, judging another in the same way as he himself is judged by another, so that they may grant one another's crimes mutual favor and defend themselves in another's crime. Whoever is great and, as it were, most learned in the law speaks not God's will but his own. And they have troubled it, either the city or the truth, or the land, of which it is said above: The holy man has perished from the land.\n— Commentary on Micah","The one returning has perished from the land, either because the Antichrist kills the saints, or because all fall away at the greatness of the stumbling-blocks. And there is none among men to correct; all are judged in blood, not in light and small sins, but in the greatest and in those that concern blood. Not neighborliness, not friendship, not kinship will hold back the crime; all will raise their hands to evil, so that even he who has not been able to do evil nevertheless sins by his will while he prepares his hands. The prince himself asks, and the judge speaks peaceful words; for he receives gifts, the desire of his soul. Since this is plain, and I am wary of the ill will of princes and judges, I leave it to the reader's understanding and will add only this: Gifts blind the eyes even of the wise; they also give life to a soul to which they ought not to have given life, and kill the soul that lives by its own merit and virtues, and they do this for the sake of gifts which they shamelessly demand and more shamefully receive.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.4-5":["Now we must discuss it according to the earlier interpretation, in which we spoke of the heretics: Hear, O tribe, and who has adorned the city? Is it fire and the house of the wicked? And again, of the Church: Woe is me, for I have become like one who gathers stubble in the harvest. And once more: Woe is me, O soul, the reverent man has perished from the earth, and there is none among men to correct. And thereafter: The prince asks, and the judge has spoken peaceful words, by the desire of his soul. Hence a double curse follows: Woe, woe, your vengeances have come; now shall be their lamentation, and let us say that it is written of the heretics: Do not believe in friends, O simple peoples, and in depraved leaders who promise that they are friends and princes of the heresies; for they seek not your salvation but their own gains, and they trample the deceived flock under their feet; and in her who sleeps with you beware of believing anything; I can understand her to be nothing other than the flesh, so that we may not readily believe the blandishments of the flesh, lest the hardness of the mind and its manly constancy, subdued, should grow soft by her allurements.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.4":["For he who is best among them is like a brier, pricking and holding fast, wounding whoever comes near it and seizing him with a hooked tooth; and he who has been found upright is like a thorn from the hedge, so that pain is found where help was thought to be. This according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Micah","These the Lord threatens, saying: And I will take away their goods, which they think goods, which seem goods to them. But in the truth of the matter those things will never be called goods which both despoil the giver and kill the receiver; although it is not so much a threat as a blessing to take away evils from them, and for the Lord himself and the divine word to enter into their consciences and, like a moth, to devour whatever is perverse, and to lay waste their plunder and their evil thoughts, and to walk upon the norm and rule of truth, drawing back to what is right those very men who were led by depraved opinions; and to do this in the light of truth, and on that day when those who are holy and chosen from the Church will go up to the watchtower and, in the loftiness of their doctrines and works, will discourse on heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Micah","The day of your watching, your visitation, comes; now shall be their devastation.\n\nDo not believe a friend, and do not trust in a leader. From her who sleeps in your bosom guard the bars of your mouth, for the son does insult to the father, and the daughter rises up against her mother, the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; a man's enemies are his own household. But I will look to the Lord, I will wait for God my Savior; my God will hear me. LXX: Woe, woe, your vengeances have come, now shall be their weeping; do not believe in friends, nor hope in leaders. From her who lies with you, beware lest you believe her, for the son does insult to the father, and the daughter rises against her mother, the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; a man's enemies are the men of his own household. But I will gaze upon the Lord, I will wait for God my Savior? My God will hearken to me. Except for the beginning of the section, in the remaining parts both editions agree; and for the present, according to the history, he signifies the day of the watching of Samaria, or of Jerusalem, which she had often expected and feared would come, and her visitation, the captivity, saying: Your laying waste comes; now shall be their devastation, that is, of the inhabitants, or their siege: for mabucha in the Hebrew means rather πολιορκίαν and φρούρησιν, that is, siege and guard, than devastation.\n— Commentary on Micah","There follows the exposition of the Seventy, who said: Woe, woe, your vengeances have come, that is, the punishments that are to be inflicted for crimes. To me, he says, belongs vengeance, and I will repay, says the Lord. And in another place: The days of your retribution have come. For the Lord executes vengeance for those who cry out to him day and night and say: How long, O Lord, holy and true, do you not judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth? Therefore the vengeances have come, and now shall be their weeping, that is, the weeping of the vengeances, so that those who laughed before may lament, and, as soon as they go out of the world, may endure torments, such as that rich man, once clad in purple and abounding in delights, endures in hell, where there is weeping and gnashing of teeth. But as for what follows: Now shall be, understand it either of the end of each one's life, or of the consummation of all things and the day of judgment, when general vengeances will come upon all.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.5-6":["Thus far it is commanded that we should not readily believe friends, leaders, wives. And a reason is given that does not quite answer to the proposition; for he says: For the son dishonors the father, the daughter rises against the mother, the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, and a man's enemies are his own household. For what has it to do with a friend, a leader, a wife, if son and daughter and daughter-in-law rise up against father and mother and mother-in-law? It seems to me, therefore, that it can be joined with what precedes in this way. Do not believe friends, and leaders, and wives, who can change and are only for a time, since even son and daughter, forgetting their upbringing and their infancy, rise up against the authors of their life and of their bodies, and do insult to those whom it is a crime to hurt even by a look. But this exposition by no means fits the daughter-in-law rising up against her mother-in-law, and the man whose enemies are his own household. Terence in the Hecyra: What is this? All mothers-in-law hate their daughters-in-law; and although this is ambiguous, yet it is almost natural that the daughter-in-law should hate the mother-in-law, and the mother-in-law the daughter-in-law. So much for what the prophetic word may have described concerning the consummation and end of the world: what kind of generation is to precede the coming of the Antichrist.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.5":["Therefore believe none of the voices of the prophets, and do not lend your ear to the deceiving flattery of the diviners, for if among dear names and the affection of kinship faith is rare, how much more so among those who, flattering you, lie for their own booty, and, as for sick men, prescribe not what is useful, but what is delightful and pleasing. Do not believe a friend; for indeed Ahithophel rose up against David, and the true Ahithophel, Judas, against Christ. And do not trust in a leader, as the men of Shechem trusted in Abimelech. For they themselves made him king, and were oppressed by him. From her who sleeps in your bosom, guard the bars of your mouth, lest you suffer what Samson suffered from Delilah.\n— Commentary on Micah","Therefore do not believe friends, for every friend supplants with supplanting, and he who is a friend for the sake of something is not so much the friend of him whom he pretends to love, for a friend takes his name from love, as of the thing that he loves. A certain man, asked what a friend was, replied: Another self. But if the example of the Pythagoreans is set against us, who gave themselves to the tyrant as sureties for one another, we say that the sentence was pronounced by God not generally against all friends and the affections of love, nor against every time, but concerning that time of which the Apostle says: In the last days perilous times will come: for men will be lovers of themselves, covetous, disdainful, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, wicked, without affection, not keeping covenant, informers, incontinent, harsh, without kindness, traitors, wanton, puffed up, lovers of pleasures rather than of God, and so on. For then brother will hand over brother, and father son, and mother daughter, and a man's enemies will be his own household. But even now faith is rare, when one thing is on the lips and another turns in the heart, and the honey of the tongue covers the poison of the mind. The rich have many friends, but from the poor even those who seem to be friends depart. Hence it is said: If you have a friend, possess him in temptation. I have read in someone's declamation: \"A friend is long sought, scarcely found, hard to keep\". Theophrastus wrote three volumes on friendship, preferring it to every other love, and yet he testified that it is rare in human affairs. There is also Cicero's book on friendship, which he entitled Laelius, in which that precept of our own writers, That a friend should be to us like old wine, and that we should drink it in sweetness, is set down in almost the same words. Friendship either finds equals or makes them; where there is inequality, the eminence of one and the subjection of the other, there is not so much friendship as flattery. Hence we also read elsewhere: \"Let a friend be the same soul\". And the Lyric poet, praying for a friend: Preserve, he says, the half of my soul. Do not, therefore, believe friends, that is, those men who seek gain from friendships. If you wish to delight in true friendship, be a friend of God, like Moses, who spoke to God as a friend to a friend. Be a friend, like the apostles, to whom the Savior says: No longer will I call you servants, because the servant does not know what his master does, but I will call you friends, because you have persevered with me in all my temptations. Dainty is the friendship that follows the good fortune and the riches of friends. Men of this kind seem to me to love not their friends but themselves. Let us consider more attentively the words of the Lord: But I will call you, he says, friends. And he gives the reasons why he calls them friends: Because you have persevered with me in temptation, and he did not stop there, but in all, he says, my temptations. For it sometimes happens that one who persevered with us in one temptation is overcome by others and withdraws. Secondly it is commanded: Nor hope in leaders: Cursed indeed is the man who puts his hope in man. Hope in man is vain, and true hope is in God. Hence Paul also says: And from among your own selves men will arise speaking perverse things. And the Lord himself through the prophet: The leaders of my people have not known me; they are foolish sons, and without understanding; they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have not known. They were indeed called my leaders, he says, and leaders of my people, but because they did not know me, and destroyed the name by their deeds, therefore they are foolish sons and without understanding; they have prudence only to this end, that they may subject the simple flock to themselves and trample it under their feet; but to do good and to rule the people they have not known. Do not believe in leaders: not in a bishop, not in a presbyter, not in a deacon, not in any dignity of men. Nor do I say this meaning that you ought not to be subject to ranks of this kind in the Church: Whoever indeed curses father or mother shall die the death; and the Apostle teaches that those set over us in the Church are to be obeyed; but I say it because it is one thing to honor leaders, another to have hope in leaders. Let us honor the bishop, defer to the presbyter, rise for the deacon, and yet let us not hope in them, for hope in man is vain, and sure hope is in the Lord. The third command: From her who lies with you, guard yourself, lest you believe her. Hence the apostle also calls women the weaker vessel, and commands that honor be shown them by their husbands. For man was not created for the sake of woman, but woman for the sake of man, and: The wife, he says, is to fear her husband. It belongs to the wife to fear, and to love her husband with fear. To husbands it belongs only to love, because love is of the perfect: Husbands, he says, love your wives and do not be bitter towards them, even though they provoke you to anger and do such things as to deserve to endure bitterness. For this is what παραπικραίνεσθαι means; yet do not repay them in kind with bitterness. And Solomon too, in Ecclesiastes: And one man, he says, out of a thousand I found, and a woman among all these I did not find; taught perhaps by his own example that women, through whom he had offended God, are not to be believed. And the sublime poet too — not another Homer, as Lucilius supposes of Ennius, but the first Homer among the Latins —: A varied and changeable thing ever is Woman.\n\nThe Greek and Latin histories are full of how many men were deceived by their wives and had their lives betrayed. From the Scriptures, moreover, the examples both of Delilah, whom we mentioned above, and of another woman before Delilah bear witness, who wrung from Samson his secret of seven days by her tears, and by feigned love discovered what lay hidden. Hence Samson afterwards says: If you had not tamed my heifer, you would not have found out my riddle.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.6":["For I have come to separate a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; and a man's enemies are those of his household. This passage is written in nearly the same words in the prophet Micah. And note, wherever a testimony is set down from the Old Testament, whether the sense alone agrees or the wording as well.\n— Commentary on Matthew","For the son does insult to the father; Absalom, namely, to David: he defiled not only the kingdom but also the concubines of his father by incestuous intercourse. The daughter rises up against her mother: although for the present we find no testimony of this in the holy Scriptures, there are so many examples of it in daily life that we ought rather to mourn that they are so many than to search for them. The daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, as the wife of Esau rose up against Rebekah. A man's enemies are the men of his own household. Here I do not seek examples, since there are too many of them for us to need testimonies.\n— Commentary on Micah","For the son who was born of God, neglecting his Creator, blasphemes him by whom he was made, as Scripture says: Has not one God created you? Is not one the father of all of you? And the soul despises the heavenly Jerusalem, and scorns her mother the Church, and whoever despises her shall die the death. And the daughter-in-law rises up against her mother-in-law: this seems difficult to understand according to tropology; but whoever has read the Song of Songs, and has understood the bridegroom of the soul to be the word of God, and has believed the Gospel according to the Hebrews, which was published and which we lately translated, in which it is said in the person of the Savior: Just now my mother, the Holy Spirit, took me by one of my hairs, will not hesitate to say that the word of God arose from the Spirit, and that the soul, which is the bride of the word, has as her mother-in-law the Holy Spirit, who among the Hebrews is called by a word of feminine gender, rua. Heretics, therefore, although they formerly believed in the Scriptures, which were written and published by the Holy Spirit, transfer themselves to new doctrines, and to the leaven of the Pharisees, and to the commandments of men, and while they despise the word of God, they do injury to their mother-in-law. And lest perhaps you doubt that the Word and Son of God is born of the Holy Spirit, consider the words of Gabriel to Mary: The Holy Spirit, he says, will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; therefore the holy thing that will be born of you will be called the Son of God. After this there follows: A man's enemies are the men of his own household. This it seems to us should be expounded thus according to tropology: The head of every man is Christ; and Christ is the head of the Church; his enemies are often those who are thought to be in his house, that is, in the Church, and they do not indeed withdraw from the head, but they think against their own head: those who, without a teacher and the grace of the Lord, promising knowledge of the Scriptures by their own judgment, are puffed up and know nothing; and they languish over questions and contentions and battles of words: those who, though they truly stand within the house, are enemies of the truth. We ought to know, moreover, that in the Gospel there are nearly the same words that we now read in the prophet, and that according to the context of that place they have another sense; whether they were taken from the prophet or commanded on his own authority, it belongs to the Lord to know, who spoke both in the prophets and in the Gospels. Now there the Lord says: I came to divide a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, and a man's enemies are his own household.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.7":["Since, then, these things are so, do not believe, Samaria and Jerusalem, the false prophets. But I, says the prophet, will look to the Lord; I will exult in God my Savior, or my Jesus, and my God will hear me.\n— Commentary on Micah","These things having thus been set forth, if indeed we have been able to reach the sense of the Scriptures, the holy man, understanding that love has grown cold, and that at the consummation of the world men are not lovers of God but lovers of themselves, while others believe in friends and leaders and wives, and son and daughter and daughter-in-law rise up against father and mother and mother-in-law, and a man's enemies are his own household, himself believes in the Lord, and all his contemplation is in his God; and although he is pressed by the tribulations and pressures of the world, yet, having confidence in no one except him who says: Do not fear, I have overcome the world, he waits for God his Savior, and believing in him, and ever directing his eyes towards him, he hopes that he will be heard as often as he calls upon him.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.8-10":["Do not rejoice over me, my enemy, because I have fallen; I shall rise; when I sit in darkness: the Lord is my light. I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he judges my cause and executes judgment for me; he will bring me out into the light, and I shall see his justice, and my enemy will look on, and she will be covered with confusion, who says to me: Where is the Lord your God? My eyes will look upon her; now she shall be trodden down, like the mud of the streets, the day for your walls to be built. On that day the law shall be far off; on that day they will come even to you from Asshur, and as far as the fortified cities, and from the fortified cities as far as the river, and to sea from sea, and to mountain from mountain. And the land shall become a desolation because of its inhabitants, and because of the fruit of their thoughts. LXX: Do not insult me, my enemy, because I have fallen, and I shall rise again; for if I walk in darkness, the Lord will enlighten me. I will endure the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, and executes judgment for me, and brings me out into the light, and I shall see his justice, and my enemy will see me and confusion will cover her, who says to me: Where is the Lord your God? My eyes will see her, now she shall be for trampling, like mud in the ways, the day of the daubing of brick, your blotting out, and that day will thrust back your ordinances, and your cities will come into enclosure and into the division of the Assyrians, and your fortified cities into division from Tyre as far as the river, and from sea to sea, and from mountain to mountain; and the land shall become a ruin with its inhabitants because of the fruits of their devisings. It seems to me that according to the letter Jerusalem is speaking against Babylon and the other nations which had insulted her: Do not rejoice at my ruin, for, the Lord having mercy, I shall rise; after I have sat in captivity he will bring me out of the darkness, and he will be my light. I will endure the wrath of the Lord, because I know that I deserved what I have suffered, until I am avenged on the nations, and judgment is executed for me. For I know that he will bring me out into the light and I shall see his justice, and my enemy Babylon will look on, and the other nations round about, and she will be covered with confusion, who now says insultingly: Where is the Lord your God? My eyes will see her, and not after a long time, but now and at present, trodden down like the mud of the streets.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.8":["Now let us come to the spiritual understanding, and, with the Holy Spirit himself expounding in what sense these things were written, let us toil even in the most difficult places. It seems to me that every soul, a Jerusalem in which the temple of the Lord was built, and the vision of peace, and the knowledge of the Scriptures, and which was afterwards overcome by sins, led into captivity and handed over to torments, speaks against Babylon, that is, the confusion of this world, and against the opposing power that presides over this world: Do not insult me, my enemy, because I have fallen, and I shall rise again; The Lord indeed lifts up those who are dashed down, and he speaks through the prophet: Shall he who falls not rise again? And: I do not desire the death of the sinner, only that he turn back and live. But if you despise me for this reason, that I endure torments, learn through Ezekiel that punishments are inflicted first on the holier, and that the Lord says: Begin from my holy ones. For if I walk in darkness, the Lord is my light. For although the rulers of this darkness have deceived me, and I sit in darkness and the shadow of death, and my feet have stumbled on the dark mountains, yet to those who sit in darkness and in the shadow of death a light has risen, and the light shines in the darkness. The Lord is my illumination and my salvation; whom shall I fear; and I will speak to him and say: Your word, O Lord, is a lamp to my feet, and a light to my paths. For he himself commanded me, placed as I am in the darkness of this age: Let your loins be girded, and burning lamps in your hands.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.9":["There follows: I will endure the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, and executes judgment for me, and brings me out into the light; and I shall see his justice. All correction for the present seems to be a matter not of joy but of sorrow, but afterwards it will yield the peaceful fruit of justice to those who have been trained by it. The soul, therefore, perceiving that it has sinned, and has the wounds of sins, and lives in dead flesh, and needs cautery, says steadfastly to the physician: Burn my flesh, cut away the wounds, bind up all the humors and the noxious ῥεῦμα (flux) with a harsh draught of hellebore. It was my fault that I was wounded; let it be my pain that I endure so many torments, and afterwards receive health. And the true physician shows the soul, now safe and secure, the reason for the remedy, and teaches that he rightly did what he did. In short, after tortures and punishments, the soul, brought out of the outer darkness, and having paid the last farthing, says: I shall see his justice, and I will say: Your judgments are justified, O God. But if Christ has been made for us by God wisdom, and justice, and sanctification, and redemption, he who says that he sees justice after the wrath of God promises himself the sight of Christ. And this holds only of penitents. But it is much better not to have wounds, and not to need a physician. Healing is not the blessedness of those who are healed, but a solace after pain. Therefore let him who has been healed beware lest he sin again, and something worse befall him once more. We read in Leviticus, if indeed we read with open eyes, and the veil that is placed upon the law does not shut out the gaze of the inner eye, that leprosy is wont to arise in the weal and scar of a burn, and to change the color of the hair, and that a new foulness is added to the former disfigurement of the scar. This is so for this reason: that no one, secure about repentance because after sin he can say: I will endure the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he justifies my cause, should sin and need cautery, and, once healed, be wounded again.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.10":["But when the Lord has brought us out into the light, and we have seen his justice, then our enemy Babylon will see, and will be covered with confusion, she who formerly said to us: Where is your God, thinking that Jerusalem after her wounds could not be healed. And our eyes will look upon her, and she will be trodden down like the mud of the streets.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.11-13":["Thus far Jerusalem has spoken, or the prophet in the person of the people; now God is brought in answering Jerusalem: O Jerusalem, the days have come for your walls to be built, which had been destroyed by the Babylonian when he laid you waste. On that day the law shall be far off, or the precept and command, as Symmachus and Theodotion translated, saying ἐπιταγὴν καὶ πρόσταγμα; and the sense is: You will by no means lie subject to the rule of the Babylonians; on that day on which your walls will be built, they will come to you from Asshur, and from the fortified cities; from the fortified cities, I say, as far as the Jordan, across which the people also passed before, and from the Red Sea; and from all the nations as far as the Dead Sea, which is near your land, and to Mount Zion, from the mountains of the Persians and the Medes, into which they had earlier been carried away; and the land will be the remnant of the Chaldeans, and of those who had laid you waste, a desolation, because of its inhabitants, and because of their evil works. This the Jews promise themselves to this day; and in that place in which we have expounded: On that day the law shall be far off, as it seemed to us, and as the more prudent among them discuss it, some lie frivolously and say: On that day on which the walls of Jerusalem have been built by Christ, the holy Scriptures of the Law and the Prophets, which are now held by us, will be taken out of our hands and handed over to the people of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Micah","And since every end of punishments is the beginning of good things, and pain advances toward health, bricks will be made from her mud, and her daubing will be the shaping of bricks. And on that day she will cast off her old errors, and her cities, which had been badly fortified, will come into enclosure, or into division, and will be divided by the Assyrians; from Tyre too, which is interpreted συνοχὴ, that is, distress, other powers will rise up, and there will be sedition even as far as those who delight in the flux of this age and beget lusts in men. And from sea to sea, and from mountain to mountain, wars will rise up against one another, so that bitterness fights against bitterness, and a loftiness that exalts itself and is to be humbled contends against another height; and then truly it may be fulfilled: Come, let us go down and confound their languages, so that each may not hear the voice of his neighbor; for indeed it is profitable that the most wicked powers should have no concord among themselves. And when Satan has been divided against Satan, then at last his whole kingdom will be destroyed. And what frequently happens in great armies, that when the tyrant has been killed his attendants divide the kingdom among themselves and rise up against one another, and there is civil war among them, this will happen also at the consummation of the world, when the walls of Jerusalem have been built, and Babylon has fallen, and the Assyrians, and the Tyrians, from the river, and from the sea, and from the mountains, that is, all the nations of demons, will fight among themselves; and, their kingdom having been scattered, the kingdom will be the Lord Jesus', and every knee shall bow, of things in heaven, and on earth, and under the earth, and every tongue shall confess that the Lord Jesus is in the glory of God the Father. But that you may know that the end of this sedition is the advance of the virtues, the land of Babylon will then become a ruin with all its inhabitants, and will by no means bear Babylonian fruits.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.11":["For as to what is said according to the Seventy, The day of the daubing of brick, your blotting out, let us understand that it is said not to Jerusalem, as we expounded according to the Hebrew, but still to Babylon: that she too is to be blotted out, and trodden down like brick. And that day will thrust back the ordinances: not the ordinances of God, but those ordinances which, O Babylon, you had commanded to be observed against the law of God.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.12-13":["And your cities will come into enclosure, or into division, with the Assyrians attacking you — since indeed Babylon was a city of the Chaldeans, not of the Assyrians —. And your fortified cities will be for the division of a hostile army, from Tyre as far as the river Tigris, by which you are encircled, and from the Great Sea as far as the Red Sea, which, on the flank, for those traveling to India, touches your regions. And from mountain to mountain; namely, from the mountains of Judea as far as the mountains of Media and of the Persians, all Mesopotamia and the whole region which is now held by you in the middle will be subjugated to the rule of your adversaries. And the land shall become a desolation because of the most wicked fruits of your pursuits. Where the Seventy translated from Tyre, let us know that in the Hebrew there is written masor, a word which, if it is divided into the preposition Ma and the noun Sor, is understood as from Tyre; but if it is one word, it means a fortification. In short, all rendered περιοχὴν, καὶ περίφραγμα, καὶ πολιορκίαν, not from Tyre, as the LXX did, but a fortification and the circuit of a walled city. Let this, according to the Hebrew and the hopes of carnal Israel and of the people of the concision, be what our leisurely discourse has played through as a prelude, as it were from superfluity.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.14-15":["Shepherd your people with your rod, the flock of your inheritance, dwelling alone in the forest in the midst of Carmel; they shall feed on Bashan and Gilead according to the days of old, according to the days of your going out from the land of Egypt; I will show him wonders. The nations shall see, and shall be confounded at all their strength. They shall put their hands upon their mouth; their ears shall be deaf. They shall lick the dust like serpents, like the creeping things of the earth; they shall be troubled out of their dwellings. They shall dread the Lord our God, and they shall fear you. LXX: Shepherd your people with your rod, the sheep of your inheritance, dwelling alone in the forest; in the midst of Carmel they shall feed on Bashan and Gilead according to the former days, and according to the days of your going out from the land of Egypt; I will show them wonders. The nations shall see, and shall be confounded in all their strength; they shall put their hands upon their mouth; their ears shall be deaf. They shall lick the ground like serpents that drag the earth; they shall be troubled in their enclosures; at the Lord our God they shall dread, and they shall be in fear of you. As to what is said, Shepherd your people with your rod: God the Father is speaking to the Son, that is, to our Lord Jesus Christ, that, since he is the good shepherd and lays down his life for his sheep, he should shepherd his people with his rod, and the sheep of his inheritance. And lest we think that the peoples are the same as the sheep, we read in another place: But we are your people, and the sheep of your pasture. \"People\" refers to all who are rational, but \"sheep\" to those who, not yet using reason, are content with simplicity alone, and are called of the inheritance of God. But the people as much as the sheep need the shepherd's rod, of which the Apostle too speaks: What do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in charity and the spirit of meekness? For this reason, I think, because the people of Israel was stiff-necked and was always sighing for the meats of Egypt, Moses used, not only against the Egyptians, whom he struck with ten plagues, but also against the people in the wilderness, the rod of the law, a rod that strikes and breaks all the earthen and fragile vessels; but from the apostles of the Lord the Savior, who spoke wisdom among the perfect, the rod was struck out of their hands, because perfect love casts out fear. But if anyone objects to us how it can now be said to Christ, that is, to the good shepherd, who is certainly greater and better than the apostles, that he should use a rod, when it is a mark of greater progress to have no rod than to correct peoples and sheep with a rod, we shall answer them according to what the Lord promises his apostles, that they would do greater signs among the peoples than he himself had done. And because the Lord was still speaking to carnal Israel, and not yet to the one who could perfectly know the mysteries, for that reason it was said of him that he should shepherd his people and his flock with the rod, but that the rod was struck out of the hands of the apostles, and the severity of the law tempered by the clemency of the Gospel. Moreover, these peoples and these sheep will be struck, and will be shepherded with the rod, for this reason, that they had dwelt alone in the forest. This we can indeed take also of those who, separating themselves from the Church, give themselves up to the banquets and friendships of the Gentiles, and also of those who out of hatred of the human race seek a solitary life, such as we read that Timon was at Athens; not that the solitary and prophetic life which Elijah and John had is to be condemned, but because, if someone, despising the rest, is himself proud and dwells in the forest of vices, he must be corrected with the rod. He who dwells alone and does not dwell in the forest is in the praise of virtues; but he who is alone and does not do the works of justice, and enjoys only the pleasure of quiet, and does not toil in the work and labor of Christ, nor seek his food with his own hands, as the Apostle commands, and is lifted up into pride, he dwells in the forest and moves among the fruitless trees. But yet, because he is the good shepherd, and his rod strikes for this purpose, to amend, the prophetic word promises better things, and says: In the midst of Carmel they shall feed on Bashan and Gilead, according to the days of eternity, and according to the days of your going out from Egypt. Carmel means knowledge of circumcision, Bashan confusion, and Gilead transmigration of testimony. Therefore the people of God, and the sheep of his pasture, which before were fed apart from the Lord's flock and outside his Church, and moved about in the forest of errors, will afterwards be transferred to the knowledge of the true circumcision, and will serve God in the spirit, and will glory in the Lord, and will not trust in the flesh, and will be the true circumcision, and not the concision. And when they have been fed with the spiritual circumcision, understanding their former sins, they will be confounded in their vices, and will blush, and will be in the confusion that leads to life; for there is also another confusion that leads to death, in which Og, king of Bashan, once dwelt, since Bashan means confusion; and from this worst confusion the Lord also promises that he will free his people: The Lord said: From Bashan I will turn back, I will turn back from the depths of the sea. And when we have known the true circumcision, and have been confounded over our sins, then we shall be in Gilead, which means transmigration of testimony, in the Church of Christ, into which the testimonies of the law and the utterances of the prophets have passed over; and this will be done for us according to the days of old, according to the days when we went out from the land of Egypt, of which Moses says: Remember the days of eternity, not the days of this age, which are called evil, but the everlasting days. And he remembers the days of eternity who does not look at present things, and has risen with Christ, and sits with him in the heavenly places, already anticipating in mind that he has been freed from the days of the present age.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.15-16":["The divine word also promises that he will show wonders to his people and to the sheep of his inheritance: Then, he says, the nations shall see, and shall be confounded in all their strength, because they had once laid waste and prevailed against the people of God; and their confusion will bring them progress, when they have understood their own evils. For they will put their hands upon their mouth, and their evil works will take from them all freedom of speaking. But just as the hands of the impious nations close their mouths, so the hands of the just will open their mouths, as they receive from good work the faculty of speaking with God. Their ears too will grow deaf, because malice has not only blinded the gaze of the eyes but has also made the ears grow deaf; for they would not hear the voice of the charmers, and of the sorcerer who charms wisely. And according to Isaiah: With their ears they heard heavily, although it is much less to hear heavily than not to hear at all, and to become deaf so as not to hear the word of truth.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.16-17":["And these things will happen so that the nations may see, and be confounded in all their evil strength, and put their hands upon their mouth, and grow deaf in their ears, and lick the earth like serpents that drag the ground, so that first they may be shut in, and, once shut in, be troubled, then, terrified, be astonished before the Lord God of the saints, and at last they too may fear him. This is according to the LXX. Moreover, since our edition, at least in the present passage, does not differ much from them, let us consider that what has been said on theirs has been said on this also.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.17":["After such great evils it is said of them that they will lick the ground like serpents that drag the earth, walking on their belly and eating earth all the days of their life. And doing the works of the flesh, that is, earthly works, and dragging with them, up to the day of the vengeance and visitation of the Lord, not dust, not small traces of earth, but the whole ground. And when they have done this, and have come to the judgment of God, and have been shut in and troubled, they will be troubled and shut in for as long as the earth which they had dragged like serpents remains in them. But when that has failed, they will be astonished and will marvel, not at the Lord their God — for they will not yet deserve that he be called the Lord their God —, but at the Lord our God. And suddenly there is an ἀποστροφὴ (a turning of address) to Christ, and it is said to him: And they shall be in fear of you. For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.18-19":["Moreover, what follows, For those who are left of his inheritance, he did not hold his anger as a testimony, has this sense: if he spared the nations, which would not believe his law, and would not impute their iniquities to those who were left over and left remaining of the people, nor brought his anger as a testimony of their just punishment, what will he do with his own flock, which feeds in the midst of Carmel, and in Bashan, and in Gilead? For he is one who wills mercy, and returning he will have mercy on us; and our heavy sins, and the iniquities that sit upon the talent of lead, he himself will carry, and will sink in the sea, and will not let them appear.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.18-20":["I too will say at the end of the volume, sealing the labor of my little work with an invocation of the Lord: God, who is like you? Take away the iniquity of your servant; pass over the sin of the remnant of my soul; do not send your fury upon me, nor rebuke me in your anger, since you are merciful, and your mercies are many. Return, and have mercy on me; hurl down my iniquities, and cast them into the depth of the sea, so that the saltiness and bitterness of vices may perish in a false region. Give the truth that you promised to your friend Jacob, and the mercy that you promised to your friend Abraham, and free my soul from the persecutors of your prophets, Ahab and Jezebel, as you swore to my fathers in the days of old, saying: As I live, says the Lord. I do not wish the death of the sinner, only that he turn back and live, and elsewhere: As soon as you have turned and groaned, you will be saved. Then my enemy will see, and will be covered with confusion, she who now says to me: Where is the Lord your God? I will see your vengeance on her, and she will be like the mud of the streets, and will be trodden down, so that she may no longer build Egyptian cities out of mud and straw.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.18":["Who is a God like you? You who take away iniquity, and pass over the sin of the remnant of your inheritance. He will not send his fury any more, because he is one who wills mercy. He will return and have mercy on us; he will put away our iniquities, and will cast all our sins into the depth of the sea. You will give truth in Jacob, mercy to Abraham, which you swore to our fathers from the days of old. LXX: Who, O God, is like you? Taking away iniquities, and passing over injustices for those who are left of your inheritance? He did not hold his anger as a testimony, because he is one who wills mercies. He will return, and will have mercy on us, he will sink our sins; and all our sins shall be cast into the depth of the sea. He will give truth to Jacob, mercy to Abraham, as you swore to our fathers according to the former days. The prophet, understanding that the multitude of the nations was troubled in its enclosure for this reason, that it might be astonished and fear God, and that the Lord rages for this reason, that he may take away sins and give salvation, praises the Lord and marvels, and says: Who is a God like you? Taking away iniquities, and passing over injustices, so that, just as the destroyer in Egypt passed over the people of Israel and did not lay it waste — and was it not from this passing-over that the phasa, that is, the pascha, received its name? — so you may spare the nations, in no way imputing to them their iniquities.\n— Commentary on Micah","Moreover, where we translated, He will not send his fury any more, for \"any more\" Symmachus rendered \"forever\", Theodotion \"to the end\", the Seventy and the Fifth Edition \"as a testimony\", for which laed is set in the Hebrew; and it can be understood as \"any more\" as well as \"forever\" and \"testimony\".\n— Commentary on Micah"],"MIC.7.20":["He will give truth to Jacob, and mercy to Abraham, so that to the people that is a supplanter, and new, and always set in wrestling, he may render his truth in Christ, and to the multitude of the nations — for Abraham is called the father of many nations — he may grant mercy, as he swore to those who were the fathers of our faith according to the days of old, that out of the whole multitude of men he would save some in truth, others in mercy.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"JOL.1.1":["The order of the twelve prophets among the Seventy translators is not the same as that which is kept in the Hebrew truth. For they put Amos second, Micah third, Joel fourth, Obadiah fifth, Jonah sixth, Nahum seventh, Habakkuk eighth, Zephaniah ninth, Haggai tenth, Zechariah eleventh, Malachi twelfth; but the Hebrews, after Hosea, who is first among both, read Joel second, Amos third, Obadiah fourth, Jonah fifth, Micah sixth, Nahum seventh, Habakkuk eighth, Zephaniah ninth, Haggai tenth, Zechariah eleventh, and twelfth, who is also the last, Malachi. And since we have once enumerated all the prophets of the one volume, it seems useful to us to note briefly the ἐτυμολογίας (etymologies) of each, both in Greek and in Latin. Hosea means σώζων, whom we can call one who saves. Joel ἀρχόμενος, that is, beginning. Amos βαστάζων, which among the Latins is called carrying. Obadiah δοῦλος Κυρίου, that is, servant of the Lord. Jonah περιστερὰ, that is, dove. Micah τίς ὡς, a name composed of two parts of speech, which with us sounds who as, or who like? Nahum παράκλησις, that is, consolation. Habakkuk περιλαμβάνων, that is, embracing or wrestling. Zephaniah κεκρυμμένος Κυρίου, that is, the hidden one of the Lord. Haggai, ἑορτάζων, whom we can call festive or solemn. Zechariah μνήμη Κυρίου, that is, remembrance of the Lord. Malachi ἄγγελός μου, that is, my messenger. In what sense all these are to be taken will be discussed in their own volumes. But the four remaining prophets, that we may complete sixteen, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Jeremiah, Daniel, have this meaning: Isaiah is called σωτηρία Κυρίου, that is, salvation of the Lord; Ezekiel κράτος Κυρίου, which we can call the strength or the dominion of the Lord; Jeremiah ὑψηλὸς Κυρίου, that is, the exalted one of the Lord; Daniel ἔκρινέ με Κύριος, that is, the Lord has judged me. Since these things are so, I beseech you, my Pammachius, who fight against the devil with every art of combat, that, while we fight against Amalek, you lift up your hands to the Lord with Moses, and overcome the adversaries of Israel by your prayers. We have lately come out of Egypt, and, Pharaoh having been drowned in Hosea, we have crossed the Red Sea; now, after the broad waste of the desert, we must sweat with much labor, that we may be able to reach the seventy palms and the apostolic springs. Though the scorpion rise up and try to strike with its arched wound, you, companion of the journey and charmer of venomous bites, show yourself a spiritual ψυλλέα (a Psyllian, a snake-charmer) for us; and what we promised to the holy and venerable Paula, your mother-in-law, take up as a dutiful heir; and whatever we can do less than your expectation, whether in talent or in learning, sustain with a friend's favor, and do not consider in us our strength, but our will.\n— Commentary on Joel","The word of the Lord which was made to Joel, the son of Phatuel. The Seventy translators rendered Bathuel for Phatuel, which in Hebrew has no meaning at all. But Phatuel is rendered into our language as breadth of God, or God opening, as we read in Mark that the Savior said to the deaf and dumb man: Ephpheta, which is, be opened. For since he could say with the Apostle: Our mouth is open to you, O Corinthians, our heart is enlarged, and heard from the Lord: Make wide your mouth, and I will fill it; and since this very opening of the mouth is not in the power of man but in the power of God, as Paul says: A great and evident door has been opened to me; but there are many adversaries; therefore he is called God opening. And since the saint always makes progress, knowing in part and prophesying in part, until that which is perfect comes, he who is begotten of breadth and opening is called Joel, which in our language means beginning, or is God, as the Apostle says: Brothers, I do not consider that I have laid hold of it; and growing by this humility he deserves to hear with Moses: He who is, has sent me. For by way of distinction from those who are not, of whom we read also in Esther: Do not hand over your scepter to those who are not, God and his saints are said to be. Let these things about the name of Joel and of his father be touched on briefly, so that the word of God, which was in the beginning with God, may rightly be said to have been made, as John the Baptist relates: He who is to come after me was made before me, because he was before me. Moreover, that the word was made is referred to the merit of him to whom it is made, not to the condition of him who is said to be made, as we read elsewhere: The Lord was made salvation for me. And just as in the prophet Hosea, whom we expounded before this book at the beginning of the Twelve Prophets, where the prophecy is directed under the name of Ephraim to the ten tribes, which are often called either Samaria or Israel, so in Joel, who according to the Hebrews is the second, everything that is said must be believed to pertain to the tribe of Judah and to Jerusalem, and no mention at all is made in him of Israel, that is, of the ten tribes. We must also take the times in which he prophesied to be the same as those we read in Hosea: In the days of Uzziah, Jotham and Ahaz and Hezekiah, kings of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam, son of Joash, king of Israel.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.2":["Hear this, old men, and perceive with your ears, all inhabitants of the earth, if this has happened in your days, or in the days of your fathers; tell of this to your sons, and your sons to their sons, and their sons to another generation. LXX: Hear these things, old men, and perceive with your ears, all inhabitants of the earth, if such things have happened in your days, or in the days of your fathers; for these things, tell your sons, and your sons their sons, and their sons to another generation. The old men are commanded to hear, the inhabitants of the earth to perceive with their ears. To the old men it is not said: Hear, all; to the inhabitants of the earth is added: Perceive with your ears, all. But hearing in the holy Scriptures is not that which sounds in the ear, but that which is perceived by the heart, according to what the Lord says in the Gospel: He who has ears to hear, let him hear. But what we have translated as Perceive with your ears is one word both among the Greeks and among the Hebrews: among the Greeks ἐνωτίσασθε, among the Hebrews eezinu, which properly is perceived not by the heart but by the ear. And so that we may know that hearing is more sacred than that which sounds in the ears, let us learn it from Isaiah, who says: Hear, O heaven, and perceive with your ears, O earth. Those who are old men and heavenly hear spiritually; those who dwell on the earth and are called earthly perceive with their ears. And this must be noted in all the Scriptures where these two words are placed together. We read this also in the case of Lamech, the sinner, who spoke to his wives Adah and Zillah: Hear my words, wives of Lamech, perceive my words with your ears, for I have killed a man to my wounding, and a young man to my bruising; he knew that what he was saying was obscure, and therefore he called his wives not only to the simple sound of the words, but also to the hidden understanding of what was said. If, then, anyone is an old man, and an old man chosen of ripe age in the Lord, as we read further on according to the Seventy translators, and has left behind the infancy of little children, let him hear what is said. But he who still dwells on the earth, and cannot say: I am a stranger and a sojourner, like all my fathers, let him perceive with his ears. If this has happened, he says, in your days, or in the days of your fathers. With rhetorical art he makes the hearer attentive by the greatness of the matter. No age, he says, remembers the things I am about to say; they did not happen in your time, nor in the time of your fathers and forebears. But by fathers understand grandfathers and remote ancestors, and by sons the sons of sons, all the offspring that follow, according to that Vergilian line: And the sons of sons, and those who will be born of them.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.3":["Therefore, both old men and inhabitants of the earth, tell it to your sons and to your posterity: let him who is an old man teach his children the mysteries; let him who is an inhabitant of the earth tell the simple history. Hence to this very day we who believe in Christ, from whose eyes the veil has been taken away with Moses, and of whom it is said: The gray hairs of a man are his wisdom, tell our sons things secret and wonderful. But the Jews, who dwell on the earth, speak earthly things that cling to the ground, of whom it is written: He who is of the earth speaks of the earth; he who comes from heaven is above all.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.4":["What the caterpillar left, the locust has eaten, and what the locust left, the cankerworm has eaten, and what the cankerworm left, the blight has eaten. LXX: likewise. The narration follows the exordium: there, to make the hearer attentive, he promised that he would say great and incredible things, which neither ancient history knew nor the present age knew to have happened. Here he has set down the caterpillar, and the locust, and the cankerworm, and the blight, so that things each of which rarely happens may be recorded as having happened all at once, and may therefore be wonderful. The caterpillar, which in Hebrew is called gezem, in Greek κάμπη, the Hebrews interpret as the Assyrians, the Babylonians and the Chaldeans, who, coming forth from one region of the world, laid waste everything belonging to the ten tribes as well as to the two, that is, to the Israelite people. The locust they interpret as the Medes and Persians, who, after the overthrow of the empire of the Chaldeans, held the Jews captive. The cankerworm, as the Macedonians and all the successors of Alexander, and above all King Antiochus surnamed Epiphanes, who settled in Judea like a cankerworm and devoured all the remnants left by the former kings, under whom the wars of the Maccabees are recounted. The blight they refer to the empire of the Romans, who, fourth and last, so oppressed the Jews as to drive them out of their own territory. Josephus writes of it more fully in seven volumes, recounting the triumphs of Vespasian and Titus. We read also of the expedition of Aelius Hadrian against the Jews, who so overthrew Jerusalem and its walls that out of the remains and ashes of the city he founded a city named Aelia after himself. These four kingdoms which overthrew Judea Zechariah writes that he saw in four horns, the angel saying to him: These are the horns which have scattered Judah and Israel and Jerusalem. And again: I lifted up, he says, my eyes and saw; and behold, four chariots coming out from between two mountains, and the mountains were mountains of bronze; in the first chariot, red horses; and in the second chariot, black horses; and in the third chariot, white horses; and in the fourth chariot, dappled, strong horses. And when the prophet had said to the angel who spoke in him: What are these, my lord? The angel answered: These are the four winds of heaven, which go forth to stand before the ruler of all the earth; and the sense is: These are they who go forth from the face of the Lord, to fulfill his will. Since with the inhabitants of the earth we perceive with our ears what the caterpillar, the locust, the cankerworm and the blight signify, let us now hear with the old men what has been said. All the schools of the philosophers cry out together that there are four perturbations by which the health of souls is overthrown. Two are present and contrary to one another, two are future and at variance with each other. The present ones are distress and joy. We say distress of the mind; otherwise, of the body it is called not distress but illness. Either, then, we are sad and consumed with grief, and the state of our mind is overturned; whence the Apostle also warns that a brother should not be swallowed up by too much sorrow. Or, on the contrary, we rejoice and exult with gladness, and cannot bear our good things with moderation; and it belongs to a just and brave man neither to be broken by adversity nor to be lifted up by prosperity, but to be moderate in both. We have spoken of the perturbation of present things; let us speak also of that of future things, in which there is fear or hope. We fear adverse things, we await prosperous ones; and what distress and joy work in the present, fear and hope do concerning the future, while we either fear more than is fitting that adverse things are to come, or the prosperous things we hope for make us exult so much that we do not keep measure, especially in things that are uncertain, because future things are hoped for rather than held. These perturbations the illustrious poet comprehended in one little verse, and that not even a full one: These fear and desire — this of the future —, grieve and rejoice — this of the present —, nor, he says, do they look up at the air, shut in darkness and a blind prison.\n\nFor those who are wrapped in the darkness of the perturbations cannot behold the clear light of wisdom. We must beware, then, lest distress, like the caterpillar, eat us; lest the locust lay us waste in joy, flitting here and there, and tossing itself in different directions with exultant gladness; lest the cankerworm, that is, dread and fear of things to come, devour the roots of wisdom; lest the blight, the desire of things to come, covet useless things and carry us off to ruin; but that in all things we may govern with the reins of wisdom the four chariots and the four horns, and the four horses, red, and dappled, and white, and black, that is, adverse things, or prosperous ones, or those joined from both. I think that the caterpillar is the passion beginning in the mind, which is slow and cannot run about, and by its very lingering and persistence drinks up and sucks out all the greenness; and if we do not kill it, it grows in us and flies off, and now it devours whatever it has touched; now, leaving things half-eaten, it goes on to others, and returning to its former seat it becomes the cankerworm, so that by its slowness it devours not only the crops and the leaves and the bark, but even the very pith. But if it happens, though this is rare, that even after the cankerworm something of the vital spirit remains in us, the blight ravages everything, so that it turns the stubble and the worthless hay to blackness, so that they are useless not only for food but even for dung. Of these four perturbations we shall discourse also at the beginning of Amos, if life remains our companion, where it is written: For three crimes, and for four, of Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Idumea, the sons of Ammon, and Moab, and Judah, and Israel, I will not turn them back, says the Lord. What we have rendered as perturbations the Greeks call πάθη; if we render these κακοζήλως (affectedly) as passions, we shall have expressed the word rather than the sense of the word.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.5":["Wake up, you drunkards; and weep and howl, all you who drink wine in sweetness, because it has perished from your mouth. LXX: Awake, you who are drunk with your wine; and wail and lament, all you who drink wine in drunkenness, because it has been taken away from your mouth. As old men and elders we ought to hear: nothing so makes one drunk as the perturbation of the mind. There is a sorrow that leads to death: this is a drunkenness to be abhorred. There is an anger that does not work the righteousness of God, and, being very near to madness, it makes a man not master of his own mind, so much that the lips tremble, the teeth chatter, the face changes with pallor. And rightly is that saying of Archytas of Tarentum praised, who, when he was angry with his steward, said: I would kill you now, he said, if I were not angry. What shall I say of joy and pleasure, and above all of love, which blinds the eyes of the heart and permits the lover to think of nothing but what he loves? Must it not be called drunkenness when, for the sake of a worthless harlot and a shameful part of the body, the freedom of the soul is bent down to servile flatteries? When it makes its own labor the delights of another? When by theft, crime and perjuries it prepares wealth for future pleasure? And when, though it is seen by all, it thinks itself unseen, so long as it gains possession of what it desires. But avarice too blinds the mind of him for whom nothing is enough; and so do womanish fear and the craving for sweet vices. Hence it is said to them: Awake and wake up, you who are drunk, by no means with wine, as is contained in the LXX alone, but with every perturbation of vices. Weep and lament, and do penance, and take up the sorrow that leads to life, and howl, all you who drink wine in sweetness, or in drunkenness. For vices are sweet, because honey drips from the lips of a harlot woman; and for that reason it is not offered in the sacrifices of God, because the wine has perished, or has been taken away from your mouth, the drunkenness and sweetness that had deceived you. For it often happens by the providence of God that those who did not know him in prosperity know him in adversity, and that those who have wickedly abused riches are corrected toward virtues by want. According to this sense let the old men hear, and let the inhabitants of the earth perceive with their ears, that the command now concerns that wine in which there is debauchery, and those who have been made drunk with it cannot possess the kingdom of God. Let him who has been put to sleep by the drunkenness of wine awake, and wail that he has been drunk, and howl, so that afterward his howling and weeping may be turned into laughter, and he may rejoice that he no longer has the matter of drunkenness, which through abundance had made him drunk and mad.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.6-7":["For a nation has come up over my land, strong and without number; its teeth are like the teeth of a lion, and its molars like a lion's whelp. It has made my vine a desert, and has stripped the bark from my fig tree; stripping it, it has despoiled it, and cast it away; its branches have become white. LXX: For a nation has come up over the land, strong and without number; its teeth are the teeth of a lion, and its molars as of a lion's whelp. It has made my vine a destruction, and my figs a breaking; searching it has searched it, and cast it away; it has whitened its branches. The Jews think that in the days of Joel so countless a multitude of locusts came over Judea that they filled everything; and they left behind, I will not say the crops, but not even the bark and the branches of the vines and the trees, so that, all the greenness consumed, the whitening branches of the trees and the dry shoots of the vines remained. Whether this happened or not we cannot clearly affirm; for the history of Kings and Chronicles does not recount it. Certainly, had it happened, Scripture would never have kept silent, just as we read of the famine of three years and six months under Elijah. We say only this, that under the metaphor of locusts the coming of enemies is described: either of the Assyrians and Babylonians, who were then threatening; or of the Medes and Persians, who were to come afterward; or of the Macedonians, who we know came a long time after; or finally of the Romans, of whom we spoke above. Although it now seems to us rather to be said about the Babylonians and the Chaldeans, whose cruelty and savagery against the people of God is described; and, unless I am mistaken, I seem to have found something in this prophet. The impiety of the enemies is recounted under the figure of locusts, and again it is said of the locusts themselves as though they were compared to enemies, so that when you read of locusts you think of enemies, and when you have thought of enemies you return to the locusts. So a nation of locusts has come up out of the wilderness, or the army of the Chaldeans, over the land of God, strong and without number. For what is more countless and stronger than locusts, which human industry cannot resist? Its teeth, that is, of the nation of locusts — but understand everything τροπικῶς (figuratively) —, are like the teeth of a lion, and its molars like a lion's whelp, so that those who are equal to locusts in strength and multitude are compared to lions in ferocity and cruelty. This nation, he says, has made my vine a desert, the vine which I brought over out of Egypt and planted, and has stripped the bark from my fig tree, the people of the Jews, to whom the Savior came to eat its fruit, and did not find any, and cursed it, and it withered forever. But let us recognize that all these things are said under the metaphor of locusts, which so ravage everything that they gnaw off the bark of trees and cast it away stripped off, and, all the greenness consumed, leave the branches white and dry.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.6":["We have spoken according to the history; let us pass on to the spiritual understanding, so that with the old men we may be able to hear: A nation has come up over the land of God, that is, the human soul. For all souls are God's; as the soul of the father, so also the soul of the son. And there comes up the nation of the princes of this world, and of darkness, and the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places, against whom we have our wrestling and struggle, of whom it is said: If the spirit of him who has power comes up over you, do not leave your place; the teeth of this nation are like the teeth of a lion, of whom the apostle Peter speaks: Our adversary the devil, like a roaring lion, goes about seeking whom he may devour. And its molars are like a lion's whelp. By the lion's whelp understand either him who is lifted up against everything that is called God and religion, or certainly every perverse dogma. Over the crushing of his molars, which lie hidden within and are not seen, so that they are not easily avoided, the psalmist also rejoices, saying: The Lord will break the molars of the lions.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.7":["If, then, we give place to this nation to come up over us, at once it will make our vine a desert, the vine from which we used to make the wine that gladdens the heart of man; and it strips the bark from our figs, or breaks them, so that we may not have in us the sweetest gifts of the Holy Spirit, so that the holy man may not rest under our vine and fig tree, under which, when he is there, he will not fear the assault of the adversaries. Nor is it enough for this nation to destroy the vine and break the fig tree, unless, searching, it searches them, and kills whatever is living in them, so that, all the greenness consumed, white and dead twigs remain, and there is fulfilled in us: If they do these things in the green wood, what will they do in the dry?\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.8":["Lament like a virgin girded with sackcloth over the husband of your youth. LXX: Lament to me over a bride girded with haircloth, over her virgin husband. The husband of youth, or, as the Seventy translated, παρθενικὸς, whom people commonly call the virgin husband, because he was the first to take away the flower of virginity, is understood to be none other than God, who in Abraham, Isaac and Jacob betrothed to himself a virgin bride stained by no filth of idolatry. To her he speaks also through Jeremiah: Did you not call me lord, and father, and prince of your virginity?. Hence the Apostle also says to the believers: I have betrothed you to one husband, to present you as a chaste virgin to Christ. As long as the bridegroom is with this bride, you cannot fast, nor lament, nor show by tears your longing for an absent bridegroom. But when the bridegroom has been taken away from her, she laments and wails, and girds herself with sackcloth and haircloth, and instead of a belt she is girded with a rope. We have understood who the virginal bridegroom is; but because this bridegroom, or husband, not only received a virgin as his bride, but also, in Hosea, took a harlot as his wife, for this reason it is written in Deuteronomy: If you go out against your enemies, and the Lord your God delivers them into your hand, and you lead them captive, and see among the number of the captives a beautiful woman, and love her, and wish to have her as your wife; you shall bring her into your house, and she shall shave her hair and pare her nails, and put off the garment in which she was taken, and sitting in your house she shall weep for her father and her mother for one month, and afterward you shall go in to her and sleep with her, and she shall be your wife. The husband of such a woman, that is, her lord, is not a virginal one; rather, he takes as his wife a harlot from the filth of the Gentiles. This we can say also of every soul of the believers. If it has believed in the Lord from a tender age, it has the Lord as a virginal bridegroom. But if it has come to the truth of the faith from the Jews or the Gentiles, and from the most filthy dregs of the heretics, and has passed from darkness to light, it will indeed have a bridegroom, but not a virgin one; and of such people it is said: In Egypt your breasts were crushed, and there you were deflowered.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.9-10":["According to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy) this seems to us to be the sense: after the bride who had received the teaching of God has been separated from the bridegroom through her sins, and instead of garments of fine linen and linen has been girded with haircloth, that is, has taken on the dress of one who mourns, then the sacrifice will perish, of which it is written: A sacrifice to God is a contrite spirit, and the libation of wine, which gladdens the heart of man, from the house of God, which is the Church, as the Apostle says to Timothy: That you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God. And the sacrifice and the libation will be taken away from the house of the Lord when, iniquity having multiplied, the love of many has grown cold; and the leaders of the people and the ministers of the altar see that the plains and level ground of the believers do not bear the fruits of virtues, but that everything bristles with vices, when the caterpillar, the locust, the cankerworm and the blight have consumed the grain and the barley, and the vines and the oil, and we no longer have the cup of which it is written: Your inebriating cup, how splendid it is. And the oil will have perished, of which we read in Ecclesiastes: At all times let your garments be white, and let oil not be lacking from your head, by which the face is gladdened and the head of the one who fasts is anointed.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.9-12":["The sacrifice and the libation have perished from the house of the Lord, and the priests, the ministers of the Lord, have mourned; the region is laid waste, the ground has mourned, because the wheat is devastated. The wine is confounded, the oil has languished, the farmers are confounded. The vinedressers have howled over the grain and the barley, because the harvest of the field has perished; the vine is confounded and the fig tree has languished; the pomegranate and the palm and the apple tree, and all the trees of the field have dried up, because joy is confounded from the sons of men. LXX: The sacrifice and the libation have been taken away from the house of the Lord; mourn, you priests, ministers of the Lord, because the plains have been made desolate; let the land mourn, because the wheat is afflicted, the wine has dried up, the oil is diminished, the farmers have dried up; mourn, you possessions, for the grain and the barley, because the vintage has perished from the field, the vine has dried up, and the figs are diminished; the pomegranate and the palm and the apple tree and all the trees of the field are dried up, because the sons of men have confounded joy. As far as the history is concerned, because of the multitude of locusts, or of the enemies who are described under their likeness, when everything had been laid waste and consumed, the sacrifice and the libation perished from the house of the Lord, of which they were accustomed to offer the one from fine flour, the other from wine. Therefore the priests, who are the ministers of the Lord, mourned, since neither the sacrifices nor the libations were duly celebrated, above all because the tithes too, which they had been accustomed to receive, were by no means offered. For the whole region was laid waste. The ground mourned, μετωνυμικῶς (by metonymy), for: those who dwelt on the ground; the wheat, the wine and the oil languished; the farmers and vinedressers too were confounded and howled, because not only the grain but also the barley, which is cheaper and more fruitful, dried up. And the legumes too, which I think are signified where he says: The harvest of the field has perished, that is, all that the ground is wont to produce. Why should I speak of grain, wine and oil and barley, when even the fruits of the trees have dried up, the fig tree has languished, and the pomegranate, and the palm, and the apple tree, and all the trees, whether unfruitful or fruit-bearing, have been consumed by the ravaging locust? All these things happened so that joy might be taken away, or confounded, from the sons of men. We can say that these same things happened also to the people of the Jews after the coming of the Savior, when, crying out with equal fury, they said: Crucify, crucify such a one; we have no king but Caesar; when Jerusalem was surrounded by an army, and they came into such extremity of famine and pestilence that they fed on the bodies of their sons, not yet grown; and all the sacrifices were taken away, and joy was confounded from the sons of men, because they refused to receive the joy of which the angel speaks to the shepherds: I announce to you great joy.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.11-12":["Then the farmers will be confounded, when they see that in their possessions there springs up no wheat, by which men are nourished, and no barley, by which the irrational beasts of burden are sustained, and that the vine is confounded, of which it is written: Israel is a leafy vine, abundant fruit is in it; the Lord waiting for it to make grapes, and it made thorns. The fig tree too has languished, under which Nathanael was before he believed, and the pomegranate, to whose rind the cheeks of the bride are compared in the Song; and the palm, which, having lost its greenness, has dried up, of which it was once said: But the just man will flourish like the palm; and the apple tree, of which we read in the same Song: As the apple tree among the trees of the wood, so is my kinsman among the sons. What need is there to run through all the trees, since all have dried up, and instead of joy and gladness grief and confusion have overwhelmed the sons of men?\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.13":["Gird yourselves and lament, you priests; howl, you ministers of the altar; go in, lie in sackcloth, you ministers of my God, because the sacrifice and the libation have perished from the house of your God; sanctify a fast, call an assembly, gather the old men, all the inhabitants of the earth, into the house of your God, and cry to the Lord. LXX: Gird yourselves and lament, you priests; bewail, you who minister at the altar; go in, sleep in sackcloths, you ministers of God, because the sacrifice and the libation have failed from the house of your God; sanctify a fast, proclaim a healing; gather the old men, all the inhabitants of the earth, into the house of your God, and cry to the Lord vehemently. Let him who is a holy priest, and eats the Passover of the Lord, be girded with the belt of chastity, and hear with the apostles: Let your loins be girded, and burning lamps in your hands. But let him who is a sinner, and whom his own conscience gnaws, be girded with haircloth and lament, either his own sins or those of the people, and let him enter the Church, from which he had gone out because of his sins, and lie, or sleep, in sackcloth, so that by austerity of life he may make up for the past delights by which he had offended God. For those who are clothed in soft garments are in the houses of kings. And let the priests gird themselves and lament and howl, and sleep in sackcloth, the prophet exhorting them to repentance, who says: Ministers of my God, because the sacrifice and the libation have perished from the house of your God, of which we spoke above.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.14":["Nor is it enough to weep, or to lament and take on the dress of one who mourns, unless they sanctify a fast and call an assembly. If every fast pleased God, he would never say: Sanctify a fast, and: I have not chosen such a fast, says the Lord. And in the Gospel those are condemned who disfigure their faces so that they may appear to men to fast; and in the days, he says, of your fasts you strike with fists, and oppress the poor. Therefore he now says: Sanctify a fast. The Manichaean fasts, and many heretics, above all the Encratites, whose chief is Tatian; but this fast is worse than surfeit and drunkenness. And call an assembly, or a healing, so that through repentance we may heal our sins. For assembly, in the Hebrew one reads arasa, which Symmachus translated as synod, Aquila as day of gathering. Gather the old men, whose age, near to death and of riper judgment, takes up the fear and worship of God the more. And also all the inhabitants of the earth, of whom he had said above: Hear this, old men, and perceive with your ears, all inhabitants of the earth, so that both the old men and the inhabitants of the earth may be gathered into the house of God, which is the Church. And when they are in the Church, and from the unlike flock of priests and people, of old men and inhabitants of the earth, one flock has been made, cry, he says, to the Lord in your hearts and say:\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.15":["Ah, ah, ah for the day, because the day of the Lord is near, and like devastation from the mighty one it will come. LXX: Woe is me, woe is me, woe is me for the day, because the day of the Lord is near, and like misery from misery it will come. For what we have translated from the mighty one, and what in Hebrew is said saddai, which we have said several times is one of the ten names of God, the LXX translated misery, reading sud for saddai. So the voice of the priests and of the people crying to the Lord is brought in, so that they say three times: Woe is me. I believe it is because, through the sins they had committed, they had offended the holy Trinity. But the day of retribution for all sins is that of which all the prophets write, and above all Isaiah cries aloud: Behold, the incurable day of the Lord is coming, of fury and of wrath, to make the whole world a desert, and to destroy the sinners out of it. This day is rightly called incurable, because when the day of judgment has come there will be no place for repentance; and compared with eternity it is near and not far off. And what follows: And like misery from misery it will come, or, like devastation from the mighty one it will come, has this sense: that evils follow upon evils, and every affliction is dealt out with God as judge, who can destroy body and soul in Gehenna. What we have said generally of the day of judgment, let us refer specially to the time of the Jewish captivity, when Jerusalem was taken and the temple destroyed; for not much time afterward there came also the captivity of the ten and the two tribes by the Assyrians and Chaldeans, and it is foretold as about to come, so that when it has come it may not seem to have happened by chance, or by the strength of the enemies, but by the wrath and threat of God; or at least so that, if the people do penance, what is to come may not come, which will come if they persist in their sins.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.16":["Has not the nourishment perished before our eyes, from the house of our God, gladness and exultation? LXX: Before your eyes the nourishment has perished, from the house of our God gladness and joy. Before the eyes of sinners their nourishment perishes, when the crops already hoped for are taken out of their hands, and the locust forestalls the reaper, so that what in hope has been stored in the granaries the cankerworm and the blight consume. From those too who dwell in the Church, according to the quality of their merits, if they have sinned, the wheat of the spirit and the barley of the letter is taken away, so that they suffer hunger for the word of God. And when the nourishment has been taken away, it follows that joy and gladness are taken away from the house of God, so that those who before heard the Apostle saying: Rejoice in the Lord always; again I say, rejoice, afterward hear the Lord calling them to repentance: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.17":["The beasts of burden have rotted in their dung; the granaries are demolished, the storehouses are scattered, because the wheat is confounded; why has the animal groaned, have the herds of cattle lowed? Because there is no pasture for them, and even the flocks of small cattle have perished. LXX: The calves have leapt about in their stalls; the treasures are scattered, the winepresses are dug under, because the wheat has dried up; what shall we lay up for ourselves? The herds of oxen have wept, because there were no pastures for them, and the flocks of sheep have perished. After the nourishment had perished, and joy and gladness had been taken away from the house of God, the beasts of burden too rotted in their dung, or, according to the spiritual understanding, frolicked in their stalls and kicked against their creator, so that there was fulfilled what is written: If they are not filled, they will murmur. He rots in his dung whose god is his belly, and who says: Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die; for him the granaries of future happiness are demolished, and the storehouses of eternal abundance are scattered, or the winepresses are overturned, because if there is no grain and no wine, granaries and winepresses are prepared in vain.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.18":["And when everything has dried up, then they will complain with a tearful voice and say: What shall we lay up for ourselves? And what follows according to the LXX: The herds of oxen have wept, because there are no pastures for them, compels us to take what has been said not of oxen and herds, but of those who because of their simplicity are called oxen and sheep. Of their pastures the Savior speaks: He will go in and go out, and will find pasture. But understand all these things under the metaphor of the caterpillar, the locust, the cankerworm and the blight: that when the crops had been taken away and laid waste, famine and pestilence took possession of everything.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.1.19-20":["To you, O Lord, I will cry, because fire has eaten the beautiful places of the desert, and the flame has set on fire all the trees of the region; and even the beasts of the field, like a garden bed thirsting for rain, have looked up to you, because the springs of waters have been dried up; and fire has devoured the beautiful places of the desert. LXX: To you, O Lord, I will cry, because fire has consumed the beautiful places of the desert, and the flame has set on fire all the trees of the field, and the beasts of burden of the plain have looked up to you, because the outflowings of waters have been dried up; and fire has devoured the beautiful places of the desert. As the prophet cries to the Lord, or the people through the prophet, because fire has eaten the beautiful places of the desert and the flame has set on fire all the trees of the region, the beasts and the beasts of burden of the field, or of the plain, have looked up to the Lord, like a little garden bed thirsting for rain. For Aquila signifies this by one word, saying ἐπρασιώθη; and they have looked up to him because the springs, or the outflowings, of waters have dried up, and fire has devoured the beautiful places of the desert: namely, that the caterpillar and the locust and the cankerworm and the blight have done this, what fire does in stubble, and flame in brambles. And by the beautiful places of the desert, which in Hebrew are called naoth, let us understand either the level stretches of the plains, or the flowering meadows, or places green with grass, which provided pasture for the beasts of burden. Now what made the people cry to the Lord through the prophet, and the beasts of burden look up to him, was the fire which devoured the beautiful places of the desert, and the flame which set on fire all the trees of the region, so that crops and fruits perished alike, and the fact that the springs of waters were dried up; and whatever could be found in the desert the devouring flame consumed. And at the same time let us consider that, except for distress and the taking away of their delights, neither the prophet nor the beasts would have cried to the Lord or looked up to the Lord; and this indeed can also be referred to a man once just, who, when he has turned aside and done evil, his former virtues will profit him nothing; but the Lord will judge him in those things in which he finds him. We can call beautiful places of the desert those of whom it is written: More are the sons of the deserted one than of her who has a husband. But the flame which sets on fire all the trees of the region must be believed to be that which is sent in with the burning arrows of the devil, so that nothing of good fruits may remain in us, but all may be consumed by the fire. For all adulterers, their hearts are like an oven; and not only the prophet, or the people through the prophet, which is a rational animal, but the beasts of burden too, of which it is said: Men and beasts of burden you will save, O Lord. And elsewhere: I have become like a beast of burden before you. And again: I will sow them with the seed of men and of beasts of burden, have looked up to the Lord, and have begged for the dew of his mercy, because the springs of waters have been dried up, which the hart longs for. And of which the Lord speaks through Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water. And when the springs of waters, which water and refresh all dry things, have been dried up, it follows that whatever was beautiful in us has been consumed by the heat of the fire, of which the Lord speaks in the Gospel: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.1-2":["Blow the trumpet in Zion; wail on my holy mountain. Let all the inhabitants of the land be troubled, for the day of the Lord comes, for the day of darkness and gloom is near, a day of cloud and whirlwind; like the morning spread out over the mountains, a people many and strong; there has not been its like from the beginning, and after it there will not be, even to the years of generation and generation. Before its face a devouring fire, and behind it a burning flame; the land before it is like a garden of delight, and behind it the solitude of the desert, and there is none who escapes it; like the appearance of horses is their appearance, and like horsemen so shall they run; like the sound of four-horse chariots they shall leap upon the tops of the mountains; like the sound of a flame of fire devouring stubble, like a strong people prepared for battle; before its face the peoples shall be tormented; all faces shall be brought to a pot; like mighty men they shall run, like men of war they shall climb the wall; men shall walk in their ways, and shall not turn aside from their paths; none shall press upon his brother, each shall walk in his own track. Moreover they shall fall through the windows and shall not be destroyed; they shall enter the city, they shall run upon the wall, they shall climb up the houses, they shall enter through the windows like a thief; before its face the earth trembled, the heavens were moved, the sun and the moon were darkened, and the stars withdrew their splendor; and the Lord gave his voice before the face of his army, for his camps are exceedingly many, for they are strong and doing his word. For great is the day of the Lord and very terrible, and who shall endure it? LXX: Blow the trumpet in Zion, proclaim on my holy mountain; let all the inhabitants of the land be troubled, because the day of the Lord is at hand, because the day of darkness and gloom is near, a day of cloud and frost; like the morning a people many and strong shall be poured out over the mountains; there has not been its like from the beginning, and after it there will not be, even to the years of generation of generations. Before it a consuming fire, and behind it a kindled flame. Like a paradise of delight is the land before its face; and its hinder parts like a plain of solitude, and there shall be none who is saved from it; like the appearance of horses is their appearance, and like horsemen so shall they pursue. Like the voice of four-horse chariots they shall leap upon the summits of the mountains, and like the voice of a flame of fire devouring stubble; and like a people many and strong prepared for battle; before its face the peoples shall be crushed, every face like the scorching of a pot; like fighters they shall run, and like men of war they shall climb upon the walls, and each shall walk in his own way, and they shall not turn aside from their paths; none shall withdraw from his neighbor; weighed down with their arms they shall go, and they shall fall upon their javelins, and shall not be consumed; they shall seize the city, and run about upon the walls; they shall climb upon the houses and enter through the windows like thieves; before its face the earth shall be confounded, and the heaven shall be moved; the sun and the moon shall be darkened, and the stars shall withdraw their light; and the Lord shall give his voice before the face of his strength, because his camps are exceedingly many, because strong are the works of his words, for great is the day of the Lord, great and exceedingly illustrious, and who shall be sufficient for it? Again, by a metaphor of locusts, the onset of the Chaldeans is described, and the prophet is commanded, or rather through the prophet all who can hear the word of God, to lift up their voice like a trumpet, and to proclaim in Zion, and to sound on his holy mountain, so that at the noise of the cry and of the trumpet all the people of Jerusalem may tremble. And when, he says, you have sounded, say this: The day of the Lord will by no means be put off any longer, that is, the day of retribution and vengeance; captivity is close at hand, already the army of the Babylonians has gone out from its place, near is the day of darkness and of distress, the day of gloom and cloud and whirlwind, in which every light of gladness will be taken away, and all things will be overwhelmed by darkness. As, he says, the morning and the dawn suddenly put the darkness to flight, and the daybreak lights up all the mountains, so the army of the Babylonians will be poured out over all your land.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.1":["I did not wish to divide a pericope joined and coherent in itself, lest what was one in sense should be plucked apart into different sections. Let us pass to the spiritual understanding, taking up each point again. The blast of trumpets and horns we read of not only in Leviticus and in the book of Numbers, in which it is commanded that Moses make two silver trumpets, with which, on the kalends, and in the seventh month on the first day of the month, and at the jubilee, and at the other festivals, and when they go forth to war, those who are bound to this office should sound; but it is also written that the walls of Jericho fell at the blast of the trumpets. And the Lord says that he will send his angel with the blast of a trumpet, and the Apostle proclaims that the resurrection of the dead takes place at the sound of the trumpet. And in the Apocalypse of John too we read that seven angels received a trumpet each, and as they sounded in order, the things came to pass which the Scripture describes. Therefore the priests and teachers are now commanded to lift up their voice like a trumpet, and to fulfill what is written: Go up on a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice, you who announce to Jerusalem, that they may blow the trumpet in Zion, that is, in the Church, which is interpreted watchtower and height. And on the holy mountain of God, which is Christ, so that all the inhabitants of the land may be troubled, or confounded, and confusion may draw them to salvation. But by the day of the Lord understand the day of judgment, or the day of each one's departure from the body. For what will come to pass for all on the day of judgment is fulfilled in each one on the day of death.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.2-3":["Nor should you think it a feeble crowd: it is a people many and strong, and no other nation like it can be found, either in the past or in the future; whatever it touches it will consume like a ravenous flame, and will leave nothing whole behind it; like a garden and paradise of delight is all the land which it has not touched; like a solitude and a desert is whatever it has plundered, nor can anyone be found who escapes its hands.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.2":["It is a day of darkness and of distress, a day of cloud and whirlwind; for all things are full of punishments and torments. A people of angels, many and strong, will come, to render to each according to his works; and as the morning and the rising dawn first takes hold of the mountains, so judgment will begin from the lofty and the powerful, that the mighty may suffer torments mightily. There has not been its like from the beginning, and after it there will not be, even to the years of generation and generation. For all the evils which are contained in the ancient histories, and which befell men either by the flooding of the sea, or by the overflowing of rivers, or by pestilence, diseases, famine, beasts, the devastation of enemies, cannot be compared with these punishments which are to be rendered on the day of judgment.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.3-5":["Before the face of this people, which is strong and many, there will be a devouring, or consuming, fire, to consume all the hay, wood and stubble in us. Whence it is also said of God: God is a consuming fire, and behind him a burning flame; so that he leaves nothing without punishment. Whomever this people has not touched, and in whom it has not found material for burning, will be likened to the garden of God and to the paradise of delight, which in Hebrew is called Eden. But if it burns anyone, it will reduce him like a solitude to ashes and embers, nor is there anyone who can escape its fury; its cruel appearance will have the likeness of snorting horses; and so they will run about to torment those whom they have received into their power, like horsemen flying hither and thither; terrible will be their sound, like that of four-horse chariots running down a slope; and they will leap upon the tops of the mountains, desiring to torment all the lofty and those set on the summit of the Church. And because before their face is a devouring and consuming fire, they will lay waste all things as a flame consumes stubble; so will they come to punish, so ready and prepared will they be, like a strong people for battle.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.4-5":["Their appearance is like that of fierce horses, and like horsemen they will run about hither and thither. Not that the Babylonians are compared to horsemen, but that the locusts, by a figure for the enemies, are said to run about hither and thither like horsemen: As the sound, he says, of four-horse chariots and of carts, so will be the sound of the locusts. They will leap upon the tops of the mountains, so as to climb all the lofty places; and as a flame quickly consumes stubble, so the sound and the terror of their voice will lay waste all things. And what he adds: Like a strong people prepared for battle, he refers again to the locusts, so that he seems to speak not as of enemies but as of locusts; and yet while we read of locusts, let us think of the Babylonians.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.6":["So great will the terror be that all the nations round about will shudder both in mind and in body, or rather will be tormented; and from the greatness of their dread the faces of all will be turned into the likeness of a pot, which, burned by the fire, shows the blackness and soot of a foul appearance.\n— Commentary on Joel","So great will be the fear of all, so great the consciousness of sins, that no one will shine or have the splendor of joy, but like a scorched pot his face will be turned into darkness.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.7-8":["They will run about, he says, like mighty men, no doubt the locusts: and like men of war they shall climb the wall, so that the height of the walls cannot check the onset of the strong, and they enter by no means through the gates, but over the walls: Each one shall walk in his own ways, and they shall not turn aside from their paths. This we saw lately in this province. For when the hosts of locusts came and filled the air which is between heaven and earth, they flew in such order, by the disposition of God who commanded them, that, like the little tesserae which are fixed in pavements by the hand of the craftsman, they kept their place, and did not swerve even by a point and, so to speak, by a nail's breadth, to another. And to make the metaphor plain, and through the windows, he says, they shall fall, and shall not be destroyed. For nothing is impassable to locusts, since they penetrate fields, and crops, and trees, and cities, and houses, and the secret places of bedchambers. But this is said of locusts so that it may be understood of enemies. And where we have translated they shall fall through the windows, and shall not be destroyed, the LXX rendered weighed down in their arms they shall go, and they shall fall upon their javelins and shall not be consumed, which seems to me by no means to fit the order of the exposition; rather the error arose from this, that the Hebrew word sala they understood as javelins, we as windows.\n— Commentary on Joel","They will climb the wall and every fortification; and fulfilling the work enjoined on them, they will not turn aside to another part, but will cling to the punishments of the wretched, nor will they be sated with their torments, but each will carry out the punishments entrusted to him against sinners.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.8-9":["Moreover they will enter through the windows, or will fall, or, weighed down with their arms, will walk, and upon their javelins will fall those whom they have received to be consumed. These are the windows of which Jeremiah also says: Death has entered through our windows; for the enemies desire to enter through all our senses, and to take the city of a good conscience, and to run about through our fortifications, and to destroy the houses which we have built for ourselves by good works. And they do all things entering through the windows like a thief, for they do not enter openly, that they may shoot in the dark at the upright of heart.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.9":["And he keeps up the narration he has begun, so as to say that they enter the city, run about on the walls, climb up the houses, and enter through the windows like thieves; not that those who are victors have the fear of thieves, but as thieves are wont to enter through windows and to steal in secret, so they, the doors being shut, lest there be any delay, will burst in through the windows with complete boldness.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.10":["Before the face of locusts of this kind the earth trembled, and the heavens were moved, which we should take as said hyperbolically: not that the force of locusts or of enemies is so great that it could move the heavens and shake the earth, but that to those who suffer adversity, from the greatness of their terror, the heaven seems to fall and the earth to heave. Finally, because of the multitude of locusts covering the sky, the sun and the moon will be turned into darkness, and the stars will withdraw their splendor, while the cloud of locusts placed in between does not let the light reach the earth.\n— Commentary on Joel","Before the face of this people, which is many and strong, the earth trembled, and the heavens were moved. For heaven and earth shall pass away, but the word of the Lord abides forever. Moreover the sun and the moon will not be able to look upon such great punishments of the wretched, and they will mourn, not having the righteousness of their office, and instead of clear light they will be covered with dreadful darkness; the stars too will withdraw their brightness, while even those who are holy will not see the presence of the Lord without dread.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.11":["The Lord also will give his voice before the face of so powerful an army, for his camps are many, and the greatness of his power is shown even in small creatures. And these many and innumerable camps are exceedingly strong, and doing his word. By this saying it is shown that the Babylonians will come by the will of God, and will obey his command. Great is, he says, the day of the Lord, in which Jerusalem is to be taken, and very terrible; and no one will be able to endure it, and to escape the necessity of captivity or of death.\n— Commentary on Joel","In all these things the Lord will give his voice before the face of his army. For as the Babylonians, punishing Jerusalem, are called the army of God, so the most wicked angels, of whom it is written: Fury and wrath and distress, a sending by most wicked angels, are called the army of God and are named his camps, while they do the will of the Lord. Great is the day of the Lord and terrible, of which it is written elsewhere: Why do you desire the day of the Lord? And this is darkness, and not light; and very terrible; and hardly anyone, or no one, will be able to endure it without offering in himself material for its raging.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.12":["Now therefore says the Lord: Turn to me with your whole heart, in fasting, and in weeping and in mourning; and rend your hearts, and not your garments, and turn to the Lord your God, for he is kind and merciful, patient and of much mercy, and placable over evil; who knows if he will turn and pardon, and leave behind him a blessing, sacrifice and libation to the Lord our God? LXX: And now says the Lord our God: Turn to me from your whole heart, in fasting, and in weeping and in mourning; and rend your hearts, and not your garments; and turn to the Lord your God, for he is merciful and compassionate, patient and of much mercy, and repenting over evils; who knows if he will return and repent, and leave behind him a blessing, sacrifice and libation to the Lord our God? The preceding section, from the place where it is written: Blow the trumpet in Zion, wail on my holy mountain; let all the inhabitants of the land be troubled, up to the place where we read: Great is the day of the Lord and very terrible, who shall endure it? announces, by the figure of locusts, the coming of the Chaldeans, and what evils are to come upon the people. Now he calls them to repentance, and exhorts them to turn to the Lord, so that, corrected with their whole mind, they may not suffer what the Lord threatens; and the sense is: All that is contained in the preceding discourse I spoke for this reason, to terrify you with my threat. Therefore turn to me with your whole heart, and show the repentance of your mind by fasting and weeping and mourning; so that, fasting now, you may afterwards be filled, weeping now, you may afterwards laugh, mourning now, you may afterwards be comforted.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.13":["And since it is the custom that in sad and adverse things you rend your clothes, which the high priest too is recorded in the Gospel to have done, to aggravate the charge against the Lord the Savior; and which we read that Paul and Barnabas did on hearing words of blasphemy. Therefore I command you by no means to rend your garments, but your hearts, which are full of sins, and which, like wineskins, unless they have been rent, will burst of themselves. And when you have done this, return to the Lord your God, whom your former sins had made a stranger to you; and do not despair of pardon because of the greatness of your crimes, for great mercy will blot out great sins. For he is kind and merciful, preferring the repentance of sinners to their death, patient and great in mercy, one who does not imitate human impatience, but waits a long time for our repentance; and placable, or repenting over evils, so that if we do penance for our sins, he too may repent of his threat, and not bring upon us the evils which he has threatened, and with the change of our resolve he too may be changed. But here we ought to take evil not as the contrary of virtue, but as affliction, according to what we read elsewhere: Sufficient for the day is its own evil. And: If there is evil in a city which the Lord has not done.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.14":["And likewise, because he had said above, kind and merciful, patient and great in mercy, and placable, or repenting over evils, lest perhaps the greatness of his clemency should make us careless, he adds in the person of the prophet and says: Who knows if he will turn and pardon, and leave behind him a blessing? I, he says, exhort to repentance, which is my part, and I know that God is ineffably merciful, as David says: Have mercy on me, O God, according to your great mercy, and according to the multitude of your compassions blot out my iniquity. But because we cannot know the depth of the riches and of the wisdom and knowledge of God, I temper my statement, and I wish rather than presume, saying: Who knows if he will turn and pardon? When he says who, it is to be understood as either impossible or difficult: Sacrifice and libation to the Lord our God; so that after he has given the blessing and has forgiven us our sins, we may be able to offer victims to God.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.15-16":["Blow the trumpet in Zion, sanctify a fast, call an assembly, gather the people, sanctify the Church, assemble the old men, gather the little ones and those that suck the breasts. Let the bridegroom go forth from his bed and the bride from her bridal chamber. Between the vestibule and the altar the priests, the ministers of the Lord, shall weep, and shall say: Spare, O Lord, your people, and do not give your inheritance to reproach, that the nations should rule over them. Why do they say among the peoples: Where is their God? LXX: Sound the trumpet in Zion, sanctify a fast, proclaim a healing, gather the people, sanctify the Church, choose the old men; gather the little ones and those that suck the breasts, let the bridegroom go forth from his bed, and the bride from her bridal chamber. Between the base and the altar the priests, the ministers of the Lord, shall weep, and shall say: Spare, O Lord, your people, and do not give your inheritance to reproach, that the Gentiles should rule over them, lest they say among the nations: Where is their God? He still exhorts them to repentance, before the hostile army comes. Above, he says, I had said: Blow the trumpet in Zion, wail on my holy mountain, and the rest, for the day of the Lord comes, for the day of darkness and gloom is near, a day of cloud and whirlwind, announcing to you that a people many and strong would come, to overturn your possessions and cities. Now, because I am kind and merciful, patient and great in mercy, again I command and say: Sound the trumpet in Zion, and proclaim repentance among the peoples; sanctify a fast, proclaim a healing, or an assembly, of which we have already spoken before: gather the people, so that the people that sinned when scattered may cease to sin when gathered. Sanctify the Church, so that there may be no one in the Church who is not holy, lest perhaps your prayers be hindered, and a little leaven corrupt the whole lump. Assemble, or choose the old men, so that not age but holiness may be chosen in them. Gather also the little ones and those that suck the breasts, so that there may be no age which does not turn to the Lord. Little ones and sucklings, of whom we read in the psalms and in the Gospel: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise. Whom Peter says are nourished with rational milk, without guile; to whom Paul speaks: I gave you milk to drink, not solid food; and whom the Savior too remembers: Do not despise one of these least ones. Let also the bridegroom go forth from his bed, and the bride from her bridal chamber, so that in the time of fasting, of calling and assembly, of the sanctification of the Church, of the choosing of the old men, of the gathering of the little ones and those that suck the breasts, bridegroom and bride may not serve the work of marriage; to whom it is also permitted by the law not to go forth to war. Hence the Apostle too commands withdrawing for a little while from intercourse, so that we may be free for prayer. Therefore he who says that he does penance by chastening his diet, and by fasting and almsgiving, promises this in vain with his words, unless he goes forth from his bed and fulfills a holy and pure fast with a chaste repentance.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.17":["And as to what follows: Between the vestibule and the altar the priests shall weep, for vestibule the LXX translated base; Symmachus προπύλον (gateway); Aquila πρόδομον (front hall); Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, ulam, which we can call the space before the doors of the temple, and the portico. And note what he commands the priests who are ministers of the Lord: that they weep between the temple and the altar, and say with the Apostle: Who is weak, and I am not weak? Who is made to stumble, and I do not burn? and: To weep with those who weep. And the place fit for repentance and confession is the temple and the altar; and he teaches what the priests ought to say, or rather how they ought to entreat the Lord: Spare, O Lord, your people, which, when it sinned, was called not your people; now, after it has withdrawn from its vices, is called your people. And do not give your inheritance to reproach, that the nations should rule over them. The riddle that lay hidden is laid open. For that people many and strong, which above was described under the name of the caterpillar, and the locust, and the cankerworm, and the blight, is now shown more plainly who it is: That the nations, he says, should rule over them. And the inheritance of the Lord is given to reproach when they have served enemies, and the nations have said: Where is their God, whom they boasted to be their protector, their defender? We can also interpret the nations as the adverse powers, which, so long as we do not do penance, rule over us, and taunt us and say: Where is their God? The Jews refer this passage to Gog and Magog, most savage nations which they say will come against Israel in the last time, of which it is written more fully in Ezekiel.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.18-19":["The Lord was jealous for his land, and spared his people; and the Lord answered and said to his people: Behold, I will send you grain and wine and oil, and you shall be filled with it, and I will no longer make you a reproach among the nations; and him who is from the north I will put far from you, and I will drive him into a land pathless and desert; his face toward the eastern sea, and his hinder part toward the last sea, and his stench shall go up, and his rottenness shall go up, because he has acted proudly. LXX: And the Lord was jealous for his land, and spared his people, and the Lord answered, and said to his people: Behold, I will send you grain and wine and oil, and you shall be filled with them, and I will no longer make you a reproach among the nations, and him who is from the north I will drive back from you, and I will lead him out into a land which is without water, and I will destroy his face into the first sea, and his hinder parts into the last sea; and his stench shall go up, and his rottenness shall go up, because his works have been magnified. The priests prayed for the people and said: Spare, O Lord, your people, and do not give your inheritance to reproach, and the people did what was commanded, sanctifying a fast, proclaiming a healing, gathering the multitude, sanctifying the Church, choosing the old men, gathering the little ones and those that suck the breasts, and the bridegroom went forth from his bed, and the bride from her bridal chamber, and they served by no means the flesh and pleasure, but the soul and weeping. After this, the Lord was jealous for his land, which he had before despised as if it were another's, and had allowed to be ravaged by the devastating locust; and he spared the penitent to such a degree as to make them worthy of his answer, and to say: Since the locust, the cankerworm and the blight have laid waste all your crops, therefore I will give you grain, and the rest which the prophet describes, and I will no longer hand you over to captivity.\n— Commentary on Joel","But according to tropology, every soul is the land of the Lord, in which the householder sows his seed; and when it has produced tares instead of wheat, that is, wild oats and darnel, and has offended its Lord, and afterwards has done penance, and has said in mourning: Spare, O Lord, your people, the Lord is jealous for his land, and will spare it, which he had long despised, and deigns to address it and says: I will send you grain, of which it is written: Amen, amen, I say to you, unless a grain of wheat, falling into the earth, dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears many fruits and wine which gladdens the heart of man, and oil which makes the face cheerful, so that the old sadness of sins may be tempered by the gladness of grain and wine and oil, that is, of the virtues; and they will have such an abundance of all good things that they will be filled and sated with them. And when they have obtained this, they will by no means be given over to reproach among the nations, of which the Apostle speaks: We have no wrestling against flesh and blood, but against principalities and powers, against the rulers of the world of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.20":["And the Assyrian and Chaldean, who is from the north, I will put far from you, of whom it is written above: A people many and strong, before its face a devouring fire, and behind it a burning flame; like the appearance of horses is their appearance. And I will drive him, he says, into a land of solitude; and his front parts will fall into the eastern sea, and his hinder parts into the last sea, and his stench will go up, that is, the stench of him who is from the north, and his rottenness, because he acted proudly. I have often pointed out that under the figure of locusts is described the onset of the Chaldeans, by which Judea was laid waste. Accordingly he keeps the metaphor in what remains, and speaks according to the situation of the province, so that he seems to speak not of enemies but of locusts. Even in our own times we have seen hosts of locusts cover the land of Judea, which afterwards, by the mercy of the Lord, while the priests and peoples were entreating the Lord between the vestibule and the altar, that is, between the place of the cross and of the resurrection, and were saying: Spare your people, were hurled, as a wind arose, into the first and the last sea. By the first sea, which is near the desert and faces the east, understand that in which Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim once stood, which is now called the Dead Sea, because no living creatures live there. And the last sea is this one which is toward the west, and which leads to Egypt, on whose shore Gaza, and Ashkelon, Azotus and Joppa, and Caesarea, and the other maritime cities are situated. And when the shores of both seas were filled with heaps of dead locusts, which the waters had vomited up, their rottenness and stench were so noxious that they corrupted even the air, and a pestilence arose among beasts and men alike. Does the learned reader ask where this happened according to the letter in the case of the Chaldeans? Not long after the time when these things were prophesied: for in Isaiah we read that one hundred and eighty-five thousand of the Chaldeans were struck down in one night under King Hezekiah, the angel raging against them. This we have said according to the history.\n— Commentary on Joel","Him also who is from the north — of whom Jeremiah speaks: From the north evils shall blaze up upon all the inhabitants of the land; of whom Solomon also writes: The north wind is a most harsh wind —; I will put him far from you, he says, and I will drive him into a land pathless and desert, which has no knowledge of God, in which the Holy Spirit does not dwell. And his face will be in the first sea and in the last sea, in those who have opened to him the door of sins, and with whom they have remained to the last moment of life; and his stench and rottenness goes up even upon those who promise great things to themselves, and fall through pride, because human frailty is never safe, and the more we grow in virtues, the more we ought to fear lest we fall from the heights. According to the letter, it is the south wind rather than the north wind that usually brings swarms of locusts, that is, they come not from the cold but from the heat. But since he was speaking of the Assyrians, setting forth the likeness of locusts, he therefore put in the north wind, so that we should understand not a real locust, which usually comes from the south, but under the locust the Assyrians and Chaldeans.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.21-22":["Let us pass to the order of the spiritual understanding: Do not fear, O land, nay rather take courage and be glad, you who before had lost the Lord's seed by your dryness, because the Lord has magnified himself so as to show mercy to you, to such a degree that even the cattle of the region and of the desert solitude were filled with glad fallow lands, and the tree of the cross bore its fruit, and the most sweet gifts of the Holy Spirit bestowed their bounty on all.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.21-24":["Do not fear, O land: exult and be glad, for the Lord has magnified himself in acting; do not fear, animals of the region, for the beautiful places of the desert have sprouted, for the tree has borne its fruit, the fig tree and the vine have given their power; and sons of Zion: exult and be glad in the Lord your God, for he has given you a teacher of righteousness, and he will make the morning rain come down to you, and the late rain in the beginning; and the threshing floors shall be filled with grain, and the presses shall overflow with wine and oil, and I will restore to you the years which the locust, the cankerworm, and the blight, and the caterpillar have eaten. My great strength which I sent among you; and you shall eat, feeding, and be filled, and you shall praise the name of the Lord your God, who has done wonders with you, and my people shall not be confounded for evermore; and you shall know that I am in the midst of Israel, and I am the Lord your God, and there is none besides, and my people shall not be confounded forever. LXX: Take courage, O land, rejoice and be glad, for the Lord has magnified himself in acting; take courage, cattle of the field, for the plains of the solitude have sprouted, for the tree has borne its fruit, the fig tree and the vine have given their strength; and sons of Zion, rejoice and be glad in the Lord your God, for he has given you foods of righteousness, and has rained on you the early and the late rain, as from the beginning, and the threshing floors shall be filled with wheat, and the presses shall overflow with wine and oil. And I will repay you for the years in which the locust, and the cankerworm and the blight, and the caterpillar have eaten. My great strength which I sent among you, and you shall feed, eating, and be filled; and you shall praise the name of the Lord your God, who has done wonders with you, and my people shall not be confounded forever, and you shall know that I am in the midst of Israel, and I am the Lord your God, and there is none besides me, and my people shall not be confounded forever. For all that he had threatened above, he now promises the contrary. Above he had said: Fire has eaten the beautiful places of the desert, and flame has kindled all the trees of the region, and the beasts of the field, like a threshing floor thirsting for rain, have looked up to you, because the springs of waters are dried up, and fire has devoured the beautiful places of the desert. Now he softens sadness with glad things, and turns tears into laughter. Do not, he says, fear, animals of the region, for the beautiful places of the desert have sprouted, for the tree has borne its fruit, the fig tree and the vine have given their power; and so great will be the abundance of all things that I will not merely give you grain, wine and oil with which to be filled, but the threshing floors will be filled with grain, and the presses will overflow with wine and oil, so that you may be able not only to eat yourselves, but also to supply others.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.23-27":["He speaks also especially to the sons of Zion, that they should exult and rejoice, not in any trifling thing, but in the Lord their God, who has given them the nourishment of righteousness, and, as the Seventy translated, and the early and the late rain, so that they may eat and be glad, and praise the name of the Lord their God, who has done wonders with them, and may by no means be confounded; and may know that the Lord God of Israel dwells in their midst, and besides him there is no other, because the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father, and his people will not be confounded forever. These things, according to the letter, since they were promised by the Lord, we believe to have come to pass, and the past barrenness to have been made up by new crops, so that whatever the locust, the cankerworm, and the blight, and the caterpillar had consumed, τροπικῶς (figuratively), was replenished in the following years.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.23":["You too, whom after repentance I rightly call sons of Zion and of the Church, be glad and rejoice, because God the Father has given you a teacher of righteousness, or has granted you nourishment of righteousness, and has made the early and late rains come down to you. The seasonable rain is when we first receive doctrine, the late rain when we receive the fruits of our labor, and come to the perfect knowledge of the holy Scriptures. The early and the late rain can also be taken as the Old and the New Testament.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.24":["And not only, he says, has he given this, but he has made you abound in new crops of virtues, and be filled and inebriated with grain, wine and oil of which we have often spoken.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.25":["We ask why the caterpillar was called the strength, or the power, of the Lord, and not only power, but great power? As the might of God was shown in the plagues of Egypt through small creatures, and especially the gnats, which are midges so small that they can scarcely be seen by the eyes, so now too in a small and slow little worm, which can scarcely move, and is crushed by a light touch, the might of God and human frailty are shown. Not that God cannot, at his nod and by the majesty of his might, overturn the earth, and cover all things with a flood, or burn them up with lightning, but through small things and, so to speak, mere points of bodies, he shows human frailty. Hence we are also accustomed to answer Marcion and the other heretics who tear the Old Testament to pieces, that God made fleas and midges and bugs, and creatures of this kind, for this reason, to show the frailty and weakness of our flesh, which is so much nothing that it is wounded by these things that are small. But if the caterpillar, a slow and small little worm, is stronger than man, why does earth and ashes glory; and, lifted up by pride — though it is from the humus, from which the human being is named —, despise human things? A certain one interprets this passage thus: On the right and on the left we read of powers and strengths of God, which the Greeks call δυνάμεις. On the right the Seraphim and Cherubim, and all the angelic powers; on the left the contrary strengths, of which it is written: He sent upon them the wrath of his indignation, fury and wrath, and tribulation, a sending by evil angels; of which Micaiah too speaks in the book of Kings: I saw the Lord God of Israel sitting on his throne, and all the host of heaven stood beside him on his right and on his left. I think that it was a left-hand spirit which went forth, and stood before the Lord, and said: I will deceive Ahab, and I will go forth, and I will be a lying spirit in the mouth of all his prophets. To this left-hand and contrary power, which was apt for deceiving, and practiced in ruin, and had deceived many before, God says: You shall deceive and prevail; go forth and do so. That spirit also which choked Saul, of which his servants said to him: Behold, an evil spirit of God has stupefied you, was from the left-hand parts, which minister to the Lord for punishing those who have deserved to be tormented for their sins. For not only are men ministers and avengers of his wrath upon those who do evil, and not without cause do they bear the sword, but also the contrary strengths, which are called the fury and the wrath of God, which the prophet, deprecating them, says: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath. To such, so to speak, inquisitors and torturers the Apostle handed over sinners for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit might be saved; among whom is Satan too, to whom he handed over others that they might learn not to blaspheme. So much on why the caterpillar was called the power of God.\n— Commentary on Joel","And as for the years which you had lost through the perturbations that formerly reigned in you, when the locust, the cankerworm, the blight and the caterpillar had consumed your works. God did not suffer them to perish for you.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.26-27":["Then you will eat the fruits of righteousness, and be filled, and praise the name of the Lord your God, who has done wonders with you. But if after repentance God promises such plenty of all things, what will Novatus answer, who denies repentance, and that sinners can be restored to their former state, if they do works worthy of repentance? For God receives the penitent to such a degree that he calls them his people, and declares that they are by no means to be confounded, and promises that he will dwell in their midst, and that they will no longer have another God, but will trust with their whole mind in him who abides in them forever.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.28-29":["But all flesh, upon which the Lord promises that he will pour out of his spirit, is that of which Isaiah speaks: And all flesh shall see the salvation of God. Therefore the salvation of God cannot be seen unless the Holy Spirit is poured out. And whoever says that he believes in Christ, not believing in the Holy Spirit, will not have the eyes of perfect faith. Hence also in the Acts of the Apostles, those who had been baptized with the baptism of John into him who was to come, that is, in the name of the Lord Jesus, because they answered Paul when he asked them: But we do not even know whether there is a Holy Spirit, are baptized again; or rather they receive true baptism, because without the Holy Spirit and the mystery of the Trinity, whatever is received in one or another person is imperfect. Nor indeed will everyone who has received the Holy Spirit at once have spiritual grace as well, but through the outpouring of the Holy Spirit they will obtain diverse graces. Some prophecy, like the sons and daughters, who are of greater merit; others dreams, like the old men, now of more advanced age; others visions, like the young men, who have overcome the evil one; but the servants and handmaids, who still have the spirit of fear and not of love, for perfect love casts out fear, will have no prophecy, no dreams, no visions, but, content with the outpouring of the Holy Spirit, will possess only the grace of faith and salvation.\n— Commentary on Joel","The word of outpouring too, which in Hebrew is called esphoch, and which all have translated alike, shows the bounty of the gift, in that the gifts of the Holy Spirit have come down by no means upon a few prophets — as used to happen in the Old Testament —, but upon all who believe in the name of the Savior; by no means upon this one and that one, but upon all flesh. For there is no distinction of Jew and Greek, slave and free, man and woman, for we are all one in Christ. Hence also Agabus prophesied at Caesarea, and in Antioch there were very many prophets; and the Apostle commands that if, while one is prophesying, something has been revealed to another, the first should be silent. And we read that the four daughters of Philip the evangelist prophesied. Old men too saw dreams, when Paul, already an old man, heard a man of Macedonia saying to him: Come over to Macedonia and help us. And all the young men who stood out as most strong in Christ, and fought the battles of the Lord, saw visions, the Lord fulfilling his promises: I multiply visions, and in the hands of the prophets I have been likened.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.28-32":["And it shall be after these things, I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, and your young men shall see visions; moreover upon my servants and my handmaids in those days I will pour out my spirit, and I will give wonders in heaven and on earth, blood, and fire, and vapors of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and dreadful day of the Lord comes. And it shall be, everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. LXX: And it shall be after these things, I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your old men shall dream dreams, and your young men shall see visions; and upon my servants and upon my handmaids in those days I will pour out of my spirit, and I will give wonders in heaven, and upon the earth blood, fire and vapor of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and illustrious day of the Lord comes. And it shall be, everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. This passage the blessed apostle Peter explained as fulfilled at the time of the Lord's passion, when on the day of Pentecost the Holy Spirit came down upon the believers, and all spoke as the Holy Spirit gave them, so that those who were present marveled and said: Are not all these who are speaking Galileans? And how have we heard our own language in which we were born? Parthians, and Medes, and Elamites, and the rest; but others said: What does this mean? And when others mocked and said: They are full of new wine. Peter, standing with the eleven, lifted up his voice and said: Men of the Jews, and all you who dwell in Jerusalem, let this be known to you, and give ear to my words; for these men are not drunk, as you suppose, since it is the third hour of the day, but this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel. And it shall be after these things, says the Lord: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and they shall prophesy, and the rest, up to the place which we set out in the section. Since, therefore, Peter records that the prophecy of Joel was fulfilled at the time when the Lord suffered, we ask only this: how the earlier parts and the middle, and what follows up to the end of the volume, can cohere with one another, lest under one text and sequence of discourse a diverse explanation, dissonant with itself, should seem to gush forth? From the beginning, then, up to the place where it is written: Great is the day of the Lord and very terrible, and who shall endure it, it is a threat and a description of what the Lord is going to bring upon the sinful people. But from the place where we read: Turn to me with your whole heart, and in fasting, and in weeping, and in mourning, up to the place where he says: Why do they say among the peoples: Where is their God, it is the exhortation of the Lord, calling to repentance after punishments and torments. Again, from the place which follows: The Lord was jealous for his land and spared his people, and the Lord answering said, up to the place where it is written: And my people shall not be confounded forever, it is a promise of things to come, of the good things which they ought to wait for after they have done penance. And after many things which it would be long to go over now, these too are said in the promise, which at present we are trying to expound: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and the rest. I seem to myself to have kept the thread of the discourse from the beginning up to this place. It is a matter of the greatest labor how what follows is to be fitted to what we are now discussing. One says that what was promised generally for the last time has now been fulfilled in part; and that, because the apostles perceived the reward of things to come at the first coming of the Lord, they recorded that certain foretastes and first fruits of the wonders had been fulfilled, so that until what is perfect comes we might perceive what had gone before in part. But another asserts that it was the custom of the apostles, according to what is written of the holy man: He will dispose his words in judgment, that whatever they saw to be useful to their hearers, and not in conflict with the present, they confirmed by testimonies of another time; not that they abused the simplicity and inexperience of their hearers, as the impious Porphyry slanders, but that, according to the apostle Paul, they preached in season, out of season. And following the rule of the prophets, whatever the Jews promise themselves carnally in the last time, they said had been fulfilled spiritually in the first coming of the Lord the Savior; especially since both they and we say that what has been promised is to be fulfilled in Christ; differing only in this, that they contend it is future, we prove it already done; and of this we must speak more fully in what follows.\n— Commentary on Joel","This passage is most difficult and admits a manifold explanation. Let us, then, under tropology, refer all that has been said to those times to which the apostles Peter and Paul referred it, that is, when the Lord suffered and rose again. For it cannot be that we understand the earlier things of the time of the passion, and those that follow of the day of judgment, especially since there follows: For behold, in those days, and at that time, and this little verse, connecting what comes after with what precedes, says that all things were accomplished at one time.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.30-31":["Then the Lord will give wonders in heaven and on earth: in heaven, because the sun was turned into darkness and the moon into blood; on earth, because the earth trembled with so violent and unusual a motion that the tombs were opened and the rocks split. And as to his saying blood and vapors, or vapor of smoke, that blood is the one of which we read in the psalms: That your foot may be dipped in blood. And in Isaiah: How red are your garments. And the blood which, when the Savior's side was pierced, the Roman soldier shed mixed with water. And the fire of the Holy Spirit, which came down from heaven, as we read in the Acts of the Apostles: There appeared to them divided tongues as of fire, and it sat upon each of them, and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit; and they began to speak in various tongues, as the Holy Spirit gave them to speak. This is the fire which the Lord came to send upon the earth, and which, before he suffered, he longed to burn in his disciples. Fire is of a double nature: it has light for believers, it has darkness and punishments for unbelievers, who are called vapors of smoke. This smoke is most bitter, which blinded the eyes of the Jews, of which we read in Proverbs: As smoke is harmful to the eyes, and an unripe grape to the teeth, so is iniquity to those who use it. Of this smoke Isaiah too speaks in the great vision in which the blindness of the Jews was foretold: And the lintel was moved, and the house was filled with smoke. Whence there follows afterwards: Go, and say to this people: Hearing you shall hear, and shall not understand, and seeing you shall look, and shall not see; for the heart of this people has been made gross, and with their ears they have heard heavily. The sun too was turned into darkness, when it did not dare to look upon its Lord hanging, and the moon into blood; which either we should believe happened according to the history and was passed over in silence by the evangelists, for not all the things that Jesus did are reported as written; which if they were written one by one, I think that not even the world itself could contain the books that would have to be written; or certainly, as the sun was turned into darkness, not that it was itself changed into darkness, but that it brought darkness upon the world, so also the moon was not turned into blood, but condemned the Jews, covered with the horror of blasphemies and of denial against Christ, by the eternal blood of their own testimony, as they said: His blood be upon us, and upon our children.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.31-32":["He describes all these things as to come before the great and dreadful day of the Lord comes. But the great and dreadful day of the Lord is to be believed to be either that of the resurrection, or certainly, many ages later, the day of judgment, which is truly great and dreadful. But since there follows: And it shall be, everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved, and the apostle Paul refers this to the time of the Lord's passion, it is rather to be understood of the day of the resurrection. For he says, writing to the Romans: There is no distinction of Jew and Greek. For the same one is Lord of all: rich toward all who call upon him. For everyone, whoever calls upon the name of the Lord, shall be saved. How then shall they call upon him in whom they have not believed? Or how shall they believe him whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear without a preacher? And how shall they preach unless they are sent. And as to his saying: Everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved, let us believe that it is no light thing; for although the same Apostle writes: No one says Lord Jesus except in the Holy Spirit; yet this very saying is to be weighed not by speech but by the affection of the heart. For everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord shall be saved. Hence Paul and Sosthenes also write to the Church of God which is at Corinth: To those sanctified in Christ Jesus, called saints, with all who call upon the name of the Lord Jesus Christ; according to what we read in the psalms: Moses and Aaron among his priests, and Samuel among those who call upon his name. They called upon the Lord, and he heard them; in the pillar of cloud he spoke to them. If, therefore, it is written of the believing Corinthians, and of Moses and Aaron and Samuel, that they called upon the name of the Lord, let us believe that this grace belongs not to beginners, but to the perfect.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.2.32":["For in mount Zion and in Jerusalem there shall be salvation, as the Lord has said; and in the remnant whom the Lord shall call. LXX: For in mount Zion and in Jerusalem there shall be he who has been saved? As the Lord has said, and announcing those whom the Lord called. In the place where we have put salvation, or the one saved, and in the Hebrew is written phaleta, Symmachus translated, he who has fled. And again, where we said, in the remnant, and the LXX translated, announcing, in the Hebrew is read saridim, which the Jews think is the name of a place. After, therefore, the great and dreadful day of the Lord came, and whoever after the Lord's resurrection called upon his name was made safe, everyone who was on mount Zion and in Jerusalem was saved; for out of Zion went forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem, of which it is written: It cannot be that a prophet perish outside Jerusalem. The beginning, then, of those who were saved was in Zion and Jerusalem, in the watchtower and in the vision of peace, and in those who were the remnant, whom the Lord called. By the remnant we ought to understand those of the people of the Jews who believed, of whom Isaiah speaks: Unless the Lord of Sabaoth had left us seed, we would have become like Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah. These, the remnant, the Lord called, or he evangelized those whom he called: for so the LXX translated.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.1-2":["For behold, in those days and at that time, when I shall turn back the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem, I will gather all the nations, and I will bring them down into the valley of Jehoshaphat, and I will plead with them there over my people and my inheritance Israel, whom they have scattered among the nations; and they have divided my land, and over my people they have cast the lot; and they have put a boy in a brothel, and sold a girl for wine, that they might drink. LXX: For behold, I, in those days and at that time, when I shall turn back the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem, will gather all the nations, and I will bring them down into the valley of Jehoshaphat, and I will plead with them there for my people and my inheritance Israel, who have been scattered among the Gentiles; and they have divided my land, and over my people they have cast lots; and they have put a boy in a brothel, and they were selling girls for wine, and they were drinking. Let us speak as we promised: first, according to anagogy, joining what comes after to what went before; then, if we can, we shall try to refer the same things to the day of judgment. When the believers have been saved on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem, and the remnant of the people of the Jews, who believed with the apostles and through the apostles, has been called, at that time, after the Lord, who had come to preach sight to the blind and release to the captives, has turned back the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem, and has set free those who confess the Lord and who dwell in the Church, in which is the vision of peace, he will gather all the nations that refused to believe, and will bring them down into the valley of Jehoshaphat; which, according to the Hebrew truth, is said more expressively in Greek κατάξω, that is, I will lead downward, and draw down from the heights to lower places. And consider at the same time that when the Lord summons all unbelievers, or the adverse powers, to judgment, and pleads with them for his people: I will bring them down, he says, into the valley of Jehoshaphat. But when he exhorts believers to battle, he says: Rouse the strong; let them approach, let all the men of war go up; let the nations arise and go up into the valley of Jehoshaphat: they are urged not to go down, but to go up. For everyone who is judged on account of his sins is placed in the valley which is called Jehoshaphat, that is, the judgment of the Lord. For he who believes in the Lord shall not be judged. And there he will dispute with them, convincing them not by the power of his majesty but by the truth of reason: those who have parted the people of God among themselves and have scattered his inheritance among the nations, so as to compel the servants of God to serve them; and who have divided his land, separating it among themselves by many errors, so that some worshiped Jupiter, others Juno, and Minerva, and Fever, and Blight, and Anubis, and the crocodile, and the ibis, owls, hawks and storks.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.1-3":["All of which the Jews recall to have been fulfilled according to the letter in the times of Vespasian and Titus, and above all of Hadrian. But if we wish to refer what is written to the day of judgment, we say that everyone who is saved is saved in the Church, or in the heavenly Jerusalem. And after the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem has been turned back, then all are to be gathered and brought down into the valley of Jehoshaphat; and there the Lord will plead with those who have persecuted his people, and have divided among themselves the inheritance of the Lord, and have scattered them among the nations, and have cast the lot over his land. And these things we ought to take not only of the heretics, who have divided the people of God among themselves and make them Gentiles, but also of every harsh and proud teacher, who under the name of teaching and of the priesthood lord it over those allotted to them and oppress those who are subject to them. And when they have been negligent, and have caused one of the least to stumble, they even put boys into the brothel, or hand them over to harlots; and, for the sake of their own pleasure, whose God is the belly, and their glory is in their confusion, they sell girls that they may drink wine. And they hand over young men to harlots and sell girls to luxury who, for the sake of shameful gain, do not rebuke those who sin.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.3":["Under these names the people of God was once divided; and the adverse nations, that is, the princes of this world and the rulers of darkness, not only divided the people of God among themselves, but put boys into the brothel, to compel them to change their male nature, of whom the Apostle speaks: For this reason God gave them up to passions of disgrace; for their women exchanged the natural use for that use which is against nature. And likewise the males also, having left the natural use of the woman, were inflamed in their desire toward one another, males working shamefulness on males, and receiving in themselves the recompense of their error which was due. And not content to have done this, they sold a girl for wine, that they might drink, and put gluttony before lust.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.4-6":["They refer this against Tyre and Sidon so as to maintain that the vengeance for the blood of Christ and the overthrow of Jerusalem, which happened by the judgment of God, happened of itself. But let us, following the tropology we have begun, interpret Tyre and Sidon and the Philistines as those who hem in and afflict and persecute the people of God — for this is what Tyre sounds in our language —, and hunt him to death, which the name of Sidon indicates, and, drinking blood, fall down, or wallow in the mire, which is what the Philistines and Galilee signify. Swiftly and quickly the Lord will repay them what they deserve, because they have persecuted him. And his silver and gold, namely the words of the Scriptures, and the νοήματα, that is, the meanings, and everything that was beautiful in the Church, they have made the slaves of their errors. The heretic, whomever he has deceived and made to worship his own images, sells the sons of Judah and the sons of Jerusalem to the Greeks, or Gentiles, and makes them from Christians into heathens, to banish them from their borders, in which they had been begotten in Christ, so that they dwell no longer in Judea and in the confession of the truth, but in the error of the Gentiles. All these things we can also refer to the day of judgment, the vengeance not differing, although in time they seem to differ from the things above.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.4":["But what are you to me, Tyre and Sidon, and all the border of the Philistines? Will you render vengeance to me? And if you avenge yourselves against me, quickly, swiftly I will return your recompense upon your head. For you have taken my silver and my gold, and my desirable things, and the most beautiful you have carried into your shrines; and the sons of Judah and the sons of Jerusalem you have sold to the sons of the Greeks, that you might remove them far from their borders. LXX: What have I to do with you, Tyre and Sidon and all Galilee of the foreigners? Are you rendering me retribution, or do you keep anger in your heart against me? — for this is what μνησικακείτε means in Greek —. Swiftly and quickly I will return your retribution upon your heads, because you have taken my silver and my gold, and my choice and best things you have carried into your temples, and the sons of Judah and the sons of Jerusalem you have sold to the sons of the Greeks, that you might cast them out of their borders. And these things too the Jews think are said against Tyre and Sidon and the borders of the Philistines, or Galilee of the foreigners, because at the time of the Jewish captivity, when they were conquered by the Romans, these persecuted the people of God; nay rather, in the people of God, God himself, who was set over the people, according to what is written: He who receives you receives me. Therefore, conversely, he who persecutes the people of God persecutes God himself, whose people it is.\n— Commentary on Joel","For Galilee, in the Hebrew is written Galiloth, which Aquila translated Θῖνας, Symmachus borders; and Θῖνας, that is, mounds of sand, let us refer to the shores of Palestine, not to a Galilee of the Philistines, which does not exist at all.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.5":["I will repay you, he says, what you have done to my people, because you have taken my silver and gold, that is, the vessels of the temple, and whatever was most precious and most splendid in it, the golden lampstand and the golden table of the showbread, and the two golden Cherubim, and the mercy seat, and the golden bowls and censers, and have consecrated them to your idols. But history relates that it was rather the Chaldeans who did these things, who placed the vessels of the temple of the Lord in the temple of Bel, from which Belshazzar afterward drinks in the bowls, and at once his kingdom is transferred to the Medes and Persians. But since these things are said to be going to happen after the great and terrible day of the Lord, which the apostles interpret of the resurrection of the Lord, and the Hebrews defer to the future time of judgment, it is rather to be understood of the Romans; for Vespasian and Titus, having built the temple of Peace at Rome, consecrated the vessels of the temple and all its offerings in that shrine, as Greek and Roman history relates.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.6":["At that time the sons of Judah and Jerusalem — by no means Israel and the ten tribes, which to this day dwell in the cities and mountains of the Medes —, were sold to the sons of the Greeks, to be banished from their borders, and the whole world was filled with the Jewish captivity.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.7-8":["The Jews, then, promise themselves, or rather dream, that in the last time they will be gathered by the Lord and brought back to Jerusalem. And not content with this happiness, they assert that God himself will deliver the sons and daughters of the Romans into their hands, so that the Jews may sell them, not to the Persians and Ethiopians and the other nations that are near, but to the Sabeans, a most distant nation, because the Lord has spoken and avenges the injury of his people. So say they and our Judaizers, who promise themselves a thousand-year kingdom within the borders of Judea, and a golden Jerusalem, and the blood of victims, and sons and grandsons and incredible delights, and gates adorned with a variety of gems. But let us say that the Lord both raised up after his coming, and raises up daily, and will raise up those whom manifold error had led out of their borders. And he says beautifully, I will raise up, as of those lying down and fallen, so that those who lay in heresy may stand in the Church, repaying the heretics what they had done: so that he may deliver their sons and daughters, whom they had instructed both in mystical and in carnal things, into the hands of the sons of Judah, into the hands of those who have stood forth as princes of the Churches, and are equipped with the armor of the Apostle, and have the shield and spear of the Old and New Instrument; so that, when they have taken them, they may sell their sons and daughters to the Sabeans, and make them captives, that they may be far from their own borders; and, converted to better things, may begin to be subject to the dogmas of the Church.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.8":["Behold, I will raise them up from the place in which you sold them, and I will turn your recompense upon your head, and I will sell your sons and your daughters into the hands of the sons of Judah; and they will sell them to the Sabeans, a nation far off, for the Lord has spoken. LXX: Behold, I will raise them up from the place in which you sold them, and I will return your recompense upon your heads, and I will sell your sons and your daughters into the hands of the sons of Judah; and they will sell them into captivity to a nation that is far away, because the Lord has spoken. The Hebrew word Sabaim, which Aquila and Symmachus translated just as it is written, the LXX rendered as captivity, which would more properly mean captives. Now Sabaim is said to be a nation beyond India, to which also belonged the queen of Sheba, who came to hear the wisdom of Solomon, of whom Isaiah too speaks: And Sabaim, men of stature, shall pass over to you. From there also incense is said to come, as Vergil says: ..., and a hundred altars glow with Sabaean incense. Though some suppose the Sabeans to be Arabs.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.9-11":["Proclaim this among the nations, sanctify war, rouse the strong. Let them approach, let all the men of war go up. Beat your ploughshares into swords, and your mattocks into spears. Let the weak say, I am strong. Burst out and come, all you nations round about, and gather yourselves together; there the Lord will cause your strong ones to fall. LXX: Proclaim these things among the nations, sanctify war, rouse the fighters, bring them up and go up, all you men of war. Beat your ploughshares into swords, and your sickles into spears. Let the strong say, I prevail. Gather yourselves together and come in, all nations round about; and gather yourselves there; let the gentle one be a warrior. This passage is understood in two ways. For some think that these things are proclaimed to the holy nations, that they may be prepared for war and fight for the people of God, that they may turn all the instruments of husbandry into swords and spears, that the weak may say he is strong, and all may come from round about and be gathered into the Lord's contest; above all because, according to the LXX, there follows: Let the gentle and meek one be a warrior, according to what is written in Chronicles: And beyond the Jordan, of Reuben and Gad and the half tribe of Manasseh, with all the instruments of war, one hundred and twenty thousand; all these men of war ready for battle with a peaceful mind; these, they will have it, are the imitators of the true David, of whom we read: Remember, O Lord, David and all his gentleness. And he himself says in the Gospel: Learn from me, for I am humble and meek, or gentle. They also want those to be understood as men who, putting away the beginnings of little children, have come to the strength of mature age, and have turned all the zeal with which they formerly worked the fields of their souls to the need of battle. But to us, following the opinion of the Hebrews, it seems to be the opposite. For the nations hostile to Israel are made ready for battle, and change their ploughshares and mattocks, or sickles, into swords and spears, and the weak says he is strong, and they burst out and come from round about, and gather together against the army of the Lord, for this purpose: that the Lord may cause their strong ones to fall, and that they may understand that they have been overcome because God opposed them. These things wretched Israel promises itself according to the letter. We, following the tropology we have begun, can take them both of the nations of demons, and of those who daily fight against the Church, and in the end of those who under the Antichrist are going to fight against the saints of the Lord, who are gathered together for this purpose, that they may perish.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.12-13":["Let them arise and go up into the valley of Jehoshaphat, for there I will sit to judge all the nations round about; put in the sickles, for the harvest is ripe. Come and go down, for the winepress is full; the vats overflow, for their wickedness is multiplied. LXX: Let all the nations arise and go up into the valley of Jehoshaphat, for there I will sit to judge all the nations round about; send forth the sickles, for the vintage is at hand. Come in and tread, for the winepress is full and the vats overflow, for their evils are filled up. These things too they interpret in two ways, following the sense given above. For some understand in a good sense that the nations go up into the valley of Jehoshaphat, which is interpreted the judgment of the Lord, and that God sits there to judge all the nations that come from round about: namely, that the saints too are gathered there against the adversaries of God, and put in their sickles to reap the ripe harvest of the enemy, and come and go down, and tread the winepresses, because the vintage is at hand, and the new wine overflows to such a degree that the vats cannot hold it. And so that we might know what the vintage and the full winepress are, he added: Their wickedness is multiplied, without doubt meaning those who have gathered together against the Lord. Others, however, assert that the nations arise and come together into the valley of Jehoshaphat, and that the Lord sits to judge all the nations, for this reason: that they may prepare themselves and take up all the arms of fighting men, and be mown down by the sickles of the Lord in the valley of Jehoshaphat. For the time of judgment, they say, has come against them, and their evils have grown so far as to overcome the patience of God. For the Amorites too were driven out at the time when their sins were fulfilled. The Jews refer this passage to Gog and Magog, most savage nations, of whom we spoke above, thinking that in the last time, when Jerusalem has been restored, they will come against the people of God under the thousand-year rule, and will fall in the valley of Jehoshaphat, which lies on the eastern side of the temple; for the time of their slaughter will have come, and the vintage of the shedding of blood be at hand.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.14":["Peoples, peoples in the valley of cutting down, for the day of the Lord is near in the valley of cutting down. The sun and the moon have been darkened, and the stars have withdrawn their brightness. LXX: Sounds have been heard in the valley of judgment, for the day of the Lord is near in the valley of judgment. The sun and the moon will be darkened, and the stars will make their brightness set. In the place where we have put peoples, peoples, and the Seventy translated sounds have been heard, in the Hebrew is written amonim, amonim, which certainly signifies the multitude and the crowds of those who are to be judged, not noise and sound. Again, where we said in the valley of cutting down, following the translation of Aquila and Symmachus and the Fifth edition, the Seventy and Theodotion translated τῆς δίκης καί τῆς κρίσεως, that is, of the case and of judgment; in place of which in the Hebrew is written harus, which the Hebrews think means not only cutting down but also gold: namely, that in the valley of judgment, which they suppose to be Gehenna, when the dross of sins has been burned out, pure gold may remain. And the peoples of the nations of which we spoke above are gathered in the valley of judgment, or of cutting down, for this reason: that they may be slain and fall, and be judged by the Lord.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.15":["The grief of that day and the torments of those who perish not even the sun and moon and the other stars will be able to look upon, but they will withdraw their brightness, and will not dare to behold the severity of him who judges and renders each one's work upon his own head. Not that they are more merciful than the judgments of God, but that every creature, at the torments of others, trembles for its own judgment.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.16":["And the Lord will roar from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will utter his voice, and the heavens and the earth will be moved; and the Lord is the hope of his people and the strength of the sons of Israel; and you will know that I am the Lord your God, dwelling in Zion, my holy mountain; and Jerusalem will be holy, and strangers will not pass through her any more. LXX: But the Lord will cry out from Zion, and from Jerusalem he will utter his voice, and heaven and earth will be moved; and the Lord will spare his people, and will strengthen the sons of Israel; and you will know that I am the Lord your God, dwelling in Zion, my holy mountain; and Jerusalem will be holy, and the foreigner will not pass through her any more. When the brightness of the sun and moon and all the stars has been changed into darkness, the Lord will roar from Zion like a lion, or cry out; and his voice will be so loud and terrible that the hinges of the heavens and the foundations of the earth will be shaken. And though he will be so severe toward those who are to be punished, he will be merciful toward his people, and will give strength to those who are called the sons of Israel, that is, a mind seeing God; or εὐθύτατος Θεοῦ, which we can call the most upright of God; those who have not walked in crooked paths, but, going in the way of Christ, have done all things rightly.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.17":["Then both those who will be punished and those who will be taken up into glory will know that the Lord dwells in his watchtower Zion, and in Christ, his holy mountain, or in him who has prepared himself as a worthy dwelling place of God. Then Jerusalem, the vision of peace, will be holy, from which Solomon too received his name, and strangers will not pass through her any more. Understand by strangers either the demons, who are alien to God, or all the worst thoughts and sins, of which the prophet speaks: From strangers spare your servant; which would by no means find a way into us any more, if we have the peace of God and our heart has not lain open to the adversaries. These things the Jews and, as we have said, our Judaizers refer to the fable of the thousand years, when they think that Christ will dwell in Zion, and that the peoples of the saints will be gathered into a Jerusalem of gold and gems, so that those who in this age have been oppressed by all the nations may in this same age rule over all the nations.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.18":["And it will be in that day: the mountains will drip sweetness, and the hills will flow with milk, and through all the streams of Judah waters will go. LXX: And it will be in that day: the mountains will drip sweetness, and the hills will flow with milk, and all the outlets of Judah will flow with waters. When the Lord dwells in Zion and on his holy mountain, when no one will attempt to pass through holy Jerusalem, whoever in her is a mountain, and has reached the heights of the virtues, will sweat sweetness and honey; and from him will drip spiritual graces, of which the prophet speaks: How sweet are your words to my throat, above honey to my mouth. But he who is lower than the mountains, and has not yet climbed to the summit of perfection, will be called a hill, and from him will flow streams of milk, by which the untaught infancy in Christ is nourished, and the rivers of water which the Lord testified would flow from his belly. For all the streams, or outlets, of Judah will be filled with waters, and nothing in them will be dry, since all things overflow with spiritual grace.\n\nAnd a fountain will go forth from the house of the Lord and will water the torrent of thorns. For the torrent of thorns the Seventy translated the torrent of cords, that is, σχοίνων, which means either cords or, among the Egyptians, the measure of a fixed stretch of road, as the psalmist says: My path and my cord you have searched out. For on the river Nile, or in its channels, they are accustomed to haul ships with ropes, having fixed stretches, which they call cords, so that fresh necks of haulers may take over the labor of the exhausted. Nor is it any wonder if each nation calls the fixed stretches of roads by its own names, since the Latins speak of a thousand paces, the Gauls of leagues, the Persians of parasangs, and all Germany of rastae, and with each of these names the measure is different. I say this because the Seventy translated the Hebrew word settim as cords. Moreover, no one doubts that everything we read of in the tabernacle of God was made of settim wood, which the LXX rendered incorruptible wood. Now it is a kind of tree in the desert, like the white thorn in color and leaves, not in size. For otherwise the trees are so large that very broad planks are cut from them, and the wood is very strong, and of incredible lightness and beauty, so that all the wealthiest and most painstaking even make from it the vessels of the winepresses, which they call ἄρσενας (males) and θηλύας (females); and this wood is not found in cultivated places and on Roman soil, apart from the desert of Arabia. For the torrent of cords, or of thorns, Symmachus rendered the valley of thorns. Now there is a place near Livias, beyond the Dead Sea, six miles distant from Livias, where Israel once committed fornication with the Midianites. This place the prophet Micah mentions in the person of God, saying: My people, remember, I pray, what Balak king of Moab devised, and what Balaam son of Beor answered, from Settim to Gilgal. For which there too the Seventy translated ἀπὸ τῶν σχοίνων, that is, from the cords to Gilgal. Therefore a fountain will go forth from the house of the Lord, which is interpreted as the Church. Of this Ezekiel and Zechariah also write at the end of their books: that they may change our thorns, and vices and sins, which have borne no fruit of righteousness, into the Lord's new-broken fields, and water our dryness with most abundant waters, and that instead of thorns and briars we may bring forth manifold flowers of the virtues. And in that place where Israel once committed fornication and was initiated into Baal-peor, may lilies of chastity and roses of modesty and virginity abound.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.19":["Egypt will be a desolation, and Idumea a desert of perdition, because they have acted wickedly against the sons of Judah, and have shed innocent blood in their land. LXX: Egypt will be in perdition, and Idumea a plain of desolation, because of the iniquities of the sons of Judah, because they have shed righteous blood in their land. In this place too the Jews sleep a very heavy sleep, imagining with a most vain hope that in the last time, when they are to receive not Christ but the Antichrist, there will be vengeance for them upon the Egyptians, who are their neighbors, and upon the Romans, whom they interpret as the Idumeans: namely, that just as Pharaoh and all his army, who held the people of God captive for four hundred and thirty years, were drowned in the Red Sea, so also the Romans, who are to possess the Jews for the same span of years, may be destroyed by the vengeance of the Lord. Let them promise themselves this with an empty hope. For they cannot explain why, when there are so many nations in the world, two nations alone, the Egyptians and the Idumeans, are singled out for punishment; or why the Romans should be understood by the Idumeans, when with the same license of lying we could on the contrary assert that under the name of the Idumeans either the Persians, or the Elamites, or the Franks, the Alamanni, and other nations are signified. But let us say that at the time of the Lord's resurrection, or on the day of judgment — for we accept both —, both Egypt and Idumea are to be destroyed, insofar as they are Egypt and Idumea. Egypt is called mesraim, which is interpreted ἐκθλίβουσα, that is, persecuting and afflicting the saints of God; Idumea, earthly, or bloody. Whoever, therefore, has persecuted the people of God, and has given himself to earthly works, and by a multitude of offenses has daily shed innocent blood, the blood, namely, of those whom he deceives, will be brought to perdition, just as we know that the souls of the martyrs too cry out under the altar: How long, O Lord, holy and true, do you not judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.20":["And Judea will be inhabited for ever, and Jerusalem to generation and generation; and I will cleanse their blood, which I had not cleansed; and the Lord will abide in Zion. LXX: But Judea will be inhabited for ever, and Jerusalem to generations of generations; and I will seek out their blood, and will not suffer it to be unavenged. And the Lord will dwell in Zion. Surely it is not this Judea that will be inhabited for ever, which we see to be deserted, nor this Jerusalem, whose ruins we behold, but that Judea whose daughters have exulted and rejoiced in all the judgments of the Lord, and of which we read at the end of the fiftieth (51st) psalm: Deal kindly, O Lord, in your good will with Zion, that the walls of Jerusalem may be built. In this province of confession and glory, and in this city in which the peace of the Lord is seen, there will be an eternal habitation, not for one, or for three or more generations, but to generation and generation, that is, in the two generations of those who have believed from the Jews and from the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"JOL.3.21":["And the Lord will cleanse the blood of all sins, which he had not cleansed before, so as to cleanse in the Gospel those whom, sinning under the law, he had left unclean. For he has shut up all things under sin for this purpose, that he may have mercy on all; or he will avenge the blood of his servants, which they have shed in martyrdom for the confession of his name. And the Lord will abide in Zion, of which it is written: Its foundations are on the holy mountains; the Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tents of Jacob.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"OBA.1.1":["When I was a little child, I spoke as a little child, I understood as a little child, I thought as a little child; after I became a man, I put away the things that were a little child's. If the Apostle makes progress and, forgetting daily the things that are behind, stretches himself forward to those that are before, and, according to the precept of the Lord and Savior, holding the plough-handle, does not look back, how much more ought I, who have not yet come to the age of the perfect man and to the measure of Christ, to deserve pardon because in my youth, roused by ardor and zeal for the Scriptures, I interpreted the prophet Obadiah allegorically, though I did not know his history. My mind was burning with mystical knowledge, and because I had read that all things are possible to those who believe, I did not know that the gifts are diverse; I knew the literature of the world, and on that account I thought I could read the sealed book. Foolish I was: the twenty-four elders, holding bowls and harps in their hands, and the four living creatures full of eyes rise up from their throne, confess their want of skill, sing glory to the Lamb; and to the rod from the root of Jesse, and I thought that I was able, because I believed, I in whose hand the word of God did not come, nor could I say: From your commandments I have understood, nor did I remember that beatitude of his from the Gospel: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Not yet had the coal taken from the altar cleansed my lips. Not yet had the error of my old ignorance been circumcised by the fire of the Holy Spirit, and boldly I was saying to the Lord: Behold, here am I, send me. I hoped that what I had written lay hidden in my little book-chests, and I had vowed to the flames the first rashness of my little talent, when suddenly a copy was brought from Italy by a certain young man of as many years as I myself had once been when I wrote it, who praised my little work. I confess I was amazed that, however badly someone has written, he finds a reader like himself. He was extolling it, and I was blushing; he was raising its so-called mystical senses to the sky, I, with lowered head, was kept from confessing my shame. What then? Do we condemn the things in which we played as boys? By no means. For we know that into the tabernacle of God both gold and goats' hair were offered alike. We read in the Gospel that the two mites of the poor widow were approved more than the substance of the rich. Both then we gave what we had, and now, if indeed we have made some progress, we render to the Lord what is his. For by the grace of him I am what I am. Nor do I deny that through these thirty years I have sweated in his work and labor. The Father is merciful; he quickly receives the son who returns, nor does he wait until someone opens the door: he himself goes out to meet him, makes ready the ring and the robe, and although the brother is jealous and calls him a whoremonger and a spendthrift, over his salvation the symphony of the angels' gladness and of all the powers resounds in the heavens. This is that time, my Pammachius, sweeter than this light, when, having left the school of the rhetoricians, we were borne along by different pursuits, when Heliodorus, dearest of men, and I together wished to dwell in the desert of Chalcis in Syria; what I thought lay hidden has been made public. I shall walk again in my old footsteps, correcting, if it can be done, the crooked strokes of the letters. I was an infant, I did not yet know how to write, my hand faltered, my fingers trembled. Now, even if I have made no other progress, at least I have that saying of Socrates: I know that I do not know. Your Tullius too says that certain unfinished and rough things slipped from him when he was a very young man. If he could say this, both of the books to Herennius and of the books on Rhetoric, which I for my part think most perfect, in comparison with the skill of his old age, how much more freely shall I profess both that the one was the work of a boyish talent and that this is the work of a ripe old age. In the books against Marcion, too, Septimius Tertullian underwent this same thing, and Origen in the Song of Songs, and Quintilian in the twelve books of the Training of the Orator. From these it is clearly shown that each age is perfect in its own kind and ought to be judged by the number of its years. But it is now time to set forth the opening of Obadiah and, with the help of your prayers, for whom this volume is written, to cross the rugged sea and the winding whirlpools of the world.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","The vision of Obadiah. The Hebrews say that this is the man who, under Ahab king of Samaria and the most impious Jezebel, fed a hundred prophets in caves, who had not bowed the knee to Baal and were of the seven thousand whom Elijah is shown not to have known; and his tomb, together with the mausoleum of the prophet Elisha and of John the Baptist, is held in veneration to this day in Sebaste, which was once called Samaria. Herod, king of Judea, son of Antipater, called it in the Greek tongue Augusta, in honor of Augustus Caesar. Therefore, because he had nourished a hundred prophets, he received the prophetic grace, and from a leader of the army he becomes a leader of the Church. Then he had fed a little flock in Samaria; now he feeds the Church of Christ in the whole world; and φερωνύμως (true to his name), just as Stephen in the Acts of the Apostles was crowned with martyrdom, so this man too was called the servant of the Lord. Furthermore, that, seeing against Idumea, he does not see a burden, that is λῆμμα, and a weight of Idumea, according to the rule and distinction that we wrote of in Nahum, has this as its cause: either that Edom, that is Esau, the son of Isaac and the brother of Jacob, is not reckoned among the foreign nations, whence too his land is not given to Israel for a possession, nor is Israel let loose to make war against him, and arms against a brother are forbidden; or certainly it is a vision not of Idumea, which, had it been so written, could have raised a question, but of Obadiah, that is, of the servant of the Lord, which he sees for the nations to whom the Lord sent an envoy, and to whom it is said: Arise, and let us rise up against her in battle. For the destruction of Idumea is the vision of Nahum. But if it is asked why The vision of Obadiah is set in the title, and nothing that was seen is shown afterwards, as in that passage of Isaiah: I saw the Lord of hosts sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and in Ezekiel: The heavens were opened, and I saw visions of God, and at once it is added: And I saw, and behold, a carrying-off wind was coming from the north, and a great cloud, and brightness round about it; whereas after the vision of Obadiah there follows immediately: Thus says the Lord to Edom, and again: Behold, I have given you small among the nations; we shall take an example from Deuteronomy, in which not things but words are seen: Take heed, he says, to yourself, and keep your soul diligently, lest perhaps you forget the words which your eyes have seen. And John in his epistle: What we have seen, he says, with our eyes, what we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the Word of life. And Moses says that he saw the voice of the Lord which spoke to him. If, then, after the vision there are brought in the things that were said, and things that are usually perceived by the ears are seen by the eyes of the mind, rightly does the seer, since prophets were formerly called seers, mark the vision at the head of the title.\n\nThus says the Lord God to Edom: A small prophet by the reckoning of verses, not of meanings. For otherwise, as among the three books of Solomon the Song of Songs is the more difficult the shorter it is; and what meaning the Epistle to Philemon held, we have set out. The word of the Gospel, too, though shortened, is narrower than the straggling works of the Law. So this prophet too, as a servant of the Lord, like Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and Moses the servant of God, and the Apostle the servant of Christ, sees those things, hears those things, that are worthy of prophetic service. Many think, following the meaning of the name, that this is that servant of the Lord to whom it is said in Isaiah: It is a great thing for you to be called my servant, who emptied himself, taking the form of a servant. But if we understand it so, we shall have to seek another envoy who was sent to the nations. And while we follow tropology, we lose a most manifest prophecy. We ask, therefore, who this Edom, or Idumea, is, to whom the Lord now speaks through Obadiah. In the book of Genesis we read most plainly that Esau the son of Isaac was called Edom because he sold his birthright for the red food of lentils. For Edom means πυῤῥὸς, that is, red. In the same volume it is written that this same man was called Seir, that is, hairy, because he was shaggy and did not have the smoothness of Jacob. One and the same man, therefore, is called by three names, Esau, Edom, Seir; and he possessed the region which is now called Gebalene and lies in the territory ἐλευθεροπόλεως (of Eleutheropolis), where the Horites had dwelt before, who are translated free men, whence the city itself also afterwards obtained its name. What, then, is called Edom in Hebrew and ἰδουμαία (Idumea) in Greek is now a little village of Palestine, the name having been so given by its founder, whom Latin and Greek history also mention. This is he to whom it is said through Amos: For three crimes of Edom, and for four, I will not turn him back, because he pursued his brother with the sword, and violated his mercy, and held his fury still further, and kept his indignation to the end. In Isaiah too, according to the Hebrew, we read: The burden of Dumah: he calls to me out of Seir: Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night? And Jeremiah agrees with the same prophecy with a like voice, saying: Is there no more wisdom in Teman? and the rest, up to the end of that same prophecy, where it is said: And the heart of the mighty men of Idumea shall be in that day as the heart of a woman in labor. Except that the order is changed, and except for other things which seem to differ, a great part of Obadiah is contained in the volume of Jeremiah. These are the mountains of Seir against which the face of Ezekiel is strengthened, and it is said: I will make mount Seir desolate and deserted; and a little after: You shall be scattered, mount Seir, and all Idumea. It would be long if I chose to sift all the Scriptures and bring into the open what they say about the mountains of Seir, and above all Malachi, where at the very beginning it is written: Was not Esau Jacob's brother, says the Lord, and I loved Jacob, but Esau I hated? And I made his mountains a waste, and his inheritance a place for the dragons of the desert. But if Idumea should say: We are destroyed, but we will return and build the things that are desolate; thus says the Lord of hosts: They shall build, and I will destroy; and they shall be called the borders of impiety, and the people with whom the Lord is angry for ever. Since, then, we have learned that the land of the Idumeans is hostile to the land of promise, and we read that Esau is the enemy of Jacob, and that they are the people with whom the Lord is angry for ever, we ought to know that, by the laws of tropology, the word was spoken either against the Jews — who are the rivals of the Christians and persecute their brother Jacob, the supplanting people that snatched the birthright from them —, or certainly against all heresies and doctrines contrary to the truth, which seem indeed to be near to us but are rather hostile, and strive to drive out from the paternal inheritance the simple man, the dweller in the house of Jacob. Furthermore, because Idumea means both earthly and, on account of the color red, can also be understood as bloody, for this reason the Savior, carrying back to the Father the victory over the world, when the angels were crying out: Open your gates, O princes, and the king of glory shall enter in. And in Isaiah, astonished, they were asking: Who is this who comes up from Edom, with garments tawny from Bozrah, so beautiful in a white robe? And one spoke to him face to face: Why is your clothing red, and your garments like those of men who tread in the winepress? He himself speaks in triumph, displaying the palms of his cross: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there is no man with me. There are those who refer Idumea to the flesh, and think that the soul is called out to fight against it, so that, mortifying our members which are upon the earth, fornication, uncleanness, passion, we may attain eternal victory in Christ. The Jews dream in vain that this prophecy is made against the city of Rome and the Roman empire; and that in what is written in Isaiah, The burden of Dumah, with the stroke of a letter slightly changed, res can be read in place of deleth, and it sounds Rome, since the letter vau in their language is taken both for u and for o.\n\nWe have heard a hearing from the Lord, and he has sent an envoy to the nations: Arise, and let us rise up against him in battle. LXX: I have heard a hearing from the Lord, and he has sent a stronghold among the nations: Arise, and let us rise up against him in battle. We have already said above that Edom is the same as Idumea, because in the Hebrew it is the man who founded it, in the Greek the city that was founded by him, that is named. Either Obadiah, then, heard, or all the prophets alike heard — for all of them write against Edom — that an envoy was sent to the nations, the mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus, of whom the Father also speaks through Haggai: I will move all the nations, and he who is desired by all the nations shall come. He it is who in Isaiah is written as the Angel of great counsel, and Father of the age to come. But as for the fact that, for envoy, that is sir, the Seventy translated περιοχὴν, that is, stronghold, we can say that he himself is our envoy and he himself our stronghold, saying the things which the prophetic word has woven together: Arise, and let us rise up against him, or her, in battle. The envoy who was sent to the nations says this: Arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ shall give you light. And lest perhaps the fight should seem to you heavy and unaccustomed, you have me, he says, first in the battle line. I shall be the ἀρχιστρατηγος (chief commander) of the battles, I who also appeared to Joshua the son of Nun holding a sword, and, while Moses fought, overcame Amalek under the standard of my cross. Jeremiah, in his vision against Idumea, does not differ much from these words: I have heard a hearing, he says, from the Lord, and an envoy has been sent to the nations: gather together, and come against her, and let us rise up to battle.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.2-4":["There follows in the prophet Obadiah: Behold, I have given you small among the nations; you are exceedingly contemptible. The pride of your heart has lifted you up, dwelling in the clefts of the rock, exalting your throne, who say in your heart: Who will drag me down to the earth? If you are exalted like the eagle, and if you set your nest among the stars, from there I will drag you down, says the Lord. LXX: Behold, I have given you least among the nations; you are exceedingly without honor. The pride of your heart has raised you up, dwelling in the holes of the rocks, exalting your dwelling-place and saying in your heart: Who will bring me down to the earth? If you are exalted like the eagle, and if you set your nest in the midst of the stars, from there I will drag you down, says the Lord. Jeremiah, of whom we made mention above, agrees in almost the same words, saying: Behold, I have given you small among the nations: contemptible among men; your arrogance has deceived you, and the pride of your heart, you who dwell in the caverns of the rock and strive to lay hold of the height of the hill; when you have exalted your nest like the eagle, from there I will drag you down, says the Lord. In prophetic interpretation we ought to follow our custom, so as first to lay the foundations of history, then, if we can, to raise up lofty towers and the tops of roofs. O Edom, he says, though you are the least among all the nations round about, and small in number in comparison with the other peoples, you are lifted up by pride beyond your strength. And though you dwell in grottoes, nay rather in the caverns of the rocks, lowly and poor, and not possessing the lofty roofs of buildings, you are borne up on high like an eagle, and you swell so much in thought that you believe you dwell among the stars; even if, beyond nature, you could penetrate the heights of heaven, from there I would drag you down and bring you down to the earth, says the Lord God. But that which is added in Jeremiah, And you strive to lay hold of the height of the hill, has opened the riddle, signifying by the hill Mount Zion; and by this he means to be understood either the city of Jerusalem itself or the temple which was founded in it. That the eagle flies higher than all birds, even those who discuss the nature of birds have handed down to memory; and it is said to be of such keen sight that, when it is borne over the seas on motionless wing and is not visible to human eyes, from so great a height it sees little fish swimming, and when they have come near the shore, it descends like a missile from an engine and drags the prey it has seized to the shore with its wings.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","I said above that this very thing can also be understood against the flesh: that its powers are left without defense at the coming of Christ, and that it is contemptible and subject to the rule of the soul, and is lifted up in vain, since it dwells in the caverns of the rock, whether in the senses or in the thoughts; and it wishes to lord it over the soul, exalting its throne, and not believing that its works can be overcome. To it it is said that, however much it lifts itself up and imitates the heights of the eagle and has deceived many of the saints, yet it has been overcome and cast down by the Lord. What we have understood of the heretics and of the flesh can also be understood against the Jews.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.2":["If we have learned the history, let us follow the spiritual understanding. Although you seem to yourself to be great, O heretic, and despise the fewness of the Church, yet you are small among the nations, and contemptible, and not only contemptible, but, with ἐπιτάσει (intensification), exceedingly contemptible.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.3":["The pride of your heart has lifted you up. For which of the heretics is not lifted up into pride, making little of the simplicity of the Church and reckoning faith to be want of skill? Dwelling in the clefts of the rock, exalting his throne. Although the rock is frequently put either for the person of the Lord or for solidity, whence the prophet too says: He has set my feet upon the rock, and to Peter it is said: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church, yet it is also frequently taken in the contrary sense: I will take away, he says, the heart of stone, and I will give you a heart of flesh; and: God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham; and above all here, where he did not say, dwelling upon the rock, on which the prudent builder builds his house, but in the clefts of the rock, so as to signify the cleavings of the heresies from the rock of Christ and from the Church. Furthermore, what follows, Exalting your throne, and, Who says in his heart: Who will drag me down to the earth?, shows the swelling of the heretical mind, of men who trust in their own mysteries and secrets and, as far as lies in them — but we ought to take this hyperbolically —, promise themselves the kingdoms of heaven. Whence the Apostle writes to men of this kind: Already without us you reign; and would that you did reign, that we also might reign with you. David too says something of the kind: They have set their mouth against heaven, and their tongue has passed over the earth.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.4":["If, then, they are exalted like the eagle — for they too promise themselves the likeness of eagles, which are wont to gather to the Lord's carcass — and if they set their nest in the midst of the stars, from there I will drag them down, says the Lord. For just as, while the householder sleeps, the enemy sows tares over the good seed, so that great eagle, with great wings, long in the stretch of its limbs, full of feathers and of variety, which has the way of entering into Lebanon, and takes from the top of the cedar and plants it upon many waters, so that the slips may sprout into a vine, is wont to set its nest among the stars of the Church, of whom it is also said in another place: But the just shall shine like the stars.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.5-6":["If thieves had come in to you, if robbers by night, how would you have kept silent? Would they not have stolen what was enough for them? If grape-gatherers had come in to you, would they not at least have left you a cluster? How have they searched out Esau, tracked down his hidden things? LXX: If thieves had come in to you, or robbers by night, where would you have been cast away? Would they not have stolen what was enough for them? And if grape-gatherers had come in to you, would they not have left a cluster? How has Esau been searched through, and his hidden things been found out? Jeremiah likewise, though with the order changed: If grape-gatherers had come upon you, would they not have left a cluster? If thieves in the night, they would surely have seized what was enough for them. But I have uncovered Esau, I have revealed his hidden things, and he shall not be able to hide. What he says is this: If thieves and robbers, who are accustomed by night to dig through houses and to seize what is in the houses, had come in to you, and, hidden by the darkness, had gone round the corners of your house, they would surely have carried off what they thought could be enough for them, and would have left something in your dwelling, either through being sated or through not knowing of it. If grape-gatherers had come into your vineyard, and wished either to lay it waste as enemies or, hired by you, to harvest it, however diligent their vintage had been, they would still have left among the branches clusters hidden by the vines and leaves. But the enemies who came to you at the Lord's command — and he means the Babylonians and the army of Nebuchadnezzar — have tracked down everything, all your secrets, and have ranged through the caverns and the holes of the grottoes in which you dwell. And indeed, that we may say something also about the nature of the place, the whole southern region of the Idumeans, from Eleutheropolis as far as Petra and Aila — for this is the possession of Esau — has little dwellings in caves; and because of the excessive heat of the sun, since it is a southern province, it uses underground huts. I have uncovered, he says, Esau, that is, what was pressed down under the earth I have brought into the open, and all the things that you were shutting up have been laid open, and, since I searched out your substance together with the enemies, no secret places could hide it.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.5":["Alternatively: the thieves and robbers who go out by night, because they are sons of the night and of darkness, I take to be the heretics, who preach doctrines contrary to the truth, who steal what is enough for them, and every day hasten to seize from the flocks of the Church. These, entering the vineyard of our Lord, which he brought over out of the Egypt of this world, and of whose produce he promises that he will drink wine in the kingdom of the Father, desire so to plunder everything that they scarcely leave a cluster in it.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.6":["But the Lord does the contrary; for all their secrets and hidden mysteries, and those of the patriarchs of Esau — for by these I understand those who first invented heresies —, he brings into the open through his saints and through churchmen and teachers; and his first victory is that the hidden things that were covered lie open. Whence it is also said with wonder: How has Esau been searched through, have his hidden things been found out? See how Marcion and Valentinus and all the heretics, having their conscience seared, applaud themselves in doctrines of demons, and, as if initiating simple souls into certain divine mysteries, deceive them with artful speech. But when discourse has brought into the open the thirty aeons, and teams of four, and ogdoads and dodecads, and the twofold god, and the monstrous Abraxas, then the prudence of Esau will be shown to be folly, and his secrets will be tracked down.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.7":["They have sent you out as far as the borders, all the men of your covenant; they have mocked you; the men of your peace have prevailed against you, who eat with you; they will lay snares beneath you; there is no prudence in him. LXX: They have dismissed you as far as the borders; all the men of your testament have withstood you; your men of peace have prevailed against you; they have laid snares beneath you; there is no wisdom in him. Some divide what we expounded above, How has Esau been searched through, and his hidden things been caught?, so as to run on to 'as far as the borders', so that the sense is: your hidden things and secrets have been revealed as far as the borders. To us, however, it seems better that it be joined with what follows. When, then, Nebuchadnezzar came, of whom it is said in Jeremiah against Idumea: Behold, like a lion he shall come up from the pride of the Jordan to the strong beauty, for I will suddenly make him run to it, and then: Behold, like an eagle he shall come up and fly, and spread out his wings over Bozrah, and the heart of the mighty men of Idumea shall be in that day as the heart of a woman in labor, all who had before been allies of Edom and had been in the garrison of that most proud city abandoned him; and, joined to the enemies, they spread snares against him and prevailed against him, and then it was made known that there was no wisdom in Edom, since he hoped in those who were shown to be adversaries. Alternatively: when the hidden things of Esau, and his so-called magnificent mysteries, by which the peoples had formerly been ensnared, have been brought into the open, so that a churchman can say: For we are not ignorant of his wiles, they will forsake the borders of Edom and dismiss him, and, migrating as far as the borders of the Church, they will bring the most wicked doctrines into the open. Then they will mock and withstand their former master, saying that what they had learned is false; they will prevail against him, and, instructed in the faith of the Church, they will refute the false doctrine. Those who formerly ate among the heretics, not the bread of the Eucharist but the bread of mourning, and the hearth-cake that is not turned over, will propose questions from the Scriptures, will lay snares for the Idumean and earthly, and in all things fleshly, teacher — for we read in the Epistle to the Galatians that heresies are numbered among the works of the flesh —, and then it will be shown that there is no prudence in Edom.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.8-9":["Shall I not in that day, says the Lord, destroy the wise from Idumea, and prudence from Mount Esau? And your mighty ones shall fear from the South, so that the man may perish from Mount Esau. LXX: In that day, says the Lord, I will destroy the wise from Idumea, and understanding from Mount Esau. And your fighters from Theman shall tremble, so that man may be taken away from Mount Esau. When the enemy has possessed your borders, and all the men of your covenant have mocked you and prevailed against you, then wisdom will perish from Idumea, and its cunning will be shown to be folly. The Lord himself will also take away prudence from Mount Esau, that is, from the mountains of Seir, either because the city of Idumea is situated on a mountain, or because that whole region which slopes toward the south and borders on the desert is raised high with steep mountains. Hence it is also said: Your fighters from Theman shall tremble, which we have translated \"south\". Now, just as I had said above that Esau was called by three names, so also the tract of his kingdom which slopes toward the south is called by three terms: Darom, Themam, Nageb, all of which, according to Ezekiel, signify the south, the southwest and the noonday. But after the mighty ones of his kingdom, who dwelt in the south, have feared, then the man from Mount Esau will perish, who was accustomed both to fight for the city and to give counsel prudently.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.8":["Alternatively: after those who had formerly been deceived have been converted to the Church, then, with the Lord himself doing battle, the wise of Idumea will perish, who were wise in carnal and earthly things, and prudence will be taken away from Mount Esau, the prudence which was lifted up against the knowledge of God.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.9":["And those who formerly fought for Esau and Idumea with the art of dialectic, and were in the garrisons of Theman, which means consummation, will cease to fight for their former masters. Or else: those who previously promised themselves the light of knowledge, and thought that they were at noonday, will fear and be terrified, as a churchman gets the better of their sophisms, so much so that no one remains who could do battle, whether with the counsel of a king or with the strength of a fighter, for the pride of the heretics and their false doctrine.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.10-11":["Because of the slaughter and the iniquity against your brother Jacob, confusion shall cover you, and you shall perish for ever. On the day when you stood against him, when strangers were capturing his army, and foreigners were entering his gates, and were casting lots upon Jerusalem, you too were as one of them. LXX: Because of the slaughter and the impiety against your brother Jacob, confusion shall cover you, and you shall be taken away for ever. From the day on which you stood against him, on the day when aliens captured his strength, and strangers entered his gates, and cast lots upon Jerusalem, you also were as one of them. Therefore the man shall perish from Mount Esau, and wisdom shall perish from Idumea, and prudence from Mount Esau, because you slew your kinsman and acted wickedly against your brother Jacob, when, as the Chaldeans and Babylonians were laying Jerusalem waste, and besieging the city, and entering its gates, and casting lots in the division of the spoils, you were their ally and were counted among the number of the enemy.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.10":["Alternatively: All the things written above you shall suffer, O bloody, earthly, cruel Edom, because by a false and deadly doctrine, one that speaks impiety against God, you have slain your brother Jacob. We read in Solomon: There are those who speak and slay with the sword. And in another place: The venom of asps is under their lips, and: Their tongue is a sharp sword. Confusion therefore shall cover you, and you shall say: The confusion of my face has covered me, and you shall perish, not for a short time, but for ever. For, as far as lay in you, you struck your brother with an everlasting wound.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.11":["But this too shall be a cause of torment: for when strangers were laying waste the army of Jacob and were entering through its gates into Jerusalem, once peaceful, and were casting lots in order to divide its spoils among themselves, you were one of the enemy. We read it, we have seen it, we prove it daily: when persecution arises against the Church, the Jews and the heretics become far worse persecutors of the Christians than the heathen. We can call the foreigners entering the gates of Jerusalem λογισμοὺς, that is, the most wicked thoughts, and we can interpret the gates of Jerusalem, that is, of the soul that is at rest and sees God, as the five senses, through which the enemies enter and divide the spoils of Jerusalem. If we have looked upon a woman to lust after her, death has entered through our windows. If we have taken in through our ears a lie and a judgment of blood, the enemy has entered through another gate. Smell also, taste and touch: if one of them has been captured either by various scents, or by sweet foods, or by soft embraces, the adversaries have entered through other gates, and they divide the spoils of wretched Jerusalem. At that time, therefore, when anyone has fallen from the Church through the onslaught of persecution and through deadly pleasures, we may see the heretics exult, the Jew rejoice and be one of the persecutors, and them reckoned in the number of the heathen.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.12-13":["And you shall not look down on the day of your brother, on the day of his sojourning abroad. And you shall not rejoice over the children of Judah on the day of their destruction, and you shall not magnify your mouth on the day of distress, nor shall you enter the gate of my people on the day of their ruin. LXX: And you shall not look down on the day of your brother on the day of strangers. And you shall not rejoice over the children of Judah on the day of their destruction; and you shall not speak great things on the day of distress, nor shall you enter the gates of the peoples on the day of their labors. The same sense as above: when the man has perished from Mount Esau because of the slaughter and the impiety against his brother Jacob, and everlasting confusion has covered him, he shall by no means do what he did before against his kinsman; for he shall not look down on him and think him of little account when he sees his brother going into captivity; nor shall he rejoice over the children of Judah. For the two tribes, apart from the tribe of Levi, which reigned in Jerusalem and were called Judah, were captured by the Chaldeans. And you shall by no means speak great things, and, believing yourself to be as one of the victors, laugh at your brother's distress. Nor, on the day of the devastation and ruin of my people, shall you march boastfully through the gate of Jerusalem. And you shall therefore not do these things, because you too shall suffer the like.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.12":["Alternatively: when you see your brother taken captive by various persecutions, and led away in bonds from the faith of the Church, following not his own city but whatever is foreign, you shall by no means rejoice, because you too shall suffer the like. For you rejoiced when Jacob was being captured, and over the children of Judah, whom we understand as the disciples of Christ, you exulted on the day of their destruction. And it is signified that the soul is placed midway between vices and virtues, and can be turned to either side from one hour's moment to the next. By no means, he says, shall you magnify your mouth on the day of distress. This we ought to take according to a twofold sense: of bodily distress, in persecutions and in sins; of spiritual distress, when the soul, captured by enemies and vices, has been led into Babylon.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.13-14":["Nor shall you too look down on his misfortunes on the day of his devastation, and you shall not be sent out against his army on the day of his devastation.\n\nNor shall you stand at the exits to slay those who have fled; and you shall not shut in his remnant on the day of tribulation. LXX: And you also shall not look down on their gathering on the day of their destruction, nor shall you be added to their strength on the day of their destruction, nor shall you stand at their outlets to slay those who have been saved; nor shall you shut in their fugitives on the day of tribulation. When you yourself have been captured and destroyed by the ravaging Babylonian, among the other things that you have done, do not do these that follow. You shall not look down on, nor despise, your brother Jacob on the day of his devastation, nor shall you be added to the Babylonian army, or, you shall not be sent out against the army of Judah when, overcome by its adversaries, it has turned its back. And when they begin to flee by the ways known to them, and the byways, and the paths leading to the wilderness, you shall not stand at the fork in the road, nor wait for them as they come at the crossroads, to slay those who have been set free, and to shut up others whom you have seized, and either take them captive yourself or keep them for captivity by the enemy. This we have said according to the history: for we ought to pluck briefly at the plainer matters and press on to those that are obscure.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.13":["Nor shall you enter the gate of my people on the day of their ruin; for when either denial or pleasure has overwhelmed us, and an unhappy conscience has not kept its former strictness, then we easily slip into contrary doctrines that flatter our error, and do not cure the wound but stroke it. It is a solace in misery for one, however lost, to have something to hope for.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Which of the heretics does not look down on the churchmen? Which does not exult in their misfortunes, if ever, because of the sins of the people, they have been handed over to persecution, and many, either of weak faith or sown upon stony ground, have fallen into denial: you may see them exult, rejoice, think our ruin their own victory, so much so that they join themselves to the nations, and the persecution becomes more savage, whether from the Jews or from those who pretend to be our brothers and are reckoned under the same name.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.14":["And when someone has slipped away, either by flight or by repentance, they stand in the byways and put forward sophisms, and bring out testimonies as if from the Scriptures, in order to offer sewn cushions to the weary and exhausted, and to put them under every elbow of the hand. And so it comes about that those who perhaps either overcame persecution by courage or escaped it through fear are, deceived by perverse doctrines, held again in the prison of errors, and this tribulation becomes far worse than that which came from the nations; for you would more easily free one captured by the heathen than one entangled in the conjuring tricks of the heretics.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.15-16":["For the day of the Lord is near upon all the nations; as you have done, so shall it be done to you. He will turn your recompense back upon your own head. For as you drank upon my holy mountain, so all the nations shall drink continually; and they shall drink and swallow up, and they shall be as though they were not. LXX: Because the day of the Lord is near upon all the nations; as you have done, so shall he do to you: your recompense shall be repaid upon your own head. Because as you drank upon my holy mountain, all the nations shall drink wine; they shall drink and swallow up, and they shall be as though they were not. And you shall cease to do these things, O Idumea, for this reason: because the vengeance of the Lord shall come upon you. For if, according to Jeremiah, those for whom there was no judgment that they should drink the cup have indeed drunk it, are you to be left as though innocent? You shall not be innocent, but you shall surely drink, as one who drinks. Further, as to what he says: The day of the Lord is near upon all the nations, let us read the same Jeremiah and we shall see the cup of the Lord passed round to all the nations. Hence it is said in the same prophet: Babylon is a golden cup in the hand of the Lord, making all the nations drunk. For the Assyrians and Babylonians held all the nations as far as the Propontis and the Scythian sea, and the Ionian or the Aegean. Let us read Herodotus and the Greek and barbarian histories, and we shall see how what is said was fulfilled under the Babylonians and Assyrians: The day of the Lord is near upon all the nations. As for what follows: As you have done, so shall it be done to you. He will turn your recompense back upon your own head, this is the sense that we read in the psalm: Remember, O Lord, the children of Edom on the day of Jerusalem, who said: Empty it, empty it, even to the foundation in it. Hence the prophet also calls down a curse upon Babylon: Wretched daughter of Babylon, blessed is he who shall repay you your recompense, with which you repaid us. Blessed is he who shall take and dash your little ones against the rock. For just as you drank with the Babylonians upon my holy mountain, Zion, and rejoiced, so all the nations that the Babylonian king had with you shall turn against you and drink and rejoice. And not only shall they drink, but they shall so swallow you up that the Idumeans shall be as though they were not; or at any rate the nations themselves, when they have swallowed you up, shall be swallowed up by the Medes, and vengeance shall proceed in this circle: that you consume Israel, the Babylonian you, the Mede and the Persian the Babylonian. Let us follow the order of the interpretation. The day of the Lord is near, O heretic, upon all the nations; the time of judgment is at hand, in which all the nations are to be judged. As you have done against the churchmen, so shall your pain be turned back upon your own head, and your iniquity shall come down upon your own crown. For as you rejoiced at their slaughter and held a banquet, and upon my holy mountain, that is, the Church, drank not my cup but the devil's, of which it is said in Habakkuk too: Woe to him who gives his neighbor drink of a turbid overthrow, so all the nations, either the opposing powers assigned to punishments or whatever adverse forces there are, shall drink and swallow up your blood, and at the last, when torment comes upon all, they too shall be as though they were not. For he who perishes to Him who is, and who says to Moses: He who is has sent me to you, is said, according to the rule of the Scriptures, not to be. Hence we read in Esther as well: Do not hand over, O Lord, your kingdom to those who are not. We can also interpret this passage in another way: Because you rejoiced at the ruin of my servants, the same persecution shall come against you too, and you shall suffer whatever you have done. And just as you rejoiced against my people together with the remaining nations, so all the nations shall rejoice against you, and they shall devour and drink, and crush you with a like persecution.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.17-18":["And the house of Jacob the supplanter shall possess those who had possessed them as an inheritance, making Christians of persecutors, and receiving the Idumeans themselves into the faith of the Church: but the house of Esau shall be turned into stubble. And as straw cannot bear the nearness of fire, so the house of Esau shall not be able to bear the disputation of Jacob, which is set on fire by the word of the Lord: For the words of the Lord are tried by fire, nor the flame of Joseph, whose name means increase, and who, sold by his brothers, fed the people in Egypt; but at the first encounter their sophisms shall be turned to nothing. And they shall be devoured unto their own salvation, according to what is said in Isaac's blessing to Esau: But I have made him your lord; and I have subjected all your brothers to him as servants, and you shall serve your brother. And there shall be no remnant of the house of Esau when all things in heaven and on earth and under the earth bend the knee to Christ, and all things are subjected to him, that God may be all in all. But because Esau, through his own fault, had built upon the foundation of Christ hay, wood and stubble, therefore the house of Jacob and of Joseph shall be turned into fire and flame, imitating its God, who says: I am God, a consuming fire, so that, when the chaff of sins has been devoured, the pure grain may be gathered into the barns. All that we have said, and all that we are going to say, the Jews promise to themselves for a time to come, when in place of Christ they will receive the Antichrist; and so the prophecy of the Lord and Savior is fulfilled: I came in the name of the Father and you did not receive me; if another comes in his own name, him you will receive. And whatever we have interpreted against Idumea, they dream of as being against the Roman empire; which we say has either already been done according to the history, under Zerubbabel, or at any rate, according to prophecy and the mystical senses, is done daily in the Church, and is fulfilled in each person, in the kingdom of the soul against the flesh. As for the grain-carrier, according to tropology we say that he ceases to exist among the heretics when there is no one among them who boasts that he has the grain of wheat that died in the earth, and the bread from heaven. There are some who think that the Seventy translated not πυροφόρον, that is, grain-carrier, but πυρφόρον, that is, one who can carry a little fire. Accepting, therefore, the different interpretations, we shall say according to tropology that not only the grain-carrier will cease among the heretics, but also the one who pretends to have the light of Christ. And indeed Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.17":["But on Mount Zion there shall be salvation, and there shall be a Holy One, and the house of Jacob shall possess those who had possessed them. And the house of Jacob shall be a fire, and the house of Joseph a flame, and the house of Esau stubble, and they shall be kindled among them, and shall devour them, and there shall be no remnant of the house of Esau, for the Lord has spoken. LXX: Upon Mount Zion there shall be salvation, and it shall be holy, and the house of Jacob shall possess those who had possessed them. And the house of Jacob shall be a fire, and the house of Joseph a flame, but the house of Esau stubble, and they shall be kindled against them, and shall devour them, and there shall be no grain-carrier for the house of Esau, for the Lord has spoken. When Idumea has been overthrown and devoured by the hostile nations with which it had earlier made a pact against Jacob, on Mount Zion there shall be a remnant, and there shall be salvation, and there shall be a Holy One; that is, either the Lord himself shall return to the temple which he had abandoned because of their sins, or there shall be, absolutely, what is holy, that is, the holy of holies. And the house of Jacob, under Zerubbabel and Ezra and Nehemiah, shall possess those who had possessed them as an inheritance.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Alternatively: When the works of the flesh have been destroyed and the earthly empire laid desolate, there shall be in the Church the salvation of those who have not gone out from their mother. And in her shall dwell the Holy One, of whom it is said in Isaiah: Holy, holy, holy, the Lord God of Sabaoth, because both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified are all from one.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.18":["And the house of Jacob, that is, of Judah, shall be a fire, and the house of Joseph, that is, of the ten tribes, a flame. For of Joseph was born Ephraim, from which tribe came the rule of Samaria; but the house of Esau, that is, of the Idumeans, who had shown themselves so savage and cruel against their brother, shall be turned into stubble. And as fire and flame swiftly devour stubble, so the two kingdoms, bound in alliance into one rod, according to Ezekiel, shall lay Idumea waste and devour it, and there shall be no one left of the people who could announce the overthrow of the adversaries to the neighboring nations. For this is what the Seventy translated πυροφόρον, which we have rendered as grain-carrier, following the idiom of the old speech; for those whom they now call agents in affairs, or post-couriers, the ancients called grain-carriers. But it is better that we follow the Hebrew itself, that is, sarid, which is translated either 'remaining', according to Aquila, or 'escaping', according to Symmachus, or, according to Theodotion and the Fifth edition, 'left over'. And all these things shall come to pass because the Lord has spoken, and for him to have commanded is to have done.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.19":["And those who are toward the south shall inherit Mount Esau; and those who are in the plains, the Philistines; and they shall possess the region of Ephraim and the region of Samaria; and Benjamin shall possess Gilead. LXX: And those who are in Nageb shall possess Mount Esau; and those who are in Sephela, the foreigners; and they shall possess Mount Ephraim, and the plain of Samaria, and Benjamin, and Gilead. When Judah has returned to its kingdom, Judah which dwelt in the south and possessed the whole region, according to the division made by Joshua the son of Nun, that slopes toward the Scorpion, that is, all Acrabattene, those who were formerly confined within narrow borders shall possess Mount Esau, that is, the mountains of Seir and the hill country which Edom had possessed before. And those who dwelt in Sephela, that is, in the plains (he means Lydda and Emmaus, that is, Diospolis and Nicopolis), shall possess the Philistines, the five cities of the Philistines, Gaza, Ashkelon, Ashdod, Ekron, Gath, or all that tract which, according to the Acts of the Apostles, is called Sharon. Others, however, think that it is the Sephela, that is, the level region around Eleutheropolis, that is promised; that it is to be extended to Rhinocorura and as far as the sea, that is, that those of the tribe of Judah are not only to hold Eleutheropolis but to reach as far as the coastlands, and to subjugate to their rule the Philistines, whom they had not previously held in subjection. The border of the children of Judah shall also be widened as far as Ephraim, where Neapolis now is, and as far as the region of Samaria, where Sebaste was founded. And Benjamin, whose borders spread out from Jerusalem immediately toward the north, shall possess all Arabia, which was formerly called Gilead and is now named Gerasa. According to the Seventy, however, those who are in the south shall possess Mount Ephraim, and the plains of Samaria, and Benjamin, and Gilead; whether this has been done, God will have seen. For it may have been fulfilled in part over the five hundred years up to the coming of Christ; as I know most certainly, it is fulfilled daily and is confirmed in the kingdom of the Church. For those who dwell in the south, that is, in Nageb, are also in the true light; and those who hold the plains and the low places, that is, his disciples, to whom he says: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart, shall possess Mount Esau and the Philistines, whom, on account of the pride of their doctrines and their worldly eloquence, we can take as standing for the heathen. So that on the mountain are the teachers, and in the Philistines and the foreigners the disciples, who are led by the authority of the teachers. And not only shall they possess Mount Esau and the foreigners, but also the region of Ephraim and of Samaria. Ephraim and Samaria we read often in Hosea, and we have interpreted them of the heresies which divide the Church under the Christian name. For there too fruitfulness is hoped for, and there too they promise the safekeeping of the faith. Further, Benjamin, the son of the right hand and of power, where the temple of God is, shall possess Gilead, which means the migration of the testimony, signifying fleshly Israel; for the testimony of the Lord migrated from them to us. According to the Seventy, however, those who are in the south shall possess both Benjamin himself and Gilead.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.20":["And the exile of this host of the children of Israel, all the things of the Canaanites as far as Sareptha. And the exile of Jerusalem, which is in the Bosphorus, shall possess the cities of the South. And saviors shall go up onto Mount Zion, to judge Mount Esau, and the kingdom shall be the Lord's. LXX: And this is the beginning of the exile for the children of Israel, the land of the Canaanites as far as Sareptha, and the exile of Jerusalem as far as the Euphrates; they shall possess the cities of the Nageb. And those who had been saved shall go up from Mount Zion, to punish Mount Esau, and the kingdom shall be the Lord's. In this place our translation differs greatly from the common edition; hence in the exposition of the history we must follow the Hebrew truth. Those who, according to the book of Ezra and Nehemiah, had returned from Babylon into Judea will also rightly be called the children of the exile. That whole host of the children of Israel shall possess, to the south, and to the west, and to the north, the Idumeans, and the Philistines, and Mount Ephraim, and Samaria. Benjamin also, because it borders on the wilderness, shall in particular hold Gilead. Toward the east, however, they shall rule over all that belongs to the land of the Canaanites, as far as Sareptha of the Sidonians, where the widow once fed Elijah. Further, those who had been carried away from Jerusalem itself, the mother city, to Sapharad, which we have rendered 'Bosphorus', shall possess the cities of the South, which are in the tribe of Judah; for, having returned to their own city, they shall hold the places that neighbor the city.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Where we have put 'Bosphorus', the Hebrew has Sapharad, and I do not know why the Seventy could have translated it 'Ephrata', since Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion agree with the Hebrew truth. We, however, learned from the Hebrew who instructed us in the Scriptures that the Bosphorus is so called; and, speaking as a Jew, this, he said, is the region to which Hadrian transferred the captives. When, therefore, our Christ comes, then that captivity too will return into Judea. We can, however, understand any place whatever of the kingdom of Babylon, although I also think something else. For it is the custom of the prophets, when they speak against Babylon, the Ammonites, the Moabites, the Philistines and the other nations, to make use of their language in many words, and to preserve the idioms of the provinces. Since, therefore, in the language of the Assyrians a boundary, which in Hebrew is called Gebul, is called Sapharad, I conjecture this to be the sense: The exile of Jerusalem, which is divided among all boundaries and regions, shall recover the cities of the South, that is, of its own tribe.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Since we have interpreted according to the history as well as we could, and have steered our little boat among rugged rocks, let us spread the sails of spiritual understanding, so that, with the Lord breathing upon us and unlocking his mysteries, we may joyfully reach the harbor. At the time when Benjamin had possessed Gilead, the exile of the host of the children of Israel, or at any rate the beginning of the exile of the once fleshly Israel, shall be this: that it reaches the land of the Canaanites; and that the prophetic word, which was in danger of famine in Judea, once the fleece of Israel was dried up, passes over to the dew of the nations, and there is both fed and feeds, while it is both received by the believers and itself feeds the believers. Sareptha either is compounded of two words and means 'straitness of bread', or is a single word and is translated 'burning'. Canaan, on the other hand, is rendered σάλον, that is, 'motion', or else it is called 'merchant' and 'lowly'. Therefore the exile of the children of Israel, when they have left behind the letter that kills and have come to the Spirit that gives life, will move all the things that belong to the law. Out of many pearls it will trade for the one most precious pearl, and, having laid aside the pride of the Jews, it will pursue Christian humility; and it will come as far as the place where formerly there was tribulation of bread, and the widow woman, deserted by her maker, could barely nourish her orphaned son, and where all her sins and vices are burned up together. But the captivity or exile of Jerusalem, where once there was the vision of peace, and which is now scattered through the whole world, shall possess the cities of the south, that is, the churches of the true and perfect light, and, repenting, shall say with the bride: Where do you pasture, where do you lie down at midday? And, received with Joseph into its ancient brotherhood, it shall be made drunk with the blood of the shepherd and prince. But if, following the Seventy, we read Ephrata, no one can doubt that the faith of Christ is meant. For Ephrata means καρποφορία, that is, fruitfulness, and is δυώνυμος (having two names), and is called Bethlehem, in which the heavenly bread was born.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"OBA.1.21":["And when these things have been fulfilled, just as in the book of Judges the Lord would send saviors to save the people from captivity, so they shall go up and come onto Mount Zion, to judge and pass sentence on Mount Esau, that is, the Idumeans, as subject to them and serving them; and once all are subdued, the kingdom shall be the Lord's.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Now, where we have translated 'and saviors shall go up', and the Seventy rendered 'those who shall have been saved', in the Hebrew is written mosim; and these are to be understood not passively, as Aquila and the Seventy and Theodotion have it, σεσωσμένοι or ἀνασωζόμενοι (being saved), but, following Symmachus, σώζοντες, that is, actively, saviors. For σεσωσμένοι, that is, 'the saved', are called pheletim in the Hebrew tongue.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","These things having thus been done, the saviors, or those who shall have been saved from the remnant of the Jewish people, shall go up onto Mount Zion, to judge and take vengeance on Mount Esau. Just as the Lord, the Light, calls his apostles light, and says: You are the light of the world; and the Rock himself granted to Peter that he should be a rock; and the good Shepherd also bestowed on them the name of shepherds, and whatever he himself is called he granted to his servants that they too should be called; so the Savior himself willed his apostles to be saviors of the world, who went up to the watchtower of the mountain of the Church and, bringing down the pride of the Jews and of all the mountains that were raised up against the knowledge of God, prepared the kingdom for the Lord.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"JON.1.1-2":["\"Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying, Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me.\" Apart from that which the Septuagint translates as, \"the noise of their wickedness has risen up even to me\", it has translated the rest similarly. Jonah is sent to the gentiles to condemn Israel, because Nineveh had to repent, but the Israelites still persisted in their sin. And when God says, \"their wickedness has come up to me\", or \"the noise of their wickedness…\" it is exactly the text of Genesis: \"the noise of Sodom and of Gomorrah is very loud\" [Gen. 18:20], and to Cain: \"the blood of your brother cries to me from the earth\" [Gen. 4:10]. According to tropology the Lord, our Jonah, that is to say 'dove' or 'suffering', (he is given both meaning, either because the Holy Spirit descends in the form of a dove and stays with him [Mark. 1:10; Luke 3:22; John 1:32-33], or because he has suffered for our wounds, wept for Jerusalem [Luke 19:41], and because we have been cured by his malice [Is. 53:5]) is truly the son of Truth, for God is Truth [John 14:6]. He is sent to Nineveh the beautiful, that is to the world, where there is nothing more beautiful to our eyes than flesh. In Greek the idea of adornment is in the word cosmos. And when everything had been completed, each one by one, it was said, \"and God saw that it was good\" [Gen. 1:10]. It is to Nineveh that he goes, the great city, so that although Israel has not wanted to listen, the whole world of peoples will hear God's word. And this is because their wickedness has gone up to God. For although God had made the most beautiful house for man who was devoted to serving his creator, man deprived himself of this by his own will; from childhood his heart fixed upon wickedness [Gen. 8:21; 6:5]. He turned his face to the heaven [Ps. 72:9] and constructed a tower of pride [Gen 11]. He deserves then God to come down to him so that he may be able to rise to heaven by the destruction of repentance, he that did not succeed by the swell of pride.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.1":["About three years have now passed since I first started writing the commentaries on the five Prophets, Micah, Nahum, Habakkuk, Zephaniah, and Haggai. Detained by another work, I was not able to finish what I had undertaken. For I was writing a book on famous men and two volumes against Jovinian, an apology and an essay on 'the best way to translate', which was addressed to Pammachius, two books to or about Nepotian, and other works which it would be lengthy to recount. Therefore I retake up my commentaries with Jonah after such a long absence. Jonah, a type of Saviour, who prefiguring the resurrection of the Lord by spending \"three days and three nights in the belly of a whale\" [Mt. 12:40], was able to attain the first ardour so that we might deserve the arrival of the Holy Spirit to us. If indeed Jonah is to be translated as 'dove', and if the dove can be seen as the Holy Spirit, then we can also interpret the Dove as signifying the dove's entrance into us. I know that some classical authors, both Latin and Greek, have spoken much about this book, and through all of their Questions have less enlightened than obscured the ideas, so that in effect their interpretation needs to be interpreted and with the result that the reader comes away feeling less sure of the meaning than beforehand. I am not saying this to criticise these great minds, to abase others in order to extol myself, but rather because it is the place of the commentator to clarify in short and clearly what is obscure; they should be less concerned with displaying their eloquence than with explaining the meaning of the author. We ask therefore where else the prophet Jonah appears in the Holy Scriptures apart from this book and the allusion made to him by the Lord in the Gospels [Mt. 12:39, Luke 11:30]. And if I am not mistaken he is mentioned in the book of Kings in this way: \"in the fifth year of Amasiah, the son of Joash, King of Judah, began to rule the son of Jeroboam son of Joash King of Israel in Samaria, for forty-one years. He did much wickedness before the Lord and did not distance himself from all the sins of Jeroboam, son of Nebat, who caused Israel to sin. He re-established the frontier of Israel in Samaria from the entrance of Emathia to the Sea of Solitude, according to the word of the Lord God of Israel, which was spoken by the mouth of his servant Jonah, son of Amittai the prophet, from Gath which is in Ofer.\" [4 Kings 14:23-25] The Hebrews recount that he was the son of the widow of Sarepta, incited by the prophet Elijah; his mother later said to him, \"I know now that you are indeed a man of God, and that the word of God is truly in your mouth\" [3 Kings 17:24]; on account of this the child was called Truth. For Amittai in Hebrew can be rendered 'truth' in our language, and because Elijah spoke true, he who was encouraged was called the son of Truth. And Gath is located two miles from Sepphoris, which is now called Diocaesarea, when you are travelling to Tiberia: there is a small castle where his tomb can be seen. Others, however, prefer to place his birth and tomb near Diospolis, which is in Lydia. They do not see that when he writes 'Ofer', this is to distinguish Gath from other towns of this name that can be seen now near to Eleutheropolis or Diospolis. The book of Tobit, though not in the canon, is all the same used by the men of the Church, and it mentions Jonah when Tobit says to his son, \"my son, I am old and ready to leave this life. Take your sons and go to Media, my son. For I know what the prophet Jonah has said about Nineveh: she will be destroyed\" [Tob. 14:3]. And, indeed, according to the Hebrew and Greek historians, Herodotus in particular, we read that Nineveh was destroyed in the time of King Josiah according to the Hebrews, and King Astyage of the Medians. From this we understand that in the past Jonah predicted that the Ninivites would repent and seek pardon; but afterwards, as they persisted in their sins,they brought the judgement of God upon themselves. The Hebrew tradition is that Hosea, Amos, Isaiah and Jonah prophesied at the same time. This is historical tradition. Not forgetting the others of course: the venerable Pope Chromatius, who took great pains to recount to the Saviour the story of the prophet: he flees, he sleeps, he is thrown into the sea, he is swallowed by a whale, thrown back onto the shore and prays for repentance. And saddened by the safety of this town of many people, he finds comfort in the shade of a fig tree. There he is reproached by God for having taken more care of a green vine which had dried up, than of such a great number of men, and the other details I will try to explain in this volume. But to grasp the complete meaning of the prophet in this short preface there is no better interpretation than that which inspired the prophets and which marked out the lines of the truth of the future for its servants. He therefore speaks to the Jews who do not believe his words and are ignorant of Christ, the son of God: \"the men of Nineveh will rise up at the time of judgement with that generation and they will condemn it, for they repented as Jonah required, and here there is more than Jonah!\" [Mt. 12:41]. The generation of the Jews is condemned, while the world has faith and Nineveh repents, Israel the disbeliever dies. The Jews have the books themselves, we have the Lord of books; they hold the prophets, we have an understanding of the prophets; \"the letter kills them\", \"the spirit makes us live\" [2 Cor. 3:6]; with them Barabbas the robber is released, for us Christ the Son of God is freed.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Prologue"],"JON.1.3":["\"But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD.\" The Septuagint here is similar. The prophet knows by an inspiration of the Holy Spirit, that the repentance of the people is the destruction of the Jews. In this situation it is not that he is trying to save Nineveh, but that moreover he does not want to see it destroyed. In another place Moses prays for his people: \"if you can spare them this sin, spare them; if not, erase me from your book that you have written\" [Ex. 32:31-32]; to this prayer, Israel was saved and Moses was not erased from the Book: even better the Lord indeed profited from his servant by sparing his other servants. For when God says, \"release me\", he shows that he can be held. This is similar to what the apostle says: \"I wished to be anathema for my brothers who are Israelites according to their flesh\" [Rom. 9:3]. Not that he desires to die however, for whom to live is Christ and to die is a profit [Phil. 1:21]; but he deserves life more when he wants to save others. Besides, seeing the other prophets sent to the lost flocks of the house of Israel [Mt. 10:6] to incite the people to repent, and Balaam [Num. 23.24] the divine author of a prophecy about the deliverance of the Israelite people, Jonah feels himself punished by being chosen alone to speak against the Assyrians, the enemies of Israel, in the foreign capital where idolatry and ignorance of God still ruled. And what is more he feared that in spite of his prophesying they would still not be converted to repent, and that Israel would not be completely abandoned. For he knew by this Spirit which had entrusted him with the role of hero among the gentiles, that once the nations had come together in belief, then Israel would surely perish. And he feared that whatever was to happen in the future would not happen in his time. Thus Jonah does as Cain does: he flees from the face of the Lord [Gen. 4:16] and wants to flee to Tarshish, which Josephus interprets as that Tarsus of Cilicia, but changes the first letter. This can also be seen in the book of the Paralipomenon [2 Chron. 20:36-37], which says that there is a place in India which is called the same. According to the Hebrews Tarshish means more generally 'sea', according to this passage: \"by a fierce wind you will break the ships of Tarshish!\" [Ps. 47:8], or the ships of the sea. And in Isaiah: \"cry out, O ships of Tarshish!\" [Is. 23:1,14]. I remember that I have already spoken about this several years ago in a letter to Marcella. The prophet did not intent to flee to such a place, but throwing himself into the sea, he just wants to go anywhere. And this is more pertinent when talking of a fugitive or one who is afraid, that he does not choose carefully where he wants to flee to, but just jumps at the first opportunity to take to the seas. We can also say this: he thought that God was \"known\" only \"in Judea\", \"and in Israel his name is great\" [Ps. 75:2]. After he had seen that God was also in the waves he confesses and declares: \"I am a Hebrew and I fear the Lord of heaven\" [Jonah 1:9], who made the sea and the dry land. But if he had made the sea and the dry earth, why believe when you leave the land that you can escape the creator of the sea on the sea? At the same time when he sees the others sailors saved and converted, he learns that all the wickedness of Nineveh can be saved and converted by a similar confession. We can say too about our Lord and Saviour that he abandoned his home and country: at the incarnation he fled in some manner the heavens for Tarshish, the sea of that age, according to what is written in another place: \"here is the sea, great and wide; there are numerous beings, animals great and small; there the boats come in and go out, and this dragon that you created to be crushed\" [Ps. 103:25-6]. And he says too in his passion, \"Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass me by!\" [Mt. 26:39], lest at the unified complaints of the people, saying, \"Crucify him, crucify him!\" [Luke 23:21], and \"we have no king except Caesar\" [John 19:15], the crowd of people should enter all together; and lest the branches of the olive-tree should be broken, and in their place the shoots of the wild olive should grow [Rom. 11:17-25]. He had such honour and love of his country in light of the choice of the patriarchs and of the promise of Abraham, that he said on the cross, \"Father, forgive them; they know not what they do\" [Luke 23:34]. Or even since Tarshish can be translated as 'the contemplation of joy', the prophet, coming to Joppa, whose name means 'beautiful', hastens to hurry towards the joy and to rejoice in the pleasure of rest, to give himself completely over of contemplation. For he thinks that it is better to rejoice in beauty and in the variety of knowledge than to save the other people by letting that people die, from whom Christ would have been born.\n\n\"And went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD.\" LXX: \"and he went up to Joppa, and he found a boat going to Tarshish; after paying his fare he went on board to sail with them to Tarshish, far from the face of the Lord.\" Joppa is a port of Judea [2 Chron. 2:16], and it has been seen in the book of Kingdoms [i.e. 'Kings'] and of the Paralipomenon. It was there that the King Hiram of Tyr transported wood from Liban by raft, then they were taken by chariot by road to Jerusalem. In this place even to this day rocks can be seen on the shore on which the chained Andromeda was saved by Perseus. The learned reader will know the story. And in light of the nature of the countryside, it is said quite rightly that the prophet came from a direction that is mountainous and precipitous, and went down to Joppa in the plain. He found there a ship that was moored and he went upon the sea. He paid his fare or the price of embarking, that is of his journey, according to the Hebrew, or the fare for himself, as the Septuagint has translated it. \"and he went down into it\" as the Hebrew itself says, (for iered in Hebrew is translated as 'went down'), for in his flight he took great care to find a hiding place. Or \"he goes up\", as it is written in the Vulgate edition, for going where the boat is going, thinking that he has escaped if he has left Judea. But our Lord is also at the edge of the shore of Judea, which is called 'very beautiful' because since he was in Judea, he did not want to take the bread of sons to give it to dogs. [Mt. 15:26] But because he had come for the lost flocks of the house of Israel [Mt. 10:6] he paid the price to those who transport him. Thus he who at first wants to heal his people, saves the inhabitants of the sea, and through great winds and storms, (that is his suffering and the reproof of the cross) he is plunged into Hell and saves those whom had not noticed by appearing to sleep on the boat [Mt. 8:24-5]. The wise reader will not want to try to make tropology and history concur. For the Apostle refers Agar and Sara [Gal. 4:22-31] to the two Testaments, and all the same we are not able to interpret everything that is recounted in this story in a tropological way. And when explaining about Adam and Eve to the Ephesians, he says, \"this is why man leaves his mother and father to join with a wife, and both will become one flesh. [Gen. 2:24] There is a great mystery: I mean Christ and the Church.\" [Eph. 5:31-2] Are we then first to refer the beginning of Genesis, the creation of the world, the formation of mankind, to Christ and to the Church under the pretext that the Apostle has used regarding this text? Let us admit what is written here: \"thus man will leave his father\" [Gen. 2:24], we can apply this to Christ by saying that he left God his Father in heaven to unite the people of the world in the Church. But how can we interpret what follows, \"his mother\"? Unless perhaps we are to say that he left heavenly Jerusalem, that mother of saints, and other ideas that are more complicated? And this too is written by the same Apostle: \"they were drinking from a spiritual rock which was accompanying them, and this rock was Christ\" [I Cor. 10:4], but let us not try to relate the entire book of Exodus to Christ. For what can we say? That this stone was hit by Moses not just once, but twice [Ex. 17:6; Num. 20:11], that the waters flowed [Ps. 77:20] and that the floods were filled up. Are we to regard the entire story of this passage in this case as allegory? Is it nor rather that each passage ought to receive a spiritual meaning according to the diversity of history? Therefore just as these texts each in this way have their interpretations and do not entail the same allegory in their context, so the prophet will not be able to be taken completely to the Lord without difficulty for the interpreter. And if it is said in the Gospel, \"O wicked and adulterous generation, that she asks for a sign? As a sign she will only have the sign of the prophet Jonah. For just as Jonah spent three days and three night in the belly of a fish, so the son of man will spend three days and three nights in the bosom of the earth\" [Mt. 12:39-40]. The remainder of this account does not concern Christ to the same extent. Indeed wherever this reading can be said to apply without discrepancy, we also try to make it fit.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.4":["\"But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken.\" LXX: \"and the Lord induced a great wind over the sea and a great storm was over the sea, and the boat threatened to break up.\" The flight of the prophet can be related to man in general, who, forsaking the commands of God, flees from his face and goes out into the world. But in consequence a storm of wickedness and the shipwreck of the entire world are sent against him, and he is made to pay attention to God and to return to that which he had fled. From this we can understand that what appears to be advantageous to mankind, turns into their downfall by God's will. And not only is their aid no use to those whom it is offered, but even those who offer it are destroyed. Therefore we read that the Assyrians conquered Egypt because she helped Israel against the will of the Lord [Is. 20:3-6]. The boat is in danger because it has taken on board a dangerous passenger. The waves are aroused by the wind, a storm begins over a calm sea. When God is opposed nothing is safe.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.5":["\"Then the mariners were afraid, and cried every man unto his god, and cast forth the wares that were in the ship into the sea, to lighten it of them.\" LXX: \"and the sailors were afraid and each one cried out to his God and they threw the boat's cargo into the sea to lighten the boat\". They believe that the ship with its normal cargo is too heavy, and do not understand that all the weight comes from the fleeing prophet. The sailors are afraid, each one cries out to his God. They do not know the truth, but they do not forget providence, and with a false religion they know that there is something to pray to. They cast their cargo into the sea so that the ship might cross the immensity of the waves more lightly. But for Israel, neither prosperity nor wickedness can lead her back to know God. Christ weeps for the people, but He has dry eyes.\n\n\"But Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship; and he lay, and was fast asleep.\" LXX: \"Now Jonah went down to the heart of the boat and slept and snored\". According to the history of this passage it describes the peace of the spirit of the prophet. He is troubled by the storm, or by the dangers; he just keeps the same manner of spirit when the storm is imminent, as when the weather is calm. The others though cry out to their gods, and cast the cargo overboard: each man to his own. But Jonah is so peaceful, so calm, his spirit is so at rest that he goes down to the heart of the ship to enjoy a peaceful sleep. Indeed we can also say: he knows he is a fugitive and a sinner, because he has not obeyed the commands of the Lord. It is because all the other men do not know why there is a storm that Jonah knows that he alone is the cause of it. This is why he goes down to the interior of the ship and hides himself sadly, so that he does not see the waves, like the avengers of God, rise up against him. And if he sleeps, this is not necessarily a sign of his security, but of worry. For we read that the apostles gave in to sleep on account of great sadness at the sight of the Lord's suffering [Luke 22:45]. For if we interpret the sleep of the prophet as a sign, his terrible torture, they represent a man who has fallen asleep from the drug of his wickedness: not only has he fled from God but moreover he ignores the wrath of God as his spirit is clouded by a sort of madness. He sleeps therefore in a kind of false security and his deep sleep sounds out through his nostrils.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.6":["\"So the shipmaster came to him, and said unto him, What meanest you, O sleeper? arise, call upon thy God, if so be that God will think upon us, that we perish not.\" LXX: \"and the helmsman come to him and he said to him, what are you doing sleeping? Get up, and call upon your God. If he can find a way to save us then we may not die. It is natural that each one has more confidence in someone else when they feel themselves to be in such danger. This is why the helmsman or captain, who should have been encouraging the frightened crewmembers, but saw the seriousness of the danger, woke and reprimanded the sleeper for his thoughtless security and asked him to pray to his God immediately. He shared everyone's danger, and therefore he had to pray along with everyone else. According to tropology there are many men sailing with Jonah, who each have their own God and hasten towards the 'contemplation of joy'. But when Jonah has been discovered by chance and his death has appeased the all-encompassing storm and made calm the waters, then the one God is revered and spiritual victims are sacrificed, which according to the text were not found when they were amongst the waves.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.7":["\"And they said every one to his fellow, Come, and let us cast lots, that we may know for whose cause this evil is upon us. So they cast lots, and the lot fell upon Jonah.\" LXX: 'and they said to each other: come, let us draw lots to see who it is that has brought this wickedness upon us. And they drew lots, and the lot fell to Jonah.' They knew the ways of the sea and knew the causes of the storms and winds in such weather. Without a doubt they had seen the waves rise up as usual, and as they must have seen many times before, but they must never before have found the person to blame for the shipwreck, and through him tried to avoid certain danger. We should not be driven by this example to believe in fate, or to believe that this text should be connected to that of the Acts of the Apostles where Matthias is chosen by lot [Acts 1:26], because personal privileges do not make common law. For just as an old lady speaks up for the condemning of Balaam [Num. 22:28], as Pharaoh [Gen. 41] and Nebuchadnezzar [Dan. 2], in their own judgement, knew the future through dreams and yet do not see that there is a divine judgement in this, like Caiaphas prophesies unknowing, that it is better for one to die for all [John 11:50; 18:14]: just as this fugitive is betrayed by fate, not by the powers of the fates, above all the powers of the pagan fates, but by the will of hi who controlled uncertain fate. With regard to the meaning of the expression \"to know by whom this wickedness had come upon us\", we ought to take 'wickedness' as a synonym of affliction, of disaster, as in this passage: \"every day his wickedness was enough\" [Mt. 6:34], and in the prophet Amos: \"is there wickedness in a town without God being the author?\" [Amos 3:6]. And in Isaiah: \"It is I the Lord, who make goodness and wickedness\" [Isaiah 45:7]. But in other places too wickedness can be seen to be the opposite of virtue, as in the passage of our prophet that we have read above: \"the cry of their wickedness went up to me\" [Jonah 1:1].\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.8":["\"Then said they unto him, Tell us, we pray you, for whose cause this evil is upon us; What is thine occupation? and whence do you come? what is thy country? and of what people art you?\" LXX: 'and they said to him, 'tell us how this wickedness has come upon us: what is your occupation, where do you come from, where you are going to, from which country, and which people you are from?' '. Fate had shown him to them: they force him to admit why such a great storm, or for what reason divine wrath had come against them. Tell us, they say, where this wickedness comes from, which has come upon us, what work you do, from what land, from what people you flee, and where you are going to so quickly? Let us note the brevity here that is also seen in Virgil [Aen. 8:112]: young men, what cause has brought you to try out unknown ways? Where are you going? He says. Your people? From which land? Do you bring war or peace? This questioning brings his identity, his country, his journey, the town he comes from, so that the reason for the wickedness can be known.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.9":["\"And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land.\" LXX: 'and he replied: I am a worshipper of the Lord, and I revere God of the heavens who made the sea and the dry land'. He did not say, 'I am a Jew', the name given to the people after the schism between the ten and two tribes [3 Kings. 12:19; 14:21], but 'I am a Hebrew', that is to say perates [Grk. 'a pilgrim and traveller'] , passing by as Abraham who was able to say: \"I am a guest and a traveller as all my fathers\" [Ps. 38:13], and about whom it is written in another psalm: \"they passed from one nation to another, from one realm to another people\" [Ps. 104:13]. Moses says, \"I will go so that I might see this great vision.\" [Ex. 3] I fear the Lord God of the heavens, not the gods that you have invoked and who cannot save us, but the God of heaven who made the sea and the dry land. The sea that I flee to, the earth that I flee from. And appropriately the land is not just called land, but rather dry land so that it contrasts with the sea. In short here he mentions the creator of the universe who is the Lord of heaven, earth, and sea. But one question begs to be asked: how do they know that he speaks the truth? 'I fear the Lord God of heaven', since he has not done what this God has actually commanded him to do. The reply would surely be that the sinners themselves would fear God, and that it is appropriate for servants of the Lord not to love, but to fear. Here however you can see fear in the cult according to the meaning of those who were listening and until now knew not God.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.10":["\"Then were the men exceedingly afraid, and said unto him, Why have you done this? For the men knew that he fled from the presence of the LORD, because he had told them.\" LXX: 'Then the men were very afraid and said to him, \"why have you done this?\" for the men knew that he had fled from the face of the Lord, since he had told them.' The chronological order is reversed here, for you could have said there was no reason to fear because of his declaration: \"I am a Hebrew and I fear the Lord God of heaven, who made the sea and the dry land\". Immediately we are told why they were afraid: because he had told them that he was fleeing the presence of the Lord without having carried out his commands. Then they make excuses and say, \"why did you do this?\", and this means, \"if you fear God, why did you do this? If this God that you revere is so powerful according to you, then how can you believe that you will be able to escape him?\". They are seized by a great fear, for they realise that he is holy, and from a holy nation (having set out from Joppa they must have known the privilege of the Hebrew people), yet nonetheless they are not able to hide the fugitive. For he who flees may be powerful, but he who seeks is all the more powerful. They do not dare to hand him over to the Lord, yet they cannot hide him. They reprehend blame, and avow their fear. They pray to Jonah to give himself up for the sin he has committed. Or indeed, when they say, \"why have you done this?\", they are not inciting him, but questioning, wanting to know the cause of his flight, the flight of a servant from his master, of a son from his father, of a man from his God. They ask, therefore, what is this great mystery that makes you flee from the land and seek the seas, leave your country and set out for foreign lands?\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.11":["\"Then said they unto him, What shall we do unto you, that the sea may be calm unto us? for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous.\" LXX: 'then they said to him, what should we do with you, so that the sea is calm for us? For the sea was surging its waves more and more'. It is because of you, you say, that the winds, the waves, the sea and swells have been unleashed. You have revealed the cause of this wickedness, now tell us how to stop it. The sea swells against us, and we know that a God is angry because we took you on board. If we have sinned by taking you in, then what can we do so that the Lord does not become angrier? \"What should we do with you?\" that is to say: \"shall we kill you?\" but you are faithful to the Lord. Are we to protect you? But you flee from Him. All we have to do is carry out whatever you command, all you have to do is give the command that the sea be calm, for now its wildness attests the wrath of the creator. The narrator also adds the reason for this question. The sea, he says, was continually increasing in wildness. It was swelling, in the known way; it was swelling for the revenge of its Lord; it was swelling, following the fleeing prophet. And at every moment it was becoming more and more wild, and to the delaying sailors' eyes it rose in greater waves to show that it would not put off for long the creator's revenge.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.12":["\"And he said unto them, Take me up, and cast me forth into the sea; so shall the sea be calm unto you: for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you.\" LXX: 'and he said to them, take me and throw me into the sea, and the sea will become calm for you. For I know well that it is on account of me that these great waves are against you.' It is against me that the thunder sounds, it seeks me, it threatens to shipwreck you in order to reach me. It will seize me so that my death might let you live. For I know this, he says. This great storm is on my account. And I am not unaware that this is my punishment, this confusion of the elements, this trouble of the world. This wrath is for me, but you are going to be the victims of a shipwreck. The waves themselves command you to throw me into the sea. And since I will have felt the full effect of the storm you will be in calm seas again. We must note here the greatness of spirit of our fugitive: he is not evasive, he does not hide or deny his guilt, but having confessed his flight he accepts his punishment willingly. He would rather die so that the other sailors do not perish on account of him, and so that he does not add murder to desertion. That's it for the story. But we are also not unaware of the wild winds, which the Lord orders in the Gospel to quiet, that the ship in danger in which Jonah was sleeping, and that the raised sea which is reprimanded: \"silence, and calm down\" [Mk. 4:39], refer to the Lord the Saviour and to the Church in peril, or even to Christ awaking the apostles, and they themselves leaving their sufferings behind throw him somehow headlong into the waves. Our Jonah says, \"for I know that it is on account of me that this great storm is upon you\", for the winds are watching me journey to Tarshish with you, that is travel to the contemplation of joy to lead you with me to goodness so that wherever I am, so is the Father and you will be there too [John 14:3; 17:27]. This is why this anger rumbles, why the world which is in wickedness [1 John 5:19] groans. It is in this way that the elements are disturbed. Death wants to devour me so that you may be killed as well: she does not see that as she took food in a net, my death will cause her death. Take me and throw me into the sea. For we do not have to run away from death, but receive it with open arms when it takes us from others. Thus, in the persecutions it is not allowed to kill oneself, unless chastity is in danger, but one must put ones neck to the executioner. Go, he says, calm the winds, pour libations on the sea: the storm which savages against you on account of me will be calmed by my death.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.13":["\"Nevertheless the men rowed hard to bring it to the land; but they could not: for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous against them.\" LXX: 'and the sailors strive to turn the ship to dry land but they cannot, for the sea swelled up against them'. The prophet has pronounced sentence against himself; but the sailors do not dare touch him because they have learned that he is a follower of God. They were striving to return to the dry land, to get out of this danger; they refused to shed blood, preferring rather to die than kill. O how changed are they now! The people that had served God [Deut. 10:12] saying, \"crucify him, crucify him\" [Lk. 23:21]. They are ordered to kill him: the sea is raging, the storm commands this, and they forget their own danger and only think to save another. Therefore the phrase of the Septuagint is appropriate: parebiazonto, they wanted to use all their force and conquer nature so as not to offend the prophet of God. If the sailors rowed to regain the land, it was because they believed they could deliver the ship from danger without realising what Jonah, who ought to have suffered, had said. All the while Jonah was in the sea the ship sat safely in the water.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.14":["\"Wherefore they cried unto the LORD, and said, We beseech you, O LORD, we beseech you, let us not perish for this man's life, and lay not upon us innocent blood: for you, O LORD, have done as it pleased you.\" LXX: 'and they cried to the Lord and said, but no, Lord, let us not die to let this man live. Lay not innocent blood upon us. For O Lord you have done as you wished.' The sailors' faith is strong: they are all in danger of losing their lives, and yet pray for the lives of another. They know well that spiritual death is worse than natural death of the body. Do not lay innocent blood upon us, they say. They take the Lord as witness not to visit them for what they are about to do, and say something like this: 'we do not want to kill your prophet, but he himself has proclaimed your wrath, and the storm shows us that you have done what you wished, O Lord. Your wish is accomplished by our doing'. This seems to be the confession of Pilate, as he washes his hands and says, \"I am clean of the blood of this man\" [Mt. 27:24]. The gentiles do not want Christ to die, and affirm that it is innocent blood. And the Jew say, \"let his blood fall upon us again and on our son\" [Mt. 27:25]. This is why when they raise their hands to the sky, they will not be heard, for they are full of blood. For your will has been done, Lord. We welcomed the passenger, and the whirlwind began, the winds blew and the sea swelled in waves. The fugitive was brought by fate, and tells what we must do: all of this, Lord, is the effect of your will. Yes, Lord, your will has been done. In this way the Saviour speaks in the Psalm, \"Lord, I wanted to do your will\" [Ps. 39:9].\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.15":["\"So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging.\" LXX: 'and they took Jonah and they threw him into the sea, and the sea became ceased from its agitation'. He did not say, they grabbed him and threw him but they raised him up as if they were carrying him with respect and honour, and they threw him into the sea without him struggling, but rather he went willingly. And the sea ceased because it had found the man it was searching for. Just as when you pursue a fugitive, and running, catch up with him, then stop to grab hold of him; so too the sea was wild without Jonah, and then when it had in its lap what it desired it rejoiced in having him and cherished him, and the calm returned by this joy. If we consider before the suffering of Christ, the confessions of the world, the contrary winds of different opinions, the ship and all human kind, that is all creation to be in danger, then, after the suffering of Christ there is the calm of faith, the peace of the world, universal safety, conversion to God, and we will see how after Jonah has been thrown overboard the sea ceases from its raging.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1","The text does not say they seized him or that they threw him in, but that they took him, carrying him as one [deserving] respect and honor. They discharged him into the sea not in repugnance; rather, he submitted himself of his own volition into their hands. And the sea ceased [its turmoil] because it found what it sought. When one continues as a fugitive and keeps running away as fast as one can, sooner or later he is caught and stops his running, and whatever was chasing him stands still. It is the same way with the sea, which, absent Jonah, was irritated. But as soon as it lays hold of what is at the center of its desire it rejoices to have it, and from that joy it returns to tranquillity. If we will give consideration to the time before the passion of Christ, [we will see that time as one disturbed by] the errors of the world and the headwinds of various opinions. The entire boat of humanity, that is, the creation of the Lord, was in peril. But then, after his passion, we see a world where there is the calm of faith, a world at peace and secure for everyone. We see a turning toward God. In this way we may understand how, after Jonah goes into the sea, the sea is alleviated of its turmoil.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.16":["\"Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows.\" LXX: similar. Before the anger of the Lord the sailors implored their gods under the effect of their fear; after his anger they fear the Lord, that is they revere and worship Him. They do not worship Him in the usual way, as we have seen in the beginning, but with \"a great fear\", according to that which is said: \"from all their spirit and all their heart and all their soul\" [Deut. 6:5; Mt. 22:37]. And they sacrificed victims that indeed, to take this literally, they were not able to have out at sea. But this is because sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit. [Ps. 50:19] And it is said in another place: \"offer to God a sacrifice of praise, acquit your vows to the Highest.\" [Ps. 49:14] And again: \"we acquit ourselves to you of our vows that we have promised\". [Hos. 14:3] This is how they offer a sacrifice in the middle of the sea, and they promise others vowing never to be far from Him whom they have begun to revere and worship. They were seized by a great fear for they recognised from the calm sea and the disappearing storm that the prophet had spoken true. Jonah at sea, a fugitive and shipwrecked, once dead saves the ship in the waves, saves the pagans who had been beforehand divided in different beliefs by the wickedness of the world. And Hosea, Amos, Isaiah, and Joel, who prophesied at the same time did not manage to convert the people in Judea. This shows that the shipwreck could only be saved by the death of the fugitive.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.1.17":["\"Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah.\" LXX: 'and the Lord ordered a great fish to swallow Jonah'. The Lord commanded death and the underworld to receive the prophet. To the eager jaws of death he seemed a prey: she had such joy in swallowing him, and such sadness in spitting him out. Thus happened what is written in Hosea: \"I will be your death, O Death! I will be your bite, Hell!\" [Hos. 13:14]. In the Hebrew we read \"a great fish\", which the Septuagint and the Lord in the Gospel call a whale, to explain the matter in short. For the Hebrew says dag gadol that we translate as 'a big fish'. Evidently this means a whale. We must note too that where he awaited death, he found his salvation. And when it says, \"he had prepared\", this is even right at the beginning of creation, the animal which is mentioned in the psalm: \"this dragon which you have created to play with him\" [Ps. 103:26]. Or even he makes a fish come near to the ship to take in its belly Jonah who has been thrown over board, and to provide his rescue not his death. So he who felt the wrath of God in the boat was to feel his benevolence in his death.\n\n\"And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.\" LXX: 'and Jonah was in the belly of the whale for three days and three nights'. The Lord shows in the Gospel the symbolism of this passage [Mt. 12:40], and it is superfluous to say in the same terms or even in other terms what he who has suffered has already said. But we ask ourselves this: how was he three days and three nights in the belly of the earth. Some scholars take the view according to paraskeuen, because of the solar eclipse from the sixth to the ninth hour when night followed day, this would be two days and nights, and adding the Sabbath, believe that we should count this as three days and three nights. But I prefer to understand this by reason of synecdoche, seeing the whole as a part: where he is dead in paraskeuen [Lk. 23:54], let us count one day and one night; two with the Sabbath; the third night which arises from the day of the Lord, let us take that as the beginning of the next day, for, in Genesis [Genesis 1:5-8] the night is not of the preceding day, but of the following day, that is to say the beginning of the next day, not the end of the previous. To understand this better I will say it more simply: if a man leaves his house at nine and the next day he arrives at his other house at three. And if I say that he has been two days in travelling, I will not be reprimanded as a liar, because he has not used all the hours of two days, but only a part for his journey. Nonetheless this seems to me to be the interpretation. If someone does not agree with this, and he can explain the meaning in a clearer way, then we should follow his interpretation.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1"],"JON.2.1":["\"Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish's belly.\" LXX: similar. If Jonah is compared to the Lord, and his time of three days and three nights in the belly of the whale is a sign of the suffering of the Saviour, his prayer also ought to be a kind of prayer of the Saviour. Some people, I don't doubt, will find it difficult to believe that a man can spend three days and three nights in the belly of a whale, especially after a shipwreck. These people can either be religious or not. But if they have faith, they will believe this all the more: how three children thrown into a furnace of hot fire were so well protected that their clothes were not even singed [Dan. 3:94/27]; how the sea drew back on itself into two sides and held itself up like a wall to offer a route for the people who wanted to pass [Ex. 14:22-29]; how with all human moderation the anger of a lion that had been increased by hunger was taken by fear at the sight of his prey, and didn't want to touch it [Dan. 6:23]; and even other such miracles. If they do not have faith, let them read the fifteen books of Ovid's Metamorphoses, and all Greek and Latin history. Therein they will see Daphne changed into a bay-tree, or the sister of Phaeton changed into poplars; how Jupiter the highest god, was transformed into a swan, flowed in gold and became a raging bull, and other adventures where the ugliness of the stories attest the holiness of the divinity. They believe in these stories and say that everything is possible for one god. And while they believe these ugly stories and defend the absolute power of a god, they do not attribute this same power to honest deeds. With regard to these words: 'then Jonah prayed to the Lord his God out of the belly of the fish and said…' we understand that feeling that he is safe in the belly of the whale he does not despair of divine mercy and concentrates wholly on praying. For God, who had said, \"I am with him in his distress\" [Ps. 90:15], and when he calls to me, I will reply, \"I am here.\" [Is. 58:9], came to his aid and he whose prayer had been answered was then able to say, \"in distress you have made me greater\" [Ps. 4:2].\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.2":["\"And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and you heard my voice.\" LXX: similar except: 'from the belly of hell I threw out my cries'. He does not say, \"I cry\", but \"I cried\". He does not pray for the future, but gives thanks for the past. That shows us that from the moment he is thrown into the sea and sees the whale, that great bulk, that immense mouth which opened wide to swallow him, he remembered God and cried out, either by the waves giving passage for his cry, or by a feeling from the depths of his heart, according to that which the apostle says: \"crying in your hearts\" [Col. 3:16]: \"Abba! Father\" [Rom. 8:15]. He cried to him who alone knew the hearts of men and said to Moses, \"why do you cry out to me?\" [Ex. 14:15], while the Scriptures remember that Moses had never cried out before this speech. This is the text that we read in the first psalm of the steps: \"I cried to the Lord in my distress and he replied to me.\" [Ps. 119:1] By the \"belly of hell\" we understand the stomach of a whale of such great size that it took the place of hell. But this can better be referred to the person of Christ, who under the name of David, sings in the psalm: \"you will not leave my spirit in hell, and you will not allow your saint to see putrefaction\" [Ps. 15:10], living in hell free among the dead.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.3":["\"For you had cast me into the deep, in the midst of the seas; and the floods compassed me about.\" LXX: 'you cast me into the deep of the heart from the sea, and the waves surrounded me'. The interpretation of the person of Jonah is not difficult: from the moment when he was closed in the stomach of the whale and found himself at the deepest and middle of the sea, he was surrounded by waves. For the Lord, the Saviour, prefiguring psalm 68 in which he says, \"I am enshrouded in the deep mud where there is no ground. I have come to the deepest part of the sea and the storm engulfs me\" [Ps. 68:3]. It is said of him in another psalm: \"but you, you have rejected, despised and disquieted your Christ; you have cursed the covenant of your master, you have dishonoured his sacred place on earth, you have destroyed all its walls\" [Ps. 88:39-41], and so on. For in this comparison of divine blessing and that place about which is written, \"his home is in sacred peace\" [Ps. 75:3], all habitation on earth is full of waves, full of storms. And the \"heart of the sea\" means hell, for which we read in the Gospel, \"in the heart of the earth\" [Mt. 12:40]. For just as the heart is at the middle of animal, so we say that hell is in the middle of the earth. Or according to anagoge he recalls that he is \"in the heart of the sea\", that is in the middle of temptations. However, although he has been among the bitter waters and been tempted by all things without sin, he has not felt the bitter waters, but has been surrounded by the waves about which we read elsewhere, \"an impetuous wave rejoices in the city of God\" [Ps. 45:5]. Others drank the salty waves; myself, surrounded by temptation, I endured sweeter currents. And do not think what the Lord says now is impious: \"you have cast me into the deep\", who says in the psalm, \"for they have followed him that you smote\" [Ps. 68:27], according to the phrase which in Zechariah is spoken by the Father: \"I will smite the shepherd, and the flocks will be scattered\" [Zechariah 13:7].\n\n\"All thy billows and thy waves passed over me.\" LXX: 'all your whirlwinds and your waves passed over me'. No one can doubt that the swelling waves of the sea encompassed Jonah, that there was fierce thunder in the storm. But we ask how all the whirlwinds, billows and the waves of God encompassed the Saviour. \"The life of men on earth is temptation\" [Job 7:1], or as there is in the Hebrew, \"a military service\", for we serve here to be crowned elsewhere. There is no man who can sustain all the temptations, except him who has been tempted by all, in our image, except sin [Heb. 4:15]. This is why it is said in Corinthians, \"no temptation will take you, I hope, unless it is human. God is faithful, he will not let you try beyond your ability, but he will produce an exit that you may hold on to.\" [1 Cor. 10 ,13] And like all persecutions and all wicked things that happen to us they do not happen without the will of God, we speak of whirlwinds and waves of God, which have not crushed Jesus, but have come down upon him with a simple threat of shipwreck which does not happen. Thus all persecutions and whirlwinds which tortured mankind and broke all the ships have passed thundering on my head. And myself, I have sustained storms and broken whirlwinds which were raging, to allow others to sail more easily.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.4":["\"Then I said, I am cast out of thy sight;\" LXX: 'I said, I am cast far from your sight'. Before I cried out from the depths of my distress and before you heard me, me who had taken the position of slave and imitated its weakness, I said, \"I am cast out of your sight\". When I was with you enjoying your light and you, light, being light, I did not say \"I am cast out\". But once at the bottom of the sea and surrounded by the flesh of a man, I say: \"I am cast out of your sight\". I said this as a man. And as God being in that condition I did not think of my equality with you, because I wanted to raise mankind to you, so that wherever I am with you they are there as well and those who have believed in me and in you, I say: yet I will look again toward thy holy temple. LXX: 'do you not think I will be able to see your holy temple again?'. To express the Greek ara, the Vulgate edition's 'do you think' can be interpreted as 'therefore', like the last conclusion of the proposition, of the assumption and of the confirmation and syllogism, not in the uncertainty of someone who hesitates but in the confidence of someone who affirms. This has been translated by, \"yet I will look again on your holy temple\", according to that which is said in another psalm by the spokesperson of Christ: \"Lord, I have loved beauty of your house and the tabernacle where your glory lives\" [Ps. 25:8], and the passage of the Gospel in which it says, \"Father, glorify me with you by that glory which I had before the world existed\" [John 17:5]. And the Father replied to heaven: \"I have glorified him, and I shall glorify him\" [John 12:28]. Or even because he says, \"the Father is in me, and I am in Him\" [John 10:38; 14:10.11; 17:21], for the temple of the Father is the Son, thus the temple of the Son is the Father. He Himself said, \"I left my Father and have come\" [John 16:28], and \"the word was with God and the word was God\" [John 1:1]. Or even the Saviour, the one and the same, asks as man and promises as God, and he is sure of the right that was always his. For the person of Jonah you can clearly see that with a feeling of desire and confidence, at the bottom of the sea, he wished to see the temple of the Lord, and with a prophetic spirit he found himself elsewhere and thought of other things.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.5-6":["\"the weeds were wrapped about my head. I went down to the bottoms of the mountains; the earth with her bars was about me for ever:\" LXX: 'my head has penetrated to the base of mountains; I descended to into the earth whose bars are eternal bonds'. No one doubts that the ocean covered Jonah's head, that he went down to the roots of mountains and came to the depths of the earth by which as bars and columns by the will of God the earthly sphere is supported. This earth about which is said elsewhere, \"I consolidated her columns\" [Ps. 74:4]. With regard to the Lord Saviour, according to the two editions, this seems to me to be what is meant. His heart and his head, that is the spirit that he thought worthy to take with a body for our safety, went down to the base of the mountains which were covered by waves; they were restrained by the will of God, the deep covered them, they were parted by the majesty of God. His spirit then went down into hell, into those places to which in the last of the mud, the spirits of sinners were held, so too the psalmist says: \"they will go down to the depths of the earth, they will be the lot of wolves\" [Ps. 62:10.11]. These are the bars of the earth and like the locks of a final prison and tortures, which do not let the captive spirits out of hell. This is why the Septuagint has translated this is a pertinent way: \"eternal bonds\", that is, wanting to keep in all those whom it had once captured. But our Lord, about which we read these lines of Cyrus in Isaiah: \"I will break the bronze bars, I will crack the iron bars\" [Is. 45:2], He went down to the roots of the mountains, and was enclosed by eternal bars to free all the prisoners.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.5":["\"The waters compassed me about, even to the soul: the depth closed me round about,\" LXX: 'the water ran about me up to my spirit; the last depth closed around me'. These waters, near to the deep, which cycle and slide about the earth, which drag much mud with them, tend to kill not the body but the soul, for they are friendly to the body and warmed by its desires. This is why, according to that which I have said above, the Lord says in the psalm, \"save me, Lord, because the waters have penetrated even to my soul\" [Ps. 68:2], and in another passage, \"my soul has passed a torrent\" [Ps. 123:5], and, \"let not the well press its mouth on me\" [Ps. 68:16], let hell not imprison me! Let it not refuse me an exit! I freely made the descent; so let me make the ascent back again freely. I became a captive voluntarily, I ought to free the captives so that this verse is fulfilled: \"ascending into the higher parts he led the captives\" [Eph. 4:8]. For those who were beforehand captives in death, he brought them to life again. We must heed certain wicked forces in the deep, or the specific powers in torture and supplication; demons, in the Gospel, ask not to be forced to go to them [Mt. 8:30; Mk. 5:10; Lk. 8:31]. This is why \"the darkness was over the deep\" [Gen. 1,2]. Sometimes the deep is taken to mean the sacraments in a deeper sense, the judgements of God: \"the judgements of the Lord are a great abyss\" [Ps. 35:7], and \"the deep cries out to the deep in a cry of your cataracts.\" [Ps. 41:8]\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.6":["\"yet have you brought up my life from corruption, O LORD my God.\" LXX: 'and from corruption my life comes up to me, O Lord my God.' He says rightly \"you have brought up\" or \"let my life come up from corruption\", because it had descended to corruption in hell. This is what the apostles interpret in the fifteenth psalm as prophetic speech of the Lord: \"for you will not leave my spirit in hell, and you will not permit your holiness to see the corruption\" [Ps. 15:10], given that David is dead and has been buried, but the Saviour's flesh has not known corruption. Others understand that compared to celestial blessing and to the Word of God the body of man is corruption itself, for \"it is sown in corruption\" [1 Cor. 15:42], and in the psalm one hundred and two, the meaning is applied to a righteous man: \"he who cures all illnesses, who has brought his life back from death\" [Ps. 102:3.4]. This is why the Apostle says, \"O wicked man that I am! Who will deliver me from this body of death?\" [Rom. 7:24]. It is called \"the body of death\", or \"body of misery\". These people take the text in the sense of their heresy, to see an Antichrist in the place of Christ, and to take the Churches in order to feed a fat stomach and discuss contrary to the flesh living in the flesh. But we, we know that the body taken from the pure Virgin was not the corruption of Christ, but his temple. If we pass then to the thought of the Apostle in Corinthians, where there is the question of a spiritual body, we would say, in removing any appearance of chicanery, that the same body, the same flesh rises again, which has been buried and placed in the soil; but the only thing that changes is the glory, not nature. \"for this corruptible being must cover incorruptibility, this mortal being must clothe immortality.\" [1 Cor. 15:53] When he says \"this being\" it is almost as if one showed the body by pinching it between two fingers: in which we are born, in which we die, that those who are guilty fear to receive as punishment, that virginity awaits in recompense, that the adulterer fears in punishment. For Jonah, this is how we can understand it: he who would have had to corrupt himself physiologically in the belly of the whale, and get by on the food of beasts and survive by drinking from the veins and arteries, still managed to remain safe and sound. And when he says, \"Lord my God\", this is a feeling of flattery: he thinks that God, who is common to all, is also common to him, and feels he is his own because of the greatness of his benevolence.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.7":["\"When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD.\" LXX: 'when my spirit failed in me, I remembered the Lord'. Although I hoped for no aid, he says, the memory of the Lord saved me, according to this passage: \"I remembered the Lord and I rejoiced\" [Ps. 76:4], and in another passage, \"I remembered former days and I remembered the days of eternity\" [Ps. 76:6]. I had lost all hope of finding a way out: my body was so frail in the intestines of the whale that I could not hope for my life. And so, everything that seemed impossible I found to be surpassed by the thought of the Lord. I saw myself imprisoned in the intestines of the whale, and all my hope was the Lord. From this we can learn that, according to the Septuagint, at the time when our spirit fails us, it is wrenched from its union with the body, and we ought not to turn our thoughts from Him who inside and outside our body is the Lord. For the Saviour the interpretation is not very difficult because he said, \"my spirit is sad to die\" [Mt. 26:38; Mk. 14:34], and \"My Father, if it is possible let this cup pass me by\" [Mt. 26:39], and, \"I place my spirit in your hands\" [Ps. 30:6; Lk. 23:46], and other passages which are similar to this. And my prayer came in unto you, into thine holy temple. LXX: similar. In my distress I remembered the Lord so and my prayer came in to heaven from the depths of the sea and from the roots of the mountains, and came to your holy temple where you reside in eternal beatitude. This new kind of speech should be noted here: a prayer made for a prayer. Jonah asks that his prayer rise up to the temple of God. He wishes like the Pope that in his body the people should be freed.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.8":["\"They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy.\" LXX: 'those that keep mistaken vanities lose their mercy'. By nature God is merciful and ready by his mildness to save those whom he can't save by justice. But because of our vices we lose the mercy which is reserved for us and is offered to us. Jonah did not say, \"those who make vanities\", for \"vanity of vanities, all is vanity\" [Eccl. 1:1], not to have an air of condemning everyone, and of refusing mercy to all mankind, but \"those who keep vanities\" or the lie \"those who have come to love their heart\" [Ps. 72:7], who are not happy with doing, but who keep their vanities as if they cherished them, thinking they have found some kind of treasure. Note too the greatness of the prophet's spirit: at the bottom of the sea, surrounded by an eternal night in the intestines of a great beast he is not thoughtful of his danger, but philosophises on the question of nature. \"they will lose\" he says \"their mercy\". Although mercy is offended and we can understand that it is God Himself: for \"God is merciful and good, patient and full of pity\" [Ps. 144:8], yet mercy does not abandon those who keep their vanities, she does not curse them, but waits for them to return, while they intentionally abandon the mercy which is before them, offered to them. This can also be prophesised for the Lord on the subject of the infidelity of the Jews, who think themselves to observe the precepts of mankind [Mk. 7:7] and the commandments of the Pharisees, this is vanity and a lie, and they have abandoned God who always had pity for them.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.9":["\"But I will sacrifice unto you with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD.\" LXX: 'but I will sacrifice to you with the voice of praise and the action of thanksgiving. I will pay all that I have vowed to you, Lord, in salutation.' Those who keep their vanities have abandoned their mercy. But I who have been eaten for the sake of the safety of the multitude, will offer you sacrifices with the voice of praise and thanksgiving, offering myself. For \"Christ, our Easter, has been sacrificed\" [1 Cor. 5:7]. A as a true Pope and lamb he offers himself for us. And I will give thanks to you, saying, \"I bless you Father, lord of heaven and earth\" [Mt. 11:25], and I will keep those vows to the Lord that I made for the safety of others, so that all that \" you have given me never dies\" [John 6:39; 10:28; 17:12]. We see what the Lord promised in his suffering for our safety: let us not make Jesus a liar [1 John 1:10], and let us be pure, delivered from all the uncleanness of sins so that he offers us to God the Father as the victims he had promised.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.2.10":["\"And the LORD spoke unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land.\" LXX: 'and he ordered the whale to vomit Jonah out onto the dry land'. That which we read above as being about Jonah, the Lord prayed for in the stomach of the whale about which Job speaks in an unclear way: \"let he who curses this day curse him, he who will capture the great whale\" [Job. 3:8 LXX]. The great whale, the deep and hell are then ordered to give back the Lord to the dry earth; thus he who had died to free those detained by the chains of death, can lead with him many others towards life. With regard to the expression 'vomited' we must take this to be said in a very emphatic way, to mean that triumphant life has emerged from the deepest and most impenetrable parts of death.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 2"],"JON.3.1-2":["\"And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying, arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid you.\" LXX: 'and the message of God came to Jonah a second time, saying, arise, go to Nineveh, the great city, and preach there this message that I have told you'. He did not say to the prophet, \"why have you not done what you were ordered to do?.\" But the punishment of the shipwreck and his drowning are enough for him to understand the Lord, the liberator, whom he hadn't known to be ordering. Moreover it is superfluous to see his wounds as those of a false servant of God, once he has been smitten, for such a punishment is less of a correction than a reproof. And our Lord is sent to Nineveh a second time after his resurrection: he who had fled by whatever means beforehand when he said, \"My Father, if it is possible let this cup pass me by\" [Mt. 26:39], and who had not wanted to give bread of children to dogs, now the children have cried out, \"crucify him, crucify him! we have no king except Caesar\" [Lk. 23:21; John 19:15], he makes his way towards Nineveh of his own accord to preach after his resurrection that he underwent as he was ordered to do before his suffering. The command is given, he hears it, he refuses, then he is forced to want, and the second time he carries out the will of the Father: all of this is connected to man and to the \"form of a slave\" [Phil. 2:7], to whom such expressions are appropriate.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3"],"JON.3.3":["\"So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days' journey. [And Jonah began to enter into the city a day's journey]\" LXX: 'so Jonah arose and went to Nineveh, according to the word of the Lord. Now Nineveh was a city of godly size, around three days in journey. Jonah began to enter the city, about one day's travel.' Jonah immediately carries out the command that he has been given. Nineveh to which the prophet was journeying, was a great city, which it took around three days' journey to circle. But he remembers the command he has been given and the recent shipwreck and makes the normal journey of three days in one day. However, there are some people who believe that he simply proclaimed his message in a third of the city, and that his speech quickly was made known to the other inhabitants. And our Lord is said to arise and speak of his own accord after being in hell, and announces the word of the Lord when he sends the apostles to baptise those who were in Nineveh in the name of the Father the Son and the Holy Spirit [Mt. 28:19]. So there are the three days of journey! And this sacrament of mankind's safety is \"a journey of one day\", that is it is finished by the proclamation of one sole God. Jonah preaches not so much to the apostles but more by the method of the apostles. He himself says, \"and I will be with you always until the end of the world\" [Mt. 28:20]. There is no doubt that Nineveh was a city of godly magnitude because the world and all things have existed through God and because without Him nothing would ever have existed. [John 1:3] Note too that he has not said, \"of three days and three nights\" or \"of one day and of one night\", but simply \"and of three days\", and \"of one day\", to show that in the sacrament of the Trinity and of the confession of one sole God there is no darkness.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3"],"JON.3.4":["\"[And Jonah began to enter into the city a day's journey], and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown.\" LXX: 'he proclaimed and said, another three days and Nineveh will be destroyed\". The umber three written in the Septuagint does not agree with the penitence, and I am quite astonished at this translation, for in Hebrew neither the letters or syllables or accents or the word show any common element. For three is said, salos and forty arbaim. Moreover the prophet who was sent from Judea to the Assyrians was to claim after such a journey penitence worthy of his prediction to cure with a long-present dressing his old and putrid wounds. Moreover the number forty is appropriate to sinners, to hunger, to prayer, to sackcloth, to tears and to perseverance in prayer. In this way Moses fasted for forty days on mount Sinai [Ex. 34:28; Deut. 9:18] and Elijah fleeing Jezebel [3 Kings. 19:8] is presented to us as having fasted for forty days after having told Israel about the famine [3 Kings. 17:1], when the anger of God was upon them. And the Lord Himself, the true Jonah who is sent to preach to the world fasts for forty days [Mt. 4:2]. And he leaves us as hereditary fasting to prepare our spirits, by this number of forty, as the food of his body. \"he cried out\": the Gospel shows this expression more fully: \"standing, he cried out in the temple: if anyone is thirsty, let him come to me and he shall drink\" [John 7:37], for all speech of the Saviour is called a cry because he speaks about weighty subjects.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3"],"JON.3.5":["\"So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them.\" LXX: similar. Nineveh believed but Israel did not believe; the foreskin believed, but circumcision remained without faith. First of all the men of Nineveh believed who had arrived at the age of Christ [Eph. 4:13]: they announced a fast and dressed in sackcloth, from the greatest to the smallest of them. This regime and clothing is very worthy of penitence, so that those who had offended God through their indulgence or lust appeased him by condemning all that they had previously offended with. Sackcloth and fasting are the weapons of penitence, the rescue of sinners. First of all fasting, then sackcloth; first of all what is not seen, then what is visible; the one is always shown to God, the other sometimes to man. And if it were necessary to remove one from the two then I would rather keep fasting without sackcloth than have sackcloth without fasting. Elder men give the example which pertains to youths: for no one is without sin; and if his life only lasted one day, the years of his life would still be counted [Job 14:5. LX]. For if the stars are not pure before God, they are still more so than a worm or putrefaction, and those who are held by the sin of Adam, the great offender. Note here too the order, which is well written: God commands the prophet, the prophet proclaims to the city. First of all the men believe, announce fasting, and then everyone puts on sackcloth. The men do not announce the putting on of sackcloth, but only the fasting. All the same, with reason, those to whom penitence has been proscribed wear sackcloth and fast so that empty stomach and mourning clothes give the Lord more of an opportunity to remit.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3"],"JON.3.6-9":["\"For word came unto the king of Nineveh, and he arose from his throne, and he laid his robe from him, and covered him with sackcloth, and sat in ashes. And he caused it to be proclaimed and published through Nineveh by the decree of the king and his nobles, saying, Let neither man nor beast, herd nor flock, taste any thing: let them not feed, nor drink water: But let man and beast be covered with sackcloth, and cry mightily unto God: yea, let them turn every one from his evil way, and from the violence that is in their hands. Who can tell if God will turn and repent, and turn away from his fierce anger, that we perish not?\" LXX: 'the message reached the king of Nineveh, and he arose from his throne, took off his robe and covered himself with sackcloth, and he sat down upon the earth. And by the order of the king and his nobles it was announced throughout Nineveh, saying, it is forbidden for any man or beast or oxen or sheep to eat anything, to drink any water. Men and beasts were covered in sackcloth and cried out to the Lord mightily. Let each one turn away from his wicked practises and from the unfairness that was in his hands, saying, who knows if God will turn and repent, if he will not abandon the fierceness of his wrath so that we might not die?'. I know certain men for whom the king of Nineveh, (who is the last to hear the proclamation and who descends from his throne, and forgoes the ornaments of his former vices and dressed in sackcloth sits on the ground, he is not content with his own conversion, preaches penitence to others with his leaders, saying, \"let the men and beasts, big and small of size, be tortured by hunger, let them put on sackcloth, condemn their former sins and betake themselves without reservation to penitence!) is the symbol of the devil, who at the end of the world, (because no spiritual creature that is made reasoning by God will perish), will descend from his pride and do penitence and will be restored to his former position. To support this opinion they use this example of Daniel in which Nebuchadnezzar after seven years of penitence is returned to his former reign. [Dan. 4:24, 29, 33] But because this idea is not in the Holy Scripture and since it completely destroys the fear of God, (for men will slide easily into vices if they believe that even the devil, the creator of wickedness and the source of all sins, can be saved if he does penitence), we must eradicate this from our spirits. Let us remember though that the sinners in the Gospel are sent to the eternal fire [Mt. 25:41], which is prepared for the devil and his angels, about whom is said, \"their worm will not die and their fire will not be extinguished\" [Is. 66:24]. All the same we know that God is mild, and we sinners do not enjoy his cruelty, but we read, \"the Lord is kindly and righteous, and our God will be merciful\" [Ps. 114:5]. The justice of God is surrounded by mercy, and it is by this route that he proceeds to judgement: he spares to judge, he judges to be merciful. \"Mercy and Truth are to be found in our path; Justice and Peace are to be embraced\" [Ps. 84:11]. Moreover if all spiritual creatures are equal and if they raise themselves up by their virtues to heaven, or by their vices take themselves to the depths, then after a long circuit and infinite centuries, if all are returned to their original state with the same worthiness to all conflicting, what difference will there be between the virgin and the prostitute? What distinction will there be between the mother of the Lord and (it is wicked to say) the victims of public pleasures? Will Gabriel be like the devil? Will the apostles be as demons? Will the prophets be as pseudoprophets? Martyrs as their persecutors? Imagine all that you will, increase by two-fold the years and the time, take infinite time for torture: if the end for all is the same, all the past is then nothing, for what is of importance to us is not what we are at any given moment, but what we will be forever more. I am not forgetting what is often said to argue against this point, preparing hope for oneself and some kind of safety with the devil. But this is not the appropriate time to write at length against the opinion of the wicked and against the synphragma of the devil from those who teach one thing in private only to deny it in public. It is enough for me to have shown what I believe this passage signifies, and as is appropriate in a commentary, to remark briefly who the king of Nineveh is, he who is the last to hear the word of God. Just how much eloquence and secular knowledge are worth to mankind can be seen in Demosthenes, Cicero, Plato, Xenophon, Theophrastus, Aristotle and the other philosophers and orators who are considered kings and their precepts are not taken as the work of mortals but as oracles of the gods. About which Plato says, happy are those states where philosophers rule, or if kings are philosophers. How difficult it is for such men to believe in God! I am neglecting though those examples from daily life, and pass over the stories of pagans and content myself with the text of the apostle who writes in Corinthians, saying, \"look, brothers, to your vocation, among you. For there are not many who are wise about their flesh, nor many powerful, or noble. But there is much madness in the world, and this is what God has chosen to confuse wise men. That which is weak in the world, this is what God has chosen to confuse strength, and that which is in the world without good birth this is what God has chosen…\" [1 Cor. 1:26-8] and again he says, \"I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and I will reprove the knowledge of those who know.\" [Is. 29:14; 1 Cor. 1:19] And: \"see that no one robs you, through philosophy, this is a vain seduction\" [Col. 2:8]. From this the predication of Christ is clear, the kings of the world hear last; then they put down the clamour of eloquence and the beautiful appearance of words, they abandon themselves completely to all simplicity and rusticity, and return to the ways of peasants, sitting in the dirt and destroying what they had formerly said was good before. Let us take as an example the benevolent Cyprian: who is firstly the champion of idolatry, and had such a reputation of good speaking that he taught the art of rhetoric at Carthage. He finishes by listening to the speech of Jonah, is converted to repent and gains such courage as to preach about Christ in public and lays his neck under the sword for him. For sure we know that the King of Nineveh descended from his throne, exchanged his red gown for sackcloth, his perfumes for mud, and cleanness for uncleanness- not uncleanness of meanings but of his words. In the same way in Jeremiah it is said about Babylon that \"Babylon is a golden chalice which makes all the earth drunk\" [Jer. 51:7]. Which man has not been made drunk by secular eloquence? Whose spirit has not been shot through by the composition of words and by the brightness of his elegant speech? Those powerful, noble and rich have great difficulty in believing in God; then how much more so for the masters of speech! Their spirit is blinded by riches, wealth, abundance, they are prevented by their sins and cannot see their virtues; they judge the simplicity of the Holy Scripture not on the majesty of its meanings, but out of the baseness of its words. But when they who have previously taught wickedness are converted to repent and start to teach what is good then we will see the people of Nineveh converted with a single proclamation, and the speech that we read in Isaiah will come true: \"is a people thus born in one go?\". [Is. 66:8. LX] Men and animals are covered with sackcloth, crying out to the Lord, this is to be understood by the same meaning as this: that those who have reason and those who do not, the wise and the simple repent according to that phrase said elsewhere: \"You will save men and the animals O Lord\" [Ps. 35:7]. It is possible however to interpret differently the animals covered in sackcloth, especially according to those passages in which we read, \"the sun and moon will be dressed in sackcloth\" [Joel 2:10], and in another passage, \"I will cover the heavens with sackcloth\". [Is. 50:3] This will be the clothing of mourning, the worry and sadness that are designated metaphorically by sackcloth. And this phrase: \"who knows if God will turn and pardon?\" places us in uncertainty and doubt. Thus men in hypothetical cleanness repent with more intent and arouse even more God's mercy.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3"],"JON.3.10":["\"And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not.\" LXX: 'God saw their works since they turned from their wicked ways. And God repented for their wickedness that he had said he would do to them and he did not do it.' According to the two meanings of this passage God is threatening the town of Assyria and threatens the people of the world every day so that they repent: if they convert then he will change his judgement, and it will be changed by the conversion of the people. Jeremiah and Ezekiel explain this more clearly: the Lord has not fulfilled the good that he has promised to do if the good turn to sinners; nor the wickedness that he threatened the wicked if they return to safety. Thus now God sees their works, since they turn from their wicked way. But he did not hear those vain promises that Israel was in the custom of making: \"all that God has said, we shall do\" [Ex. 24:3.7], but he sees the works. And because he prefers a sinner's repentance rather than his death [Ez. 33:11.] he willingly changes his sentence because he has seen a change in the works. Or rather God has continued in his proposition, since he wanted to pity right from the beginning. No one in fact who desires to punish, threatens what he will actually do. The word 'wickedness' as we have noted above, can be taken to mean supplication or torture, not that God could think to do nothing on account of the wickedness.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 3","But the evil which the Lord does in the city is not the contrary of virtue, but affliction and torment, of which we read: Sufficient to the day is its own evil, that is, tribulation and distress. And in the prophet we read: I am God, who made light and darkness, who make peace and create evils. For just as darkness is contrary to light and day, so war too is contrary to peace; it is not evil in itself, but seems to be evil to those who endure it. And that we may know that in the holy Scriptures evil is sometimes taken not as the contrary of virtue but as affliction, punishments and distress, let us take one more example, from Jonah: And God saw their works, that they had turned from their most wicked ways, and he repented over the evil which he had spoken of doing to them. The evil there is surely the overthrow of the Ninevites, which God had threatened through the prophet, and not the evil contrary to virtue, which belongs to sin, on account of which he was threatening torments to sinners.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"JON.4.1-2":["\"But it displeased Jonah exceedingly and he was very angry. [And he prayed unto the LORD, and said]\" LXX: 'Jonah was saddened by a great sadness, and he was confounded. And he prayed to the Lord, and he said'. Seeing the crowd of gentiles enter [Rom. 11:25], and that fulfils what is written in Deuteronomy: \"they annoyed me with these gods who are not gods, so I will annoy them with a people that is not one; I shall anger them like a foolish nation\" [Deut. 32:21]. He despairs of Israel's safety and is hit by a great suffering which breaks out in words. He shows the signs of his suffering and more or less says this: 'I have been the only one of the prophets chosen to announce my people's ruin to them through the safety of others.' Thus he is not sad that the crowd of gentiles should be saved, as some people believe, but it is the destruction of Israel. Moreover our Lord wept for Jerusalem and refused to take bread away from the children to give to the dogs [Mt. 15:26; Mk. 7:27]. And the apostles preach firstly to Israel, and Paul wishes to be anathema for his brothers who are Israelites [Act. 13:46] and have adoption, glory, alliance, promises and law, and from whom the patriarchs come, and from them too according to the flesh came Christ. [Rom. 9:3-5] But suffering in vain, which is interpreted as the word Jonah, he is smitten by suffering, and 'the spirit is sad until death' [Mt. 26:38; Mk. 14:34]. For lest the people of the Jews should die, he has suffered as much as he was in power. The name of the sufferer also is appropriate to the story, since it signifies the toil of the prophet, weighed down by the miseries of his journey and the shipwreck.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.2-3":["\"[And he prayed unto the LORD, and said], I pray you, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that you are a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and you repent of the evil. Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech you, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live.\" LXX: 'O Lord, is this not what I said when I was still in my country? This is why I made haste to flee to Tarshish. For I know that you are rich in mercy and are kind, patient, and full of compassion, and ready to repent for the evils that you promised. But now all-powerful Lord, take my spirit, because it is better for me to die than to live.' What I have interpreted as 'I pray you' and which the Septuagint has translated as 'O indeed' [[Gr. 'w dh']] is read as anna in Hebrew, which seems to me to express the prayer with a kind of coaxing . For when he had said quite justly that he wanted to flee his prayer accuses the Lord of injustice in a certain manner, and he tempers his complaints by a suppliant and rhetorical speech. Was this not what I said when I was in my country? I knew that you would do this. I am not unaware that you are merciful: this is why I refused to denounce you as harsh and cruel. Therefore I wanted to flee to Tarshish, to be free to think, and I preferred the quiet and rest on the sea of this age. I abandoned my home and left my inheritance, I left your lap and came here. If I had said that you are merciful, gentle, that you pardon wickedness, no one would have repented. If I had denounced you as a cruel God only fit to judge, I should have know that such is not your nature. In this dilemma I preferred to flee, rather than to deceive the repenters with mildness, or to preach things about you that you are not. \"Therefore Lord take my spirit for death is better for me than life.\" [3 Kings 19:4] \"Take my spirit which has been sad even until death.\" [Mt. 26:38; Mk. 14:34] \"Take my spirit. I place my spirit in your hands.\" [Ps. 30:6; Lk. 23:46] I was not able to save the whole nation of Israel by living, but I will die and the whole world will be saved. The story is clear and regarding the prophet's character, we can note as has often been said before that he is saddened and wants to die so that Israel should not be destroyed for ever after the conversion of such a multitude of gentiles.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.4":["\"Then said the LORD, Do you well to be angry?\" LXX: 'The Lord replied to Jonah, are you so much afflicted?' The Hebrew word hara lach can be translated as 'are you annoyed?' and are you afflicted?'. And each one pertains to the prophet and to the Lord: either he is annoyed and fears appearing a liar to the inhabitants of Nineveh, or he is afflicted, knowing that Israel is going to be destroyed. And with reason God does not say to him: 'you are wrong to get angry' or 'to be afflicted', not wanting to reprehend one suffering, nor does he say, 'you have reason to be angry or afflicted', so as not to contradict his former sentence. But he asks him whether he is angry or afflicted so that he replies the causes of his anger or suffering, or even, if he remains quiet, so that God's truth can be proved by his silence.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.5":["\"So Jonah went out of the city, and sat on the east side of the city, and there made him a booth, and sat under it in the shadow, till he might see what would become of the city.\" LXX: similar. Cain who initiated civilisation by fratricide and homicide in killing his brother was the first to build a city, and he gave it the name of his son Enoch. [Gen. 4:17] This is why the prophet Hosea declares, \"I am God, and not a man, amongst you I am a saint, and I will not come into the city\". [Os. 11:9] For the Lord, says the psalmist, is the charge of \"the transition of the dead\" [Ps. 67:21]. This is why one of this cities of refuge is called Ramoth [Deut. 4:43], which is translated as 'vision of death'. Therefore quite justly anyone who is a fugitive and on account of his sins does not merit living in Jerusalem lives in the city of death and is across the waves of the Jordan, which signifies 'descent'. The dove, or the suffering, comes out from such a town and lives in the east whence the sun rises. And it is there in his tent, where having contemplated every hour that passes, he hears what is going to happen to this city. Before Nineveh was saved and before the gourd dried up, before the Gospel of Christ becomes famous and the prophecy of Zechariah is realised: \"here is a man whose name is East\" [Zac. 6:12], Jonah was under his shelter. And nor had Truth come, about which the apostle of the Gospel says: \"God is truth\" [John 3:33; 14:6; 1 John 5:6], and he adds elegantly, \"and he made there a shelter\" near to Nineveh. He makes it himself, for no inhabitant of Nineveh of that age would have been able to live with the prophet, and he was seated under the shade in the attitude of a judge or if you like, constrained by his majesty, \"having pulled in vigorously his reins\" [Prov. 31:17], so that his robe did not fall upon his feet and upon us who are low down, but was held together by a straighter belt. More precisely with regard to what he says, \"to see what would happen to the city\", this uses the accustomed usage of recourse to Scriptures to preach to God about human feelings.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.6":["\"And the LORD God prepared a gourd, and made it to come up over Jonah, that it might be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. So Jonah was exceeding glad of the gourd.\" LXX: 'and the Lord commanded a gourd to grow up over the head of Jonah to form a shade to protect him from his evils. And Jonah was very glad of the gourd indeed. In this place a certain Canterius from the ancient family of Cornelii, (or as he himself says from the lineage of Asinius Pollion), has accused me recently, it seems, of sacrilege for having translated 'ivy' instead of 'gourd'. Apparently he feared that if ivy were taken instead of gourds that there would not be anything to drink in his secret place and his shade. And justly on the veins of this gourd, which are called saucomariae in general, it is customary to paint the image of the Apostles from which this individual has borrowed his name, which is not his own. If it is this easy to change ones name, (after having been the Cornelii, seditious consuls, they renamed themselves Paul Emile consuls), I ask myself why in surprise I should not be allowed to translate ivy instead of gourd. But let us return to more serious matters. For gourd or ivy in Hebrew we read qiqaion, which is also written qiqaia in the Syriac and Punic languages. It is a type of shrub or sapling with wide leaves like a vine, and which casts a large shadow and is supported by a trunk and often is found growing in Palestine especially in sandy areas. It is interesting to note that if the seed is cast on the ground it germinates quickly and in a few days it can be seen to have grown from a seedling to a bush. For my part when I was translating the prophets I wanted to just transliterate the Hebrew word seeing that Latin has no word for this kind of tree. But I feared that the men of letters would find in this some argument, imagining those animals of India or the mountains of Boeotia or even other marvels of this type. I have also followed the example of the former translators who translated it as ivy, in Greek chissos, because they had no other word to use. let us now look carefully at the story, and having looked at the mythical meaning then go on to study each word individually. The gourd and the ivy creep along the ground by their nature, and if they have no restraints or ladders as support they do not try to climb. How is it possible then that a gourd could grow up without the prophet knowing in one night to provide shade, if its nature is not to climb unless it has some supports, reeds or pegs to hold on to? Although the gourd, offering a miracle in its sudden appearance, and showing the power of God in the protection of a leafy shade, was only following its own nature. Even this though can refer to the person of the Lord Saviour, let us not completely abandon our gourd on account of our philocholochunthon, so that we remember that passage of Isaiah, which says, \"and the daughter of Zion is left as a cottage in a vineyard, or as a lodge in a garden of cucumbers, as a besieged city.\" [Is. 1:8] And because we do not find a gourd mentioned elsewhere in the Scriptures let us say then that where the cucumber grows gourds usually grow too. And Israel is compared to this kind of plant because, at a certain time, it protected Jonah with its shadow whilst he was waiting the conversion of the gentiles and made him feel greatly happy. It made more a shady shelter for him rather than a house, and that suggests a roof of some kind but not having the foundations of a house. Moreover the gourd, our little bush, which grows quickly and dries quickly, could be compared to Israel, pushing its little roots into the ground and trying to raise itself up, but is not able to equal the height of cedars [Ps. 79:11] and cypress trees [Is. 37:24; Zac. 11:2] of God. It seems to me that one could interpret the locusts that were food for John similarly, who said symbolising Israel, \"It must grow but I must die\" [John 3:30]. The locust, a small animal with weak wings managing to rise up from the ground but not able to fly very high so that it is better called a reptile yet not similar either to birds.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.7-8":["\"But God prepared a worm when the morning rose the next day, and it smote the gourd that it withered. And it came to pass, when the sun did arise, that God prepared a vehement east wind; and the sun beat upon the head of Jonah, that he fainted, and wished in himself to die, and said, it is better for me to die than to live.\" LXX: 'and God commanded a worm early the next morning, which smote the gourd that it withered. When the sun had risen the Lord immediately commanded a hot and burning wind. The sun hit upon Jonah's head in his distress and suddenly became very exhausted and he said, it is better for me to die than to live.' Before the sun of justice [Mal. 4:2] rose the shade was verdant and Israel was not dry. But after it rose, and when the darkness of Nineveh had been dispersed by its light, a worm obtained for the first light of the next day smote the gourd, (the worm, which is mentioned in the title to psalm twenty-one: \"in honour of the morning incarnation\", and which was born from the earth without any seed, can say, 'I am a worm and not a man' [Ps. 21:7]. And Jonah, abandoned by God's aid, loses all his strength. The Lord ordered a hot and burning wind, which was prophesied by Hosea: \"the Lord will bring a wind out of the desert, which will dry up the rivers and abandon his fountain\" [Hos. 13:15]. And Jonah began to get hot and once again he wants to die in the baptism of Israel to receive in this basin the moisture which he lost in his refusal to do God's word. This is why Peter speaks to the Jews who are parched, saying, \"Repent, and let each of you be baptised in the name of Jesus Christ for payment for your sins, so that you might receive the gift of the Holy Spirit\" [Act. 2:38]. There are those for whom the worm and the burning wind represent the Roman generals who, after the resurrection of Christ, completely destroyed Israel.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.9":["\"And God said to Jonah, Do you well to be angry for the gourd? And he said, I do well to be angry, even unto death.\" LXX: 'and the Lord God said to Jonah, are you so afflicted for a gourd? He replied, 'I am very afflicted even to the point of death'. When he was asked about the repentance of the inhabitants of Nineveh and the safety of the city of the gentiles, 'do you well to be angry?', the prophet replied nothing, yet justified God's question by his silence. For he knew that God is kind, merciful, patient, and full of pity [Ex. 34:6; Ps 102:8], pardoning wickedness and he did not feel sad for the safety of the gentiles; but once the gourd, (Israel) had dried up, when he is asked, 'do you well to be angry for the gourd?', he replies with assurance, 'I do well to be angry and to suffer even unto death. I did not want to save one only to see the others perish, to gain foreigners only to lose my own'. And in truth up until this day Christ weeps for Jerusalem and he weeps until death; not his own death, but that of the Jews, so that they die refusing and rise up again confessing the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"JON.4.10-11":["\"Then said the LORD, You have had pity on the gourd, for the which you have not laboured, neither made it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night: and should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?\" LXX: 'and the Lord said, 'you wanted to keep safe a gourd which has done you no wickedness, that you have not cared for, which was born in one night and died in one night. But should I not spare Nineveh the great city in which live over three thousand people who are unknowing of their right and their left, and an equal number of cattle?' It is too difficult to explain how according to tropology this is said to the Son of man: 'you worry for a gourd that has done you no harm, that you did not plant' [John 1:3], since all has been done by him and with him absent nothing has been done. This is why someone interpreting this passage and wanting to resolve the question which he asked himself, fell into blasphemy. For, if we look at the text of the Gospel, which says, \"why do you call me good? Nothing is good except God himself.\" [Mk. 10:18] He interprets the Father as good and places the Son one place lower, in a comparison with one who is perfectly and completely good. And he has not seen that this opinion made him fall into the heresy of Marcion, who proposes a God that is uniquely good, with another for judging and for creating, rather than the opinion of Arius who proposed a superior Father and an inferior Son yet admits the Son as creator. We must be indulgent therefore for that which we are about to say, and our attempts ought to be encouraged with good criticism and prayer, rather than declaimed by an argumentative audience. Criticism and declamation are easy for those who are most ignorant, but one must be learned and know the labours of workers to stretch out ones hand to those weaker or to show the way to those who are lost. Our Lord and Saviour did not work for Israel as for the people of the gentiles. In this instance Israel declares in faith, \"Look these many years do I serve you, neither transgressed I at any time thy commandment: and yet you never gave me a kid, that I might make merry with my friends: But as soon as this thy son was come, which hath devoured thy living with harlots, you have killed for him the fatted calf.\" [Lk. 15:29-32] And in spite of all he is not reprimanded by the Father, but he says to him kindly, \"Son, you art ever with me, and all that I have is thine. It was meet that we should make merry, and be glad: for this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found.\" The fat calf has been slaughtered for the people of the gentiles, and its precious blood has been spread about, about which Paul to the Hebrews (9 and 10) explains in great detail. And David in the psalm says, \"the brother does not redeem, man will redeem\" [Ps. 48:8]. Christ decided that this people would be great and he died so that they might live; he went down to the underworld so that this people might rise up to heaven. For Israel there is no comparable toil. This is why he is jealous of his young brother, seeing that after having spent his fortune on his prostitutes and pimps, he receives the ring and the robe and recovers his former dignity. The phrase 'which was born in one night' can be applied to the time just before the arrival of Christ, who was the light of the world [John 8:12;9:5], about which is said, \"the night has passed, and the day is near\" [Rom. 13:12]. And this people died in one night when the sun of righteousness [Mal. 4:2] set for them, and they lost the word of God. The city of Nineveh which is great and very beautiful, prefigures the Church in which there is a greater number of inhabitants than the ten tribes of Israel: this is what the rest of the twelve baskets in the desert represent [Mat. 14:20; Mk. 6:43; Lk. 9:17; John 6:13]. \"they do not know the difference between their right and their left\", either on account of their innocence and their simplicity (to show first childhood and let it be known what the number of those is who have reached an older age, when the very young are so numerous), or even, (because the city was great, and \"in a great house there are not only golden and silver objects but also some made of wood and pottery\" [2. Tim. 2:20]) because there was a great crowd that needed to repent and was ignorant of the difference between good and bad, between their right and left. And there is a great number of animals and of men who do not possess the faculty of reason and who can be compared to mad animals to whom they are similar. [Ps. 48:21.]\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 4"],"NAM.1.1":["According to the Seventy translators, in the order of the twelve prophets Nahum is placed after Jonah, because they seem to prophesy about the same city. For it is written in Jonah: The word of the Lord came to Jonah, son of Amittai, saying: Arise and go to Nineveh, the great city, and preach in it. But in Nahum this is the opening: The taking up of Nineveh. The book of the vision of Nahum the Elkoshite. Therefore both weave their prophecy about Nineveh, the metropolis of the Assyrians, which is now called Ninus. Moreover, in the Hebrew, Micah is placed after Jonah, and Micah is followed by Nahum, which means consoler. For the ten tribes had already been led away into captivity by the Assyrians under Hezekiah, king of Judah, under whom now also the vision against Nineveh is seen, for the consolation of the people carried away. Nor was it a small consolation, both for those who were already serving the Assyrians and for the rest, who under Hezekiah, of the tribe of Judah and Benjamin, were being besieged by the same enemies, to hear that the Assyrians too were to be taken by the Chaldeans, as will be shown in what follows in this book. But it must be understood — since Nineveh in our language, from the Hebrew, sounds beautiful; and this world is called beautiful, whence among the Greeks too κόσμος (world) received its name from adornment —, that whatever is now said against Nineveh is proclaimed figuratively of the world. For this reason the taking up, which the Seventy translate λῆμμα, and which Aquila translated ἄρμα, is given among the Hebrews as massa, that is, a heavy burden, because it presses down on her against whom it is seen, and does not allow her to raise her neck. Moreover, as to what is added, Nahum the Elkoshite, some think that Elkoshite was Nahum's father, and that according to the Hebrew tradition he too was a prophet, whereas Elkosh is to this day a hamlet in Galilee, small indeed, and scarcely showing in its ruins the traces of old buildings, but still known to the Jews, and pointed out to me as well by the man who was leading me around. At the same time this too must be noted, that this very taking up, or burden, or weight, is the prophet's vision. For he does not speak in ἐκστάσει (ecstasy), as Montanus and Prisca and Maximilla rave, but what he prophesies is the book of the vision of one who understands everything he speaks, and who makes the weight of the enemies a vision among his own people. Therefore, concerning the consummation of the world according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), O Paula and Eustochium, the prophecy is woven for the consolation of the saints, so that whatever they see in the world they may despise as passing and perishable, and may prepare themselves for the day of judgment, where the Lord is to be the avenger against the true Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.2":["A jealous God, and an avenging Lord. The voice of the prophet praising God because he has avenged on the Assyrians the wrong done to his people. Or, according to a higher understanding, because he hears the groaning of his saints, and at the consummation of the world makes the adversaries feel punishments. That zeal is taken in a good sense, the apostle Paul too shows, saying: Be zealous for the greater gifts, and in another place: For I am zealous for you with the zeal of God, and the Lord himself in the psalm: The zeal of your house has consumed me, and Elijah: Zealous, he says, I have been zealous for the Lord Almighty, the God of Israel. We read also of the zeal of Phinehas, and of Mattathias. And Simon the Zealot, the apostle of Jesus Christ, whom Mark the evangelist calls Simon the Cananaean. But the Lord is zealous for the salvation of those over whom he is zealous, so that those whom his clemency could not save, his jealousy may save. Hence also to Jerusalem, which because of its excessive sins did not deserve the visitation of his zeal and fury, God speaks in Ezekiel: My zeal has departed from you, and I will be angry with you no more. So as long as the world was doing repentance, its consummation did not come about; but after iniquity was multiplied and the charity of many grew cold, so that even the elect of God are tempted, then the zealous Lord comes for vengeance, not that he himself is an enemy and avenger, which is said of the devil, but that his vengeance is hostile, and like fire consumes wood, hay and stubble, so that pure gold and silver may remain.\n\nThe Lord avenging, and having fury: the Lord avenging on his foes, and he himself angry with his enemies. LXX: The Lord avenging with fury, the Lord avenging on his adversaries, and he himself taking away his enemies. According to both understandings, because the Lord loves the one whom he rebukes, and chastises every son whom he receives, he is therefore angry, so that he may take away whatever is adverse and hostile to him, and so that, when the foes and the contrary thoughts are broken and their words taken away, they may return to their former state. Then in what follows he says: The Lord is patient, and his strength long. But since we have once undertaken to expound the history as well, let us understand the enemies and foes of God to be the Assyrians, against whom, though he has long been patient, he will afterwards show himself an avenger with fury and wrath.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.3":["The Lord is patient and great in strength, and, cleansing, he will not make innocent. This is said more expressively in the Greek: καὶ ἀθῳῶν οὐκ ἀθῳώσει (and clearing he will not clear). And the sense is: he was indeed long patient with the crime of the Assyrians, and by the strength of his magnanimity bore their iniquities, calling them to repentance; but since, having despised the goodness of God, according to their impenitent heart they stored up for themselves wrath on the day of wrath, he who before was patient will by no means allow them to go away unpunished as though clean and innocent. Or certainly thus — since we wish at the same time to take what is said in a good sense —, he is patient who upholds all who are falling, and raises up those who are dashed down, who heals the troubled in heart and binds up their bruises, and great is his power, dissolving the enmities in the flesh, and not making the guilty innocent, since even he who applauds himself overmuch will be convicted of having been saved not by his own merit but by the mercy of God. For although he may say: Behold, I have served you so many years, and I have never passed over your command, yet since the Lord is good to all, and his mercies are over all his works; and all have sinned, and lack the glory of God, justified by him freely, he will hear: Or is your eye evil, because I am good? And so it will come about that in the very act by which he reproves and pardons, he allows no one to go away as innocent.\n\nThe Lord, in storm and whirlwind are his ways, and clouds are the dust of his feet. For storm and whirlwind the Seventy translated consummation and commotion, although where we have put in storm, and where in the Hebrew basupha is written, it can also be understood as in commotion. This signifies that at the end of the world all things will be moved, according to what is written in Haggai: Yet once more I too will move heaven and earth, sea and dry land; and when all things have been moved — so that they may believe the ways of the Lord, who says in the Gospel: I am the way, and the life, and the truth, and: When the Son of Man comes in the clouds, which in Isaiah are commanded not to rain rain upon the vineyard, and to which the truth of God reaches, as the psalmist says: Your truth even to the clouds —, then the clouds themselves too, namely the prophets and the souls of the saints, which before had been weighed down by their union with the flesh, will be lifted on high and become of a finer substance, and, brought down to be the footstool of God's feet, will minister among the angels in each of the lowest offices. For they will not be able to know the former and ancient things that pertain to the head. But another will take the clouds in the contrary sense, because they always trouble what is serene, and strive to veil the clear light of the sun and stars with their darkness, and because afterwards, subjected to the Lord's power, they are reduced to dust and to nothing, and the earth in them, which is fat, is dissolved.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.4":["Rebuking the sea, and drying it up, and leading all the rivers to the desert. According to the letter, the power of God is described: since he is about to vindicate Israel against its adversaries, it is no great thing for him to destroy the Assyrians, whose majesty is such that he can even change the elements. Or certainly, since we have said once for all that the prophecy concerns the consummation of the world, take this too in the simple sense. When the consummation of the world comes, and heaven and earth pass away, the sea too and the rivers are dried up. But to me, as I read that passage in the psalms: This great and wide sea, there are creeping things without number, small animals with great. There the ships pass, that dragon which you formed to mock him, it seems worthy of the goodness and clemency of God to destroy by his threat all the bitterness and saltiness of the sea, and to humble the dragon that reigns in the waters, and to dry up the whirlpools of malice in which the small creeping things swim, of which there is no number; for the things that dwell with the dragon are not worthy of number. Leading the rivers also to the desert: all knowledge falsely so called, which, raising itself against God, uses the river of eloquence and volubility of words, and, rolling its swelling whirlpools, is borne headlong to the wonder of those who watch. Look at Plato, behold Demosthenes, and Tully too, philosopher and orator alike, and consider the chiefs of the heretics, among whom were Valentinus, Marcion, Bardesanes, Tatian, and you will have no doubt about the rivers. But all these the Lord Jesus will consume with the spirit of his mouth, and destroy with the illumination of his coming, and will lead to the deserts. And at the same time observe that, according to the title, which is inscribed The taking up of Nineveh. The book of the vision of Nahum the Elkoshite, rightly both is the world understood figuratively in Nineveh, and its sea and rivers of eloquence are dried up at the consummation.\n\nBashan has grown weak, and Carmel, and the flower of Lebanon has languished. LXX: Basanitis has been diminished, and Carmel, and the things of Lebanon that flourished have failed. Μεταφορικῶς (metaphorically), through Bashan, Carmel and Lebanon, a fertile region and planted mountains, the devastation of the Assyrian is shown, because he, once powerful and flourishing and ruling over very many nations, is to be laid waste when the Lord is angry. But we can also understand it of the consummation of the world, that all the powerful and noble, and those abounding in excessive wealth, suddenly perish, and it is said to them: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be? Moreover, according to the interpretation of the names, since Bashan sounds confusion and disgrace, we assert that at the consummation of the world, when the Lord comes, everything that is worthy of disgrace and blushing grows weak, and that not only are sins reduced to nothing, but also Carmel, which is interpreted knowledge of circumcision, and those who seem to themselves rich and flourishing in good works, tremble at the coming of Christ, and that the saying in the Gospel is fulfilled: When the Son of Man comes, do you think he will find faith on the earth? For as iniquity increases, the charity of very many will grow cold, and on account of these things the wrath of God will come.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.5":["The mountains were moved by him, and the hills were made desolate, and the earth trembled before his face, and the world, and all who dwell in it. LXX: The mountains were moved by him, and the hills were shaken, and the earth shrank back before his face, the universe and all who dwell in it. It can also be taken simply: that at the consummation of the world, when the Savior comes in his majesty, the mountains and hills, the world and the earth, and all things are moved. For if at his passion the sun fled, the rocks were split, the earth trembled, much more will all things be thrown into turmoil at his glory. But figuratively too the mountains and hills are to be understood as the lofty and powerful, who at the coming of the Lord will be cast down to the ground, and, deposed from their throne, will cleave to the pavement. The face for of the Lord is upon those who do evil, to destroy their memory from the earth. Then too the earth will tremble, and the world and the universe will dread the face of the Lord, counting it the supreme torment and punishment that they will not dare to look on his face.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.6":["Before the face of his indignation who will stand, and who will resist in the wrath of his fury? LXX: From the face of his wrath who will endure, and who will resist in the wrath of his fury? In place of what we have put: And who will resist in the wrath of his fury? Symmachus translated more plainly: And who will endure the wrath of his fury? Therefore one will be found rarely, or not at all, who is not deserving of the wrath that rebukes. Nor will there be any soul that does not tremble at the judgment of God, since even the stars are not clean in his sight. Moreover, the Hebrew word iaccum, which Aquila and the Seventy translated will resist, let us transfer to that understanding of which it is said, in the second book of Kingdoms (2 Samuel) and in the first book of Chronicles, concerning the wrath of God, in the masculine gender. And no one doubts that there the wrath of God is understood as the devil, and the most wicked angels, who are sent to punish those who are worthy of wrath. So it will be hard to find, at the consummation of the world, one who, spotless and pure, dares to say: Behold, the prince of this world comes, and finds nothing in me, and stands against him with a free brow. But against the Assyrians it must be understood thus: that when the Lord comes in storm and whirlwind, drying up the empire of Babylon, which is interpreted sea, and overturning all its kingdoms, which are understood as rivers, and reducing to nothing its power and fertility, which μεταφορικῶς (metaphorically) are called Bashan and Carmel and the flower of Lebanon, and mountains and hills, and shaking the breadth of its empire, which is called the world, then no power will be able to resist God when he is angry and vindicating his people.\n\nHis indignation has been poured out like fire, and the rocks have been dissolved by him. LXX: His fury consumes principalities, and the rocks have been crushed by him. In place of what we have put, has been poured out, Aquila translated συνεχωνεύθη, that is, has been fused, Symmachus and Theodotion ἔσταξεν, that is, has dripped. Whether, then, the indignation of God has been fused like fire, or his fury has dripped in the likeness of a blaze, so that the hard hearts of men, which are called rocks, might be crushed and dissolved, the indignation of God is useful, which, long bearing our sins through patience, has scarcely ever been fused, and yet does not burst forth wholly into punishment, but will drip on us with a moderate heat. But if a drop of his indignation consumes the principalities, against whom we have the wrestling of battle, what would happen if the whole wrath of God were poured out on us? May Jesus grant that the heart of stone be taken away and changed in us into a heart of flesh, and that, its hardness softened, it may receive into itself the precepts of the Lord which are written: For a sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit; a contrite and humbled heart God will not despise. And that you may know that what has been said sounds of the clemency of God, not of severity, learn from what follows.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.7":["Good is the Lord, and strengthening in the day of tribulation, and knowing those who hope in him. LXX: Sweet is the Lord to those who wait for him in the day of tribulation, and recognizing those who fear him. When he has begun to be angry with the nations and to lay waste kingdoms once most powerful, he will know those who are his, and will not overwhelm those at sea in one and the same storm. But let us understand the day of tribulation, according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), as the day of judgment, of which Isaiah wrote: Behold, the day of the Lord comes, incurable, of fury and of wrath, to make the world a desert and to destroy sinners out of it. Let us hope in the Lord, and through patience await his coming, so that when he comes we may experience him as good, not as a judge, and that he may recognize either those who hope in him or those who fear him. For the Lord knows those who are his.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.8":["And in a flood passing by he will make a consummation of its place, and darkness will pursue his enemies. LXX: And in a flood passing over he will make a consummation; darkness will pursue those rising up and his enemies. The Lord is patient and of great mercy, and he will not be angry to the end, nor will he be indignant forever; but when wickedness has grown upon the earth, and all flesh has corrupted its way, he will bring on a flood that is to pass through, not to remain forever. But he will make a consummation, or an end, of its place, that is, of the flood, so that, just as it is said of the impious man: And I passed by, and his place was not found, and: The way of the impious will perish, so the way of the flood may perish after the wrath of the Lord, with mercy alone appearing. This indeed can also be understood according to the history: when he has laid Israel waste, and has inundated the land of promise as if with a flood, he will make an end of the captivity, calling him back to his former dwellings. But on the contrary, darkness will pursue his enemies the Assyrians, who led the people into captivity. But what we have said of Israel and the Assyrians can be understood of the consummation of the world, and of the saints and of the persecutors, or the opposing powers: that God has mercy on the saints after his wrath; but as for the persecutors and their enemies, who have chosen darkness and not light, the very darkness that they chose will seize them; for they will be sent into the outer darkness: There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The word macoma, which we have translated \"of its place\", all have divided into two parts of speech, so that they turned ma into the preposition \"from\", that is, ἀπὸ, and interpreted coma as \"rising up\". Finally Aquila says ἀπὸ ἀνισταμένων, that is, \"from those rising up\"; the Seventy, \"those rising up\"; Theodotion, \"for those rising up against him\"; the Fifth edition, \"from those rising up against him\". Symmachus alone, agreeing with our translation, says: And in a flood passing over, he will make a consummation of its place. Some of our own interpret \"those rising up and the enemies\" as Marcion and all the old heretics, who dispute against the Creator.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.9-11":["Moreover, since, having been taught by the Hebrews, we wish to follow their tradition too, and to set out for our own, that is, for Christians, an explanation of the history, it must be said: A twofold tribulation will not rise up, that is, the two tribes will not be captured by the Assyrians as the ten were captured. For while the Assyrians are still in the land of Judah, and like thorns embrace one another, glad and rejoicing, they will be consumed by the angel, when in one night one hundred and eighty-five thousand of the enemy were slain. Beautifully, too, he compared the multitude of their army to a banquet of drunkards, and said that the banquet itself was not of roses, not of lilies, not of flowers, but like thorns embracing one another, which are always handed over to the fire and, like stubble full of dryness, are burned up by a slight blaze. But what follows: Out of you will go forth one plotting wickedness against the Lord, turning over transgression in his mind, they want it understood of Rabshakeh, because, having come forth from the Assyrians, he blasphemed God and wished to persuade the people to despair of the Lord's help and hand themselves over to the Assyrians, to serve not God but idols.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.9":["What are you plotting against the Lord? He himself will make a consummation; a twofold tribulation will not rise up. LXX: What are you plotting against the Lord? He himself will make a consummation, he will not avenge twice on the same thing in tribulation. Symmachus more plainly: They will not withstand the onset of a second distress; Theodotion: A second tribulation will not rise up. But according to tropology he is speaking against Marcion, of whom we spoke above, and against all the old heretics, who, inventing some good God or other, say that he is the one who will make the consummation of the world, and accuse the God of the law as if of cruelty, because he punishes many and inflicts torments for sins. What then, he says, are you plotting against the Lord? He himself who created the world will also make its consummation. But if he seems to you cruel, harsh and bloody, because he blotted out the human race in the flood, rained fire and sulphur upon Sodom and Gomorrah, drowned the Egyptians in the waves, and laid low the corpses of the Israelites in the desert, know that he exacted punishments in the present for this reason: that he might not punish forever. Surely what the prophets say is either true or false. If what they seem to say about his severity is true, they themselves have said: The Lord will not avenge twice on the same thing in tribulation. But if they are false, then this too is false that is said: A twofold tribulation will not rise up; false, therefore, is also the cruelty that is described in the law. But if it is true, as they will not be able to deny, since the prophet says: The Lord will not avenge twice on the same thing in tribulation, then those who have been punished are not punished afterward. But if they are punished afterward, Scripture lies, which it is impious to say. Therefore those who perished in the flood, and the Sodomites, and the Egyptians, and the Israelites in the wilderness received their evil things in their lifetime. Here someone may ask, if a believer caught in adultery is beheaded, what becomes of him afterward. For either he will be punished, and this saying is false: The Lord will not avenge twice on the same thing in tribulation, or he will not be punished, and it is something for adulterers to wish for, that by a short and swift penalty in the present they may escape eternal torments. To this we will answer that God, as he knows the measures of all things, so knows the measures of punishments too; that he is not forestalled by the sentence of a judge, nor is the power taken from him of exercising punishment on the sinner thereafter; and that a great sin is washed away by great and long-lasting torments. But if anyone has been punished, like him in the law who had cursed the Israelites, and him who had gathered wood on the sabbath, such men are not punished afterward, because a light fault has been compensated by the present punishment. The Hebrew expounded this passage thus: What do you think, O Assyrians, you who plot iniquity against the Lord, that he himself would consume the people of Israel, that is, the twelve tribes, to the point of extermination? A twofold tribulation will not rise up, that is, he will not hand over Judah and Jerusalem to you as he handed over the ten tribes and Samaria.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.10":["For as thorns embrace one another, so is their banquet of men drinking together; it will be consumed like stubble full of dryness. LXX: For even to their foundations they will be reduced into brambles, and like bindweed wound about it will be devoured, and like stubble full of dryness. The three distinctions which the Lord set down in the parable of the sowing, apart from the good soil, which bears fruit thirtyfold and sixtyfold and a hundredfold, the prophet seems to me to unfold again: one, the seed that fell beside the way, another that fell among stones, and a third that fell among thorns; and the Apostle too, speaking of those who do not build well upon the foundation of Christ, indeed intimates this, that there are some who build wood, hay, stubble. Let us therefore refer the wood to what is now said: For even to their foundations they will be reduced to brambles. But the hay to what follows: And like bindweed that is wound about, it will be devoured. Moreover, the stubble is plainly referred to the stubble of which it is now said: And like stubble full of dryness. For this reason, then, the Lord will not avenge twice on the same thing, because the wickedness that had arisen in the authors of heresy will be consumed down to the foundation and to its roots. And if anyone among them seemed to have a pomp of words, that is, empty leaves that delight only the eye, he will be devoured like bindweed, which in Greek is called σμίλαξ, and will be consumed into nothing. Now bindweed is a plant like ivy, which commonly winds itself around vines and shrubs and creeps a long way. Whatever in them, too, seems to have the show of a crop, but has no ears and grains of wheat, will be handed over to the fire like stubble full of dryness. This is according to the Seventy. But according to the Hebrew, truly the alliances and close ties of the heretics are like thorns embracing one another, and their banquet and their mysteries, since they too say that they have the Lord's table, are a league of thorns, eating together and drinking together alike. For when, drunk from the vine of Sodom, they blaspheme against the Maker with rabid mouth, is it not a banquet of briars? Yet it will be consumed together with its authors, like stubble full of dryness.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.11":["For out of you will go forth one plotting wickedness against the Lord, turning over transgression in his mind. LXX: Out of you will go forth a thought against the Lord, most evil, thinking contrary things. Truly from the heretics will go forth a thought hostile to the Lord. Does it not seem to be wickedness and transgression against God to say, as Valentinus does, that the Creator was brought forth last, as if the abortion of an erring Wisdom? Is not the shamelessness of Basilides a thought against God, and the monstrous name ἄβραξας (Abraxas), which is set above the Lord the Maker?\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.12":["Thus says the Lord: If they are complete, and so more numerous, even so they will be shorn, and he will pass through; I have afflicted you, and I will not afflict you any more; and now I will break his rod from your back, and I will burst your bonds. LXX: Thus says the Lord, reigning over many waters: and so they will be divided, and your report will not be heard any more; and now I will break his rod from you, and I will burst his bonds. According to the letter the sense is plain: Although, he says, the Assyrians are strong, and their might is increased by the number of all the nations, even so, as the angel lays waste, they are shorn. For just as the number of the hairs does not resist the sharp shears, so too the number of God's adversaries will be removed by an easy cutting down, and Asshur will pass through: either he will cease to exist, or, his army laid waste, he will return to his homeland, leaving you safe. And again the word is directed to Judah and Jerusalem: I have afflicted you, and I will not afflict you any more, not that he promises perpetual security, but only for that time, and from those enemies by whom it was then besieged.\n— Commentary on Nahum","But according to the LXX the sense is far different. For he still seems to be speaking against those to whom he had said above: What are you plotting against the Lord? and: Out of you will go forth a thought against the Lord, most evil, thinking contrary things. Thus, then, says the Lord, reigning over many waters, that is, over the powers, which are called the waters above the heavens, and which are commanded to praise the Lord, or else over the understandings and the wisdom and the teachings of God. For just as rivers will flow from the belly of the righteous man, and abundant springs unto eternal life, through the various and manifold meanings over which the word of the Lord rules, so the heresiarchs too have their own waters, over which they rule, and which first flowed from their spring. But what follows: And so they will be divided, can be understood either of the heavenly powers that serve God on high, in that each one is in its own office and ministry, or of the manifold variety of wisdom; lest, because he had said: Reigning over many waters, the number of meanings should be thought confused and undistinguished, but rather that each statement has meanings divided among themselves, and separate matters and its own ὑποθέσεις (subjects). For what is said: Your report will not be heard any more, is a rebuke against those who had thought things contrary to God: that, once the sophisms and snares by which the people of God was entangled have been exposed, their word may no longer run its course, nor be received by the peoples.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.13":["Finally he adds: And now I will break his rod, that is, the Assyrian's, from your back, and I will burst your bonds; either signifying his power by a metaphor, or else the rod with which he was trying to strike, and the bonds which he was preparing for the captives; although the siege of the shut-in multitude can also be taken for the bonds.\n— Commentary on Nahum","But this too, which he adds: And now I will break his rod from you, and I will burst your bonds, is said on behalf of the very ones against whom the threat is made, that they may by no means be beaten by the devil, nor be subject to him at whose instigation they had devised and fabricated such great things. Therefore his dominion over them will be broken, and the bonds by which the souls of sinners were bound will be burst by the word of God, saying to those who are in bonds: Go out.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.14-15":["But according to the Seventy translators, join what follows to what precedes: It shall not be sown, he says, from your name any more. By no means, O heretics, will the souls of the deceived take from your dogmas the names which they had previously invoked upon their lands, as is sung figuratively in the forty-eighth (49th) psalm. And this very cessation of sowing will profit you, a sowing which used to kill first the soul of the sower, then that of him in whom he sowed. The dogmas of errors, therefore, will die for you; and you too, who before seemed to yourself to be alive, will die to error, and, dead for your own good, you will have as your grave the idols which you used to worship. And so it will come about that from your breast, which before had been the temple of your god, whom you had feigned, all errors will be taken away. And these things will happen to you, who once used to think things contrary to the Lord, when the word of God, which always ascends upon the mountains, that is, into lofty and sublime souls, has come swiftly to you, and, having trodden down the waves of your former errors and brought them to calm, has restored to you the peace and the sense of faith. Pardon my prolixity; for in following both the history and the tropology I cannot take in both briefly, especially since I am tormented by the variety of the translations, and am sometimes compelled, against my conscience, to weave together the sequence of the common edition.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.14":["And the Lord will command against you: It shall not be sown from your name any more. From the house of your god I will slay the graven and the molten image; I will make your grave, for you are without honor. LXX: And the Lord will command concerning you: It shall not be sown from your name any more. From the house of your god I will destroy the graven images and the molten images, I will make your grave, for they are swift; behold upon the mountains the feet of one who brings good news and announces peace. I have set down more from the Seventy translators for this reason, that the second περικοπὴ (pericope) could not be separated from the first. For where it says, for you are without honor, for which the Fifth edition put ὅτι ὑβρίσθης, the Seventy translated: for they are swift, which in the Hebrew reads chi calloth. But unless you refer \"swift\" to the feet of the following section, the sentence is left hanging. Therefore, according to my custom, I will sift first the history, and afterward the sense of the common edition. The Lord, he says, will command against you, O Asshur, so that what you are to suffer may not come by chance and without any judge, but you may suffer it at God's pronouncement. It shall not be sown from your name any more: for indeed Sennacherib, as soon as he had returned to Nineveh, was killed by his sons. Read Isaiah: and he was killed in the house of his god, which he had entered to worship. For this is what he says: From the house of your god I will slay: you will be punished from the place from which you hoped for help. The graven and the molten image will be your grave, so that amid the altars and the sacred couches the impious blood of one worshipping idols may be poured out.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.1.15":["If ever there has been a most grievous persecution, such as under Valerian and Decius and Maximian, and the vengeance of the Lord has appeared upon his adversaries, let us say to the Church: LXX: Celebrate, O Judah, your feasts, pay your vows, for they will never again add to pass through into oldness; it is fulfilled, it is consummated; he has gone up breathing into your face, rescuing you from tribulation. I have said once already that according to the variety of the translation the sections themselves are also divided differently, and that the sense of the Hebrew translation cannot agree with the sections. Therefore what is now said is of this kind: O man of the Church, since from the name of your adversaries there shall be no more sowing, and their rod is broken, and their bonds are torn apart, and he has come who should announce peace to you, celebrate your feasts, not in wine and banquets, as the carnal Jews suppose, but in spiritual delights and the torrent of pleasure. O Judah, pay your vows, for never again will enemies pass through you to lead you into oldness, that is, who wish you to bear the image of the old man. For what is old grows aged, and what grows aged is near to perishing: the world is fulfilled, the adversary is consumed. Christ has come to you, who earlier had breathed into your face when he formed you from the clay, and after the resurrection too, breathing into the face of the apostles, said: Receive the Holy Spirit; he it is who frees you from tribulation. For when Nineveh is laid waste and the world passes away, tribulation too will pass away.\n— Commentary on Nahum","Behold upon the mountains the feet of him who brings good tidings and announces peace. Celebrate, O Judah, your feasts, and pay your vows, for Belial will no more pass through you; he has wholly perished. For a little while I shall put off the LXX translators, because among them the very sections are also confused by the variety of their translation. And when I have briefly set out the history, I shall temper their edition with my own words. It is written in the book of Chronicles that while Sennacherib was besieging them, they could not keep the Passover in the first month; but when his army had been slain by the angel, and his flight and death had been announced, they celebrated the day of the Passover in the second month with the greatest festivity. What he says, then, is this: O Judah, you who reign in Jerusalem, do not be anxious, now that your enemy has been killed in the temple of his god. Behold, a messenger comes to you, running over the mountains and hills, and as if from a lofty watchtower announcing from afar that Sennacherib is dead and the world freed from his rule. Celebrate your festivals; pay the vows for the death of your enemy which you promised to God; never again will the transgressor and ἀποστάτης (apostate) pass through you, for this is what Belial means. He has wholly perished, that is, both the army and the king and the empire of the Assyrians have utterly fallen. And this indeed according to the letter. But according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy) it is said to the Church, it is said to the souls that confess the Lord, that the devil, who formerly laid you waste and pressed you down under a most heavy yoke, has perished in the idols and with the idols which he had fashioned: Celebrate your feasts and pay your vows to God, singing continually with the angels, for never again will Belial pass through you, of whom the Apostle also says: What fellowship has Christ with Belial? Because, Nineveh being overthrown, he has wholly perished.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.1-2":["He has come up who will scatter before your face, keeping the siege; watch the way, strengthen the loins, make your power very strong, for as the Lord has repaid the pride of Jacob, so the pride of Israel, because the ravagers have scattered them and have corrupted their branches. I am compelled by necessity to steer the course of my discourse between history and allegory, as between rocks and crags with shipwreck threatening, and to take care that it does not suddenly strike. For indeed, according to the fables of the poets: Scylla holds the right side, implacable Charybdis the left; if we flee the rocks, we run into the deep; if we avoid the twisting whirlpools, we are carried onto the rocks. The Lord is my witness that in all that I discuss according to the Hebrew I do not speak from my own sense, which is the charge laid against the false prophets, but follow the exposition of the Hebrews, by whom I was taught for no short time, and I am bound simply to point out to my readers what I have learned. Certainly it will be at the reader's discretion, when he has run through both, to judge which he ought rather to follow. Now therefore the discourse is turned to Nineveh — and it is above all on this account that the prophets are obscure, that suddenly, while one thing is being treated, the person is changed to others —, and it is said to her: Nebuchadnezzar has come up against you to besiege you, to lay waste the fields before your face, to pursue the farmers, to ravage the countryside, and to hold you yourself shut in as well. Since, then, war threatens you, behold, I the prophet now rejoicing foretell it: watch carefully and look, and observe what is coming upon you. Strengthen the loins, that is, gird yourself; make your power very strong, that is, gather armies, for as the Lord avenged Judah for the pride of Sennacherib, when his army was slain in Judea and he himself was also murdered by his own sons, so will he avenge Israel, that is, the ten tribes which are held in possession by Nineveh. For the Assyrians laid waste and overthrew both, Judah and Israel, and, under the metaphor of a vine, corrupted the branches of both.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.1":["LXX: Watch the way, hold the loin, be strengthened in strength exceedingly, for the Lord has turned away the insolence of Jacob, as the insolence of Israel, because those who shake out have shaken them out, and have demolished their branches. Three things are commanded to Judah. First, that he watch the way and look carefully at the road by which he is to walk, according to what is written in Jeremiah: Stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths, and see which is the good way, and walk in it, so that when we have stood in many ways, we may come to that way which says: I am the way. Next it is said to him that he hold the loin, that is, that after choosing the way he mortify his body and bring it into subjection, lest, preaching to others as a king and master, he himself be found reprobate. It would take long now to tell how the strength of the devil is above all in the loins, and how the promise is made to David: Of the fruit of your loin I will set upon your throne, and that saying of the Apostle: For he was still in the loin of his father Abraham, Levi was, when Melchizedek went to meet Abraham. And how John is girded with a leather girdle, and how the disciples are commanded by the Savior: Let your loins be girded. And the Apostle to the Ephesians: Stand therefore, having your loins girded in truth; for although ἄσκησις (ascetic discipline) and continence of life avail very much toward the mortifying of the loins, yet nothing so mortifies them as the knowledge of the truth. Hence it is said: Gird your loins in truth. For if Christ is the truth, he who has believed in Christ with his whole mind has mortified his loins in Christ. Thirdly it is commanded: be strengthened in power exceedingly. You have chosen, he says, the way; you have held the loin; take up power, so that you may fight with the enemies.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.2":["And lest perhaps you lose confidence, a reason is given you for hoping: The Lord, he says, has turned away the insolence of Jacob, as the insolence of Israel; which is ambiguous: for either the Lord has turned away the insolence of Jacob himself, with which he did injury to others, or he has turned away the insolence which Jacob endured from others. But it seems better to me that the insolence which Jacob used to do to others has been turned away by the Lord. For it is not of such great virtue to endure an injury done by others as it is of the Lord's grace to be calm, gentle and tranquil, unable to do injury. It is asked how the injury of Jacob was turned away, as that of Israel too had been turned away: after Jacob wrestled with the angel, he was found worthy to receive the name of Israel, and because he saw God, he ceased to do injury. As therefore Israel, sense or a man seeing God, and ever thinking of God, does not know how to do injury, so all wantonness and insolence has been turned away from Jacob, that is, from the supplanter, from him who, still set in the contest, supplants his enemies. And that we may know how injury is taken in the bad sense, Solomon is witness, saying: Insolent eyes, an unjust tongue. And how, according to both understandings, the injury has been turned away from Jacob which had earlier been turned away from Israel, the following words make plain: Because those who shake out have shaken them out, and have demolished their shoots, or have corrupted them. The angels, he says, of each one, who daily see the face of the Father, have shaken out whatever dust had clung to Jacob and Israel. Hence Peter's feet too are washed. And through the prophet it is said: Shake off the dust, and arise, O Jerusalem. The disciples too are commanded by the Savior: Shake off the dust of your feet. And in the psalms it is written: As arrows in the hand of the mighty, so are the sons of the shaken out. So the mind prompt to insolence has been turned away from the true Jacob and from the true Israel, because whatever had been earthly in them, and compacted of the lower dregs, has been shaken out and cleansed by the ministering angels, or by admonishers and teachers, who not only shook them out, but also scattered the vices, which soothe the senses with pleasure only for the present, and which are like shoots and branches that are full of leaves without fruit, as the Lord says: Every branch that abides in me and bears fruit, my Father cleanses, that it may bear more fruit; but what does not abide in me and does not bear fruit, my Father will cut off and cast into the fire.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.3-4":["Now let us come to the Seventy translators. Those who shake out, who shook out Jacob and Israel and scattered their shoots, also broke the arms which they had been accustomed to have while they were insolent, and with which they had oppressed all the weak; and they did not do this only, but they also destroyed in fire the mighty men who were mocking. Consider whether you can call the mighty men mocking in fire the opposing powers, which serve the fiery darts of the devil, which, mighty and once mocking Jacob and Israel in fire, had chariots and horses, by which they were borne, urged on, into war in the day of their preparation. The reins of these chariots, then, and the horsemen will be thrown into confusion on the roads and will be dashed together in the streets, when, Jacob and Israel having been healed by the enlightening of the Lord, both the demons and those who serve their will shall be overthrown by the Lord. We can understand these things of his first coming, when the mighty men and the drivers of the chariots and the horsemen said: What have we to do with you, son of David? Have you come before the time to torment us? But since we have once taken the prophecy against Nineveh as concerning the consummation of the world, it is better for us to say that the arms of the devil's power are then taken away from men, and his mighty ministers, who mocked men in fire. For all their hearts are adulterers, like an oven; and we may say that the bonds too by which the captives were led into vices, and the riders of the chariots, are loosed. For the horsemen will fear in the outgoings, that is, in the consummation of the world, and they will be confounded, and the chariots will be dashed together in the streets; for although broad and spacious is the way that leads to death, yet, under the pressure of a time cut short, they will not be able to find a straight road; but they will be dashed against one another, and none the less they will breathe out their old fury, and like lightnings they will run to and fro hither and thither. I saw, says the Lord, Satan like lightning falling from heaven.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.3":["The shield of his mighty men is fiery, the men of the army are in scarlet; fiery are the reins of the chariot in the day of his preparation, and the charioteers have been lulled to sleep. They have been thrown into confusion on the roads; the four-horse chariots have been dashed together in the streets. Their appearance is like torches, like lightnings running to and fro. He will remember his mighty men; they will fall on their roads; swiftly they will go up to her walls, and a shelter will be prepared. The gates of the rivers have been opened, and the temple has been torn down to the ground, and the soldier has been led away captive, and her handmaids were driven along, moaning like doves, murmuring in their hearts. LXX: The arms of his power from men, mighty men mocking in fire, the reins of their chariots in the day of their preparation, and the horsemen will fear in the outgoings; and the chariots will be confounded and will be dashed together in the streets. Their appearance is like torches of fire, and like lightnings running to and fro, and their nobles will be remembered, and they will flee in the days, and they will grow weak on their way, and they will hasten to the walls, and they will prepare their bulwarks; the gates of the cities have been opened, and the royal things have fallen, and the substance has been uncovered; and she herself was going up, and her handmaids were being led like doves speaking in their hearts. According to the history the sequence continues against Nineveh, and the army of the Babylonians coming against her is described. And as for his saying, Fiery reins of the chariots: by the burning thongs he signifies the speed of those making ready, and the pomp, as it were of ἐπισκευῆς (of equipment), of those preparing themselves for battle is recounted. And the Scripture is woven together in a mingled way, now about what Israel once suffered, now about what the Assyrian did, now about what the Babylonians carry out against the Assyrians. It is not, therefore, he says, surprising if they come so swiftly to lay waste, since the charioteers and mighty men, either of Israel before or of the Assyrians afterward, had been lulled to sleep.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.4":["And returning again to the order of the description, he says: So great is the multitude of those coming that the column on the march is mingled together and cannot be told apart. The four-horse chariots themselves too, while they cannot find a way, are dashed against one another in the streets because of the multitude. The appearance of the Babylonians is like torches, like lightnings running to and fro, so that they terrify their adversaries by the sight before they lay them low with the sword.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.5":["Then Asshur will remember his mighty men and will look for those who fell on the roads, and he will swiftly go up the walls of Nineveh, and because of the very long siege he will prepare shelters to ward off the heat.\n— Commentary on Nahum","And when the devil and all his nobles have understood this, they will remember the consummation which had long ago been foretold, and they will flee in the days. For they will by no means move about in the nights, but as the day grows bright the darkness will be put to flight, and they will grow weak on the way, not making progress nor fulfilling their endeavors, and they will hasten to the walls. For so great a terror of the coming Lord will seize them, and so feeble will they be for resisting, that they will flee to the bounds of the world, by which the world is shut in and encircled as by walls, and they will prepare themselves to resist. Just as, if someone flees an enemy, not daring to resist him, when he has come into a solitary place, if perhaps the foe follows, he is compelled by necessity to fight back.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.6-7":["But what does it profit to build a house unless the Lord has built it? What does it help to shut the gates which the Lord unbars? The gates of Nineveh have been opened, which had a multitude of citizens like rivers, and the temple, that is, her kingdom, has been destroyed, and the soldier has been led away captive, that is, all have been led into Babylon. But by the handmaids of Nineveh understand, by a metaphor, the lesser cities and villages and forts. Or certainly the captive women will be driven along before the faces of the victors; and so great will be the terror that their grief will not even break out into sobbing and wailing, but they will moan silently within themselves and with a dark murmur swallow their tears, after the manner of cooing doves. These things according to the Hebrew tradition.\n— Commentary on Nahum","But while they are thinking this, all the things which had been held and possessed by them will be brought out into the open, and the gates which they had shut will be opened, and their kingdoms will fall, and the substance, that is, the riches, will be uncovered. But the substance of the world itself and all her handmaids, after they have subjected themselves to Christ and have begun to serve him, will be led glad and rejoicing, and believing from the inmost heart of confession, so that they are compared to the cleanness of doves, they will murmur or speak in their hearts. And then will be fulfilled what is said in the sixty-seventh (68th) psalm of the victory of the Savior: Ascending on high, he led captivity captive.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.8-9":["And Nineveh: like a pool of waters are her waters, but they have fled. Stand, stand, and there is none who turns back; plunder the silver, plunder the gold, and there is no end of riches from all the desirable vessels. LXX: And Nineveh: like a pool of waters are her waters, and they, fleeing, did not stand, and there was none who looked back; they were plundering the silver, they were plundering the gold, and there was no end of her adornment; she has been weighed down above all the vessels of her concupiscence. It is plain that, when the cities of Nineveh — which Scripture calls her daughters — have been led into captivity, Nineveh herself, who had nourished such great peoples that they were compared to the waters of pools, has a useless multitude, since there is no one to resist and to bear the onslaught of the Babylonians rushing in. For she had peoples who only fled, and while their mother cried out: Stand, stand, shut the gates, mount the walls, fight back against the enemy, there was no one who turned back, no one who looked back to his mother, but all, turning their backs, deserted the city as plunder for the enemy. Hence it is said to the Babylonians, since those have fled: Plunder the silver, and make spoil by a sudden ravaging of the wealth gathered over so long a time. For there is no end of the riches, the furniture and the vessels which are stored in Nineveh, nor can you carry off as much as she offers herself to be plundered.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.8":["But since we have once said that Nineveh is the beautiful one, that is, the world, let us see what the pool of the world is. Scripture does not say that the waters of Nineveh are like the waters of the sea, nor like the waters of rivers, nor like the waters of springs, nor like the waters of wells, but like the waters of a pool, so that, just as in Jeremiah the people is rebuked which forsook the fountain of living water and dug for itself broken cisterns that cannot hold water, so in Nineveh too all those waters are such as have fallen from heaven and, leaving their ancient height, have slipped down to the depths. For all the dogmas of this world which are outside the fountain of the Church and the sealed garden, and cannot say: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God, nor are of those waters which above the heavens praise the name of the Lord, though they seem great, are nevertheless small and shut in within a narrow bound. And let it not disturb anyone that we take the pool in the bad sense, when that pool to which Isaiah the prophet, son of Amoz, is commanded to go up is taken in the good sense. For there it is said with an addition: The pool of the aqueduct, and the pool of the fuller, which is accustomed to wash away filth and to cleanse the stains of garments. And because it is set on high, the prophet is therefore commanded to go up to it and, in meeting the king, to promise victory over the two burnt firebrands. There follows: Fleeing, they did not stand, namely the inhabitants of Nineveh. First, indeed, they ought not to have fled from God; then, even if they had fled, they ought at some time to stand. For there is a great difference between him who fled and stood, and him who, fleeing, never stood. For he who stands has ceased to flee; he who does not stand has gone forward in perpetual flight. In so great a crowd of those fleeing, therefore, there was no one who looked back and did repentance and heard the Lord speaking: Return to me, O sons, returning, and I will heal your bruises. Hence the saint too says in the psalms: Flight has perished from me. I think that this very thing is also signified by the mystery of leprosy, concerning which it is said in Leviticus: When the leper has been separated by the priest outside the camp, if the leprosy has stood still, the man is clean, and he who had been cast out as a leper is cleansed and returns to the camp and dwells among the people. But if, he says, the leprosy has spread, that is, has not stood still but has grown and made progress in evil and changed the color of its former health, then the leprosy is most plainly proved by him who has the knowledge of inspecting and purging leprosy. But we too are commanded by the true Solomon to dwell in Jerusalem and never to go out from it. And if what was formerly subject to us flees from us and goes to the Philistines, let us not go out of the walls of our city nor follow the footsteps of the fugitives; lest, while we wish to save those who flee, we ourselves perish; but rather let us let them go, that the dead may bury their dead, and, while we may, let us pluck out and cut off from ourselves the eye, the hand and the foot that cause us to stumble.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.9":["And as for what is said: They were plundering the silver, they were plundering the gold, and there was no end of her adornment: she has been weighed down above all the vessels of her concupiscence, it is said of the waters of Nineveh, and of those fleeing who had fled and did not stand, nor was there one who looked back; and who, not content only to have fled and not to have looked back, were plundering the silver besides, whatever eloquence was seen in the world; they were plundering the gold, whatever was splendid in the way of maxims in the learning of the age, in order to adorn Nineveh, in order to compose their dogmas with every flower of thoughts and words. Because of this Nineveh has been weighed down above all the vessels of her concupiscence; for the more she had a great possession of gold and silver and varied furniture, which was heavy, the more she herself was weighed down, since she loved heavy things. Hence also iniquity in Zechariah sits upon a talent of lead, and the Egyptians, who were heavy with sins, sank in the sea like lead. And in the person of the sinner it is said in the psalm: Like a heavy burden they have weighed heavily upon me. And Peter, who before, being light, trod the waves with suspended step, after he was weighed down by unbelief and was being swallowed by the billows, is lifted up by the hand of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.10":["She has been scattered and torn and rent apart, and the heart is melting, and there is a loosening of the knees and a failing in all the kidneys, and the faces of all are like the blackness of a pot. LXX: A shaking out, a shaking out again, and a boiling up, and a breaking of heart and a loosening of knees, and pains upon every loin, and the faces too of all like the scorching of a pot. Under the metaphor of a captive woman, Nineveh is described as scattered, torn, rent: her heart melting, her knees loosened, and her kidneys broken, and the faces of all her inhabitants, through terror of the enemy and the greatness of their fear, appear scorched like pots, wasted, and disfigured with pallor. Moreover, according to the LXX we must understand at a higher level what shaking out, shaking out again signifies. He who is of the people of God and, being a man, has at some time sinned, and returns to his former state, is told, as Jerusalem is: Shake off the dust, and rise up, O Jerusalem. And of him who is such that, after he has been shaken out, he deserves to pass into the arrows of God and to be drawn by the Lord against the enemies, it is sung: As arrows in the hand of the mighty, so are the sons of the shaken out. And when his footprints, as of one walking upon the earth, have been sprinkled with dust, he will hear from the Savior: Shake off the dust of your feet for a testimony to them, no doubt meaning those who refused to receive the preachers. But he who is of Nineveh, which has been weighed down above all the vessels of her concupiscence, is shaken out not once but often. And after he has been shaken out again, and his surface has been cleansed — lest any filth remain within —, there comes to him also a boiling up, which in Greek is more expressively called ἐκβρασμὸς; for ἐκβρασμὸς is properly used of things of this kind, when what lay hidden within breaks out upon the surface. Hence also the pimples which arise on the lips after an illness are called ἐκβράσματα, and it seems a sign of health that the disease has broken out onto the surface. And not only is this remedy applied to Nineveh, that she be shaken out frequently and the hidden sickness be forced to come out of her vitals, but a breaking of heart and a loosening of the knees are also foretold, so that, just as the hard and stony heart of Pharaoh was broken, which, as long as he had it, would not let the people of God go, so the broken heart of Nineveh too may be softened and changed into one of flesh, and the stiff knees which before were not bent to God may be loosened and bow to God, from whom all fatherhood in heaven and on earth is named, and in the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those in heaven, on earth and in the underworld. So that, after they have come to know their creator, they may hear: Be strengthened, you loosened hands, and make strong the feeble knees. And pains, he says, upon every loin. We have already said above that sexual union is signified by the loin, and that all the works which pertain to intercourse are shown by the name of the kidneys. Great, therefore, will be the pains in the kidneys at the consummation, because all the strength of the dragon is in the loins; and in return for all these things on account of which there came before the shaking out, the shaking out again, the boiling up, and the breaking of heart, the loosening of knees, the pains of the kidneys, the face of all will be like the scorching of a pot, so that it is either close to fire and burning, or, losing the sheen of oil, grows black like coals and is covered with eternal confusion, from which the saint is far removed when he says: The light of your countenance, O Lord, is sealed upon us. For with unveiled face he beholds the glory of God. And I think that, just as other is the glory of the sun, other of the moon, other of the stars, and star differs from star in brightness, so in the resurrection of the dead there will be a great distance both of brightness among the saints and of blackness among the sinners.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.11-12":["Where is the dwelling of the lions, and the pasture of the lions' whelps, to which the lion went, that the lion's whelp might enter there, and there is none to frighten them? The lion took enough for his whelps, and killed for his lionesses; and he filled his caves with prey, and his den with plunder. For caves and lionesses the Seventy translated nests and whelps; in the rest the sense is the same. But this is still said of Nineveh: that it was the dwelling of kings and the court of nobles, to which the lion went, the Babylonian king, that is, Nebuchadnezzar, and the lion's whelp, that is, his under-kings as well, and there was none to resist them. The lion took enough for his whelps, and killed for his lionesses. That is to say, this same Nebuchadnezzar possessed everything by the right of victory, and handed over the captives into slavery to his children and to his cities, or certainly to his wives; and he filled his caves with prey, or, as it is in the Hebrew, his pits, and his den with plunder, filling both his treasuries and his cities with gold, silver, garments and every adornment, so that he might possess what Nineveh had held and Babylon had conquered.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.11":["But since according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), both in Jonah and in this prophet, we have interpreted Nineveh as this world, and according to John: The whole world is set in the evil one, when the world, the dwelling of beasts and the place in which lions used to pasture, has passed away, then in wonder and exultation we shall say: Where is the dwelling of the lions and the pasture of the lions' whelps, to which the lion went, the devil, of whom Peter also speaks: Your adversary the devil, like a roaring lion, goes about seeking whom he may devour, and the lion's whelp, the antichrist, and all perverse teachings, the contrary word? You have heard, says John, that antichrist is coming; but now there are many antichrists; for there are as many antichrists as there are false dogmas. And before the coming of Christ there was none to frighten them; but after our Lord came into the world and entered Nineveh, he saw Satan falling from heaven like lightning, and Lucifer, who rose in the morning, fell from heaven. For the true Samson came to the Philistines, and while he was going to Timnah, which means its consummation, to take to wife the poor little Delilah from the Gentiles, he killed the lion to which its consummation had come, and when it was dead he ate the honey of sweetness. But the true David too, keeping his father's sheep, seized the lion and killed it; and Benaiah, which means the Lord a builder, went down into the pit of this age, which is wont to cool hot waters, and killed the lion. And in the vision of Isaiah which is seen against the four-footed beasts, it is first said of the distress of these beasts: In tribulation and distress the lion and the lion's whelp.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.12":["This lion, before he was killed by Christ, took very many as prey for his whelps, and killed for his young lions, his attendants, that is, the demons. Look at the conventicles of the heretics, and you will not have to seek the many taken by the lion. Consider the dead who have left life behind, and you will not call their churches the sheepfold of the shepherd, but the caves of the lion, which he has filled with corpses and the blood of the dead. You have made, says David, darkness, and it became night; in it all the beasts of the forest will pass through. The lions' whelps, to snatch and to seek their food from God. The lion is hard to find by day, but he always prowls in the nights, to snatch from the Church of Christ, so that, according to Habakkuk, he may be sated with choice foods. Finally, Judas was of the sheepfold of Christ, and, snatched by the lion, he was strangled with a noose. And that prophet too, whom the Lord had commanded not to eat bread in that region where the golden calves were, and a lying religion, was struck by a lion because he ate. Jeremiah also speaks of sinners: A lion from the forest struck them, and a wolf destroyed them even to their houses; and a leopard kept watch over their cities. All who go out from them will be taken. In this, note that no one is taken except one who has gone out from the cities of God. In the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) too, those dwelling in the cities of Samaria and not knowing the judgment of the God of the land were killed by lions, until they learned to worship God, and they were freed from the lions. For this reason, I think, whatever has been taken by beasts is not offered in the sacrifices of God, and the prophet says: What is unclean and taken by a beast has not entered my mouth. The lion, therefore, has been killed, and, false dogmas having been refuted, out of the eater came forth food, and out of the strong came forth sweetness.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.2.13":["Behold, I am against you, says the Lord of hosts, and I will burn your chariots even to smoke; and the sword will devour your whelps, and I will cut off your prey from the earth, and the voice of your messengers will be heard no more. LXX: Behold, I am against you, says the Lord Almighty, and I will burn your multitude in smoke, and the sword will devour your lions, and I will take away your prey from the earth, and your works will be heard no more. O Nineveh, all that has been said you will suffer, and I am its author. I the Lord will burn your chariots even to smoke and consumption, and I will cause all your nobles and under-kings to be devoured by the sword. No longer will you lay waste lands, nor exact tributes, nor will your emissaries be heard throughout the provinces; or else, I will no longer hear the angels who are your governors interceding for you. But these very things are also said to the world, in which is the multitude that goes by the broad and spacious way to death, the multitude which the Lord threatens, once set on fire, to be suffocated by the smoke of its own malice, because of its excessive vanity. The sword also, the word of God, living and sharp and filed on every side, will devour the lions; and he promises, moreover, that he will take away its prey from the earth, so that no one may any longer be taken in Nineveh, and as one merciful he takes away the evil works and suppresses them, so that their voice and sound can be heard no more. Hence he says: And your works will be heard no more.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.1":["Woe, city of bloods, all full of the laceration of falsehood; plunder will not depart from you. The voice of the whip, and the voice of the onrush of the wheel, and of the snorting horse, and of the seething chariot, and of the horseman going up, and of the flashing sword, of the gleaming spear, and of the multitude slain and of heavy ruin; and there is no end of corpses, and they will fall upon their own bodies. Because of the multitude of the fornications of the harlot, beautiful and pleasing and having sorceries, who sold nations in her fornications, and families in her sorceries. LXX: O city of bloods, all lying, full of iniquity, the hunt will not be handled; the voice of whips, and the voice of the commotion of wheels, and of the pursuing horse, and of the bearing chariot, of the horseman going up, and of the shining sword, and of the gleaming arms, and of the multitude of the wounded and of heavy ruin, and there will be no end to her nations, and they will grow weak in their bodies from the multitude of fornication. A harlot beautiful and pleasing, leader of sorceries, who sells nations in her fornication and tribes in her sorceries. Where we have put, full of laceration, in the Hebrew there is pherec malea, which Aquila translated ἐξαυχενισμοῦ πλήρης, that is, full of stiff-neckedness; but Symmachus ἀποτομίας πλήρης, which we can render full of cruelty, or of severity. In another edition of his I found μελοκοπίας πλήρης, that is, of cuttings of flesh and of pieces cut up limb by limb; and then he immediately added: where there is ceaseless plunder. Moreover, the Hebrew interpreted pherec not as stiff-neckedness, for which in Aquila's edition we find ἐξαυχενισμὸν, but as a rudder, that is, κυβερνισμὸν, to show both that the city had been royal and that, as on a ship, it had held the rudder of all the nations. Now its power, that is, Nineveh's, is described, and under a lament it is convicted of cruelty. Woe, city of bloods, in which there is no truth, but all is falsehood, full of plunder and of the laceration of prey.\n— Commentary on Nahum","These things have been said of Nineveh in the simple sense. But if, on account of the name of beauty, we have reasonably understood Nineveh as the world, rightly the world, which is set in evil, is called a city of bloods, because of the multitude of archers and of those who kill men with their tongues as with swords. Hence, consequently, it is also all lying, which refers to the perversity of the teachings, since the word of God has nowhere in it to lay its head, while perverse dogmas possess everything. There is none who understands or seeks God; all have turned aside, together they have become useless; there is none who does good, there is not even one. Although these things happen in part even now, yet they are fulfilled more at the consummation, when, iniquity having multiplied, the love of many will have grown cold. How many are captured by Nimrod, the giant and most savage hunter, because, proud against God, he has entangled very many from his forest in his snares, whose prey, or hunt, will not be handled by many. For he has many attendants, and hunters together with him, who rejoice in his hunting and stand by him after the manner of captives.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.2-3":["The voice of the whip of an empire ever cruel and raging, and the voice of the onrush of the wheel. Let us take voice for sound; the seething wheel, a running about in different directions, and of the snorting horse and of the seething chariot: in all of them voice is understood. So beautiful, according to the Hebrew, and so like a painting is the description of an army preparing itself for battle, that every word of mine would be poorer. For what he says, And of heavy ruin, and there is no end of corpses, let us understand of the adversaries who were slain by them. And they will fall upon their own bodies: either they will fall from their own multitude, while they are crowded together by one another, or they will fall upon the corpses of the slain; for αὐτῶν means both \"their own\" and \"their\".\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.2":["But the voice of whips too is heard in the world, since many are the tribulations of the just, and those who are scourged by them cry out and testify with a tearful voice to the greatness of their pain: when one is seized by a demon, another by anger, which is like madness, another by lust, hatred, envy, pride, the whip of the Assyrian king sounds in them. And in the ills of the body too we understand the whip of the devil, of which it is said to the just man: And the whip will not come near your tent; if ever we see this man rotted with jaundice and outliving his own corpse, another with water under the skin swimming in a swollen body, and, as the limbs grow, the form of the former man shrinking, as we lately saw in the Hydra; that man coughing up certain purulent matter and the wastings of a wounded lung; this one, with the humor dried into stones, feeling the bitterness of urine and the torments of the bladder, let us not hesitate to say that this is the voice of the whips of Nineveh. Granted, some suspect that these things happen either from corrupted air or from the diversity of foods and of bodies. Let us, who read both that a fever was rebuked, and that the woman who had been bound by the devil for eighteen years was cured, with the Lord as her physician, know that all these are the whips of Nineveh. Hence there follows: And the voice of the commotion of wheels, while the human race is swept hither and thither, and in uncertain runnings about through all things we do not know where the danger is, where safety; of which wheel it is written also at the beginning of Ezekiel; in the seventy-sixth (77th) psalm we read: The voice of your thunder in the wheel. Nineveh also has the pursuing horse, whose neighing and hoof digging the ground and heaving breast always desire wars, as the Lord speaks against the devil: From afar he smells the battle, when with leaping and shouting he does not spare those who flee, nor lets those who turn their backs get away, but pursues to lay them low, kill, trample, dash them down. There is also in Nineveh the voice of the seething chariot, such chariots as I think Pharaoh had, which were sunk by the Lord. To this chariot four horses are yoked, namely four perturbations, about which the philosophers dispute, and Maro is not silent, saying: These desire and fear, grieve and rejoice... With these horses and with this chariot Nineveh throws everything into turmoil.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.3":["And the voice of the horseman going up also resounds in her, who, prepared for war by a certain art and by wheeling, advances not without danger to the one fighting against him. This horseman has the sword of speech, sharpened on the whetstone of dialectic and smoothed with the oil of the rhetorical art; he has gleaming arms, as Satan transforms himself into an angel of light, arms which are contrary to the apostolic armor. Nor is it any wonder if there is a multitude of the wounded in Nineveh, since there is a multitude of arrows. And just as the four with which we fight and are protected are the shields of the virtues, prudence, justice, temperance, fortitude, so on the contrary there are four vices, folly, iniquity, luxury, fear, by which we are struck by the enemy. Each of these has within itself sprouting and manifold kinds of arrows, by which wounds are inflicted; and unless medicine heals them at once, there follows a heavy ruin. And would that as many in Nineveh were falling lightly and lightly wounded, as there are those who, through the weight of their ruin, are plunged down even to hell. And there is no end to her nations; her malice has no end, and as many as are the kinds of sins, so many are the nations of Nineveh, which will grow weak in their own bodies from the multitude of fornication. Although this can also be taken of those who, on account of venereal pleasures, are weakened even in body, and with the perdition of the soul also break the flesh which they serve, yet these nations of which we have spoken do not fall, according to the Hebrew, except upon their own bodies, and do not stumble — as Symmachus translated — except upon the corpses of the dead, which have been laid low by the multiplication of fornication.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.4":["Because of the multitude, he says, of the fornications of the harlot, since she fornicated with many nations, and worshiped the idols of the whole world which she had subjected to herself. Beautiful and pleasing, and having sorceries: it means magicians; who sold nations in her fornications, and families in her sorceries, that is, she who had power over all the nations.\n— Commentary on Nahum","In this place the LXX translators, although we have put, according to the Hebrew, Because of the multitude of the fornications of the harlot, wanted, as it were, another beginning, so as to say: For the multitude of fornication, and, the sentence being finished there, then to begin: A harlot beautiful and pleasing, leader of sorceries. For leader of sorceries Aquila and Symmachus translated, having sorceries. Nor will anyone wonder that Nineveh is now the most pleasing of harlots, who has seen so great a multitude of men fornicating with her, and almost all being drawn to love of her by her sorceries and by certain incantations. She sold nations in her fornications, nations which take the members of Christ and make them the members of a harlot, and tribes in her sorceries. For she makes them love what they ought to have hated, and detest what they ought to have loved, so that, when they have been deceived, according to what is written: Evil conversations corrupt good morals, they too may trip up others by the art of sorcery. I have read in the holy Scriptures that sorcerers are also taken in a good sense: And of the sorcerer who enchants wisely. But such a sorcerer enchants for this purpose: to draw back to soundness of mind those bound by love of the harlot Nineveh.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.5-6":["Behold, I am against you, says the Lord of hosts, and I will lay bare your shameful parts before your face, and I will show the nations your nakedness, and the kingdoms your disgrace; and I will cast abominations upon you, and I will heap insults upon you, and I will make you an example. LXX: Behold, I am against you, says the Lord Almighty, and I will uncover your hinder parts over your face, and I will show the nations your shame, and the kingdoms your disgrace; and I will cast abomination upon you according to your uncleannesses, and I will make you an example. Because you have sold nations, O Nineveh, in your fornications, and families in your sorceries, and like a public brothel have spread your feet for all, therefore I myself will come to you to overthrow you; I will not send an angel, I will not entrust your judgment to others. I will uncover your shameful parts in your face, so that what you did not see before may be set before your sight. I will show the nations your nakedness, and the kingdoms your disgrace, so that those who have fornicated with you may themselves despise you, mock you, heap insults upon you; and you will be an example to all who see you. Now all these things are told under the metaphor of an adulterous woman, who, when she has been caught, is brought out into the open and paraded before the eyes of all. This, indeed, the prophetic word in Ezekiel also describes most fully, by a transference, against Jerusalem. Yet they are said more truly and more usefully to the world, to which the true physician came from heaven both to cut and to cure.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.5":["Behold, I am against you, says the Lord Almighty; because I am almighty, I can heal all diseases, and what is impossible for others is possible for me. I will lay bare your hinder parts in your face, that is, my virtues, precepts and words, which you have cast behind your back: though you do not deserve it, I will make you see them. For I had commanded you concerning my words, that they should always move before your eyes, be bound on and hang there. But you, scorning the command of him who gave it, left them behind your footsteps, so that you not only did not do what I had commanded, but did not even deign to look at it. Or certainly I will make you see and understand your errors, which before, when you were borne along blind and headlong, you thought to be virtues. After this I will also show your nakedness to the nations whom you sold in your fornication, so that they may by no means be captured by love of you, but, seeing within the filthy and foul body by whose surface they were at first led on, they may cease to fornicate with you. To the kingdoms also, which are greater than nations, I will show your disgrace, which you yourself brought about, so that you should have it.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.6":["And I will cast abomination upon you according to your uncleannesses, so that, as you are unclean, so you may be seen to be unclean, and may not deceive very many who, cleaving to you before, were made one body with you. And I will make you an example, so that the dreaded likeness of the punishment may forbid the likeness of the sinning.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.7":["And it shall be: everyone who sees you will spring back from you and will say: Nineveh is laid waste. Who will shake the head over you? Whence shall I seek a consoler for you? In the Hebrew the word head is not found, but we added it, so that the sense might become clearer. Indeed, Symmachus translated it thus: And everyone who sees you will withdraw from you and will say. Nineveh is torn apart, who will mourn with her? The Seventy, moreover: And it shall be, everyone who sees you will go down from you and will say: Wretched Nineveh, who will groan for her? Whence shall I seek consolation for her, tuning the string. Whoever sees the ruins of Nineveh, and her set as an example for all, will be terrified and will marvel, and will say: Nineveh is torn apart, who will shake the head over you? That is, who will grieve over you, who will be able to be your consoler? You who, as long as you were powerful, like a cruel mistress, had no pity on the old man, nor regarded the little child, nor prepared for yourself a companion in your mourning, you who would have no partner in reigning. But he who despises these earthly things, and has scorned the sorceries of Nineveh the poisoner, and has not been ensnared by her false beauty, when he has seen all her foulness within and has begun to hate what the rest love, will flee and spring back from her, or, as is said by the Seventy, will go down. For as long as we honor earthly things and think them lofty, we are, as it were, once at the pinnacle of pride, and we admire the beauty of Nineveh. But when we have considered her nature, and have despised all bodily goods as lowly, subjecting ourselves to the power of the hand of God, then we shall pity Nineveh, and judge all earthly goods worthy of lamentation, and we shall say: Wretched Nineveh, how many have been ensnared by your snares, how many you hold bound with your chains. Who, do you think, will spring back from you and go down from your pride, and judge you wretched? Now what he says, who, we ought to take not so much as meaning what is difficult, as what is rare, as we have often said: Who, do you think, is the faithful and prudent steward? and: Who is wise, and he will understand these things? and: Who will ascend into the mountain of the Lord? Who, then, will groan over Nineveh? Who can be found who, weighed down by this tabernacle, may say with Paul: Wretched man that I am, who will deliver me from the body of this death? We see daily that if death has come near to anyone, and he has understood that he is being taken away from this world by fever, or by a wound, or by any kind of disease, he panics, he trembles, and, shaking in his whole body, he clings to the embraces of Nineveh and is scarcely torn away from the body of the beautiful harlot. Now what follows, Whence shall I seek consolation, or a consoler for you, who may tune the string? It is still said in the person of him who will spring back or go down from Nineveh, and will say: Wretched Nineveh, who will groan for her? He is discoursing on the confused way of life of this age, in which nothing can please anyone for ever; but what pleased displeases, and what had displeased pleases again. Who, then, will be able to be found as such a consoler? And, so to speak, a lyric writer and a lyre-player who could draw her discordant strings into one harmony, and make of them a concert of voices in the praises of God? This which we have expounded, who may tune the string, or tuning the string, which in Greek is said ἁρμόσαι χορδὴν, we have found neither in the Hebrew nor in the other translators, but in its place the beginning of another passage: Are you better than Amon, which dwells among the rivers? Hence it seems to me that it should rather be joined with what follows.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.8-12":["But if we wish to interpret Ammon of the sons of Lot, let us say that Lot had two sons by his two daughters, Moab and Ammon, of whom the elder, Moab, means 'from the father' or 'paternal water', and the younger, Ammon, either 'son of my race' or 'our people'. And I think that, just as to one who had been born of Judah it is said because of his sin: Seed of Canaan, and not of Judah, and in Ezekiel to sinful Jerusalem: Your root and your generation are from the land of Canaan, your father an Amorite, and your mother a Hittite, so whoever were of the former people, that is, of the Jews, and of the younger, that is, of ours, are figuratively called Moabites and Ammonites. And because they turned aside from their father — for Lot means turning aside — they are to be subjected to punishment and will suffer all that we have set out above. But if the severity of God begins with those who were once holy, and she who dwelt among the rivers will be purged by the fire of Gehenna, how much more will Nineveh, which before did not have the law nor receive the yoke of God's commandments, because of her pride at the last fall into the mouth of the devourer.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.8-9":["Are you better than Amon, which dwells among the rivers? Water is round about her, whose riches are the sea, waters are her walls; Ethiopia is her strength, and Egypt, and there is no end; Africa and the Libyans were your help. Yet she too is led away into exile, into captivity; her little ones will be dashed at the head of all the ways, and over her renowned ones they will cast lots; and all her nobles will be fastened in fetters. And you therefore will be drunk and will be despised; and you will seek your help from the enemy; all your fortresses, like fig trees with their early figs, if they are shaken, will fall into the mouth of the eater. LXX: Tune the string, part of Ammon, you who dwell among the rivers, waters round about her, whose beginning is the sea, and water is her walls; Ethiopia is her strength, and Egypt, and there is no end of your flight. And the Libyans became her helpers, and she herself will go into exile as a captive, and they will dash her little ones at the beginning of her ways, and over all her renowned things they will cast lots, and all her nobles will be bound with fetters; and you will be drunk and will be despised, and you will seek there to make a stand against the enemies; all your fortresses are like fig trees that have early figs: if they are shaken, they will fall into the mouth of the eater. Where in the Seventy one reads Tune the string, part of Ammon, the other translators too rendered: Are you better than Amon? The Hebrew who instructed me in the Scriptures asserted that it can be read thus: Are you better than No, Amon? And he said that in Hebrew Alexandria is called No, and Amon a multitude, or peoples; and that the order of the reading is: Are you better than Alexandria the populous, or of the peoples, which dwells among the rivers, water round about her? Not that it was called Alexandria at that time, since it received its name long afterwards from Alexander the Great of Macedon, but because under its first name, that is, No, it was always the metropolis of Egypt and most abounding in peoples. Finally, those too who have handed down to memory the deeds of Alexander affirm that it was the chief city of Egypt. Moreover the prophet Jeremiah too understands Amon, or No, as Alexandria in the vision against Egypt, to which he says: Egypt is a beautiful heifer; a goader will come to her from the north. He also adds this more plainly: The daughter of Egypt is confounded, she has been delivered into the hand of the people of the north, says the Lord of powers, the God of Israel. Behold, I will visit upon Amon Menno, that is, upon the people from Alexandria. For Amon, as we have said, means peoples, men the preposition 'from', and No Alexandria. And I will visit, he says, upon Pharaoh, and upon Egypt and upon her gods; and upon her kings, and upon Pharaoh, and upon those who hope in him; and I will give them into the hands of those who seek their lives, and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and into the hand of his servants. And so it is said to Nineveh: Are you more populous, or more powerful, than Alexandria? And the site of Alexandria is described, which, set upon the Nile and the sea, is surrounded on this side and that by waters and rivers. Water is round about her, whose riches are the sea, waters are her walls. For on one side she is girded by the stream of the Nile, on another by Lake Mareotis, on another side by the sea. And that Ethiopia and Egypt, and Africa, for which in the Hebrew Phut is put, and the Libyans are her protection, the very situation of the provinces and of the city shows.\n— Commentary on Nahum","Let these things be said παραφραστικῶς (by way of paraphrase) according to the Seventy translators: for we have once for all resolved to follow the common edition too, lest we seem to have given the Hydra and Sardanapalus any occasion for censure. However, the example of the overthrow of Nineveh does not seem to me to fit well enough with the sons of Lot, who are called Amman. For first, it is called Ammon and not Amman; then Ammana, which is now called Philadelphia, is not set upon rivers, nor is its wealth gathered from the sea, since it is inland; nor are waters its walls, nor does it have Ethiopia and Egypt and Africa and the Libyans as allies; whereas all these things, as to the example, as to power, and as to the description of the place and region and of the friendly nations, are rather to be fitted to Alexandria; and Nineveh, a most powerful city, compared with lesser Philadelphia, would never hear from the prophet: Are you better? But the one to whom it is said, Are you better, is shown to be less than the one with which she is compared, and that she ought not to take it ill if she is captured, since a greater, firmer and more powerful city, both by the nature of its site and by its brave men, has been overcome by the same enemy.\n— Commentary on Nahum","But since we have interpreted Nineveh also as this world, she is commanded to tune and set in order her strings and to prepare herself for a mournful song; for the part of the sons of Ammon, which had been much better than Nineveh and had dwelt upon rivers, because it was caught in error, has paid the penalty of its crime. And first, as must be said according to the history of Alexandria, Ammon means peoples, and the sense according to the laws of allegory is this: Consider the peoples of the Church, which dwells upon the streams of the prophets, and has teachers round about her, out of whose belly rivers flow, and whose beginning is the sea. For from the reading of the law, which without the wood of Christ is bitter, like Marah, one comes to her mystery, and she has Ethiopia for her strength. For Ethiopia indeed will be first to stretch out her hands to God; and she has Egypt, into which the Lord came on a light cloud; and the Libyans, who before dwelt in parched places and afterwards became her help.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.8":["For what is read in the Seventy, Tune, or set in order, the string, is still said to Nineveh. And the sense is: What is disordered and out of tune in you, and jarring in different directions, tune like strings, Nineveh, for your beauty and your greatness, which you believe you possess in a pre-eminent degree, will profit you nothing unless you tune yourself for singing. For consider the whole part of the lot of the sons of Ammon, and how whatever goods they are reckoned to possess did not defend them from going into captivity and from their little ones being dashed in the ways. What did the rivers profit her, beside which the city of Ammon is set? What did so great a multitude of wells and springs, besides the rivers, beginning from the Dead Sea and walling in her region, profit her?\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.9-11":["Moreover, what we passed over, And there is no end of your flight, since it is said to Nineveh and is set among the things written about Ammon, and seems to be inserted irregularly in a place not its own, let us refer to Nineveh as if by ὑπέρβατον (hyperbaton), so that the order is: And you will be drunk, and there is no end of your flight, and you will be despised, and the rest that is said to Nineveh. And we will interpret it to mean that there is no end of Nineveh's flight from God, because she always makes progress in fleeing and never wishes to stand still, according to what we said above: And they, fleeing, did not stand, and there was none to look back. And we will say that holy Scripture is woven with these difficulties, and above all the prophets, who are full of riddles, so that the difficulty of the language too may wrap up the difficulty of the meanings, that what is holy may not easily lie open to dogs, nor pearls to swine, nor the holy of holies to the profane.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.9":["What protection did Ethiopia and Egypt, once her allies, afford her? As, then, the help of her allies did not aid her, so neither will there be an end of your fleeing, O Nineveh, but you will be laid waste hither and thither. Why should I speak of the Ethiopians and the Egyptians, who were the protectors of the sons of Ammon, when even the Libyans were her allies?\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.10":["She too, therefore, whom my discourse, says the prophet, describes, will be taken by the Babylonian king, and the same man will be the devastator both of you and of her. These things Josephus too, the writer of the Jewish history, relates in his books: Her little ones will be dashed at the crossroads; her nobles will be divided by the lot of the victors, and princes once most powerful will be led captive in chains.\n— Commentary on Nahum","She too, therefore, will be led into captivity; her little ones, because they will not be able to walk, will be slain in the ways, struck down before the faces of their parents, and all her wealth will perhaps be divided by the victors. Nor will any of her princes escape, for they will be bound with iron and fetters.\n— Commentary on Nahum","She too, therefore, if she does not take heed to herself and keep her heart with all diligence, will be led captive and will mourn her sons. Her little ones too, who are still at the beginnings of the ways and have not reached the middle of the journey, will be dashed at their very beginnings, and the most savage enemies will hasten to divide among themselves by lot all her splendid things, and they will drag into captivity her nobles, whom we can understand as princes and overseers, bound with chains and hampered by the heaviest weight of fetters.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.11-12":["And you therefore, Nineveh, that is, unbelieving men, men clinging wholly to the world, will feel punishments and will be stupefied by my cup, since those too have drunk who had been of my portion and fell by their own fault. And you will be despised by me; you will seek an end amid the vices and passions that will press upon you, and nonetheless you will not be able to find a resting place and an end of evils; but all your desires and delights, and the powers of the age, and the dogmas you seemed to yourself to hold most firmly, will be devoured by the eater, of whom Samson speaks in a parable: Out of the eater came forth food, and out of the strong came forth sweetness. For then all your strong things, and those that promised sweet fruits to the eyes of onlookers, will tumble at the first shaking of the tree into the mouth of the devouring devil, by whom Nineveh had always been held and possessed.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.11":["Since, then, Alexandria is to suffer these things, you too, O Nineveh, will drink from the same cup, and will be drunk, and lying stupefied you will be despised, and you will come into such need that you will ask help from the Babylonians, or against the Babylonians, from your enemies.\n— Commentary on Nahum","And you therefore, O Nineveh, will be drunk, and you who were once rich and beautiful, who had so many lovers, will be despised by all, and with your enemies pursuing you will seek rest and will not find it.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.12":["All your strongholds, and your walls stretched up on high, and the heights of your towers, which you now think impregnable, and your brave men and warriors, will be compared to first-ripe figs, which, if they are shaken with a light touch, will fall into the mouth of the devourer.\n— Commentary on Nahum","All your warriors and all your auxiliaries will be a prey to the enemies, and will be taken without any labor, like the early figs of a fig tree, which, when shaken, fall not to the ground, so that there may not be even the small labor of gathering them, but straightway into the mouth of the devourer.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.13-16":["These things I have explained more plainly according to the Hebrew, treading in the very footsteps of Scripture, for the reader's easier understanding. But I will speak also according to the LXX, following the τροπολογίαν (tropology) I have begun, first briefly, that is, as in an epitome, and afterwards discussing each point more broadly: Your people, O Nineveh, that is, the men of this age, who are properly called the people of the Assyrian city, are so unnerved by passions and have so languished in vices that they are compared to the weakness of women; for they possess nothing strong in their souls, nothing robust and manly. Hence the enemies, prevailing against them, have opened all their senses and have entered through the bodily doors. And significantly the senses of bodies are called the doors of the land of Nineveh. But even those who have enslaved themselves to vices have, from God's first creation, an occasion of knowing God, like the stoutest bars, with which they may block and close the gates of the senses. But fire will consume these too, the fire that is winged by burning arrows; wherefore it is said to Nineveh: Draw yourself water, and sprinkle yourself with the word and with reason, and make full use, for fighting, of the occasions of understanding God and of exercising the virtues that are implanted in you. But you, with slackened hands, that is, by the works of pleasure, have lost whatever strength you had in you; therefore turn back, and do repentance, and hold your fortifications again. And since she has once entered the clay, and you are shut up in a body — which, as from earth and straw and water, so is compacted of flesh, blood, veins, sinews and bones — bear the injury and the necessities of the body, and be trodden down by the enemies, and suffer all things that are worthy of repentance for subduing the flesh. For having once taken on the clay and the straw, and being entangled in the empty dealings of this age, you ought willingly to be trodden down through injury; and yet do not utterly despair of salvation: be confident, and bring your body, that is, your brick, into servitude, because of the word you have taken up like water; and subject it to yourself, so that you may rule over your brick; otherwise, unless you do this, a living flame will afterwards consume you, kindled either for punishment through Gehenna or by the burning darts of the enemy, and you will not only be laid waste by fire, but the sword too will eat you, as the locust eats the green things of the earth. And this you will suffer if you are not over the brick: weighed down by your own weight and losing all flight, you will be dragged wholly down to the earth, as the cankerworm suddenly falls to the earth when with wearied flight it can go no further. Have, therefore, virtues as innumerable as the cankerworm, lest you be dragged down to the earth by your weight like the cankerworm. All these things you have suffered because you multiplied for yourself riches and the tradings of diverse dogmas, thinking them brighter than the stars and shining more than the constellations of heaven.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.13-17":["Behold, your people are women in the midst of you; to your enemies the gates of your land will be thrown wide open; fire will devour your bars. Draw yourself water because of the siege, build up your fortifications; go into the clay, and tread it, and working it hold the brick. There fire will devour you; you will perish by the sword; it will devour you like the cankerworm; gather yourself like the cankerworm, multiply like the locust. You have made your tradings more than the stars of heaven are. The cankerworm spread out, and flew away. Your guards are like the locust, and your little ones like the locusts of locusts, which settle in the hedges on the day of cold. The sun has risen, and they have flown away, and their place where they were is not known. LXX: Behold, your people are like women in you; to your enemies the gates of your land will be opened with opening; fire has eaten your bars. Draw yourself water of the siege, hold your fortifications; go into the clay and be trodden in the straw, prevail over the brick. There fire will eat you, the sword will destroy you, and it will eat you like the locust; you will be weighed down like the cankerworm; multiply like the cankerworm; you have multiplied your tradings like the stars of heaven. The speaker's discourse is still woven as addressed to Nineveh. No wonder if your strong men and fighters, like early figs, fall at once into the mouth of the devourer, since your people has become effeminate and cannot resist. Your gates, then, will be thrown open, and the city will lie open to the enemies; fire will consume the stoutest bars with which the gates were closed. Draw water, therefore, and take care that drink is not lacking to the besieged fortress; make bricks, so that you may build up the breaches of the walls: for the siege is near. And when you have done all these things, you will be devoured by the sword as the ground is by the cankerworm. But even when you have been multiplied like the cankerworm, and gathered together into one like the locust, and have gathered your riches like the stars of heaven, then, just as locusts and the cankerworm, and the small offspring of locusts which are called attelabi, fly away when the sun grows hot and are not found, so you will be scattered and will flee. For this is the nature of locusts, that, torpid in the cold, they fly about in the heat. Moreover, the attelabus, which Aquila has rendered more expressively as 'the glutton', is a small locust, between the locust and the cankerworm, creeping rather than flying with its small wings and always hopping, and for this reason, wherever it has sprung up, it consumes everything down to the dust, because until its wings grow it cannot go away.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.13":["These things, as I said, have been said by us briefly so that the sense may be grasped; now, returning to the beginning of the section, let us unfold each point as we can. Who would not say that beautiful Nineveh, a soul beautiful by nature, beguiled by the luxury and pleasures of this age, has come to womanish delights, and, her manliness lost, has languished into a woman? For if the soul of the righteous man, coming to the perfect man and keeping the firmness of its creation, and cleaving to God, is made one spirit with him, why should not, on the contrary, the soul that loves the world become one with the world, and, reduced to womanly softness, lose its manly firmness? I think that for this reason in Exodus Pharaoh commands that every male born to the Hebrews be thrown into the river, and every female be kept alive. For the Egyptian king, who says elsewhere: The rivers are mine, and I made them, could command nothing other than this, that whatever is strong and manly in the Hebrews, and in those who pass through this age, be thrown into the waters and carried down by their streams into the sea. And on the contrary, whatever seemed womanly and soft and comely in this age, that should be kept alive, should grow up and beget. And consider at the same time that the Egyptian ruler cannot kill the men of the Hebrews, nor those who have already come out of infancy, but only those whose age is still tender, and their body soft, and their progress just beginning; he knows that the females cannot be reared unless the males have been killed. So he wishes to drown in the whirlpool of his river whatever is strong and manly in the Hebrews, so that what is womanly may grow up alone more freely. As for what follows, To your enemies the gates of your land will be opened with opening, you will be able to understand it by taking testimony from Jeremiah, in whom it is written: Death has come up through your windows. And what in Jeremiah is shown in the windows, here you will declare is shown in the gates, and you will refer these very gates to the senses. For the divine word, knowing that the senses are twofold, says in Proverbs, to distinguish them from the evil senses: You will find a divine sense. But here you should not take sense for the soul and the mind, which in Greek is called νοῦς, but for αἰσθήσει (sense-perception), from which the five senses also are named: sight, smell, taste, touch, hearing. The gates of the land of Nineveh, then, are understood as the bodily senses; but the gates of the heavenly Jerusalem as every divine sense, and one coming from above. These gates of Nineveh her people opens through sight and hearing and all the remaining senses, as through the broad and spacious way that leads to death, seeking to seize the bodily pleasures against which the men of God close their gates, stopping their ears lest they hear the judgment of blood, closing their eyes lest they see iniquity, stopping their nostrils lest they take in by smell the chief ointments to the effeminacy of their soul, closing the mouth to the gullet and to the greedy belly, and drawing back their hands from soft touch, lest the belly, boiling over into lust, drive the burning soul into womanly embraces. But those who are men of God open their senses, that is, the gates of the heavenly Jerusalem, so that the word of God may enter into them. Of the evil gates this is an example: You who lift me up from the gates of death. Of the good, this: That I may announce all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion. When you see a lover of pleasures rather than a lover of God, and one given over to luxury, say of him at once: He has opened the gates of his land to his enemies; for it is not to the friends of their soul, but to the enemies, that they open the gates of the land of Nineveh. And if those too who are reckoned princes among the people do the same, you will not fear to say of them also: The leaders of my people have been cast out of the house of their delights. But if you see them hampered by pleasures and by luxury surrounding them on this side and that, having no mercy toward the poor and no concern for the people of God, you will fit to them what follows: Who sleep upon beds of ivory and flow with pleasure on their couches, who eat kids from the flocks and sucking calves from the herds; who drink strained wine and are anointed with the chief ointments; and they suffered nothing over the affliction of Joseph. Moreover, what is said, Fire has eaten your bars, is of this kind: If there seemed to be anything of natural good in your soul which, like bars, could hold back and repel the enemies who were trying to break in through the gates of your senses, this has been burned up by the Babylonian fire.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.14":["Also, Draw to yourself water of the fortification, is said of the word of God, so that the soul may surround herself with the teaching and reason of the Scriptures as with a most firm wall, lest the enemy be able to break into her inner parts. Hold, he says, your fortifications. Whatever good you have in you by nature, which preserves in you the beginning given by the best Creator, this, unhappy soul, Nineveh, hold for your defense, and do not let it flow out of the ruling part of the heart. As for what is added after this, Walk into the clay, and be trodden in the straw, another perhaps may think it is said of the soul which, fixed in the clay of the body and the straw of this world, empty, that is, and perishable, turning itself about wholly, is trodden down by demons. But to me this seems to be said to her: Bear the temptations, the injuries to which you have been handed over, the punishments which you suffer by your own desert; for if you walk upon the empty and fragile dealings of this world, know that you are bearing them as a remedy, if only you prevail over the brick and subjugate the flesh to the rule of the soul.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.15-16":["Then follows: There fire will eat you. If you are not over the brick and do not have dominion over the flesh, but remain in the brick, and love the straw, and live in the flesh according to the flesh, not only will the burning darts of the enemy devour you, but his sword too will kill you, and like locusts it will consume with greedy tooth whatever in you seemed green and sprouting of its own accord from the good of nature, and as the cankerworm, its flight lost and weighed down by its own weight, so you too, weighed down by the burden of sins, will be dragged down to the earth. Therefore, lest you suffer such and so great things, multiply like the cankerworm, and as great as its number is, so many virtues must you have. For you who have multiplied your tradings, and of every kind — as if you desired to possess heavenly things — have gathered for yourself by right and by wrong riches that will perish, ought to equal the multitude of sins with a multitude of virtues.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.16-17":["Above, according to the Hebrew, I had come as far as the place where it says: The sun has risen, and they have flown away, and their place where they were is not known. And I said what seemed to me in the context of the discourse itself. Now, because the LXX seem to have the beginning of a sense of their own, I will set down their testimony and follow the order of the explanation I have begun.\n— Commentary on Nahum","LXX: The cankerworm rushed in and flew out; your commingled one leapt out like the attelabus, like the locust that goes up upon the hedge on the day of frost; the sun rose, and it leapt out, and did not know its place. It seems to me that the multitude of Nineveh, without a ruler, mingled together from here and there without order, and rushing wherever impulse carries it, is compared to the cankerworm, a small and innumerable creature, which seems to raise itself only a little from the ground. And the attelabus too, which in Greek is called συμμικτὸς and in Latin has been translated as 'commingled one', we can understand as the ignoble rabble and a people gathered from here and there out of diverse nations, that is, not citizens but foreigners. Hence the people of Israel too, as it went out of Egypt, is related to have had πολὺν σύμμικτον (a great mixed multitude), that is, of Egyptians and Ethiopians and various nations. This mixed one of Nineveh, then, so to speak, is compared to the leaping attelabus and to the locust, which on the day of cold, because it cannot fly away, settles on the hedge, and afterwards, when the sun has risen, warmed by its heat leaps out, and flying away to other regions by no means remembers the hedge on which it had sat in the time of cold. Let these things be said παραφραστικῶς (by way of paraphrase), so that the prophet's discourse itself may be more easily understood.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.16":["However, you will not be able to hesitate to call cankerworm the multitude of men which, going along the broad way, busies itself in the world, when you see them wholly given to the earth, running about hither and thither with fickleness of opinion, and unable to fly to higher things. Look at Rome, and at Constantinople exchanging its former poverty together with its earlier name. See Alexandria, the head of Egypt, and when a riot has been stirred up, either for scarcity of the grain supply or, what is matter for shame and blushing, on account of charioteers and mimes and actors, see the people rush like the cankerworm and, clinging wholly to vices, flit hither and thither through its fickleness and its change of opinion from moment to moment; then truly you will be able to say: The cankerworm went off with a rush and flew away.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.17":["Moreover, as to what follows, The attelabus leapt out, and your mixed one like the locust, I think the mixed one differs from the cankerworm in this, that the cankerworm is compared to the unskilled and innumerable multitude, but the mixed one is gathered from here and there out of all nations. And just as in cities some are citizens and others foreigners, who are pleased to live in a city not their own, so I think that the mixed one who dwells in Nineveh are those who seem to themselves, in their own opinion, to follow some dogmas of truth, and to be better than the cankerworm in this, that the cankerworm does nothing else but stay always on the ground and, without wings, serve its food and its belly; while the attelabus at least takes on small wings, and though it cannot fly on high, still strives to leap up from the ground, and at length, coming to be a locust, it flies about indeed, but its flight is not lasting; for when its little wings fail and are stiffened by the cold, even the locust settles, and settles not on a fruitful tree and on green leaves, but on a hedge woven of brambles and brushwood, or on a dry-stone wall put together from stones picked up at random here and there. Let us consider the wise men of Greece, and of the Egyptians and Persians, and the Gymnosophists of the Indians, and the Samaritans, and the various opinions among them, and the Jews and their Pharisees, and the Sadducees, and the manifold heresies of the Church, and we shall see the attelabus lifting itself a little from the ground, and the locust flying indeed, but not at full course, and, because it does not have the heat of the sun of righteousness, its love toward God growing cold, sitting in thorny hedges. For all their dogmas, since they are cold and cannot fly, find a seat and rest for themselves among the thickets of Aristotle and Chrysippus. From there Eunomius brings forth: What was born did not exist before it was born. From there Manichaeus, to free God from the creation of evils, introduces another author of evil. From there Novatus withdraws pardon, so as to take away repentance. And to sum up everything at once in a brief word, all the dogmas draw the streams of their argumentations from those springs, so that they have even entitled the very places from which arguments are taken τοπικὰ (Topics). This locust, then, which now sits in the hedges, when the time of judgment has come and the world has grown warm at the rising of the sun, will leave its seat and its places to which it clung in the time of cold, and, turned to better things, will not remember its former seat. But what we have said generally of the time of judgment can be understood in part even now: that through learned and erudite men the light of the sun of righteousness may rise for locusts of this kind, and that, leaving their thorns, they may pass into a pure and free flight.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.18-19":["But according to the Hebrew as well, before we discuss the edition of the Seventy — for in them the sense is far other and different —, we ought to climb a little from the history to the heights, and to teach that in the last prophecy of Nahum an ἀποστροφὴ (apostrophe) is made to the devil, the great sense, the prince of the Assyrians, who once said boastfully: By strength I will do it, and by the wisdom of understanding I will take away the bounds of the nations, and I will feed upon their strength, and I will shake the cities that are inhabited, and that it is said to him: O Lucifer, who rose in the morning, who sent your sparks to all the nations, how have you fallen to the earth and been crushed? Nineveh, your beautiful and mighty city, has been destroyed, in which you presumed so great an empire for yourself that you dared to say even to the Son of God: All these things have been delivered to me; if you fall down and worship me, I will give them to you. Your shepherds and petty kings have slumbered, who did not feed men unto salvation, but nourished them for your slaughter, so that you might devour fatter victims. All your people, and the throng of peoples that once worshiped you, has deserted you and your city, and has fled to the mountains, and has placed itself under the hiding places of the apostles of Christ and of the teachers; and meanwhile there is none of your leaders to call back to himself the throng that was once yours. Your stroke and your wound have resounded through the whole world; all who had once been deceived by your supplanting have insulted you; for there is either no one, or scarcely anyone, whom you have not at some time deceived, and through whom your malice has not passed. And it must be noted that the one in whom the malice of the devil has stood fast cannot insult his ruin and wound, since he is of the shepherds and the people of the Assyrian king; but the one through whom it has passed insults him, and with good and upright works, as with hands, claps over him. And fittingly, according to the Hebrew, up to this point it has been spoken of the ruin of the world, and last of all the stroke and wound of the devil himself too is proclaimed, who was the prince of the world: For the world is set in the evil one. But in the Seventy it is still said to the σύμμικτον, that is, to the mixed one of the world, that its shepherds have slept, and that, so that they might sleep, they have been lulled to sleep by the Assyrian king; and so it comes about that, while in them is described what the Assyrian practiced among others, and not what he himself suffers, nothing is said of the wound and stroke and slaying of the devil.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.18":["Your shepherds have slumbered, king of Asshur; your princes will be buried, your people is scattered upon the mountains, and there is none to gather them. Your crushing is not obscure, most grievous is your stroke. All who have heard the report of you have clenched the hand over you, for upon whom has your malice not passed continually? LXX: Woe to them. Your shepherds have slumbered, the king of Asshur has lulled your mighty ones to sleep, your people has gone away upon the mountains, and there was none to receive them, and there is no healing for your crushing; your stroke swells. All who have heard the news of you will clap their hands over you, for upon whom has your malice not rushed continually? It is no wonder if your shepherds have slumbered, O king of Asshur, you who held dominion in Nineveh, and if your princes are buried, or wander, your petty kings, that is, and the leaders of all the nations who served you before, since your people have become women in the midst of your city, and its gates have been opened to your enemies, and the whole multitude sitting on the wall has been likened to the locust, which, as at the rising of the sun, so has fled from the ramparts at the coming of Nebuchadnezzar, and has turned its back to the enemies, and its place has not been found. Therefore, since God was angered because you had laid waste his people, O Asshur, and had raised your nest up to heaven, whence you are also called great sense, your city has been destroyed. And with all the princes slain who were able to resist the adversaries, the rest of your people, unwarlike and ignoble, has been scattered on the mountains, and none of the leaders can be found to gather them and from those gathered to muster an army again.\n— Commentary on Nahum","Woe, therefore, to those who are masters of perverse doctrines in Nineveh. And fittingly it is said to them: Your shepherds have slumbered; for they have given sleep to their eyes, and slumber to their eyelids. And therefore they did not find a place for the Lord, nor a tabernacle for the God of Jacob. Nor did they hear of Ephrathah, that is, the fruitful Church, nor did they find it in the thickets of the woods. But not only have the shepherds of this mixed one, and of the locust, which, when frost threatens, sits in the hedges, slumbered, but they have also been lulled to sleep by the king of the Assyrians. For the Assyrian king knows that he cannot deceive the sheep unless he has first lulled the shepherds to sleep. It is ever the devil's endeavor to lull wakeful souls to sleep. Finally, in the Lord's passion too he weighs down the eyes of the apostles with heavy sleep; and the Savior, rousing them, says: Watch and pray, that you may not enter into temptation. And again: What I say to you, I say to all: watch. And because he never ceases to lull the wakeful to sleep, whomever he has deceived, and enticed to sleep as with the sweet and deadly song of the Sirens, the divine word rouses, and says: Rise, you who sleep, and be lifted up, and Christ will enlighten you. At the coming, then, of Christ, and of the word of God, and of the Church's teaching, and of the consummation of Nineveh, once the most beautiful harlot, the people that had before been lulled to sleep under its masters will be lifted up and will hasten, and will go to the mountains of the Scriptures, and there it will find as mountains Moses and Joshua son of Nun, as mountains the prophets, as mountains the apostles and evangelists of the New Testament; and when it has fled to such mountains, and has been occupied in the reading of mountains of this kind, if it does not find anyone to teach it: For the harvest is great, but the laborers are few, then both its zeal will be approved, because it fled to the mountains, and the sloth of the masters will be convicted. For he adds: And there was none to receive them.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"NAM.3.19":["Your wound is not obscure, nor is it such a stroke as could be cured by a physician's hand. All who hear that Nineveh has been overthrown and the Assyrian king overcome, and that the most mighty city, and the king once lord of the city, has been wounded, and lies half-dead, and wallows in his own blood, will either be stunned at the greatness of the event and the unhoped-for news, and will clench their hands, or surely, for the greatness of their joy, insulting you, they will clap their hands and resound with a kind of clamor of joy. For there is no one who can grieve over you and grant tears to your overthrow and your wound, since there is no one over whom your malice has not continually passed. And \"has passed\" is well said: for the malice of the Assyrian king cannot remain perpetually in his enemies. Thus far let the order of the history have been woven.\n— Commentary on Nahum","There follows: There is no healing for your crushing; your stroke swells. Therefore the mixed one of Nineveh cannot be healed, because he does not lay aside his pride, and the wound is always fresh, and he is struck every day as the devil smites him. And after all these things there is no healing for his crushing; for although he seems to himself to be sound, yet his soul is broken and crushed, as the hammer of the whole earth strikes from above, and it is not healed, because it is always lifted up. But if he is humbled and submits himself to Christ: A crushed and humbled heart God does not despise, and: A sacrifice to God is an afflicted spirit. At the end is placed: All who have heard the news of you will clap their hands over you. For upon whom has your malice not rushed continually? When you begin, ὦ σύμμικτε (O mixed one), to undergo punishments, all who hear the news will insult you and rejoice with a harmonious and vocal clapping, and, so to speak, with the sound and concert of works. For there is no one, or surely scarcely anyone, upon whom your malice has not rushed, or come, continually. For if the city of Nineveh has συμμίκτους shepherds and mighty ones, and every false doctrine and lying opinion of knowledge comes from the symmictus, it is to be feared that perhaps there is no one upon whom the malice of the συμμίκτου has not come. And observe carefully that he did not say: Into whom your malice, σύμμικτε, has not entered, but: Upon whom it has not come. For often the darts of false dogmas come upon us, and desire as it were to enter into the secret place of the soul; but when we shut the gates, the σύμμικτος does indeed come upon us; and as far as in him lies, he rushes in, and always does this; but with Christ the Lord helping, and guarding our heart with all watchfulness, he rushes in indeed, but cannot enter.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"HAB.1.1":["First, Chromatius, most learned of bishops, it is fitting for us to know that among the Greeks and Latins the name of the prophet is read corruptly as Ambacum, who among the Hebrews is called Habakkuk, and it is interpreted embrace, or, to render it more expressively in Greek, περίληψις, that is, embracing. Next, that where the Seventy translators and Symmachus and Theodotion translated λῆμμα, that is, assumption, in the Hebrew there stands massa, which Aquila translated as weight; on this we have argued more fully in the prophet Nahum. Massa, moreover, is never set at the head of a title except when what is seen is heavy and full of weight and toil. Hence the present prophecy too must have something of severity, so that, as in Nahum the weight that was seen against Nineveh, the city of the Assyrians, was heavy, so in this one also it may be asked for whom the weight is that is revealed to the prophet's sight. Now there are four prophets in the volume of the twelve prophets, of whom three have at the beginning the title λῆμμα, that is, weight: Nahum, Habakkuk and Malachi. Zechariah, moreover, sets two titles of this kind, in the middle and near the end, of which one is: The burden of the word of the Lord in the land of Hadrach, and of Damascus his rest; the other at the end: The burden of the word of the Lord upon Israel. On Nahum, through your prayers, a book has already been published; on Zechariah and Malachi, if life keeps me company, there will be a discussion. Now we have Habakkuk in hand, who, either because he is beloved of the Lord, is called embracing, or, because he comes to grips with God in contest and wrestling and — so to speak — in an embrace, has received the name of one who embraces, that is, of one who wrestles. For no one has dared with so bold a voice to challenge God to a dispute about justice and to say to him: Why, in human affairs and in the πολιτείᾳ (commonwealth) of this world, does so great an iniquity of things hold sway? Shall I cry out to you, suffering violence, and will you not save? Why have you shown me iniquity and toil to see? The law is torn apart, and judgment does not come through to the end, because the wicked prevails against the just; therefore perverse judgment goes forth. Do you see that it is a rash voice, and in a way that of a blasphemer, to challenge one's Creator to judgment in this way, and for a fragile vessel to argue against the potter why it was made thus or thus? This too must be noticed: that the assumption or weight, which we have already said are heavy things, is the vision of the prophet; and, against the perverse doctrine of Montanus, he understands what he sees, and he neither speaks like a madman nor, in the manner of raving women, gives forth sound without mind. Hence the Apostle also commands that, if while others are prophesying something has been revealed to another, those who were speaking first should be silent. And immediately: For God, he says, is not of dissension, but of peace. From this it is understood that when someone falls silent by his own will and gives place to another to speak, he can both speak and be silent when he wishes. But he who speaks in ecstasy, that is, unwillingly, has neither silence nor speech in his own power. Learn this also — since you once and for all demand of me forcefully that I interpret the history for you as a kind of steps and ladders for one striving toward higher things —: that the prophecy is against Babylon and Nebuchadnezzar, king of the Chaldeans, so that, just as the earlier prophet Nahum, whom Habakkuk follows, had a prophecy against Nineveh and the Assyrians, who laid waste the ten tribes which were called Israel, so Habakkuk has a prophecy against Babylon and Nebuchadnezzar, by whom Judah and Jerusalem and the temple were overthrown. And so that you may know that Habakkuk lived at the time when the two tribes which were called Judah had already been led into captivity, Daniel can teach you, to whom Habakkuk is sent with a meal into the lions' den, although among the Hebrews this very history is not read. Therefore, whether one accepts that scripture or does not accept it, either way it is on our side: for either he accepts it, and then the book of Habakkuk is composed after the event had taken place; or he does not accept it, and Habakkuk writes as a prophet the things that he knows are to come.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.2-3":["How long, O Lord, shall I cry out, and you will not hear? Shall I cry aloud to you, suffering violence, and will you not save? Why have you shown me iniquity and grief, to see plunder and injustice against me? LXX: How long, O Lord, shall I cry out, and you will not hear? Shall I cry aloud to you, suffering violence, and will you not save? Why have you shown me toils and griefs, to see misery and impiety? Meanwhile, according to the letter, the prophet complains against God: why does Nebuchadnezzar lay waste the temple and Judah; why is Jerusalem, once the city of the Lord, destroyed? Why does the people cry out and is not heard? Why does it cry aloud to the Lord, oppressed by the Chaldeans, and is not saved? Why, too, has the prophet himself, or the people in whose person he now speaks, lived for this and been brought this far, to see the iniquity of the enemies and his own toil? Why does injustice prevail against him? And he says this out of anguish of mind, not knowing that gold is refined in fire, and that the three boys came out of the furnace purer than they went in. But we can also take it generally: that the prophet, speaking in the person of human impatience, seeing sinners abounding and possessing riches in the world, their sons like a new plantation in their youth, and their daughters adorned like the likeness of the temple, their storehouses full, overflowing from this into that, their sheep fruitful and multiplied in their ways, and the other things that are written more fully in Psalm 143 (144), bursts out into a complaining voice full of grief. Why do you look upon the despisers, and keep silent while the wicked tramples down one more just than himself, and you make men like the fish of the sea, and like creeping things that have no leader? Something of this kind we read also in Psalm 72 (73): But my feet were almost moved, my steps had almost slipped, and the rest. And again in the same psalm: And if there is knowledge in the Most High. For behold, the sinners, abounding in the world, have obtained riches, and the rest, as far as my hands. But they say these things not knowing the unsearchable judgments of God and the depth of the riches of his wisdom and knowledge, and that God does not see as man sees. Man looks only at present things; God knows the things to come and the eternal. And it is as if a sick man, burning with fever, were to ask for cold water and say to the physician: I suffer violence, I am tormented, I burn, I am dying; how long, physician, shall I cry out, and you will not hear? And the most wise and most merciful physician were to answer him: I know at what time I ought to give what you ask; I do not take pity now, because that mercy is cruelty, and your will asks against yourself. So also the Lord our God, knowing the weights and measures of his clemency, sometimes does not hear the one who cries out, in order to test him, and to stir him the more to ask, and, as if refining him in the fire, to make him more just and purer. Understanding this, the Apostle, according to the fact that he obtained mercy from the Lord, says: Let us not fail in tribulations, and he blesses God at all times, and knows that he who perseveres to the end, he shall be saved. And he glories in toil and grief. And with Jeremiah he says: I will call upon tribulation and misery. So that, just as another calls upon God, so the holy man and unconquered warrior, in order to exercise and prove himself, longs for tribulation and misery to come.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.3-4":["And judgment has come about, and contradiction is the stronger.\n\nBecause of this the law is torn apart, and judgment does not come through to the end, because the wicked prevails against the just; therefore perverse judgment goes forth. LXX: Against me judgment has come about, and the judge takes; therefore the law is torn apart, and judgment does not come through to the end, because the wicked prevails against the just; therefore perverse judgment goes forth. The prophet or the people is still speaking to the Lord: that judgment was given against him not by truth but by power, and that he obtained nothing from the law and justice. Hence the judgment itself did not have its end. Now the end of judgment is to judge justly. And why he dares to say this he shows in what follows, saying: Because the wicked Nebuchadnezzar prevailed against the just Judah; and that this is the reason why he said that judgment did not come through to the end, because it is unjust and perverse that Josiah, a just king, should be slaughtered by the Egyptian king, that Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah should be slaves, and the Babylonian emperor should rule, and Belshazzar, among his harlots and concubines, should drink from the bowls of God. These things the prophet says about the state of his own times — for we follow even the lowliness of the history, since you once wished it —. But according to the LXX, it is the common complaint of the saints to God: why unjust judgment is passed against them, and they shed innocent blood in persecutions; and if ever they have stood before the tribunal of secular judges, the judge, having taken bribes, condemns the innocent and frees the guilty. This indeed can be said not only of the judges of the world but sometimes also of the leaders of the churches: that for the sake of bribes they tear the law apart and do not bring judgment through to the end, and the wicked prevails against the just, and in judgment the sin of the rich man is defended rather than the truth of the poor man. Hence the complaint that perverse judgment goes forth; but we ought not to be troubled over this inequality of things, seeing that even at the beginning of the world the just Abel was killed by the wicked Cain, and afterwards, while Jacob was in exile, Esau reigned in his father's house; and the Egyptians afflict the children of Israel with clay and brick; and the Lord, against whom the complaint is now directed, is crucified by the Jews, and Barabbas the robber is chosen. The day will fail me if I wish to enumerate how in this world, while the wicked prevail, the just are oppressed.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.5":["Look among the nations and see, and marvel, and be astounded, for a work has been done in your days which no one will believe when it is told. LXX: See, you despisers, and look, and marvel at marvels and perish, for I work a work in your days which you will not believe if anyone tells it. Symmachus, for what we have said, For a work has been done in your days, translated: For a work will be done in your days, the rest likewise. Again, at the beginning of the chapter, where in the Hebrew is written rau baggoim, and we translated Look among the nations, and the LXX put See, you despisers, while Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion agree with our translation, in a certain other edition, ἀνωνύμη (anonymous), I found: You will see the slanderers; and in another, likewise without the author's title: You will see those who turn aside. Therefore, to the earlier complaints of the prophet, who protests and says, How long, O Lord, shall I cry out, and you will not hear? and the rest up to the end of this opening, the voice of the Lord answering is brought in: that he should see this iniquity, which he thinks is done in Israel alone, among the nations; and that not, as the prophet supposed, Judah and Jerusalem alone have been handed over to the Chaldeans, but all the nations round about; and that he would grow so strong, and would fall afterwards, that if anyone foretold what is to come, it would seem incredible because of the greatness of the thing. But this too that the LXX and the other translators have given, See, you despisers, or you will see the slanderers and those who turn aside, agrees with the sense of this place; and by the very word are rebuked the audacity and contempt toward the Lord of those in whose person the prophet had cried out: why they dared to despise the majesty of God, and to speak rashly, and, as far as lies in them, to slander the providence of God, and to turn away from the Lord, accusing him of iniquity. You will see, therefore, you despisers, and then you will marvel, and you will count all your complaint as nothing, when you have perceived what I do in your days, lest perhaps you say: What does the future concern us? A work that will be so great, and will crush all your protest, that if anyone now foretold that it would be, you would not easily lend belief. But what this work is, is shown in what follows.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.6-7":["For behold, I will raise up the Chaldeans, a bitter and swift nation, walking over the breadth of the earth, to possess tents not its own. It is dreadful and terrible; from itself is judgment, and its burden will go forth. Its horses are lighter than leopards, and swifter than evening wolves; and its horsemen will spread abroad; for its horsemen will come from afar, they will fly like an eagle hastening to eat. All will come for plunder; their face is a burning wind; and it will gather captivity like sand. And he will triumph over kings, and tyrants will be his laughingstock; he will laugh at every fortress, and will heap up a mound, and will take it. Then the spirit will be changed, and he will pass through and fall; this is his strength, that of his god. LXX: For behold, I will raise up the Chaldeans, a bitter and swift nation, which walks over the breadth of the earth, to possess tents not its own. It is terrible and illustrious; from itself will be its judgment, and its assumption will go forth from itself. And its horses will leap beyond leopards, and will be swifter than the wolves of Arabia, and its horsemen will ride, and will come with a rush from far away; and they will fly like an eagle ready to eat. Consummation will come upon the wicked, who resist their faces from the opposite side; and it will gather captivity like sand; and he will take delight in kings, and tyrants are his sport; and he will make sport of every fortress, and will cast up a mound, and will take it; then he will change his spirit and pass through, and will be propitiated; this is the strength of my God. What I had said to you, Look among the nations, and see, and wonder, and be astounded, for a work will be done in your days which no one will believe when it is told: this is what the following discourse describes: Behold, I will raise up Nebuchadnezzar and the Chaldeans, a most warlike and swift nation, to whose strength and boldness in making war almost all the Greeks who have written barbarian histories are witnesses. This nation is not content with its own borders, but, running to and fro, it roams over the breadth of the earth. And this is its work: not to work the land with the plowshare, but to live by plunder and the sword, and to possess cities not its own; before it lays a hand on them, before it bursts out into war, it carries terror in its countenance.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.6":["Now let us come to the LXX, so that, setting out each sentence, we may fit to it the allegorical interpretation. Behold, I will raise up the Chaldeans, a bitter and swift nation, which walks over the breadth of the earth, to possess tents not its own. God threatens the despisers and slanderers of his providence that he will raise up the Chaldeans, who are interpreted as it were demons; signifying either the most wicked angels, who minister to his fury and wrath and to the tribulation he brings upon sinners, or the souls of the most wicked men, through whom he torments those who deserve it. Now these Chaldeans are a bitter and swift nation, not sparing and quickly fulfilling what has been commanded them. And it walks over the breadth of the earth — for wide and spacious is the way that leads to death —, along which that rich man in the Gospel also walked, who shone in purple, and those of whom it is said: Who sleep on beds of ivory, and abound in luxuries on their couches. Who eat suckling calves from the herds, and drink refined wine, and are anointed with the finest ointments; who, because they walk on the wide way, are called the breadth of the earth, which is trodden by the footsteps of the Chaldeans. For they would not walk along the strait and narrow way that leads to life, along which Paul walked, glorying in tribulations and distresses. And the Chaldeans walk over the breadth of the earth to possess tents not their own. For every rational soul, although through its own vice and fault it has become a lodging of the Chaldeans, is nevertheless by nature a tent of God. And although in the Gospel the most wicked demon says, I will go into my house from which I came out, he is not to be believed; for no rational creature was made for this, to be a dwelling of a demon.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.7":["But as for what he says, From itself judgment and its burden will go forth, for which Symmachus translated, It will judge for itself, and will go forth by its own decree, it is to be understood either thus: it will appoint rulers from its own nation, and its power and its sword will have no attendants from other nations; or certainly: as it has done, so it will be done to it; and it will be laid waste just as it laid waste.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","There follows: It is terrible and illustrious; from him will be its judgment, and its assumption will go forth from him. The Chaldean is terrible because of the many and varied punishments he brings upon the despisers; illustrious, because he takes to himself the glory of divinity. And through his oracles and false responses, and the cures of diseases which he himself had sent in, he seems illustrious among the ignorant and the despisers of God. For the judgment and punishment of the despiser will be from him, that is, from himself, or from the Chaldean. For according to the Apostle they will be handed over to him for punishment, that they may learn not to blaspheme; and whoever repents and is converted to God will go forth from him, though before he was held in his hands and was the assumption of the Chaldean. If ever we see that someone has served the devil for a long time, and afterwards has been converted to God, let us say of him: And his assumption has gone forth from him.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.8-9":["For whoever repent and forsake the demons, to whom, like horses, they formerly offered their backs to be sat upon, and with the swiftness of leopards and of wolves at evening have thrown off and dashed down their riders, and have come with an empty and light back to carry him who, meek and poor, mounted the foal of an ass, these, as if hurrying from afar, and not content with running and rushing, will take to flight, and will come like an eagle, to feed on the flesh of the word of God and to satisfy the hunger of so long a time. For in what is said, Καὶ ἐξιππάζονται οἱ ἱππεῖς αὐτοῦ, which we too translated, And its horsemen will be ridden, according to the sense we set out above, Symmachus translated: Its horsemen will be poured out, that is, they will fall and be dashed to the ground. Now they are rightly called wolves of Arabia, that is, of evening and of the west, in whom a most wicked way of life has set, and who, once placed in darkness, abandon the evening with all their stride; and when they have left it, and have flown, ready to eat the flesh of the word of God, then consummation will come upon the wicked, that is, upon the Chaldeans, who resisted the faces of the penitent so that they might not return to their Lord. Wherefore consummation will come upon the wicked, who resist their faces from the opposite side.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.8":["The horses also and the horsemen, who will come from afar, will be so quick to pursue and to lay everything waste that they will surpass leopards and evening wolves. For wolves are said to be fiercer as night draws near, roused to madness by hunger the whole day. The horsemen, then, will fly, not to fight, since no one will resist, but to range about like the eagle, to which everything among flying creatures is subject, hastening to devour.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.9-10":["And when these have been consumed, and the captivity snatched from their hands, then the divine word will gather like sand the captivity of the Chaldeans, and will take delight in kings, and tyrants will be its sport, seeing that the devil, once most powerful, and his kingdoms — showing which to the Savior also, he says, All these I will give you, if you fall down and worship me —, have been destroyed by his coming; for it is the delight of prudence and the pleasure of wisdom when folly is destroyed, and the once great power of tyrants, overcome and cast down, is turned into derision. For not the dragon alone, who is the beginning of the Lord's creation, made a sport for the angels, was formed to be held in sport by the Lord; nor will God give him alone, like a sparrow to a little child; but if anyone else also is cruel and of a tyrannical mind, he will be handed over to the word of God for derision.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.9":["And just as at the blast of a burning wind all green things wither, so at the sight of them all things will be laid waste; and so great will be the number of captives and of plunder that, by ὑπερβολὴν (hyperbole), it could even be made equal to the sand.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.10":["He too, that is, Nebuchadnezzar, will reign over the whole world, and, triumphing over kings before his chariot, he will hold them in derision and count them among his amusements; and he will be of such great power and pride that he will strive to overcome nature and to take the most strongly fortified cities by the might of his army. For he will come to Tyre, and, throwing a mound into the sea, he will make a peninsula of the island, and land will provide an entrance into the city between the waves of the sea. For this reason he will both laugh at every fortress, and heap up a mound, and take it, that is, the fortress, or Tyre; and this indeed is clearly shown in Ezekiel, where it is said: Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, subjected his army to great labor against Tyre. Every head was bald, and every shoulder stripped of hair, and no wages were given to him and to his army against Tyre; and for the work in which he served against it.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And it says: He will make sport of every fortress. But what other fortress is there, except that of which the Apostle speaks: For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty for God for the destruction of fortresses, destroying thoughts, and every height that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God. If therefore there are any fortifications, either in discourses which promise that they have a height and greatness contrary to the truth, or in all the glory and riches and strength that is praised in the world, all will be destroyed, and the word of God will make sport of every fortress. And it will cast up a mound, and will take it by the mound and the earthly things it brings forward, convicting it of fragility in those things in which it formerly seemed to itself to have some strength.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.11":["And when he has heaped up a mound, and nothing has stood in the way of his strength, then his spirit will be changed into pride, and, believing himself to be God, he will set up a golden image in Babylon, which he will compel all the nations to worship. When he has done this, he will pass into the form of a beast, and afterwards will fall; for which Aquila and Symmachus translated καὶ πλημμελήσει, that is, and he will offend; holy Scripture having this custom, that it puts uasam, that is, he will offend, for what means: he will cease to be what he had been. We also have something of this kind in the idiom of our own language, when we say: The army has been beaten, for what means: it has been killed and cut down; and: The vineyard and the field have sinned, for what means: the vintage and the crops have had no yield. But what is said at the end of the chapter, This is his strength, that of his god, is to be read εἰρωνικῶς (ironically), so that the sense is: This is his strength, which Bel his god gave him. To his worship he compelled all nations, even by written decree and the threat of death, with a most savage command. These things according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And when this has been fulfilled, then the spirit will be turned, and will by no means punish as it had punished before, but, passing through the sinners, it will pray for them and reconcile them to their former Lord; in all of which is shown the strength of our God, which has accomplished such great things. You see how rugged the places are, and opposed to the truth of the history; and how the things we interpreted according to the letter concerning the Chaldeans now seem, according to tropology, to sound the clemency and liberty of those who have escaped from the hands of the Chaldeans. The history is strict and has no freedom to wander. Tropology is free, and bounded only by these laws: that it follow the piety of understanding and the context of the discourse, and not be violent in joining things that are greatly contrary to one another.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.12":["Are you not from the beginning, O Lord my God, my Holy One? And shall we not die? O Lord, you have set him for judgment, and you have established him strong, that you might chastise. LXX: Are you not from the beginning, O Lord my God, my Holy One? And shall we not die? O Lord, you have set him for judgment; and he has formed me, that I might reprove in his discipline. Symmachus more plainly: Are you not from the beginning, O Lord my God, my Holy One, so that we should not die? O Lord, you have set him to judge, you have appointed him strong to chastise. To the complaint of the prophet, who said: How long, O Lord, shall I cry, and you will not hear? God had answered, saying: Look among the nations and see; and after this preface he had added: Behold, I will raise up the Chaldeans, a bitter and swift nation. And when the whole description was complete, whether of Nebuchadnezzar or of the devil, he recalls at the end: Then his spirit will be changed, and he will pass through, and he will fall; this is his strength, of his god. The prophet, hearing and understanding this, that Nebuchadnezzar against Judah, or the devil against believers, receives power for this reason, that he may chastise them, and that after the chastisement he himself also may be punished at the end, answered the Lord: Are you then, O Lord my God, my Holy One — and he says this with the feeling of one who coaxes and repents —, you who created us from the beginning? By whose mercy we subsist up to now? For I did not know that our adversaries could do so much, nor had I known that Nebuchadnezzar, or the devil of this world, had received power over all the nations as well. As far, therefore, as his strength is concerned, none of us can resist him. But as far as your mercy is concerned, all that we live is yours, that we have not been slain by him and led on to the works of death. For you, O Lord, have set him to judge, that he himself may be enemy and avenger, and that through him you may chastise whoever has sinned against you. But since we have once interpreted the Chaldeans as the demons, and Nebuchadnezzar as their king, the devil, we ought briefly to touch upon the description of the devil and his power, so that the prophet may justly say: O Lord, you have set him for judgment, and you have established him strong, that you might chastise. A nation of demons is raised up against the unbelievers and the despisers, bitter for punishing, and present everywhere. It walks also wherever the breadth of the earth extends, to possess men in whom Christ ought to have dwelt. It is horrible and terrible, and can hardly be overcome by anyone, nor is it broken until, weighed down by the greatness of its sins and the weight of its iniquity, it has come to the consummation. Its horses and horsemen, in the likeness of leopards and wolves, will always thirst for blood, and they will long for plunder; they will feign absence, and while they are not expected, they will come swiftly from afar. They will fly like an eagle, which, lifting up its flight, wants to set its nest among the stars of heaven, and always hastens to the prey. And in Ezekiel too the devil is described under the figure of Nebuchadnezzar and of the king of Egypt. There is none of the demons who spares; all will come hastening to the prey; before their face is a burning wind; whatever they look upon, whatever meets their gaze, they will long to burn and destroy. But the king will be most mighty in the midst of the captives, and he will receive their number, like the sand of the sea, from his attendants on every side, and he himself will triumph over kings, and tyrants will be his laughingstock. For he will deceive many saints by his tripping up, and those who seemed to themselves most mighty, and to exercise a tyranny against the demons and to cast them out of possessed bodies, he will subject to his servitude and count as a joke. But he himself, most strong, having gathered a band and assembled an army of the lost, will laugh at every fortress, and whatever is strong he will strive to overthrow. For he will heap up a mound, that is, he will surround it with earthworks, and when the earth has been heaped up, he will easily take every fortress. But after so great a victory his spirit will be changed, and his mouth will pass through even to heaven, and making himself God, he will begin to blaspheme his Creator. When he has done this, he will fall, and his ruin will show how strong his divinity was, and the pretense of the idols under whose images he was subjugating men to his worship. The prophet, therefore, hearing these things, that the king of this world is so great and of such a kind that he gathers captives like the sand of the sea and triumphs over kings; and that tyrants are his laughingstock, and he laughs at every fortress, and first heaps up a mound and afterwards takes it; and that he is of such great pride that he dares to resist against his Creator and to make himself as a god — the prophet, who before was speaking boldly to the Lord, and had declared that he himself, or his people, or those in whose person he was complaining, were just — now bursts out into words of coaxing and says: Are you then from the beginning, O Lord my God, my Holy One? And that we do not die, nor are taken by so great an enemy, is of your clemency. For you, O Lord, have appointed him as an executioner, and you have made him strong, so that either no one or hardly anyone can resist his strength. Moreover, according to the LXX, what is said at the end, And he has formed me, that I might reprove in his discipline, can be referred to the person of the prophet, so that the sense is: But I have been inspired as a prophet for this reason, that I may reprove those who transgress and teach the discipline of the Lord. Some think it is said of the Lord, who was formed by the Father, and assumed a body, for this reason, that he might instruct men in the doctrine of God the Father. But how far this disagrees with the context of what goes before, and with the tenor of the whole passage, will be not so much my judgment as the reader's.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.13-14":["Your eyes are pure, so that you may not see evil, and you will not be able to look upon iniquity. Why do you not look upon those who do iniquitous things, and are silent while the wicked devours one more just than himself. And you will make men like the fish of the sea, and like a creeping thing that has no prince? LXX: The eye is pure, so that it may not see evils, and you will not be able to look upon sorrow. Why do you look upon the despisers, will you be silent when the wicked has devoured one more just than himself? And you will make men like the fish of the sea, and like creeping things that have no leader. Jeremiah too says something of this kind to God: You are just, O Lord, I confess; yet I will speak judgments to you: Why is it that the way of the wicked prospers; that all abound who transgress and do wrong? You planted them and they have taken root, they have begotten sons and have borne fruit; you are near to their mouth, and far from their reins. Therefore Habakkuk also, in the same thought: Pure, he says, O Lord, are your eyes, and I know that you do not willingly look upon what is evil and unjust, nor can anyone doubt your justice. Yet why do you suffer the Babylonians to vaunt themselves with such great cruelty, and Israel, the just, to be oppressed by the wicked Nebuchadnezzar? Not that the one who is oppressed is perfectly just, but that he is more just than the one who oppresses him. And just as fish that have no prince, and irrational beasts, and the multitude of creeping things, being without providence, are subject to the stronger, and whoever is more powerful in strength rules over the other, so among men, a rational animal, made in your likeness, reason will have no force, nor merits, but bodily strength and irrational might.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.13":["But if we wish to understand it generally, of providence, the prophet asking: Why does the devil have so much power in the world, and, while God rules, another exercises tyranny? — the sense will be of this kind, and this exposition will be joined to what goes before: I know, O Lord my God, my Holy One, that because you provide and defend, we do not die, and I know that you have set the adversary for this reason, that through him, as through an executioner, you might chastise sinners and not slay them. I know that nothing unjust pleases you, and that your eyes are pure from all iniquity, and that you cannot look upon the sorrows of those who have been subjected to injustice. Yet I cannot find the cause why the unjust Cain kills the just Abel, and you are silent. Why, while the whale rages and devours everything, not only the smaller fish but also your own Jonah himself is devoured? Why does the wicked conquer, and the just is conquered? Nor do I say this as though I knew of anyone being justified in your sight and being without sin, or as though I were ignorant of human frailty; but just as Sodom and Gomorrah are just in comparison with Jerusalem, and the publican in the Gospel becomes more just by comparison with the Pharisee, so too this one who is oppressed by the devil is indeed a sinner, but he is more just than the one who oppresses him. Why, then, is there no measure, nor weight, so that, if once the just is oppressed and subjected, he may be subjected not to the wicked but to one more just than himself? Shall I say that anything happens without you, and that against your will the wicked can do so much? To think this is blasphemous. Since, therefore, you are the ruler and Lord of the universe, it must be you who do what cannot happen without you. And he says these things, not because the prophet himself thinks so, as I have testified above, but because he expresses human impatience in his own person; just as we often see the Apostle take upon himself the various opinions of men, and now say: But I see another law in my members, fighting against the law of my mind, and taking me captive in the law of sin, which is in my members. And, as though he were a beginner: Brothers, I do not consider that I have laid hold of it, and: We know in part, and we prophesy in part. And again, as though he were perfect: As many, therefore, as are perfect, let us be of this mind, when surely it does not belong to one and the same man to say that he knows in part and that he is perfect. And lest perhaps you think that this is not the Apostle's custom but our own argument, he himself says to the Corinthians: But these things, brothers, I have transferred to myself and Apollos for your sakes, that you may learn in us.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.14":["Otherwise, how can God so hold men as the fish of the sea, and as creeping things that have no prince, when the angels of each one daily see the face of the Father who is in heaven, and the angel of the Lord encamps round about those who fear him, and delivers them? Just as, therefore, among men the providence of God runs even through each individual, so in the other animals we can understand a general dispensation, indeed, and order, and course of things. For example, how the multitude of fish is born and lives in the waters, how creeping things and four-footed beasts arise on the earth, and by what foods they are nourished. But it is absurd to bring down the majesty of God to this, that he should know at each moment how many gnats are born, or how many die, what multitude of bugs and fleas and flies there is on the earth, how many fish swim about in the water, and which of the smaller ought to yield as prey to the larger. Let us not be such foolish flatterers of God that, while we drag his power down even to the lowest things, we do injury to ourselves, saying that there is the same providence for rational as for irrational things. Hence that apocryphal book is to be condemned for its folly, in which it is written that a certain angel named Tyri presides over creeping things, and that in this likeness proper angels have been assigned to the fish too, and the trees, and all the beasts, to guard them.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.1.15-17":["He lifted up the whole with a hook, he dragged it in his dragnet, and gathered it into his net; over this he will rejoice and exult. Therefore he will sacrifice to his dragnet, and offer sacrifice to his net; because through them his portion has been made fat, and his food choice. For this reason, therefore, he spreads out his dragnet, and never ceases always to slay the nations. LXX: He lifted up the consummation with a hook, and drew it in his net, and gathered it in his dragnets. For this reason he will rejoice and be glad; therefore he will sacrifice to his dragnet, and burn incense to his net, because through them he has made his portion fat, and his foods are choice. Therefore he spreads out his net, and never ceases always to slay the nations. Because above he had named fish, saying: And you will make men like the fish of the sea, and like a creeping thing, which is said more expressively in the Hebrew remes, that is, κινούμενον, namely everything that can be moved, for that reason he keeps the μεταφοράν (metaphor) of fish in the rest, so that, as a fisherman casts a hook, and a net and a dragnet, so that what the hook could not, the net may catch, and what has escaped the net may be surrounded by the wider dragnets, so too the king of Babylon laid waste all things and made the whole race of men his prey. Moreover, what he says: He will rejoice and be glad, and will sacrifice to his dragnet, and offer sacrifice to his net, signifies the idol which he made in the plain of Dura and the image of Bel, to which, as to a great dragnet, he sacrificed the fattest victims, compelling all the nations he had conquered to its worship. For believing that through them, that is, through his idols, he had been made fat, and had his portion, that is, all riches, he subjugated to his rule even all the princes and kings, as great fish, whom he calls choice foods. And because he has once been sated with a most abundant catch, and has filled his dragnet, that is, his army, therefore he never ceases to slay the nations, that is, always to fight and to butcher. Moreover, according to the Seventy, the wicked devil — who oppresses the just, and holds men as the fish of the sea, and lays all things waste as creeping things that have no leader — sent his hook, opposed to that hook by which, through the apostle Peter, the first fish was caught, in whose mouth a stater was found. And Adam stuck to his hook, and he drew him out of paradise with his net, and covered him with his dragnets, with various and manifold snares and deceits. Therefore he will rejoice, and he will think his traps to be more than the command of the Lord. And he will sacrifice not to the hook — which is understood as perverse speech, and set still at the beginning —, but to his net, because in it he caught the fattest victims. And: Through one man very many were made sinners; and in Adam we all died, and all the saints afterwards were cast out of paradise together with him. Hence also his foods are choice, as according to the psalmist: That he may seek his food from God, wishing to overthrow the prophets and apostles. And because he first deceived man, he never ceases daily to slay the whole human race. But it can also be taken of the perverse and manifold doctrine of the heretics, that they too, with their hook, and their net, and their dragnets, catch very many fish and seize many creeping things, and for that reason rejoice, and adore as God their speech, by which they have been able to deceive and persuade, and worship it, and polish it; they serve it with every art, that speech through which they know that so many victims have been slain by them, and so many of the mighty and of the saints have been deceived, whom Scripture now names the fat portion and the choice foods. Therefore, in the likeness of beasts, which, once they have tasted blood, always thirst for it, they spread out their net, and this is all their zeal, to kill not a few, as at the beginning, but more. He has no doubt about the slaughter of many nations who has seen so great a multitude of heresies and perverse doctrines caught by the hook, the net and the dragnets of the devil; and yet the end of their capture is destruction.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.1":["I will stand upon my watch, and fix my step upon the fortification, and I will look out, to see what is said to me and what I may answer to the one who reproves me. LXX: I will stand upon my watch, and I will go up upon the rock, and I will look out, to see what he speaks in me, and what I may answer to my correction. Symmachus has followed it out more plainly: Like a watchman I will stand upon the lookout, and I will stand as one shut in, and I will look out, to see what is said to me, and what I may answer, and gainsay against the one who reproves me. For fortification and rock, in place of which Symmachus translated shut in, there is put in the Hebrew masur, which Theodotion translated circuit, Aquila and the Fifth Edition compass. To the first complaint the Lord had answered: Look among the nations and see, and wonder and be astonished. To which the prophet, as though doing penance for what he had said before, had indeed moderated his question, saying: O Lord my God, my Holy One, and we shall not die. But nonetheless, with veneration and praises of God, he had questioned him: Your eyes are pure, so that you do not see evil, and you do not know how to look upon iniquity. Why do you not look upon those who do iniquitous things, and are silent, while the wicked devours one more just than himself? And what the devouring of the just would be, he has set out in its parts: that men would become like the fish of the sea, and like creeping things; and that with his hook, and dragnet, and net all would be dragged to destruction, and there would be no end of their slaughter. Therefore, because he is a prophet, and asks for this reason, and says he is in doubt so that what was answered to him might be answered to all: I will stand, he says, upon my lookout, that is, upon the height of my prophecy, and I will see what follows after the captivity of the people and the overthrow of the city and temple, and thereafter. Or certainly thus: I will guard my heart with all diligence, and I will stand upon Christ the rock. And with this circuit and compass I will be fenced as with a wall, so that the roaring lion may not be able to break in upon me, and I will see what God answers me after my second question, and, after he has answered me and rebuked me for having complained wrongly, what I too ought to answer him. But elegantly and with wonderful sense he describes human impatience, which we are always accustomed to show in disputes, so that before anyone on the opposing side answers us, and we know in what he has reproved us, we are made ready to answer. From this it is shown that the answer is not of reason but of contentiousness. For if it were reason, the answer ought to have been awaited, and so he should see whether he ought to reply, or to assent to a reasonable answer. But this too is to be noted from what he had said: To see what he speaks in me, that the prophetic vision and the utterance of God come to the prophets not from without, but within, and answer to the inner man. Whence Zechariah also: And the angel, he says, who spoke in me. And in the psalms: I will hear what the Lord speaks in me.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.2-3":["However, I am not unaware that according to their translation it can also be taken thus: Write the vision in which Christ is promised, and, whether on boxwood, or on tablets, or, as Symmachus translated, on pages, weave this discourse of your prophecy: that at the appointed time and at the consummation of the world my Son will come, to save the lost sheep of the house of Israel, and to join other sheep also to the ancient sheep, and, making one flock, to join the two rods, which Ezekiel, that is, the strength of God, holds joined and cleaving to one another in his prophetic hand. But if, O prophet, or you, O people — in whose person my prophet also seemed to doubt —, Christ withdraws himself a little and seems to delay, wait for him; for coming he will come, and will not tarry, and the rest which we have already expounded above.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.2-4":["And the Lord answered me, and said: Write the vision, and make him plain upon tablets, that he who reads him may run through; for the vision is still far off, and he will appear at the end, and will not lie; if he delays, wait for him, for coming he will come, and will not tarry. Behold, he who is unbelieving, his soul will not be upright in himself. But the just shall live in his faith. LXX: And the Lord answered to me, and said: Write the vision, and plainly on boxwood, that he who reads may pursue these things; for the vision is still for a time, and will rise at the end, and not in vain. If he fails, wait for him, for coming he will come and will not tarry; if he draws himself back, it will not please my soul in him. But the just shall live by my faith. For tablets and boxwood, which in Hebrew is called alluoth, Symmachus translated pages. And where the Seventy put: But the just shall live by my faith, all alike translated, shall live by his faith. Indeed Symmachus, translating more expressively, says: But the just shall live through his own proper faith, which in Greek is said ὁ δίκαιος τῇ ἑαυτοῦ πίστει ζήσει; for baemunatho, which means in his faith, if it had the letter iod and not uau at the end, as the Seventy thought, and were read Baemunathi, they would rightly have translated in my faith. But as it is, the likeness of the letters uau and iod, which differ from one another only in size, was the cause of the error. Why this was said, the following discussion takes in.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Moreover, as to what we translated for vision: For the vision is still far off, and if he delays, wait for him, that is, the vision (uisum), let no one, deceived by error, think that I put uisus, which is masculine and less usual in Latin, for uisio, which is feminine. For hazon, which Aquila translates ὁραματισμὸν (vision), is called among the Hebrews uisus, in the masculine gender, and to the end, under the same gender of the vision, that is, of uisus, the masculine inflection is kept. But the Seventy, saying: Write the vision, and afterwards: If he fails, wait for him; for coming he will come, and will not tarry; if he withdraws himself, it will not please my soul in him, first translated uisio in the feminine gender, which among the Hebrews, as we have said, is masculine. Then, following the Hebrew gender, where it is inflected in the masculine, wait for him, and it will not please my soul in him, they too inflected it in the masculine gender. Whereas surely they ought, in keeping with their first translating uisio, to have put the feminine gender of the vision in the rest also, so as to say: Wait for her for coming she will come; but if she withdraws herself, it will not please my soul in her, that is, in the vision. This for this reason, that we might not seem to keep silent about what we knew.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.2":["According to the promise which is made to the holy man in Isaiah, saying: While you are still speaking, I will say, Behold, I am here, now too the Lord answered the prophet, and commanded him to write the vision and to set her out upon tablets, that is, to write more plainly. But I think the tablets are those of which the Apostle also speaks to the Corinthians: You are our letter, written in our hearts, which is known and read by all men; being made manifest that you are the letter of Christ, ministered by us, and written not with ink but with the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on fleshly tablets of the heart. And Solomon too signifies something of the kind in Proverbs, saying: Write her upon the breadth of your heart. And he is commanded to write more plainly, so that the reader may be able to run, and his speed and his desire of reading may be held back by no hindrance.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But what he says is of this kind, provided only that we mix in the translation of the Seventy too. Write in your heart; and as little children who receive the first elements of letters train their crooked strokes and trembling hand on boxwood, and by practice grow used to writing correctly, so you too, who have spoken in the person of the doubting people, write what I say on the tablets of your heart and on the boxwood of your breast. For it is a vision which is promised and which is commanded to be set out and written more plainly, so that it may be wrapped in no cloud and obscured by no windings of riddles; let a clear hope have a clear pledge. And this, O prophet, I command, not because you do not know — for you would not be a prophet if you were ignorant —, but so that what you have written more plainly the reader may be able to read and run through without hindrance and difficulty, which the Seventy translated: Ὅπως διώκῃ ὁ ἀναγινώσκων, that is, that he who reads may pursue, according to that sense which is written to Timothy: Pursue justice, piety, faith, charity, patience, gentleness. And to the Romans: Pursuing hospitality. And to the Corinthians: Pursue charity.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.3":["And he commands this because the vision is still far off, and set in an appointed time. And when the end of things has come, then he too will come, and the prophecy will be proved true, fulfilled in deed. But if perhaps, through your desire, O reader, and your ardor to see the vision, what has been promised should seem to you to delay a little, do not despair of its coming, but wait patiently: for you have me, who promise you and say: Coming he will come, and will not tarry.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But the vision itself, of which I said to you: Write the vision, and make it plain on boxwood, that he who reads may be able to pursue, is still set in an appointed time, of which the Savior says: In an acceptable time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have helped you. And it will rise at the consummation of the world, and in the last hour of the day, of which John also speaks: Little children, now it is the last hour, and it will not come in vain; for it will save many, and with the remnant of the Israelite people it will gather a multitude of the nations. But if she withdraws herself a little, and the vision begins to come more slowly than your wishes, O reader, who are commanded to read on the boxwood and on the tablets which the prophet wrote out, wait for her, for coming she will come, and will not tarry.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.4":["But since in the Law no one is justified before God, it is manifest that the righteous lives by faith. But the Law is not of faith, but he who has done these things shall live in them.\n\nThe example by which it is proved that the righteous lives by faith, and not by works, he took from Habakkuk, which the Seventy translators rendered thus: \"But the righteous lives by my faith\" (Hab. 2:4). Aquila and Theodotion: \"But the righteous lives by his faith\", that is, God's. It must therefore be considered that he did not say \"a human being\" or \"a man lives by faith\", lest he should give occasion for despising the works of the virtues; but the righteous lives by faith: so that whoever was faithful and was to live through faith could not otherwise come to faith, or live in it, unless he had first been righteous, and had ascended by purity of life as by certain steps to faith. It can therefore happen that someone is righteous, and yet does not live, being without faith in Christ. If a scruple is stirred in the reader, let him take up the words of Paul, in which he says of himself: \"As to the righteousness which is in the Law, without reproach\" (Phil. 3:6). Paul therefore was at that time righteous in the Law, but could not yet live, because he did not have within himself Christ, who says: \"I am the life\" (John 11:25). Believing in him afterwards, he began also to live. Let us too make something similar to what is said, the righteous lives by faith; and let us say: the chaste lives by faith, the wise lives by faith, the brave lives by faith, and from the remaining parts of the virtues let us bring forward a like sentence against those who, not believing in Christ, think themselves brave and wise, temperate or righteous: so that they may know that no one lives without Christ, without whom every virtue is in vice. The present testimony can also be read thus: that the righteous by faith, so that there is then added, lives.\n— Commentary on Galatians","But if anyone is unbelieving of this pledge of mine, and while I say: Coming he will come, and will not tarry, begins to doubt and to waver silently within himself, thinking that what delays for a time will not come, this man will displease my soul, according to that saying: Your new moons and sabbaths my soul hates. But that God spoke of his soul, we ought to take for mind and thought, so that the meaning is: he will displease my sense. And just as he will displease who, when I promise, doubts that what I pledge will come, so the just man who believes my promise will live in his faith. With these lines, as it were, the picture of this chapter has been sketched.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But if your faith doubts, and you think that what I pledge will not come to pass, you will have it as a great punishment that you will displease my soul. But the just man, who believes my words and does not doubt the things I promise, will have as reward life everlasting. Nor ought you at once to charge, either in my slaying you or in my giving life to him, that there is respect of persons with me; for he himself is the cause of his own giving of life, who lives by his faith, just as you, by having withdrawn yourself and refused to believe, have displeased my soul. And in these words there is plainly a prophecy of the coming of Christ. Hence the question that was put forward is also solved: that until he comes, iniquity rules in the world, and judgment does not come to its end, and the true Nebuchadnezzar catches men with his net and dragnet like little fish, and the rational animal like a creeping thing that has no prince.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Moreover, why the Apostle, writing to the Romans, made use rather of the testimony of the LXX, But the just shall live by my faith, and not of what is found in the Hebrew, the reason is plain. For he was writing to Romans, who did not know the Hebrew Scriptures; nor was he concerned about the words, since the sense was safe, and the present argument suffered no harm from it. Otherwise, wherever the sense is different, and it is written one way in the Hebrew and another in the LXX, note that he uses those testimonies which he had learned from Gamaliel, the teacher of the law.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.5-8":["We can also, according to the apostle John — who writes: As you have heard that the Antichrist is coming, but now there are many antichrists; whence we also know that it is the last hour —, call arrogant and proud all heretics and every perverse doctrine of those who claim knowledge for themselves and despise the simplicity of the Church, who bring nothing to completion but take delight in the deaths of very many; and adapt the whole content of the chapter to an understanding concerning them, who truly multiply for themselves what is not their own, and, gathering to themselves as it were very heavy clay and a weighty collar by which they may be dragged to punishment, despoil many nations, and shed the blood of men, and exercise impiety against the Church and against all its inhabitants. But the remaining peoples, namely churchmen, who have not been deceived by their error, will suddenly rise up and awake as from a heavy sleep, and will bite them, and will lay snares for them, and will have them as a prey.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.5":["And as wine deceives the one who drinks it, so will the proud man be, and he will not be adorned; who has enlarged his soul like hell, and he himself is like death, and is not filled. And he will gather to himself all nations, and will heap up to himself all peoples. Will not all these take up a parable against him, and the speech of his riddles, and it will be said: Woe to him who multiplies what is not his own, how long does he also weigh down thick clay against himself? Will not those suddenly rise up who bite you, and those who tear you be stirred up, and you will be for a plunder to them? Because you have despoiled many nations, all who are left of the peoples will despoil you, because of the blood of man, and the iniquity of the land, of the city and of all who dwell in it. LXX: But he who is arrogant and a despiser, a proud man, brings nothing to completion; who has enlarged his soul like hell, and he is like death, he is not filled. And he will gather to himself all nations, and will receive to himself all peoples; will not all these take up a parable against him, and a proposition for his narration, and they will say: Woe to him who multiplies for himself the things that are not his own, how long does he weigh down his collar heavily, for suddenly those biting him will rise up, and your ambushers will keep watch, and you will be for a plunder to them? Because you have despoiled many nations, all the remaining peoples will despoil you, because of the blood of men and the impieties of the land, and of the city, and of all its inhabitants. Since these things have been promised concerning the coming of Christ, or, as some prefer, concerning the end of the vision and the fulfillment of God's help, which whoever believes is to come will live by his faith; but whoever is unbelieving will displease the soul of the Lord: Nebuchadnezzar, the Babylonian king, will be deceived by his pride. And as wine works against the one who drinks it, and after he has risen neither foot nor mind keeps its proper office, and all gladness and cheerfulness of mind is turned into ruin, so the proud man will not be adorned, nor will he bring his will to completion, and, according to Symmachus, οὐκ εὐπορήσει, that is, he will be in want of all things. He, like death and hell, is not sated with the slain, and, subjugating all nations and all peoples to his rule, conceived no end to his avarice.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Let us examine the Seventy as well. All that we have said of Babylon and Nebuchadnezzar can be referred to this world and to the devil, who, truly arrogant and proud, and believing himself to be something, will bring nothing to completion. For indeed his endeavor and all his labor will come to destruction, he who, in the likeness of hell and of death, is not sated with the slain, and rejoices in the deception of all nations, and gathers peoples to himself.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","This same thing can also be interpreted of the Antichrist, who will be so arrogant and proud that he will sit in the temple of God, making himself God. And like hell and death he will slay so many, and gather so many to himself, that, if it were possible, he would deceive even the elect of God. He will also gather to himself all nations, so as to lead all peoples into his error.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.6-8":["When afterwards they see him slain by the breath of Christ's mouth, they will understand that the things foretold of him beforehand are true, and they will say all that follows, with the same understanding with which we have expounded it concerning the devil. As for what he says, all the remaining peoples will despoil you because of the blood of men and the impiety of the land, and of the city and of all who dwell in it, let us understand the remaining peoples as the holy ones who did not serve the Antichrist; by whom the impious one will be despoiled because of the impiety which he exercised against the whole earth, and the laying waste of the city of the Church, and the persecution of all who have dwelt in it. For so great a devastation and so great an impiety will stalk through the churches at the end of things, while the Antichrist rages, and, as the iniquity of many is multiplied, charity will grow so cold, that the Lord, who knows the secrets of the heart and is not ignorant of what is to come, said: Do you think the Son of man, when he comes, will find faith on the earth?\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.6":["When he has been made drunk with the cup of the Lord and lulled to sleep by the undiluted chalice, will not all speak over him by way of comparison, a πρόβλημα (a riddle)? Woe to him who, laying waste the whole world, is not sated with plunder, and does not cease to despoil those already stripped, and rages only to this end, that he may devour, and presses himself down with the burden of iniquity and of spoils as with a very heavy collar. And at the same time consider how elegantly he has called multiplied riches thick clay.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","When they see him sent into the abyss and handed over to Tartarus, seeing fulfilled the things which they had read in the prophets in parables and αἰνιγματωδῶς (in riddles), and interpreting them concerning him, they will recount with one harmonious voice: Woe to the devil, who has multiplied for himself what is not his own. Woe to the partridge, which has gathered what it did not bring forth. How long? This is either the voice of those who rebuke, or of those who point to the day of judgment. And weighing down his collar with infinite weight. And beautifully: since he is arrogant and proud — and pride is properly shown in the stretching out of the neck and the raising of the nape —, the collar is very heavy, so as to bend what had been raised up.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Some think that what is said: Woe to those who multiply for themselves the things that are not their own, and the rest, can be taken of rich men, who extend the boundaries of their possessions and, thinking that the things which are not man's are man's, gather to themselves things by which they will suddenly be left. But that earthly possession is not man's, that is, not the rational animal's, the Lord too shows, saying: If you have not been faithful in what is another's, who will give you what is your own? And they expound the whole tenor of the chapter of this person. But I do not know whether they can keep the order of the prophetic question and solution.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.7-8":["Will not the Medes and Persians suddenly rise up, who, destroying the empire of the Babylonians, will bite him first and afterwards tear him; and will not Nebuchadnezzar become a plunder for them, and the ravager of the whole world be despoiled by the remaining peoples, who were able to escape his hand and his cruelty? But this will happen to him because of the blood of man, that is, of Judah, and the iniquity of the land, namely of Israel, and of the city, doubtless Jerusalem, and of all who dwell in it, signifying the people in general.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.7":["And this will come about because suddenly those will rise up who bite him: either the angels with whom the devil will be handed over to punishment, or those who had been tempted by him and afterwards do penance; and, turned to the standards of Christ, they will bite him, according to what is said elsewhere: Your men of peace have laid snares for you. Then follows: And your ambushers will awake, that is, you will lie exposed to the snares of those whom you had earlier lulled to sleep, whom you had made drunk, as they lay waste your kingdom and bring back those who were captives into the troops of Christ.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.8":["For you have plundered many nations, and have stripped the Jewish people of the adornment and the garments which I had given it; therefore all the remaining peoples, who have not bowed their neck to your rule, will despoil you and make you naked. For you too have slain very many nations and have shed their blood. But also the impiety of the land, that is, of the land of the Jews, and of the city of Jerusalem, and of all its inhabitants, who said against their Creator: Crucify, crucify him; his blood be upon us and upon our children, will return upon your head and will be the causes of your despoiling.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.9":["Woe to him who gathers an evil avarice for his house, so that his nest may be on high, and who thinks he is freed from the hand of evil. You have devised confusion for your house, you have cut down many peoples, and your soul has sinned. For the stone will cry out from the wall, and the timber that is between the joints of the buildings will answer. LXX: O he who multiplies an evil avarice for his house, so as to set his nest on high, and to be rescued from the hand of evils. You have devised confusion for your house, you have consumed many peoples; and your soul has sinned, for which reason the stone will cry out from the wall, and the beetle from the wood will speak these things. The discourse is still addressed to him who heaps up evils for himself and does not understand that the multitude of his riches is the cause of the ruin of his house; and at the same time, through a metaphor, he is accused of pride, because, like the birds, he has set his nest on high, and has thought that he would be rescued from the hand of evil, that is, that he would never come into the power of his enemies; a counsel of pride and an arrogant thought which had ignominy as its end.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","What their translation means we shall say in the exposition of the Seventy translators, in whom O is put for woe, and the discourse is directed either to the devil or to the Antichrist, or to the heretics, who multiply an evil avarice for themselves. Now avarice is called evil to distinguish it from good avarice, so that there is a good avarice of the churchly teacher, who is never sated with the multitude of his followers, and the more disciples he has, the more he is roused to zeal for teaching. Woe, therefore, to him who multiplies for himself a most wicked avarice, so as to gather perverse conventicles for his house, and to set his nest on high, so as to be rescued from the hand of evils. For the devil and the Antichrist and the heretics promise that those who accept their doctrine will be in the heavenly places and will possess the kingdoms of heaven, and will not feel the fires of Gehenna.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.10-11":["You have slain many peoples, and in killing others you have raged against your own soul, and you have run riot in such cruelty that, if it can be said, the stones of the city and the timbers of the walls which you have overthrown cry out your ferocity. Something of this kind the Lord too said in the Gospel against the Pharisees, who reproached him for not rebuking the children who acclaimed him: Hosanna in the highest to the son of David, blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord; Hosanna in the highest. Have you not read, he says, that it is written: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise? And if these are silent, the stones will cry out. For although very many think it is to be understood thus, if the Jews are silent, the multitude of the nations will confess me, yet this is the more expressive and truer understanding: even if men were silent, and the envious tongue would not speak of the multitude of my signs, yet the stones themselves, and the foundations of the walls, and the structure of the walls will be able to proclaim aloud my greatness. And to make this more significant, let us set down examples from secular literature too. Crispus says in his Histories: \"The Saguntines, renowned for loyalty and sufferings beyond mortals, with greater zeal than resources; for among them even then the half-ruined walls, the unroofed houses, and the scorched walls of the temples displayed the Punic hands\". Something similar Tullius too says to Caesar for Marcellus: \"The walls, so help me heaven — as it seems to me — of this senate house long to give you thanks, because in a short time that authority will be in these seats of its ancestors and its own\". Moreover, where we have translated: And the timber that is between the joints of the buildings will answer, for which the Seventy put: And the beetle from the wood will speak these things, Symmachus, after his manner, translated more plainly, καὶ συνδεσμὸς οἰκοδομῆς ξύλινος ἀποφθέγξεται αὐτὰ, that is: And the wooden joint of the building will speak these things. Theodotion too, καὶ συνδεσμὸς ξύλου φθέγξεται αὐτά, and also the Fifth Edition, καὶ συνδεσμὸς ξύλου φθέγξεται αὐτά, and these translations also agree with that of Symmachus and with ours. For what is called chaphis in the Hebrew language signifies the timber that is set in the middle of a structure to hold the walls together, and among the Greeks it is commonly called ἱμάντωσις (a tie-beam). This, therefore, is what the prophetic discourse signifies according to the history: the stones of the walls which were destroyed by you, and their burnt timbers, will sound forth your cruelty. Besides the five editions, namely of Aquila, Symmachus, the Seventy, Theodotion, and the Fifth, I found in the twelve prophets two other editions as well, in one of which is written: For the stone will cry out from the wall like a worm speaking in the wood, and in the other: For the stone will shout from the wall, and the σκώληξ from the wood will speak these things. But Aquila too put something other than we said, καὶ μάζα, that is, and a lump from the wood will answer.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.10":["And though they have promised these things, their counsel will not be able to come to completion; but it will be a counsel of confusion and ignominy for their house, after the very end of things has shown to be false what had been promised, when it is proved to be a counsel of confusion, not of salvation. This teacher of perversity, as we have said, has consumed many peoples, and the more he had in his company, the more he sinned against his own soul.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.11":["Then the stones of his church, and the κάνθαρος, that is, the beetle from the wood, will cry out against the avarice of the proud one, because he has deceived all nations by his persuasion. By the stones we can understand the dull hearts of those who believe the doctrines of the heretics, and by the beetle from the wood, all perverse masters who, taking up the preaching of the cross for the sake of shameful gain, speak from the belly. For their god is their belly, and they do all things for the sake of food, which is turned into dung — for the beetle or cantharus is a dung worm —, and they take up the cross only for this, that with viperous mouth they may teach the avarice and pride of their master the devil. If ever you see one of the heretics speaking against the Church as if of hidden and secret mysteries, and preferring the house of the devil to the house of Christ, say: The stone cries out from the wall, and the cantharus speaks from the wood. I have read in someone's volume that the cantharus is understood of the heretics for this reason, that they hold doctrines to be compared with dung. Hence the Apostle too says that he counted the error of the old teaching as scybala, that is, as dung. Not that the old law, as the Manichaeans think, is reckoned as scybala in comparison with the Gospel — which it is impious to say, since both Testaments belong to the one God —, but that the teachings of the Pharisees, and the precepts of men, and the Deuterosis of the Jews are called dung by the Apostle. I know that one of the brethren understood the stone that cried out from the wall as the Lord Savior, and the beetle speaking from the wood as the thief who blasphemed the Lord; and although this can be understood piously, yet I do not find how it can be fitted to the whole context of the prophecy. There are some who think that the cantharus speaking from the wood can also be referred to the person of the Savior, which is shown to be impious by the very order of the discourse. For the cantharus from the wood will speak these things is understood not in a good but in a bad sense, that is, it will speak of the most wicked avarice of him who multiplies against his own house, and of the confusion of the devil, and the rest that went before concerning his iniquity and crime. As for what Aquila says, and a lump from the wood will answer, we refer the lump to that sense which the Lord set down in the Gospel: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees. And when the apostles were in doubt and could not know what it was, the evangelist interpreted it, saying: Now he had spoken to them of the teaching of the Pharisees. Rightly, therefore, does the teaching of the heretics speak from the wood; for they cannot persuade otherwise unless they hold out the glory of the wood in front of their perversity. And also concerning what we had said: For the stone will cry out from the wall like a worm speaking in the wood, or the stone will shout from the wall, and the σκώληξ from the wood will speak these things, some of our people say that the worm speaking in the wood is he who says in the psalm: But I am a worm and no man; and they refer the singing bird to the person of the same one, who says: I have become like a sparrow alone on the housetop, and other things like these.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.12":["Woe to him who builds a city with bloodshed, and prepares a town in iniquity. Are not these things from the Lord of hosts? For peoples will labor in much fire, and nations in vain, and they will fail; for the earth will be filled, so that they may know the glory of the Lord, like waters covering the sea. LXX: Woe to him who builds a city with bloodshed, and prepares a town in iniquities. Are not these things from the Lord Almighty? And many peoples have failed in fire, and many nations have been straitened, for the earth will be filled so that it may know the glory of the Lord, like water to cover the seas. No one doubts that according to the letter the prophetic discourse is still speaking against Nebuchadnezzar, and lamenting him because he builds Babylon with bloodshed, and constructs its walls with the ruin and death of very many.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But it is clear that the devil, and the Antichrist, and the perverse doctrine of the heretics build a city with bloodshed, that is, their own church on the destruction of those whom they have deceived, and prepare a town in iniquities, speaking iniquity against God and setting their mouth on high. And when they have done this, it is plainly shown that they build the city of themselves with bloodshed, and prepare it in iniquities.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","It can also be understood — although it does not suit the order of the reading, nor fit the present text of Scripture — of Jerusalem, the city full of the blood of the prophets, of which Scripture records that the blood of the saints overflowed in it from gate to gate. And which says at the Lord's passion: His blood be upon us and upon our children. And to which God speaks in Isaiah: When you lift up your hands to me, I will not hear you; for your hands are full of blood. This city was built in iniquities, in which, according to the same prophet, justice slept.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.13-14":["For there follows: Are not these things from the Lord Almighty? That is, such a building is not from the Lord Sabaoth, whom the LXX have here translated Almighty. For many peoples will fail, and although countless nations are led into their error, yet either they will grow weary, which ὠλιγοψύχησαν (they lost heart) rather signifies, or at any rate they will be pressed into a narrow space, and will not be able to be compared with the multitude of the Church. For while they fail in the fire, — which is understood either as their being consumed by the fire of the devil, their prince, or as their being kindled by the fire of the Lord, of which he says: I came to cast fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn, and drawn back from their former course, and doing penance, and abandoning the journey they had begun, which is what ὀλιγοψυχία (faintheartedness) means —; the whole earth will be filled with the glory of the Lord, when, at the preaching of the apostles, their sound has gone out into the whole world, like waters covering the sea, that is, so that the waters of the Lord may cover all the saltness and bitterness of the world, which the earth had drunk as the devil rained it down from above, and may cause the place of the sea and of the former bitterness not to appear. Hence it is also said in the psalm: Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Nor is its building from the Lord Sabaoth. Hence also many peoples failed in fire, and many nations grew weary at the time when it was surrounded by the armies of Vespasian and Titus, and those who came for the solemn day of the Passover were shut up in the city as in a prison, and failed through famine and want, and the siege of Hadrian brought them to the final ruins. And when the city of bloodshed and the town of iniquities had been overthrown, and the peoples consumed by fire, and the nations which had come to their help scattered this way and that and letting fall their weary hands, the whole earth was filled with the glory of Christ, and as with waters, so with his words and teaching the whole world was covered.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.13":["And because he did this to the city which he had built with bloodshed, he afterwards hears what is brought upon him by the Lord. For there follows: Are not these things from the Lord of hosts? That is, the things that are said: Peoples will labor in much fire, and nations in vain; that is, when Babylon has been burned, the peoples will labor in vain and strive for nothing, and the peoples of the Chaldean nation will fail.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.14":["For the earth will be filled so that it may know the glory of the Lord, that is, when Babylon has been overthrown, the might of God's power will become manifest to all; like waters covering the sea, so the whole earth will be filled with the glory of the Lord, just as the waters cover the bed and the bottom of the sea. These things, as we have said, are according to the letter.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.15-16":["Now these things are said through the metaphor of a drunken man, disgraced by nakedness, because Nebuchadnezzar made all men drunk with the cup of his fury, and saw them all stripped and captive, and those who had once been glorious were reduced to the utmost slavery; for this is what he says: He is filled with shame instead of glory, so that we supply \"the friend\" and \"the neighbor\", or the partner in the kingdom, who drank your cup, O Nebuchadnezzar. Hence, because you have made very many drunk, you also drink from the cup of the Lord and fall into a stupor; you will be surrounded by the punishments of the right hand of the Lord, and with the shame of your vomit you will discharge all that you had swallowed, and from lofty glory you will be brought down to the utmost evils.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","I heard at Lydda one of the Hebrews, who was called a wise man among them and a δευτερώτης (a teacher of the traditions), telling a tale of this sort: Zedekiah, he said, blinded by King Nebuchadnezzar at Riblah, that is, Antioch, and mocked in various ways, was led to Babylon. There, when one day Nebuchadnezzar was celebrating the festivities of a banquet, he ordered a potion to be given to him which, once drained, would loosen the drinker's belly into a flux; and Zedekiah, suddenly brought in before the faces of the banqueters and compelled by the force of his belly, was befouled with excrement; and this, he said, is what Scripture now says here: Woe to him who gives drink to his friend, putting in his gall and making him drunk, that he may look upon his nakedness, and shame instead of glory; namely, that he who had been a most powerful king was brought by him to such disgrace. Hence God also threatens him that he himself will drink a potion of this sort, and suffer everything that Zedekiah suffered. How ridiculous this is, you know without my saying it. For if what follows: Drink, you also, and fall into a stupor, and the cup of the right hand of the Lord will surround you, and the vomit of shame will be upon your glory, they understand not of a cup but of the evils which Nebuchadnezzar is to drink, then that cup too with which drink was offered to Zedekiah must be understood as evils, and not, as they will have it, as a καθαρτικὸν (a purgative). But if they say that it really happened, and that a potion of this sort was mixed, as I related above, then this cup too which Nebuchadnezzar is to drink must be reckoned full of a καθαρτικῷ, so that God Sabaoth and the Lord Almighty, for a great vengeance, offers Nebuchadnezzar a καθαρτικὸν to drink, and makes him befouled with his own excrement. So much against the Jewish tradition.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.15-17":["But if we wish to understand it of the Antichrist, or of the devil who will work in the Antichrist, he too, with his cup, with which he will desire to overturn the discipline of Christ, will make very many drunk, so that, drunk, they enter his caves; but after the end has come, instead of that glory with which he had magnified himself, he will be filled with shame. And he will be filled, because he will drink the cup of punishments, and he will be shaken, in no way firm in his malice, but trembling, and repenting too late. For the cup of the right hand of the Lord will surround him, the Lord who is also the Savior, when he slays him with the spirit of his mouth and destroys him with the illumination of his coming. Then all the shame which he gathered for himself by thoughts, deeds and words will come upon his glory, so that as renowned as he was formerly thought, so full of shame he will afterward be. For he blasphemed against God, and the impiety which he exercised in Lebanon will cover him, and the fury of many men who raged against the Church of God will be imputed to him, nor will he be able to lift his neck, but he will be pressed down to the earth by terror. For he slew many men, and laid waste with his impiety the whole world, that is, the Church of Christ and its inhabitants. It must be known, therefore, that this chapter too, which we have now expounded, that is: Woe to him who gives drink to his neighbor with a turbid overturning, and the three before it, in which it was said: Woe to him who multiplies for himself what is not his, and: Woe to him who gathers evil covetousness for his house, and: Woe to him who builds a city in blood, can be taken equally both according to the history and according to anagogy, either against Nebuchadnezzar or against the devil and the Antichrist and the heretics.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.15":["Woe to him who gives drink to his friend, putting in his gall and making him drunk, that he may look upon his nakedness. He is filled with shame instead of glory; drink, you also, and fall into a stupor. The cup of the right hand of the Lord will surround you, and the vomit of shame will be upon your glory, for the iniquity of Lebanon will cover you, and the devastation of the cattle will terrify them, because of the blood of men and the iniquity of the land, and of the city and of all who dwell in it. LXX: Woe to him who offers drink to his neighbor with a turbid overturning, and makes him drunk, that he may look into their caves; a fill of shame from glory drink, you also, and be shaken. The cup of the right hand of the Lord has surrounded you, and shame has been gathered upon your glory, for the impiety of Lebanon will cover you, and the misery of the beasts will terrify you, because of the blood of men, and the impieties of the land and of the city, and of all who dwell in it. For \"with a turbid overturning\", Symmachus translated: Καὶ ἀφιὼν ἀκρίτως τὸν θυμὸν ἑαυτοῦ, that is: And sending forth his fury without judgment. Theodotion: Ἀπὸ χύσεώς σου, which means: From your pouring out. The Fifth Edition: Ἐξ ἀπροσδοκήτου ἀνατροπῆς τῆς ὀργῆς σου, which signifies: From the unexpected overturning of your wrath. Aquila: Ἐξ ἐπιτρίψεως χόλου σου, which we can render: From the sending forth of your fury. In another edition I found: Ὀυαὶ τῷ ποτίζοντι τὸν ἐταῖρον αὐτοῦ ἀέλλην πετομένην, which in our language sounds: Woe to him who gives drink to his friend, a flying whirlwind. But I have also read it translated elsewhere: Woe to him who gives drink to his neighbor ἔκστασιν ὀχλουμένην, that is, turbid madness. I give this so that it may be known with how great a variety the Hebrew word maspha, which the LXX translated overturning, disagrees among all the editions. Therefore the invective is still against Nebuchadnezzar, because, forgetful of his condition, and as if not knowing that he was a man, he offered gall and bitterness to drink to another man. But we can understand him either as the king of Judea, or, generally, as all men, in that he made them drunk with evils, so that he might see the nakedness of Zedekiah and of all the captives. For this Symmachus and the Fifth Edition translated: That he might see their shames.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But let us come to the spiritual understanding. Woe to you, devil, or Antichrist, or perverse doctrine of the heretics, who with your teachings and your turbid potion make drunk the peoples of the deceived, and overturn their former faith, giving them a potion not from Siloam, not from the Jordan, not from the springs of Israel, but from the brook Kidron, and from the river of Egypt, of which Jeremiah says: What have you to do with the way of Egypt, that you should drink the water of Gihon? For this in the Hebrew is written sior, that is, turbid and muddy; for although the rivers of Egypt are believed to go out from the paradise of the Scriptures, yet because they are trampled by the feet of Pharaoh, they have lost their splendor, and, defiled by the Egyptian mud, have been turned into torrents, at having escaped which the holy one rejoices, saying: Our soul has passed through the torrent. But if anyone should object the brook Cherith, from which Elijah drank, and another torrent, from which the Lord drank on the way, for so it is written: He will drink from the torrent on the way, this must be said: that whoever is in Egypt and on the way of this age, though he be Moses and Aaron, though he be Jeremiah and Elijah, must nevertheless drink of the temptations of Egypt and of the wilderness; hence also the Word of the Lord, who had assumed flesh for this, that he might drink from the torrent, considering his majesty, said: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Again, seeing that he was in Egypt, and that the waters could not be cleansed unless he himself had drunk, he says: Nevertheless not as I will, but as you will. All this is said because the devil, with overturning and a turbid cup and every perverse doctrine, makes his neighbors drunk, that is, the rational animal, and makes those whom he has deceived look to his caves. For the doctrines of the Church are free; they rejoice in the day and the light. But those who are drunk are drunk at night; and those who make them drunk do not lead them into the courts of the Lord, which are weighed down by no shadow of roofs, but into caves. For they have made the house of the Father, which had been a house of prayer, caves of robbers, promising certain initiations and mysteries, and hidden secrets known only to heretics, of which Isaiah too speaks: And they will hide all things made by hand, bringing them into caves and into the clefts of the rocks and the holes of the earth. Let us not, then, enter the caves of the heretics, nor hide ourselves there, where the impious Saul was accustomed to void the excrement of his doctrines, but rather let us go up to the lofty cave of Mount Sinai, where Elijah saw the Lord, and Moses before him beheld his back parts. And Isaiah, crying out concerning the Lord: This one will dwell, he says, in a lofty cave. But if anyone does not have the turbid cup and heretical doctrine, and is a teacher of the Church, and does all things for the sake of shameful gain, and sells doves in the temple, that is, the gifts of the Holy Spirit, and in the priestly chair strangles the free birds, such a one does not indeed make the house of prayer a cave of robbers, but he makes the house of the Father a house of trade.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.16-17":["Moreover, because you offered drink to your neighbor with a turbid overturning, shame will be gathered upon you, and upon your glory, which you at first believed you had; and this you will suffer because the impiety of Lebanon will cover you, according to what is said: And let his prayer become sin. For Lebanon, in the Greek language, is ὀμώνυμος (of the same name) with frankincense; and frankincense is a σύμβολον (a symbol) of the spiritual incense, which is the worship of God. Therefore the perverse prayer of the heretics, not directed by the simplicity of the Gospel, will be turned for them into sin, and the impiety of their worship of God will cover them. Hence there follows: And the misery of the beasts will terrify you because of the blood of men, and the impieties of the land, and of the city and of all who dwell in it. And the sense is: When you see those whom you deceived by your frauds, and from sheep of Christ made into your beasts, in misery and enduring punishments for their error, then you will be terrified, then you will lie prostrate. And lest you think, because I have named Lebanon and its beasts, that I am speaking of brute animals and not of men, I tell you more plainly: You will endure these things because you shed the blood of many men, whom you caused to perish to God. And you exercised impiety in the land of the living, in the land of the meek, and your impiety raged also in the city of the Lord, that is, in his Church, and you made many who dwelt in it partakers of your impiety. Let this be said in the person of the heretics.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.16":["After this follows: A fill of shame instead of glory drink, you, and be shaken, that is: O devil, O perverse doctrine, O heretic, who thought yourself a golden cup with which all the nations are made drunk, so that they might see your caves and your secrets, instead of the greatness of your glory be filled with a fill of shame, and be reckoned as an earthen vessel, the works of the potter's hands; drink, you also, from the cup of the Lord, of which it is said in the psalm: A cup is in the hand of the Lord, full of pure wine mixed, and he has tilted it from this into that; yet its dregs have not been emptied out. And be shaken from your former opinion, and do not think firm and stable the things in which you at first desired to stand, because the cup of the right hand of the Lord has surrounded you.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.17":["For the iniquity of Lebanon will cover you: your pride, he says, and the overthrow of the temple, and the plundering of the sanctuary will lay you waste and devastate you. And because he had once named Mount Lebanon, under the metaphor of the same mountain he compares the victims and the sacrifices, or certainly the multitude of the peoples which was slain in Jerusalem, to animals or beasts, saying: And the devastation of the animals will crush you. But all this you will suffer because you laid waste Judah, overturned the land of promise, and the city of Jerusalem and all its inhabitants.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.18":["What does the graven image profit, since its maker has graven it, a molten thing and a false image? Since its maker has hoped in his own fashioning, so as to make dumb idols. LXX: What does the graven image profit, since they have graven it; they have formed it a molten thing, a false image? Since the maker trusts in his own fashioning, so as to make dumb idols. In sequence with what precedes, it is said of Nebuchadnezzar that he fashioned a statue of the idol Bel and set it up in the plain of Dura, or, as it is written in the Hebrew, Dora, about which we read more fully in Daniel. And so Scripture marvels at the madness and folly of the king, in that he himself ordered a golden statue to be made, and the maker trusts in the image that he has fashioned; and this we can indeed also take generally against all the worshipers of idols. Nor let us think that what is graven and what is molten are one and the same. For carving we can take as done in stones and in marbles, while casting is understood of those metals which can be melted and cast: for example, gold, silver, bronze, lead and tin. Let this be said so that, according to tropology, we may know what the difference is between a graven thing and a molten thing. We read in Deuteronomy: Cursed is everyone who makes a graven and a molten thing, the work of the hands of the craftsman, and sets it in a hidden place. The graven and the molten thing, I think, are perverse doctrines, which are adored by those by whom they were made. See how Arius carved for himself the idol of a creature, and adored what he carved. Behold how Eunomius cast a false image, and bows his neck to his own casting. And Scripture says pointedly: And he will set it, he says, in a hidden place. For they too have their orgies, and to certain disciples, as if to the perfect, they hand on hidden mysteries, which, if they come forth to the light, are at once convicted of being fictions. Their carving and their casting, therefore, will profit them nothing. Carving, which refers to stones, is understood of those doctrines which display their folly at first sight. Casting is found where there seems to be some reasoning of secular wisdom, and, as if with a kind of gold, so with the disciplines of the philosophers and the splendor of eloquence, the idol has been cast. The fashioned thing, then, will profit its maker nothing. And the dumb and deaf image will not be able to hear its worshiper. If ever you see someone unwilling to believe the truth, and, though the falsity of his doctrines has been shown, persevering in the pursuit he has begun, you will fittingly be able to say: He hopes in his own fashioning, and makes dumb or deaf images; for κωφά among the Greeks signifies both, although Symmachus, translating ἄλαλα, seems to have understood dumb rather than deaf. Nor let anyone be troubled by that ἰδίωμα (idiom) of the Scriptures, of which we have often spoken: Who, do you think, is the faithful and wise steward? And in another place: Who is wise, and will understand these things? In that \"who\" or \"what\" is taken for \"rare\", since we can also take this very expression from another place for \"impossible\": Who will separate us from the love of Christ: tribulation, or distress, and so on? And from the present chapter: What does the graven image profit, since its maker has graven it? For in both, impossibility is shown: that neither can any tribulation and distress separate the apostle's love from Christ, nor is there any profit in idols.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.19":["Woe to him who says to the wood: Wake up; arise, to the silent stone; can it teach? Behold, it is overlaid with gold and silver, and no spirit at all is in its inward parts. But the Lord is in his holy temple; let all the earth keep silence before his face. LXX: Woe to him who says to the wood: Awake and rise up, and to the stone: Be lifted up. And it is an image, and it is a beaten work of gold and silver, and no spirit at all is in it. But the Lord is in his holy temple; let all the earth revere him before his face. This too can likewise be taken either against Nebuchadnezzar or against all who venerate idols. And human error is described, in that men think the silver, gold, gems and silk with which the idols are either wrapped or covered to be gods, because of the brilliance of the material, although the craftsman could indeed give them form, but could not give the soul by which limbs are quickened.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","But if anyone should object, how can it be said that there is no spirit in idols, when unclean spirits attend all images, let him learn the custom of holy Scripture: that a perverse spirit is never called spirit absolutely, but always with some addition, as there: They were led astray by a spirit of fornication. And in the Gospel: But when an unclean spirit has gone out of a man, and other things like these. But wherever spirit is read alone and absolutely, without any addition, it always refers to the good side, that is, to the Holy Spirit, as in that saying of the Apostle: He who sows in the Spirit will reap from the Spirit eternal life, and elsewhere: But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, and in another place: Walk by the Spirit, and you will not fulfill the desires of the flesh. Nor do we say this as though the Holy Spirit too were not set down with an addition: for he is called both the Holy Spirit, and the princely spirit, and the right spirit, and the spirit of God, and other things like these; but we say that the Holy Spirit is often set down both with an addition and alone, whereas a perverse spirit has nowhere been read except with an addition. But this too can be said, if anyone contentiously wishes to take a perverse spirit in this place: that it is one thing to say: No spirit at all is in it, and another if he had said: No spirit at all is around it; for a spirit can attend images, but it cannot be within them. Hence Aquila too rendered the Hebrew more expressively, saying: And its spirit is not in its inward parts, or in its midst. Moreover, it should be known that in certain Hebrew volumes \"all\" is not added, but spirit is read absolutely. But if anyone, overcome by reason, takes spirit in the good sense, and asks why, if it is said of the Holy Spirit, it is read with an addition: And no spirit at all is in it, let him know that by \"every spirit\" the various graces of the Holy Spirit are understood; so that the sense is: It will be able to have in itself nothing of grace, nothing of power. And this indeed is better understood according to tropology: that in all the idols of the heretics and the fashionings of the devil there is no grace of the Holy Spirit, but they seem to display an image of divinity and a beauty of doctrines, while in them there is nothing breathing and living. Let us say this too, lest we seem to have hidden from the reader what we know: that spirit and wind are called among the Hebrews by the same word, that is, rua, and that according to the sense of the passage it is customarily taken either as spirit or as wind. Therefore in this place too we can take spirit either for wind, in that nothing breathes in idols, or for the soul, in that the images are animated. And that spirit is taken for the soul the Savior's prayer clearly signifies: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit. For Jesus could not commend to the Father either a perverse spirit, which it is impious even to think, or the Holy Spirit, who is himself also God, rather than his own soul, of which he had said: My soul is sorrowful even to death, and: No one can take my soul from me, but by my will I lay down my soul, and of my own accord I take it up again.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.2.20":["And on the contrary the Lord is said to be in his holy temple; not in a temple made by hands, but either in the heavens, or in each of the saints, according to the Apostle, who says: Do you not know that you are the temple of God, and the spirit of God dwells in you? And elsewhere: Your bodies are the temple of God; or in the Son, as he himself says: The Father who abides in me, he does the works. Or certainly according to that saying, that the heavens and the lands, the seas and the whole world:\n\nA spirit within sustains them, and mind, poured through all the limbs,\n\nmoves the whole mass, and mingles itself with the great body.\n\nThe whole world, which consists of heaven and earth and is enclosed by the circles of the heavens, is said to be the house of God. Hence the Apostle too says confidently: For in him, he says, we live, and move, and are.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.1":["A prayer of Habakkuk the prophet for ignorances. LXX: A prayer of Habakkuk the prophet with a song. Aquila and Symmachus and the Fifth Edition translated, as we did, for ignorances; Theodotion alone ὑπὲρ τῶν ἐκουσιασμῶν, that is, for voluntary sins, and for those who sin of their own accord. This is said so that we may understand — the Seventy excepted — that no one translated a prayer with a song. For in the Hebrew too it has al segionoth, which is rendered Ἐπὶ ἀγνοημάτων, and we translated for ignorances. Now the sense is this: earlier he had rashly said: How long, O Lord, shall I cry, and you will not hear? Shall I cry out to you, suffering violence, and you will not save? And then, in the second complaint: Why do you not look upon those who act unjustly, and keep silent, while the wicked devours one more just than himself? To this he had heard: Write the vision, and make it plain upon tablets. And after other things: Behold, he who is unbelieving, his soul will not be upright in himself; but the just shall live in his faith. And he had learned that either Nebuchadnezzar, or the devil, or the Antichrist had been set for the judgment of sinners, and strong to chastise the nations; now he does penance, and laments that he spoke rashly, and asks pardon, so that he may obtain mercy, because he acted in ignorance. Hence David too says: The sins, he says, of my youth, and my ignorances, do not remember. There are some who think that it is a prayer of the prophet that at the coming of Christ the error of men may be taken away, and that for this reason the name of prophet is set at the head of the title, because by the prophetic spirit he prays that the darkness be removed and the light restored, the image taken away and the truth bestowed. But that it is a prophecy, the whole fabric of the canticle shows, and above all this: In the midst of two living creatures you will be known, and: When the years draw near, you will be known, and, concerning the judgment: When my soul is troubled in wrath, you will remember mercy, and again, concerning the coming of Christ: God will come from Teman, and the Holy One from the shady and thick mountain, and also concerning the future: The tents of the land of Midian will tremble, and you will mount upon your horses, and stretching you will stretch your bow over kingdoms. But if anyone asks why Theodotion alone translated for spontaneous sins, we can say that it shows either the confession of the prophet, that he sinned not under some necessity but of his own accord, judging wrongly of the judgment of God, or certainly the future faith of the nations, in that, voluntarily abandoning their former error, they were going to believe in him who is promised in the canticle. We read in Psalm 16 (17): A prayer of David. And in another likewise. A prayer of David. And in Psalm 89 (90): A prayer of Moses, the man of God. And in Psalm 101 (102): A prayer of the poor man when he was in anguish, and poured out his petition before the Lord. And wherever else the name of prayer is brought in, yet in no place is it read with a song. And I do not know whether it is fitting to pray with a song, unless perhaps, following the Seventy, we say that the prophet prays for the coming of Christ, and prophesies this very thing with delight, and with psalms and song, so that in that it is a prayer he may entreat the Father, and in that it is a song, praise may be spoken of the Father, who sent the Son, and of the Son, who came. So much on the title of the canticle. Let us now see what is said in the canticle.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.2":["O Lord, I have heard your hearing, and I was afraid. O Lord, your work, in the midst of the years, revive it. LXX: O Lord, I have heard your report, and I was afraid. O Lord, I considered your works, and I was astonished; in the midst of two living creatures you will be known. Where we and Aquila and Theodotion translated revive it, Symmachus rendered revive it again. But what the Seventy said, I considered and I was astonished, is found neither in the Hebrew nor in any of the other translators, so that, when the things not found in the Hebrew are removed, one can read according to the Seventy: O Lord, your works in the midst of two living creatures you will be known; and because this seemed ἀδιανόητον (unintelligible), the preceding words were joined to it. Now we read in the Hebrew adonai, that is, O Lord, phalach, your work, bacereb, in the midst, sanim, of years heieu, revive it. This is said so that we may recognize that what is superfluous in the Seventy has plainly been added. The Hebrews explain this place according to the history thus: O Lord, I have heard your hearing, and I was afraid. I have heard, he says, the punishments which you have prepared for Nebuchadnezzar and for the devil, in which you said to him: Woe to him who multiplies what is not his. And secondly: Woe to him who gathers evil avarice for his house. And thirdly: Woe to him who builds a city in bloodshed, and prepares a town in iniquity. And fourthly: Woe to him who gives drink to his friend, putting in his gall and making him drunk. And fifthly: Woe to him who says to the wood: Awake; arise, to the silent stone. And just as I was terrified with fear that the great dragon is to be pierced with so many wounds, so I pray, O Lord, that you fulfil what you have promised, and, when the time is completed, render your Christ. For you said that the vision is still far off, and will appear at the end, and will not lie. Revive, therefore, what you have promised, that is, fulfil your promise; let your word not die unfulfilled, but be accomplished in deed. This indeed, according to our version, can also be understood of the resurrection of the Savior, that he who died for us may rise from the dead and be brought to life. But according to the Seventy the sense is far different, and we ought also to set down the explanation of the common edition. O Lord, I have heard your word in the Scriptures, and since you gave me an ear, according to what Isaiah says: He gave me in addition an ear to hear, I heard in such a way as you wish your word to be heard. And having contemplated your works more diligently, lest it be said to me: But the works of the Lord they do not regard, and the works of his hands they do not consider, I understood the Creator from the creatures; and at each of the things which you have made, and which you work daily in the whole world, I was wholly astonished, and having lost the sense of human nature, I was turned into a holy madness. Or certainly, troubled with wonder, I break out trembling into your praises, saying: In the midst of two living creatures you will be known. Many think this is to be understood of the Son and the Holy Spirit, because the Father is understood through the Son and the Spirit. These indeed they interpret as both the two Seraphim in Isaiah and the two Cherubim which are written of in Exodus, which look toward each other and have the oracle in the midst; and that in Isaiah, veiling the head and the feet of the Lord, they fly in the present age only, and cry out to one another the mystery of the Trinity, and that one of the Seraphim, which means burning, is sent and comes to the earth and cleanses the prophet's lips, and says: I have come to send fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn. So others judge, and for this interpretation they use many testimonies of the Scriptures. Moreover, the simple interpretation and the opinion of the common people understand it of the Savior, in that he was recognized when crucified between two thieves. But those who interpret better say this: that in the first Church, which was gathered from the circumcision and from the foreskin, with the two peoples surrounding him on this side and on that, the Savior was understood and believed. There are some who understand the two living creatures as the two testaments, the new and the old, which are truly living beings, which are full of life, which breathe, and in whose midst the Lord is known.\n\nIn the midst of the years you will make it known. LXX: When the years draw near, you will be known. When the time comes, he says, and you have fulfilled your promises in deed, you will show that what you promised is true. Or else: when the consummation draws near, and at the last hour your Son comes to destroy sins, you will be known more manifestly. There follows:\n\nWhen the time comes, you will be shown, when my soul is troubled. This, apart from the Seventy, is found neither in the Hebrew nor in any of the other translators. And the sense is: When the time comes of which it is said: In an acceptable time I have heard you, the time of the coming of the Lord Jesus Christ, then, O God the Father, your name will be known, which before was hidden from men, of which the Lord says in the Gospel: Father, I have revealed your name to men. But what is added: When my soul is troubled, in the edition of the LXX translators is joined with what follows, so that it can be read: When my soul is troubled in wrath, and there the division falls; next is added: You will remember mercy, meaning that for the prophet trouble alone is enough as punishment, and that, his soul being troubled at the wrath of God, no penalty at all is inflicted, but mercy shuts out wrath. Now the wrath of God too has its measures: how much, and for how long, and for what causes, and on whom it is inflicted, according to what is written: You will feed us with the bread of tears, and give us tears to drink in measure. But if the prophet is troubled at the wrath of God, and he who is troubled obtains mercy, what ought we to hope, or rather to fear, all of whose works are worthy of the wrath of God? But as for what follows according to the Hebrew.\n\nWhen you are angry, you will remember mercy, we must not think that God forgets and after his wrath remembers his mercy, but that we, set in punishment, think he forgets, according to this: How long, O Lord, will you forget me, to the end? For whenever we too are covered by temptations as by waves, and the swift storm of the demons rages against us, we speak as to one asleep: Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord? And at the same time observe the clemency of God: he did not say: When you inflict punishments, you will remember mercy, but: When you are angry. Now he who is angry sometimes does not strike, but only threatens. The Apostle too, perceiving this, says: For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and iniquity of men. But where it is revealed, it is not inflicted, it does not strike, but it is revealed to terrify, and so that it may not be inflicted on those who are terrified.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.3-4":["His glory covered the heavens, and the earth is full of his praise.\n\nHis brightness will be as light, horns in his hands. There his strength is hidden. LXX: His power covered the heavens, and the earth is full of his praise, and his brightness will be like light; horns in his hands, and he placed a mighty love of his strength. Where the Seventy translated: And he placed a mighty love of his strength, and we said: There his strength is hidden, Aquila translated: And he placed the hiding of his strength; Symmachus: And he placed his strength hidden; Theodotion alone, agreeing with our translation, says: And there is the hiding of his strength. For the word sam, according to the character of the place, is understood both as he placed and as there, and in the present place there should be read rather than he placed, so that the sense and order of the sentence is: Horns in his hands, and let there be understood, there, that is, in the horns his strength is hidden. Now it is manifest according to the Hebrew that at the coming of Christ all things were filled with glory, according to what is said in the Gospel: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace to men of good will. And elsewhere: He made peace in the heavens and on earth through the blood of the cross, and sat at the right hand of the majesty: For his word runs swiftly. And elsewhere: O Lord, our Lord, how wonderful is your name in all the earth. And again in Psalm 18 (19): Their sound has gone out into all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world. His brightness also, as the sun of justice, shone forth with clear light, and horns are in his hands, the standards and trophies of the cross, and in those very horns his strength is hidden: For though he was in the form of God, he did not think it robbery to be equal to God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant. He became obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross. On the cross, therefore, his strength was for a little while hidden, when he said to the Father: My soul is sorrowful even unto death. And: Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. And on the cross itself: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.3":["God will come from the south, and the Holy One from Mount Paran. Always. LXX: God will come from Teman, and the Holy One from the shady and thick mountain. Diapsalma. Aquila and Symmachus and the Fifth Edition set down the Hebrew word itself, Teman; Theodotion alone translated what Teman means, saying: Eloim will come from the south, and the Holy One from Mount Paran, to the end. From this we understand that From the shady and thick mountain was set down by the Seventy alone. But also what the Seventy translated διάψαλμα, and we rendered always, Symmachus translated: For ever; Theodotion: To the end; the Fifth Edition the Hebrew word itself: sela. God, therefore, will come from the south, that is, from the midday, from the clear light, and from those who are called sons of the days. Hence in the Song of Songs too the bridegroom drives away the north wind and calls the south wind, saying: Arise, north wind, and come, south wind, blow through my garden, and let my spices flow. God is always at midday: Where, she says, do you pasture? Where do you lie down? At midday. And to Abraham, when he was under the oak, God came only at midday. And Joseph, who went before as a type of the Savior, makes a banquet for his brothers at midday. The knowledge of God the Father, therefore, comes to those who are worthy in full light. And the knowledge of the Holy One, that is, of the Son of God, comes from the shady and thick mountain. The shady and thick mountain is understood either as the Father himself, full of powers and of all wisdom, protecting all things with his majesty, and stretching out his wings, and cherishing his young; or certainly as paradise and the heavenly places, full of angels, full of powers, full of most fruitful trees. And would that it might befall me too that, at my voice and my exposition, God may come in clear light, and his Son, of whom it is written: Be holy, for I am holy; that from the loftiness of a discourse shady and thick, and woven together on this side and that from the testimonies of the Scriptures, with the Father and the Son coming together, the hearer may become their dwelling, and the Scripture be fulfilled which says: I and my Father will come to him, and will make our abode with him. But since in place of the shady and thick mountain it is written in the Hebrew Mount Paran, and Paran means the mouth of one seeing, then beautifully, according to our interpretation, the knowledge of the Son comes from the discourse of a learned man, and not from any discourse whatever, but from one which is full of light, full of eyes, so that it may be carried, clear and pure, to the ears of the hearers. And as for his saying from the mountain, understand lofty doctrines. I have heard a Hebrew discuss this place thus: that Bethlehem, in which our Lord and Savior was born, lies to the south, and that he is the one of whom it is now said: The Lord will come from the south, that is, he will be born in Bethlehem and will rise up from there. And because he who was born in Bethlehem once gave the law on Mount Sinai, he is the Holy One who comes from Mount Paran. For Paran is a place near Mount Sinai. And what is added, diapsalma, that is, always, has this sense: He who was born in Bethlehem, and who gave the law on Sinai, that is, on Mount Paran, is always, in all benefits, past and present and future, both their author and their giver. Of the diapsalma, which in Hebrew is called sela, there has been fuller discussion on the psalter. And at the same time observe that, according to the Seventy, diapsalma is not set down except in the psalter and in the present place. From this we understand that the song of the prayer was rightly noted in its title by the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","Moreover, according to the Seventy, in what is said: His power covered the heavens, we ought to know that what is covered is less than what covers it: provided, however, that what is covered is covered wholly and not in part. Since, therefore, the power of God covers the heavens, his power, by which the heavens are covered, is greater than the heavens. And we often read that the heavens are those who bear the image of the one above the heavens, and who tell the glory of God. That the Lord, the Savior, is also the Power of God, the Apostle proves: Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God. This power is, as it were, the mother of all the particular virtues; for example, he is called power, wisdom, strength, justice, temperance, truth, holiness, redemption. But Christ was made for us by God wisdom, and justice, and sanctification, and redemption. These particular virtues, therefore, in which Christ is shown — according to the progress of those who receive him either as wisdom, or as strength, or as justice, and the other things of this kind —, are contained in the general power of God, that is, in the Lord, the Savior; and in this way we understand it of the earth too: that those who at first, because of the image of the earthly one, were called earth, and to whom it was said: You are earth, and to earth you shall go, are filled at the coming of the Savior with the praise of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.4":["And when both the heavens have been covered by the power of God — that is, protected and clothed on every side —, and the whole earth has been filled with the praise of God, then his brightness will be as light. Now the Apostle is not silent that God the Savior is the image of God and the brightness of his glory. He, after he appeared to us as the brightness of the glory of God, returned to his former majesty. For even if we had known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we no longer know him according to the flesh, but according to the spirit, because: What was made, in him was life, and the life was the light of men. The Savior too shows this more manifestly in the Gospel, saying: Father, glorify me with the glory which I had with you before the world was made, so that after the ascension to the heavens the brightness may be what the light is, that is, that the Son may begin to be what the Father is. And as for what follows: Horns in his hands, it is the custom of the Scriptures always to put horns for kingdoms. For that too which Hannah says in the first book of Kingdoms (1 Samuel): He exalted the horn of his anointed, signifies the magnificence of the kingdom of the Savior. And in Daniel the ten horns show ten kingdoms. And it is now said: Horns in his hands, in the same way as we read written elsewhere: The heart of the king is in the hand of God, meaning that the mind and the ruling part of the heart of a holy man — who is going on to the kingdom of heaven, who while still placed on earth reigns over his body without sins — does not wander outside, but is set in the keeping of God. But since both in the Hebrew and in the other editions it is not written: Horns in his hands, but in his hand, which is said iado, let us understand the strong and mighty hand of God to be his Son. And let us say that in this hand are placed all the kingdoms of heaven, and of those who strive to ascend to heaven, as Isaiah too sounds, saying: A vineyard was made for the beloved on a horn, in a fertile place, meaning in a kingdom. For this reason, I think, no horned animal is placed among the unclean in Leviticus, and the unicorn in the psalms, or ῥινοκερότα (rhinoceros), signifies this same thing, and this too: In you we will toss our enemies with the horn. And as for what we read according to the LXX: And he placed a strong love of his power, this too is to be understood of Christ: that God the Father covered the heavens with his power, and filled the earth with praise, and made his brightness to be as light, and placed the kingdom in the hand of his Son, for this reason, that he might make his beloved be loved by men, and loved not lightly, but vehemently and strongly, so that no one might take out of his hand those who had loved him strongly and clung in his love. On the contrary, the devil makes us love the world, and in place of the love of virtue love vices, and not lightly, but strongly, so that it can be said of us: And the devil placed a strong love of his vices.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.5":["Before his face death will go, and the devil will go forth before his feet. LXX: Before his face the word will go, and it will go forth into the field after his feet. Where we translated death, in the Hebrew three letters are set down, daleth, beth, res, without any vowel; if they are read dabar, they signify word; if deber, pestilence, which in Greek is called λοιμός. Accordingly Aquila too translated thus: Before his face pestilence will go; Symmachus: Before his face death will go before; the Fifth Edition: Before his face death will walk; the LXX and Theodotion alone translated word in place of death. So too in the following verse, where we said: The devil will go forth before his feet, and the LXX translated otherwise, following whom we shall discuss it afterwards, Aquila translated, in place of devil, a winged thing; but Symmachus and Theodotion and the Sixth Edition, a bird, which in Hebrew is called Reseph. Now the Hebrews hand down that, just as in the Gospel the prince of demons is said to be Beelzebub, so Reseph is the name of a demon who holds the chief place among the others, and because of his excessive swiftness and his running about in different directions he is called a bird and a winged thing; and that he is the one who in paradise spoke to the woman under the figure of a serpent, and received his name from the curse by which he was condemned by God, since Reseph means creeping on the belly. This, therefore, is what is said: As soon as the Lord has come and has been baptized in the Jordan, and at the descent of the dove the voice of the Father has thundered: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased, the devil will meet him as he comes out of the waters, and before his feet will stand death and the ancient serpent, who tempted him for forty days in the wilderness. But if we read according to the LXX: Before his face the word will go, and it will go forth into the fields after his feet, this signifies that the word of God goes before his visitation, which is now allegorically called his face, and prepares the hearts of believers, so that it may make the crooked straight and level the uneven into a plain, and the soul of the hearer, like a ploughed field, may be able to receive the spiritual seed.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.6":["He stood and measured the earth; he looked, and dissolved the nations, and the mountains of the age were crushed; the hills of the world were bowed down, by the journeys of his eternity. LXX: He stood, and the earth was shaken; he looked, and the nations wasted away; the mountains were crushed by violence, the hills of the age of his everlasting journey melted away. The Savior, standing and surveying all things, and measuring with his eye the whole of the world, scattered the multitude of the nations; and when these were scattered and dissolved, the mountains of this age were crushed, and the hills of this world were bowed down. For there are also other mountains and hills, which the bridegroom leaps and bounds over in the Song of Songs, of which it is said also in the second Psalm of Ascents: I lifted up my eyes to the mountains, from whence help will come to me. But the mountains of the age are the very ones that are also the dark mountains, concerning which Jeremiah commanded that our feet not strike against them. These are the hills on which Saul reigned when he was killing the priests of God: for Gibeah means hill. And elegantly, he says, the hills of the world were bowed down. For before the coming of the Savior they walked with neck erect, and no one could humble their pride. And they were crushed and bowed down by the journeys of his eternity, that is, of God's, because his eternity deigned to come to us; or else because always, from the beginning of the world up to his incarnation, he came to the saints, and the word of God came to be in the hand of each one, and in all who conquered he himself prevailed, and his eternal journey bowed down and crushed the hills and the mountains. Let these things be said by way of metaphor according to the Hebrew. Moreover, according to the LXX, after the word has gone before the face of God and has gone forth into the plains, God the Father comes there, where a region has been prepared for him by his word, and he comes after the feet of his word and stands; never going before, but always waiting for the word to pave the way for him. And where he has stood beside the feet of his word, there at once the earth, that is, the works of the flesh and of bodies, which cannot endure the presence of God, are shaken. And when they have been shaken, the power of the word and the presence of God looks upon all the nations of the soul, which we can understand as the thoughts and the manifold opinions pulling in different directions, which are at once dissolved and waste away. If anything too had exalted itself on the earth against the knowledge of God, and had taken possession of the mind of the hearer, it will be broken and crushed, as the word goes before and God comes. And when the mountains have been broken and crushed at the sight of God, the hills will clearly be consumed and brought to nothing. For they are not the mountains of God, but the mountains of the age. For the everlasting journey of God, looking to the things which his word goes before, and being stronger than the hills of the age, will consume and destroy them. Now the mountains can also be understood as the demons who dwell in the heretics and lift themselves up against the knowledge of God; and the hills as the other powers of the demons, which make men admire the beauty of bodies, ranks, riches, nobility of birth, and the other goods of the world. One may see, after the coming of the word of God and the presence of God the Father, how human souls are shaken, and all that is earthly is dissolved, and former thoughts are brought to nothing. Then the demons are destroyed, then the heights of the age are brought down to nothing, and all the knowledge of the heretics, which at first was swelling, is humbled, crushed and consumed at the coming of the word of God. And what before seemed beautiful and very great is cast away as despised and small. And this happens because of the coming of God and the lodging of Christ, according to what is written elsewhere: I will dwell in them, and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.7":["For iniquity I saw the tents of Ethiopia; the skins of the land of Midian will be troubled. LXX: For labors I saw the tabernacles of the Ethiopians; the tabernacles of the land of Midian also will tremble. The Ethiopians, foul and lovers of darkness, and strangers to all light, who feed on the flesh of the dragon, of which it is written: You gave him as food to the peoples of the Ethiopians, are understood as the demons, whose tabernacle becomes whoever in this age has labored for honors and riches; and this is significantly shown under the single word of iniquity: for every rich man is either unjust or the heir of an unjust man. See men cross the seas, keep watch before the doors of the powerful, suffer everything that the condition of slaves scarcely suffers, in order to gather riches, to obtain some rank, and, once they have achieved this, give themselves over to luxury and pleasures and every iniquity, so that what avarice has gathered, luxury may consume. These, therefore, for their labors are made a lodging of demons, and those who ought to have been the temple of God become the tabernacle of the Ethiopians. But also in what follows: The skins of the land of Midian will be troubled, or the tabernacles of the land of Midian also will tremble, understand the same people as the tabernacles of the Ethiopians and the tabernacles of the land of Midian. For after they have grown rich, and by right and wrong have climbed to the highest rank, then, from the consciousness of their sins, they will always dread death, always dread judgment, and at a slight fever, like robbers in prison, so they will sigh over the eternal punishments. Now Midian in our tongue sounds \"from judgment\", that is, condemnation; and it is shown that through fear of judgment and of eternal penalties they are always in dread, and that the torments which they feel they deserve, they endure in daily terror.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.8-9":["Were you angry with the rivers, O Lord, or was your fury against the rivers, or your indignation against the sea? For you will mount upon your horses, and your chariots are salvation. Rousing, you will rouse your bow, the oaths to the tribes which you have spoken. Always. LXX: Were you angry with the rivers, O Lord, or was your fury against the rivers, or your onrush against the sea? You who mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation, bending, stretching your bow against the scepters, says the Lord. Diapsalma. Where the Seventy have diapsalma and Aquila always, the others translated likewise as above. And since my discourse hastens on to the tropological interpretation, I shall briefly take in the sense of the passage according to the letter and proceed to the rest. Just as you dried up the Jordan and the Red Sea, fighting for us — for you were not angry with the rivers and the sea, nor could senseless things incur any offense —, so now, mounting your chariots and seizing your bow, you will give salvation to your people, and the oaths which you swore to our fathers and to the tribes you will fulfill forever.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.8":["But as for what he says: Were you angry with the rivers, O Lord, or was your fury against the rivers, or your onrush against the sea? He says it ambiguously, and with the speech of one asking rather than of one affirming. For there are both good rivers and bad ones. There is a very evil sea, and there is a very good sea. Of good rivers this is an example: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God. And that whoever drinks of the water of the Lord, from his belly will flow rivers of water springing up into eternal life. Of bad ones, that which Pharaoh says in Ezekiel: The rivers are mine, and I made them, in which the dragon dwells, and many things like these. And that the sea is taken in a good sense, Psalm 23 (24) is witness, in which it is said tropologically of the Church under οἰκουμένη, that is, the world: The earth is the Lord's and the fullness thereof, the world and all who dwell in it: he founded it upon the sea, and prepared it upon the rivers: that is, the world. And that it is founded by the Lord upon the rivers and prepared upon the sea is certainly taken in a good sense. So also what is said of the vine which was transplanted out of Egypt: You stretched out its shoots as far as the sea, and its branches as far as the rivers; as far as the sea, I think, can be taken in a good sense. And we say that the divine words which are plainer, and offer as it were drink to the thirsty, are called rivers; but those which are full of mysteries and set in the depth, of which the Apostle says: O the depth of the wisdom and knowledge of God, and the prophet agrees: Out of the depths I have cried to you, O Lord, are called the sea in the Scriptures. So much for the fact that the sea too can be understood in the better part. And that it is also taken in the contrary sense, there are many testimonies, among which is this in the psalms: This great and spacious sea; there the ships will pass through, small creatures with great. This dragon which you formed to make sport of it. And from the Gospel, when the Savior rebuked the winds and the sea and said to it: Be silent and be still. For what is rebuked is evil, according to that word of Zechariah: The Lord rebuke you, O devil. And to Timothy: Reprove, console and rebuke. So when the prophet asks: Were you angry with the rivers, O Lord, or was your fury against the rivers, or your onrush against the sea?, let us say: if they are the rivers of Egypt, and the red and bloody sea, the Lord is angry and strikes them, and is borne with all his force against the whirlpools that raise themselves against the sentence of God. Hence the sea saw and fled, not enduring to bear the presence of God. And the Jordan was turned back, yielding to the glory of the people passing over, the Jordan which is parted both by Elijah and by Elisha. But to speak more plainly, understand as the rivers with which the Lord is angry the eloquence of the heretics, which flows against the truth, even the truth of the Church. And the souls of those who are carried about by every wind of doctrine, and always toss with malice, and are overwhelmed by salt whirlpools: regard them as the sea upon which the Lord's onrush comes, so that it may feel his coming, and know by what bounds and barrier it is shut in, and hear: In you your waves will be broken. But if the rivers are good and the sea good, Jesus is washed in them, and he sets his Church upon a sea of this kind. After this follows: You who mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation. I ask what horses the Lord mounts, and I think they are none other than the souls of the saints, upon which the divine Word mounts, to save both them and others through them. Let us set out examples of horses. The Bridegroom speaks in the Song of Songs: To my cavalry among the chariots of Pharaoh he has likened you, my near one. Not that Christ compares the Church, or the Word of God the soul which he calls his bride, to the chariots of Pharaoh, but that every soul, however holy and perfect it may be, compared with God is as it were a chariot of Pharaoh, and a beast of burden. Hence Moses too says to the Lord: But I am ἄλογος, that is, irrational. And David: I have become like a beast of burden before you. Not that he is a beast of burden absolutely, but that he is a beast of burden before God. Opposed to these horses are those which Pharaoh has, and of which it is said: Horse and rider he has cast into the sea; riding of this kind is not salvation but perdition. Let us seek other horses also, which the Lord mounts. In the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) we read that Elisha's servant rose early, and going out saw an army surrounding the walls of the city, and horses and chariots. And after his eyes were opened at the prophet's prayers: He looked, it says, and behold, the mountain was full of horses, and chariots of fire round about Elisha. Attend carefully to this: horses are seen, and chariots, and yet among so many thousands of horses and chariots there is no rider; of these horses he was the charioteer and the driver of whom the psalmist sings: You who sit upon the Cherubim, show yourself. With such horses and such a chariot Elijah too was snatched up to heaven. But if anyone wishes to learn from Zechariah which are the red horses, and which the black, and which the dappled, and which the white, coming out of the myrtle groves and from the mountains set in the depth, or, as is written in the Seventy, the mountains of bronze, we shall try to explain it in that prophet himself, if the Lord grants a span of life. John too saw white horses and their riders, of whom I think the bodies of those who rise in glory are the white horses, and the souls of the saints the riders. But if anyone is a sinner, and like me, he will sit on a black horse, and it will be said of him: All who mounted horses have slumbered. Of such horses it is written: A horse is deceitful for salvation. For the flesh lusts against the spirit, and its wisdom is hostile to God. Let this be said of those who love bodies and sit on black horses. But let us prepare our souls as horses and chariots of the Lord, who mounted in Paul, mounted in Peter, and riding in chariots of this kind, went over the whole world.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.9":["Bending also his bow or his arrows, he uprooted, destroyed and laid waste the scepters, that is, the kingdoms against which Jeremiah was sent, and brought it about that sin should not reign in our mortal body. And by the scepters, that is, the kingdoms of the devil, which he showed even to the Lord, understand the various sins: avarice, luxury, anger, detraction, thefts, perjuries, against which the Word of God, sitting on his horses and in his chariots, aims the brandished darts of his lightning, and meanwhile does not let them fly, so that whoever has been terrified at the bent bow may not feel the shooting of the arrows. And this he does always, which Aquila translated in place of diapsalma. For he always sits in his saints, he is always armed. And preparing sharp arrows on their tongues he rides, and runs to and fro for the salvation of the world.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","You will split the rivers of the earth. LXX: The earth will be split by rivers. Since the Lord has roused his bow to render to the tribes the oaths which he spoke, it is said consequently: You will split the rivers of the earth, that is, the kings of the earth fighting against your people you will divide and scatter. But according to the Seventy, who said: The earth will be split by rivers, let us first set out an example, so that by this as by a step we may come to higher things. We read in those who have composed volumes on marvels, and who have carried the Olympiads of Greece down to our own memory, setting out what new thing happened in the world in each year, that among other things rivers burst forth through an earthquake which had not existed before, and again others were swallowed up and went to the bottom; namely, that all the veins of the earth, as the human body has blood, so have within them hidden waters, which are broken open by the shaking of the earth and burst out into rivers. If we have understood this, let us see that the human soul naturally has waters and rivers within it, and that through our sloth they are hidden and do not flow. But when it has been shaken by the preaching of the Word of God and moved from its former state, then what lay hidden bursts forth and flows for the refreshment of those who drink. I think this same thing is signified also by what is in Genesis, that wells are dug by the servants of Isaac which had been made by Abraham and which the Philistines had stopped up with earth. As long as Abraham lives, his wells are not closed; but when he is dead and the wells are stopped up, if servants dig, the Philistines oppose them, and there is strife. But if Isaac himself comes and digs a well and finds water, the Philistines have no power to oppose him. See Peter and Paul, and you will not doubt about the wells and rivers of Christ. Look at all the apostles, and you will understand that not four rivers now, but twelve rivers go out of the paradise of the Scriptures. These rivers, before the earth was moved, lay hidden, and while they were in the veins of the earth they offered no drink to the thirsty. But after the world and all the earth were shaken at the coming of Christ, they suddenly burst forth, and then was fulfilled: He made rivers into a desert, and the outgoings of waters into thirst, a fruitful land into saltness, because of the wickedness of those who dwell in it. He made the desert into pools of water, and the land without water into springs of water, and made the hungry dwell there, and they established a city to dwell in. For after the Lord came into the world, and that was fulfilled which he said in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world, that those who did not see might see, and those who saw might become blind, then the land of Israel, from which rivers formerly flowed and watered the whole people of Judea, dried up, and its springs were stopped. But the whole world, which was deserted and barren and did not have the waters of the Lord's preaching, was turned into pools of water, and as many teachers as it sent out, so many springs it also had. Nor is it enough for them, that is, for the springs and rivers, to water the peoples of the world; but in each province, gathering into one people those who were hungry and suffered famine of the Word of God, they built up the Church, which is called a city to dwell in, and which the rush of the river makes glad.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.10":["They saw you, and the mountains grieved; the flood of waters passed; the abyss gave its voice; the height lifted up its hands. The sun and moon stood still in their dwelling; in the light of your arrows they will go, in the splendor of your flashing spear. In roaring you will trample the earth; in fury you will astound the nations. You went forth for the salvation of your people, for salvation with your Christ. You struck the head from the house of the wicked one; you laid bare the foundation up to the neck. Always. We have set down our edition alone, so that, following it, that is, following the Hebrew, we may weave together the sequence of the passage, and afterwards discuss the LXX clause by clause, section by section. They saw you, O God, and the mountains grieved; that is, the lofty kingdoms and the exalted powers of this world, and the four chariots in Zechariah which go out from the mountains of bronze: these saw you and trembled. And the flood of waters passed, that is, all their onrush, and the persecution with which they harassed your people, passed after they saw you. Then the abyss, that is, those below, praised you; then also those above, that is, the angels, clapped their hands in applause, to show forth the victor as it were by a kind of gesture and dance of uplifted hands.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: They will see you, and they will grieve, or, the peoples will be in labor: for ὠδινήσουσι means both. Accordingly, the earth having been split open and the rivers flowing forth, the peoples who had drunk from the rivers of God will see God and will be in labor. For from the very fact that they see God, they at once conceive by the word of God and say: From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived in the womb, and have been in labor, and have brought forth; we will make the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. Blessed, he says, are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. These peoples, therefore, washed by the rivers, have not already seen, but are going to see God; and when they have seen, they will conceive, so that they may bring forth the fruits of doctrines. But because they are called peoples, and it does not belong to peoples to see the face of God, although the speech is extended into the future, they will see and they will bring forth, nevertheless, according to tropology, the Hebrew is rather to be followed, where it says: They saw you, and the mountains brought forth; for it belongs to mountains to see God and to bring forth sons, whom they have conceived from the word of God.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: You will scatter the waters of the way. The waters are of different kinds: some are everlasting, others short-lived. Of the everlasting waters, and those which flow from the fountains of Israel, it is said: The earth will be split by rivers. Of the sudden ones, which run for a time: All torrents go into the sea. For the end of such waters is destruction. God, therefore, will scatter all the waters which have been trampled by perverse doctrines, when he has dispersed the counsels of princes and the wisdom of this world. If ever you see some heresy flourish for a short while and afterwards be dispersed by the grace of God, say that this has been fulfilled: You will scatter the waters of the way. Moreover, to what is said, of the way, one may supply of the devil, so that the sense is: The waters which the devil has trampled, and which have afforded a way through themselves to many, that is, have lain open to many errors, the Lord will divide and scatter. Hence the other translators too, wishing to describe the fury of heretics, translated: The dashing, or the rush of waters will pass. For they are borne along headlong on the downhill course of eloquence, so as to drag along with them whomever they find in their path and light-minded.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: The abyss gave its voice, the height of phantasy. The abyss is often taken in a good sense, often in a bad one, sometimes indifferently. In a good sense: Your judgments are a great abyss, and: Abyss calls upon abyss, and the rest. In a bad sense: The waters saw you, O God, and the rest. Moreover the demons too beg not to be sent into the abysses. And in Genesis: The abyss over which there was darkness: I do not know whether it can be taken in a good sense. But it is set down indifferently there: The fountains of the abyss were broken up, and the cataracts of heaven were opened. And that in Psalm 148: Dragons and all abysses, fire and hail and the spirit of storms; unless perhaps, because it is set among dragons and fire and hail, it is to be drawn into a bad sense. Which I do not know whether anyone can say who has seen it sounding together with the rest in the praise of the Lord. If, then, we take the abyss in a good sense, let us say: when the waters of the most wicked way were scattered, your wise ones saw you, and, borrowing from your countenance the height of knowledge which they had — because they saw you and the mountains were in labor —, whatever they had previously thought about you they brought forth in the praises of their voice. And he beautifully calls the opinion of phantasy height, in accordance with Jesus son of Sirach, who says: The abyss and wisdom, who will search them out? Hence also, from the little mountain, that is, the assumption of a human body, which Daniel calls a stone cut from the mountain without hands, that is, without the work of marriage, Christ the abyss calls upon the Father, the other abyss, in the voice of his cataracts, that he may give to those who preach the good news the word with great power. Or certainly the abyss, the New Testament, in testimony of the little mountain by which the prince of Tyre was wounded, calls upon the abyss of the Old Testament, so that through the cataracts of Christ, that is, the apostles, the preaching may become firmer. But if anyone wishes to take what is said, The abyss gave its voice, the height of its phantasy, in a bad sense, he will use this argument: that after the scattered waters of the way, which were certainly taken in a bad sense, this too is rightly understood in the contrary sense. And at the same time observe that he did not say its height, but the height of its phantasy, that is, of shadow and image. For they seem to have height and knowledge of the Scriptures, but all their height, compared with the truth, is phantasy, and they lift up their voice in vain, because the waters of the way have already been scattered. Let us search in the Scriptures whether we can anywhere find phantasy in a good sense; and since it has been found either rarely or never, we shall interpret the abyss and its phantasy in a bad sense with greater firmness.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.11":["Your sun and moon, and all the splendor with which you had formerly shone upon your people, and which afterwards, under the weight of evils, had all been covered with the horror of darkness, recovered their light and had their former brightness. Your arrows and your flashing spear, that is, your blows and your instruction, gave light to your people. Finally, in the light of your arrows and in the splendor of your spear, which chastised them to this end, that it might amend them, your people walked in your roaring.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: The sun was lifted up, and the moon stood in its order. If we follow the simple interpretation, the present words show the advance of the sun and moon, in that, according to Isaiah, in the age to come the sun will shine sevenfold, and the moon will gleam with the brightness of the sun. Because indeed the creation will be set free from the bondage of corruption into the liberty of the glory of the sons of God, the creation which is now subjected to vanity because of him who subjected it in hope of liberty: when, at the consummation of the world, every creature has been set free, the sun and moon too will be set free, and will stand in their order. But if we wish to take Christ as the sun of justice, in whose wings is healing, and the Church as the moon, which is lit by the splendor of this sun, it is not difficult to say that the true light, and the light of men, and the splendor of the glory of God, and the splendor of eternal light illumines her who now, in this age, waxes and wanes according to prosperity and afflictions. But when the sun has been lifted up, and, according to the Apostle, God has exalted him and given him a name above every name, then the Church too, which in the present age cannot keep her order, will return to her due order, and will by no means be changed, but will stand with fixed step, and will hear with Moses: But you, stand here with me.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: In light your darts will go, in the splendor of the flashing of your arms. The darts of God, that is, the arrows going forth and proceeding, are not sent in order to kill, but to illumine. To distinguish these arrows and darts, Christ is called a chosen dart, crying out through Isaiah: He has set me as a chosen dart, and in his quiver he has hidden me; and he said to me: It is a great thing for you to be called my servant. This arrow will have many arrows, to send into the whole world. Hence also the bride, wounded by the chosen dart, says: Wounded by charity am I, in accordance with which we too can say: Wounded by chastity am I, wounded by wisdom am I. Wounded by this dart of wisdom, the queen of the south too was beside herself, and, astounded, had found more in the true Solomon than report had told her. These arrows, then, which are sent into light, also proceed in the splendor of the flashing of his arms, that is, of God's. For whoever is armed so as to stand against the wiles of the devil, and girded with the armor of the Apostle, to him will come the darts of light, so that it may be said to him: You are the light of the world. But if anyone is a sinner, and groans that he dwells in the tents of Kedar, there are sent to him sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals, so that first he may be pricked by the words of God and say: I was turned about in misery while the thorn is fixed in me. And after he has been pricked, then let the desolating coal be sent to him through the Seraphim, that is, the burning word of God, to burn out not only the lips, which alone Isaiah had unclean, but also all the parts of his members, and to bring him back to a solitude of sins.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.12-13":["When, therefore, you avenge the wrong done to your people, you will trample the earthly kingdoms and make all the nations marvel, because you went forth for the salvation of your people and came to them with your Christ; although in the Hebrew it is written: You went forth for the salvation of your people with your Jesus Christ, or with your Savior Christ. For Jesus means Savior.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.12":["LXX: In threatening you will diminish the earth, and in fury you will drag down the nations. This can be taken of the consummation of the world, when, the multitude having been slain in frequent wars, men will be found few, and those who did not wish to be of the people of God, but remained Gentiles and heathens, will be led down to Tartarus by the fury of the Lord. But it is better that we interpret the earth diminished by threatening as earthly works, and as those who, set in the Church as sinners, do not wait to be chastised by the fury of the Lord, but, hearing in the Scriptures what punishments hang over sinners, do penance, and little by little diminish their earth and advance toward heaven. If any of us dreads the threatening of the Lord, for him the earth is diminished; but he who persists in the number of the Gentiles, and does not want to be among those whose earth decreases, nor of the people of God, of whom it is said: They will see you, and the peoples will be in labor, he will be dragged down with the nations into punishment.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.13":["But when Jesus Christ your Son came, you struck the Antichrist from the house of the wicked one, that is, in this world, which lies in the evil one; or you struck the devil himself, who is the head of wickedness, and laid bare his foundation up to the neck, that is, you made his hidden things manifest, not for a short time but for ever; for this is what sela means, that is, always.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: You went forth for the salvation of your people, to save your anointed ones. First let us see how many kinds of anointed ones there are, and afterwards we shall treat how the Lord went forth for the salvation of his anointed ones. In the Old Testament the patriarchs too were called anointed ones, of whom it is written in the psalms: He rebuked kings for their sake: Do not touch my anointed ones, and do no evil to my prophets. And in the first book of Chronicles all who went out of Egypt are called anointed ones. Chrism, too, is prepared for the priesthood in Exodus, with which the priests are afterwards reported in Leviticus to have been anointed. There is also another ointment, with which kings are anointed into the kingship, and it is divided in two. For if they are David and Solomon, that is, strong of hand and peaceful, they are anointed from a horn. But if they are Jehu and Hazael, they are anointed from a flask; for so an earthenware vessel is called, that is, φακὸς. Moreover Cyrus too, king of the Persians and Medes, who released the people from captivity — although many err and think it written of the Lord the Savior —, hears through Isaiah: Thus says the Lord to my anointed Cyrus, whose right hand I have held, that the nations might hear before him, and so on. And at the end it is said: But you have not known me, which it is impious to understand of the Savior. There is a prophetic ointment, with which Elijah is commanded to anoint Elisha as prophet. And above all kinds of ointments there is a spiritual ointment, which is called the oil of exultation, with which the Savior is anointed, and it is said to him: Therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows. And I think his fellows are those to whom John too speaks: And you have an ointment from the Holy One. And a little after: These things I have written to you concerning those who lead you astray; and the chrism which you received from him, let it abide in you; and you have no need that anyone teach you, but the chrism itself will teach you about all things, and it is true and is not a lie, and as it has taught you, abide in it. And lest perhaps those who have lost the chrism of baptism should despair of the chrism being restored, it is written in Leviticus that when the leper cast outside the camp has come to the priest, and his leprosy has been cleansed, the priest shall pour oil into his own left hand, and, having dipped his finger, sprinkle the oil seven times before the Lord, and touch with the same oil the ear of him who had been a leper, and his right hand, and his right foot, and whatever remains of the ointment he shall put entirely on his head. And when he has duly completed all these things, then he shall offer a holocaust for him, and the man himself shall be called the anointed of God. I wish to say something, but I fear lest I give the careless an occasion of ruin: that in the holy Scriptures the same man is often found anointed. Indeed, David was anointed three times; which let us not understand of one who has sinned and is anointed again — for it suffices for the leper that, after the first ointment has been lost, he be anointed a second time —; but of one who advances day by day, and whose anointing is ever increased; and he passes from the oil of the leper to the oil of the people and of the saints, and from the oil of the people he arrives at the oil of the priests, and from the priests he rises to the chrism of the high priest, from the high priest also to the king, from the king to the patriarchs, and from the patriarchs he proceeds to Christ; and he is anointed with the oil of exultation, and whoever has been smeared with it is made one spirit with God, and where the Father and the Son are, there he too will be. But this is rare, and these are the prayers of believers. Besides, I do not know whether the effect follows. For it is said: God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows, that is, with that oil which those who are your fellows will be able to find either rarely or never. For the salvation of these anointed ones, then, God went forth from his place, as Micah too says: And he will go forth from his place to save. For because those who needed salvation were unwilling to come in to him, he himself went forth from his majesty and from his place, to lead those who were outside into the land of the meek and the region of the living, from which Adam had been expelled, from which Cain went out and dwelt in the land of Nod. But it must be known, as we said above, that where the LXX put in the plural number: To save your anointed ones, there in the Hebrew is laiesua eth messiach, which Aquila translated: For salvation with your Christ; not that God went forth to save the people and to save his Christ, but that he came for the salvation of the people with his Christ, according to that saying of the Gospel: The Father is in me, and I in the Father; and the Father abiding in me, he does his works. Moreover the Fifth Edition also translated similarly: You went forth for the salvation of your people, for salvation with your Christ. But Theodotion, truly like a poor man and an Ebionite, and Symmachus too, of the same doctrine, following a poor sense, translated in Jewish fashion: You went forth for the salvation of your people, to save your Christ, and: You went forth to save your people, to save your Christ. I am about to say something incredible, but nevertheless true. These half-Christians translated in Jewish fashion, and Aquila the Jew translated as a Christian. The Sixth Edition, disclosing the mystery most plainly, rendered thus from the Hebrew: You went forth to save your people through Jesus your Christ, which in Greek is ἐξῆλθες τοῦ σῶσαι τὸν λαόν σου διὰ Ἰησοῦν τὸν Χριστόν σου. To this sense can be adapted the fact that the Father went forth with the Son from the temple and from the ceremonies of the Jews, saying: Your house will be left to you desolate, and came for the salvation of the Gentiles, to save believers through Jesus Christ his Son.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: You sent death into the head of the wicked. Let us not think this is said of this common death by which we all die, and Abraham died and was gathered to his fathers, and the prophets, and Christ himself died; but death was sent into the iniquitous, so that those who formerly lived to iniquity, having died to sin, might live to justice. This Hannah too signifies in her prayer: The Lord kills and makes alive. For he kills sinners, sending death into the heads of the wicked, in order to make them alive to justice. I will say something more daring: Christ came into the world for this purpose, to send death into the heads of the iniquitous. And just as he himself died to sin once, so they too might die to iniquity, and those who had been made partakers of this death might also become partakers of life. But according to the Hebrew, where it is written: You struck the head from the house of the wicked one; by the head, as I said, let us understand the prince of this world, and by his house, the world and every soul of a sinner, in which the devil had lodging. And the head is struck in the house of the wicked one for this reason, that, once it has been struck and cast out, it may become the house of God, and justice may dwell there and walk about in it. And this is worthy to be thought of God, who went forth for the salvation of his people with his Christ, so that, once such a head has been struck, he may become head in us who is the head of every man and of his Church. If anyone, therefore, still feels himself to be the house of the wicked one, let him pray for the coming of the Son of God, that the head of the wicked one may be crushed in him.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: You raised up bonds up to the neck, to the end. The Lord raised up the bonds of charity, so that, the former burden having been laid down, and the most heavy yoke by which we were pressed having been thrown off, we might take up the light yoke of Christ, and, set in his chariot, might carry the best of charioteers. Theodotion too, taking this in a good sense, says: You adorned the foundation up to the neck. The Fifth Edition: You laid bare, or, you emptied the foundation up to the neck, sela, that is, always. For because the foreigners had buried with earth the foundation of Christ which was in the soul of each one, the heaped-up soil is carried out, and the most excellent foundation is laid bare and adorned, so that what lay hidden may appear and recover its brightness; and this is done for ever, which in Hebrew is called sela. And at the same time consider that the LXX themselves, compelled by the necessity of the matter, who always translated sela as diapsalma, have here translated, to the end.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.14":["You cursed his scepters, the head of his warriors, who came like a whirlwind to scatter me. Their exultation was like that of one who devours the poor man in secret. You made a way in the sea for your horses, in the mud of many waters. I heard, and my belly was troubled; at the voice my lips trembled. Let rottenness enter into my bones, and swarm beneath me, that I may rest in the day of my tribulation, that I may go up to our girded people. And now we discuss only the Hebrew, so that we may discuss the edition of the Seventy separately: for it differs greatly from the translation of all the others. You cursed, he says, the scepters, that is, his kingdoms; no doubt those of the wicked one, of whom he had said above: You struck the head from the house of the wicked; you laid bare the foundation up to the neck. Now we understand the wicked one either as Nebuchadnezzar or as every adversary of the people of God. And not only his scepters, but also the head of the warriors whom you had struck, who came like a whirlwind to scatter me, that is, to overthrow Israel and lead it captive in different directions. But they exulted in this way, devouring the poor man, Israel made subject to them, as if they were doing it in secret and were devouring us without your knowledge.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: You divided in amazement the heads of the mighty. Just as Christ is the head of the Church and of every man, so of all the demons who rage in this world the head is Beelzebub, the prince of the demons, and each of their troops has its own heads and princes. For example, the spirits of fornications have their own commander, the spirits of avarice have their own ἄρχοντα (ruler), the spirits of vainglory, the spirits of lying, the spirits of unbelief have the presidents of their warfare. Therefore the most merciful God, who had sent death upon the heads of the wicked, who had raised up bonds up to the neck, at the end divides also the heads of the mighty in amazement, so as first to separate the princes from their subjects, and as it were to behead the body from its head, and that where there had been a most evil head, there the best head may be set in its place. Let us give an example, so that what we say may become clearer: whenever a tyrant is cut down, his images and statues are also taken down, and, only the face being changed and the head removed, the face of the one who conquered is set on top, so that, while the body remains and the heads are cut off, another head is substituted. I like to understand this of the conventicles of the heretics: that when the heads of the heresies have been separated from the rest of the peoples, Christ begins to be the head in their place. And at the same time consider the expressiveness of holy Scripture, that it did not say, you cut off, or you cut down the heads of the mighty, but you divided; for what is divided is not so much amputated and destroyed as separated into parts. So that, just as at the building of the tower the tongue which had been wrongly united was separated, and a most evil pact was cut asunder by a useful division, so also these heads, which together with their bodies seemed to have agreement among themselves — for the heads of the heretics are many, and though they have different eyes, they yet bark against the Church with one, so to speak, tongue of blasphemy —, might be divided into parts, and, separated from the deceived bodies, might make room for the good head. We can use this little verse whenever we see their kings and leaders shed Christian blood, and afterward the vengeance of the Lord follow, as we lately saw in Julian, and before him in Maximian, and earlier in Valerian, Decius, Domitian, Nero, and say to the Lord in exultation and prayer with a song: You divided in amazement the heads of the mighty, that is, in the amazement of believers, or in the amazement of all the nations, who did not think they could fall so quickly. While I was still a boy and was being trained in the school of grammar, and all the cities were polluted with the blood of victims, and suddenly, in the very heat of the persecution, the death of Julian was announced, one of the pagans said elegantly: How, he says, do the Christians say that their God is patient and ἀνεξίκακον (forbearing)? Nothing is more wrathful, nothing more prompt than this fury; he could not put off his indignation even for a short space. He may have said this in jest. But the Church of Christ sang with exultation: You divided in amazement the heads of the mighty. I too shall say something like it: Divide, O Lord, in the amazement of all, Ahab and Jezebel. I am not Elijah, but they are an Ahab and a Jezebel: they have killed Naboth, and taken his vineyard, and made it the garden of their luxury. Let some servant of yours, an Obadiah, be found to feed your poor man and beggar; let the harlot's blood be given to the dogs; let wicked and avaricious Ahab be slain by the javelin of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: They will be moved in her, they will open their bridles like a poor man eating in secret. When the heads have been divided from their bodies, and divided in no other way than in amazement, which in Greek is called ἐν ἐκστάσει, whence it is declined in agreement with her and is said ἐν αὐτῇ, that is, in her, they will also open their own bridles, or the bridles of those others — for both can be understood —, so that, relaxing that rule by which they formerly held dominion over the bodies subject to them, they may give place to a better rider and a better charioteer. And let them do this as if eating like poor men in secret, having no liberty nor any abundance of food, but scanty fare, eating which in secret they would want no one to see what they are doing. It can also be interpreted otherwise: When the heads have been divided in amazement, as if beheaded from the rest of the body, they will open their mouth, which had been bridled as it were with the bit of condemnation, and in the likeness of those who eat they will clatter with their teeth struck together, wanting to eat again, but not having the power to devour. Understand how after the coming of Christ the heads of the demons, separated from the nations which had formerly been subject to them, wish to exercise their ancient power again. But because they have been cut off from the bodies, they do not have full liberty to eat; they eat like poor men, and not only are they poor, but poor in secret. They are poor, because they have lost their former riches; they eat in secret, because they always sit in ambush, to kill the innocent in hidden places. These heads have teeth that are the very same as their arrows. And although he said before: I will ascend above the stars of heaven, I will set my nest on high, and I will hold the whole world in my hand like eggs, yet they will be dragged down from the heights, and, losing their former furniture and all the substance of their house, they will scarcely attempt, like poor men, to eat and bite in secret. I know that the Hebrew differs much from what has been said; but what can I do, I who have once resolved to translate both the Hebrew itself and the Scriptures current throughout the world?\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.15":["You came therefore to battle for your people, and, sending your chariots into the waters, that is, into many nations, you made a way for them in the mud of many waters, that is, so that you might trample them and crush them like mud with the hoof and the wheel of your horses and chariots.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: And you brought your horses over into the sea, troubling many waters. After God had sent death upon the heads of the wicked, and had cut down the heads of the mighty in amazement, and had broken them in the sea — for it is written in the psalms: You broke the heads of the dragon —, when the princes have been killed or broken, and the strong man has been conquered, one comes to his house, and all his vessels are plundered. But the vessels, and the house of the strong man, and the furniture of the prince, what else can we interpret them as, except the sea of this world, in which the dragon dwells? God therefore, an excellent rider and an outstanding charioteer, brings his horses, that is, the angels and the lofty powers, over the sea of this world, to trouble the many waters, the demons and the opposing powers. But if we wish to interpret it of the coming of Christ, according to what is written in the Apocalypse, that the Word of God sits on a white horse and the whole army follows him on white horses, we shall see how Christ mounted in his apostles, saying to them: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age, and: Going, go, baptize all nations, and the rest. And afterward he mounted upon one white horse, which I think is none other than the apostle Paul, riding upon whom he went round the whole world. Now the Word of God mounted his horses so that the many waters might be troubled, that is, either the many peoples who were formerly in the sea and were held subject to the dragon — For wide and spacious is the way that leads to death, and so that they might be troubled, first deserting their former error, then, being troubled, might receive the rider as he came —, or certainly, those of whom we spoke above, so that the hosts of demons would no longer rule in the sea as before, but, troubled, would give way and dread the wounds of the fighting rider. And would that the Word of God might mount in me too, and through the lance of my mouth kill him who reigns in many waters, so that at the king's death the waters which had been subject to them, being troubled, might offer their necks to my rider, and that we, brought together into one four-horse chariot, might become the Cherubim of the Lord, which means a multitude of knowledge. For never is a charioteer so well set, and never is he borne so adorned, as in those who are joined to one another by the multitude of knowledge and the reins of wisdom.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.16":["But as for what follows: I heard, and my belly was troubled; at the voice — so that 'your' is understood —, my lips trembled, let rottenness enter into my bones, and swarm beneath me, that I may rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to our girded people, this is the sense: Now we all gladly suffer distresses, and at your threatening we tremble with all our inward parts. Now my lips tremble, and the dread of a fearful mind is stamped upon my mouth; and not only this, but of my own accord I also seek and of my own accord I desire something more. Let rottenness enter into my bones, and swarm beneath me, that is, I gladly suffer what Job suffered: I long that not only my flesh but the marrow of my bones too should waste away, and that my bed should swarm with the rottenness of my body and with countless worms, so that after I have endured these things here for my sins, I may rest in the bitter day, in the day of tribulation, in the day of necessity and distress. And I shall go up to our girded people, that is, strong, warlike and combative. And beautifully, I shall go up: for one always goes up to a girded people. And elegantly, our: for he who has been afflicted, and has gladly endured pressures, and has set future rewards against present evils, boldly says our, so that, like Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, he too may sleep in a good old age, full of days, and be gathered to his fathers. But if anyone should say: Look, in expounding the history you have, without knowing it, been shut up in the nets of allegory, and you have mixed tropology with the history, let him hear that a metaphor in the history does not always amount to allegory, because the history itself is frequently woven metaphorically, and under the image of a woman or of one man something is proclaimed about a whole people. And now therefore we can say in the person of the people: I gladly undergo captivity, I endure distresses with an even mind, and I am pressed by the most heavy yoke of the Babylonians; and whatever belongs to the last and harsh necessity I shall suffer rejoicing, provided only that I may rest at the time when you will curse the scepters of the wicked one, and your horses will trample the mud of many waters, so that afterward, with your holy ones Zerubbabel and Joshua son of Jehozadak, and Ezra the priest, and Nehemiah, I may return to the land of promise. Up to this point, so that we might not seem to pass over the history altogether, we have in some measure done violence to the understanding, and we dragged into the captivity sentences that do not follow; now let us return to the Seventy translators and to the tropological exposition.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: I kept watch, and my belly was afraid at the voice of the prayer of my lips; and trembling entered into my bones, and beneath me my strength was troubled. Or, as we find written elsewhere, ἡ ἕξις μου, which we can call my condition; for differing copies are found. These things can also be said in the person of the prophet, cohering with what precedes: Because you, O Lord, sent death upon the heads of the wicked, and raised up bonds up to the neck, and divided in amazement the heads of the mighty, and brought into the sea your horses troubling many waters, therefore I have kept my heart with all watchfulness, and my inward parts trembled, and my whole strength, or condition, was troubled, lest I should suffer the like. It can also have, as it were, its own beginning, the prophet relating his fear, and how he dreaded lest he had sinned in anything, and, admonished at the voice of the prayer of his lips, feared God so much that trembling entered into his bones, and, placed under the lofty hand of the Lord, he was troubled in all the strength, or condition, of his soul. But what he says: Trembling entered into my bones, is to be taken ἐμφατικώτερον (more emphatically), so that we may see the greatness of the fear of God penetrating the whole of the soul and moving the whole man, lest he should do anything that would displease God. And since Scripture allegorically, following the members of the body, mentions members of the soul as well, let us take the fearful belly as that power of the soul which receives spiritual food, and the lips as those by which the mind speaks with itself, and the bones as the robust and solid doctrines by which the whole framework of the soul is made firm. These things have been said by me briefly. But if anyone has found things shrewder and truer than these, give your assent rather to his explanation.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","LXX: I shall rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to the people of my sojourning. Because I have kept my heart with all watchfulness, and my belly was afraid at the voice of the prayer of my lips, and trembling entered into my bones, and beneath me my strength or condition was troubled, and through so great a watchfulness I have been made a stranger to sins, therefore I now say confidently: I shall rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to the people of my sojourning, that is, the people which, just as I, has sojourned as a stranger in this world. And I shall go up, placed below as I am, and, as if striving from the valley toward higher places, I shall press on with all eagerness, so that at the time when the rest are in tribulation and distress, my care may be for the ascent, how I may rest with the people of my sojourning in the higher places. Now the day of tribulation I think is the consummation of the world, of which Isaiah also says: The day of the Lord is incurable, of fury and wrath, to make the whole world a desert, and to destroy sinners.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.17":["For the fig tree will not blossom, and there will be no bud in the vineyards; the work of the olive will lie, and the fields will not yield food; the flock will be cut off from the fold, and there will be no herd in the stalls. LXX: For the fig tree will not bear fruit, and there will be no buds in the vineyards; the work of the olive will lie, and the plains will not produce food; they have failed, because the sheep were eating, and there are no oxen in the stalls. According to the Hebrew, in which we said above: Let rottenness enter into my bones and swarm beneath me, that I may rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to our girded people, what preceded will be joined in this way to what follows: I wished to endure tribulation for the present, and afterward to go up to the strong people, for this reason, that the day of tribulation and necessity will come, and while the rest are set in distress, I shall rejoice in your majesty: For the fig tree will not blossom, and there will be no bud in the vineyards; the work of the olive will lie, and the fields will not yield food, and the rest. And since these words do not differ much from the LXX, let us discuss them together with their translation. When the day of tribulation has come, and I have gone up to the people which once sojourned with me, or certainly when the day of the overthrow of the Jews and of the former people has come, and the daughter of Zion has been left like a booth in a vineyard, and like a hut in a cucumber field, and like a city which is being besieged, I, who have been chosen out of a perishing people — of which it was said: Unless the Lord had left us a seed, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like Gomorrah —, shall join myself to the disciples of Christ, and because he teaches them on the mountain, leaving the crowds and the weak below, I shall climb up to the mountains. For the fig tree did not give fruit, the one to which the Lord came hungry in the Gospel, and he did not find fruit on it, and he cursed it, saying: You shall not bear fruit even unto the age. And consider diligently what he said: You shall not bear fruit unto the age; he does not say, unto the ages of ages, but when this age has passed away and the fullness of the Gentiles has entered, then this fig tree too will bear its fruits, and all Israel will be saved. This is the fig tree to which the householder came for the third time, and which he wishes to cut down as not producing fruit; for it the farmer, to whom it had been entrusted, pleads, that he may give it time, and he says: Lord, let it alone this year also, until I dig around it and put on manure, and if indeed it bears fruit; but if not, then you shall cut it down. That farmer is either Gabriel or Michael, to whom the people of the Jews was entrusted, who pleads with the Lord at the passion and says: Lord, give it time for repentance, and do not overthrow them, and if indeed they bear fruit; but if not, then you shall overthrow them. If they bear fruit, he says: he did not say what they would undergo; nor did he say: If they bear fruit, they will remain as they were; but, if they bear fruit: the sentence is left suspended, so that this is understood: you will transfer them into the church of the Gentiles, and transplant them into another vineyard. The Lord came for the third time and did not find fruit in them. First he gave the law through Moses, the second time he spoke through the prophets, the third time he himself came down. And after the passion, when forty-two years had been given for repentance, because they did not bear fruit, the fourth time they were overthrown. This, however, is left to our understanding. For in the parable it is not written what the householder did afterward, but only what the farmer pleaded for. From this we perceive that those of this fig tree who bore fruit were transferred to the people of the Gentiles, to which the prophet too goes up, saying: I shall rest in the day of tribulation, that I may go up to the people of my sojourning; but that those who did not bear fruit and remained in their hardness were overthrown. The voice of John in the Gospel also signifies this very thing: Behold, the axe is laid to the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not bear fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire. We have spoken of the fig tree, showing that it is the people of the Jews; let us speak also of the vineyard, which indeed anyone who has read Isaiah will easily understand: A vineyard was made for the beloved on a horn, in a fertile place, and afterward: And I waited for it to bear fruit; but it bore thorns, and not judgment, but a cry, and in Jeremiah: But I planted you a fruitful vineyard, wholly true; how have you been turned into the bitterness of a foreign vine? and more plainly in the psalms: You brought a vineyard out of Egypt, you cast out the nations, and planted it. This vineyard, then, to which the householder had often sent servants to receive from it wine, which gladdens the heart of man, because it was turned into bitterness, and at the last even dared to kill the householder's son, producing not grapes but thorns, and not judgment but a cry: Crucify, crucify him, and: We have no king but Caesar: therefore the boar from the forest has ravaged it, and the solitary wild beast has fed upon it. That the olive too is the people of the synagogue will be clearly proved by anyone who has read in the Apostle of the broken branches of the olive, and of us grafted in from the wild olive, so that in the branches we understand that the Jewish multitude was cut off, and in the roots that the election of the apostles was preserved; grafted into them we shall remain, if we bear fruit, and it will be said of us: Your sons like young shoots of olives around your table. Many think that the fig tree, the vineyard and the olive are taken as the mystery of the Trinity: that the fig tree, because of the sweetness of its fruit, is understood as the Holy Spirit; the vineyard as our Lord Jesus Christ himself, who says in the Gospel: I am the vine; and the olive as God the Father almighty, by whom all things are enlightened, and from whom light goes forth, and to whom we can say: O olive, in your light we shall see light, that is, in the Son we shall see the Holy Spirit. To these fruitful trees and to the most fertile vineyard come the unfruitful trees in the book of Judges and ask them to reign over them. But the olive, the fig tree and the vine never reign over the trees of the forests, which are destined for burning; rather the buckthorn rules over them, full of briars and like the hedgehog, which dwells in Babylon and always lives in pits. This little tree has not only thorns but fire too, wounding and burning whatever it touches. And at last fire went out and consumed the trees of the forests. But so that you may know, according to the higher understanding, in which we have taken it as said of the synagogue: The fig tree will not bear fruit, and there will be no produce in the vineyards, that this is said not of fruits but of good works, in the olive the riddle is plainly opened, and it is said: The work of the olive will lie. For the fruits which they ought to have borne are shown in works; and the work of the olive will lie, as of one who promises one thing and does another: they said to Moses: All things whatsoever the Lord shall say, we will do; and they were unwilling to believe in him who was preached by Moses. The plains also will not bear fruit. Consider that Jerusalem, which was once set on mountains, with the mountains around her, and her foundations on the holy mountains, is now called lowly and flat, a place that nourishes not only no men, that is, no rational animals, but not even flocks and oxen, of which Solomon also speaks in Proverbs: Take care of the regions that are in the plain, and cut the grass, and gather hay, that you may have sheep to eat. Oxen also will not be in the stalls, because where the stalls are full, the strength of the ox is manifest. The ox is a worker; the ox of the Lord bearing the yoke; the ox in whose track he who sows is blessed. All these things will be taken away from the people, because it acted wickedly against God its Creator. But if you wish to take the day of tribulation as the day of the consummation, you will refer everything to those who say they are of the Church and do not have the works of justice. And the fig tree, the vineyard and the olive, namely the mystery of the Trinity, will not bear their fruit in them; and they have not only no grain and food of rational beings, but not even fodder for flocks and beasts of burden in their fields, and their stalls are empty, and instead of lofty mountains they dwell in flat and lowly places.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.18-19":["But if we wish to take it of the consummation of the world, it must be expounded thus: Just as in Exodus, when Egypt was struck, and God struck their vineyards and their fig trees, and killed the firstborn of men and of beasts in the hail, and the caterpillar and the locust consumed the Egyptian crops, the fig tree in Egypt did not bear fruit, nor was there produce in their vineyards, the work of the olive, wherever it could be found in Egypt at all, was lying, and their plains did not give food, and they were failing, because their flocks had no food, and there were no oxen in the stalls; but the people of Israel exulted in the Lord and rejoiced in God their salvation: so in the consummation of the world, when, iniquity having been multiplied, charity has grown cold, and the fig tree has not borne fruits, and the vineyards have had no grapes, and the work of the olive has lied, and the plains have not brought forth food, and the rest that follows, then whoever has been found just and worthy of God's election will say, exulting: But I will exult in the Lord, I will rejoice in God my salvation, the Lord God is my strength. And as one set by God above the consummation of the age, so that he may afterward climb to higher things and be brought out by God to the summit, he will say: And he will set my feet unto the consummation, he will set me upon the high places, as it will be when a prize has been set for the singers by Jesus, the ἀγωνοθέτῃ (the president of the games), who was the first to win in the contest. I shall conquer in his song, and let my hands put together the cithara and the psaltery and every kind of instrument, and I will write a panegyric for him who triumphs. And I, who at the beginning said: How long shall I cry, and you will not hear? Shall I cry out to you, suffering violence, and you will not save? and found fault with his justice and judgment, shall afterward praise his equity, and surpass the other singers with my song.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.18":["But I will rejoice in the Lord, and I will exult in God my Jesus. God the Lord is my strength, and he will set my feet like those of harts, and upon my high places he, the victor, will lead me singing in psalms. LXX: But I will exult in the Lord, I will rejoice in God my salvation. The Lord God is my strength, and he will set my feet unto the consummation, he will set me upon the high places, that I may conquer in his song. When the fig tree and the vineyard and the olive, as I have expounded, do not produce their fruit, and the fields of the Jews do not yield food, when the flocks too have been cut off from the fold and the herds from the stalls, after they heard from the Lord: Your house will be left to you desolate, and when the people too has been handed over to captivity and scattered over the whole world, the prophet, from the people of the Jews, whose name means embrace — because he loved the Lord, and clung to him, and joined himself to him —, speaks in the person of the apostles and of the people believing in Christ: But I will rejoice in the Lord, and I will exult in God my Jesus. For this the LXX translated τῷ Σωτῆρί μου, that is, my Savior. Gabriel too interprets the very same thing: And he will be called Jesus, for he will save his people.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"HAB.3.19":["God the Lord is my strength: for I shall have no other power except in Christ, and I shall count all the righteousnesses of the law as refuse. And he will set my feet like those of harts, that I may tread upon the asp and the basilisk; and like a little child I shall put my hand into the hole, and draw out the serpent, and play with the snake: For my kinsman is like a roe, or a young hart. And because he himself is the hart, he has granted to me too that I should be a hart, lofty in horns, cleaving the hoof, chewing the cud, and putting serpents to flight by my scent, of which it is said in Psalm 17 (18): Who made my feet perfect like those of harts, and will set me upon the high places. And in Psalm 28 (29): The voice of the Lord perfecting the harts. He will therefore set my feet among his other harts, and lead me to heavenly places, that among the angels I may sing the glory of the Lord, and announce upon earth peace to men of good will. And I shall sing his victory and triumph and the trophy of the cross. This is according to the Hebrew and the Fifth Edition, so that we may refer everything to the time of the overthrow of the Jews and to the coming of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk"],"ZEP.1.1":["Before I take up Zephaniah, who is ninth in the order of the twelve prophets, it seems I must answer those who think I deserve to be laughed at because, passing over men, I write above all to you, O Paula and Eustochium. If they knew that Huldah prophesied while men were silent, and that Deborah, a judge and a prophetess alike, overcame the enemies of Israel while Barak was afraid; and that Judith and Esther, in a type of the Church, both slew the adversaries and delivered Israel, which was about to perish, from its peril; they would never crook their hand into a stork behind my back. I say nothing of Anna and Elizabeth and the other holy women, whose little fires, like those of the stars, the bright light of Mary hides. I will come to pagan women, so that they may see that even among the philosophers of the world it is the differences of minds, not of bodies, that are wont to be considered. Plato introduces Aspasia disputing; Sappho is written of together with Pindar and Alcaeus; Themista philosophizes among the wisest men of Greece; Cornelia of the Gracchi, that is, your own, the whole crowd of the city of Rome admires; Carneades, the most eloquent of philosophers, the sharpest of rhetoricians, who had been accustomed to rouse applause among men of consular rank and in the Academy, did not blush to dispute on philosophy in a private house with a matron listening. Why should I mention the daughter of Cato, the wife of Brutus, whose virtue makes us not marvel so greatly at the constancy of her father and her husband? History, Greek as well as Latin, is full of the virtues of women, and of such as would demand whole books. For me, since another work presses upon me, let it be enough to have said at the end of the prologue that the Lord, when he rose again, appeared first to women, and that they were the apostles of the apostles, so that men might blush not to seek the one whom the frailer sex had already found.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","The word of the Lord which came to Zephaniah the son of Cushi, the son of Gedaliah, the son of Amariah, the son of Hezekiah, in the days of Josiah the son of Amon, king of Judah. LXX likewise. The Hebrews hand down that whenever the father or grandfather of any prophet is set in the title, they too were prophets. Hence Amos also, one of the twelve prophets, who had said: I am not a prophet, nor the son of a prophet, but a herdsman of goats, plucking sycamore fruit, does not have his father's name in the title. If this is true, Zephaniah the prophet, whom we are now attempting to expound, was begotten by a prophetic custom, so to speak, and of a glorious stock of ancestors; for he had as father Cushi, as grandfather Gedaliah, as great-grandfather Amariah, as great-great-grandfather Hezekiah, and he himself, like the last charioteer, completed such a four-horse team. Some have translated the name of Zephaniah as 'watchtower', others as 'the secret of the Lord'. So whether it is interpreted 'watchtower' or 'the hidden thing of the Lord', both suit the prophet. For it is said also to Ezekiel: Son of man, I have set you as a watchman for the house of Israel, and in another place: The Lord will not do anything unless he has revealed his teaching to his servants the prophets, and the title of Psalm 9 is rendered 'for the hidden things of the son'. This prophet, then, who was on the watchtower and set on the heights, and knew the mysteries of the Lord, was the son of Cushi — which is interpreted 'humility', or 'my Ethiopian', of which we shall treat later —; he had also as grandfather Gedaliah, who is called 'the greatness of the Lord', and as great-grandfather Amariah, who is himself also rendered 'the word of the Lord', and as great-great-grandfather Hezekiah, which sounds 'the strength of the Lord'. So from the strength of the Lord was born the word of the Lord, and from the word of the Lord was born the greatness of the Lord, and from the greatness of the Lord was born humility, so that when someone has arrived at the perfect, he may say: I am not worthy to be called an apostle, and that in the psalms: Lord, my heart is not exalted, nor are my eyes lifted up. Up to this point, running as if on a downward slope and over level ground, we have stumbled on the fact that Cushi is also interpreted 'my Ethiopian'. For after such great virtues, how could the name of an Ethiopian sound in praise? And indeed, if Scripture had said Cush, that is, 'Ethiopian', the question would seem insoluble; for Cush was born of Ham. But in that it says Cushi, that is, 'my Ethiopian', a mystery seems to sound; namely, that he who was once an Ethiopian, turned to repentance — according to what is said: Ethiopia will stretch out her hand first to God, and in another place: Before him the Ethiopians will fall down —, may say with the bride in the Song of Songs: I am black and beautiful, daughter of Jerusalem. We read also in Jeremiah that Ebed-melech the Ethiopian eunuch pleased God; and in the Acts of the Apostles that the Ethiopian eunuch of Queen Candace had such zeal for the Scriptures and the law of God that he read in his carriage, and came to Jerusalem to worship the Lord in his temple. Hence such faith is crowned with a worthy reward, and Philip the evangelist is sent to him, and at once he is taught, believes, is baptized and is saved. And he is not set down only as a eunuch, but with the addition of man: a eunuch, a man, an Ethiopian. For because he was a eunuch of Christ, and had made himself a eunuch for the sake of the kingdom of heaven, for that reason he had not lost the name of man. Rightly also does Zephaniah, as the son of Cushi, that is, of the Ethiopian, write in the following parts of the book about the repentance of the Ethiopians: Across the rivers, he says, of Ethiopia, from there they will bring victims to me. This much on the genealogy of Zephaniah, who prophesied in the days of Josiah. Now just as those who were enlightened by Elijah are called the days of Elijah, so too are they called the days of Josiah, who had lifted himself up to the Lord, since Josiah is interpreted 'the elevation of the Lord'; and he was a just man, and the history of Kingdoms and of Chronicles also writes of his praises. And he had as father Amon, as grandfather Manasseh. We read that Manasseh, after many crimes and after the captivity in Babylon, repented, and, turned to better things, obtained the mercy of the Lord. Hence he called his son ἐπώνυμον (named after it) of his faith, by which he had believed God, that is, Amon; for Amon is interpreted 'faith'. And at the same time consider that by no means, as above, are the kings of the ten tribes, that is, the kings of Israel, set down, but only the kings of Judah. For the ten tribes had already been led away into captivity by the Assyrians under King Hezekiah, the father of Manasseh. This much in the preface and in the title, on Zephaniah's lineage and time. Now let us see what the prophecy itself also contains.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.2-3":["Gathering I will gather all things from the face of the earth, says the Lord, gathering man and beast, gathering the bird of the heaven and the fish of the sea; and there will be ruins of the ungodly, and I will destroy men from the face of the earth, says the Lord. LXX: Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth, says the Lord; let man fail and the cattle; let the birds of the heaven fail, and the fish of the sea; and the ungodly will be weakened, and I will remove the wicked from the face of the earth, says the Lord. What we have set down in the Seventy, And the ungodly will be weakened, was added from the translation of Theodotion; in its place Symmachus translated: And stumbling blocks with the ungodly, so that 'will be gathered' or 'will fail' is understood; and the Fifth Edition: And weakness with the ungodly will fail. We ought therefore, following our custom, first to weave the history, and afterwards to discuss the loftier things. For no one doubts that the last reign of the two tribes, which were called Judah and Benjamin, was under Josiah. For after he was killed, his sons who reigned afterwards, and his grandsons, are to be thought not so much to have reigned as to have been held in mockery by the king of Egypt and by the Chaldeans, and tormented by various captivities and deaths. Since, then, the people could excuse themselves by their bad kings and say: We wish to serve God, but we are prevented by the kings, a just king is given; and while he was zealous with the zeal of the Lord, and the people nonetheless persevered in the worship of idols, a just cause of wrath is brought forward by the Lord, and there is prophecy of the overthrow of Jerusalem, and the captivity of Judah, and the victory of Nebuchadnezzar. And the Lord says through the prophet: Never again will I grant repentance, but I will consume everything from the face of the earth; neither man nor beast nor bird nor the fish of the sea will remain. For even the brute animals feel the wrath of the Lord; and that, when cities are laid waste and men killed, there comes about a desolation and scarcity of beasts too, and of birds and fish, Illyricum is witness, Thrace is witness, witness is the soil on which I was born, where, apart from the sky and the earth and the growing brambles and the thickets of the woods, all things have perished. And this, says the prophet, will happen because the multitude of the ungodly was too great. So the ungodly will fall and men will be destroyed, and there will be a desolation upon the face of the earth. But we can also take this very thing of the consummation of the world: that men and cattle and birds and the fish of the sea and all things will fail, and the ungodly will be weakened, and iniquity will be removed from the face of the earth.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.2":["But if we wish to understand something deeper as well — because of what is said by the Seventy: Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth —, and to take failing in a good sense, according to that saying: And failing, Abraham died in a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people; and from what Scripture relates of Isaac and Jacob, we shall see how they fail with failing from the face of the earth, and that they fulfil this precept: Who, having their conversation in heaven, and in the flesh not warring according to the flesh, because they know that those who live in the flesh cannot please God, do everything, as far as in them lies, that they may not be in the flesh but in the spirit; and withdrawing from the earth they say: He has raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ. But if anyone should set against our taking Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth in a good sense that which is written of Ishmael: These are the years of the life of Ishmael, a hundred and thirty-seven, and failing he died, and was gathered to his kind, we shall answer him, first, that Ishmael himself too was a son of Abraham, and received gifts and portions from his father, according to his measure; then, that it is written absolutely: Failing he died, and there is not added what is written of Abraham: In a good old age, an old man and full of days, and was gathered to his people. Or of Isaac: And the days of Isaac which he lived were a hundred and eighty-five years, and failing Isaac died, and was gathered to his kind, an old man and full of days. And also of Jacob: And Jacob ceased charging his sons, and lifting his feet upon the bed he failed, and was gathered to his people. From this we understand that it is one thing merely to fail, and another, along with failing, to have many virtues together.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.3":["And what it had first said in general, Let it fail with failing from the face of the earth, divine Scripture afterwards divided into parts: let man fail, and the cattle, let the birds of the heaven fail, and the fish of the sea. There are four things which are commanded to fail: first man, the rational being, then three which are subject to man, the cattle, and the birds, and the fish, which I think are set down also in Psalm 8: Moreover also the beasts of the field, the birds of the heaven and the fish of the sea, which pass through the paths of the sea. But what it had first said: all sheep and oxen, it separated as the chief of the cattle, and did not wish to number them with the cattle that remained. So let man fail, let the cattle fail, let the birds of the heaven fail, let the fish fail; and it did not say: let the wild beasts fail, let the creeping things of the earth fail. For these ought not to fail but to perish; but let those things fail which can receive correction. Just as the ways of women had failed Sarah, and Abraham is commanded to listen to whatever Sarah should enjoin. One fails as a man if he despises human things, and no longer dies as a man, and hears: I have said, you are gods. Another fails as cattle who, climbing to higher things, is not accused by the prophetic word: Man, when he was in honour, did not understand; he was compared to the senseless cattle, and was made like them. He fails like a bird of the heaven who makes himself the wings of an eagle, and returns rich to the house of his teacher, forsaking all poverty. He fails like a fish of the sea who, caught in the nets of the Lord, is separated along with the good fish. When these things have been accomplished according to the Lord's precept, the ungodly will be weakened, not having as much strength as before. But the wicked will also be removed — he did not say 'will be killed', but 'will be removed' —, so that, converted to better things, carried over from ungodliness and iniquity to godliness and justice, they may begin to be what they had not been before. These things according to tropology. For we ought to set down the interpretation of our elders as well. It will now be in the reader's judgment whether he wishes what has been said to sound severity or clemency.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.4-6":["Both the temple-keepers, then, and those once priests of God, and those who on the housetops worshipped the host of heaven, the sun and the moon and the rest of the stars; and those who swore by the name of the Lord and by the name of the idol of the Ammonites, Milcom — which was turned by the LXX into 'king' —, the Lord will remove from the place of Jerusalem, or will destroy; for it is understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (in common). And I will destroy from this place the remnants of Baal, and I will destroy the names of the temple-keepers with the priests, and I will destroy those who worship the host of heaven upon the roofs, and I will destroy those who worship and swear by the Lord, and swear by Milcom, and I will destroy those who turn away, or turn aside, from behind the Lord, and who do not seek him, or do not hold him fast. Now Israel turn away behind the Lord, who, abandoning his worship, swear by Milcom, and worship the host of heaven, and venerate Baal, the idol of the Sidonians. Up to this point the sense of the history has been expounded; let us see the anagogy as well. Because of the Lord, who arose from the tribe of Judah, and because of Jerusalem, in which Judah reigned, that is, the Lord and Savior, let us say that when iniquity has been multiplied, and the love of many has grown cold, and at the Lord's coming faith appears rare on the earth, so much that even the elect of God are tempted, then the Lord stretches out his hand for the punishment of sinners over Judah, who seems to himself to confess the name of the Lord, and over Jerusalem, the Church, which has obtained its name from peace, and removes from the Church the names of Baalim, which is interpreted 'in the higher places'. The Lord will remove the names of vainglory and of false admiration which have their place in the Church, in which, according to James, the one who has a gold ring is venerated, and the poor man is despised, when at the arrival of a judge and a senator, and generally of all the rich, the whole people rises up, and to the holy poor man not even a place to stand is granted among the throngs of the powerful and the concessions they grant; and also the names of the priests with the priests, who vainly applaud themselves in the name of bishop and in the dignity of the presbyterate, and not in their work. Hence, pointedly, he does not say 'and the works of the priests with the priests', but 'the names', those who only display the false names of dignities and destroy their own names by evil works; and those who worship the host of heaven upon the roofs, who are lifted up against the knowledge of God, and, promising themselves a feigned knowledge, refer everything that happens in the world to the risings and settings of the stars, and follow the errors of the astrologers; and those who worship the Lord and Milcom, who think they can serve the world and the Lord alike and satisfy two masters, God and mammon; who, as soldiers of Christ, entangle themselves in worldly business, and offer the same image to God and to Caesar, and, though they say they are priests of Christ, consecrate their sons to Milcom, that is, to their king. For rightly do they have a man as king who have lost the Lord as king; and those who through evil works turn aside from the Lord and do not seek him, they hold fast their sins while he flees from them. And if anyone wishes, according to the interpretation of the names Judah and Jerusalem, to understand this very thing of each one's soul, he will not err: that the Lord removes all the things we have mentioned either at the consummation of the world or at the departure of each person, when they will hear: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you. And he stretches out his hand over him who does not confess the Lord, and over him who boasts that he has the mind of peace, to remove and destroy from such a Jerusalem all pride, and false worship of God, and the errors of various doctrines, and servitude to God and the world alike, and, through daily sins, aversion from the Lord and neglect of God.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.4":["And I will stretch out my hand over Judah and over all the inhabitants of Jerusalem; and I will destroy from this place the remnants of Baal, and the names of the temple-keepers with the priests, and those who worship the host of heaven upon the roofs, and worship and swear by the Lord, and swear by Milcom; and those who turn away from behind the Lord, and who have not sought the Lord nor searched for him. LXX: And I will stretch out my hand over Judah, and over all who dwell in Jerusalem; and I will remove from this place the names of Baalim, and the names of the priests with the priests, and those who worship the host of heaven upon the roofs, and those who swear by the Lord, and swear by their king, and those who turn aside from the Lord, and who do not seek the Lord, and who do not hold fast the Lord. After the ruin of the ungodly and the removal of the wicked from the face of the earth, it is said consequently against Judah and against Jerusalem in the person of the Lord: And I will stretch out my hand over Judah and over all the inhabitants of Jerusalem. For the stretching out of the hand shows the gesture of one who strikes. And I will destroy from this place the remnants of Baal. Not that, according to the LXX, the names of Baalim are to be utterly rooted out, but that, according to the Hebrew, he threatens to remove his worshippers from the people, the few who had remained in Judah and Jerusalem from the slaughter by the enemies. The names also of the temple-keepers with the priests, because Judah and Benjamin had come to such impiety that in the temple of the Lord — according to what Ezekiel writes, and the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) shows — they set up a statue of Baal, which the Lord calls the image of jealousy, and in the same sanctuary they venerated the idols and the Lord alike. Hence, pointedly, he called the priests of the idols not priests but τεμενίτας, that is, temple-keepers or fanatics, which in Hebrew is called acchumarim.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.7":["Be silent before the face of the Lord God, for the day of the Lord is near, for the Lord has prepared a victim, he has sanctified his called ones. LXX: Fear before the face of the Lord God, for the day of the Lord is near, for the Lord has prepared his sacrifice, he has sanctified his called ones. Where the LXX translated fear, and we have set down be silent, in the Hebrew there is an interjection of one commanding silence, which the comic poets also often use; but silence is also enjoined absolutely on all, because the day of the Lord is to come. And by the day of the Lord let us understand the day of captivity and of vengeance on the sinful people, and the victim of the overthrow of Jerusalem, and the sanctification of those whom he has dedicated to slaughter, according to what is said in Jeremiah: Sanctify them for the day of their slaughter. And the sense is: The captivity foretold long ago is coming against the ungodly people; it is already near. For the prophecy is woven under King Josiah; once he has been killed, fear before the face of the Lord God, for the day of the Lord is near, for the Lord has prepared his sacrifice, he has sanctified his called ones. Utter devastation is coming, of which it is said in Ezekiel too: The end, he says, has come; the end has come, and so on. This sacrifice pleases me; these victims I have sanctified for myself. And what he says, He has sanctified his called ones, can be taken also of the Babylonians, whom, for vengeance on the people, he even calls his servants, as they avenge the wrong done to him. I have called, he says, Nebuchadnezzar my servant. And in the same volume he calls him not only servant but also dove: From the face of the sword of the dove. Moreover, according to tropology, since the face of the Lord is against those who do evil, to destroy the memory of them from the earth, and the day of judgment is near — because in comparison with eternity the whole time of this world is short —, or the departure of each person; let all fear and be silent, lest the face of the Lord — of which the holy one says: The light of your countenance is sealed upon us, O Lord — consume the hay, the stubble and the wood of sins. For the Lord has prepared his victim, the whole mystery of Leviticus, when through fire and the shedding of blood and the true offering those who are to be saved will be saved, and the called will be sanctified. Some of our writers understand the day of the Lord and his victim and the sanctification of the called of the coming of the Savior, when the Lamb was sacrificed, and in his blood the apostles were sanctified, and the rest who were called through them.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.8-9":["But since we have once begun to expound tropologically as well: The Lord will visit, at the coming and passion of the Savior, that is, on the day of the victim of his Son, upon the high priests and priests of the Jewish people and upon the royal house. For up to that time the kings of Judah from the stock of David continued, according to the prophecy of Jacob: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up, and he will be the expectation of the nations. For after the Lord's victim the kingdom was taken from the Jews. And upon all, he says, who are clothed in alien garments, who have withdrawn from the protection and clothing of God and are covered with their own error. And I will take vengeance openly upon all who are in the porches, that is, who have gone out from the temple of God; and though they ought to have been within, because of their sins they have gone outside and withdrawn from the Church of God, filling his temple with ungodliness and deceit. Let this be understood of the first coming of the Savior. But since we have once expounded it of the consummation of the world and the day of judgment, which all interpret as the day of the Lord, we ought to know that at that time the Lord will visit upon the princes and upon the shepherds, who eat the milk of the sheep and, shearing the wool, have no care for the breaking of the flock; and upon the king's sons, who boast that they are Christians and glory that they are sons of Christ the king; and upon all who are clothed in alien garments. The garment of the king's sons and the clothing of the princes is Christ, whom we receive in baptism, according to that saying: Put on Christ Jesus, and: Put on bowels of mercy, of kindness, of humility, of meekness, of patience, and so on. In these we are commanded to be clothed with the new, heavenly man, according to our Creator, and to cast off the clothing of the old man with his works. Though, then, we ought to be clothed with such garments, instead of mercy we are clothed with cruelty, instead of patience with impatience, instead of justice with iniquity. And to say it once for all, instead of virtues with vices; that is, instead of Christ with the Antichrist. Hence it is said of a man of this kind: And he was clothed with cursing as with a garment. The Lord will also avenge most openly at his coming even upon those who, though they ought to have been in the Church with good works, have cast themselves out because of their most wicked way of life; and, handed over to Satan, they abide in the porches, or rather not even in the porches but before the porches, which is said more expressively in Greek, ἐπὶ τὰ πρόπυλα. And he will avenge upon all who fill the Church with various iniquities and sins, and with ungodliness and lying, and mingle blood with blood. But if we wish to take this very thing of the souls of individuals, let us understand by the princes and the house of the king the λογισμοὺς, that is, the thoughts and senses, and the soul itself, which ought to be the lodging of the king; and according to the exposition above let us also refer the alien clothing, and all that follows, to each one of the believers who, though they ought to have been clothed with Christ and always to abide within, have covered themselves with the various garments of sins, and, gone out from the Church, that is, from the congregation of the saints, have filled the temple of their body, instead of with virtues, with iniquity and deceit.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.8":["And it will be on the day of the Lord's victim, I will visit upon the princes, and upon the king's sons, and upon all who are clothed in foreign apparel. And I will visit everyone who arrogantly enters upon the threshold on that day, who fill the house of the Lord their God with iniquity and deceit. LXX: And it will be on the day of the Lord's victim, and I will take vengeance upon the princes, and upon the house of the king, and upon all who are clothed in alien garments; and I will take vengeance openly upon all who are in the porches on that day, who fill the house of the Lord their God with ungodliness and deceit. On the day of the Jewish captivity, when the whole people is to be sacrificed, the Lord will visit both upon the princes, who drank strong drink in the morning, and upon the king's sons, either all of the royal stock or certainly, properly, the sons of Josiah, whom we read were either killed or captured; and upon all who are clothed in foreign apparel, that is, who instead of the worship of God venerated idols.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.9":["And upon all who arrogantly enter upon the threshold on that day, that is, against the proud, who with a certain disdain and haughtiness of rank ascend the steps of the temple and the threshold of the sanctuary. Moreover, where we translated: Who arrogantly enter upon the threshold, it can be understood according to the Hebrew as 'who leap over the threshold'; and this is to be taken historically: I will avenge upon those who, according to the first book of Kingdoms (1 Samuel), do not tread on the threshold of the idols, serving superstitions, who have filled the house of the Lord their God not only with the worship of idols but with iniquity and crime and every lie, so that to the error of religion there might be joined also iniquity toward subjects and lying toward neighbours.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.10":["And it will be on that day, says the Lord, the sound of a cry from the Fish Gate, and wailing from the Second, and a great crushing from the hills. LXX: And it will be on that day, says the Lord, the sound of a cry from the gate of those who pierce, and wailing from the Second, and a great crushing from the hills. On the day of the Lord's victim, when he stretches out his hand over Judah and over all who dwell in Jerusalem, and a hostile army has besieged it, there will be the sound of a cry from the Fish Gate and wailing from the Second, and a great crushing from the hills. They called the Fish Gate the one which leads to Diospolis and Joppa, and of all the roads of Jerusalem it was the nearest to the sea; of it Ezra too relates: But the Fish Gate the sons of Hassenaah built; they covered it, and set up its doors and locks and bars. And what he says, And wailing from the Second, signifies the gate of the second wall in the same region, of which it is written also in the book of Kingdoms: And Hilkiah the priest went, and Ahikam, and Achbor, and Shaphan, and Asaiah, to Huldah the prophetess, the wife of Shallum son of Tikvah, son of Harhas, keeper of the garments, and she dwelt in Jerusalem in the Second. And by the great crushing from the hills he speaks of Mount Zion and the higher part of the city, because when the heights and the citadel of the city have been taken, the descent to the lower parts is easier. But if we wish to take that day with which the Lord threatens, as above, as the day of judgment, at that time when the Ancient of days is to sit, and the books are to be opened, and the consciences of each are to be laid bare, then the sound of a cry will be fulfilled from the gate of those who pierce. For the first gate will be that of the eyes, when our sins will be displayed to us before our eyes, and all the pomp and image of our old crimes and vices and luxury will be brought out into the open. Then that will be true which is written: Behold the man and his works before his face. His conscience, then, will torment him, and after he has cried out, pierced to the heart, from the first gate, that of the eyes, he will wail also from the second, which we can understand as the ears. For it is through these senses above all, by which the vices had slipped in, that their punishment will be felt, when we discern what we have done and, hearing the discourse of the learned one and the whole order of our sins, we are driven to wailing, and whatever had been lofty in us is crushed, and was unknown to us because of blindness and deaf ears. Or certainly when lofty words and instruction coming from on high will crush and break us, and this will be fulfilled in deed: I roared from the groaning of my heart, so that there may be in us a sacrifice to God, a troubled spirit; in us, who are men and have not committed sins so great as to be compared to mountains, the hills are crushed. But in the devil and his angels the heights of the mountains will be crushed. Many think that what we have referred, according to the history, to the times of the Babylonians is to be understood of the first coming of the Savior, when, because of their excessive sins and the clamour of the people crying out: His blood be upon us, and upon our children, Jerusalem was surrounded by an army, and by two bears, namely Vespasian and Titus, the crowd of mocking boys was consumed. This understanding indeed suits our faith better, but in such a way that we know it can also agree with the earlier history, or certainly that the earlier captivity is a type of the second and complete overthrow of Jerusalem. This too must be noticed — since adagim in the Hebrew plainly sounds not 'the gate of those who pierce' but 'the Fish Gate' —: allegorically, the Fish Gate is in Jerusalem, through which are brought in the good fish that have been separated from the bad, and those who have remained outside will mourn as the rest go in. Or certainly at the end of the world and at the consummation those will mourn from the first gate, the Fish Gate, who have not kept their baptism; those will mourn from the second who have not done worthy repentance for their sins. And there will be a great crushing upon the hills, who were not even bowed down for their sins so as to submit their necks and bewail their crimes. For through these two gates, of baptism and of repentance, there is either entry or return into Jerusalem, that is, into the Church of God.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.11":["Wail, inhabitants of the Mortar; all the people of Canaan has fallen silent. LXX: Lament, you who inhabit the one cut to pieces, for all the people of Canaan has been made like. The Mortar, which in Hebrew is called machtes, and which was rendered by Aquila εὶς τὸν ὅλμον (into the mortar), must be read with its first syllable not short, lest we think it means σφαῖραν (a ball), but long, so that we may know it is said of the mortar in which grain is pounded, a hollow vessel, suited also to the use of physicians, in which, properly speaking, ptisans are usually crushed. Someone may say that he has understood what the Mortar means, but wishes to know why it has been taken up in the present place. Because there was once a description of the captured city, and it is said: A voice of crying from the Fish Gate, and wailing from the Second, and a great crushing from the hills; now the same order of description is kept, and the wailing is spoken of those who dwell in the valley of Siloam. And Scripture has beautifully not said, you who inhabit the valley, you who inhabit the hovel, but you who inhabit the Mortar, namely because, just as grain is pounded when the pestle strikes from above, so the army, rushing out from the Fish Gate, and from the Second Gate, and from the hills, will run down upon you. Now he has called the people of the Jews the people of Canaan, according to what we read in Daniel: Seed of Canaan and not of Judah; and to Jerusalem: Your father an Amorite, and your mother a Hittite; and in another place: Canaan, in your hand, a balance of iniquity. But if we wish to weave a tropology according to both translations, those are rightly roused to wailing and lamentation who dwell in the uttermost lees of sins, and, sunk in the depths of their crimes, say: I am stuck fast in the mire of the deep, and there is no, he says, substance. Hence it is also added: You who inhabit the one cut to pieces, that is, the soul wounded by many iniquities, or the Church, which is torn by schisms and heresies, and at each wound laments her slain sons. But when he says: Has fallen silent, or has been made like, all the people of Canaan, he signifies that their blasphemy will cease on the day of judgment, and that the mouth which they set on high, and the tongue reaching all the way down to the earth, will fall silent in eternal silence. And because Jerusalem has sinned a sin, and has therefore been turned into commotion, the people is called Canaan, which means commotion. For it cannot say: He has set my feet upon a rock, but, uncertain and wavering, it is always in motion. Hence also Noah, a holy man, after he awoke from sleep, gave Canaan a name out of a curse, saying: Cursed be Canaan the boy; a servant he will be to his brothers. Not only are sinners likened to Canaan, however, but according to the quality and diversity of the sin, one is likened to Pharaoh, another to Nimrod the giant. And on the contrary, through good works and virtues, since the paths of the virtues too are diverse, one takes up the spirit of Abraham, another that of Moses, another that of Elijah; for which reason it is said by the Apostle: Be zealous for the better gifts. But he who is perfect, yet according to that perfection which the human condition can receive, is marked with the likeness of God.\n\nAll who were wrapped in silver have perished. LXX: All who are lifted up by silver have perished. Those, he says, who trusted in riches and had such great wealth that they reckoned themselves as it were wrapped and fenced about by their riches, or certainly, according to the LXX, those who were lifted up into pride and despised the poor, were laid waste when wrath came. And consider at the same time that he did not say: They will perish who are lifted up by silver, but that already now, before the day of punishment comes upon them, in the very fact that they are proud, and always think about their treasures and guard them, they have perished and collapsed. Whoever has understood this, I think, will not much desire riches, in which those who are lifted up will not perish, but have perished. Nor indeed should it be thought that only those have perished who are lifted up by silver, but according to this definition he too will perish who boasts in the nobility of his birth. He will perish who is puffed up by dignities; he will perish who glories in the strength of his body; he will perish who, softened by a womanish languor, grows his hair, plucks out his hairs, polishes his skin, and adorns himself at the mirror, which is properly the passion and madness of women. But if anyone wishes to be lifted up and to be proud with a holy pride, let him be lifted up with the apostles, when he has been found worthy to suffer insult for the name of Jesus Christ; let him glory with the Apostle, who exulted in tribulations, knowing that tribulation works patience, patience hope, and hope does not confound.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.12":["And it will be at that time, I will search Jerusalem with lamps, and I will visit upon the men settled on their lees, who say in their hearts: The Lord will not do good, and he will not do evil. LXX: And it will be on that day, I will search Jerusalem with a lamp, and I will take vengeance upon the men who despise their charges, and say in their hearts: The Lord will not do good, and he will not do evil. At the time and on the day of the captivity of Jerusalem, whether by the Babylonians or by the Romans — because she forsook the law of the Lord, and acted impiously against the Lord her Creator —, the Lord will search out with a lamp all the hidden places of Jerusalem, and will let no one escape unpunished. Let us read the histories of Josephus, and there we shall find it written that princes and kings and powerful men and priests, who had hidden themselves in them for fear of death, were dragged out of sewers too, and caves, and grottoes, and tombs. And I will visit, he says, upon those who trust in their bodies and in their strength, which he disparagingly (διασυρτικῶς) calls lees, or else their sins, in which they were deeply settled; who, doing away with providence, said that God is the author neither of good nor of evil, that is, that he would render neither good things to the good nor evil things to the evil, but that all things were governed by the will of fortune and borne along by uncertain chance. But at the consummation of the world, since that is understood as the day of the Lord itself, the Lord will search Jerusalem, that is, his Church, with a lamp; and he will take vengeance upon the men who are despisers, who would not keep their charges, that is, who despised the commandments of the Lord, and moreover, saying that they sinned with reason, blasphemed in their hearts, that it was of no profit to do good, nor of any harm to do evil, because God would render neither the reward of good works nor the punishment of evil ones. And rightly Jerusalem, that is, the Church — which was formerly called Jebus, which means trodden down —, was called Jebus when she was being trodden down by the nations and was the plaything of demons, and after the peace of the Lord began to dwell in her, and his place was made in peace, she obtained the name Jerusalem. Since, therefore, in the last time, as we have often said already, charity will grow cold because iniquity is multiplied, and the light of the sun will withdraw from Jerusalem, and there will be such great desolation that even the elect of God will scarcely be saved, then the Lord will search out all the vices in Jerusalem with the lamp of his word and reason, and will bring them out into the open, and judgment and vengeance will be passed even for an idle word, not upon sinners — for even sin could merit pardon —, but upon despisers, of whom it is said in Habakkuk: Behold, you despisers, and look, and in another place: Why do you not look upon the despisers?, and then: But he who is arrogant, and a despiser, a proud man, and so on. Vengeance will fall upon these, who did not keep the Lord's charges, and say in their hearts: The Lord will not do good, and he will not do evil; not that God does evil, but that punishment seems evil to him who suffers it. Otherwise the physician's scalpel too will be evil, because it cuts open wounds and amputates putrid flesh; and a father will be evil when he beats his son to correct him from his vices, and a teacher evil when he rebukes his pupil to instruct him: For all discipline for the present seems to be not of joy, but of sorrow; but afterwards it will yield the peaceful fruit to those who have been trained by it.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.13-14":["And their strength will be for plunder, and their house for a desert. And they will build houses, and will not inhabit them, and they will plant vineyards, and will not drink their wine. Close is the great day of the Lord; it is close, and exceedingly swift. LXX: And their strength will be for pillage, and their houses will be scattered. And they will build houses, and will not dwell in them, and they will plant vineyards, and will not drink their wine, for close is the great day of the Lord; close and exceedingly swift. It is plain that in both captivities their whole army was cut down, and their houses torn down, and their fields and vineyards laid waste, and that the patience of God no longer put them off. But while they were saying to the prophets, this will be for a time, and for many days, the great day of the Lord came upon them, exceedingly swift.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.13":["According to tropology, however, when the time of judgment comes, or of each one's death and departure from the world, then all their strength will be for plunder, so that what was strong for evil, and was lifted up against the Lord, may be made weak and broken and turned to something better. Just as, if someone plunders the strength of a robber and a pirate and a thief, and renders them weak, their weakness profits them; for with their limbs disabled, which they formerly did not use well, they will cease from evil work. As for what follows: And their house for a desert, there are many in the Church building Zion in blood and Jerusalem in iniquity, whom it profits that such houses be destroyed. Let us read Leviticus, where a leprous house is ordered to be destroyed. And because the leprosy is lasting and spreads, its stones and timbers and all its dust are ordered to be thrown outside the city into an unclean place. And in the beginning of Jeremiah too something of the kind is written: Behold, I have put my words into your mouth; behold, I have set you today over nations and kingdoms, to root up and to undermine, and to destroy, and to build, and to plant. A very bad building is destroyed so that afterwards a good building may be put up; a wicked planting is rooted up so that a planting may be set in its place. Hence we also read in Solomon: It is better to dwell under the open sky than in the plaster of strife with iniquity, and in a new house. As a good God, therefore, he destroys the houses of those who were settled on their lees, and had said in their heart: The Lord will not do good and he will not do evil; and he does not allow them to dwell in leprous and unclean houses, nor does he let them drink wine from the vineyards which they had planted. For if they had planted a vineyard of Sorek, and a chosen vineyard, wholly true, they would have drunk their wine, and would have been inebriated with the patriarch Noah, and with Joseph at noon; but because they said: The Lord will not do good and he will not do evil, and: Their vine was of the vine of Sodom, and their shoot of Gomorrah. Their grape is a grape of gall, and theirs is a cluster of bitterness; the fury of dragons is their wine, and the incurable fury of asps, therefore they have planted vineyards and will not drink their wine. And it is said mystically of Sodom and Gomorrah that all their planting perished. For if they had remained in what they had begun, so as to be like the paradise of God, and had not ended in evil, so as to be like the land of Egypt, their planting would surely have remained. Something of the kind is sounded also by that verse concerning the Egyptians in the psalms: He killed their vineyards with hail, and their mulberry trees with frost. For as a most merciful God he killed and overturned every Egyptian planting, and the shrubs which, rooted in Egypt, bear blood-red fruits, so that those who planted badly may not drink, and may not eat fruit from them.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.14-16":["The voice of the day of the Lord is bitter; there the strong man will be afflicted.\n\nThat day is a day of wrath, a day of tribulation and distress, a day of calamity and misery, a day of darkness and gloom, a day of cloud and whirlwind, a day of trumpet and clangor against the fortified cities and against the lofty corners. LXX: The voice of the day of the Lord is bitter and harsh; it has been set, strong. A day of wrath is that day, a day of tribulation and constraint, a day of misery and destruction, a day of darkness and whirlwind, a day of cloud and gloom, a day of trumpet and shouting against the fortified cities and against the lofty corners. In keeping with what precedes, take it either of the Babylonian captivity or of the last one, which they suffered at the hands of the Romans, over which the Lord also wept for Jerusalem, saying: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets, and stone those who are sent to you, and so on. For truly vengeance was exacted from the blood of Abel the just to the blood of Zechariah, whom they killed between the temple and the altar, and at the last for the Son of God; saying: His blood be upon us and upon our children, they experienced a bitter day, because they had provoked the Lord to bitterness; a day appointed by the Lord, on which not just any weak men, but the strongest men will be pressed down, and wrath will come upon them to the end. For they had often endured the wrath of the Lord before as well, but that wrath had not been one of consummation and of the end. What need is there now to describe how great the calamities were that they endured in both captivities, and how those who spurned the light of the Lord passed their lives in darkness and gloom, and those who would not hear the trumpets of the solemn days heard the shouting of men at war? As for the fortified cities and the lofty corners of Judea, which have been torn down to the very ground, I think it is a matter for the judgment of the eyes rather than of the ears, especially for us who are now in this province: we may see, we may test what is written. In what were once great cities we scarcely discern small traces of ruins. Of Shiloh, where the tabernacle and the ark of the covenant of the Lord were, scarcely the foundations of the altar are shown. Gibeah, that city of Saul, has been torn down to its foundations. Ramah and Beth-horon and the other noble cities built by Solomon are pointed out as small hamlets. Let us read Josephus and the prophecy of Zephaniah, and we shall see the history of the latter; and this is to be said not only of the captivity, but to the present day the faithless tenants, after the killing of the servants and at the last of the Son of God, are forbidden to enter Jerusalem except to lament; and to be permitted to weep over the ruin of their city, they pay a price, so that those who once bought the blood of Christ buy their own tears, and not even their weeping is free to them. You may see, on the day on which Jerusalem was captured and destroyed by the Romans, a mournful people coming, decrepit little women and old men covered with rags and years flocking together, showing in their bodies and in their dress the wrath of the Lord. A crowd of wretches gathers, and while the gibbet of the Lord gleams, and his ἀναστάσει (the Resurrection: the Church of the Anastasis in Jerusalem) shines, and the standard of the cross blazes from the Mount of Olives as well, you may see the wretched people lamenting the ruins of their temple, and yet not deserving pity; the tears are still on their cheeks, their arms are livid, and their hair dishevelled, and the soldier demands a fee so that they may be allowed to weep longer; and does anyone, seeing these things, doubt about the day of tribulation and distress, about the day of calamity and misery, about the day of darkness and gloom, about the day of cloud and whirlwind, about the day of trumpet and clangor? For they have trumpets even in their mourning, and, according to the prophecy, the voice of solemnity is turned into lamentation. They wail over the ashes of the sanctuary, and over the destroyed altar, and over the once fortified cities, and over the lofty corners of the temple, from which they once cast down James the brother of the Lord. Let this be said about the captivity of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.14":["Near is the great day of the Lord and exceedingly swift, which no one can withstand; and it is near either because of eternity, since nothing is long to it, or because of the greatness of the punishment, since to one who suffers it, the torment that is to be inflicted never seems far off. Or else it is near, as we said above, when we depart from the world, and each one's death has brought about the consummation of the world; and not only near, but also exceedingly swift, so that the swiftness of its coming may be shown in what is added, exceedingly.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But if, as above, we refer the day of the Lord to the consummation of the world, or to the end of each one's life, the interpretation will be plain: that the voice of the day of the Lord is bitter, and full of constraint and of wrath, and that the strong man is afflicted there, since even those who are holy are saved indeed, but yet so as through fire.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.15-16":["It will be a day of cloud and whirlwind; for the tempest of the Lord will come upon him, and the clangor of the trumpet which the Apostle signifies, saying: At the last trumpet. For it will be a day of trumpet and clangor against the fortified cities, which they had built for themselves with Cain in the multitude of their sins. And against the lofty corners: perverse works, turning aside from the right way of the Lord. Hence the Pharisees, ὑποκρίται (hypocrites), are also rebuked by the Savior, because they worship at the corners of the streets. For the way that is straight and tends toward paradise is narrow, and strait is the way which leads to life. But the way that is broken, having corners, and crooked, is broad and spacious, the way which leads to death. And at the same time, amid the words of the Lord's severity, discern his clemency: that the day is bitter, and a day of wrath, and a day of tribulation, and a day of trumpet and clangor, for this reason, that the badly fortified cities and the perverse corners may be destroyed.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.15":["That day is a day of tribulation, and distress, and calamity, and misery, on which they will say: Woe to us, for we have become wretched. A day of darkness: For everyone who does evil hates the light and does not come to the light, and it must be that darkness overtakes him who hates the light.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.17-18":["And I will afflict men, and they will walk like the blind, because they have sinned against the Lord; and their blood will be poured out like soil, and their bodies like dung; but even their silver and gold will not be able to deliver them on the day of the wrath of the Lord; in the fire of his zeal the whole earth will be devoured, for he will make a consummation with haste for all who inhabit the earth. LXX: And I will afflict men, and they will walk like the blind, because they have sinned against the Lord, and he will pour out their blood like dust, and their flesh like the dung of oxen, and their silver and their gold will not be able to rescue them on the day of the wrath of the Lord; and in the fire of his zeal the whole earth will be consumed, for he will make a consummation and haste upon all who inhabit the earth. It is not difficult to say, according to the former sense, that Jerusalem suffered these things, which she endured because of the cross of the Lord; for the visitation of the Lord departed from her, and all the men in the whole of Judea were afflicted, and because of the greatness of the oppression they walked like the blind, not knowing what to do. And they suffered these things because they had sinned against the Lord, that is, against the Son of God. For because they shed the blood of the prophets and the blood of Christ, their blood was poured out like soil throughout the whole region, and their bodies remained unburied, like dung upon the face of the earth. Their rich men too, who had gathered silver and gold by excessive iniquity, could not be delivered by their wealth from the day of the wrath of the Lord; for the fire of the Lord's zeal, kindled against them, devoured the whole province. Nor was the time in between long, for forty-two years after the Lord's cross Jerusalem was surrounded by an army, and her consummation was made with haste, and not hers alone, but that of all the inhabitants of the land of Judea.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","On the day of consummation, therefore, whether general or particular, all the blood that has been poured out will cry to the Lord and will appear in the midst, and the works of blood and the earthly works will lie like dust and dung, and silver and gold will not be able to deliver the rich from the day of wrath, while he who dies hears: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be? Not that we deny that gold and silver deliver the rich from death — For the ransom of a man's soul is his own riches —, but that they cannot deliver them at that time when they leave their riches behind of necessity. For the whole earth and all things that are earthly will be devoured by the zeal of the Lord. And when he says, by zeal, understand the Lord as still loving. For if he did not love the human soul, he would never be jealous over it, and, like a husband, take vengeance on the sin of his wife; for if he did not love her, he would not even be angry at her adultery. And the Lord will do this with haste for all who inhabit the earth, for those who have given themselves wholly to the earth, and were not strangers and sojourners, like the just man who speaks: I am a stranger on the earth, and a sojourner like all my fathers. And again elsewhere, not wishing to dwell any longer in the tabernacle of the flesh, he bears witness with a tearful voice, saying: Woe is me, that my sojourning has been prolonged. And indeed we who are in the tabernacle of this body groan, complaining: Wretched man that I am, who will deliver me from the body of this death?\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.1.17":["But at the consummation, whether of the world or of each one, all men will be afflicted who remained men and died as men. And they will walk like the blind, because they have lost the light of the virtues, and they will have no place for repentance; and they will suffer these things because they have sinned against the Lord. For if the Lord is justice, truth, holiness, and the other virtues, whoever has acted unjustly, and has lied, and has pursued harlots and vices, has sinned against the Lord. But what follows: And their blood will be poured out like soil, and their bodies like the dung of oxen, seems absurd, that we should say that in the resurrection of the dead, and at the consummation of the world and the judgment, blood is poured out and bodies lie like dung. Therefore what is said to Noah: And the blood of your souls I will require at the hand of all the beasts, and at the hand of man, and at the hand of his brother I will require the soul of man; whoever sheds the blood of man, for his blood shall his blood be poured out, is both ridiculous to believe of the resurrection and cannot hold in this life. For how many have shed blood, and their own blood has not been poured out? And others have killed a man by poison, or by hanging, and yet, though the man is dead, has blood been poured out? How then is the Lord going to pour out their blood in retaliation, when he who killed did not pour out blood? The blood of a man, therefore, must be understood as τὸ ζωτικὸν αἶτος, that is, the vital principle, by which he is quickened and sustained and lives; and whoever has poured this out, whether by a stumbling block or by perversity of doctrine, will have it poured out from him on the day of judgment, that is, he will be forced to lose what he seemed to himself to have as his vital principle. In keeping with blood of this kind, flesh too is understood, of which Isaiah also says: All flesh is grass, and the Lord in Genesis: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh, and the Apostle, of both: Flesh and blood will not be able to possess the kingdom of God, nor will corruption inherit the incorruption of God.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.1-2":["Come together, gather yourselves, unlovable nation, before the command brings forth the day like passing dust; before the wrath of the fury of the Lord comes upon you, before the wrath of the indignation of the Lord comes upon you. LXX: Be gathered and be collected together, untaught nation, before you are like a flower passing away in a day, before the wrath of the Lord comes upon you, before the day of the wrath of the fury of the Lord rushes upon you. After the description of the evils which are to come on the day of the Lord — according to the twofold explanation of the captivity which we gave above —, the people is called to repentance, and it is said to it: Come together, and gather yourselves, or, as is written in the LXX: Be collected together, that is, according to the Apostle, be bound to one another by the bond of charity, unlovable nation, you who are unworthy of the love of God, or untaught nation, of which it is said in Deuteronomy: A foolish people, and not wise, and in Jeremiah: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not received discipline, before what has been prophesied comes to pass, before the effect follows the command, which will come as easily as passing dust, before the fury of the Lord is fulfilled upon you. And at the same time discern the clemency of God: for the prudent it would have been enough for him to have described the onset of the coming evils; but because he does not wish to inflict punishments, but only to frighten those who are about to suffer them, he himself calls them to repentance, so that he may not do what he has threatened. In a general sense, however, the whole multitude of believers, and of those who are called the people of God, is gathered into the Church, and it is said to it: Come together into the Church, join yourselves to one another in charity and peace, O untaught nation, you who will not receive the discipline of God, nor have knowledge of his commandments, but take delight in riches and in bodily health and in the beauty of this world, and in the pleasures of the flesh as well, which passes like a flower that withers in a single day. And for this reason I say to you: Come together, join yourselves, lest, when the time of judgment has come and all your glory has passed away, you should wish to repent only then, when there will be no place for repentance, but for punishments. Someone may ask how this same thing can be understood of each person departing from the world. It is said, then, to each one: O you who, occupied with the business of the world, run about in all directions, return to the Church of the saints, and join yourself to the life and company of those whom you see pleasing God, and draw the loosened limbs of your soul, which do not hold together, into the one framework of wisdom, and cling to her embrace, and hear in a mystical sense: Be strengthened, you slack hands; you feeble knees, be made firm; and do not glory in the goods of the flesh and in its flower, which passes away: For all flesh is grass, and all its glory like the flower of grass. The grass has withered, and the flower has fallen; but the word of the Lord abides for ever. We can use this passage as occasion requires, if ever we see someone occupied with the honors of the world and with gathering riches, coming to the Church either rarely or never, and say to him: gather yourself and join yourself to the people of God, you who do not hear the commandments of the Lord, before your glory passes away, before the day of the fury of the Lord comes upon you.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.3":["Seek the Lord, all you meek of the earth, who have wrought his judgment; seek the just one, seek the meek one, if somehow you may be hidden on the day of the fury of the Lord. For Gaza will be destroyed, and Ashkelon into a wilderness. Ashdod they will cast out at noon, and Ekron will be uprooted. LXX: Seek the Lord, all you humble of the earth; work judgment, and seek justice and answer them, that you may be protected on the day of the wrath of the Lord; for Gaza will be plundered, and Ashkelon into a wilderness; and Ashdod will be cast forth at noon, and Ekron will be uprooted. He is called humble of the earth who has been humbled not by humility, which signifies a virtue, but by sins, and cannot say with Christ: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart. For everyone who humbles himself will be exalted. And in another place the word is directed to the holy man: The greater you are, the more humble yourself, and before God you will find grace. But he who has been humbled by sins and weighed down by the conscience of his offenses, and says: Like a heavy burden, they have grown heavy upon me, that man ought to hear: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you. Let this be said at the beginning according to the LXX translators. But according to the Hebrew there is another sense. For it is said to the saints: O you who keep my precepts, who, set on the earth and knowing that everyone who humbles himself will be exalted, have imitated my meekness and have wrought judgment, seek the Lord in your meekness. And if you wish to know who this Lord is, seek the just one, seek the meek one: For the Father has given all judgment to the Son, who is going to judge justly. And because you are meek, therefore seek the meek one, so that whatever is lacking in your meekness may be filled up by him who is the fountain of meekness. And this I say to you: If somehow you may be hidden on the day of the fury of the Lord; that is, if perhaps, from the fact that you have sought the Lord and have wrought his justice, you may be able to avoid the wrath of the coming day and to escape the captivity that is to be brought upon the people of the Jews either by Nebuchadnezzar or by the Romans.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But according to the Seventy, the humble of the earth, of whom we spoke above, are commanded to work judgment and to seek justice, which I do not think is anything other than Christ. And because everyone who seeks will find, what they have found let them answer to others, that is, let them teach others: For wisdom hidden and a treasure that does not appear, what profit is there in both? And this, he says, I command you, that you may be protected on the day of the wrath of the Lord, either at the consummation of the world or at the departure of each one from this world.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.4":["But if he is in doubt about those who have done his judgments, saying: If somehow you may be hidden on the day of the fury of the Lord, what will become of sinners? For so great a devastation will come upon the land of Judea, and so lofty a conqueror, the Babylonian army, will come up hither, that the same captivity will seize even the most powerful cities of the Philistines, which always withstood you in equal combat. For Gaza will be destroyed, and Ashkelon will be reduced to desolation, and Ashdod they will drag into captivity not by stealth but by war, that is, in open daylight and by victory; and Ekron, which is interpreted uprooting, will undergo what its name signifies, that is, it will be uprooted. This is according to the letter and the Hebrew truth.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","For Gaza and Ashkelon and Ashdod and Ekron will undergo different punishments. For Gaza is interpreted its strength. All, therefore, who applaud themselves in strength of body and worldly power, and say with the devil, By strength I will do it, will be plundered on the day of the wrath of the Lord and brought to nothing. Ashkelon also, which is called weighed, or murderous fire, when the day of the wrath of the Lord comes, will feel the measure of its crime and will be pressed down by the same weight with which it acted. And because it burned to shed blood, and caused many souls to stumble, and in it was fulfilled: The man of blood and deceit the Lord will abhor, it will not be plundered like Gaza, but, reduced to desolation, it will be burned to dust by the fires of Gehenna. Ashdod too, which in Hebrew is called esdod and in our language means fire of generation, will be laid waste in clear light; for it burned with lust and raged in the fire of generation. And because all adulterers, their hearts are like an oven, and they have been wounded by burning arrows, not in darkness, not in a hidden judgment, but at noon, that is, when the saints will receive their full brightness, they will be cast forth into darkness, and they will not have the fellowship of the saints. But Ekron, which is interpreted barrenness, or uprooting, because it bore no fruits and by the perversity of its teaching uprooted very many, will itself also be uprooted. Understand all these varieties in the vices and sins of souls, and that on the day of judgment the fire will test of what sort each one's work is.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.5-7":["Woe to you who inhabit the cord of the sea, nation of the lost; the word of the Lord is upon you, Canaan, land of the Philistines, and I will destroy you so that there is no inhabitant. And the cord of the sea will be a resting place of shepherds, and folds of flocks; and the cord will be his who has remained of the house of Judah; there they will feed, in the houses of Ashkelon they will rest at evening, for the Lord their God will visit them and will turn back their captivity. LXX: Woe to you who inhabit the cord of the sea, sojourners of the Cretans; the word of the Lord is upon you, Canaan, land of the foreigners, and I will destroy you from habitation. And Crete will be a pasture of shepherds, and a fold of flocks, and the cord of the sea will be for those who are left of the house of Judah; upon them they will feed in the houses of Ashkelon; after noon they will turn aside from the face of the sons of Judah, for the Lord their God will visit them and will turn back their captivity. As regards the history, it is not difficult to interpret, because above he had said: Gaza will be destroyed, and Ashkelon into a wilderness. Ashdod they will cast out at noon, and Ekron will be uprooted. Four great cities of Philistia having been named, it is plain that the word of the Lord is now addressed to the province itself in general, and that it is foretold to it: Woe to you who inhabit the cord of the sea, when the Babylonian comes, for those too who dwell beside the sea will perish and be carried off. And that the land of the Philistines is the land of Canaan, no one doubts. And I will destroy you, he says, so that there is no inhabitant; and you who rejoiced at the overthrow of Judea will come to such devastation that all your most fortified cities will become folds of shepherds. And after the Lord has visited his people and made them return under Zerubbabel and Joshua, and they have built the temple and raised up Jerusalem again, you will be so uncultivated, and so covered with briars and nettles, that shepherds from the remnant of the Jews will rest in Ashkelon after noon and make their flocks lie down in a once noble city; and this will happen because the Lord will visit his people and turn back its captivity. Whether this happened or not, that is for God to see. For it is not now our purpose to weave together the truth of the history, but to make known to our own people what we have received from the Hebrews.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Again, where we said: And the cord of the sea will be a resting place of shepherds, and all the translators agreed with this translation, in the LXX is written: And Crete will be a pasture of the flock, and a fold of flocks. Therefore, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, and keeping to the path of the common edition that we once began, we ask whether we have read the name of Crete elsewhere in the holy Scriptures. And, unless I am mistaken, this is ready to hand: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy bellies. This testimony is true. Those who waver and are carried about by every wind of doctrine, in the deceit of men, in the craftiness of error, though they ought to have dwelt in the land of confession, that is, in the land of Judea, chose rather to be strangers of the Cretans, who are beaten this way and that by the various waves of the sea, and sound with Corybantian bronze, and are, according to the Apostle: Like a tinkling cymbal. And because they are strangers of the Cretans, therefore the word of God, that is, a threat, comes to them, and they are called the land of Canaan, always in the surge, always in motion, and the land of the foreigners; for, being alien from God, they dwell in the cord of the sea and in the region of Crete. And so the word of God comes to them, either at the consummation and end of the world, or daily through churchmen and those who can say with the Apostle: Or do you seek a proof of Christ, who speaks in me? so that they may be cast out of Crete and be destroyed from their former habitations, and that region which formerly held a lost flock may begin to be a fold of the flocks of Christ; and Judah, that is, true confession, may dwell in the cords of the sea. And when the world has now begun to be at evening, and of many called few are chosen, and those who are now called the remnant of the house of Judah have fed those who formerly fed in the sea and in Crete and in falsehood, they will turn aside into the houses of Ashkelon, that is, where formerly the fire of the devil and the blood of the slain flowed. For Ashkelon is interpreted murderous fire. And this will happen because the Lord will visit his people, and those who before were easily caught by the sophisms of heretics will, as if coming out of captivity, conquer their adversaries and dwell in their tents.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.5":["But according to the spiritual sense and the translation of the LXX the understanding is difficult, especially since they also disagree in the interpretation. For where we translated nation of the lost, they said strangers of the Cretans; and where in Hebrew is written goi chorethim, for goi, that is, nation, they read car, that is, stranger; and chorethim, which means of the lost, they took for the name of the island of Crete. Indeed both Aquila and the Fifth Edition translated ἔθνος ὀλέθριον (a destructive nation); and Theodotion ἔθνος ὀλεθρίας (a nation of destruction); Symmachus too ἔθνος ὀλεθρευόμενον (a nation being destroyed), all of which agree with our translation.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.7":["But what is read in the LXX, From the face of the sons of Judah, we have marked with an obelus, for it is neither in the Hebrew nor found in any of the translators, and it disturbs the context and the sense of the chapter; not that it would have been difficult to weave a meaning somehow even with this set down; but we have once for all resolved to follow the truth of the translation, and the judgment of the more learned reader rather than that of the crowd.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.8":["I have heard the reproach of Moab and the blasphemies of the sons of Ammon, with which they reproached my people, and they were magnified over their borders. Therefore, as I live, says the Lord God of hosts, the God of Israel, Moab will be as Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah, a dryness of thorns and heaps of salt, and a wilderness for ever; the remnants of my people will plunder them; the residue of my nation will possess them. This will befall them for their pride, because they blasphemed and were magnified over the people of the Lord of hosts. Terrible is the Lord over them, and he will waste away all the gods of the earth, and men will worship him from their place, all the islands of the nations. LXX: I have heard the reproach of Moab and the insults of the sons of Ammon, in which they reproached my people and were magnified over my borders. Therefore, as I live, says the Lord of powers, the God of Israel, Moab will be like Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah, and Damascus left like a heap of the threshing floor, and scattered for ever; and those who are left of my people will plunder them, and the residue of my nation will possess them; this will be to them for their insult, because they reproached and were magnified over the Lord Almighty; the Lord will be manifest over them, and he will destroy all the gods of the nations of the earth, and they will worship him, each from his place, all the islands of the nations. Except for the prophet Daniel, who quite often sees visions of the four kingdoms and sets out their differences under one image after another, Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel do this: after the vision of Judah they turn their prophecy against the other nations that are round about it, and announce, according to the ἰδιώματα (particular characters) of each, what is going to come upon them, and dwell at some length on describing them; and this the prophet Zephaniah now does, briefly indeed, yet in the same order. For after the Philistines, against whom the threat ran on before above, Gaza, he says, will be destroyed, and Ashkelon into a wilderness, Ashdod they will cast out at noon, and Ekron will be uprooted. Now the prophecy is woven against Moab and the sons of Ammon, or, as is added in the LXX, against Damascus, which in Isaiah is called Aram, because by furnishing aid to Nebuchadnezzar they laid waste Judah, trampled its sanctuary, overthrew the temple as well, and, when the people of Israel had been subjugated, blasphemed the Lord. For Nebuchadnezzar and the Chaldeans, after demolishing the cities of the Jews, oppressed the other nations too, and so it came about that those who had insulted the people of God were themselves also pressed by the same straits of evils, and had Judah as a fellow servant, whom they had thought they had as a subject. Therefore, before the captivity comes, while Josiah is still king and Jerusalem and the temple are not yet overthrown, the prophecy is directed against those who insult, so that the evils once suffered by the people of God might be lightened by the evil of the other nations. I have heard, he says, the reproach of Moab, which is now called Areopolis, and the blasphemies of the sons of Ammon, which is itself also a city of Arabia, second after Bozrah, and is called Philadelphia; in which they reproached my people and, having cast out the Jews, extended their own boundaries in their land.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But the man who is learned, and compares spiritual things with spiritual, and seeks not the things that are below but those that are above, and rises with Christ from the underworld, and, putting off the old man, is clothed with the new, will refer the reproaches of Moab and the blasphemies of the sons of Ammon to the masters of doctrines contrary to the Church, who themselves also seem to be of the stock of Abraham, and to have escaped the burning of Sodom and Gomorrah, and to dwell in little Zoar. But because their generation is in darkness, and they cannot look upon the light of truth — for they have turned aside from God the Father, which is interpreted from the names Lot and Moab —, and they have ceased to be sons of God, which is what my people means, and, conceived of incestuous unions in a dark cave, they came into being, therefore to this day, insulting the simplicity of the sons of Judah, they desire to magnify their own possession over its borders; of whom it is said in Proverbs: Do not remove the everlasting boundaries which your fathers set. See the heretics applauding themselves in dialectic and rhetoric and the doctrines of every sophism, despising the rusticity of the Church, and looking down on it as unworthy of their mysteries, which they have fashioned for themselves like idols, and holding it for nothing, and you will not ask what the reproaches of Moab are, and the insults of the sons of Ammon, in which they reproached the people of God.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.9-10":["And so Moab and Ammon and Damascus, which have prepared themselves against the knowledge of the Lord, and have blasphemed the people of God, and spoken very many insults against it, and wished to extend their borders in the land of the Church and to possess the people of God, will be deserted and destroyed; and the remnant of the people of God, that is, churchmen learned in the Lord's Scriptures, will plunder them, and the residue of the Lord's nation will possess them, and this will be to them for the insult with which they reproached and were magnified against the Lord Almighty. See the clemency, see the mercy of the Lord: he suffers insult, he is blasphemed, his borders are plundered, and what does he do? He sends to the remnant of his people, of whom we have spoken, that he may divide out for himself those who blaspheme him and lead them into his own possession. For it is much better for a fool to serve a wise man, and for his folly to be corrected by the wisdom of the Lord, than for him to be left to his own stupidity.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.9-11":["Now let us return to the LXX translators, and let us compel the Jews, who follow only the history, to explain to us when Moab and the sons of Ammon became like Sodom and Gomorrah, and like heaps of salt, and deserted for ever; they must show the sulphurous rains, the vineyards, the land turned into ashes and embers, and that the sea which is now called Dead flooded over from what were once wells of salt, when the Jews plundered them, when the remaining Israelite peoples possessed them. Or what indignation of the Lord is it, for blasphemy and insults, to waste away and destroy them? Not Moab and Ammon, but the whole earth, so that they may worship him, each from his place, all the islands of the nations? This he will rather bestow on the blasphemers as a benefit, that they may return from error to salvation. But if they wish to say that after the return from Babylon these nations were subjected to the people of Israel, first we shall demand the authority of the Scriptures by which they may prove that this happened; then, when they are quite unable to demonstrate it, we shall grant it to them out of superfluity and say: Suppose that what you say happened; what justice of God would it be that the grandfathers blasphemed and the forefathers reproached, and afterwards it was repaid to the grandsons? Especially since that sentence by which it was formerly said in the law that the sins of the fathers are repaid upon the sons in the third and the fourth generation has been loosed through Ezekiel: As I live, says the Lord; this parable shall no longer be spoken, but the soul that sins, it shall die. And at the same time notice that what was said is a parable, and does not mean what they bring forward on the surface of the letter. But if it is unjust to repay grandsons for what their grandfathers did wrong, how much more unjust is it, according to the folly of the Jews, to hope that this very thing will happen at the consummation of the world, when not their Christ, as they think, but the Antichrist is going to come. For wherever they are hard pressed in the history to show that what was said has been fulfilled, they at once leap over to the future times of Christ, and everything that they cannot explain they promise themselves after many ages; and they say that Moab and the sons of Ammon, and Egypt and the Philistines, and Idumea, which now insult the Jews, are to be punished at that time. Let us ask them, therefore, why God punishes these nations above all, and not the whole world, in which the Jews are scattered far and wide. For if Moab deserves to be chastised for insulting the Jews, and the sons of Ammon and the other nations round about, why is Gaul not chastised? Why does he not put the Britains in the threat? Why are the Spains exempt from punishment? For what reason is nothing said of Italy? Why is there silence about Africa? And to say it once for all, since the whole world holds the Jews captive, what so great a crime have the nations alone committed that are round about it, that they alone above all should be named? These things against the Jews, and as a general explanation in all the prophets wherever any prophecy is spoken against the nations.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.9":["Therefore, because they have blasphemed me and reproached my people, I, the Lord of hosts, who am able to fulfil what I threaten, and the God of Israel, who myself suffer injury in my people, will make Moab to be as Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah. Let us read visions of this kind in Isaiah and Jeremiah, and we shall find the same things that we now read here. A dryness of thorns and heaps of salt, and a wilderness for ever: in its place the LXX, meaning I know not what, translated: Damascus torn up and left, deceived, unless I am mistaken, by the ambiguity of the word; for dryness, which in Hebrew is called mamasac, if its first letter mem is changed and daleth is taken, has the same remaining letters as Damascus too, and in place of the former word damasec can be read. But it is asked how these cities, that is, Moab and the sons of Ammon, have been reduced to Sodom and Gomorrah, and, like an aridity of thorns and heaps of salt, are not to be built up for ever? And that they were indeed laid waste like Sodom and Gomorrah, there is no difficulty in explaining. But what follows, They will be deserted for ever, either we shall interpret of the destruction of their kingdom — because afterwards, overthrown by the Chaldeans, they lost their kingdom, and then, held either by the Antiochi or by the Ptolemies, they at last bowed their necks to the Roman empire —, or else it must certainly be taken as said ὑπερβολικῶς (hyperbolically); for lolam signifies both eternity and an age, so that it can be taken both for one age and for some length of time, and for one lifetime. And those who are left of the people of Israel will lay them waste, and will possess the once blaspheming auxiliaries of the Chaldeans.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Now what we said above, that Damascus is not found in the Hebrew, nor in any other translator, we shall prove also from the very order of Scripture. For to what he had said, I have heard the reproach of Moab, and the blasphemies of the sons of Ammon, he afterwards related: That Moab will be as Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah. As for what follows, then, And Damascus deserted, like a heap of the threshing floor, he ought also to have said something about Damascus beforehand, so that, just as he had said of the two nations, Moab will be as Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah, whose sins he had already stated before. So he would also have described either the reproaches or the blasphemies of Damascus, so that afterwards he might be seen to inflict punishment deservedly. But this very phrase too, Like a heap of the threshing floor, which in Greek is said ὡς θιμωνιὰ ἄλωνος, we think the LXX translated ἀλος, that is, of salt; but that by unskilled men, who took θιμωνίαν, that is, a heap of grain or of crops, in place of ἀλος, with the two letters ω and ν added, as though to follow on from the crops, ἄλωνος, that is, of the threshing floor, was set down. Let this be said about the variety and error of interpretation, and about the difficulty of the history.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","And so the Lord swore by himself, saying: As I live, says the Lord. And beautifully, to distinguish himself from the dead gods, who are called idols, the God of Israel, that is, of the people that sees God, says that he lives; whereas these blaspheming nations, that is, Moab and the sons of Ammon, of whom we said above: They will be as Sodom and Gomorrah, seem indeed to themselves, in that they are not Gentiles, to have come out of Sodom and Gomorrah; but because they blaspheme the people of God and act against Israel, they will be reckoned as Sodom and Gomorrah; and they will be blotted out as those were blotted out before, having in themselves no trace of greenness and life. Nor is it surprising if we understand this of heretics, that they are reckoned as Sodom and Gomorrah, since even to churchmen who have not observed the commandments of God and have gone out from his precepts it is said through Isaiah: Hear the word of the Lord, rulers of Sodom, and attend to the law of the Lord, people of Gomorrah. And to the elders who desire, under the figure of Susanna, to corrupt the chastity of the Church, Daniel may say: This is the judgment of God, seed of Canaan, and not of Judah. And that you may know that whenever Sodom and Gomorrah and Egypt are named, it is not said of these provinces which we see with our eyes but of other, spiritual ones, which the prophetic word threatens, read in the Apocalypse of John: But the place in which the Lord was crucified is called spiritually Sodom and Egypt. If, then, Jerusalem, in which the Lord was crucified, is called spiritually Sodom and Egypt, why should not, conversely, Egypt and Sodom and Gomorrah, if they have done the works of Jerusalem and of the land of Judah, be transferred into the land of the Lord's lot? Indeed David too was not of the priests, nor was it lawful for him to eat the loaves of proposition; but because he grew through his works one by one, and the persecution of Saul was the advancement of his virtues, therefore in his flight, without knowing it, he is suddenly made a prelate, and receives the loaves of proposition, and does not violate the commandment of God. We have said all this because Moab will be as Sodom, and the sons of Ammon as Gomorrah. Damascus too, which is interpreted drinking blood, or blood of sackcloth, will be left by the mercy of God like a heap of salt. For because its ruler is King Aretas, and the Damascenes desire to kill Paul, and he is let down through the wall in a basket, it is not said to Damascus: You are the salt of the world; nor is it called that salt which is always offered with the sacrifices, but the salt that has lost its savor, of which it is written in the Gospel: But if the salt has lost its savor, with what will it be salted? It is useful for nothing, neither for the earth nor for the dunghill, but they cast it out, so that it may be trodden underfoot by men.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.10-11":["And this will befall them for their pride, because they blasphemed and were magnified against the people of the Lord of hosts; and this Lord of hosts will be terrible over them, and his terror will not kill the proud; he will not shed the blood of the blasphemers, but will destroy and waste away all their idols, so that those who before were held by error and did not perceive the benefits of the Lord, pressed by the necessity of evils, may know that idols profit nothing, and may worship him, each one from his place, all the islands of the nations. Thus far according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.11":["But let us also call the churches islands. And indeed it is said in another place too: \"Many islands shall be converted to me.\" Do you wish to know that the churches are called islands? It is said in the prophet in the person of the Lord: \"Speak,\" he says, \"to the inhabitants of this island.\" Let the many islands be glad. For just as islands are set in the midst of the sea, so also the churches are set as it were in the midst of the sea of this world, and thus they are battered by persecutions as if by diverse waves. But these islands are battered daily, yet they are not overthrown. They are indeed in the sea, but they have Christ for their foundation, who cannot be moved.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","The Lord Almighty will therefore come and will be manifest over them, he whom they do not now know, of whom they are ignorant; and he will destroy all their doctrines, that is, their gods, and the idols of the various nations, so that, after the images which they had composed for themselves out of their own mind have been overthrown, the nations may turn to the Lord, and each one in his own place may worship him whom he did not know before.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.12-15":["But you also, Ethiopians, will be slain by my sword; and he will stretch out his hand over the north and will destroy Asshur; and he will make the beautiful one a wilderness, and a pathless place, and as it were a desert. And flocks will lie down in the midst of her, all the beasts of the nations, and the pelican and the hedgehog will dwell on her thresholds; the voice of one singing in the window, the raven on the lintel, because I will wear away her strength. This is the glorious city, dwelling in confidence, which said in her heart: I am, and outside me there is no other any more; how has she been made a desert, a lair of beasts? Everyone who passes through her will hiss and will move his hand. LXX: And you, Ethiopians, will be wounded by my sword; and I will stretch out my hand over the north and I will destroy the Assyrian; and I will make Nineveh a wilderness, and waterless as a desert. And flocks will feed in the midst of her, and all the beasts of the earth; and chameleons and hedgehogs will lie down in her stalls, and beasts will cry out in their pits, and ravens in her gates, because her height is a cedar; this is the city given over to evils, which dwells in hope, which says in her heart: I am, and there is none after me any more, how has she been made a wilderness, a pasture of beasts? Everyone who passes through her will hiss and will move his hands. The Jews refer this whole section, and the two preceding ones against the Philistines and Moab and the sons of Ammon, to the coming of Christ, who they think will come at the consummation of the world, to build up Jerusalem and to free his people from the hand of the nations by which they are held; and they say that this is the meaning of what is said: And men will worship him from their own place, all the islands of the nations. And not only the nations above, they hold, but also the Ethiopians and the Assyrians and Nineveh, the city of the Assyrians, are at that time to be made a wilderness, and all the beasts of the earth will lie down in her, or, as is written in the Hebrew, all the beasts of the nations, by which they think all the peoples that are to overthrow Nineveh are signified. And because Nineveh means beautiful, in the present passage they refer the beautiful one to Babylon. And all that follows, The pelican and the hedgehog will dwell on her thresholds, and so on, they say suits Babylon rather, whose same desolation is foretold in Isaiah also. Others, on the contrary, affirm that it is clearly said of the Assyrian, of whom it had already gone before: And he will stretch out his hand over the north, and will destroy Asshur, and will make Nineveh a wilderness. For by the Assyrians Nineveh is meant rather than Babylon, which is a city of the Chaldeans. And as for his saying, the pelican and the hedgehog in its hinges, and the raven on the lintel, these are signs of desolation; and there are two kinds of pelicans: one of the water, the other of the wilderness. And by what he says, The voice of one singing in the window, either demons are meant, or the voices of various birds that are wont to dwell in deserted cities. Moreover, where both we and the LXX have translated alike, The raven on the lintel, in the Hebrew is set hareb, which, according to a difference of reading, is taken as either dryness, or sword, or raven. Hence Aquila translated sword, and others dryness. And after the overthrow, as if mocking her ruin, the prophetic word speaks: This is the glorious city, dwelling in confidence, which said in her heart: I am, and outside me there is no other any more; how has she been made a desert, a lair of beasts? Everyone who passes through her will hiss and will move his hand. But according to what we said above, either beasts will truly dwell in the deserted city, or at any rate under the figure of beasts the variety of many nations is indicated. If anyone should ask how, according to the history, this prophecy is referred to the times of Nebuchadnezzar, when the Ethiopians and the Assyrians are also named, to whom the Medes and Persians did nothing, let him read the histories, and he will see that the Assyrians too and the Ethiopians were subject to the Medes, and he will see the reign of Cambyses, and the power of Cyrus, and all that followed thereafter. Let these things have been said in the Jewish way.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.12":["But if we note that in all the Scriptures those are called Ethiopians who are sunk deep in vices, according to what we read in Jeremiah: If the Ethiopian will change his skin, then, when the Ethiopians are converted to better things, there will be hope that no one who is willing to repent will be a stranger to salvation. Hence also the soul, at first polluted and stained with the filth of sins, says: I am black. And afterwards, at the end of the Song of Songs, it is written of that same soul, now cleansed and washed through repentance: Who is this who comes up made white; Moses too, that is, the spiritual law of the Lord, took to wife the Ethiopian woman from the nations. And Miriam, that is, the synagogue of the Jews, and Aaron, that is, the carnal priesthood, and not according to the order of Melchizedek, murmur against the law, but in vain. For at once the synagogue is drenched with leprosy, and, cast out of the camp, when the time is completed, at the prayer of Moses himself, she is brought back into the camp. For Ethiopia's hand had already come before God. So now the divine word threatens those who, clinging to sins and smeared with the filth of their sins, are unwilling to be converted to better things and to wash out their dark color; and it threatens them with the sword of which I think it is written also in Genesis: He set the Cherubim, and a flaming sword, which turns about to guard the way of the tree of life, and in Isaiah: He will bring a great and holy sword upon the dragon, the twisting serpent, and will kill it on that day. And he says that they will be either wounded or slain by the sword, so that, fearing the punishments, they may imitate Nineveh, and repent, and not undergo what the Lord threatens. This indeed is what the prophecy of Jeremiah also declares: I will speak, he says, concerning a nation and concerning a kingdom, and I will remove and destroy them; and if they repent, I too will repent of all the evils that I spoke of doing to them. And that you may know that the Lord now threatens the Ethiopians with the sword for this reason, that he may convert them to better things, a little later in this very book he adds concerning the Ethiopians: Beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, from there my suppliants, the daughter of my dispersed ones, will bring a gift to me, or, as it stands in the LXX: From the ends of the rivers of Ethiopia I will receive my dispersed ones, and they will offer victims to me.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.13-15":["Moreover, what follows about Nineveh laid waste: And flocks will feed in her, and all the beasts of the earth, chameleons, and hedgehogs will lie down in her stalls, and beasts will rest in her hinges, and ravens in her windows, and so on, seems to me to have to be understood in accordance with what is written either in Jonah or in the prophet Nahum. And in Jonah indeed we interpreted Nineveh, that is, the beautiful one, which repented at the preaching of Jonah, that is, of the dove, as the Church gathered from the nations. In Nahum, however, we tempered the interpretation toward the world; and concerning the world, indeed, it is not difficult to interpret and to say that, when the Ethiopians have been wounded by the sword of the Lord, and he has stretched out his hand over the north, and has destroyed the Assyrian, the prince of the world, the world itself too perishes with its prince, and is reduced to the utmost desolation, and is pitiable to no one, but all hiss at its ruin and move their hands. But concerning the Church it seems at first sight to be blasphemous that she should become pathless and deserted, and that beasts should dwell in her, and that afterwards it should be said to her with mockery: This is the city given over to evils, which dwelt in hope, which said in her heart: I am, and there is none besides me any more, how has she been made a wilderness, a pasture of beasts? But whoever has considered that apostolic saying in which it is said: In the last times very evil times will set in, and men will be lovers of themselves, greedy, arrogant, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, wicked, without affection, without peace, accusers, intemperate, cruel, hating the good, traitors, rash, puffed up, lovers of pleasures more than of God; having the form of piety, but denying its power. And also this, which is written in the Gospel, that as iniquity is multiplied the love of many grows cold, so much so that at that time this is fulfilled: Yet when the Son of man comes, do you think he will find faith on the earth? He will not wonder at the final devastation of the Church: that, while the Antichrist reigns, she is to be reduced to a wilderness, and handed over to beasts, and is to suffer whatever the prophet now describes. For if God, because of unbelief, did not spare the natural branches, but broke them off, and turned rivers into a desert, and springs of waters into thirst, a fruitful land into saltness, because of the wickedness of its inhabitants, why should he not, conversely, overthrow those of whom he had said: He turned a desert into pools of waters, and a land without water into springs of waters, and made the hungry dwell there, and so on, and those whom he grafted from the wild olive into the root of the good olive, if, unmindful of his kindness, they withdraw from their Creator and worship the Assyrian, and bring them back to the same thirst in which they had been before? Although this can be understood generally of the coming of the Antichrist, or of the end of the world, yet it can be taken daily of those who pretend to be of the Church of God and deny it by their works, and are hearers of the law and not doers, who vainly boast that they are beautiful, when flocks dwell in them, namely a multitude of vices, and brute animals serving the body; and all the beasts of the earth, which devour their hearts, and chameleons, which do not have one color, but are changed from moment to moment by different sins, now by avarice, now by luxury, now by cruelty, now by lust, now by sadness, now by exultation. And hedgehogs in their stalls, an animal spiny and full of thorns, and wounding whatever it touches. But beasts too will lie down in the holes, that is, in their hearts, and ravens, unclean birds, in their gates, either in the mouth or in the ears, so that they may always either speak or hear evil things. After this it is added and said: the Church will suffer these things, or has suffered them, for this reason, that she raised herself up in pride and, like a cedar, lifted up her top, given over to evil works, and nonetheless promising herself future blessedness, and despising the rest in her heart, and not thinking that anyone exists besides herself, and saying: I am, and there is no other besides me any more; how then has she been made in the wilderness a pasture of beasts? For where previously the Father dwelt, and the Son and the Holy Spirit, and his angels presided over its ministries, there beasts will then dwell, about which the prophet too laments, saying: Do not hand over to the beasts the soul that confesses to you.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.13":["After this it is not the Lord who speaks, as above, but the prophetic spirit speaks about him: And he will stretch out his hand over the north, and will destroy Asshur. The north, of which Jeremiah wrote: From the north evils will blaze up upon all the inhabitants of the earth. And of which Solomon makes mention: The north wind is a harsh wind, but by name it is called the right. And God stretches out his hand, the hand that inflicts punishments, for this reason: that both the north may feel the penalties, and those who are in its land, to which, according to Zechariah, the black horses go, of which it is said: There were black horses, they were going into the land of the north. And fittingly, whoever has moved his feet and withdrawn from the East, of which the same prophet says: Behold a man, the East is his name, and has looked toward the west, goes at once toward the north, which is not the right, but by name is called the right. This indeed is understood bodily as well: whoever stands in the east and turns himself, looking toward the west, has the north on his right; those who have grown cold from its blast call it the right, though it is the right only in name, but in reality and in deed is rather on the left. And after the Lord has stretched out his hand over the north, he will also destroy Asshur, which means εὐθύνων, which many think means one who directs, but that is false; for εὐθύνων means one who reproves and convicts. And since the devil himself is the enemy and the avenger, and he himself suggests sins, and afterwards convicts sinners in their sins, for that reason he is called εὐθύνων. I reckon him to be the prince of the Assyrians, who dwells toward the north, and has Nineveh as his capital, and says in Isaiah: By strength I will act, and by the wisdom of understanding I will take away the boundaries of the nations, and I will devour their strength.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.2.15":["Everyone who passes through her will hiss and will move his hands. But if we understand this of the angels, we will interpret it thus: when the angels pass through her, and do not remain in her, as they had been accustomed to do before, they will be stunned and will marvel, and will not prop her up nor hold her up with their hand as she falls, but will lift up their hands and pass on through. Or else with a hiss they will raise their hands, and, as though lamenting in the manner of mourners, they will clap them together. If, however, we wish to take this of the devil and his angels, who also laid waste the vine that had been transplanted out of Egypt, we will say that through the soul from which Christ has withdrawn, and which before had been the temple of God and has ceased to be so, the serpent passes, and hisses in it, and vomits out the venoms of his malice, and not only does this, but also moves his works, which τροπικῶς (figuratively) are called hands. And lest you think, because we have named a serpent, that we forcibly understand the hands of a serpent as its work, take the testimony of Solomon: Death and life are in the hands of the tongue. We have said these things serving the allegorical interpretation as best we could. But if anyone finds things more probable and more reasonable than those set out by us, let the reader rather be led by his authority.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.1-2":["Nor let it disturb anyone, as I have often said already, that I interpret these things as spoken against the Church, since he knows that in the holy Scriptures Jerusalem always bears the type of the Church; whoever sins away from her is either led off into Babylon, or, if he chooses of his own accord to go down to Jericho, is wounded by robbers. For what is so illustrious as the Church, which has been founded in the whole world, so redeemed by the blood of Christ, and a dove because of the grace of the Holy Spirit, as the Church gathered from the nations? In her very many who say that they believe in Christ have neither heard his voice, nor received discipline, nor wished to be near him.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.1":["Woe, provoking and redeemed city, the dove. She did not hear the voice, and did not accept discipline; she did not trust in the Lord, she did not draw near to her Lord. Her princes in the midst of her, like roaring lions; her judges, wolves of the evening, they left nothing for the morning. Her prophets mad, faithless men. Her priests have polluted the holy, they have acted unjustly against the law. The just Lord in the midst of her will not do iniquity. In the morning, in the morning he will give his judgment in the light, and it will not be hidden; but the unjust one knew no confusion. I have destroyed the nations, and their corners have been scattered; I have made their ways deserted, because there is no one who passes; their cities have been made desolate, with no man remaining, nor any inhabitant. I said: Yet you will fear me, you will accept discipline, and her dwelling will not perish because of all the things in which I visited her. Nevertheless, rising at dawn, they corrupted all their thoughts. LXX: O illustrious and redeemed city, the dove, she did not hear the voice, she did not accept discipline; she did not trust in the Lord, and did not draw near to her Lord. Her princes in her like roaring lions, her judges like wolves of Arabia, they did not leave anything till the morning; her prophets bearing the spirit, men who are despisers. Her priests pollute the holy things, and act impiously against the law. But the just Lord is in the midst of her, and will not do iniquity. In the morning, in the morning he will give his judgment in the light, and he is not hidden, and he does not know iniquity in exaction, nor injustice for ever; I have pulled down the proud, their corners have been scattered, I have destroyed their ways, so that they may not pass through at all; their cities have failed, because no one remained, nor dwelt there. I said: Nevertheless you will fear me, and you will accept discipline, and you will not perish from his eyes in all the things in which I have taken vengeance upon her. Prepare yourself, rise up at dawn, all their clusters have perished. Many think that, because of the sequence of the discourse, this is said against Nineveh, of which it was said above: And he will destroy the Assyrian, and will make Nineveh a wilderness. But Scripture would never call Nineveh a dove, although some think that in Jeremiah the words from the face of the sword of the dove were said about Nebuchadnezzar. But it should be known that others, on the contrary, assert that in place of dove, Ἑλλάδα, that is, Greece, can be understood there, so that the sense is: From the face of the sword of Iona, that is, from the face of the sword of Greece. For Iona signifies both dove and Greece. Hence even to this day the Greeks are called Ionians, and the sea is called the Ionian Sea, and among the Hebrews their old name remains. So too the Roman princes, retaining an ancient name among the barbarian nations, are called Caesars. The whole discourse, then, is against Jerusalem: Woe, city once a dove, always sinning, and handed over to captivities, and redeemed again by the Lord. Woe, provoking city, which is said more expressively in Hebrew, mara, that is, παραπικραίνουσα, which we can render making God bitter, that is, by your fault turning the sweet and merciful Lord to bitterness, so that he who wishes to have mercy is compelled to punish.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But the illustrious city, redeemed by the blood of Christ, is clearly understood, in keeping with what was said above, as the Church, which is also called a dove because of the simplicity of the multitude of believers within her.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.2":["She did not hear the commandment of the Lord, and when rebuked she would not accept discipline; nor, when evils pressed upon her, did she ever trust in the Lord her God; nor did she walk behind him; nor, when he said: I am the Lord who draws near, and not from afar, was she herself willing to draw near to him.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","She did not hear the voice of the Lord, nor was she willing to accept discipline, nor did she trust in the Lord, because she would not draw near to the Lord her God so as to merit pardon of sins. For in vain does anyone say that he hears the voice of the Lord his God and trusts in the Lord, when he destroys faith by his works, and is joined to mammon more than to the Lord his God, and approaches him with a double heart, and believes that he can serve as a soldier under two masters, the world and God.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.3-4":["Her princes and judges and prophets and priests are also described, so that in the city we may understand the people, and in these titles of rank that I have mentioned, the leading men. Her princes, therefore, like lions, were always busy with plunder and with shedding the blood of their subjects; her judges were rapacious, leaving to others nothing they could seize. Her prophets, raving or astounded, which in Hebrew is said phoezim, and Aquila translated θαμβευταὶ (the astonished), spoke as if from the mouth of the Lord, and proclaimed everything against the Lord; the priests committed sacrilege in the place of the sanctuary, and while they acted against the law, they offered sacrifices according to the law.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.3":["Her princes are like roaring lions. We have no doubt about the roaring and the ranging of lions when we see her princes thunder so against the peoples subject to them, and crush the common people with a tyrant's voice and rabid abuse, that you would think it not a shepherd among his flock, but a lion gnashing its teeth among the little sheep. Her judges too are like wolves of Arabia, killing in the evening and leaving nothing for the morning; not looking toward the rising of the sun, but lingering always in darkness, and turning the small possessions of the Church, and the things brought as offerings to God, to their own gain, so that the poor have nothing to eat in the morning; they lay everything waste as if in the night, with no one seeing; and when, after the manner of wolves, they plunder everything, they do not leave even small portions of food to the needy.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","As for the saying: Her princes in her like roaring lions, I know that I will offend very many, because I interpret these things of bishops and elders, though the wicked elders who desired to violate Susanna do not condemn the other elders who have lived well. And the wicked princes whom the prophetic word describes are no reproach to good princes: For when a fool is scourged, the senseless man will be wiser; but if the senseless man becomes wiser, how much more he who is wise? But her judges and princes, taking gifts and selling justice, are they not rightly called wolves of Arabia, or evening wolves, as Symmachus translated? For they do not deserve to be called wolves of Benjamin, which seize prey in the morning and at evening give food, but evening wolves, which eat by night and leave nothing for the morning.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.4":["Her prophets also, that is, the teachers who think that they teach the peoples and discourse on the Scriptures, πνευματοφόροι, that is, bearing the spirit, or spiritual — and this is to be read εἰρονικῶς (ironically) —, are men who are despisers; for what befits the Church is not to teach but to do, not to destroy words by deeds. But you, when you teach another and do not do it yourself, are to be called not so much a teacher as a despiser, of whom it is also written in Habakkuk: Look, you despisers, and behold, and wonder at the wonders, and perish. The priests too, who serve the Eucharist and distribute the blood of the Lord to his peoples, act impiously against the law of Christ, thinking that it is the words of the one who prays that make the εὐχαριστίαν (the Eucharist), not his life, and that only the solemn prayer is necessary, and not the merits of the priests, of whom it is said: And a priest in whom there is any blemish shall not approach to offer offerings to the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","As for what follows: Her prophets πνευματοφόροι, that is, bearing the spirit, men who are despisers, let it disturb no one that, interpreting this of teachers, we call the same men both prophets and despisers, since the Apostle too commands: Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you were sealed in the day of redemption. And David says in Psalm 50 (51): Do not take your Holy Spirit from me. For unless the Holy Spirit, when grieved, were accustomed to flee his former lodging and to abandon his dwelling, Paul would never have commanded what I said above, nor would David after his adultery have dreaded lest he lose what he had received; of which it is also written to the Hebrews: How much worse punishments do you think he deserves who has trampled on the Son of God, and has held unclean the blood of the covenant, in which he was sanctified, and has done insult to the spirit of grace? Moreover, it is written in the third book of Kingdoms (1 Kings) that the man of God, no doubt a prophet, who had said to the altar in Samaria: Altar, altar, thus says the Lord: Behold, a son of David will be born, and so on, because he despised the words of the Lord and ate with the false prophet — for so Josephus, expounding this passage, interpreted it —, was killed by a lion. And lest it be thought chance and not the judgment of the Lord, both the false prophet who had deceived him foretold that this would happen, and the lion itself, punishing the despiser, spared the ass. It is no wonder, then, if teachers who had been full of the Holy Spirit can become despisers, since among the careless, who do not keep their heart with all watchfulness, this very thing is frequently the cause of pride against the Lord and of contempt: that they have the knowledge of God, and know his great goodness, which he has hidden for those who fear him, and despise the riches of his goodness, storing up for themselves wrath in the day of wrath and of revelation. Let the priests too — who give baptism and at the Eucharist pray for the coming of the Lord, make the oil of chrism, lay on hands, instruct catechumens, appoint Levites and other priests — not so much be indignant with us for expounding these things, and with the prophets for prophesying them, as pray to the Lord, and take earnest care that they may not deserve to be among the priests who violate the holy things of the Lord. For it is not rank and the names of rank, but the work of the rank, that is accustomed to save princes, and judges, and prophets, and priests: He who desires the episcopate, he says, desires a good work. See what he said: He desires a good work, not a rank. But if, despising the work, one looks only at the rank, swiftly the tower in Siloam falls, and the heights of the cedar are struck by lightning, and the upraised neck is broken, and the swan, with neck outstretched and straining aloft, is reckoned among the unclean birds.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.5-6":["And when he begins to demand from each one the money he had entrusted to them, he will not be unjust, nor will he make injustice prevail for ever, but he will pull down the proud princes, whom God resists, from their seats and from the height they held, and their corners will be scattered, that is, their perverse wills, turning aside from the straight road, corners in which the Pharisees too were always accustomed to pray, having despised the corner stone. But I think that it benefits the proud too to be pulled down from their arrogance, and to have their alleys and corners scattered, so that afterwards they may walk on the straight road. Then follows: And I will make their ways deserted, because there is no one who passes through, according to what is written in Psalm 1: And the way of the impious will perish. And also in Hosea, where it is said of Jerusalem committing fornication: Behold, I will close her ways with thorns, and I will block up her paths, and she will not find her track, and she will pursue her lovers, and will not overtake them, and she will seek them, and will not find them; and she will say: I will go and return to my former husband, for it was better with me then than now. Notice that unless the ways had been closed and the paths fenced off, and unless the Lord had destroyed her ways, the fornicating soul could never have said: I will go and return to my former husband. So the paths of the proud and their corners are scattered, that they may not walk in pride and in perversity, and their cities, which had been wickedly built in arrogance and pride, are destroyed, so that they may not stand and have most wicked inhabitants.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.5":["Therefore, because they acted unjustly, the just Lord will not do iniquity, but will render to the most wicked city what it deserves. In the morning, in the morning, that is, openly and without any ambiguity, he will pass judgment on her, and there will be nothing that can be hidden from him. And the Lord will do this so that the city, rebuked, may be converted to the better. But unjust Israel did not recognize its confusion, nor did it understand that the blows were inflicted on it for this purpose, that it might repent.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Though the princes, judges, prophets, priests of Jerusalem do these things, the Lord is nonetheless merciful and just. Merciful, in that he does not withdraw from his Church; just, in that he renders to each one what he deserves. For after the morning has come, and the night of this age has passed, he will give his judgment in the light, and neither he himself nor his judgment will be hidden.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.6-7":["I have avenged you, he says afterwards, on the nations, and I have destroyed their empires, so that you who had not perceived me through blows might at least know me through benefits. Or else it is to be understood thus: I have destroyed all your cities, O Judah, and all the villages and tribes subject to you, and the various borderlands, and so great was the desolation of men that there was no one to dwell in your cities; and after I did this, I sent my prophets, rising at dawn and calling to repentance, and I said: I have indeed done these things to you, O Jerusalem, but I did them that you might fear me, and accept discipline, and that your dwelling, that is, the temple, might not perish because of all the crimes you had committed. But on the contrary, the inhabitants of Jerusalem, while I was calling them to repentance, rose at dawn as if on purpose and out of contention, so that with the haste with which they ought to have returned to me they might fulfil all their thoughts, and show in deed what they had conceived in mind. This according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.7":["And lest anyone think that we do violence to Scripture, let him learn from what follows. But I did these things, he says, that I might say to them: Behold, the paths of malice have been destroyed; from now on fear me, and learn discipline, lest my discipline also perish, finding in you no fruit of conversion, and all the things by which I wished to correct you be in vain, and that word which is written in Jeremiah may be applied to you: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not accepted discipline. Or else: Fear me, and receive discipline, so that all things may not perish from the sight of Jerusalem, nor she be brought utterly to desolation over these evils with which I threatened her.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Moreover, what we have expounded according to the Hebrew, Nevertheless, rising at dawn, they corrupted all their thoughts, in place of which it is written in the Seventy: Prepare yourself, rise up at dawn, all their foliage has been scattered, since it differs much from the Hebrew, and according to the LXX seems rather to agree with what follows, we will expound in what follows.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But we, who follow not the letter that kills but the spirit that gives life, nor Jewish fables, hear from the Lord: Prepare yourself, rise at dawn: all their clusters have been scattered, and, prepared, we say: My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready. And we hear the command in Proverbs: Prepare your works for your going out. And that which is said in a sacred manner in Leviticus, where in the seventh month, on the tenth day of the month, Aaron offers for the people the scapegoat, and that a living one, and, laying his hands upon its head, calls down upon it all the sins of the people of Israel, and hands it over into the hands of a prepared man, and sends it into the desert, this we understand of ourselves, and, preparing ourselves at the command of the true priest, we remove evil from the midst of the Church. And when we have done these things, the night passes, the day draws near, and, walking honestly as in the day, we say: O God, my God, to you I keep watch from the dawn. And at once we add: In the morning you will hear my prayer, in the morning I will stand before you, and I will see. For unless we have been prepared, the sun of justice will not rise for us. But when the sun has risen, all the clusters from the vine of Sodom are scattered and perish, so that, as the lamp of Christ shines, not only the great bunches but even what seemed to be small in us perishes.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.8-9":["Therefore wait for me, says the Lord, on the day of my resurrection in the future; for my judgment is to gather the nations and to collect the kingdoms, to pour out upon them my indignation, all the wrath of my fury; for in the fire of my zeal all the earth will be devoured, because then I will restore to the peoples a chosen lip, that all may call upon the name of the Lord and serve him with one shoulder. LXX: Therefore wait for me, says the Lord, on the day of my resurrection for a testimony, for my judgment is upon the gatherings of the nations, that I may receive kings, that I may pour out upon them all my wrath, the wrath of my fury; for in the fire of my zeal all the earth will be consumed, for then I will turn among the peoples a tongue into its generations, that all may call upon the name of the Lord, that they may serve him under one yoke. The Jews interpret these things of the coming of Christ, whom they hope is yet to come, and they say that, when all the nations have been gathered and the fury of the Lord has been poured out upon them, the earth will be devoured in the fire of his zeal. And just as before the building of the tower there was a time when all peoples spoke one tongue, so, when all have been converted to the worship of the true God, they will speak Hebrew and the whole world will serve the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","It should be noted, however, that in the place where we translated: I will restore to the peoples a chosen lip, instead of chosen the Seventy said: into its generation, so that of the earth is to be understood. And the error arose from this, that the Hebrew word barura, which Aquila and Theodotion translated as chosen and Symmachus as pure, the Seventy read as badura, thinking the letter res (resh) to be daleth, because of the excessive similarity of the character, which is distinguished by a small stroke. And likewise, where we translated: On the day of my resurrection in the future, all the others too translated: for a testimony. The Hebrew who instructed me in the Scriptures maintained that laed in the present passage ought to be understood rather as εἰς ἔτι, that is, for the future, than as for a testimony. For Ed, which is written with the letters ain and daleth, is understood as ἔτι and μαρτύριον, that is, both the future and a testimony. We can also expound this passage of the first coming of Christ, when, all error having been removed, the demons trampled and earthly works destroyed, the apostles spoke in all tongues, and, the old error having been removed, the one lip of confession was restored. But the kings, too, who are destroyed and consumed by the divine burning, are to be understood as the leaders of perverse doctrines.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.8":["And, promising us a reward for all these things, God says: Wait for me on the day of my resurrection for a testimony; for after vices and sins God will rise again in us. And according to what he commands in another place: Be my witnesses, and I am a witness, says the Lord God, and the servant whom I have chosen, the Father with the Son and the Holy Spirit is a witness for us, so that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may stand. And it seems to me that the sentence stands rather in this way, and that the truth is confirmed under these three witnesses, rather than through the letter. For there were two witnesses against Susanna, and against the Lord Savior himself, and yet the word did not stand in their mouth. Against Naboth, too, almost the whole city bore witness, and the agreement of most wicked witnesses had not the firmness of truth but the conspiracy of a crime. For my judgment, he says, is upon the gatherings of the nations, that I may receive kings, namely in the place of their punishments, that I may pour out upon them my wrath, all the wrath of my fury. He who is lesser quickly earns pardon and is very near to mercy: But the mighty will suffer torments mightily. Hence the nations and the multitude of the nations are gathered to judgment; but the kings, that is, the leaders of perverse doctrines, will be brought to punishment, so that all the wrath of the Lord's fury may be poured out upon them. And this is done not out of any cruelty, as the bloodthirsty Jews suppose, but out of mercy and the counsel of one who heals. For there follows: For in the fire of my zeal all the earth will be consumed. For when the nations have been gathered to judgment, and the kings to punishment, so that wrath may be poured out upon them, and not in part but whole, and with wrath and fury joined together, whatever is earthly in the whole world may be consumed, whatever pertains to the works of the earth, that is, of the flesh; laying waste all its brambles and thorny thickets, the blaze of my zeal will devour it.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.9":["And then I will turn upon the peoples a tongue into its generation, so that each one, having laid aside error, may return to the ancient speech of confessing the Lord, and at the name of Jesus every knee shall bend, of those in heaven, on earth and under the earth, and every tongue confess the Lord Jesus in the glory of God the Father. And casting away the bricks and the bitumen which we had in place of stones and clay, with which we were building up the pride of our error against the Lord, let us recover the tongue which we lost before, and let us be under the yoke of Christ, who says: My yoke is sweet, and my burden is light.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.10":["Beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, from there my suppliants, the sons of my dispersed ones, will bring a gift to me. On that day you will not be confounded over all your devices by which you have transgressed against me; for then I will take away from your midst the boastful ones of your pride, and you will no more be exalted on my holy mountain. And I will leave in your midst a poor and needy people, and they will hope in the name of the Lord. The remnant of Israel will not do iniquity, nor speak a lie; and a deceitful tongue will not be found in their mouth, for they will feed and lie down, and there will be none to terrify. LXX: From the ends of the rivers of Ethiopia I will receive my dispersed ones; they will bring sacrifices to me. On that day you will not be confounded by all your devices by which you have acted impiously against me, for then I will take away from you the disparagement of your insolence, and you will no more be magnified upon my holy mountain; and I will leave in you a meek and humble people, and those who are left of Israel will revere the name of the Lord, and they will not do iniquity; and they will not speak vain things, and a deceitful tongue will not be found in their mouth, for they will feed and lie down, and there will be none to terrify them. When the Lord has restored to the peoples of believers a chosen lip, and all have called upon the name of the Lord and have carried his yoke, then also from beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, whence the queen of Sheba came to hear the wisdom of Solomon, they will bring sacrifices to the Lord: And Ethiopia will hasten her hand to God; and the Ethiopian woman will be married to the true lawgiver, who struck Egypt with ten plagues, while the synagogue of the Hebrews envies. But what he says according to the Hebrew: From there my suppliants, the daughter of my dispersed ones, will bring a gift to me, is of this kind: O Israel, O synagogue, once a daughter, whom I dispersed through the whole world, though you envy, though you are tormented by jealousy, nevertheless sacrifices will be brought to me from Ethiopia, that is, from the people of the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But we, taking from this fable the occasion of a true history, will say that, once a chosen lip, or a pure and white one, as Symmachus translated, has been restored to us, we leave behind in the rivers of Ethiopia all the blackness of the soul, and the foul colour, and the venom of the dragon, with which we had been dyed by vices and sins, and the masters of perverse doctrines by whom we were formerly watered, and that we, with the once-dispersed Israel, will bring gifts to Christ.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.11-12":["On that day, that is, when the multitude of the nations has believed, you too will not be utterly confounded over all your errors by which you transgressed against me, by choosing Barabbas and crucifying the Son of God. Then I will take away from your midst the scribes and priests and Pharisees, the boastful ones of your pride, and you will by no means boast any longer on my holy mountain, but you will have a poor people, unlettered men and fishermen, who will hope in the name of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","On that day, on which the light of Christ has risen for us, it will be said to each one of us: You will not be confounded by all your devices, namely the most wicked thoughts by which we acted impiously against the Lord; and all the pride and insolence will be taken away through which we were raised up against the Lord and against the mountain, his holy one, our Lord and Savior; and in place of proud and vain names a meek and humble people will be left in us, so that we may think nothing arrogant, nothing swollen, nothing that displeases God. And at the same time consider that on the day of judgment and in the consummation of the world all the names of dignities will be taken away, and one people will remain, and a flock under the good shepherd, which is meek and humble.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.13":["The remnant of Israel, not the multitude that cried out: Crucify, crucify him, not the high priests and the nobles, but the remnant, will by no means do iniquity, nor speak a lie in Christ, since they believe the truth; nor will a deceitful tongue be found in their mouth, since they know that every lie is from the devil; for they will feed and will say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me. Beside the water of refreshment he has brought me up; he has turned my soul; and there will be none to terrify, as the faith of believers overcomes the pride of persecutors. Let this be understood of the first coming of Christ, which the Jews promise themselves at the end; and they hope that they will dwell in Jerusalem, and, after the manner of cattle, will be filled with the bodily gifts and riches of Judah and fed on green grass, and that, when all the nations have been destroyed and made subject to them, no one can remain who might terrify them.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Then also the people of Israel, when the fullness of the nations has come in, for God has shut up all under sin, that he may have mercy on all, will fear the name of the Lord. And the remnant of Israel will no longer do the iniquity through which they denied the Lord, nor speak vain things, promising themselves foolish fables; nor will a tongue of lying be found in their mouth, since Christ, who is the truth, speaks through them. For then they too will feed in one flock, and will lie down in the Church, and will not fear the onslaughts of the true Nebuchadnezzar. Seeing and reading these things, such great mysteries, let us cry out with the Apostle and say: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God, how inscrutable are your judgments, and your ways unsearchable. The prophet, too, perceiving this and turning it over within himself, conjectures concerning the judgments of God: In the night I was exercised with my heart, and I swept my spirit, and I was saying: Will God cast off for ever, or will he not add to have mercy any more, or will he hold back his mercies in his wrath? And I said: Now I have begun; this is the change of the right hand of the Most High. And the sense is: This, that I thought the Lord was abandoning sinners for ever and, with wrath taking its place, restraining his mercies, I understood to have been done for this reason, that by the change of his right hand, which is the right hand of the Most High, he might change all things and have mercy on those whom he had previously cast away. Therefore let us also, both we and the remnant of Israel, knowing that we are to render an account for every idle word, and that the Lord will destroy all lying lips, not speak vanity. For vanity of vanities, and all is vanity, and: Every living man is utter vanity. Nor let us bring forth a lie with our mouth, but, having received the power to tread upon serpents and scorpions and upon all the power of the enemy, let us fear no terror, nor, with Christ as our guardian, dread the snares of wolves, but let us say: The Lord is my illumination and my savior; whom shall I fear? and the rest that is contained in Psalm 26 (27).\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.14-15":["Finally, let us consider the order of the reading, and we shall see that what is said pertains not to the Jews but to the Church of Christ. For after what had gone before: My judgment is upon the gatherings of the nations, that I may receive kings, up to the place where he says: That all may call upon the name of the Lord, and serve him under one yoke, and: Beyond the rivers of Ethiopia I will receive among my dispersed ones those who will bring me sacrifices; and after the remnant of the people of Israel, believing in Christ and saved, of whom it is said: And those who are left of Israel will fear the name of the Lord, and there will be none to terrify them. The Holy Spirit, preaching of the general consummation of the world, speaks: Rejoice, daughter of Zion, proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem, rejoice and delight with all your heart, daughter of Jerusalem. For every soul of the Church, which is set upon the watchtower and contemplates peace, is glad and rejoices that its iniquities have been taken away from it, and redeemed by him who redeemed all with his precious blood. For Christ has been made for us wisdom from God, and justice, and sanctification, and redemption, and the King of Israel has redeemed us, who dwells in our midst, saying: I and my Father will come, and we will make our abode with him. And: I will dwell and walk among them; and we will no longer see evils, thinking and doing virtues alone.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.14-18":["Praise, daughter of Zion, shout for joy, Israel; be glad and exult with all your heart, daughter of Jerusalem. The Lord has taken away your judgment, he has turned away your enemies; the King of Israel, the Lord, is in your midst; you will not fear evil any more. On that day it will be said: Jerusalem, do not fear, Zion; let your hands not be weakened. The Lord your God in your midst, the mighty one, he will save; he will rejoice over you with gladness, he will be silent in his love for you; he will exult over you with praise. Triflers (nugas) who withdrew from the law I will gather, because they were of you, so that you may no longer have reproach over them. LXX: Rejoice, daughter of Zion; proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem; exult and delight with all your heart, daughter of Jerusalem. The Lord has taken away your iniquities, he has redeemed you from the hands of your enemies; the King of Israel, the Lord, is in your midst; you will not see evils any more. At that time the Lord says to Jerusalem: Take courage, Zion, let your hands not be weakened. The Lord your God in you, the mighty one, will save you; he will bring gladness upon you, and will renew you in his love, and will rejoice in you with delight, as on a solemn day; I will gather your broken ones. Woe to him who has taken up reproach against her. Let it not seem strange, as we have often said, that the Hebrew chapters end in one way and those of the LXX, that is, the Greek and the Latin, in another. For where the translation differs in sense, there the beginnings or the ends must also differ. The Jews promise themselves all these things together with the Christ whom they think is yet to come, things which we, who have received Christ, have already obtained together with him. If, then, any of the Christians, and especially of the new wise men, whose names I pass over in silence lest I seem to wound anyone, thinks that the prophecy has not yet been fulfilled, let him know that he bears the name of Christ falsely, and has a Jewish soul, lacking only the circumcision of the body. For if these things have not yet happened but are still to come, we have believed in the coming of the Savior in vain; but, since our believing is not in vain, we understand that the mystery has been fulfilled in us which was kept silent through eternal ages and has now been made manifest through the prophetic scriptures and the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","These things, meanwhile, if we wish to take them of the second coming of the Savior. Moreover, since the prophet Zechariah exhorts Zion and Jerusalem to a similar gladness, and Matthew says that this very prophecy was fulfilled in the first coming of Christ, we are compelled by necessity, or rather led by the very order of truth, to hope for what is said in Zephaniah not as future but as done. For it is written in Zechariah: Rejoice greatly, daughter of Zion; proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem. Behold, your king comes to you, just and a savior, himself humble and mounted upon a beast of burden and a new colt. Let these things have been said according to the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.14":["But according to the Hebrew, the Church is commanded to praise, and Israel, seeing God with the mind, to shout for joy, and the place of peace to exult and be glad with all its heart, to which it was said: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.15":["For at the end and in the consummation of the world he has taken away her judgment, by no means judging and rebuking her, but saving her, and he has turned away her enemies, the hosts of demons. The King of Israel, the Lord, will be in her midst; she will not fear evil any more.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.16":["On that day, says the Lord, as we see peace and are set on high, let your hands not be weakened, he who also said through Isaiah: Be strengthened, weakened hands, and let your works be strong.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","On that day it will be said to Jerusalem the free, who is not in slavery with her children, but who is the mother of the saints: Do not fear, Zion, for she herself is also Jerusalem; your works will by no means be weakened, nor will you do things which you will mourn having done.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.17-18":["For the mighty Lord, whom no one can resist, your savior, will himself restore to you the gladness which you lost, and, the old man cast away, will make you walk in the new, and he will do these things in his love, not by your merit but by his mercy. And he will rejoice in you and delight, receiving your salvation as the richest victim of your solemnity; and he himself will say to you: I will gather your crushed ones. A heart, indeed, crushed and humbled God will not despise, and: A crushed reed he will not break.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.17":["The Lord your God, who will save you, mighty and powerful, will himself dwell in your midst; he will rejoice over you with exultation and gladness, and will keep silent about your sins in the love with which he loved you; and he will exult over you with praise, either because you are praiseworthy or because you sing with your praises.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.18":["Triflers (nugas), or, as Aquila translated, those carried away, who withdrew from you, I will gather, because they were of you; that is: Those who through vices and sins had fled from your bosom and had come under the power of demons will come to you when the state of all things has been restored, and you will by no means suffer reproaches any longer over your lost sons. What we have rendered nugas, let us know that in the Hebrew it is the Latin word itself, and for that reason it was set down by us just as it stood in the Hebrew, so that we may know that the Hebrew tongue is the mother of all tongues, a matter which it is not the time to discuss now. But I wonder that Aquila and the Seventy, where we translated they were, namely in the place where we said: I will gather, because they were of you: instead of they were chose to translate woe, or ὄι, which Aquila always uses not for lamenting but for calling and crying out. For Hai, of the verb whose beginning is sum, signifies the past tense in the plural number: they were, or they had been. I know that these things are tiresome to the reader; but if he notices that I am writing not controversies and declamations, nor exulting in commonplaces, but commentaries, and commentaries on the prophets, he will rather blame me if anywhere I have chosen to play in the manner of the rhetoricians than censure me for lingering, as is fitting, amid such great obscurities.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","But since our hope is certain and the vows of the Jews are empty, let us now weave the present and last chapter according to the former understanding, and bring forward a testimony from Jesus son of Sirach: He who throws a stone on high throws it upon his own head. For since Zion and Jerusalem are set on high, whoever disparages Zion and Jerusalem and throws at her the stones of insults hurls them upon his own head, and his reproach will return upon his own head: And his pain and his iniquity will come down upon his crown. How many today reproach the soul that pursues the mysteries of God and wishes to see his peace, and say: He is mad, and drunk, and full of new wine; he flees the gatherings of men; he spurns delights, counts gold as mud, loves poverty alone. But those who are unbelievers reproach him even with the cross of Christ, and if ever they see him in distress and in temptation, they say: Where are your mercies, and the works of justice which you have performed? Why should I speak of unbelievers, when some leaders of the churches reproach men of this kind, and judge their life to be madness, and do not praise their present way of life but reproach them with their old sins? Nor do they hear that it is commanded: Do not upbraid a man who turns from his sins.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.19-20":["Behold, I will kill all who have afflicted you at that time, and I will save the lame one, and her who had been cast out I will gather; and I will set them for praise and for a name in all the land of their confusion. At that time when I will bring you in, and at the time when I will gather you; for I will give you for a name and for praise to all the peoples of the earth, when I have turned back your captivity before your eyes, says the Lord. LXX: Behold, I will act in you, for your sake, at that time, and I will save the pressed one, and the rejected one I will receive, and I will set them in glory; and named in all the earth. And they will be confounded at that time when I have done well to you; and at the time when I have received you, for I will give you named and in glory among all the peoples of the earth, when I have turned back your captivity before you, says the Lord. And this the synagogue, which does not limp but is maimed in both feet, promises itself at the coming of its Christ, whom it hopes is yet to come; and it thinks that all the nations which afflicted Israel are to be killed by the Lord, and the synagogue saved, and she who had received a bill of divorce gathered, and that they are to be set for praise and for a name in all the land of their captivity, in which they had formerly been confounded; and that this happens at the time when the captivity has been brought back to Jerusalem, and the temple built, and the remaining order of ceremonies observed. These things it promises itself, and therefore it does not repent, and while it hopes for uncertain things, it loses certain salvation. Nor do I wonder that the synagogue says these things, which, because it does not receive Christ, suffers in its eyes, and is bleary-eyed with Leah, and is not loved by Jacob, and, with Rachel taking her place, is neglected. I wonder at Christians, or rather half-Jews, who seem to themselves to be of the Church, speaking thus and professing those things which, if they are true, we believe in Christ in vain, and our whole mystery is taken away, and we are more wretched than all men, believing that he has come who has not come.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.19":["Woe therefore to him who has taken up and undertaken reproach, and has bound himself over to such a task as to disparage Zion and the city of God, for the Lord is the avenger of this injury to his city, and he will say to Zion: Behold, I will act in you for your sake, that is, I will carry out your vengeance, and I will save the afflicted one, or, as it is said in the Greek, the pressed one, so that the sense is: I will save her who at present, like the grape and the olive, has been so pressed and afflicted by temptations and pressures, as by the winepress and the beam, that she might make wine and oil, and that Jesus might drink of the wine in the kingdom of his Father, and be anointed with the oil above his fellows. I think that it was for the pressing out of such wine and oil that Job suffered many things, and that, after he had made the wine and the oil, he heard from the Lord: Do you think that I answered you otherwise than that you might appear just? As if he were speaking to the grape and the olive: Do you suppose that I pressed and afflicted and crushed you for any other reason than to press wine and oil out of you? There follows: And her who had been rejected I will receive. God seems to reject us when he leaves us to temptation. Hence Job too: The visitation, he says, of the Lord has looked upon me. And not only do the just speak and say: But now you have rejected and confounded us, as is written in Psalm 43 (44), but the Lord and Savior himself, in the person of the man whom he had assumed: But you, he says, have rejected and despised, you have put off your Christ; you have overturned the covenant of your servant. That it is the person of the just in Psalm 43 (44) who say: But now you have rejected and confounded us, is proved from what follows: All these things have come upon us, yet we have not forgotten you, nor have we acted wickedly in your covenant; nor has our heart turned back. Her, therefore, who seemed cast away in temptations the Lord will receive at the end, and will set them in glory and named in all the earth, namely the sons of the pressed and rejected one, that is, of the Church. But what other sons of the Church can we understand as named, except the apostles? Look, I pray you, at Peter and Paul and Matthew and John, and consider that what was promised to Abraham: I will magnify your name, has been accomplished in deed in them. Daily it is named in the Church, daily their name is magnified, not because it profits them to be named by us in the Church, but so that we, magnifying their name and reading over what they wrote, may obtain salvation.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ZEP.3.20":["At that time, he says, when the pressed and rejected one has been received, and her sons set in glory — for the Lord glories in his athletes when he sees them crowned, as he gloried over Job against the devil; hence the Apostle too, exulting in the progress of his disciples, says: Yes, by your glory — those who had been your adversaries and had brought reproach against you will be confounded; and then they will see that those whom they had thought wretched are happy, and that those whom they reckoned poor and cast down have become powerful and glorious; then they will see their captivity, through which in this age they had been subjugated under a harsh rule, brought back into the heavenly Jerusalem, and themselves rising again to reproach and to everlasting confusion. Grant to us, Jesus Christ, who are pressed and afflicted and rejected in this age, that you may receive us and set us in glory; let the water-snake (excetra) be confounded in time, let the hissings cease, let the venoms grow numb, and let its confusion profit for salvation. These things according to the Seventy. But since the Hebrew, except for the beginning, about which we spoke at the end of the preceding chapter, does not differ much from their translation, I do not think it needs exposition.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"HAG.1.1":["That in the second year of Darius, king of the Persians, son of Hystaspes, the seventieth year of the desolation of the temple, which Jeremiah had prophesied, was completed, the prophet Zechariah is also a witness: for when he had set at the head of his vision the title, in the second year of the same king, in the eleventh month, Shebat, on the twenty-fourth day, he added, saying: O Lord of hosts, how long will you not have mercy on Jerusalem and the cities of Judah, with which you are angry? This is the seventieth year. And Ezra too, when only the altar had been built and the foundations of the temple laid, relates that the work was forbidden by the letters of King Artaxerxes: Then the work of the house of God in Jerusalem was interrupted, and it was not done until the second year of the reign of Darius, king of the Persians. And at once he added: But Haggai the prophet and Zechariah the son of Iddo prophesied, prophesying to the Jews who were in Judea and Jerusalem, in the name of the Lord of Israel: then Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel and Joshua the son of Jehozadak rose up and began to build the temple of God in Jerusalem, and with them the prophets of God, helping them. At that time Tarquinius Superbus, the seventh from Romulus, was reigning among the Romans, in the twenty-seventh year of his rule; eight years later he was driven out by Brutus; and thereafter consuls administered the commonwealth for four hundred and sixty-four years, down to Julius Caesar. We have said this, O Paula and Eustochium, so that from the very title you may at once recognize in what age Haggai the prophet sang. And it must be known meanwhile that, according to the letter, both Haggai and Zechariah were prophets of great spirit, in that, against the edict of King Artaxerxes, and against the Samaritans and all the nations round about, who were hindering the building of the temple, they ordered the temple to be built; and that Zerubbabel too, and Joshua the son of Jehozadak, and the people who were with them, were of no less faith, in that they listened rather to the prophets who commanded than to the order of the king who forbade.\n— Commentary on Haggai","In the second year of King Darius, in the sixth month, on day one of the month, the word of the Lord came in the hand of Haggai the prophet. Because the people, who seemed to themselves to have returned from captivity, had not yet built the temple nor raised the walls of the city, nor had it the glory of the former Jerusalem, but dwelt in hollow houses, and, as it is said more expressively in Greek, κοιλοστάθμοις, that is, set low down and sunk in a valley; for that reason the word of God comes to them not under King Hezekiah, or Amon, or Josiah, who had ruled the people of God as long as Jerusalem stood, but under King Darius, prince of the Persians, of whom Daniel too speaks mystically in his volume. Now Darius sounds in our language 'generations made', or 'which have been', which in Greek is said γενεαὶ γενομέναι; and a people without a temple and dwelling low down did not deserve to have any other king than one who was a slave to generations, and loved the flesh, and kept the first sentence given to man while he was still untaught and cast out of paradise: Increase and multiply, and fill the earth. Therefore, because Darius was a lover of intercourse and a companion of the dragon, whose whole strength is in his loins, for that reason the vision is seen by the people in the second year, in an unclean number, which, after the unity of virginity and the nakedness of paradise, signifies the tunics of skin. Indeed in Genesis, although on the first day, and on the third, and on the fourth, and on the fifth, and on the sixth, after the completion of each work, it is said: And God saw that it was good, on the second it is not found according to the Hebrew, and Aquila, and Symmachus, and Theodotion. For the second day, which makes the number that divides from unity, could not be approved by the sentence of God as being good. The sixth month too is assigned, which does not have the solemnities of God, as the seventh month does, but is neighbor to the six days in which the world was made; in which there is toil, and bread is eaten in the sweat of the face; and the earth brings forth for us thistles and thorns, and when it has received a sowing of wheat, it is more fruitful in darnel and wild oats. But because the altar had already been built, and they had wished to build the temple against the enemies who resisted them, and had not built it, on day one of the sixth month the word of the Lord came in the hand of Haggai the prophet, so that the people, forsaking the second year of King Darius, which divides from unity, and the sixth month, which had passed, a number that is assigned to toil, might return to the unity of God, and follow the odd and single number, which even a pagan poet knew to be clean, saying: ... God delights in an odd number. And the word of the Lord, seeking someone to come to and to teach, comes about in the hand of Haggai the prophet, who had good works, and in whose deeds the word of God could rest. But where hands are full of blood, and Jesus is killed, and the people dare to say: His blood be upon us and upon our children, there the word of God does not come about. To this day carnal Israel, having unclean hands, stretches them out to the Lord; but because they are full of blood, the Lord speaks to them through the prophet: If you stretch out your hands, I will turn away my eyes from you; for your hands are full of blood. The word of the Lord does not come about in their hands, because they are unclean; the word of God does not come to them, because they killed the word of God in themselves. Nor is it to be thought that the word of the Lord came about only in the hand of Haggai the prophet; but because Haggai means festive, everyone who can celebrate feasts, not in the old leaven of malice and wickedness, but in the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, can receive the word of God. Let us too have clean hands and be called ἑορτάζοντες, that is, celebrating feasts, and the word of God will come to us. Let us set before our eyes, since the law is spiritual, spiritual solemnities, of which it is written: Three times in the year you shall celebrate feasts for me. You shall keep the solemnity of unleavened bread; for seven days you shall eat unleavened bread, as I commanded you, at the time of the month of new things; for in it you came out of Egypt. You shall not appear in my sight empty; and the solemnity of the harvest of the firstfruits of your works, whatever you have sown in the field; and the solemnity of the consummation at the end of the year, at the gathering of your works from the field. If we wish the word of the Lord to come to be in us, let us be Haggais, that is, celebrating feasts, and let us not appear in the sight of God empty; and sowing in the spirit, let us reap from the spirit eternal life, so that we may celebrate the solemnity of the consummation at the end of the year, that is, the gathering of our works from the field, and the solemnity of the firstfruits of our works, whatever we have sown in the field which the Lord has blessed. Let us also be Haggais, and through our whole life, until the consummation of the year, that is, until our departure from this world, let us celebrate feasts in our works, which have sprung up for us in our field. There are many things, and not for this occasion, which might be said about the solemnity, to which the diligent reader can fit a spiritual understanding from all of Scripture.\n\nTo Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and to Joshua son of Jehozadak, the great priest, saying. We read in Chronicles that Shealtiel was the son of Jeconiah, who was led away into Babylon, and that from him Zerubbabel was born. Matthew too, enumerating the generation of the Savior, says this: And after the transmigration of Babylon, Jeconiah begot Shealtiel; and Shealtiel begot Zerubbabel; and Zerubbabel begot Abiud. This Zerubbabel, of the tribe of Judah, that is, descending from the stock of David, is a type of the Savior, who truly built the destroyed temple, that is, the Church, and led the people back from captivity. And he built the Church both from the stones of the old temple and from new ones, which had previously been unhewn; that is, he raised a tabernacle to God the Father both from the remnants of the Jewish people and from the multitude of the nations. And according to the manifold accents of the Hebrew language, the name is interpreted either ῥεῦσις παρακειμένη, that is, a flux lying near, set out, or 'born in Babylon', or 'prince from Babylon'. Contrary to the first interpretation is that flux which is signified in the name Jezebel, that is, vain flux, or the flux of a menstruous woman, which certainly sounds plainly of uncleanness. And Zebulun too is expressed as flux of the night. Leaving, then, the vain and sordid and dark flux of this world, let us follow the flux of Jesus, which is set out for us to drink, and offers itself in a most abundant fountain, according to what is said in the Gospel: Jesus stood in the temple and cried out, saying: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. And we shall then be able to drink right here, when we have asked it of God the Father, according to what we read: Ask, and it shall be given to you. For Shealtiel means petition of God. And no one comes to Jesus unless the Father has drawn him. A flux, then, will be born to us, set out through supplication to God, and it will be of the tribe of Judah, the royal tribe, that is, and the one that confesses or praises the Lord, because Judah means both. Moreover, 'born in Babylon' is truly said of Zerubbabel according to the history, because he was born there. But according to a higher understanding too, it sounds of our Lord Jesus, because he too was born in the confusion of this world, and was near the most grievous river Chebar, and saw that great vision which Ezekiel, under his type, beholds at the beginning of his volume. But when I said that Zerubbabel is interpreted 'prince from Babylon', I did not say it because 'prince' is truly its meaning: for among the Hebrews his name is reported to be composed of three whole words: zo means 'this'; rob, 'master' or 'greater'; Babel properly sounds 'Babylonian'; and the name Zerubbabel is formed, this master from Babylon. But for brevity of understanding, in place of the minutiae given above, I chose to interpret it 'prince from Babylon', or 'in Babylon'. So that, just as Joshua son of Nun, who was himself also a type of the Savior, led the people from the wilderness into the land of promise, so this man too was born in Babylon for this purpose: to lead back those who were in Babylon into the land of promise, from which they had been carried off captive, and to say to those who were in chains: Come out, and to those who sat in darkness: Be enlightened. The word of the Lord, however, which came in the hand of Haggai, is directed not only to Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, of whom we have already spoken, but also to Joshua the son of Jehozadak, the great priest. As far as the history is concerned, Zerubbabel of the royal tribe is one person, and Joshua of the priestly tribe another. But as far as spiritual understanding is concerned, our Lord and Savior is one and the same, king and great priest; the type of him, according as he was king, Zerubbabel bore; but according as he was high priest, it was borne by his namesake Joshua (Jesus), which means Iao salvation, that is, salvation of the Lord; and he is the son of Jehozadak, which itself also is rendered in our language Iao just, that is, just one of the Lord. For God the Father is just and holy, and God the Son, and there is no iniquity in him. Against Marcion and the other heretics, who assert that the Lord Savior is the son of another, good God, I know not which, and not of the Creator, whom they call just. And truly Jesus is the great priest, compared with whom all the prelates of God are small and nothing. For if he is called the great priest, it is surely said to distinguish him from those who are lesser. And all who are outside him and after him are lesser, so that, just as he is the firstborn of all creation, and the firstborn from the dead, so he is the prince and the great one among all priests.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.2":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, saying: This people says: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built. Note carefully that it is not Zerubbabel or Joshua who say: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built, but the people, which, still placed under King Darius, had not yet shaken off the yoke of servitude. And always those who are held in captivity, and have gone out of Jerusalem, put off and delay building the temple of God, and say: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built. And you, when you see someone who has gone out of the Church, who had been delivered to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit might be saved, thinking and planning to build through chastity the temple which he had before destroyed through lust, and yet putting it off from day to day, say to him: Truly you too are of the people of the captives; you too say: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built. For him who has once resolved to restore the temple of God, every time is fit for building; neither the devil as king can hinder him, nor the enemies round about, nor the feigned affection of parents, kinsmen, children. As soon as you turn and call on the name of the Lord, he too will say: Here I am.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.3-4":["And the word of the Lord came in the hand of Haggai the prophet, saying: Is it a time for you to dwell in panelled houses — or, as the LXX translated: Κοιλοστάθμοις, that is, hollow —, and this house deserted? On the same day as above, this vision is seen again. And as the works of the prophet advance, the gifts of prophecy also grow; and after a little silence, to what the people had said: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built, the Lord's response, as if thought over, is brought forth, and it is said to them: Is it then time for you to dwell in houses set low down and placed in a valley, and shall my house, which is on the mountain, be deserted? Or, as it stands in the Hebrew: That you should dwell in panelled houses, that is, adorned and well arranged, and which serve not so much for use as for delight; and shall my dwelling, in which were the holy of holies, and the Cherubim, and the table of the showbread, be drenched by the rains, lie squalid in desolation, be scorched by the sun? Moreover, according to anagogy: every time in which a dwelling in the valley is chosen, or we serve pleasures, is unseasonable. Hence the Stoics too, who take care to define single words, said that time is the time of correction or of accomplishment, which is said more expressively in Greek χρόνον εἶναι κατορθώσεως. For every time in which we devote ourselves not to virtues but to vices perishes, and is reckoned as nothing, as if it had not been. If, then, any of us either dwells in a valley, or panels his house in worldly pleasure and luxury, he does not build a temple to God, nor does the Lord have in him where to lay his head; and while he builds a house with the foxes, he suffers what was once the dwelling of God to be deserted.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.5":["And now thus says the Lord of hosts: Set your hearts upon your ways. Let there not be one time for speaking and another for doing; turn my precepts at once into deeds. For it is the Lord Almighty who commands, and surely the command of God Almighty is no light thing. Until now you have had hearts that served vices, without order, without a teacher, going wherever your desires dragged them. But now the Lord commands you to set charity in order toward him, and to set your hearts upon your ways, so that you do nothing without judgment and consideration, but the lamp of the law always goes before your feet, and you say: Your law is a lamp to my feet and a light to my paths. Or certainly thus: Because you say, it is not the time for building the house of the Lord, and you yourselves dwell in dwellings sunk in the depths, while my house is deserted, consider, at my command, the Lord's, and call back to your memory what you have done and what you have suffered.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.6":["You have sown much, and brought in little; you have eaten, and have not been satisfied; you have drunk, and have not been inebriated; you have covered yourselves, and have not been warmed. And he who gathered wages put them into a bag with holes. All your labor, you who built your own houses and neglected the house of God, has had no result. For you sowed more, and gathered much less than had been sown; nor can you say that famine followed because the farmer ceased from working the land. You ate too, lest perhaps any of you should say that the fast had been voluntary; and you were not filled, because you had gathered small fruits into your barns. You drank wine from the vineyards, but not so much that your heart was gladdened and it could be said of you: And wine gladdens the heart of man. You had a cloak, but one that would neither keep off the cold nor keep in the warmth. Whoever of you, too, gathered wages, whether by trade or by hired hand, spent his labor in vain, without the price of the labor. For all the money flowed away, as if someone were putting it into a bag with holes. But according to spiritual understanding too, those who have returned from Babylon and have not yet built the temple of God, but, putting off its building from day to day, have said: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built, are neither captives nor yet endowed with full freedom, but are placed as it were in a kind of middle ground: they have sown much, and brought in little; they have eaten, and have not been satisfied; they have drunk, and have not been inebriated; they have covered themselves, and have not been warmed; they have gathered wages, and have lost them, as if they were putting them into a purse with holes. If you ever see someone, among many works of sin, doing some things that are just, God is not so unjust as to forget a few good deeds on account of many evils; but he will make him reap only those things which he has sown in good earth, and gather them into his barns. But whoever is wholly an ἀποστάτης (apostate) will not eat at all, but will perish of hunger. Moreover, he who sows much and brings in little eats little, and not to satiety, according to what the Lord threatens among the curses of Leviticus: And you shall eat and shall not be filled. But he who is holy will eat to satiety, and in him will be fulfilled what is written: The just man, eating, fills his soul. Likewise, whoever does not drink at all will perish of thirst; as also in Judith, if one is willing to accept the woman's book: And the little ones perished of thirst. But he who drinks little does indeed drink, but not to inebriation. Moreover, there is he who can say to the Lord: Your cup that inebriates, how glorious it is. And he is inebriated with Noah; and although placed in Egypt, yet at the banquet of Joseph, with the patriarchs and brothers, he is drenched with wine.\n\nThis man, for the greatness of his joy and his daily gladness, transported with the apostles into ecstasy, will be called full of new wine. But how the fact that the sons of Jonadab son of Rechab did not drink wine, and are praised by the Lord, is not contrary to this exposition will be discussed more fittingly in Jeremiah. After this it is said to those who neglected to build the temple of the Lord: You have covered yourselves, and have not been warmed. This we understand from Psalm 103 (104), in which it is said of God: The abyss, like a garment, is his clothing. For although according to the Hebrew truth it is referred to the lands, which are encircled by the Ocean, yet according to the LXX translators, who said Τὸ περιβόλαιον αὐτοῦ (his garment), in the masculine gender, and not αὐτῆς (her), in the feminine, we are compelled to understand what was said of God, because his wisdom is unsearchable, and the Lord makes darkness his hiding place, and his mysteries are not betrayed to any who are unworthy. Hence the just man too rejoices and says: In my heart I have hidden your words, that I may not sin against you. This cloak, woven of the senses and words of manifold wisdom, does not allow those who are fervent in spirit to grow cold, nor the warmth of love to freeze when the north wind blows. But he who is in the middle has a cloak indeed, but one with which he is not wholly covered; just as he carried little into the barns, and eats, but not to satiety, and drinks, but not to inebriation, so he will indeed be covered with the cloak of his senses and works, but will not be warmed. But he who, because of the excessive poverty of his soul, has no cloak, has none for this reason: because iniquity has multiplied, charity has grown cold in him. Hence also concerning a man of this kind, whose cloak is held by another, it is commanded in the law: You shall give his garment back to him before the sun sets, since he is poor and has his hope in it. And this too befell those who dwelt in valleys, or in panelled houses, and said: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built: that they gathered wages into a bag with holes. If any of us does good works, worthy also of a wage — which the Lord will render to us, of whom it is said: Behold the Lord, and his wage is in his hands, to render to each according to his works, and that other saying of the Apostle: If anyone's work which he has built upon remains, he will receive a wage —, he gathers wages to be kept and to endure, and, always joining virtues to virtues, heaps up money in a bag without holes. But he who after good works sins not once or twice but frequently, and darkens and soils his past charity with the vices that follow, gathers money into a bag with holes. And all these things befell those who said: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built, and who, while they themselves dwelt in valleys, suffered the house of the Lord to be deserted.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.7-8":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Set your hearts upon your ways; go up into the mountain, bring wood, and build the house; and it shall be acceptable to me, and I shall be glorified, says the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Set your hearts on your ways; go up upon the mountain and cut down wood. The rest likewise. Again I, the Lord, command you, I who had also ordered it before, that you set your hearts upon your ways, and consider all that you do, and, leaving off your zeal for your own lowly houses, go up into the mountain, where there is not wood to be burned, but wood that serves for the works of my house; and so that you may do this more attentively, know that what you are doing will please me. The Hebrews say that only wood for roofing was needed, since the walls of the temple were left standing after the fire. So they say. But we are commanded not to set our hearts outside our ways, but to set again in our ways the hearts we had set before; and after we have done this, to go up from the hollow houses up the mountain, so that when we have come to the height of the mountain, in which is the wood needed for building the temple of God, from the whole mountain of holy Scripture, in which the various trees of the virtues and of paradise are planted, we may cut them down, and build the temple of the Lord with good works and the doctrines of truth; and when it has been built, may it please the Lord, and may he be glorified in it. Therefore, since these things have been commanded us, that we set our hearts in our ways, let us go up the rational mountain, and, seeking for each problem the fitting wood from the testimonies of the Scriptures, let us cut it down and build the house of wisdom in ourselves; for after this has been built, the end of its building will be that the Lord be glorified in us.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.9":["You looked to more, and behold, it became less; and you brought it into the house, and I blew it away. LXX: You looked to many things, and they became few, and you brought them into the house, and I blew them away. So that, casting aside delay and laying down all hesitation, you may build my house more zealously, I tell you also another thing that has befallen you, who put off building my house: not as above, You have sown much, and because the earth did not return the seed, you have brought in little; but when the crops were already whitening, and the time of harvest was at hand, and you thought you held the grain in your hands, you reaped empty stalks and gathered hollow straw without the fruit of the ears. The threshing floors were full: hope in the eyes, grief in the hands. But even this very thing which had scarcely been picked out of the great crop and the countless heaps, you brought into the house, and it was scattered by my power. For I blew it away and reduced it to nothing, because the dead grain and the empty husks had not the flour and the fine flour which are useful for eating. Now what he said, You brought it into the house, and I blew it away, can also be taken of the gifts which they offered on the altar, and that God blew them away. But since he said, you brought it into the house, if we understand the things brought in as gifts, we are certainly saying that they were offered in the temple. And that will not fit our case, because at that time the house of God had not yet been built. This indeed happens to very many even to this day, who dwell in lowly buildings and, as far as in them lies, despise the desolate house of God, and though they could build it, disdain to; and as if already seeing ripe crops, they promise themselves the fruits of their works, and, when hope deceives them, instead of great things they scarcely find small ones. But even these small things, which they had stored in the house and granary of the mind, are blown away by the word of God, as unworthy of his keeping and protection. How often have I myself seen very much hoped for from certain men, both in doctrine and in way of life; and after it came to the point that they had their time of harvest, that is, the time of teaching and of offering their life as an example to the peoples, I have seen them cast down from on high, and less found in them than the opinion of all had promised itself. Whence it happened that little by little, as negligence crept in, they lost even the very little that they seemed to have. And they suffered this for the reason that they were secure in their former houses, and did not go up the mountain of the Scriptures, cutting from it the wood for the Lord's building, nor did they build up the house of the Lord in themselves daily, but, despising its desolation, they lost even what they believed they had. For this is the cause given for the aforesaid evil.\n\nFor which cause, says the Lord of hosts, because my house is deserted, and you hasten each one into his own house.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.10":["Because of this, over you the heavens were forbidden to give dew, and the earth held back its growth. Not only, he says, did the heavens not give rain, by which the watered soil produces crops, but not even the morning and the night dew, so that the parched fields might at least be tempered by a little moisture. The earth too devoured, and, not meaning to return the seed to the farmers, held back in its greedy bosom even what it is accustomed to produce of its own accord. I think that this is the dew of which it is said to Jacob in the blessing: May God give you of the dew of heaven; and the dew of Hermon which descends upon Mount Zion, and descends not from the air, in which flits a manifold number of eagles and hawks and vultures, but from heaven, so that if anyone's soul burns with passions and has been wounded by the dart of the devil, it may be cooled by this dew and temper its heats. When this is withheld, the earth also does not bring forth its growth. For no soul can bring forth grain without the dew of Christ.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.11":["And I called a drought upon the earth, and upon the mountains, and upon the wheat, and upon the wine and upon the oil; and whatever the ground brings forth, and upon men and upon beasts, and upon all the labor of hands. For drought the Seventy translated μάχαιραν, that is, sword; but in the Hebrew too I found it written with three letters, heth, res, beth, which, if we read harseb, means sword; if oreb, καύσωμα, which we have translated drought, although it could better be translated burning wind. And indeed, since the discourse is about the earth and about the barrenness of the fields, it seems to me that burning wind ought rather to be understood in the present place than sword, although every plague that is brought upon men for their sins can also be taken for a sword. Now a drought, or a sword, was called upon the earth and upon the mountains, so that they should not bring forth wheat, and wine and oil, and whatever the ground produces of its own accord. And with famine going before, death consequently comes upon men and upon beasts. And the same sword or burning wind consumes all that the hands of men have labored on. Therefore the word of God, living and effective and sharper than any two-edged sword, is called or brought in, so that the negligent soul, which is interpreted as parched earth, and which desires rather to dwell in hollow places than to build the house of God, may be struck by its point, and may lose whatever fruits it thinks it has. The sword is brought also upon the mountains that lift themselves up against the knowledge of God, and upon the grain and the wine and the oil, with which, as with food and drink and refreshment, the conventicles of the heretics flatter the deceived peoples. One might fittingly say that their bread is the bread of mourning, and that their wine is the fury of dragons and the incurable fury of asps. Their oil too is the promise of heavenly things, with which they as it were anoint their disciples and promise them rewards for their labors, which the prophet detests, saying: But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head. But also the other things which, without the authority and testimonies of the Scriptures, they find and invent of their own accord, as if by apostolic tradition, the sword of God strikes; and men and beasts we may take either as λογισμοὺς and αἰσθήσεις, that is, their thoughts and their senses, or at any rate as the rational and the irrational among them, that is, the learned and the unlearned alike; and all the labor of hands as their fasts, and their various observances, and chameunias, that is, sleeping on the ground. Those who fast three Lents a year, and humble their soul with ξηροφαγίαις (dry-food fasts), and above all spring from the root of Tatian, hear concerning labors of this kind: You have suffered so much without cause. Now all these things that I have said can be understood of the rulers of the Church, who, building a carnal house and providing for their children and their possessions, do not care either to build up the temple of God in themselves or to restore the Church of the Lord, which is unroofed and demolished; their life and incongruous speech often scandalize very many and cast them out of the Church, and bring the house of God to desolation. Nor do we say this in order to accuse all in general, but because in every office and rank there are some who build and others who destroy the temple of God, and because of their vice neither do the heavens give dew nor the earth its growth; the ground is dried up, the mountains are parched, the wheat and the oil, and all that the earth brings forth, the men themselves too and the beasts, and all the labor of hands perish under the cutting sword, and by drought, or burning wind.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.12":["And Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, and Joshua son of Jehozadak, the great priest, and all the remnant of the people heard the voice of their God, and the words of Haggai the prophet, as the Lord their God sent him to them, and the people feared before the face of the Lord. Attend carefully (because of the two understandings of the Savior in Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the priest, for he himself is both king and priest) to this: that he did not say, Zerubbabel and Joshua feared; but when Zerubbabel and Joshua and the people heard the words of Haggai the prophet, which are the words of the Lord, the people alone feared before the face of the Lord, that is, the multitude alone, which had not yet come into one and perfect man, and which, not joined to the spirit, did not merit to be what the spirit is. But the people feared before the face of the Lord, knowing that the face of the Lord is upon those who do evil, to destroy their memory from the earth.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.13":["And Haggai, the messenger of the Lord, from among the messengers of the Lord, spoke to the people, saying: I am with you, says the Lord. Some think that John the Baptist, and Malachi, which means angel of the Lord, and Haggai, whom we now have in hand, were angels, and that by the dispensation and command of God they took on human bodies and lived among men. Nor is it surprising that this should be believed of angels, since for our salvation even the Son of God took on a human body; and for this reason they also offer testimony from the apocrypha, where it is said that Jacob, who was afterwards called Israel, was an angel, and that for this reason he supplanted his brother in his mother's womb; also that John, at the voice of Mary, the mother of the Lord, leapt for joy in the womb of Elizabeth; and that all rational beings have one nature, and that for this reason men who have pleased God become equal to the angels. Let them think so. But as for us, let us take the messenger of the Lord, that is, the angel, which in Hebrew is called Malach, to be called simply a prophet from the fact that he announced the Lord's will to the people; or else, since in many places our Lord and Savior is called the angel of God, as in that place: Angel of great counsel; we say that a type of the Savior went before in Haggai. Moreover, what he says: The messenger of the Lord, from among the messengers of the Lord, is as if he had said: a prophet from among the prophets. What he says: The messenger of the Lord to the people, saying: I am with you, says the Lord, is not spoken to Zerubbabel and Joshua, with whom and in whom the Lord always was — for we have said once for all that, according to different understandings, they are taken in the person of the Savior —, but to the people, which had feared before the face of the Lord. And they, because they were the people, had not yet come to the love of God, which casts out fear. The people, therefore, receives as the reward for the fear of God that the Lord should be with them; and the sense is: I will be your helper; build my house, which has been destroyed in you; with me set in your midst, no one will be able to hinder your building.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.1.14-15":["And the Lord stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and the spirit of Joshua son of Jehozadak, the great priest, and the spirit of the rest of all the people; and they went in and did work in the house of the Lord of hosts, their God. On the twenty-fourth day of the month, in the sixth month, in the second year of King Darius. For governor of Judah the Seventy translated, of the tribe of Judah, and for work, works; the rest likewise. But wherever we put: the Lord of hosts, they variously render either the Lord Almighty, or the Lord Sabaoth, or the Lord of powers. So the spirit of Zerubbabel is stirred up, and the spirit of Joshua, for the present according to the letter, so that the kingdom and the priesthood may build the temple of God. The spirit of the people is stirred up too, which before was sleeping in them: not the body, not the soul, but, as we have said, the spirit, which knows better how to build the temple of God. And they went in, for they had been outside, and did works, such as were worthy of the inner chambers of the house of the Lord; in the same year of King Darius as above, in the month that was also set down at the beginning, but not on the same day. For there the first day of the month is set down, but here the twenty-fourth, so that between the first day, on which the Lord speaks through Haggai, and the twenty-fourth, on which they went in and did work in the house of the Lord, there were twenty-two days in between, as many as the letters among the Hebrews. For they needed to be taught what the elements of the beginning of the words of God were, which would keep them from saying: The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built, and from living in hollow houses and, as I said above, being sunk into the depths. But they were to be roused to set their hearts upon their ways, and to remember how much they had suffered through negligence. And they were to go up into the mountain, and cut wood, and build the house of the Lord, and not suffer the things they had suffered before. Finally, in these twenty-two days the people feared before the face of the Lord, and they went in on the twenty-fourth day of the month, in the sixth month, in the second year of King Darius, and did work in the house of the Lord of hosts, their God. But we are commanded, so that a spiritual house may be built into a holy priesthood, for offering spiritual sacrifices pleasing to God, to show ourselves such that the Holy Spirit may be stirred up in us, and that we may go into the house of the Lord and do the works of the Lord. For Zerubbabel of the line of David and Joshua, priest for ever, has already been raised up by the Father in power, according to the spirit of sanctification, from the resurrection of the dead, so that he may preside over the work; and let us do it, with him helping and directing. Yet we cannot do these works of God unless we first fear before the face of the Lord, and believe, and go into the temple of God, and accomplish the things that are worthy of the house of God. But since we are still in the world, and the time of our building falls under King Darius, in which generation, marriage and the flesh are served, for this reason, in the sixth number, in which the world was made, and in the second year, which divides unity — for the matter in which the restoration of the world and of offspring consists loves the double number —, we go into the house of the Lord, and with both peoples gathered together we build the temple of God. For from two twelves the sum of the number twenty-four is made up, because the Church of Christ, the first, which had been overthrown, was built from the circumcision as well as from the Gentiles. But we can also say this: since the number eight, which is holy and is taken as a type of the true circumcision, when tripled makes the number twenty-four, we are taught figuratively to build the house of the Lord in the cutting off of the flesh and the circumcision of vices, and to believe that true purity is in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. Moreover, another has reckoned the number as four six times, and refers the four to the elements of the world, of which we consist, and the six to the creation of the world, in which those very elements are found. And he says: since we are still in matter, and are surrounded by a heavy body, and serve generations, we do indeed build the temple, and enter the house of God, and our spirit is stirred up as if from a heavy sleep, but we still do this very thing on the twenty-fourth day.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.1-2":["So the word of God comes to those who had begun to work in the house of the Lord Almighty, now in rest, that is, in the seventh month and in the most complete mystery of the Trinity, on the twenty-first of the month, and to Haggai, who was celebrating the feasts of God and had again prepared his hand for the word of the Lord, and he says to him: Speak to Zerubbabel of the tribe of Judah, and to Joshua the great priest, who for our sake deigned to become both man and priest, and to the remnant of the people; for in comparison with the whole world, the part of those who believed was small in the beginning.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.1-9":["In the seventh month, on the twenty-first of the month, the word of the Lord came in the hand of Haggai the prophet, saying: Speak to Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and to Joshua son of Jehozadak, the great priest; and to the rest of the people, saying: Who is left among you that saw this house in its first glory? And how do you see it now? Is it not so, as if it were not, in your eyes? And now take courage, Zerubbabel, says the Lord, and take courage, Joshua, son of Jehozadak, great priest, and take courage, all people of the land, says the Lord of hosts, and do it. For I am with you, says the Lord of hosts. The word that I covenanted with you when you came out of the land of Egypt, and my spirit will be in your midst; do not fear. For thus says the Lord of hosts: Yet there is one little while, and I will shake heaven and earth, and the sea and the dry land, and I will move all nations. And the one desired by all nations will come, and I will fill this house with glory, says the Lord of hosts. Mine is the silver, and mine is the gold, says the Lord of hosts. Great will be the glory of this latter house, more than of the first, says the Lord of hosts. And in this place I will give peace, says the Lord of hosts. In the same year, but in the seventh month, in which the solemnities of God fall, on the twenty-first day of the month, that is, when three weeks had been completed and rest had been made perfect in the mystery of the Trinity, the word of the Lord came for the third time in the hand of Haggai the prophet, who sweated in continual labor, so that the word of God might always have access in him. For, forgetting what was past and stretching forward into the future, he labored every day as if he had nothing of his previous works. So it is said to him: Speak to Zerubbabel, and to Joshua, and to the rest of the people, who saw the ancient house of God and are now present at its restoration: in comparison with the former, is not this one which is seen such that in a certain way it seems not to exist? But do not despair, and do not let your weary hands drop; but you too, Zerubbabel, and you, Joshua, and all you people, take courage, and do the work in my house. For I am with you, and my word, which I covenanted with you when you came out of the land of Egypt. And my spirit will not depart from you; do not fear: it is I who command, the Lord Almighty, for whom to have said is to have done. When I was giving the first covenant and was seen on Mount Sinai, I shook heaven and earth, and the Red Sea and the desert, to set up a covenant with you; but now I promise you that I will yet once more shake heaven and earth, and the sea and the dry land, so that, when they are shaken, all the nations may be moved, and there may come, according to the LXX, the things that are chosen of the Lord from all the nations; but according to the Hebrew, the one desired by all nations will come, our Lord and Savior. Then I will fill this house with a glory greater than the former's, and I will always add to you, says the Lord Almighty. And lest perhaps you think the guarantor weak, mine is the gold, and mine is the silver, and mine are all riches. I will give gold and silver for the adornment of the temple, so that the glory of this house may become greater than that of the former. And because what I promise seems difficult, and human unbelief always hesitates before greater promises, I who promise therefore say again: I am the Lord Almighty. Moreover, since I know that nothing serves so well for the building of a renowned house, one that is to surpass the former house, as peace, I pledge this too. For I will give peace in this place, says the Lord of hosts, so that the peace which surpasses all understanding may guard my house, and my place may be in peace. For the present I have drawn these lines of exposition παραφραστικῶς (by way of paraphrase), so that from them the prudent reader may ascend to a more sublime understanding, even while we are silent.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.3":["Let us therefore hear what he said. There was once in Israel a house of God, which is now so deserted that it is not even believed to have existed, since she who had been beloved became not beloved, and they who were not the people of God began to be the people of God. And that house, once glorious, is now in the sight of Zerubbabel and Joshua and the remnant of the people as if it were not. And we ought to take this not only of the buildings of the temple, which we see have collapsed, but of all the things which the Jews once had as glorious.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.4":["Nevertheless, because the former house has become as if it did not subsist, Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the priest are roused, so that the kingdom and the priesthood of Christ may be strengthened, and his people, which was once the people of the land, may do works in the house of the Lord and know that God is present to it.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.5":["Let it also do the word which the Lord covenanted with them when they were coming out of the land of Egypt. And would that we too may come out of Egypt, so that we may fulfil the word of the covenant which we have received. The Lord God also promises his spirit to those who do works in his house and fulfil the word they have received, saying: And my spirit will be in your midst. Behold the mystery of the Trinity: I am with you, and my Spirit, and the Word, in which I set the covenant when you came out of Egypt. But what he says, in your midst, is to be understood in the sense of what we read written in the Gospel: For he stands in your midst, whom you do not know, who will come after me.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.6":["These things, then, says the Lord of hosts to you who see that the former house is as if it were not. I moved heaven, when my voice was heard from heaven. I moved the earth, when I delivered the covenant to the former people, and at my coming gloom, whirlwind and darkness were seen. I moved the Red Sea, when I provided a way for the people passing over. I moved the dry land, either Egypt, emptied of the worship of God through the plagues, or the wilderness through which I led the people about for forty years. These I will move yet once more. This we see fulfilled in the coming of the Lord the Savior. For at the time of his passion, as the sun fled, heaven was moved, and darkness came over all the earth from the sixth hour until the ninth hour. The earth was moved, and the rocks were split, and the tombs were opened; the sea was moved, when the dragon that was in it was slain; the dry land too was moved, the once fruitless wilderness of the nations.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.7":["And in this trembling of the universe all the nations too were shaken, because into all the earth has gone out the sound of the apostles of the Lord, and their words have penetrated to the ends of the world. And all the nations were moved for this reason, that from their movement the chosen things of the nations might come, and whatever is excellent anywhere. For example, the chosen things from Corinth, because there was much people of God in it; the chosen things from Macedonia, because the great Church of God gathered in Thessalonica had no need to be taught about love; the chosen things from Ephesus, so that they might know the secrets of God and mysteries revealed to none before. Why say more? All the nations were shaken to which the Savior had sent the apostles, saying: Go, teach all nations; and out of the many called, the few chosen built the Church of the firstborn. Hence the apostle Peter too: Greets you, he says, she who is elect in Babylon, and Mark my son; and John: The elder, he says, to the elect lady; and then he mentions the children of the elect lady. So when the nations had been shaken, which indeed we can also take as the opposing powers, unable to bear the splendor of the Lord, the chosen things of all the nations came, and the house of the Lord, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth, was filled with glory. This is according to the LXX. But in the Hebrew it stands better and more expressively, as we set it down above: And I will move all nations, and the one desired by all nations will come. For truly, after he came, the house of the Lord was filled with glory.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.8-9":["And as far as the Lord is distant from the servant, so much better is the house of the Lord, over which the Lord presides, than the former house, over which a servant presided. And as for what he says: Mine is the silver, and mine is the gold, says the Lord of hosts, I do not think that anyone supposes that he is speaking of the silver and gold that are possessed by the rich and by kings. For in this way not only silver and gold belong to God as Creator, but also the other metals, bronze, tin, lead, and iron, which tames all things. But I take the silver with which the house of God is adorned to be the utterances of the Scriptures, of which it is said: The utterances of the Lord are pure utterances; silver tried by fire, proved by the earth, purged sevenfold; and the gold to be what dwells in the hidden thought of the saints and in the secret place of the heart, and shines with the true light of God; which, it is clear, the Apostle also meant concerning the saints who build upon the foundation of Christ, gold, silver, precious stones, so that in the gold there is hidden thought, in the silver seemly speech, in the precious stone works pleasing to God. With these metals the Church of the Savior becomes more illustrious than the synagogue once was; with these living stones the house of Christ is built, and eternal peace is granted to it. Moreover, what follows in the Seventy: And peace of soul for acquisition to everyone who is created to raise up this temple, we have omitted as superfluous and scarcely coherent, since it is found neither among the Hebrews nor in any other translator.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.10-14":["On the twenty-fourth of the ninth month, in the second year of Darius, the word of the Lord came to Haggai the prophet, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Ask the priests the law, saying: If a man carries sanctified flesh in the border of his garment, and with its edge touches bread, or pottage, or wine, or oil, or any food, shall it be sanctified? And the priests answered and said: No. And Haggai said: If one polluted in soul touches any of all these, shall it be defiled? And the priests answered and said: It shall be defiled. And Haggai answered and said: So is this people, and so is this nation, before my face, says the Lord; and so every work of their hands, and all that they have offered there, shall be defiled. LXX: On the twenty-fourth of the ninth month, in the second year under Darius, the word of the Lord came to Haggai the prophet, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Ask the priests the law, saying: If a man takes holy flesh in the edge of his garment, and the edge of his garment touches bread, or cooked food, or wine, or oil, or any food, will it be sanctified? And the priests answered and said: No. And Haggai said: If one unclean in soul touches any of all these, will it be defiled? And the priests answered and said: It will be defiled. And Haggai answered and said: So is this people, and so is this nation before me, says the Lord, and so are all the works of their hands. And whoever draws near there will be defiled; because of their morning gifts they will grieve before the face of their wickednesses, and you scent those who reprove in the gates. We have set down the edition of the Seventy translators as well for this reason, that they seemed to disagree in certain words. And this which is said, Because of their morning gifts, they will grieve before the face of their wickednesses, and you scent those who reprove in the gates, is found neither in the Hebrew nor among the other translators.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.10":["It is to be noted, however, that in this place, On the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month in the second year, it is not said, as it was above for the third time, The word of the Lord came in the hand of Haggai the prophet, but to Haggai the prophet. For there — because he was still making progress and had only clean works, but his heart had not yet received full wisdom, or because he still dwelt among those who said, The time has not yet come for the house of the Lord to be built — the word of the Lord came only in his works. Here, however, since the foundations of the temple have now been laid, and the people with its rulers has entered the house of God and is doing work befitting the temple of God, and has heard the mystery, I will move all the nations, and the one desired by all the nations shall come, and is full of prophecy, therefore the word of the Lord comes wholly to Haggai. Of the twenty-fourth day and the second year we have already spoken. The number of the ninth month, which is added here, is never read in a good sense. The people sacrifices the Passover and celebrates other festivals; every solemnity of theirs ends on the eighth day and does not reach the ninth. Those who prepare the Passover lamb begin to prepare it when the ninth day is past. The day of propitiation and expiation of the seventh month, too, is celebrated after the ninth day. And in Jeremiah, as will be plain to those who read, Jerusalem is besieged by the Babylonians in the ninth year. Since, therefore, the prophecy was to concern the uncleanness of the people, for that reason the ninth month is joined to the second year of Darius. Again, because room is given for repentance after the rebuke of uncleanness, the word of God comes to Haggai the prophet on the twenty-fourth day, so that, as if in the person of the Lord, he may put to the priests a question arising from the law; and it is said to him: Ask the priests the law, saying.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.11":["And at the same time consider that it is the office of priests to answer one who asks about the law. If he is a priest, let him know the law of the Lord; if he does not know the law, he himself proves that he is not the Lord's priest. For it belongs to the priest to know the law and, when questioned, to answer concerning the law. This indeed we read in Deuteronomy too: that if anywhere in the cities of Israel a question should arise between blood and blood, between judgment and judgment, leprosy and leprosy, dispute and dispute, they are to go to the priests and Levites, and to the high priest who shall have been appointed in those days, and ask of him the law of the Lord; and when these have answered, they are to do what they are commanded. But if they do not do it, they are to be cut off from their people. And lest perhaps these precepts should seem to be in the old Instrument alone, the Apostle too speaks to Timothy, saying that a bishop ought to be not only irreproachable, and the husband of one wife, and wise, and chaste, and well-ordered, and hospitable, but also a teacher. And lest he should seem to have said this by chance, the same caution is observed also to Titus concerning the ordaining of presbyters, whom he wishes to be understood as bishops too: For this reason I left you in Crete, that you might set right what remained and ordain presbyters throughout the cities, as I instructed you: if anyone is irreproachable, the husband of one wife, having faithful children, not under accusation of dissipation or insubordinate. For a bishop must be irreproachable, as the steward of God, not insolent, not irascible, not given to wine, not a striker, not greedy for base gain, but hospitable, chaste, kind, just, holy, continent, holding in his teaching to the faithful word, that he may be able to console in sound teaching and to refute those who contradict. For there are many who are also not subject, vain talkers and seducers, especially those who are of the circumcision, on whom silence must be imposed. I have set these things down at some length so that we may know, from the Old as well as from the New Testament, that it is the office of priests to know the law of God and to answer whatever they are asked; and that simplicity and abstinence in food are not enough in a teacher unless he can also instruct others in what he himself does. Certainly, since I think they will reply that this belongs to those who prepare themselves for teaching from their youth, but that often, by the judgment of the Lord and the vote of the peoples, simple men are chosen for the priesthood: let them at least have this, that after they have been ordained priests they learn the law of God, so that they may teach what they have learned, and increase their knowledge rather than their wealth; and let them not blush to learn from laymen who know the things that belong to the office of priests; and let them spend nights and days in the study of the Scriptures rather than in accounts and reckoning.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.12-13":["But what is it that Haggai, in the person of the Lord, asks the priests? If a man carries sanctified flesh in the border or in the edge of his garment, and with its edge touches bread, or pottage, or wine, or oil, or any food, shall it be sanctified? Before we treat of the question, we ought meanwhile to know, according to the letter, what sanctified flesh is and what one unclean in soul is. As for the victim that was sacrificed on the altar, its flesh was holy, and among these sanctified meats themselves there was much diversity. For some the priests ate in the inner sanctuary of the temple, some their kinsfolk ate at home, some those of the priests who were held to be blemished, some Israelites who had no uncleanness. The full account of this diversity is given in Leviticus. But one was called unclean in soul who had touched the body of a dead man. Here it is to be observed that as long as the soul is in the body the human body is not unclean, but as soon as the spirit that quickens the limbs has withdrawn, what is earthly becomes unclean, as is written in the same Leviticus: And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying: Speak to the priests, the sons of Aaron, and you shall say to them: They shall not be defiled by souls among their nation, but by kinsfolk who are near to them: over a mother, and over a father, and over sons and daughters, and over a brother and his sister, a virgin, who is near to him and has not been given to a husband; over these he shall not be defiled, and he shall not be polluted suddenly among his people to his defilement. Consider that these priests are commanded not to go in to any dead person whatever, except to those kinsfolk and near relations of whom it was said above. But the great priest, that is, the high priest, had something more than the other priests: neither filial devotion nor affection could bend him to become unclean in the cases named above. For the Scripture says: And he shall not go in to any soul that is dead: over his father, nor over his mother, and he shall not be defiled.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.12-14":["But according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), the man of the Church who has sacrificed the lamb without blemish and a year old, and has put on Christ, if he takes some of its flesh and ties it in the edge of his garment, and that edge touches the bread of the Scriptures, which strengthens the hearts of believers; or cooked food, the apostolic epistles, which, as it were, cut up the meats of the old law and cook them through and offer them seasoned for eating; or wine, which gladdens the heart of man; or oil, by which the face of the hearer is made cheerful; or any food: the milk on which the Corinthians are nourished, and the herbs on which the weak feed, and other things like these; those to whom they are given will not at once eat them as sanctified. For it is not from their being spoken but from their being received that they sanctify the hearers, because many are hearers of the law and not doers. But I think also that all these things I have named are by no means conveyed sanctified to those who eat from the touch of the garment for this reason: that they are touched only by the edge of the cloak, and the sanctified meats do not know, inwardly, moisture, blood, veins and sinews. As, therefore, the edge of the Lord's garment and a light touch do not sanctify, except him who has eaten the flesh of the Lamb and drunk his blood, so, on the contrary, one unclean in soul, all perverse doctrines, must needs make unclean whatever they touch. For they have in their mysteries bread, and wine, and oil, and every food; but their mysteries are like the bread of mourning: all who touch them will be defiled. They too read the Scriptures, and, as it were, from the testimonies of the Scriptures they mix the dough for bread and bake it all night in the oven; but when it has been given to eat, it provokes those who eat it to madness. They have pottage and cooked food, attempting, according to the sense of their perversity, to weave every mystical thing out of the Scriptures, and, as it were, to cook and season the flesh of the lamb; but that cooked food is perdition. They have wine too, but not from the vine of Sorek, the one the Lord planted in Jeremiah, choice and wholly true, but from the vine of Sodom. They have oil also, which they forcibly press out of the testimonies of the Old and the New Scripture, and which they promise to deceived and weary minds as if for refreshment; but the holy man abhors it and says: But let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head. They have also diverse foods, namely the manifold and diverse treatises of various ὑποθέσεων (of arguments), which, because those who wrote them are unclean and they were uttered from an unclean mouth, everyone who touches will become unclean and will be drawn into their error. Hence Haggai answered, who knows the differences between the solemnities, and for that reason received the name of festive. So is this people and so is this nation, that is, of the Jews, and of the Gentiles, and of all heretics, in my sight, says the Lord. Everything they do, whatever they offer me, whether vows for welfare, or peace offerings, or offerings for sin, or for trespass, or as a holocaust, or alms, or fasts, or abstinence in diet and chastity of body, will be defiled in my sight. For although the things offered by such people seem holy in their appearance, yet because they have been touched by one who is polluted in soul, all of them will be polluted.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.12":["Knowing, then, what sanctified flesh is, and one unclean in soul, let us see what the prophet asks. If any man: here it is not said of the high priest, or of a priest, or of a Levite, but of any man whatever; where no person is specified, all are freely permitted to touch the flesh. If, he says, he carries sanctified flesh and has tied it in the edge of his garment, and the edge of the garment itself touches bread, or any other cooked food, or wine, or oil, or anything besides these on which a man can feed, can the bread or pottage, or wine, or oil or any food be sanctified by the touch of the garment in which the holy flesh is tied? And the priests answered and said: It cannot; that is, none of the things you ask about will be sanctified, but each will remain as it was.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.13":["Again another question is put to the priests, since, that is, they had answered the former one well, and a sort of likeness of the problem is fitted to it, in which an unskilled man could easily slip. For suppose someone does not know the law: just as he answered concerning the sanctified flesh that bread, or pottage, or wine, or oil, or any food is not sanctified, so in this case too he will answer alike and say: One polluted in soul does not defile the things that holy flesh could not sanctify. He asks, therefore, and says: If he who is polluted in soul, that is, who has been made unclean by contact with the dead, touches any of all these, namely bread or pottage, or wine, or oil, or the other foods, will the things he has touched be polluted by contact with them? And the priests answered, whose chief was Joshua son of Jehozadak, whence they also knew the law, and they said that by the touch of him who is unclean all things touched by him are polluted.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.14":["And Haggai answered and said, passing over in silence the earlier point, that sanctified flesh cannot sanctify the other foods, and weaving the occasion of his discourse from the second question alone: So is this people, and so is this nation before my face, says the Lord. He who is unclean in soul and has touched a carcass, everything he touches and whatever he offers to me will be unclean. What he says according to the letter is of this kind: O people, who, with only the altar built and my house in ruins, offer me sacrifices on the altar and think that you are sanctified by its victims and its flesh, know that you are not so much sanctified by the sacrifices, which, with the temple in ruins, can be of no profit to you, as that all your works and everything you do are defiled, from the fact that you are negligent and have striven to build your own house rather than mine. What is offered on the altar is indeed holy; but you are not so much sanctified by the sacrifices as you are polluted by the fact that you dwell in valleys and are present among dead works. This according to the history, although we have drawn in it the lines of a spiritual understanding.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.15-17":["And now set your hearts from this day and upward, before stone was laid upon stone in the temple of the Lord. When you came to a heap of twenty bushels, and they became ten, and you went in to the winepress to press out fifty jars, and they became twenty. I struck you with burning wind, and with blight and hail all the works of your hands, and there was none among you who would return to me, says the Lord. LXX: And now set upon your hearts from this day and upward, before stone was laid upon stone in the temple of the Lord, you who were, when you put twenty measures of barley into the cypsele, and they became ten measures of barley; and you went in to the winepress to draw fifty amphoras, and they became twenty. I struck you with barrenness, and with corruption of the air, and with hail, all the works of your hands; and you did not return to me, says the Lord. Although everything you offered me on the altar was defiled because you had not built the temple — for without the building of the temple every gift is polluted — yet now I exhort you, O people, to turn back in thought to the past and to consider what has happened; that is, from this twenty-fourth day of the ninth month in the second year of Darius, embrace in your mind whatever was done before, and for what causes you have endured such great things, so that when prosperity comes to you hereafter you may know for what cause it has come. So, before you began to build the temple and to lay stone upon stone, when you came to the heap and thought that you had twenty bushels, could you not barely gather half? Or, according to the LXX: When you put twenty bushels of barley into the vessel that is called a cypsele, and supposed that, although you were putting in barley, the food of beasts, you were at least secure of those twenty bushels, did you not afterwards, returning to the vessel, find barely ten bushels? When you came to the winepress, too, and saw the grapes, and your eyes promised you fifty amphoras, you could press out, I do not say half, but barely twenty amphoras. And I did these things, striking you with burning wind, and corruption of the air, and dying crops, and empty husks of the ears and clusters of the vines, so that by the weight of evils I might rouse you to the knowledge of me; and even so there was none who would return to me.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.15-19":["The Hebrew explained the whole tenor of this passage, from what is written, And now set your hearts from this day and upward, up to the place where he says, The vine and the fig and the pomegranate, and the olive tree has not flowered: from this day I will bless, in this way: Certainly the foundations of the temple have now been laid; from this day, therefore, on which you laid the foundations — although in the past I punished you with barrenness, and famine, and hail, and drought, and there was none of you who returned to me through these plagues — set your hearts toward the future and onward, and see that all things flow for you on a prosperous course. And this will come about because you have begun to build my temple, and do not, putting your trust in the altar alone, despise the building of my house. According to this sense we can say briefly that some offer gifts to God in vain, and think that the Lord can be appeased by alms and oblations, when they themselves have not built in themselves a temple for the Holy Spirit. For alms, and the gifts that are offered on the altar, are of profit when one has built himself up as a temple of God, and after the building of the temple has offered gifts on the altar.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.15":["Moreover, according to tropology, it is said also to us who now believe in Jesus Christ, if indeed we believe and show its truth by the work of faith, that we should return in mind to that time when we were Gentiles, daily serving vices, and had not built in ourselves a temple to God. And as an architect and most skilled mason joins stone to stone, and cements the lower to the upper with lime and plaster, so also the architect — whom the Apostle too says he is, As a wise architect I have laid the foundation, and whom the Lord threatens that he will take away from Jerusalem — knows how to join works to works and little by little to build up the temple of God. And the foundation of this temple is laid: Jesus, upon whom let each one see what he builds. One builds gold, silver, precious stones; another wood, hay, stubble. And to the three goods three evils are opposed on the contrary side. These are the stones of which the Lord promises that he will build Jerusalem: Behold, I will prepare for you the carbuncle as your stone, and your foundations sapphire, and I will make your bulwarks jasper, and your gates of crystal stone, and your wall of choice stones. For it must not be thought, according to Jewish fables and foolish fictions, that God will build Jerusalem of gold and gems, and not of living stones, which now roll upon the earth, and which, according to the natures of the stones, are either fiery with faith, like the carbuncle; or wholly heavenly and brought up into the throne of God, like the sapphire; or shining with innocence and the simplicity of good works, like crystal. It is said to us, therefore, that we should consider what we suffered in the past before we built the temple of God in ourselves.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.16":["When you came, he says, to a heap of twenty bushels, and they became ten: Or, according to the LXX: When you put twenty bushels of barley into the cypsele, and they became ten bushels of barley. For whatever virtues and good works we seemed to have before Christ was not wheat but barley; and that very barley did not, according to our hope and prayers, yield a hundredfold harvest, as we read of Isaac, but we could barely find in it half of our labor, and it was said to us: Have you suffered so many things without cause? But also, when we went in to the winepress and reckoned on fifty amphoras of wine — a number which, when seven weeks are completed, the unity of the Godhead closes — and thought that we had wine, which gladdens the heart of man, the holy number thirty was taken away from us — the number at which the Lord is baptized, and Ezekiel sees his vision at the beginning of his prophecy, and, according to the Hebrew, the priests came to the ministry of God — and twenty were left. This number Esau loves; hence Jacob too, knowing that he delighted in this number, sends certain animals as a gift, twenty and twenty. And at the same time observe that Jacob himself, though holy — yet at the time when he was not with his father Isaac, that is, laughter, and his mother Rebekah, that is, patience, but had the Assyrians as neighbors and dwelt in Mesopotamia, with rivers girding him on this side and on that — served for the space of twenty years the most cruel and most avaricious Laban. Nor let it trouble anyone if we say that some, before faith in Christ and the building of his temple, can receive half of their labor, although among unbelievers there is no fruit of good works. For he does not store up twenty and find twenty, but when he has stored up twenty he finds ten, that is, half of his labor. Jews and heathens and the philosophers of this world, and the others who boast of wisdom, receive the fruit and the glory of their way of life and their labor in the present time only, and all their hope and reward of the world to come is taken away. And this is done so that they may not, utterly despairing, despise repentance, but may at some time be converted and lay stone upon stone and build the temple of God. But if they remain in unbelief, they will lose even that half which they seemed to have.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.17":["For there follows: I struck you with burning wind and with blight and hail all the works of your hands. And whatever is struck by blight and hail and burning wind is reduced to dust and ashes, and nothing is found in it that serves for use or for food. All these things indeed the Lord did, because there was not found among them one who would return to him. But if they return and build the temple of the Lord, from the day on which they begin to build they will have what the word of the prophecy goes on to add.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.18-19":["Set your hearts from this day and into the future, from the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month, from the day on which the foundations of the temple of the Lord were laid, set upon your heart. Is the seed already in the sprout, and have the fig and the vine and the pomegranate and the olive tree still not flowered? From this day I will bless. LXX: Set your hearts from this day and into the future, from the four and twentieth of the ninth month, and from the day on which the foundations of the temple of the Lord were laid, set in your hearts. If the threshing floor will any longer be known upon the earth, and if still the vine and the fig and the pomegranate, and the olive trees that do not bear fruit? From this day I will bless. I have set forth to you, he says, what you endured before you began to build my temple; now I will set forth what prosperity is to come to you, because you have begun to build my temple. From the twenty-fourth day, therefore, of the ninth month, in which the foundations of the temple were laid, consider how great an abundance of things there will be.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.18":["The ninth month is the one we call November or December. For Nisan is the first month among the Hebrews, which is called the month of new things, at the time when they keep the Passover, that is, at the first beginning of spring; following the course of the moon it often occupies some part of the month of March, and sometimes it begins in April. So if we understand Nisan as April, the ninth month by the reckoning of the Hebrews will be December. So it is the tenth month, at the time when the seeds lie hidden in the earth and future fruitfulness cannot be guessed.\n— Commentary on Haggai","That the ninth month is not taken in a good sense, both the fourth book of Kingdoms (2 Kings) and the history of Jeremiah relate, in which Jerusalem is said to have been besieged. Yet since the foundations of the temple are laid at the end of the ninth month, we can understand this: that the building of the temple of the Lord is not begun unless evil works come to an end. Hence also on the twenty-fourth day of the same month the foundation of the temple is laid; in this number there are a double δωδεκὰς (dodecad), and three ὀγδοάδες (ogdoads), and four ἑξάδες (hexads), of which we have already discussed more fully above.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.19":["Is the seed, he says, already in the sprout? This is better said in Hebrew, in the husk, so as to signify the sheath of the grain. Have the vine and the fig, and the pomegranate and the olive tree given their flower? So that from the flower the fruit too may be understood. Certainly not; for in the month of December, as we have said, there are no signs of the crops to come. So that you may not say, then, that I conjecture this by shrewd reasoning and surmise the future fruitfulness from the flowers of the trees and the blades of the crops: behold, there are no signs, and yet, because you have begun to build my temple, I foretell to you a fruitfulness of all crops at my blessing. This we have said according to the Hebrew. But according to the Seventy the sense is far different, and this too we must first explain according to the letter, so that afterwards the course of tropology we have begun may be treated. Set your hearts from this day on which the temple was founded, toward the future, and you will see that the harvests will be so great, and so much grain carried in from all the fields, that the threshing floor will not know its own crops; or that there will not be separate threshing floors for each man, but, because of the abundance, threshing floor will be joined to threshing floor, and the division between threshing floors will not be known upon the earth. The vine also and the fig and the pomegranate and the olive tree, which before, through your fault, did not bring forth fruit, because you had not yet begun to build me a temple, will be bowed down with such an abundance of grapes and fruits that manifest fruitfulness will point to a manifest blessing.\n— Commentary on Haggai","Whoever, therefore, has dedicated himself to the worship of God, and has despised the ῥάθυμον, that is, the negligent patron, who in the volume of Ezra, according to the Seventy translators, forbids the temple of God to be built, he does not know the measure of his crops and of his reward. Or certainly, on account of what is said, If the threshing floor will still be known upon the earth, he who has sown in the spirit and from the spirit has reaped eternal life will by no means store up treasure for himself on earth, but all his works and the rewards of his works will be gathered in the heavens. The vine also, that is, the word of God, whose husbandman in each one is the Father; and the fig, the sweetest gifts of the Holy Spirit; and the pomegranate, the doctrines of the Church and the knowledge of the Scriptures, which is compared to the cheeks of the bride in the Song of Songs; and the olive trees will give refreshment and illumination of heart to him who has begun to build the temple of God. That the vine and the fig and the olive — I put off the pomegranate for a little — refer to the person of the Savior, and of God the Father, and of the Holy Spirit, is read more fully in the book of Judges, where the unfruitful trees go to set a king over themselves, and say in turn to the vine and to the fig and to the olive that they should reign over them, and the vine as well as the fig and the olive refuse such rule and do not deign to reign over unfruitful trees. Then they come to the tree that is king of their barrenness, that is, to the bramble, a thorny shrub and a little tree woven of briars and hooks, which holds whatever it has touched, and wounds what it has held, and delights in the blood of the wounded; and besides sends forth fire from itself and consumes the trees it rules. We refer the bramble to the devil, and according to the nature of the shrub we shall interpret his nature. Moreover, the vine and the fig and the olive will be there where the pomegranate is; and this tree, because of the very great multitude of its seeds, and the kind of geometrical arrangement made by its interwoven membranes, and its little dwellings, separate indeed yet all enclosed by one rind, is always set in the Scriptures for the person of the Church.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.20-23":["And the word of the Lord came a second time to Haggai on the twenty-fourth day of the month, saying: Speak to Zerubbabel, governor of Judah, saying: I will move heaven and earth alike, and I will overturn the throne of kingdoms, and I will crush the strength of the kingdom of the nations; and I will overturn the chariot and its rider; and the horses and their riders shall go down, each man by the sword of his brother. On that day, says the Lord of hosts, I will take you, O Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, my servant, says the Lord, and I will set you as a signet, because I have chosen you, says the Lord of hosts. In the LXX there is added: The sea and the dry land, and it lacks: I will crush the strength of the kingdom of the nations, which will be recognized more fully from reading their text.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.20":["It is to be noted, moreover, that on the same day, that is, on the twenty-fourth of the ninth month, with the number of the month passed over in silence because the prophecy concerned the coming of Christ and his kingdom, the word of our Lord comes a second time to Haggai: not in his hand, as before, nor to Haggai the prophet, as in the fourth vision, but simply to Haggai, that is, to one celebrating the feasts of the Lord. For he was to announce and to behold him not as one who would come at some time, but as one coming. And as Abraham saw the day of Christ and rejoiced, and John pointed out the Lamb of God with his finger, so he too, seeing the kingdom of the Son of God, was to have within himself all the solemnities.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.21-22":["On this passage most interpreters hold differing opinions: for some suppose that it is said of his first coming; others, of the second, when he is to come in his majesty. We accept both, because he both reigned then, when he came, and is to reign hereafter. Yet if we wish to take it of the end of the world, we shall say what the Apostle says to the Corinthians: To destroy every principality and every power and virtue, that God may be all in all. And because what is said is mystical and pertains to the end of things, the prophet is therefore commanded to speak to Zerubbabel alone, whom we have shown to have come forth as a type of Christ because of the assumption of a body from the seed of David. To him, therefore, are told the things that are to come at the end: that the form of this world passes away, and a new heaven and a new earth come to be, and the Lord shakes heaven and earth, and destroys every principality, and power, and virtue, and scatters the seats of kings, or, as it stands in the Hebrew, of kingdoms, and slays every opposing strength, so that the destruction of their kingdom may profit even those who had reigned before and had held the nations under their sway, and that, with all zeal for warfare scattered, peace may follow.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.22":["For this is what he says: And I will overturn the chariot, or chariots, and their riders, and the horses and their riders shall go down. And that you may know that what we have said is signified in the overturning of the chariots and of the horsemen falling headlong, see how it is said of Christ in Zechariah that a gentle king comes, mounting upon a yoke-beast and a young colt of an ass, and destroys the chariots out of Ephraim and the horse from Jerusalem, so that there may be one flock and one shepherd, and both hosts, from the nations as well as from the Jews, may be held under a peaceable shepherd. But so that these things which are perverse may be destroyed, each one with the sword — which I take to be the sharpest word of doctrine, cutting away whatever is perverse — will rise up against his brother, cutting off everything opposed.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"HAG.2.23":["But the end of all these things is the best. For after the destruction of the thrones, and the strengths of those who reign, and of the chariots and the horses and the horsemen, on that day, says the Lord Almighty: I will take you, Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, my servant. Now he is called servant because of the human body, since then the Son himself also will be subjected to him who subjected all things to himself, and among all things subjected he too will be seen subjected. But when this has been fulfilled, God will set him as a signet in his hand: For him God the Father has sealed; and he is the image of the invisible God and the form of his substance, so that whoever has believed in God may be sealed with him as with a ring.\n— Commentary on Haggai"],"ZEC.1.1":["Already at the very end of autumn our brother, your son the monk Sisinnius, delivered to me a letter from your Grace; on reading it I rejoiced that you are safe and well, and mindful of me and of all the brothers who serve the Lord in the holy places, in whose relief you make friends for yourself of the unrighteous mammon and prepare eternal tabernacles, so that you can say with David: How lovely are your tabernacles, O Lord of powers; my soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord. For if the sparrow has found a house for itself, and the turtledove a nest for itself where it may lay its young, why should you, who are a high priest of the Lord, and tread the winepresses at the end of the world so as to give to thirsting peoples the wine of the blood of Christ, not freely cry out and say: My heart and my flesh have exulted in the living God; blessed are those who dwell in your house. Having heard that you, in the valley of tears, in the place which God has set for the contest so as to give a crown to those who conquer, are arranging ascents in your heart, and going from strength to strength, and imitating the poverty of the Lord, that with him you may become rich, and that he may lay his head upon you, and day by day be received, visited, fed and clothed; and that above all you are fervent in the reading of the holy Scriptures. And since I desired to offer you some little gift of my poor talent, and the exposition I had begun on the twelve prophets was coming to its close, I was unwilling to abandon the work I had undertaken, but what I would have dictated even without you I have consecrated above all to your name; and I am glad that the most obscure book of the prophet Zechariah, and the longest among the twelve, is to be expounded at a time when, because of the haste of him who is to return, the interpretation suffers no delay; but, willing or unwilling, I am compelled to dictate, at least in hours gained and stolen by night, what I am to send you. Origen wrote two volumes on this prophet, reaching from the beginning to the third part of the book. Hippolytus also published commentaries, and Didymus, at my request, dictated five books of exposition, which, with three others on Hosea, he also προσεφώνησεν (dedicated) to me; but their whole ἐξήγησις (exposition) was allegorical, and they barely touched a few points of the history. Therefore, desiring to imitate that householder who brings forth from his treasure things new and old, and the bride of the Song of Songs, who says: New with old, my kinsman, I have kept for you, I have mingled the tropology of our writers with the history of the Hebrews, so as to build upon the rock and not upon the sand, and to lay a firm foundation, which Paul the architect wrote that he had laid. It will be for your kindness to look up, not to our learning, which is either none or small, but to the will that leans toward you, so that you may spur us on to the rest and urge us to run in the long field of the Scriptures. But if there are any to whom I previously promised an interpretation both of these and of other books, let them pardon my incredible love for you, and let them consider whatever I have written to you as written to themselves: For love is kind, love is not envious, it does not seek its own. But now it is time that, setting down the words of Zechariah, we spread the sails of interpretation to the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","In the eighth month, in the second year of Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, the son of Berechiah, the son of Iddo, the prophet, saying. LXX: In the eighth month of the second year under Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah of Berechiah, the son of Iddo, the prophet, saying. That in the second year of Darius son of Hystaspes the seventieth year of the desolation of the temple, which had been foretold by Jeremiah, was completed, Zechariah himself is witness, saying: O Lord of hosts, how long will you not have mercy on Jerusalem and on the cities of Judah, with which you are angry? Behold, this is the seventieth year; and no one doubts that Cyrus, king of the Persians, who destroyed the empire of the Chaldeans, in the first year of his reign sent back from Babylon into Judea about fifty thousand captives; he also returned the vessels which Nebuchadnezzar had carried off, and ordered the temple to be rebuilt in Jerusalem, and he appointed as leaders of the people that returned Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel and Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest. So when Cyrus, who reigned thirty years among the Persians, had been killed among the Massagetae by Tomyris, queen of the Massagetae, his son Cambyses succeeded to the kingdom; and when he had completed eight years, he had as successors two brothers, magi; and when these had been killed by the people, Darius son of Hystaspes was made king of the Persians by the decision of the seven families. In the second year of his reign the word of the Lord came to the prophet Haggai, and to Zechariah, the son of Berechiah, the son of Iddo. On Haggai we have already spoken; on the title of Zechariah a few things must be said. Since he is the son of Berechiah, the question is asked why he is called the son of Iddo. Nor is it doubted, according to the book of Chronicles, that he is that Iddo who was sent to Jeroboam son of Nebat, under whom the altar was torn down and the king's hand withered, and at his prayers was restored again. Therefore from the first year of King Cyrus to the second year of Darius son of Hystaspes forty years are reckoned, in which only the altar had been built and the foundations of the temple laid, since the neighboring nations round about were hindering the work, and so was Cambyses, king of the Persians, who had sent letters on this matter to the governors of Syria Κοίλης (Hollow), and of Phoenicia and Samaria. Ezra wrote the history of this more fully. But Haggai began to prophesy in the sixth month, on the first day of that month; Zechariah, for his part, in the same year indeed, but not in the same month, for two months later, in the eighth month of the year, he poured out his prophecy. Hence, rightly, according to the order of the twelve prophets, the former is placed tenth and the latter eleventh. And it is a mark of great confidence, nay rather of hope and faith in God, that what had been left undone for so long a time, whether through the king's prohibition or the nations', they should begin to do at the stirring of two men. These things, according to the history, we have set down first in our usual manner. Now we must discuss them briefly according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy). The eighth month among the Hebrews, which among them is called Marcheshvan, among the Egyptians Athir, and among us November, is the beginning of winter; in it, the heat of summer being spent, all the earth is stripped of its green, and the bodies of mortals are contracted, and there is no solemnity from the Law. For it is written that three times in the year every male must appear before the Lord our God. The first festival is that of unleavened bread, the Phase, which among us is called Pascha in Greek and Latin speech. The second is that of Pentecost, after seven weeks, in which the loaves of proposition are first offered from the new crops. The third, in the seventh month, is that of trumpets, of propitiation and of tabernacles, that is, σκηνοπηγίας, whose last day is called the day of departure and of the end. So the people, placed in captivity and still under the king of the Persians, with the temple not yet built and the ramparts and walls of the city not yet raised, sees prophecy not in a time of gladness, but after gladness. And yet it had the remembrance of the Lord, and because of the remembrance, blessing, and on account of the blessing, testimony. For this reason Zechariah, the son of Berechiah, the son of Iddo, is sent to it. Zechariah is interpreted remembrance of the Lord; Berechiah, blessing of the Lord; Iddo, his testimony. Let us see, then, what the word of the Lord, which was in the beginning with God the Father, spoke to the people in the prophet Zechariah.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.2":["The Lord was angry with your fathers in anger, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Turn to me, says the Lord of hosts, and I will turn to you, says the Lord of hosts; be not like your fathers, to whom the former prophets cried, saying. LXX: The Lord was angry with your fathers with great wrath, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Turn to me, says the Lord of powers, and I will turn to you, says the Lord of powers; and do not become like your fathers, whom the prophets who were before rebuked, saying. The wrath of the Lord does not signify a disturbance in him who is angry, but the deserts and sins of those against whom the wrath of God rages; for the rest, the Lord never takes vengeance in anger, but rebukes in order to amend. Hence the prophet also prays in the psalms: O Lord, do not reprove me in your fury, nor rebuke me in your wrath, and in another place we read: Rebuke us, O Lord, yet not in your fury, but in mercy. So the Lord was angry with the fathers of those who are now being rebuked, not, as the Seventy translators have it, with great wrath, but simply with wrath. For if he had been angry with great wrath, he would never have called the captives to repentance through Daniel and Ezekiel.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.3-4":["And although he was angry with them, it is to you that the Lord of hosts speaks, for which Sabaoth is set down in the Hebrew, which the Seventy sometimes translated 'Almighty', sometimes 'of powers'. And he says: Turn to me, and I will turn to you, according to what we read: Draw near to the Lord, and he will draw near to you. To which this is the contrary: You have withdrawn from me, and I have withdrawn from you. Now he commands those who have returned from Babylon to return to the Lord, so that the Lord too may return to them, lest the sons begin to suffer what their fathers once suffered, to whom the prophets of that time likewise spoke. It is to be noted that in a few short verses and brief sentences, in Haggai and Zechariah, says the Lord of hosts is always added, so that they may know that it is God who commands, both against the command of the king and against the enemies raging round about; and that by this confidence they may be roused to the building of the temple.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.4":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Turn from your evil ways, and from your most wicked thoughts; and they did not hear, nor did they attend to me, says the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Turn from your most wicked ways, and from your evil devices, and they did not attend so as to hear me, says the Lord. The sentence hangs on what precedes, and what follows must be fitted to what came before. For Zechariah is not speaking to the people, but is showing what the former prophets said to their fathers, to whom they cried, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Turn from your evil ways and from your most wicked thoughts. This is what Isaiah cried, this Hosea and Joel, Amos and Jeremiah: that they should forsake their evil ways and abandon their most wicked thoughts, by which they were sinning both in deed and in mind; and nevertheless they did not hear the Lord warning them through the prophets, nor did they attend, not at all to the prophets who were speaking to them, but to me, says the Lord: for in them it was I who was speaking and being despised. Hence the Lord also says in the Gospel: He who receives you, he says, receives me.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.5-6":["Your fathers, where are they, and the prophets? Will they live forever? But my words and my ordinances, which I commanded my servants the prophets, did they not overtake your fathers? And they turned and said: As the Lord of hosts purposed to do to us according to our ways and according to our devices, he has done to us. LXX: Your fathers, where are they, and the prophets? Will they live forever? But receive my words and my ordinances, which I command in my spirit to my servants the prophets, who overtook your fathers. And they answered and said: As the Lord Almighty was prepared to do to us according to our ways and according to our devices, so he has done to us. Where, he says, are your fathers, who did not hear me nor attend to me? Where are your prophets? For it is to be understood Ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (from the shared word): Your fathers, where are they, and your prophets, where are they? that is, the false prophets; for he would never say of the holy prophets: Will they live forever? So, when both those who sinned and refused to return to me, and those who deceived the sinners with false promises, have been taken away by death, my words, which I spoke through my prophets, remain forever; they overtook your fathers and showed that what I said was true. And your fathers, proving upon themselves by the outcome of events the prophecies of my prophets, turned to repentance and said: As the Lord of hosts foretold that he would do to us, according to our works and sins he has repaid us. Let us read Daniel confessing to the Lord, in his own person and in that of the people, that they had refused to hear him, and that whatever they had suffered had befallen them justly.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.7":["On the twenty-fourth day of the eleventh month, Shebat, in the second year of Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, the son of Berechiah, the son of Iddo, the prophet, saying. LXX: On the twenty-fourth, in the eleventh month, which is the month Shebat, in the second year under Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah of Berechiah, the son of Iddo, the prophet, saying. Some put the twelfth month in place of the eleventh, and, interpreting the twenty-fourth day of that month by arithmetical reckonings, suppose it to be a square, firm and stable number, wishing the things that are written to be certain and stable. But let us say that in the same second year of King Darius, in the third month after the first vision, that is, in the eleventh after the eighth, which among the Hebrews is called Shebat, the word of the Lord came again to Zechariah. That the second number is unclean, and pertains to the union of the flesh, and to the things of the world and of matter, we have often taught. Now the eleventh month, which is called Shebat, and in our language is rendered 'rod', sounds of severity and rebuke, according to that saying of the Apostle: What do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod? And of the psalmist, who says: Your rod and your staff, they have comforted me. And it falls in the very bitter season of winter, and is called mechir by the Egyptians, περίτϊος by the Macedonians, and February by the Romans. The twenty-fourth day of the month, too, signifies a conspicuous shadow of night, as the moon wanes into darkness and the blind and continual dread of night increases. Hence, fittingly, those who were still in captivity, and for the greater part of them were in exile among the Medes and Chaldeans and Assyrians, in the second year, and in the most severe cold of the eleventh month, and on the twenty-fourth day of that month, see for the Jewish people the things that follow.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.8":["I saw by night, and behold, a man mounting a red horse, and he was standing among the myrtle groves that were in the deep, and behind him horses red, dappled and white. And I said: What are these, my lord? And the angel who was speaking in me said to me: I will show you what these are. And the man who was standing among the myrtle groves answered and said: These are they whom the Lord has sent to walk through the earth. And they answered the angel of the Lord, who was standing among the myrtle groves, and said: We have walked through the earth, and behold, all the earth is inhabited and at rest. And the angel of the Lord answered and said: O Lord of hosts, how long will you not have mercy on Jerusalem and on the cities of Judah, with which you are angry? This is the seventieth year. And the Lord answered the angel who was speaking in me good words, words of consolation. LXX: I saw in the night, and behold, a man mounting a red horse, and he was standing between two shady mountains, and behind him horses red, and dappled, and white. And I said: What are these, lord? And the angel who was speaking in me said to me: I will show you what these are. And the man who was standing between the mountains answered and said to me: These are they whom the Lord has sent to go round the earth. And they answered the angel of the Lord, who was standing between the mountains, and said: We have gone round all the earth, and behold, all the earth is inhabited and at rest. And the angel of the Lord answered and said: O Lord Almighty, how long will you not have mercy on Jerusalem and on the cities of Judah, with which you are angry? This is the seventieth year. And the Lord answered the angel who was speaking in me good words and consoling speeches. Let us first discuss the history: I saw, he says, by night, not by day, like Moses, who saw God face to face, and Isaiah, who said: I saw the Lord upon a throne high and lifted up, before the people was led into captivity, before Jerusalem and the temple were destroyed; but now, with the people still set in the evils of servitude, he discerns in darkness the things he sees on their behalf. And behold, a man mounting a red horse. This the Hebrews think is the angel Michael, who is the avenger of the iniquities and sins of Israel. And as to his having stood among the myrtle groves that were in the deep, they want the myrtles to be understood as the prophets and saints who were living in the midst of the captive people and were in the deep; and the man, that is, Michael, was followed by horses red and dappled and white, the order, as they suppose, being changed, so that the white signify the Medes and Persians, because under them the captivity was released and the temple restored; the dappled, the Macedonians, some of whom were friends, others persecutors — the last vision of Daniel speaks most fully on this —; and the red, the kingdom of the Romans, bloody and murderous, which slew the people and overthrew the temple. Others, however, keeping the order as it is written, interpret the man who mounted the red horse, and the red, dappled and white horses, as four kingdoms. The first and second, the bloody ones, are the Assyrians and Chaldeans, the first of whom led the ten tribes captive under Shalmaneser, the second Judah and Benjamin, when the city of Jerusalem had been burned and the temple overthrown. The third and fourth, the dappled and white, they think signify the Medes and Persians, some of whom were merciful, like Cyrus, and Darius son of Hystaspes, and Ahasuerus, whom the Greeks call Artaxerxes, under whom the story of Esther is fulfilled, and others cruel, like Cambyses and the rest.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But the teachers of the churches understand the night in which the vision is seen as the obscurity of a mystical vision. For God made darkness his hiding place. And according to the Seventy translators it is said of him: The abyss like a garment is his clothing. Now the man who was mounting the red horse is the Lord and Savior, who, taking it up for the dispensation of our flesh, hears in Isaiah: Why are your garments red? He who is now shown as red to the captive people is described in the Apocalypse of John as sitting in white garments and on a white horse. And as to his being said to stand between two shady mountains — although 'two' is not found in the Hebrew — they take them as the New and the Old Testament, which is said to be covered with very dense foliage, and grove, and shady woods, because it is wrapped in many obscurities. And the myrtle groves that were in the deep, among which he is described as standing, they understand as the angelic powers, which ministered to him even when he was set in the flesh. Or else, removing what is not written, 'two', we can take the mountains absolutely and without number as very many saints, and the apostles and apostolic men, who are recorded as having traveled through the whole world, red, and dappled, and white; of whom some are crowned with martyrdom and are called red; others, for their works and teaching and the variety of their signs, are named dappled; others have received the rewards of virginity and of uncorrupted doctrines, and of the pure heart, which sees God.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.9-10":["So when the prophet saw a man mounting a red horse, and behind him red, dappled and white horses, and did not know what he had seen, and desired to know the meaning of his vision, and said: What are these, my lord? the angel who was speaking in him promises that he will tell; and while he keeps silent, the man who was standing among the myrtle groves relates it. These are the kingdoms which the Lord commanded to walk through the earth and to subject it to their power.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.9-11":["Then the prophet questions the same man and angel who was standing among the myrtle groves, and says: What are these, my lord? And the angel who was speaking in him, namely the prophetic sense, promises that he will show what he sees. And when he has promised, it is not he himself who answered, but another who was standing among the myrtle groves, and he says: These are they whom the Lord has sent to walk through the earth. For the apostles were sent by the Lord to fill the whole world with the preaching of the Gospel; and after they had fulfilled the work, they returned to the angel of great counsel who was standing among them (they who said with the Apostle: We are the good odor of Christ among those who are being saved and among those who are perishing) and among the peoples of both callings. And they said to him: We have walked through the earth, we have fulfilled the work enjoined on us, and behold, all the earth is inhabited and at rest, which before was deserted by God; now it is his dwelling, it rests from wars, from the battles of the vices and from sins, because it has received the inheritance of the Lord as he returned to the Father and said: My peace I give you, my peace I leave you.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.9":["I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me. The prophet prays on behalf of the people, and he who speaks within himself while God speaks, and he says, I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me. You see that God speaks not in the ears but in the heart; just as Zechariah too says: \"And the angel who spoke within me said to me.\" There the angel is understood to be our Lord, who announces the will of the Father, who in Isaiah is called the \"angel of great counsel.\" I will hear what the Lord God speaks within me: I will incline the ear of my heart, that I may hear what the Lord God speaks within me.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 64"],"ZEC.1.11":["In short, the angels who presided over the kingdoms and nations come not to another, but to the man himself and angel of the Lord who was standing among the myrtle groves, and say: We have walked through the earth, and behold, all the earth is inhabited and at rest. And the sense is: All the kingdoms under us are safe and pacified, and are not oppressed by any distress.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.12":["From this answer the angel who interceded for Israel, taking the occasion to pray for the people, also says to the Lord: Since the whole world is quiet and at peace, why, O Lord, will you not have mercy on Jerusalem and on the cities of Judah, especially since, according to your promises, the time of the captivity has been fulfilled?\n— Commentary on Zechariah","And as to the plea, full of mercy, that, with the seventieth year at hand, God should have mercy on Jerusalem and on the cities of Judah, they refer it to this understanding: that until true peace comes, and the sabbath rest, and the number of seven decades, that is, the seventieth year, is fulfilled, neither does Jerusalem, which is interpreted vision of peace and is the Church, receive full liberty, nor do the cities of Judah, the souls that confess the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.13":["And the Lord answered the angel who was speaking in the prophet good words, words of consolation: good concerning the promise of things to come, consoling concerning the need of things present. These things we have given according to the letter, lest we should seem to hide from our own people what we have learned from teachers of a foreign tongue.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","In short, the Lord answered good words and words of consolation, which now we see in part and prophesy in part. But when that which is perfect has come, may the things that are in part be done away, and, set free from the Babylon of this world, may we cry out: When the Lord turned back the captivity of Zion, we became as those consoled.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.14-15":["Jerusalem and Zion, the vision of peace and the watchtower, which looks not on the wars of this world, nor on things lowly and earthly, but on peace and concord and the heights of heaven, can be understood as the Church. Angry with her because of vices and sins and charity growing colder every day, the Lord will hand her over to persecutions, so that, as in a smelting furnace, pure gold and silver may appear. But the adversaries to whom she has been handed over, striving to blot her out, fill Jerusalem with the blood of the slain from gate to gate.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.14":["And the angel who spoke in me said to me: Cry out, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: I am jealous for Jerusalem and Zion with a great jealousy, and with great anger I am angry with the wealthy nations; for I was angry a little, but they helped toward evil. Therefore thus says the Lord: I will return to Jerusalem in mercies, and my house shall be built in it, says the Lord of hosts; and the plumb line shall be stretched out over Jerusalem. LXX: And the angel who spoke in me said to me: Cry out, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: I am jealous for Jerusalem and Zion with a great jealousy, and with great anger I am angry with the nations that have been set on top, because I indeed was angry a little, but they have set themselves on top unto evils. Therefore thus says the Lord: I will turn to Jerusalem in mercy, and my house shall be restored in it, says the Lord Almighty; and the measure shall be stretched out over Jerusalem. These are still good words, words of consolation, which the prophet is now commanded to cry out: that the Lord has been jealous for Jerusalem and Zion with a great jealousy. But he who is jealous shows that he loves her for whom he is jealous, by no means saying through Jeremiah: As a woman despises her husband, so the house of Israel has despised me. To her the Lord used to speak through Ezekiel: Being angry, I will no longer be angry with you, and my jealousy has departed from you.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.15":["He, then, who was jealous for Jerusalem and Zion, which are, moreover, one and the same city, is now angry with the wealthy nations, which he named above as the red, white and speckled horses: because he himself handed them over to be chastened, but they raged against those handed over; he wished them corrected as a son by a tutor, they tried to kill and punish them as an enemy. Like this is that passage in Isaiah: I gave them into your hands, but you showed them no mercy. On the old man you made the yoke very heavy, and you said: I shall be mistress forever. Now jealousy is taken ἀνθρωποπάθως (in human fashion), in the same way as anger. By this passage the heretics who disparage the Old Testament are refuted, since God, when he is angry, desires not to destroy those with whom he is angry, but to amend them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.16":["Because, therefore, he says, I am jealous for Jerusalem and Zion with a great jealousy, which the adversaries have pressed down more than I wished, therefore thus says the Lord: I will return to Jerusalem not in one mercy but in many mercies, and my house, that is, the temple, shall be built in it under Zerubbabel and Joshua son of Jehozadak; and the plumb line of the masons shall be stretched out over Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Hence the Lord, promising peace and mercy, says that he will build her again, and that the plumb line, or the cord, is to be stretched out in her according to the measures and ranks of each one. What the cord is, is shown in the following passage, where the prophet says: I lifted up my eyes and saw: and behold, a man, and in his hand a measurers' cord. And the temple of the Lord which the adversaries destroyed can also be understood as the venerable body of the Lord, of which he himself said: Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up. Destroyed in the passion, it was raised in the resurrection, and it was the seedbed and the beginning of all temples, of which the Apostle too speaks: You are the temple of God, and the Spirit of God dwells in you.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.17":["Cry out yet, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Yet my cities shall overflow with good things; and the Lord shall yet comfort Zion, and shall yet choose Jerusalem. LXX: And the angel who spoke in me said to me: Cry out yet, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Yet the cities shall be poured out in good things, and the Lord shall yet have mercy on Zion, and shall yet choose Jerusalem. The angel who had said to the prophet above: Cry out; thus says the Lord of hosts, now too urges him to cry out, not with the straining of the voice but of the mind; and this is what he commands him to cry out: Yet my cities shall overflow with good things; those which you now see laid waste by the Babylonian fire shall again abound in all things, and with present good things the Lord shall console their past misery, and shall choose Jerusalem, which a little before he had cast off. But if we refer it to the Church, to which he promises true and everlasting good things, those good things must be believed to be the ones of which we read it written: May you see the good things of Jerusalem, and in another place: If you are willing and listen to me, you shall eat the good things of the land, and that saying: You shall trust in the Lord, and he shall lift you up above the good things of the earth. These a wise man, to whom the Lord had revealed the uncertain and hidden things of his wisdom, promises himself in the hope of things to come, and says: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living. With these good things, now that peace has been restored after the fires of the most savage persecution which the churches of the Lord endured both from the Gentiles and from the Arian heretics, we see the churches of the Lord overflow, and Zion consoled, and Jerusalem chosen, which he had formerly cast away. This very thing we can also understand both of the temple of the Lord and of each one of the believers. Some refer the consoled Zion and the chosen Jerusalem, and the other things that are proclaimed in this manner by all the prophets, to the heavenly Jerusalem, which, destroyed through a fall, is to be built up through virtues. All of which we shall more rightly interpret of the Church.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.18-19":["These four kingdoms which overthrew Judea Zechariah writes that he saw in four horns, the angel saying to him: These are the horns which have scattered Judah and Israel and Jerusalem. And again: I lifted up, he says, my eyes and saw; and behold, four chariots coming out from between two mountains, and the mountains were mountains of bronze; in the first chariot, red horses; and in the second chariot, black horses; and in the third chariot, white horses; and in the fourth chariot, dappled, strong horses. And when the prophet had said to the angel who spoke in him: What are these, my lord? The angel answered: These are the four winds of heaven, which go forth to stand before the ruler of all the earth; and the sense is: These are they who go forth from the face of the Lord, to fulfill his will.\n— Commentary on Joel","And I lifted up my eyes and saw. And behold, four horns; and I said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these? And he said to me: These are the horns that have winnowed Judah, and Israel and Jerusalem. And the Lord showed me four smiths, and I said: What are these coming to do? He spoke, saying: These are the horns that have winnowed Judah man by man, and none of them lifted up his head, and these came to terrify them, to cast down the horns of the nations which have lifted up the horn over the land of Judah, to scatter it. LXX: And I lifted up my eyes and saw. And behold, four horns, and I said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these, Lord? And he said to me: These are the horns that have scattered Judah, and Israel, and Jerusalem. And the Lord showed me four smiths, and I said: What are these coming to do? And he said: These are the horns that have scattered Judah, and have broken Israel, and none of them lifted up his head, and these have gone out to sharpen them in their hands. The four horns are nations that have lifted up the horn over the land of the Lord, to scatter it. That the four horns which winnowed and scattered Judah, and Israel, and Jerusalem are four nations, the Babylonians, the Medes and Persians, and the Macedonians and the Romans, both the Lord now explains when questioned by the prophet, and Daniel unfolds most fully, who in the vision of the image that had a golden head, and a silver breast, and a bronze belly, and legs and feet of iron and of clay, interpreted these four nations. And again in another vision, by four beasts, a lioness and a bear, and a leopard, and another horrible beast whose name he did not set down, he showed the same nations under another figure. That the Medes and Persians were one kingdom after the victory of Cyrus no one will doubt who has read both secular and sacred literature. And most rightly he has put horns for kingdoms, since holy Scripture has this custom, that it always signifies a kingdom by horns, as in that verse: And he exalted the horn of his Christ. And in another place: And he has raised up a horn of salvation for us in the house of David his servant. And the same prophet Daniel testifies to ten horns that are to reign at the last. At the time, then, when this vision was seen, the kingdom of the Babylonians had already passed away, that of the Persians and Medes was at hand, and that of the Greeks and Macedonians, and of the Romans, was yet to come. What the Babylonians, what the Medes and Persians, what the Greeks, that is, the Macedonians, did to Judah and Israel and Jerusalem, a learned man knows, above all under Antiochus, surnamed Ἐπιφανεῖ (Epiphanes), under whom the history of the Maccabees is woven. After the coming of the Lord and Savior as well, when Jerusalem was besieged, what the Israelites suffered both the Gospel foretells and Josephus, the native writer of the Jewish history, teaches most fully.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But that the prophet says he lifted up his eyes is to be fitted to the spiritual sense: I lifted up my eyes to the mountains, from where my help shall come, and: Lift up your eyes, and see the regions, for they are already white for harvest, and what we read in Isaiah: The Holy One said: Lift up your eyes, and see who has shown all these things. There is need, therefore, of the lifting up of the eyes and of spiritual understanding, that we may see the opposing powers which have lifted up their horn against us, and of which the Apostle speaks: Our wrestling is not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of this world, of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. These four adversaries, who were always fighting against the saints, Christ crushed at his coming. And of these the psalmist says over again: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon. We can also take the four horns, which reigned against the people of God, as the four πάθη (passions), which learned men, not rendering them word for word κακοζήλως (with misplaced zeal) as passions, translate as perturbations: distress and gladness of mind, two present, and two future, fear and desire, of which the illustrious poet also gives indication:\n\nHence they fear and desire, grieve and rejoice.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.1.20-21":["These horns winnowed and scattered Judah almost man by man, so that none of them, pressed down by the heavy weight of evils, lifted up his head. The four smiths too, or craftsmen, whom the Greeks call τέκτονας, the prophet does not behold by himself, but the Lord shows them to him, and explains who the smiths and craftsmen are, whom we understand as angels obedient to the Lord's power, so that what the nations had destroyed, these may build. Now this, for which we translated from the Hebrew: These came to terrify them, namely the kingdoms, the Seventy translated: These came to sharpen them, in their hands. But sharpening them is to be taken for terrifying them. Or else they terrify, so that all may submit their necks under the yoke of Christ, and not, as someone has interpreted: when they have been sharpened, let them become worse. For if the angels come for this, to make the wicked worse, they are not to be called smiths, that is, destroyers of evils and builders of good things; but they too are to be taken as wicked and as destroyers. And this is to be noted, that these four horns of the nations lifted up one horn against the people of God; for they did not reign together and oppress Israel at the same time, but succeeded one another: the Medes and Persians the Babylonians, the kingdom of the Macedonians the kingdom of the Persians, the empire of the Romans the kingdom of the Macedonians. These things we have said following the order of the history.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Against these, four smiths and, so to speak, physicians and good craftsmen are not seen by the prophet, for he could not yet see them by himself without God's showing them, but they are shown to him by the Lord: namely the four virtues, prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance, of which Tully disputes most fully in the books On Duties, writing also a book of its own on the four virtues. Those, then, who destroy vices and build virtues, and all the saints who, possessing these remedies, are ever restoring the Church, are to be called smiths. Hence the Apostle too said: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation. And the Lord in his anger says that he will take away from Jerusalem the architect and the wise man. And the Lord himself, the Son of God Almighty and of the Creator of all things, is called the son of a smith. We have read in someone's commentaries that the four smiths are understood as the four evangelists, who restore to its former seat the oppressed Israel, that is, the mind that sees God, and Judah, who confesses the Lord, and Jerusalem, the vision of peace; and those whom the nations of the vices had scattered from the Church, they have drawn back to salvation through the preaching of the gospel.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.1-2":["And I lifted up my eyes, and saw; and behold, a man, and in his hand a measurers' cord. And I said: Where are you going? And he said to me: To measure Jerusalem, and to see how great is its breadth, and how great its length. LXX: And I lifted up my eyes, and saw; and behold, a man, and in his hand a geometer's cord. And I said to him: Where are you going? And he said to me: To measure out Jerusalem, and to see how great is its breadth, and how great its length. He who before, with eyes lifted up, had seen sad things in the four horns, now again lifts up his eyes to see the man of whom it is written: Behold a man, the Rising is his name, of whom we also read above: And behold a man mounting upon a red horse, and he was standing among the myrtles that were in the deep, or, among the shady mountains. And of whom the Father speaks: He has built my city, whose craftsman and founder is God. He is also seen by Ezekiel, holding a cord in his hand, and a reed for the measure of a man's cubit, to measure Jerusalem, whose description, that is, its breadth and length, the same prophet pursues in mystical speech. Some think this was fulfilled in part under Zerubbabel, and Joshua, and Ezra, and Nehemiah and the other leaders or kings who ruled the people of Israel up to the coming of the Lord and Savior. But others, and above all the Jews, interpret it of the kingdom of a thousand years, bodily men seeking bodily things. Most of our writers, however, refer it to the heavenly Jerusalem, and say that it is to be built up again, having been left desolate by the fall of sinful souls. Let heretics suspect this. But let us interpret Mount Zion, and the mother of the firstborn and of the saints, as the Church, which was built in the passion and resurrection of the Lord, and is built every day by him who knows the measures and the merits of each one. What we have said of the Church can also be taken of the souls of believers, which are built every day by the Lord, and behold his peace, which was left to the apostles.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.3-5":["And behold, the angel who spoke in me was going out, and another angel was going out to meet him, and he said to him: Run, speak to this boy, saying: Jerusalem shall be inhabited without a wall, because of the multitude of men and cattle in its midst. And I will be to it, says the Lord, a wall of fire round about, and I will be in glory in its midst. LXX: And behold, the angel who spoke in me was standing, and another angel was going out to meet him, and he said to him, saying: Run and speak to that youth, saying: Jerusalem shall be inhabited fruitful from the multitude of men and cattle in its midst. And I will be to it, says the Lord, a wall of fire round about, and I will be for glory in its midst. The angel who spoke in the prophet, and was standing, or, as is read in the Hebrew, was going out, for that is what iasa means, the Hebrews think to be Michael. And the other angel, who was going out and coming to meet him, they suppose to be Gabriel, who speaks to Michael so that he may tell the prophet how great an abundance of all things, and multitude of men and cattle, and strength of walls Jerusalem shall have, which at present seems destroyed down to embers and ashes.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.4-5":["But we, according to the spiritual sense, interpret all these things of the Church, which is inhabited without a wall, or, as the Seventy translated, κατάκαρπος, that is, with an abundance of all fruits, and has a multitude of men and cattle, and the Lord is fire round about her, and he himself dwells in glory in her midst. This is the city of which we read elsewhere: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, and again: Great is the Lord and exceedingly to be praised, in the city of our God, in his holy mountain. Whoever dwells in her can say: But I am like a fruitful olive tree in the house of God, and: The Lord rules me, and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me. By the water of refreshment he has brought me up.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","All these things the Jews think will come carnally under the ἠλειμμένῳ (anointed one), whom they promise themselves with the vainest hope: that Jerusalem will have such great blessedness that, because of the multitude of men and of all cattle, it cannot have a wall; but the defense of the Lord himself will be its wall, and it will enjoy the glory of him who dwells in its midst.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.4":["And Jerusalem, he says, shall be inhabited without a wall because of the multitude of men and cattle, or, with an abundance of all fruits. Some interpret men and cattle as two peoples, of the Jews and of the Gentiles: that those who were versed in the law and came to the faith of Christ are called men, while we, who after idolatry were as it were in the desert of the law and the solitude of the prophets, and have received his passion, ought to be called cattle. But others want all rational men, learned in the knowledge of the Scriptures, to be understood as men, and as cattle all the simple among the believers, and that of these it is said: Men and cattle you will save, O Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.5":["These cattle and these living creatures hear the voice of the good shepherd, and know it, and follow him, because he is both the shepherd, who also rules us, and the door, because through him we enter into the Church and to the Father, and a wall of fire round about, so that he may warm those who believe and dwell in the midst, who before had grown lukewarm from the coldness of charity, and that they may be fervent in spirit. But the wolves and the most savage beasts — of which it is written: Do not hand over to the beasts the soul that confesses you, and whose fruit is hay, wood, stubble, and they bring forth thistles and thorns —, let him consume with his fire; and he who is fire to the adversaries, let him be glory in the midst of the believers. That the Lord is the wall of Jerusalem we read in another place too: Mountains round about it, and the Lord round about his people.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.6-7":["O, O, flee from the land of the north, says the Lord; for I have scattered you into the four winds of heaven, says the Lord. O Zion, arise, you who dwell with the daughter of Babylon. For thus says the Lord of hosts: After glory he has sent me to the nations that despoiled you; for he who touches you touches the pupil of his eye. For behold, I lift up my hand over them, and they shall be spoil for those who served them, and you shall know that the Lord of hosts has sent me. LXX: O, O, flee from the land of the north, says the Lord, for from the four winds of heaven I will gather you, says the Lord. In Zion be saved, you who inhabit the daughter of Babylon. For thus says the Lord Almighty: After glory he has sent me to the nations that despoiled you, for he who touches you, as it were touches the pupil of his eye. Behold, I will bring my hand upon them, and they shall be spoil for those who had served them, and you shall know that the Lord Almighty has sent me. The regions of the world, which the Greeks call κλίματα, are understood according to the situation of Jerusalem and of the temple. The Assyrians, therefore, and the Babylonians, who laid waste the people of God, dwell in the land of the north. In short, in Jeremiah the pot which according to Ezekiel is full of flesh — and by it the city of Jerusalem is understood —, is kindled from the face of the north. Since, therefore, the angel who had come to meet the angel who prophesied in Zechariah had commanded him to speak to the boy — for in comparison with the dignity of angels all human nature is called boyhood, because it is not angels who advance into us, but we who advance into angels —, and to say to him: Jerusalem shall be inhabited without a wall, and the rest. Now the same word of the Lord is addressed to those who dwell in the north, that they may be gathered from the four winds into which they had been scattered over the whole world, and that those who dwelt in Babylon may return to Zion. Or else thus: You who have been scattered into the four regions of heaven, flee from the land of the north; and O Zion, you who now dwell in Babylon, flee and return to your former seat. But that O is said a third time, in the vocative case, is an exhortation to flight, so that they may know that they have been warned to flee not once, but more often. It must also be said another way: the north wind is a very harsh wind, which makes very hard the hearts of those who dwell in its land, and from which evils are kindled upon all who dwell on the earth, and toward which, according to this very prophet Zechariah, the black horses go out, to take their stand in its region. He too who in Isaiah had said in his boasting: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, I will sit on a lofty mountain, above the high mountains in the north, vaunts that he holds his kingdom in the coldest place of the earth. We read also in another place: Behold, smoke comes from the north; and of those who had departed far from the holy city it is said: Behold, these come from afar, from the north and the sea. And through Jeremiah the Holy Spirit speaks, calling those who are in captivity to return to Jerusalem: Go and read these my words toward the north, and you shall say: Return to me, dwelling of Israel, says the Lord. It is commanded, therefore, to us who have dwelt in the north and have lost the warmth of the Lord's fervor, who have been scattered over the world, and of whom the Gospel proclaims that the Lord sends his angels into the four winds and gathers us, and who are caught up in the vices and confusion of this world, that we flee to Zion, the Church of the Lord, and, leaving lowly things behind, take our stand on her watchtower and on the sublimity of her doctrines.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.8-9":["He was sent, therefore, after the glory of the divine majesty, to the nations that despoiled the people of God, so that those who had formerly plundered them might be spoil for those who served them, and the whole crowd of those who once served might know that the Lord Almighty has sent him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.8":["But what follows: Thus says the Lord of hosts, after glory he has sent me, and the rest, introduces the voice of the Savior speaking, who, being almighty God, says that he was sent by the almighty Father, not according to that by which he is almighty, but according to that by which he was sent after glory. Who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, and became obedient to the Father unto death, the death of the cross. Nor is it a wonder that Christ is called almighty, in whose person we read in the Apocalypse of John: Thus says the faithful witness, the beginning of the creation of God, who is, and who was, and who is to come, the Lord God Almighty. That too which we read in Psalm 23 (24): Lift up your gates, O princes, and be lifted up, eternal gates, and the king of glory shall enter, and which again is said by other angels who did not know the mystery of the flesh assumed: Who is this king of glory? The Lord of powers, he is the king of glory, is referred to Christ. For where we read the Lord of powers, in the Hebrew Sabaoth is written, which is rendered almighty by the LXX translators. From this we understand that wherever the Lord of powers is said of Christ, he must be understood as almighty. Nor is it a wonder if Christ is called almighty: To whom has been given all power in heaven and on earth, and who says: All things that are the Father's are mine. But if all things, that is, God from God, Lord from Lord, light from light, then also almighty from almighty; for it cannot be that those whose nature is one should have a different glory.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But as for what he says: He who touches you touches the pupil of his eye, take the touching as harassment and injury, according to what we read: Do not touch my anointed ones, and do no evil to my prophets. For whoever touches the saints of the Lord is as one who desires to harm the pupil of his eye, and strives to deprive him of the clear light, of which he speaks in the Gospel: You are the light of the world.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.9":["And God lifts up his hand to strike the hostile nations, and to lead his people back either into Jerusalem or into the Church. By the hostile nations, according to tropology, understand the opposing powers, which every day subject sinners to their power and compel them to serve them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.10-11":["But who this people is that is comforted through the Apostles and churchmen, not Israel and Jacob and Judah, as Scripture records in other places, but the people of God, the prophet Zechariah testifies, saying: Rejoice and be glad, daughter of Zion: for I will come and dwell in the midst of you, says the Lord. And many nations shall flee to the Lord in that day, and shall be his people, and they shall dwell in the midst of you, and they shall know that the Lord almighty has sent me to you (Zech 2:10, 11). By this testimony it is clearly shown that very many nations are to be turned into the people of God. And these things the Lord says, sent by the Lord whose name is Almighty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ZEC.2.10-12":["Praise and rejoice, daughter of Zion, for behold, I come, and I will dwell in the midst of you, says the Lord. And many nations shall be joined to the Lord on that day, and they shall be my people, and I will dwell in the midst of you, and you shall know that the Lord of hosts has sent me to you. And the Lord shall possess Judah as his portion in the sanctified land, and he shall yet choose Jerusalem. LXX: Rejoice and be glad, daughter of Zion, for behold, I will come, and I will dwell in the midst of you, says the Lord. And many nations shall flee for refuge to the Lord on that day, and they shall be his people, and they shall dwell in the midst of you, and you shall know that the Lord Almighty has sent me to you, and the Lord shall possess Judah as his portion in the holy land, and he shall yet choose Jerusalem. These words too are to be understood as spoken in the person of the Lord: that he exhorts his people, restored from captivity to its former seat, to be glad and rejoice because the Lord himself is coming and will dwell in its midst; and that many nations are to believe in him, of whom it is said: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations as your inheritance, and the ends of the earth as your possession; and that he dwells in its midst, saying to the disciples: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age; and that the Lord possesses Judah as his portion, Judah that confesses and believes in his name, and possesses it nowhere else than in the holy land, which is interpreted as the Church; and that he yet chooses Jerusalem, which he had abandoned to temptation in the persecutions.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.10":["But rightly, after the captivity, is the daughter of Zion called forth to joy, of whom it is also said in the psalm: When the Lord turns back the captivity of his people, Jacob shall exult, and Israel shall be glad.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.11-12":["Some of the Jews think that these things were in part fulfilled under Zerubbabel and Joshua, Ezra and Nehemiah, especially since Jerusalem is chosen and Judah is possessed; that is to say, the two tribes which returned from the Babylonian captivity and were called Judah, and not Israel, which lives among the Medes to this day. Others, however, defer them to the future: that then the nations are to believe in him who is sent by the Lord, and Jerusalem is to be chosen; although surely all the nations have already believed in the Lord Savior, and a city that has been utterly destroyed cannot be chosen.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.2.13":["Let all flesh be silent before the face of the Lord, for he has risen up from his holy dwelling. LXX: Let all flesh fear before the face of the Lord, for he has risen from his holy clouds. Since these things are so, and the Lord is going to possess Judah as his portion and to choose Jerusalem, let the whole race of men dread the coming of the Lord, for he has risen up from his holy dwelling. Now the Lord is said to rise, and as it were to awaken from sleep, when he rises up to avenge his people, according to what is written: Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord? Or else, according to the LXX, who said: He has risen from his holy clouds, understand the patriarchs, prophets and apostles, whom the Lord commanded not to rain rain upon Israel. These are the clouds of which it is written: And let the clouds sprinkle justice; for these bodily clouds, into which the denser air is gathered, cannot rain justice. In these clouds and with these clouds the Lord says in the Gospel that he will come. And in Isaiah we read that the Lord came into Egypt on a light cloud. Egypt is taken as the world, into which the Lord is recorded to have descended in the flesh, which, brought forth from a virginal womb, was weighed down by no weight of human seed and of sins. But that all flesh is to be taken not absolutely, of men and of beasts and of birds and of fish, but specifically of men, is shown by that scripture: Hear my prayer; to you all flesh shall come. For it is not all the flesh of irrational living creatures that is to come to the Lord, but that flesh which is to receive the gift of the Holy Spirit, and of which it is said: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.1":["And he showed me Joshua the high priest standing before the angel of the Lord. And Satan was standing at his right hand, to oppose him. And the Lord said to Satan: The Lord rebuke you, Satan, and the Lord rebuke you, he who has chosen Jerusalem. Is not this a brand plucked out of the fire? And Joshua was clothed in filthy garments, and was standing before the face of the angel, who answered and said to those who were standing before him, saying: Take away the filthy garments from him. And he said to him: Behold, I have taken away your iniquity from you, and I have clothed you with changes of raiment. And he said: Put a clean tiara upon his head; and they put a clean tiara upon his head, and they clothed him with garments. LXX: And the Lord showed me Joshua the high priest, standing before the face of the angel of the Lord, and the devil was standing at his right hand, to oppose him. And the Lord said to the devil: The Lord rebuke you, devil, and the Lord who has chosen Jerusalem rebuke you. Is not this like a brand plucked out of the fire? And Joshua was clothed in filthy garments, and was standing before the face of the angel, and he answered and said to those standing before his face, saying: Take away the filthy garments from him. And he said to him: Behold, I have taken away your iniquities from you; and clothe him with a robe reaching to the feet, and set a clean tiara upon his head; and they set a clean tiara upon his head, and they put garments about him. Before we come to the spiritual understanding, we must say succinctly and briefly how the Hebrews explain this passage. Joshua (Iesus, the same name as Jesus) the high priest, whom the Greeks call ἀρχιερέα, the Latins pontiff, they take to be the son of Jehozadak, who presided over the people together with Zerubbabel. At his right hand the adversary was standing (for this is what satan means), to oppose him. And rightly was he standing at his right hand, not at his left, because the accusation was true, since he too, along with the rest, had taken a foreign wife, as is written most fully in Ezra and in Malachi, who follows this prophet.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Our writers, however, explain it thus: that he is the high priest to whom it is said: You are a priest forever, according to the order of Melchizedek. Since he cannot be seen of himself, he is shown to the prophet by the Lord, standing before the angel of the Lord, whom they take to be the angel of great counsel: not that he is one and another, so that we should admit two persons in the Son, but that he is one and the same, and is both shown as a man in soiled clothing and said to appear as an angel, the mediator of men and God. And that he is not Joshua son of Jehozadak they try to show from this, that the words son of Jehozadak are not added in the present place, whereas in other places, where it is truly Joshua son of Jehozadak who is spoken of, he is always registered by his father's name. So Jesus is seen standing, set firm with a steady step: And Satan standing at his right hand, to oppose him. For he was tempted in every way, without sin. And in the Gospel the tempter approaches him, seeking always to oppose his right-hand works and his virtues.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.2":["But perhaps an inquisitive reader will object this too: how we can assert that God stands at the right of the just man, and the devil at the left of the sinner, when concerning our Lord it is read in Zechariah, \"And the devil was standing at his right hand to oppose him.\" Let him, then, who objects this read also what follows: that when the devil was standing at his right hand, Jesus had filthy garments. Rightly did he who had taken up our sins and was bearing our offenses have the devil at his right hand, not so much for himself as for the filthy garments. And at the same time let him consider that it is not said, \"The devil was standing as victor and triumphing,\" but as still set in the contest and in the battle-line and in doubt—indeed, when he had not yet begun to fight. For it follows, \"That he might oppose him.\" But the devil was opposing him, as we said, because he had filthy garments from our sins. But after he heard, \"May the Lord who chose Jerusalem rebuke you,\" he did not stand at the right, but went behind his back and under his feet, and at once Jesus the great priest receives the ankle-length robe (poderis), and is clothed in white vesture, and, royal dignity being joined to him—that is, Zerubbabel being coupled with him—builds the Church, and constructs the spiritual Jerusalem.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS, ALTERNATE SERIES 61","And the Lord said to Satan, to the accuser and his adversary, for he is the enemy and avenger, the accuser of his brothers: The Lord rebuke you, Satan, as the Lord rained from the Lord, and the Lord rebuke you, he who has chosen Jerusalem. Since, then, out of all the cities of Judah Jerusalem has now been chosen, the Lord in no way imputing to her the sins she committed, why do you try to overwhelm Joshua like a brand, which is commonly called a firebrand, Joshua who escaped as it were half-burnt from the Babylonian captivity?\n— Commentary on Zechariah","As for what follows: The Lord rebuke you, Satan, and the Lord rebuke you, he who has chosen Jerusalem, they explain it thus: that the Father and the Son is Lord, and in Psalm 109 (110) we read: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit at my right hand; he speaks of the other Lord, not because the Lord himself who speaks cannot rebuke, but because, from the unity of their nature, when the other has rebuked, he himself who speaks rebukes: For he who sees the Son sees the Father, and he it is who has chosen Jerusalem, the Church, which contemplates the peace of the Lord. Now the brand plucked out of the fire can most rightly be understood as him who, though he was in Babylon, was not consumed by the Babylonian fire, nor touched by the flame of this world. Hence Moses too, in the wilderness, sees a great vision, in which the bush was burning and was not consumed.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.3-4":["As for what follows: Joshua was clothed in filthy garments, they interpret it in three ways: either on account of the unlawful marriage, or on account of the sins of the people, or because of the squalor of the captivity. Now the angel before whose face Joshua was standing commanded the other angels, in the person of the Lord, to take away from him the filthy garments of which we spoke above. When they had fulfilled the command in deed, the same angel speaks again to Joshua: Behold, I have taken away your iniquity from you; these are the filthy garments; and I have clothed you with changes of raiment; that is, I have joined an Israelite wife to you; for which the Seventy translated ποδήρη, which we can call an ankle-length tunic, because it flows down to the ankles and feet.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.3-5":["This Jesus was clothed in filthy garments, who, though he had committed no sin, was made sin for us. And he bears our infirmities, and grieves for us, and we reckoned him to be in sorrow, and under a blow, and in anguish. But he was wounded for our iniquities, and was made weak for our sins. And in the apostle Paul we read: Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us. It is he who speaks in Psalm 21 (22): Far from my salvation are the words of my offences. And in Psalm 68 (69): O God, you know my foolishness, and my offences are not hidden from you. All these are called filthy garments, and they will be taken away from him when he has blotted out our sins, so that, because he was clothed in filthy garments, we, rising again, may hear in him after baptism: Let your garments be always white; and the whole Church of believers hears through Isaiah: Wash yourselves, be clean. And of her it is prophesied in the Song of Songs: Who is this who comes up made white? Ποδήρην, moreover, take to be his incarnation, which is from the earth and is signified in the feet. By the clean tiara upon his head understand the splendor of the divine majesty, so that one and the same may be seen to be adorned, according to the man, with the ποδήρη, and according to God, with the tiara.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.3":["There are many things that might be said. But to sinners the devil stands at the right hand. Yet that discussion is another matter. Whoever is a sinner, the devil stands at his right hand. But someone says: How is it that in Zechariah it is said to stand also to Jesus the son of Josedech? \"And the devil was standing at his right hand.\" How is it written in Zechariah that the devil stands at the right hand of the Savior? You see that there is a question here. But at that time when the devil was standing at the right hand of the Savior, \"Jesus,\" it says, \"had filthy garments.\" He was bearing our sins, he was wrapped in our vices: therefore the devil was standing at his right hand.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 36"],"ZEC.3.5":["And as for what follows: Put a clean tiara upon his head, for tiara we read in the Hebrew Saniph, which most people call a mitre; and by this they want the dignity of the priesthood to be understood, because, once the filth of his sins had been taken away, he held a clean priesthood. This is what the Jews say.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.6-7":["And the angel of the Lord was standing.\n\nAnd the angel of the Lord solemnly charged Joshua, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: If you walk in my ways and keep my charge, you also shall judge my house, and shall keep my courts, and I will give you those who walk, from among these who now stand by here. LXX: And the angel of the Lord was standing. And the angel of the Lord solemnly charged Joshua, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: If you walk in my ways and keep my precepts, you too shall judge my house; and if you keep my court, I will give you those who shall move about in the midst of these who stand here. The Hebrews, following the course of the interpretation they have begun, understand these things to have been said by the angel of the Lord to Joshua (Iesus, the same name as Jesus) son of Jehozadak: that after the removal of the filthy garments and the restoration of the dignity of a clean priesthood, he is given a command according to what is written in the Gospel: Behold, you have been made well; sin no more, lest something worse befall you; and a reward is promised him, if he walks in the ways of the Lord and keeps his precepts: that he himself should be the judge of his house, that is, should continue as high priest in the temple, and should keep his courts and porches; and that the Lord should give him some from the number of the angels who at that time stood in his sight, so that he might be walled about by their help and be secure from every treachery of his enemies. According to our writers, who refer all these things to the Lord Savior, this seems to be difficult: that it is said to Jesus by the angel that if he walks in the ways of the Lord and keeps his precepts, he too shall judge his house and keep his courts, and the Lord shall give him those who walk, from among those who stand by him. This is easily resolved if we consider him who deigned to take the form of a servant. And though he was rich, he became poor for us. Whatever, then, is said of the members is referred to the body; our progress is the Lord's victory. And when we have come to the perfect man, to the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ, he will judge the house of God, according to what the Apostle said: But Christ as a son over his house, whose house we are, and to Timothy: If I delay, that you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. And not only will he judge his house, but he will also keep the courts of the Lord, of which it is written: Worship the Lord in his holy court. And he will judge, or discern, his house, and according to the merits of each, giving to one ten cities and to another five, and appointing some in the Church as prophets, some as apostles, some as teachers, some as workers of signs, holding some as eyes, some as hands, some as feet, according to what we read: God has stood in the assembly of the gods, and in the midst he discerns the gods. He also gave him ministers from the number of the angels, who, though set in the flesh, are like the angels, and of whom the Apostle spoke: Our citizenship is in the heavens. For if the angels neither marry nor are given in marriage, and those who persevere in virginal continence are like the angels, why should we not think that the apostles and all the saints, being of angelic dignity, were given to Jesus, to stand by him in the Church and never to have wavering feet, but to stand with the Lord who stands?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.8-9":["Hear, O Joshua, high priest, you and your friends who dwell before you, for they are men who portend. For behold, I will bring my servant the Rising, for behold the stone which I have given before Joshua: upon one stone are seven eyes. LXX: Hear then, O Joshua, high priest, and your neighbours who sit before your face, for the men τερατοσκόποι, that is, of portents, are beholders, for behold, I will bring my servant the Rising, for the stone which I have given before the face of Joshua, upon one stone are seven eyes. Our writers are hard pressed in this place by the Jews, on the ground that, following the sequence and texture of the discourse, they ought rather to understand Joshua (Iesus, the same name as Jesus) the priest, son of Jehozadak, than the Lord Savior. For if the word is addressed to the Lord, and to Christ it is said: Hear, O Joshua, high priest, who is the one of whom it is added: Behold, I will bring my servant the Rising, who by another name is called the stone, and has been given before Joshua, and upon this stone are seven eyes? Our writers, on the contrary, strive to maintain that Christ is called, according to different understandings, both Jesus the high priest, and the Rising, and the stone. But how they could explain that this is said of him, to him, as though of another, is most difficult. Those, then, who want the high priest to be Joshua son of Jehozadak interpret his friends who dwell, or sit, before him, and who are men who portend, as his disciples and the prophets. For the prophets were set as a sign of things to come. What is it, then, that Joshua and his friends are made to hear: I will bring my servant the Rising, and the rest? Because above God had promised Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, that if he walked in his ways and kept his precepts, he himself would judge his house and keep his courts, and that he would give him ministers of angelic dignity, now he tells him and his friends that full happiness and perfect blessedness will come then, when the Rising comes, of whom it is written: Behold a man, the Rising is his name. And in Malachi: For you who fear my name the sun of justice shall rise, and healing in its wings. And in Numbers: A star shall rise out of Jacob, and a man out of Israel. In the Gospel too we read most plainly of Christ: In which the Rising from on high will visit us. To give light to those who sit in darkness and in the shadow of death, to guide our feet into the way of peace. He himself is called the Rising and the cornerstone, because he joins both peoples together and unites two walls into one house. To those who do not believe he is a stone of stumbling, of whom it is also said in the psalms: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner. This was done by the Lord. Upon this stone are seven eyes, of which Isaiah speaks: A rod shall come forth from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from his root, and the spirit of God shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and fortitude, the spirit of knowledge and piety; and the spirit of the fear of God shall fill him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.8":["Those who want the high priest and his friends to be understood as the Lord Savior and his disciples take the men τερατοσκόπους, men who portend, in this way: they refer them to the apostles, who perceived his mystical signs and recognized the future from the present; while in the man who was blind from birth they interpret the restored eyes as concerning the people of the Gentiles, and in the woman with the flow of blood they explain the Church as set free from the works of blood.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.9-10":["As for what is written according to the Seventy: Behold, I will dig a pit, we can understand the ditch or pit in place of the carving of the stone: for everything that is carved is also dug. Yet if someone, more contentiously, will not refer this to the stone, but would have it hold, as it were, its own beginning and its own meaning, let us say that the Lord Almighty, on the day of his Son's passion, or else — as the Jews think, at the final end of the world, when he comes to judge — digs out and brings into the open the works of each person, and handles the whole earth on the day of judgment; for this is the one day, and on that day each of the saints calls his companions and friends, who are themselves also saints, to rest, and rejoices in the virtue of his works.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.9":["As for what follows: For behold the stone which I have given before Joshua, the lovers of the history understand it of Christ in this way, saying that Christ was to come after Joshua son of Jehozadak. For this, they say, is what before Joshua means, that is, in his sight and before his face, so that it signifies things to come; and he is called a stone for his strength and hardness, with which he crushed all kingdoms, the stone which we also read in Daniel was cut from the mountain without hands.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Behold, I will engrave its carving, says the Lord of hosts, and I will take away the iniquity of that land in one day.\n\nOn that day, says the Lord of hosts, a man shall call his friend under the vineyard and under the fig tree. LXX: Behold, I will dig a pit, says the Lord Almighty, and I will handle all the iniquity of that land in one day. On that day, says the Lord Almighty, each one shall call his neighbour under the vine and under the fig tree. Above he had said: Behold the stone which I have given before Joshua, upon one stone are seven eyes. Now, consistently, he keeps the μεταφορὰν (metaphor) taken from the stone, and says: I will engrave its carving, or I will carve its engraving. For where it is written in Hebrew maphate phethee, Aquila translated διαγλύψω ἄνοιγμα αὐτῆς, that is, I will carve its opening; Theodotion and Symmachus, I will carve its carving. And the sense is: I will cause this stone to be wounded by the nails of the cross and the soldier's lance, and in his passion I will take away the iniquity of the land in one day, the day of which it is written: This is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and rejoice in it.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.3.10":["On that day of the passion of Christ, the man who is perfect in Christ, and who with the apostles ascends to the summit of the Lord's goodwill, shall call his neighbour, either the believers from among the Jews or at any rate the people of the Gentiles, under the vineyard which is called Sorek, and of which he says in the Gospel: I am the vine, and whose fruit gladdens the heart of man; and under the fig tree, the sweetest fruits of the Holy Spirit, so that they may rest in eternal peace and, the sedition of the world and the gore of warriors having been suppressed, may know that they are under a king whose name, mystically in Solomon, is that of peace. Of this same sitting under the vineyard and under the fig tree the prophet Micah also makes mention, saying: On that day each one shall call his neighbour and his brother under the fig tree and his vineyard, and there shall be none to terrify them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.1":["And the angel who was speaking in me returned, and roused me like a man who is roused from his sleep. LXX: And the angel who was speaking in me returned, and roused me as a man is wont to be roused from his sleep. For where had he gone, that he should return, especially he who was speaking in the prophet, and who could not be apart from him in whom he was speaking? But whenever human frailty is left to its own weakness, we must believe that God, and the help of his angels, departs from us. So the prophet too, stunned with amazement at the great vision, was numb in his whole mind and could not see the clear light of truth; hence he is roused from the past vision to another vision, as though waking from sleep, so that he may see what with closed eyes he did not see. Let us also say it another way. There is a testimony in the Proverbs of Solomon: If you sit down, you will be without fear; but if you sleep, you will sleep sweetly, and you will not fear the terror coming upon you, nor the onslaughts of the ungodly bearing down on you. Whoever sleeps this sleep, and hears said of himself what we read written of Lazarus: If he sleeps, he will be well, will be able to sing with the bride: I sleep, and my heart keeps watch. Let us see, then, for the contemplation of what things the prophet rises as if from sleep.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.2-3":["Now let us discuss what has been written by churchmen in their commentaries. They understand the golden lampstand of purest gold as the Church, which in the holy Scriptures searches out the sense and the mind rather than the words. And that gold is taken to mean the sense is shown by the back and shoulders of the dove in Psalm 67 (68), which are said to shine with the green or the gleam of gold. The lamp they too understand as Christ himself, because he shines in the Church, he who said of himself: No one lights a lamp and puts it under a bushel, that is, under the measure of the law, but upon a lampstand, that is, the freedom of the Gospel, so that it may give light to all who are in the house. The seven lamps and their pourers they too understand as the graces of the Holy Spirit, through which the Church receives the oil of the mercy of God and of all the virtues. The two olive trees upon the lampstand they too understand as Moses and Elijah, who were talking with the Lord on the mountain and signifying what he was to suffer in Jerusalem. For the whole law and the prophets proclaim the passion of Christ. Others interpret the two olive trees on the right and on the left as the law and the Gospel, so that the Gospel is on the right and the law on the left; of which right and left it is said in the Song of Songs: His left hand is under my head, and his right hand shall embrace me.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.2-6":["And he said to me: What do you see? And I said: I have seen, and behold, a lampstand all of gold, and its lamp upon its head; and its seven lamps upon it, seven and seven pourers for the lamps which were upon its head. And two olive trees upon it, one on the right of the lamp, and one on its left. And I answered, and I say to the angel who was speaking in me, saying: What are these, my lord? And the angel who was speaking in me answered, and said to me: Do you not know what these are? And I said: No, my lord. And he answered, and said to me, saying: This is the word of the Lord to Zerubbabel, saying: Not by an army, nor by strength, but by my spirit, says the Lord of hosts. Who are you, O great mountain, before Zerubbabel? Into a plain! And he shall bring out the chief stone, and shall make equal the grace of its grace. LXX: And he said to me: What do you see? And I said: I have seen, and behold, a lampstand all of gold, and a lamp upon it; and seven lamps upon it, and seven pourers of the lamps which were upon it. And behold, two olive trees upon it, one on the right of the lamp, and one on the left; and I asked, and said to the angel who was speaking in me, saying: What are these, lord? And the angel who was speaking in me answered and said to me: Do you not know what these are? And I said: No, lord. And he answered and said to me, saying: This is the word of the Lord to Zerubbabel, saying: Not by great might, nor by strength, but by my spirit, says the Lord Almighty. Who are you, O great mountain, before the face of Zerubbabel, that you should set right? And I will bring out the stone of inheritance, the equality of grace, its grace. The angel who roused Zechariah, like a man rising from sleep, asks him what he has seen, so that, when he answers and does not know, and inquires what it is that he sees, and says: What are these, my lord?, we may hear from him: This is the word of the Lord to Zerubbabel: Not by an army, nor by strength, but by my spirit, says the Lord. Let us therefore go through the details one by one and say first what the Hebrews think, by whom we have been taught in the Old Testament; then by these, as by steps, let us climb to the heights of the Church. The lampstand of solid gold they interpret as the law, that is, νόμον. The lamp, that is, the flame shining and gleaming on the top of the lampstand, they take as Christ, who is the head of the law and gives light to the whole world. The seven lamps upon the lampstand they take as the seven graces of the Holy Spirit, of which we spoke above, saying that on one stone there are seven eyes. For no one doubts that the law was written at the dictation of the Holy Spirit. And by the seven pourers, in which is the oil that is sent into the lamps that shine upon the lampstand, they would have it understood that these seven graces come down from heaven to men through the law. And the two olive trees upon the lampstand, on the right side and on the left, between which the lamp shines in the middle, they interpret as the law and prophecy. And when the prophet had told his vision, not knowing what he was seeing, he asks the angel who was speaking in him, that is, his mind enlightened by God. For this is our angel, who, understanding wisely, shows us the will of God, and he says: What are these, my lord? But the angel does not explain the vision to the prophet, as he had been asked by him, but questions in turn the one who questions him: Do you not know what these are? And when he answers, I do not know, he himself also answers: This is the word of the Lord to Zerubbabel, saying: Not by an army, nor by strength; but by my spirit, says the Lord. The word of the Lord to Zerubbabel, then, which spoke not by an army, nor by strength, but by my spirit, is itself the interpretation of the vision: that not by an army nor by a multitude of warriors, but by the spirit of God, the people had been brought back and would be brought back more fully, and the adversaries would be laid waste.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.7":["And what follows: Who are you, O great mountain, before Zerubbabel? Into a plain!; they understand it as said to the devil, who stood at the right hand of Joshua to oppose him, and lifted himself up against Zerubbabel and the people of the Jews. But he has been turned into a plain and humbled, and he lies under the feet of Israel, because God shall bring out the chief stone, Christ his Son, who was always a help to the people of Israel. And his grace, that is, the grace of the stone, toward those whom he brought back from captivity, shall make equal the grace which he always exercised toward their fathers. This we have found said by the Hebrews.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But most of our writers interpret the mountain as the devil, and the Antichrist, who dares to stand before Zerubbabel, from whom Christ was to be born, and to raise himself up, and to say in the Gospel: All these things have been delivered to me, and I will give them to you, if you will fall down and worship me. And the Lord, laying him low under his feet, and making him humble instead of proud, and cast down, says: Get behind, Satan; for it is written: You shall worship the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve. And they give as an example in the Gospel the lunatic, whom the apostles had not been able to heal; they ask why they could not heal him, and they hear: Amen I say to you, if you have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain: Pass from here, and it will pass; and nothing will be impossible to you. But this kind is not cast out except by prayer and fasting. There the mountain is plainly taken to mean the devil. But others, with no small rashness, refer what is plainly said of the devil to Christ, who in the holy Scriptures is quite often called a mountain. Nor is there any need to give examples, of which there is a great abundance. They are led into this error because it is written in the Seventy: Who are you, O great mountain, before the face of Zerubbabel, that you should set right? Namely, that this mountain which is before the face of Zerubbabel, that is, which descends from the stock of Zerubbabel, would himself set the world right, and that what follows is about him: I will bring out the stone of inheritance, of which it is written: It is you who will restore my inheritance to me. And in another place: He chose for us his inheritance, the beauty of Jacob whom he loved. And again: Jacob became the portion of the Lord, Israel the cord of his inheritance. And in Psalm 2: The Lord said to me: You are my Son, today I have begotten you. Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance. But God shall bring out the chief stone, of which we read: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God, and: All things were made through him, and without him nothing was made. And what he says: He shall make equal the grace of its grace, signifies this: Of his fullness we have all received, and grace for grace, that is, for the grace of the law, the grace of the Gospel, so that both those who believe from Israel and the people of the heathen may receive an equal grace and a like gift. Hence Gabriel too says to Mary: You have found grace with the Lord. And the apostle Paul writes: For by grace you have been saved; and John the evangelist: The law, he says, was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.9-10":["Many of our writers interpret the hands of Zerubbabel, who founded the house and himself finished it, as Christ. But if we accept this, we shall be compelled to explain who the stone of tin in the hand of Zerubbabel is. For surely another Christ is not to be shown in the hand of Christ? Although some have taken the stone of tin as the body of the Lord, which was soiled by no stains of sins, and is not called lead, but purest tin.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.9":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: The hands of Zerubbabel have founded this house, and his hands shall finish it, and you shall know that the Lord of hosts has sent me to you. For who has despised the small days? And they shall rejoice, and shall see the stone of tin in the hand of Zerubbabel. These seven are the eyes of the Lord, which run to and fro through the whole earth. LXX: And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: The hands of Zerubbabel have founded this house, and his hands shall complete it. And you shall know that the Lord Almighty has sent me to you; for who has despised in the small days? And they shall rejoice, and shall see the stone of tin in the hand of Zerubbabel; these seven are the eyes of the Lord, which look upon all the earth. Much is said both by the Hebrews and by our writers; following most of it and rejecting some, let us set out what we think, keeping the truth of the history, so that from it we may be able to recognize him who is prophesied through the history. The hands of Zerubbabel, the prince of the Jews who returned from Babylon, laid the foundations of the temple; and his hands shall reach to the top of the temple, completing what they had begun, and building up all that had been begun. We read in Ezra that the temple was begun and finished by Zerubbabel. And when, says the prophet Zechariah, you see the roof set by you upon the temple which you are now building, then you will understand that I was sent by the Lord, and that what I have spoken I said at his command: For who has despised the small days?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.10":["Here 'who' is taken for 'rare', according to that saying: Who, do you think, is the faithful and wise steward? and: Lord, who dwells in your tabernacle, and who shall go up into your holy mountain? Rare, then, is the one who despises the small days of this world and thinks royal power worth nothing. When we see the powerful of the world gleaming with gold, glowing with purple and gems, surrounded by an army, let us say within ourselves: Who, do you think, despises the small days? Hence Jacob too, understanding that the life of men is short: Small, he says, and very evil are the days of my life. Those, then, who have despised the small days — and he refers this to royal power, to admonish Zerubbabel and Joshua and the people building the temple of God, by no means to fear the adversaries who were hindering them, but to listen to the Lord exhorting them — those, from the fact that they despise royal grandeur, shall also rejoice and see the help of the Savior, who is promised from the stock of Zerubbabel, and because of his strength is called a stone, and a stone of tin. For this in the Hebrew is written abdil, because he is the wall and the strength and the might of those who believe. For just as tin defends other metals from fire, and though bronze and iron are by nature very hard, yet if it is without tin it is burned and consumed, so all the strength of angels and of men, if the help of the Savior is not there, is proved weak and frail. Now this stone, that is, this mass, which among the Hebrews is written abdil, that is, of tin, ἐτυμολογεῖται (is etymologized as) ἀποχωρίζων, that is, separating and setting apart, so that, as tin through fire parts metals that are mixed and adulterated, so the Lord, the true assayer and χωνευτὴς (refiner), may set apart the bronze and the lead of the vices from the gold and silver of good works, so that pure gold and silver may remain. In different words this ἀποχωρίζων and separator is described in the Gospel: Whose winnowing fan is in his hand, and he will cleanse his threshing floor, and will separate the chaff from the wheat, he who cries through Jeremiah: What has the chaff to do with the wheat? says the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","As for the seven eyes, which run to and fro through the whole earth and judge all things, we explained above that they are the seven spirits; and that nothing is hidden from the Lord, who is aware of things past and present and to come, and renders to each according to his works, above all when he comes in the person of the one who separates the good and the wicked, and of the refiner.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.11-12":["Of the two olive trees which were on the right of the lamp and on the left we have read above. And so the prophet, now asking about them, does not deserve to hear, because he does not retain the former things; or else because what was said there more obscurely he desires to hear here more plainly; or at any rate his obstinacy is refuted by the angel's silence, because he insists that he knows greater things; although the Hebrews maintain that he heard nothing when he asked about the olive trees for this reason, that he did not ask well, and did not inquire into the whole of what he ought to have known. In short, afterwards he inquires more fully, adding the ears or branches of the olive trees, about which he had been silent above. For there he said: What are these two olive trees? Here he asks: What are the two ears of the olive trees? Μεταφορικῶς (metaphorically), because the trees are upright in the manner of ears of grain, and as ears are covered with their beards, so these are covered with a kind of palisade of branches and leaves, and rise up on high.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.4.11-14":["And I answered, and said to him: What are these two olive trees on the right of the lampstand, and on its left? And I answered a second time, and said to him: What are the two ears of the olive trees, which are upon the two golden beaks, in which are the pourers of gold? And he said to me, saying: Do you not know what these are? And I said: No, lord. And he said: These are the two sons of oil, who stand by the Ruler of the whole earth. LXX: And I answered, and said to him: What are these two olive trees on the right of the lampstand, and on the left? And I asked a second time, and said to him: What are the two branches of the olive trees, which are in the hands of the two golden nostrils, which pour in and draw back the golden pourers. And he said to me: Do you not know what these are? And I said: By no means, lord. And he said to me: These are the two sons of fatness, who stand by the Lord of the whole earth. When the prophet asked what the two olive trees signified, one of which stood on the right of the lampstand, the other on the left, the Lord, or the angel of the Lord, would not answer. The prophet, understanding this, inquires a second time, and said: What are the two ears of the olive trees, or the two branches? He asks about lesser things, since he did not deserve to hear the greater. Now these two branches are in the hand of the two nostrils, or upon the two golden beaks, which in Hebrew are called sinthoroth, in Greek μυξωτῆρες; and these two μυξωτῆρες themselves, upon which are the two ears, or the two branches of the olive trees, are of purest gold. And when the prophet asked also about the two branches, and was asked again by the angel whether he knew what the two branches signified, and he had said: No, lord, the angel of the Lord answered: These are the two sons of oil, as Symmachus would have it, or of στιλπνότητος, as Aquila translated, that is, of splendor; or of πιότητος, that is, of fatness, as the Seventy rendered; or of λαμπρότητος, that is, of brightness, as Theodotion translated, who stand by the Ruler of the whole earth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Some of our writers interpret the two olive trees as the Son and the Holy Spirit, and the lamp in the middle as God the Father. But I do not know how, without blasphemy, they can take one as on the right and the other as on the left. The branches, too, or ears of the olive trees, they explain as the incarnation of the Savior and the likeness of a dove of the Holy Spirit, because we cannot see the olive trees whole, but a certain part, and, so to speak, the little branches of the incarnation of Christ and of the manifestation of the Holy Spirit have been shown to us. Others understand the two testaments, the Gospel on the right, the law on the left, because in the one there is the spiritual sense, in the other the bodily; and because we can explain neither the whole Gospel nor the whole law: And now we know in part, and we prophesy in part, and we cannot yet understand what is perfect. There are some who interpret the two branches of the olive trees, or the two ears, and the sons of fatness or of splendor, as the priesthood and the law, which afford joy to the whole earth. Others take them as Enoch and Elijah, of whom one pleased God in uncircumcision, the other in circumcision, and was caught up into heaven with his body. For splendor and oil, and fatness and brightness, we read in the Hebrew Isaar, according to what is written in the psalms of the joy and happiness of the saints: They shall be inebriated with the plenty, or with the fatness of your house: for πιότης means fatness rather than plenty. These things we have spoken as best we could, and as far as the powers of our small talent could bear, touching briefly on the various opinions both of the Hebrews and of our writers; if anyone says something better, or rather truer, we too gladly yield to what is better.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.1-2":["And I turned, and lifted up my eyes, and saw, and behold, a flying scroll. And he said to me: What do you see? And I said: I see a flying scroll; its length twenty cubits, and its breadth ten cubits. And he said to me: This is the curse that goes forth over the face of the whole earth, for every thief, as it is written there, shall be judged, and everyone who swears shall likewise be judged by it. I will bring it forth, says the Lord of hosts, and it shall come to the house of the thief, and to the house of him who swears falsely by my name, and it shall remain in the midst of his house, and shall consume it, and its timbers, and its stones. LXX: And I turned, and lifted up my eyes, and saw a flying sickle. And he said to me: What do you see? And I said: I see a flying sickle, of twenty cubits in length, and ten cubits in breadth. And he said to me: This is the curse that goes forth over the face of the whole earth, for every thief shall by it be punished unto death, and every perjurer shall by it be tormented unto death. And I will bring it forth, says the Lord Almighty, and it shall enter into the house of the thief, and into the house of him who swears falsely by my name, and it shall rest in the midst of his house, and shall consume it, and its timbers, and its stones. Having turned, says the prophet, to another vision, and lifting up my eyes from joyful and happy things to sadder ones, I see a flying scroll, which in Hebrew is called megella, and was rendered by Aquila and Theodotion διφθέρα (hide), by Symmachus κεφαλὶς, that is, a heading, according to what we read in the psalm: At the head of the book it is written of me; or, according to the Seventy, Δρέπανον πετόμενον, that is, a flying sickle. For all that he had seen above had announced the building of the temple, the coming of the Lord Savior, the liberation of the people from Babylon. And so, lest his heart be lifted up, as with the Apostle — to whom was given an angel of Satan to buffet him, lest he exalt himself —, he too sees things that are sad, so that whatever arrogance had grown from the revelation of good things might shrink from the threat of sad ones. And a flying scroll is shown, in which the sins of all are written down, so that each may receive according to his works, whether good or evil, as Daniel says: Thrones were set, and the books were opened. But if we take it as a sickle, as the LXX translated, let us take an example from the Apocalypse of John, in which it is written: And the angel answered, and said to him who sat upon the horse: Put in your sharpest sickle, and reap your vineyard, for its grapes are ripe. Instead of a sickle we read in Deuteronomy of arrows and a sword: I will make my arrows drunk with blood, and my sword shall devour flesh. And because God did not spare the angels who sinned, who by their own fault lost their heavenly dwelling, therefore he himself says through Isaiah: My sword has been made drunk in heaven. For all the sinners of the people shall perish by the sword, not, surely, by a bodily sword — since there are many and various ways to death besides the sword —, but by a spiritual sword, with which those are to be struck who have not repented, and whom the psalmist threatens, crying out: Unless you are converted, he will brandish his sword; he has bent his bow and made it ready, and in it he has prepared the vessels of death. And in Jeremiah we read: How long will you cut, O point, or sword, of the Lord? How long will you not be still? Return into your sheath. This sword is called not only a sickle, which cuts down hay, stubble and thorns, but it is also called the axe of the trees, which will cut down those who have not brought forth fruits worthy of repentance; and of whom John the Baptist cried out: Behold, the axe is laid to the roots of the trees; every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and thrown into the fire. This scroll, in which the sins of all are written down, or this sickle, which cuts down the offences of all, is sent into the vineyard of Sodom, of which it is written: Our enemies are without understanding, for their vine is of the vineyard of Sodom, and their shoot from Gomorrah. Their grape is a grape of gall, and theirs a cluster of bitterness. Their wine is the fury of dragons, and the incurable fury of asps. This sickle has twenty cubits of length and ten of breadth, in which number joyful things are joined with sad. For the Lord rebukes in order to amend; and in the twenty, which is made up of two decades, harsh and sad things are announced; in the ten, that is, one decade, better and prosperous things; for Israel is instructed through all scourges and punishments.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.2":["I have read that the twenty cubits of length and the ten of breadth refer to the age of the Lord Savior, that is, they make the number thirty, because the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son, and by him the world is to be judged.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.3-4":["And at the same time let us warn those who think theft and perjury small crimes, that the curse which is described in the scroll and the sickle is brought into the house of the thief and of the perjurer, and remains in it, and consumes all its timbers and stones. But if in these things which are thought lesser — theft, I mean, and perjury — there is so great a threat of punishments, what shall we say of fornication, adultery, homicide, sacrilege, and all the crimes which are numbered by the Apostle among the works of the flesh?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.5-6":["The angel who was speaking in the prophet, having gone out from him and speaking as it were face to face, bids him lift up his eyes and see the sins of the people of Israel heaped up into a full measure, and the offences of all filled up; and that this is their eye, which in Hebrew is called enam, and is written with ain, iod, nun, mem. Or their iniquity, which, if it were written with the letter uau, would rightly be read onam, as the LXX rendered; and this error has often crept into the common edition, that because the letters uau and iod are of the same shape but differ in size, one is read for the other. This jar or measure is their eye in the whole earth, that is, the showing of sins, so that those whose vices lay hidden, scattered, might be heaped up into one and laid open to the eyes of all, and Israel might go forth from its place, and it might be shown to all the nations what Israel had been in its own land.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.5-8":["And the angel who was speaking in me went out, and said to me: Lift up your eyes, and see what this is that goes forth. And I said: What is it? And he said: This is a jar going forth; and he said: This is their eye in the whole earth. And behold, a talent of lead was being carried, and behold, a woman was sitting in the midst of the jar. And he said: This is impiety, and he cast her into the midst of the jar, and put a lump of lead into its mouth. LXX: And the angel who was speaking in me went out, and said to me: Look up with your eyes and see what it is that goes forth; and I said: What is it? And he said: This is the measure that goes forth. And he said: This is their iniquity in all the earth. And behold, a talent of lead was lifted up; and behold, a woman was sitting in the midst of the measure, and he said: This is iniquity, and he cast her into the midst of the measure, and cast a stone of lead into its mouth. A jar, or a measure, was going forth and being carried in the air. And so that we should not be in doubt by what word it is properly named, the angel himself who had shown the jar, or measure, gives it a name; and he says, according to the Seventy: This is their iniquity in the whole earth; according to the Hebrews: This is the eye, that is, the showing of all sins. And behold, a woman was sitting in the midst of the jar, or measure, which among the Hebrews is called epha, and is often rendered by the LXX οἴφι; and this woman herself was called impiety. While he was seeing these things, behold, a talent of lead, that is, a lump like a stone, was being carried, either by its own impulse, or by the Lord's command, or it was carried by another whose name is passed over in silence. And this angel who was speaking in the prophet, and going out from him was showing him everything, seized the woman who was called impiety, and threw her headlong into the midst of the jar, she who before was being carried freely and, sitting upon the jar, was visible to all. And lest she should perhaps raise her head again, and rejoice in her iniquity and impiety, he puts the talent of lead, like a very heavy stone, into the mouth of the jar, to press down impiety in the midst and shut her in, so that she cannot in any way break out. These things we have drawn as certain shadows and outlines of the picture to come, so that we may fill in the rest with its proper colors.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.7-8":["And upon this measure and jar of all offences sat impiety in the midst, which by another name we can call idolatry, and the denial of God. Hence it is said by the Savior to the Jews: Fill up the measure of your fathers. This impiety, which sat upon the sins of Israel and gloried in its wickedness, is afterwards sent into the midst of Babylon, and is crushed by the evil of captivity. Or, according to Theodotion, she casts herself and hides in the midst of the jar, and draws over herself the very heavy weight of lead, so that she has her mouth stopped and can no longer boast herself. Or else she is crushed by the angel of God, so that she who before rejoiced in wickedness may fall silent in eternal silence. But whither, and by whom, she is carried shut in, the following reading teaches.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.7":["And behold, a talent of lead was being carried. For talent of lead we read in what follows stone of lead. A talent is called chachar; a stone, aben. It is, then, the stone of lead which is also the talent of lead, which we, expressing it more plainly, have translated as a lump or ball of lead, by which is signified the very heavy weight of sins.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.9-11":["If we choose to take the two women as the peoples of the heretics and of the Jews — both of whom go out from the face of the Lord, and are borne by an unsteady spirit, and have the wings of the kite, the heron and the hoopoe, since, after the manner of the partridge, they are forever gathering riches, and not with judgment, and hasten to snatch from the Church, and delight in contentions and quarrels, and drag to destruction whomever they have deceived, and wallow in the mire of lusts and in everlasting filth —, these women lift up the most heavy weight of impiety, and build their house in confusion, and serve the Babylonian king, so that the peoples of the heretics and of the Jews may dwell there, where the idolaters abide, serving wood and stones.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.9":["And I lifted up my eyes and saw, and behold, two women coming out, and the spirit was in their wings, and they had wings like the wings of a kite, and they lifted up the jar between earth and heaven. And I said to the angel who spoke in me: Where are these carrying the jar? And he said to me: That a house may be built for it in the land of Shinar, and that it may be established, and set there upon its base. LXX: And I lifted up my eyes and saw, and behold, two women coming out, and the spirit was in their wings, and they had wings as are the wings of a hoopoe. And they lifted up the measure between earth and heaven. And I said to the angel who spoke in me: Where are these carrying the measure? And he said to me: To build a house for it in the land of Babylon, and to prepare it, and to set it there upon its seat. The Jews take the two women coming out to be the kingdom of the Medes and that of the Macedonians, each of which afflicted the Babylonian people, and there their impiety set up its seat. But this they invent with cunning, so that what was said of themselves may not be understood of themselves. For the two women who come out, no doubt from the land of Judea, are to be understood as the ten tribes and the two, of which the one were taken captive by the Assyrians, the other by the Chaldeans. And in their wings was the spirit, that is, the power of the devil, of which it is written in Ecclesiastes: If the spirit of him who has power rises up against you, do not leave your place. And in the Gospel we read of the unclean spirit which, when it has been cast out of its house, goes about through desert and dry places and returns to its former house with seven other spirits more wicked than itself. By this spirit, then, these women were snatched along as by wind and blast and borne in swift flight, and they had wings; in the Hebrew asida, which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated heron, the LXX alone hoopoe. The Hebrews think the asida is the kite, a most rapacious bird, forever lying in wait for the birds of the farmyard; but of the heron, those who have written on the nature of birds declare that there are three kinds: one white, another starred, the third black, which is both the fiercest and bloodthirsty, and quarrelsome, and so impatient in mating that blood bursts from their eyes. The hoopoe, again, whose name we drew from the likeness of the Greek name (for they too call it epopa, because it settles on human dung), they say is a most filthy bird, forever lingering in tombs, forever in human dung; in short, it is even said to make its nest of it, and to feed its young on the little worms of rotting dung. Whichever of the three birds you choose to understand by asida, it suits these women, Judah and Israel, who because of their stinking sins were handed over to the power of demons and were led by them into captivity. And they took up the jar, or measure, in which impiety was held shut in, with a lump of lead laid on top so that it could not get out, and the most heavy weight of all sins, through the air between earth and heaven.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.5.10-11":["The prophet, understanding this, does not ask who these women are, for that was plain to the prophetic spirit, nor what they carry, about which he had been instructed above, but where they carry it. In short, there follows: I said to the angel who spoke in me: Where are these carrying the jar? He answered: That a house may be built for it in the land of Shinar. For which the Seventy translated, in the land of Babylon. For Shinar is a plain of the Chaldeans, in which those who had moved their feet from the east, and could not stand in the service of God, built the tower of pride; whence the city itself also was called Babylon, that is, confusion, because there the tongues of all were confounded and mingled. Impiety, therefore, is carried by these women into Babylon, so that there its house may be built, and it may be established, and set upon its base, and rest in an everlasting abode. For truly in Babylon is the seat of impiety, both according to the history and according to the mystical senses.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.1-3":["According to allegory, let us take in the present place too whatever we said about the four horns and the four smiths. I have read in a certain person's volume that the four chariots, in which are red, and black, and white, and spotted and strong horses, are to be understood as the four Gospels, and the horses as the apostles, possessing different graces through the diversity of the colors: of whom some are red with martyrdom, others dark and black, knowing the mysteries of Christ, of whom it is said in the psalm: Darkness was under his feet, and: He made darkness his hiding place. Others white, with the grace of virginity; others spotted and strong, having the grace of healings and of various powers.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.1-5":["These four kingdoms which overthrew Judea Zechariah writes that he saw in four horns, the angel saying to him: These are the horns which have scattered Judah and Israel and Jerusalem. And again: I lifted up, he says, my eyes and saw; and behold, four chariots coming out from between two mountains, and the mountains were mountains of bronze; in the first chariot, red horses; and in the second chariot, black horses; and in the third chariot, white horses; and in the fourth chariot, dappled, strong horses. And when the prophet had said to the angel who spoke in him: What are these, my lord? The angel answered: These are the four winds of heaven, which go forth to stand before the ruler of all the earth; and the sense is: These are they who go forth from the face of the Lord, to fulfill his will.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"ZEC.6.1":["And I turned, and lifted up my eyes and saw. And behold, four chariots coming out from between two mountains, and the mountains, mountains of bronze. In the first chariot were red horses, and in the second chariot black horses, and in the third chariot white horses, and in the fourth chariot spotted and strong horses. And I answered, and said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these, my lord? And the angel answered, and said to me: These are the four winds of heaven, which go out to stand before the Ruler of all the earth. The one in which were the black horses went out into the land of the north, and the white went out after them, and the spotted went out toward the land of the south. But those which were the most robust went out, and sought to go and run to and fro through all the earth. And he said: Go, walk through the earth; and they walked through the earth. And he called me, and spoke to me, saying: Behold, those who go out into the land of the north have made my spirit rest in the land of the north. LXX: And I turned, and lifted up my eyes, and saw. And behold, four chariots coming out from between two mountains, and the mountains were mountains of bronze. In the first chariot were red horses, and in the second chariot black horses, and in the third chariot white horses, and in the fourth chariot spotted, starling-colored horses. And I answered, and said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these, lord? And the angel who spoke in me answered and said: These are the four winds of heaven, which go out to stand by the Lord of all the earth. The one in which were the black horses went out over the land of the north, and the white went out after them, and the spotted went out into the land of the south, and the starling-colored went out, and they looked to go around the earth. And he said: Go and go around the earth; and they went around the earth, and he cried out; and he spoke to me, saying: Behold, those who have gone out over the land of the north have made my fury rest in the land of the north. I passed, he says, to another vision, and lifted the eyes of my heart higher, toward heaven, and saw: four chariots came out from between two mountains which were of bronze, that is, unconquerable and most strong, and such as could be consumed by no age. For those he had called above mountains of myrtles, or shady and wooded ones, he now calls mountains of bronze.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.2-3":["In the first chariot were red horses, bloody and bloodthirsty, and terrible with the cruelty of Babylon. In the second chariot, black horses, the kingdom of the Medes and Persians, which, seated in a two-horse chariot and going out through the edict of King Ahasuerus, bore before it, with sad tidings, the death of all the Jews. In the third chariot, white horses, the Macedonians, under whose king Antiochus we read of the victory of the Maccabees. In the fourth chariot, spotted, strong horses. For we know that of the kings of the Romans some were merciful toward the Jewish nation, such as Gaius Caesar, Augustus and Claudius; others persecutors and terrible, such as Gaius Caligula, Nero, Vespasian and Hadrian. For strong, which Aquila translated κρατεροὺς (strong), the Seventy ψαροὺς (dappled), in the Hebrew is written amasim. And certain copies wrongly have πυῤῥοὺς, confusing the differences of the colors and of the kingdoms, since πυῤῥοὶ, that is, red, are called not amasim but adamim.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.4-5":["So when the prophet asks what the things he saw meant to signify, the angel who spoke in him answers, and relates that they are the four winds of heaven, that is, the four quarters of the world, which the Greeks call κλίματα, which stand by and obey the will of the Lord. For these four kingdoms of which we have spoken did nothing apart from the will of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.5-7":["These chariots, four in number, or cars, were sent into the four winds of heaven, that is, to the hinges of the world, and the whole circle of the lands, to fulfil the will of the Lord; to whom it is afterward said by the angel: Go, and walk through the earth, and sow the Gospel in all the ends of the earth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.6-7":["The one in which were the black horses, he says, went out into the land of the north. How finely the first chariot, in which were the red horses, is passed over, and what the second and the third and the fourth did is described! For already at the time when the prophet was relating these things, the kingdom of Babylon had passed away, and the power of the Medes held all Asia; under their king Darius, in his second year, in the eleventh month, which is called Shebat, on the twenty-fourth day of the month, he beholds all that we have expounded above. The one, then, in which were the black horses went out into the land of the north, so that the might of the Medes might overthrow the kingdom of the Chaldeans. And this is to be noted, that the κλίματα, that is, the quarters of the globe and of the world, are named in the holy Scriptures according to the situation of Jerusalem and of the temple. The white also went out after them: the kings of the Macedonians, following the footsteps of the Medes and Persians, to subjugate the Chaldeans and Babylon to their own rule. But the fourth, that is, the starling-colored and spotted, for which in the Hebrew is read borodim, and which by another name are called amasim, that is, strong and robust, went out toward the southern quarter, and traversed the whole earth with swift onset. And the angel who spoke in the prophet cries out to the empire of the Romans: Go, walk through the earth, and go around the circle of the lands, and lay all kingdoms beneath your feet.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.8":["And turning to the prophet he testifies with a clear voice: Behold, those who go out into the land of the north have made my spirit rest in the land to which they set out. In a passage that is indeed most difficult, it was handed down to me by the Hebrews that Alexander and all the Macedonians, that is, the white horses which went out after the Medes and Persians, who had themselves also gone into the land of the north, made the prophetic spirit rest in the land of the north, because against the Medes they fulfilled the will of God, and in a short time the empire of the Medes and Persians was destroyed by the Macedonians. For great is the consolation of those who are oppressed, if they know that their enemies will soon perish. These things, as we were able, or rather as we received them, we have handed on to those devoted to our language, following the truth of the history; not hunting out with tenacious memory, after the fashion of the Egyptian exposition, the chariots of Pharaoh and the Babylonian cars; for what must be considered is not where something is written, but for what reason it is written.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","This too, which follows: Behold, those who go out into the land of the north have made my spirit rest in the land of the north, he explained in such a way as to say that the spirit of the Lord, or of the angel, rested when, in the land of the north, the most harsh kingdoms of the devil were overthrown by the apostolic preaching; and that these are the kingdoms which the devil, showing them to the Lord and Savior on a high mountain, boasted had been handed over to himself.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.10-11":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Take from the exiles, from Heldai and from Tobijah, and from Jedaiah, and you shall come on that day, and enter the house of Josiah son of Zephaniah, who came from Babylon. And you shall take silver and gold, and make crowns, and set them on the head of Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, and you shall speak to him, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts, saying: Behold a man, the Rising is his name, and beneath him it shall rise, and he shall build a temple to the Lord. And he himself shall raise a temple to the Lord, and he himself shall bear glory, and shall sit and rule upon his throne, and he shall be a priest upon his throne, and the counsel of peace shall be between the two of them. And the crowns shall be for Helem and for Tobijah and for Jedaiah and for Hen son of Zephaniah, a memorial in the temple of the Lord. And those who are far off shall come and build in the temple of the Lord, and you shall know that the Lord of hosts has sent me to you. And this shall be, if with hearing you hear the voice of the Lord your God. LXX: And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Take the things that are from the captivity, from the rulers, and from its useful men, and from those who have known it; and you shall go in on that day into the house of Josiah son of Zephaniah, who came from Babylon, and you shall take silver and gold, and make crowns, and place them upon the head of Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, and you shall say to him: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Behold a man, the Rising is his name, and beneath him it shall rise, and he shall build the house of the Lord, and he himself shall receive power and shall sit, and shall rule upon his throne. And there shall be a priest at his right hand, and the counsel of peace shall be between the two; but the crown shall be for those who wait, and for its useful men, and for those who have known it, and for the favor of the son of Zephaniah, and for a psalm in the house of the Lord; and those who are far from them shall come and build in the house of the Lord. And you shall know that the Lord Almighty has sent me to you, and it shall be, if with hearing you hear the voice of the Lord your God. I resolved once for all to bring forth to Latin ears the secrets of Hebrew learning and the hidden teaching of the masters of the synagogue, only so much of it, at any rate, as agrees with the holy Scriptures. For this reason I must, in the most obscure places, draw the lines of the history, and so set forth openly what I have received from churchmen, leaving it to the reader's judgment which he ought rather to follow. And first the order of the reading must be rendered, so that what is said may become clear according to the letter. Take, he says, from Heldai and from Tobijah, and from Jedaiah, who came from the Babylonian captivity, and you shall take from them the gifts that are offered, silver and gold, and you shall enter the house of Josiah son of Zephaniah; and there you shall make crowns, set off by the variety of gold and silver, not one crown but two or more; for ataroth, that is, στέμματα, signifies not the singular but either the dual or the plural number.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But upon us lies the necessity of saying, according to the LXX translators, what our forefathers said. Take from the captivity, and from the rulers, and from its useful men, and from those who have known it, gold and silver, and having gone into the house of Josiah son of Zephaniah, who himself too came from Babylon, make in it crowns of gold and of silver, which you shall place upon the head of Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest. The captivity of the Jews, that is, of the people that confesses the Lord, is vices and sins, which he who has begun to do repentance and to long for the former Jerusalem, that is, the Church of Christ, abandons and leaves behind in Babylon; and it is offered by the rulers of the captives, and by its useful men, that is, of the captivity, who themselves too were handed over for their own profit to an angel of Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit might be saved; and by those who know that it, namely the captivity, is to be turned to good account after the punishments. And the gold and silver is offered by those who confess the Lord both in understanding and in speech, and from it, that is, from the gold and silver, crowns are made in the house of Josiah, who is interpreted saved, and is the son of the visitation of the Lord, that is, ἐπισκοπῆς κυρίου. For he is visited by the Lord who before had been grievously sick. And rightly is he called Josiah, saved, because he too returned from Babylon.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.11-12":["And when you have made the crowns, you shall set one of them on the head of Joshua (Iesus, the same name as Jesus) son of Jehozadak, the high priest, and you shall speak to him: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Behold a man whose name is the Rising, which in Hebrew is called sema, written not with the letter sin but with sade. He is called the Rising, that is, ἀνατολὴ, or ἀναφύη, or βλάστημα, that is, shoot, for this reason: because he shall suddenly spring up of himself, and shall sprout from his own root in the likeness of a shoot; and this man shall build the temple of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.11":["And the crowns, or the crown, are set upon Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, because, as we advance and return to better things, the Lord is crowned through each of our virtues; or rather, as we are crowned through the virtues of repentance, the Savior receives a crown in each one, according to what Paul also says: For the rest, there is laid up for me a crown of justice, which the Lord, the just judge, will render to me; and not to me only, but also to all who love the coming of our Lord Savior Jesus Christ. Whence the father of the Lord Savior also took his name from the crown of justice. For Jehozadak is interpreted Lord of justice, because he renders to each according to his works. Of this crown James too speaks in his epistle: Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been proved he will receive the crown of life, which God has promised to those who love him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.12-13":["And beneath him shall rise the multitude of believers, and he shall build the house of the Lord, the Church, and he himself shall receive the power and beauty and progress and glory of each one, and shall sit and rule on his throne, on the throne of David, of which it is also written in the Gospel: And there shall be a priest at his right hand, or, according to the Hebrew, upon his throne, because he himself is both king and high priest, and shall sit on the royal as well as on the priestly throne, and there shall be a peaceful counsel between the two, so that neither shall the royal eminence depress the priestly dignity, nor the dignity of the priesthood the royal eminence, but both shall agree in the glory of the one Lord Jesus. I have read in a certain person's book that what is said: And a peaceful counsel shall be between the two, refers to the Father and to the Son, because he came to do not his own will but the Father's, and the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.12":["And the prophet is commanded, after he has placed the crown, or the crowns, upon the head of Joshua son of Jehozadak, the high priest, to speak to him and say: Thus says the Lord: Behold a man, the Rising is his name, and beneath him it shall rise, and the rest. We do not divide Jesus, nor can we make two persons in one person, but he who is called Jesus because he saved the world is himself also called the Rising, because in his days justice has risen. And it is sung in the psalm: Truth has risen from the earth, because, begotten from a virgin's womb at the consummation of the ages, he said: I am the truth, and he came at the last hour, according to the epistle of John, who says: Little children, it is now the last hour, in which he hired for a denarius the people of the nations, who were standing and doing nothing, and sent them to the vineyard. Otherwise, if he becomes different according to the diversity of names, the shepherd will be one, the ram another, the lamb another, the door another, the stone of stumbling and the rock of offense another. He, therefore, who has been crowned with our virtues shall rise and shall be called the Rising. To him the Father said: You are my Son, today I have begotten you.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.13-14":["And there shall be peace between those two, that is, between him who is of the royal tribe and him who descends from the Levitical stock, so that priesthood and kingdom alike may rule the people of God; and the crowns themselves which you have made of gold and silver, after they have been set on the head of Joshua son of Jehozadak and on the head of Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, you shall consecrate in the temple with the names of those by whom they were offered, that is, of Helem, and Tobijah, and Jedaiah, and Hen. The second and third names are the same as above. The first has been changed; for in place of Heldai he has now put Helem; a fourth, which he had not mentioned above, has been added, that is, Hen; of these we shall speak in what follows.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.13":["And when he has raised it, he himself also shall bear glory, that is, the second crown, which in Hebrew is hod, and is written by one ἐπιδοξότης, by another εὐπρέπεια, by another ἀρετὴ, by another δόξα; which mean either renown, or beauty, or virtue, or glory. And they think Zerubbabel is signified, who, rising suddenly from a lowly man and a captive to be leader of the Jewish people, built the temple of the Lord, and sat upon his throne, and ruled with the power of a prince; but the high priest too, he says, Joshua son of Jehozadak, shall sit on the priestly throne, and with minds and counsels joined they shall govern the people of God.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.14-15":["And those who said in Babylon: By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat and wept, when we remembered Zion; on the willows in the midst of it we hung up our instruments, once they have returned to Jerusalem, let them take up psalm and timbrel, and sing in the Church of the Lord; and let those who through greater sins had been far from Judea come, with a most true confession too, and through each of their advances build the house of the Lord; and then let all with one mind know that the Lord Almighty has sent the prophet to them. And all that has been promised shall come to pass, if they are willing to hear the Lord, and, having done repentance, remain in good works.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.14":["Heldai is interpreted the entreaty of the Lord, which in Greek is said more expressively λιτάνευσις κυρίου. Tobijah, good of the Lord. Jedaiah, known of the Lord. In place of Heldai he has now put in the first place Helem, which means dream; and in the fourth place he has added Hen, that is, grace. The crowns of these four men shall be with the son of Zephaniah, whom we read above was Josiah, who is the guardian and warden of the temple of the Lord, and there they shall rest in an everlasting abode. The Hebrews relate that Hananiah, Azariah and Mishael, coming from the captivity, brought gold and silver as gifts for the temple, and the crowns of the high priest and of the leader; and that Hen, whom he had not mentioned above, that is, grace, means Daniel, who came with a gift; and that Helem was put in place of Heldai for this reason, so that from the interpretation of the name, which means dream, it might be shown that Daniel, set in captivity, and the three young men knew the mysteries of the king's dream. For Daniel, taking the three young men with him, prayed to the Lord and obtained the interpretation of the dream. This is how the circumcision tries to expound these things according to the history.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","And the crown, that is, the emblem of victory, once Christ has been crowned, is promised also to those who wait for him, and understand the profit of their former captivity, and know all the mysteries of God, so that they may be for the favor of the son of Zephaniah, that is, of the visitation of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.6.15":["And when the crowns, he says, have been laid up in the temple of the Lord, and consecrated for an everlasting memorial, nations from the whole circle of the lands and set far off shall come, and shall build in my temple, each according to his strength. And then, by the outcome of events and the prosperity of all, you shall know that the Lord has sent me, and that at his command I foretold all things. And all these things shall come to pass if you obey the precepts of the Lord and do his commandments.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.1-3":["And it came to pass in the fourth year of King Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, on the fourth of the ninth month, which is Chislev. And Sharezer and Regem-melech, and the men who were with him, sent to the house of God, to entreat the face of the Lord, to speak to the priests of the house of the Lord of hosts and to the prophets, saying: Must I weep in the fifth month, or ought I to sanctify myself, as I have done now for many years? And the word of the Lord of hosts came to me, saying: Speak to all the people of the land and to the priests, saying: When you fasted and mourned in the fifth and the seventh through these seventy years, did you fast a fast to me? And when you ate and drank? Did you not eat for yourselves and drink for your own selves? Are these not the words which the Lord spoke by the hand of the former prophets, when Jerusalem was still inhabited and was wealthy, she and her cities round about her, and it was inhabited toward the south and in the plains? LXX: And it came to pass in the fourth year under King Darius, the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, on the fourth day of the ninth month, which is Chislev. And Sharezer and Arabesser the king, and his men, sent to Bethel to entreat the Lord, saying to the priests of the house of the Lord Almighty and to the prophets, saying: Sanctification has come in here in the fifth month, as they have done now for many years; and the word of the Lord of powers came to me, saying: Say to all the people of the land and to the priests, saying: If you fast and mourn in the fifths and in the sevenths, and behold, seventy years, did you fast a fast to me? and if you eat or drink, is it not you who eat, and you who drink? Are not these the words which the Lord spoke by the hands of the prophets who were before, when Jerusalem was inhabited and was abundant, and her cities round about, and the hill country and the plains were inhabited? In the fourth year of King Darius, in the ninth month, which is called Chislev, and on the fourth day of the same month, Sharezer and Regem-melech and the others who were with them, who the Hebrews maintain were Persians, generals of King Darius who feared God, sent to the house of the Lord, that is, to the temple which had already been restored by Zerubbabel and by Joshua, so that, since they had already heard that the temple had been built, they might ask of the priests of the house of the Lord and of the prophets whether they ought to weep and fast according to the former custom, or to change mourning into gladness. And the sense of those inquiring is this: In the fifth month, which among the Romans is called July, Jerusalem was overthrown by Nebuchadnezzar; for which reason, because of the desolation of the temple, we have fasted and mourned until now, and have consoled our grief with weeping and fasts. Now, since it is said that the temple has been built, and we see that the cause of sadness by no means remains, answer, we beg you, whether we ought to do this, or to exchange grief for joy? And at the same time it must be considered that weeping and fasting are called sanctification. Hence in Joel too the priests are commanded to sanctify a fast and to proclaim a healing. For abstinence and fasting heal the wounds of the offender, and sanctify all whom they have healed.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.2":["What is written in the Seventy, Sharezer and Arabesser the king sent from Bethel, none of our writers has been able to expound; for what has been badly translated from the Hebrew cannot be explained by any reasoning. For who is this Sharezer, or Arabesser the king? Or king of what province? Or into which Bethel, or from which, did he send? Or of what Bethel could Arabesser be king, a place that had long before been left desolate together with Judea, and at that time was by no means called Bethel, that is, house of God, but Beth-aven, that is, house of an idol?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.3-5":["This too which follows, Sanctification has come in here in the fifth month, as they had done now through many years, they try to expound thus: that the sanctification which came in is the vessels of the temple, which had been carried off by Nebuchadnezzar, restored at that time. As for the fast of the fifth and the seventh, they tried to refer it to the days of the week. But since the fast of the tenth follows, they were compelled to refer it to months, and as to what this fast of the fifth, seventh and tenth month itself is, they were entirely silent. Hence let us be content with the former explanation, and let us not incline to the false attempts of the commentators, which came from an error of translation.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.4-6":["After the generals of the Persians had made their inquiry through those whom they had sent, and the embassy of the inquirers to the priests and prophets had been carried out, the word of the Lord came to the prophet, commanding him to speak to the common people and to the priests, telling them what they ought to answer the envoys. When you fasted and mourned in the fifth month, that of the captivity of Jerusalem, and in the seventh month, when Gedaliah was slain by Ishmael, through the seventy years of the desolation of the temple and of the overthrow of Jerusalem, was it of any profit to me that you fasted? And on the other hand, whenever you eat and drink, is it not for yourselves that you both eat and drink? For God is not appeased by these things, but by good works, and if we do his commandments, because food does not commend us to God. Neither, if we do not eat, shall we lack; nor, if we eat, shall we abound.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.7":["Are not these my words, while Jerusalem and the cities of Judah were still standing, which I spoke to you by the hands of my prophets, when Jerusalem still stood, and the cities of Judah abounded in all things, and both the hill country and the plain overflowed with crops and enjoyed a most secure peace? Now these were the words of the Lord, as the Scripture that follows testifies: that he wills true judgment and mercy toward one's neighbors, and that one not wrong the widow and the orphan and the stranger and the poor, and not devise evil in one's heart. These things, he says, they would not do, and with deaf ears they despised my commands; for which reason great indignation came upon Jerusalem; and as they would not hear me, so I too did not hear them; and now they ask with such scrupulousness when they ought to fast and mourn, although I said before through Isaiah: This is not the fast I have chosen, says the Lord, nor that a man should humble his soul; but loose every bond of iniquity, undo the obligations of violent contracts, give your bread to the hungry from your heart. If you see the naked, cover him; and the poor and the roofless clothe within your tent. Then your early light shall break forth, and your healings shall quickly arise.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.9":["And the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts, saying: Judge true judgment, and show mercy and compassion, every man with his brother. And do not wrong the widow and the orphan and the stranger and the poor; and let no man devise evil against his brother in his heart. And they would not attend, and they turned a withdrawing shoulder; and they made their ears heavy, that they might not hear; and they made their heart adamant lest they hear the law and the words which the Lord of hosts sent in his spirit by the hand of the former prophets, and great indignation came from the Lord of hosts. And it came to pass as he spoke, and they did not hear: so they shall cry, and I will not hear, says the Lord of hosts. And I scattered them through all the kingdoms which they do not know; and the land was left desolate by them, because there was none passing through and returning; and they made the desirable land into a desert. LXX: And the word of the Lord came to Zechariah, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty, saying: Judge just judgment, and let each one show mercy and compassion toward his brother; and do not oppress the widow and the orphan and the poor; and do not, each of you, remember the malice of his brother in your hearts. And they would not attend, and they gave a scornful back; and they made their ears heavy, so as not to hear; and they made their heart disobedient, so as not to hear my law and my words, which the Lord Almighty sent in his spirit by the hands of the former prophets. And great wrath came from the Lord Almighty, and it shall be as I said, and they did not hear him. So they shall cry, and I will not hear them, says the Lord Almighty; and I will cast them out among all the nations which they did not know; and the land was made desolate after them, of anyone passing through and returning, and they made the chosen land into a wilderness. These things I wanted rather, these I sought, and because you did not do them you were handed over to captivity; not the fast of desolation and death of the fifth and the seventh month. Judge just judgment, lest you hear in the psalms: How long will you judge iniquity, and accept the persons of sinners? Judge for the orphan and the widow; justify the humble and the poor, lest Isaiah too say to you: Who justify the wicked for gifts, and take away from the just what is just; and lest, because of you, Habakkuk, in the person of those who are oppressed, cast reproach upon God: Judgment has been given against me, and the judge takes; therefore the law is scattered, and judgment does not come through to the end, because the wicked oppresses the just. And let us not think that this is a new commandment of God; long ago he had enjoined these things through Moses: So shall you judge the great as the small; you shall not accept a person, and you shall not pity the poor in judgment, since the judgment is God's. Show also mercies and compassions, every man with his brother. After truth of judgment let clemency follow toward all, and most of all toward brothers, whom we perceive to be either of the same blood as we or of one faith with us.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.10":["The widow too, and the orphan, concerning whom we have been commanded: Be a father to orphans, and in place of a husband to their mother; judging the orphan and justifying the widow. And do not wrong the stranger and the poor, because sojourning abroad makes the one lowly, and need the other. And let no man devise evil against his brother in his heart, or, as it is said by the Seventy: And let each one not remember the malice of his brother in their hearts. By brother and neighbor we ought to understand either the whole race of men, because we are begotten of one parent, or those who are of the household of the faith, according to the parable of the Gospel, which wants the neighbor to be understood not as a blood relative but as all men. And that anger ought to be ended before the sun goes down, and the memory of all the evils we have suffered from others be blotted out, we read both in many places and most of all in Jeremiah, who speaks in the person of God: And do not, each one, remember in your hearts the malice of his neighbor.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.11-14":["We can also refer these things to those who, sinning in the Church, have been cast out of the land of confession, because they would not hear the Lord, and turned a withdrawing shoulder to him, and made their ears heavy, and made their heart adamant. And the indignation of the Lord came upon them, and they were scattered through all the kingdoms of the vices, and their land was made desolate, whether soul or body, not having God as its inhabitant, nor the spirit returning into it. And the land once desirable, which was the lodging of the Trinity, was turned into a desert and a dwelling of dragons. Let us pass quickly over what is plain, so that there may be room for discussion in what is obscure; for we are not writing long and florid treatises, in which a speech that seeks applause plays, but commentaries, whose office is to pass over what is evident and to discuss what is obscure.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.11":["Though I had commanded these things, they would not attend, and turned a withdrawing shoulder, or a scornful back, rejecting my commands by the bearing of their body. For when, with wrinkled brow and contracted nostril, we scorn those who admonish us, we are accustomed to turn our back; according to what is written: They turned their backs to me, and not their faces. And they made their ears heavy, he says, lest they hear, like the ears of the deaf asp that stops its ears, which will not hear the voice of the charmers, and of the sorcerer who charms wisely. For they made their ears heavy so as not to hear, and they set their heart so as not to yield to the law of God. Hence Isaiah speaks to them threateningly: The heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and they have closed their eyes, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and be converted, and I should heal them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.12-13":["And because they had, nay rather made, their heart adamant, esteeming hardness of heart by their own will, so as not to hear the words of the Lord which he sent in his spirit, that is, in the Holy Spirit, by the hand of the former prophets, Isaiah, Hosea and the rest, who had clean hands, and who, it is plain, were before the captivity: therefore, for great sins, great indignation came about, and the words of the Lord were fulfilled, who repays like for like, so that, as they walked perversely toward him, he too should go perversely against them, and not hear the words of those crying out, because they too had despised the words of the Lord with a deaf ear.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.12":["Moreover, what is said according to the Hebrew, And they made their heart adamant, shows hardness of heart, and a heart of stone, in that they would not receive the words of God. For adamant, a very strong stone, which in Hebrew is called samir, is so hard that it breaks all metals and is itself broken by none. Hence it is called untamable by the Greeks. With this adamant the heart of Pharaoh was hardened, so that he would not let the people of God go.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.7.14":["For which reason he also scattered them through all the kingdoms which they do not know, those of the Assyrians and Chaldeans, of the Medes and Persians, and of the other nations which lay subject to these empires and in whose lands they were sown abroad. And all Judea was deserted, because it had no inhabitant, and there was none in it passing through and returning. And the land which was a honeycomb above all lands, and for the greatness of its abundance flowed with milk and honey, they turned into a wilderness.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.2-3":["And the word of the Lord of hosts came, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: I have been jealous for Zion with a great jealousy, and with great indignation I have been jealous for her. Thus says the Lord of hosts: I have returned to Zion, and I will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem; and Jerusalem shall be called the city of truth, and the mountain of the Lord of hosts, the sanctified mountain. LXX: And the word of the Lord Almighty came, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: I have been jealous for Jerusalem and Zion with a great jealousy, and with very great fury I have been jealous for her. Thus says the Lord Almighty: I will return to Zion, and I will dwell in the midst of Jerusalem; and Jerusalem shall be called the true city, and the mountain of the Lord Almighty, the holy mountain. That the Lord took Jerusalem, that is, the Israelite people, in the wilderness as a wife, and, as she lay in the blood of idolatry, covered her with his cloak and cherished her with a husband's love, we learn more fully in Ezekiel; and she afterward, eating fine flour and honey and oil, adorned with the most beautiful garments, and having from her husband's bounty every ornament of gems, committed fornication with the Assyrians and Chaldeans; and she heard from the Lord: As a wife despises her husband, so the house of Israel has despised me. But handed over to captivity and mocked by her lovers, and stripped of her former beauty, after she had spread her feet to every passer-by and was defiled to the crown of her head, mindful of her former happiness she says again and again: I will return to my former husband, for it was better with me then than now. And he, receiving her and having her again in marriage, he who had said to her before: I will not be angry with you, and my jealousy has departed from you, now says: I have been jealous for Zion with a great jealousy, and with great indignation I have been jealous for her. As much as I was indignant before, because she was disfigured by many lovers and stained my bed, for which reason I also handed her over to her lovers, not at all as an adulteress under a husband, but as a harlot and a vile slave, prostituted in brothels, so much the more have I now returned to her, because she has done repentance and has built my temple, in which I will dwell in the midst of her. And she shall be called the city of truth, she who before was called the city of falsehood, of which it is also written in Isaiah: Truth slept in her, but now murderers. And the mountain of the Lord Almighty shall also be called the sanctified mountain, on which, the temple being restored, victims are sacrificed and the order of the ceremonies is kept. These things according to the history. But no one doubts that Zion and Jerusalem, watchtower and vision of peace, can be taken as the souls of the faithful, whom, when they have sinned, the Lord in anger hands over to captivity, so that those who had not perceived God through good and prosperous things may perceive him through evil and adverse ones. And when they have done repentance, the Lord will return to Zion, and dwells in the midst of Jerusalem, which we understand to be one and the same city; and in those in whom the falsehoods of sins and vices formerly reigned, Christ, the truth, will afterward abide. And the mountain of the Lord of hosts shall be called the holy mountain, of which it is said: Those who trust in the Lord are like Mount Zion, and: Great is the Lord and exceedingly to be praised, in the city of our God, on his holy mountain. Of which Isaiah and Micah cry aloud: In the last of days the mountain of the house of the Lord shall be prepared on the top of the mountains and lifted high above the hills; and all peoples shall flow to it, and many nations shall prosper; and they shall say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, and to the house of the God of Jacob. Of this mountain and of this city the apostle Paul also — if indeed, in receiving the epistle, the Latin tongue does not reject the authority of the Greeks —, discoursing in a sacred speech, says: You have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and many thousands of angels, and the Church of the firstborn, who are written in the heavens.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.4-5":["Thus says the Lord of hosts. Old men and old women shall yet dwell in the streets of Jerusalem, and a man's staff in his hand for the multitude of days. And the streets of the city shall be filled with infants and girls, playing in its streets. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Old men and old women shall yet sit in the streets of Jerusalem, each one holding his rod in his hand, for the multitude of days; and the streets of the city shall be filled with boys and girls, playing in its streets. So great, he says, once I have returned to Zion and am dwelling in the midst of Jerusalem, will be the prosperity of all things and the rest and tranquillity from wars, that, with no enemy remaining, old age in both sexes will reach the furthest span of life and support its trembling limbs with a guiding staff. The streets of the city too shall be filled with boys and girls playing. Now this is wont to happen when there is security and deep peace in the cities, so that playful youth celebrates the joy of the cities with games and dances.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.4":["But if we refer it to the Church, of which it is said: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, and: The rush of the river gladdens the city of God; the Most High has sanctified his tabernacle; God in the midst of her shall not be moved, who can doubt that the streets of the Church are the virtues, in which wisdom acts confidently and is proclaimed on the tops of the walls? Hence the psalmist too cries out to the Lord: Your commandment is exceedingly broad. Seeking the Lord and Savior in these streets, the bride says in the Song of Songs: I will rise and go about the city, in the market and in its streets, until I find him whom my soul has loved. So there shall dwell, or sit, old men and old women, of whom it is written — if, however, anyone is pleased to receive the book —: Honorable old age is not that of long time, nor is it reckoned by the number of years. But grey hairs are men's prudence, and a spotless life is old age; of such the Lord also speaks to Moses: Appoint with you seventy elders, whom you yourself know to be elders; for which reason, too, before Abraham no one was called an old man, of whom we read it written: Abraham, failing, died, nourished in a good old age, an old man and full of days. For grey hairs are the glory of old men, of which it is said: A man's grey hairs are his wisdom. These, for the multitude of their days, shall hold rods and staffs in their hands, and shall say to their disciples: What do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in a spirit of gentleness and meekness? For he who brings forth wisdom from his lips strikes the senseless man with a rod. And on the contrary: He who spares his staff hates his son, but he who loves him corrects him diligently. And not only old men but old women too shall sit in the streets of Jerusalem, whom Paul describes with apostolic mouth: Honor, he says, widows who are truly widows, and in another place: Let a widow be chosen not less than sixty years of age, who has been the wife of one husband, having testimony in good works; if she has brought up children, if she has received guests, if she has washed the feet of the saints, if she has ministered to those placed in tribulations, if she has pursued every good work.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.5":["Old men and old women of this sort shall sit in the streets of Jerusalem, and shall hold staffs in their hands, and the streets of the city shall be filled with boys and girls playing. These are the boys and girls, the old men and the young, whom the psalmist exhorts to sing to the Lord, saying: Young men and maidens, old men with the younger, let them praise the name of the Lord. And John, the evangelist and apostle: I write, he says, to you, children, because your sins are forgiven you for the name of the Savior. I write to you, fathers, because you have known him who is from the beginning. And of these Solomon speaks in Proverbs: To give shrewdness to the innocent; and to the younger boy sense and understanding. And again: Hear, children, the instruction of a father, and attend, that you may know understanding. Of these boys, little maidens and girls, Psalm 44 (45) also speaks: There shall be brought, he says, to the king virgins after her, according to what is written: The daughters of Judah exulted and rejoiced in all your judgments, O Lord. And when they hear from the Apostle: Rejoice, again I say rejoice, they will show the gladness of the mind by the gesture of the body, and, leaping in the dance, they will say with David: I will dance and play in the sight of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.6":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: If it shall seem difficult in the eyes of the remnant of this people in those days, shall it be difficult in my eyes? says the Lord of hosts. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: If it shall be impossible before the remnant of this people in those days, shall it also be impossible before me? says the Lord Almighty. At each of the words and sentences in which prosperity is promised to Israel, and which, because of the greatness of the things, seem almost incredible, the prophet sets forth: Thus says the Lord Almighty; saying this in other words: Do not think that the things I pledge are mine, and do not disbelieve them as though they came from a man; the promises I unfold are God's. He had said above that old women and old men would sit in the streets and, for the length of their time, would hold staffs in their hands; that the streets would be thronged with a multitude of people; that boys and girls would lead dances as on feast days; and that Jerusalem was to be built up and restored to the state of its former happiness. This seemed incredible to the remnant of the people, who had come from the captivity, as they saw the city utterly abandoned, and the ruins of the walls and the burnt houses displaying the hands of Babylon. Therefore he joins to it: If to you who are the remnant of the captive people what I pledge seems either difficult or impossible, that in those days in which Jerusalem is to be built there should be such happiness, will it be either difficult or impossible in the sight of the Lord, who by my mouth promises that these things shall be? For the things that are impossible with men are possible with God. We have seen these things fulfilled in the churches of Christ in the time of persecution, when the ferocity of the persecutors was roused to such madness that they even destroyed our meeting places and handed the divine books over to the flames, and all the islands, mines and prisons were filled with chained flocks of confessors and martyrs. Who at that time would have believed that churches would be built again by those very men who had destroyed them before? Not that they were the same men, but that the same royal power which formerly sat in ambush with the rich, and, as though by a decree of the senate, tried to extinguish the name of Christ, now at the public expense builds the basilicas of the churches and raises their highest gables, so as not only to adorn ceilings and roofs gleaming with gold, but to clothe the walls with veneers of varied marble; and the divine books which formerly it handed over to burning, it now venerates, gilded and purpled and set off with a variety of gems, for the safekeeping of the Roman state.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.7-8":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Behold, I will save my people from the land of the east and from the land of the setting of the sun. And I will bring them, and they shall dwell in the midst of Jerusalem; and they shall be my people, and I will be their God, in truth and in justice. LXX: likewise. Some of the Jews say that these things were fulfilled after Zerubbabel and Nehemiah, when the temple had been built, the walls of the city raised and the Jewish state restored, by the Maccabees and the various princes who ruled Judea down to Herod. Others relate that they are to be fulfilled at the consummation of the world under the Christ, for whom they wait in vain. But we say that they were both partly fulfilled at that time, that is, after Zerubbabel and Nehemiah, and went before as it were in types and images, when the people was brought back from captivity and dwelt in Jerusalem and was called the people of God, and the Lord was again called their God, by no means in falsehood and iniquity but in truth and justice; and that now, most fully, under the Lord and Savior in the Church, that is, in the true Jerusalem, the promise is fulfilled in deed, especially because it is said: Behold, I will save my people from the land of the east and from the land of the west; of whom the Lord also spoke in the Gospel: Many shall come from the east and the west, and shall recline in the kingdom of heaven with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. And long before, the psalmist had promised, saying: The God of gods, the Lord, has spoken, and has called the earth from the rising of the sun to its setting. Out of Zion the loveliness of his beauty. For there shall come out of Zion one to rescue and to overturn iniquities from Jacob, when from the east and the west incense is offered to the name of the Lord in every place, and a pure sacrifice, by no means in the victims of the Old Testament, but in the holiness of evangelical purity; of which incense we read elsewhere too: Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight. And what this incense is, the words that follow show: The lifting up of my hands an evening sacrifice. For after there sprang up from the rod of Jesse one who should rule the nations, and in him the nations hoped, and from the east and from the west both the first people and the last believed in the Lord, and there was made one flock, then all the nations were called to gladness and stirred to joy, as the prophet says: Rejoice, you nations, with his people. According to what is written elsewhere: All the ends of the earth shall remember and turn to the Lord, and all the families of the nations shall worship before him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.8":["And what follows, In truth and justice, signifies that the shadow of the old law departs and the truth of the Gospel comes, by no means in the justice of the Jews, but in Christian justice; for the Lord himself is truth and justice, of whom we read: Truth has risen from the earth, and justice has looked down from heaven. Hence in Psalm 14 (15) too justice and truth are named together: He who walks without blemish and works justice, and speaks truth in his heart.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.9":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Let your hands be strengthened, you who hear in these days these words through the mouth of the prophets, on the day on which the house of the Lord of hosts was founded, that the temple might be built. The Seventy translated \"the Lord Almighty\" for \"the Lord of hosts\", the rest likewise. The temple having been built under Zerubbabel and Joshua (for it was in the fourth year of King Darius, on the fourth day of the ninth month, which is called Chislev), all these things are said when the temple had already been raised; the same prophets, Haggai and Zechariah, who had exhorted both the leaders and the people that it might be built, now exhort them, so that from the truth of the earlier things they may lend faith to those which are promised for the future; and that they may strengthen their hands, in no way fearing the onset of the Medes and the plots of the surrounding nations who desire to hinder them; and that they may be strengthened through the mouth of the prophets, from the day on which the foundations of the temple were laid until the day on which the temple was built up on them, and may hear what follows. We explain the history briefly, referring spiritually to the Church whatever is said of Jerusalem and of the temple: in the Church hands are strengthened through good works, and houses are founded when the foundations of faith are laid, and the temple is raised when the multitude of believers is made firm, and lives in such a way that it deserves to be the temple of God.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.10":["For before those days there was no wage for men, nor was there a wage for beasts, nor was there peace for him who came in and went out, because of the tribulation; and I let loose all men, each one against his neighbor. LXX: For before those days the wage of men shall not be for gain, and the wage of beasts shall not stand, and for him who goes out and him who comes in there shall be no peace because of the tribulation; and I will send forth all men, each one to his neighbor. The Seventy referred everything to future time, but it is better referred to the past, as it stands in the Hebrew, and the truth of the exposition will bear this out. Before the house of the Lord was founded and the temple of the Lord was built, all your labor was in vain. And men as well as beasts, in farming, in trading and in various works, were frustrated in empty efforts; enemies abroad and sedition at home disturbed the peace, and everywhere there was a standstill of public business because of the frequency of wars and the plots at home, while brother keeps no faith with brother, and all kinship is hostile. Haggai the prophet, embracing this sense in other words, says: And now set your hearts from this day and upward, before stone was laid upon stone in the temple of the Lord. When you came to a heap of twenty measures, and they became ten; and you went in to the winepress to press out fifty flagons, and they became twenty. I struck you with a burning wind and with blight, and with hail all the works of your hands, and there was none among you who would return to me, says the Lord; and this we can take both of the Church and of each one of the believers. For before the foundations of the house of God are laid in us, and we are built up as a temple for God, and hear from the Apostle: You are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you; whatever good work we seemed to have, whether as rational beings, who are called men, or as simple ones, who are called beasts — For men and beasts, he says, you will save, O Lord — has no wage with God; and there are wars and discords in us, and tribulation everywhere, and we are without the peace of Christ, which, as he went to the Father, he left to the apostles; and the Lord's saying is fulfilled in us: A man's enemies are those of his own household. For every brother supplants with supplanting; and every friend walks deceitfully, and a man mocks his brother and does not speak the truth; their tongue has learned to speak a lie. But if we turn to Christ and are made his temple, at once we shall hear the Apostle proclaiming: Each one shall receive his own wage according to his work.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.11-12":["But now I will not deal with the remnant of this people as in the former days, says the Lord of hosts, but there shall be a seed of peace. The vine shall give its fruit and the earth shall give its shoot; and the heavens shall give their dew, and I will cause the remnant of this people to possess all these things. LXX: And now I will not deal with the remnant of this people as in the former days, says the Lord Almighty, but I will show peace. The vine shall give its fruit and the earth shall give its shoots; and heaven shall give its dew, and I will cause the remnant of my people to possess all these things. Before the foundations of the house of God were laid and the temple was built, there was no wage for men, nor a wage for beasts, nor, because of tribulation and distress, peace for those who came in and went out, and all men were at variance among themselves with hostile hatred. But now, since the foundations of the house of the Lord have already been laid and the temple has been built, I will by no means do as I had done before to those who have returned from the Babylonian captivity; but there shall be peace and joy everywhere, and the abundance to come will make up for the drought and famine of the former time. For the vine shall give its fruit, and all the winepresses shall be filled, the earth shall be clothed with glad crops, and with the rains and the night dew watering it, all things shall sprout; all that I have said, I will cause the remnant of my people to possess; the foundations of the house of the Lord have been laid, and the temple has been raised. The prophet Haggai too speaks these same things at the same time, who had said above: I struck you with a burning wind and with blight and with hail all the works of your hands. After the foundations of the temple were laid: Set, he says, in your hearts from this day and into the future, from the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month, from the day on which the foundations of the temple were laid, set it upon your heart. Is the seed already in the shoot, and has the vine and the fig and the pomegranate and the olive tree yet flowered? From this day I will bless, and again I will move heaven and earth alike. Let us also say it another way, following the explanation begun concerning the Church. Before anyone receives the faith of Christ, and the foundations of the Holy Spirit are laid in him, no one will be able to hear: There is a wage for your work. Whether he be a Jew, or a heretic, or a Gentile, whatever good work he has done, unless he has done it in the name of Christ, he will not have the wage of his good work. We see the virgins of the heretics, the rigor of the philosophers, the observance of the Jews in the variety of foods, and yet we say, according to Haggai, that they eat and are not satisfied; they drink and are not made drunk; they are covered and are not warmed; and he who gathers wages puts them into a bag with holes. But after they have received the faith of Christ, both those and these who had been sinners in the Church, and for the greatness of their offenses had been handed over to the captivity of this world and burned by the Babylonian fire, and have heard the Lord preaching: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to proclaim release to the captives, and sight to the blind, to heal those who are of a contrite heart, and when that which is said through Amos has been fulfilled in them: I will turn back the captivity of my people Israel, and they shall build the ruined cities, and I will plant them, then in those days justice shall rise, and abundance of peace.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.12":["The vine shall give its fruit, the vine that says in the Gospel: I am the vine, you are the branches. Every branch that abides in me, the Father cleanses, that it may bear greater fruit. And when its branches have been cleansed, that is, its shoots and layers, and with budding eyes have begun to promise the hope of fruits to come, so that what is written may be fulfilled: The flowering vines have given forth their fragrance, then the sun of justice has colored the hanging clusters, so that, sent into the winepresses of Psalms 8 and 83 (84), which are entitled for the winepresses, they may be trodden by the foot of the Lord, who comes up from Bozrah, that he may pour out the wine that gladdens the heart of man. The earth too shall give its shoot, not the dry and stony earth full of thorns, but the good earth which yields fruit a hundredfold and sixtyfold and thirtyfold, so that those who have sown in tears may reap in joy. But the heavens too shall give their dew, the heavens of which it is written in Psalm 18 (19): The heavens tell the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims the works of his hands. Of these heavens it is said in the song of Deuteronomy: Rejoice, O heavens, with him, that is, with the Lord and Savior, whom he foreknew and predestined to be conformed to the image of his Son; it is he who speaks with Moses: Let my speech be awaited like the rain, and let my words come down like the dew. For those who were dead in sins shall rise again, and those who lay in whitewashed sepulchres, which are full of dead men's bones, shall be raised up, and those who dwell in the earth shall rejoice. And what the cause of the joy is, the following words show: For the dew which is from you is their healing. And all these things, namely peace and the fruit of the vines, and the abundance of the lands, which has grown up from the dew of the heavens, the remnant of my people shall possess, of whom Isaiah speaks: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like Gomorrah. For the remnant according to the election of grace has been saved, not by works, otherwise grace would not be grace.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.13-15":["But concerning the Church and each one of the believers it can be understood thus: that in the time of persecution the Christians were a curse and an example to all the nations round about, because they had offended their Lord; and afterwards they are in blessing, when peace has been restored; and all this comes about because the Lord, who had at first been angry, afterwards has mercy on Jerusalem, the vision of peace, and on Judah, which confesses the faith of the Lord. Each of the believers, too, who are cast out of the Church for their vices and are handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that they may learn not to blaspheme, when they have done repentance, will return to their former state and will see the peace of the Lord, and will possess the glory of their confession.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.13":["And it shall be: as you were a curse among the nations, O house of Judah and house of Israel, so I will save you and you shall be a blessing: do not fear, let your hands be strengthened. For thus says the Lord of hosts: As I purposed to afflict you, when your fathers had provoked me to anger, says the Lord, and I did not have mercy, so, having turned, I have purposed in these days to do good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah: do not fear. LXX: And it shall be: as you were in a curse among the nations, house of Judah and house of Israel, so I will save you, and you shall be in a blessing; take courage and be strengthened in your hearts, for thus says the Lord Almighty. As I purposed to afflict you in that your fathers had provoked me to anger, says the Lord Almighty, and I did not repent, so I am ready and have purposed in these days to do good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah: take courage. He promises these things as to come after the building of the temple: that as the house of Judah and the house of Israel, namely the two and the ten tribes, were among all the nations a curse and a hissing and an example, so, when they have been saved and have returned to Judea, they may be a blessing to all. Do not fear, he says, the adversaries who rebel; be confident that what the Lord promises through me is true, let your hands be strengthened, finish the works which you have begun.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.14-15":["The cause of the strengthening is the Lord's promise: For thus says the Lord Almighty, to whom nothing is impossible, who is able to fulfil what he promises: As I devised to afflict you and to hand you over to captivity, because your fathers provoked me to anger, and I did not have mercy, which the LXX translated οὐ μετενόησα, that is, I did not repent, which in Hebrew is written ulo naamathi; but I did not have mercy, so that I might correct you by captivity and train you through every torment and scourge; so now, in the present time, I have purposed to do good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah. And it is to be noted that when he is angry, the house of Judah and the house of Israel, that is, all twelve tribes which were handed over to captivity, are a curse among the nations. But after he has purposed to do good, he does not do it to Judah and Israel, that is, to the two and the ten tribes, namely to Jerusalem and Samaria, Oholah and Oholibah; but, leaving Israel in captivity, he does good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah. And he adds at the end: Do not fear, or take courage, in the sense which we have explained above.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.14":["What is written, I did not have mercy, or I did not repent, the heretics slander, as though the Lord were either cruel or changeable, if he either does not repent or repents. For if he has repented, they say that he is changeable; if he has not repented, they assert that he is cruel. Yet God repented that he had anointed Saul as king. And among the Ninevites, to whom he had announced through the prophet: Yet three days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown, because they did repentance, he himself too is said to have changed his sentence, not through the fault of an improvident mind, but from the difference in what they do, badly or well. For if they do evil, he threatens; if they have blotted out their former sins, he has mercy; God is not changed, who is one and the same and cannot be changed, but when they have changed from evil to good works, he himself also changes his own decree. He it is who also speaks in Genesis: The cry of Sodom and Gomorrah has been multiplied, and their sins are exceedingly great; I will go down, therefore, and see whether they are fulfilling it according to their cry which has come to me; and if not, that I may know. What he said is this: If they persist in their fury, punishment will not be lacking to the sinners; if they cease from their madness, they will become most worthy of my knowledge. For the Lord knows those who are his. And the Apostle writes to the Galatians: But then, not knowing God. And although God knows all, and nothing past, nor present, nor future is hidden from him, in the Gospel he says that he does not know the wicked: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you. Therefore let us take both the knowledge and the repentance, and the wrath and indignation, and all the affections of God, not according to the defect of human speech, but according to the sense of the divine majesty.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.16":["These, then, are the words which you shall do: Speak the truth, each one with his neighbor; judge truth and the judgment of peace in your gates; and let none of you devise evil in your hearts against his friend, and do not love a lying oath; for all these are things that I hate, says the Lord. LXX: These are the words which you shall do: Speak the truth, each one with his neighbor, and a peaceable judgment, judge in your gates, and let each one not devise evil against his neighbor in your hearts, and do not love a lying oath, for all these things I hate, says the Lord Almighty. I have promised that I will not deal with the remnant of the people of the captives as in the former days. And as I purposed to afflict them, when their fathers had provoked me to anger, and I did not have mercy, so now, having turned, I have purposed in these days to do good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah. So that my sentence, then, may stand, and the promise may not be made void, do these things which I command: Speak the truth with your neighbors. Let us take neighbor to mean the whole race of men, since we have been begotten of one parent; otherwise, if neighbor is taken to mean kinsman, we must lie to strangers and foreigners. The Apostle says this same thing: Putting away lying, speak the truth, each one with his neighbor. Truth, he says, and the judgment of peace judge in your gates. In judgment let truth and justice come first; then mercy follows. For this is the judgment of peace: that the judge should have as his purpose to make peace between those at variance, according to that saying of the Gospel: Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. And what follows, In your gates, agrees with that prophetic saying: They hated him who reproves in the gates, and they abhorred the holy word. And in another place: They shall not be put to shame when they speak with their enemies in the gate. David too used to judge in the gates, when Absalom, promising the truth of judgment, was laying a trap for his father. And it is asked why among the Jews the place of judging was in the gates. So that the farmers should not be forced to enter the cities and undergo some loss, the judges sat in the gates, so that they might hear townsmen and countrymen alike at the going out and coming in of the city, and when the business was finished, each might at once return to his own dwelling.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.17":["And do not devise evil, he says, against your friends in your hearts; raath, which all with one voice have translated κακίαν, that is, evil, we can take in two ways, both for affliction and for wickedness. For affliction: If there is evil in a city which the Lord has not done, and: Sufficient for the day is its own evil. For wickedness, God speaks in the prophet Jonah: The cry of their evil has gone up to me, and in the Apostle we read: Filled with all iniquity and evil. In both ways, therefore, he who is holy neither afflicts his friend nor devises evil against him in his heart. And do not love, he says, a lying oath; for the Lord commands in the Gospel: But I say to you, do not swear at all, but let your speech be, yes yes, no no; for he who does not swear will never be able to swear falsely. Let him who swears hear what is written: You shall not take up the name of the Lord your God in a vain thing. All these are things that I hate, says the Lord, according to the words of Malachi, who says: And all the things that I hated, you did. In precepts which pertain to life and are plain, we ought not to look for allegory, lest, as the Comic poet says, we seek a knot in a bulrush.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.19":["And the word of the Lord of hosts came to me, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: The fast of the fourth, and the fast of the fifth, and the fast of the seventh, and the fast of the tenth shall be to the house of Judah for joy and for gladness, and for glorious solemn feasts; only love truth and peace. LXX: And the word of the Lord Almighty came to me, saying: Thus says the Lord Almighty: The fourth fast, and the fifth fast, and the seventh fast, and the tenth fast shall be to the house of Judah for joy and for gladness, and for good festivals; — for in Hebrew this is said tobim, that is, ἀγαθάς —, and rejoice, and love truth and peace. To what Sharezer and Regem-melech had asked above through envoys, whether they ought to fast and lament in the fifth month and in the seventh month, as it stands in the LXX, or after the building of the temple to end the fast and lay aside their mourning, the prophet, after many things have been set out in between which they were to do and which they were to hope for, answered in the person of the Lord: The fast of the fourth, and the fast of the fifth, and the fast of the seventh, and the fast of the tenth — for month is understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (from the shared word) —, to the house of Judah, and to Jerusalem, shall be turned into festal days and joy. God seeks only truth and peace. On this passage many of our writers have said many things, and things at variance with one another. Some, confessing the obscurity by their silence, have passed over it in their commentaries as though it were a very deep pit, judging it more right to say nothing at all than to say too little. We are therefore compelled to have recourse to the Hebrews, and to seek the truth of knowledge from the spring rather than from the streams, especially since this is not some prophecy of Christ, where they are wont to dodge and to hide the truth with a lie, but the order of a history woven from what precedes and what follows. The fast of the fourth month, which among the Latins is called July, on the seventeenth day of that same month, they think was the day when Moses, coming down from Mount Sinai, threw down and broke the tablets of the law; and, according to Jeremiah, the walls of the city were first breached. In the fifth month, which among the Latins is called August, when because of the spies of the holy land a sedition had arisen among the people, they were ordered not to go up the mountain but for forty years to go round toward the holy land by long and wasteful wanderings, so that, except for two, Caleb and Joshua, all fell in the wilderness. In this month the temple at Jerusalem was burned and destroyed, both by Nebuchadnezzar and, many ages later, by Titus and Vespasian; the city of Bether was taken, to which many thousands of the Jews had fled; the temple was ploughed up by Turannius Rufus to the disgrace of the oppressed nation. And in the seventh, which among us is called October, as we said above, Gedaliah was slain, and the remnant of the tribe of Judah and of Jerusalem was scattered. Let us read Jeremiah. In the tenth month, which among us is called January, because it is the door of the year and its beginning, Ezekiel, set in captivity, heard, and all the people of the captives, that in the fifth month the temple had been overthrown, as we learn most fully in that same prophet. This, then, is the whole of what is said: The days of lamentation and of fasts which until now you have kept in mourning, know that, since I have purposed to do good to Jerusalem and to the house of Judah, they are to be turned for you into gladness and joy and solemn feasts; provided only that you love truth and peace. According to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy): since we fast when the bridegroom is taken away from us and we do not deserve to have his presence, when the Lord has returned to us and has purposed to do good to us, all sadness shall be turned into joy; and the former famine of the word of God shall be made up for by the presence of his teachings and the fullness of the heavenly bread.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.20-22":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Until peoples come, and dwell in many cities, and the inhabitants go one to another, saying: Let us go and entreat the face of the Lord, and let us seek the Lord of hosts. I too will go, and many peoples and strong nations shall come to seek the Lord of hosts in Jerusalem, and to entreat the face of the Lord. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Many peoples shall yet come, and the inhabitants of many cities; and those who dwell in the cities shall be gathered into one city, saying: Let us go to entreat the face of the Lord, and let us seek the countenance of the Lord Almighty. I too will go, and many peoples and very many nations shall come, to seek the face of the Lord Almighty in Jerusalem, and to entreat the face of the Lord. The fast of the fourth, and of the fifth, and of the seventh, and of the tenth month shall be changed into excellent solemn feasts, so much so that the cities of Judea which before were deserted shall be thronged with many inhabitants; and one city shall go to another, and they shall exhort one another and say: Because for these seventy years the ways of Zion mourned, since there was no one to go to the solemn feast, all her gates deserted, and her priests groaning, now that peace has been restored, let us go to Jerusalem, where it is commanded in the law that we should sacrifice victims, and that three times in the year every male of ours should appear in the sight of the Lord; and when one city says to another: Let us go and entreat the face of the Lord, and let us seek the Lord Almighty, the other city will answer: I too will go: at which time many peoples and countless and strong nations shall come, to offer sacrifices in Jerusalem to the Lord of hosts, and to entreat his face. For the Lord draws near to those who do not tempt him, and shows his face to those who are not unbelieving. He who has seen the Son sees the Father also; and the Lord and Savior is the image of the invisible God, not that the Son is visible and the Father invisible, but that when the Son is named, the Father is perceived. For he is by no means Father if he has no Son. Hence he himself also says in the Gospel: Father, I have manifested your name to men. What we said of Jerusalem and Zerubbabel, or of the time after Zerubbabel, is more rightly and more fully referred to Christ and to Jerusalem, which is understood as the Church; and that then from the whole world both peoples and nations will flock together to offer sacrifices in the temple of the Lord. In a time of persecution also, as we touched on briefly before, let the teachers and priests of the Church boldly promise the captives and the believers that the παροικίαι (sojourning communities) are to be built again, and the tranquillity of peace restored, and the face of the Lord entreated in the churches. We run quickly over what is plain, so as to linger over what is more obscure.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.8.23":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: In those days, in which ten men of all the tongues of the nations shall take hold, and they shall take hold of the hem of a man who is a Jew, saying: We will go with you, for we have heard that God is with you. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: In those days, if ten men of all the tongues of the nations take hold, and grasp the hem of a man who is a Jew, saying: We will go with you, because we have heard that God is with you. Some of the Jews say that these things were fulfilled under Zerubbabel and after Zerubbabel. Others put them off to a future time, when they hope that the Christ will come. But we understand them both more rightly and more truly of the coming of the Lord and Savior, when he was born of the virgin Mary. In short, it is written: Until peoples come? When it says until, it signifies not the present time, in which Zerubbabel and Joshua were, but the future, when many peoples and strong nations shall come, to seek the Lord of hosts in Jerusalem, and to entreat the face of the Lord. At that time, therefore, and in those days, ten men of all the tongues of the nations shall take hold of the hem of a man who is a Jew, saying: We will go with you, for we have heard that God is with you. And in Isaiah we read: Seven women shall take hold of one man, saying: We will eat our own bread and be clothed with our own garments; only let your name be called upon us, take away our reproach. Those, then, who there are called seven women, that is, Churches, whose number is contained also in the apostle Paul — for he writes to seven churches, to the Romans, to the Corinthians, to the Galatians, to the Ephesians, to the Philippians, to the Colossians, to the Thessalonians. And in the Apocalypse of John the Lord, girded with variety and with purest gold, walks in the midst of the seven lampstands, that is, of the churches of the Ephesians, the Smyrnaeans, the Pergamenes, the Thyatirans, the Sardians, the Philadelphians, the Laodiceans —, are now named ten in the prophet Zechariah, whom the Lord also sought, so that, if he had found them in Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim, he might deliver them from destruction. For the letter iota, with which the name of the Savior takes its beginning, signifies the number ten not only among the Greeks but also among the Hebrews. And by this mystical language it is shown that all who are counted under the name of Christian, whom the Lord also says he left, seven thousand, in the time of the persecution of Jezebel and the flight of Elijah, who did not bow the knee before Baal, and who have come to the measure of the perfect man out of all tongues and nations, shall take hold of the hem of a man who is a Jew, that is, of the Lord and Savior, of whom it is also said in the psalm: Judah is my king, and: Judah, your brothers shall praise you, and again: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he shall be the expectation of the nations. For there shall be the root of Jesse, and he who shall rise up to rule the nations, in him the nations shall hope. And when they have taken hold of him, they will long to cling to his footsteps, since God is with him. Or else, whoever out of all tongues and nations have believed shall take hold of a man who is a Jew, the apostles who are of the Jews, and shall say: Let us go with you; for we have heard through the prophets, and have learned by the voice of all the Scriptures, that the Son of God, Christ, God and Lord, is with you. Where the prophecy is most manifest, and the coming of Christ and of his apostles and the faith of all the nations are proclaimed, let us seek nothing further. As for our having said that the number seven thousand pertains to the name of Christians, reckon in Greek ἑπτάκεις χιλίους (seven thousand) and χριστειανούς (Christians), and you will find the same number and sum, that is, one thousand nine hundred and forty-one. But they also refer to the mystery of this number the parable of the ten virgins in the Gospel, whom we interpret of the senses of the flesh and of the spirit, if they have prepared the oil of good works for their lamps, and have doubled the number five, so as to be holy, according to the Apostle, in body and in spirit. So too the ten cities, which he who has governed the senses of the body excellently shall receive in the future, the number doubled.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.1":["The burden of the word of the Lord in the land of Hadrach, and of Damascus his rest, for the eye of man is the Lord's, and of all the tribes of Israel. LXX: The taking up of the word of the Lord in the land of Sedrach and of Damascus his sacrifice, for the Lord looks upon men, and all the tribes of Israel. This whole vision, or weight and burden of the word of the Lord, as Aquila translated it, pertains to the calling of the nations and the building of the Church. And the order of the words is: The taking up of the word of the Lord, sharp toward sinners, soft toward the just. For Hadrach, a name compounded of two whole words, sounds this: ad meaning sharp, rach soft and tender, and not, as it is wrongly read by some, Sedrach. Some refer Hadrach to the people of the Jews, Damascus to the calling of the nations. Hence the people, or the taking up of the word of the Lord, comes about in the land of Hadrach, over which the Lord exercised both his severity and his clemency: severity toward those who would not believe, clemency toward those who returned with the apostles; but Damascus is properly the rest of the Lord, according to what is written in Isaiah: And his rest shall be honor. For before the child knew how to choose good or evil and to call father and mother, he received the strength of Damascus and the spoils of Samaria. Hence Damascus too is rendered in our language drinking blood, or blood of sackcloth, so that the first interpretation signifies a bloodthirsty people, the second repentance joined to its cruelty. And what follows: For the eye of man is the Lord's and of all the tribes of Israel, they expound thus: For this reason the temple of God is to be built from both peoples, that is, both from the land of Hadrach and from Damascus his rest, because whoever looks to God and hopes in him, both from the nations and from all the tribes of Israel, is the Lord's; or, according to the LXX, because the Lord looks equally upon all men, and upon all the tribes of Israel. Let us also say it another way: In the land of Hadrach is the burden of the word of the Lord, and a most heavy weight. But in Damascus are his victims and sacrifice, for the Lord looks upon both the faith of the nations and the faithlessness of the circumcision without any respect of persons, and is equally the God of all, laying the heavy weight of his sentence upon some, and resting in the seat of others. The Jews say that all these things are to be fulfilled at the coming of the Christ, whom they promise themselves in the last time, so that both the land of Hadrach and Damascus, and Hamath and Tyre, and Sidon and Ashkelon and Gaza, Ekron also and Ashdod and the Philistines, shall bow their necks to a most mighty king and serve him as he dwells in Jerusalem, and that no one shall dare to raise a hand against Israel, all around being at peace. All of which we show to have been accomplished spiritually at the coming of the Lord and in his Churches.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.2-3":["But Tyre and Sidon — of which the one is interpreted συνοχὴ, that is, distress, the other θήρευμα, that is, hunting —, took to themselves wisdom to such a degree that, with its sophisms and the nets of dialectic and the webs of the sophists, which Scripture calls fortifications, Tyre put riddles to Solomon, that is, to the peaceful king; and gathered its silver, namely the brilliance of eloquence, and its gold, the cunning of its thoughts, like the dust and mud that lie in the streets and roads, so as to possess the kingdom of idolatry that it had fortified. But against these fortifications the Apostle speaks: For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty to God for the destruction of fortifications, destroying reasonings and every height that exalts itself against the knowledge of God. And under the figure of Nineveh, which is interpreted adorned, or beautiful, for nothing is more adorned than the world, the word of God is directed through the prophet against the men of the world: And you shall be made drunk, and shall be despised, and you shall seek help from the enemy; all your fortifications are like a fig tree with its early figs: if they are shaken, they shall fall into the mouth of the eater.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.2":["Hamath also on its borders, and Tyre and Sidon, for they have taken to themselves exceeding wisdom. And Tyre built her fortification, and heaped up silver like dust, and gold like the mud of the streets. Behold, the Lord shall possess her, and shall strike her strength in the sea, and she shall be devoured by fire. LXX: And Hamath on its borders, Tyre and Sidon, since they were exceedingly wise, and Tyre built her fortification, and gathered silver like dust, and gold like the mud of the roads. Therefore the Lord shall possess her, and shall strike her strength in the sea, and she shall be burned up in fire. Hamath, which is interpreted χόλος, that is, wrath, is the same city that received the name Epiphania from Antiochus Ἐπιφανεῖ (Epiphanes), and is now a city of Coele Syria. This one, therefore, and Tyre and Sidon shall be within the borders of Damascus, or within the borders of the land of Hadrach, so as to believe in the Lord and Savior, to whom it was said by the Father: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession. The daughters of this Tyre, that is, the souls that believe in Christ, shall bring gifts to the king, that what is written may be fulfilled: The daughters of Tyre shall worship him with gifts. And when the city of God, the Church, has been built, of which the psalmist sings: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, at once the rest will follow: Behold the foreigners, and Tyre, and the people of the Ethiopians, these were born there. Of the foreigners it is said in two psalms: Foreigners serve me, or are subject. Of the Ethiopians we read in the prophet Zephaniah: From beyond the rivers of Ethiopia they shall bring gifts to me, and in the psalm: Ethiopia shall stretch out her hands first to God. And of Tyre the Gospel speaks: Woe to you, Chorazin, woe to you, Bethsaida; for if the mighty works that were done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, perhaps they would long ago have repented in sackcloth and ashes. From the borders of Tyre and Sidon that Canaanite woman, the Syrophoenician, whose daughter was badly afflicted by a demon, came to meet the Lord and Savior; and she dedicated the first fruits of the faith of the Tyrians and Sidonians, so that those who had not believed in the time of Christ, because they had not yet seen the signs and mighty works, should afterwards see them through the apostles and believe, and should share in that blessedness which the Lord promises, saying: More blessed are those who have not seen and have believed.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.4":["And so the Lord shall possess both Tyre and Sidon, when he has first struck their strength in the most salt and most bitter sea of this world; and, lest anything of their former wisdom remain in them, that is, of the swollen folly through which they procured for themselves feeble fortifications, and silver and gold like mud, he will smelt them with fire and cleanse them to purity; of which the Savior speaks in the Gospel: I came to cast fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it were kindled, according to what is written in Mark: Everyone shall be salted with fire.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.5":["Ashkelon shall see and fear, and Gaza, and shall grieve exceedingly; and Ekron, because her hope is confounded, and the king shall perish from Gaza, and Ashkelon shall not be inhabited. And the separator shall sit in Ashdod, and I will destroy the pride of the Philistines. And I will take away his blood from his mouth and his abominations from between his teeth, and he too shall be left to our God, and he shall be as a leader in Judah, and Ekron as the Jebusite. And I will surround my house with those who fight as soldiers for me, going and returning, and the exactor shall no more pass over them, for now I have seen with my eyes. LXX: Ashkelon shall see and fear, and Gaza, and shall grieve exceedingly; and Ekron, because she is confounded in her hope, and the king shall perish from Gaza, and Ashkelon shall not be inhabited. And foreigners shall dwell in Ashdod, and I will destroy the insolence of the foreigners, and I will take away their blood from their mouth, and their abominations from between their teeth, and these too shall remain to our God, and they shall be as a χιλίαρχος, that is, tribune, in Judah, and Ekron as the Jebusite; and I will set up a height in my house, so that no one may pass through, or return, and no driver-off and towering one shall come upon them any more, for now I have seen with my eyes. Ashkelon is interpreted ignoble fire, or weighed; Gaza, strong, or rule; Ekron, barren, or uprooted; Ashdod, which in Hebrew is called Esdod, generating fire, or fire of the paternal uncle, or fire of the breast; Jebusite sounds trodden down. We have set out the etymologies of the names in order to run briefly through the sense. Ashkelon and Gaza and Ekron, seeing that Hamath was within the borders of Damascus, and that Tyre and Sidon, after they were struck round about, and all their hay, wood and stubble burned up in the conflagration, were possessed by the Lord; they too, terrified by fear and grief and confusion, began to hope for better things. In short, Ashkelon, in which there was before the ignoble fire of the devil, and which had come to the full measure and weight of sins, trembled with dread because she ceased to have inhabitants. And Gaza grieved exceedingly, doing penance for her former crimes, she who had once been strong and hard, untamable, and had promised herself the rule over all, because her king and princes, the contrary word and the power of the enemy, had lost their rule. Ekron too, barren because without the law and the knowledge of God she had no sons, was uprooted, so that she might hear that prophetic word: Rejoice, barren one who do not bear; break forth and cry, you who are not in labor, for many more are the sons of the desolate than of her who has a husband.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.6":["And when Ashkelon, Gaza and Ekron have been terrified, and have grieved because they either had no inhabitants, or lost their king, or their former hope was frustrated, foreigners shall sit in Ashdod, where the fire generates which the Lord sent upon the earth and desires to be kindled. For he baptizes in the Holy Spirit and fire, where there is the cousin and the paternal uncle, ἀδελφὶδος καὶ πατράδελφος, whom the bride desires in the Song of Songs; where there is the fire of the breast and of the most bounteous bosom, of which we read in the same Song: He shall abide between my breasts, and in the Apostle: I gave you milk to drink, not solid food. Where according to the LXX we said: And foreigners shall dwell in Ashdod, in the Hebrew is read, the mamzer shall dwell, or shall sit, in Ashdod, for which we have put: And the separator shall sit in Ashdod. Understand the separator to be the Lord, who separates the wheat from the chaff, and the good fish from the bad fish, and distinguishes silver and gold from filth and dross. And when he has done this, he promises the rest also: I will destroy the insolence, or the pride of the Philistines, for whom the LXX translated foreigners. The Philistines are interpreted in our language falling by the cup, because they drank from the cup of Babylon and, being drunk, fell down. These therefore in the time of the calling of the nations and the coming of Christ shall not have pride, but shall follow the humble and meek Jesus.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.7":["And he shall take away from their mouth the blood, words of blasphemy, and the abominations, the worship of idols and the eating of things sacrificed to idols, from between their teeth, so that, after these have been taken away, the Philistines themselves, that is, the foreigners, may be left to the Lord, and may be a leader in Judah, that is, among the people that confesses the Lord, so that the former people which was at the head may be turned into the tail, and the last, which was at the tail, may pass to the head; and that Ekron, once barren and for that reason uprooted, may be as Jebus, that is, as Jerusalem. For this city is called by three names, Jebus, Salem and Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.8":["And I will surround, he says, my house, that is, the Church, with those who fight as soldiers for me, that is, with those who serve me in various mysteries, and at my command run hither and thither, going and coming back. Or: I will surround my house with the protection of the angels, of whom it is also written in another place: The angel of the Lord shall send round about those who fear him, and shall deliver them, so that there may be no one who goes and returns, that is, who plots ambushes against my people. Nor shall the exactor pass over it any more, of whom Isaiah says: The exactor has ceased, or else ἐξελαύνων, that is, one who leads out of doors, and drags the bound into captivity, for with his eyes, which we can understand as the prophets and all the saints, the Lord saw the calling of the nations and the security of the Church.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.9":["Moreover, since the prophet Zechariah exhorts Zion and Jerusalem to a similar gladness, and Matthew says that this very prophecy was fulfilled in the first coming of Christ, we are compelled by necessity, or rather led by the very order of truth, to hope for what is said in Zephaniah not as future but as done. For it is written in Zechariah: Rejoice greatly, daughter of Zion; proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem. Behold, your king comes to you, just and a savior, himself humble and mounted upon a beast of burden and a new colt.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Exult greatly, daughter of Zion; shout for joy, daughter of Jerusalem. Behold, your king will come to you, just and a savior, himself poor and riding upon a she-ass, and upon a colt, the foal of a she-ass. And I will destroy the chariot from Ephraim, and the horse from Jerusalem, and the bow of war shall be scattered, and he shall speak peace to the nations, and his power shall be from sea to sea, and from the rivers to the ends of the earth. LXX: Rejoice exceedingly, daughter of Zion; proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem: Behold, your king will come to you, just and saving, himself meek and riding upon a beast of burden and a new colt; and he will destroy the chariots from Ephraim, and the horse from Jerusalem, and he will destroy the bows of the fighters and the multitude, and peace from the nations, and he shall rule over the waters as far as the sea, and the rivers, the outlets of the earth. The evangelists write that this prophecy was fulfilled when the Lord entered Jerusalem, sitting upon a she-ass and the foal of a she-ass, and a crowd of children with branches of palms came to meet him, crying: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, hosanna in the highest; and when the Pharisees rebuked him for not checking the shouting children, he answered: Have you not read: Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings you have perfected praise? Zion therefore exults and Jerusalem shouts for joy, one and the same city — for Zion is the citadel of Jerusalem —, because her king comes to her, who was promised by the oracles of all the prophets. Just, and himself the Savior, that is, Jesus, as the angel interpreted it, speaking to the virgin: And he shall be called Jesus, because he himself shall save his people from their sins. Poor also, or, as the LXX translated, meek, he who, though he was rich, became poor for our sakes; and he says in the Gospel: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart. And riding upon a she-ass, a beast of burden, or upon a new colt, that is, upon both peoples, of the circumcision and of the foreskin, of which the former had borne the most heavy yoke of the law; as it is written in the Acts of the Apostles: Neither we nor our fathers were able to bear the heavy yoke of the law. Hence Paul also writes to the Galatians, who wished to be circumcised: Stand fast, and do not be held again by the yoke of slavery. But the new colt, the multitude of the Gentiles, having no bridle of the law, nor guided by anyone, but always dashed down and broken among the precipices and in the abysses of idolatry, learned by the Lord's sitting upon it to walk and to enter upon the right way.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Now this was done that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled, who says: Say to the daughter of Zion: Behold, your king comes to you, meek, sitting upon an ass and a colt, the foal of a beast of burden. This is written in the prophet Zechariah, of whom it will be spoken more fully in its own place, if span of life is granted. For now we must touch briefly on this: that according to the letter, in the short stretch of the journey, he did not rest sitting upon both animals. For either he sat on the ass and the colt was without a rider, or, if he made use of the colt for sitting (which is more fitting), the ass was led along free. Therefore, since the history involves either impossibility or unseemliness, we are carried over to higher things, so that this ass, which was a beast of burden and tamed and had drawn the yoke of the law, may be understood as the synagogue, and the foal of the ass, frisky and free, as the people of the Gentiles, upon whom Jesus sat, his two disciples having been sent to them, one to the circumcision and the other to the Gentiles.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ZEC.9.10":["And I will destroy, he says, the chariot from Ephraim. It is still spoken in the person of God the Father, that the four-horse chariot, or the chariot, should perish from Ephraim, and the horse from Jerusalem. And meanwhile according to the letter he says this: There shall be no battles, all things having been set at peace by the coming and birth of Christ. Moreover, according to a higher understanding, Ephraim, which is interpreted καρποφορία, that is, fruitfulness and abundance of crops, is referred to the multitude of heresies; of which it is written in Psalm 77 (78): The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle. Of these chariots and cars we read: Some in chariots and some on horses, but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God; they are bound and have fallen, but we have risen and are set upright. The horse that is destroyed from Jerusalem is the one of which we read: A horse is a deceitful thing for safety. And in Jeremiah those who gave themselves up to luxury and lust hear: They have become to me horses mad after females; each one neighed after his neighbor's wife. Hence they are called back to repentance, as the psalmist says: Do not become like the horse and the mule, in which there is no understanding. And when God has destroyed the chariots from Ephraim and the horse from Jerusalem, those who have been set free from such horses and chariots shall be transferred into the service of the Lord, and shall become Cherubim, and of them it shall be said: The chariot of God is ten thousandfold, thousands of those who rejoice, and: To my cavalry among the chariots of Pharaoh I have likened you, my nearest one, and: You shall mount your horses, and your riding is salvation. The bow of war shall be scattered, so that the fiery darts may not be shot that could strike the hearts of the pleasure-loving. And he shall speak peace to the nations, of which it is written: He shall be the expectation of the nations, and again: In him the nations shall hope, and: His power from sea to sea, and from the rivers to the ends of the world. This is not to be weakened by allegory, but to be believed as truly fulfilled, according to what we read: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession. In Psalm 71 (72) also it is said, under the person of Solomon and of the true peaceful one: And he shall rule from sea to sea, and from the river to the ends of the earth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.11-12":["With this proviso, however, that they interpret the earlier testimony otherwise than we have explained it, and say: O Christ, whom we hope will come, and who are to reign in all the ends of the earth, by the blood of your testament, in which, according to Ezekiel, you found Jerusalem spattered with her own blood, and by which you entered into a covenant with Abraham in the dividing of the calf and the ram and the goat, you have released your people Israel from the captivity and the furnace of the Chaldeans, in whom there was no mercy. Therefore you too, O Israelites, who were prisoners and hoped in the Lord, return to most strongly fortified Jerusalem, for today you have the Lord promising you that for the brief injury of captivity you shall receive all things doubled, as we read that Job also received them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.11":["You also, by the blood of your testament, have sent forth your prisoners from the pit in which there is no water; turn to the stronghold, you prisoners of hope; today also, announcing, I will render double to you. LXX: And you, by the blood of the testament, have sent forth your prisoners from the pit that has no water; you shall sit in the stronghold, prisoners of the congregation, and for one day of your sojourning I will render double to you. After the word of the prophet, or rather God the Father Almighty himself, had announced to Zion and Jerusalem that their king was coming to them, meek and mounted upon a she-ass, a beast of burden, and upon the foal of an ass, and that his power would be from sea to sea, and from the rivers to the ends of the earth, he makes an apostrophe to Christ himself, of whom the prophecy speaks, and says: You also, by the blood of your testament, or covenant, have sent forth your prisoners from the pit in which there is no water. This is understood as follows: By the blood of your passion you have, in your clemency, set free those who were held bound in the prison of hell, in which there is no mercy. In short, after the Lord rose again, those who were held by the bonds of Adam's sins, or, as some would have it, of ingrained error, and of death, rose with him and appeared in the holy city. Of this blood of the testament he himself also spoke to the disciples, pointing to his future passion: Take and drink of this, all of you; for this is the cup of the new testament in my blood. In prefiguration of this pit which has no waters, Joseph was sent into one by his brothers, and Daniel and Jeremiah by the Chaldeans and by the people of the Jews. Benaiah too, in the time of snow and cold, went down into a pit to kill a lion there. Jeremiah, however, is plunged not into the water of the pit but into the mud and mire of the pit, which could suffocate a thirsty man rather than refresh him. Hence it is also written in the psalm: I am stuck fast in the mire of the deep, and there is no substance. In this pit of hell there abode also that rich man once clothed in purple, whose boastful tongue was burned by the fires of punishment, and who was so far from having any refreshment of waters that he begged for the refreshment of the tip of the poor man's finger dipped in water.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.12":["And again the word is directed to those very ones who were prisoners and who were to be set free by the mercy of Christ: Turn to the stronghold, you prisoners of hope. And the sense is: You who are now prisoners and are held in a harsh and terrible hell, you who hope for the loosing of your bonds at the coming of Christ, turn to the stronghold, or, you shall sit in the stronghold, of which it is written: The fear of the Lord is the bulwark of the holy one, so that you may say: Be to me a God, a protector, and a fortified place, that you may save me; and so that the prophet may also say of you: Behold, a strong city, and our salvation shall set a wall and an outer wall. But this stronghold, to which God exhorts the prisoners of hope, or the hoping prisoners of the Church, we ought to take as nothing other than the dwelling of paradise, into which the thief was the first to enter with the Lord; and for this reason they are called forth to the stronghold through Zechariah, because already then, and from that time, the Lord was promising that for a brief tribulation they would receive eternal rewards. Or, as is read in the LXX: For one day of your sojourning, I will render double to you. For in comparison with eternity, all that we suffer in the world must be called one day, a day not of dwelling but of sojourning, because we are strangers and sojourners, as all our fathers were. For what is at present momentary and light in our tribulation works in us, beyond measure, in sublimity, a weight of eternal glory, while we look not at the things that are seen, but at those that are not seen. For the things that are seen are temporal, but those that are not seen are eternal.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.13":["For I have stretched Judah for myself, as a bow I have filled Ephraim; and I will raise up your sons, O Zion, over your sons, O Greece, and I will set you as the sword of the mighty. LXX: For I have bent you, O Judah, for myself, as a bow I have filled Ephraim; and I will raise up your sons, O Zion, over the sons of the Greeks, and I will handle you as the sword of a warrior. The Jews refer this to the times of the Maccabees, who conquered the Macedonians and, after a space of three and a half years, cleansed the temple that had been defiled by idolatry. And what follows: As a bow I have filled Ephraim, they think signifies those who had come, under Hezekiah, from the ten tribes that are called Israel, over whom Josiah too is recorded to have reigned.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","According to tropology this place can be explained thus: Judah is stretched as a bow when the Lord and Savior is sent by the Father into this world, he who is himself both the bow and the archer and the arrow. The bow, as in the present place. The archer, as in Psalm 44 (45): Your arrows are sharp, most mighty; and when someone has been wounded by them, he says: I am wounded with love. And the arrow is he himself who speaks through Isaiah: He has set me as a chosen arrow, and in his quiver he has hidden me. The chosen arrow is the Word of God; the quiver in which the arrow is hidden is the dispensation of the flesh he assumed. With this Ephraim is filled, so that, when he has been armed and made ready for war, he too, together with the nations, may be wounded by the arrows of the Lord; Ephraim, from whose tribe Jeroboam first split the people, whom the prophet Hosea shows more fully to be taken in the person of heretics; and what we said above: The sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle. For when the Lord raises up the sons of Zion, that is, the sons of the Church, both the masters of contrary doctrines and every assertion of the philosophers and the arguments of the Gentiles will be destroyed, because the Lord himself is the sword of the mighty, of whom it is said: Gird your sword upon your thigh, O most mighty. In your comeliness and your beauty, set forth, proceed prosperously and reign, because of truth and meekness and justice; and your right hand shall lead you wonderfully. With this sword the Ethiopians are wounded, of whom it is written: And you, O Ethiopians, shall be wounded by my sword; and after they have been wounded by the sword of Christ, they will lay aside their dark color and, exulting, will say: The splendor of the Lord our God shall be upon us; which David too promises himself after his repentance: You shall wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow. This is the sword of which the Apostle writes: The word of God is living and effective, and more piercing than any two-edged sword, and reaching even to the division of soul and spirit.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.14-15":["And the Lord God shall be seen over them, and his javelin shall go forth like lightning; and the Lord God shall sound the trumpet, and shall go in the whirlwind of the south. The Lord of hosts shall protect them. LXX: And the Lord God shall appear over them; and his arrow shall go forth like lightning; and the Lord God Almighty shall sound with the trumpet, and shall go in the commotion of his terror. The Lord Almighty shall protect them. This place too they refer to the times of the Maccabees: that when these fought against Antiochus and conquered, the fight and the victory were the Lord's, who went forth mighty to battle, and his power appeared like lightning, and, when the adversaries had been conquered and scattered as by a whirlwind, he protected the people of the Jews. We, however, shall refer everything to the understanding of the Savior, of whom it was said above: I have stretched Judah for myself as a bow. When he has been stretched, and the heretics and the Gentiles have been slain by the sons of Zion, the glory of the Lord will appear, and his javelin will go forth like lightning; of which lightning we read in Habakkuk too: In the light of your arrows they shall go, in the splendor of your flashing spear. This lightning and splendor is called by another name the trumpet and its blast, so that, when the holy cry has sounded, he who before had deaf ears may say: The instruction of the Lord has opened my ears, and has given me an ear to hear. And what follows: And he shall go in the whirlwind of the south, or, he shall go in the commotion of his threatening. He threatens and says that he will inflict punishments for this reason, that he may have mercy on those who repent. In short, he adds and says: The Lord Almighty shall protect them, those whom he had first terrified by his threatening. Let us read the history of the Ninevites.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.15-16":["And they shall devour and subdue with sling stones, and drinking they shall be made drunk as with wine, and they shall be filled like bowls, and like the horns of the altar.\n\nAnd the Lord their God shall save them in that day as the flock of his people, because holy stones shall be lifted up over his land. LXX: And they shall consume them, and overwhelm them with sling stones, and they shall drink their blood like wine, and they shall fill the altar like bowls. And the Lord shall save them in that day as sheep, his people, because holy stones shall roll upon his land. For what we have rendered shall be lifted up, and what is written in the Hebrew as methnosasoth, can be interpreted as wandering, or fleeing. When the sons of Zion have been protected, and the Lord has sounded the trumpet and gone in the whirlwind against their adversaries, so great will be the ruin of the Greeks that they will be crushed, I will not say by swords, but by the casting of stones and the whirling of slings, so that they will be a prey and a meal for their enemies. Then, drinking, they shall be made drunk as with wine. It is not those who have been slain who will be drunk with their own blood, but those who have conquered will fight with desperation, as though drunk, and will please the Lord like the horns of the altar and its libation. For this is what is understood by the bowls, with which libations are poured out upon the altar. The Lord will also save them as sheep and the flock of his people; for they will not fight against the Macedonians as an armed army, trained in the art of war, but will come like a flock ready for death, and, with the Lord's help, will overcome. And the holy stones that have been oppressed — and he calls them stones because of the hardness of their tribulations and their fortitude of mind — will be lifted up from their lowliness, and will be glorious in his land. Otherwise: his holy stones, of priestly stock, fleeing in different directions, will, by his gift, obtain the victory.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.15":["Let us speak also according to anagogy, or rather let us explain a prophecy wrapped in many obscurities. The sons of Zion, protected by their Lord, will devour their adversaries, whom we understand as the sons of Greece. And they will subdue them with sling stones, with the threatenings of the Scriptures, making their adversaries subject and humble; for nothing strikes so hard as an example from the holy Scriptures, and a testimony sent forth by the whirl of the mouth. But what is said in the LXX: And they shall drink their blood like wine, we do not read so in the Hebrew, but: Drinking they shall be made drunk as with wine, so that they may hear that word of the Song of Songs: Drink, friends, and be drunk. And so their drunkenness will please like the sacrifice of the altar, and like the horns, or corners, of the altar.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.16":["This we have said in a general way: but let us speak also in particular of the inner man. The wheel stands upon the earth with only some small trace of contact, and it not only stands, but as it were runs on: it does not stand still, but touches and passes by: finally, when it is rolled, it always mounts up to higher things. So too the holy man, because he is in the body, has the necessity of thinking some things about earthly matters; and when he has come to food and clothing and the rest of such things, having food and clothing, with these he is content, touching the earth, and he hastens to higher things. In him who runs and hastens to higher things, in him is your word. We read also in the prophet: \"Holy stones are rolled upon the earth.\" See what he said: Holy stones are rolled upon the earth. For because they are wheels, therefore they are rolled upon the earth and hasten to higher things. Do you wish to know of more wheels? We read: \"And a wheel within a wheel\"; we read in Ezekiel: \"The wheels cling one to another.\" The two wheels are the new and the old testament. This wheel clings to that, and that to this. Yet see what is said in Ezekiel: \"And wherever,\" it says, \"the spirit was leading, thither the wheels also followed.\" This is said also in Ecclesiastes, at the consummation of the world: \"And the wheel,\" it says, \"shall be rolled over the cistern.\" There are many things that might be said about wheels: but the discourse hastens on to the rest.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 10","The Lord will also save them as the flock of his people, because holy stones shall roll upon his land, stones that will be so light and so striving upward that they will not wait for the hands of the builders, but will themselves hasten to be laid upon the foundation of Christ and to be held together by the cornerstone; of whom the apostle Peter also speaks: As living stones be built up into a spiritual house, and a holy priesthood, offering spiritual victims, pleasing to God. These are the stones that will cry out if the people of the Jews keep silent, and they will roll upon the earth as long as they are in the body, because the earthly habitation weighs down the mind that is anxious about many things; and, set in the flesh, the holy man says: Who will give me wings like a dove? As far as lies in him, he strives and rolls and is raised toward the heights, but he is held back by the frailty of the flesh. And the earth upon which the stones roll is that of which we read: Sing to the Lord, all the earth, and: Let all the earth worship you, and sing psalms to you.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.9.17":["For what is his goodness, and what is his beauty, but the grain of the elect, and wine that makes virgins spring forth? LXX: For if there is anything best of his, and if anything good from him, grain for young men and wine of good odor for virgins. Therefore, they say, the Maccabees, fleeing hither and thither, will overcome with the Lord's help, so that, when the Macedonians have been cast out of the land of Israel, the temple may be cleansed, the precepts of the law may be kept, and the learning of the Scriptures may again make virgins spring forth, that is, peoples believing in one God, who before had been defiled by the worship of idolatry. By the grain of the elect, that is, baurim, they would have us understand not young men, as the Seventy translated, but chosen and learned men, who are found worthy to eat the grain, that is, the law of God. For wine, which in Hebrew is called thiros, Aquila translated οἰνίαν (vintage), which itself also can be referred to the abundance of the vintage. So the Jews think. But we understand the grain of the elect, or of the young men, and the wine that makes virgins spring forth, or the wine of good odor for virgins, as the Lord and Savior, who says in the Gospel: Unless a grain of wheat falling into the earth dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears greater fruit. From this wheat is made that bread which came down from heaven and which strengthens the heart of man. This bread is eaten by those who are strong in Christ, and to whom John the Evangelist speaks: I write to you, young men, because the word of God abides in you, and you are strong, and you have conquered the evil one. He who is the grain of the elect, or of the young men, is himself also the wine that gladdens the heart of man, and it is drunk by those virgins who are holy both in body and in spirit, so that, made drunk and rejoicing, they may follow the Church; and that it may be said of them: Virgins shall be brought to the king after her, her companions shall be brought to you; they shall be brought with gladness and exultation. For how shall they not have gladness, who, made drunk by the cup of the Savior, are begotten into virgins, and dare to say: Bring me into the little chamber of wine, strengthen me with ointments? This wine is of good odor, whence it is said in the same song: You shall give me to drink of the perfumer's wine, of the streams of your pomegranates. With this wine are made drunk those who follow the Lamb of God wherever he goes, clothed in white garments; because they have not defiled themselves with women, for they remained virgins.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.1":["Ask of the Lord rain in the late season, and the Lord will make snows and will give them showers of rain, to each one grass in the field. For the idols have spoken what is useless, and the diviners have seen a lie, and the dreamers have spoken in vain, they comforted vainly; therefore they have been led away like a flock, they shall be afflicted, because they have no shepherd. LXX: Ask rain of the Lord in due season, early and late. The Lord has made phantasies, and he will give them winter rain, to each one grass in the field, for those who spoke have spoken troubles; and the diviners false visions, and they have spoken false dreams, they comforted vainly; therefore they have been dried up like sheep, and they have been afflicted because there was none to heal. This happiness which was promised for the time of the Maccabees, when the holy stones were lifted up over the land and the Israelite state grew greater, so much that the peoples again fed on the grain of the law, and your virgins were made drunk with the wine of the Holy Spirit, is promised in part. But since it is now the last time of the prophets, and the world is declining toward its end, and all the things that have been foretold await their fulfilment, ask of the Lord that he give you the late rain, that the Christ who has been promised may come and grant you dews and snows, for which azizim is written in the Hebrew. And I do not know what the LXX meant in translating phantasies, unless perhaps they wished to describe the greatness of the grace and the wonder of the gifts by the name of phantasy. So the Lord, who is to make the snows and is to water the dryness of the whole earth with the rains of the Gospel preaching, will himself give showers to those who believe, and all things will be filled with abundance, so that, after the nations have believed in Christ, they may understand that the things they worshipped before are vain.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","But according to the spiritual understanding we can say that the Lord exhorts those who believe in Christ to ask for the late rain at the consummation of the world, when the fullness of grace is to be given, and grass will grow up for each one in his own field, so that they may be able to say: The Lord rules me, and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me, beside the waters of refreshment he has brought me up.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.2":["Or else Israel itself, which was once deceived and held fast by the errors of idolatry, may understand that it worshipped idols in vain, and listened to the lies of diviners, and acquiesced in dreams, which Scripture commands us not to believe. And for this reason they were led like a flock into captivity, and afflicted, without God as their shepherd, because they did not have knowledge of the law. This whole place is obscure and doubtful, and the reader ought to pardon us if, in things that are ambiguous, we too walk with a hesitant step.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","For all the idols, and the diviners and the dreamers, have spoken in vain, and comforted vainly. These things he says of the heretics, who under the Christian name understand neither what they are saying nor what they are affirming, and who give heed to erring spirits and to teachers of demons who speak lies in hypocrisy, and have their conscience seared, so that, being by no means believers, they are led away by the consolations of those who promise vain things; and for this reason they have been handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, and led away into the captivity of the king of Babylon, and they shall be afflicted, because they do not have Christ as their shepherd, whom they promise themselves under a false name.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.3":["My fury is angered against the shepherds, and I will visit upon the he-goats; for the Lord of hosts has visited his flock, the house of Judah, and has made them like the horse of his glory in war. From him the corner, from him the peg, from him the bow of battle, from him shall go forth every exactor together; and they shall be like mighty men treading down the mire of the streets in battle, and they shall fight, because the Lord is with them. LXX: My fury is stirred up against the shepherds, and I will visit upon the lambs; and the Lord God Almighty will visit his flock, the house of Judah, and will make them like his comely horse in battle; and from him he looked, and from him he set, and from him the bow in fury, there shall go forth everyone who goes forth together, and they shall be like warriors treading down the mire of the streets in battle, and they shall be made ready, because the Lord is with them. In this place too the Jews have a twofold exposition. For some think that all of it is to be fulfilled at the coming of the Christ; others, that it has already been fulfilled under the Maccabees. And this is their explanation of the things the Lord promises: the Lord was angered against the shepherds, both the princes and the priests, and he visited upon the he-goats, that is, the people, according to that which is written: My people has become a lost flock, the shepherds have cast them out, so that the disciples were punished for the fault of their masters; not by the injustice of a judge who repays the sins of the fathers upon the sons, but because, when those men sinned, the people applauded along with them. And then indeed the Lord visited his he-goats, or his fattest lambs, and, according to the Seventy, caused them to be dried up with drought. But afterwards the Lord Almighty visited his flock, the house of Judah; for he raised up Judas Maccabeus, and the others descended from him, against the generals of Antiochus, and made them like the horse of his glory in battle, that is, those who were begotten of his stock; for they oppressed the Macedonians for a long time.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Our writers, however, refer these things to the times of persecution, because for the fault of the priests the people too is often handed over to its adversaries; and yet the Lord Almighty afterwards visits his flock, the house of Judah, which confesses God both in word and in mind, and makes them like the horse of his glory in war, of which the saints say: Mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.4-5":["And what follows: From him the corner, from him the peg, from him the bow of battle, from him shall go forth every exactor together, they understand μεταφορικῶς (metaphorically), so that they interpret the corner as the royal power, because it holds the walls together. And from him, he says, the peg, that is, the priesthood. Read Isaiah, in whom Eliakim is fastened in the temple of God like a peg. From him the bow of battle: men strong for war; from him also every exactor together, which in the Hebrew is written noges, and Aquila translated εἰσπράσσων (exactor), so that not only were there strong and good men, but others among them were unworthy of their lineage. For Judas Maccabeus, and all who were princes among the people from his line, were both the corner, because they held the people together with royal power, and the peg, for they themselves were priests, and the bow of battle, because they proved most valiant men, so that they not only marshalled the army and the battle line, but were the first to leap forward into battle. Concerning what follows: From him shall go forth every exactor together, for which the LXX translated: From him shall go forth everyone who leads out together, we can also say this: there will be no rank in the army that is not arranged at his discretion. And they shall be most valiant men, treading down the Macedonians like the mire of the streets in battle; and they shall be most valiant, and shall fight, because the Lord is with them.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.4":["He himself will also be the bow of the Lord's fury, of which he says: I will make my arrows drunk with blood, and again: My arrows shall consume them. Of this bow, and of these arrows, we read in Psalm 7: He has bent his bow and made it ready, and on it he has made ready the instruments of death; he has made his arrows for the burning.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.5-7":["And the riders on horses shall be confounded.\n\nAnd I will strengthen the house of Judah, and I will save the house of Joseph; and I will bring them back, because I will have mercy on them; and they shall be as they were when I had not cast them off, for I am the Lord their God, and I will hear them. And they shall be like the mighty men of Ephraim, and their heart shall rejoice as from wine; and their sons shall see and shall rejoice; and their heart shall exult in the Lord. LXX: And the riders on horses shall be confounded, and I will strengthen the house of Judah, and I will save the house of Joseph, and I will make them dwell, because I have loved them. And they shall be as when I had not cast them away, for I am the Lord their God, and I will hear them; and they shall be like the warriors of Ephraim, and their heart shall rejoice as in wine, and their sons shall see and shall rejoice, and their heart shall be glad in the Lord. These things too, in keeping with the sense above, they expound in two ways, so that either they say they have already been accomplished under the Maccabees, or they relate that they are to be accomplished under the Christ in the last age. And the sense is: when Judas has been made like a comely horse in battle, and has trodden down his adversaries like mire, and, with the Lord fighting beside him, has overcome, then all the cavalry of Greece will fall, and the house of Judah and the house of Israel — for he calls it the house of Joseph, that is, of the ten tribes — will be saved alike; and I will bring them back from the captivity in which they had been, and they shall be as they were at that time when I had not cast them off; for I am the Lord their God, who will hear them when they pray. And Ephraim, who are now held captive, shall afterwards be set free, and shall come to such joy that you would think them drenched with wine. Their sons also shall behold the triumphs of their fathers, and shall rejoice in the Lord, by whose gift they obtained the victory. Let us look for the history: when did Judah and Israel fight together against the Greeks, or at what time did Ephraim return from the Assyrian captivity, when, according to Ezekiel, the two staffs, that is, Judah and Ephraim, were joined to each other and coupled into one rod?\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.5":["And they shall tread down, he says, their adversaries, crowned in martyrdom, and they shall say: The Lord is my light and my salvation, whom shall I fear? The Lord is the protector of my life, of whom shall I be afraid? While the wicked drew near against me to eat my flesh, my enemies who trouble me, they themselves were weakened and fell. Though camps should stand together against me, my heart shall not fear; though battle should rise up against me, in this will I hope. Then shall that saying be fulfilled: One of you shall pursue a thousand, and two shall put many thousands to flight.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","This can be explained according to tropology thus. When the Lord Almighty visits his flock, the house of Judah, then the riders on horses shall be confounded, of whom it is written: Let us sing to the Lord, for he has been gloriously magnified; horse and rider he has cast into the sea. Of whom it is also sung in the voice of the psalmist: At your rebuke, O God of Jacob, they who mounted horses have slumbered. And the choir of the saints redoubles it: Some in chariots, and some on horses, but we will call upon the name of our God; they have been bound and have fallen, but we have risen and stand upright. In this cavalry the king of Egypt trusted; and, hurled headlong into the sea, he sank like lead into the depths, and learned by his own example that what is written is true: A horse is a deceitful thing for safety. These chariots and four-horse teams we interpreted above as the four passions, which, unless they are governed by the reins of a good charioteer, are carried over the precipice. But there is another charioteer, of whom Elisha speaks: Father, father, the chariot of Israel and its charioteer.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.6":["Then God says that he strengthens the house of Judah and the house of Joseph, so that those who were divided while Jeroboam reigned may be united while Christ rules, and there may be one shepherd and one flock. For both Judah and Joseph, under different names, are brought together into the one understanding of the Savior, because Joseph lavished grain on the peoples hungering in Egypt, and the Lord satisfied the hunger of the world with his presence. And he will bring them back, and will have mercy on them, and so they shall be one, as they were before he cast them off. Take all these things of the persecutions, when, all hope being lost, the Lord heard his servants.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.7":["And they shall be like the mighty men of Ephraim, who, at first weak and infirm, shall afterwards be mighty and worthy of their name. For Ephraim is interpreted fruitfulness. And their heart shall rejoice when they have drunk the wine that was pressed from the vine of Sorek. Their sons also, to whom the apostle Paul speaks: My little sons, with whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you. And Peter, the prince of the apostles: As sons, he says, of obedience, not conformed according to your former manner of life. And in the psalm we read: Come, sons, hear me; I will teach you the fear of the Lord. These sons, therefore, shall exult and rejoice, and their heart shall be glad in the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.8-10":["I will whistle for them, and I will gather them, because I have redeemed them, and I will multiply them as they had been multiplied before. And I will sow them among the peoples, and from afar they shall remember me, and they shall live with their sons, and shall return. And I will bring them back from the land of Egypt, and from the Assyrians I will gather them, and I will lead them to the land of Gilead and of Lebanon, and room shall not be found for them. LXX: I will signal to them, and I will receive them, because I will redeem them, and they shall be multiplied as they were very many, and I will sow them among the peoples, and from afar they shall remember me. They shall nourish their sons and shall turn back, and I will bring them back from the land of Egypt, and from the Assyrians I will receive them, and into Galaaditis and into Lebanon I will bring them, and none of them shall be lacking. As the sign of their gathering, whether under the Macedonians or at the consummation of the world, as we said before, they shall have my whistle, he says, so that by my whistle I may gather them and show myself to be the shepherd. For I have redeemed them and freed them from captivity with an outstretched arm; and I will multiply them, I who had multiplied them before, so that the dispersion among the peoples may be seen to be not a division but the work of sowing, and, with the harvest of children and grandchildren multiplied, they may live with their sons; and they may return not only from the land of Egypt, but also from the Assyrians, and come back from both parts of the world to Judea, and to the land of Gilead and of Lebanon, which the ten tribes, called the house of Joseph and Ephraim, had possessed before; and so great shall be the number of those brought back that the land will not hold the multitude.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.8":["According to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), the Lord signals and whistles to those who had before been captives to sins, and speaks to them: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and you shall find rest for your souls. Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart. For my yoke is sweet, and my burden is light. What is more merciful than this signal and whistle, by which the scattered people is gathered? And they are gathered because the Lord has redeemed them, not with corruptible silver and gold, from their vain manner of life, but with the precious blood, as of a spotless lamb, the Lord Jesus. Hence he speaks in the psalm: My exultation, deliver me from those who surround me, and again: You have redeemed me, O Lord God of truth. And he will multiply them as they had been multiplied before, so that what was promised to Abraham might be fulfilled: I will multiply him and I will bless him, and he shall be blessed, so that he may become the father of many nations. But we ought not to take as sons of Abraham those to whom the Lord says: If you were sons of Abraham, you would do the works of your father, but those of whom the Apostle says: As many as are of faith, they are the sons of Abraham. Of this multiplication Isaiah too testifies in mystical speech, saying: He who is little shall become thousands, and he who is small, a great nation. But let us not take the multiplication and blessing of children carnally: otherwise Elijah and Elisha and Jeremiah and John the Baptist, than whom there was none greater among those born of women, lacked this blessing; rather, let us take it of that blessing by which multitudes of the nations are called to the faith.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.9":["In short, there follows: I will sow them among the peoples, and from afar they shall remember me. They who heard the word of the Lord were sown among the peoples: Go, teach all nations, and in another place: You have not chosen me, but I have chosen you, and I have appointed you that you should go and bear fruit, and your fruit should remain forever. And when the apostles have been sown among the peoples, the nations and peoples set far off shall remember me, that prophetic word agreeing with this saying: All the ends of the earth shall remember and shall turn to the Lord. And all the families of the nations shall worship in his sight. For the kingdom is the Lord's, and he shall rule over the nations. And what is said: From afar they shall remember me, is like this: You who are far off, remember the Lord, and let Jerusalem come up upon your heart. The whole multitude of the nations was far off, but it remembered God, because we are all begotten of one parent. And when, the apostles having been sown among all the nations, the nations set far off have remembered God, they shall live with their sons, so that those who before were dead through unbelief may begin to live by faith. These are the sons whom Paul nourished in the gospel, writing to the Corinthians: I gave you milk to drink. And Peter writes to the faithful in the dispersion, who had been reborn: Not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, through the word of the living and abiding God, like newborn little children, that they might long for the rational milk and grow by it to salvation. To these sons John the evangelist too was writing: Little sons, keep yourselves from idols.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.10":["And I will bring them back, he says, from the land of Egypt, and from the Assyrians I will gather them. From the land of Egypt, in which King Pharaoh ruled, who once said boastfully: The rivers are mine, and I made them. And from the land of Asshur, which the king of Assyria held, the great mind, who himself also said: Above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, and I will be like the Most High. And when the Lord has gathered the nations from the darkness of Egypt, and from the tribulation of the world, and from the power of the Assyrian, who is interpreted 'reproving and convicting', for he is the enemy and the avenger, he will lead them into the land of Gilead and of Lebanon. Gilead, turned into our language, is 'the testimony of migration', when we pass over from carnal things to spiritual, come from the part to the whole, leave earthly things behind and migrate to the heavenly. And Lebanon is interpreted λευκασμὸς, that is, whitening. So we are led out of the darkness of Egypt so that, after we have passed over and migrated out of the world, we may be made white in the Lord, and it may be said of us: Who is this who comes up made white? Others, following an ambiguity of the Greek tongue, by which both the mountain of Phoenicia and frankincense are called Libanus, here interpret Lebanon as frankincense, and think that God is signified by the frankincense, so that, after we have passed over from earth to heaven, the Savior's prayer may be fulfilled in us: Father, grant them that they may be one in us, as I and you are one. Hence the bride too says to the bridegroom in the Song: The fragrance of your garments is like the fragrance of Lebanon. And when he has led us into the land of Gilead and of Lebanon, room shall not be found for us, so that we shall in no way be confined by the narrow bounds of the earth, but shall enjoy the breadth of the heavens.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.11-12":["And he shall pass through the strait of the sea, and shall strike the waves in the sea; and all the depths of the river shall be confounded, and the pride of Asshur shall be humbled, and the scepter of Egypt shall depart. I will strengthen them in the Lord, and in his name they shall walk, says the Lord. LXX: And they shall pass through in the narrow sea, and they shall strike the waves in the sea, and all the depths of the rivers shall be dried up; and all the injury of the Assyrians shall be taken away, and the scepter of Egypt shall be removed. And I will strengthen them in the Lord their God, and in his name they shall glory, says the Lord. The Hebrews relate that the captive people of the Jews was carried off by the Assyrians and Chaldeans not only to the Medes and Persians, but also to the Bosporus and the northern region, and that afterwards they were in part called back, as the mercy of God turned them to himself, and that this is what is now said: They shall pass through in the narrow sea: the narrows of the Propontis, which divide Chalcedon and what was once Byzantium by a short strait. And the Lord, he says, going before his people, shall strike the waves of the sea, and all the depths of the rivers shall be confounded, as was done of old when the Jordan was dried up and the people crossed the Red Sea in the desert. And when it has been freed from the depth of captivity, from the bitterness of evils, Asshur shall be humbled, and the dominion of Egypt shall depart from them. And they shall be strengthened in the Lord, and in his name they shall walk in the land of Israel, says the Lord. These things, as they were handed down to us by the Hebrews, we have set out for the men of our own tongue, referring the trustworthiness of what is said to those by whom it was said.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","And when they have passed through the strait of the sea, and have trodden its waves under their foot, all the depths of the river shall be confounded, of which it is written: He who turns the sea into dry land; they shall pass through the river on foot; so that the pride, or the injury, of Asshur may be humbled, and the dominion of Egypt may depart from the saints and believers, and those who have been freed may be strengthened in the Lord and say: My strength and my praise is the Lord, and again: I will love you, O Lord, my strength, and with Jeremiah: My strength and my help and my refuge, O Lord my God.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.11":["For the rest, we who are reckoned by the name of Christ leave behind the letter that kills and follow the spirit that gives life, or rather, comparing spiritual things with spiritual, we look not for the Bosporus, and the drying of the sea once more, and the dry streams of the Jordan, which are like fables, but for the worthy mercy of God toward his servants, by which, as he goes before and opens the way for us, we pass through the strait of the sea, or the narrow sea, which for sinners is very broad: For broad and spacious is the way that leads to death, strait and narrow the way that leads to life. Of this sea we also read in the psalms: They who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters, they have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep. Into this sea of the world, in which the number of the saints is hemmed in and set in tribulation, the apostles went down, and first of all the Lord, that he might free us from the bitter waves. Into this sea is cast the dragnet of the gospel, which is woven of the testimonies of the Scriptures, so that it may draw out into the free air many fish overwhelmed by the whirlpools of this world, that they may be able to sing praises to God. These are they who have gone down to the sea, and in the ships of the churches do many works, and behold the wonders of God in the depth of the riches of his wisdom and knowledge, and in the Holy Spirit, who searches even the deep things of God; to whom the Lord says: Come after me, and I will make you fishers of men. Of these fishers, who go down to the sea and are in ships, Isaiah too prophesies: They shall fly in the ships of foreigners, and together they shall plunder the sea. For in the churches gathered from the number of the nations the apostles flew through the whole world, to plunder the sea and to carry off the spoils of the dragon who reigned in the sea, and of whom it is written: This sea, great and spacious in its arms; there are creeping things without number, small creatures with great. There the ships shall pass through; this dragon whom you formed to mock him. The sea is broad and spacious for the small and great living creatures that dwell with the dragon, and whose number is beyond count. But they who are upon the sea, and fly upon the sea itself in ships, shall hear, together with the Savior: You rule the power of the sea; you calm the motion of its waves.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.10.12":["Hence the Apostle too, in whom Christ was speaking, said: I can do all things in him who strengthens me; and not only shall they be strengthened in the Lord, but they shall also glory in him and say: But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world, and: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord, and in another place: Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, nor the strong man in his strength, nor the rich man in his riches; but let him who glories glory in this: to understand and know the Lord, and to do mercy and judgment in the midst of the earth. But since in the Hebrew it is written: And in his name they shall walk, it is to be understood thus: that Christians walk in the name of Christ, and a new name is written on their pebbles, and under the dignity of so great a name they walk with the Lord, as Enoch walked, and pleased God, and was translated.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.1-2":["Some, not understanding this place, refer Lebanon and the fir trees and pines and the oaks of Bashan, and the wooded or fortified forest, to the opposing powers, of which it is also said in Ezekiel, under the name of Asshur and of Pharaoh: Behold, Asshur was a cypress on Lebanon, fair in its branches, and thick with shade, and lofty in its height, and its top was among the clouds; water nourished it, the abyss raised it on high, and the rest, which are said both of Asshur and of Pharaoh, and they think these are said either of the opposing powers, or of all the proud and the princes, of whom we also read in the psalm: The voice of the Lord breaking the cedars, and the Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon, and in another place: The day of the Lord Sabaoth upon everyone who is insolent and proud, and upon everyone who is lofty and exalted, and a little after: And upon every cedar of Lebanon that is lofty, and upon every oak tree of Bashan. And of this Lebanon, they assert, it is prophesied: Lebanon shall fall with its lofty ones. But let us follow the former interpretation, especially since what follows also agrees with this sense.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.1":["Open your gates, O Lebanon, and let fire devour your cedars. Howl, O fir tree, for the cedar has fallen, because the magnificent have been laid waste. Howl, O oaks of Bashan, because the fortified forest has been cut down. LXX: Open your gates, O Lebanon, and let fire devour your cedars. Let the pine howl, for the cedar has fallen, for the nobles have been crushed. Howl, O oaks of Bashanitis, because the wooded forest has been brought down. Plainly Lebanon, to which the prophetic word turns, saying: Open your gates, O Lebanon, is understood as the temple of the Jews, which, restored, or rather built, by Zerubbabel, is sung as destined to be overthrown again by Vespasian and Titus. And because he had called the temple Lebanon μεταφορικῶς (metaphorically), he keeps the figure in the rest as well, so that by the cedars and fir trees and oaks of Bashan and the wooded forest he signifies the princes and priests and people of the Jews. And Lebanon opens its gates so that the Roman army may enter, and fire devours its cedars, so that either all things are laid waste by burning, or the leaders and princes are consumed by the onslaught of the enemy.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.2":["The fir trees howl because the cedar has fallen, the princes and priests mourning one another in their own devastation. And what he said obscurely before, he now sets out more plainly: Because the magnificent have been laid waste. I want to know what are the cedars of Lebanon that were burned, what are the fir trees to which howling is enjoined, what is the pine that has fallen; the magnificent, he says, have been laid waste. Howl, O oaks of Bashan, that is, of confusion and ignominy, because the most fortified forest, which in Hebrew is called besor, and was translated by the LXX as wooded, has been cut down; because the temple, which had grown up in impregnable strength, and had been built by various kings and princes, and afterwards by Herod, was destroyed when the Roman attacked it.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.3":["The voice of the howling of the shepherds, because their magnificence is laid waste; the voice of the roaring of lions, because the pride of the Jordan is laid waste. LXX: The voice of shepherds mourning, since their magnificence has been made wretched. The voice of lions roaring, because the roaring of the Jordan is afflicted. Part of the preceding passage is contained in these little verses too. Those whom he had called cedars, firs, pines and oaks of Bashan — and, explaining what these trees were, he had added, saying: Since the magnificent are laid waste — he now, by another μεταφορὰν (metaphor), says are shepherds, that is, princes and teachers; and those who were first among the people, he says, ought to weep and mourn, because their magnificence and beauty and glory has been laid waste and consumed, namely the temple, in which they gloried. And the voice, he says, of the roaring of lions: those whom he calls lofty trees, and again shepherds, the same he now calls lions. And because he had said lions, he keeps the metaphor, so as to add: Since the pride of the Jordan is laid waste, or the roaring and sound of the water running down, which in Hebrew is called gaon. And just as, according to the lie of the land of Judea, he compared the height of the temple to the height of Lebanon — for nothing in the land of promise is loftier than Lebanon, nor more wooded and dense —, so to the river Jordan, which is the greatest in Judea, and near which lions dwell, he joined the roaring of lions, on account of the burning of their thirst; and on account of the nearness of the desert and the breadth of the vast wilderness, and the reed-beds and sedge-beds. Hence it is also said through the prophet: The lion has come up from the Jordan, meaning to show that Nebuchadnezzar went out from his seat, like a lion from its lair, against Jerusalem. Another way: The voice of the roaring of lions, since the pride of the Jordan is laid waste. The voice, he says, of the nobles, because the temple has been destroyed, from which they always hoped for help, and which once nourished just, warlike and mighty lions.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.4-5":["Thus says the Lord my God: Feed the cattle of slaughter, which those who had possessed them were killing, and were not grieving; and they were selling them, saying: Blessed be the Lord, we have become rich; and their shepherds were not sparing them. LXX: Thus says the Lord Almighty: Feed the sheep of slaughter, which those who possessed them were killing, and they did not repent; and those who were selling them were saying: Blessed be the Lord, and we have become rich; and their shepherds suffered nothing over them. Where the prophecy is most manifest, and through the metaphor the true order of the history is narrated, the interpretation of tropology is superfluous: that we should say that some shepherds are good and others bad, and refer this as much to the old people as to the new, that is, as much to the priests and princes of the Jews as to the bishops and presbyters of the Christians; and that whatever shepherds have been negligent and have scandalized one of the least, they are the ones who feed the cattle of slaughter, and suffer nothing over them, nor grieve, nor can say with the Apostle: Who is scandalized, and I do not burn? And that they sell the peoples entrusted to them, of whom it is written: Who devour my people as a meal of bread; and gain riches from the deaths of others, praising those who take plunder from the wretched, and blessing the wicked. Let others interpret these things as they will: let us keep the order of explanation which we once took up. Since Lebanon has been opened, and its cedars and firs have been consumed by burning flame, and the howling of the shepherds has been heard, and the roaring of the lions, because all the beauty and pride of the Jordan has been laid waste and consumed, therefore the Lord my God said these things to me: O prophet Zechariah, this word is addressed to you: Feed the flock of slaughter, that is, let it be nourished meanwhile for now and grow, which afterwards is to be slaughtered by enemies; which sheep and cattle the princes of the Romans, who possessed them and held them by right of victory, were killing and not pitying, or else were sacrificing; and they did not feel grief, that is, they suffered no harm for so great a cruelty; and they were selling those whom they kept not out of clemency but for the price. Let us read the old histories, and the traditions of the lamenting Jews, how at the Tent of Abraham — where now a most crowded market is held every year —, after the last overthrow, which they suffered under Hadrian, many thousands of men were sold; and those who could not be sold were carried over into Egypt, cut down as much by shipwreck and famine as by the slaughter of the nations. These victors, then, and avengers of the Lord, who were killing and not grieving, and were selling the cattle and saying: Blessed be the Lord, we have become rich, held this opinion: The Jews offended because of their sins, therefore we have crushed them, and from their price we have become rich. And no wonder, he says, if the enemies by the right of victors were killing the cattle and not grieving, were selling them and glorying in their crime; since their own shepherds and teachers did not spare them, and by their fault the flock was handed over to wolves.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.6":["Because, then, their shepherds were not sparing them, and they themselves were the first to devour my people, therefore I too will no longer spare the inhabitants of the land of Judea, says the Lord. For he speaks of this land, of which his discourse was, and not of the whole world, as the Jews, interpreting badly, wish to twist God's sentence away from themselves in another direction. For after the overthrow of the temple — which befell them for this reason, that for forty-two years after the Lord suffered, though granted a space of time, they would not repent —, I will let loose, he says, each one against his neighbor; and they will cut one another down in mutual slaughter, or else in the very siege of the temple, when the Roman army shall surround Jerusalem; for there will be such wars and discords among the Jews themselves that the people will be divided into three parts. Let us read Josephus and the seven books of the Jewish Captivity, and we shall see by the truth of history that this prophecy has been fulfilled. And they shall cut down, he says, the land: there is no doubt that the Romans destroyed all the land and all the cities of the Jews. And I will not rescue them from their hand. Hear, O Jew, you who promise yourself the vainest hopes, and do not hear the Lord saying and affirming: I will not rescue from their hands, that your captivity among the Romans is to be eternal.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.7-9":["And I took for myself two staffs: one I called Beauty, and the other I called Cords; and I fed the flock.\n\nAnd I cut off three shepherds in one month, and my soul was straitened over them, since their soul also varied against me. And I said: I will not feed you; what dies, let it die, and what is cut off, let it be cut off, and let the rest devour each one the flesh of his neighbor. LXX: And I will take for myself two staffs: one I called Beauty, and the other I called Cord; and I will feed the sheep. And I will take away three shepherds in one month, and my soul will grow heavy upon them, since their souls also were roaring against me. And I said: I will not feed them; what dies, let it die, and what fails, let it fail, and let the rest devour each one the flesh of his neighbor. Above he had said: For this, O poor of the flock; and, a break being made by ἀποσιωπήσει (by aposiopesis), there was understood: Give heed, because the gates of Lebanon are opened, and the oaks of Bashan, the firs and the cedars are cut down; and he had added, threatening: I will no longer spare the inhabitants of the land, says the Lord. And he seemed to say this of the Jewish people alone, because, having killed the prophets, they had also laid hands on the Son of God, and with rash voice had cried out: His blood be upon us, and upon our children. Now the Creator and Lord of the universe sets forth the mysteries of his world, and says that he had two staffs, one of which he called Beauty, the other Cords, and that with the two staffs he fed the flock bound together; and also that he cut off three shepherds in one month, and that his soul was straitened, or indignant, or — as the Seventy translated —, my soul will grow heavy upon them; there is no doubt that he means the shepherds whom he cut off in one month. And he gives the reason why he was so indignant with the shepherds as to cut them off in one month: Since their soul also, he says, varied against me. And the sense is: They did not love me with a full heart, nor did the hireling shepherds love the true shepherd: For the good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep. Wherefore I too, in indignation, pronounced sentence, and to those very shepherds whom I had cut off in my indignation, or else, the shepherds being cut off, to the flock left behind, I said: I will not feed you; but what has chosen death, let it die by its own will, and let them devour one another and tear one another apart in mutual slaughter. We have said this παραφραστικῶς (by paraphrase), to pave the path for the explanation to come.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.7":["And I will no longer spare those who dwell on the land, says the Lord. Behold, I will deliver men, each one into the hand of his neighbor, and into the hand of his king; and they shall cut down the land, and I will not rescue from their hand; and I will feed the cattle of slaughter for this, O poor of the flock. LXX: Therefore I will no longer spare the inhabitants of the land, says the Lord. And behold, I will deliver men, each one into the hands of his neighbor, and into the hand of his kings; and they shall cut down the land, and I will not rescue from their hand, and I will feed the sheep of slaughter in Canaanitis. What the LXX translated at the end of the passage: And I will feed the sheep of slaughter in the land of Canaan, is not in the Hebrew, and is translated by none of the other translators.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","And I will feed the cattle of slaughter, so that the Jews may always be nourished for death; because this is the sentence of God: O you who are the poor of the flock, that is, the just of Israel, who have believed in the Lord Jesus and received the Son of God, hear what is said, and learn the mysteries of the following passage. But what we read according to the Seventy: And I will feed the sheep of slaughter in the land of Canaan, we can understand thus: that the Lord feeds the people of the Jews, which is destined for death and scattered through the whole world — that is, in the land of Canaan and of the Gentiles —, and nourishes it for sacrifice.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","The Creator and shepherd, whose sheep know his voice and follow him, took for himself two staffs, or two σκυτάλας, that is, sticks, which in Hebrew are called macaloth, to show the bearing of a shepherd. One staff he called Beauty: that is, under Noah he protected the whole human race with his blessing, when, the world being restored, sins had not yet made it foul. And therefore the calling of all the nations is called beautiful and fair, because nothing is more just than that the parent of the universe should call all equally, whom he begot in equal condition. And the other he called Cords; for when the Most High was dividing the nations and scattering the sons of Adam, he set the bounds of the nations according to the number of the angels of the Lord. And his people Jacob became the portion of the Lord; Israel the cord of his inheritance. And I fed, he says, the flock: either Israel itself, or, together with Israel, the whole human race.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Some have understood the two callings as those of the Jews and of the Gentiles: by the first name Israel, and by the last the Christians. But how the Christian calling was cast away first, and the Jews remained and were fed by God, they did not sufficiently consider.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.8":["Let us understand this according to the letter of Moses and Aaron. Moses indeed was led out, and Aaron, but not led into the land of promise. Jesus succeeded him. See what is written. Moses died in the desert, Aaron died, Miriam died; and this is what is written in the prophet: \"And I will strike,\" it says, \"three shepherds in one month.\" They died: for they could not enter into the land of promise. They only saw over against the land of promise, and could not enter. The Jews saw the land of promise, and could not enter. For they died in the desert, and their corpses are in the wilderness, of those who died in the desert. We, the sons, under Jesus as leader, came to the Jordan, and entered into the land of promise, and came to Gilgal, and were a second time circumcised with a spiritual circumcision, and the reproach of Egypt was taken away from us. Then Jesus himself our leader holds the sword, and always goes before us, and fights for us, and conquers our adversaries, and for seven days we go around Jericho, that is, this world. We sing with the priestly trumpets, and we go around Jericho, that is, this world, and its walls fall, and we enter in, and we are the victors. Then we conquer the city of Ai: afterward we come to Jebus, we come to Hazor, we come to other cities; we conquer the adversaries whom under Moses we could not conquer.\n— HOMILIES ON THE PSALMS 10","And I cut off, he says, three shepherds in one month. I have read in the commentaries of a certain writer that the shepherds cut off by the Lord's indignation are to be understood as the priests and false prophets and kings of the Jews; because after the passion of Christ all were cut off at one time, those of whom Jeremiah speaks: The priests did not say: Where is the Lord? Those who held my law did not know me. And the shepherds transgressed against me, and the prophets prophesied by Baal, and followed idols. Not content with this explanation, he wants the three shepherds cut off in one month to be taken as those who have sinned against the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit; for all heretics sin either against one, or against two, or against three persons at once. Let him have said what he wished, for one must not detract from another's sense. To us the three shepherds who were cut off in one month seem to be Moses, and Aaron, and Miriam; of whom Miriam died in the first month, which is called Nisan, in the desert of Zin, and in the same place, on account of the water of contradiction, and in the same month, Moses and Aaron were condemned not to enter the land of promise. And so it came about that of the three shepherds, one was cut off by present death, the others by the sentence of a death to come. And my soul, he says, was straitened over them, namely over the three shepherds whom I had set over my flock. Because their soul also varied against me; for they did not glorify me at the water of contradiction. But if we refer it to the people, that the soul of the people varied against God, it must be explained thus: I was indignant with them for this reason, that they too, wavering between me and idols, fought against me in various ways.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.9":["Wherefore in indignation I said to Moses: I will not feed you; let me alone and I will destroy them. But when, in anger, he says, let me alone, he invites entreaty, and shows that he can be held back by him. What dies, he says, let it die, and what is cut off, let it be cut off; let the bodies of all fall in the wilderness, and, turned to sedition, let them be torn by one another's bites like beasts, and let none enter the land of promise.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.10":["And I took my staff which was called Beauty, and I cut it off, to make void my covenant which I had struck with all peoples. And it was made void on that day, and thus the poor of the flock who keep watch for me knew that it is the word of the Lord. LXX: And I will take my fair staff, and I will cast it away, to scatter my testament which I disposed toward all peoples; and it shall be scattered on that day, and the Canaanites shall know, the sheep that are kept for me, that it is the word of the Lord. After the calling of Israel, and the taking up of the second staff, which was called Cord, the Lord took the first staff, the nations of the whole world, which were called beautiful, and cut them off from his worship, because they venerated idols, and all have turned aside, together they have become useless. And setting out more plainly what this staff was, he added: To make void my covenant which I had struck with all peoples. For as soon as Israel was taken up, the crowd of the nations was cast away; and as soon as the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue was born — who later in the Gospel, when she was twelve years old, is related to have fallen sick and died —, the αἱμοῤῥοῦσα (the woman with a flow of blood) began to flow with blood and to be tormented by a perpetual infirmity.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.11":["And it was made void, he says, my covenant which I had entered into with all the nations. And the poor of the flock understood, that is, the people Israel which keeps my commands, that it is the word of the Lord, that is, that it is the Lord's will to cast off the nations that deny, and in Abraham to take up Israel that confesses. For what we have translated: And thus the poor of the flock knew, the Seventy translated: And the Canaanites shall know, the sheep that are kept for me, which some expound thus: The people of the Jews shall know, to whom it was said: Seed of Canaan, and not of Judah, which is now kept by my ceremonies; or else, because Canaanites is interpreted 'ready for humiliation', those shall know who were once made ready to be humbled by the Lord and to be abandoned. But why, for what we have translated: Thus the poor, they said: Canaanites, the cause is manifest; for among the Hebrews 'thus' is called chen, 'poor' anie; they, joining the two words into one, for what is: Thus the poor, that is, chen anie, translated the name of the Canaanite nation.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.12-13":["The Jews, interpreting this place maliciously, relate the thirty pieces of silver to thirty commands of the law which they are ordered to do in the law, and again thirty-six others which are forbidden in the law; and they say that they are told to return the silver of the Lord's commands to their modeller and moulder, God, and that because they would not do it, they were cast away. I wished to indicate briefly what they think; otherwise it is wearisome to unroll their most long-winded explanation, so that we may pass on to the rest.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Then was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah, saying: And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of the one valued, whom they valued from the sons of Israel, and they gave them for the potter's field, as the Lord appointed for me. This testimony is not found in Jeremiah; but in Zechariah, who is almost the last of the twelve prophets, a certain likeness is reported, and although the sense does not differ much, yet both the order and the words are different. I read recently, in a certain Hebrew volume which a Hebrew of the Nazarene sect brought to me, an apocryphal Jeremiah, in which I found these things written word for word. But still it seems to me that the testimony is rather taken from Zechariah, in the common manner of the evangelists and apostles, who, passing over the order of the words, bring forward only the sense from the Old Testament as an example.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ZEC.11.12":["And I said to them: If it is good in your eyes, bring my wage, and if not, be still; and they weighed out my wage, thirty pieces of silver. And the Lord said to me: Cast them to the statuary, the fine price at which I was priced by them; and I took the thirty pieces of silver, and cast them into the house of the Lord to the statuary. LXX: And I will say to them: If it is good in your sight, give, setting my wage, or refuse. And they set my wage, thirty pieces of silver. And the Lord said to me: Put them down into the furnace, and consider whether it is tested, as I was tested for them. And I took the thirty pieces of silver, and put them into the house of the Lord into the furnace. Since the poor of the Israelite flock, who keep my commands, knew that what I spoke was the word of the Lord, and that what I said was true — what I said, that is, to the poor of the flock: If it pleases you; for you are men, and once for all I created you with free will, you to whom I also spoke in the desert: If you hear me, you shall eat the good things of the land; in return for this taking up, by which I cast off the whole human race and chose you for myself as a flock of my own, and, the Creator of all, willed to have a small cord, give me back my wage, that is, keep my precepts. But if you do not wish to pay the wage, and it does not please you in your eyes to be reckoned by my name, refuse openly, and do what you will. And they then indeed answered Moses: All things whatsoever the Lord says, we will do. But at the end of the times, in return for my having chosen them from the midst of the nations, and freed them from the iron furnace of Egypt, they weighed out my wage, thirty pieces of silver, giving them to Judas the traitor for my blood.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.13":["And the Lord said to me, says the prophet, or else the Savior himself, whose are the preceding words, bearing witness that the Father spoke to him: Cast it to the statuary, for which in the Hebrew is read ioser, that is, τὸν πλάστην, whom we can call moulder and potter. And the sense is: Cast my price to the potter, who is the Creator and moulder of all. Nor did he say, put it down, but cast it, so that the wage of the Lord might be weighed by the judgment of the moulder and modeller. And εἰρονικῶς (ironically), seeing his price, that is, the price of the divine majesty, in thirty pieces of silver, and himself betrayed for so cheap a wage: A fine price, he says, at which I was priced by them; and this must be read with more emphasis, with the mockery and derision of the speaker: At so much did my people, once the poor of the flock and chosen by me as sons, judge that I should be bought and sold! And I took, says the Lord through the prophet, as I had been commanded by the Lord, the thirty pieces of silver, and I did not put them down, but cast them into the house of the Lord to the moulder; in the house of the Lord I caused them to be returned by the traitor to the priests and Pharisees, the very man who sold me confessing: I have sinned, betraying just blood. But they, since it was the price of blood, would not put it into the corbona, that is, into the treasury; but with it they bought the potter's field for the burial of strangers. For all of us who were strangers and sojourners from the law, redeemed by his most precious blood, are buried and rest in the house of the potter and Creator of all. For πλάστῃ and moulder I once translated statuary, compelled by the ambiguity of the word, because it signifies statuary and moulder by a single term.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.14":["And I cut off my second staff, which was called Cord, so that I might dissolve the brotherhood between Judah and Israel. LXX: And I cast away the second staff, which was called Cord, so that I might scatter the covenant that was between Judah and Israel. In this place too I know that I have read a long explanation by a certain writer, or rather inextricable tangles, who brings forward from the book of Ezekiel the two staffs joined into one fellowship, and fits to this passage everything that is to be understood spiritually about the concord of Judah and Ephraim. But let us follow the order we have begun. After the Lord was crucified by the Jews and valued at thirty pieces of silver, and the price of his blood availed for the burial of the Gentiles, who were strangers to the law and the commandments of God, at once the Lord, no longer putting off his sentence, says: I cut off my second staff, which was properly mine, which clung to me in such a way that in Jeremiah it was called a loincloth and a girdle and a waistband, or, to use the everyday word, a hip-band; this second staff, once called Cord and my portion, was therefore cast away by me, so that I might dissolve the pact and the brotherhood and the concord that had been between Judah and Israel, and separate the brothers from one another. So that, in the number of the apostles and of those who repented, the name Judah should again be given, as to one who also confesses his God; while those should be called Israel and Ephraim and Joseph who, persisting in hardness of heart, said: We have no king but Caesar. Hence he did not add: So that I might make void the pact, or covenant, which I had with Judah and Israel — for to this day he stretches out his hand to the repentant —, but also that he might divide from one another, out of the one people, some who believe and others who do not believe, saying: Do not think that I came to send peace on the earth; I did not come to send peace, but a sword. For I came to separate a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, and a man's enemies are those of his own household.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.15":["And the Lord said to me: Take for yourself yet again the instruments of a foolish shepherd. For behold, I will raise up a shepherd in the land, who will not visit what is forsaken, will not seek what is scattered, and will not heal what is broken, and will not nourish what stands, and he will eat the flesh of the fat ones and break apart their hooves. O shepherd and idol, forsaking the flock, a sword upon his arm and upon his right eye; his arm shall be dried up with withering, and his right eye, growing dark, shall be darkened. LXX: And the Lord said to me: Take for yourself yet again the shepherd's instruments of an unskilled shepherd. For behold, I will raise up a shepherd upon the land, who shall not visit what is forsaken and shall not seek out what is scattered, and shall not heal what is broken, and shall not guide what is whole, and shall devour the flesh of the choice ones, and twist their ankles. O you who pasture vain things, and who have forsaken the sheep, a sword upon his arm, and upon his right eye; his arm shall be dried up with withering, and his right eye shall be darkened with blindness. When he says: Take for yourself yet again the instruments of a foolish shepherd, he means this: that above he had taken to himself two staffs, one Beauty and the other Cord. And because through their own vice and sin he cast them away, and, the brotherhood between Judah and Israel being dissolved, we have been grafted into the root of the good olive, and blindness has come in part upon the house of Israel until the fullness of the Gentiles should come in, now the prophet is told to take up the most grievous prophecy of the foolish, or unskilled, shepherd. The foolish and unskilled shepherd is without any doubt the Antichrist, who is said to be coming at the consummation of the world, and what sort he will be is indicated. By the instruments of the shepherd we ought to understand his insignia and his garb: the wallet, the staff, the pipe and the whistle. And just as Isaiah, to show the captivity of the people, walks about naked, and Jeremiah goes to the potter's house, so that from the shattering of the vessel which was turning on the wheel and being shaped by the craftsman's hand he might show both the destruction of Israel and the power of God, and Ezekiel, to show the overthrow of Jerusalem and the flight of Zedekiah and the burdens of the captives not only by his voice but also by his garb, digs through the wall and is carried out upon his shoulders, so Zechariah takes on the garb of a foolish and unskilled shepherd, to announce him who is to come.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.16":["This shepherd will rise up in Israel for this reason, because the true shepherd had said: I will no longer feed you. Under another name, both in the prophet Daniel and in the Gospel and in Paul's epistle to the Thessalonians, he is prophesied as the abomination of desolation, who is to sit in the temple of the Lord and make himself out to be God; and through Isaiah too he is called the great mind. And he comes for this, not to heal but to destroy the flock of Israel. For the good shepherd visits the sick animals, seeks out the scattered, brings back those left behind, supports the weary. On the contrary, the bad shepherd does everything the opposite way, and having devoured the flesh of the fat ones, he breaks apart and twists the hooves of the rams and the sheep, so that they may not walk with a straight foot. This shepherd the Jews received, whom the Lord Jesus will slay with the spirit of his mouth and will bring to nothing by the brightness of his coming, so that those who did not believe the truth, lest they should be saved, may believe a lie and be judged, because they consented to iniquity.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.11.17":["Having described the worst shepherd, foolish and unskilled, the prophetic discourse turns to the shepherd himself: O shepherd and idol. So wicked is the shepherd that he is called not a worshiper of idols but himself an idol, since he calls himself God and wishes to be adored by all. He forsook the flock to be devoured by beasts, the flock which the Lord had guarded for so long a time. A sword upon his arm, and his strength, and upon his right eye, by which he boasted that he keenly discerned the mysteries of God and saw more than all the prophets before him had seen, so much so that he called himself the Son of God. And that sword is the one of which we both learn above and shall now speak in part, the one of which Isaiah too speaks: My sword has been made drunk in heaven. And it is for this reason that the sword of the Lord will be upon his arm and upon his right eye, that his strength and all the boasting of his might may be dried up with withering, and the knowledge which he promised himself under a false name may be darkened with eternal darkness.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.1":["The burden of the word of the Lord upon Israel. The Lord said, stretching out the heaven and founding the earth and forming the spirit of man within him: Behold, I will make Jerusalem a lintel of drunkenness for all the peoples round about; but Judah too shall be in the siege against Jerusalem. And it shall be: on that day: I will make Jerusalem a stone of burden for all the peoples; all who have lifted it shall be torn with gashing, and all the nations of the earth shall be gathered against her. LXX: The taking up of the word of the Lord upon Israel. The Lord says, stretching out the heaven and founding the earth, and forming the spirit of man within him: Behold, I will make Jerusalem like lintels that are shaken for all the peoples round about, and in Judea. And there shall be a siege around Jerusalem, and it shall be on that day, I will make Jerusalem a stone that is trodden down by all the nations; everyone who treads her down, mocking shall mock, and I will gather against her all the nations of the earth. There is a threefold interpretation from the place in which we read: Behold, I will make Jerusalem a lintel of drunkenness for all the peoples round about, as far as the place where it is written: Sword, awake against my shepherd, and against the man who is my companion, says the Lord of hosts. Strike the shepherd and the sheep shall be scattered. For some of the Jews think that these things have already been partly fulfilled, from Zerubbabel down to Gaius Pompey, who was the first of the Romans to take Judea and the temple, a history which Josephus writes. Others, however, think that they are to be fulfilled at the end of the world, when Jerusalem shall have been restored, which the wretched Jewish nation promises itself with its ἠλειμμένῳ (anointed one), whom we read of above as the foolish shepherd. But others, that is, we who are reckoned by the name of Christ, declare that they are daily being fulfilled, and are to be fulfilled, in the Church until the end of the world. And so as not to stretch the measure of the volume by going through every point, setting it out thus: Those men say this, others suppose so, we think thus, we have set down three opinions, so that from the variety of interpretation the prudent reader may understand what is to be fitted to what. The Lord, then, who stretched out the heaven like a skin, and made the earth firm with a lofty mass, and formed the spirit of man within him, is likewise the Creator of all souls, so that out of the two substances of soul and body he might put together one living being. For spirit is frequently taken for soul, as there: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit, and: You will take away their spirit and they will fail, and they will return to their dust.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.2-3":["Concerning the Church it can be interpreted thus: that all the persecutors who have fought against the house of the Lord are made drunk with that cup which Jeremiah gives to all the nations to drink, so that they drink and are drunk, and fall, and vomit, and go mad. I know that in the time of persecution many of our own were compelled to fight against the Church; but whoever wishes to lift this weight will indeed lift it, and will bear it up in his hands for the wrath of the Lord by which he chastises sinners; yet he himself will not go unpunished, since the sword of God fights against him. For the stone of burden, which we have rendered as best we could, the Seventy translated: A stone trodden down by all the nations. Everyone who treads it down — or her, that is, Jerusalem —, mocking shall mock. Its sense is plain: that in both ways Jerusalem is to be trodden down both by hostile nations and by persecutors, and is to be mocked at the pleasure of those who mock. But the former sense is better and truer. We expound each point briefly, so that we may at last come to the end, lest, if we write at greater length, the reader's mind be confused both by the obscurity of the matters and by the length of the discourse.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.2":["This Creator, then, and Lord of the universe testifies that he will make Jerusalem a lintel of drunkenness for all the peoples round about, so that whoever touches its threshold may be made drunk and fall, or else the lintel itself may fall upon the one by whom it is touched. But Judah too, when Jerusalem is besieged, is captured by the nations, and passing into their fellowship he will be compelled to besiege his own mother city.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.3":["And not only will the Lord make Jerusalem like a lintel of drunkenness for all the peoples; he will also make her like a stone of burden for all the peoples, and whoever tries to lift it will be torn with gashing. For all the kingdoms of the earth will be gathered against Jerusalem. It is the custom in the cities of Palestine, and to this day the old practice is kept throughout all Judea, that in villages, towns and forts round stones of very great weight are set, on which young men are accustomed to exercise themselves; and they lift them according to the variety of their strength, some up to the knees, some up to the navel, others to the shoulders and the head, some above the crown of the head, with arms held straight and hands joined, raising the weight and showing the greatness of their strength. On the citadel of the Athenians, beside the statue of Minerva, I saw a bronze sphere of very great weight, which I, because of the weakness of my poor body, could scarcely move. And when I asked what it meant, the answer was given by the inhabitants of that city that the strength of athletes was proved by that mass, and that no one went down to a contest until it was known, from his lifting of the weight, with whom he ought to be matched. So the sense is this: I will make Jerusalem for all the nations like a very heavy stone to be lifted. They will indeed lift her, and lay her waste according to the variety of their strength; but it must be that, while she is being lifted, in the very straining and raising of the weight, the very heavy stone leaves some gash or scrape on the bodies of those who lift it.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.4":["On that day, says the Lord, I will strike every horse with astonishment, and its rider with madness; and upon the house of Judah I will open my eyes, and every horse of the peoples I will strike with blindness. LXX: On that day, says the Lord Almighty, I will strike every horse with astonishment, and its rider with madness; but upon the house of Judah I will open my eyes, and all the horses of the peoples I will strike with blindness. At that time — for this is what 'day' signifies —, when Jerusalem shall have been besieged, so that Judah too is compelled to besiege her, the Lord will strike, both carnally and spiritually, all the horses of the adversaries with astonishment, so that all who see that they have been struck are astonished, and their riders, under the pressure of the greatness of their ills, will be turned to madness; and upon the house of Judah, who were being compelled to do something against their mother city, the Lord will open his eyes, to have mercy on them, and to make them worthy of his gaze, and to illumine them with the light of his eyes; but all the horses of the peoples he will strike with eternal blindness. What horses are to be understood spiritually we have said above: A horse is deceptive for safety, and: These in chariots, and those in horses, and other things like these. Their riders too we said were either demons or false teachers, who will all be turned to astonishment and madness, so that they are convicted of knowing nothing, but darkness that can be felt overwhelms them, such as there was when the firstborn of the Egyptians were struck. But upon the house of Judah, that is, the people that confesses God and that, broken by the straits of persecution and terrified by fear, seems to be in the number of the persecutors, the Lord will open his eyes, so as to look back upon many who say to him: Look upon me, and have mercy on me; and they may deserve to hear: And the Lord shall be to you an everlasting light. This is the tearing by the very heavy stone, which the Lord threatens against his adversaries, who tried to lift and harass Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.5":["And the chiefs of Judah shall say in their heart: Let the inhabitants of Jerusalem be strengthened for me in the Lord of hosts, their God. LXX: And the tribunes — that is, οἱ χιλίαρχοι —, of Judah shall say in their hearts: We shall find for ourselves those who dwell in Jerusalem in the Lord Almighty, their God. When the Lord has opened his eyes upon Judah, and has struck all the horses of the nations with blindness, the chiefs of Judah, of whom it was said above: But Judah too shall be in the siege against Jerusalem, will make vows in their hearts, since they will not dare to speak freely, that Jerusalem may conquer, and that Judah, conquered along with the enemies, may conquer along with his fellow citizens. Where we said: Let them be strengthened for me, and the LXX translated εὑρήσομεν ἑαυτοῖς, that is: we shall find for ourselves, in the Hebrew is written emsa li, which Aquila translated καρτέρησόν μοι, that is, strengthen for me, so that the sense is: The chiliarchs and tribunes, and the chiefs of Judah, will wish, and will make vows in the hidden secret place of their minds, that God may strengthen the inhabitants of Jerusalem in the Lord their God, and that they may conquer their adversaries. According to tropology, the chiefs and tribunes are the apostles, and all apostolic men and teachers who have been set over the army of Christ, who would wish to find for themselves no others but those who dwell in Jerusalem, the vision of peace, and who dwell in the Lord Almighty, their God. Of these chiefs was Paul the apostle, who found Titus and Timothy, Luke and Silvanus; Peter too, who instructed Mark, the writer of the Gospel; and the rest of the apostles, who filled the whole world with their teaching and learning, so as to have as disciples inhabitants of Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.6-7":["All, then, who are on the right way, to whom it is said: Do not be overly just, and on the left, who hear: The ways that are on the left are perverse, the devouring flame shall consume; and when the adversaries have been destroyed and removed, Jerusalem, that is, the Church, will again receive her former glory; and she will be in her own state, and the tents of Judah will be saved, the gatherings of Christians scattered throughout the whole world, from which, as from tents and tabernacles, we long to go to the house that is not made with hands, and to the heavenly Jerusalem. And it is for this reason that the tents of the common people, and of all who are reckoned by the Christian name and are counted among the crowd, receive peace first, while the princes of the churches are besieged and put to flight, so that the masters and teachers may in no way think that peace was restored to the Churches by their own teaching and wisdom, but by the help of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.6":["On that day I will make the chiefs of Judah like a furnace of fire among wood, and like a torch of fire in hay; and they shall devour to the right and to the left all the peoples round about; and Jerusalem shall be inhabited again in her own place, in Jerusalem. And the Lord shall save the tents of Judah, as in the beginning, so that the house of David may not glory magnificently, and the glory of the inhabitants of Jerusalem against Judah. LXX: On that day I will make the tribunes of Judah like a firebrand among wood, and like hay of fire in stubble; and they shall devour on the right and on the left all the peoples of the Lord round about, and Jerusalem shall be inhabited still in herself; and the Lord shall save the tents of Judah, as from the beginning, so that the glory of the house of David may not be magnified, and the exaltation of the inhabitants of Jerusalem over Judah. When the chiefs of Judah have said in their hearts: Strengthen for me, O Lord, the besieged of Jerusalem, so that those who have been conquered through their own weakness may prevail by your help, then I, the Lord Almighty, their God, will make the princes of Judah like a furnace of fire among wood, and like a torch in stubble, so that they may devour the adversaries to whom they were joined in feigned friendship. And let them devour on the right and on the left, and slay all the peoples round about, so that Jerusalem may again be inhabited in her own place and may in no way fear hostile assaults.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Let us say that, in the time of the Church's persecution, the chiefs and tribunes of the Christian name, of whom we spoke above, when the Lord has restored peace to Jerusalem and has slain the adversary with the spirit of his mouth, will be like a furnace of fire among wood, to devour the unfruitful trees, and like torches in stubble, so that whatever has no grain, and is carried about by every wind of doctrine, may be given over to burning. And the chiefs of Judah and the tribunes, he says, shall devour to the right and to the left those who were unwilling to walk in the middle of the road, who do not know that ὑπερβολὰς (excesses) belong to κακίας (vice); whence the people of God promises: we will turn aside neither to the right nor to the left, we will walk in the straight way. The right way is stinginess, which the Greeks call φειδολίαν; the left, extravagance; the middle and straight way, frugality.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.7":["Let them restore too the cities and towns, and the farms and villages of the tribe of Judah, which had been plundered and laid waste, as they had been before they were laid waste; and let the royal house, and the renowned and magnificent men of the tribe of Judah, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem in no way glory against the tribe of Judah, that it is ruled by their command and governed by their counsel; but let them know that the victory on both sides is the Lord's. Whether these things according to the history have been done or are yet to come, we leave the truth of the matter to the judgment of the Lord, and to his saints, who have received from him the spirit of wisdom and truth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.8":["On that day the Lord shall shield the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and he who has stumbled among them on that day shall be as David. And the house of David as God, as the angel of the Lord in their sight. LXX: And it shall be on that day, the Lord shall shield the inhabitants of Jerusalem; and he who is weak among them on that day shall be as the house of David; and the house of David as the house of God, and the angel of the Lord in their sight. When Judah obtains the victory, and the Lord restores its tents as they had been from the beginning, so that the royal house may in no way glory against the people, on that day and at that time the Lord — by whose help Judah shall devour its adversaries on the right and on the left — shall shield also the besieged of Jerusalem, and all things shall be changed into such great happiness and blessedness that he who was thought the most worthless shall be as the royal house; and he who was of the royal house shall be as of the house of God, that is, as a messenger of the Lord and of angelic dignity in the sight of those who shall have been saved again at that time. According to anagogy: the Lord shall shield the inhabitants of the Church, when the peace of the churches has been restored after a most grievous persecution, when she shall also be adorned by the interpretation of her own name. For Jerusalem is rendered as vision of peace. And those who have fought as soldiers for the Church and have confessed the Lord in persecution shall be so blessed that he who was least, and who, as a man, has stumbled in some sin and word, shall be placed in the rank of the teachers; and the teachers who have kept their own station shall be as the house of God and as the angel of the Lord, because this is what we labor for with all zeal, set on earth and sweating in varied toil: that we may be transformed into angelic glory.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.9":["And it shall be on that day: I will seek to crush all the nations that come against Jerusalem. And I will pour out upon the house of David and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem the spirit of grace and of prayers. LXX: And it shall be on that day: I will seek to take away all the nations that come against Jerusalem; and I will pour out upon the house of David and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem the spirit of grace and of mercy. On that day when the Lord shall shield the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the Lord will seek to crush all the nations that come against Jerusalem. But he will crush them not unto perdition but unto amendment, so that they may cease to wage war against Jerusalem and begin to be of Jerusalem. For if he created all things out of nothing, he did not make them in order to destroy what he had created, but so that the things that were created might be saved by his mercy. Hence also in the Wisdom that bears the name of Solomon — if, however, anyone is pleased to accept the book — we find it written: He created all things that they might be, and the generations of the world are healthful; and there shall be no deadly poison in them. For just as the Lord came to seek what had perished, and saved the human race, so too he destroyed the nations in this respect, that they were hostile nations.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.10":["In short, there follows: I will pour out upon the house of David and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem the spirit of grace and of mercy. Of this grace the apostle Paul also writes: The charity of God has been poured out in our hearts through the Holy Spirit, who has been given to us. And in the above-mentioned volume it is contained: The things that are in heaven, who has searched out? Except that you gave wisdom and sent your Holy Spirit from on high; and so the paths of those who dwell on earth were corrected; and men were taught the things that please you. And in Isaiah the Lord says: I have given my spirit upon you. And again Scripture records of the same: I have given my Spirit upon him. But the word of pouring out shows the sense of largess, as is plain in what we have said: The charity of God has been poured out in your hearts. And in another place, in the person of God: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh. And what the spirit of grace is, the same Apostle tells the Hebrews: How much worse punishments do you think he deserves who has trampled upon the Son of God, and has counted polluted the blood of the covenant in which he was sanctified, and has done outrage to the spirit of grace. Hence also the graces of the Holy Spirit are said to be diverse. And the greeting of the Apostle: Grace, he says, and peace be multiplied to you, so that after he has forgiven us our sins, then peace may follow through mercy. The Jews record these things as already done in part, and to come more fully at the consummation of the world. But we understand and prove that they are fulfilled daily since the coming of Christ.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","And they shall look upon me whom they pierced; and they shall mourn him with a mourning as over an only-begotten, and they shall grieve over him, as one is wont to grieve at the death of a firstborn. LXX: And they shall look upon me, because they mocked; and they shall mourn over him a mourning, as over one most dear. And they shall grieve with grief, as over a firstborn. The Hebrew letters daleth and res, that is, d and r, are alike and are distinguished only by a small stroke. From this it comes about that those who read the same word differently translate it now one way, now another. For the sake of understanding let us give one example: And Samuel, it says, was clothed in an ephod bad, that is, in a linen garment; for linen is called bad; hence also linens are called baddim. In place of this Hebrew and Latin expression some wrongly read ephod bar; for bar means either a son, or a sheaf of grain, or chosen, or οὖλος, that is, curly. What happened there by an error of interpretation we detect has happened here too. For if dacaru is read, it is taken as ἐξεκέντησαν, that is, they pricked or they pierced; but if, in the reverse order, with the letters exchanged, racadu, it is understood as ὠρχήσαντο, that is, they danced. And the error was born from the likeness of the letters. But John the evangelist, who drew wisdom from the breast of the Lord, a Hebrew of the Hebrews whom the Savior loved most, did not greatly care what the Greek letters contained, but rendered word from word, as he had read it in the Hebrew, and said that it was fulfilled at the time of the Lord's passion. But if anyone does not accept this, let him bring evidence from what passage of the holy Scriptures John brought forth these words, and when he has not found it, he will be forced against his will to accept the truth. And the Jews shall mourn as over an only-begotten and a firstborn, the same thing being signified in the Lord Savior, both only-begotten and firstborn. He is called Only-begotten on account of the property of his nature; Firstborn, according to the Apostle, of those who rise from the dead. For only-begotten the Seventy translated most dear, of whom we read in the Gospel: This is my most dear Son, in whom I am well pleased. Then they shall grieve that he was crucified by them, when they see him reigning in glory. The word κατωρχήσαντο (they danced over), among the Greeks, is compounded not from mockery but from dancing, namely because they danced against the Lord as if in sport, when, mocking and laughing, they said: Vah, you who destroy the temple and in three days build it, save yourself, coming down from the cross. These and other things they spoke as they mocked, dancing in a kind of frenzied leaping.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.11":["On that day there shall be a great mourning in Jerusalem, like the mourning of Adadremmon in the plain of Megiddo. And the land shall mourn, families and families apart; the families of the house of David apart, and the women — or wives —, of them apart — for the Hebrew word nese, that is, γυναῖκες, signifies both —, the families of the house of Nathan apart, and their women apart, the families of the house of Levi apart and their women apart, the families of Shimei apart and their women apart. All the remaining families, families and families apart and their women apart. LXX: On that day there shall be a great mourning in Jerusalem, like the mourning for the pomegranate that is cut down in the plain, and the land shall mourn by tribes and tribes; the tribe of David apart and their women apart, the tribe of the house of Judah apart and their women apart, the tribe of the house of Nathan apart and their women apart, the tribe of the house of Levi apart and their women apart, the tribe of Simeon apart and their women apart; all the remaining tribes apart and their women apart. Adadremmon, for which the LXX translated ῥοῶνος (of the pomegranate grove), is a city near Jezreel which was once called by this name, and today is called Maximianopolis, in the plain of Megiddo, in which Josiah, the just king, was wounded by the Pharaoh surnamed Necho; over him Jeremiah wrote Lamentations, which are read in the Church, and that he wrote them the book of Chronicles testifies. Just as, therefore, at that time, after the sinful kings, all the hope of the people was in Josiah, and when he was slain a great mourning was stirred up in the city, as we read in the Hebrew: The spirit of our mouth, Christ the Lord, was taken in our sins, to whom we said: In your shadow we shall live among the nations. Although others, according to the spiritual understanding, refer this to the Lord Jesus; so, when the Savior has been crucified, the mourning shall be renewed in Jerusalem, as once it was in the city of Adadremmon, in the plain of Megiddo.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","Let us say also that according to the LXX it is called ῥοῶν, not a single tree of pomegranates, that is, of the Punic apple, but a place planted with these trees, of which, according to the spiritual understanding, the bridegroom says in the Song of Songs: I went down to see in the produce of the torrent whether the vine had flowered, whether the pomegranates had flowered. For the Savior went down to the torrent of this world and its turbid waters, from which Elijah too, in a type of him, is described as having drunk, so that after the flowers of the vine and of the pomegranate he might receive the fruit of both, and, making his Church drunk, might hear from her: You shall give me to drink of the perfumer's wine, of the wine of my pomegranates. A drink of this kind not only drives away the heats of the stomach, but is also said to heal a corrupted belly and to benefit the other inner organs. For nothing is more beautiful than this fruit; in its redness it signifies the modesty of the Church, in the order of its seeds the ranks and members of the whole body distributed through their several offices. When the Savior has not found fruit in a vine and pomegranates of this kind, he will say: Every branch that does not bear fruit the Father will take away; and every one that bears fruit he will prune, that it may bring more fruit. And in another place John the Baptist cries out: Already the axe is laid to the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not make good fruit shall be cut down and cast into the fire. At the cutting down of the vines or of the pomegranates, when on the day of judgment all titles of dignity shall be laid down and that which is written shall have been fulfilled: Behold the man and his works; and when the chaff has been separated from the wheat, there shall be a great mourning not in another place but in Jerusalem. For the blow and the judgment shall begin from the saints, and kings and priests and prophets and teachers shall beat their breasts with their hands when they see that the most beautiful pomegranates have been cut down, and him whom they had pierced reigning in the majesty of the Father and his own.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.12-13":["And what follows: The families and families shall mourn, or tribes and tribes, apart: the families of the house of David apart, and the wives or the women of them apart; this signifies that in a time of tribulation and grief we ought not to be given over to marriage and the work of wedlock. Hence also in Joel, when the captivity was near, it is said to the Jews: Let the bridegroom go forth from his chamber, and the bride from her bridal room. And when the flood was impending, Noah is commanded: Enter into the ark, you and your sons, and your wife, and the wives of your sons. And afterwards, when the flood was over, it is said to him: Go out, you and your wife, and your sons, and their wives, so that those who had been separated in the ark while the peril hung over them, once restored to the world, might devote themselves to begetting and to children. And this is done not only in a time of distress but also in a time of prayer, when we wish to entreat the Lord, as the Apostle says to the Corinthians: Do not defraud one another, except perhaps by consent for a time, that you may give yourselves to prayer. Therefore now too the tribe of the house of David, and the tribe of the house of Nathan, and the tribe of the house of Levi, and the tribe of the house of Shimei are separated from their wives, so that they may mourn the only-begotten and firstborn Lord Jesus, of whom it had been said: His blood be upon us and upon our children. In David the royal tribe is understood, that is, Judah. In Nathan the prophetic order is described. Levi refers to the priests, from whom the priesthood arose. In Shimei the teachers are understood, for from this tribe the hosts of the masters sprang up.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.12.14":["He was silent about the remaining tribes, which have no privilege of dignity. But in saying: All the remaining tribes, tribes and tribes apart, and their wives apart, he includes them all without a name.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.1":["On that day there shall be a fountain open to the house of David and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, for the washing of the sinner and of the menstruous woman. LXX: On that day every place shall be opened in the house of David and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, both for transformation and for sprinkling. Of this fountain, which goes forth from the house of David, it is written also in the prophet Ezekiel that a fountain bursts forth in the house of the Lord and grows into a river, which is called the water of remission and pardon, and goes on to the wilderness and to the sea which is now called the Dead Sea, and gives life to all the fish, and on either bank of the river trees of various kinds rise up, always abounding with new fruits month by month. And so that we may know that the house, that is, the temple of God, is itself the house of David, which in Ezekiel is called the house of God and in Zechariah the house of David, this fountain going forth from the house of God is referred to the Church and to the knowledge of the Scriptures, so that we may all be reborn in Christ and our sins may be forgiven us in the water of baptism. Nothing is more unclean than a menstruous woman, who makes unclean whatever she touches, and yet her filth shall be washed away by the baptism of Christ. For the washing of sins and of the menstruous woman the LXX translated transformation and sprinkling, because we pass over from the law to the Gospel, from the letter to the spirit, from the shadow to the truth, and things future and eternal succeed in place of things brief and present. And the sprinkling signifies the blood of the Lord, of which the apostle Peter also speaks: Grace to you, and peace be multiplied, unto obedience and the sprinkling of the blood of Jesus Christ, and again: Knowing that you were redeemed not with corruptible things, silver and gold, but with the precious blood as of a lamb unspotted and most pure; whoever has been sprinkled and redeemed with it will be able to say with the prophet: You shall sprinkle me, O Lord, with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed; you shall wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.2":["And it shall be on that day, says the Lord of hosts, I will destroy the names of the idols from the land, and they shall be remembered no more; and the prophets and the unclean spirit I will take away from the land. LXX: And it shall be on that day, says the Lord Sabaoth, I will destroy the names of the idols from the land, and there shall be no more remembrance of them; and the false prophets and the unclean spirit I will take away from the land. On that day, which he mentions so often, all idols shall be taken away from the land: whether those idols of which the psalmist says: The images of the nations are silver and gold, the works of men's hands, so that there may be no other religion but only that of the Christian name, and of which it is written in the prophet: Howl, you carved images in Jerusalem and in Samaria. For as I have done to Samaria and to her carved images, so will I do to Jerusalem and to her idols; or those idols of which the Apostle speaks: But the Spirit says manifestly that in the last times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits and doctrines of demons, in the hypocrisy of liars who have their conscience cauterized. For just as idols are made by the hand of a craftsman, so the perverse doctrine of the heretics turns into an idol whatever it has feigned, and causes the antichrist to be worshipped in place of Christ. For false prophets, in the Hebrew prophets is read absolutely, that is, nebim, who themselves also signify false prophets; but besides, the priests of the idols are also called prophets by the heathen.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.3":["And it shall be, when anyone prophesies any more, his father and his mother, who begot him, shall say to him: You shall not live, because you have spoken a lie in the name of the Lord; and his father and his mother, who begot him, shall pierce him when he prophesies. LXX: And it shall be, if a man prophesies any more, his father and his mother, who begot him, shall say to him: You shall not live, because you have spoken a lie in the name of the Lord; and his father and his mother who begot him shall fetter him when he prophesies. When the names of the idols have been taken away from the land, and the false prophets, and the unclean spirit that spoke in them, if anyone wishes to try any more and to prophesy something in the person of the Lord, at once his father and mother will forget that they are parents, so as to hold fast to the service of God, and they will pronounce the sentence of death against their son; and the minds of all will be so devout toward God that no public judgment will be awaited, but those who are such will perish by the sentence of their kinsfolk. For what we have rendered, they shall pierce him, there is in the Hebrew the same word as above, dacaru. By what reasoning, then, did the LXX translators choose to translate there κατωρχήσαντο, that is, they insulted, or they mocked, and, to render word for word, they danced against him, and here συμποδιοῦσιν, that is, they shall fetter, when Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion rendered alike both there and here: they pierced? And rightly is he who has prophesied falsehoods called a man, as the Apostle says: For since there are among you rivalries and contentions, are you not carnal, and do you not walk according to man? And in the psalms: But you shall die like men, and fall like one of the princes. And those unworthy of the Spirit of God deserve to hear: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.4":["And it shall be on that day, the prophets shall be confounded, each one by his vision when he has prophesied, nor shall they be covered with a cloak of sackcloth in order to lie. But he shall say: I am not a prophet, I am a man, a tiller of the ground, for Adam has been my example from my youth. And it shall be said to him: What are these wounds in the middle of your hands? And he shall say: With these I was wounded in the house of those who loved me. LXX: And it shall be on that day, the prophets shall be confounded, each one by his vision when he has prophesied, and they shall put on a skin of haircloth because they have lied; and he shall say: I am not a prophet, for a man begot me from my youth. And I will say to him: What are these wounds in the middle of your hands? And he shall say: Those with which I was struck in the house of the one who loved me. Whoever has attempted to prophesy, and has been condemned by the judgment of his parents, and whose prophecy has been proved false by the contrary outcome of events, shall be confounded by his vision, and shall no longer be covered with haircloth in order to lie. For this was the habit of the prophets: that when they were calling the people to repentance, they put on haircloth. Hence Isaiah too is commanded to take the sackcloth from his loins and to walk naked; for it was by no means a time of repentance, but of impending captivity.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","What is written, Nor shall they be covered with a cloak of sackcloth in order to lie, the Hebrews expound thus: and they shall not be filled with the demonic spirit, those whom we read of in Isaiah as τριχιῶντας, that is, hairy ones, lest on this occasion of a feigned habit they belie the utterances of God within them, or deny God. For Chaesu can be interpreted both 'they shall lie' and 'they shall deny'.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.5":["Therefore this false prophet too will by no means take up the prophetic habit, lest under another's clothing and covering he deceive all the simple, but rather he will cleave the earth with the ploughshare and, given over to farming, will prove himself a man, and subject to the sentence of God, who spoke to Adam: Cursed is the ground in your work; in toil you shall eat of it all the days of your life; thorns and thistles it shall bring forth for you, and you shall eat the plants of the earth; and in the sweat of your face you shall eat bread.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.6":["And when he has shown that he was born for this, to eat his bread in the sweat of his face, another will question him and say: What do these wounds mean, and these gashes that are in the middle of your hands? And the sense is: Why do you cling to the gibbet? Why are your hands pierced with nails? What have you committed, that you should be subjected to this penalty and torment? And he will answer and say: These gashes and these wounds I received, condemned by the judgment of my parents and of those who did not hate me but loved me. And truth will prevail so far, once falsehood has been put to flight, that even he who has been punished for his own fault confesses that he has suffered justly.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.7":["O sword, be roused against my shepherd and against the man who cleaves to me, says the Lord of hosts. Strike the shepherd and the sheep shall be scattered, and I will turn my hand to the little ones. And there shall be in all the land, says the Lord: two parts in it shall be destroyed and shall fail, and the third part shall be left in it. And I will lead the third part through fire, and I will burn them as silver is burned, and I will test them as gold is tested. He shall call on my name and I will hear him; I will say: You are my people; and he shall say: The Lord is my God. LXX: O sword, arise against my shepherd and against the man his fellow citizen, says the Lord Almighty. Strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered, and I will bring my hand upon the shepherds. And it shall be on that day, says the Lord, two parts shall perish and shall fail, and the third part shall be left in it; and I will lead the third part across through fire, and I will burn them as silver is burned and I will test them as gold is tested. He shall invoke my name, and I will hear him and will say: This is my people; and he shall say: You are the Lord my God. For sword (framea), which the LXX translated ῥομφαίαν, we find in the Hebrew areb, and in Aquila and Symmachus μάχαιραν, that is, sword or point. And for what we have rendered, Against the man who cleaves to me, that is, προσκεκολλημένον μοι, Aquila translated: Against the man of my tribe, that is, σύμφυλόν μοι. Symmachus: Against the man of my people, which in Hebrew is said amithi. The LXX: Against the man his fellow citizen. Theodotion: Against the man his neighbor; they read vav at the end of the word instead of yod, letters which differ from each other only in size; and if yod is read it means 'my', if vav, 'his'. But I am amazed that some wish to thin out with allegorical interpretations this prophecy — which the evangelist Matthew referred to the Lord and Savior after the disciples fled at his passion, and says that it was then fulfilled —, and, while they want to seem to know more than the rest, do not hold to the rule of truth. For the evangelist Matthew relates: Then Jesus says to them: You will all take offense at me this night. For it is written: I will strike the shepherd and the sheep of the flock shall be scattered. And again: But all this was done that the scriptures of the prophets might be fulfilled. Then all the disciples, leaving him, fled. Nor should it be thought that the testimony was taken from another place because in the Gospel God says that the shepherd was struck by himself, while in the present place we read that the sword and the point are commanded: Strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered. This is the sword, and this is the broadsword and the sword (framea), of which the Lord also speaks to the Father in Psalm 21 (22): Deliver my soul from the sword and my only one from the hand of the dog. Of this broadsword it is also written in Amos the prophet: All the sinners of the earth shall die by the sword. This cannot stand at all according to the letter, for many sinners perish by shipwreck, others by poison; these are choked by water, those a fire consumes. But by this sword and this broadsword all sinners perish, and the Ethiopians of black color too, of whom Zephaniah testifies with his sacred mouth, saying: But you also, Ethiopians, shall be slain by my sword. After the good shepherd, who laid down his life for his sheep, who spoke to the Father: Him whom you struck they have persecuted, and they have added to the pain of my wounds, was struck by the Father's will, and the man who cleaves to God, who says: I am in the Father, and the Father in me, hung on the gibbet and said: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit, at once the sheep were scattered, the whole multitude of believers in Christ. And the Lord turned his hand, as is read in the LXX, to the shepherds, whom many wrongly interpret as the princes of the Jews. But as it is written in the Hebrew, to the little ones, to whom the Lord had said in the Gospel: Fear not, little flock, and in Isaiah: Behold I and the children, or the little ones whom the Lord has given me.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","For it is written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered. This is written in other words in Zechariah the prophet, and, unless I am mistaken, it is said in the person of the prophet to God: Strike the shepherd, and let the sheep be scattered, Psalm 68 (69) also, which is sung throughout in the Lord's person, agreeing with this sense: For they have persecuted him whom you have struck. And the good shepherd is struck so that he may lay down his life for his sheep, and that out of many flocks of errors there may be made one flock and one shepherd. Of this testimony more has been said in the little book which we wrote on the best kind of translating.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ZEC.13.8":["And two parts were made in all the land, of the Jews and of the Gentiles, who perished alike and failed. For at his passion the voice of the psalmist was fulfilled, saying: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed, and: All have turned aside, together they have become useless; there is none who does good, there is not even one. As the Gentiles and the Jews were perishing, a third part in the world, that is, the third people, of Christians, suddenly sprang up. And he says beautifully: The third part shall be left in it, that is, in the land, because of the Jews and the Gentiles those alone who confessed the Lord were kept for life and for dwelling in the land.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.13.9":["And that this third part too might not be soft, and its confession careless, it is led through fire and tested like silver and gold, the fire which the Lord too longs to blaze in believers and Paul desires to be fervent. Hence the apostles too, baptized in the Spirit and in the fire of the Lord, speak in the psalm: For you have tested us, O God; you have tried us with fire as silver is tried. And a little after: We passed through fire and water, and you brought us out into refreshment. And in another place it is said to the believer: If you pass through fire, the flame shall not burn you, because I am with you. And the reason the fire does not consume and the flame does not devour is this: For the voice of the Lord cuts through the flame of fire. When the little ones, over whom the Lord turned his hand, have been tested in this way, and through their calling the whole throng of the nations has believed, then the people of believers will call Christ by his name, and when he says: You are my people, the people will answer: You are the Lord my God. The Jews refer these things to Christ and contend that they will come to pass in the last time, but the difference between us and them is this: we say that they have already been fulfilled, they speak of them as yet to be fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.1":["Behold, the days of the Lord shall come, and your spoils shall be divided in the midst of you. And I will gather all the nations to Jerusalem for battle, and the city shall be taken, and the houses shall be laid waste, and the women shall be violated, and half of the city shall go forth into captivity, and the rest of the people shall not be taken away from the city. LXX: Behold, the days of the Lord come, and your spoils shall be divided in you, and I will gather all the nations against Jerusalem, to make war; and the city shall be taken, and the houses shall be plundered and the women shall be defiled, and half of the city shall go forth into captivity; but the rest of my people shall not perish from the city. The days which the Lord threatens shall come, so that the spoils of Jerusalem are divided in the midst of it, and the other things that the prophetic word includes, are those of which we also read in Isaiah: The day of the Lord shall come, incurable, of fury and of wrath, to lay the whole world waste, and to take away the sinners from it. But how great will the distress be, that its spoils are divided in the midst of it? It often happens that what has been plundered in a city by a sudden assault is divided outside, in the field or in the wilderness, lest perhaps an enemy come upon them. But here so great a weight of evils will press down that what has been plundered is divided in the middle of the city, so secure is the victory.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.2":["And not only shall Jerusalem be taken, when all the nations have been roused against it in battle, but also the houses of those who dwell in Jerusalem shall be laid waste, and the women shall be violated, to the grief of their masters and husbands, who will be able to prevent neither the pillaging of their houses nor the rape of their wives by the enemy, according to what we read elsewhere: Those who are gathered in the midst of you shall fall by the sword, and they shall dash your sons to pieces in your sight, and they shall plunder your houses, and they shall have your wives; nothing crueller and nothing more wretched than this can be found, that from fear of their own death they dare to defend neither the safety of their sons nor the chastity of their wives. Amos the prophet too says this very thing, threatening Amaziah the impious priest: Your wife shall play the harlot in the city, and your sons and daughters shall fall by the sword, and your ground shall be measured with a line. And all these things shall befall the people of the Jews: For the nations raged, and the peoples meditated vain things. The kings of the earth stood by and the princes came together as one against the Lord and against his Christ. And he laughed at them and derided them, and troubled them in his fury, so much so that the Apostle too, seeing that the years which had been given them for repentance were now completed, and that those who killed the Lord and persecuted the prophets and the apostles nevertheless persisted in denial, said: Wrath has come upon them to the end. Josephus, who wrote the Jewish history, relates most fully that they endured all these things, and things much greater than we read in the prophets; so too Cornelius Tacitus, who set down in thirty volumes the lives of the Caesars after Augustus up to the death of Domitian. But how half of the city was taken and the rest of the people remained in the city is proved both at that time and at others: the northern and lower part of the city was taken, but the mountain of the temple, and Zion, on which the citadel was, remained intact. The Jews say that these things are to be fulfilled under Gog, others that they were in part accomplished in the times of the Macedonians and the Egyptians and of various nations. We, leaving the variety of the times to the judgment of the Lord, let us explain what is written.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.3":["And the Lord shall go forth, and shall fight against those nations, as he fought on the day of conflict. And his feet shall stand on that day upon the Mount of Olives, which is opposite Jerusalem to the east; and the Mount of Olives shall be split from the middle of itself to the east and to the west, with a very great chasm. LXX: And the Lord shall go forth, and shall fight against those nations, as on the day of engagement, and on the day of battle. And his feet shall stand on that day upon the Mount of Olives, which is opposite Jerusalem on the eastern side. And the Mount of Olives shall be split, half of it to the east, and half of it to the sea, with an exceedingly great abyss. That God goes forth, and fights against the nations, and that his feet stand upon the Mount of Olives, and the other things that are said ἀνθρωποπάθως (in human fashion) in the holy scriptures and are contained there in a fleshly way, we ought to take in a manner worthy of God. Otherwise, since the Apostle says: Who is the image of the invisible God, and again: To the King of the ages, incorruptible, invisible, God, and it is written in the Gospel: No one has ever seen God; the only-begotten Son, who is in the bosom of the Father, he has made him known, and again: Not that anyone has seen God, except him who is from the Father; just as we take his wrath and repentance and soul and hands, and feet and belly, and eyes and the other members of the body according to the variety of the occasions and of the senses of interpretation, so too what is written, The Lord shall go forth, and shall fight, is to be taken according to what we read in Habakkuk: You went forth for the salvation of your people, for salvation with your Christ, and in Micah: For behold, the Lord shall go forth from his place, and shall come down and tread upon the high places of the earth, and the mountains shall be consumed beneath him, and the valleys shall be split, and in Isaiah too: The Lord of powers shall go forth and crush the war, and shall stir up zeal and shall cry out over his enemies with strength. God therefore goes forth from his place when he is compelled to break off his quiet and gentleness and clemency for the correction of those who sin; though he is sweet by nature, by our fault he παραπικραίνεται, that is, is made bitter, not to himself, but to those who suffer, to whom the torments are bitter. He who elsewhere says through the prophet: I am God, and I do not change, and to whom it is said: But you are the same, and you endure, and in the epistle of James: With whom there is no change, now goes forth and fights as on the day of conflict, when he drowned Pharaoh in the Red Sea and fought for the people of Israel.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.4-5":["Let us speak παραφραστικῶς (by paraphrase), so that what is written may become clear: When the Mount of Olivet has been broken open by a great abyss, so that one part of the abyss faces east, the other west, suddenly in that very abyss, sheer and lofty on either side, another abyss will break open, one toward the north, another toward the south, and the chasm will become four-cornered, so that the abyss stretches in four directions, to the four quarters of east and west, of north and south. And you shall flee, he says, to the valley that is between the temple and Zion. For these two mountains, of the temple and of Zion, are called the mountains of God; because that valley of the Mount of Olivet, which is girded on this side and that by sheer mountains, will draw its abyss as far as the mountain of the temple, which is holy. But the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Judah is said to belong to that time when Uzziah, who is called by another name Azariah, trying to claim for himself a priesthood not lawful for him, was struck with leprosy on his forehead; of this earthquake we also read at the beginning of Amos: The words of Amos, who was among the shepherds of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake. In short, after this wicked king died, at whose impiety the whole earth was shaken, Isaiah saw the great vision which he wove into his book. Let us pass to the spiritual understanding. After the Mount of Olives has been separated toward the east and the west by the calling of the nations and the casting off of the Jews, there will again be another cleft, of north and south. The north will be joined to the west, the south to the eastern quarter; on the left will stand the circumcision, on the right the Christian people. Of these two winds the Church speaks: Arise, O north wind, and come, O south wind, so that, as the north wind, a most cold wind, which is interpreted as the devil, withdraws, the south wind, a warm wind, may come; seeking him, the bride says: Where do you pasture, where do you lie down, at noon? Of him Habakkuk too speaks mystically: God will come from Teman; for which it is written in the Hebrew: God from the south, that is, from the fullest light. Of this light the psalmist elsewhere cries out: You give light wonderfully from the everlasting mountains. But when the division of the two peoples in the whole world has become so great that some are separated toward the east and the south, that is, to the right, others toward the north and the west, that is to say, to the left, then whoever is holy will flee to the valley of the mountains of God, of which we spoke above, of the temple and of Zion, that is to say, the two testaments, for that abyss which had formerly belonged to each mountain will stretch as far as Asael, that is, as far as the house of God, which is near to paradise, and to the heavenly Jerusalem, and to the holy mountain on which the temple is set. And as at that time when, under King Uzziah the leper, a most violent earthquake terrified the hearts of mortals and scattered them, terrified, this way and that. So the separation of the two peoples, and again the fellowship of believers in one faith, will rest between the two mountains in a peaceful seat, for the old and the new instrument will both be joined to each other. But what is said, As far as Asael, that is, as far as the nearest, means this: that the two instruments, of the law and of the Gospel, are indeed joined to each other, but joined in such a way that they are neighbors rather than one; for we have let go many things of the old law, and have taken up many of the new grace. And when this has been accomplished in this manner, then, he says, the Lord my God shall come, and all the saints with him. But if, for what we have said, you shall flee, we wish to follow what the other translators translated, The valley of their mountains shall be stopped up, or shall be filled, we shall say this: that the coming of the Lord and Savior, when the two peoples have been separated from each other, stops up and fills that abyss which divided the two peoples, so that they may walk toward each other on a level road.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.4":["And his feet shall stand for those on whom he has had mercy, and shall by no means be moved in such a way that it could again be said of him: And the Lord shall sound the trumpet, and shall walk in the threat of his wrath. Nor shall he walk when the sun is already declining and the darkness is near, after midday, as we read that he did over Adam. And when he has stood, he will not stand in a valley and in low places but on a mountain, one that has no fruitless trees and barren forest, but where olive groves grow, by which the eternal light is fed, and infirmities are loosed, and rest is given to the weary. And this Mount of Olives, on which the feet of the Lord stand, is opposite Jerusalem and to the east, whence the sun of justice rises, and it is planted with those olive trees of which it is said: Your sons like young olive plants, round about your table. Half of it shall be split toward the east, the half in which are the trees planted from the nations, one of which says: But I, like a fruitful olive tree in the house of God, have hoped. And the other half shall be split toward the west and the sea, with a very great chasm: this is the people of the circumcision, to whom God speaks through the prophet: Why has my beloved done abomination in my house? Shall vows and holy flesh take away your wickedness from you, or will you escape by these? This beloved did abomination in the house of God, so that she crucified the Son of God and heard: The Lord called your name a shady olive tree and a wooded beauty; and your branches became useless, which were broken off from the good root, so that we might be grafted in their place. And note the mysteries of the scriptures, how the half that slopes toward the west, and is beaten by salt and bitter waves, is said to be not simply in the sea, but in the sheer abyss of the sea, an exceedingly great one, of which Micah too speaks: I will drag down its stones into the valley — Jerusalem's, no doubt —, and I will lay bare its foundations. These things we have said, as in very difficult and very obscure places, according to the slenderness of our powers. But the Jews, following the letter that kills, try to show that God stands upon the Mount of Olivet, and that the mountain itself is divided into two parts, so that the cleft has its beginning in one part toward the east, and the other part stretches its end toward the west, and in the middle, very steep, one part is divided toward the north, the other toward the south.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.5":["And the middle of the mountain shall be divided toward the north, and its middle toward the south.\n\nAnd you shall flee to the valley of their mountains, for the valley of the mountains shall be joined as far as the nearest; and you shall flee as you fled from the face of the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and the Lord my God shall come, and all the saints with him. LXX: And half of the mountain shall lean toward the north and half of it toward the south. And the valley of my mountains shall be stopped up, and the valley of the mountains shall be joined as far as Asael; and it shall be filled as it was filled from the face of the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and the Lord my God shall be present, and all the saints with him. For what we have said, you shall flee, the LXX and the other translators translated ἐμφραχθήσεται, that is, shall be stopped up, or shall be filled. And for what we have put: For the valley of the mountains shall be joined as far as the nearest, for nearest the LXX translated Asael, Aquila put the Hebrew word itself, asel, with a short e (ἀζὲλ), Theodotion with a long one (ἀζὴλ); Symmachus alone interpreted it as nearest, and we too have followed him.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.6":["And it shall be on that day, there shall be no light, but cold and frost. And there shall be one day, which is known to the Lord, not day nor night, and at evening time there shall be light. LXX: On that day there shall be no light, but there shall be cold and frost on one day, and that day shall be known to the Lord; and not day nor night, and toward evening there shall be light. Plainly the second coming of the Savior is foretold, of which John too speaks in his Apocalypse: Behold, he shall come with the clouds, and every eye shall see him, and those who pierced him. And the Lord declares in the Gospel that the Son of man will come on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory. He will come with the clouds, that is, with the angels, who are ministering spirits and are sent to various offices, and with the prophets and apostles, of whom it is written: Your truth reaches to the clouds. And when the day of his coming has been fulfilled, there shall be no light, but cold and frost, since the charity of all has grown cold, and, because of the multitude of evils that are to come upon them, all grow chill and lose the warmth of their former faith.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.7":["But after their cold and frost has gripped all sinners, there will be one day, and a perpetual one; light and darkness, day and night, by no means succeeding one another, but the Lord himself will be the light of all; of whom Isaiah says more fully: You shall have no sun, he says, for the light of the day, nor shall the rising of the moon give you light by night, but the Lord shall be your eternal light, and your God your glory. He also cries out to Jerusalem in another place: Be enlightened, be enlightened, O Jerusalem; for your light has come upon you, and the glory of your God has risen upon you. For behold, darkness and gloom shall cover the earth over the nations, but over you the Lord shall appear, and the glory of your God has risen upon you. And kings shall walk in your light, and the nations in your splendor. If gloom and darkness shall cover the earth over the nations, how in turn shall the nations walk in the splendor of the Lord? But those nations will be in darkness which the cold and frost have gripped; and those will walk in the light of the Lord which have followed the kings, the apostles and prophets, of whom it is written: The heart of the king is in the hand of the Lord. In short, at evening time, that is, the time of the darkness and sorrow of all sinners, there will be light for the saints, and one eternal day; and if it shall be known to the Lord — for he will not be able to be ignorant of the day he has created —, we must consider how he says in the Gospel that neither the angels nor the Son know that day, but the Father alone.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.8-9":["And it shall be on that day, living waters shall go out from Jerusalem, half of them to the eastern sea, and half of them to the last sea; in summer and in winter they shall be. And the Lord shall be king over all the earth. LXX: On that day living water shall go forth from Jerusalem, half of it to the first sea, and half of it to the last sea; in summer and in spring it shall be so. And the Lord shall be for a king over all the earth. At that time — for this is what is meant by the day which is known to the Lord alone, in which light and darkness shall not succeed one another, but there shall be perpetual light, according to what we read in the Apocalypse: And the city shall have no need of the light of the sun, since the Lord God Almighty shall be its light, living waters shall go forth from Jerusalem, about which we argued above when we set out the testimony of Ezekiel; half of them shall go to the eastern sea, which the books of the Greeks call λίμνην ἀσφαλτῖτιν (the Asphalt Lake), and which is commonly called the Dead Sea, because nothing can live in its waters. And half to the last sea, which leads to Egypt and forms the shores of Palestine. In summer, he says, and in winter it shall be so, that these waters which shall go out from Jerusalem shall neither be bound by the frost of winter nor dried up by the excessive heat of summer; although for winter the Seventy translated springtime, which does not fit as a contrast to summer. And when the life-giving waters have entered both seas and have softened the bitter waters with their sweet stream, then the Lord shall be king over all the earth. The water which goes out from Jerusalem, that is, from the Church, signifies the teaching of the Savior: For out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem, according to what is written elsewhere: All things are filled with the knowledge of the Lord, as much water covering the sea. Half of these waters shall go to the eastern sea, the people of the circumcision, which was chosen in the apostles and through the apostles; and half of them to the last sea, so that from the east and the west they may come who shall recline with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob. Or at any rate let us understand by the eastern sea and the last sea the Old and the New Instrument, which, unless it has been sweetened by the stream of the Savior and by his spiritual understanding, is most bitter, since the letter kills and the spirit gives life. And in what follows: In summer and in winter they shall be, 'the living waters which go out from Jerusalem' is understood, so that both in peace and in persecutions these living waters do not cease to flow. Or else, since the LXX translated: In summer and in spring it shall be so, let us say this, that at that time there will be no winter, but spring and perpetual summer, when the voice of the turtledove shall be heard in our land, and the fig trees shall bring forth their green figs, and the vines shall flower, and the winter shall pass, and the rain shall withdraw and go its way. At that time spring will be necessary for this reason, that flowers shall appear in our land so that we may celebrate the Pasch and Pentecost, in which we may pass from earthly things to heavenly ones and offer all our fruits to God. For at the Phase is the end of winter and the beginning of spring, at Pentecost the beginning of summer, when we offer to God the labors of our hands and their fruit. Of this perpetual summer and spring the just man speaks to the Lord: You made the dawn and the sun; summer and spring you fashioned. Then the Lord shall be king over all the earth, when we shall say: The Lord has reigned, let the earth rejoice, and again: Say among the nations that the Lord has reigned. For he has set right the world, which shall not be moved. And that the living water has rightly been explained by us as the teaching of the Savior, he will know who has drunk it, and who has heard the Savior preaching: If anyone drinks of the water that I shall give him, there shall be in him a fountain of water living and springing up into eternal life, and again: He who believes in me, as the scripture has said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. For just as he who has drunk of his teaching shall have a living fountain in himself, so he who has believed in him, according to what is contained in the words of the scriptures, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. Many refer the living waters to baptism, which are to be lavished on the thirsty in spring and in summer, that is, at the Pasch and at Pentecost, when what is written shall be fulfilled: Wash yourselves, be clean.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.9-11":["On that day the Lord shall be one, and his name one.\n\nAnd all the land shall be turned back as far as the desert, from the hill of Rimmon to the south of Jerusalem; and it shall be exalted, and shall dwell in its place, from the gate of Benjamin as far as the place of the former gate, as far as the gate of the corners, and from the tower of Ananeel as far as the king's winepresses. And they shall dwell in it, and there shall be no more anathema, but Jerusalem shall sit secure. LXX: On that day the Lord shall be one, and his name one, going round all the land and the desert from Geba as far as Rimmon to the south of Jerusalem. But Rama shall remain in place, from the gate of Benjamin, as far as the first gate, as far as the gate of the corners, and as far as the tower of Anamael, as far as the king's winepresses they shall dwell in it; and there shall be no more anathema, and Jerusalem shall dwell confident. The building of the city of Jerusalem, and the going forth of waters from the midst of it, to flow down to both seas, the Jews and Judaizing Christians promise themselves in the last time, when circumcision is to be practised again, and victims sacrificed, and all the precepts of the law kept, so that it is not Jews who become Christians, but Christians who become Jews. On that day, they say, when Christ shall sit in a Jerusalem of gold and jewels to reign, there shall be no idols nor diverse worship of divinity, but the Lord shall be one, and all the land shall be turned back as far as the wilderness, that is, to its ancient state. And he sets down the names of the places, from which place to which place Jerusalem is to be built: from the hill of Rimmon, for that is what Geba means, where there is a pomegranate tree, as far as the southern quarter of Jerusalem. And as for what follows: But Rama shall remain in its place, Aquila and the rest translated it better, who put: it shall be exalted. For Rama means exaltation, according to that saying of the prophet and the Gospel: A voice was heard in Rama, that is, it sounded on high. Jerusalem therefore shall be exalted, and shall be built in its former place, from the gate of Benjamin as far as the gate which is called of the corners, and from the tower of Ananeel, not Anamael as the Greeks and Latins wrongly read, as far as the king's winepresses, which is written in Hebrew ammelech. And they shall dwell, he says, in it, that is, in Jerusalem, and there shall be no more anathema, that is to say no fear of hostile attack, no dread, but Jerusalem shall sit, or shall be inhabited, resting in eternal peace. These things the Jews dream according to the letter, and our χιλιασταὶ (chiliasts), who long to hear again: Increase and multiply, and fill the earth, and in return for the continence of this life and a brief fast promise themselves bulbs, and sows' wombs, and birds of the Phasis, and the francolin, by no means the Ionian but the Jewish one; of whom the Lord can truly say: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh. For the flesh fights against the spirit, and the spirit against the flesh. And let them not set the Apocalypse of John against us, which itself too must be expounded spiritually.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.9":["But let us interpret the heavenly Jerusalem as the Church, which, walking in the flesh, does not live according to the flesh, and whose citizenship is in heaven. For after the Lord Jesus has become king over all the earth, of whom the Holy Spirit speaks to the Father through the prophet: O God, give your judgment to the king, and your justice to the king's son, then there shall be one Lord, of whom it is written: Know that the Lord himself is God. And his name one, every perverse religion having been trampled down, according to what the prophet sings: O Lord, our Lord, how wonderful is your name in all the earth, and again: You have magnified your holy name above all, and elsewhere: As your name, O God, so also your praise over all the earth; of which Habakkuk too speaks: The earth is full of his praise. Of the glory of this name God himself speaks: Without me you shall not know God, and besides my name, which has been magnified above all.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.10":["Then all the land in which the Jews dwelt shall be turned back as far as the desert, that is, as far as the people of the Gentiles, which was formerly deserted and had no knowledge of the law; from Geba as far as Rimmon, that is, from the hill as far as the height, because from the earth and from the desert we rise to the hills, and from the hills to the mountains. Hence too the bridegroom in the Song of Songs leaps over the hills, which are lower, and springs upon the high mountains, of which it is written: The high mountains are for the harts. But if we wish to take Rimmon as pomegranate, for among the Hebrews it is interpreted both ways, let us say this, that the boundaries of the Church begin from the hills, so that the whole ecclesiastical order may be built up, of which we spoke above when we interpreted the mourning for the only-begotten and the mourning like that of Hadadrimmon. Nor is the Church content with this boundary, but it shall reach as far as the south, in the fullest light, on which we commented some time ago, and so we are silent about it now, lest by inculcating the same things too often we become tiresome to the reader. And the Church shall be exalted, which began from the hills and reaches to the south; and it shall dwell in its place, of which it is written: In a place of pasture, there he has set me. From the gate of Benjamin, which is interpreted son of the right hand and not son of days, as some wrongly suppose. For it is one thing if it ends in the letter nun, another if in mem. And it shall reach as far as the place of the eastern and former gate, as far as the gate of the corners. We begin from virtue, for that is what the right hand signifies, and we reach as far as the former gate, so that through it we may enter the others; and at once the gate of the corners meets us, where the cornerstone is: Which the builders rejected, and it has become the head of the corner. This cornerstone joins both walls and brings the two peoples into one, of which God also speaks through Isaiah: Behold, I will lay in Zion a cornerstone, chosen and precious, in its foundations; and he who believes in it shall not be confounded. This cornerstone willed that other cornerstones too should be built up upon it, so that the apostle Paul could freely say: Built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the chief cornerstone. These stones the heretics do not imitate, who do everything for the sake of glory and gain, and pray at the corners of the streets, leaving the straight path. For every corner breaks a straight line; and therefore the harlot in Proverbs, however we may wish to take her, whose feet do not rest at home, but she either wanders out of doors or lies in wait at every corner of the streets, when she has seen a foolish young man — for she does not invite one whom she has perceived to be prudent and mature with age, of whom it is written: A man's grey hairs are his wisdom —, at once seizes him, and kisses and flatters him, and leads him to the brothel, and invites him to intercourse. Let us therefore leave the feigned corners, and pass over to the firm and strong corners of Christ; and when we have reached them, at once the tower of Ananeel meets us, which is interpreted most pleasing of God. For what is more pleasing than the tower of Solomon? Of which it is said to the bride: Your neck is like the tower of David, which is built in Thalphioth; a thousand shields hang upon it, all the darts of the mighty. This tower the Lord in the Gospel wishes to be built, once the expenses have first been reckoned and the costs counted; and of it it is said to Jerusalem: Let peace be in your strength, and abundance in your towers; of it also the holy man speaks to God: You have led me, for you have become my hope; a tower of strength from the face of the enemy. From the tower of Ananeel we come to the king's winepresses, for which three psalms also have their title, and the Lord says in Isaiah: I have trodden the winepress alone, so that our vintage may abound in them, and we may press out the clusters, and tread the new wine reddening in the blood of Christ, so that we may drink the wine which gladdens the heart of man, and which the bride desires, speaking to the bridegroom's companions: Bring me into the wine cellar, set charity upon me.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.11":["If winepresses of this kind have made us drunk, we shall dwell in Jerusalem, in which there shall be no more anathema, that is to say curse and abomination. Hence the Apostle also says: If anyone does not love the Lord, let him be anathema. And in another place: No one speaking in the Spirit of God says anathema to Jesus. And for the salvation of his brothers he longs to be anathema, wishing to imitate his Lord, who, though he himself was not a curse, was made a curse for us. And when every anathema has been taken away, Jerusalem shall dwell secure and confident, expressing in itself that saying: He who trusts in the Lord is blessed, and: It is better to trust in the Lord than to trust in man; of which security and confidence the prophet Jeremiah makes mention: Blessed shall be the man who trusts in the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.12":["And this shall be the plague with which the Lord shall strike all the nations that have fought against Jerusalem. The flesh of each one shall waste away as he stands upon his feet, and his eyes shall waste away in their sockets, and their tongue shall waste away in their mouth. LXX: And this shall be the ruin with which the Lord shall strike all the peoples who have made war against Jerusalem. Their flesh shall waste away as they stand upon their feet, and their eyes shall flow out of their sockets, and their tongue shall waste away in their mouth. What the nations that shall fight against the city of the Lord are to suffer, the present scripture testifies. They shall stand, he says, upon their feet, and their flesh shall waste away and flow off, and their eyes shall rot and fall out of their sockets; the boastful tongue, which used to blaspheme the people of God, shall be dissolved into corruption and rot within the rampart of the teeth. That the Romans who overthrew Jerusalem did not suffer these things is plain to all, unless perhaps the Jews will assert that those nations shall suffer them which are to fight against the golden and jewelled Jerusalem. But we shall say that all the persecutors who afflicted the Church of the Lord, to say nothing of the torments to come, have received even in the present age what they did. Let us read the ecclesiastical histories, what Valerian, what Decius, what Diocletian, what Maximian, what Maximin, the most savage of all, and lately Julian suffered, and then we shall prove by the facts that the truth of the prophecy was fulfilled even according to the letter: that their flesh rotted, and their eyes wasted away, and their tongue was dissolved into stench and corruption. Moreover, if the heretics think that the cruelty of God is shown by this saying, let them hear that all these things happen so that, as evils fail, good things may grow up in their place. For whoever stands in the Lord and with the Lord, his carnal parts shall flow away so that spiritual ones may arise, and the eyes which saw ill shall fall out of their sockets so that others may be put in their place, which can receive the Lord, with the prophet who says: To you I have lifted up my eyes, you who dwell in the heavens. And the blaspheming tongue shall rot for this reason, that another tongue may be born, which may glorify God and be able to say: My tongue shall meditate on your justice, your praise all the day. Hence Simeon too, taking the infant in his arms and foretelling things to come, says: Behold, this one is set for the ruin and the resurrection of many, so that evil things may fall and the best things be raised up. Hence the Lord too: For judgment, he says, I have come into this world, so that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind. The people of the Gentiles did not see, and after faith in Christ began to behold the light of the truth, which speaks through the prophet: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to proclaim release to the captives and to the blind that they may see, and be enlightened with wisdom, of which it is written: A man's wisdom enlightens his face, and in the psalms we read: The Lord enlightens the blind, or makes them wise. For that is rather what σοφοῖ signifies. The Jews saw, and because they would not receive the light, they were covered with eternal blindness.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.13-14":["On that day there shall be a great tumult from the Lord among them, and a man shall seize the hand of his neighbor, and his hand shall be locked upon the hand of his neighbor. But Judah too shall fight against Jerusalem, and the riches of all the nations round about shall be gathered together, gold and silver, and garments in great abundance. LXX: And it shall be on that day, a great stupor from the Lord upon them, and each one shall seize the hand of his neighbor, and his hand shall cling to the hand of his neighbor. And Judah shall be made ready in Jerusalem, and shall gather the strength of all the peoples round about, gold, silver, and garments exceedingly many. When these wonders have been accomplished, so that the flesh of the enemies flows away, their eyes waste, and the tongue rots in the mouth of the blasphemers, then there shall be a great tumult, or stupor, among them; for this is what ἔκστασις signifies, which the LXX translated: And each one shall seize the hand of his neighbor, and hand shall be joined with the hand of another, for dread and the weight of the evils that shall come upon them. Judah also shall fight against the mother city, of whom we spoke above as well, and, victory having been granted by the Lord, the riches of all the nations that made war against Jerusalem shall be gathered together, gold and silver, and a multitude of garments, which are the most precious of possessions. These things unhappy Judea promises herself, hoping to receive gold, she who valued the Lord at thirty pieces of silver. But let us, following the order we have begun, refer all these things to the blessedness of the Church: that all who are in it marvel at the subjection of their enemies and at their own happiness, and each one seizes the hand of his neighbor, so that they join right hands and are bound together by mutual faith and kinship. And as for what we read in the Hebrew: And Judah shall fight against Jerusalem, for which the LXX translated: And Judah shall be made ready in Jerusalem, let us take it both ways: that Judah, who once confessed the name of the Lord and, under compulsion in the persecutions, persecuted the people of Christ, may himself also be turned to joy. Or at any rate Judah, every one who confesses and is faithful, does not fight against Jerusalem, but makes himself ready in Jerusalem to fight against the adversaries. And this Judah shall gather the riches of all the nations round about, gold, silver and garments exceedingly many. We have often said that gold and silver are to be understood of sense and of speech; hence we ought to take the garments likewise, with which the Church of Christ is adorned, of which it is written: The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety. Clothed with these garments the throng of believers rejoices and says: I will exult in the Lord, for he has clothed me with the garments of salvation, and with the tunic of gladness. For the Lord had said to her: I clothed with both τριχάπτοις (fine-spun stuffs) and fine linen. By τριχάπτοις let us understand fine garments, which are so beautiful and delicate that they seem to resemble hairs. These garments the Church gathers, so as to have the wherewithal to clothe her people, to whom it was commanded by the true Preacher: At all times let your garments be white.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.15":["And so shall be the ruin of the horse, and of the mule and of the camel, and of the ass, and of all the beasts that shall be in those camps, like this ruin. LXX: And this shall be the ruin of the horses and of the mules and of the camels and of the asses, and of all the beasts that shall be in those camps, according to this ruin. These things too the Jews contend are to be fulfilled carnally under their ἠλειμμένῳ (anointed one). Great indeed is the strength of the Lord, that in the camps of the enemies the horses and mules, camels and asses, and all their beasts should fall, just as the men had fallen. A great triumph, a glorious victory, that brute animals should be overcome with God fighting! Let us say, therefore, according to the tropology we have begun, that all the evil things which formerly fought against the Church fall for this reason, that good things may suddenly rise up. In short, he who had formerly been a horse, neighing after his neighbor's wife, and was carried away, unrestrained, to lust and lay in the foulness of pleasures, when he has begun to follow chastity, it shall be said to him: Arise, let us go hence, and: Arise, come, my neighbor, and in the Apostle: Arise, you who sleep, and rise up from the dead, and Christ shall enlighten you. And when the horses that had fallen before have risen, and have offered the Lord soft backs to sit on, they shall say to him: Mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation. In accordance with this sense let us take the mules, which are sterile and do not beget offspring, but frolic in pleasure, of whom the psalmist makes mention: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding. As, then, those who are prone to lust are called horses, so we shall rightly call mules those who are virgins in the flesh, not in the spirit, who make themselves eunuchs not for the sake of the kingdom of heaven but for the sake of men's pleasure. When, therefore, these mules and these eunuchs have been turned to begetting offspring and have produced spiritual sons, they shall hear through Isaiah: And let not the eunuch say: I am a dry tree. For thus says the Lord to the eunuchs who keep my sabbaths and choose what I have willed and hold fast my covenant: I will give them in my house and within my wall a named place, and a name better than sons and daughters; I will give them an everlasting name which shall not fail. Such mules and she-mules the kings of Israel had, and above all David, who is referred to Christ. If we have understood who are the horses and mules that fall and rise again, let us pass on to the camels, an animal that chews the cud and does not divide the hoof, and let us say that the camels are all the sinners of the earth, who are weighed down by the heavy load of their sins, and seem to themselves to read the holy scriptures, but do not divide the hoof, chewing over the divine sayings and neglecting the things that are written. But more rightly can the people of the Jews be called a camel, which itself also meditates on the law of God, and chews it over and turns it in its breast, but does not divide the hoof so as to believe in the Father and in the Son; and it is unclean in this, that it by no means separates the letter from the spirit, the shadow from the truth, and it carries the burdens of the law and hears through the prophet: Woe, sinful nation, a people full of offences. Of a camel of this kind, which chews the cud and does not divide the hoof, it is said in Proverbs too, as though to a son: He who leaves off keeping his father's discipline shall meditate on evil sayings. After the camel let us pass on to the ass, which once was carried along unbridled, and was unclean, and had many masters, and slipped over precipices, so that, having fallen, it might suddenly rise again and carry the Lord and Savior, and enter holy Jerusalem, and the throng of little ones who believed might receive him in triumph. This ass the Savior is said to have tied to the vine and the vineyard, of which it is written in the psalms: You brought a vine out of Egypt; you cast out the nations, and planted it. To which the vinedresser himself speaks: I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true. All the beasts and living creatures too, which the prophet includes under one name, are to be interpreted according to the nature of each, and this befits the Son of God more than the things which the people of the Jews presume in their foolish prophesying.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.16":["And all who are left of all the nations that came against Jerusalem shall go up from year to year to worship the king, the Lord of hosts, and to celebrate the feast of tabernacles. LXX: And it shall be, whoever are left of all the nations that came against Jerusalem shall go up every year to worship the king, the Lord Almighty, and to celebrate the feast of tabernacles. All, he says, who are left of the nations that came against Jerusalem shall go up every year to worship the king, the Lord of hosts, and to celebrate the feast of tabernacles. These things too the Jews, with empty hope, promise will come to pass in the kingdom of a thousand years. The origin of this solemnity is as follows. When the people of Israel had gone out of Egypt through the vast, terrible and wide wilderness, in which there was no house, farmstead, town or cave, they made themselves tabernacles and tents — which are now called papiliones from their likeness to a little bird —, in which they might stay with their wives and children, and take their food, and avoid the heat of the sun by day and by night the damp and the cold and the harm of the dew; and it was commanded that in the seventh month, on the fifteenth day of the month, the solemnity of tabernacles should be held: And when your son, he says, asks you tomorrow, saying: What do these tabernacles mean? You shall answer him: For a long time we sojourned in Egypt, out of which the Lord led us into the wilderness, and therefore we set up tabernacles, so that we may remember the benefits of God at all times, now that we have begun to dwell in cities. He commanded also that they make the tabernacles of the most beautiful wood, which the Jews call citron, and of the branches of palms and the boughs of the thickest tree, and of the willow, and of the poplar. We have laid the foundations of the history, so that from these we may pass to spiritual things. As long as we are advancing and in the race and the contest, we dwell in tabernacles, striving with our whole mind for this, that from tabernacles we may pass to a firm and stable dwelling, that is, the house of God. Hence the holy man also says in the psalm: Woe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged, and: I am a stranger and a pilgrim, as all my fathers were. This is the speech of one who is in Egypt and still set in the world. But he who goes out of Egypt, which in Hebrew is called mesraim and is interpreted tribulation, and enters the wilderness of the vices, takes up his journey and says in the psalm: I will pass over into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, as far as the house of God. For it is a wonderful thing to refuse to dwell with the Egyptians, and, Pharaoh having been drowned, to long to enter the land of promise. Hence he also says elsewhere: How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of powers. My soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord, and a little after: Blessed are all who dwell in your house; for ever and ever they shall praise you. The voice, for, of exultation and salvation is in the tabernacles of the just. And in Proverbs we find it written: The houses of the just remain, and the tabernacles of those who act rightly shall stand. Of the houses he promises that they remain, of the tabernacles that they shall stand. The holy man also says in another place: One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek, that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life, and see the delight of the Lord, and visit his temple. He who dwells in tabernacles of this kind, and hastens to go from the tabernacles to the courts, and from the courts to the house, and from the house to the temple of the Lord, ought to celebrate the solemnity of tabernacles with the most beautiful wood of wisdom, of which it is said in Proverbs: It is a tree of life to all who draw near to it, and to those who lean upon it, as upon the Lord of firmness. And with the branches of palms, in which the sign of victory and the reward of virtue are contained; and with the boughs of the thickest tree, which the Jews understand as the myrtle, because of the mortification of vices and lusts. Hence myrrh too is offered as a gift to the Lord and Savior by the magi. And with the branches, he says, of the willow and of the poplar, which some call one tree; and the very name of the wood, which in Greek is called ἀγνὸς, indicates chastity. Physicians, and those who have written on the natures of trees and herbs, say that if anyone drinks the flower of the willow, or of the poplar, mixed with water, all heat in him grows cold, and the vein of lust is dried up, and he can no longer beget children. Whoever has been sheltered by the branches of such trees, let him keep the solemnity of tabernacles, passing through the sixth month, which is referred to the world, and keeping in the seventh a spiritual sabbath, on the fifteenth day of that same month, when the moon of the night is at its fullest, and all its darkness is dispelled by clear light. These things we have said briefly, having long had in view the size of the books, so that we may pass on to what remains.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.17":["And it shall be that whoever of the families of the earth does not go up to Jerusalem to worship the King, the Lord of hosts, upon them there shall be no rain. LXX: And it shall be that whoever of the families of the earth do not go up to Jerusalem to worship the King, the Lord Almighty, these also shall be added to those. Where the Seventy translated, and these also shall be added to those, in the Hebrew is written ulo alehem eie gesem, which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion have likewise interpreted: And upon them there shall be no rain. The Church of the Lord Jesus is called the heavenly Jerusalem, of which the Apostle writes: But the Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all, and: You have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem. And this Jerusalem is not set in low places, but on a lofty mountain, of which the Savior speaks: A city set on a mountain cannot be hidden. Hence whoever is to worship the Lord of hosts in Jerusalem must climb up to the mountain heights. But whoever is of the families and tribes of the earth, and for that reason cannot worship the Lord, upon him there shall be no early and late rain, nor shall his land be watered by the rains of heaven. Or else, as the Seventy translated: Whoever of the families of the earth do not go up to Jerusalem to worship the King, the Lord Almighty, they shall be reckoned with those who fought against Jerusalem, and whose flesh shall waste away, and whose eyes shall melt away, and whose tongue shall rot.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.18":["And if the families of Egypt do not go up and do not come, neither shall it be upon them; but there shall be the ruin with which the Lord will strike all the nations that do not go up to celebrate the solemnity of tabernacles. This shall be the sin of Egypt, and this the sin of all the nations that do not go up to celebrate the festival of tabernacles. LXX: But if the tribe of Egypt does not go up, nor come there, upon them also shall be the ruin with which the Lord will strike all the nations that did not go up to keep the solemnity of the scenopegia. This shall be the sin of Egypt, and the sin of all the nations that do not go up to celebrate the solemnity of the scenopegia. Whoever is an Egyptian, or of the other nations, as long as he remains an Egyptian and a heathen, will not go up into Jerusalem; and because he cannot go up, nor lift his step to the heights, therefore upon him there shall be no rain of the Lord's blessing.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.19":["And this shall be the greatest sin for the Egyptian, the Assyrian, the Chaldean, the Syrian, the Moabite and the Ammonite, if they are unwilling to go out from their lands and go up to Jerusalem, so that through the tabernacles they may pass into Jerusalem and find an eternal house, and cease to be men of other nations, and become Israelites in whom there is no guile. All these things, which we touch on in brief speech, the Jews and our judaizers, or rather not ours, since they are judaizers, hope will come to pass bodily, promising themselves, of course, both circumcision and marriages in the reign of a thousand years, so that the curse which is written may not be fulfilled in them: Cursed is the barren woman who does not bear seed in Israel, and: Blessed is he who has seed in Zion and household members in Jerusalem. If this is true, all the virgins whom the kingdom of a thousand years finds will lie under the curse and perpetual barrenness, or else they will marry so as to escape the curse.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.20-21":["Let us pass on to the cauldrons, which are to be in the house of the Lord like the bowls of the altar. For every cauldron in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be sanctified to the Lord Almighty. Let them love bronze cauldrons who loved the pots of Egypt and the meat, and the melons and the garlic, and the onions and the cucumbers. Let us turn the Jewish cauldrons, in which the flesh of the victims was cooked, into bowls of spices before the altar of the Lord, of which the bride says to the bridegroom: My kinsman has gone down into my garden, to the bowls of spices, to feed in the gardens and to gather lilies. The garden and paradise into which the bridegroom goes down to the bride is the reading of the holy scriptures, from which he plucks lilies and violets and roses and various spices, to fill the bowls of believing souls and to pour out libations to the Lord from them. When cauldrons of this kind have been turned into bowls of the Lord and can say: We are the good fragrance of Christ, and, instead of the rankness of meats, have begun to bear the various flowers of the virtues, then they will be in Jerusalem and in Judah sanctified to the Lord Almighty; of which we have said quite often that Jerusalem expresses vision of peace, and Judah one who confesses.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.20":["On that day what is upon the bridle of the horse shall be holy to the Lord; and the cauldrons in the house of the Lord shall be like bowls before the altar. And every cauldron in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be sanctified to the Lord of hosts. LXX: On that day what is upon the bridle of the horse shall be holy to the Lord Almighty; and the cauldrons that are in the house of the Lord shall be like bowls before the house of the altar. And every cauldron in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be holy to the Lord Almighty. The Hebrew word mesuloth Aquila and Theodotion interpreted as βυθὸν, that is, depth; Symmachus as περίπατον σύσκιον, that is, a shady walk. The Seventy alone translated χάλινον, that is, bridle, and we too have followed them in this place, so as not to seem to bring something new into a well-known question. When I asked a Hebrew what it meant, he told me that we ought not to read mesuloth but mesaloth, which means the trappings of horses and warlike adornment, and that apart from this place the word is found in no volume whatever of the holy scriptures; that a bridle, moreover, is called resen in the Hebrew tongue, and not mesuloth, which the LXX translated; and that the sense is: At the time of the perpetual solemnity and of the kingdom of Jerusalem, when all things are pacified and tranquil, there will be no need at all of cavalry, which is the strongest kind of fighting force, but all the adornment and beauty of the trappings is to be devoted to the worship of the Lord. So they may say. Let us refer the depth of the horses and their σύσκιον walk, shady or dark, to mystical knowledge, which David too, like a most excellent horse, boasted that he had, saying: The uncertain and hidden things of your wisdom you have made manifest to me. And the Apostle: O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God. How inscrutable are his judgments, and untraceable his ways. From this depth the prophet cried to the Lord, and he heard him. In these depths and darkness God set his hiding place; into this darkness and these divine mysteries Moses entered in the thick cloud of Mount Sinai, that he might see God; of them David too spoke in another psalm: The judgments of the Lord are a great abyss. These secrets and these mysteries are holy to the Lord, which John the evangelist knew, who dared to say what perhaps the angels did not know: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. And for that reason he is loved by the Lord, because he possessed a most excellent depth; and he had reclined upon the breast of Jesus, whence he had also drawn wisdom. But if we wish to take the bridle, as the LXX translated, as the word of God, let us understand by the bridle him who reins in from vices horses raging with lust, and mules barren and wanton, and restrains them, and does not allow them to rush headlong; of whom it is said: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding. With bridle and bit you will bind fast the jaws of those who do not draw near to you. Of this bridle James also speaks: We put bridles into the mouths of horses, and we turn about their whole body, so, that is, that they may walk on a straight road and be able to offer the Lord backs gentle for sitting. Such a bridle and such a word, wrought with a variety of gold and silver, prepares wild horses for the Savior to sit upon, and makes them holy and properly consecrated to his worship. I have heard from someone a thing said with pious intent indeed, but ridiculous: that the nails of the Lord's cross, out of which Constantine Augustus made a bridle for his horse, are called the holy thing of the Lord. Whether this is to be taken so, I leave to the reader's prudence.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ZEC.14.21":["And all these shall come sacrificing, and taking from them, and they shall cook in them; and there shall no longer be a merchant in the house of the Lord of hosts on that day. LXX: And all who sacrifice shall come and shall take from them, and they shall cook in them; and there shall no longer be a Canaanite in the house of the Lord Almighty on that day. In place of Canaanite, Aquila translated merchant, and we too have followed him in this place. When the cauldrons have been turned into bowls, all the nations round about, or those who are left of all the nations, will come sacrificing, and will take the cauldrons and cook in them the flesh of the victims, so that they may not eat the flesh of the lamb raw, but, all the juice of the meat having been boiled away, what has been prepared by fire may remain for eating. What the bowls before the altar of the Lord are, into which the Israelite cauldrons are turned, we have both said above and shall now say in part. In the Song of Songs the bride praises her bridegroom: His cheeks are like bowls of spices. By the cheeks is understood speech, which, uttered by the Lord, brings forth various ointments; and so great will be the fragrance of its good odor that the Lord makes himself a scourge woven from the testimonies of the scriptures, and casts out of the temple those who sell and buy, and says to them: It is written, My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations; but you have made it a house of trade. Some fit to this place what is written in Daniel, although it is not read in the Hebrew: Seed of Canaan, and not of Judah, and what is said by the prophet Hosea of Ephraim: Ephraim is a partaker of idols; he has set stumbling blocks for himself, he has provoked the Canaanites; committing fornication, they have committed fornication; and they would have every fornicator called a Canaanite and a foreigner, who, they assert, must be removed from the house of God.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"MAL.1.1":["We wish to interpret Malachi, the last of the twelve prophets, whose name the LXX translated \"his angel\", saying: The taking up of the word of the Lord upon Israel in the hand of his angel, in place of which we read in the Hebrew Malachi, which is more correctly and expressly rendered \"angel\", that is, \"my messenger\". Nor is it to be thought, according to the opinion of some, that an angel came from heaven and took up a human body, in order to speak to Israel the things that had been commanded by the Lord. For if names are to be interpreted, and if from names not a spiritual understanding but the order of the history is to be woven, then both Hosea, who is called savior, and Joel, who is interpreted the Lord God, or beginning, and the rest of the prophets will not be men, but either angels or the Lord and Savior, because this is what their names sound. In short, apart from the Seventy, the others, interpreting the name Malachi, translated it as it is read in the Hebrew. Now the Hebrews consider Malachi to be Ezra the priest, because all the things that are contained in his book this prophet also recalls, saying: The lips of the priest shall guard knowledge, and they shall seek the law from his mouth, because he is the angel of the Lord of hosts. The time also and the title agree, as we have said in the psalms too, that those which have no titles are to be believed to belong to those by whose names the preceding psalms are headed. Therefore Malachi too, that is, Ezra, must be believed to have lived after Haggai and Zechariah, who prophesied under Darius. And for this reason he has no title, because his book stands in place of a title; in it we learn that in the reign of Artaxerxes king of the Persians, Ezra son of Seraiah, and of the others, up to the place where it is said: son of Phinehas, son of Eleazar, son of Aaron the priest from the beginning, went up from Babylon, and the king gave him, according to the hand of the Lord his God, all his request; and there went up with him some of the sons of Israel, and of the sons of the priests, and of the sons of the Levites, and of the singers, and of the doorkeepers, and of the Nethinim to Jerusalem, in the seventh year of King Artaxerxes, and they came to Jerusalem in the fifth month; this is the seventh year of the king, because on the first day of the first month he began to go up from Babylon, and on the first day of the fifth month he came to Jerusalem. These things, my Minervius and Alexander, you who are one not so much in blood as in religion, I have said briefly in the Preface, so as not to abandon the work on the prophets that I had undertaken and toil for you at an extraordinary labor, so that, setting out the words of Malachi first according to the Hebrew truth, then according to the LXX translators, and upheld by the help of your prayers, I may expound what follows. Origen wrote three volumes on this book, but he did not touch the history at all, and, after his custom, he was wholly occupied in the interpretation of allegory, making no mention of Ezra, but thinking that it was an angel who wrote, according to what we read of John: Behold, I send my angel before your face. This we do not accept at all, lest we be compelled to admit falls of souls from heaven. I am not aware of having read other commentaries on this prophet, except a short booklet of Apollinaris, of which one should speak not so much of an interpretation as of the bare points of an interpretation.\n— Commentary on Malachi","The burden of the word of the Lord to Israel, in the hand of Malachi. LXX: The taking up of the word of the Lord upon Israel, in the hand of his angel. What burden signifies, that is, weight, which in Hebrew is called massa and by Aquila ἄρμα, or what λῆμμα signifies, that is, taking up, which both the Seventy and the other translators rendered, we have said in other prophets. For Nahum too writes: The burden of Nineveh, the book of the vision of Nahum the Elkoshite, and Habakkuk: The burden which Habakkuk the prophet saw, and Zechariah: The burden of the word of the Lord in the land of Hadrach, and of Damascus his rest. And again in what follows: The burden of the word of the Lord upon Israel. Let us therefore be content with that explanation, and now say only this, that the weight of the word of the Lord to Israel, or, as the LXX say, upon Israel, is indeed heavy, because it is called a weight, but has something of consolation, because it is taken up not against Israel but to Israel. For it is one thing when, for example, we write to this man or that, another when we write against this man and that, because in the one there is a share of friendship, in the other an open avowal of enmities. And it must be known that once Israel, that is, the ten tribes, had been led away into captivity, the two tribes too, Judah and Benjamin, are called Israel indifferently by the former name. But as for what is said: In the hand of his angel, or of Malachi, take the hand for works. Hence the word of God came both in the hand of Haggai, and in the hand of Jeremiah, and in the hand of Moses. For in those in whose hands is iniquity, and whose right hand is filled with gifts, and whose hands are full of blood, the word of God does not come, but in those who wash their hands among the innocent. With these waters Pilate too tried to wash his hands, so as not to consent to the blasphemies of the Jews; over them the psalmist rejoices, saying: Upon the waters of refreshment he has brought me up. Of this water the Lord promises through the prophet: I will sprinkle you with most clean water. But he who is a sinner is made drunk with the Babylonian cup, and it is said of him: Thorns spring up in the hand of the drunkard.\n— Commentary on Malachi","LXX: Set it upon your hearts. This is not found in the Hebrew, but I think it was added from Haggai, in whom we read: And now set it upon your hearts from this day and upward. After the title of the prophet, then, or the preface, this is to be taken in two ways: Set it upon your hearts, that is, take note of and consider that which was said above: The taking up of the word of the Lord upon Israel in the hand of his angel. Or else: take careful note of the things that are to be said afterward, so that you may know them not with the ears of the body but with the understanding of the mind and heart, and may make for yourselves treasuries in which to receive the riches of the words of God, and wisdom may act with confidence when you have been enlarged, and, your heart filled with the words of God, you may drive out the most wicked thoughts, the murders, adulteries, fornications, thefts and the rest that come forth from the heart, and may fulfill what was said by the Savior: He who has ears to hear, let him hear.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.2-3":["And in both directions, before the little ones were born: \"God loved Jacob, but Esau he held in hatred\" (Mal. 1:1, 2). Here the heretics find an opening, who claim that there are diverse natures, namely the spiritual and the animal and the χοϊκὴν (earthy), and that one is saved, another perishes, and another stands between the two; for, they say, neither would the righteous man ever be chosen before he had done anything good, nor the sinner be hated before his sin, unless the nature of those who perish and of those who are to be saved were different. To this it can be answered simply that this comes about from the foreknowledge of God: that the one whom he knows will be righteous he loves before he comes forth from the womb, and the one whom he knows will be a sinner he hates before he sins; not that there is injustice in God, either in his love or in his hatred, but that he ought not to regard them otherwise, because he knows that they will be either sinners or righteous; we, as men, judge only of present things, whereas he, for whom future things are already done, passes sentence on the end of things, not on their beginnings. And these things indeed have been said rather simply, and without a loftier discussion they can in some measure satisfy the reader. But those who strive to assert that God is unjust, after the words we set down a little earlier, \"sinners are estranged from the womb\", also bring in the rest that follows: \"They have gone astray from the womb, they have spoken falsehoods\". And they say: how did sinners go astray straight from the womb and speak falsehoods, when they could have neither speech nor understanding? Or what is this righteousness of the foreknowledge of God, to love and guard one before he is born, and to detest another? And they refer the causes of this to a former life, holding that each one, according to his merit, is assigned from his very first birth to good or to evil angels. And that whole passage about Jacob and Esau, which we have just mentioned, as it is written to the Romans (Rom. 9), they toss about in such a way that they cannot be answered without sweat, and even the hellebore of Chrysippus.\n— Commentary on Galatians","I have loved you, says the Lord. And you have said: In what have you loved us? Was not Esau the brother of Jacob, says the Lord, and I have loved Jacob, but Esau I have hated? And I have made his mountains a wilderness, and his inheritance a place for the dragons of the desert. But if Idumea should say: We are destroyed, but returning we will build the things that are destroyed; thus says the Lord of hosts: They will build, and I will destroy; and they shall be called the borders of impiety, and the people with whom the Lord is angry for ever. And your eyes shall see, and you shall say: May the Lord be magnified upon the border of Israel. LXX: I have loved you, says the Lord, and you have said: In what have you loved us? Was not Esau the brother of Jacob, says the Lord; and I have loved Jacob, but Esau I have hated; and I have made his borders a desolation, and his inheritance the dwellings of the desert. For Idumea will say: It is destroyed; let us return and rebuild the deserted places. Thus says the Lord Almighty: They will build, and I will destroy, and they shall be called, for them, the borders of iniquity, and the people against whom the Lord has been prepared for ever. And your eyes shall see, and you shall say: The Lord has been magnified upon the borders of Israel. Israel, that is, Judah, to whom the word of God and the vision of the Lord used to come, is compelled to carry his burden and the heaviest weight of punishments, so that it may lay down its heavier sins and feel through torments what it did not feel through benefits. And lest the punishment of his own should seem unjust, the Lord adds: I have loved you. For whom the Lord loves he rebukes: and he chastises every son whom he receives. And by saying I have loved, he denies the present while he confesses the past. And they, forgetful of his benefits, answer with the same rashness with which they sin: In what have you loved us? To which the Lord says: To pass over everything else, and the fact that you have lately come from the Babylonian captivity, I will deal with your cradles: before you were born, nay rather before Rebekah poured forth Esau and Jacob from her womb, in Jacob I loved you, in Esau I hated the Idumeans. The apostle Paul, sifting this passage in a mystical disputation, writes to the Romans, joining together two testimonies, from Genesis and from Malachi: But Rebekah too, having conceived by one act of intercourse with Isaac our father. For when they were not yet born, nor had done anything good or evil, so that the purpose of God according to election might stand, not from works but from him who calls, it was said to her: The elder shall serve the younger; as it is written: Jacob I have loved, but Esau I have hated. For what is said: As it is written, refers both to the book of Genesis and to the prophet Malachi. Not only, he says, did I love Jacob before he was born, and hate Esau before he was poured forth from his mother's womb, but I have kept my love and my hatred toward their posterity: my hatred toward Esau, whose mountains, which are called Seir, I have reduced to a wilderness, and whose cities I have made to be deserted and to be held by serpents and beasts.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.2-5":["Now let us come to the spiritual understanding. Israel, a man or a mind seeing God, or, as I think better, εὐθύτατος Θεοῦ, that is, most upright of God, is loved by the Lord, and wishes to know the reason for his love toward him. And the Lord answered that Esau and Jacob were begotten of one stock, that is, that vices and virtues proceed from the one fountain of the heart, since by the freedom of choice we incline to either side as we will; but the vices are born first, through infancy, childhood and youth, and afterward a firmer age reproves and supplants them. The elder brother is hairy and bloody with his hunting, and delights in woods and beasts. The younger is smooth and simple, and dwells innocently at home. God makes the borders of Idumea, that is, of the earthly and bloody, a wilderness, and allows nothing to grow from the earth and to endure. But if shameless malice should strive to build again the things that have been destroyed by the word of God, the Lord declares himself the adversary of the things that are restored by the vices. And after everything within the hostile borders has been overthrown, then we can see the eyes of Israel, and all the saints can say: May the Lord be magnified in the borders of those who behold God with the mind. Moreover, the love and the hatred of God arise either from foreknowledge of what is to come, or from works; otherwise we know that God loves all things, and hates nothing of what he has created, but properly claims for his love those who are enemies of the vices and rebels against them. And on the contrary he hates those who long to build up again what has been destroyed by God. Now God is said to hate ἀνθρωποπάθως (in human fashion), as he is said to weep, to grieve, to be angry, so that when we hear of his hatred toward the wicked, we may flee the things that we understand God to hate.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.4-5":["The Jews falsely flatter themselves that the Romans are prophesied as Edom, and they themselves as Israel, at the consummation of the world, on the ground that once the Roman empire, that is, the Idumean, has been destroyed, the kingdom of the world will come to the Jews. These things we have said as well as we could, laying the foundations of the history.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.4":["But if Edom, that is, Esau, should say: we have indeed been destroyed, unto the wrath of God, but we will build the cities again, know this from the Lord's foretelling, that as they build, I will destroy, and their everlasting desolation will prove my wrath.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.5":["So I have shown my hatred toward Esau in deeds, but my love toward you, that is, toward Jacob, I will prove by what follows. When they have been destroyed and reduced to a wilderness, your eyes will see, and you will say: May the Lord be magnified upon the border of Israel; and from the comparison with the evils your brother suffers, you will feel the benefits of God toward you.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.6-7":["To you, O priests, who despise my name, and you have said: In what have we despised your name?\n\nYou offer polluted bread upon my altar, and you say: In what have we polluted you? In that you say: The table of the Lord is despised. LXX: You, O priests, who despise my name, and have said: In what have we despised your name? Offering polluted loaves at my altar; and you have said: In what have we polluted them? In that you say: The table of the Lord is despised. But what follows: And the things set upon it you have despised, we have marked with an obelus, because it is not found in the Hebrew, and was added from what follows. To you, then, O priests, who despise my name, is this word directed, you who, having returned from Babylon, ought, from fear of your past servitude, to have turned to the Lord with your whole mind; and not only do you not do this, but, imitating Cain and answering God with proud words, you question him whom hidden things do not escape, and you say: In what have we despised your name? so that by the shamelessness of your pretence you may cover the wound of your conscience. Do you wish, then, to know in what you have despised my name? You offer polluted bread upon my altar, namely the showbread, which, according to the Hebrew traditions, you yourselves were bound to sow, yourselves to reap, yourselves to grind, yourselves to bake; and now you take any loaves at all from the common stock, and answer with rash voice and say: In what have we polluted them, or you? For when the mysteries are violated, he himself whose mysteries they are is violated. But what follows: In that you say: The table of the Lord is despised, we can also interpret thus: that having returned from Babylon, with the temple not yet built, and living in huts and in the ruins of the ancient city, they had built only an altar, not of the same glory as the former one had been, and they thought that the sanctity of religion was lacking because the grandeur of the building was lacking.\n— Commentary on Malachi","We have drawn faint lines, on which the spiritual explanation is to be impressed. The divine word rebukes negligent bishops, and presbyters and deacons, or else, since we are a priestly and royal race, all who, baptized in Christ, are reckoned by the name of Christ, as to why they despise the name of God; and when they ask in what they have despised his name, it shows them the causes of the offence. You offer, he says, polluted bread upon my altar. We pollute the bread, that is, the body of Christ, when we approach the altar unworthy and, filthy as we are, drink the clean blood, and we say: The table of the Lord is despised; not that anyone would dare to say this, and to utter with criminal voice what he impiously thinks, but the works of sinners despise the table of God. We can also put it another way: a teacher of the Church who makes the spiritual bread and distributes it to the peoples, if, either for human glory or for the worldly gains that follow on glory, he speaks among the peoples and flatters the rich, and honors sinners, and, according to James, looks up to those who come to him with gold rings, and repels the poor saints, despises the name of God, and pollutes the bread of doctrines, and hurls insults at God himself, thinking the table of his Scriptures to be common with the tables of idols and of worldly doctrine.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.6":["A son honors his father, and a servant his lord; if then I am a father, where is my honor? And if I am a lord, where is my fear? says the Lord of hosts. LXX: A son glorifies his father, and a servant will fear his lord. And if I am a father, where is my glory? And if I am a lord, where is my fear? says the Lord Almighty. Although before you were born I began to love you as sons in Jacob, still, choose by which name you will call me: either I am your father, or your lord. If a father, render the honor due to a father, and offer a devotion worthy of a parent. If a lord, why do you despise me? Why do you not fear your lord? Now he is speaking to those who returned from the Babylonian captivity under Zerubbabel and Joshua son of Jehozadak, and Ezra the priest, and Nehemiah, and who, though the altar had been built, had not yet built the temple nor raised the walls of the city, and nonetheless remained in their former sins, venerating God neither with love nor with fear. Now what we have called glory, or honor, is one word both among the Greeks, δόξα, and among the Hebrews, chabod, but we have put honor in keeping with the proper idiom of the Latin language. For in the Gospel too, where the Lord and Savior says: Father, the hour has come; make your Son illustrious, that your Son may make you illustrious, among the Greeks δόξασον is read, that is, glorify, a passage which most of the Latins have translated honor. And at the same time let us consider that in the holy Scriptures one becomes a son or a servant by will, not by necessity of nature. For whoever has received the spirit of adoption is turned into a son of God; but whoever has received the spirit of servitude unto fear is made a servant of God. So God wishes first that we be his sons, and do good by our will; if we wish to attain this, that he may at least have us as servants, and that we may withdraw from evil through dread of punishments. We read of sons in a bad sense: We were sons of wrath, and sons of gehenna, whom the Pharisees, having gone round sea and land, begot worthy of torments. And Judas the traitor is called the son of perdition, and in Psalm 88 (89) it is written of the Lord: The son of iniquity shall not go on to harm him. In Hosea too those are called sons of fornication who were begotten of a harlot mother, of whom it is written: Your mother has committed fornication. And in the Gospel the Jews are branded as sons of the devil: You are born of your father the devil, and you wish to do the desires of your father. We read of sons in a good sense, as sons of God: As many as received him, he gave them power to become sons of God. And sons of wisdom, as the Gospel proclaims: Wisdom has been justified by her sons. And sons of Abraham: Amen, amen, I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up sons of Abraham. And sons of the Apostle: My little sons, with whom I am in labor again until Christ be formed in you; and many things of this kind. A son therefore honors, or makes illustrious, his father, according to what is written: So let your light shine before men, that they may see your works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven. A servant too honors his lord, not with the same love as a son, but it is understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ (from the shared word): a son honors his father, a servant his lord; and yet God Almighty, knowing the difference between a son and a servant, seeks glory from the son and fear from the servant: For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord, so that from the fear of servants we may pass over to the glory of sons.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.8":["If you offer the blind for immolation, is it not evil? And if you offer the lame and the sick, is it not evil? Offer it to your governor, whether it will please him, or whether he will accept your face, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: For if you offer the blind for a sacrifice, is it not evil? And if you offer the lame and the sick, is it not evil? Offer it to your governor, whether he will receive you, whether he will accept your face, says the Lord Almighty. About the diversity of victims, and which ought to be offered and which not, we learn more fully in Leviticus. So the people that had returned from Babylon, and the priests, the Levites, the doorkeepers and singers, and the Nethinim and Solomon's servants, of whom Ezra writes a κατάλογον (catalogue), were offering to God victims that were unlawful, namely blind and lame ones, and ones worn out by various kinds of debility; for this is what he means by sick, taking in everything with one word. If, he says, you were to offer gifts of this kind to your governor, would he not spurn them? Would he not think an injury had been done to him? And do you dare to offer to God what you do not dare to give to men? It would take long now to open the mysteries of all the victims; I shall speak only of those contained in the present passage. Blind is the victim of the soul that is not illumined by the light of Christ and has no eye that looks from the Gospel. Lame is the prayer of one who asks, which comes to pray with a double mind, and hears with the people of the Jews? How long will you limp on both feet? And sick, and covered with every infirmity, is the one that does not have Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God. Prayers of this kind, which are without the light of truth, and do not have the firm footsteps of wisdom, and waste away with various debilities, if they are offered to the leader of churches, or to any learned and wise teacher, will they not be rejected, and fall back as an insult upon him who has dared to offer such things?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.9-10":["And now entreat the face of God that he may have mercy on you, for by your hand this has been done, if in any way he may accept your faces, says the Lord of hosts. Who is there among you that will shut the doors and kindle my altar gratuitously? LXX: And now entreat the face of your God, and ask him; by your hands these things have been done, if I shall accept your faces from you, says the Lord Almighty, because among you too the doors shall be shut, and my altar shall not be kindled for nothing. In this place the Seventy translators differ widely from the Hebrew truth; and it must be that, where the translation is different, the sense too is different. Since you have offered lame and blind and feeble victims, for you have done all these things that I have spoken of, do penance, if in any way God may have mercy on you. For there is no one among you, down to the lowest ministry, I do not say high priest, not priest, not Levite, not singer, but not even the doorkeeper, and he who puts the fire under the altar to burn the whole burnt offerings, who does not receive from me the wage of his labor. Now he says this signifying the tithes of all the produce which are offered by the peoples. From this it is shown that the service which does not demand a wage in the present is more acceptable to the Lord. Hence the Apostle too preaches the gospel freely, and labors night and day with his own hands so as not to be a burden to anyone, and testifies that this glory of his, of gratuitous preaching among the nations, must by no means be made void.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.9":["Moreover, the LXX suggest a far different sense: O priests, who sacrifice feeble sacrificial victims, turn to repentance, and entreat the face of the Lord, and pray for the works of your hands. But as for what he adds: If I shall accept your faces from you, I do not know whether it fits an exhortation to repentance. For no one says: Pray to me, and I will not spare you.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.10":["There follows: Among you the doors shall be shut, namely those of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. Hence the Savior says: I am the door. Or otherwise: The doors of the Scriptures will not be opened to you, nor will you be able to look into the holy of holies, nor to know the mysteries of the Lord, nor to kindle his incense at the altar, because your prayers will not reach him. And this word which is said δωρεὰν, and which we have translated gratuitously, those who follow the understanding we are now setting out turn into grace, namely that they do not have the grace of serving the altar of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.11-12":["Some think that this, which is said specifically to the Jews, because their victims are unclean and polluted and the sacrifice is transferred to the nations, ought to be understood of the priests of the church, who offer victims to the Lord negligently. But if we accept this, then the victims must again be transferred from the Church to another religion. And as the gospel succeeded the law, so in turn things that will never be will succeed the gospel. They also refer the defiled table of the Lord to the holy Scriptures, if they are understood otherwise than as they were written.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.11":["I have no pleasure in you, says the Lord of hosts, and I will not accept a gift from your hand.\n\nFor from the rising of the sun even to its setting my name is great among the nations, and in every place sacrifice is made, and a clean oblation is offered to my name, because my name is great among the nations, says the Lord of hosts. And you have polluted it, in that you say: The table of the Lord is defiled, and what is placed upon it is contemptible, with the fire that devours it. And you have said: Behold, from toil, and you have blown upon it, says the Lord of hosts. And you have brought in from plunder the lame and the sick, and you have brought in a gift. Shall I accept it from your hand? says the Lord. LXX: My will is not in you, says the Lord Almighty, and I will not accept sacrifice from your hands, because from the rising of the sun even to its setting my name is glorious among the nations; and in every place incense is offered to my name, and a clean sacrifice, because my name is great among the nations, says the Lord Almighty. But you have defiled it, in that you say, the table of the Lord is polluted, and its foods that are placed upon it are despised, and you have said, behold, they are from affliction, and you have blown upon them, says the Lord Almighty. And you brought in plunder, and the lame and the feeble, and you offered sacrifice: shall I accept them from your hands, says the Lord Almighty? It is a rule of the Scriptures: where a most manifest prophecy of things to come is being woven, not to weaken what is written through the uncertainties of allegory. Therefore the word of the Lord is now addressed in its proper sense to the priests of the Jews, who offer the blind and the lame and the sick for sacrifice, so that they may know that spiritual victims will succeed carnal victims; and that it is by no means the blood of bulls and goats, but incense, that is, the prayers of the saints, that is to be offered to the Lord; and that not in one province of the world, Judea, nor in one city of Judea, Jerusalem, but in every place an oblation is offered, by no means unclean, as by the people of Israel, but clean, as in the ceremonies of the Christians. For from the rising of the sun even to its setting the name of the Lord is great among the nations, as the Savior says: Father, I have manifested your name to men.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.12":["And although my name, he says, is going to be great among the nations, you, O leaders of the Jews, have polluted it and are polluting it. For he so weaves the prophecy of things to come as not to abandon the present time. And it is for this reason, O priests and leaders of the Jews, that in every place a clean oblation is offered to me, and my name is great among the nations: because you say the table of the Lord is defiled; and what is placed upon it is contemptible, with the fire that devours it. The people, having returned from Babylon, built, according to the book of Ezra, only an altar of chance and unhewn stones, without a temple, without the buildings of the city, without the building of the walls; and they thought the worship of religion was the less, because the adornment of the temple was lacking. To them the Lord says: You think the altar polluted, and the whole burnt offerings and victims that are placed upon it; and that the fire too which devours the victims is polluted. Nor do you understand that almighty God seeks not gold and gems and a multitude of sacrificial victims, but the will of those who offer. But as for those who judge that not the altar but the table on which the loaves were placed ought to be understood, how they can interpret what follows, with the fire that devours it, I do not see at all; for fire does not devour the showbread, which they always exchanged, new for old, and which, when taken away, went to the use of the priests. Or at any rate it is to be understood thus: You have polluted my name in that you say: What does it profit if we offer the best? Whatever the things offered may be, they are to be devoured by fire. Now the fruit of the altar is fire, and the food of the fire is the sacrificial victims or the whole burnt offerings.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.13":["Nor is the impiety of your former speech enough, but you have also said this: Behold, from toil, and you have blown upon it, says the Lord of hosts. The sense of this speech is this: You have said: We have returned from captivity, we have been a prey to enemies, we have toiled much on a long journey, we are poor, whatever we were able to have has been consumed by the toil of the road, we offer such things as we have; and by saying these things you have blown upon your sacrifices, that is, you have made them worthy of my blowing upon them. Or, as can be read in the Hebrew: And you have blown upon me by saying these things: you have done injury not to the sacrifice but to me, to whom you were sacrificing. Wherefore I will by no means accept it from your hand, says the Lord Almighty.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.1.14":["Cursed is the deceitful man who has a male in his flock, and making a vow sacrifices a feeble one to the Lord. For I am a great king, says the Lord of hosts, and my name is dreadful among the nations. And now this commandment is for you, O priests: If you will not hear, and if you will not set it upon the heart, to give glory to my name, says the Lord of hosts, I will send want upon you, and I will curse your blessings, and I will curse them, since you have not set it upon the heart. LXX: And cursed is he who was powerful, and had a male in his flock, and his vow was upon it, and sacrifices a feeble one to the Lord, because I am a great king, says the Lord Almighty, and my name is illustrious among the nations. And now this commandment is for you, O priests: If you do not hear, and if you do not set it in your heart, to give glory to my name, says the Lord Almighty, I will send a curse upon you, and I will curse your blessing, and I will curse it, and I will scatter your blessing, and it shall not be in you, because you do not set it in your heart. What is written: And I will scatter your blessing, was added by the LXX, and is not found in the Hebrew. Whatever the human mind can find in excuse of its sin, to defend itself with a false satisfaction, the divine word foresees, and rebukes and condemns. And the sense is: Because you offer the blind and the lame and the sick, and this very thing not from what is yours, but from plunder and spoils and the tears of the wretched, and moreover you say, from our toil and poverty we offer what we have, therefore I speak in general terms, so that your own conscience may convict you. Certainly you plead poverty and the injury of captivity and the slenderness of your household means; hear what I add: Cursed is the deceitful man who has a male in his flock, and making a vow sacrifices a feeble one to the Lord. If you do not have a male, the curse does you no harm. Now by saying these things he shows that they have what is best, and offer what is bad. To you, therefore, O priests, this commandment is given: you have done what is impious, in contempt of me. But because I prefer the repentance of the sinner to his death, even now I say: If you will not hear me, nor understand, so as to give glory to my name, which is dreadful among the nations, I will send real penury upon you, so that, by no means lying, but forced by want of all things, you may say that you do not have the best things to offer. And I will curse, he says, your blessings, that is, the things which you now possess by my blessings; or else, whatever is blessed by you will be cursed by me. And I will curse them: your blessings is understood, since you would not understand what is said; for this is what is said: You have not set it upon the heart.\n— Commentary on Malachi","We can take the cursed and deceitful one to be the people of the Jews, who, though they had a male in their flock, the Lord Savior, the spotless lamb who takes away the sins of the world, and though admonished by the prophecy of all the prophets to sacrifice and receive the ram that was caught by its horns in the thicket Sabec, would not do this, but sacrifices a feeble one to the Lord, crucifying the Savior and choosing Barabbas, the author of robbery and sedition, who according to the mystical senses is referred to the devil; and while they despise him and prefer the devil to the Savior, the name of Christ is dreadful among the nations, which have received the passion of the Lord and venerate him with awe-struck honor. It can also be said of us: if, created wise by God and having a nature suited to sacred disciplines, we neglect our talent and give ourselves up to vices and luxury, and, having a male, sacrifice a feeble one to the Lord. Now our prayer is feeble and blemished when it is corrupted by anger, envy, enmities, and other disturbance of the mind; if, offering a gift at the altar and remembering that our brother has something against us, we do not first go and make satisfaction to him. Hence the Apostle too commands: I will therefore that you pray in every place, lifting up holy hands without anger and disputing. And the shepherd of our thoughts and virtues is the understanding, of which it is said in Psalm 102 (103): Bless the Lord, my soul, and all that is within me, his holy name. If it rules its flock well, that blessing is fulfilled in it: Blessed the herds of your oxen, and the flocks of your sheep. But if, having by nature a male, that is, what is strong, firm and robust, it sacrifices what is light, or through excessive softness effeminate and wanton, or feeble and corrupted by various disturbances of the soul, it will feel fulfilled in itself what is written: The mighty shall mightily suffer torments, and: To whom they have given more, of him they will demand more.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.1-2":["Hence it is said in its proper sense to the priests that, if they will not hear and keep it in their heart, so as to give glory to the name of the Lord by good conduct, but on the contrary his name is cursed among the nations because of them, he will send upon them want of all good things, and turn their blessings into a curse. Those who abuse health for lust, and turn riches into luxury, and disfigure a good reputation by sordid conduct, these change the blessings of God into a curse. Or else, since the commandment is given in its proper sense to the priests, their blessings are turned into a curse when they do not bless the saints from a true affection of the heart, as Isaac blessed Jacob, and Jacob the patriarchs, and Moses the twelve tribes, but through sweet words and blessings deceive the hearts of the innocent; and those who act wickedly are blessed by them, and they flatter sinners, provided they are rich, and fawn upon their vices; of whom it is said: My people, those who call you blessed lead you astray, and supplant the paths of your feet.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.3-4":["And it will take you, he says, with it, that is, the dung of your solemnities, so that your stinking faces may be befouled by the putrid admixture of dung; and so that, from the fact that I cast you away, you may understand that I am the very one who once chose your father Levi, and through Levi Aaron, and granted you the glory of the priesthood, that my covenant or testament might be with Levi, and he might minister to me in an eternal priesthood. It is to be noted that the Hebrew word brith Aquila translates συνθήκην, that is, covenant; the LXX always διαθήκην, that is, testament; and that in very many places of the Scriptures testament means not the will of the dead, but the covenant of the living.\n— Commentary on Malachi","And because, for what we have translated: The dung will take you with it, the Seventy translated: And I will take you up together, an interpretation is supplied: that after the priests have been covered with shame and disgrace, and have understood their sin, and have done penance, they are then taken up by the Lord; and that what follows fits this sense: And you shall know that I have sent this commandment to you. But the former interpretation seems to me the true one, lest among words of threatening he should have placed the words of one who coaxes, especially since what follows also speaks of the indignation of God.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.3":["Behold, I will cast the arm at you, and I will scatter upon your face the dung of your solemnities, and it will take you with it. And you shall know that I have sent to you your commandment, that my covenant might be with Levi, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: Behold, I will separate the shoulder from you, and I will scatter the stomach upon your face, the stomach of your solemnities, and I will receive you together; and you shall know that I have sent to you this commandment, that my testament might be with the Levites, says the Lord Almighty. Since he is speaking to the priests: I will cast, he says, into your faces the things you think holier in the law, and granted to you by God from the sacrificial victims as a reward for virtues. The arm, for which the LXX translated shoulder, that is, the right shoulder of the animal. And I will scatter, he says, ἔνυστρον, that is, the stomach, or according to the Hebrews, dung, that is, phares, μετωνυμικῶς (by metonymy), naming that which is contained in place of that which contains it; for the shoulder and the breast and the tongue and the stomach, and the things described in Leviticus, the priests received from the sacrificial victims. All these, he declares, he casts away for their sins and throws into the faces of the priests, so that, namely, those who offer may be such as the things that are offered.\n— Commentary on Malachi","Now God willed all men indeed, but especially priests, to have no blemish, and to adorn either the shoulder or the arm with good works. In the breast a good conscience is signified; in the tongue holy confession, so that what we believe with the heart unto justice we may confess with the mouth unto salvation. In the stomach too, to have nothing deadly, but what sustains and quickens our life; for unless food is digested in the stomach, and the body is watered by its juice, the body wastes away, lacks strength, and is borne toward death. But we turn everything to the contrary, so that the Lord casts the arm at us, or separates our works from the works of the saints; and we, who were commanded to anoint our head, and when fasting to wash our face from the filth of sins, and with unveiled face to contemplate the glory of the Lord, now say: The confusion of my face has covered me; and like Cain our face has fallen, nor can we fulfil what was commanded: Lift up your eyes and see, but we have faces smeared and polluted with the dung of vices, and we say: My scars have putrefied and are corrupted, from the face of my folly. Hence all these things have been turned into dung, as Scripture says: And the dung will take you with it, namely the dung with which our faces are smeared, we who ought to have come full and clean to the solemnities of God, and either to eat the flesh of the lamb with our shoes on, or, when seven weeks were completed, to offer God the fruits of our works, or to live as passers-through in the tabernacles of this world, and to say: I am a stranger and a sojourner, as all my fathers were.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.5":["My covenant with him was of life and peace, and I gave him fear, and he feared me, and he trembled before the face of my name. The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found on his lips; in peace and in equity he walked with me, and he turned many away from iniquity. For the lips of the priest shall keep knowledge, and they shall seek the law from his mouth, because he is the angel of the Lord of hosts. LXX: My testament with him was of life and peace, and I gave him fear, that he might fear me and be afraid before the face of my name. The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found on his lips. In peace, directing, he walked with me, and he converted many from iniquity. For the lips of the priest shall keep knowledge, and the law shall be sought from his mouth, because he is the angel of the Lord Almighty. The office of the perfect priest is described in the person of God: what he ought to be, and what he wished him to be who commanded that he should be. For above he had said: That my covenant might be with Levi, says the Lord of hosts, and through the patriarch Levi it came down to his posterity, Aaron and Eleazar, Phinehas and the rest who were born of his stock. Now at the end he says: You have made void the covenant of Levi, says the Lord of hosts. From this it is clear that everything said through Levi pertains to the priests, and especially to the high priest. We read in Numbers of Phinehas, who struck Zimri with the Midianite harlot with a dagger: Phinehas son of Eleazar, son of Aaron the priest, has made my fury rest from the sons of Israel, because he was jealous with my zeal against them; and I did not destroy the sons of Israel in my zeal, saying thus: Behold, I will give him a testament of peace, and it shall be to him and to his seed after him a testament of an everlasting priesthood; because he was zealous for his God, and made entreaty for the sons of Israel. Now let us not think that the testament, or covenant, of life is of this life which we share with beasts and all living things, but of that life which says: I am the life. For indeed our life is hidden with Christ in God, of whom we can say that he himself is also our peace, as the Apostle confirms: For he himself is our peace. God therefore granted to Levi, and through him to his posterity, that they should fear him: For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord; and that they should be afraid before his face, or draw back and shrink together, showing the dread of the mind by the shuddering of the body, according to what is written: Upon whom shall I rest, but upon the humble and quiet and him who trembles at my words?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.6":["The law of truth was in his mouth, that is, the teaching of the apostles, which in a priest ought to be disfigured by no poisoning, but should proceed wholly from the fountain of truth. And iniquity was not found on his lips, so that he may imitate his Lord, of whom it is said: Who did no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth. In peace and in equity he walked with me, so that he may both have peace in himself and make peace for others, and accept no one's person in judgement, and therefore walk with God, as Enoch walked, who was translated to the Lord and was not found: And he turned many away from iniquity. He who is a priest, and does not rebuke those who offend, passes over the office of a priest.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.7":["The lips of the priest shall keep knowledge; he did not say, shall bring forth, but, shall keep, so that they may speak in season, and give their fellow servants their food in due season. And they shall seek the law from his mouth. In Haggai the same is written: Ask the priests the law of the Lord. It belongs to the discipline of a priest, when asked, to answer concerning the law. If, diligent in other matters, he pleads an ignorance that is negligent in the holy Scriptures, he boasts in vain of a dignity whose works he does not display. This is what the apostle Paul writes to Titus: That he may be able to exhort in sound doctrine, and to refute those who contradict. And to Timothy: Since from infancy you have known the sacred letters, which can instruct you unto salvation, that you may rebuke sinners before all. There follows: Because he is the angel of the Lord of hosts. Ezra, the priest of God, sets forth his own name, that is, Malachi, which is interpreted angel of the Lord; and the priest of God is most truly called an angel, that is, a messenger, because he is the mediator between God and men and announces his will to the peoples; and for this reason the rational is on the breast of the priest, and on the rational doctrine and truth are placed, so that we may learn that a priest ought to be learned, and a herald of the Lord's truth. Some falsely think that what is said to Levi, and through Levi to the priests, is to be understood of Christ, not observing that what follows is contrary to the person of Christ.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.8":["But you have departed from the way, and have made very many stumble in the law; you have made void the covenant of Levi, says the Lord of hosts. Wherefore I too have made you contemptible and lowly to all peoples, as you have not kept my ways, and have accepted the face in the law. LXX: But you have turned aside from my way, and have made many weak in the law; you have corrupted the testament of Levi, says the Lord Almighty. And I have made you contemptible and dissolute among all the nations, because you have not guarded my ways, but accepted persons in the law. That these things do not pertain to the person of Christ, nor to those who are dedicated to his worship, the prudent reader understands even if I am silent. And the sense is: I wished you to do what is contained in the previous passage, and what I spoke of through Moses in Deuteronomy: Give to Levi his doctrine, and his truth to the just man, and the rest; but you have departed from the right way, or turned aside, though I said: Turn aside neither to the right nor to the left; and you have made many weak in the law, or made them stumble, as Aquila and Symmachus translated. To turn aside to the right is to abstain from foods which God created to be used, to condemn marriage, and to run into what is written in another place: Be not overmuch just. To turn off to the left is when someone gives himself over to luxury and lust, and makes many stumble in the law, someone for whom it is better that an ass's millstone be tied about his neck and he be cast headlong into the sea, than that he should make one of the least stumble. Now those make many weak who, when believers in Christ have received the strength of faith, make them grow weak by the negligence of their own conduct; to them it is most rightly said: You have made void the covenant of Levi, the covenant of life and peace, and the other things which we said pertain to the office of the priest.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.9":["For which reason I too, he says, have made you contemptible and lowly to all peoples, so that they might despise you, and instead of honor and glory trample on you as cast down and lowly: As you have not kept my ways, and have accepted the face in the law. Among all the sins of Levi, or of those who are from Levi, the priests of God, this one is set down as both the last and the greatest: that they accept the face in the law, so that they consider not causes but persons, and, despising the just poor man, receive and honor the unjust rich. To them it is said in Psalm 81 (82): How long do you judge iniquity, and take up the faces of sinners? And Paul to the Galatians: God, he says, does not accept the person of man. And the apostle James rebukes and condemns this sin more fully. But whatever is said to the former people, let us think said to us also, so that we may more cautiously withdraw from vices and not accept the face in the law, nor, we who are worshippers of God, prefer falsehood to truth.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.10":["Is not one Father of us all? Has not one God created us? Why then does each one of us despise his brother, violating the covenant of our fathers? Judah has transgressed, and abomination has been done in Israel and in Jerusalem, because Judah has defiled the sanctification of the Lord, which he loved, and has had the daughter of a strange god. May the Lord destroy the man who shall have done this, the master and the disciple, out of the tabernacles of Jacob, and him who offers a gift to the Lord of hosts. LXX: Is not one Father of us all? Has not one God created us? Why has each one forsaken his brother, so as to make abominable the testament of your fathers? Judah has been forsaken, and abomination has been done in Israel and in Jerusalem, because Judah has defiled the holy things of the Lord, in which he loved, and has devised strange gods. May the Lord destroy the man who does these things, until he is driven out of the tabernacles of Jacob, and from among those who offer sacrifice to the Lord Almighty. Before we discuss the present passage, the tradition of the Hebrews must be set down, or rather the truth of Scripture must be unfolded. We read in the volume that bears the title of Ezra, in the person of Ezra himself speaking: The princes came to me, saying: The people of Israel, and the priests and the Levites, have not separated themselves from the peoples of the lands and from their abominations, namely of the Canaanites, and the Hittites and the Perizzites, and the Jebusites, and of the Ammonites, and of the Moabites, and of the Egyptians, and of the Amorites. For they have taken of their daughters for themselves and for their sons, and have mingled the holy seed with the peoples of the lands. The hand also of the princes and magistrates has been first in this transgression. And when I had heard this word, I tore my cloak and my tunic, and plucked out the hair of my head and of my beard, and sat down mourning. And again in what follows: And there were found among the sons of the priests those who had married foreign wives: of the sons of Joshua, the son of Jehozadak, and his brothers: Maaseiah, and Eleazar, and Jarib, and Gedaliah; and they gave their hands that they would put away their wives, and offer a ram of the flock for their offence. At the end of the passage also, after the enumeration of those who had taken foreign wives, Scripture records: All these had taken foreign wives, and there were among them women who had borne sons. Having returned, then, from the Babylonian captivity, the princes and the priests and Levites, as well as the rest of the people, cast off their wives of the Israelite race, who, through poverty and the hardship of the long journey, and through the frailty of their sex unable to bear the toil, had been broken down, and had contracted infirmity and unsightliness of body; and they had joined themselves in marriage to foreign women, either in the flower of their age, or more beautiful in the adornment of their bodies, or the daughters of the powerful and rich. So Ezra the prophet rebukes them and calls them to the repudiation of their new spouses, that they may take back the wives whom they had let go: Is not, he says, one father Abraham of us all? Of whom it is written in Isaiah: Look to Abraham your father, and to Sarah, who bore you, for he was one, and I called him. Has not one God created us, who chose our race out of Abraham? Why then do we despise our old wives, and cast off the daughters of our brothers, so as to forsake the covenant of our fathers and not take wives according to the law?\n— Commentary on Malachi","Some, not understanding this place, nor knowing the truth of the history, understand what he says, Is not one father of us all?, of Abraham in such a way that they recall that Abraham is the father of the nations too, according to what is written: Is God able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham? Or at any rate they affirm one God the Father, according to what we read in Deuteronomy: Is not this the very Father who possessed you, and made you, and created you? And again: You have forsaken the God who created you. And elsewhere: I have begotten sons and exalted them: but they have forsaken me. And in the volume of the psalms: Strange sons have lied to me, strange sons have grown old and have gone lame from their paths. And those who sinned, departing from this one father, made for themselves many fathers of their vices: For everyone who commits sin is born of the devil. And what follows, Why does each one of us despise his brother, violating the covenant of our fathers, they have interpreted so as to say: that we all, begotten of one father, ought to be one and to have one lip of confession, but that afterwards, through pride and the building of the tower against God, we were divided into many tongues and opinions. They refer it also to almsgiving, so as to say: that we despise our brothers when we do not share with them what we have received from God for the sustaining of life. And they go on to this, that they assert that according to the Scriptures the Lord is called our brother, he who commanded Mary Magdalene to announce to his brothers that the Lord had risen; and he speaks in the psalm: I will declare your name to my brothers, in the midst of the Church I will sing to you. So the Jews deserted their brother, and defiled the covenant of their fathers, which God had made with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, that in their seed, which is interpreted as Christ, all the nations should be blessed. These things they conjecture in various ways, because they do not find a sure way.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.11-12":["In short, what follows according to the LXX, Judah has been forsaken, and abomination has been done in Israel, and in Jerusalem, they expound thus: that the Jews have been forsaken because they blasphemed the Lord Savior, and have suffered what is written: According to the multitude of their impieties drive them out, for they have provoked you, O Lord, so that they were scattered as wanderers and fugitives through all the provinces. For they defiled the holy things of the Lord, loving, in place of the Son of God, and devising for themselves, strange gods: For he who does not receive the Son does not receive the Father, who sent him. And therefore, because they did this, they perish for so long, until they are humbled, either by the hardship of captivity or through the consciousness of their sins; and they are cast out of the tabernacles of Jacob, so that, the temple and the altar having been destroyed, sacrificial victims are no longer offered among them. Not content with this explanation — because Judah means confession of the Lord —, they transfer the interpretation to the penitent, who, after he has confessed the Lord, if he sins, does abomination in Israel and in Jerusalem, in the mind that sees God and in the vision of peace. For he pollutes the mysteries of Christ, receiving his body and blood unworthily, because he has loved vices and has devised for himself strange gods, having as many gods as he has sins. According to the apostle Paul: The belly is the god of the gluttonous; and according to James: By whatever one is overcome, to that he is made subject. And he who has done this is driven out of the Church, and out of the tabernacles of Jacob, who supplants vices and sins, until he is humbled for his own good, and a victim is offered for him to the Lord Almighty.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.11":["Judah has transgressed; for this tribe returned from Babylon with the priests and Levites; and abomination has been done in Israel and in Jerusalem. Not at all among the ten tribes, which were held by the Assyrians, but among those which returned from Babylon by command of King Cyrus under Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah. Because Judah has defiled the sanctification of the Lord, which he loved, and has had the daughter of a strange god, by mingling with foreigners the seed of Israel and of the Gentiles; that is, by taking as wives the daughters of the heathen, who serve idols. Since, therefore, they have done this, the word of the prophet is directed to them, and they are drawn back from sin by curses.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.12":["May the Lord destroy the man who shall have done this. How beautifully he does not cut off pardon from error, nor did he say, May the Lord curse him who has done this, but, who shall have done, stretching the curse into the future, so as to call sinners to repentance. The teacher, he says, and the disciple, out of the tabernacles of Jacob, whether he be a priest or a layman, will be struck by one curse, and there will be no difference of punishment among those in whom the sins are equal. And him who offers a gift to the Lord of hosts: it is understood, may the Lord destroy him also who would wish to offer a gift at the altar for men of this kind, whose only remedy is not to do what they have done.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.13-14":["And this again you have done: you covered the altar of the Lord with tears, with weeping and groaning, so that I no longer look upon sacrifice, nor accept anything pleasing from your hand. And you have said: For what reason? Because the Lord has borne witness between you and the wife of your puberty, whom you have despised; and she is your partner and the wife of your compact. Did not one make, and is the remnant of the spirit his? And what does the one seek, but the seed of God? Guard therefore your spirit; and do not despise the wife of your youth. Since you have hated, put away, says the Lord God of Israel; but iniquity shall cover his garment, says the Lord of hosts. Guard your spirit, and do not despise. LXX: And these things which I hated, you have done: you covered the altar of the Lord with tears, with lamentation and groaning, from toil still worthy to look upon sacrifice, or to receive anything acceptable from your hands. And you have said: Wherefore? Because the Lord has borne witness between you and the wife of your youth, whom you have forsaken, and she is your partner, and the wife of your testament, and no other made her, and the remnant of your spirit. And you have said: What else does God seek but seed? And keep guard in your spirit, and do not forsake the wife of your youth; but if, hating her, you put her away, says the Lord God of Israel, impiety shall cover your thoughts, says the Lord Almighty; and keep guard in your spirit, and do not forsake. Let us interpret the history, and, fitting brief statements to the individual verses, let us set out what has been handed down to us by the Hebrews. The Israelite wives, forsaken, and seeing women of foreign nations in their husbands' beds, fled to the help of God alone; prostrate day and night before the altar of the Lord, with tears, groaning and wailing, they cast reproach upon his providence, that it did not look upon human affairs and did not help the wretched. Hence God says that he cannot accept sacrifice and sacrificial victims from the hands of the priests who committed these things, hindered as he is by the weeping and lamentations of the wives; and moreover that they ask for what reason he does not accept sacrifice from their hands; and at once he adds: Because the Lord has borne witness between you and the wife of your puberty, whom you have despised, saying: For this reason a man shall leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife, and the two shall be in one flesh. And therefore she is called partner, and wife of union and of compact, she who was made by God from the rib of the man.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.13":["What we have expounded specially, according to the book of Ezra, concerning the forsaken wives, others think is said generally against those who plunder what belongs to others and, gathering riches unjustly, dare to offer gifts to God, which he says he can by no means accept, prevented by the tears and weeping and groans of those who have been laid waste; and that this place agrees with that testimony: Honor God from your just labors. But they also insert this sense: that those who, because of the loss of their property, and the death of their children, and shipwreck, and the other things that belong to the loss of worldly goods, are turned to weeping and give themselves wholly to lamentation and groaning, and do not, by strength of mind and hope in God and the rewards of things to come, despise all things, are not received by him even if they direct prayer to God, because they are disfigured by lamentations unbecoming and unfitting for a man.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.14":["Now what follows, Because the Lord has borne witness between you and the wife of your puberty, or of your youth, whom you have despised; and she is your partner and the wife of your covenant, or of your testament, ; and no other made her: and the remnant of your spirit, they interpret thus, so as to call the wife of youth the natural understanding and the law written in the heart, which is implanted in all men. Whence also the nations, not having the law of God, do the things that are of the law. Of this spouse, they say, it is proclaimed in Proverbs: A wife is joined to a man by God; and we are commanded to drink from our own waters and fountains, and that no one be partner in the drinking, and that we rejoice with the wife of our youth. This wife impels even the credulous to say: May God judge and may God see; and to him I leave all things between me and you to be judged; of her Ecclesiastes also speaks: And live life with the woman whom you have loved, all the days of your vanity which have been given you under the sun.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.15-16":["This wife is the remnant of our spirit, because she is always joined to our sense; and if she withdraws from us, at once we offend God, and our impiety covers us. Hence again it is urged: Guard your spirit; not the flesh, for those who are in it cannot please God; not the soul: The natural for man does not receive the things that are of the spirit; but the spirit: Because the spirit intercedes for us with unutterable groanings.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.15":["And the remnant of his spirit, whether of God, as some think, or of the husband, as others suppose, because through affection one soul seems in a certain manner to be in two, joined in spirit, united in mind. Since, then, one made both, both the man and the woman, the union of both was therefore made by the Lord, that children might be born. For the one God, what does he seek but the seed of God, that is, sons begotten of the Israelite stock? Since, then, you have fruitful wives and rejoice in children, why do you seek beauty in wives, which is fitting for harlots, not for wives? So God commands through the prophet, and says: Guard your spirit, that you be not led away by lust, that you be not overcome by love of foreign women. And do not despise the wife of your youth, so that she who was first joined to you in virginal marriage may continue until old age.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.16":["But it could happen that the princes, priests, Levites and people would answer: God commanded through Moses that when we have hated our wives, we should put them away. And it is to be read: You tell me, it is written: Since you have hated, put away your wife, says the Lord God of Israel. And at once he answered: This was indeed commanded in the law, but because of the hardness of your heart. Which the Lord pursues more fully in the Gospel: But whoever, except for the cause of fornication, unjustly puts away his wife, iniquity shall cover his garment, that is, the body with which the soul is clothed, says the Lord of hosts, so that he may be punished in that very thing in which he sinned. Wherefore, this little question having been resolved, he presses home and repeats what he had said above: Guard your spirit, and do not despise, either the guarding of your spirit, or at any rate your wife, if she is poor or unsightly.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.2.17":["You have made the Lord labor with your words, and you have said: In what have we made him labor? In this, that you say: Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the Lord, and such please him; or at any rate, where is the God of judgement? LXX: You who have provoked the Lord with your words, and you have said: In what have we provoked him? In this, that you say: Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the Lord, and in these he is well pleased; and where is the God of justice? This passage is pursued more fully by Psalm 72 (73), whose beginning is: How good is the God of Israel to those who are of right heart. But my feet were almost moved; my steps had well-nigh been poured out. For I was jealous over the wicked, seeing the peace of sinners. For there is no regard for their death, and firmness in their stroke. They are not in the labor of men, and with men they will not be scourged. And then: And I said: Then in vain have I justified my heart, and washed my hands among the innocent. The people, then, having returned from Babylon, and seeing all the nations round about, and those very nations that serve the idols of Babylon, abounding in riches, vigorous in body, possessing in the world all the things that are thought good, while itself, which has the knowledge of God, is covered with squalor, hunger and servitude, is scandalized, and says: There is no providence in human affairs; all things are borne along by uncertain chance and are not governed by the judgement of God; rather, the wicked please him and the good displease him; or at any rate, if God judges all things, where is his equitable and just judgement? A question of this kind the mind that does not believe in things to come raises daily against God; and when it has seen the wicked powerful and the holy humble, the former abounding in all things, the latter not having even what is necessary for sustenance, and sometimes weighed down with deaf ears and blind eyes, and with sores in every part of their limbs, and with infirmity, such as we read of Lazarus in the Gospel, who before the doors of the rich man clothed in purple longed to sustain his needy soul with the crumbs that are thrown away from the leftovers of the tables; but the rich man of such savagery and cruelty that man did not pity man, whom even the tongue of the dogs pitied; not understanding the time of judgement, nor that those are the true goods which are everlasting, they say: The wicked please him, and where is the God of judgement?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.1":["Behold, I send my angel, and he will prepare the way before my face; and at once the Ruler whom you seek will come to his temple, and the angel of the testament whom you desire. LXX: Behold, I will send my angel, and he will prepare the way before my face; and suddenly the Lord whom you seek will come to his temple, and the angel of justice whom you desire. The Lord in the Gospel interpreted this of John the Baptist, saying: This is he of whom it is written: Behold, I will send my angel before your face, and he will prepare your way before me; nor did he use the same words which the LXX translators rendered. Mark the evangelist also, weaving together two testimonies, of Malachi and of Isaiah, under the utterance of one prophet, began thus: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, as it is written in Isaiah the prophet: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who will prepare your way. This, though in other words, we read in Malachi. But what follows, The voice of one crying in the desert, prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight, is said by Isaiah the prophet; and at once, explaining of whom both testimonies are said: There was, he says, John in the desert, baptizing and preaching the baptism of repentance, for the remission of sins. But the evangelists too interpreted the prophecy of the prophets concerning John, and while they agree in sense, they differ in words. For whereas the LXX rendered: Make the paths of our God straight, Mark and Luke said: Make his paths straight. But John: Make straight, he says, the way of the Lord. From which it is clear that the apostles and evangelists, and the Lord Savior himself, do not follow the authority of the LXX translators, which, having knowledge of the Hebrew tongue, they do not need, but translate from the Hebrew what they had read, not caring about the syllables and points of the words, provided that the truth of the sense is carried over. That they did this in many places we have indeed shown, and especially in that book which we entitled 'On the Best Kind of Translating' it has been proved by many testimonies together. This, then, which is said, Behold, I send my angel and he will prepare the way before my face, is said in the person of Christ, because he sent John into the desert of Judea to preach the baptism of repentance, for the remission of sins. But what follows, And at once the Ruler whom you seek will come to his temple, and the angel of the testament whom you desire, he thus speaks of himself as of another, according to the custom of the Scriptures. And no one doubts that this Ruler is the Savior, who is the Creator of all things, and is called the angel of the testament and the angel of great counsel. Others, however, think that what is said, Behold, I send my angel and he will prepare the way before my face, is said in the person of the Father; and what follows, At once the Ruler whom you seek will come to his temple, and the angel of the testament whom you desire, they understand to have been said by him of the Lord Savior. Now John prepares the way and makes the paths of our God straight in the hearts of believers, in which, because of depravity and unbelief, God could not walk before. And the temple they interpret either as the Church or as each one of the believers in the Church: Be built up as spiritual houses, a holy priesthood; to offer spiritual victims, and acceptable to God through Christ Jesus. And those who have believed: Do you not know that you are the temple of God, and the Spirit of God dwells in you? The Jews understand what is said, Behold, I send my angel, as said of Elijah the prophet, and what follows, At once the Ruler whom you seek will come to his temple, and the angel of the testament whom you desire, they refer to the ἠλειμμένον, that is, their Christ, who they say will come in the last time. But I wonder how the outcome of events does not teach them the truth. For what temple of his will the Ruler find, when it has been destroyed to its foundations? Or if it is to be built by another before Christ comes, what more will their Christ do, when all things have been restored by another? Our Lord in the Gospel, interpreting Elijah the prophet as John the Baptist, says: If you wish to know, he is Elijah who is to come, of whom also this same prophet whom we are now discussing writes at the end: Behold, I send you Elijah the prophet, before the great and terrible day of the Lord comes. And how John is Elijah, he has given us the way of understanding, recalling that he came in the power and spirit of Elijah.\n— Commentary on Malachi","Behold, he comes, says the Lord of hosts.\n\nAnd who will be able to think of the day of his coming? And who will stand to see him? For he is like a refining fire, and like fullers' herb. And he will sit refining and cleansing as silver, and he will purify the sons of Levi, and will strain them like gold and like silver, and they will be offering sacrifices to the Lord in justice. And the sacrifice of Judah and Jerusalem will please the Lord, as the days of the age, and as the ancient years. And I will draw near to you in judgement, and I will be a swift witness against sorcerers and adulterers, and perjurers, and those who wrong the hireling over his wages, the widows and orphans, and oppress the stranger, and have not feared me, says the Lord of hosts. For I am the Lord, and I do not change. LXX: Behold, he will come, says the Lord Almighty, and who will endure the day of his entrance? Or who will be able to bear to look upon him? For he enters like the fire of a smelter and like the herb of the washers; and he will sit refining and cleansing as silver, and as gold; and he will cleanse the sons of Levi, and will pour them out like gold and like silver, and they will be offering sacrificial victims to the Lord in justice; and the sacrifice of Judah and Jerusalem will please the Lord, as in the former days, and as in the earlier years. And I will draw near to you in judgement, and I will be a swift witness against the sorcerers, and against the adulterers, and against those who swear falsely by my name. And those who defraud hirelings of their wages, and oppress widows by their power, and strike orphans, and pervert the judgement of the sojourner, and who do not fear me, says the Lord Almighty, for I am the Lord your God, and I do not change. Above we read of a question raised against the Lord by those who say: Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the Lord, and such please him; or at any rate, if they displease him, where is the God of judgement? That is, where is the truth of justice? To resolve it the word of the Lord answered: I will send my angel, who will prepare my way, and the Ruler whom you seek will come to his temple, he who is the judge of truth, of whom it is said in the psalm: O God, give your judgement to the king, and your justice to the king's son. For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgement to the Son. And he is the angel of the testament whom you seek, whom evil things do not please, who does not accept persons in judgement, who is changed by no mercy and no severity: For in the hand of the Lord there is a cup of pure wine, full of mixture, and he has poured it from this into this, that is, mercy tempers justice, and justice mercy. This one, therefore, will come at once and swiftly to his temple, that is, the Church.\n— Commentary on Malachi","For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew","And because to the prophetic privilege there was added also the Baptist's reward, that he should baptize his own Lord, he therefore brings in an αὔξησιν (increase) of his merits, producing the testimony from Malachi in which he is even proclaimed an angel. But let us not think that John is called an angel here by a sharing of nature, but by the dignity of his office, that is, a messenger, because he announced the Lord who was to come.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAL.3.2":["And who will be able to think of the day of his coming? If no one can think of it, because of the power of his majesty, who will be able to bear it? And who will stand to see him, that is, who will be able with bleary and half-blind eyes to look upon him in his brightness, who is the Sun of justice, and in whose wings is healing? He will come like a refining fire, and like fullers' herb. Fire for shall burn before him, and round about him a mighty tempest. Then he will call the heaven from above, and the earth, that he may judge his people; rivers of fire will flow before him, rolling along every sinner. Now the Lord is called fire, and a consuming fire, that he may burn up our wood, hay and stubble. And not only fire, but also fullers' herb, which in Hebrew is called borith, and the LXX render πόαν, that is, fullers' herb. To those who sin gravely he is a refining and consuming fire; but to those who commit light sins, fullers' herb, so as to restore cleanness to those who have been washed, according to what is written in Isaiah: The Lord will wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion, and will cleanse the blood from their midst, by the spirit of judgement and by the spirit of burning. Those who have filth need the spirit of judgement, that they may be washed; those who are full of blood need the spirit of burning, that the blood which has been smeared on them from without may be taken away.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.3-4":["When they have been cleansed and strained, then they will offer just sacrifices to the Lord, and their sacrifice will please, which they offer for Judah and Jerusalem, that is, for those who confess the Lord and behold his peace with the mind, as the days of the age, and as the ancient years, so that as in the beginning they pleased God, so after sin and repentance they may begin to please, when they have been purged of every filth of sins.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.3":["And he will sit refining and cleansing as silver, so that, according to Ezekiel, whatever bronze, tin, iron and lead has been mixed into our gold and silver, that is, into our sense and our speech, may be thoroughly smelted in the furnace of the Lord, so that pure gold and silver may remain. Hence the Lord also says in the Gospel: I came to send fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn, and he will purify the sons of Levi. For it is time that judgement begin from the house of God. And elsewhere it is written: Begin from my holy ones. In the sons of Levi, understand the whole priestly dignity. But if the priests must be purified and strained, so that pure gold and silver may remain, what is to be said of the rest?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.5":["And I will draw near, he says, to you in judgement, and I will be a swift witness. How great is the dread of the judgement, since he himself is both witness and judge. And he is a witness against all sorcerers and adulterers, for these crimes are committed in secret, and therefore they are brought out into the open, lest they lie hidden long. After the sorcerers are placed the adulterers, after the adulterers the perjurers, after the perjurers those who wrong the hireling over his wages and will not pay what they owe for the labor of the work. Widows also and orphans they wrong, as is understood; and they oppress the stranger and the foreigner, or at any rate the catechumen, who has not yet been made a citizen in the city of Christ. And even if they have not done all these things, the one crime that they have not feared the Lord suffices for their punishment. Let us therefore by no means think them light sins, perjury, and not paying the laborer his wages, and wronging the widow and the orphan, and oppressing the foreigner and the stranger, which are compared to sorcery, and poisonings, and adultery. These things we understand according to the history and according to tropology, so that what was said at that time to the leaders of the Jews is now said to the leaders of the churches.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.6-7":["And you, sons of Jacob, have not been consumed.\n\nFor from the days of your fathers you have departed from my ordinances, and have not kept them. LXX: And you, sons of Jacob, have not departed from the sins of your fathers; you have turned aside from my ordinances, and have not kept them. He had said above: I will be a swift witness against the sorcerers and the adulterers and the perjurers, and those who wrong the hireling over his wages, the widows and the orphans, and oppress the stranger and have not feared me, says the Lord of hosts. And saying that he is a just judge, in that he does not accept persons in judgement, he added: I am the Lord, and I do not change. And the sense is: You change every day through sorceries and adulteries and perjuries and false accusations and violence; I in judgement am changed by no difference of persons. And though I confess that I am a severe and just judge, O sons of Jacob, yet you have not been consumed by the variety of torments, according to that which is written in Jeremiah: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not received discipline; nor do you do this lately, nor once, so that you might deserve pardon for error, but you hold an inherited impiety, departing from my ordinances from the days of your fathers, and not keeping what I commanded. But the sons of Jacob according to ἀναγωγὴν (anagogy), sons of the supplanter who snatched his brother's birthright, who do not depart from the sins of their fathers, and turn aside from the ordinances, and do not keep what has been commanded, let us understand as those who, though placed in the Church, do not depart from vices, and take to themselves the false name of the Christian religion.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.6":["And what follows, I am the Lord and I do not change, he sets down for this reason, because he had said above, He is like a refining fire, and like fullers' herb, lest we should think that he changes the nature of his divinity when for our sake he is named either angel, or fire, or borith.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.7":["We have heard the history of Ezra; now let us take up again the words of the prophet, looking more carefully whether prophecy and history agree. When it is said: Return to me, and I will return to you, says the Lord Almighty, it is clear that those whom he exhorts to come back had departed from the Lord. And see the mercy of the Lord: he promises like for like, that with the measure they have measured, it may be measured back to them. And just as it is written in Leviticus: If you walk perversely towards me, I too will walk against you with perverse fury, so now too he exhorts the people to return, so that he himself also may return to them. They, not understanding that they have departed from the Lord, shamelessly ask: In what shall we return? And they say: For when did we depart, that we should rightly be compelled to come back?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.8-10":["Return to me, and I will return to you, says the Lord of hosts. And you have said: In what shall we return?\n\nWill a man fix God, since you fix me? And you have said: In what have we fixed you? In tithes and in first fruits. In want you are cursed, and you have fixed me, the whole nation. Bring every tithe into the storehouse, and let there be food in my house, and prove me in this, says the Lord: if I do not open to you the floodgates of heaven, and pour out on you a blessing even to abundance. And I will rebuke for your sake the devourer, and he will not spoil the fruit of your vine, nor will the vine in the field be barren, says the Lord of hosts. And all the nations will call you blessed; for you will be a desirable land, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: Return to me, and I will return to you, says the Lord Almighty. And you have said: In what shall we return? Does a man supplant God, since you have supplanted me? And you have said: In what have we supplanted you? Because the tithes and the first fruits are with you, and you, seeing, look on, and you supplant me. The year is completed, and you have brought the fruits into your treasuries, and there will be plunder in your houses. Return, therefore, in this, says the Lord Almighty. If I do not open to you the floodgates of heaven, and pour out on you my blessing until it suffices, and I will deal out food to you, and I will not destroy the fruit of your land, and your vine in the field will not be weakened, says the Lord Almighty; and all the nations will call you blessed, because you will be as a willing land, says the Lord Almighty. We also said at the beginning that by the prophet Malachi Ezra the priest ought to be understood, and that all the things that are written in his history are contained in this volume too; and now we say that in the times of him and of Nehemiah — who clearly lived at the same time — there was a most grievous famine, and because of the famine an uprising, and that the poor, compelled by the necessity of their circumstances, sold their sons and daughters, and every possession, and their whole substance. In short, they say: Our sons and our daughters are very many; let us take grain as their price, and let us eat and live. And there were those who said: Let us pledge our fields and vineyards and our houses, and let us take grain in the famine. And I was very angry, says Ezra, when I had heard their outcry according to these words, and my heart took counsel with me, and I rebuked the nobles and the magistrates, and the rest. So in the time of famine the price of grain was very dear, so much so that they were compelled to sell their own sons; and both those who had little and those who had gathered much produce into their storehouses, by necessity or by the greatness of the price, were unwilling to render the tithes to the Levites, who had no share in the inheritance of Judea, but the first fruits and the tithes were their inheritance. And lest this be thought to be our own, let us set down the testimony of Ezra: And I learned, he says, that the portions of the Levites had not been given, and each one of the Levites, and of the singers, and of those who ministered, had fled to his own country. And I pleaded the case against the magistrates, and said: Why do we forsake the house of God? And I gathered them together, and made them stand in their stations. And all Judah brought the tithes of grain, wine and oil into the storehouses. And we set over the storehouses Shelemiah the priest and Zadok the scribe, and so on.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.8-12":["We can also interpret the tithes and first fruits thus: If someone learned and instructed in the law of God is able to instruct others, he ought not to attribute it to his own prudence and to the talent that he possesses; but let him give thanks first to God, who bestows all things, then to his priests and teachers, by whom he was taught. For if he does not give thanks, but claims knowledge for himself, he will be cursed in want. But if, understanding God to be the giver, and giving thanks to those through whom he was instructed by God, he humbles himself and brings food into the storehouse of God, that is, ministers the nourishment of holy Scripture to the peoples in the Church, at once the floodgates of heaven will be opened over him, and spiritual rain will be poured out; and God will command his clouds to rain a shower upon him, and he will enjoy abundance of all things; and he will also rebuke for his sake the devourer, that is, the opposing powers, and his labour will bring forth fruit, and he will attain that which is written: Blessed is he who speaks into the ears of those who hear. He will also lift up his eyes and see the regions, that they are already white for the harvest; and he will gather fruits unto eternal life. Nor will the vine be barren in his field, he who says in the Gospel: I am the vine. And he who speaks through the prophet: I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true; and such — through the confession of humility, and the giving of thanks to God and to the teachers of his Church — blessedness will he attain that all nations will call him blessed, and those who have heard him discoursing in the Church will long to dwell in his land and his teaching.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.8-9":["What we have said of the tithes and first fruits, which were once given by the people to the priests and Levites, understand also of the peoples of the Church, who have been commanded not only to give tithes and first fruits, but also to sell all that they have and give to the poor and follow the Lord and Savior. But if we are unwilling to do this, let us at least imitate the beginnings of the Jews, so that we give to the poor a part of the whole, and pay due honour to the priests and Levites. Hence the Apostle too says: Honour widows who are widows indeed; and that the presbyter is to be honoured with double honour, especially he who labours in the word and in the teaching of God. Whoever does not do this is convicted of defrauding and supplanting God, and he is cursed in the want of all things, so that he who has sown sparingly will also reap sparingly, and he who has sown in blessing will gather fruits abundantly in blessings. If ever famine and want and the lack of all things oppress the world, let us know that this comes down from the wrath of God, who says that he is defrauded in the poor, if they do not receive alms, and is deprived of his own possession.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.8":["The Lord answered: Will a man fix God, since you fix me? The Hebrew word that is written haiecba the LXX translated 'if he supplants', in place of which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion put 'if he defrauds', so that the sense is: Does a man defraud God, since you defraud me? And indeed, according to the order of the history, because the people were not rendering the tithes and the first fruits to the Levites, the Lord says that he himself suffers fraud, since his ministers, compelled by famine and want, had deserted the temple. For if it is he who is visited in prison through others, and taken in when sick, and receives food and is given drink when hungry and thirsty, why should he himself not receive tithes in his ministers; and, if they are not given, himself be deprived of his share? This haiecba of which we have spoken is translated in the language of the Syrians and Chaldeans as 'if he will fix'; whence we too translated it so many years ago, referring it more to the mystery of the Lord's passion, in which men crucified God, than to the tithes and first fruits that are written of. Let the prudent reader ask how our translation agrees with what follows: In tithes and in first fruits, and let him see whether we can say this: That you might fix me to the cross, that you might lay wicked hands on your God, you brought about by the premeditation of many things beforehand, by withholding the tithes and first fruits, I do not say from my priests and Levites, but from me, who commanded through Moses that they be given. Let this be said by us about a single word, as we leave the judgement of its interpretation to the reader's discretion; now let us follow the order of the prophecy.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.9":["Because you have not rendered to me the tithes and first fruits, therefore you are cursed in famine and want, and you supplant me, or defraud and deprive me, the whole nation. For 'nation', which in the Hebrew is written aggoi, the Seventy translated 'year', ἔτος instead of ἔθνος. And the sense is: Behold, the year is completed, and you have carried nothing into my treasuries, but everything into your own storehouses. And for the sake of tithes and first fruits, which were small things if they had been given by you, you have lost the richness of your possessions and all abundance of produce.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.10":["But so that you may know that this was brought about by my anger, because you defrauded me of my share, I exhort you and admonish you to bring the tithes into the storehouses, that is, into the treasuries of the temple, and let the priests and Levites who minister to me have food; and prove me, whether I do not pour out such great rains that the floodgates of heaven will be believed to have been opened. And I will pour out on you a blessing even to abundance. The word 'pouring out' shows the name of bounty.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.11-12":["But it can happen that, with rains watering the fields, there is indeed fertility; but either the locust comes, or the cankerworm, or blight, or the caterpillar, and the labours of men perish; therefore he joins to it and says: And I will rebuke for your sake the devourer, the locust, that is, and the rest that we have mentioned: And he will not spoil the fruit of your land. The vine also will fill the winepresses, and all the nations round about will marvel at the fertility of your land, so much so that all will long to dwell in it, and you will be an example to all nations of abundance in all things.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.13-15":["Your words have grown strong against me, says the Lord, and you have said: What have we spoken against you? You have said: Vain is he who serves God, and what profit is it that we have kept his commandments, and that we have walked sorrowful before the Lord of hosts? So now we call the arrogant blessed, since those who work impiety have been built up, and they have tempted God, and have been saved. LXX: You have made your words heavy against me, says the Lord. And you have said: In what have we spoken against you? And you have said: Vain is he who serves God; and what more. Because we have kept his commands, and because we have walked as suppliants before the face of the Lord Almighty? And now we call strangers blessed, and all who work iniquity are built up, and they have resisted God, and have been saved. He had said earlier: You have made the Lord labour with your words, and you have said, in what have we made him labour? In this, when you said: Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the Lord, and such men please him; or else, where is the God of judgement? Now he repeats the same thing more fully. For the people that had returned from Babylon seemed to have knowledge of God, and to observe the law, and to understand its sin, and to offer victims for sin, to render tithes, to observe the sabbath, and the other things that are commanded by the law of God; seeing all the nations round about abounding in all things, while itself was placed in want and famine and misery, it was scandalized at each thing, and said: What does it profit me that I worship the one and true God, abhor idols, and, pricked by the consciousness of my offences, walk sorrowful before God? This passage, as we said above, Psalm 72 (73) pursues more fully and at greater length.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.13":["Hence the prophet, who is a spiritual physician, heals all the wounds, and solemnly declares that the words of blasphemy fall back upon the Creator, and he says in the person of God: Your words have grown strong against me, or have been made heavy; for indeed, according to Zechariah, iniquity sits upon a talent of lead, and what is said against God is weighed down by the heavy mass of blasphemies. They, not understanding their most grievous words and their blasphemy, ask, what have we spoken against you?\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.14-15":["To them the Lord answered: You have said: Vain is he who serves God, and what profit is it that we have kept his commandments? In the present age they demand a wage for the service of God; therefore they do not receive it. And that we have walked sorrowful before the Lord, according to that which is written in the psalms: All the day long I went about sorrowful; therefore we call the arrogant blessed, who are proud against God and hurl impious words of blasphemies. Since they have been built up, and after their crimes and blasphemies enjoy all prosperity, they have tempted God, or resisted God, and have been saved. They think that salvation is the happiness of the present age. And for that reason they are deceived by errors. This we can also understand of the heretics Marcion and Valentinus, and the others who do not receive the Old Testament and speak against the Creator of the world, that they make progress in their impiety and have many companions in crime. Scandalized at them, those who persevere in the Church, and not knowing the causes of God's judgement, weave together the words of the prophet.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.16":["Then those who feared God spoke, each one with his neighbour. LXX: These things those who fear the Lord spoke, each one to his neighbour. According to the Hebrews it is to be understood thus: while those men were blaspheming about the judgement of God, those who fear God spoke to one another, saying that the recompense of the good or the evil is not in the present and brief age, but in the future and eternal one; and that man cannot know the judgements of God, and dispute about his equity and justice, and the other things that a just man ought to speak with a just man. Nor did he say what they spoke, but from the fact that he added: Then those who feared God spoke, each one with his neighbour, we ought to understand that those who feared God spoke the things that are contained in the voices of all the Scriptures. According to the LXX, however, it is to be read εἰρωνικῶς (ironically), and with a lowered voice, so that we say: These things those who fear the Lord spoke, each one to his neighbour; that is, those who boast in vain and say, what profit is it that we have kept his commandments, and have walked sorrowful before the Lord? For if they feared the Lord, they would not say such things.\n— Commentary on Malachi","And the Lord attended and heard, and a book of remembrance was written before him for those who fear the Lord, and think upon his name.\n\nAnd they shall be mine, says the Lord of hosts, on the day that I make, for a special possession, and I will spare them, as a man spares his son who serves him. And you shall turn, and you shall see what difference there is between the just and the impious, and between him who serves God and him who does not serve him. LXX: And the Lord attended and heard; and he wrote a book of remembrance in his sight for those who fear the Lord, and who revere his name. And they shall be mine, says the Lord Almighty, on the day that I make, for an acquisition, and I will choose them as a man chooses his son who serves him; and you turn, and you shall see what difference there is between the just and the unjust, and between him who serves God and him who does not serve him. While the just and those who fear the Lord were saying these things, each with his neighbour, men who were willing neither to hear nor to speak words of blasphemy, the Lord attended and heard, and a book of remembrance was written before him for those who fear and think upon his name, so that when the day of judgement comes, he may render both punishments to the blasphemers and rewards to those who fear him. Now the book was written of which we read in Daniel: Thrones were set, and the books were opened.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.17":["And those who fear the Lord shall be to the Lord of hosts for a special possession, on the day when the time of judgement arrives. For 'special possession' in the Hebrew is read sgolla, which Aquila translated περιούσιον (special), and the others περιποίησιν (possession). Those, therefore, who fear the Lord, who spoke with their neighbour and answered the words of blasphemy, will be on the day of judgement for a special possession, and he will spare them, because every man is under sin. Or else he will choose them, as a man is wont to choose his son who serves him. In this there is a twofold affection, both of fatherly love toward a son and of service toward a servant.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.3.18":["And then you who now blaspheme and say: What profit is it that we have kept his commandments, and have walked sorrowful before the Lord? will recognize your own misery too from their election and blessedness, and, turned to repentance, you will see what difference there is between the just and the impious, and between him who serves God and him who does not serve him.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.4.1":["For behold, the day will come, kindled like a furnace; and all the proud and all who work impiety will be stubble; and the coming day will set them ablaze, says the Lord of hosts, which will not leave them root and shoot. And for you who fear my name the Sun of justice will rise, and healing in his wings; and you will go forth, and leap like calves from the herd. And you will tread down the impious when they are ash under the soles of your feet, on the day that I make, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: For behold, the day comes burning like an oven, and it will burn them up; and all the foreigners, and all who work iniquity, will be stubble; the coming day will set them on fire, says the Lord Almighty, and neither root nor branch will be left in them. And for you who fear my name the Sun of justice will rise, and healing in his wings and you will go forth, and leap like calves loosed from their bonds, and you will trample down the unjust, and they will be ash among your feet, on the day that I make, says the Lord Almighty. Because the impious made me labour with their words and said: Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the Lord, and such men please him; and vain is he who serves God; and: What profit is it that we have kept his commandments and walked sorrowful before the Lord of hosts? And those who feared God spoke against their rash words, and each one conferred with his neighbour about the things that pertain to the fear of God: the Lord attended and heard, and a book of remembrance was written for those who fear the Lord and think upon his name, and he promises that when the day comes he will have them for a special possession, and will spare them as a man spares his son who serves him, and he drives it home more fully and says: Behold, the day will come, that is, the day of judgement, which will be light to the saints and darkness to sinners; and that day will be kindled like a furnace, or an oven, so that all the impious are turned into stubble and burned up by the heat of the furnace. And when it has set them ablaze and burned them up, it will not leave in them any root and shoot of wickedness. This is about the impious, what they will suffer on the day of judgement.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.4.2":["On the contrary, it is said what will befall those who fear the name of God: And for you who fear my name the Sun of justice will rise. He will judge all things truly, and will allow neither good things nor evil, neither virtues nor vices, to lie hidden. And healing will be in his wings, so that he may carry on his shoulders those healed through repentance, according to that which is written in Deuteronomy: Spreading out his wings he took them up, and carried them on his shoulders. Then those who are now shut up in the world as in a prison will go forth, and they will leap like calves from the herd, or like calves freed from their bonds. Hence the Apostle too says: I desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ, so that he may go forth and leap like a calf freed from its bonds, and like a victim of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.4.3":["Nor is he content with this end of joy, but he will tread down the impious when they are ash. Hence also there is this prayer for the just: And may God crush Satan under your feet swiftly. Abraham spoke to the Lord, perceiving that in comparison with the divine majesty he was ash: I am earth and ash; and therefore he will see the Sun of justice and will rest in the midst of his lots, and, carried by him, will be lifted up to the heavenly places. But he who through pride said: I will set my seat above the stars, I will be like the Most High, will be brought down to the earth, and will be like ash under the feet of the saints, when the day of the Lord who judges has come.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.4.4":["Remember the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded him in Horeb for all Israel, precepts and judgements. LXX: likewise. In the future there will be a just retribution of deserts, when the devouring flame will consume and burn up the root and the shoot of the proud, and for those who fear the Lord the Sun of justice will rise, and healing in his wings. Therefore remember the law of Moses my servant, which I gave him on Mount Horeb, which is Sinai, for all Israel, precepts and judgements. But since the Apostle says: We know that the law is spiritual, and blessed David: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law; and since all ate the heavenly manna spiritually, and the whole people of Israel drank from the same spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ; those who believe in Christ ought to keep spiritually the precepts of the law which he gave in Horeb, which is interpreted 'dryness', through which the moisture of all vices is boiled away, and at the rays of the Sun of justice the flux of lust is dried up. Now the Lord spoke to all Israel, which with the mind sees God, and of whom it is said in the Gospel: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"MAL.4.5-6":["Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the great and terrible day of the Lord comes; and he will turn the heart of the fathers to the sons, and the heart of the sons to their fathers, lest perhaps I come and strike the earth with anathema. LXX: Behold, I will send you Elijah the Tishbite, before the great and illustrious day of the Lord comes, who will turn the heart of the father to the son, and the heart of a man to his neighbor, lest perhaps, coming, I strike the earth utterly. After Moses — whose commandments, as we have taught, are to be kept spiritually — he says that Elijah is to be sent, signifying by Moses the law and by Elijah prophecy, as Abraham says to a certain rich man clad in purple: They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them. And the Lord and Savior, transfigured on the mountain, had Moses and Elijah in white garments speaking with him, and they told him what he was to suffer in Jerusalem. For the law and the whole choir of the prophets proclaim the passion of Christ. Therefore, before the day of judgement comes and the Lord strikes the earth with anathema, or utterly, or suddenly, as the LXX translated — for this is what ἄρδην means — the Lord will send, in Elijah — who is interpreted 'my God', and is from the town of Tishbe, which means conversion and penitence — the whole choir of the prophets, to turn the heart of the fathers to the sons, namely of Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and of all the patriarchs, so that their descendants may believe in the Lord Savior, in whom they too believed: For Abraham saw the day of the Lord and was glad; or the heart of the father to the son, that is, the heart of God to everyone who has received the spirit of adoption. And the heart of the sons to their fathers, so that both Jews and Christians, who now disagree among themselves, may agree in an equal religion in Christ. Hence it is said to the apostles, who brought forth the seedbed of the Gospel in the whole world: In place of your fathers, sons have been born to you. For if Elijah has not first turned the heart of the fathers to the sons, and the heart of the sons to their fathers, when the great and terrible day of the Lord comes — great for the saints, terrible for sinners — the true and just judge will strike, not heaven, nor those who dwell in heaven, but the earth with anathema: those who do earthly works. The Jews and the Judaizing heretics think that before their ἠλειμμένον (Anointed One) Elijah will come and restore all things. Hence the question is also put to Christ in the Gospel: What of the Pharisees saying that Elijah is to come? To whom he answered: Elijah indeed will come, and if you believe, he has already come, understanding John in Elijah.\n— Commentary on Malachi","And the disciples asked him, saying: Why then do the scribes say that Elijah must come first? Unless we know the causes why the disciples asked about the name of Elijah, their question seems foolish and out of place. For what has it to do with the things written above, to ask about the coming of Elijah? It is a tradition of the Pharisees, according to the prophet Malachi, who is the last of the twelve, that Elijah will come before the coming of the Savior and turn back the heart of the fathers to the sons and of the sons to the fathers, and restore all things to their ancient state. The disciples therefore suppose that this transformation of glory is the one they had seen on the mountain, and they say: If you have already come in glory, how is it that your forerunner does not appear? especially since they had seen Elijah withdraw. But when they add: The scribes say that Elijah must come first, by saying first they show that, unless Elijah has come, it is not the coming of the Savior according to the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"MAL.4.5":["There are those who think that John is called Elijah for this reason: that, as at the second coming of the Savior, according to Malachi, Elijah is to go before and announce the judge who is to come, so John did at the first coming, and each is a messenger, either of the first coming of the Lord or of the second.\n— Commentary on Matthew"]}});
